SGO_COMMENTARY.push({"id":"jerome-more-isa-lam","author":"St Jerome","work":"Commentary on Matthew; Commentary on Isaiah; Commentary on Ezekiel; Homilies on Psalms; and other works","translator":"New English translations from the Historical Christian Faith Writings Database, made from Migne's Greek and Latin text or the 19th-century Russian and dedicated to the public domain, many of them machine-assisted; plus public-domain ANF/NPNF texts","lang":"en","license":"Public Domain","source":"https://github.com/HistoricalChristianFaith/Commentaries-Database (Jerome/)","versification":"kjv","tradition":"west","notes":{"ISA.1.1":["No one, when he will have seen the Prophets to be written in verses, would think them to be bound in meter among the Hebrews, and to have anything in common with the Psalms or the works of Solomon. But what is customary to be used in Demosthenes and Cicero, as they are written in words with divisions, who certainly wrote prose and not in verses, we also, providing ease of reading, have divided a new translation with a new kind of writing. And first, knowing of Isaiah what is presented in his speech, certainly as a man noble and of urbane elegance he does not have anything of rusticity mixed into (his) speech. For this reason it happens that in comparison with others the translation was not able to preserve the flower of his speech. And then adding this, that it is being spoken not only by a prophet, but by an evangelist. For thus all the mysteries of Christ and the Church are pursued to clarity, so that you would not think them to be prophesied of the future, but they covered the history of things past. For this reason I suppose the Seventy interpreters to have been unwilling at that that time to set forth clearly for the gentiles the sacraments of their faith, not throwing holy things to dogs or pearls to swine, which things, when you will have read this edition, you will note were hidden by them.\nNor am I unaware of how much work it is to understand the Prophets, or for anyone not to be easily able to judge from the translation, unless he will have before understood those things which he will have read, and we to suffer from the bites of many, who, being goaded by jealousy, what they are not able to follow, they despise. Therefore, knowing and being wise, I place my hand in the fire, and nevertheless I pray this for the scornful readers: that just as the Greeks after the Seventy translators read Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion, either for study of their doctrines or so that they better understand the Seventy through their collation, that these are deemed worthy to have at least one translator after the earlier ones. Reading first and afterward despising, they are seen not to condemn by judgment, but rather by the ignorant presumption of hatred.\nAnd Isaiah prophesied in Jerusalem and Judea when the ten tribes had not yet been led into captivity, and the oracle covered both kingdoms, now together, now separately. And while he sometimes looks at present history, and indicated the return of the people to Judea after the Babylonian captivity, yet is all his concern for the calling of the nations and for the coming of Christ. Who, how much the more you love, O Paula and Eustochium, the more strive for him, so that for the present disparagement, by which the envious incessantly tear me into pieces, the same One may restore a reward to me in the future, Who knows me to have exerted myself in the learning of foreign languages: the Jews might not jump all day on the errors of the Scriptures in His Church.\n— Prologue to Isaiah","Having at last, after a long time, completed the twenty books of explanations on the twelve Prophets, and the Commentaries on Daniel, you compel me, Eustochium, virgin of Christ, to pass on to Isaiah, and to render to you what I promised your holy mother Paula while she was living. I remember, indeed, that I promised it also to your brother Pammachius, a most learned man; and though you are his equal in affection, you surpass him by being present. And so I pay to you, and through you to him, what I owe, obeying the commands of Christ, who says: Search the Scriptures (John 5:39); and, Seek, and you shall find (Matt 7:7). Let me not hear, along with the Jews: You err, not knowing the Scriptures, nor the power of God (Matt 22:29). For if, according to the apostle Paul (1 Cor 1:24), Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God, and he who does not know the Scriptures does not know the power of God and his wisdom, then ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ. Therefore, supported by the help of your prayers, you who meditate day and night on the law of God and are a temple of the Holy Spirit, I will imitate the householder who brings forth from his treasure things new and old, and the bride who says in the Song of Songs: New and old, my kinsman, I have kept for you (Song 7:13); and I will so expound Isaiah as to show him to be not only a Prophet but an evangelist and an Apostle. For he himself says of himself and of the other evangelists: How beautiful are the feet of those who preach good tidings, who preach peace (Isa 52:7). And God speaks to him as to an Apostle: Whom shall I send, and who will go to this people? And he answered: Here am I, send me (Isa 6:8).\n\nAnd let no one think that I wish to comprehend the subject of this volume in a brief discourse, since the present Scripture contains all the mysteries of the Lord, and proclaims Emmanuel both born of a Virgin and the doer of illustrious works and signs, dead and buried, and rising again from the lower world, and the Savior of all the nations. What shall I say of physics, ethics and logic? Whatever belongs to the holy Scriptures, whatever the human tongue can utter and the understanding of mortals receive, is contained in this volume. Of its mysteries he who wrote it himself testifies: And the vision of all shall be to you as the words of a sealed book, which, when they give it to one who knows letters, they shall say: Read this. And he shall answer: I cannot, for it is sealed. And the book shall be given to one who does not know letters, and it shall be said to him, Read, and he shall answer, I do not know letters (Isa 29:11-12). Whether, then, you give this book to the People of the nations, who do not know letters, it will answer: I cannot read, because I have not learned the letters of the Scriptures; or whether you give it to the Scribes and Pharisees, who boast that they know the letters of the Law, they will answer: We cannot read, because the book is sealed. Why then is it sealed to them? Because they did not receive him whom the Father sealed (John 6:27), who has the key of David: Who opens, and no one shuts; who shuts, and no one opens (Rev 3:7).\n\nNor indeed, as Montanus dreams along with his mad women, did the Prophets speak in ecstasy, so as not to know what they were saying, and, while they instructed others, themselves to be ignorant of what they said. Of such the Apostle says: Understanding neither what they say, nor the things about which they affirm (1 Tim 1:7). But, according to Solomon, who speaks in Proverbs: The wise man understands what he brings forth from his mouth, and on his lips he will carry knowledge (Prov 16:23), they too knew what they were saying. For if the Prophets were wise, which we cannot deny; and Moses, instructed in all wisdom, spoke to the Lord, and the Lord answered him; and of Daniel it is said to the prince of Tyre: Are you wiser than Daniel (Ezek 28:3)? and David was wise, who gloried in the Psalm: The uncertain and hidden things of your wisdom you have made manifest to me (Ps 50 [51]:8): how did the wise Prophets, like brute animals, not know what they were saying? We read also in another place of the Apostle: The spirits of the Prophets are subject to the Prophets (1 Cor 14:32), so that they have it in their own power when to be silent and when to speak. And if this seems weak to anyone, let him hear this from the same Apostle: Let two or three Prophets speak, and let the others judge; but if something is revealed to another who is sitting, let the first be silent (ibid., 29). By what reasoning can they keep silence, if it is in the power of the spirit that speaks through the Prophets either to be silent or to speak? If, then, they understood what they said, all things are full of wisdom and reason. Nor was it air struck by a voice that reached their ears; but God spoke in the mind of the Prophets, according to what another Prophet says: The angel who spoke in me (Zech 1:9); and: Crying in our hearts, Abba, Father (Gal 4:6); and: I will hear what the Lord God speaks in me (Ps 84 [85]:9).\n\nHence, after the truth of the history, all things are to be taken spiritually: and so Judea and Jerusalem, Babylon and Philistia, and Moab and Damascus, Egypt and the desert of the sea, Edom and Arabia, and the valley of Vision, and finally Tyre, and the Vision of the four-footed beasts, are to be understood in such a way that we seek everything in the sense, and that in all of these Paul the apostle, as a wise architect, may lay the foundation, which is no other than Christ Jesus. And it is a task of great labor and toil to wish to expound the whole book of Isaiah, over which the talents of our elders have sweated: of the Greeks, I mean. Among the Latins, however, there is a great silence, apart from the martyr Victorinus of holy memory, who could say with the Apostle: Though I be unskilled in speech, yet not in knowledge (2 Cor 11:6). For Origen wrote on this Prophet, according to the four editions, as far as the Vision of the four-footed beasts in the desert, thirty volumes, of which the twenty-sixth book is not found. There are also current under his name two other books on the Vision of the τετραπόδων, addressed to Grata, which are thought to be pseudepigraphal; and twenty-five Homilies, and Σημειώσεις, which we may call Excerpts. Eusebius Pamphili also published fifteen volumes according to the historical explanation; and Didymus, whose friendship we lately enjoyed, published eighteen tomes from the place where it is written: Comfort, comfort my people, you priests; speak to the heart of Jerusalem (Isa 40:1), to the end of the volume. Apollinaris, however, after his manner, so expounds everything that he runs through it all, and with certain points and intervals, or rather abridgments, flies past the stretches of a great road, so that we think we are reading not so much Commentaries as indexes of chapters. From this you perceive how difficult it is that our Latins, whose ears are fastidious, who are nauseated at understanding the holy Scriptures and are delighted only by the applause of eloquence, should pardon me if I speak at some length, since Isaiah, by the number of his verses, is either equal to the twelve Prophets or greater. And wherever, passing over the Seventy, I have disputed from the Hebrew, the reason is that most things are either the same as or similar to the others, and, having set forth the twofold edition, I did not wish to lengthen the books of explanation, which even in a single explanation exceed the measure of brevity. But now let us carry out what we have set before us.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The vision of Isaiah, the son of Amoz, which he saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem, in the days of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah, kings of Judah. For Judah, in which two tribes are signified, the Seventy and Theodotion put Judea, which denotes the whole land of the twelve tribes. And for what we have translated from the Hebrew, concerning Judah and Jerusalem, they translated, against Judea and Jerusalem. Symmachus, more plainly as is his custom, has about Judah and Jerusalem, so that he would have the title point to neither prosperous nor adverse things, but to those things which the prophetic word foretold about Judah and Jerusalem in either direction. Isaiah therefore speaks principally of two tribes, Judah and Benjamin, apart from the ten which were in Samaria and were called Ephraim and Israel, and which Pul king of the Assyrians had already begun to lay waste under Uzziah king of Judah and Jerusalem. Then, in the fifty-second year of his reign, while Pekah son of Remaliah was reigning in Samaria, Tiglath-pileser king of the Assyrians came and took Ijon and Abel, the house of Maacah, and Janoah and Kedesh and Hazor and Gilead and Galilee, all the land of Naphtali, and carried them away to the Assyrians (2 Kgs 15:29). From this it is shown that all these things are told, when the overthrow of Samaria was near, as a warning to the two tribes.\n\nNow Uzziah is the same man who is also Azariah, with a double name. And from the kings who are set in the title we learn that Isaiah, Hosea, Joel and Amos prophesied at one and the same time; but the beginning of the word of the Lord was in Hosea the son of Beeri. Amos, however, the father of Isaiah, is not, as most suppose, the third of the twelve Prophets, but another; and among the Hebrews they are written with different letters. This one has as its first and last letters ALEPH and SADE (אמוץ); that one AIN and SAMECH (עמוס); and this one, according to some, means strength or robust; that one a hard people or heavy: about which we have spoken more fully in Amos. And not only this prophet, but others too, though they have in their title: The vision which Isaiah saw, or Obadiah, do not go on to say what they saw (for example: I saw the Lord Sabaoth sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, and two Seraphim round about him), but they relate the things that were said, that is: Hear, O heaven, and give ear, O earth (Isa 6:1, 2). And: Thus says the Lord God to Edom. I have heard a hearing from the Lord, and he has sent an envoy to the nations (Obad 1:1). For the Prophets were formerly called seers, who could say: Our eyes are ever toward the Lord (Ps 24 [25]:15). And: To you have I lifted up my eyes, who dwell in heaven (Ps 122 [123]:1). Hence also the Apostles are commanded by the Savior: Lift up your eyes and see the fields, for they are already white for the harvest (John 4:35). These eyes of the heart the bride too had in the Song of Songs, to whom the bridegroom says: You have wounded my heart, my sister, my bride, with one of your eyes (Song 4:9). And in the Gospel we read: The lamp of your body is your eye (Matt 6:22). In the old Instrument also it is said that the people saw the voice of God (Exod 20:18). So let the ravings of Montanus fall silent, who thinks that the Prophets spoke of things to come in ecstasy and in madness of heart; for they could not see what they did not know. I know that some interpret Judea and Jerusalem of heavenly things, and Isaiah as in the person of the Lord Savior: that he foretells the captivity of that province in our land, and afterwards its return and ascent to the holy mountain in the last days. We judge these things contrary to the faith of Christians and reject them altogether; and, following the truth of the history, we interpret spiritually in such a way that whatever they dream about the heavenly Jerusalem we refer to the Church of Christ, and to those who either go out from it because of their sins or return through repentance to their former seat; of which we think this too is said in this same Prophet: Be enlightened, be enlightened, O Jerusalem, for your light has come, and the glory of the Lord has risen upon you; and, In you the Lord will appear, and his glory will be shown upon you; and kings will walk in your light, and the nations in your brightness; when all her sons come from afar, and her daughters will be carried on the shoulders; and the camels of Midian and Ephah will cover her, bringing gold and frankincense; and the flocks of Kedar will be gathered in Jerusalem, and the rams of Nebaioth will hasten, and they will be acceptable victims upon the altar of the Lord (Isa 60:1ff.)\n\nAnd as for its being set in the title that he prophesied under Uzziah and Jotham and Ahaz and Hezekiah, kings of Judah, this is not to be understood confusedly, as in other Prophets, so that we do not know what was said specifically under which king; rather, up to the end of the volume it is recorded in writing what was revealed to him by the Lord separately under Uzziah, and what under Jotham, and what under Ahaz, and what under Hezekiah. Let us know also that Hezekiah began to reign in Jerusalem in the twelfth year of Romulus, who founded in Italy the city of his own name, so that it may clearly appear how much more ancient our histories are than those of all other nations. Now Isaiah means saved of the Lord; Judah, confession; Jerusalem, vision of peace; Uzziah, strength of the Lord; Jotham, perfection of the Lord; Ahaz, holding or robust; Hezekiah, dominion of the Lord. He, then, who is saved under the Lord's protection, and is the son of Amoz, that is, of the strong and robust, spiritually beholds the vision of confession while he bewails his old sins, and of peace when after repentance he passes over to the light and rests in eternal peace; and all his times pass under the strength of the Lord and under his perfection and might. And when he has done all things, he will say that word of the Gospel: We are unprofitable servants; for we have done what we ought to do (Luke 17:10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.2":["Hear, O heaven, and give ear, O earth: for the Lord has spoken. Above, it was shown by the title who the prophet was, whose son, what he saw against Judah and Jerusalem, or concerning Judah and Jerusalem, and at what time; now he summons heaven and earth to listen. By heaven he signifies the supernal and angelic powers; by earth the race of mortals, μετωνυμικῶς, naming the things contained by the things that contain them. Or else because the Lord, through Moses, had called heaven and earth as witnesses when he gave his law to the people of Israel, and had said: Attend, O heaven, and I will speak; let the earth hear the words of my mouth (Deut 32:1), after the transgression of the people he calls the same again to witness, so that all the elements may know that God has been justly roused to anger in vengeance for his commandments. For heaven the Hebrew SAMAIM (שמים) sounds heavens, in the plural number, especially since he said hear, that is, SEMU (שמעו), which is expressed in the plural number, not the singular. But some hold that it is indeed said heavens in the plural but understood in the singular, as we call single cities Thebes and Athens. And it is an idiom of the Hebrew script that all words ending in the syllable IM (ים) are masculine and plural, like Cherubim and Seraphim; and those in OTH (ות), feminine and plural, like Sabaoth. And this too is to be noted, that to the heavens it is said, hear, to the earth, give ear; for the things that are on high have greater understanding, while the lower ones are wrapped in earthly senses. Hence the Savior too says in the Gospel: He who has ears to hear, he says, let him hear (Matt 11:15). If anyone, then, is heaven and has his citizenship in the heavenly places, let him hear mystically the things that are said; if anyone is earthly, let him follow the simple history. This also is to be observed, that he did not say: Hear, O heaven, and give ear, O earth, what the Lord has spoken to you, but what he has spoken to me, so that what I have heard in the spirit I may report to you, who do not deserve to hear him speaking himself. Some [the Origenists] think that heaven and earth are summoned to listen as if they were living beings, according to what is said of the earth in another place: Who looks upon the earth and makes it tremble (Ps 103 [104]:32); whereas this belongs to the power of God, not to an understanding in the earth.\n\nSons have I begotten and exalted, but they have despised me. For which Symmachus and Theodotion translated thus: Sons have I nourished, and exalted (Exod 4:22). From this point the Prophet relates what the Lord has spoken: that the people of Israel, whom he had created as servants under the common law, he changed into sons, and said: Israel is my firstborn son. Then too the Lord in the Gospel promises the Apostles that if they do his will he will no longer call them servants but friends (John 15:15). But if Israel should grow proud on hearing that he is the firstborn, let him understand that he is called firstborn for this reason, that second sons are signified from among the nations. For he is not called only-begotten, which would exclude other brothers, but firstborn, to show that others too will follow; and yet, according to the mysteries of the Scriptures, it is not the firstborn who receive the inheritance but the second. Cain was the firstborn, but the gifts of Abel pleased God. Ishmael was the firstborn, but Isaac received the inheritance. Esau was the firstborn, but Jacob the supplanter snatched away the father's blessing. Reuben was the firstborn, but the blessing of the seed of Christ is nevertheless transferred to Judah. Thus they were first according to the order of calling, and were called the head; we, the second, who were called the tail, have been turned into the head and are called sons of God. For as many as received him, to them he gave power to become sons of God (John 1:12). Nor have we received the spirit of bondage in fear, but the spirit of adoption, in which we cry: Abba, Father (Rom 8:15); for perfect love casts out fear (1 John 4:18). And it is better to read according to the Hebrew, Sons have I nourished, rather than begotten, lest it seem contrary to that saying which we read in the Epistle of John: Everyone who is born of God does not sin (1 John 3:9). For if these were born of God, how could they sin, since everyone who is born of God cannot sin?\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And so that we may know that hearing is more sacred than that which sounds in the ears, let us learn it from Isaiah, who says: Hear, O heaven, and perceive with your ears, O earth. Those who are old men and heavenly hear spiritually; those who dwell on the earth and are called earthly perceive with their ears. And this must be noted in all the Scriptures where these two words are placed together. We read this also in the case of Lamech, the sinner, who spoke to his wives Adah and Zillah: Hear my words, wives of Lamech, perceive my words with your ears, for I have killed a man to my wounding, and a young man to my bruising; he knew that what he was saying was obscure, and therefore he called his wives not only to the simple sound of the words, but also to the hidden understanding of what was said.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"ISA.1.3":["The ox knows his owner, and the ass his master's crib. Israel has not known, my people has not understood. For which the Seventy alone translated: But Israel has not known me, and the people has not understood me; so that, according to the others and the Hebrew, Israel did not know his owner, and the people did not understand the crib of its master. And the sense is clear: I adopted them as sons, and made them a peculiar people, my portion and the cord of my inheritance, and called them firstborn; but they did not do even what brute animals do, so as to be won over by kindnesses and to recognize their shepherd and nourisher. Nor did he compare them to dogs, which are the most sagacious kind of animal and guard their masters' houses for a little food, but to the ox and the ass, of duller sense: of which the one, drawing carts, turns the hardest clods of earth with the ploughshare, the other carries burdens and lightens men's labor in walking; whence also they are called beasts of burden (jumenta), because they help (juvent) men. This passage can also be understood of God the Father, but it is referred rather to the Son, because the people of Israel did not know him nor receive him: whose day Abraham saw and rejoiced, and on whose coming the prayers of all the Prophets hung. He too says to Jerusalem in the Gospel: How often would I have gathered your children, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not (Matt 23:37)! Do we ask where we have read of the ox and the ass together? In Deuteronomy it is written: You shall not plough with a calf and an ass together (Deut 22:10). And in this same Isaiah: Blessed is he who sows upon every water, where the ox and the ass tread (Isa 32:20). Ebion ploughs with the ox and the ass together, worthy, for the lowliness of his understanding, of the poverty of his name; for he so receives the Gospel as not to abandon the ceremonies of the Jewish superstitions, which went before in shadow and image. But blessed is he who sows in the words of the Scriptures, of the old as well as of the new Instrument, and treads the waters of the letter that kills, so as to reap the fruit of the life-giving spirit. The ox, according to the anagogy, is referred to Israel, which bore the yoke of the Law and is a clean animal. The ass, weighed down with the burden of sins, is taken as the people of the nations, to whom the Lord said: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you (Matt 11:28). So while the Pharisees and Scribes did not believe, who had the key and the knowledge of the Law and were truly called Israel, that is, a mind seeing God, part of the people of the Jews believed, so that on one day three thousand believed together, and on another day five thousand. And while the wise of the world did not receive the cross of Christ, the unlearned crowd of the nations took it up. Hence the Apostle too said: See your calling, brethren: that not many are wise according to the flesh, not many powerful, not many noble; but the foolish things of the world God has chosen, to confound the wise; and the weak things of the world God has chosen, to confound the strong (1 Cor 1:26, 27). But these things are forced; the former interpretation is the true one.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.4":["Woe to the sinful nation, to a people heavy with iniquity, to a wicked seed, to criminal sons: they have forsaken the Lord, they have blasphemed the Holy One of Israel, they are estranged backward. What I have put last, they are estranged backward, the Seventy Translators do not have; and for they have blasphemed there is written in them, they have provoked to anger; and for a wicked seed, a most evil seed. Following which the others translated the Hebrew MEREIM (מרעים) as of the most evil, so that the fault would lie not so much in the seed (lest the nature of good and evil be thought to be different) as in the malice of those who forsook the Lord by their own will.\n\nAt the beginning of the volume is the title, recording the person, the subject and the time; in the second verse he makes the hearers attentive; in the third he relates what the Lord has spoken; in the fourth, as if in an outburst, he rebukes the sinful nation and the people full of, or heavy with, iniquity. Not that the nation is one thing and the people another, as some think; but Israel itself is called both nation and people, and criminal or wicked sons: so that those who were first by kindness called sons of the Lord were afterwards, by their own fault, called sons of iniquity; or, as the rest have rendered with one voice, corrupting sons, that is, υἱοὶ διαφθείροντες, who by their own fault destroyed the good of their nature. And what is joined to it, They have blasphemed the Holy One of Israel, is said properly of the Jews crying out: We have no king but Caesar (John 19:15); and: Is not this the carpenter's son? (Matt 13:55); and, He has a demon, and is a Samaritan (John 8:48). Because, then, they forsook Christ and blasphemed the Holy One of Israel, therefore they are estranged backward, so that those who were called the portion and the sons of God were afterwards spoken of thus: Strange sons have lied to me (Ps 17 [18]:46). The Savior commanded that, having taken hold of the plough, we should not look behind our back (Luke 9:62), lest we imitate Lot's wife. Hence the Apostle too, stretching forward to what lies before, forgets what lies behind (Phil 3:13). And as for what he said according to the Seventy, to a people full of sins (Zech 5:7), it shows that there was no kind of sin that was not in the people of Israel. But if we read, as it is in the Hebrew, To a people heavy with iniquity, let us remember that testimony, that iniquity sits upon a talent of lead, and that it is said in the person of the sinner in Psalm 37 (38): My iniquities have gone up over my head; like a heavy burden they have weighed heavy upon me (Ps 37 [38]:5). We pass quickly over what is plain, so as to dwell on the more obscure things and on those that need explanation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.5":["Upon what shall I strike you, you who add transgression still further? By this testimony we learn that the Lord strikes those who offend for this purpose, that he may correct them once struck, and that it may be not so much a punishment as an amendment. And the sense is: I do not find what medicine I can apply to your wounds; all your limbs are full of wounds; I find no part of the body that has not already been struck. Or certainly in this way: I find no blows with which to break your hardness. For the greater the torments are, the more impiety and iniquity grow, or, as Theodotion translated, turning aside, so that you withdraw and turn aside from the Lord. Like that saying of Jeremiah: In vain have I struck your sons; you have not received discipline (Jer 2:30). Hence he speaks in anger through Hosea: I will not punish your daughters when they commit fornication, nor your daughters-in-law when they commit adultery (Hos 4:14). And through Ezekiel: My jealousy shall depart from you, and I will be angry with you no more (Ezek 16:42). Of whom we read in the Psalms too: There is no firmness in their stroke; and they are not in the labors of men, nor shall they be scourged with men (Ps 72 [73]:4, 5).\n\nEvery head is sick, and every heart grieving. Gladness of mind sometimes eases pain of body; but if sickness of mind is added to the illness of the body, the infirmity is doubled. Among the senses themselves, too, and all the members of the body, the head holds the chief place, for in it are sight and smell, hearing and taste. When, therefore, the head is in pain, all the members are weak. And by a metaphor he teaches that from the princes down to the lowest common people, from the teachers down to the unlearned crowd, there is soundness in none, but all agree in impiety with equal ardor.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.6":["From the sole of the foot to the crown of the head there is no soundness in it: wound and bruise and swelling sore. He keeps up the figure he has begun: from the feet up to the crown, that is, from the lowest to the highest, from the last to the first, they are pierced through in the whole body. Wound, he says, and bruise and swelling sore: for bodies are either livid from beatings, or swollen with blows, or gaping with wounds. We ask to what time these things should be fitted. After the Babylonian captivity, under Zerubbabel and Ezra and Nehemiah, Israel returned to Judea and recovered its ancient state. Under various princes and kings also the temple was built more magnificently, so much so that they even earned the friendship of foreign nations, of the Lacedaemonians and the Athenians and the Romans. Therefore this which he says, There is no soundness in it, he refers to the last captivity, when, after Titus and Vespasian and the final overthrow of Jerusalem under Aelius Hadrian, there has been no remedy down to the present time, and what is written is fulfilled: All have turned aside, together they have become unprofitable; there is none who does good, there is not even one (Rom 3:12). And as for what is added, there is no soundness in it, understand it either of the people, or of the body, or of the head.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","It is not bound up, nor treated with medicine, nor soothed with oil. For which the Seventy translated: It is not possible to apply a plaster, nor oil, nor bandages. To this day the wound and bruise and swelling sore of the people of Israel is not bound up with bandages, nor treated with medicine. Which Aquila translated μότωσιν, namely the lint which is laid on wounds to dry up the matter and draw out the pus. Nor soothed with oil: that their hardness might be softened by tears of repentance. For the bandages, with which the wounds of Israel are by no means bound up, the Seventy translated plaster. Israel therefore lies pierced through and cut to pieces, because he killed the physician who had come to heal the house of Israel. Hence in Jeremiah too the Angels speak τροπικῶς in the person of Babylon: We have cared for Babylon, and she is not healed (Jer 51:9), that is, the city of confusion and of vices. And in the Gospel (Luke 10) we read of the man going down from Jerusalem to Jericho who was wounded by robbers and cared for by the Samaritan, and of the softness of oil poured into his wounds after the sharpness of wine. From the place, then, where he had said above, Upon what shall I strike you, and every head is sick, down to what is added here, It is not treated with medicine, nor soothed with oil, the likeness of the figure is kept, and the description of incurable wounds expresses the eternal captivity of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.7":["Your land is desolate, your cities are burned with fire; strangers devour your country in your sight, and it shall be made desolate as in a devastation by enemies. These things were partly fulfilled under the Babylonians, when the temple was burned and Jerusalem overthrown, when the Samaritans took possession of the region of the ten tribes, and the land of promise was reduced to such desolation that it was laid waste by lions. But what was to come under the Roman captivity is described more fully and more perfectly: when the Roman army laid waste all Judea, and the cities were burned, and strangers at the present time devour their country, and the desolation of the Jews will continue until the end of the world. We can also take these things tropologically of sinners who have fallen from their former holiness, after they have been handed over to the opposing powers: that all their goods come to desolation, and God does not remember their former righteousness, and all things are set on fire by the fire of the devil, and they become the food of beasts, of which it is written in another place too: Do not deliver to the beasts the soul that confesses to you (Ps 73 [74]:19).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.8":["The daughter of Zion shall be left like a shelter in a vineyard, and like a hut in a cucumber field. That all Israel is called a vineyard the same Prophet testifies in what follows, saying: For the vineyard of the Lord Sabaoth is the house of Israel, and the man of Judah his young and beloved planting (below, Isa 5:7); and in the Psalm: You have brought a vine out of Egypt; you have cast out the nations and planted it (Ps 79 [80]:9). As long as this vineyard bore the richest fruit, it had God as its keeper, of whom it is written: He who keeps Israel will neither slumber nor sleep (Ps 120 [121]:4). But after all who passed along the way had stripped it of its grapes, and the boar out of the wood had laid it waste, the Lord left his temple and, rising up in anger, said: Arise, let us go hence; and: Your house shall be left to you desolate (Matt 23:38). And through Jeremiah: I have left my house, I have abandoned my inheritance. My inheritance has become to me like a lion in the forest; it has given forth its voice against me, therefore I have hated it (Jer 12:7). The likeness of the devastation of the Temple and of Jerusalem is taken from farmers, who, as long as the vineyard is full of grapes, place keepers in shelters. In a cucumber field too, which the Seventy call a watch-place for fruit, little huts are made to keep off the heat and the rays of the sun; and from there they drive away either men or the small beasts that are wont to lie in wait for the growing crops. But when such produce has been carried away, the withered shelters of branches and the huts remain, the keeper departing, because he no longer has anything to guard. So too almighty God has forsaken the Temple and made the city desolate: which there is no need to prove with words, especially to us, who see Zion desolate and Jerusalem overthrown and the Temple razed to the ground. And in calling her the daughter of Zion he shows the affection of a most merciful parent. Nor is it surprising that Zion should be called a daughter, since even Babylon is very often named daughter. For we are all by nature sons of God, but by our own fault we become strangers. According to the anagogy, our soul can be called the vineyard of God and the orchard of fruit: if the mind, that is, νοῦς, presides over it, it has God as the keeper of the mind; but if vices, like certain beasts, have plundered us, we are left by God our keeper, and all our things will be reduced to desolation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.9":["Unless the Lord of hosts had left us a seed, we would have been as Sodom, and we would have been like Gomorrah. Paul the apostle discusses this passage more fully to the Romans, writing: I say then, has God cast away his people? God forbid: for I too am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin. God has not cast away his people whom he foreknew (Rom 11:1, 2). And a little later: So then at this present time also a remnant has been saved according to the election of grace (ibid., 5). From this it is shown that the foregoing threats which the prophetic word uttered against Jerusalem and Judah are not to be referred to the time of the Babylonian captivity, but to the last, the Roman one, when in the Apostles the remnant of the people of the Jews was saved, and on one day three thousand believed and on another five thousand, and the Gospel was sown in the whole world. For the Lord of hosts, which we, following Aquila, have rendered in Latin, in the Hebrew is read the Lord Sabaoth, which the Seventy Translators translate in two ways according to the character of the passages, either the Lord of powers or the Lord almighty. And it must be asked whether it is said of the Father or of the Son. Nor is there any doubt that what is read in Psalm 23 (24), Lift up your gates, O princes, and be lifted up, O everlasting gates, and the king of glory shall come in. Who is this king of glory? The Lord Sabaoth (Ps 23 [24]:7, 8), that is, the Lord of powers, he is the king of glory, is referred to Christ, who after the triumph of his passion ascended victorious to the heavens. And in another place it is said of the Lord that he is the king of glory: For if they had known, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory (1 Cor 2:8). Therefore not only according to the Apocalypse of John and the apostle Paul, but in the Old Testament too, Christ is called the Lord Sabaoth, that is, the Almighty. For if all things of the Father are the Son's, and, as he himself says in the Gospel, All power has been given to me in heaven and on earth (Matt 28:18), and, All mine are yours, and I am glorified in them, why should not the name of the Almighty also be referred to Christ, so that as he is God of God and Lord of Lord, so he is the Almighty Son of the Almighty?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.10":["Hear the word of the Lord, you princes of Sodom; give ear to the law of our God, you people of Gomorrah. Once the remnant of the people of Israel had been saved through the Apostles, the prophetic word turns to the Scribes and Pharisees and the people shouting: Crucify, crucify such a man (John 19:6), and calls them princes of Sodom and people of Gomorrah, according to what we read in what follows: They have proclaimed and displayed their iniquity like Sodom. Woe to their soul, for they have devised a most evil counsel against themselves, saying: Let us bind the righteous man, for he is unprofitable to us (below, Isa 3). Therefore they are called princes of Sodom and people of Gomorrah for this reason, that they devised a most evil counsel and bound the righteous one, and said, We have no king but Caesar (John 19:15). And again: We know that God spoke to Moses; but as for this man, we do not know who he is (John 9:29). And at the same time, glorying in the Gospel, We are the seed of Abraham, and have never been slaves to anyone (John 8:33), they hear from the Lord Savior: If you were sons of Abraham, you would surely do the works of Abraham; and again: You are born of the devil as your father, and you wish to do the works of your father (ibid., 39, 44). Ezekiel too says something of the kind to Jerusalem: Your father was an Amorite, and your mother a Hittite (Ezek 16:45). The Hebrews say that Isaiah was put to death for two reasons: because he called them princes of Sodom and people of Gomorrah, and because, though the Lord said to Moses, You cannot see my face (Exod 33:20), he dared to say, I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up (below, Isa 6:1); not considering that the Seraphim covered the face and feet of God, or their own, for in the Hebrew it reads ambiguously, and that Isaiah writes that he saw only his middle parts. Man, then, cannot see the face of God. But the Angels even of the least in the Church always see the face of God (Matt 18:10). And, Now we see in a mirror and in a riddle, but then face to face (1 Cor 13:12): when we have advanced from men to Angels and can say with the Apostle: But we all, with unveiled face beholding the glory of the Lord, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord (2 Cor 3:18). And yet no creature sees the face of God according to the proper character of his nature; and he is then discerned by the mind when he is believed to be invisible.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.11":["To what purpose is the multitude of your victims to me, says the Lord? I am full. Burnt offerings of rams, and the fat of fatlings, and the blood of calves and of lambs and of goats I did not want. For what is I did not want, the Seventy translated I do not want, the present tense for the past. But according to the Hebrew, God shows that he never wanted the victims of the Jews; which we read also in Psalm 49 (50): I will not take calves from your house, nor goats from your flocks: for all the wild beasts of the forests are mine, the cattle on the mountains and the oxen. I know all the birds of the sky, and the beauty of the field is with me. If I am hungry, I will not tell you: for the world is mine and the fullness thereof. Shall I eat the flesh of bulls, or shall I drink the blood of goats (Ps 49 [50]ff.)? And having rejected the ceremonies of the old Law, he passes on to the purity of the Gospel, and shows what he desires in their place: Offer to God the sacrifice of praise, and pay your vows to the Most High. And call upon me in the day of trouble, and I will deliver you, and you shall honor me. So the whole content of this chapter, down to the place where he says, Judge for the orphan, defend the widow, and come, and let us reason together, rejects the sacrifices of victims and teaches that obedience to the Gospel is above sacrifice. And what he added, I am full, is to be taken in this sense: I lack nothing; The earth is the Lord's, and the fullness thereof (Ps 23 [24]:1). Hence we too have all received of his fullness. This can also be taken of those who, while not doing God's commandments, believe that they can buy God off with gifts and offerings; or of those who offer to the altar and to the poor what they have taken by robbery and greed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.12":["For who has required these things at your hands? Let the Ebionites hear, who think that the Law, abolished after the passion of Christ, is to be kept. Let the companions of the Ebionites hear, who decree that these things are to be observed only by the Jews and by those of the stock of the Israelite race. Victims, then, and the immolation of sacrifices were not required by God as a principal thing, but so that they should not be made to idols, and so that from carnal victims, as through a type and image, we might pass over to spiritual sacrifices. And by saying that he did not require victims, he shows that the Law is spiritual, and that all that the Jews do carnally is fulfilled by us spiritually.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","You shall no more tread my court. Observe that after the Babylonian devastation the Temple was built again by Zerubbabel, and that sacrifices were offered in the Temple through a long series of years (Ezra 5). Therefore he points to the last destruction of the Temple under Vespasian and Titus, which is to last until the consummation of the world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.13-14":["Your new moons, and sabbaths, and other festivals I will not bear: your assemblies are wicked. Every gathering that does not offer spiritual sacrifices, nor hears what is sung in Psalm 50 (51), A sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit; a contrite and humbled heart God does not despise, is abominable to God. And therefore he adds and says: Your calends and your solemnities, so as to call them not his own festivals but those of the people who abuse them wickedly. And as for what the Seventy translated, fasting and idleness, we can say that that fast is accepted by God which has no idleness from good works. My soul hates. Ἀνθρωποπάθως: not that God has a soul, but he speaks in terms of our feelings.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.14":["You have become a surfeit to me: I will by no means forgive your sins. For which Aquila translated, I have labored in bearing; Symmachus ἐκοπώθην ἱλασκόμενος, that is, I have grown weary of showing mercy; to show that he will by no means have mercy any longer, because it is one thing to kill the servants sent to them, another to kill the Son. This sense we read also in the prophet Hosea: Your destruction, O Israel; only in me is your help (Hos 13:9). Which is understood thus: You would perish, Israel, because you are saved not by your own merit but only by my help.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.15":["Your hands are full of blood. He makes plain the reason why God turns his eyes away from them and does not hear their multiplied prayer: because they have shed the blood of the righteous one, and the wicked husbandmen have killed the heir sent to them. Hence the Savior too says to them: Fill up, then, the measure of your fathers (Matt 23:31). For they killed the Prophets sent to them; you, slay the Son of the householder. This testimony is to be used also against those who, while their hands are full of blood from their daily deeds, join day to night in prayer.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.16-18":["If your sins are like scarlet, they shall be made white as snow; and if they are red like crimson, they shall be like white wool. An excellent order: for it is not enough to have said, wash yourselves, unless he adds, be clean, so that after the washing of water they may have purity of heart. For blessed are the clean of heart, for they shall see God (Matt 5:8). And when they have purity of heart, they must take away evil from their minds, not in the sight of men but in the sight of God, from whom nothing can be hidden. And what he adds, Cease to act perversely, echoes that word of the Gospel: Behold, you have been made whole; sin no more, lest something worse befall you (John 5:14). Withdrawing, then, from vices, let him learn good, and seek judgment, relieve the oppressed, support the orphan and the widow; and if he does this, then the sins which before were blood-red like scarlet will be forgiven, and the works of gore and blood will be changed by the garment of the Lord, which is made from the fleece of the lamb, whom in the Apocalypse (ch. 5) those follow who shine with the whiteness of virginity.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.16":["Wash yourselves, be clean. In place of the former victims and burnt offerings and fat of fatlings and blood of bulls and goats, and in place of incense and new moons, sabbath, feast day and fasts, calends and other solemnities, the religion of the Gospel pleases me: that you be baptized in my blood through the washing of regeneration, which alone can forgive sins. For unless one is born again of water and the spirit, he will not enter the kingdom of heaven (John 3:5). The Lord himself too, ascending to the Father: Go, he says, teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit (Matt 28:19).\n\nTake away the evil of your thoughts from my eyes. As John the Baptist said: Brood of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come? Bear therefore fruits worthy of repentance (Matt 3:7; Luke 3:7): so that he who has obtained the baptism of Christ should take away evil from his heart, and cease to act perversely, and afterwards learn to do good, according to what is commanded elsewhere: Turn away from evil, and do good (Ps 36 [37]:27).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.17-18":["Seek judgment, relieve the oppressed, judge for the orphan, defend the widow: and come, and reprove me, says the Lord. The commandments of the Gospel succeed to the victims of the Jews, and therefore provision is made for orphans and widows, so that, secure about the protection of their wives and children, martyrs may go forth to battle. And when, he says, you have done these things, reprove me if I do not render the rewards I have promised. And when he says, Seek judgment, he shows that it is not for all to judge rightly, but for those who are prudent. So too Solomon, in a vision in a dream, asked this of the Lord, that having received wisdom he might judge the people justly.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.17":["Learn to do well. Virtue, then, must be learned, and the good of nature alone does not suffice for righteousness unless one is trained by fitting disciplines (1 Pet 2:11). Jesus the son of Sirach too says something of the kind: You have desired wisdom: keep the commandments, and the Lord will give it to you. And in what follows the same Isaiah recalls: Everyone who has not learned righteousness on earth will not do truth (Isa 26:10 LXX). Righteousness, then, must be learned, and the thresholds of the masters of wisdom must be worn down.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.19-20":["If you are willing and will hear, you shall eat the good things of the land; if you are not willing and will not hear (or, as it has in the Hebrew) and provoke me to anger, the sword shall devour you. For the mouth of the Lord has spoken. He preserves free will, so that in either direction there may be punishment or reward, not from God's prejudgment but from the merits of each. And I think those are called the good things of the land of which we read in the psalm: I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living (Ps 26 [27]:13); and: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land (Matt 5:3). Or certainly, because he was speaking to the Jews, who could not yet grasp spiritual things, he promises them the good things of the present age, so that, enticed at least by present things, they might do what was commanded. And because they would not hear, but on the contrary provoked the Holy One of Israel to anger, therefore the sword devoured them, that is, the Roman army destroyed them. And all these things, he says, will come to pass, For the mouth of the Lord has spoken; whose sentence, while men's sins remain, cannot be changed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.21":["How has the faithful city become a harlot, full of judgment! Righteousness rested in it, but now murderers. The Hebrew word JALIN (ילין), for which the Seventy translated slept, means both rested and will rest, that is, it signifies both the past and the future tense. Hence Aquila and Theodotion too speak as of the future. And he marvels in the prophetic spirit that the city which was once faithful, or the dwelling of the faithful, has suddenly become a harlot. This can indeed be understood also of the times of Isaiah, but it is referred more fully to the passion of Christ, when all turned aside and together became unprofitable (Ps 13 [14]:3). And although Zion is not found in the Hebrew, the Seventy nevertheless added it to make the sense plainer. Now Zion is the mountain on which the city of Jerusalem was founded; after it was taken by David it was called the city of David. Nor do I doubt that there were holy men in it when it had the tabernacle of God, and afterwards when the Temple was built; when Nathan and Gad prophesied, and Asaph and Jeduthun and Heman and the sons of Korah were set over the choirs (which are described more fully in the book of Days), so that religion might pass little by little from the sacrifices of victims to the praises of the Lord (1 Chr 25). The city of the faithful, then, which before was full of judgment, and righteousness rested in it, is now full of murderers, who killed the prophets and the Lord Savior himself. And the fornication of Jerusalem, how she spread her legs to everyone who passed by, Ezekiel sets out under the name of Oholibah, which means my tabernacle in her; which is now said in other words, righteousness rested in it. For righteousness, in the Hebrew is written SEDEC (צדק), which sounds rather like the righteous one than righteousness, so that we may understand that the Lord formerly dwelt in it, of whom it is said elsewhere: But what has the righteous one done? The Lord is in his holy temple; the Lord, his seat is in heaven (Ps 10 [11]:5). All of which we can refer, according to the anagogy, to the soul of a man once holy, in which the righteousness of God formerly dwelt, if afterwards he has sinned, and in place of God as guest murderous demons have dwelt in it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.22":["Your silver is turned into dross. He speaks to the city of Zion, in which righteousness formerly rested: that its silver, namely the teaching of the Scriptures, of which we read in the Psalms, The words of the Lord are pure words, silver tried by fire, proved in the earth, refined sevenfold (Ps 11 [12]:7), is turned into dross, which in Hebrew is called SIGIM (סיגים): namely the rust of metals, or the refuse and filth that are smelted out by fire; so that, since he had named silver, he keeps the metaphor. This can also be said: that the righteous and holy men who formerly lived in the city were afterwards turned into the filth of sins.\n\nYour innkeepers mix wine with water. For which Symmachus translated, Your wine is mixed with water. And the sense is: the Law of God, pure and sincere, and (so to speak) resting on unmixed truth (Matt 15), has been violated by the traditions of the Pharisees; as the Lord teaches more fully in the Gospel, that they had abandoned the law of God and followed the commandments of men. And every teacher who turns the severity of the Scriptures, by which he could correct his hearers, into something pleasing, and speaks so as not to correct but to delight his hearers, violates the wine of the holy Scriptures and corrupts it with his own sense. The heretics too corrupt the truth of the Gospel by perverse understanding, and are the worst of innkeepers, making water out of wine, whereas on the contrary our Lord turned water into wine (John 2), and such wine that the steward of the feast marveled at it: such as the queen of Sheba too marveled at in Solomon's banquet, praising with her own voice his cupbearers and the servers of the wine (2 Chr 9). And Ecclesiastes too describes in mystical language the services of the wine and of his banquet (Eccl 2). Hence for wine Aquila translated συμπόσιον, that is, banquet, which among the Greeks is named ἀπὸ τοῦ πότου, from drinking, but among us more rightly from living together (convictus).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.23":["Your princes are disobedient, companions of thieves. For disobedient, Aquila translated withdrawing, Symmachus turning aside. And by princes he means the Scribes and Pharisees, who, withdrawing from the Lord, or rather leaving the path of truth, walked on a perverse road and were companions of Judas, the betrayer and thief. This indeed we too must beware of, lest, by accepting gifts from men of the world who gather riches through robberies and the tears of the wretched, we be called not so much thieves ourselves as companions of thieves, and it be said to us: You saw a thief and ran with him, and you put your portion with adulterers (Ps 49 [50]:18).\n\nAll love gifts, they follow after rewards. In the catalogue of vices are numbered also those who love gifts. He did not say, those who accept them, for this often happens out of necessity; but those who count none their friends except those from whom they have received presents, and look not at the faces of their friends but at their hands, and judge those holy whose purses they drain; of whom Ecclesiastes too speaks: He who loves money will not be filled with money (Eccl 5:9). Such men follow after rewards, so that they praise those from whom they have received something, or certainly give nothing to anyone except one from whom they think they will receive in return. For rewards, Symmachus translated requitals, or vengeances, so that these too are at fault who render evil for evil, and tooth for tooth, eye for eye (Exod 21), and do not imitate that word of David: If I have repaid those who rendered evil to me (Ps 7:5); and of Jeremiah, who says of the righteous man: He will give his cheek to the one who strikes him, he will be filled with reproaches (Lam 3:30); so that he may fulfill the man of the Gospel, to whom it is said: Whoever strikes you on the cheek, offer him the other also (Matt 5:39).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.24":["Therefore says the Lord of hosts, the mighty one of Israel. For the mighty one of Israel, which all translated alike, the Seventy alone, intending I know not what, put: Woe, you mighty of Israel; which we can expound thus, by saying that the princes too and the strong are rebuked, of whom it is written: The mighty shall mightily suffer torments (Wis 6:7); and: To whom more is given, more will be required of him (Luke 12:48). And we may turn this testimony to use, whenever there is need, against princes of the Church who destroy their own dignity by their deeds.\n\nAh, I will be comforted over my foes, and I will avenge myself on my enemies. Again in this place too the Seventy put what is not found in the Hebrew: For my fury has not ceased against my enemies. And he rebukes the Scribes and Pharisees, of whom he also speaks in the Gospel: Woe to you, Scribes and Pharisees (Matt 23:13ff.). And in another place: An adulterous and perverse generation seeks a sign, and no sign shall be given to it but the sign of Jonah the prophet (Matt 12:39). And the most merciful father laments the offending princes, and calls them his foes and names them his enemies, because they perish, since they will not repent, since they did not receive him when he came. For drawing near to Jerusalem he wept and said: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the Prophets and stone those who are sent to you, how often would I have gathered your children, as a hen gathers her chicks, and you would not (Matt 23:37)! The consolation of God, then, is over his foes and enemies: that those who did not feel his benefits may be corrected by punishments.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.25":["And I will turn my hand upon you, and I will smelt away your dross to purity, and I will take away all your tin. For dross, which is Symmachus's translation, Aquila translated στέμφυλα, that is, grape-husks, Theodotion γιγαρτῶδες, that is, grape-stone; the Seventy alone, the unbelieving or the disobedient, rendering the sense rather than the words. For since he had said above, Your silver is turned into dross, he now keeps the metaphor: that he will turn upon her, that is, stretch out his hand to punish and to purge, and smelt away all the filth and vices of sins, so that, the tin being separated, pure silver may remain; which cannot be done without fire: by which he signifies that they are to suffer torments. We read also in Malachi of the Lord: He himself will go forth like the fire of a smelter, and like fullers' herb, and he will sit smelting and cleansing as silver and gold, and he will cleanse the sons of Levi (Mal 3:2, 3); so that, after they have been cleansed, it may be said of them: And they shall be offering victims to the Lord in righteousness. Ezekiel too says that the whole house of Israel is mixed with bronze and iron and lead and tin, and is afterwards to be purged, so that, after it has been purged, it may know that he is the Lord (Ezek 22:18). And in the Gospel too the same sense is shown under another metaphor: Whose winnowing fan is in his hand, and he will purge his threshing floor, and will cleanse the wheat, and will gather the grain into barns; but the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire (Matt 3:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.26":["And I will restore your judges as they were before, and your counselors as of old. In the faithful city of Zion, which afterwards became a harlot, murderers dwelt in place of the righteous one, or of righteousness. Therefore the Lord turns his hand, to smelt her to purity and take away all her tin, and to restore her judges as they were at the beginning, and her counselors as of old. The former judges were Moses and Joshua son of Nun and the others, from whom a book of holy Scripture also took its name; and afterwards David and other righteous kings. He will therefore restore the likeness of the Judges: either, after the Babylonian captivity, as the Jews would have it, Zerubbabel, Ezra and Nehemiah and the other princes who were set over the people up to Hyrcanus, whom Herod succeeded in the kingdom; or certainly, more truly and more rightly, the Apostles and those who believed through the Apostles and were appointed princes of the Churches; as we said at the beginning of this vision, that both the threat and the promise belong to the time of the Lord's passion, and to the faith which after his passion founded the Church.\n\nAfter this you shall be called the city of the righteous one, a faithful city. These things the prophetic word plainly embraces concerning the Church, which is to believe in the Lord, from the Jews as well as from the nations. And the city of the righteous one, that is, of the Lord Savior, or she herself will be called righteous, of whom it was said: A city set on a mountain cannot be hidden (Matt 5:14). And by calling her faithful, or, according to the Seventy, metropolis, he shows that she is to be so named by those who are to believe in the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.27-29":["Zion shall be redeemed in judgment, and they shall bring her back into righteousness, and he shall crush the wicked and the sinners together; and those who have forsaken the Lord shall be consumed; for they shall be confounded by the idols to which they sacrificed, and you shall blush over the gardens which you had chosen. Not all will be redeemed, nor will all be saved, but the remnant, of whom it was spoken above too. And they will be brought back in righteousness when the wicked and the sinners have been crushed and those who forsook the Lord consumed. And when these have been saved, those who formerly sacrificed to idols will be confounded, and will blush in the gardens which they had chosen. And he signifies places of luxury, groves and woods.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.30":["For they shall be like a terebinth whose leaves fall off, and like a garden, or a paradise, without water. To this day the Jews, reading the holy Scriptures, are a terebinth, or an oak, as Symmachus translated. And according to the Gospel (Matt 21) they are the fig tree that withered, on which the Lord sought fruit and, not finding it, cursed it with everlasting barrenness. But even the very leaves and fruits of words have now ceased to be among them; the watered garden too, that is, the knowledge of the Scriptures, or the paradise of various trees, which is without spiritual grace, does not bear even herbs, of which the Apostle speaks: Let him who is weak eat herbs (Rom 14:2); and with its roots dried up, all its greenness has turned to dryness and squalor.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.1.31":["And your strength shall be like the ash of tow. For ash, Symmachus translated ἀποτίναγμα: when tow is combed, and whatever filth it has is thrown away. So all the strength and pride of the sinners and wicked of Israel, who forsook the Lord and were therefore consumed, and sacrificed to idols, and will blush in the gardens which they chose, will be reduced to the refuse of tow, which is devoured by a light fire. For there follows: And its work, that is, the work of your strength, or of the idolatry in which you had gone astray, a tiny spark will consume.\n\nAnd both shall be set on fire together, and there shall be none to quench it. Both, namely, the knowledge of the Jews and all the works they do; or else both the idol and Jerusalem, in which the idol was; and when the Lord sets it on fire, no one will be able to quench it. All of which we can also take of contrary doctrines: that both masters and disciples perish alike, and all their work is food for fire.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.1":["The word which Isaiah the son of Amoz saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem. In the former Vision too, which we have already expounded, where the Seventy translated, which he saw against Judea and against Jerusalem, there is written in the Hebrew AL JUDA UJERUSALEM (על יהודה וירושלם); and in this one, which is the second, the Hebrew likewise has it. And I wonder why the Seventy Translators said in that one, against Judea and Jerusalem, and in this one about Judea and about Jerusalem; unless perhaps, because there the nation is called sinful, a people full of sins, a most evil seed, sons of iniquity, and princes of Sodom and people of Gomorrah, and a harlot city, and other things of that kind, they translated the sense rather than the word; and here, because prosperous things are promised at once, In the last days the mountain of the Lord shall be manifest, and the house of God on the summit of the mountains, they understood the prophecy not as against Judea and Jerusalem but as about Judea and Jerusalem: although in that one too we read prosperous things after the threat, I will restore your judges as they were before, and your counselors as of old: after this you shall be called the city of the righteous one, a faithful city; and in this one, after the prosperous things, there is a fierce threat: Behold, the ruler, the Lord of hosts, will take away from Jerusalem and from Judah the mighty and the strong, all the strength of bread and all the strength of water, and the rest. Therefore, according to the Hebrew, both in that vision and in this word which Isaiah the son of Amoz saw, it is to be understood as about Judah and Jerusalem: not against Judea and Jerusalem, nor for Judah and Jerusalem, as Symmachus translated, but absolutely, about Judah and Jerusalem, in which both glad and sad things can be contained. And this must be considered, that there he sees a vision, here the Word, which was in the beginning with God; and that in that one, threatening the Jews, he comes to the salvation of the nations; in this one, beginning from the salvation of the nations, and Israel being punished, he gathers believers from both callings into the Church of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.2-3":["On my holy mountain, on the high mountain of Israel, says the Lord God, there all the house of Israel shall serve me: all, I say, in the land in which they shall please me; and there I will require your firstfruits and the beginning of your tithes in all your sanctifications. In an odor of sweetness I will receive you, when I have led you out from the peoples and gathered you from the lands into which you were scattered, and I shall be sanctified in you in the eyes of the nations. And you shall know that I am the Lord. Of this mountain, on which all the house of Israel is to serve God, Isaiah and Micah sang with one voice: In the last days the mountain of the house shall be manifest on the summit of the mountains and shall be raised above the hills; and all the nations shall flow to it, and many peoples shall go and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord and to the house of the God of Jacob, and he will teach us his ways and we will walk in his paths; for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem; and again Isaiah: Go up onto a high mountain, he says, you who proclaim to Zion; lift up and exalt your voice, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem. This mountain, Zion, we understand either as the Church, which means 'watchtower' and is set on the height of holy doctrines, or as the Lord Savior himself, in whom the firstfruits and the tithes and all worship are turned into an odor of sweetness, so that all the nations round about, seeing that the people of the Lord has been saved, may glorify God, and those who have been saved may know that he is the Lord.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"ISA.2.2-4":["It must also be known that the Jews and the heirs of their error refer both this section which we have now expounded and the one like it in Isaiah to the thousand-year reign of Christ and of the saints; and that what is said: All peoples shall walk, each in the name of the Lord his God, they interpret in this way: that each nation is to be tormented with its idol and sent into the fire of eternal punishment. But they are refuted by what follows: this is said not of the end of the ages but of the first coming of Christ, in which the remnant of her that halts is gathered, and the nations are saved first. Isaiah, then, set down a testimony of this kind: The word that came to Isaiah son of Amoz concerning Judah and Jerusalem: for in the last days the mountain of the Lord shall be manifest, and the house of God upon the summit of the mountains, and it shall be exalted above the hills, and all nations shall come to it. And many nations shall go, and shall say: Come, let us go up into the mountain of the Lord, and into the house of the God of Jacob, and he will announce to us our way, and we will walk in it; for out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem. And he shall judge among many nations, and reprove a very great people, and they shall beat their swords into plows, and their hunting-spears into sickles, and nation shall not take up sword against nation, and they shall not learn to make war any more. Significantly it is said here that the word of God, going forth from Jerusalem, judges among many nations, and properly reproves the people of the Jews. For us, as sinners, he will judge according to the measure of our works; but them, as impious men and deniers, he will not judge, but will reprove them as condemned.\n— Commentary on Micah"],"ISA.2.2":["And in the last days the mountain of the house of the Lord shall be prepared on the top of the mountains. We read of the last days in Genesis too: that Jacob, calling his sons and saying, Come, that I may announce to you what is to come in the last days (Gen 49:1), afterwards said to Judah, from whose seed Christ was born: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up; and he shall be the expectation of the nations. In these last days will be the last hour, of which John the Apostle speaks: My little children, it is the last hour (1 John 2:18); in which the stone cut from the mountain without hands grew into a great mountain and filled the whole earth; by which the prince of Tyre is said in Ezekiel to have been wounded (Ezek 28:16 LXX). This mountain is in the house of the Lord, for which the Prophet sighs, saying: One thing have I asked of the Lord, this will I seek: that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life (Ps 26 [27]:4); and of which Paul writes to Timothy: But if I am delayed, that you may know how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God, which is the Church of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth (1 Tim 3:15). This house is built upon the foundation of the Apostles and Prophets, who themselves too are mountains, as imitators of Christ (Eph 2). Of this house, Jerusalem, the Psalmist cries out, saying: Those who trust in the Lord are like mount Zion; he who dwells in Jerusalem shall not be moved for ever. Mountains are round about it, and the Lord is round about his people (Ps 124 [125]:1). Hence too Christ founds the Church upon one of the mountains, and says to him: You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my Church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it (Matt 16:18). Something similar the holy man says to his soul, which longs and impatiently desires to see the house of God: Why are you sad, my soul, and why do you trouble me (Ps 41 [42]:6)? And again: These things I remembered, and I poured out my soul within me: for I shall pass over into the place of the wonderful tabernacle, even to the house of God, with the voice of exultation and confession, the sound of those who feast (ibid., 5). The Seventy translated, The manifest mountain of the Lord, and the house of God upon the heads of the mountains. This testimony the prophet Micah too has set down in the same words: which I have expounded in its place (Mic 1).\n\nAnd it shall be lifted up above the hills. He who was shown and prepared on the heads of the mountains, he shall be lifted up above the hills. Of these mountains and hills the bride too speaks in the Song of Songs: The voice of my kinsman: behold, he comes leaping upon the mountains, skipping over the hills; my kinsman is like a roe or a young hart on the mountains of Bethel (Song 2:8, 9).\n\nAnd all nations shall flow to it, and many peoples shall go. For all nations shall serve him to whom it was said: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession (Ps 2:8); so that they may serve him, according to Zephaniah, under one yoke; of whom the same prophet testifies: From the borders of the rivers of Ethiopia they shall bring you victims (Zeph 3:10). And in Psalm 71 (72) we read: Before him the Ethiopians shall fall down (Ps 71 [72]:9). For at the name of Jesus every knee shall bow, of things in heaven, on earth and under the earth (Phil 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.3":["And they shall say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord and to the house of the God of Jacob; and he will teach us his ways, and we shall walk in his paths. The nations and peoples, by no means content with their own salvation, will exhort one another and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord and to the house of the God of Jacob. We have said above what the prepared mountain of the Lord is, and what the house of the Lord on the top of the mountains. And the house of the Lord is called the house of the God of Jacob, so that we may receive the old Instrument, and not, after the manner of Manichaeus, seek another house outside the house of the God of Jacob. And when we are in the house of the God of Jacob, then he will teach us his ways, by which we may go to him, and we shall walk in his paths, which the others too have given. So too Jesus, going up into the mountain, taught his disciples the eight beatitudes and the other things which the word of the Gospel contains (Luke 6). First, then, the ways of the Lord must be learned, and afterwards we must walk in his paths.\n\nFor out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem. All the nations and many peoples will exhort one another for this reason and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord, and the rest that follows: For out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem: by no means from Sinai, and from the desert, and from mount Horeb, but from mount Zion, on which Jerusalem was built, and from Jerusalem, in which is the Temple and the worship of God. We read that the Lord often taught in the Temple (Matt 13), and that it was not fitting for a Prophet to die outside Jerusalem; and above, the word and the law were named together. The princes are commanded to hear the word, the people to give ear to the law. For whoever has first kept the law afterwards comes to the speech of God. And indeed the Church, founded first in Jerusalem, sowed the Churches of the whole world. And this must be said, that whoever is in the watchtower and in the vision of peace, in him both the law and the word of the Lord are established. And he finely did not say: the word and the law of the Lord will be and remain in Zion and in Jerusalem, but will go forth, to signify that all nations are to be watered by the teaching of God from that fountain.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.4":["And he shall judge the nations, and rebuke many peoples. Therefore there is to be judgment among the nations too; nor are all unbelievers to be condemned by the same sentence, but according to the diversity of their merits they will suffer diverse things. And after he has judged the nations, then he will rebuke many peoples, or, as the Seventy translated, a great people. And note the order: the nations will be judged, because they are going to believe: For he who does not believe is already judged (John 3:18). But the great people, which is understood as Israel, will by no means be judged, but rebuked, because it did not receive the Son of God sent to it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And they shall forge their swords into ploughshares, and their spears into sickles. Every zeal for war will be turned to peace, and instead of discord there will be concord in the whole world. Swords will be changed into ploughshares and spears into sickles, so that, putting aside the fury of war, they may serve agriculture and cut the richest harvests with sickles. This indeed can also be understood spiritually, when all the hardness of our heart is broken by the ploughshare of Christ, and the thorns of vices are rooted out, so that the seed of the word of God may grow into crops; and afterwards we shall eat the labors of our hands, when coming they shall come with exultation, carrying their sheaves (Ps 125 [126]:6).\n\nNation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they be trained any more for battle. Let us turn over the ancient histories, and we shall find that up to the twenty-eighth year of Caesar Augustus (in whose forty-first year Christ was born in Judea) there was discord in the whole world, and the several nations burned with the zeal of fighting against their neighbors, so that they slew and were slain. But when the Lord Savior arose, when under Quirinius, governor of Syria, the first enrollment of the world was made, and by the peace of the Roman empire the way was prepared for the teaching of the Gospel, then all wars ceased, and they were by no means trained for battle throughout the towns and villages, but for the cultivation of the fields, the zeal for war against the barbarian nations being delegated only to the soldiers and the Roman legions: when that harmony of the Angels was fulfilled, Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace to men of good will (Luke 2:14); and in his days righteousness arose and abundance of peace.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.5-6":["House of Jacob, come, and let us walk in the light of the Lord: for you have cast off your people, the house of Jacob. After the calling of the nations and the showing of the mountain of the Lord on the top of the mountains, the Prophet turns to his own people, that is, the people of the Jews, which is called the house of Jacob, and exhorts them that, being in the darkness of error, they should receive the light of truth and walk in the light of the Lord. And in a manner he sings that word of David: Come to him and be enlightened, and your faces shall not be confounded (Ps 33 [34]:6). For everyone who does evil hates the light, and does not come to the light, lest his works be reproved. But you, house of Jacob, house of my people, come with me, and let us walk together in the light of the Lord: let us receive the Gospel of Christ, let us be enlightened by him who says: I am the light of the world (John 8:12). And when he had said this to the people of the Jews, seeing their impenitent heart and their soul most hardened by unbelief, he makes an apostrophe to the Lord, and says: I exhort them to come to you and enjoy your light with me for this reason, that, in requital for their sins, you have forsaken your people, once the house of Jacob.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.6":["Because they have been filled of old, and have had soothsayers, like the Philistines. For Philistines the Seventy always translated foreigners, a common noun for a proper one: this is today the nation of the Palestinians, as it were of the Philisthinians, because the Hebrew tongue does not have the letter P, but uses the Greek PHI in its place. Hence what is said in the Psalms, with the enumeration of the other nations, The foreigners have been made subject to me (Ps 59 [60]:10), signifies not all foreign nations but properly the Palestinians. And he gives the reasons why God has cast off his people, the house of Jacob: because they have been filled, he says, as they were at the beginning, with soothsayers and omens and all the filth of idolatry. We know from the writing of Moses that the omens and auguries which the nations had whom the Lord drove out before Israel, the Canaanites, Amorites and Hittites, were not to be heeded. The commentators of the Church expound this passage in one way and another. For some will have it to signify that, the people of the Jews having been cast off, the Roman army entered the land once of promise, and, the Jews being expelled, foreigners dwelt in Judea, settlers of diverse nations from the whole world, brought in by Titus and Vespasian and Hadrian and the other emperors. Others, however, think that this does not pertain to Roman times but to earlier ones, before they were laid waste by the Babylonians, because they are related to have lived under impious kings and were forsaken by the Lord.\n\nAnd they have clung to strange boys. For which the Seventy translated: And many foreign sons were born to them. Symmachus: And with strange sons they applauded. For which there is written in the Hebrew JESPHICU (ישפיקו), which the Hebrews interpret ἐσφηνώθησαν, and we have rendered they have clung, so that the shamefulness of the vices in the Jewish nation may be shown. And the Greeks and the Romans once suffered from this vice to such a degree that even the most famous philosophers of Greece kept male concubines publicly; and Hadrian, learned in the arts of philosophy, consecrated Antinous as a god and established a temple, victims and priests for him, and from him a city and a region of Egypt received its name. Among the harlots too, in the arches of the theaters, boys stood exposed to public lust, until under the emperor Constantine, when the Gospel of Christ shone forth, both the unbelief of all the nations and this shamefulness were done away. The Seventy, however, signify that their wives were violated, in bearing strange sons to the Jews. Symmachus, by a certain circumlocution and in the decent word of applauding, has shown the same shamefulness with boys.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.7":["And the land is filled with silver and gold, and there is no end of its treasures. Among the other vices of the land of the house of Jacob is numbered also the abundance of gold and silver. Among omens and soothsayers, horses and chariots, which God forbade the kings of Israel to multiply, among idols, which are the works of men's hands, avarice is condemned. Hence the Lord commanded in the Gospel that we should not lay up for ourselves treasures on earth, and that we should not make treasures from which a thief can snatch, adding at the last: You cannot serve God and mammon (Matt 6:24). And finely has the Prophet expressed in other words that phrase of the little verse, The greedy man is ever in want, saying: And there is no end of its treasures; not that treasures have no end, but that the mind of those who possess them is not filled. And by these words both nations, the Jews and the Romans, are branded for avarice. Which the histories too, Greek as well as Latin, relate: that nothing is more avaricious than the nation of the Jews and of the Romans. Hence too the law on extortion was established, and daily we see that word of the Apostle fulfilled: You who preach that one must not steal, do you steal? (Rom 2:21). A more thievish judge hears and condemns a thief, pronouncing in another's case a sentence upon himself.\n\nAnd its land is filled with horses, and its chariots are innumerable. For what is possessed against God's command is not worthy of number. Hence it is said in the Psalms: The horse is deceptive for safety (Ps 32 [33]:17). And in Exodus: The horse and the rider he has thrown into the sea (Exod 15:1). And in another psalm: Some in chariots and some in horses, but we will call upon the name of the Lord our God (Ps 19 [20]:8). Both can be understood: that the people of the Jews multiplied horses and chariots for themselves against God's precept, and that the land of Judea was filled with the horses and chariots of the conquerors.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.8-9":["And its land is filled with idols; they have worshiped the work of their own hands, what their fingers have made; and man has bowed himself down, and the man has been humbled. Where once was the Temple and the worship of God, there the statue of Hadrian and the idol of Jupiter were set up. Which many interpret of that testimony which we read in the Gospel: But when you see the abomination of desolation standing in the holy place (Mark 13:14). And they bowed down to the work of their hands, and man, a rational animal, worshiped bronze and stone. But there are those who interpret these things of the Jews, that before they were taken by the Babylonians they did all these things, and were therefore abandoned by God. Hence at the end of the chapter too is set: Do not, therefore, forgive them. We can say this too according to the anagogy, that every doctrine contrary to the truth worships the works of its own hands and sets up idols in its land, and man bows down and the man is humbled and cannot raise himself up, because he is bound by the devil, unless the Lord raises him up, after the example of that woman whom Satan had bound for eighteen years, so that she could by no means look at the sky but always at the earth (Luke 13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.9":["Do not, therefore, forgive them. For which the Seventy translated: And I will not forgive them. If God is speaking, it is to be understood thus: Because they have done such things, I will not spare them, nor will I forgive such innumerable sins. If the Prophet, it is to be taken thus: Do not, therefore, forgive those who have committed such crimes. And if we understand it of the Romans, the interpretation is truer: who, overthrowing the temple of God, did not worship him who gave them the victory, but the idols of their own hands. But if of the Jews, it is a fierce sentence of the Prophet, so that he seems to pray against his own people, to whom he had said above: House of Jacob, come, and let us walk in the light of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.10":["Enter into the rock, hide yourself in the dug-out ground from the face of the fear of the Lord and from the glory of his majesty. I indeed exhorted the people, saying, House of Jacob, come, and let us walk in the light of the Lord. But because the Lord has cast off the people of Jacob, and has cast them off because they either did or suffered the things which we have expounded above, therefore I foretell to you the evils that are to come, and I exhort you to enter the rocks and hide in the caves from the face of the Babylonian or the Roman army, when all things will be laid waste, according to what we read in the Gospel: Then they will say to the mountains, Fall upon us; and to the rocks, Hide us (Luke 23:30). But according to the anagogy we are commanded, before the face of the Lord's majesty, to take on the strength of the rock, of which it is said: The rock is a refuge for the hares (Ps 103 [104]:18); and, On a rock you have exalted me (Ps 26 [27]:6). Moses too is set in a cleft of the rock to see the back parts of God (Exod 33). And, The people drank from the spiritual rock that followed them (1 Cor 10:4). And he hides himself in the rock who enters his chamber and, having shut the door, adores the Father, so that, though placed in an earthly body, he does not feel the passing storm of the world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.11":["The lofty eyes of man are humbled, and the height of men shall be bowed down; but the Lord alone shall be exalted in that day. When the enemies have come, and the Babylonian or Roman sword has laid waste the whole province, and the columns of warriors have besieged Jerusalem, then neither riches nor nobility of birth nor the power of offices will be able to defend anyone; but there will be one captivity for all, and God alone will be exalted, whose wrath no one will be able to avoid. Many understand these things of the day of judgment: that every creature, in comparison with the divine glory, will be humbled and bowed down, and will feel itself to be nothing.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.12":["For the day of the Lord of hosts is upon everyone proud and lofty, and upon everyone arrogant. These things too are joined to the former. In that day, he says, in which the Lord alone will be exalted, that is, in the day of the Lord's vengeance, all the lofty and the arrogant and the high will be subjected to captivity and the sword. Μεταφορικῶς the word is about the great and the princes: that the more they have been proud, the more they are brought low. For the Lord resists the proud and gives grace to the humble (1 Pet 5). Those who understand it of the day of judgment think that the devil is called proud and lofty and high and arrogant; who in his pride says: I will set my seat above the stars of heaven; I will sit on the lofty mountain, above all the lofty mountains, toward the North; I will ascend above the clouds; I will be like the Most High (Isa 14:13, 14): when out of the mouth of infants and sucklings praise will be perfected, that the enemy and the avenger may be destroyed (Ps 8:3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.13":["And upon all the cedars of Lebanon, high and lifted up, and upon all the oaks of Bashan. In Psalm 28 (29) too it is sung: The voice of the Lord breaking the cedars; the Lord will break the cedars of Lebanon, and will crush them like the calf of Lebanon (Ps 28 [29]:5, 6). And in Psalm 36 (37): I saw the wicked man exalted and lifted up like the cedars of Lebanon; and I passed by, and he was not; and I sought him, and his place was not found (Ps 36 [37]:35, 36). The oaks of Bashan too, which Aquila translated δρύας, Symmachus and Theodotion βαλάνους, we know to be acorn-bearing trees, which, even if they bear fruit, are food for swine, not for men. Bashan is a region of Arabia, over which Og ruled, who was called king of Bashan; and it means αἰσχύνη, that is, ignominy; and if we chose to translate it confusion, that would rather signify σύγχυσιν, that is, Babylon, than Bashan. Upon all, then, who are lifted up in pride, and do works of ignominy, and wallow in the mire of lusts, the vengeance of the Lord will rise. But if the prudent reader should ask why cedar wood was set in the temple of the Lord, and why in Psalm 103 (104), according to the Hebrew truth, we read, The trees of the Lord shall be satisfied, and the cedars of Lebanon which you have planted; there the sparrows will build their nests (Ps 103 [104]:16, 17); and why among the other trees the cedars too are summoned to the praises of the Lord; and why at the coming of the Savior, when all the trees and the wood of the field will clap with their branches, it is written: I will set in the waterless land the cedar and the box, and the cypress and the pine (Isa 41:19 LXX); and now the prophetic word threatens the day of the Lord upon the cedars of Lebanon? it must be said that out of the same kind of men some are raised up to the kingdom, others dragged down to punishment; and the cedars of Lebanon, which will be broken because of their pride, are chosen when they are of good odor and have said with the Apostle: We are the good odor of Christ (2 Cor 2:15).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.14":["And upon all the lofty mountains, and upon all the hills that are lifted up. Just as in a good sense, according to the variety of virtues, men are called mountains and hills, so among the impious, according to the diversity of vices, and above all of pride, some are mountains, others hills, upon which the day of the Lord will be; of whom it is written in Ezekiel: Thus says Adonai the Lord to the mountains and the hills: Behold, I will bring a sword upon you, and your high places shall be scattered, and your altars shall be broken, and the rest (Ezek 6:3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.15":["And upon every lofty tower, and upon every fortified wall. Those who refer these things to the times of Vespasian and Hadrian say that what is written was fulfilled bodily: that no lofty tower, no strongest wall, no multitude of ships and industry of trade could prevail against the might of the Roman army; but that the inhabitants of Judea came into such terror that they, with their wives and children, buried themselves in pits of the earth with the gold and silver which they thought would be a help to them, and sought out the deepest caves. For if the belly is the god of the gluttonous and the luxurious, why should not gold and silver be called the god of the avaricious? Others refer these things to Babylonian times. So I will run through each point according to the laws of tropology. A tower is built either for the defense of a city or for a lookout, so that the enemy may be seen coming from afar. Each of us, then, ought to build a tower, having first reckoned the cost, lest, according to the parable of the Gospel (Luke 14), he be laughed at when he cannot finish the work. This tower, well built, stands firm. But if, raised up in pride, it has no firm foundations, it will fall upon him by whom it was built, like that one in Siloam which killed eighteen men. Hence the Lord too says to his hearers: And unless you repent, you will likewise perish (Luke 13:3). In what follows too we shall read that the Lord built a tower in his vineyard, and made a winepress, and put a wall around it, but that all these things were destroyed and laid waste because they swelled with the vain brow of pride (Isa 5). Lofty walls too are built so that the city may not be quickly destroyed, so that it may not lie open to enemies: for the Churches are surrounded with the wisest men and fortified with every reason, lest any perverse doctrine prevail against the truth. Of such walls God speaks to Jerusalem: Behold, upon my hands I have painted your walls, and they are always in my sight (below, Isa 49:16 LXX). But if those who before had attacked the Church, having come to know the truth, pass over to the faith and fight for that which they had before attacked, then this will be fulfilled: You shall quickly be built by those by whom you had been destroyed (ibid., 17). In Leviticus too we read that a house which is in walled cities, if it has not been redeemed within the year, is confirmed to the buyer as a perpetual possession; but if it is in country places and villages which have no walls, it can always be redeemed, and the buyer's price remains unsettled (Lev 25).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.16":["And upon all the ships of Tarshish, and upon all that is beautiful to see. For Tarshish, which all translated alike, the Seventy alone translated sea. The Hebrews think that in their own tongue the sea is properly called THARSIS (תרסים), and that when it is called IAM (ים), it is called so not in the Hebrew tongue but in the Syriac. Jehoshaphat too had ships which he sent to Tarshish (1 Kgs 22:49), but they were broken at Ezion-geber. Solomon too had ships which went to Tarshish (1 Kgs 10:22) and returned after three years and brought the king silver and gold, ivory and apes. But because both kings had sinned, the one given to pleasure and a lover of women of foreign nations, the other joining himself with the king of Samaria, both of which things are referred to the pagans and the heretics, in whom there is nothing but the glitter of eloquence, and a sense built up by diabolical art, and dead speech, which is interpreted in the ivory teeth, and a likeness of human reason, which is perceived in the apes; therefore we read in the Psalms, With a violent wind you will shatter the ships of Tarshish (Ps 47 [48]:8). And of these ships the same Isaiah speaks: Woe to the wings of the ships which are beyond Ethiopia (below, Isa 18:1 LXX). But there are on the contrary good ships too, of which it is said in the same psalms: Those who go down to the sea in ships and do business in many waters, they have seen the works of the Lord and his wonders in the deep (Ps 106 [107]:23). For those who are not idle in the waves of this world, but work, and carry the Lord's merchandise, and hasten to come to the harbor of rest, they see the works of the Lord and his wonders in the deep, when they have come to deep knowledge and, searching all things, that is, even the deep things of God, have beheld his wonders. Josephus thinks Tarshish is Tarsus, a city of Cilicia; others think it a region of India, and that by this name too one of the twelve gems is called, the stone which among us is called chrysolite, from the likeness of its color to the sea. But it is better to take Tarshish absolutely as either the sea or the open main. For Jonah, sailing from Joppa, could not reach India, to which one cannot sail by that sea; but simply go out to the open sea and make for whatever islands. And what follows, And upon all that is beautiful to see, or, as the Seventy translated, And upon every sight of the beauty of ships, is to be taken in the same sense: that whatever seems beautiful in words and is built up by human reason will be destroyed by the day of the Lord, if it lifts itself up against the knowledge of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.17-18":["And the loftiness of men shall be bowed down, and the height of men shall be humbled, and the Lord alone shall be exalted in that day, and the idols shall be utterly broken. Human speech seems to have reason so long as it has not been compared with divine knowledge. But when falsehood draws near to the truth, like stubble to fire, it is quickly devoured and perishes, and all the doctrines of falsehood, which are now called idols because they are feigned and fabricated, will be utterly broken.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.20":["In that day man shall cast away the idols of his silver and the images of his gold, which he had made for himself, to worship moles and bats; and he shall go into the clefts of the rocks and the caverns of the stones from the face of the terror of the Lord and from the glory of his majesty, when he rises to strike the earth. We have often said that silver and gold are taken for speech and sense; and though these were given by God to men so that they might either speak of God or perceive God and praise their creator, they abuse this gift for the fashioning of idols; according to what is written: I gave them silver and gold, but they made Baal of my silver and gold (Hos 2:8). When, then, someone, terrified by the fear of the Lord, has first hidden his idols in the cave of his breast and concealed them in the abysses of the earth, not daring to bring out what he had wickedly fashioned, the second advance is that he cast away what he had first concealed and not suffer it to be in him. For moles, as we have translated, the Seventy have vain things, Aquila ὀρυκτὰ, Symmachus unfruitful things; Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself, PHARPHAROTH (פרפרות). It is an animal without eyes, which always digs the earth and throws out the soil, and, eating the roots underneath, is harmful to crops; the Greeks call it ἀσπάλακα. The bat, a bird of the night, which received from them the fitting name νυκτερὶς because it flits about in the night, is a small animal, like a mouse, sounding not so much with voice and song as with squeaking; and though it is seen to fly, it shuns the light and cannot bear to see the sun. To such animals are compared the idols, which, blind and dark, are worshiped by the blind, and all doctrines contrary to the truth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.21":["And when these have been abandoned in the day of the Lord, those who have cast them away will enter the clefts of the rocks and the caverns of the stones, so as to dwell not in the dust of the earth and in vile mud but in firm reason, and find for themselves the various openings of the virtues, through which they may be able to come to the truth. These things, according to the anagogy, I have touched on as I could in brief words; the Hebrews refer them to Babylonian times and the overthrow of Jerusalem, when the Lord rose to strike the land of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.2.22":["Cease, therefore, from the man whose breath is in his nostrils, for he himself is reckoned lofty. The Seventy omitted this, and in the Greek copies it was added by Origen under asterisks from the edition of Aquila; in the Hebrew it reads thus: HEDALU LACHEM MEN AADAM ASER NASAMA BAAPHPHO CHI BAMA NESAB HU. Where we have said, he himself is reckoned lofty, Aquila translated, in what is this one reckoned. The Hebrew word BAMA (במה) means either ὕψωμα, that is, a high place, which we read both in the books of Kingdoms and in Ezekiel, or else in what, and it is written with the same letters, BETH, MEM, HE; and according to the character of the passages, if we wish to read in what, we say BAMMA; but if high place or lofty, we read BAMA. The Jews, then, understanding that the prophecy is about Christ, interpreted the ambiguous word in the worse sense, so that they might seem not to praise Christ but to count him as nothing. For what consequence of words is there, and what order of reason and sense, that we should say: Since these things are so, and the day of the Lord is to come, in which the whole state of Judea is to be overthrown and all things broken, I warn and command you to cease from the man who breathes and lives as we men do, because he is to be reckoned as nothing? Would any man so praise someone as to say: Beware of offending him who is altogether nothing? Therefore it must be understood the other way: Since all these things are to come upon you, and are foretold by the prophetic spirit, I warn and command you to cease from him who according to the flesh is indeed a man, and has a soul, and so breathes and draws breath through his nostrils as we men breathe and live, but who according to the divine majesty both is lofty and is reckoned and believed so. Turning it over silently in my mind, I cannot find the reason why the Seventy would not translate into Greek so clear a prophecy about Christ. For as to the others, who did translate it but dragged the ambiguous word to an impious sense, it is no wonder why they translated badly and would not say anything glorious about Christ, in whom they did not believe: being, namely, Jews or half-Jews, that is, Ebionites. And that Christ is lofty or most high, which among the Hebrews is called by another word, ELION (עליון), we read in Psalm 86 (87): Shall not a man say to Zion, and a man was born in her, and the Most High himself founded her (Ps 86 [87]:5)? And in the Gospel: And you, child, shall be called the Prophet of the Most High (Luke 1:76). And not to draw out a long rope (for in the exposition of the holy Scriptures we ought to follow the truth, not contention), in this place BAMA among the Hebrews means not lofty but a high place, that is, loftiness itself and sublimity: as if we were to say of someone, he is not divine but divination, not a stream but a fountain, not a man but humanity itself. Origen interpreted this passage thus: Because it is said in the singular of one man, it can be referred also to the Lord Savior, the Prophet commanding them to cease from him who is reckoned as something great, although at present he appears as a man and to have breath in his nostrils, as other men too breathe.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.1":["For behold, the ruler, the Lord of hosts, will take away from Jerusalem and from Judah the mighty and the strong: all the strength of bread, and all the strength of water. Up to this point they think it is spoken of the judgment. What follows concerns the captivity to come, which some refer to the Babylonians, others to the Romans. But it is better, as we said above, for everything to be referred to the Lord's passion. For after he was slain, all graces and gifts were taken away from the Jews, according to what is written in the Gospel: The Law and the Prophets until John the Baptist (Matt 11:13). And the order is this: Because you would not cease from man, whose breath is in his nostrils, who has been reckoned exalted, but on the contrary you shed the blood of the righteous one and devised the worst counsel, saying: Let us bind the righteous one, for he is useless to us (Wis 2:12), therefore you shall eat the fruit of your own devices. Among the Jews after the Passion of the Lord nothing is strong, nothing is mighty, but all things are weak and languid. Nor can anyone among them say: I can do all things in him who strengthens me (Phil 4:13), Christ Jesus our Lord. And since according to the Seventy we read the mighty man and the mighty woman, to the mighty man we can fit this: Until we all meet in the perfect man, in the measure of the age of the fullness of Christ (Eph 4:13). And further, of the mighty woman: I wish, he says, to present you all as a chaste virgin to Christ (2 Cor 11:2). Now the strength of bread and the strength of water is taken for all food and drink. We read that Moses was on Mount Sinai, and for forty days did not eat bread and did not drink water (Exod 34). That too which is said to Adam: In the sweat of your face you shall eat your bread, until you return to the earth from which you were taken (Gen 3:19), is to be heard in the same way. So also what is said by the Savior: Man does not live by bread alone, but by every word that goes forth through the mouth of God (Matt 4:4). The strength of bread, then, will be taken away from them: he who says, I am the living bread, who came down from heaven (John 6:51). And the strength of water, of which the same Lord spoke: Everyone who drinks of this water will thirst again; but whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst for ever; but the water that I shall give him will become in him a spring of water leaping up into eternal life (John 4:13). Of such loaves we read in Proverbs as well: Open your eyes, and be filled with loaves (Prov 20:13). The Jews have bread, but without strength; they have waters, but without vigor. For they read the Scriptures, but do not understand them; they hold the parchments, and have lost Christ, who is written in the parchments (1 Cor 3). For they are fed on milk, like little children, and not on solid food. And because they have lost their strength and are weak, therefore they eat vegetables. But the food of athletes is solid, which both sustains human life and furnishes strength to the living (Rom 14). Of such bread and water, that they are to be taken away from the Jews, another Prophet too bears witness, saying: Behold, the day will come, says the Lord, and I will send a famine upon the land: not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but a famine of hearing the words of the Lord (Amos 8:11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.2":["The strong man and the man of war, the judge and the Prophet, and the soothsayer, and the old man. For strong man, which alone is in the Hebrew, the Seventy translated two words together, giant and strong man, meaning the same man to be the giant who is the strong man. Of the strong man we have spoken above. Now a giant in the good sense, that is, of the Lord and Savior, we read in the eighteenth psalm: He has exulted as a giant to run his course; his going forth is from the height of heaven, and his circuit even to its height (Ps 18 [19]:8). But if we read of Nimrod the giant (Gen 10), who was a hunter before the Lord, and of the giants (Gen 6) on whose account the flood came upon the earth, they are to be taken in the contrary sense. The man of war, too, we understand according to the history in this way: that they have been taken captive, and to this day are in servitude, and have not laid aside the yoke of slavery; that they do not even have judges of their own, but are subject to Roman judges, so much so that Roman rulers judge those of their princes who seem to be of standing among the people. But this too must be said: that there is no man of war among them in the Law, having the knowledge of judging, but all things are vain and perishable and full of folly. As for the Prophet, that he has ceased to exist among them no one doubts. We ask, according to the Hebrew, how we are to interpret soothsayer, whom all have translated diviner, except the Seventy, who for this translated conjecturer. And it must be said that future things are often told even through soothsayers, as we read of the diviner Balaam, and of the diviners of the five cities of the Philistines, Gaza and Ashkelon, Gath and Ekron and Ashdod, who give counsel how the ark of the Lord is to be sent back (1 Sam 6). And the sense is: both true things and false will alike be taken away from the Jews. The old man too, whom the Seventy translated elder, he understands to be taken away from the Jews, he who would know that in the holy Scriptures elders are chosen for merit and wisdom, not for age. For old men have not ceased to exist among the Jews; we often see them reach a decrepit old age. And we read in the beginning of Daniel, according to Theodotion, of two elders grown old in evil days (Dan 13). For Moses too is commanded to choose elders whom he knows to be elders (Exod 18). And the apostle Paul writes most fully to Timothy what kind of elder ought to be chosen (1 Tim 5). Hence it is said in Proverbs as well: The glory of old men is gray hair (Prov 20:29). What is this gray hair? Doubtless wisdom, of which it is written: Prudence is the gray hair of men (Wis 4:8). And though we read that from Adam to Abraham men lived nine hundred years and more (Gen 24), no one before was called an elder, that is, an old man, except Abraham, who is shown to have lived far fewer years. Hence John too, after the children and the young men, writes also to the old, saying: I have written to you, fathers, because you have known him who is from the beginning (1 John 2:13). And Rehoboam the son of Solomon lost the kingdom for this reason, that he would not listen to the elders (1 Kgs 12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.3":["The captain of fifty. Συνεκδοχικῶς, the whole from a part. For just as those who command a hundred soldiers are called Centurions, and those who command a thousand Chiliarchs, whom we call Tribunes, from the fact that they command a tribe: so in the army of Israel those who were at the head of fifty soldiers were called captains of fifty. Hence too we call Decurions those who command ten men. Nor is it a wonder if among the Jews every rank of warriors has perished, since they have no power to serve under the sword and to bear arms. But since the number fifty is always referred to penitence, and Christ is the prince of penitence and the head of those who are saved through penitence, let us briefly lay open the mysteries of this number. In Genesis we read, when Abraham speaks to the Lord, that he said first that the city should be saved for fifty righteous, if they were found in it. And in Numbers there is the most celebrated festival of the seven weeks, that is, of Pentecost. The Jubilee too, that is, the year of remission, which is woven of seven weeks of years, that is, of forty-nine years, in which the trumpets sound and the ancient possession returns to everyone, contains this mystery. Hence David too writes the Psalm of penitence at the fiftieth number. We read also in the same book of Numbers that of the booty and spoils the priests were to receive the fiftieth head, of men as well as of beasts, and the Levites who guard the doors of the tabernacle the fiftieth. That parable of the Gospel as well (Luke 7 and 16), in which two debtors are spoken of, one who owed five hundred denarii and the other fifty, refers to this understanding. Hence the Apostle too, among the Ephesians, to whom he had unfolded every mystical thing, wishes to remain until Pentecost, until they should be perfectly converted to the Lord, saying: For a great and evident door has been opened to me, but there are many adversaries (1 Cor 16:9). But the devil also imitates the army of Israel, concerning which Jethro had given counsel to his son-in-law Moses, that he should set over the people tribunes and centurions and captains of fifty (Exod 18). Hence the impious king (2 Kgs 1) sends two captains of fifty with the soldiers under them to bring Elijah: they were consumed by divine fire. But the third of them is saved, who, having turned to prayers, had come to know the mysteries of penitence. Hence the Jews too, unwilling that the Lord should be perfect or the captain of fifty, say to him: You are not yet fifty years old, and you have seen Abraham? (John 8:57). And he, knowing himself to be the prince not only of the penitent but also of the righteous, answered: Before Abraham was, I am (ibid., 58). And that the Lord is the Captain of Fifty we can understand from a single festival, of which he says: The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath (Matt 12:8). If of the Sabbath, then of the Kalends too. If of the Kalends, then of the Pasch, that is, of the Unleavened Bread. If of the Pasch, then of the Jubilee. If of the Jubilee, then of Tabernacles. If of Tabernacles, then of Pentecost, which is referred to the fiftieth number.\n\nAnd the honorable in countenance, and the counselor. For two, that is, honorable in countenance, and counselor, the Seventy translated one, wonderful man counselor. Among the other graces the Lord will take this too away from Judea, that they have no counselor, but do everything without counsel. And there is that praiseworthy and admirable saying of a Greek poet: \"He is first in happiness who is wise of himself; second, he who listens to the wise.\" But he who lacks both is useless, both to himself and to all. Hence we read this too in our books: Let your friends be very many, but your counselor one of a thousand (Sir 6:6). And again: Do all things with counsel (Prov 13:10); and prudence will give you a wonderful counselor. Decrees of the Senate too are called consulta, and the chief men of Rome were once called consuls, either from consulting for the citizens or from ruling all things by counsel. And we in the Church have our senate, the assembly of elders. Since, then, among other things Judea has lost even its old men, how will it be able to have counsel, which properly belongs to the elders?\n\nAnd the wise architect. For which Aquila translated the wisest of craftsmen, to show that they once had many craftsmen: such as was that Bezalel son of Uri, son of Hur, of the tribe of Judah, whom God filled with the spirit of wisdom and understanding and knowledge, so that in every work he was a most accomplished workman, not only in gold and silver but also in bronze and the other things that Moses enumerates, he who built the Tabernacle of God (Exod 31). Since, then, they have lost their architects, all the grace of building has been transferred to the Church. Hence the apostle Paul says: As a wise architect I have laid the foundation (1 Cor 3:10). Jeremiah too was an architect, who not only rooted up and dug under and destroyed, but also built (Jer 18). Hence the same Apostle says: We are God's husbandry, God's building (1 Cor 3:9). Our Lord too is called the builder of walls (Eph 2). And in the Gospel he who made all things is taunted as the son of a carpenter (Matt 13). In the volume of Kings too we read that king Solomon sent and took Hiram the Tyrian, the son of a widow woman, who was of the tribe of Naphtali and had a Tyrian father, a worker in bronze. And he came in to the king and did all the works for king Solomon: in him there is a type of the Church to be built, which was begotten not only of the tribe of Judah but also of Naphtali, and of a Tyrian father, he who had a widowed mother, because she had lost her former husband (1 Kgs 7). And many more are the children of the desolate than of her who has a husband (Isa 54). But in the book of Chronicles too we read of the valley of craftsmen: And Seraiah begot Joab, the father of the valley of craftsmen: for craftsmen were there (1 Chr 4:14). They are said to dwell in a valley for this reason, that knowledge did not puff them up, but they followed the humble and meek Jesus, and could say with the Apostle: For I am the least of the Apostles, who am not worthy to be called an Apostle (1 Cor 15:9). And elsewhere: We know in part, and we prophesy in part, and now we see through a mirror in an enigma (1 Cor 13:9).\n\nAnd the one prudent in mystic speech. For which the Seventy translated the prudent hearer; Theodotion, the prudent enchanter; Aquila, the prudent whisperer; Symmachus, the one prudent in mystic speech, whom we too have followed in this place. First it must be said, according to the Seventy, that among the other spiritual graces a prudent hearer too is necessary in the Church. For to one is given prophecy, to another the discerning of spirits (1 Cor 12:10). Hence there were also established among the people of the Jews priestly ranks, to discern prophets and false prophets, that is, to understand who spoke from the Spirit of God and who from the contrary spirit. Let us read Jeremiah. But the prudent one in mystic speech (Jer 14), or the whisperer, and, as Theodotion would have it, the enchanter, seems to me to be a man learned and practiced both in the Law and the Prophets and in the Gospel and the Apostles, who can heal each disturbance of the soul by his teaching and bring it back to a settled state of mind: while the fornicator recovers chastity, and the glutton frugality, and the man once greedy gives alms. Sift through all the synagogues of the Jews, and you will be able to find no teacher who enjoins holy things and, despising riches, teaches that poverty is to be pursued. Of such enchanters against serpents and asps, that is, against sinners who have abandoned the righteousness of God, David spoke in the Psalm: Sinners have been estranged from the womb; they have gone astray from the belly, they have spoken falsehoods. Their fury is after the likeness of a serpent: like the deaf asp that stops its ears, which will not hear the voice of the enchanters, nor of the sorcerer who charms wisely (Ps 57 [58]:4ff.). Every word of the Apostle is an enchantment against serpents and asps, which sinners and heretics do not hear, because they shut their ears so as not to hear the truth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.4":["And I will give children to be their princes. If it is said in the person of the Prophet: For behold, the ruler, the Lord of hosts, will take away from Jerusalem and from Judah the mighty man and the mighty woman: all the strength of bread, and all the strength of water, and the rest, how does the same prophet now say: I will give children to be their princes, and the effeminate shall rule over them? Therefore, according to prophetic custom, while the Prophet is speaking, God suddenly speaks through the Prophet in his own person, and says: I will give children to be their princes: for having removed the things I had given before, and having taken away good things as though in anger, now on the contrary I will give evil things. I took away the old man and the captain of fifty, and the wonderful counselor, and the wise architect, and the prudent hearer, and so on. In their place I will give children as princes. For those who have lost the elder, such as the discourse above has shown, such as Abraham too was who was first called an elder, rightly receive young men as princes (Gen 7). Of these we read in Ecclesiastes too: Woe to you, O city, whose king is a youth, and your princes eat in the morning. Blessed is the land whose king is a son of the freeborn (Eccl 10:16). Such a young king was Rehoboam the son of Solomon, who followed the counsels of the young men (1 Kgs 12): not that he was young in age, but in wisdom. Otherwise, he is related to have received the kingdom at forty years and more. And on the contrary, Solomon was twelve years old when he took up the rule, and because he had wisdom, he was therefore not called a youth. For there was in him breadth of heart and amplitude of wisdom as great as the sand on the shores of the sea. Hence the Apostle too writes to Timothy: Let no one despise your youth (1 Tim 4:12). For he who is young in age is an old man in maturity. And Daniel, according to Theodotion, was called a boy before he judged (Dan 13); but after God stirred up his spirit in him and he judged the elders, he received the dignity of the eldership. To Jeremiah too, who had said to the Lord who was sending him, You who are ruler, O Lord: Behold, I do not know how to speak, for I am a youth (Jer 1:6), the Lord answered: Do not say, I am a youth; for before you were formed in the womb I knew you, and before you came forth from your mother's womb I sanctified you, and I appointed you a prophet among the nations. For this reason, I think, the young widows too are commanded by the Apostle (1 Tim 5) to take husbands and be mothers of families and bring up their children, because many have gone back after Satan; and when they have grown wanton against Christ, they wish to marry, having condemnation, because they have made void their first faith. Nor is a widow supported on the Church's provisions unless she is sixty years old and has maturity of character as well as of age. One might think that what the Apostle forbids (1 Tim 3), that a neophyte be made a bishop, pertains to this, because he is a little one in faith, lest, puffed up into pride, he fall into the judgment of the devil. And the judgment of the devil is nothing else but pride, on account of which he fell from heaven. Hence the Savior too says: I saw Satan falling like lightning from heaven (Luke 10:18).\n\nAnd the effeminate shall rule over them. For which in the Hebrew is written THALULIM (תעלולים), whom the Seventy and Theodotion interpreted as mockers, Aquila ἐναλλάκτας, those who change themselves and take turns in shameful acts, such as we read of in the book of Judges concerning the Levite's concubine in Gibeah (Judg 19). Let us consider the Patriarchs of the Jews, and the young men or boys, effeminate and abounding in luxuries, and we shall see that the prophecy has been fulfilled. We may also call mockers the teachers of the people of Israel, who devour the people of God like a morsel of bread, and interpret the holy Scriptures perversely, and make sport of the folly of their disciples.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.5":["And the people shall rush, man upon man, each one upon his neighbor: the boy shall riot against the old man, and the base against the noble. When the princes are young men, and mockers of the Lord, such as the prophetic discourse describes, then no order will be kept, whether of dignity or of age or of knowledge; but boys will rebel against old men, and the base against the noble, and they will fall upon one another. And that saying of the Apostle will be fulfilled: Biting one another, you have been consumed by one another (Gal 5:15). That these things befell the people of the Jews under Titus the son of Vespasian, Josephus writes (Jewish War, Book 5): and that, when they were being besieged by the Romans, Jerusalem was divided by sedition into three parts, and some held the citadel and the Temple, others the lower, others the upper part of the city.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.6":["For a man shall take hold of his brother, of the household of his father: You have a garment, be our prince; and let this ruin be under your hand. For ruin, Symmachus and Theodotion translated weakness; the Seventy, food. It signifies the scarcity of men, and above all the want of all things, so that whoever has food and clothing is believed to be powerful and rich and illustrious. And what it says according to the Hebrew, And let this ruin be under your hand, has this sense: Let our misery and calamity be sustained and protected by your help. And this must be considered, that each man chooses for himself his own prince, and that there is sedition in the choosing of princes, while some think some men worthy of rule and others others. Nor do they say to them, Your possessions and riches and revenues will be able to sustain us; but, This weakness, or my food, will be healed by your judgment and will hang upon it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.7":["And he will answer in that day, saying, I am not a physician, and in my house there is neither bread nor garment; do not appoint me prince of the people. For physician, Symmachus and the Seventy translated prince; Theodotion, ἐπιδεσμεύοντα, one who binds up wounds and provides for health. He will answer is not found in the Hebrew, but has been added from the Seventy. For in the Hebrew it is joined to what is written above: In that day he will say, I am not a physician, and the rest. And he will say it who has been chosen prince. And just as the people desires to have as prince the one whom it perceives to be richer in comparison with itself, so the one who is chosen, considering his poverty and his weakness, testifies that he is unworthy of the honor conferred and cannot heal vices, that is, cure the sick, give food to the hungry, clothe the naked, since he himself is scarcely able to sustain himself. Let us not, then, at once acquiesce in the judgment of the multitude; but when chosen for leadership, let us know our measure and be humbled under the mighty hand of God, because God resists the proud and gives grace to the humble (1 Pet 5). How many, having neither bread nor clothing, though they themselves are hungry and naked, and have no spiritual food and have not kept the tunic of Christ whole, promise others both nourishment and clothing, and, full of wounds, boast that they are physicians; nor do they keep that saying of Moses: Provide another whom you may send, and that other command: Seek not to be made a judge, lest perhaps you be unable to take away iniquities (Sir 7:6). Jesus alone heals all sicknesses and infirmities, of whom it is written: Who heals the broken in heart, and binds up their bruises (Ps 146 [147]:3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.8":["For Jerusalem has fallen, and Judah has collapsed: because their tongue and their devices are against the Lord, to provoke the eyes of his majesty. The recognition of their countenance has answered them: and they have proclaimed their sin like Sodom, and have not hidden it. It is the Prophet who says this, not, as many think, the one who had been chosen prince. And he says that no one is willing to be set over a sinful people for this reason, that both Judah and Jerusalem, both the city and the province of Judea, or the tribe of Judah, have fallen alike. And he gives the causes of their impiety: that they blasphemed against the Lord and said, Away, away, crucify such a one; we have no king but Caesar (John 19:15, 16), and by the fury of their tongue provoked the most merciful Lord to bitterness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.9-11":["Woe to their soul, because evils have been repaid to them. Say to the righteous that it is well, because they have eaten the fruit of their own devices. Woe to the wicked unto evil: for the reward of his hands shall be done to him. According to the Hebrew and the other Translators it has this sense: Woe to them, because they have received back their crimes. Therefore you who hear, or who read the book of the Prophet, praise the righteousness of God, because he has done well: because the wicked have eaten the labors of their own hands. And woe to the wicked people unto evil: for it has received what it deserved. And he who handed over his prince to the Roman power himself lies subject to Roman servitude. But according to the Seventy Translators, who said: Woe to their soul, because they have devised the worst counsel against themselves, saying: Let us bind the righteous one, because he is useless to us; therefore they shall eat the fruits of their works, it is clearly said of the passion of Christ, that they entered upon the worst counsel, not so much against the righteous one as against themselves and their own soul; and now they eat the fruits of their works. For whatever a man sows, that he will also reap (Gal 6:5-8), and each one will bear his own burden.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.9":["The recognition of their countenance will answer them: that is, they have received back their own sins; or, as the Seventy translated, the confusion of their countenance has stood against them: that is, they have always had their own offenses before their eyes. And just as the Sodomites, sinning with all license and having not even any shame in their crime, said to Lot, Bring the men out, that we may lie with them (Gen 19:5): so these too, crying out publicly, proclaimed their sin and had no modesty in blaspheming. For to hide one's impiety is the second plank after shipwreck and a consolation of miseries. Hence they are also called princes of Sodom, because they had the sins of Sodom.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.12":["My people, their exactors have despoiled them, and women have ruled over them. For women, whom Symmachus alone translated so, and who in Hebrew are called NASIM (נשים), Aquila and the Seventy translated ἀπαιτοῦντας, which means exactors; Theodotion δανειστὰς, that is, usurers. Now the prophetic discourse speaks against the Scribes and Pharisees, who for the sake of base gain, to receive tithes and first-fruits, denied the Son of God. And he does not call them masters, scribes and teachers, but exactors (Luke 20), who think that piety is a means of gain, and devour, not, as in the Apostle, only the houses of widows, but the whole people; and, accusing their luxury and their shameful way of life, he calls them not only exactors, as men seen to exact money from the unwilling, but women, because they do everything for lust's sake and are given over to pleasures. Let us too, then, beware that we be not exactors among the people; that, as the impious Porphyry says, matrons and women be not our senate, ruling in the Churches, and that the favor of women judge not of the priestly rank.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","My people, those who call you blessed, they deceive you, and scatter the way of your steps, or throw it into confusion. He had called the Scribes and Pharisees exactors, not masters; and above, mockers, who because of gifts, which blind the eyes even of the wise, not only did not rebuke the sinners among the people, but for the sake of riches and profits extolled them with praises, calling them blessed, and pillars of the house of God, and the other things flatterers are wont to say. He, then, is the churchman teacher who moves tears, not laughter, who rebukes sinners, who calls no one blessed, no one happy, and does not anticipate the sentence of his judge, since holy Scripture says (Sir 11:30): Call no man blessed before death. But in another place too we read (Prov 27:14): He who blesses his friend in the morning with a loud voice differs in nothing from one who curses. Therefore, despising the judgments of men, let us neither be lifted up by their praises nor saddened by their disparagements; but let us enter upon the right way and the paths trodden by the holy prophets, and let us hear the prophet Jeremiah saying: Stand in the ways and see, and ask for the everlasting paths of the Lord, which is the good way, and walk in it (Jer 6:16). And if ever we go astray and, being men, proceed by a perverse road, let us await the promises of the Lord, who says through Ezekiel: I will give them another way, and another heart (Ezek 36). But the exactors have perverted and troubled the way of the Lord, so that, having the key of knowledge, they neither entered themselves nor suffered the people to enter, but made them lose the way of the truth, which says in the Gospel: I am the way, and the life, and the truth (John 14:6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.13-14":["The Lord stands to judge, and he stands to judge the peoples. The Lord will come to judgment with the elders of his people, and with its princes. The people, which was deceived through simplicity and inexperience, is still called the people of God, and is therefore judged that it may be saved. Nor does the Lord sit in the attire of one judging, as we read in Daniel: Thrones were set, and books were opened (Dan 7:9); but he stands to judge, and he stands to judge the peoples, wishing those to stand whose way had been scattered. But against the princes and elders of his people he comes to judgment, not to judge but to be judged alike with them, giving them room for defense, if they can have anything to answer, according to what is said in the fiftieth psalm: That you may be justified in your words, and may overcome when you are judged (Verse 6). In the prophet Micah too we read something of the kind (Mic 6), which we have interpreted in its place. This present passage, then, is taken against the Pharisees and the Δευτερωτὰς. And between the old men and the princes there was, I think, in the old people what there is now between the elders and the bishops.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.14-15":["For you have grazed down my vineyard: the plunder of the poor is in your house. Why do you wear down my people, and grind the faces of the poor?, or crush them, and as the Seventy interpreted, put them to confusion. He keeps the prophetic custom of suddenly changing the persons: for above the Lord himself had said, My people, those who call you blessed, they deceive you; and afterwards the Prophet had added, The Lord stands to judge: the Lord will come to judgment. Therefore after the prophet, the Lord himself, who had come to judgment with the elders of his people and its princes, speaks to them and rebukes the offenders: Why do you graze down my vineyard?, of which it is written: You have brought a vine out of Egypt (Ps 79 [80]:9). And in this same prophet: The vineyard of the Lord Sabaoth is the house of Israel (Isa 5:7). This vineyard the Lord in the Gospel too let out to most wicked husbandmen, who killed the son of the householder sent to them (Matt 21). The plunder of the poor, he says, is in your houses. Take the poor man either simply, as one who needs alms, or rather as the poor in spirit, of whom it is written: Blessed is he who understands concerning the needy and the poor (Ps 40 [41]:1). And the apostle Paul: Only, he says, that we should be mindful of the poor (Gal 2:10). And what follows, Why do you wear down my people, and grind the faces of the poor?, or put them to confusion, is plainly said to the princes of the Jews. But it can also be referred to our own princes, if they wear down the common people subject to them, and publicly reprove and confound the poor when they offend, but do not dare even to say mu to the rich who sin worse. And the plunder of the poor is in their houses when they fill their treasuries and abuse the wealth of the Church for their luxuries, and either keep for themselves or distribute to their relatives the public offerings given for the support of the poor, and make the want of others into the riches of themselves and their own.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.16":["And the Lord said: Because the daughters of Zion are lifted up, and have walked with outstretched neck, and went with nods of the eyes, and clapped, and walked, and stepped with their feet in measured gait. Above, both the prophet and the Lord had rebuked both the people and the princes; now the prophetic discourse turns to the women of whom he had said before, And women have ruled over them, lest they should think themselves strangers to the crime, they on account of whose luxuries and wantonness the exactors grazed down the Lord's vineyard, and the plunder of the poor is in their houses, and they wore down his people and put to confusion the faces of the poor. Some think these are truly the women of the Jews; others judge that it is said metaphorically of the cities of Judea, which are called daughters of Zion: the lesser towns, that is, villages and townships. Hence in the book of Joshua too the names of the cities are set down tribe by tribe (Josh 15), and afterwards the villages and fortresses are described and are called daughters. Others, according to tropology, take the women to be souls, which, if they walk with outstretched neck and raise themselves up in pride, and pursue not manly hardness but the softness of women, are rebuked by the prophetic discourse and lose all the ornaments of the virtues, which are described under the crescents and necklaces and jewels and bracelets, the headbands and hair-ornaments and the rest of this kind. This testimony is to be turned also against the women of the Church who walk with outstretched neck and speak with nods of the eyes and clap with their hands as well as their feet, and who, to step in measured gait, do not follow nature as their guide, but hire actors as their teachers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.17":["The Lord will make bald the crown of the daughters of Zion, and will lay bare their hair, and instead of adornment there will be disgrace. This will befall us too whose sins are concealed, when that which is written has been fulfilled: Nothing is hidden that will not be revealed (Luke 8:17). For so long as we are covered by the hair and garment of ignorance, and men think us, who are within full of dead men's bones, to be whitened sepulchers (Matt 23), we seem to have some cleanness. But when what is hidden has been laid open, all the hair will be taken away, and a foul baldness will appear to all.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.18-21":["And the crescents, and the necklaces, and the jewels, and the bracelets, and the headbands, and the hair-ornaments, and the anklets, and the little chains, and the scent-boxes, and the earrings, and the rings, and the gems hanging on the forehead. He describes the jewels of the women, and through them the insignia of the cities; or, according to the anagogy, the ornaments of the various virtues. Women have little bosses hanging down in the likeness of the moon, which we transfer to the ornaments of the Church, which is illumined by the sun of righteousness. Necklaces too, hanging down as far as the breast, show the understanding and the ruling part (ἡγεμονικόν) of the mind in the heart. And jewels, which in a single word signify all ornaments; and the good works of bracelets, such as Rebecca received at her betrothal (Gen 24); and headbands, the ornaments of the head; hair-ornaments, that it may have judgment of each thing; and anklets, by which our step is adorned, that we may hear: Your foot will not stumble (Ps 90 [91]); and: He will rescue my feet from falling (Ps 55 [56]); and little chains, which are woven of rods of gold and silver, that is, of the sense and the words of the Scriptures; and scent-boxes, that we may be the good odor of Christ (2 Cor 2:15); and earrings, that we may not hear the judgment of blood, but the words of the Lord who says: He who has ears to hear, let him hear (Luke 8:8); and rings, with which we are sealed for the service of the Lord, whom God the Father has sealed. Hence it is said to the prince of Tyre: You are the seal of likeness (Ezek 28:12). The prodigal son too received a ring with the robe (Luke 15), and shoes; and gems hanging on the forehead, by which our faces are adorned. Of the adornment of this head we read in the psalm as well: Like ointment on the head, which runs down upon the beard, the beard of Aaron (Ps 132 [133]:1). Although the Seventy Translators, Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translate all these in different ways, we have woven them together as we could, either from the Hebrew or from their translation; nor do we wish to linger longer on each, lest the exposition become trifling and produce weariness in the prudent reader.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.18":["In that day the Lord will take away the adornment of the shoes. In what day? The Jews think, that of the Babylonian captivity. We prove that it is more truly when they were taken by the Romans and lost all the ornaments of clothing and gems, of gold and jewels, and their various furnishings. Or else, through the metaphor of the women, all the adornment of the cities is related to have been destroyed. Understand the ornaments of the cities to be in the streets and porticoes, the forum and the gymnasia and the public walls. If we refer these to the state of souls, let us recall that we have read of the shod feet of him who is to eat the flesh of the lamb and celebrate the Pasch, and that neither the garments nor the shoes of those passing through the wilderness were worn out. What are these shoes? Those of which the Apostle writes to the Ephesians: Your feet shod in preparation of the Gospel of peace (Eph 6:15). These shoes souls will lose when they walk with outstretched neck and drag their garments with their feet, and instead of matronly cleanness sweep up the filth of the ground.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.22-23":["And the pins, and the mirrors, and the fine linens, and the fillets, and the summer veils. Women have pins, by which the arrangement of the dressed hair is held together, so that it may not flow too loosely and be scattered into straggling locks. They have mirrors too, in which they look at their face, and if they see anything lacking, they add to their adornment. They have fine linens, which are called wraps; and fillets, with which the hair is bound, which they call ταινίας. They have summer veils too, which we can call mantles, with which Rebecca also was covered; and today too the women of Arabia and Mesopotamia are veiled with them. In Hebrew they are called ARDIDIM, in Greek θέριστρα, from the fact that in θέρει, that is, in summer and the heat, they protect the bodies of women. The daughters of Zion, therefore, because of their pride lost the pins by which the rule of all the precepts was held fast. They lost the mirrors which the women staying at the doors of the tabernacle offered in Exodus for making the laver of the Lord (Exod 38); of which the apostle Paul too spoke: But now we see through a mirror in an enigma (1 Cor 13:12). They lost the fine linens and the fillets, with which they covered their shoulders and bound fast the mind that flowed this way and that; and the summer veils, with which they were covered as by a most safe shade in the heats. We say these things lest we seem altogether to shun the tropology of this place. But it is a very great labor to linger on each item and to seek a broad explanation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.22":["And the changes of raiment, and the mantles, and the linen cloths. For linen cloths the Seventy interpreted transparent Laconian garments, wishing to signify the very fine garments with which the bodies of the Lacedaemonians were covered, who were most ready for war and of a rather austere life; although neither the Hebrew nor any other of the translators has Laconian. Changes of raiment and mantles, which Symmachus translated more expressively ἀναβολαια, are ornaments of women's clothing, by which the shoulders and breasts are covered. Changes of raiment, according to the anagogy, are those of which it is said: They will go from strength to strength (Ps 83 [84]:8); and linen cloths, that, washed in the Lord, we may enjoy delights, according to what is written in the Psalms: You will give them to drink of the torrent of your pleasure (Ps 35 [36]:9). But all these things the daughters of Zion lost, because they walked with outstretched neck and with nods of the eyes made a show of pride, thinking that what they had was of their own power and not of the grace of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.24":["And instead of a sweet odor there will be a stench, and instead of a girdle, a cord. The penitent says that sins stink: My wounds have putrefied and are corrupted because of my folly (Ps 37 [38]:6). The Apostle commanded the loins to be girded with a girdle, saying: Stand, having your loins girded in truth (Eph 6:14). And of the sinner we read: Each one is bound fast with the cords of his own sins (Prov 5:22). Therefore, instead of the good odor of the virtues, the daughters of Zion will have the rottenness of sins; and instead of the girdle of truth they will be bound with the cords of falsehood, such as those had upon their heads who with rags and filth begged their life from the king of Syria.\n\nAnd instead of a breast-band, haircloth. For breast-band, as Symmachus interpreted it, the Seventy translated a tunic μεσοπορφύραν, that is, striped with purple, which Aquila expressed as girdle of exultation. Theodotion set down the Hebrew word itself, PHTHIGIL (פתיגיל), which is a kind of women's ornament. The band covers the breast, and holds that place in women which the Rational holds in the High Priests. Of this breast-band Jeremiah too spoke in mystical speech: If a bride can forget her adornment, or a virgin her breast-band, I too will forget you, says the Lord (Jer 2:32). The virgin, such as the Apostle desires to present us to Christ (2 Cor 11), so long as she is not corrupted in Egypt and her breasts, which are bound with the breast-band, are not broken, is joined to the bridegroom; and though daily she bears sons of the virtues, she by no means ceases to be a virgin. But if at some time she has spread her feet to every passer-by and followed her lovers, and, according to the prophecy of Hosea, the Lord has hedged in her ways and shut off her paths, she will return to her former husband and will hear: Put off the garments of mourning, and put on the garments of your glory (Hos 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.25":["Your most beautiful men too shall fall by the sword, and your strong ones in battle. For most beautiful men the Seventy interpreted, and your most beautiful son, whom you love, shall fall by the sword. If we understand this of the state of a soul which has sinned after the virtues, let us take her most beautiful son to be good works, which have fallen by the enemy's sword; and the strong too to have perished in battle, because the righteousness of the righteous man will not deliver him in whatever day he has gone astray (Ezek 33:12). But if we follow the history, we shall be taught by these words that the prophet's discourse is not about women whose husbands fell in battle, but about the cities of Judea, which he called daughters of Zion, and whose warriors fell in the conflict. Lastly, the following verse speaks of the same Zion.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.3.26":["And her gates shall lament and mourn, and the city itself, desolate, shall sit upon the ground. This we see with our eyes to this day. According to tropology the interpretation is easy: that whoever has given place to the devil and has not kept his heart with all watchfulness, his gates mourn; and, the bridegroom being absent, he is ever in mourning, and, falling from the heights, sits in the dust of the earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.4.1":["And seven women shall take hold of one man in that day, saying: We will eat our own bread, and be covered with our own garments; only let your name be called upon us: take away our reproach. When the most beautiful men of the city of Jerusalem have fallen by the sword, and the strong have perished in battle, and her gates have lamented and mourned, and she herself has been made desolate through the very great scarcity of men, the warriors having been slain, seven women will take hold of one man, desiring to have seed in Zion and a household in Jerusalem, saying that they have food and clothing, only so that they may not be seen to be without a husband and lie under that curse which is written: Cursed is the barren woman, and she who does not make seed in Israel (Deut 7). Zechariah too agrees with this sense: In those days ten men out of all the tongues of the nations shall take hold of the fringe of a Jewish man, saying: We will go with you, for we have heard that God is with you (Zech 8:23). The numbers seven and ten are familiar to the Jews on account of the sabbath and the ten precepts of the Law, and therefore they make frequent use of them; although, according to the ambiguity of the Hebrew language, in which they interpret the word SABA (שבע) now as seven, now as many, now as oath, it may in this place signify not seven but many. This is according to the letter. But at the coming of the Lord and Savior, seven women, that is, the seven graces of the Holy Spirit, of which the same Prophet is going to speak in what follows: There shall come forth a rod from the root of Jesse, and a flower shall rise up from his root; and the spirit of the Lord shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and strength, the spirit of knowledge and piety; and the spirit of the fear of the Lord shall fill him (Isa 11:1, 2), will take hold of Jesus, whom they had long desired, because they had been able to find no other in whom they might rest in an everlasting abode. Hence John the Baptist too relates in the Gospel: And he who sent me said to me himself: He upon whom you see the Holy Spirit descending and remaining upon him, he it is (John 1:33). In Zechariah too we read of seven eyes upon one stone, and on one golden lampstand seven lamps and seven pipes, and two olive trees on either side of the lamp, founded with a firm root (Zech 3). And that the grace of the Holy Spirit possesses all things needs no exposition. But because among men it always suffered reproach, no one living as the gifts of the Holy Spirit demanded, therefore they desire the name of Jesus to be called upon them, so that what was imperfect in the Law may be completed in the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And in Isaiah we read: Seven women shall take hold of one man, saying: We will eat our own bread and be clothed with our own garments; only let your name be called upon us, take away our reproach. Those, then, who there are called seven women, that is, Churches, whose number is contained also in the apostle Paul — for he writes to seven churches, to the Romans, to the Corinthians, to the Galatians, to the Ephesians, to the Philippians, to the Colossians, to the Thessalonians. And in the Apocalypse of John the Lord, girded with variety and with purest gold, walks in the midst of the seven lampstands, that is, of the churches of the Ephesians, the Smyrnaeans, the Pergamenes, the Thyatirans, the Sardians, the Philadelphians, the Laodiceans —, are now named ten in the prophet Zechariah, whom the Lord also sought, so that, if he had found them in Sodom and Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboiim, he might deliver them from destruction.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ISA.4.2":["In that day the bud of the Lord shall be in magnificence and in glory, and the fruit of the earth exalted, and exultation for those who shall have been saved of Israel. And it shall be that everyone who is left in Zion, and remains in Jerusalem, shall be called holy, everyone who is written in life in Jerusalem. When the daughters of Zion have lost all adornment because of their pride, and her gates have lamented and mourned, and she herself has fallen desolate, and all her warriors have perished in battle, so much so that many women can scarcely have one husband, then the bud of the Christian name will spring up, and the earth will give its fruit; and there will be exultation for those who have been saved of Israel, of whom it was said above as well: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us a seed, we should have been as Sodom, and should have been like Gomorrah (Isa 1:9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.4.3":["And note that not all Israel is saved, but the remnant of the people of Zion, and he who remains in Jerusalem: everyone who is written in life in Jerusalem, to whom the Lord too spoke: Rejoice, because your names are written in heaven (Luke 10:20). It signifies the Apostles and those who believed through the Apostles.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.4.4":["When the Lord shall have washed away the filth of the daughters of Zion, and shall have washed the blood of Jerusalem from her midst by the spirit of judgment and by the spirit of burning. Then the remnant of Israel will be saved, when in the baptism of the Savior their sins have been forgiven them, and that blood has been washed away which the erring people invoked upon itself: His blood be upon us, and upon our children (Matt 27:25). Hence we read above: When you stretch out your hands, I will not hear you: for your hands are full of blood (Isa 1:15, 16). And afterwards, calling them to penitence, he adds: Wash yourselves, be clean. And note that he washed the filth of the daughters of Zion by the spirit of judgment, but the blood of Jerusalem by the spirit of burning. For what is light is washed; what is heavier is burned out. Of this spirit of judgment and spirit of burning John the Baptist spoke in the Gospel: I baptize you in water, but he who comes after me will baptize you in the Holy Spirit and in fire (Matt 3:11). From which we learn that man gives only water, but God gives the Holy Spirit, by whom both filth is washed away and the sins of blood are purged.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","To those who sin gravely he is a refining and consuming fire; but to those who commit light sins, fullers' herb, so as to restore cleanness to those who have been washed, according to what is written in Isaiah: The Lord will wash away the filth of the sons and daughters of Zion, and will cleanse the blood from their midst, by the spirit of judgement and by the spirit of burning. Those who have filth need the spirit of judgement, that they may be washed; those who are full of blood need the spirit of burning, that the blood which has been smeared on them from without may be taken away.\n— Commentary on Malachi"],"ISA.4.5":["And the Lord will create over every place of mount Zion, and where he has been called upon, a cloud by day, and smoke and the brightness of flaming fire by night. For in Christ there is a new creation, of which we read elsewhere: The old things have passed away: behold, all things have been made new (2 Cor 5:17). For which the Seventy translated: And he will come, and it will be; every place of mount Zion, and all things that are round about it, a cloud will overshadow by day, and the light of flaming fire by night. And who will come, if not he of whom it is written: There will come out of Zion one who will deliver (Isa 59:20 LXX)? And of whom another prophet makes mention: Yet a very little while, and he who is to come will come, and will not delay (Hab 2:3). When he has come, the former happiness will be restored to the people, which they once had in the wilderness, when the Lord went before them by day in a pillar of cloud and by night in a pillar of fire (Exod 13), so that they may never be troubled either in prosperity or in adversity. This is said in the psalm as well: The sun shall not burn you by day, nor the moon by night (Ps 120 [121]:6). And smoke in this place signifies not error and ignorance but glory, according to what, in the opinion of some, we shall read in this same prophet (below, Isa 6): And the house was filled with smoke. And in Joel it is said of the grace of the Holy Spirit which descended upon the apostles: I will pour out of my spirit, and they shall prophesy (Joel 2). And: I will give wonders in the heaven above, and signs on the earth below, blood and fire and vapor of smoke (Acts 2:17). The same thing, I think, is signified also by that in the Psalms: Who touches the mountains, and they shall smoke (Ps 103 [104]:32).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.4.6":["And over all the glory a protection. And there shall be a tabernacle for a shade by day from the heat, and for security, and for a hiding place from the whirlwind and from the rain. This place the Jews refer to Antichrist, who they judge is signified by the whirlwind and the storm, when the Lord is to defend them from a most powerful adversary. But let us refer everything to the first coming of Christ, of which we read in the psalms as well: He has protected me in the secret place of his tabernacle: upon a rock he has exalted me (Ps 26 [27]:5); upon which the Church, being founded, is shaken by no storm, overthrown by no whirlwind and winds. Very many of the Jews understand both these things and all those with which they are joined of the Babylonian captivity and the return to Jerusalem under Zerubbabel and Ezra and Nehemiah.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.1":["I will sing now to my beloved the song of my cousin concerning his vineyard. The prophet sings a lamentable song to the people of Israel, which he composed of whom it is written in the Gospel: But when he had seen it (doubtless Jerusalem) he wept over it, and said: If you too had known the things that are for your peace, for the days will come upon you, and your enemies will throw up ramparts around you, and will hem you in, and will lay you low, and your children (Luke 19:41ff.). And again: How often would I have gathered your children, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not? Behold, your house will be left to you desolate (Matt 23:37, 38): to which this is like, which is now said in this song: I will abandon my vineyard. And that Christ is called the beloved and dearest one, whom Aquila interpreted πατράδελφον, uncle, or cousin, the title of the forty-fourth psalm teaches us: A song for the beloved. And the voice of God the Father in the Gospel: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased (Matt 3:17). And in the sixty-seventh psalm we read: The Lord will give the word to those who preach the good news with great power. The King of powers of the beloved (Ps 67 [68]:12, 13). This beloved, then, made a mournful song for his vineyard, which I will sing to my beloved and pitiable people. Or certainly it is to be understood thus: I will sing to Almighty God the Father the song of Christ, who is my cousin, that is, born of the same nation as I. And that the people of Israel is called the vineyard of God we read also at the end of this song: The vineyard of the Lord of hosts is the house of Israel, and the man of Judah his praiseworthy shoot. And in the seventy-ninth psalm (Verse 9): You have brought a vine out of Egypt, you have cast out the nations, and planted it. In the Gospel too, in almost the same words in which the Prophet now speaks, the Lord weaves a parable: There was a certain householder who planted a vineyard, and set a wall around it, and dug a winepress in it, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, etc. (Matt 21:33). And in Jeremiah we read: I planted you a fruitful vine, wholly true: how have you been turned into bitterness, a strange vine? (Jer 2:21). And so Jerusalem, as we have said, is lamented, and her ruin is sung in prophetic speech. But for the Church, and the people once of the nations, another song has been put forth, of which we read in the psalms: Sing to the Lord, all the earth; announce his salvation from day to day. Announce his glory among the nations, his wonders among all peoples (Ps 95 [96]:2, 3). And again: Sing to the Lord a new song, for he has done wonders. The Lord has made known his salvation, in the sight of the nations he has revealed his righteousness (Ps 97 [98]:1, 2).\n\nMy beloved had a vineyard on a horn, the son of oil. Theodotion and Aquila translated this according to the Hebrew; but Symmachus, in his manner, more plainly: My beloved had a vineyard on a horn in the midst of olive trees. The Seventy, moreover, interpreted the sense rather than the word: My beloved had a vineyard on a horn, in a fat place, or a fertile one, for πίων signifies both. And indeed nothing is fatter than the land of Promise, if you consider not the mountainous and desert parts but its whole breadth, from the river of Egypt to the great river Euphrates toward the East, and on the northern side as far as Mount Taurus and Zephyrium of Cilicia, which overhangs the sea. And we often read that horn signifies kingdom and power, as in that saying in the Gospel: He has raised up a horn of our salvation in the house of David his servant (Luke 1:69). And: In you we will toss our enemies with the horn (Ps 43 [44]:6). And in Zechariah four horns denote four most powerful kingdoms (Zech 1). The Hebrews expound this place thus: A vineyard was planted for Christ on a horn, that is, in a strong and lofty place, which is called the son of oil, either because it stands in need of God's mercy and is sustained by his help, or because it has furnished the clear light of the knowledge of God to all nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.2":["And he fenced it, and chose out the stones from it, and planted the vine Sorec, and built a tower in its midst, and set up a winepress in it. Through the metaphor of the vineyard, as we said before, he describes the people of the Jews, which he fenced with the help of the Angels. And he chose out the stones from it, either idols or all things that could hinder the worship of God. And he planted it with the vine Sorec, which Symmachus alone interpreted as choice, expressing, as it seems to me, not word for word but the sense contained in the word. For the Hebrews say that Sorec is a kind of excellent vine, because it produces abundant and lasting fruit. Lastly, Sorec is interpreted by some as καλλίκαρπος, which we can render as of most beautiful fruit. He also built a tower in its midst, that is, the temple in the midst of the city; and set up a winepress in it, by which some think the altar is signified. For just as all the grapes are carried to the winepress and trodden, so that wine may be pressed out of them, so the altar receives the fruits of all the people and devours the victims offered, according to what we read of Benjamin, in whose tribe the temple and the altar were: Benjamin is a ravening wolf; in the morning he will eat, and at evening he will give food (Gen 49:27). All that is said of the vineyard can also be referred to the state of the human soul, which, planted by God for good, has brought forth not grapes but wild grapes, and has afterwards been handed over to the beasts to be trampled, and has not received the divine rain of teachings, because it despised the former gifts.\n\nAnd I waited for it to make grapes, and it made wild grapes. For wild grapes, as we have rendered it, in the Hebrew is written BUSIM (באשים), which Aquila interpreted σαπρίας, that is, most worthless fruits; Symmachus ἀτελῆ, that is, imperfect; the Seventy and Theodotion thorns, with which the Jews crowned the Lord. For while he waited for them to bring grapes to the winepresses at vintage time, for which the eighth, the eightieth and the eighty-third psalms have their titles, they, sinking themselves in the cares and vices of the world, which in the Gospel (Mark 4) are interpreted as thorns, offered the goads of blasphemies. But I think it better that Busim be understood as wild grapes rather than thorns, so that the likeness of the metaphor begun may be kept. Hence the Savior too says in the Gospel: Do they gather grapes from thorns, or figs from thistles? (Matt 7:16).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.3-4":["Now therefore, inhabitants of Jerusalem and men of Judah, judge between me and my vineyard: what is there that I ought to have done further for my vineyard and have not done for it? Or is it that I waited for it to make grapes, and it made wild grapes? I have done, he says, all that I ought: I planted the vineyard in a most fertile place, I set a wall around it, I chose out the stones, and I propped up its shoots with stakes and reeds; the vine itself too was not one of many, but choice and καλλίκαρπος. I built a very strong tower, in which to store the crops and from which to watch the beasts that lie in wait for the crops. I set up a winepress, so that the grapes might be pressed above all in that same place and pour out wine. Since, then, I have done the things I ought to have done, let the inhabitants of Jerusalem and of Judah answer me; nay rather, let them judge between me and my vineyard, what is there that I ought to have done and have not done? And as they keep silent, he answers himself: Unless perhaps I erred in this, that for my labor I waited for it to make grapes, not wild grapes, which an untilled and deserted vineyard is wont to bring forth. Something of the kind the Prophet Nathan, sent to David, says too (2 Sam 12), and he questions him through a parable, so that while judging of another he may pronounce sentence on himself. So here too the people is questioned as though about a vineyard, so that it may answer against itself. This place the Savior carries out more fully in the Gospel (Matt 21), and what is passed over here, he puts as a question to the Scribes and Pharisees. For in Isaiah nothing is said of the husbandmen, nor is it indicated what they are to suffer, but the discourse is only of the vineyard; there, however, as though the vineyard were one thing and the husbandmen another, he speaks of the people and of the teachers in such a way that he destroys the wicked wickedly and lets out the vineyard to other husbandmen, signifying the apostles and those who were to succeed the apostles. Nor is there a ταυτολογία, as many think, in his saying: Or is it that I waited for it to make grapes, and it made wild grapes? For above he speaks silently within himself, but here he asks others what he had been thinking.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.5-6":["And now I will show you what I will do to my vineyard: I will take away its hedge, and it shall be for plunder; I will break down its wall, and it shall be for trampling. And I will make it desolate; it shall not be pruned, and it shall not be dug: and briers and thorns shall come up over it. I challenged you above, he says, O inhabitants of Jerusalem and men of the tribe of Judah, to judge between me and my vineyard, and I asked you to say what I ought to have done that I have not done. And since, according to the Gospel parable, you will not answer what I ask, I myself will answer for you, showing what I am going to do: Since I have done for my vineyard all that I ought, and instead of grapes it has brought forth wild grapes, I will take away all that I had given. I will take away the help of the angels, of whom it is written in the psalms: The Angel of the Lord encamps round about those who fear him, and will deliver them (Ps 33 [34]:8); and it will be plundered by its adversaries. I will destroy the walls, and it will lie subject to hostile nations, and it will be desolate and will not be pruned for ever, nor dug, so that it may be reduced to briers; and thorns will come up in it. These things are said, under the metaphor of the vineyard, of the desolation of Jerusalem and Judea, which most of the Jews think took place under the Babylonians: and we cannot deny that this was in part fulfilled. But since there follows, And I will command the clouds not to rain rain upon it, this cannot be understood of that captivity. For Jeremiah too prophesied among the people when the city had already been taken; and Ezekiel, Daniel too and the three boys, are related either to have prophesied or to have done wonderful signs even in the captivity; and afterwards Haggai and Zechariah foretold the future for the consolation of the people in servitude. And the help of God is taken away from those who are unworthy, so that, since they had not perceived God through his benefits, they may perceive him through punishments. Or else he threatens certain harsh and rough things for this reason, that, having turned to penitence, they may avoid the impending torments. The Hebrew word SAITH (שית) all have interpreted by a similar word as thorns. If, then, Saith in the present place is called thorns according to Aquila and Theodotion and the Seventy, let them answer how above they translated Busim not as wild grapes but as thorns.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.6":["And I will command the clouds not to rain rain upon it. These are the clouds which the Lord brought out from the end of the earth, of which we read in the psalm as well: Your truth reaches to the clouds (Ps 35 [36]:6). These clouds, since under Elijah all were idolaters, did not rain upon the land of Israel for three years and six months (1 Kgs 18). This indeed we can understand not only of the prophets but of the apostles: that after the passion of the Lord the Jews had neither prophets nor apostles, lest instead of grapes they should bring forth thorns, but that they might pray, for their barrenness and dryness, to him who can supply the rains of the virtues. Hence in Leviticus too he says to them: I will make the heaven of iron for you, and the earth of bronze (Lev 26:19). And in Deuteronomy: The heaven over your head shall be of bronze, and the earth beneath you of iron (Deut 28:23, 24). And again: The Lord will give rain to your land. And: Ashes shall come down from heaven upon you, until it uproots you and destroys you; because land which receives the rain that often comes upon it, and produces not a crop but thorns and thistles, is reprobate and near to a curse, whose end is burning.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","I will command, he says, the clouds not to rain rain upon them—that is, upon the people of the Jews. The clouds are the prophets: the Lord commanded them not to rain rain upon Israel. The prophetic word has been turned toward us. These clouds, if they are dashed against one another, make thunder and lightnings.\n— Homily 56, on Psalm 146"],"ISA.5.7":["But the vineyard of the Lord of hosts is the house of Israel, and the men of Judah his delightful shoot. That is, of God; or, as the Seventy translated, a most beloved young planting. Israel and Judah differ from each other in this: that the whole people was at first called Israel, and afterwards, when David reigned over the tribe of Judah, and Rehoboam the son of Solomon over two tribes, Judah and Benjamin, those who were in Samaria, that is, the ten tribes, were called Israel; and those over whom men of David's line reigned were called Judah. And since calves were worshiped by Israel in Dan and in Bethel, Samaria was therefore taken first by the Assyrians; and a long time afterwards Judah was led away by the Chaldeans into Babylon, because they had sinned less. Hence in Ezekiel too, for purging the sins of both peoples, there are set on the left side for Israel, according to the Seventy, one hundred and ninety days, or, as is more truly contained in the Hebrew, three hundred and ninety, but for Judah forty days (Ezek 4, according to the Seventy and according to the Hebrew). I say this to show that, in comparison with Israel, that is, the ten tribes, Judah was lovable and beloved, in which were the priests and the Levites, and in which the worship of God was carried on at the time when the prophet Isaiah was speaking to the people. And beautifully Israel, that is, the whole people, is the house; but Judah, which sprang up afterwards when the tribes were separated, is called the delightful young plant. And this too is to be noted, that, according to prophetic custom, what was first said through a metaphor or through a parable is afterwards explained more plainly: that the vineyard and the young planting are Israel and Judah.\n\nAnd I waited for it to do judgment, and behold, iniquity; and righteousness, and behold, a cry, or, as the Seventy translated, I waited for it to do judgment, and it did iniquity, and not righteousness, but a cry. We wish to convey to Latin ears what we have learned from the Hebrews. Judgment among them is called MESPHAT (משפט); iniquity, or scattering, as Aquila interpreted, MESPHAA (משפה). Again, righteousness is called SADACA (צדקה), and cry is called SAACA (צעקה). So, by either adding or changing one letter, he so tempered the likeness of the words as to set MESPHAA for MESPHAT and SAACA for SADACA, and to render an elegant structure and sound of words according to the Hebrew tongue. Now God waited for the people of the Jews to do judgment, that is, grapes; and it did iniquity, that is, wild grapes. And he waited for righteousness, that it should receive the bestower of such great gifts sent from the Father; and it made a cry, with which it shouted against the Lord, and cried out, saying: Away, away, crucify such a one (John 19:15). Hence the apostle Paul too writes: Let all anger and clamor and blasphemy be taken away from you, with all malice (Eph 4:31). Or else, since they had shed the blood of the righteous one, the blood of the Lord's passion cried out to the Lord; therefore they rendered a cry instead of righteousness, according to what we read in Genesis: The voice of the blood of your brother Abel cries to me (Gen 4:10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.8":["Woe to you who join house to house, and link field to field, as far as the limit of the place: will you alone dwell upon the earth? For what we have said, as far as the limit of the place, the Seventy translated, that you may take something from your neighbor; Symmachus and Theodotion, until place fails, or there is none, so that, though the land fails, greed is by no means sated. This I think is said both generally against all for whom nothing is enough, and specially against the vineyard of the Lord, which instead of grapes made wild grapes, that is, instead of judgment iniquity, and instead of righteousness a cry. For what madness it is, when houses and fields ought to be had to keep off the rains and to sow crops, to desire to have those in all of which you cannot dwell and which you are not able to till, and to make another's need your pleasure! According to tropology some think this said against the heretics, who, moving their feet from the East, come into the plain of Shinar, which means shaking out of teeth, and build the city of confusion and the tower of pride, and hear in other words: Hear these things, leaders of the house of Jacob, and you remnant of the house of Israel, who abhor judgment and pervert all that is right, who build Zion in blood and Jerusalem in iniquity (Mic 3:9, 10). These join houses to houses, that is, doctrines to doctrines; to whom it is said through Micah, Do not build a mockery in the house (Mic 3), lest upon the foundation of Christ, which the apostle Paul laid (1 Cor 3), and on which they ought to have built gold, silver, precious stones, they on the contrary build wood, hay, stubble, whose end is burning. Of such houses the Savior speaks in the Gospel: Everyone who hears my words and does not do them shall be likened to a foolish man who built his house upon the sand. The rain came down, the floods came, the winds blew and beat upon that house, and it fell: and great was the ruin of that house (Matt 7:26, 27). And the heretics go on seeking to join new things to old, and to change the same for more recent ones, until both human sense and speech fail.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.9-10":["We ask, according to the anagogy, how the houses of the heretics are great and beautiful, which, when the time of judgment has come, have no inhabitant. For all the pomp and structure of words and the arguments of dialectic will be reduced to nothing. And since, according to the apostle Paul (1 Cor 3), we are not only God's building but also his husbandry, which the heretics imitate and are dug under and rooted up by Jeremiah: therefore where ten acres of vineyards, or where ten yoke of oxen are at work, they will make one bath, and thirty measures of seed will make an ephi (Jer 18), so that from the number ten, which in the holy Scriptures is mystical and perfect, and from thirty, at which Ezekiel prophesied (Ezek 1) and the Lord was baptized (Luke 3), they are reduced to an ephi, which, in comparison with a manifold number, is confined to unity. And that building and husbandry are referred to spiritual understanding the apostle Paul teaches in another place too (Eph 4), testifying that believers are founded and rooted in love. Hence Ecclesiastes too (Eccl 2) not only built himself houses but planted vineyards, and made gardens and orchards, and set out every fruit-bearing tree, and constructed pools to water the woodland. But the heretics, having only the image and shadow of the virtues and not the truth itself, without the fruit of works, promise the leaves of words. Of their trees the Lord says: Every planting which my heavenly Father has not planted shall be rooted up (Matt 15:13). For their vine is of the vine of Sodom, and their offshoot from Gomorrah: their grape is a grape of gall, and theirs is a cluster of bitterness; their wine is the fury of dragons, and the incurable fury of asps (Deut 32:32ff.).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.9":["These things of the Lord of hosts are in my ears: Unless many houses are made desolate, great and beautiful, without an inhabitant. For what we have rendered according to the Hebrew, These things of the Lord of hosts are in my ears, that is, the words which the Lord spoke still resound in my ears, the Seventy translated, These things have been heard in the ears of the Lord Sabaoth: not that the Prophet heard the words of the Lord, but that the things the Prophet was going to say were heard in the ears of the Lord; whereas it is more consistent to understand that the Prophet heard what the Lord spoke. And the Lord spoke this: that after the captivity great and most beautiful houses would be desolate, having no inhabitant.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.10":["For ten acres of vineyards shall make one little flask, and thirty measures of seed shall make three measures. For little flask, which the Seventy alone translated, all the others interpreted bath, which in Hebrew is called BETH (בת). And for thirty measures, which we have set down for a cor, which in Hebrew is called OMER (תמר), the Seventy rendered six artabas, which is an Egyptian measure and makes twenty measures. In the excessive barrenness, then, which follows the captivity, ten acres of vineyards will make a bath, that is, three amphorae; and thirty measures of seed, that is, a cor, will make an ephi, which the Seventy interpreted as three measures, that is, three modii. Now bath is said of liquid kinds, and ephi or epha, of the same measure, of dry ones. As we read in Ezekiel according to the Hebrew: You shall have a just ephi, and a just bath. The ephi and the bath shall be equal and of one measure, so that the bath holds the tenth part of a cor, and the ephi the tenth part of a cor: their balance shall be equal according to the measure of the cor (Ezek 45:10, 11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.11-12":["Woe to you who rise early in the morning to pursue drunkenness, and to drink until evening, so as to be inflamed with wine. Harp and lyre, timbrel and pipe, and wine are in your feasts; and you do not regard the work of the Lord, nor consider the works of his hands. For drunkenness, as Aquila and Symmachus interpreted it, the Seventy set down the Hebrew word itself, SICERAM (שכר), which signifies every drink that can intoxicate and overturn the settled state of the mind. And, following the sequence of the explanation begun, he rebukes the husbandmen of the vineyard, who, with barrenness impending and the burning near at hand by which the briers and thorns are to be consumed, gave themselves over to luxury and pleasures, not only in eating and drinking but also in the delight of the ears and in the various kinds of the art of music. While they do these things, they do not regard the work of the Lord, nor consider what is to come. Let us turn this testimony against the princes of the Church, who rise in the morning to pursue strong drink, and to drink until evening; of whom it is said in another place: Woe to you, O city, whose king is a youth, and your princes eat in the morning (Eccl 10:16). Occupied with delights, they do not understand the Creator from the creatures, nor consider the works of his hands, of which we read: By the word of the Lord the heavens were made firm, and all their power by the breath of his mouth (Ps 32 [33]:6). According to a higher understanding, every disturbance of the soul can be called drunkenness, which is conceived from the wine of the fury of dragons and from the incurable venom of asps, which some drink from youth to old age, that is, from morning until evening (Deut 32). Others are roused from the banquet at the third, sixth, or ninth, and the eleventh hour, and it is said to them: Awake, you who are drunk with wine. Upon this wine and upon such grapes and vineyards the Lord rains sulfur and fire from the Lord; and whoever drinks of their wine is inflamed and burned up. He seeks the harp and the psaltery, whose inventor is Jubal, born of the stock of Cain (Gen 4), and does not hear the Lord saying to Aaron: You and your sons shall not drink wine and strong drink when you enter into the tabernacle of testimony, or approach the altar (Lev 10:8). These harps and lyres and timbrel and pipes the Babylonian king had, at whose sounding together the peoples of all nations prostrated themselves on the ground and worshiped the golden statue (Dan 3). And that the first time of human wisdom, when we leave childhood and come to the age of reason, is understood as morning in the Scriptures, many testimonies can teach us; of which, by way of example, a few are to be set down: In the morning I sent the Prophets; and: In the morning you will hear my prayer. In the morning I will stand before you, and will see (Ps 5:4, 5); and: From the night my spirit will rise early (Isa 26:9 LXX); and: O God, my God, to you I watch from the dawn (Ps 62 [63]:1); and: In the mornings I put to death all the sinners of the land, to destroy from the city of the Lord all who work iniquity (Ps 100 [101]:8); and in another place: Weeping will tarry until evening, and gladness until the morning, and other things like these. And we rise in the morning when we leave our vices behind in childhood, and can say: Remember not the sins of my youth and of my ignorance (Ps 24 [25]:7). And when the sun of righteousness has risen for us, the darkness is put to flight, and at once we put to death all the thoughts that provoke us to sins, and we destroy from the city of our mind those sinners of whom the Savior speaks: Out of the heart come evil thoughts, murders, adulteries, fornications, thefts, false testimonies, blasphemies (Matt 15:19), and the rest. But most unhappy are those who, occupied from morning until evening with drunkenness, gluttony and divers pleasures, do not understand the works of the Lord in themselves, nor consider why they were created.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.13":["Therefore my people has been led away captive, because it had no knowledge; and its nobles have perished with hunger, and its multitude is dried up with thirst. That these things befell the people of the Jews according to the letter under Vespasian and Titus, the Roman emperors, both Greek and Latin history relates. And this indeed they suffer spiritually today too, enduring not a famine of bread nor a thirst for water, but a famine of hearing the word of God; because they neither regarded the work of the Lord nor considered the works of his hands, nor had knowledge of him who has always spoken through the Prophets (Amos 8). Hence in the Psalms too it is said of them: They shall return at evening, and shall suffer hunger like dogs, and shall go round about the city (Ps 58 [59]:16). And that every word of teaching is called bread and water, that saying of the Gospel teaches: Man does not live by bread alone, but by every word that goes forth from the mouth of God (Luke 4:4); and: Whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst for ever (John 4:13). And in the psalm it is said: He has brought me up beside the waters of refreshment (Ps 22 [23]:2). Hence the Lord too will not send away the hungry in the wilderness, lest they grow weary and collapse and be killed by hunger (Matt 15). And of the righteous man it is said: I have been young, and have grown old, and I have not seen the righteous forsaken, nor his seed seeking bread (Ps 36 [37]:25). Some wish to interpret what is said, My people has been led away captive, because it had no knowledge; and its nobles have perished with hunger, and its multitude is dried up with thirst, generally of the underworld and of Gehenna, in which everyone will be punished who has not had knowledge of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.14-15":["Therefore the underworld has enlarged its soul and opened its mouth without any limit, and its strong ones and its people, and its exalted and glorious ones, shall go down into it. And man shall be bowed down, and the man of rank shall be humbled, and the eyes of the lofty shall be brought low. Those who rose in the morning to pursue strong drink, and continued in drunkenness until evening, and, occupied with pleasure and delights, would not regard the work of the Lord nor contemplate the works of his hands, were therefore led into captivity, because they had no knowledge of the Son of God, who himself says to them, You know neither me nor him who sent me (John 8:19); and in the captivity itself they perished with hunger and were dried up with thirst. Hence the underworld and death enlarged its soul and opened its mouth, and without any number or satiety devoured those who are to be punished for ever: so that the princes and the people, and the exalted and glorious ones of the land of Judah, went down into it, and all pride was humbled, and the eyes of the lofty were brought low, as they understood that they were mortal; and all that the Lord had announced through the Prophets as to come was fulfilled in fact. Now the underworld is said to have a soul, not because it is a living creature, according to the error of some, but because in words of human usage we express the condition of insensible things: that it is insatiable and is never filled by the multitude of the dead. Just as death too is said to be in the hand of the tongue (Prov 18), and Almighty God says that his soul hates the Sabbaths and New Moons of the Jews (Isa 1). Whatever we have said of the people of the Jews can be referred tropologically to those who, occupied with the delights of the world and not regarding the works of God, are led captive into sin and have no knowledge of God; and therefore they perish with hunger and thirst for good works and virtues, and will be dragged down into Gehenna, and there, consigned to eternal torments, they will see their power and pride changed into misery and humility.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.16":["And the Lord of hosts shall be exalted in judgment, and the holy God shall be sanctified in righteousness. When the people has been led away captive because it had no knowledge, and has perished with hunger and been dried up with thirst, and the underworld has enlarged its soul, and the strong and the exalted and glorious have gone down into the depth, and man has been bowed down and the man of rank humbled, and all have received according to their deserts: then the Lord will be exalted in judgment, whose judgment before seemed to be unjust, and the holy God will be sanctified by all in righteousness, so that what is said in the Gospel may be fulfilled: Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name (Matt 6:9); and: Righteous Father, the world has not known you (John 17:25). Hence we must beware lest we anticipate the judgment of God, whose judgments are great and unspeakable, and of which the Apostle speaks: His judgments are inscrutable, and his ways unsearchable (Rom 11:33), until he lights up the hidden things of darkness and lays open the thoughts of hearts (1 Cor 4:5), he who says in the Gospel: Judge not, that you be not judged (Matt 7:1). In agreement with this sentence the apostle Paul commanded: Who are you that judge another's servant? To his own Lord he stands or falls. But he will stand: for God is able to make him stand (Rom 14:4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.17":["And the lambs shall feed according to their order, and strangers shall eat the deserts turned into fruitfulness. For the lambs feeding according to their order, which is taken in the good sense, the Seventy, intending I know not what, translated, those that are plundered shall feed like bulls, understanding bulls in place of lambs, and again interpreting lambs in place of strangers. But when the Lord has been exalted in judgment, and the holy one sanctified in his righteousness, so that the wicked husbandmen perish wickedly and the lofty cedar is cut down by the axe of the Lord, then those who are of the number of the lambs, not of the goats, will feed in the meadows of the Church and will say: The Lord feeds me, and I shall lack nothing (Ps 22 [23]:1); and what the Jews have deserted, turned into fruitfulness, the people of the nations will eat. This is according to tropology. But to complete the order of the history, the same thing is said in other words of which we read above: Strangers devour your land in your sight, and it is deserted and overthrown by foreign peoples (Isa 1:7). For a multitude of the nations, gathered from the whole world, dwells in Judea; and, the former peoples having been cast out, blindness has therefore come in part upon the house of Israel, that the fullness of the nations might come in (Rom 11). And beautifully, according to the Seventy, those were plundered and laid waste and led into captivity like bulls of whom the Lord had said, Fat bulls have beset me, so that lambs might occupy the places of the bulls.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.18":["Woe to you who draw iniquity with cords of vanity, and sin as with the rope of a cart. Who say: Let his work make haste and come quickly, that we may see it; and let the counsel of the Holy One of Israel draw near and come, and we know it. For the rope of a cart the Seventy translated the strap of the yoke of a heifer, or of a cow. That sins are called bonds we read quite often; among them is this: Each one is bound fast with the cords of his own sins (Prov 5:22). And the Lord, rebuking the offending people, which had joined sins to sins, made a scourge of cords, showing them how they had made the house of God a den of robbers (John 2), and had turned the seat of prayer into a house of trade (Matt 21). The guest too at the Lord's supper, not having a wedding garment, is bound hand and foot and cast into the outer darkness (Matt 22). And the Lord came to say to those who were in bonds, Come out (Isa 49:9); and to those who were in darkness, Be revealed (Ps 145 [146]:8). For he himself looses the fettered and enlightens the blind, whom Jeremiah calls the prisoners of the earth. Nor does he lament over those who have begun to sin and have at once ceased; for there is no righteous man on earth who does good and does not sin at some time (Eccl 7:21); but over those who have drawn out their sins with a long cord. Hence we read in Numbers too (chapter 19) that the red heifer, whose ashes are the expiation of the people, is not sacrificed and offered at the altar of the Lord unless it has done no earthly works and has not drawn the yoke, nor been bound with the bonds of the yoke of Nebuchadnezzar. And in this same prophet, instead of the girdle of truth, the daughters of Zion are girded with a cord. Ahithophel too and Judas [one of whom betrayed David, the other the Lord], drawing their sins with a very long rope, perished by hanging, thinking that the evil of conscience would be ended by present death, and that there is nothing after death. And that according to the Hebrew and all the other translators they are called cords of vanity signifies this: that for those who commit it sin is easily woven, and is as empty and easy as the threads of spiders are woven; but when we wish to get out of it, we are tied with the most solid bonds. And the cart full and laden with sins they understand more easily who remember that it is written in Zechariah that impiety sits upon a talent of lead (Zech 5), and that the Egyptians, who were weighed down by the heavy burden of their sins, were sunk like lead in the Red Sea (Exod 15). And in another place the sinner says: My iniquities have gone over my head; like a heavy burden they have weighed upon me (Ps 37 [38]:5). These things are said to the princes of the Jews, who were rebuked above for avarice and luxury: that, called by the Lord to penitence, and afterwards by his Apostles, they persevere to this day in blasphemies, and three times every day in all the synagogues, under the name of the Nazarenes, they anathematize the Christian name.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.19":["And the sense is: Woe to you who think that the day of judgment will not be, or that the captivity which the prophetic discourse foretells will not come; who say to the Prophet: How long will you threaten us with the wrath of God? We wish to know it; let it come now. And they say this in irony, because they think it will not come, but is feigned by the Prophet.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.20":["Woe to you who call evil good, and good evil, putting darkness for light, and light for darkness, putting bitter for sweet, and sweet for bitter. It is a crime of the same kind to call good, light and sweet by contrary names as to call evil, darkness and bitter by the names of virtues. This is against those who do not think it a sin if they disparage the good, and do not judge it an offense if they praise the evil. And the Jews put good as evil, and light as darkness, and sweet as bitter, when they took up Barabbas, the author of robbery and sedition, and crucified Jesus, who had come only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel, to save what had perished. In Barabbas let us understand the devil, who, though he is night and darkness, transforms himself into an Angel of light (Matt 27). Hence the Apostle too says: What participation has righteousness with iniquity? What fellowship has light with darkness? What agreement has Christ with Belial? (2 Cor 6:14, 15). For neither is the lamp to be taken away and put under the bushel or under the bed, but upon the lampstand, that it may give light to all. Nor is a tree bearing evil fruits to be called by the name of a good tree (Matt 5). Hence in Genesis too it is related in mystical speech that God divided between the light and the darkness, which in the beginning were borne over the abyss (Gen 1). And that the Savior is called good, he himself says in the Gospel: The good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep (John 10:11). He says too that he is the light: I am the light of the world (John 8:12); and daily, filled with the heavenly bread, we say: Taste and see how sweet the Lord is (Ps 33 [34]:9). On the contrary, when we say, Deliver us from evil (Matt 6:13), and, The world is set in the evil one (1 John 5:19), we desire to be delivered from the snares of the devil. And that he himself is signified by the names of darkness and bitterness we read quite often. But we can say this too, that all doctrines contrary to the truth are bitter, and the truth alone is sweet. Hence we must beware lest we follow falsehood instead of truth, darkness instead of light. For there are many ways that seem right to men, and their ends reach to the depth of the underworld. There is also the righteous man perishing in his righteousness, to whom it is said: Be not overly righteous (Eccl 7:17). For which reasons Israel promises that he will walk by the king's way and will turn aside neither to the left nor to the right (Deut 5). And to say what I feel: with difficulty can anyone be free of this curse, since we often flatter the wicked for their power and despise the good for their poverty. Hence Aquila interpreted more expressively: Woe to him who says to the evil man, You are good, and to the good man, You are evil (Prov 17:15). And with this sense agrees what Solomon says in Proverbs: He who judges the righteous unrighteous, and the unrighteous righteous, both are abominable with God. The Scribes and Pharisees too, receiving not the words of the Savior but the traditions of men and old wives' fables, made good evil and evil good.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Hence it is said in Isaiah: Woe to those who call sweet bitter, and bitter sweet. This is said by those who in judging consider not the cases but the persons, and turn the fruit of Christ's righteousness, which is most sweet, into bitterness. Whoever, therefore, in judging is led either by kinship or by friendship, or, on the other hand, by hostile hatred or by enmities, perverts the judgment of Christ, who is righteousness, and turns his fruit into bitterness.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"ISA.5.21":["Woe to you who are wise in your own eyes, and prudent in your own sight. You who seem to yourselves, he says, to be wise, who follow the prudence not of God but of men: and though you do not receive the power of God and the wisdom of God, you think yourselves wise (1 Cor 1). Between wisdom and prudence the Stoics say there is this difference: that wisdom is the knowledge of things divine and human, but prudence only of mortal things. And these things are said consistently against the Scribes and Pharisees, who, having the key of knowledge, neither enter in to Christ themselves nor allow others to enter (Luke 11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.22":["Woe to you who are mighty to drink wine, and strong men to mix strong drink, that is, drunkenness. Of those to whom he had said above, Woe to you who rise early in the morning to pursue drunkenness, and to drink until evening, so as to be inflamed with wine, he speaks now too: who are mighty to drink wine, and strong men to mix strong drink (Deut 32). Drunk with the wine of the dragon and the incurable venom of asps, they slandered the power of the Lord. And being drunk themselves, they made the peoples drunk, so that, raving with a like fury, they cried out together against the Lord. According to tropology we have already said before that the priests who are to enter the Tabernacle of God ought not to drink wine and strong drink (Lev 10). To which we now add that it was commanded the Nazirites too, who sanctify themselves to the Lord, that they should neither drink nor eat wine and strong drink and anything that comes from grapes, not even a raisin, nor vinegar made from wine (Num 6). But in Proverbs too it is commanded: Let the mighty who are wrathful not drink wine, lest when they have drunk they forget wisdom. Between wine and strong drink, according to the anagogy, I judge the difference to be this: that wine is one disturbance out of many, for example, of lust, of avarice, of gluttony, and of envy; but strong drink, that is, drunkenness, contains in itself all the passions of the vices, which in the Latin tongue we can more rightly call disturbances, because they overturn the settled state of the mind and make the drunken not know what they do. Those, then, who are set over others ought above all to be free of vices, and most of all of anger, which is nearest to madness, lest the more they can, the more they harm those subject to them. He mixes strong drink who, though full of drunkenness, pretends, in order to deceive men, to have certain shadows and images of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.23":["You who justify the wicked for gifts, and take away from the righteous man his righteousness. This too is part of the vices of the vineyard which instead of grapes made wild grapes, and, when the Lord waited for it to do judgment, did iniquity, justifying the wicked for gifts, and considering not the cases but the presents, which blind even the eyes of the wise. Hence we must beware lest we be drunk with wine, in which is luxury (Deut 16:19; Eph 5), and the thighs of our shame be laid bare (Gen 9), and lest we flatter the wicked for gifts and despise the righteousness of the righteous man for his poverty. This is commanded in the Epistle of James as well: that we not become judges of iniquity by honoring the wicked rich and despising the holy poor.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.24":["Therefore, as the tongue of fire devours the stubble, and the heat of the flame burns it up, so their root shall be as ashes, and their bud shall go up as dust. For they have cast away the law of the Lord of hosts, and have blasphemed the word of the Holy One of Israel. For the causes given above, of pride and drunkenness and avarice, because instead of grapes they brought forth thorns, and hay, wood, stubble, and briers that are to burn, therefore the root of their malice will be reduced to ashes, and every flower and beauty of riches and of the body will be compared to dust (1 Cor 3). For they did not only do these things, but by these steps they came to blasphemy, so that they did not receive the law of the Lord and blasphemed the word of the Holy One of Israel; of which we read above: Out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem (Isa 2:3). The root can be taken as evil thoughts, and the fruit and bud as evil works and words, so that what lies hidden in the root is shown in the bud; and both will be devoured by the Lord's burning. Hence the Apostle too (Heb 12:15) speaks of the root of the bitterness of evils springing up.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.25":["Therefore the fury of the Lord is angered against his people, and he has stretched out his hand over it and struck it, and the mountains are troubled, and their carcasses have become like dung in the midst of the streets. For all this his fury is not turned away, but his hand is still stretched out. Because they cast away the law of the Lord of hosts, which the Lord promises through Jeremiah that he will give, saying: Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and I will strike a new covenant with the house of Israel and the house of Judah, not according to the pact which I made with your fathers in the day when I took their hand to lead them out of the land of Egypt (Jer 31:31, 32). And they blasphemed the word of the Holy One of Israel, saying: He has a demon, and is a Samaritan (John 8:48); and: Is not this the son of the carpenter? (Matt 13:55). Therefore the fury of the Lord is angered against his people, that fury which before had been angered against the princes and the mighty, who were wise in their own eyes and justified the wicked for gifts, and the tongue of fire had devoured them like stubble, and the heat of the flame had burned them up, so that the mighty might mightily suffer torments. And he stretched out his hand against it, which he calls his people because it was the portion of his inheritance and the cord of his possession (Deut 32). And he stretches out his hand to strike, and his fury is angered, of which we read in another place too: O Lord, rebuke me not in your fury, nor chastise me in your wrath (Ps 6:1). And Jeremiah: Correct us, he says, O Lord, but in judgment, and not in fury (Jer 10:24). And the Lord is said to rage, not because he is subject to human disturbances, but because we who offend do not fear the Lord unless we have heard him angry. Hence the Apostle too writes (Rom 2) that the goodness and patience of God call us to penitence, but we, according to our hardness and impenitent heart, treasure up wrath for ourselves in the day of wrath and of the revelation of the righteous judgment of God. And what the hand is that is stretched out or lifted up over the sinful people Job says more plainly: For the hand of the Lord has touched me (Job 19:21). Hence the devil too, knowing the mighty hand of the Lord and the arm that has been revealed to all the nations, says to the Lord: Put forth your hand and touch all that he has: and see if he will not bless you to your face (Job 2:5). And that what is to come is spoken of as past follows prophetic custom, in which the things foretold as to come are so certain that they are thought to be past. This is sung in the Psalms too: They gave gall for my food, and in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink (Ps 68 [69]:22). And again: They divided my garments among them, and upon my vesture they cast lots (Ps 21 [22]:19). And as for what follows, And he struck it, that is, his people, and the mountains are troubled: some think the mountains are the contrary powers, or those spirits which are in the ministry of God and to whom sinners are handed over to be punished. But we think it is said hyperbolically: that because of the greatness of the impending evils even the mountains are moved, and all the streets of the cities are filled with the corpses of the dead. That this befell the Jews after the passion of the Lord under Vespasian and Hadrian no one doubts. And though these things have been done, his fury is not turned away, but his hand is still stretched out, or lifted high, which shows the bearing of one who is angry and strikes. And it is to be noted in all these things that he does not reproach them with idolatry, nor with other sins by which they offended God, but because they cast away the law of the Gospel and blasphemed the word of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.5.26-30":["And he will lift up a signal among the nations far off, and will whistle to him from the ends of the earth; and behold, he will come swiftly in haste. There shall be none faint nor laboring among them; he shall not slumber nor sleep, nor shall the girdle of his loins be loosed, nor the latchet of his shoe be broken. His arrows are sharp, and all his bows bent; the hoofs of his horses are like flint, and his wheels like the rush of a tempest. His roaring is like a lion's; he shall roar like the lion's whelps, and gnash his teeth, and seize the prey, and embrace it, and there shall be none to rescue it. And he shall resound over it in that day like the sound of the sea: we shall look upon the land, and behold, the darkness of tribulation, and the light is darkened by its gloom. The Hebrews understand this place to be prophesied of the Babylonians and Nebuchadnezzar, that by the will of God he was brought into Judea and Jerusalem and overthrew the temple. But we, following the order and joining what follows to what precedes, say that the signal was lifted up among the nations far off, and that the Lord whistled for them or drew them from the ends of the earth, for this reason: because they cast away the law of the Gospel and blasphemed the holy word. For if the discourse were about the Babylonians, according to prophetic custom he would have said: I will call him who is from the North, because, relative to the position of Judea, the Assyrians and Chaldeans are in the northern region. Or certainly he would have described the Babylonians and Assyrians more openly. But now, by saying, He will lift up a signal among the nations far off, and will whistle to him from the ends of the earth, he signifies nations set far off and dwelling at the ends of the earth: doubtless the Romans and all the peoples of Italy, Gaul and Spain, who under Vespasian and Hadrian lay subject to the Roman empire. Hence Italy too, from the fact that Hesperus sets there, was once called Hesperia. And at his coming it was struck, and all the mountains of Judea were troubled, as Theodotion and Symmachus interpreted, or were moved, as Aquila set down, or were embittered, as the Seventy translated, so that by the onslaught of the enemies all the streets were filled with the corpses of the dead. The divine discourse, then, describes the swiftness of the army that comes, which came not by its own will but by the Lord's, nay rather was drawn and summoned by his whistle: that it did not faint nor grow weary on so long a march, and in its greed for plunder did not admit sleep to its eyes, and its shoes too were not worn out; the multitude of archers, the squadrons of horsemen, the heat of the chariots and four-horse teams; that like a lion it came not so much to fight as to plunder and devour; and the howling of the victorious army is compared to the waves of the sea. From this it is to be noted what the sound of the sea signifies wherever it is named in the Scriptures. When, then, the Roman army has come and seized the prey, and there has been no one to rescue it, the Prophet, with the feeling of one who suffers with them, joins himself to the people and says: We shall look upon the land, and behold, the darkness of tribulation. For we shall not dare to look upon heaven, whose dweller we have offended; and our light, which we always had in God, has been darkened by the gloom of tribulations. I have read in someone's Commentaries that what is said, He will lift up a signal among the nations far off, and will whistle to him from the ends of the earth, ought to be understood of the calling of the nations: that, when the signal of the Cross was lifted up and the burdens of sins laid down, they came swiftly and believed. But I do not know how what follows can agree with this sense.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.1-7":["The sheer size of the volumes being woven in the explanation of the prophet Isaiah is enough for me, where to pass anything over is a loss to the understanding. Therefore in the individual books, which signify only their number and order, I have set brief little prefaces. And you, Eustochium, virgin of Christ, I beg to help me with your prayers in the exposition of a most difficult vision: in which God almighty is seen in his majesty; and two Seraphim standing round about him and crying: Holy, Holy, Holy, the Lord Sabaoth, the whole earth is full of his glory; and the threshold of the temple moved and shaken, and the Jewish house filled with the darkness of error. And the Prophet, in comparison with the divine glory, saying that he has unclean lips and dwells in the midst of a blaspheming people, who cried out with the concordant voice of impiety: Crucify, crucify such a one; and, We have no king but Caesar (John 19:6 and 15). And one of the Seraphim sent to Isaiah, who, having taken a coal from the altar with tongs, was to cleanse the prophet's lips, while the people remained unclean. This, then, is the opening of the third book on Isaiah.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.1":["In the year that king Uzziah died, I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up. That Isaiah prophesied under four kings, over Judah and Jerusalem, was shown by the title of the first vision; that is, under Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah. So when Uzziah was dead, under whom all the things we have expounded above were spoken, his son Jotham succeeded him, who reigned sixteen years, and did what was right in the sight of the Lord, and built the high gate of the temple. While he was ruling, Isaiah saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, so as to show the bearing of one who reigns.\n\nAnd the things that were under him filled the Temple, or, as Theodotion and Symmachus translated: And the things that were under his feet filled the Temple. For which the Seventy translated: And the house was full of his glory. On this vision, about thirty years ago, when I was at Constantinople and was being trained in the study of the holy Scriptures under that most eloquent man Gregory of Nazianzus, then bishop of the same city, I know that I dictated a brief and hasty treatise, both to make trial of my little talent and to obey friends who bade me. To that little book, then, I send the reader, and I pray that he be content with the brief exposition of the present time. That Uzziah was struck with leprosy because he claimed for himself a priesthood not permitted to him, the sacred history relates; and when he was dead, the Lord is seen in the Temple which he had polluted (2 Chr 26). From this we perceive that while a leprous king reigns in us, we cannot see the Lord reigning in his majesty, nor know the mysteries of the holy Trinity. Hence also in Exodus, after Pharaoh was dead, who oppressed Israel with clay and straw and bricks, the people, who while he lived could not cry out, cried to the Lord. And Ezekiel, when Pelatiah the son of Benaiah, a most wicked prince, was dead, falls upon his face and cries with a loud voice to the Lord. And beautifully, according to the Hebrew, it is not the Lord himself who filled the Temple, whose throne is heaven and the earth the footstool of his feet, and of whom we read in another place: The Lord is in his holy Temple, the Lord's seat is in heaven (Ps 10 [11]:5); but the things that were under his feet filled the Temple (Isa 66). But who this Lord is who is seen, we learn more fully in the evangelist John and in the Acts of the Apostles. Of whom John says: These things Isaiah said when he saw his glory, and spoke of him (John 12:41), meaning without doubt Christ. Again Paul in the Acts of the Apostles, where at Rome he speaks to the Jews: Well, he says, did the Holy Spirit speak through Isaiah the prophet to our fathers, saying: Go to this people and say: With the ear you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not perceive; for the heart of this people has been made gross, and with their ears they have heard heavily; and they have shut their eyes, lest perhaps they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and turn, and I should heal them (Acts 28:26-27). Now the Son was seen in the bearing of one who reigns, and the Holy Spirit spoke, on account of the fellowship of majesty and the unity of substance. Someone may ask how the Prophet now says that he has seen the Lord, and not simply the Lord but the Lord Sabaoth, as he himself testifies in what follows; when the evangelist John has said: No one has ever seen God (John 1:18). And God speaks to Moses: You cannot see my face; for man shall not see my face and live (Exod 33:20). To which we shall answer that not only the divinity of the Father, but not even that of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, since in the Trinity there is one nature, can the eyes of the flesh behold; but the eyes of the mind, of which the Savior himself says: Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God (Matt 5:8). We read that the Lord was seen by Abraham in the figure of a man; and that he who was God wrestled with Jacob as a man. Hence that very place was called Phanuel, that is, the face of God: For I have seen, he says, the Lord face to face, and my soul has been saved (Gen 32). Ezekiel too saw the Lord in the form of a man sitting upon the Cherubim; from his loins and downward there was as it were fire, and the upper parts had the appearance of electrum (Ezek 1). The nature of God, then, is not discerned, but he is seen by men as he wills.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.2-3":["Impiously, therefore, a certain one understands the two Seraphim as the Son and the Holy Spirit: since, according to the evangelist John and the apostle Paul, we teach that the Son of God was seen in the majesty of one who reigns, and that the Holy Spirit spoke. Some of the Latins understand the two Seraphim as the old and the new Instrument, which speak only of the present age. Hence they are said to have six wings, and to cover the face and feet of God, and to bring forth in rivalry the testimony of the truth, and in all that they cry to show forth the mysteries of the Trinity. And to marvel together that the Lord Sabaoth, being set in the form of God the Father, took the form of a servant and humbled himself even unto death, and the death of the cross (Phil 2), and that no longer, as before, do heavenly things alone know him, but earthly things also.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.2":["Seraphim stood above it; six wings to the one, and six wings to the other: with two they covered his face, and with two they covered his feet, and with two they flew. And they cried one to another, and said: Holy, Holy, Holy, the Lord of hosts; the whole earth is full of his glory. What we, following the other Translators and the Hebrew truth, in which is written MEMMALLO ממעללו, that is, ἐπάνω αὐτοῦ, which in Latin is said above it, have rendered, the Seventy translated round about him, so that the Seraphim are described as standing not above the Temple but round about the Lord. Again, where we said that one of the Seraphim covered his face and feet, by which is understood God's: in the Hebrew is written PHANAU פניו and REGLAU (רגליו), which can be interpreted both as his and as its own: so that, according to the ambiguity of the Hebrew speech, the Seraphim may be said to cover both the face and feet of God and their own face and feet. In the seventy-ninth psalm we read: You who sit upon the Cherubim, shine forth (Ps 79 [80]:3): who in our language are interpreted multitude of knowledge. Hence the Lord is plainly shown to sit upon the Cherubim in the manner of a charioteer. But the Seraphim, apart from this place, I do not know that I have read anywhere else in the canonical Scriptures, who are said to stand above the Temple, or round about the Lord. Therefore those err who are accustomed to say in their prayers: You who sit upon the Cherubim and Seraphim, which Scripture has not taught. Now Seraphim is interpreted ἐμπρησταὶ, which we can say as kindling or burning, according to what we read elsewhere: Who makes his angels spirits, and his ministers a burning fire (Ps 103 [104]:5). Hence also the Apostle Paul in the Epistle to the Hebrews, which Latin custom does not receive: Are they not all, he says, ministering spirits, sent into ministry for the sake of those who shall receive the inheritance of salvation (Heb 1:14)? Daniel too, when he had described the Lord in the bearing of one who reigns, added: Thousands of thousands ministered to him, and ten thousand times a hundred thousand stood before him. (Dan 7:10). In the Cherubim, then, the Lord is shown; in the Seraphim he is in part shown, in part hidden. For they cover his face and feet, because we can know neither the things past before the world nor the things to come after the world; but we contemplate only the middle things, which were made in the six days. Nor is it strange to believe this of the Seraphim, since the apostles too open the Savior to believers and hide him from unbelievers; and there was a veil before the Ark of the Testament (Exod 40). They are said also to have wings, on account of their swiftness and their running to and fro in all things; or because they always dwell in higher places. For neither does that which is said of the winds, Who walks upon the wings of the winds (Ps 103 [104]:4), testify that the winds truly have wings, after the fables of the poets and the licence of painters; but rather their swift running to and fro in all things. And each has six wings, because we know only of the making of the world and of the present age.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.3":["And in that they cry one to another, or, according to the Hebrews, this one to that one, that is, one to one, they exhort one another to the praises of the Lord, and say: Holy, Holy, Holy, the Lord of hosts, so as to show the mystery of the Trinity in one Godhead; and to testify that no longer the Temple of the Jews, as before, but the whole earth is full of the glory of him who for our salvation deigned to take a human body and to come down to the earth. Indeed, when Moses too was praying the Lord, after the calf had been worshipped, to spare the sinful people, the Lord answered: I will be propitious to them. But as I live, and as my name lives: for the whole earth shall be filled with my glory (Num 14:20-21). And the seventy-first psalm sings: The whole earth shall be filled with his glory (Ps 71 [72]:19). Hence also the Angels cried to the shepherds: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace to men of good will (Luke 2:14).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.4":["And the lintels of the hinges were moved at the voice of him who cried, and the house was filled with smoke. When the Seraphim cried and proclaimed the mystery of the Trinity in all the earth, when the whole earth came to know the passion of the Lord and Savior, at once the threshold of the Temple was moved, or taken away, and all its hinges fell down, the Savior's threat being fulfilled, when he said: Your house shall be left to you desolate (Matt 23:38). And how beautiful is the order of the words! After the earth was filled with the glory of the Lord Sabaoth, the Temple of the Jews was filled with the darkness of ignorance, and with gloom, and with smoke, which is harmful to the eyes. Or certainly by the smoke of the Temple a burning is signified. For first the Gospel of the Savior was preached in the whole world, and forty-two years after the Lord's passion Jerusalem was taken and the Temple burned. The Jews think that the Temple filled with smoke signifies thymiama, that is, incense, and through this the coming of the divine majesty.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.5-8":["Let us give another example too. Isaiah, when he had seen the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up, what does he say? 'Woe is me, wretched that I am, for I am pricked with compunction, because I have unclean lips, and I sit in the midst of a people that has unclean lips.' See what he says, Woe is me, wretched, because I have unclean lips. And afterward what does he say? Because he had unclean lips, therefore one of the seraphim is sent, and takes a coal of fire from the altar, and touches his lips and his tongue, and purges it. And what does he say? 'Behold, this has touched your lips, and has cleansed your tongue.' And at once what does he say? 'Whom shall I send to this people, and who shall go?' O the mysteries of the Scriptures! As long as he had a deceitful tongue and unclean lips, it is not said to him, Whom shall I send, and who shall go? His lips are cleansed, and at once he is sent to speak. So then he who has unclean lips cannot prophesy, cannot be sent into the service of God.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"ISA.6.5":["And I said: Woe is me, because I was silent, because I am a man polluted in lips, and I shall dwell in the midst of a people having polluted lips, and I have seen with my eyes the king, the Lord of hosts. Abraham too, after he saw the Lord and heard his voice, says that he is earth and ashes (Gen 18); and Isaiah, according to the Seventy Translators, testifies that he is pierced with compunction, not for other crimes, but because he had unclean lips. Happy the conscience that has sinned only in speech, and that not by its own fault, but by fellowship with a people having polluted lips, with whom he was very often compelled to speak. From this it is shown that it is harmful to live with sinners: For he who touches pitch shall be defiled by it (Sir 13:1). But since in the Hebrew we read: Woe is me, because I was silent, the Prophet bewails himself because he was not worthy to praise the Lord Sabaoth with the Seraphim, whom we understand as the Angelic powers. And he did not dare to praise the Lord because he had unclean lips. And he had unclean lips for this reason, because he lived among a sinful people. Or certainly it is to be understood thus: Because I was silent, and did not boldly rebuke Uzziah the impious king, therefore my lips are unclean, and I do not dare to sing the praises of the Lord with the Angels, lest it be said to me: Why do you declare my righteousnesses, and take my covenant in your mouth (Ps 49 [50]:16)? For praise is not beautiful in the mouth of a sinner (Sir 15:9). And we say this, not as teaching that Isaiah was such a man, but that he himself, out of humility, and only because of unclean lips, confesses himself unworthy of the praise of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.6-7":["And one of the Seraphim flew to me, and in his hand a pebble, which he had taken from the altar with tongs, and he touched my mouth, and said: Behold, I have touched your lips with this, and your iniquity shall be taken away, and your sin shall be cleansed. One of the Seraphim, which is interpreted kindling, flew, or was sent, to cleanse the Prophet's unclean lips, and to purge them with the live coal, or pebble, which he had taken from the altar. Many, however, think that there are two Seraphim, because one cried to another, although among many too individuals can cry to individuals; and the edition of the Seventy rather signifies very many, who translated, Seraphim stood round about him. For if it were said of two, they would have said not round about, but on either side. And this fits the multitude of Angels, which is made ready for the ministry of God. And they are called Seraphim in the plural number, and Seraph in the singular: just as Cherubim and Cherub. And that an altar, under which are the souls of the martyrs, is seen in heaven, John too speaks in the Apocalypse (Rev 6); and this pebble, which by the Seventy alone was translated ἄνθραξ, that is, carbuncle, may signify not a coal or a live ember, as most think, but ἄνθρακα, that is, the carbuncle stone, which is called fiery because of the likeness of its flame-like color. From this we understand that the altar of God is full of carbuncles, that is, of fiery pebbles and live coals, which purge sins. Hence we read it written of God: Coals were kindled by him (Ps 17 [18]:9). And of the Lord himself it is said that he is a consuming fire. And the Savior in the Gospel: I came to cast fire upon the earth (Luke 12:49): that he might baptize in the Holy Spirit and in fire. For the fire will prove of what sort each one's work is (1 Cor 3). And he who is to be saved will be saved thus, as if he had passed through fire. And this is to be noted, that to Jeremiah, to whom it was said: Before I formed you in the womb I knew you, and in your mother's womb I sanctified you (Jer 1:5), because he did not have unclean lips but had only said: I do not know how to speak, for I am a youth, the Lord himself stretched out his hand and touched his mouth and said: Behold, I have put my words in your mouth. But to Isaiah, who had said: I am a man polluted in lips, and I dwell in the midst of a people having polluted lips, the hand of God is not stretched out, but a Seraph is sent by God, or flies of his own will, because he has been bound over to this office. And in his hand he holds the pebble, which, according to the Seventy and Theodotion, he took with tongs (in the singular), according to Aquila and Symmachus, who followed the Hebrew, with tongs (in the plural), that is, MALCAIM (מלקחים), so as to touch his mouth and purge his former offenses. And the hand is sent both by God and by the Seraph, so that the Prophet, seeing a member of his own body, may not be terrified by an outward touch. Some of our people think that the tongs, by which the pebble is grasped, are the two Testaments, which are joined to one another by the union of the Holy Spirit. And since the Lord is introduced sitting, and sitting in the Temple, and the house was filled with smoke, of thymiama, as the Jews think, consequently tongs too are set down, which we also read of in the priestly ministry (Exod 37).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And the Lord put forth his hand and touched my mouth, and the Lord said to me: Behold, I have given my words in your mouth. It is to be noted that here the hand of God is put forth to touch the mouth of the prophet, and it is said to him: behold, I have given my words in your mouth, whereas in Isaiah it is written: and one of the seraphim was sent to me, and in his hand he had a coal which he had taken with tongs from the altar, and he touched my mouth and said: behold, this has touched your lips, and it will take away your iniquities and cleanse your sins. For there, because he was of firm and mature age, and he himself simply confesses that he has unclean lips and dwells in the midst of a people having unclean lips, one of the seraphim is sent, to touch his mouth not with a hand but with tongs and a coal, and to take away his iniquities and cleanse his sins. But here the hand of God himself is put forth, through which he has wrought all things, and which in another place is called the arm, not to take away sins, which on account of his boyhood he had not committed in great number, but to bestow the grace of speaking.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"ISA.6.8":["And I heard the voice of the Lord saying: Whom shall I send, and who will go for us? And I said: Here am I, send me. In place of what both we and all the other translators rendered for us, which in Hebrew is said LANU (לנו), the Seventy, wishing I know not what, set down to this people, which is not found in the Hebrew at all. Now when for us is said in the person of God, it is to be taken in the sense in which we also read in Genesis: Let us make man to our image and likeness (Gen 1:26), so as to indicate the mystery of the Trinity. For just as we read in the Gospel, where the Lord says: I and the Father are one (John 10:30); and one we refer to the nature, are to the distinction of persons: so, when the Lord commands, the Trinity gives the order. And the Lord does not say whom he orders to go, but sets the choice before those who hear, so that the will may obtain the reward. And the Prophet promises that he will go not out of the rashness and arrogance of his own conscience, but out of confidence: since his lips have been cleansed, and his iniquity taken away, and his sin cleansed. So Moses too, to whom the Lord had said: Come, I will send you to Pharaoh king of Egypt (Exod 3:10), and he said: I beseech you, Lord, I am not worthy; provide another whom you may send, or as is read in the Hebrew, Send whom you are going to send (Exod 4:13), answered not out of contempt but out of humility, because he who had been instructed in all the wisdom of the Egyptians had heard nothing about his lips being cleansed. And Isaiah, not by his own merit but by the grace of the Lord, by which he was cleansed, offers himself for the ministry. Others, however, think that Isaiah offered himself for this reason, because he supposed that prosperous things were to be announced to the people: but because he heard: Go, say to this people: With your ears you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not know (Isa 6:9), for that reason, in what follows, when the voice of the Lord says to him: Cry, he does not cry at once, but asks, what shall I cry? And Jeremiah, to whom it had been said: Take the cup, and you shall give drink to all the nations to whom I shall send you (Jer 25:15), gladly accepting the cup of punishments to pledge it to the hostile nations, that they might drink and vomit and fall, after he heard, go, and first you shall give drink to Jerusalem, answered: You have deceived me, Lord, and I have been deceived (Jer 20:7). This is an observation of the Hebrews. For our part, however, we say that it was not of rashness but of obedience that he offered himself to the Lord to be sent.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.9-10":["And he said: Go, and you shall say to this people: Hear with hearing, and do not understand, and see with sight, and do not know. Blind the heart of this people, and make its ears heavy, and shut its eyes, lest perhaps it see with its eyes, and hear with its ears, and understand with its heart, and be converted, and be healed. The Seventy translated this place as the evangelist Luke set it down in the Acts of the Apostles: And when they did not agree among themselves, without doubt the Jews, they departed, Paul having said one word: Well did the Holy Spirit speak through Isaiah the prophet to our fathers, saying: go to this people and say: With the ear you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not perceive; for the heart of this people has been made gross, and with their ears they have heard heavily, and they have shut their eyes, lest perhaps they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and turn, and I should heal them (Acts 28:25ff.). And at what time this prophecy was fulfilled, the Apostle Paul himself says in what follows: Let it be known to you, therefore, that this salvation of God has been sent to the nations, and they will hear (Acts 28:28). Hence too we read in the same Acts of the Apostles that Paul and Barnabas, when the Jews would not believe, said: It was necessary that the word of God be spoken first to you; but since you have thrust it away and judged yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the Gentiles (Acts 13:46-47). For thus the Lord has commanded us: I have set you for a light to the nations, that you may be for salvation to the end of the earth (Isa 49:6). So according to the Seventy the interpretation is easy: that Isaiah the prophet, at the Lord's command, foretells what the people is going to do. In the Hebrew there is a difficulty: how God himself commands the people to hear with hearing and not understand, and seeing to see and not recognize, and afterward the prophet is introduced speaking, and he entreats the Lord and says: Blind the heart of this people, and make its ears heavy, and shut its eyes, lest perhaps it see with its eyes, and hear with its ears, and understand with its heart, and be converted, and be healed (Isa 6:10). And first we must resolve the question that can be put to us: why did the apostle Paul, disputing with Hebrews, speak not according to the Hebrew, which he knew to be correct, but according to the Seventy? The ancient expositors of the Church relate that the evangelist Luke was most learned in the art of medicine, and knew Greek letters better than Hebrew. Hence his speech too, both in the Gospel and in the Acts of the Apostles, that is, in both volumes, is more polished and has the scent of secular eloquence, and he uses Greek testimonies more than Hebrew. But Matthew and John, of whom the one wove his Gospel in the Hebrew tongue, the other in Greek, bring forward testimonies from the Hebrew, such as this: Out of Egypt I have called my son (Hos 11:1). And: For he shall be called a Nazarene (Matt 2:23). And: Rivers of living water shall flow from his belly (John 7:38). And: They shall look on him whom they pierced (Zech 12:10; John 19:37), and other things like these. The Epistle of Paul to the Hebrews too is gainsaid for this reason, that in writing to Hebrews he uses testimonies that are not found in the Hebrew volumes. But if anyone should say that the Hebrew books were afterward falsified by the Jews, let him hear what Origen answers to this little question in the eighth volume of his Explanations of Isaiah: that the Lord and the Apostles, who rebuke the other crimes in the Scribes and Pharisees, would never have kept silent about this crime, which was the greatest. But if they should say that the Hebrew books were falsified after the coming of the Lord and Savior and the preaching of the Apostles, I shall not be able to hold back my laughter, that the Savior and the Evangelists and the Apostles should have brought forward testimonies in such a way as the Jews were afterward going to falsify them. But of the present place this must be said: that we take refuge in the translation of the Seventy in vain, lest what is said according to the Hebrew should seem blasphemous: Hear with hearing, and do not understand: and see the vision, and do not know, since we find testimonies of this kind in the Seventy Translators too, such as that in Exodus which is said to Pharaoh: For this reason I have raised you up, to show my power in you (Exod 9:16). But if he himself raised him up and hardened Pharaoh's heart so that he should not believe; and of others it is said: God gave them a spirit of compunction, eyes that they should not see, and ears that they should not hear (Rom 11:8); and in the Psalms: Let their table become a snare, and a trap, and a stumbling block, and a recompense: let their eyes be darkened that they may not see, and bend their backs always (Ps 68 [69]:23-24): then those who do not see are not at fault, but he who gave them eyes for not seeing. So even apart from this testimony which we are now trying to expound, the same question remains in the Churches, and either the others will be resolved along with this one, or this one too will be insoluble along with the others. The blessed apostle Paul explains this place more fully to the Romans, and we should be doing something superfluous if we wished to comprise in a brief discourse what he pursued through almost the whole epistle. For he says, after many things: God has shut up all things in unbelief, that he may have mercy on all (Rom 11:32). And marveling at the mysteries of the Lord he added: O depth of the riches of the wisdom and knowledge of God, how inscrutable are his judgments, and how untraceable his ways. And again, disputing about the unbelief of the Jews, he says: Have they so sinned that they should fall? God forbid; but by their offense salvation came to the Gentiles, to provoke them to emulation (Rom 11:11). And a little after: For if their casting away is the reconciliation of the world, what will their taking up be? Is it not life from the dead? And again: For I would not have you ignorant, brothers, of this mystery, lest you be wise in yourselves, that blindness has come in part upon Israel, until the fullness of the Gentiles comes in, and then all Israel should be saved. And a little after (Rom 11:25): As concerning the Gospel they are enemies for your sakes, but as concerning the election they are beloved for the sake of the fathers: for the gifts and the calling of God are without repentance. For as you, he says, once did not believe God, but now have obtained mercy because of their unbelief: so these too have now not believed in your mercy, that they too may obtain mercy. For God has shut up all things under sin, that he may have mercy on all. It is therefore not the cruelty of God but his mercy that one nation should perish so that all may be saved; that a part of the Jews should not see, so that the whole world may behold. Hence the Lord himself too in the Gospel turns the mystery of the man blind from birth, who had received his eyes, to Tropology, and says: For judgment I came into this world, that those who see may not see, and those who do not see may see (John 9:39). And in another place Simeon speaks: Behold, this one is set for the fall and for the rising again of many (Luke 2:34). So while they do not see, we see; while they fall, we rise again. Understanding this, the Prophet in a certain manner says in other words: O Lord, you command me to speak to the people of the Jews, that they may hear and not understand the Savior, and see him and not recognize him. If you will that your command be fulfilled and the whole world be saved, which I too desire to come about, then blind the heart of this people and make its ears heavy and shut its eyes, that they may not understand, may not hear, may not see. For if they see, and are converted, and understand, and are healed, the whole world will not receive healing. From this we perceive that, however grave the sin, if anyone is converted he can be healed. And at the same time this too is to be understood: that for the greatness of their crime they were judged unworthy even of repentance, as the Lord himself says to Jerusalem: How often would I have gathered your children, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not (Matt 23:37).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.10":["For the heart of this people has grown fat, and with their ears they have heard heavily, and they have closed their eyes, and the rest. He gives the reasons why seeing they do not see and hearing they do not hear: because it has grown fat, he says, the heart of this people, and with their ears they have heard heavily; and lest perhaps we should think that fatness of heart and heaviness of ears belong to nature, not to the will, he adds the fault of free choice and says: They have closed their eyes, lest at any time they should see with their eyes and hear with their ears and understand with their heart and be converted, and I should heal them. In parables, therefore, and in an enigma do they hear who, with their eyes closed, do not wish to see the truth.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.6.11-12":["And I said: How long, Lord? And he said: Until the cities are made desolate without inhabitant, and the houses without men, and the land is left deserted. And the Lord will remove men far away, and she that had been forsaken in the midst of the land shall be multiplied. And yet in it there shall be a tithing, and again it shall be for a plundering, like a terebinth, and like an oak that has cast its fruits: the seed that shall have stood in it shall be holy. The Lord says: Go, and tell this people that it hears and does not hear, sees and does not see. And for this reason it neither hears nor sees: because it is not going to understand the words of the Lord nor to recognize his works, and because it has grown fat and broad and has forgotten its Creator (Deut 32). The Prophet answers, and asks anxiously about his people: How long, Lord, will this sentence remain, that hearing it should not hear, and seeing should not see? To whom the Lord answered: So long will it not hear and not see, and have its heart blinded, until the cities of Judea, when Vespasian and Titus make war, are utterly overthrown, so much so that not even their former name remains, and the houses, if any remain, are without inhabitant, and the land is reduced to a wilderness; and the Jewish people is scattered over the whole world, either by flight or by captivity; and the people of the Jews is multiplied no longer in Judea, as before, but among all the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.6.13":["And though I say that it is multiplied, so great will be the misfortune of the remnant of the people that, in comparison with its former multitude, scarcely a tenth part remains. And when there is a wilderness in the land itself also (for this place can be understood in two ways: that scarcely a tenth part remains even in the whole world, and that in Judea itself scarcely a small part of the people is preserved), again those very remnants will be for a plundering, when, after about fifty years, Hadrian comes and has utterly plundered the land of Judea, so much so that it is compared to a terebinth and to an oak that has lost its acorns. Finally, after the last devastation, public laws were even posted, and the Jews were forbidden to enter the land from which they had been cast out. But if anyone believes in Christ, and that is fulfilled which we read above: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us seed, we should have been as Sodom, and should have been like Gomorrah (Isa 1:9): when, according to the Apostle (Rom 9), the remnant will be saved; this seed will be holy, and from the shoot of the Apostles all the Churches will sprout. What we have said, the seed that shall have stood in it shall be holy, or according to Aquila, its shoot shall be a holy seed, is not found in the Seventy Translators, but was added by Origen from the Hebrew and the edition of Theodotion; it is carried in the copies of the Church, so that after the fullness of the Gentiles has entered, then all Israel may be saved; and that in this too the word of the Lord may be fulfilled, who says: I will kill, and I will make alive, and I will strike, and I will heal (Deut 32:39).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.1-2":["And it came to pass in the days of Ahaz the son of Jotham, the son of Uzziah, king of Judah: Rezin the king of Syria, and Pekah the son of Remaliah, the king of Israel, went up to Jerusalem to fight against it, and they could not subdue it. And they told the house of David, saying: Syria has come to an agreement with Ephraim; and his heart was moved, and the heart of his people, as the trees of the woods are moved before the wind. Uzziah, who is also Azariah, king of Judah, reigned in Jerusalem fifty-two years (2 Kgs 15). At the very end of his time Isaiah saw the things which the preceding discourse has set out. When he was dead and Jotham his son, a most righteous king, was reigning in his place, the prophet at once saw the Lord and Savior reigning in his majesty, and announcing the blindness of the Jewish people, and the overthrow of Jerusalem and of the other cities under Vespasian and Hadrian; and how in the Apostles a remnant was to be saved. As third king, the son of Jotham, Ahaz succeeds, a most impious man (2 Kgs 16; 2 Chr 28), who shut the doors of the temple, and in the valley of Ben-hinnom worshipped the Baals, and consecrated his son to idols; so that he even took away the bronze altar that Solomon had made, and placed in the Temple of God an altar of the idols, whose pattern he had received from Damascus. Justly, therefore, was he forsaken by the help of the Lord, and Rezin the king of Syria, that is, Aram, and Pekah the son of Remaliah, king of Israel in Samaria, rose up together against him, and came to Jerusalem to storm it (ibid.). We read in the book of Chronicles that Rezin the king of Damascus, having conquered Ahaz, carried many from Judea off to Damascus; and that Pekah the son of Remaliah, king of the ten tribes, which were called Israel, who reigned in Samaria, struck down in one day a hundred and twenty thousand fighting men of Judah, and led captive into Samaria two hundred thousand women, boys and girls, with boundless plunder. Of that contest the prophet has now kept silent, but relates the second battle, when, having had proof of their strength and victory, and lured by the greatness of the plunder, they come again to Judea and desire to subdue Jerusalem, and could not, because the Lord helped it, so that, on the occasion of the mercy by which he was freeing the besieged people, he might announce that his Son would be born of a Virgin. When the house of David, that is, the royal house, heard that Syria and Ephraim, that is, Rezin and Pekah, were coming with joined army, it was terrified and greatly afraid, and both king and people trembled so that you would think the leaves of trees were being tossed by the blasts of the winds. According to the anagogy the interpretation is easy: that while Ahaz the impious king is reigning, the king of Aram, which is interpreted lofty and sublime, so as to indicate the arrogance of secular wisdom, and Pekah the son of Remaliah, who himself too, according to the prophet Hosea, was of the tribe of Ephraim, from which Jeroboam the son of Nebat had set up the golden calves in Bethel and Dan (1 Kgs 12) and had separated the people of God from David, are referred to the heretics, who agree together to storm the Church. And when the house of David hears this, David whom we read in Ezekiel is to be raised up as the good shepherd (Ezek 34), and his people believing simply in the Lord, it trembles greatly. And it trembles for this reason, because it is compared not to fruit-bearing trees but to an unfruitful forest. And that these same heretics and the gentiles fight against the house of David with the swords of arguments and of the dialectical art, no one doubts: so that those who are at variance among themselves agree in attacking the Church; according to that instance in which Herod and Pilate, who were at discord with each other, were bound together in friendship at the Lord's passion.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.3-9":["This much we have said according to the history. But according to the tropology we have begun, we must consider that Isaiah is ordered to meet the impious king, going out from his own place, not at the beginning of the conduit, but at the outermost bounds of the upper pool, which was in the fuller's field, where filth and stains were washed away. For although Ahaz was reigning over Judah, yet because he was impious he lingered at the outermost bounds of the upper pool. So God has mercy not so much on the king, whom he judged unworthy of salvation, as on his people. And by the two tails of smoking brands, as we said before, he means secular wisdom and heretical speech, whose end is burning. They took counsel in vain to go up against Judah, and to seize it as though negligent and sleeping, and to join it to their own errors, and to set over it as king the son of Tabeel, that is, of the good God. For each adversary judges that the truth is with himself, that the best doctrine is with himself. Indeed Marcion the heretic thinks that Christ is the son of the good God, that is, of another, and not of the just God whose the Prophets are, whom he calls bloodthirsty, cruel, and a judge. While they say these things, the Lord threatens that their counsel shall not stand, but that for the meantime, now, as long as this world stands, the things also that are of the world hold sway within their own bounds and in their own cities. But when the time of the consummation has come, that is, the sixty-five years, and both the things of the world, which were made in six days, and all that pertains to the five senses have received their end, then all things are to be dissolved, which the gentiles and the heretics do not believe will come, and on account of their unbelief they do not understand the things that are said.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.3":["And the Lord said to Isaiah: Go out to meet Ahaz, you and Jasub your son who is left, at the end of the conduit of the upper pool, on the road of the fuller's field. And you shall say to him: See that you keep silent; do not fear, and let not your heart be afraid of the two tails of these smoking firebrands, in the wrath of the fury of Rezin and of Syria and of the son of Remaliah, because Syria, Ephraim and the son of Remaliah have taken most evil counsel against you, saying: Let us go up to Judah, and rouse it up, and tear it away to ourselves, and set a king in the midst of it, the son of Tabeel. Thus says the Lord God: It shall not stand, and this shall not be; but the head of Syria is Damascus, and the head of Damascus is Rezin, and yet sixty-five years, and Ephraim shall cease to be a people. And the head of Ephraim is Samaria, and the head of Samaria the son of Remaliah. If you do not believe, you shall not remain. Jasub the son of Isaiah, which is interpreted left over and returning, as a type of the people of Judah, which was to be freed from the hands of two kings, is ordered to go out with his father and to meet Ahaz king of Judah at the place of the conduit of the upper pool, on the road of the fuller's field, where we shall read later that under Hezekiah king of Judah Rabshakeh stood and, at the command of Sennacherib king of the Assyrians, blasphemed the people of God. To which place there went out, sent by Hezekiah, the princes of the city whom we read of in the volume of Kings.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And let it not disturb anyone that we take the pool in the bad sense, when that pool to which Isaiah the prophet, son of Amoz, is commanded to go up is taken in the good sense. For there it is said with an addition: The pool of the aqueduct, and the pool of the fuller, which is accustomed to wash away filth and to cleanse the stains of garments. And because it is set on high, the prophet is therefore commanded to go up to it and, in meeting the king, to promise victory over the two burnt firebrands.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"ISA.7.4":["And Ahaz (2 Kgs 18), though an impious king, is commanded, on account of the mercy of the Lord, to keep silence and not to be terrified, nor let his heart be afraid, thinking that he will suffer things like those he had endured before. And he calls Rezin king of Syria and Pekah the son of Remaliah king of Samaria two tails of firebrands, that is, of smoking brands, because in them the kingdom of Syria, that is, of Damascus, was ended, and the kingdom of Samaria, that is, of the ten tribes, which by another name were called Ephraim. For it is written (2 Kgs 16) that Tiglath-pileser king of the Assyrians, under king Ahaz, went up against Damascus and laid it waste, and carried its inhabitants off to Kir, and killed Rezin; and that Hoshea the son of Elah laid an ambush for Pekah the son of Remaliah, and struck him and killed him, and reigned in his place in Israel nine years; and that Shalmaneser king of Assyria came and besieged Samaria, which is now called Sebaste, for three years, and in the ninth year of the reign of Hoshea took it, and sent Hoshea bound into prison, and carried Israel off to the Assyrians, and placed them in Halah and in Habor by the river Gozan, in the cities, or, as the Seventy translated, in the mountains of the Medes (2 Kgs 17).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.5-9":["For although these two most evil kings took counsel to go up to Judah, whose region is set in the mountains, and to rouse it up while it was at rest and in a manner sleeping, and to subjugate it to their own rule, and to set over it as king the son of Tabeel, which is interpreted good God, so as to indicate either a man of this name or an idol; nevertheless thus says the Lord God: This counsel shall not stand, but for the meantime only Damascus shall be the head of the cities of Syria, and in Damascus itself, the metropolis, Rezin shall rule. Further, in Ephraim, on account of Jeroboam the son of Nebat, who first reigned from Ephraim, Samaria shall be the head, that is, the royal house in the city of Samaria, and the head of Samaria shall be the son of Remaliah, that is, Pekah; but the kingdom of the ten tribes, that is, of the people of Ephraim, will cease to exist after sixty-five years. Which, unless we attend more carefully, cannot stand. For in the twelfth year of Ahaz the son of Jotham in the tribe of Judah, Hoshea reigned over Samaria, and in the ninth year of his rule he was taken (2 Kgs 17). And Ahaz reigned over Judah sixteen years (2 Kgs 16), and in the seventh year of Hoshea's rule after Ahaz's death Hoshea was taken, and Samaria destroyed, and the whole people carried over into Media: so that if we choose to put sixteen years of Ahaz and seven of Hoshea, the result is twenty-three years, or at most twenty-four; and where will the sixty-five years be, within which the kingdom of Israel is said to be ended? The Hebrews therefore set out this place thus: that Amos, who began to prophesy under Uzziah, when Isaiah too had the beginning of his prophecy, was the first to prophesy against Israel, saying: But Israel shall be led captive out of his land (Amos 7:11, 17). And that the title of his prophecy is against Samaria; and that he began to prophesy in the days of Uzziah king of Judah two years before the earthquake, which they hold to have occurred at the time when Uzziah, entering the Temple of God, claimed the priesthood for himself, and the earth was struck, and the ashes of the altar were poured out, and the king himself was struck with leprosy (2 Chr 26). And they hold that it was the twenty-fifth year of Uzziah when these things happened, whose remaining years are twenty-seven. For Uzziah reigned fifty-two years (2 Kgs 15); after him reigned Jotham his son for sixteen years, and his son Ahaz for another sixteen years; after whom reigned Hezekiah, in the sixth year of whose rule Samaria was taken; and thus sixty-five years are made up together (2 Kgs 16).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.9":["When the Lord foretold these things through the prophet, both Ahaz and the people did not believe that they would come to pass. Hence it is added: If you do not believe, you shall not remain, as Symmachus translated, that is, you too shall not remain in your kingdom, but shall be led into captivity, suffering the penalties of those whose unbelief you have imitated. Or certainly, according to the Seventy, you shall not understand. And the sense is: because you do not believe the things that the Lord says are to come, you will not have understanding.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.10-11":["And the Lord spoke again to Ahaz, saying: Ask for yourself a sign from the Lord your God, in the depth of hell, or in the height above. He who had before spoken to Ahaz through the prophet: See that you keep silent; do not fear, and the rest, since Ahaz did not believe and therefore did not understand, himself speaks to Ahaz: that, terrified at least by the authority of the Lord, he may accept what is said. Because it seems difficult to you, he says, that most powerful kingdoms are to be ended in a short time, and that you with your people should be freed from great danger, ask for yourself a sign, not at all from the idols in whose error you are held, but from the Lord your God, who promises you help; and the very sign is of your own choosing, whence you would demand it, whether from the depth or from the height. Whereas the Seventy alone said this, the others, according to the Hebrew, translated more expressively, from the depth of hell. So just as the depth signifies hell, so by the height we ought to understand above the heavens: so that when you have received a sign from hell or from the height, you may believe that the things I have said will come. Do you wish, he says, that the earth be split, and that the underworld, which is said to be in the heart of the earth, lie open with a great gaping; or that the heavens be opened? Both of which pertain to the type of the Lord's death and ascension: For he who descended is the same who also ascended (Eph 4:10). And in the Apostle we read: Do not say in your heart, Who has ascended into heaven? that is, to bring Christ down. Or, Who has descended into the abyss? that is, to bring Christ back from the dead (Rom 10:6-7). And in another place, disputing about Christ, he says: What is the breadth, and length, and height, and depth of the surpassing knowledge and love of Christ (Eph 3:18). This, so far as concerns the mystical senses. But Moses too received signs from the earth, of which we read in the Psalms: He sent signs and wonders in the midst of you, O Egypt (Ps 134 [135]:9), frogs, locusts, gnats, and the dog-fly; and from heaven hail and fire and darkness of three days. I think that Hezekiah too, when the sun went back ten lines, received a sign from heaven (2 Kgs 20); and Joshua the son of Nun at Gibeon and Aijalon, when the sun and the moon stood still (Josh 10). Most think that Saul received a sign from the earth and from the depth of hell, when he seemed to have raised up Samuel through incantations and magic arts (1 Sam 28). But Jonah the prophet too, freed from the abyss, and from the deep and the jaws of death, gave and received the sign of hell (Jonah 2). I have read in the Commentaries of a certain man that this place was thinned out through allegory, so that he understood the depth and the height as αἰσθητὰ and νοητὰ, which we can call sensible things and intelligible things, of which the one is referred to the senses, the other to the mind and reason. The Virgin too he interprets as the soul which is corrupted by no consciousness of sin, and can bring forth from itself Emmanuel, God with us, that is, the present word of God. But we do not eat the flesh of the lamb boiled (Exod 12), but roasted, and such as can dry up in us all the phlegm of pleasures, lest, while we are wise beyond what we ought to be wise, we neglect the mystery of our faith.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.12":["And Ahaz said: I will not ask, and I will not tempt the Lord. It is not of humility but of pride that he will not ask a sign from the Lord. For although it is written in Deuteronomy: You shall not tempt the Lord your God (Deut 6:16), and the Savior used this testimony against the devil (Matt 4), yet, having been ordered to ask, he ought to have fulfilled the command by obedience, especially since both Gideon and Manoah asked for a sign and received it (Judg 6 and 13). And yet, according to the ambiguity of the Hebrew speech, in which is written ULO ENASSE ADONAI (ולא אנסה את יהוה), and all have likewise translated I will not tempt the Lord, it can be read I will not exalt the Lord. For the impious king knew that if he asked for a sign he would receive it, and the Lord would be glorified. So, as a worshipper of idols, who had set up altars at every corner of the streets and on the mountains and in the leafy groves, and had fanatics in place of Levites, he will not ask the sign that was commanded.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.13":["And he said: Hear then, O house of David: Is it a small thing for you to be troublesome to men, that you are troublesome to my God also? Who is this who said: Hear then, O house of David? Not at all God, who above had said to Ahaz: Ask for yourself a sign from the Lord your God; but the Prophet, as is proved from what follows: That you are troublesome to my God also. And the sense is: because you not only persecute the prophets and despise their sayings, but also contradict the sentence of God when he is present and commands; so that you cause labor to him who says in another place: I have labored in bearing it (Isa 1:14); for that reason the Lord will do the things that follow. For labor and trouble, which Aquila and Symmachus translated, the Seventy and Theodotion rendered agon, that is, wrestling and contest, because in their contentiousness they do not submit their neck to the service of the Lord, but, when he wishes to cure their wounds, spit out health. And this is to be noted, that when Ahaz, a most impious king, will not ask for a sign, the prophetic word is turned to the house of David, that is, to the royal tribe, of which we read above: And they told these things to the house of David, saying: Syria and Ephraim have come to an agreement.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.14":["Therefore the Lord himself will give you a sign. Behold, a Virgin shall conceive and bear, and you shall call his name Emmanuel. No longer at sundry times, according to the apostle Paul, and in many ways will God speak (Heb 1); nor, according to another prophet (Hos 12), will he be likened in the hands of the prophets; but he who formerly spoke through others will himself say, I am here (below, 58). Of whom the Bride too was asking in the Song of Songs: Let him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth (Song 1:2). For the Lord of powers is himself the king of glory (Ps 23 [24]:10): he himself will descend into the virginal womb, and will enter and go out by the Eastern gate which is always shut (Ezek 44); of which Gabriel says to the Virgin: The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you: therefore the holy thing that will be born in you will be called the Son of God (Luke 1:35). And in Proverbs: Wisdom has built herself a house (Prov 9:1). And when it is said: The Lord himself will give you a sign, it ought to be new and marvelous. But if a young woman or a girl, as the Jews would have it, and not a virgin, gives birth, what sort of sign can it be called, since this is a name of age, not of integrity? And in truth, to set foot to foot with the Jews and not at all, with a contentious rope, give them occasion to laugh at our lack of skill: a virgin is called in Hebrew BETHULA (בתולה), which is not written in the present place; but in place of this word is set ALMA (עלמה), which all except the Seventy translated young girl. Now ALMA is an ambiguous word among them: for it is said both of a young girl and of one hidden, that is, ἀπόκρυφος..... Hence also in the title of the ninth psalm, where in the Hebrew is set ALAMOTH (עלמות), the other Translators translated for youth, what the Seventy rendered for the hidden things. And in Genesis we read, where Rebecca is called alma (Gen 24), that Aquila translated neither young girl nor girl but hidden. The Shunammite woman too, having lost her son, when she had thrown herself at the feet of Elisha and Gehazi was keeping her back, hears from the Prophet: Let her be, for she is in grief, and the Lord has hidden it from me (2 Kgs 4:27). In place of what is said in Latin, has hidden it from me, in the Hebrew is written EELIM MEMMENNI (העלים ממני). So alma is said not only of a girl or a virgin, but, with ἐπιτάσει, of a hidden virgin and a secluded one, who has never been open to the gaze of men, but has been guarded with great diligence by her parents. In the Punic tongue too, which is said to flow from the fountains of the Hebrews, a virgin is properly called alma. And, though we may give the Jews something to laugh at, in our own speech too alma means holy. And the Hebrews use words of almost all languages: as in the Song of Songs (Song 3:9), from the Greek φορεῖον, that is, litter, Solomon made himself one, which we also read thus in the Hebrew. The words nugas (trifles) and mensuram (measure) too the Hebrews call in the same way and with the same senses. And as far as I can contend with my memory, I think I have never read alma of a married woman, but of one who is a virgin: so that she is not only a virgin, but a virgin of younger age, and in the years of girlhood. For it can happen that a virgin is an old woman, but this virgin was in the years of girlhood. Or certainly a virgin, not a little girl and one who could not yet know a man, but now marriageable. Indeed, in Deuteronomy (Deut 22:25ff.) under the name of a girl and of a young woman a virgin is understood. If, he says, a man finds in the field a betrothed girl, and, overpowering her by force, lies with her, you shall kill only the man who lay with her, and to the girl you shall do nothing: the young woman has no sin worthy of death. For as when someone rises up in ambush against his neighbor and kills his life, so this matter happened. He found her in the field: the betrothed girl cried out, and there was none found to help her. And in the volume of Kings we read (1 Kgs 1) that they sought a girl, a virgin, named Abishag, and brought her in to the king, to sleep with him and to warm him; and the girl was exceedingly beautiful, and she ministered to him, and the king did not know her. And what follows: And you shall call his name Emmanuel, both the Seventy and the three others translated likewise, in place of which in Matthew is written they shall call: which is not found in the Hebrew. So this child who will be born of the Virgin, O house of David, let him now be called by you Emmanuel, that is, God with us, because by the very facts, being freed from two enemy kings, you will prove that you have God present; and he who will afterward be called Jesus, that is, Savior, because he is to save the whole race of men, let him now be called by you by the name of Emmanuel. The word CARATHI (קראת), which all have interpreted you shall call, can also be understood as she shall call: namely, that the Virgin herself, who will conceive and bear, is going to call Christ by this name. In many testimonies which the Evangelists or Apostles have taken from the old books, we must attend more carefully to the fact that they followed not the order of the words but the sense. Hence also in the present place, for shall conceive in the womb, Matthew set shall have in the womb (Matt 1:23); and for you shall call, they shall call. The Hebrews think that this is prophesied of Hezekiah the son of Ahaz, because in his reign Samaria was taken: which cannot be proved at all. Since Ahaz the son of Jotham reigned over Judah and Jerusalem sixteen years (2 Kgs 16), and his son Hezekiah succeeded him in the kingdom at twenty-five years of age, and reigned over Judah and Jerusalem twenty-nine years. How then, granting that this prophecy was made to Ahaz in his first year, is it said of the conception and birth of Hezekiah, when at the time Ahaz began to reign Hezekiah was already nine years old? Unless perhaps they say that the sixth year of Hezekiah's reign, in which Samaria was taken, is called his infancy, not of age but of rule. That this is forced and violent is plain even to fools. A certain one of our people contends that the prophet Isaiah had two sons, Jasub and Emmanuel, and that Emmanuel was begotten of the prophetess his wife, as a type of the Lord and Savior: so that the first son, Jasub, which is interpreted left or returning, signifies the Jewish people, which has been left and will afterward return; but the second, that is, Emmanuel, and God with us, the calling of the nations, after the Word was made flesh and dwelt among us.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Now all this was done that what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: Behold, a virgin shall have in the womb and shall bear. Where the evangelist Matthew says: shall have in the womb, in the prophet it is written: shall receive in the womb. But the prophet, because he foretells things to come, signifies what is to be and writes shall receive; the evangelist, because he narrates history not of the future but of the past, changed shall receive and put shall have. For one who has is by no means going to receive. Something of the kind we read in the psalms too: Ascending on high he led captivity captive, he received gifts among men. The Apostle, setting down this testimony, did not say received, but gave, because there it was signified of the future that he would receive, here it is told of him who had already given what he had received.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.7.15":["He shall eat butter and honey, that he may know to refuse evil and to choose good. For which the Seventy translated before he knows; and what follows, to take up evil, is not found in the Hebrew. And the sense is: O House of David, who are commanded by the word of God to call, that is, to call upon, when set in danger, the name of Emmanuel, that is, of God with us: do not marvel at the novelty of the thing, if a Virgin bears God, who has such power that, though he is to be born a long time after, being called upon he frees you now; for he it is who was seen by Abraham and spoke with Moses. I will tell you something else more marvelous, lest you think he will be born in a phantasm: he will use the foods of infancy, he will eat butter and milk. And although many ages later the Evangelist testifies of him: And the child advanced in wisdom and age and grace with God and men (Luke 2:52), and this is said so that the reality of his human body may be proved; yet while still wrapped in swaddling clothes, and fed on butter and honey, he will have judgment of good and evil, so that refusing evil things he chooses good things. Not that he did this, or refused, or chose; but that he knew how to refuse and to choose, so that by these words we may know that the infancy of the human body did not prejudice the divine wisdom. Indeed, the Angels announce him to the shepherds as he lies in the manger; the Magi, coming from the East, worship him, who surely, we must believe, were chosen. And on the contrary, Herod, the Scribes and the Pharisees are refused (Matt 2), because for the sake of one infant they slaughtered many thousands of little ones.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.16":["For before the child knows to refuse evil and to choose good, the land which you abhor shall be forsaken before the face of its two kings. In the sixth year of Hezekiah's rule Samaria was taken by the Assyrians (2 Kgs 18), that is, in the thirty-first year of his age. If, then, this one who is to be born, whether of a Virgin, as we prove, or of a young woman, as the Jews would have it, will eat butter and honey, and will be so small that he cannot distinguish evil from good, and before he passes beyond the time of infancy the land of Syria and of Samaria is to be laid waste by the Assyrians: let the Hebrews answer how Hezekiah at thirty-one years is proclaimed a little infant, and of such untrained age that, eating honey and butter, like the children of Nineveh, he knows neither left nor right, that is, neither evil nor good. But referred to Emmanuel, which is interpreted God with us, it will have an easy understanding: that at the mystery and the invocation of his name the land of Syria and of Samaria is laid waste by the conquering Assyrian, and the house of David is freed from the two kings whom it fears, namely Rezin and Pekah.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.17":["The Lord will bring upon you, and upon your people, and upon the house of your father, days that have not come since the days of the separation of Ephraim from Judah, with the king of the Assyrians. This place is to be read by hyperbaton. Indeed we too, following the Hebrew truth, have interpreted it thus: O house of David, listen to what I say: so that the land of Syria and Samaria may be forsaken before the face of the two kings whom you most vehemently dread, the Lord will bring days upon you, and upon the house of your father David, such as you have never had from the time when the ten tribes were separated from the two tribes and began to have a kingdom in Samaria. And he will bring those days, that is, times, with the king of the Assyrians, so that when those kings have been conquered and overthrown, you may be freed by the presence of Emmanuel. The Seventy rendered this place thus: The Lord will bring upon you, and upon your people, and upon the house of your father, days which have not yet come, from the day on which Ephraim took away the king of the Assyrians from Judah. What sense this has we cannot know, unless perhaps this must be said: that Ephraim, that is, Samaria, by the greatness of its sins first made the onslaught of the Assyrians turn aside to itself. Otherwise: For the meantime now the two kings Rezin and Pekah, who besiege you and hasten to lay you waste, will be overthrown in a short time; but the time of your own laying waste will come when the Assyrian comes, which you had never expected, indeed never feared. By which he teaches that the house of David must fear not Syria and Samaria but the Assyrians. So he frees it from the present dread, and threatens concerning the time to come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.18-19":["And it shall be in that day, the Lord will whistle for the fly that is at the farthest part of the rivers of Egypt, and for the bee that is in the land of Assyria: and they shall come and shall all rest in the torrents of the valleys, and in the caves of the rocks, and in all the thickets, and in all the holes. When the land of the two kings, Damascus and Samaria, which you now abhor and fear, has been deserted, then, O house of Judah, the Lord will bring upon you and your people days such as you have never seen before, and the king of the Assyrians. In vain, therefore, do you fear things present, half-burnt brands that have no fire at all: for you ought to know that with his whistle the Lord is going to call the flies of Egypt and of its rivers, by which without doubt he means the seven διωρυχὰς of the Nile, and the bee that is in the land of Assyria. And he calls the Egyptians flies on account of the filth of idolatry and because they were an unwarlike people; and the Assyrians a bee, whose kingdom at that time was the most powerful and the readiest for wars; or for this reason, that almost the whole region of the Assyrians and of Persia made use of arrows. These, then, will all come and occupy your land, in the torrents of the valleys, and the caves of the rocks, and in all the thickets and holes, and in the leafy woods. These things are said by metaphor, so that, since he had once named flies and bees, he might keep the figure in the rest. Let us read the books of Kings and Chronicles, and we shall find that the holy king Josiah was slain by the Egyptians, and the people of Israel subjugated to the Egyptian power (2 Kgs 23; 2 Chr 35), so that it appointed a king for them. And not long after, Nebuchadnezzar came with a countless multitude of warriors, and having taken Jerusalem and torn down the other cities of Judea, he burned the Temple and settled Assyrian inhabitants in Judea (2 Kgs 24).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.20":["In that day the Lord will shave with a hired razor, by those who are beyond the river, by the king of the Assyrians, the head, and the hair of the feet, and the whole beard. Some think that this is prophesied of the Assyrians, who for thirteen hundred years before they overthrew Jerusalem possessed the kingdom of Asia, of Egypt and of Libya: that they were to be conquered by the Medes and Persians and their empire destroyed. Others, however, judge that the Egyptians were slain beyond the river Euphrates. But we judge that the razor hired for a wage is the Assyrian himself, whom in Jeremiah, for the punishment of the sinful people, he even calls his dove (Jer 25). Indeed in the vision of Tyre too (Ezek 29:18-20), because Nebuchadnezzar had toiled very greatly in building siege-works and in heaping up a mound, and, when they fled by fleet, had been deprived of his wage, Egypt was given to him as the wage for his labor. So with this most sharp razor, and by those who dwell beyond the river Euphrates, namely by the Assyrian king, the Lord will shave from Judea all the hair and the hairs of the whole body from head to feet, and the comeliness of the beard, which is the mark of manhood, so that nothing strong, nothing beautiful may remain in it, but they may be compared to effeminate men, or rather to disgraced women.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.21-22":["And it shall be in that day, a man shall rear a cow of the herd and two sheep, and for the abundance of milk he shall eat butter: for everyone who is left in the midst of the land shall eat butter and honey. And it shall be in that day, every place where there were a thousand vines at a thousand pieces of silver shall become thorns and briers. With arrows and bow they shall go in there: for briers and thorns shall be in all the land. And all the mountains that shall be hoed with the hoe, the terror of thorns and briers shall not come there: and it shall be for the pasture of the ox and for the treading of the flock. After the overthrow of Jerusalem, and the captivity of the people, and the burning of the Temple, Nebuzaradan the chief of the soldiery, whom the Seventy call ἀρχιμάγειρον, left in the land a few of the people, and those poor, to till the vineyards and fields. Then Gedaliah, who, left from the royal line, had been set over them, exhorts them and says: Do not fear the Chaldeans: dwell in the land, and serve the king of Babylon, and it shall be well with you (2 Kgs 25:24). In that day, then, that is, at that time, when all the wealth of the Jews has been carried off into Chaldea, so great will be the desolation in the land of Judea, and so incredible the devastation, that they will by no means have herds of oxen, nor flocks of sheep, as they had been accustomed to have before; but a rare inhabitant will scarcely be able to rear one cow and two sheep; not for ploughing, but for the food of their milk and the clothing of their fleece. For through the want of grain and of everything that the earth brings forth for eating, they will feed on milk and butter and wild honey. And what he says, For the abundance of milk he shall eat butter, signifies this: that the land, through the scarcity of tillers, is more fertile and better suited for pastures.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.21-25":["Would that we did not know that these things are wont to happen after a captivity! But now a great part of the Roman world is like Judea of old: which we do not think has come about without the wrath of God, who avenges contempt of himself not through Assyrians and Chaldeans, but through savage nations, once unknown to us, whose countenance and speech alike are terrible, and who, displaying womanish and shaven faces [he means the Goths, who shaved their beards], stab the fleeing backs of men, and well-bearded men at that. I know that I have read in these places a very broad and inextricable tropology: that all the things we have set out according to the history happened spiritually to the Jews, who scarcely have one cow and two sheep, that is, clean animals: so that they are nourished not on solid food but on the milk of infancy, and eat the honey of words that drips from the lips of the harlot woman; and they do not have wine, which gladdens the heart of man, but all their works are reduced to thorns, so that they are wounded by adversaries who shoot in the dark at the upright of heart (Ps 10 [11]). And if at any time they wish to be wise in something higher, and, sweating with excessive labor, to find something mystical in the holy Scriptures, nonetheless they bring forth no fruits of doctrine, but are filled with briers and thorns, which spring up in the hands of a drunkard. And their land and doctrine is trodden down not by men, rational living beings, but by oxen, for whom God has no care, and by brute animals.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.23":["At that time, through the fewness of men, so great will be the want of wine, the vineyards being deserted and by no means pruned for fear of the enemy, that single vines will scarcely be bought for single silver shekels.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.7.24-25":["For the whole land will be reduced to briers and thorns; and so great will be the dread, with swords raging everywhere, that no one will dare to visit his own field without bow and arrows, and, abandoning the places in the plains, they will flee to the mountains, and there, protected by the difficulty of the places, they will scarcely dig the rough mountains by hand: because they will not have oxen, ploughs and ploughshares. Wherever, then, a rare tiller is found in the mountains, they will sustain a miserable life from there. But the rest will lie open to pastures, and will be trodden down by brute animals without any keeper.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.1-2":["And the Lord said to me: Take for yourself a great book, and write in it with the pen of a man: Swiftly strip the spoils, quickly take the prey. And I took to me faithful witnesses, Uriah the priest, and Zechariah the son of Barachiah, and I went in to the prophetess, and she conceived and bore a son. And the Lord said to me: Call his name, Make haste, strip the spoils, hasten to take the prey: for before the child knows how to call his father and his mother, the strength of Damascus and the spoils of Samaria shall be taken away before the king of the Assyrians. The Seventy, for a great book, translated a new and great book. And in place of what is found in the Hebrew, I took to me faithful witnesses, they said: for it is at hand, and make faithful men witnesses for me. And for Uriah the Priest, only Uriah; the rest likewise. First the Prophet was sent to Ahaz (Isa 7), to foretell to him what the Scripture records: when he would not listen, the Lord himself speaks to Ahaz, and commanded him to ask for himself a sign in the depth or in the height. When he answered, I will not ask, and I will not tempt the Lord, the impious king is dismissed and the words of God are turned to the house of David, and there is promised to it a Virgin who will bear a son, whose name is to be Emmanuel, that is, God with us. If he is called upon often, Samaria and Syria will be overthrown: overthrown, however, by the king of the Assyrians, who is afterward to capture Judah itself too; so that the whole land of Judea is turned into a wilderness. So again, under another figure, the virginal birth is described. And the Lord told the Prophet not at all to bring out the secret of the new birth among the peoples, but to write it in this great volume which we now read. And, that it may be the more marvelous, to comprise the mysteries of God in human words, and with the pen with which men are accustomed to write. And what is it that is written with a human pen? That the child who is born should swiftly strip the spoils and quickly take the prey, that is, that he should no longer suffer the devil to reign; nor send Angels nor Prophets, but himself come down to save his creatures. So the Prophet does what was commanded, and takes to himself two faithful witnesses: Uriah the priest, who is a teacher of the law, as Malachi says: The lips of the priest shall keep knowledge, and they shall seek the law from his mouth, for he is the Angel of the Lord of hosts (Mal 2:7); and Zechariah the son of Barachiah, who, there is no doubt, was a Prophet. We read that in the reign of Ahaz, Uriah was priest of the Temple of the Lord (2 Kgs 16), whom Ahaz commanded to make an altar like the altar of Damascus. And the book of Days relates (2 Chr 29) that Hezekiah the son of Ahaz sought the Lord in the days of Zechariah, who was instructed in the fear of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.2-4":["Uriah is interpreted light of the Lord; Zechariah, memory of the Lord; and Barachiah, blessing of the Lord: by these witnesses the birth of Christ is proved. For he himself in the Gospel, to the two who were going to Emmaus (Luke 24), beginning from Moses and the Prophets, set out that all things had prophesied of him. According to tropology, in a virginal soul, stained by no filth, the word of God, conceived of the Holy Spirit, swiftly strips the spoils from the adverse powers and makes all things serve him. And since it now sees in part and prophesies in part (1 Cor 13), before it can be perfect and rightly call God its Father and the heavenly Jerusalem its mother, while still set in infancy and in progress, it will overcome the strength of Damascus, namely the doctrine of secular wisdom; and will take away the spoils of Samaria, with which the heretics have despoiled the Church, saying: We have no portion in David, nor inheritance in the son of Jesse (1 Kgs 12:16), in the presence of the king of the Assyrians himself, the devil, who will not be able to help them. And such sons are borne not only by Mary, the perpetual Virgin, to the Prophets, but also by holy women to the patriarchs: Sarah, which is interpreted ἄρχουσα, that is, princess; and Rebecca, which in our language sounds patience.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.3-4":["And Isaiah showed himself worthy of the prophetic spirit, and offered himself to the prophetess, that is, to the Holy Spirit, which in the Hebrew speech is called in the feminine gender RUA (רוה), according to what is written: Draw near to the Lord, and be enlightened (Ps 33 [34]:6). So the Lord was conceived of the Holy Spirit. And although human speech cannot unfold the mysteries of his birth, yet Gabriel speaks to the Virgin herself who conceived: The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you: therefore the holy thing that will be born of you will be called the Son of God (Luke 1:35). Some interpret the prophetess as holy Mary, who, there is no doubt, was a prophetess: for she herself says in the Gospel: For behold, from henceforth all generations will call me blessed: for he who is mighty has done great things for me (Luke 1:48-49), and the rest. And Isaiah is commanded to call that very child, who before was called Emmanuel, now Make haste, strip the spoils, hasten to take the prey. For ascending on high he led captivity captive; he received gifts for men (Ps 67 [68]; Eph 4:8). And before he took a human body and, in the manner of infancy, called God his father and Mary his mother, the strength of Damascus and the spoils of Samaria will be taken away by the king of the Assyrians: so that, not yet born, he might save his people, the house of David, by invocation alone.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.5-8":["And the Lord spoke to me yet again, saying: Because this people has cast away the waters of Siloam that go in silence, and has rather taken up Rezin and the son of Remaliah: therefore behold, the Lord will bring upon them the waters of the river, strong and many, the king of the Assyrians and all his glory: and he shall come up over all his channels, and shall flow over all his banks. And he shall go through Judah overflowing, and passing through he shall come up to the neck, and the stretching out of his wings shall fill the breadth of your land, O Emmanuel. He had said above that the strength of Damascus and the spoils of Samaria were to be taken away in the sight of the king of the Assyrians, and that the two kings would be captured when the Assyrian made war: now God speaks to the Prophet in the customary character of the Scriptures, by a figure of Siloam, the spring of Jerusalem, and of the most violent Assyrian river, that it would flood the whole land of Israel with its waters, and go out over the banks of its bed, and be borne with such force that, after it had occupied the land of the ten tribes, it would reach Judah and, passing through, would extend up to the neck: by which he signifies the captivity close at hand. For Judah was not captured at that time, but when all the cities of the two tribes had been subjugated, Jerusalem alone remained, which was freed by the incredible mercy of God. So the might of the Assyrian and of his countless army, which he had described under the flooding of a river, he now shows by another figure: that with his wings, that is, his generals and boundless multitude, he covered the land of Emmanuel, that is, of God who defends it, but yet did not possess it; although Emmanuel is fitted by the Seventy to the following section, not with its proper name, as it is called among the Hebrews, but translated, that is, God with us. And that Siloam is a spring at the roots of Mount Zion, which bubbles up not with continuous waters but at certain hours and days, and comes through hollows of the earth and caves of the hardest rock with a great sound, we cannot doubt, we especially who dwell in this province. And the sense is: because the people of the ten tribes preferred to be subject to Rezin and the son of Remaliah, that is, to the kings of Damascus and Samaria, rather than to the stock of David, which began to reign by my judgment, I will make it serve not at all these kings whom it has taken up, but the Assyrian king, whose might as it occupies the land of Samaria is compared to the flooding of a river. And an apostrophe is made to Emmanuel, that is, to God present, because the Assyrian raised himself up so far as to try to possess even his land, Judah. According to the anagogy, every heretic who joins secular wisdom to his help and seeks to attack Jerusalem, that is, the Church, forsaking the waters of the spring Siloam (John 9), which is interpreted sent, and which leaps up to eternal life, will be handed over to the prince of the Assyrians (whom we shall read of later as the great mind), and, handed over to his power, he will come into the depth of sins. For he is of such arrogance that he dared to show the Savior all the kingdoms of the earth and to say: All these have been handed over to me (Matt and Luke 4). He will try also to enter Judah, that is, the house of confession, and often, through those in the Church who are negligent, he will come up to the neck, desiring to choke those who believe in Christ, and he will stretch out his wings, filling the whole region of Emmanuel; but he will not be able to prevail, because Judah has God present. We read in the Gospel according to John (John 9) that the Lord sent the man blind from birth, whose eyes he had anointed with clay made from his spittle, to the waters of Siloam, and when the blind man had washed off the clay in them, his blindness was wiped away and he received the clear light of his eyes: which, beyond the greatness of the sign, indicates that the blindness of the Jews and of every unbeliever cannot be healed otherwise than if the darkness of former error is put to flight by the doctrine of the waters of Christ, which flows gently without the din and clamor of words. This which is read in the Seventy: And he will walk over all your wall, and will take away from Judea the man who can lift up his head or perhaps do anything, is not found in the Hebrew, and in the Greek codices it has been pierced through with ÷ the slaying spit.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.9-10":["Gather together, O peoples, and be conquered, and hear, all you lands far off: take strength, and be conquered. Gird yourselves, and be conquered: take counsel, and it shall be scattered: speak a word, and it shall not be done, for God is with us. For gather together, or be weak and be broken, as others interpreted, the Seventy set know. For the Hebrew word ROU (רעו), because of the likeness of the letter RES (ר) and DALETH (ד), they understood as DOU (דעו). So, O peoples of Samaria and Syria, know that you are weak and feeble, and that with Emmanuel present you can do nothing against Jerusalem, the city of God: and let not only you, who are neighbors, know this, but the whole land far off. For although you gather an army and gird yourselves for battle, and the number of the besiegers is greater than that of the besieged, yet not once but a second time I will say: you are conquered. And whatever counsel you take against Jerusalem will be scattered. And although you said above (Isa 7:6): Let us go up to Judah, and rouse it up while it is at rest, and set over it as king the son of Tabeel, you speak a word indeed, but it will not be done, because God is with us, that is, Emmanuel. We can use this testimony against the gentiles too in time of persecution: that although they seem strong and to overcome us in those who have lapsed, yet they are conquered in those who have shed their blood for Christ, and that after wars peace is restored to the Churches, and all their counsels against Emmanuel are scattered, because God is with us. Those who contradict the truth will also profitably hear it, so that at last they may be overcome by reason, and may not wish to win in falsehood, because whatever they say, perversity can never overcome what is right.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.11-13":["For thus the Lord says to me: As with a strong hand he instructed me, that I should not walk in the way of this people, saying: Do not say, A conspiracy; for all that this people speaks is a conspiracy: and do not fear its fear, nor be afraid. Sanctify the Lord of hosts himself: let him be your dread, and let him be your terror. And he shall be for a sanctification to you, but for a stone of stumbling and for a rock of offense to the two houses of Israel: for a snare and for a ruin to those who dwell in Jerusalem; and very many of them shall stumble, and shall fall, and shall be broken, and shall be ensnared, and shall be taken. The Seventy translated this place thus: Thus says the Lord: With a strong hand they have withdrawn from the journey of the way of this people, saying: Lest perhaps they say, It is hard. For all that this people speaks is hard; but do not fear its fear, nor be troubled. Sanctify the Lord of powers himself, and he shall be your fear, and if you have trusted in him, he shall be for a sanctification to you, and you shall not run against him as against a stone of stumbling, nor as against a rock of ruin. But the house of Jacob shall be in a snare and in a valley for those who sit in Jerusalem: therefore very many among them shall be weak, and shall fall and be broken, and shall draw near, and shall be taken. And what follows, men in safekeeping, is to be marked in front with an obelus. Because the Hebrew and the common edition differ much from each other, we have therefore set down both. And first we must discuss the Hebrew. Thus the Lord spoke to me. Who, on account of good works, and the grace which on account of good works I have obtained; or with his strong hand instructed and trained me, that I should not walk in the way of this people and be held in the same error; or certainly he made me withdraw from the most evil way of this people, and said to me: do not fear the conspiracy of the two kings; but consider this rather, that all that this people speaks against me is a conspiracy; and, according to Symmachus, who interpreted ἄνταρσιν, has risen up against me in rebellion. But you, Prophet, and those who are with you, do not fear the plots of the people, but fear the Lord, and let him be your fear. For the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Prov 1:7; Ps 110 [111]:9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to the Seventy, the people resisted the will of God with a strong hand, and says that all that the Lord has commanded is hard, according to what we read in the Gospel: This saying is hard; who can hear it (John 6:61)? Hence they are commanded to hear the Lord and to fear him alone.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.14-15":["He will be for a sanctification to believers, but to unbelievers for a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense, that is, to the two houses of Judah and Israel; and especially for a snare and a ruin to those who dwell in Jerusalem, on whom very many will strike, and will fall, and will be broken, and, ensnared in the chains of their sins, will be led into captivity. The two houses the Nazaraeans (who receive Christ in such a way that they do not give up the observances of the old Law) interpret as two families, Shammai and Hillel, from whom arose the Scribes and Pharisees, whose school Akiba took over, whom they assert to have been the teacher of Aquila the proselyte, and after him Meir, whom Johanan the son of Zakkai succeeded, and after him Eliezer, and in order Telphon, and again Joseph the Galilean, and up to the capture of Jerusalem Joshua. So Shammai and Hillel arose in Judea not long before the Lord was born, of whom the former is interpreted scatterer, the latter profane: because by their traditions and δευτερώσεις they scattered and defiled the precepts of the Law. And that these are the two houses which did not receive the Savior, who became for them a ruin and an offense.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For if they have trusted in him, they will have him for a sanctification, and will not strike against him as against a stone of offense and as against a rock of ruin. From this place the Apostle too takes a testimony, that Christ became an offense to the Jews, but to the Gentiles foolishness (1 Cor 1:23). And again: Israel, by pursuing the law of righteousness, has not attained to the law of righteousness (Rom 9:31). Why? Because not from faith, but as if from the works of the law, they stumbled against the stone of stumbling, as it is written: Behold, I will lay in Zion a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense: everyone who believes in him shall not be put to shame (ibid., 33). So those who did not receive Emmanuel, but for whom he became a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense, will be broken and led captive. And what he says, But the house of Jacob shall be in a snare and in a valley for those who sit in Jerusalem, signifies this: that they do not dwell on the mountains, nor move in the lofty places of the Scriptures, but always understand lowly things, joining sins to sins, and bound by the snares of their offenses.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.14":["On that day the Sadducees, who say that there is no resurrection, came to him. There were two heresies among the Jews, one of the Pharisees and the other of the Sadducees. The Pharisees held up a righteousness of traditions and observances, which they call deuteroses, and hence they were also called 'divided' by the people; while the Sadducees, which is interpreted 'just', themselves also claimed for themselves what they were not. And whereas the former believed and confessed the resurrection of both body and soul, and angels and spirit, the latter, according to the Acts of the Apostles, denied it all. These are the two houses of which Isaiah teaches quite plainly that they would stumble on the stone of stumbling.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.8.16-17":["Hence the Prophet answered: Because, the Gospel taking its place, the Law has been shut and sealed among the Jews, and you command that it be assigned not at all to the Jews but to the nations: therefore I will wait for Emmanuel, whom you promise is to come, of whom you said above that he himself is our dread and he himself our terror, and that he is for a sanctification to us; and I will look for the Lord who hides his face from the house of Jacob, that is, from the Jews, because they would not receive him. The Seventy translated this place thus: Then they who are sealed shall be manifest, that they may not learn the law. Which has this sense: when very many have fallen and been broken, and have struck against the stone of stumbling and the rock of offense, then those who are sealed among the people will be manifest, so that they may by no means learn the law of Moses, but obey the precepts of the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.16":["Bind up the testimonies, seal the law among my disciples, and I will wait for the Lord, who hides his face from the house of Jacob, and I will look for him. The voice of the Lord speaking to the Prophet: Because, he says, the Lord has become a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense to the two houses of Israel, and they would not receive Emmanuel sent to them: Bind up the testimonies of the old Instrument, and hand them over to my disciples, who have received the Gospel, namely the Apostles, or apostolic men. Or certainly, let the law and the prophecy until John be bound up among them (Matt 11), and be shut and sealed, so that what they read they may not understand. For in place of disciples, according to the ambiguity of the Hebrew speech, we can also interpret doctrines.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.18":["Behold, I and my children, whom the Lord has given me for signs and portents of Israel, from the Lord of hosts who dwells on Mount Zion. The Lord, he says, commanded me to bind up the testimony among the Jews and to hand over and seal the law for his disciples, because he had hidden his face from the house of Jacob: therefore I will look for him and wait for my Lord, and not only I, but also the children whom the Lord has given me, namely the other prophets and the sons of the prophets, who were born not of the will of the flesh and of blood, but of God (John 1:13). Of whom the Apostle too spoke: My little children, with whom I am again in travail until Christ be formed in you (Gal 4:19). And these children, that is, the prophets, were given for signs and for portents to Israel, according to what we read in Ezekiel: And Ezekiel shall be for a sign to you (Ezek 24:24). In Zechariah too, holy men and disciples of the prophets are called τερατοσκόποι, that is, watchers of portents and signs, because the Prophets always went before as a sign of things to come (Zech 3:8). This much meanwhile according to the letter. But the blessed Apostle, in the Epistle which is written to the Hebrews (though Latin custom does not receive it among the canonical Scriptures), teaches that this testimony ought to be understood in the person of the Lord and Savior: For which reason he is not ashamed to call them brothers, saying: I will announce your name to my brothers, in the midst of the Church I will praise you (Heb 2:12); and again: I will put my trust in him (ibid., 13); and once more: Behold, I and my children, whom God has given me. Since, then, the children have shared in flesh and blood, he himself likewise became partaker of the same sufferings (Isa 8:18). And how these children were for a sign and a portent to the wisdom of the world and to the pride of the Jews, the same Apostle teaches, who says that the Lord and Savior chose the foolish and weak things of the world, to confound the wise and the strong things (1 Cor 1). Hence the Savior too said to the Apostles: Unless you are converted as one of the children, you shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven (Matt 18:3). And the preacher of the new Gospel becomes a child, who puts off the old man, who is corrupted in the desires of seduction, and puts on the new, who is renewed unto knowledge after the image of the Creator. And that the Lord of hosts dwells on Mount Zion, the same Apostle writes: You have come to Mount Zion and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem (Heb 12:22). Hence I wonder at a certain one of our people who understands these children as the two sons of Isaiah, whom he begot from the conception of the prophetess his wife, namely Jasub and Emmanuel: of whom the former went before as a sign of the rejection of the former people, the latter of the taking up of the nations. Whoever accepts this will consequently also affirm that the prophet Hosea truly had a harlot for a wife.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.19-22":["But the Nazaraeans set out this place thus: When the Scribes and Pharisees tell you to listen to them, who do everything for the sake of the belly, and in the manner of magi hiss in their incantations so as to deceive you, you ought to answer them this: It is no wonder if you follow your traditions, since each nation consults its own idols. So we too ought not to consult you, the dead, about the living: rather God has given us the law and the testimonies of the Scriptures; and if you will not follow them, you will not have light, but gloom will always oppress you, which will pass through your land and doctrine: so that when those deceived by you see themselves in error and suffering a famine of truth, then they will be saddened, or angry, and will curse you, whom they used to think of as their gods and kings. And in vain will they look back to heaven and earth, since they are always in darkness and cannot fly away from your snares.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.19":["And when they say to you, Seek from the Pythons and from the diviners, who hiss in their incantations: does not a people inquire of its God? on behalf of the living, of the dead? To the law rather and to the testimony. But if they do not speak according to this word, there will be no morning light for them. And it shall pass through it: it shall fall and shall hunger: and when it hungers, it shall be angry, and shall curse its king and its God. And it shall look up above, and shall gaze at the earth, and behold, tribulation and darkness, dissolution, distress, and pursuing gloom: and it shall not be able to fly away from its distress. If it is the voice of the prophet Isaiah, as the Jews judge: Behold, I and my children, whom the Lord has given me for a sign and for a portent of Israel, then these things that follow too must be believed to be said by him to his disciples. When, he says, the nations and peoples say to you, those of whom it was read above: Be weak, O peoples, and be conquered, why do you want to listen to prophets, why are you deceived by the words of Isaiah and think that he knows the things to come? Seek rather from the Pythons and from the diviners, who hiss in their incantations. In place of which the Seventy translated: Who speak from the earth, who cry out from the belly. For everyone who is of the earth speaks of the earth (John 3:31). And he whose god is the belly, and whose glory is in his shame (Phil 3:19), must be believed to cry out from the belly. Do you answer them and say: Does not a people inquire of its God? on behalf of the living, of the dead? If you, he says, in keeping with the variety of your idols (for you have not one god but many), consult those whom you judge to be gods, and inquire of the dead, or of the images of dead men, about the living: how much more ought we to hear our God through the prophets?\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But, as we said above, if it is the person of Christ saying: Behold, I and my children, whom God has given me, then he himself also speaks these things to the Apostles and to the believers from the nations who have received his Gospel. If, he says, your fathers whom you have left say: seek the ventriloquists, whom we understand as pythons (such as the slave girl we read of in the Acts of the Apostles (Acts 16), who was a source of gain to her masters), and those who speak from the earth, which the magi promise to do in the calling up of souls, and the other kinds of evil arts: you ought to know this, that each nation consults its own gods and questions the dead about the welfare of the living.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.20":["So he teaches the disciples, and adds: to the law rather and to the testimony. If, he says, you are in doubt about anything, know that it is written: The nations which the Lord your God will destroy before your face listen to dreams and to diviners; but to you the Lord your God has not so delivered: The Lord your God will raise up for you a Prophet from your brothers, like me; him you shall hear (Deut 18:15). Hence, if you wish to know things that are doubtful, give yourselves rather to the law and to the testimonies of the Scriptures. But if your congregation will not seek the word of the Lord, it will not have the light of truth, but will move about in error and darkness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But to you God has given the law as a help, so that you can say: the divination of the heathen, who often deceive their worshippers, is not such as ours, which is brought forth from the law without any payment. Hence the Seventy translated: Not as this word, for which there is no giving of gifts. For freely, he says, you have received, freely give (Matt 10:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.8.21-22":["The light will pass through it, that is, through your congregation, or land, and you will fall, and you will hunger, and when you hunger you will be angry, according to what is written: And if they are not filled, they will murmur (Ps 58 [59]:16): and you will curse God your king, and in your necessities you will look up above to heaven and down to the earth: and behold, tribulation and darkness, dissolution of the knees, and distress of mind, and gloom of the eyes, and you will not be able to be rescued from the distress. This according to the Hebrews.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But upon the unbelievers will come a most grievous famine, not a famine of bread nor a thirst for water, but a famine of hearing the word of God (Amos 8). And when you hunger you will be saddened, and will curse your prince and your fathers' traditions, that is, the devil and your ancient errors. And he says this to those who have suffered famine of the truth, and will look up above to heaven and down to the earth, and will be in distress, in darkness, and in tribulation: so that they may not see until the time when they too are converted to the Lord. These places call for a broad explanation, but we spare the size of the books, to remove the reader's weariness. These things we have touched on in part according to the translation of the Seventy, which in very many places is out of tune with the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.1-2":["At the first time the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali was lightened: and at the last the way of the sea beyond the Jordan, of Galilee of the nations, was made heavy. The people that walked in darkness has seen a great light: to those dwelling in the region of the shadow of death, light has risen upon them. LXX: Drink this first, act quickly, region of Zebulun, land of Naphtali, and the rest of you who are by the sea beyond the Jordan, of Galilee of the nations. The people that walked in darkness has seen a great light: you who dwell in the region and shadow of death, light shall rise upon you. We have set down the double edition because the testimony is well known and was used by the Evangelist Matthew: so that either the difference of the translation or the likeness may be recognized. And first it must be noted that the Evangelist Matthew set down this testimony not according to the Seventy but according to the Hebrews. For the Gospel word says: Now when Jesus heard that John had been delivered up, he withdrew into Galilee, and leaving Nazareth he came and dwelt in Capernaum, which is by the sea in the borders of Zebulun and Naphtali: that what was spoken through Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: The land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali, the way of the sea beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the nations: the people that sat in darkness has seen a great light; to those sitting in the region of the shadow of death, light has risen upon them. From that time Jesus began to preach and to say: The kingdom of heaven has drawn near (Matt 4:13ff.). And John the Evangelist relates that Jesus, invited with his disciples to a wedding at Cana of Galilee, did his first sign there, turning water into wine: This beginning of signs Jesus did in Cana of Galilee: and he manifested his glory: and his disciples believed in him (John 2:11). Hence too it is said in the Seventy: Drink this first, act quickly: because the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali first saw the miracles of Christ: so that the land which had first seen the Lord doing signs might first drink the draught of faith. And according to the Hebrew it is said to have been lightened at the first time of the burden of sins: because the Savior first preached the Gospel in the regions of the two tribes. Hence too it is said in the sixty-seventh Psalm: Bless the Lord from the fountains of Israel. There is Benjamin the younger (Ps 67 [68]:27-28; Acts 9); Paul the apostle, in ecstasy of mind, who also said elsewhere: whether we are beside ourselves, it is for God (2 Cor 5:13): The princes of Zebulun, the princes of Naphtali, their leaders; because in these tribes were the little villages from which our leaders the Apostles believed. And they believed, according to Symmachus, swiftly, so that, hearing: Come, and I will make you fishers of men (Matt 4:19), they at once left both their father and their boat. But at the last time their faith was made heavy, since very many of the Jews remained in error. And here he calls the sea the lake of Gennesaret, which is formed by the inflowing Jordan; on whose shore Capernaum and Tiberias and Bethsaida and Chorazin are situated: in which region above all the Lord stayed; so that the people which either sat or walked in darkness saw a light, by no means a small one, like that of the other prophets, but a great one, like that of him who says in the Gospel: I am the light of the world (John 8:12). And those who dwelt in the region of the shadow of death, light has risen upon them. Between death and the shadow of death I think the difference is this: that death belongs to those who have gone down to the underworld with dead works: For the soul that sins, it shall die (Ezek 18:20); but the shadow of death belongs to those who, while they sin, have not yet departed from this life: for they can, if they will, do penance. For Galilee of the nations Aquila translated θῖνας of the nations, Symmachus borders of the nations; and by θῖνας we understand mounds of sand, which are either on the shores or on the banks. The Hebrews who believe in Christ set out this place thus: At the first time these two tribes, Zebulun and Naphtali, were captured by the Assyrians and led into the enemy's land; and Galilee was deserted (2 Kgs 15), which the prophet now says was lightened, because it bore the sins of the people. But afterward not only the two tribes, but also the rest, who dwelt beyond the Jordan and in Samaria, were led into captivity (2 Kgs 17 and 18). And this, they say, is what the Scripture now says: that the region whose people was the first to be led into captivity and began to serve the Babylonians, and which before was moving in the darkness of error, itself first saw the light of Christ preaching, and from it the Gospel was sown into all the nations. The Nazaraeans, whose opinion I set down above, try to explain this place thus: When Christ came and his preaching flashed forth, the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali was the first to be freed from the errors of the Scribes and Pharisees, and shook off from its neck the most heavy yoke of the Jewish traditions. But afterward, through the Gospel of the Apostle Paul, who was the last of all the Apostles, the preaching was made heavy, that is, multiplied; and the Gospel of Christ shone into the borders of the nations and the way of the whole sea. Finally, the whole world, which before walked or sat in darkness and was held by the chains of idolatry and of death, beheld the clear light of the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.1":["Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, and the rest. These were the first to hear the Lord preaching, so that where Israel's first captivity at the hands of the Assyrians had been, there the proclamation of the Redeemer should arise.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.9.3-5":["The Seventy signify that not all but a certain part of the people believed: although for what they said, you have led down in joy, others have interpreted you have magnified. And while in the rest they hold the same sense, they add that he, that is, the devil, will restore the robe gathered by deceit and the exchanged garment: namely, that he is going to restore with their former ornaments all the souls which he had stripped of God's help; and not he alone, but also his satellites, the demons: who, if the choice were given them, would rather choose to perish in flames than to lose their prey.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.3":["You have multiplied the nation, you have not magnified the joy: they shall rejoice before you, as they rejoice in the harvest, as victors exult when the prey is taken, when they divide the spoils. For the yoke of its burden, and the rod of its shoulder, and the scepter of its oppressor you have overcome, as in the day of Midian. For every violent plundering is with tumult: and the garment mixed with blood shall be for burning and food for the fire. LXX: The greatest part of the people you have led down in your joy: and they shall rejoice in your sight, as they are wont to rejoice in the harvest, and as those who divide the spoils: because the yoke that was set upon them has been taken away, and the rod from their neck. For the rod of the oppressors shall be scattered, as in the day of Midian. For every robe gathered by deceit, and the garment of exchange, they shall restore: and they shall wish that they had been burned with fire. In obscure places we set down both editions, so that the diligent reader may recognize how far the common translation differs from the other editions and from the Hebrew truth. An apostrophe is made to the light itself, which appeared to the people set in darkness, that is, to the Lord and Savior. And it is said to him: You have multiplied the nation, that is, you have filled all the nations with the knowledge of you; but you have not magnified the joy. In accordance with which the Apostle says that he has unceasing sorrow for his brothers who are Israelites (Rom 9). And Jonah is saddened that the Ninevites were saved in such a way that the gourd, or CICEION (קיקיון), withered (Jonah 4). And the Lord himself says in the Gospel: I did not come except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 15:24). And in the passion: Father, if it be possible, he says, let this cup pass from me (Matt 26:39). This place has this sense: If it can be that the multitude of the nations believe without the destruction of the Jews, I refuse the passion. But if they are to be blinded so that all nations may see, Father, let your will be done. So when Christ has come, and the nation of Christians has been gathered from all nations, then the Apostles will rejoice as reapers rejoice in the harvest, of whom the Lord spoke: The harvest indeed is great, but the laborers are few (Matt 9:37). And as victors exult who divide the captured prey. For when the strong man has been captured and bound, all his house is plundered and the spoils are divided (Matt 12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.4":["But you, O Lord and Savior, have pulled from their shoulders the yoke of his burden, that is, of the devil, who before exulted in the world, who ruled over all nations, who pressed down the necks of all with the most heavy yoke of slavery; and the rod with which he struck all men and compelled them to render him their sins as a kind of tribute; and you have made those freed serve you, without any army and without shedding of blood, by a hidden hand: as once under Gideon you gave the people of Israel victory over the Midianites (Judg 7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.5":["For just as a garment that has been bloodied with human blood cannot be washed, but, stained with blood, is burned with fire, so that the stains of the foul gore perish along with the garment: so the violent plundering of the devil, and the tumult and crowds by which he had subjected the human race to himself, have been assigned to the fires of Gehenna.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.6":["For a little child is born to us, a son is given to us: and the government is upon his shoulder; and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counselor, God, Mighty, Father of the age to come, Prince of peace. His dominion shall be multiplied, and of peace there shall be no end: upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to establish it and strengthen it in judgment and righteousness, from henceforth and for ever. The zeal of the Lord of hosts will do this. LXX: For a little child is born to us, a son is given to us, whose government is upon his shoulder: and his name is called messenger of great counsel. For I will bring peace upon the princes, and his health: great is his government, and of peace there is no bound, upon the throne of David and his kingdom: to set it right and to help in judgment and righteousness: from this time now and for ever. The zeal of the Lord Sabaoth will do these things. Therefore the devil, and all his violent plundering, by which he had oppressed the human race and mingled blood with blood, will be for burning and food of the eternal fire: because a child is born to us of a Virgin, of whom it was said above: Before the child knows to refuse evil and to choose good (Isa 7:16); and afterward, in the begetting by the Prophetess: Before the child knows how to call his father and his mother (Isa 8:4). This child, then, who was born of the Virgin, is called Emmanuel, that is, God with us; and, begotten of the Prophetess, that is, of the Holy Spirit, he was named Make haste, strip the spoils, hasten to take the prey: now he is called by many names. And although from what he had said above, Emmanuel, that is, God with us, he showed that he is God; yet now he says that his government was set upon his shoulder, either because he himself carried his cross, or, by the shoulder, showing the strength of his arm, as the same Isaiah says: The Lord God has revealed his holy arm to all the nations (Isa 52:10). And again: Lord, who has believed our report: and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed (Isa 53:1)? So after two names he will be called by six other names: Wonderful, Counselor, God, Mighty, Father of the age to come, Prince of peace. For not, as most think, are the names to be joined in pairs, so that we read Wonderful counselor, and again, Mighty God; but Wonderful is to be read separately, which in Hebrew is said PHELE (פלא); and Counselor apart, who in their tongue is called IOES (יועץ); and God separately, who in Hebrew is said EL (אל). Indeed, in what follows, where we read: For you are God, and we did not know; and again: I am God, and there is no other besides me (Isa 45:5), and many things like these: in place of what is said in Latin, God, in the Hebrew EL is written. And what follows, Mighty, is called in Hebrew GIBBOR (גבור). Hence too in the place where the same prophet records: They shall trust upon God, the holy one of Israel, in truth: and what is left of Jacob upon the mighty God (Isa 10), for mighty God the Hebrew has written EL GIBBOR. And the Father of the age to come and of the resurrection, which is fulfilled in our calling; and the prince of peace, who said to the Apostles: My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you (John 14:27): no one will doubt this who has read, according to the apostle Paul, that the Savior is our peace. Terrified by this majesty of names, I think, the Seventy did not dare to say of a child that he was openly to be called God, and the rest; but in place of these six names they set what is not found in the Hebrew, Angel of great counsel, and I will bring peace upon the princes, and his health. Which seems to me to have this sense: He is the Angel of great counsel who announced to us that Israel was to be cast off for a time and the nations to be saved; and he gave peace to his princes, the Apostles and apostolic men, and left the health of his teachings to believers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.7":["And what follows: His dominion shall be multiplied, and of peace there shall be no end, in place of which the Seventy translated: Great is his government: we ought to know that the Hebrew word MESRA (משרה), both here and above, the Seventy interpreted ἀρχὴν, that is, government. In place of which we rendered government above, here dominion. But Aquila, deceived by the ambiguity of the word, interpreted μέτρον, that is, measure, which is called by the same name in Hebrew and in Latin. Nor can anyone doubt about the manifold dominion of the Savior and his peace, which has no end, who has read in the Psalms: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession (Ps 2:8); and again: And abundance of peace, until the moon be taken away (Ps 71 [72]:7), that is, until the consummation of the age. And his government and dominion will be upon the throne and kingdom of David, which after the Babylonian captivity had been broken up, to establish it and strengthen it, and to teach that it is perpetual (lest the promise of God should seem empty), from the time of the Incarnation and for ever. And the zeal, that is, the jealousy, of the Lord of hosts did this for this reason, because they provoked him to jealousy with those who were not gods, and he provoked them to jealousy with a nation that was not a nation (Deut 32). The prophecy of Emmanuel and of the child to be born of a Virgin, begun at the place where it is said to Ahaz: Ask for yourself a sign from the Lord your God, is ended with this little verse, in which is added: The zeal of the Lord of hosts will do this (Isa 7:11). Now let us pass on to the rest.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.8":["The Lord sent a word into Jacob, and it fell upon Israel. And all the people of Ephraim shall know it, and those who dwell in Samaria, saying in pride and greatness of heart: The bricks have fallen, but we will build with squared stones; they have cut down the sycamores, but we will change them for cedars. And the Lord shall raise up the enemies of Rezin against him, and shall turn his foes into tumult, Syria from the East, and the Philistines from the West, and they shall devour Israel with whole mouth. For all this his fury is not turned away, but his hand is still stretched out, and the people has not returned to him who strikes it, and they have not sought the Lord of hosts. We read above (2 Kgs 16) that in the days of Ahaz son of Jotham, son of Uzziah king of Judah, Rezin king of Syria and Pekah son of Remaliah king of Israel went up to Jerusalem to take it by storm. And (Isa 7) that Isaiah the prophet was sent to meet Ahaz with Jasub his son, to tell him not to fear, and that his heart should not be afraid of the two tails of smoking firebrands, because in them the rule of Syria and of Israel was to be brought to an end. When he did not believe, because of the greatness of the matter and the present danger, he is bidden to ask a sign for himself. And because he was an idolater, he refused to do even this: therefore the Lord gives a sign, not to the king himself, but to the house of David, the sign of his Son who was to be born of the Virgin, at whose invocation they were to be freed from the imminent danger, and the rest, which we have interpreted up to the present place. So, many mysteries having been set in between, he now returns to what he had begun, and prophesies the overthrow of Rezin and Ephraim, that is, of Syria and Samaria: A word, he says, the Lord sent into Jacob, and it fell upon Israel. Among the Hebrews DABAR (דבר), which is written with three consonant letters, DALETH, BETH and RES, according to the nature of the passage signifies word if it is read DABAR, and if DEBER, death and pestilence. For this reason many, deceived by the ambiguity of the term, say that not a word was sent, but death. So the Lord sent a word into Jacob, and it fell upon Israel. He willed that Judah should reign, as Jacob prophesies in Genesis (ch. 49); and Israel, that is, the ten tribes, claimed the rule for themselves, of whom it was said: They have reigned, and not through me (Hos 8:4). So the dignity of rule, which had begun in David at Samuel's anointing after Saul was cast off, sent into Jacob, that is, into the twelve tribes, which were once called Jacob, fell upon Israel: not, as the Seventy translated, came: for what had been appointed and directed to the saints fell among the wicked.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to the anagogy, our writers expound this passage thus: God sent his Son to Jacob, that is, to the Jews; and he came to Israel, that is, to the people of the nations, whom the Apostle too says are called Israel (Rom 9). Others, however, thus: The Lord sent his Word to the Church, which supplanted the former people, and it fell upon Israel, that is, upon the heretics, who boast that they see God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The Fifth edition and Aquila: where are your words?, for in Hebrew it is written dabarach; they read dabar, that is, word, in place of deber, which is interpreted death, according to what we read in Isaiah: The Lord sent death into Jacob, and it came into Israel, that is, deber, in place of which we translated: The Lord sent a word into Jacob, and it came into Israel, that is, dabar.\n— Commentary on Hosea","We read in Isaiah, at least according to the Seventy translators — otherwise the Hebrew has it differently —: I sent death upon Jacob, and it came upon Israel. Jacob is the name of the body being born; Israel, a name of blessing. For because he wrestled all night and conquered in the struggle, and at the rising of the morning star could say with the Apostle: The night is far spent, the day has drawn near, for this reason he received the name Israel, of one seeing God. Death, therefore, is first sent upon Jacob, so that we may mortify our members which are upon the earth, and through the mortification of the bodily members may come to the death of Israel, so that all the incitements of the passions in us may die.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"ISA.9.9-10":["So the people of Ephraim and those who dwell in Samaria shall know, who because of their multitude are lifted up into pride and say: The kingdom of Judah is small, and in comparison with ours most worthless. So, while they collapse like bricks, let us build ourselves a house of squared stone. When their sycamores, which are cheap wood, have been cut down by hostile incursion, we will build up our rule with cedars, which do not rot, so that they may signify an everlasting kingdom.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","So let their leaders know, and all who dwell in Samaria, that is, who say that they keep the law of God, and are in an abundance of virtues, and bear the fruits of righteousness (for this is what Ephraim means), who in the pride of their heart despise the Church, and, thinking its simplicity to be want of skill, say: In place of its bricks we will build our churches with squared and very strong stones, and in place of unfruitful trees, soon to perish, we will raise up the loftiest cedars; cedars which the Lord will crush, and which the righteous man, in the person of the wicked, relates that he saw, and their place was not found (Ps 36 [37]).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.10-13":["So the Lord says that Rezin, which means worldly wisdom, whose help Ephraim used, will be turned against him, and all his enemies will fight against him, and he will be assailed both from the East and from the West, and they will devour Israel with whole mouth. In place of which the Seventy translated: And let us build ourselves a tower: and God will dash down those who rise up against mount Zion, and will scatter his enemies. For they desire to build themselves a temple in Samaria too, in the likeness of the Temple of God; but although they have moved their feet from the East and said: Come, let us build a tower, and make ourselves a name before we are scattered (Gen 11:4), with the Lord fighting against them their agreement will be dissolved and scattered, and their tongues will be divided against one another, lest by agreeing they become worse; rather they will be destroyed by one another. And when they have been so struck and devoured by their enemies, still they will not return to the Lord; but his hand is still ready to strike, according to what we read in Jeremiah: In vain have I struck your sons: you have not received discipline (Jer 2:30).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.11-13":["Let all the people of Ephraim, then, and those who dwell in Samaria know this: that the Syrian, who is now its help, will go over to the enemy. Or certainly, that against the Syrian himself sudden wars of adversaries arise, and all are turned into tumult: so that against Israel Syria moves from the East, and the Philistines, that is, the Palestinians, from the West, and together they devour Israel. And when they have done these things, my hand is nonetheless stretched out, or lifted high, over Israel, and does not cease to strike them. And struck by God they will not return to him who strikes them, nor will they seek the Lord of hosts, worshiping golden calves in place of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.14":["And the Lord will destroy from Israel head and tail, bending and perverting, in one day. The aged and honorable, he is the head; and the prophet who teaches falsehood, he is the tail. And those who call this people blessed shall be leading it astray, and those who are called blessed shall be cast headlong. Therefore the Lord will not rejoice over its young men, and he will not have mercy on its orphans and widows, because every one is a hypocrite and wicked, and every mouth has spoken folly. For all this his fury is not turned away, but his hand is still stretched out. For impiety is kindled like a fire: it shall devour brier and thorn, and shall be kindled in the thicket of the forest, and shall be rolled together in the pride of smoke. In the wrath of the Lord of hosts the earth has trembled, and the people shall be like food for the fire. A man will not spare his brother, and he will turn aside to the right and be hungry, and he will eat on the left and not be filled: each one will devour the flesh of his own arm. Manasseh Ephraim, and Ephraim Manasseh, and they together against Judah. For all this his fury is not turned away, but his hand is still stretched out. Not only will the Lord raise up the enemies of Rezin against him, and turn his foes into tumult, Syria from the East and the Philistines from the West, so that they devour Israel with whole mouth; but because it has not returned to him who strikes it, nor sought the Lord of hosts, he will stretch out his hand to strike, so as to destroy from Israel the head, and the tail bending and perverting, namely him who perverts what is right (Jer 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to tropology, the Lord will take away head and tail, the greater and the lesser alike. By naming the tail, he shows that the heretics are not men but beasts of burden, which use this member to cover their dung and to keep off small creatures. And after the head he adds the tail bending and perverting, because they overturn the precepts of the law and pervert the right way.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.15-16":["He will also destroy the aged man, because he is the head, and the false prophet, who is set in the tail, both teachers and disciples alike: the one sort who for gifts praise sinners, the other who, being praised, do not perceive their own evils nor do penance.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.15":["Who the head is, he himself has interpreted: the aged and honorable. And who the tail is: The prophet, he says, who teaches falsehood, that is, the false prophet.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.16":["He has spoken of the leaders; now he joins the people as well to the nobles, because teachers and disciples, the learned and the unlearned crowd, will perish alike. For those who called the people blessed are seducers; and those who were called blessed, not for virtue but for gifts, will be cast headlong into death. Hence also we read above: My people, those who call you blessed lead you astray, and trip up the paths of your feet (Isa 3:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.17-18":["Therefore the Lord will not rejoice in their young men: For woe to the city whose king is a youth (Eccl 10:16). And those who have lost God as Father, or the true Lord, will not obtain mercy. For every heretic is a hypocrite, doing one thing and pretending another, and whatever he says, though it may seem to be wisdom, is nevertheless folly. For these things he stretches out his hand to punish, and the devouring flame will consume all their impieties like thorns. And words without virtues, which are compared to an unfruitful forest, will be wrapped in the pride of smoke.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.17":["In such a people God will have mercy on no person: not on the orphan, not on the widow, because every one is a hypocrite, pretending one thing and doing another: promising chastity and living luxuriously, making a show of poverty and filling the purse. Hence he adds and says: And every mouth has spoken folly. For all these things the Lord does not cease to be angry, but still raises his hand to strike.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.18":["And because they have done these things, impiety will be kindled like fire, so that the ravening flame devours not the tilled field but briers and thorns, and consumes not fruit-bearing trees but the unfruitful forest in which wild beasts dwell. For this reason it is said that the thicket of the forest, when fire is set beneath it, is rolled together in the pride and height of the smoke. For everything that is lofty and rises up on high can be called proud. In place of which the Seventy, meaning I know not what, translated: and it will devour all things round about the hills.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.19-20":["Hence also at the wrath of the Lord the earth trembled, and the people became fodder for the fire, because there was such cruelty in it that it did not spare even the dearest name of brotherhood, but, deserting and despising the right, it was sated with the things that were on the left, while it hungers for pleasures and evils, and always longs to join worse things to evil ones.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.19-21":["At their punishment all the ground will tremble, and the deceived peoples will be handed over to everlasting burnings, because for the sake of shameful gain they do not spare even their own, but vie with one another in taking plunder from the peoples they have deceived, leaving good things and lingering in the worst. And though they always find what is on the left, yet they are never sated with their errors, devouring the flesh of their own arm, and fighting against the Church with equal zeal. On account of luxury and pleasures and gains from the peoples they have discord among themselves, so that from one heresy two arise, and these in turn are divided into parts, so that they may lead away flocks of their own and devour the houses of widows and of sinful women, always learning and never coming to the knowledge of the truth (2 Tim 3). In all these things the fury of the Lord will not be turned away, consuming and overthrowing vices; but the more they advance in sinning, the more he stretches out his hand for punishment.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.9.21":["Manasseh and Ephraim too, who were born of one womb and begotten of the same parent, broke out into such madness that they were at odds with one another in hostile hatred. Let us read the volumes of Kings and Chronicles, and we shall find the civil wars of Samaria among themselves, while some fight for one king and others for another, and how, when their own kings had been killed, they agree on attacking Judah.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.1-4":["Woe to those who make unjust laws, and who in writing have written injustice, to oppress the poor in judgment, and to do violence to the cause of the humble of my people, that widows might be their prey, and that they might plunder the orphans. What will you do in the day of visitation, and of the calamity coming from afar? To whose help will you flee? And where will you leave your glory? that you may not be bowed down under the chain, and fall with the slain. For all these his fury is not turned away, but his hand is still stretched out. Most think that these things too are still said against the ten tribes which dwelt in Samaria, because they joined these as well to their former crimes. To us, however, it seems that the prophetic word cries out against the judges of the tribe of Judah and of Jerusalem, namely the scribes and the δευτερωτὰς, because they wrote unjust laws against the law of God, and by their traditions overturned the truth of judgment. For when God says: Honor your father and mother (Exod 20:12), they on the contrary taught sons to say to their parents: Whatever gift comes from me will profit you, so that they do not honor father and mother (Matt 15:5-6; Mark 7:11), and other things like these. And they wrote these most evil laws for this purpose, to oppress the poor and the humble of the people, to make plunder of widows and to despoil orphans. They are asked what they will do when the day either of captivity or of judgment has come, and of the calamity coming from afar, which was foretold long before. To whose help, he says, will you flee, when you have offended God, who is the true helper, and where will you leave your glory? For riches will not profit in the day of wrath, to keep the Assyrian from leading you in chains into the enemy's land, to keep you from falling in battle (Prov 11:4). And finally, just as in the preceding passages he had often said against Samaria: For all these his fury is not turned away, but his hand is still stretched out, he speaks so against Judah too, because the final captivity is not the end of their evil, but even in the enemy's land the sword of the Lord will pursue them. According to the anagogy, every heretic writes iniquity in order to deceive the poor and humble of the people and to despoil widows and orphans. For the rich man, of whom we read: A man's own riches are the redemption of his soul (Prov 13:8), does not endure threats, is not quickly caught by them. But the poor man, and he who is humble and small among the peoples, is quickly made to stumble by them. The widow too, who has lost God her husband, and the orphan who has lost his father the Creator, of whom it is written: You have forsaken God who begot you, and have forgotten God your nourisher (Deut 32:18), are easily tripped up. What then will they do in the day of judgment, when they have deceived so many, and of the calamity coming from afar? For just as it is said to the righteous man: The word is near, in your mouth and in your heart (Rom 10:8); and in another place: The kingdom of God is within you (Luke 17:21); so the calamity of the heretics will come from afar, prepared for them over a long time. To whom will they flee for refuge, when the God they have is a fiction? To whom will they hand over their glory, when everything is feigned? Nor will they be able to escape the chain with which they have bound very many. Hence also in the vision of the valley of Zion, which, forsaking the height of God, has been deceived by heretical lowliness, it is said of the teachers of knowledge falsely so called: All your princes have fled, and those who were captured were harshly bound (Isa 22:3); and that they fall with the slain one by one, and perish themselves in the slaughter of others, and though they suffer many things, and the day of visitation has come, their destruction is not their end: but they are ever to tremble at the hand of the Lord hanging over them. Up to this point is God's threat against the ten and the two tribes, that is, against Samaria and Jerusalem, which began among the people while Ahaz was still reigning: of these, two and a half tribes were taken captive under him, and the rest under Hezekiah, who succeeded Ahaz in the kingdom (2 Kgs 16:17). The following prophecy is written against the Assyrians.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.5-11":["Woe to Assur, the rod of my fury, and he is the staff; in their hand is my indignation. Against a deceitful nation I will send him, and against the people of my fury I will give him charge, to take spoils and to seize prey, and to make it a thing trodden down like the mire of the streets. But he does not think so, and his heart does not reckon so, but his heart will be set on crushing, and on the slaughter of very many nations. For he says: Are not my princes all of them kings? Is it not: as Carchemish, so Chalanno and as Arpad, so Hamath? Is it not: as Damascus, so Samaria? As my hand found the kingdoms of the idol, so also their images from Jerusalem and from Samaria. Shall I not, as I did to Samaria and to her idols, so do to Jerusalem and to her images? Let us set down in this place the translation of the Seventy, which disagrees with the Hebrew in very many things. LXX: Woe to the Assyrians: the rod of my fury is he, in their hands; my wrath I will send against a lawless nation, and to my people I will give command to make spoils and prey, and to trample the cities, and to lay them in the dust: but he did not so think, and did not so reckon; but his mind is to crush, and to destroy very many nations. And if they say to him: You alone are prince, he will answer: I have not taken the region which is above Babylon, and Chalane, where the tower was built. But I have taken Arabia, and Damascus, and Samaria: as I have taken these, so I will take all kingdoms. Howl, you carved images in Jerusalem and Samaria; for as I did to Samaria and to her idols, so I will do to Jerusalem and to her images. First it must be known that in the Hebrew it does not have Chalane, but CHALANNO (כלנו). For the last letter of this name is not IOD, as they supposed, but VAU, letters which differ from each other only in size. Now the tower was built in the plain of Shinar, in which were Erech, Accad, and Chalanne, and Babylon, which received its name from the confusion of tongues (Gen 11). Arabia, moreover, is not found in the Hebrew, but Hamath, which the Syrians to this day call Epiphania. Howl too, you carved images in Jerusalem, they added of their own. And what is written above: If they say to him, You alone are prince, he will answer, I have not taken the region this one and that one, but I have taken Arabia, and Damascus, and Samaria, suggests this sense: When, he says, those who have been taken captive say to the Assyrian, You are prince of all, he, still feeling himself to be weak, will answer: How do you call me prince, when I have taken only Arabia and Damascus and Samaria, and have not yet brought under my power the nations set far off beyond Babylon? But as I took Arabia, Damascus and Samaria, so I will lay all kingdoms beneath me. Let us pass to the Hebrew, and briefly note what seems right to us. Woe to the Assyrian, that is, Sennacherib, because he is the rod and the staff of my fury, for in his hand is my indignation, and through him I will strike a most deceitful nation, which has always limped between me and the idols, which lied that it would do my precepts; and against the people of my fury I will command him to despoil it and plunder it, and to reduce it to dust, and to trample it like the mire of the streets, that is, to strike it and not kill it, to wound it and not take away its life. But he went beyond the measure of my anger, and raged not only against Israel, against which he had been directed, but against very many nations, so as to crush all the kingdoms round about, and his sword raged even to the slaughter of very many nations. For, lifted up into pride, he dared to say this: My captains are kings of other nations, and, being subject to me, they will rule all nations. As I took Carchemish, so I took Chalanno too; as Arpad, so I gained Hamath too; as Damascus, so I subjected Samaria to myself too. As I have put under my feet the other kingdoms which served the worship of idols, so I will subject to my power Jerusalem too, and Samaria, in which are the same idols. As I took Samaria with its idols, so I will take Jerusalem too, because it worships the same gods. For Carchemish, however, the Seventy, adding of their own, translated the region beyond Babylon, and Chalane where the tower was built, leaving out Arpad altogether. But according to their translation, and with the freedom of tropology, provided it is pious, from what he says, and I will give command to my people to make spoils and prey, and to trample the cities, and to lay them in the dust, we can say this too: that the Lord daily commands his people to reduce to dust the cities of the adversaries, which are raised up against the knowledge of God and have been built by the heretics with enormous labor, and to show that they are nothing. And he, understanding the weakness of the human heart, and that, surrounded as he is with mortal flesh, he cannot have a perfect victory of the virtues nor take all the nations, though others admire him and say: You alone are prince, will answer them: There are many things that I ought to destroy, and have not yet been able to destroy: I know that I do not know. And although I have overcome many arguments of the adversaries, yet there are very many which are still to be overcome. And at the same time, in hope of what is to come, from those which he has overcome he promises himself the victory over the rest as well. Now the images of Jerusalem and of Samaria are bidden to howl: not the things themselves which were fashioned, but those who fashioned them: μετωνυμικῶς, those who made them, by the things that were made: so that not only those who have fabricated idols in the heresies, but those who in the Church through ignorance defend a lie for the truth, may testify by howling and penitence that they have erred.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.12-14":["And it shall be, when the Lord has accomplished all his works on mount Zion and in Jerusalem, I will visit upon the fruit of the proud heart of the king of Assur, and upon the glory of the haughtiness of his eyes. For he said: By the strength of my hand I have done it, and by my wisdom I have understood, and I have removed the bounds of the peoples, and I have plundered their princes, and like a mighty one I have pulled down those who sat on high; and my hand has found the strength of the peoples as a nest; and as eggs are gathered which have been left, so I have gathered all the earth, and there was none who moved a wing, or opened the mouth, or chirped. For fruit the Seventy translated sense, or great mind. After the captivity of Samaria, and the victory of the Assyrians, and the threat against Jerusalem, that, because it had imitated the images of Samaria, it must be struck with a like sentence, the Prophet now speaks against the Assyrian himself, who was lifted up into pride and thought that what he conquered was the work of his own strength. For the Assyrian, boasting in the swelling of his heart, said that by the strength of his hand he had conquered all the nations, and by incredible wisdom had taken possession of the bounds of all. And according to the Seventy he was of such power that he overthrew from their foundations the cities of warlike men, and seized the whole world like a nest, and made it his prey like eggs abandoned by their mothers. And because he had once taken a metaphor from birds, from the nest and the eggs, he kept it in the rest, so as to say: There was none who moved a wing, or opened the mouth, or chirped. So great, he says, was the terror of my strength and victory, that the conquered did not freely utter even weeping and groaning. There are those who think these things are said generally against the kingdom of the Assyrians: that after the Lord has accomplished all his works on mount Zion and in Jerusalem, that is, after Jerusalem has been overthrown, the empire of the Assyrians is destroyed. To me, however, from what follows, the threat seems to be properly against Sennacherib king of the Assyrians. And what he says: After the Lord has accomplished all his works on mount Zion and in Jerusalem, signifies not the overthrow of the city but its siege, when Rabshakeh, Sennacherib's general, came and did the things which the same Prophet records in later chapters (Isa 37:17). According to tropology, when Samaria and Jerusalem have borne the wrath of the Lord, and have perceived that they erred in fabricating idols, then will the great mind be destroyed, the Assyrian, who was puffed up into such pride against the Lord that he reckoned that all things would yield to his wisdom, and that any lofty doctrines, even those fortified by the art of dialectic, were to be shaken and utterly destroyed: so much so that, like the chicks of birds, they would not be able to rise up on high, and, not yet quickened, would be without sense and torpid, which the example of the eggs shows; and that he would not hear anyone so much as mutter or move his tongue against the reason and strength of his discourse.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.15":["Shall the axe boast against him who cuts with it, or shall the saw exalt itself against him by whom it is drawn? As if a rod should lift itself against him who lifts it, and a staff should exalt itself, which is but wood. Against Sennacherib, or, as many think, Nebuchadnezzar, boasting and saying: By the strength of my hand I have done it, by my wisdom I have understood, and the rest, the holy Prophet answered: O most foolish of mortals, do you think the wrath of God to be your own wisdom, and do you ascribe his command to your own strength? It is as if an axe should boast against him who holds the axe, and a saw against him by whom it is drawn, and they should say that all the works done by axe and saw were accomplished by their own skill. And if someone should lift a rod, and raise a staff to strike whom he will, and the rod and the staff themselves should boast and say that he who was struck was struck by them: so you too, though you have been the instrument of God's will, are lifted up into pride, and boast that all that is done belongs to your own power. Whatever is said to the Assyrian can also be referred both to the pride of the heretics and to the devil, who is called in the Scriptures axe and saw and rod, because through him unfruitful trees are cut down and cleft, and the hardness of unbelievers is sawn, and those who do not receive discipline are struck with the rod. The heretics too are those whose mouth is set against heaven, and their tongue passes through the earth, and who abuse the sense which they received from God for good, turning it to the contrary and the perverse, so as to speak against him by whom they were made, and to turn into blasphemy the ministry of the tongue, by which the Lord should be praised (Ps 72 [73]).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.16-19":["Therefore the Sovereign Lord of hosts will send leanness among his fat ones, and beneath his glory a burning shall be kindled and burn like the burning of fire. And the light of Israel shall be in fire, and his Holy One in flame: and his thorn and his briers shall be kindled and devoured in one day. And the glory of his forest and of his Carmel shall be consumed from the soul even to the flesh, and he shall be a fugitive for terror, and the remnants of the wood of his forest shall be numbered for their fewness, and a child shall write them down. Because you have done these things, and spoken what I related above, therefore the Lord will send his Angel, and in one night a hundred and eighty-five thousand of your army will die: so that all the most powerful, whom he calls fat, are reduced to leanness and to nothing; and, as the Hebrews relate, while the garments of the Assyrians were unharmed, their bodies were burned with a hidden fire: then the light of Israel and his holy one, that is, the angel, will be in fire and in flame, and all the thorns and briers of the Assyrians, that is, their malice, will be consumed, not over a long time, but in one day and moment. And as a forest and Carmel, which is a mountain in Galilee planted with woods, is quickly burned up when fire is set beneath it, so from the soul even to the flesh all the glory of the Assyrian will be consumed. Then the despoiled army will flee, as the same Prophet attests in what follows, and of so innumerable a multitude, which was compared to Carmel and a forest, it will come to such fewness that a little child could number them and write them down. For the Hebrews relate that only ten of his army remained; some of ours will have it that this king is a type of the adverse power. And as in Daniel (ch. 10) we read of the princes of the kingdom of the Persians, and of the kingdom of the Medes, and of the kingdom of the Greeks, so there is also a prince of the Assyrians, who because of his pride is called the great mind, and in the day of judgment will be handed over to the burnings of Gehenna, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels. And that the light of Israel and the holy one, that is, the Lord himself, kindles the briers and thorns of the Assyrian, and that all his power and his innumerable peoples are to be brought to nothing. That then he, terrified with fear, like a crooked and fleeing serpent, wishes to escape the punishments hanging over him. And that those who have been able to escape from his forest and confusion and from the wood doomed to perish are worthy to be numbered and written down by the child whose government is upon his shoulders. This same thing can also be referred to the heretics: that after the Church's teaching has shone forth, and all their snares have been laid open, then they come to such desolation that, of the forest and the unfruitful trees and their innumerable multitude, which they had deceived by heretical fraud, scarcely a few remain to follow their error. Where we have rendered, the Lord will send leanness among his fat ones, the Seventy translated, the Lord will send dishonor upon your honor. And where we said: and his Holy One in flame, they rendered: and he will sanctify him in fire: by which some would have it understood that punishments and torments are applied to sinners for this purpose, that they may be purged by divine fire. And where we said: And the glory of his forest and of his Carmel, they translated: The mountains and the hills and the forests shall be quenched, whereby all the brightness and pride and multitude of the Assyrians is quenched and humbled.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.20-21":["And it shall be in that day, the remnant of Israel, and those who have been saved of the house of Jacob, shall no more lean upon him who strikes them, but shall lean upon the Lord, the Holy One of Israel, in truth. The remnants shall be converted, the remnants, I say, of Jacob, to the mighty Lord. For if your people Israel be as the sand of the sea, a remnant of it shall be converted. The consummation cut short shall overflow with righteousness. For the Lord God of hosts will make a consummation and a cutting short in the midst of all the earth. Where in the Greek it says: The remnants of Jacob shall be converted to the mighty God, for mighty God there is written in the Hebrew EL GIBBOR (אל גבור), two names of the six names by which, as we read above, the little child and son who is given to us was called. And where it is written: The remnants shall be converted, in place of that it is said in the Hebrew in the singular number, what is left shall be converted, that is, τὸ ὑπόλειμμα ἐπιστρέψει; in the Hebrew it is written SAR JASUB (שאר ישוב). And from the occasion of this expression they think that Jasub son of Isaiah went before as a sign of the people that was to be saved out of Israel. So when the light of Israel and the holy one has consumed the forests and briers of Carmel, and the Assyrian king has fled with a few, then the remnant of Israel, who with Hezekiah as their prince, when the other cities of Judea had been taken, were besieged in Jerusalem, will in no way trust in the Assyrian, as they do now under king Ahaz, who sent messengers to the king of the Assyrians, saying: I am your servant; come up and free me from the hand of the king of Syria, and from the hand of the king of Israel, who have risen up against me (2 Kgs 16:7); when he took the gold and silver that was found in the treasuries of the house of the Lord and in the king's house, and sent gifts to the king of the Assyrians, and the Assyrian king hearkened to him, and came to Damascus, and took it, and carried it away, and killed Rezin; but, freed from that very Assyrian, the smiter, who had first been a friend and afterwards proved an enemy, they will lean and rely upon the Lord the Holy One of Israel, not falsely, as they had done under the former kings, but in truth. This we read was done under Hezekiah, so that, forsaking their idols, they were converted to the worship of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.22-23":["And because he had said that a remnant would be saved, he passes to later times, and says that the full salvation will come under Christ. Understanding this, the apostle Paul too writes to the Romans: But Isaiah cries out concerning Israel: Though the number of the sons of Israel be as the sand of the sea, a remnant will be saved. For he is finishing the word and cutting it short in equity, because a shortened word will the Lord make upon the earth (Rom 9:27). And as Isaiah said: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us seed, we would have become as Sodom, and would have been like Gomorrah (Isa 1:9). Where, then, the authority of so great a man goes before, let every other interpretation cease. And indeed, if we read Josephus (bk. 10, ch. 2), and how great a multitude of men there was in Jerusalem and in Judea when the Lord suffered, we understand that scarcely a few of the Jews were saved, among the Apostles and apostolic men. Now the shortened and perfect word is the word of the Gospel, which in place of all the ceremonies of the long-winded Law gave a very brief precept of love and faith: that what we would not have done to ourselves, we should not do to another. Hence the Lord too in the Gospel: On these two commandments, he says, hangs all the Law and the Prophets (Matt 22:40). Some refer this chapter to those times when under Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel, and Jeshua son of Jozadak, and Ezra, and Nehemiah, a part of the people returned to Judea. To them we shall answer that they do not keep the order of the history, especially since the things that follow are said not against the Babylonians, whose king was Nebuchadnezzar, but against Sennacherib king of the Assyrians.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.24-27":["Therefore thus says the Lord God of hosts: Do not fear, my people, dweller in Zion, because of Assur: he will strike you with the rod, and will lift up his staff over you in the way of Egypt. For yet a little while, and a very little, and my indignation will be ended, and my fury upon their crime. And the Lord of hosts will raise up a scourge against him, like the slaughter of Midian at the rock of Oreb, and his rod over the sea, and he will lift it up in the way of Egypt. And it shall be in that day: his burden shall be taken from your shoulder, and his yoke from your neck, and the yoke shall rot away before the oil. For rock, which Aquila translated according to the Hebrew, Symmachus and Theodotion set down the Hebrew word itself, SUR OREB (צור עורב). In place of which the Seventy translated place of tribulation: of which we shall speak in its own place. It seems hard to you, he says, O dweller in Zion, that when all the nations round about have been subjugated by the Assyrian, you alone should be freed from his hands. Listen to what I say: do not fear, my people, that you will be taken when Assur conquers. For although in the fourteenth year of king Hezekiah (2 Kgs 18) Sennacherib king of Assyria is to go up against all the fortified cities of Judah, to take them, and to send Rabshakeh to terrify the people besieged in Jerusalem, yet you must know this, that he will strike you not with the sword but with the rod, and, marching against Tirhakah king of Ethiopia, and against the Egyptians and the Red Sea by the way of Egypt, he will only lift his staff against you, and will not be able to strike. For yet a little while, and when he has returned from Egypt with an infinite multitude of his army, and wishes to besiege you, he will at once be struck by my indignation, and by that scourge which I once raised up against the Midianites under Gideon (Judg 7), who by another name was called Jerubbaal: when Oreb and Zebah, princes of the Midianites, were slain upon a very hard rock, that is, flint, which in Hebrew is called SUR (צור), so that the place received the name rock of Oreb from the rock and from the king who was slain upon it. So he will lift his rod over the Red Sea when he marches against the Ethiopians, and will lift it when he returns to you by the way of Egypt: but as soon as he has come from Egypt, the burden will be taken from your shoulders, and the yoke of his rule, and you will cease to serve. And this yoke, that is, the power of the Assyrian, will rot away before the oil, that is, the mercy of God. But we can also understand what he said: He will strike you with the rod, and will lift up his staff over you in the way of Egypt; and again: And his rod over the sea, and he will lift it up in the way of Egypt, in this way too: that he struck very many of the tribe of Judah, and took the cities round about the kingdom of Jerusalem, for this reason, because they had their trust not in God but in the Egyptians. Hence Rabshakeh too reproaches them, saying: Behold, you trust upon a broken staff of reed, upon Egypt. If a man lean upon it, it will go into his hand and pierce it (2 Kgs 18:22): so is Pharaoh king of Egypt to all who trust in him. The history of the Midianites is written in the book of Judges (Judg 7), and we read it also in the Psalm: Make their princes like Oreb, and Zeeb, and Zebah, and Zalmunna (Ps 82 [83]:12). They err, therefore, who think that the time is signified when in the book of Numbers (ch. 25) the Midianites are reported to have been slain by Israel from the desert of Sur to Oreb, the mountain of God: for these are written among the Hebrews with other letters, and at that time they were not at mount Oreb, but in the wilderness of Shittim. According to the anagogy, the people that dwells in the Church is commanded not to fear the adversaries, who are always ready to fight and have overturned souls by their much disputation. For they receive a little power against the people of God, and strike not so much with the sword as with the rod, that is, not to kill but to threaten, for this reason, because the people have walked in the way of Egypt and have not trusted in the Lord. But when they have returned to God and have left the road of Egypt, then the scourge of God is to be raised up by his judgment against the adversaries. For Midian means from judgment: so that by the spirit of his mouth and by the oil of mercy the yoke of the enemies may rot away.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.28-32":["He will come into Aioth, he will pass into Magron: at Machmas he will deposit his baggage. They have passed over in haste; Gabee is our lodging (or, as we have found it written elsewhere: they have lodged a lodging); Rama was astonished, Gaba of Saul fled. Neigh with your voice, daughter of Gallim; give heed, Laisa, poor little Anathoth: Medemena has departed: inhabitants of Gebim, take courage. There is still day enough for a halt at Nob: he will shake his hand against the mount of the daughter of Zion, the hill of Jerusalem. LXX: For he will come into the city of Aggai, and will pass into Mageddo: and at Machmas he will set down his baggage: and he will pass through the valley, and will come into Aggai. Fear will seize Rama, the city of Saul: the daughter of Gallim will flee: give heed, Laisa: it will be heard in Anathot: Medemena was astonished, and the inhabitants of Gebim. Be comforted today in the way, so that he may remain: comfort the mount of the daughter of Zion, and the hill of Jerusalem. In this place the Seventy differ much from the Hebrew: for this reason we have set down both editions, so that, with Christ inspiring us, if we have deserved it, we may say what seems right to us on each point. The prophetic word describes the march and the pomp of the Assyrian as he returns from Egypt to Jerusalem, and with how great a din and how great speed he will come to attack it. And first, he says, he will come into Aioth, where, in his excessive haste, unwilling to stay, he will pass into Magron, and he will have such confidence of taking the city that he will leave his packs at Machmas, as one who will soon return when the city has been overthrown: having laid them down, he will pass it in haste; and in Gabee he will have his lodging: and while he halts there a little, to refresh his weary army, Rama, a neighboring city, will be terrified: Gaba, once the city of Saul, will flee. Then the daughter of Gallim too, which in Hebrew is called BETH GALLIM (בת גלים), will wail so that you would think it the neighing of horses. Therefore, O Laisa, and you, poor, or obedient, or humble Anathoth (for it can be interpreted in three ways), take careful heed, and turn aside from the onrush of the runner, if you can: for the city of Medemena has already departed from its seats. But you who dwell in the hills, which is what Gebim means, safe in the height of your places, take courage, that is, seize your arms. There still remained so much of the day that, standing in the little town of Nob, and looking at the city of Jerusalem from afar, he shook his hand and brandished it over mount Zion, either looking down on it and despising it, or insulting and threatening it, and marveling that, when the whole East had been subjected to him, so small a city should dare to resist his power. These things according to the Hebrews, as they were handed down to us by them, we have touched on in brief discourse. Now let us add what the churchmen think about this place according to the edition of the Seventy. When the yoke of the Assyrian, or, as some wrongly think, of the Babylonian, has been taken from your shoulders and broken, the Assyrian Sennacherib, fleeing with a few survivors, will come into Aggai, which is not found in the Hebrew. And so great will be the panic of the fugitive that he will not dare to remain there, but will pass into Mageddo, which the Scripture itself does not contain either. And because, hampered by his packs, he will not be able to flee more swiftly, he will lay down his baggage in Machmas, and with hurried step will pass through the valley, which the Hebrew text does not have either: and he will come again into Aggai, which is set twice in this place and is not found in the Hebrew. At the sound of his flight Rama, the city of Saul, will tremble: which is plainly false. For the city of Saul is called Gaba, as is contained in the Hebrew. Then he will come into Gallim: Laisa will hear, Anathoth will hear, Medemena will tremble. But the inhabitants of Gebim, and the hills which are in Jerusalem, that is, all the lofty men, are called upon to console Jerusalem: not after a long time, but at the present moment, and on the same day, while the Assyrian is on the way; so that they may remain in their places, and, though disturbed by fear, may in no way flee. This according to the letter. But a certain writer in this place, since he could not find the ἐτυμολογίας of the false names according to the Seventy, and testifies that he could not find them in the book of Hebrew Names, sends us off into uncertainty, so as to say that at the last time of the world, and at the consummation of this age, when punishments are imminent, the great mind, the prince of the Assyrians, will flee, and through various places and by varied journeys will desire to flee from the wrath of God. And when he has fled, the inhabitants of Gebim, that is, all the lofty powers, are called upon by the prophetic word to console the fugitive, and to teach him not to flee but to remain on the way and wait for the mercy of God; and not only to console the fugitive, but by good works to call back the daughter of Zion from mourning and to summon her to the salvation of penitence; and that these are the hills in Jerusalem of which we read in the latter part of this prophet: Comfort, comfort my people, O priests: speak to the heart of Jerusalem (Isa 40:1). This he said because, constrained by the truth of the matter, he had nothing else to say.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.10.33-34":["Behold, the Sovereign Lord of hosts will break the little flask in terror, and the lofty of stature will be cut down, and the high will be humbled, and the thickets of the forest will be overturned with iron, and Lebanon with its lofty ones will fall. Some think that this passage is still said of the Assyrian: that when he has been crushed, all the nations round about which lay under his dominion are cut down and humbled, and the densest forest is overturned; by which they would have it that μεταφορικῶς the people and the princes are understood. And that Lebanon too falls with its lofty ones, so that no power at all is left to Assyria. Others, however, would have it that from this place begins the passage concerning Christ: especially since both we and the Circumcision confess that what follows also is written of him. He had told above the name of the child to be born of the Virgin, that he would be called Emmanuel; and afterwards, that the conception of the prophetess's womb would be called hasten, strip the spoils, make haste to plunder (Isa 8:3), and that he himself would be a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense to both houses of Israel: that his government would be upon his shoulder, and he would be called by six names: wonderful, and counselor, and God, and mighty, and father of the age to come, and prince of peace: and that his empire would be multiplied, and of peace there would be no end. Now, in the prologue of his coming, before it is said that he will be born of the stock of Jesse and David, his passion is shown by the figure of the broken flask: that, by God's will, his flesh was handed over to death for this reason, that the loftiness of the Jews might be destroyed, and those once lofty might fall to the earth; and that Lebanon might be cut down with its cedars, of which we read in Zechariah: Open your gates, O Lebanon, and let fire devour your cedars: howl, fir tree, for the cedar has fallen, because the magnificent have been laid waste (Zech 11:1-2). And that he is said to have been crushed and struck by the Father, this too is evidence: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered (Zech 13:7). And another testimony: For they persecuted him whom you struck (Ps 68 [69]:27). The Hebrew word (פארה) PHURA, which Aquila translated κεραμεῖον, Theodotion and Symmachus rendered ληνὸν, that is, winepress, which itself too, according to the title of three psalms, signifies the Lord's passion, as he himself says in Isaiah: I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there was no man with me (Isa 63:3). But the Seventy, bringing in a new sense, translated glorious ones for winepress.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.1":["And there shall come forth a rod from the root of Jesse, and a flower shall rise up from his root: and the Spirit of the Lord shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and fortitude, the spirit of knowledge and godliness: and the spirit of the fear of the Lord shall fill him. Up to the beginning of the Vision, or burden, of Babylon, which Isaiah son of Amos saw, all this prophecy is about Christ, and we wish to explain it part by part, lest, being set forth and discussed all at once, it confuse the reader's memory. The Jews interpret the rod and the flower from the root of Jesse as the Lord himself: namely, that in the rod the power of one who reigns is shown, in the flower his beauty. But let us understand the rod from the root of Jesse as the holy Virgin Mary, who had no shoot clinging to her; of whom we also read above: Behold, a Virgin shall conceive and bear a son (Isa 7:14). And the flower as the Lord Savior, who says in the Song of Songs: I am the flower of the field and the lily of the valleys (Song 2:1). For root, which the Seventy alone translated, in the Hebrew there is written (גזע) GEZA, which Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion interpreted as κορμὸν, that is, trunk. And for flower, which in Hebrew is called (נצר) NESER, they translated sprout, to show that long after the time of the Babylonian captivity, when no one of David's stock possessed the glory of the ancient kingdom, Mary arose as if from a trunk, and Christ from Mary. That saying in the Gospel of Matthew of which all churchmen seek, and do not find, where it is written, For he shall be called a Nazarene (Matt 2:23), the learned among the Hebrews think was taken from this place. But it must be known that this NESER is written with the letter SADE, whose peculiar quality and sound, between z and s, the Latin tongue does not express. For it is a hissing sound, and is uttered with clenched teeth, barely, by the pressure of the tongue: and with it the city Zion too is written. Further, Nazirites, whom the Seventy translated sanctified, Symmachus separated, are always written with the letter ZAIN (ז).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And coming he dwelt in a city that is called Nazareth, that what was spoken through the prophets might be fulfilled: For he shall be called a nazarene. If he had set down a fixed example from the Scriptures, he would never say: what was spoken through the prophets, but simply: what was spoken through the prophet; but as it is, by naming the prophets in the plural, he shows that he took from the Scriptures not the words but the sense. Nazarene is interpreted holy. And that the Lord would be holy, all Scripture records. We can also say otherwise, that it is written in Isaiah even in the same words according to the Hebrew truth: A rod shall go forth from the root of Jesse, and a nazarene shall rise up from the root.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.11.2":["Upon this flower, then, which will suddenly rise up from the trunk and the root of Jesse through the Virgin Mary, the spirit of the Lord will rest, because in him it pleased all the fullness of the divinity to dwell bodily: not in part, as in the other saints; but, according to the Gospel written in the Hebrew tongue which the Nazarenes read: The whole fountain of the Holy Spirit shall descend upon him. Now the Lord is Spirit, and where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty (2 Cor 3:17). In the same volume of Matthew we read that the words written in what follows: Behold my servant whom I have chosen: my chosen one, in whom my soul is well pleased: I will put my spirit upon him: he shall bring forth judgment to the nations (Matt 12:18), are referred to the understanding of the Savior, upon whom the Spirit of the Lord rested, that is, remained in everlasting habitation: not so as to fly away and descend upon him again; but, according to the testimony of John the Baptist, so as to remain continually; for he says: I saw the Spirit descending like a dove from heaven, and remaining on him, and I did not know him: but he who sent me to baptize in water said to me: He on whom you see the Spirit descending and remaining on him, he it is who baptizes in the Holy Spirit (John 1:32-33). Further, in the Gospel which we mentioned above, we find these things written: And it came to pass, when the Lord had come up from the water, the whole fountain of the Holy Spirit descended and rested upon him, and said to him: My Son, in all the prophets I was awaiting you, that you might come and I might rest in you. For you are my rest, you are my firstborn son, who reigns for ever. This Spirit is called the Spirit of the Lord and the Spirit of wisdom; for all things were made through him; and without him nothing was made that was made (John 1:3). And in the Psalms it is sung: How magnified are your works, O Lord! You have made all things in wisdom (Ps 103 [104]:24). And the Apostle writes: Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God (1 Cor 1). And in Proverbs it is read: God by his wisdom founded the earth, and prepared the heavens by prudence (Prov 3:19). And as the same Word of God is called light, and life, and resurrection, so he is named the spirit of wisdom and understanding, and of counsel and fortitude, and of knowledge, and of godliness, and of the fear of the Lord: not that he is different according to the differences of the names, but that he is one and the same fountain and principle of all virtues. Without Christ, therefore, no one can be wise, nor understanding, nor a counselor, nor strong, nor learned, nor godly, nor full of the fear of God. And this is to be noted, that the Spirit of the Lord, of wisdom and understanding, of counsel and fortitude, and of knowledge, and of godliness, and of the fear of the Lord, that is, the sevenfold number, which in Zechariah (ch. 3) are called seven eyes on one stone, rests upon the rod and the flower which rose up from Jesse, and so from the stock of David.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.3":["But especially the spirit of the fear of the Lord filled him, for the sake of those who have need of the fear of the Lord, because they are little ones; the fear which perfect love casts out. For he who fears has punishment, and is not perfect (1 John 4:18). Hence the Apostle too says to believers: For you have not received the spirit of slavery again in fear: but you have received the Spirit of adoption, in which we cry Abba, Father (Rom 8:15). And in Malachi we read: If I am a father, where is my glory? and if I am Lord, where is my fear (Mal 1:6)? of which fear it is sung in the Psalm: Come, children, hear me: I will teach you the fear of the Lord (Ps 33 [34]:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","He shall not judge according to the sight of the eyes, nor reprove according to the hearing of the ears: but he shall judge the poor in righteousness, and shall reprove with equity on behalf of the meek of the earth, and he shall strike the earth with the rod of his mouth, and with the spirit of his lips he shall slay the wicked. And righteousness shall be the girdle of his loins, and faith the belt of his reins. We refer these things to the first coming of the Savior: the Jews contend that they will come to pass at the end of the world. Further, the Seventy translated: He shall not judge according to glory, nor reprove according to speech: but he shall judge with a lowly judgment, and shall reprove the lowly of the earth. For he accepts no one's person in judgment; but to the scribes and Pharisees and rulers he says: Woe to you, hypocrites: and, The kingdom of God will be taken from you, and will be given to a nation that brings forth its fruits (Matt 23:13; 21:43). Nor did he rebuke according to speech and the hearing of the ears. For when they said: Master, we know that you are truthful, and teach the way of God in truth, and you care for no one: for you do not look at the faces of men (Matt 22:16), knowing their malice he answered: Why do you test me, hypocrites? and other things like these.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.4":["But he judged in righteousness the poor in spirit, whose is the kingdom of God; and he reproved with equity the meek and humble of the earth, saying to the Apostles: Are you also still without understanding? And again: Do you not yet know nor understand? And to Peter in particular: O you of little faith, why did you doubt (Mark 8:17)? Or certainly, on behalf of the humble and meek he reproved others, who were trying to oppress them (Matt 14:31). He also struck all earthly works with the rod, or, as the Seventy translated, with the word of his mouth, saying in the Gospel: Do not think that I have come to send peace upon the earth: for I have not come to send peace, but a sword. And with the spirit of his lips he shall slay the wicked (Matt 10:34; Luke 12:51): of whom we read in Psalm 9: You have rebuked the nations, and the wicked has perished: you have blotted out their name for ever and for ever and ever (Ps 9:6). And Paul the Apostle writes: Whom the Lord Jesus will slay with the spirit of his mouth (2 Thess 2:8; Eph 6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.5":["And when the wicked has been struck, the Lord is girded with righteousness and truth and faith. For he himself was made for us by God wisdom, and righteousness, and sanctification and redemption (1 Cor 1), he who also says in the Gospel: I am the light, and the way, and the truth (John 8:12; 14:6). And of the Psalms it is said: Truth has sprung from the earth: and righteousness has looked down from heaven (Ps 84 [85]:12). Hence the Apostle too exhorts the Ephesians: Stand, therefore, having your loins girded about with truth, and clothed with the breastplate of righteousness (Eph 6:14). But if faith is read in place of truth, this must be said: that the girdle of the Lord, with which Jeremiah was girded (Jer 13), is the faith of believers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.6-8":["The wolf shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid; the calf and the lion and the sheep shall abide together, and a little child shall drive them. The calf and the bear shall feed: their young ones shall rest together, and the lion shall eat straw like the ox. And the infant at the breast shall delight over the hole of the asp; and he that has been weaned shall put his hand into the den of the basilisk. They shall not hurt, and they shall not kill in all my holy mountain, for the earth is filled with the knowledge of the Lord, as the waters of the sea that cover it. These things too the Jews, and our judaizers, contend will come to pass according to the letter: that in the glory of Christ, who they think will come at the end of the world, all beasts will be brought back to gentleness, and, laying aside their former fierceness, the wolf and the lamb will feed together, and the rest with the rest, which we now see to be opposed to one another. We must ask them whether, if everything in the present place is taken just as it is written, and nothing is referred to a spiritual understanding, according to the saying of the Apostle: Blessed be God and the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who has blessed us with every spiritual blessing in the heavenly places in Christ (Eph 1:3), then the root and the rod and the flower too are not to be referred to a sense; and the earth struck by the word of God, and the wicked slain by the spirit of his lips, are to be understood just as they are written; and whether they will be forced to teach how the reins of the Lord are girded with incorporeal things, righteousness and truth? But let us ask them this too: what is worthy of the majesty of the Lord in the wolf and the lamb feeding together, and the leopard lying down with the kid, and the lion eating straw, and a little child putting his hand into the hole of asps? Unless perhaps, according to the fables of the poets, they will restore to us the golden age of Saturn, in which wolves and lambs will feed together, and rivers will run full of honeyed wine, and the sweetest honey will drip from the leaves of the trees, and all things will be filled with fountains of milk. But if they answer that these things will come about for the blessedness of the times, so that men may enjoy all good things without harm to anyone, let them hear from us that nothing is good except virtue, and nothing evil except vice, as the Psalmist says: Who is the man who wills life, and loves to see good days? Restrain your tongue from evil, and let your lips not speak guile. Turn aside from evil, and do good (Ps 33 [34]:14; 30 [31]:27). Riches, moreover, and health of body, and abundance of all things, and their opposites, poverty, sickness, and want, are reckoned even among the philosophers of the world neither among goods nor among evils, but are called indifferent. Hence the Stoics too, who agree with our doctrine in very many points, call nothing good but honor and virtue alone, and nothing evil but baseness. We have said these things briefly to convict our judaizers of snoring in the heaviest sleep.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.6":["But according to the life-giving spirit, the understanding is easy. For the wolf, Paul, who at first persecuted and tore the Church, of whom it was said: Benjamin a ravening wolf, dwelt with the lamb, either with Ananias, by whom he was baptized (Gen 49:27; Acts 8), or with the apostle Peter, to whom it was said: Feed my lambs (John 21). And the leopard, who formerly did not change his spots, washed in the fountain of the Lord, lay down with the kid, not the one which is on the left, but the one which is sacrificed in the Lord's pasch. And this is to be noted, that it is not the lamb and the kid that dwell and lie down with the wolf and the leopard, but the wolf and the leopard that imitate the innocence of the lamb and the kid. The lion too, formerly most ferocious, and the sheep, and the calf, will abide together. This we see daily in the Church: rich and poor, powerful and lowly, kings and private men abiding together, and being ruled in the Church by little children, whom we understand as the Apostles and apostolic men, unskilled in speech but not in knowledge.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.7":["And when they have been bound together by the discipline of the Lord, so that their households too are joined, then will be fulfilled: Their young ones shall rest together. The lion too will eat not flesh but straw, namely, he will feed on simple food. And here it must be observed that it is not the ox that eats flesh, but the lion that eats straw. By straw I think the simple words in the holy Scriptures are understood; and by the wheat and the inner marrow, the sense which is found within the letter: and it often happens that men of the world, ignorant of mystical things, are fed on the simple reading of the Scriptures.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.8":["The infant too, who is a little one in malice, puts his hand into the hole of the asp, and drives demons out of the possessed bodies of men. But he who has been weaned is no longer nourished on the milk of infancy, but now feeds on solid food. He puts his hand into the den of the basilisk, that is, into the dwelling of Satan himself, and drags him out from there. Hence power was given to the Apostles too to tread upon serpents and scorpions, and upon all the strength of the enemy (Luke 10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.9":["And the animals that were once venomous will in no way be able to hurt and kill those who dwell on the holy mountain of God, which means the Church, of which it is said in the Gospel: A city set upon a mountain cannot be hidden (Matt 5:14). And lest we think that this is said of mount Zion, according to the error of the Hebrews, in the following verse the mysteries of the Gospel preaching are shown: For the earth is filled with the knowledge of the Lord: this is what was said more obscurely above: The wolf shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid; and according to their custom the prophetic words are laid open at the end: Like the waters, he says, of the sea that cover. As the waters of the sea cover the depths of the sea, that is, the earth which is covered by the waves, so the whole earth will be filled with the knowledge of the Lord. This joining together of those who were formerly of diverse ways the blessed apostle Peter too testifies that he saw in the linen vessel (Acts 10) which was let down from heaven, having four corners, which we understand as the four quarters of the world, so that we may recognize the earth filled with the knowledge of God: in which vessel there were four-footed beasts, and serpents, and wild beasts, and birds of heaven, so that what the Ark did in the flood, the Church may do in the world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.10":["In that day the root of Jesse, who stands as a sign of the peoples: him the nations shall beseech, and his sepulcher shall be glorious. LXX: And there shall be in that day the root of Jesse, and he who shall rise up to be prince of the nations; in him the nations shall hope, and his rest shall be honor. For his rest, there is set in the Hebrew MNUATHO (מנחתו), which all translated alike. For honor too there is read in the Hebrew CHABOD (כבוד), which plainly means glory. And the sense is: his death shall be glorious, so that what the Savior prays in the Gospel may be fulfilled: Father, glorify me with the glory which I had with you before the world was (John 17:5). It has been said of his birth, it has been said of the other mysteries in between: he comes to death, which, not by the customary name used of mortals, but because in Christ there was everlasting life, is called rest. But we, to make the sense plain to the reader, for sleep and rest translated sepulcher, another word but the same sense. At that time, then, when the Gospel of Christ has flashed forth in the whole world, and all the earth has been filled with the knowledge of the Lord, as the waters of the sea covering the earth, there shall be the root of Jesse, and he who shall ascend from its stock as a sign of all the peoples, so that the peoples may see the sign of the Son of man in heaven (Matt 24). He will have horns in his hands, in which his strength will be hidden, so that, being lifted up, he may draw all things to himself (Hab 3). Or, as the Seventy translated, he will rise again from the dead, to be prince of all the nations, and all the nations will hope in him. This Jacob too (Gen 49:10) attests in mystical speech concerning the tribe of Judah: A prince shall not fail from Judah, nor a leader from his thighs, until he comes for whom it is laid up, and he shall be the expectation of the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.11":["And it shall be in that day, the Lord will set his hand a second time to possess the remnant of his people, which shall be left from the Assyrians, and from Egypt, and from Pathros, and from Ethiopia, and from Elam, and from Shinar, and from Hamath, and from the islands of the sea. And he will lift up a sign to the nations, and will gather the fugitives of Israel, and will collect the dispersed of Judah from the four quarters of the earth: and the jealousy of Ephraim shall be taken away, and the enemies of Judah shall perish. Ephraim shall not envy Judah, and Judah shall not fight against Ephraim. And they shall fly upon the shoulder of the Philistines by the sea, together they shall plunder the sons of the East: Edom and Moab shall be the command of their hand, and the sons of Ammon shall be obedient. In that day, that is, at that time, of which it was said above too, when the root of Jesse has risen as a sign of the peoples, or to rule over the nations, the Lord will set his hand a second time; so that it is not, as our judaizers hold, at the end of the world, when the fullness of the nations has come in, that all Israel will then be saved (Rom 11); but let us understand all these things of the first coming. For we cannot, since one day is spoken of both now and above, refer that one to the first coming and this one to the second: lest through these things which follow, those too which went before should have to be referred to that Christ whom the Jews contend has not yet come but is to come. After the calling of the nations, then, which were once reckoned as the tail, Israel will be reckoned as the tail (above, Isa 10): so that the Lord may set his hand a second time and possess the remnant of his people, concerning which we read above too that not all Israel, but a remnant is to be saved, which shall be left from the Assyrians and from Egypt and from the various nations round about. For first the twelve Apostles, and the seventy, and the hundred and twenty souls, and the five hundred to whom the Lord appeared when they were gathered together, then three thousand and five thousand of the Jews believed in the Lord. James too speaks to the apostle Paul, who was himself also of the remnant: You see, brother, how many thousands of the Jews there are who believe? all these are zealous for the law (Acts 21:20). And in the same volume we read: Now there were dwelling in Jerusalem Jews, devout men, from every nation that is under heaven, who all marveled and said: Are not these Galileans, and how have we heard each one his own tongue, in which we were born? Parthians and Medes and Elamites, and those who dwell in Mesopotamia and Syria, Cappadocia, Pontus and Asia, Phrygia and Pamphylia, and Egypt and Libya, and Cyrene, and sojourners from Rome, Jews and proselytes, Cretans and Arabs, we have heard them speaking in our tongues the great works of God (Acts 2:5ff.). From all these nations, then, the remnant of Israel will be saved through the Apostles. The histories of the Church relate that the Apostles, dispersed through the whole world, preached the Gospel: so that some penetrated to the Persians and the Indians, and Ethiopia stretched out her hands to God, and from beyond the rivers of Ethiopia gifts were brought from there to Christ. And lest he seem to signify only the eastern peoples, he adds the rest too: And from the islands of the sea. By the islands of the sea he signifies the western region, which is enclosed by the circuit of the Ocean.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.12":["He will lift up, then, the sign of the Cross over all nations, and will first gather the people of Israel from the synagogues of the Jews, so that the Apostles might fulfill the command of the Savior, who had said: Go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 10:6). Finally Paul too speaks to the unbelievers among the Jews: It was necessary that the word of God be spoken first to you; but since you have rejected it, and judged yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the nations, as the Lord commanded us (Acts 13:46-47). By the four quarters he signifies east and west, south and north, so that through these the calling of the world may be shown.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.13-14":["At that time, says Isaiah, Ephraim and Judah, who now, while I prophesy, are at odds with each other in hostile hatred, will in no way be enemies, but according to the prophecy of Ezekiel the two rods will be joined into one rod, and those who were formerly separated will be united in the Church of Christ (Ezek 37), so that they toil with common labor among the nations, and fly upon the shoulder of the Philistines by the sea, that is, first preach to the Palestinians of the coast, and then go by sea with winged course to the other nations. Or, according to the Seventy translators: they will fly in the ships of foreigners, together they will plunder the sea: which we may understand from the example of the one apostle Paul, who through Pamphylia, and Asia, and Macedonia, and Achaia, and various islands and provinces, to Italy too (Acts 28), and, as he himself writes, to Spain, was carried in the ships of foreigners (Rom 15). So Ephraim and Judah, that is, those of the twelve tribes from among the Jews who believed in Christ, will together plunder the East, and will stretch out their hands into Edom and Moab, according to what Christ speaks mystically in the person of David: Into Edom I will stretch out my shoe: foreigners will serve me (Ps 59 [60]:10). For these nations, at the time when Isaiah prophesied, were adversaries of the people of the Jews, and therefore he now says that after the root of Jesse has risen to reign among the nations, and the standard of the Cross has been raised for the salvation of the whole world, then Edom too and Moab and the sons of Ammon, that is, the whole breadth of Arabia, give their hands to the Apostles, and in the places of idolatry churches of Christ are raised up.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.14":["Of these fishers, who go down to the sea and are in ships, Isaiah too prophesies: They shall fly in the ships of foreigners, and together they shall plunder the sea. For in the churches gathered from the number of the nations the apostles flew through the whole world, to plunder the sea and to carry off the spoils of the dragon who reigned in the sea, and of whom it is written: This sea, great and spacious in its arms; there are creeping things without number, small creatures with great. There the ships shall pass through; this dragon whom you formed to mock him. The sea is broad and spacious for the small and great living creatures that dwell with the dragon, and whose number is beyond count.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ISA.11.15":["And the Lord shall lay waste the tongue of the sea of Egypt, and shall lift his hand over the river in the strength of his spirit, and shall strike it in seven streams, so that men may cross it shod. And there shall be a way for the remnant of my people that shall be left from the Assyrians, as there was for Israel in the day when it came up from the land of Egypt. As Edom and Moab and the sons of Ammon will give their hands to the Apostles, to obey the Gospel preaching, so the Lord himself, who accomplished those things in his Apostles, will lay waste, not the sea, as according to the Seventy, but, according to the Hebrews, the tongue of the sea of Egypt, which formerly blasphemed against the Lord and presided over the Egyptian superstition. Hence also in the Psalms we read: This great and wide sea, there are creeping things without number (Ps 103 [104]:25-26). To which is added: This dragon which you have formed to mock him. He will lay waste, then, or slay, that is, ἀναθεματίσει, as Theodotion, Aquila and Symmachus translated, the tongue of the sea of Egypt, and will lift his hand over the rivers of Egypt in the strength of his spirit, or with a most violent wind, by which we understand the kingdom of the Romans. For in the reign of Caesar Augustus (Luke 2), when the flower ascended from the root of Jesse, and the first enrollment was made in the Roman world, the kingdom of the Egyptians, formerly most powerful, which had endured for many ages, was destroyed by the death of Cleopatra, and the river of Egypt was struck in seven streams, or in seven valleys. For the Nile of many waters, which formerly flowed in one channel and was impassable, was divided and cut up into seven very low valleys and streams, so that it might be crossed with shod feet. And this signifies tropologically that the nation of the Egyptians, so given over to idolatry and the vainest superstition as to consecrate hawks, owls, dogs, and goats, and asses with a divine name, he distributed from the boundless power of a kingdom among the several judges of the Roman empire, so that Thebes has one judge, Libya another, Pentapolis another, Egypt another, Alexandria another, and so the various regions which the Egyptians call νόμους. And for this reason, under the metaphor, the Nile has been divided into parts and split into streams, so that the Gospel word might run about without any impediment and reach the farthest peoples of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.11.16":["And as in the times of Moses the Red Sea was dried up so that the people might flee out of Egypt, so on the contrary the rivers of Egypt will be dried up, so that the remnant of the people of God, which will be saved from the Assyrians and from various nations, may cross into Egypt, in no way fleeing it, but taking it and treading it under their foot. Let the prudent and Christian reader hold this rule for the prophetic promises: that what the Jews and ours, or rather not ours but the judaizers, contend will come to pass carnally, we teach to have been already accomplished spiritually, lest on the occasion of fables of this kind and of inextricable questions, according to the Apostle (2 Tim 2), we be forced to judaize.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.12.1-2":["And you will say in that day: I will confess to you, O Lord, for you were angry with me: your fury is turned away, and you have comforted me. Behold, God is my Savior; I will act confidently and will not fear, for my strength and my praise is the Lord God, and he has become my salvation. You who had formerly said in the wilderness, when you came up from the land of Egypt and the Red Sea was dried up: Let us sing to the Lord, for gloriously magnified is he (Exod 15:1), and the rest: now that the tongue of the sea of Egypt has been struck, and its river dried up and cut off and humbled, glorify the Lord and say: I will confess to you, O Lord: because I who deserved your wrath and fury have obtained mercy; for you are my Savior, that is, Jesus, and I have no confidence at all in idols, nor will I fear what is not to be feared; but you are my strength and my praise, who have become my salvation. Let the most wicked heresy hear that the Lord was made so for those who are being saved, and whose Lord he formerly was not, so that we may understand creation and making in the holy Scriptures to mean not always the bringing into being of things which were not, but sometimes the grace toward those who have deserved that God should become theirs.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.12.3":["You will draw waters in joy from the fountains of the Savior. He whom above he had called Emmanuel, then, Strip the spoils, make haste to plunder, and by the other names, now, lest he seem to be another besides him whom Gabriel announced to the Virgin, saying: And you shall call his name Jesus, for he will save his people (Matt 1:21), he calls Savior, and proclaims that waters are to be drawn from his fountains, in no way from the waters of the river of Egypt, which have been struck, nor from the waters of the river of Rezin, but from the fountains of Jesus, for so Savior is expressed in the tongue of the Hebrews. Hence he himself also cried out in the Gospel: He who thirsts, let him come to me and drink. He who believes in me, as the Scripture says: Rivers of living water shall flow from his belly. This, says the Evangelist, he said of the Holy Spirit, whom those who believed in him were to receive. And in another place of the Gospel he himself says: He who drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst for ever: but the water that I shall give him will become in him a fountain of water springing up into eternal life (John 7:37-39; 4:13-14). By the fountains of the Savior let us understand the teaching of the Gospel, of which we read in Psalm 67 (68): In the churches bless God the Lord from the fountains of Israel (Ps 67 [68]:27).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.12.4-5":["And you will say in that day: Confess to the Lord and call upon his name: make known among the peoples his devices: remember that his name is exalted. Sing to the Lord, for he has done magnificently: proclaim this in all the earth. These things are commanded by the Apostles and the remnant of Israel to those of the nations who have believed: that they confess to the Lord alone, and, forsaking idols, call upon his name, and proclaim all his works to unbelievers; that they may know that he alone is exalted: to whom they must sing that he has done magnificently, and that his mercy may be proclaimed in all the world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.12.6":["Exult and praise, O dwelling of Zion: for great in your midst is the Holy One of Israel. First it must be said according to the letter: O dwelling of Zion, exult and praise your God, because he who was formerly seen as your God, and was shut within the narrow bounds of the land of Judea, has now filled all the earth with his knowledge, and, rising from death, reigns among the nations, and the nations beseech and adore him; only so, however, that he sets his hand a second time to possess what is left of his people, and to gather the fugitives of Israel, and to collect the dispersed of Judah from the four quarters of the earth: because the seed-bed of the Gospel went forth from the fountains of Israel through the Apostles, who were of the Jews. But it is better that we interpret Zion, that is, a watchtower set on the heights, as the Church, of which Psalm 50 (51) too sings: Deal kindly, O Lord, in your good will with Zion, and let the walls of Jerusalem be built (Ps 50 [51]:20); so that in her the sacrifice of righteousness may become acceptable to God, oblations and holocausts, and the calf which the most merciful father sacrificed for his penitent son (Luke 15).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.1":["The burden of Babylon, which Isaiah the son of Amos saw. The Hebrew word MESSA (משא) can be understood either as burden or as weight. And wherever it is placed at the head, the things that are said are full of threats. Hence I wonder that the Seventy translators chose to put vision in so grim a matter; but of this elsewhere. Now let us carry through what we have begun. Babylon was the metropolis of the Chaldeans, whose king Nebuchadnezzar, having overcome all the nations as far as Ethiopia, laid waste Judea too among the rest, and in the eleventh year of the reign of Zedekiah took Jerusalem after a long siege; and he led Zedekiah himself captive to Antioch, which was then called Riblah, and there, after his sons had been killed before their father's eyes, he ordered Zedekiah's eyes to be put out, and sent him, blinded, into a cage, and dragged him to Babylon like a wild beast; so was fulfilled the prophecy of Jeremiah, which sang: You shall enter Babylon, and you shall not see it (Jer 34; 39; 52). For the consolation of the people of the Jews, therefore, the ruin of Babylon is foretold: that just as Nineveh, the metropolis of the Assyrians, whose kings Pul and Tiglath-pileser and Shalmaneser and Sennacherib had captured the ten tribes, was overthrown when the Chaldean laid it waste, so this city too, which had grown proud against God, should be overthrown by the onslaught of the Medes and Persians.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The burden or vision against Babylon, which Isaiah the son of Amos saw. He saw, not with the eyes of the flesh but with those of the mind, how great and heavy a weight was to be laid upon Babylon. And since Babylon, which in Hebrew is called BABEL (בבל), means confusion, because there the speech of those building the tower was confounded, spiritually this world is understood, which is set in wickedness and confounds not only the tongues but also the works and the minds of each person. The king of this Babylon is the true Nebuchadnezzar, who grows proud against the Lord and says in his heart: I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of heaven I will set my throne; I will sit on the mountain of the Covenant, on the sides of the North; I will ascend above the height of the clouds; I will be like the Most High. He it is who showed the Lord all the kingdoms of the world and said to him: All these have been delivered to me, and I will give them to you, if you will fall down and worship me (Matt 4:9). Accordingly, in what follows the threat is in no way against Babylon, but against the whole world. The Lord Sabaoth commanded a most warlike nation to come from a distant land, from the height of heaven, to destroy the whole world. And again: Behold, the day comes of incurable fury and wrath, to make the whole world a desert and to destroy sinners out of it. And then: I will command evils for the whole world, and for the ungodly their sins. From these it is proved that everything said against Babylon pertains to the confusion and destruction of this world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.2-3":["And let the leaders enter the gates: I have commanded my sanctified ones, and have called my mighty ones in my wrath, exulting in my glory. The princes and giants of the Seventy translators Eusebius interprets as angelic powers and the worst demons, who were sent to overthrow Babylon. But we, following the order of the history, say that they are the Medes. Of them Scripture testifies more openly in what follows, saying: Behold, I will stir up against them the Medes, who will not seek silver, nor want gold. Nor is it surprising if he calls the Medes sanctified for the overthrow of Babylon, since through Jeremiah he called Nebuchadnezzar himself his servant and his dove, because in destroying transgressing Jerusalem he was serving His command. Further, when he says: My mighty ones, and those exulting in my glory, he shows that it was not by their own strength but by the wrath of God that they overthrew the power of so lofty a kingdom.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.2":["Upon a misty mountain lift up a signal, raise the voice, lift up the hand. The misty, or dark, mountain, which in Hebrew is called NESPHE (נשפה), understand as Babylon, on account of its pride. These are the dark mountains, bearing gloom and mist before them, of which Jeremiah says: Give glory to the Lord your God, before your feet stumble upon the dark mountains (Jer 13:16). And a command is given either to the angels or to any ministers whatever, that at God's bidding, and with the hand of the messengers raised, the captivity coming upon Babylon should be proclaimed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Upon a misty mountain, or a level one, lift up a sign. It is commanded to the Apostles, and to apostolic men and teachers of the Churches, that when they are about to fight against Babylon they should raise the sign of the Lord's cross, not in a low place and in sunken valleys, but on a misty or level mountain. Of these, the one signifies the hidden mysteries of the Church, to see which, and to hear the voice of God, Moses entered into the darkness and the mist. For God made darkness his hiding place, and cloud and mist round about him (Ps 17 [18]). The other teaches that we ought so to climb to the heights of the Church's doctrines as to be humbled, according to the Apostle Paul, and to say: I who am not worthy to be called an Apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God (1 Cor 15:9). He too raised the sign upon a level mountain when, with the humility implanted in him, he said: For I judged myself to know nothing among you except Jesus Christ, and him crucified. And this sign, in the passages above as well, was raised by him who arose from the root of Jesse among the nations, to gather the lost of Israel.\n\nExalt the voice, lift up the hand. For which the Seventy translated: Comfort with the hand. He exalts his voice who speaks of lofty things and despises present things as brief and transitory. He who hears from the same Isaiah: Go up upon a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion; lift up your voice in strength, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem, lifts up his hand, he who can say with David: The lifting up of my hands is an evening sacrifice (Ps 140 [141]:2); and who in every place lifts up holy hands, so that he not only lifts up his hands but also comforts with his hand: so that he does not say to the poor man, Come tomorrow and receive; but rather comforts his poverty and need with present mercy.\n\nAnd let the leaders enter the gates. LXX: Open, you princes. The leaders of the Church enter the gates of the mysteries of God and know the mysteries of the Scriptures, having the key of knowledge, so as to open them to the peoples entrusted to them. Hence it is commanded that the teachers should open and the disciples should enter.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.3":["I have commanded my sanctified ones. For which the LXX: I will command, and I will lead them. For he himself sanctifies his ministers, so that both he who sanctifies and those who are sanctified may all be of one. He who in another place also says to believers: Be holy, because I also am holy, himself commands and will lead his princes, so that they may do what has been commanded (Lev 19:2).\n\nAnd I have called my mighty ones in my wrath, exulting in my glory. LXX: Giants come to fulfill my fury, rejoicing together and doing outrage. According to the Hebrew, what is said clings to what precedes: that he who had given command to his sanctified ones himself called his mighty ones, exulting in his glory. But according to the Seventy, the giants who come to fulfill the fury of the Lord, rejoicing and glad at another's injury, are to be taken as the left-hand and hostile powers, of which we also read in the psalms: He sent upon them the fury of his wrath, fury and wrath and tribulation, a sending by evil angels (Ps 77 [78]:49). Of these is also the destroyer in Egypt, who does not dare to enter the doorposts smeared with the blood of the lamb (Exod 12), and that spirit who went out and stood in the sight of the Lord and said: I will deceive Ahab. And the Lord said to him: You will deceive him and you will prevail; go forth and do so (1 Kgs 22:21, 22). Hence in the same volume of Kings Micaiah said: I saw the Lord of Israel sitting upon his throne, and all the host of heaven stood about him on the right and on the left (ibid., 19). The right-hand powers are those of the angels who are sent for good things; the left-hand, those of the angels to whom we are handed over for punishment. Hence the Apostle too says: Whom I have delivered, he says, to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme (1 Tim 1:20). Now the name of the giants, for which in the Hebrew GEBORIM (גברים), that is, of the mighty, is set down, the Seventy and Theodotion turned into the likeness of the fables of the Gentiles, just as they also name the Sirens and the Titans and Arcturus, the Hyades and Orion, which among the Hebrews are called by other names. But if the giants are rebels against God, and all heresies, being contrary to the truth, rebel against God, all heretics are giants, who rejoice in their error and glory most of all when they have done outrage to the Church.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.4-5":["The sound of a multitude on the mountains, as of many peoples: the sound of the din of the kings of nations gathered together. The Lord of hosts has given command to the army of war, to those coming from a land far off, from the summit of heaven. The Lord and the vessels of his fury, to destroy the whole earth. The onslaught of the Medes and Persians is described: that, having gathered many auxiliaries with them, with their Lord going before the army, they come to lay Babylon waste, to destroy the whole earth; not that they laid waste the whole world, but the whole land of Babylon and of the Chaldeans. For it is an idiom of Holy Scripture that by the whole earth it signifies the whole of that province about which it is speaking; and some, not understanding this, drag the passage to the overthrow of all lands.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The Lord of hosts has commanded the army of war, coming from a far land, from the height of heaven: the Lord and the vessels of his fury, to destroy the whole earth. LXX: The Lord Sabaoth commanded a most warlike nation to come from a distant land, from the highest ÷ foundation ⁚ of heaven, the Lord and his warriors, to destroy the whole world. Foundation is to be marked beforehand with an obelus. In the volume of Kings and of Chronicles we read (2 Sam 24; 1 Chr 21) that the wrath of God was kindled against Israel when David numbered the people and offended the Lord. In the Psalms too it is written: O Lord, do not rebuke me in your fury, nor chasten me in your wrath, and other things like these, for which I have read in the Commentaries of a certain writer [he means Basil] that the wrath of God can be understood as a hostile power to which we are handed over for punishment, of which it is also sung in the eighth psalm: That you may destroy the enemy and the avenger (Ps 8:3). Those who come from a far land and from the height of heaven can be called warriors, namely also the ministering angels, who are to be sent at the consummation of the world to make bundles of the tares and, separated from the wheat, to prepare them for everlasting burning (Matt 13). The whole earth, too, is destroyed when earthly works are overthrown. There are also other warriors of the Lord, equipped with the armor of the Apostle, who daily in the Church, which is understood as the οἰκουμένη, that is, the world and the inhabited land, destroy and slay those who are lifted up against the knowledge of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.4":["The voice of a multitude on the mountains, as of crowded peoples; the voice of the sound of kings of nations gathered together. This passage is taken in three ways. First, that when the sign has been raised on the level mountain, the multitude of the nations comes, itself also set upon the mountains, and it is divided into two, namely into peoples and into kings, into disciples and teachers, of whom the Savior spoke in the Gospel: Many will come from the East and from the West, and will recline with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven (Matt 8:11). And it is called one voice, and harmonious, for this reason: that the Apostle's saying may be proved: That you all say the same thing, and that there be no schisms among you, but that you be perfect in the same mind and in the same judgment (1 Cor 1:10). Secondly, the pride of the heretics is described, who, believing themselves to be on the mountains, are lifted up against the knowledge of God, and speak iniquity on high, and set their mouth against heaven; they too have peoples, they have kings, who devour the deceived people. The third interpretation is this: since he had said above, giants come to fulfill my fury, rejoicing together and doing outrage, he describes the arrogance of the giants themselves, and their agreement, in the evil sense, in punishing those who have been handed over to them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.6-7":["Howl, for the day of the Lord is near; it will come like devastation from the Lord. Therefore all hands will be loosened, and every heart of man will melt and be crushed. Since the warriors of the Lord are to come to destroy the whole earth, howl and do penance, you for whom punishment is coming. For the day of the Lord is near, either of the consummation of the whole world and of the judgment, or of each one's departure from life. For there comes from the Lord a plundering, which we have called devastation in order to keep the metaphor, since he had spoken first of warriors. When the day of judgment or of falling asleep has come, all hands will be loosened, to which it is said in another place: Be strengthened, you loosened hands. And the hands will be loosened because no work worthy of the righteousness of God will be found: And no living person will be justified in his sight (Ps 142 [143]:2). Hence the Prophet says in the psalms: If you mark iniquities, O Lord, who will endure (Ps 129 [130]:3)? Every heart too, or soul of man, will melt, and will tremble at the consciousness of its sin. This is the fearful and terrible day of which Zephaniah also speaks: Fear before the face of the Lord, for the day of the Lord is near (Zeph 1:14). And again: Near is the great day of the Lord: near, and very swift. And Amos: Woe, he says, to those who desire the day of the Lord: why would you have the day of the Lord, which is darkness and not light (Amos 5:18)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.6":["Howl, for the day of the Lord is near: like a devastation it will come from the Lord. An apostrophe is made to the people of the Chaldeans, that they should make the coming evils resound with their weeping, and not doubt the ruin of their city, since the Lord is coming as its devastator.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.7-8":["Therefore all hands will be loosened, and every heart of man will waste away and be crushed. Gripings and pains will seize them: they will be in pain like a woman in labor: each will be astounded at his neighbor: their countenances are scorched faces. This needs no exposition; but it is briefly indicated that so great a weight of evils will press upon them that the hand of Babylon's fighting men will be loosened, and the heart will languish with fear, and pain will wring their bowels after the manner of women in labor, and each will seek the help of another, carrying about a face sallow with pallor; for it is natural that, when evils are imminent, we think others wiser than ourselves.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.8":["And what has been added according to the Seventy: the envoys will be troubled, and pains will seize them: we can interpret as those envoys whom the citizens had sent against him who had set out into a far country to seek a kingdom for himself (Luke 19:27). Hence he himself says in the Gospel: But those enemies who did not want me to reign over them, bring here and slay before me.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Twistings and pains will seize them; they will be in pain like a woman in labor; each one will be astonished at his neighbor; their faces are burnt countenances. When the warriors of the Lord have come from a distant land, and all hands have been loosened, and the heart is fearful and crushed, then strophomata, which we have translated as twistings of the belly, and pains will seize them, like the pains of women in labor. By this it is shown that they are tortured by their own conscience, and have their faces burnt with that fire which they had kindled for themselves: because they cannot say: The light of your countenance has been signed upon us, O Lord (Ps 4:7); and: We all, with unveiled face, beholding the glory of the Lord, are transformed into the same image (2 Cor 3:18). Each one will be astonished at his neighbor, seeing him in the same torments by which he himself is tormented.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.9":["Behold, the day of the Lord comes, cruel and full of indignation, and of wrath and of fury, to make the land a wilderness, and to crush its sinners out of it. He calls the day cruel, not by its own desert, but by that of the people. For he is not cruel who cuts the throats of the cruel; but he seems cruel to those who suffer. For even the robber hanging on the gibbet thinks the judge cruel. And at the same time the desolation and devastation of the Babylonian land is announced, and the cause of the devastation is set forth: that all these things come about because of its inhabitants.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Behold, the day of the Lord comes, cruel and full of indignation, and of wrath and fury, to make the land a wilderness and to crush its sinners out of it. For land the Seventy translated τὴν οἰκουμένην, that is, the world; for cruel, incurable. For when the day either of judgment or of death has come, that will be fulfilled which is read in the sixth psalm: But in hell who will confess to you (Ps 6:6)? For that is not a time of penance, but of punishments. And in another place it is said: How great is the multitude of your sweetness, O Lord, which you have hidden for those who fear you! Therefore the great mercy of God is hidden from those who are still set in fear and do not have the perfect love of God, so that, while they hear of the day of the Lord as cruel and incurable and full of indignation and fury, they may cease to sin. The world, or the land, will also be made a wilderness, and the sinners will be crushed out of it, the land which before was weighed down by the burden of sinners, so that, crimes having been blotted out and destroyed, righteousness alone may dwell and reign on the earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But the day of retribution for all sins is that of which all the prophets write, and above all Isaiah cries aloud: Behold, the incurable day of the Lord is coming, of fury and of wrath, to make the whole world a desert, and to destroy the sinners out of it. This day is rightly called incurable, because when the day of judgment has come there will be no place for repentance; and compared with eternity it is near and not far off.\n— Commentary on Joel"],"ISA.13.10":["For the stars of heaven and their splendor will not spread out their light: the sun has been darkened in its rising, and the moon will not shine with its light. The Hebrew word CHISILE (כסילי) the Seventy translated as ὠρίωνα. The Hebrew whom I used as my teacher interpreted it as Arcturus. We, following Symmachus, have used the general word star. And the sense is this: when the cruel day of the Lord has come and his fury has laid all things waste, through the greatness of their fear everything grows dark for mortals, and the sun itself and the moon and the glowing stars seem to deny their brightness. Hence also heaven is clothed in sackcloth, namely because darkness covers all things, and, under the pressure of evils, men perceive nothing else but what the mind compels them to see.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For the stars of heaven and their splendor will not spread forth their light; the sun is darkened in its rising, and the moon will not shine with its light. LXX: For the stars of heaven and Orion, and all the adornment of heaven, will not give their light: and they will be darkened when the sun rises, and the moon will not give its light. Where we have translated their splendor, that is, beyond doubt, of the stars, Aquila and Theodotion set down the very Hebrew word CHISILEEM (כסיליהם): for which the Seventy translated Orion, adding of their own and all the adornment of heaven, which is to be marked beforehand with an obelus. Now the fables of the Gentiles say that Orion has twenty-two stars, of which four are of the third magnitude, nine of the fourth, and again another nine of the fifth, and by others they are called Bootes. We read also in Job of the Hyades, and Vesper, and Arcturus, and the treasuries, or the inner parts of the South (Job 9:9), of which we must speak in their own place. Nor ought we to think that among the Hebrews these stars are called by these names which the Greek and Latin tongues sound forth, but that they have names of their own. For just as God called the light, day; and the firmament, heaven; and the dry land, earth; and the gatherings of the waters, seas (Gen 1); so too he called the several stars by their own names, whose proper meaning our language does not express. It is written of God in another place: Who numbers the multitude of the stars, and calls them all by names. When therefore the day of the Lord has come to make the whole world a wilderness and to remove the sinners utterly from it (Ps 146 [147]:4), then, in comparison with the divine majesty, the stars of heaven and all their splendor will withdraw their light. Nor is it surprising to say this of the lesser stars, since even the sun is darkened in its rising, and the moon does not have its accustomed splendor. But what the Seventy translated, that the stars and Orion and all the adornment of heaven are darkened when the sun rises, has nothing of a sign or of a wonder; for this happens at all times, that when the sun has risen the stars that are in the heaven do not appear. Nor is it surprising that he said this of the sun, since even when the moon is full and shining the whole night most of the stars do not shine. And that there are stars in the heaven even by day is proved by an eclipse of the sun: when it has been obscured by the shadow of the earth, as the philosophers argue, and by the disk of the moon set in its way, the stars are seen more clearly in the heaven.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.11":["And I will visit evils upon the world, and against the wicked their iniquity: and I will make the pride of the unbelievers cease, and will humble the arrogance of the mighty. From this place and from the one above, where it is written that the sun is darkened in its rising, and the moon is filled with gloom, and the stars withdraw their brightness, and the iniquity of the whole world is visited, some think that what is foretold is not the ruin of Babylon but the consummation of the world; whereas, surely, in keeping with what precedes, by the world as well, which in Hebrew is called THEBEL (תבל), and in Greek οἰκουμένη, Babylon is to be understood. For οἰκουμένη in our language means inhabited; and Babylon is called inhabited because of the vast multitude of its people: so that where there had formerly been a countless throng of peoples, there should be desolation and a dwelling of beasts.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And I will visit upon the evils of the world, and against the ungodly their iniquities. When God visits and strikes, he strikes for this purpose, that he may amend. Accordingly, when he is vehemently angry with those who do not do penance: I will not visit, he says, upon your daughters when they have committed fornication, nor upon your daughters-in-law when they have committed adultery (Hos 4:14). On the contrary, of those who are to believe in Christ it is said (Ps 88 [89]:33): I will visit their iniquities with a rod, and their sins with scourges: but my mercy I will not take away from them.\n\nAnd I will make the pride of the unbelievers to rest, and I will humble the arrogance of the mighty. LXX: And I will destroy the insolence of the wicked, and I will humble the insolence of the proud. The Hebrew word GAON (גאון), which plainly sounds arrogance and pride, Theodotion and the Seventy always translated insolence, expressing the sense rather than the word, because every proud man is ready for insolence. Therefore the Lord visits upon the evils of the world, and against the ungodly their iniquities, for this reason: that he may curb the arrogance of the proud and humble the pride of the mighty. For God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble (1 Pet 5). And in Proverbs we read: Before ruin the heart of a man is lifted up, and before glory it is humbled (Prov 16:18). Ruin always follows pride, and glory humility: For he who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted (Luke 14). We read in another place: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the prudent I will reject (1 Cor 1:19). Not that true wisdom and true prudence are destroyed by the Lord, but the knowledge falsely so called: those who treasure up for themselves a lying tongue, and fail searching with searching, and find nothing, speaking iniquity on high and setting their mouth against heaven. Hence the pride of heretics is properly called insolence against the truth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.12":["A man will be more precious than gold, and a human being than pure fine gold. The cause is plain why God visits the οἰκουμένην, that is, the evils of Babylon: so that, deserted by its inhabitants, it may be reduced to a wilderness. Now, everything that is rare is called precious; just as above, too, according to the history, we read that because of the scarcity of men seven women take hold of one man, saying: We will eat our own bread, and be clothed with our own garments; only let your name be called upon us; take away our reproach (Isa 4:1). And in the volume of Samuel it is contained: The word of the Lord was precious in those days (1 Sam 3:1), that is, rare. Note that in the Hebrew PHAZ (פז) is written for gold in general, and OPHIR (אופיר) for fine gold.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","A man will be more precious than gold, and a human being than pure fine gold. LXX: And those who have been left will be more honored than refined gold, and a man will be more honorable than the stone of Sophir. At the consummation of the world, when the world has been reduced to a wilderness, and the sun darkened in its rising, and the moon has not given its splendor, such signs and portents will be wrought by the Antichrist that, as iniquity increases, the love of many will grow cold, so as to deceive, if it be possible, even the elect of God (Matt 24). Then a man will be more precious than gold, which in Hebrew is called PHAZ (פז), and Aquila translated κιῤῥὸν, which is of the finest and blood-red color; and a human being than the purest fine gold, which in Hebrew is called OPHIR (אופיר), and Aquila translated σπήλωμα ὀφεὶρ, which the Seventy, intending I know not what, translated stone of Sophir. Now it is a place of India in which the best gold is produced; as we read in Genesis of the river Phison: This is it which encircles the whole land of Evila, where there is gold; and the gold of that land is the best: and there is the carbuncle and the green stone (Gen 2:11). And more precious is understood as meaning rarer. For everything that is rare is precious, as we also read in the volume of Samuel: And the word of the Lord was precious in Israel (1 Sam 3:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.13":["Upon this I will trouble heaven, and the earth will be moved from its place, because of the indignation of the Lord of hosts, and because of the day of the wrath of his fury. Either take it in the sense in which we expounded above concerning the stars, and the sun, and the moon, and the world; or ὑπερβολικῶς, that because of the indignation of God both heaven is sad and the earth is moved, and all the elements too acknowledge the wrath of the Creator.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Upon this I will shake the heaven, and the earth will be moved from its place. LXX: For the heaven will rage, and the earth will be moved from its foundations. When a man has become more precious than gold, and a human being than pure fine gold, and that has been fulfilled which is written: Do you think, when the Son of Man comes, he will find faith on the earth (Luke 18:8)? then both the heavens and the earth will be moved. For heaven and earth will pass away, not by their own will and by their own choice, as many suppose, believing them to be living beings; but because of the indignation of the Lord of hosts, and because of the day of his fury, who looks upon the earth and makes it tremble. Hence what is said in the Seventy, for the heaven will rage, is to be taken μετωνυμικῶς of those who dwell in heaven, just as if we were to say: the whole city cried out, and the entire town went out to meet the judge. And of the foundations of the earth we read in Proverbs too: God by wisdom founded the earth (Prov 3:19). And the Lord says to Job: Where were you when I was founding the earth (Job 38:4)? Not that the earth has foundations laid down as a mass in the deep; but the will and power of God, by which all things are held together, is to be called its foundations. For he himself founded it upon the seas, and established it upon the rivers, and he himself hung it upon nothing (Ps 23 [24]; Job 26).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.14-15":["And it will be like a fleeing fawn, and like a flock of sheep, and there will be none to gather them: they will turn each to his own people, and each will flee to his own land. Everyone who is found will be slain, and everyone who comes upon them will fall by the sword. Their infants will be dashed in pieces before their eyes; their houses will be plundered, and their wives will be ravished. When heaven and earth have been shaken, the devil, the crooked serpent, will flee, or every doctrine contrary to the truth, which is divided into teachers and disciples: the former of whom, because of their ὀξυδέρκειαν, are called in the Greek tongue Dorcades, that is, roe-deer; the followers are like brute animals, and wandering hither and thither they have none to direct them: for they have lost him of whom it is written: The steps of a man are directed by the Lord (Prov 20:24). And these, freed from their most evil teachers, will turn to their own people, and each will flee to his own land, from which they sprang: but whoever is found will be slain, or have his throat cut. And this happens not only at the consummation of the world, but to this very day in the Church: when the teachers have been overcome, the deceived flock returns to its people and to its own land; and in that it is found, it is slain, so that it ceases to be heretical, and whoever comes upon them will fall by the spiritual sword.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.14":["And it will be like a little doe fleeing, and like a sheep, and there will be none to gather them. He means the Babylonian and Chaldean people: that at the irruption of the Medes and Persians it will flee as fearfully as a little doe and a sheep at the roaring of a lion and the howling of a wolf; and it has no defender or prince whose command it may follow.\n\nEach will turn to his own people, and every one will flee to his own land. After Babylon has been taken and the enemy army has entered through its gates, all the auxiliaries and the troops of the various nations by which the city had formerly been defended will go back to their own provinces.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.15":["Everyone who is found will be slain: and everyone who comes upon them will fall by the sword. He who has not fled will be struck down by the blade; and he who has chosen either to resist or to come back will be of so little use to the captured city that he too will shed his own blood.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.16":["Their infants will be dashed to pieces before their eyes: their houses will be plundered, and their wives will be violated. This is what David prophesies in the spirit: Wretched daughter of Babylon, blessed is he who will repay you your recompense, which you have paid out to us. Blessed is he who will take and dash your little ones against the rock (Ps 136 [137]:8). So great will be the devastation of the city and the savagery of the victors that not even innocent age will be spared, that all the wealth of the houses will be plundered, and the chastity of wives will be violated before their husbands' eyes.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Then their infants and little ones, who have not yet come to the full age of error, they will dash in pieces before the eyes of the teachers, and will plunder their houses and ravish their wives, depraved wisdom and perverse doctrine. Hence for fathers of this sort, whose little ones are to be slain, we ought to pray and say: Give them, O Lord. What will you give them? A barren womb and dry breasts (Hos 9:14). For they have travailed with iniquity, they have conceived sorrow, and they have brought forth unrighteousness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.17-18":["Behold, I will stir up against them the Medes, who do not seek silver nor desire gold: but with arrows they kill the little ones, and on the sucklings of the womb they will have no pity, and their eye will not spare their sons. We find in Genesis (Gen 10) that Madai was the founder of the nation of the Medes, which means measure, or from the mighty one, or from the strong one. Therefore against the Babylonians, who are confused in mind, ministers of strength are stirred up by the mighty and strong God to punish, that they may render to each according to his works. For with the measure with which they have measured, it will be measured back to them. They will return into their bosom a measure shaken down and pressed together and full and running over (Matt 7, and Luke 6). They will receive neither silver nor gold, that is, neither beauty of eloquence nor sharpness of wit, in which they formerly applauded themselves; but they will afflict with punishments those handed over to them; and they will have no pity on the little ones and the sucklings of the womb, so that they may rather show pity. And would that we too might be stirred up by the Lord, and that this power might be given to us, that we should desire neither the silver nor the gold of worldly eloquence and wisdom, but should kill the sons of the heretics, and of all who have been deceived, with spiritual arrows, that is, with the testimonies of the Scriptures: and that those who are nourished with the milk of error may be slaughtered without any mercy, so that they may perish by a merciful cruelty, and that we may have pity on no infancy, and may be worthy of that beatitude: Blessed is he who will take hold of his little ones and dash them against the rock!\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.17":["Behold, I will stir up against them the Medes, who will not seek silver, nor want gold. What lay hidden has been made plain: that the mighty ones and the giants are by no means to be understood as angels and demons, but as the nation of the Medes, whose prince Darius was the first to destroy the empire of the Babylonians, having slain Belshazzar, the grandson of Nebuchadnezzar, who was the son of Evil-merodach (Dan 5). And he describes the savagery of the Medes and Persians as so great that, in their ardor to shed blood, they despise gold and silver and count the riches offered to them as mud.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.18":["But with arrows they will kill the little ones, and they will have no pity on the sucklings of the womb, and their eye will not spare the sons. The little ones will be killed, whose wounds will be no smaller than their bodies; and the wombs of pregnant women will be cut open, and the infants dashed out, and the cruel victor will kill the sons pressed to the breasts.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.19-20":["And Babylon, that glorious one among kingdoms, renowned in the pride of the Chaldeans, will be as when the Lord overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah: it will not be inhabited unto the end, and it will not be founded unto generation and generation. We have heard of the Medes, we have heard of Babylon, we have heard of her who is renowned in the pride of the Chaldeans: and we refuse to understand what was, and seek to hear what was not. And we say this, not because we condemn the tropological understanding, but because the spiritual interpretation ought to follow the order of the history; and many, ignorant of this, wander about in the Scriptures in a frenzied error. To this present day, then, the prophecy concerning Babylon is being fulfilled: and just as God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah, so this city too, once overthrown, will not be inhabited forever. For in its place they made Seleucia and Ctesiphon the renowned cities of the Persians.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.19-22":["And Babylon, that glorious one among kingdoms, renowned in the pride of the Chaldeans, will be as when God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah. It will not be inhabited to the end, and it will not be founded unto generation and generation: nor will the Arab pitch his tents there, nor will shepherds rest there: but wild beasts will rest there, and their houses will be filled with dragons: and ostriches will dwell there, and hairy ones will dance there; and owls will answer there in its dwellings, and sirens in the shrines of pleasure. Its time is near to come, and its days will not be prolonged. LXX: And Babylon, which is called renowned, the glorious one of the king of the Chaldeans, will be as when God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah; it will not be inhabited forever, nor will anyone enter it for many generations; nor will Arabs pass through it, nor will shepherds rest in it; but wild beasts will rest there, and the houses will be filled with noise, and sirens will lie down there, and demons will dance there, and onocentaurs will dwell there; and hedgehogs will make their dens in their houses. It will come quickly and will not delay. What follows: And its days will not be drawn out, ⁚ is to be marked with asterisks, because it was added from Theodotion. When the Medes have been stirred up against the city of confusion, who count neither gold nor silver among good things, who kill the little ones with arrows and have no pity on the sucklings of the womb, and whose eye does not spare their sons, the city once renowned, which was in the pride of the Chaldeans, who mean as demons, will be overthrown just as Sodom and Gomorrah were overthrown, so that no trace of its former dwelling remains in it: but after there has been a new heaven and a new earth, and the form of this world has passed away (1 Cor 7), the city of confusion will not be inhabited forever, and will by no means recover the same state. For after it has ceased to be in its former glory, the Arab, who means western and of the evening, will not pitch his tents there, so as to desire to dwell in that which he has seen to be deserted. Now the Arab in the present passage is taken in the good sense, because he always strains toward the end and, forgetting what lies behind, stretches himself out to what lies before. Nor will the shepherds rest there, who are accustomed to feed the most gentle flock of the Lord in Jerusalem; but on the contrary, wild beasts will rest there, which the Psalmist detests, saying: Do not deliver to the beasts the soul that confesses to you (Ps 73 [74]:19). For these Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion set down the Hebrew word itself, SIIM (ציים). And their houses, that is, those of the Babylonians, will be filled, according to the Seventy and Theodotion, ἤχου, that is, with noise and clamors; according to Aquila, with typhoons, which we have turned into dragons; according to Symmachus, with OIIM (אחים), the word contained in the Hebrew. And ostriches will dwell there, an animal that always seeks out solitudes, of which more is written in Job: which, though it seems to have wings, yet is not lifted high above the earth: for which the Seventy translated σειρῆνας, that is, sirens. The hairy ones, who in Hebrew are called SIRIM (שעירים), Theodotion translated as with hair erect and standing up; Symmachus and the Seventy translated demons. For the onocentaurs too, which the Seventy alone translated, imitating the fables of the Gentiles, who say that there were hippocentaurs, the three other translators set down the Hebrew word itself, IIM (איים), which we have turned into owls. And where the Seventy said, hedgehogs will make their dens in their houses, and in the Hebrew THANNIM (תנים) is set down, Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated sirens, signifying either some kind of beasts, or demons who, according to the error of the Gentiles, sing sweetly and deceive men who cannot pass through the shipwreck of this world with stopped ears. Moreover the name onocentaur, compounded of asses and centaurs, seems to me to signify those who in part have some human understanding, and again are drawn away into vices by pleasures and the mire of baseness. It is said, therefore, according to tropology, that at the end of the world, or of each one's death, all the glory and pride of the Chaldeans and the confusion of the world will depart from us; and that all things will be overthrown as God overthrew Sodom and Gomorrah; and that the state of this world will be no more, but will perish forever. Nor will the Arab pitch his tents there, of whom it is said in the sixty-seventh psalm: Make a way for him who rides upon the setting of the sun; the Lord is his name (Ps 67 [68]:5). Nor will the shepherds rest there, namely the angels who preside over the human race and daily see the face of the Father: but wild beasts and dragons and ostriches and hairy ones and owls and sirens will rest there, all of which beasts we understand figuratively as angels, or demons, and those to whom we are handed over for punishment. And in the houses once of pleasure, where there was gladness and joy, there will be the wailing of owls and the lamentable voice of the sirens, which leads its hearers to death. That the time of judgment is imminent, and that the day of the consummation of Babylon is not prolonged, he will by no means wonder who sees Babylon daily overthrown through holy men in those who believe in the Lord, and who sees that every length of time, compared with eternity, is near. Of the kinds of beasts, or rather of monsters, we have spoken briefly, because in the former book they were in part touched upon according to the history. This too is to be considered: that after the Church's preaching and the teaching of the Savior have overthrown the city of confusion, so that it is compared to Sodom and Gomorrah, it is not inhabited by holy men, nor do the shepherds rest in it who were accustomed to feed the flock of Christ: but on the contrary, wild beasts and dragons and ostriches rest there, and hairy ones dance in it. For whatever the heretics say in the synagogues of Satan is not the teaching of the Lord, but the howling of demons and of hairy ones, whom Esau imitated. And sirens will rest in the shrines of pleasure, who with sweet and deadly song draw souls down into the deep, so that, as the shipwreck rages, they may be devoured by wolves and dogs. Near, therefore, and pressing daily is the time of the ruin of the heretics, and their overthrow is not deferred.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.20-22":["Nor will the Arab pitch his tents there; nor will shepherds rest there: but beasts will be there, their houses will be filled with dragons, and ostriches will dwell there, and hairy ones will dance there. And owls will answer in its buildings, and Sirens in the shrines of pleasure. Babylon will be so devastated and deserted that it will not be useful even for the pasturing of herds and flocks. For the Arab and the Saracen will not pitch their tents there, nor will shepherds, weary with toil after the tracks of their flocks, rest there; but among the broken walls and the narrow places of the ancient ruins SIIM (ציים) will dwell: which the Seventy alone translated as beasts; the others kept the very name that is written among the Hebrews, wishing kinds of demons or phantoms to be understood. And their houses, he says, will be filled, as we have said, with dragons; as Aquila translated, with typhoons; as Symmachus, OHIM (אחים), expressing the Hebrew word itself; but the Seventy and Theodotion interpreted it as cries or sounds. And as for what follows: Hairy ones will dance there, they understand either incubi, or satyrs, or certain wild men of the woods, whom some call the fig-tree Fauns, or kinds of demons. For owls, too, all kept the Hebrew word itself, IIM (איים); the Seventy alone translated onocentaurs. And the Sirens are called THENNIM (תנים), which we shall interpret either as demons, or as certain monsters, or at any rate as great dragons, which are crested and fly. By all these things the signs of devastation and solitude are shown: that the depopulation of the once most mighty city is so great that, because of the multitude of demons and beasts, none of the shepherds, that is, of those who seek out the desert, dares to enter it. We have learned from a certain brother, an Elamite, who came out of those regions and now lives the life of the monks at Jerusalem, that there are royal hunting grounds in Babylon, and that beasts of every kind are kept enclosed merely by the circuit of its walls.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.13.22":["Its time is near to come, and its days will not be prolonged. He says that the time is at hand when it is to be taken by its enemies. For after the captivity of Jerusalem, with no long time in between, it too was laid waste by the Medes and Persians.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.1-2":["The stranger will be joined to them, and will cleave to the house of Jacob, and peoples will take them, and bring them into their own place, and the house of Israel will possess them upon the land of the Lord as menservants and maidservants: and they will take captive those who had taken them captive, and they will subdue their taskmasters. We conjecture that many from various nations came to Jerusalem with the people of the Jews, believing in the God of Israel and abandoning the idols of their errors. And that they were brought back by the king's letters, and received gifts and the expenses of the Temple, there is no doubt. The understanding seems to be hemmed in only on this point: how Israel possessed, in the land of the Lord, those who had once been its conquerors, and subdued its taskmasters, and held them as menservants and maidservants. Unless perhaps synecdochically we understand the whole from the part: that they afterwards enjoyed such prosperity that they acquired for themselves households of menservants and maidservants from the various nations round about as well. It can also be understood of the times of Ahasuerus (Esth 9; Jdt 13), when, after Holofernes had been killed, the enemy army was cut down by Israel. That I cling to the letter and, in the manner of the serpent, eat earth, is your will, who have wished to hear only the historical interpretation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.1-4":["For the Lord will have mercy on Jacob, and will yet choose out of Israel, and will make them rest upon their own soil: the stranger will be joined to them, and will cleave to the house of Jacob. And the peoples will take them and bring them to their place: and the house of Israel will possess them upon the land of the Lord as menservants and maidservants; and they will take captive those who had taken them captive, and they will subdue their oppressors. And it will be in that day, when God has given you rest from your labor, and from your trouble, and from the hard bondage in which you served before: you will take up this parable against the king of Babylon, and you will say. For stranger, that is, proselyte, the Seventy translated γηώραν, which in Hebrew is called GER (גר). Hence I think that the son of Moses too, who was a stranger in the land, was named by his father Gersam (Exod 1). Therefore georas is a Hebrew word declined after Greek custom: although someone ignorant of the Hebrew tongue tries to express a Greek etymology in it, from the fact that it has the care of an earthly thing: for γῆ, he says, is the word for earth, and ὤρα, φροντὶς, that is, care. Again, for MASAL (משל), which Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated parable, and whom we too have followed, the Seventy translated lament. Now the order is this: after Babylon has been deserted forever, so as to be a dwelling of wild beasts, the Lord will have mercy on Jacob, namely on him who supplanted vices; and he will choose Israel, him who beholds God with the mind. And note the propriety of the words: on Jacob, who is still set in the wrestling, he will have mercy; but to Israel, who received the name after the victory, not mercy but election is fitted. He will also make them rest upon their own land, of which the Savior speaks: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land (Matt 5:4), and I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living (Ps 26 [27]:13). There will also be joined to the house of Jacob those who were chosen through the Apostles, the multitude of the nations; and they will take the remnant of the house of Israel and bring them to their own place, so that they may dwell in their tents; and the house of Israel will possess them upon the land of the Lord as menservants and maidservants. For it profits the wicked to serve the good. Hence it is said to Esau too: You shall serve your brother. And to Jacob: Your brother shall serve you (Gen 27). And they will take those who had formerly deceived them with sophistries: and they will reduce to bondage their oppressors, who do all things for the sake of gain. And after Jacob and Israel have rested from their labor, in which they had labored much fighting against the world and the heretics, and from their trouble and bondage, in which they had formerly served a malignant interpretation and false doctrines, then they will take up a lament and parable against the king of Babylon, namely the discourse of heretical error and confusion, and will say what follows. The Jews interpret this passage carnally, but they will not be able to show that it came to pass after they returned from Babylon. For neither did the Babylonians, who had first taken them captive, serve them; nor, when the Babylonians were conquered, did they possess their houses; nor did they have them as menservants and maidservants. It remains, therefore, that according to their fables they think this will come about in respect of this Roman empire: that when the Romans have been overcome in the last time, the nations whom they formerly served will serve them. But if, by following the letter, they deceive themselves with a false hope, who will grant them that Rome is called Babylon, and Nebuchadnezzar the king of the Roman empire?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.1":["For the Lord will have mercy on Jacob, and will yet choose from Israel, and will make them rest upon their own soil. Of this Zephaniah speaks more fully: Praise, daughter of Zion; shout for joy, Israel; be glad and exult with all your heart, daughter of Jerusalem: for the Lord has taken away your judgment: he has turned away your enemies (Zeph 3:14-15). And it signifies the time when Cyrus king of the Persians permitted the captive people to return to Jerusalem. Read the book of Ezra (Ezra 1), Haggai (Hag 1) and Zechariah (Zech 1): when, under Zerubbabel, and Joshua the high priest, and Ezra, and Nehemiah, the altar, the Temple, and the walls of the city were built.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.3-4":["And it will come to pass in that day, when the Lord has given you rest from your labor, and from your turmoil, and from your hard servitude, because you served before: you will take up this parable against the king of Babylon, and you will say. The discourse is directed to Israel: that after it has returned to Jerusalem and shaken off the yoke of servitude, it should recall the former power of Nebuchadnezzar and the Babylonian summit, and lament him with a pitiful voice, because he has come to such calamity that he seems worthy of pity even to his enemies.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.4-7":["How has the taskmaster ceased, the tribute come to rest? The Lord has broken the staff of the wicked, the rod of the rulers, that struck the peoples in indignation, that subdued the nations in fury with an incurable blow, that persecuted cruelly: it has come to rest and fallen silent: the whole earth has rejoiced and exulted. This is the voice of Israel lamenting and pitying: how one who was once a lord and conqueror of the lands, despoiling everything with tributes, has been crushed and brought to nothing. He, I say, who was the staff of the wicked, who as sceptre and royal rod struck all in his fury, whose blow could not be endured, who cruelly pursued even those who fled: how he has come to rest and been humbled, and at his ruin the whole earth has fallen silent, uttering only the voice of exultant joy!\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.4":["How has the oppressor ceased, the tribute come to rest? The Lord has broken the staff of the ungodly, the rod of the rulers, which struck the peoples in indignation with an incurable stroke, which subdued the nations in fury, persecuting cruelly. In the Epistle of Peter we read: It is time for judgment to begin at the house of God (1 Pet 4:17). And in Ezekiel it is said to the tormentors: Begin at my holy ones (Ezek 9:6). For the order of judgment is arranged according to the diversity of sin, so that those who have offended less are purged first. And the last enemy to be destroyed is death. Therefore, when Israel has been freed from hard bondage, he will take up a parable against the king of Babylon, which we ought to understand as a parable. For if the discourse is against Nebuchadnezzar, and a simple exposition of the history, how is it called a parable, when it παραβάλλεται, that is, is compared, to nothing else? Israel therefore wonders how the oppressor has ceased, who was accustomed to exact payment down to the last farthing. And he was accustomed to exact from none but debtors, who say in the Lord's Prayer: Forgive us our debts, as we also forgive our debtors (Matt 6:12). To this exactor debtors are handed over by the judge, who sends them into prison and exacts down to the least sin. Accordingly Paul the apostle too handed over to the exactor the Corinthian fornicator, who had taken his father's wife, and also Phygellus and Hermogenes (1 Cor 5; 2 Tim 1). And of these exactors it is also said above: My people, your oppressors plunder you; and those who demand payment rule over you. For tribute Aquila translated famine. For since the bridegroom has been taken from us, we suffer a famine of the word of God and fast continually from the body of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.5-6":["And for this reason the rod and staff of the ungodly, for which the Seventy translated yoke, strikes us, or oppresses us, because we would not bear the lightest yoke, the yoke of the Savior. And with this staff and this rod he struck the nations with incurable fury, and cruelly pursued those who fled: not wishing to amend those handed over to him, but to kill them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.7-8":["The whole earth has come to rest and fallen silent; it has rejoiced and exulted; the fir trees too have rejoiced over you, and the cedars of Lebanon. Since you have fallen asleep, no one comes up to cut us down. Hell beneath was troubled: to meet your coming it stirred up the giants for you. All the princes of the earth have risen from their thrones; all the princes of the nations, all of them will answer and say to you: You too have been wounded as we were, you have become like us? Your pride has been dragged down to hell, your corpse has fallen; beneath you the moth will be spread, decay, and worms will be your covering. At the destruction of the ungodly king, who struck the nations with an incurable stroke and pursued them cruelly, the whole earth came to rest and fell silent, which before was full of tumults and sedition, so much so that the fir trees and the cedars of Lebanon rejoiced, of which it is written in the psalms: You brought a vine out of Egypt, you cast out the nations and planted it. Its shade covered the mountains, and its branches the cedars of God (Ps 79 [80]:9). These cedars of God, because at one time they sinned, the Lord handed over to punishments to be broken. Hence in another psalm it is said: The Lord will break the cedars of Lebanon (Ps 28 [29]:5). And the fir trees ought to be taken as all the lofty, those who are sublime in the service of the Lord, who said with harmonious voice: Since you have fallen asleep. Note that even the death of death is called a sleeping. No one comes up to cut us down. For when the strong man has been overcome and his vessels plundered, the rest of his attendants also have fallen. Hence the Lord too says in the Gospel (Matt 25:41): Depart into the eternal fire, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels. How many have these fellers of timber and trees cut down with their axes and made to fall?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.8":["The fir trees too have rejoiced over you, and the cedars of Lebanon: Since you fell asleep, no one has come up to cut us down. By the fir trees and cedars of Lebanon understand the princes of the nations, who were cut down when Nebuchadnezzar struck them; and they too, breaking out into a voice of joy, say: Since you were led down to the underworld, no one else could be found to cut down the great and the mighty.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.9-10":["Hell too, troubled, or turned to bitterness, to meet the coming of the true Nebuchadnezzar, is the place of punishments and torments, in which the rich man clothed in purple is seen: to which the Lord also descended, to release the bound from prison. We can also call hell, coming to meet him, that angel who is set over the punishments of hell, who stirred up all the giants: for which some have translated Raphaim, others Titans. And they are called giants according to the custom of the heathen, who suppose them earth-born, whom the earth begot. But we, according to the etymology of the Greek word, can call giants those who have served earthly works. Then there follows: All the princes of the earth have risen from their thrones, at the coming of their former prince: so that him whom they formerly honored for his power they might afterward marvel at in punishments, and say: You too have been wounded as we also were: you have become like us. These words have this sense: We thought that we, because of our weakness, had not been able to resist the power of God, and that you alone were the one who would remain in your loftiness; but, as the facts show, you too have been wounded and taken, and have become like us; so that those whom rank separated on earth, punishment unites in hell.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.9":["The underworld beneath you was thrown into confusion, at the meeting of your coming it roused up giants for you: all the princes of the earth have risen from their thrones: all the princes of the nations, every one, will answer and say to you. These things are to be read emphatically and in the manner of a stage scene; not because they happened, but because they could have happened; unless perhaps we believe that the souls of the kings whom he had killed came to meet the Babylonian king, mocking him. For it is a solace in evils when men see their enemies suffer the same things:\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.10-11":["And you too have been wounded as we were, you have become like us: your pride has been dragged down to the underworld: there has fallen your corpse; beneath you the moth will be spread, and worms will be your covering. The speech of the mighty ones and princes of the earth, whom he had called cedars and fir trees above, is addressed to the Babylonian king, now placed in the underworld. We no longer grieve that we were cut down, since you too have fallen by the same axe. All your power, and your pride lifted up to heaven, has been dragged down to the earth. So will your corpse too feed the moth, and a multitude of swarming worms cover it? Through death you will feel the worthlessness of man, you who displayed the power of God in yourself.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.11":["Your pride has been dragged down to hell, or your glory, and your corpse has fallen, or, as the Seventy translated, your great gladness, in which you formerly rejoiced when the nations were subject to you. Beneath you decay will be spread, and worms will be your covering. This is the rest of the devil, this the bed of the tempter, who was lifted up into such pride that he dared even to tempt the Lord, saying: All these I will give you, if you will fall down and worship me (Matt 4:9). The decay and the moth and the covering of worms are understood either as the eternal punishments which one's own conscience begets, or as the material of torments, which is born from one's own sins. For just as, so long as there is matter in a corpse and any moisture in the corpse, worms are born from decay, so from the very matter of sins torments are begotten. Hence the Apostle Paul too, death having been slain, to which the prophetic word spoke through Hosea: I will be your death, O death; I will be your bite, O hell (Hos 13:14), says to it: Where is your contention, O death? Where is your sting, O death (1 Cor 15:55)? For when it is dead, there is no one to cut us down: for there will by no means be sin that must be cut down, since the sting of death is sin.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.12-14":["How you have fallen from heaven, Lucifer, who rose in the morning: you have crashed to the earth, you who wounded the nations: who said in your heart: I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of God I will exalt my throne, I will sit on the mountain of the covenant, in the sides of the North; I will ascend above the height of the clouds, I will be like the Most High. In place of what we have translated, for ease of understanding, How you have fallen from heaven, Lucifer, who rose in the morning, in the Hebrew, to render it word for word, one reads: How you have fallen from heaven, howl, son of the dawn. Now in other words Lucifer is meant; and he is told that he ought to weep and mourn, he who was once so glorious that he was compared to the brightness of the morning star. Just as, he says, the morning star, scattering the darkness, shines forth burning and ruddy, so your progress too among the peoples and in public seemed like a bright star; but you have fallen to the earth, you wounder of nations, who in your pride said: I have attained such power that heaven remains for me, and the stars, so that the things on high must lie beneath my feet. The Jews, however, would have heaven and the stars of God understood of themselves, on the strength of what follows: I will sit on the mountain of the covenant, that is, in the Temple, where the ordinances of God are laid up, and in the sides of the North, that is, in Jerusalem. For it is written: The mountains of Zion, the sides of the North (Ps 47 [48]:3). Nor had it been enough for his pride to desire heavenly things, unless he had burst out into such madness as to claim for himself the likeness of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.12":["How have you fallen from heaven, Lucifer, who rose in the morning? You have crashed to the earth, you who wounded the nations? LXX: How has Lucifer fallen from heaven, who rose in the morning? He who sent to all the nations has been crushed to the earth. For Lucifer, who in Hebrew is called ELIL (הילל), Aquila translated howling son of the dawn. For truly he ought to howl and wail, who because of his pride was cast headlong from heaven to earth and crushed. Hence the Savior too says to the disciples: I saw Satan falling like lightning from heaven (Luke 10:18). Not, I see now; but I saw before, when he fell. And if he fell from so great a height because of pride, you too ought not to glory because the demons are subject to you, but because your names are written in heaven; so that whence he fell through pride, you may ascend through humility. This is the prince of the world, who rose in the morning among the other stars, and by his own fault was made from Lucifer into Vesper, and not rising but setting: who wounded the nations, or who sent his attendants to the nations, to deceive all by his fraud. These are the false apostles, deceitful workers, who transform themselves into Apostles of Christ, who sow darnel over the good seed while the teachers of the Churches sleep and are unwilling or unable to resist their malice. And Jacob, and Israel, on whom the Lord has had mercy and whom he has chosen, still says these things to the devil, or, as the Seventy will have it, about the devil, that is, not in the second person but in the third.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.13-14":["Whence did the devil fall? because he committed theft? because he committed murder? because he committed adultery? These indeed are evils: but the devil did not fall on account of these, but he fell on account of his tongue. For what did he say? 'I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of heaven I will set my throne, and I will be like the Most High.' So then let not monks suppose that they ought to be secure, and say, Meanwhile we do not commit great crimes in the monastery: I do not commit adultery, I do not commit theft, I do not commit murder, I do not commit parricide, and the rest of the great vices. In the tongue too there are great vices.\n— Homily 41, on Psalm 119"],"ISA.14.13":["You who said in your heart: I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of God I will exalt my throne: I will sit on the mountain of the covenant, on the sides of the North: I will ascend above the height of the clouds, I will be like the Most High. He said these things either before he fell from heaven or after he had fallen from heaven. If he was still set in heaven, how does he say, I will ascend into heaven (Ps 113 [115]:16)? But because we read: The heaven of heaven is the Lord's, when he was in heaven, that is, in the firmament, he desired to ascend into the heaven where the throne of the Lord is, not by humility but by pride. But if he speaks these words after he fell from heaven, we ought to understand arrogance: that even when cast down he does not rest, but still promises himself great things, to be not among the stars but above the stars of God. And while the Lord says to the Apostles: It is enough for the disciple to be as his master, and for the servant as his lord (Matt 10:25), and says to the Father: Holy Father, grant that as I and you are one, so these also may be one in us (John 17:11), this one arrogates so much to himself that he boasts he will set his throne above the stars of heaven, which have not fallen. And what he adds: I will sit on the mountain of the covenant, on the sides of the North, for which the Seventy translated, on a lofty mountain, above the high mountains which are toward the North, let us refer to what is said in Jeremiah: From the North evils will blaze forth upon all the inhabitants of the earth (Jer 1:14). And that the pot set on fire is kindled from the face of the North. This is the North, the harshest wind, from which the Lord wishes to free his captives and bring them back into the holy city, saying: I will say to the North, Bring; and to the South-west wind, Do not hold back; bring my sons from a distant land (below, Isa 43:6). But that which can seem contrary to this, the mountains of Zion, the sides of the North, the city of the great king, is easily resolved. For those who once were in the sides of the North, most hardened, afterward through penance began to be in the city of God. Always a Lucifer of this sort seeks to ascend above those who bear the image of the heavenly, and who shine in the Church like the stars of God: and to sit on the mountain of the pact or covenant, that is, in the Church, which is set on high and possesses inhabitants who were once from the sides of the North.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","It must also be said another way: the north wind is a very harsh wind, which makes very hard the hearts of those who dwell in its land, and from which evils are kindled upon all who dwell on the earth, and toward which, according to this very prophet Zechariah, the black horses go out, to take their stand in its region. He too who in Isaiah had said in his boasting: I will ascend into heaven, above the stars of heaven I will set my throne, I will sit on a lofty mountain, above the high mountains in the north, vaunts that he holds his kingdom in the coldest place of the earth.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ISA.14.14":["He has so forgotten himself that he wishes to ascend above the clouds, which the Lord commanded not to rain rain upon Israel, and to which the truth of the Lord reaches, to whom Obadiah also says: Though you be exalted like the eagle, and set your nest among the stars of heaven, thence I will drag you down, says the Lord (Obad 4). Unhappy Judas, who like a cloud was sent with the other Apostles to rain rain upon Israel, and was like a star among the other stars, to whom the Lord said: Let your light shine before men (Matt 5:16), by his own fault received the devil as his rider, who, to complete his words of pride, dared even to say this: I will be like the Most High, so that, as Christ has his Prophets and Apostles, so I too may have false apostles. All these things are to be referred to the heretics, who, though they are below, boast with their prince that they are on high.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.15":["But you will be dragged down to the underworld, into the depth of the pit. He who in his pride had said: I will ascend into heaven, I will be like the Most High, is dragged down not only to the underworld but to the lowest underworld. For this is what the depth of the pit signifies, in place of which we read in the Gospel of the outer darkness, where there is weeping and gnashing of teeth (Luke 13:28).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But you will be dragged down to hell, into the depth of the pit. LXX: But now you will go down into hell, and to the foundations of the earth. You will not go down to hell of your own accord, for this belongs to the Lord the Savior, to free the bound from hell, but you will be dragged down to hell against your will, so that you who could have climbed to the heights through virtues are dragged down to punishments through vices. The saints have the wings of an eagle and the wings of a dove, and can say: I will fly away and be at rest (Ps 54 [55]). But the ungodly, like the Egyptians, sank like lead in the mighty waters, and sank into the depth like a stone (Exod 15). Hence also iniquity, or, as it is better in the Hebrew, ungodliness, is seen sitting upon a talent of lead (Zech 5). Therefore what we read elsewhere: Everyone who exalts himself will be humbled (Luke 18:14), the king of confusion also suffered, so that he was dragged down to the foundations of the earth, or, as it is more truly in the Hebrew, into the depth of the pit. Of the foundations of the earth it is written in Deuteronomy: A fire has been kindled in my fury, it will burn down to the lowest hell. It will devour the earth and its foundations (Deut 32:22), namely those who are earthly. Of the pit too, that by it the depths of hell are signified, these are the testimonies: I am counted with those who go down into the pit. And: They have laid me in the lowest pit (Ps 87 [88]:5, 7). For just as a pit receives the waters that run down into it, so hell receives souls: into which pit Benaiah went down in the time of snow and cold and slew a lion in it (2 Sam 23, and 1 Chr 11). Hence the heretics too forsake the Lord, the fountain of living water, and dig for themselves broken pits which cannot hold water. Of these pits, which do not have the fervor of the Holy Spirit, and are not like the prophet Jeremiah, who according to the Seventy translators said: I found warm water in the desert (Jer 2), but, as love grew cold, have lost the warmth of the Holy Spirit: that is the pit of which the same Jeremiah speaks: As a pit cools water, so wickedness cools those who have it (Jer 6:7). Would that they, according to the Apocalypse of John, were either hot or cold (Rev 3), that is, either believed or did not believe at all, lest through lukewarmness and the pretense of faith they be cast out by the Lord!\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.16-17":["Those who see you will bend down toward you and gaze at you (understand: saying): Is this the man who troubled the earth, who shook kingdoms, who made the world a desert and destroyed its cities; who did not open the prison to his captives? This too is the voice of those who mock and marvel, how the devastator of all has himself been devastated. And when he says: Who did not open the prison to his captives?, the greatness of his cruelty and impiety is expressed: that he kept even men in chains in prison, and chains were not enough for the wretches unless the horror of darkness also shut them in.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.16":["Those who see you will bow down to you, and will look closely at you. Is this the man who troubled the earth? who shook kingdoms? who made the world a desert, and destroyed its cities, and did not open the prison to its prisoners? Or, as the Seventy translated: did not release those who were being led away? Those who see Nebuchadnezzar, the Babylonian king, fallen from heaven to hell, with the gaze not of the eyes but of the heart, will bow down in the humility which is contrary to pride, and will look closely at him, saying these things: Is not this the man, or the human being, who troubled, or stirred up the earth? And the sense is this: he who had said, I will be like the Most High, and boasted himself God, is proved to be a man, of whom it is said in the ninth psalm, which is properly against the devil: Arise, O Lord, let not man prevail (Ps 9:20). And in the Gospel: An enemy man sowed tares over it (Matt 13:25). Hence we read it said to him and his companions: I said, You are gods, and all sons of the Most High. But you will die like men, and you will fall like one of the princes (Ps 81 [82]:6). And to the same, under the figure of the prince of Tyre, it is said: Because your heart has been exalted, and you have said, I am God, I have dwelt in the dwelling of God in the heart of the sea: yet you are a man and not God, and you have set your heart as the heart of God (Ezek 28:2). This man, this human being, troubled the whole earth, namely those who hear with Adam: Earth you are, and into earth you shall go (Gen 3:19), and shook kingdoms, or kings, whose heart is in the hand of the Lord (Prov 21). He shook them, it says, he did not overthrow them. Hence one of those who had been shaken, and yet had not fallen, said: But my feet were almost moved (Ps 72 [73]:2). And the Apostle tells believers to take up the armor of God and stand against the snares of the devil (Eph 6). But the house that is founded upon the rock is shaken by no storm (Matt 7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.17":["There follows: Who made the world a desert, or, as according to the Hebrew all the others besides the Seventy translated, as a desert. For by vices and sins he made the world, which in Hebrew is called THEBEL (תבל), a desert, so that it had no virtue, or was subject to the same vices with which the solitude of the nations is filled. He also destroyed the cities of that same world, to make synagogues of the devil out of the Churches of Christ, and to pollute the purity of the true faith with heretical filth. But also to its prisoners, that is, the world's, he did not open the prison. We were all prisoners, and were held shut up in prison, to which prisoners the Savior said, come out, and to those who were in darkness, be uncovered. For the Lord sets free those in fetters. And those freed by him give thanks, saying: You have broken my bonds (Ps 115 [116]:17; Jer 2, Prov 5). For each one is bound fast with the cords of his own sins: and these cords and bonds the Apostles too can loose, imitating their master, who had said to them: Whatever you loose upon earth will be loosed also in heaven (Matt 18). And the Apostles loose them by the word of God, and by the testimonies of the Scriptures, and by exhortation to the virtues.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.18-20":["All the kings of the nations, every one, have slept in glory, each man in his own house. But you have been cast out of your tomb like a useless shoot, defiled and wrapped up with those who were slain by the sword, and went down to the foundations of the pit; like a rotting corpse you will have no fellowship, not even with them in burial. The Hebrews tell a fable of this kind. Evil-merodach, who had reigned earlier while his father Nebuchadnezzar lived for seven years among the beasts, was kept in chains together with Jehoiachin king of Judah, after his father had been restored to his kingdom, until his father's death; and when his father died and he succeeded to the kingdom again, and was not accepted by the princes, who feared that the man said to be dead might be alive, then, to give proof that his father was dead, he opened the tomb and dragged out his corpse with a hook and ropes. And the sense is: While all who were killed by you are buried, you alone will lie unburied. Others, however, interpret this place thus: All souls in the underworld will receive some rest; you alone will be bound fast in the outermost darkness. For you will be covered with the blood of all, and the gore of everyone will weigh upon you, as one wrapped in the putrid matter of the dead. Symmachus translated this place thus: Not even with those who are slain in war will you deserve to share in burial. And for what we have said, like a useless shoot, where in the Hebrew one reads CHANESER NETHAB (כנצר נתעב), Aquila translated like defiled putrid matter. Now NESER (נצר) properly denotes a sucker that springs up at the roots of trees and is cut off by farmers as useless; we can understand this very thing as putrid matter and corruption. And at the same time we learn that the underworld is beneath the earth, since Scripture says: To the foundations of the pit.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.18":["All the kings of the nations, all of them, have slept in glory, each one in his own house. But you have been cast out of your tomb like corrupt matter: polluted, wrapped up with the slain and those pierced through by the sword, who go down to the stones of the pit. LXX: All the kings of the nations have slept in honor, a man in his own house. But you will be cast out on the mountains like an untimely birth, abominable, and wrapped up with those slain by the sword who go down to the foundations of the earth. The kings of the nations, to whom, according to the Song of Deuteronomy, God had entrusted the nations to be governed (Deut 32), who were shaken by Nebuchadnezzar and yet not overthrown, in whom sin by no means reigns; but because their heart was in the hand of God, they did not fall at all. For indeed the Savior, just as he is called God of gods, so too is he Lord of lords and King of kings. These kings, then, all of them, have rested in their glory, each one in his own house. For with the Father there are diverse mansions (John 14): and according to the variety of the virtues, differences of dwellings.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.19-20":["Like a putrid corpse, you will not have fellowship with them even in burial. For you have destroyed your land, you have slain your people. LXX: As a garment mixed with blood will not be clean, so neither will you be clean, because you have destroyed my land and have slain my people. Since the Hebrew and the edition of the Seventy differ greatly from each other, let us discuss each separately. Like a putrid corpse, or, as Aquila translated, trampled, you will not have fellowship of burial, not even with those whom you slew. For you are the teacher, they were the disciples: and from him to whom more has been entrusted, more is required. For you have destroyed your land, you have killed your people, namely those who were entrusted to you to govern. Hence he dares to say to the Savior: All these have been delivered to me, and I will give them to you, if you will fall down and worship me (Matt 4:9). And that the corpse of the devil is putrid because of the greatness of his sins, he will not be able to doubt who reads that sin is most foul-smelling, the sinner himself saying: My scars have putrefied and are corrupted because of my foolishness (Ps 37 [38]:6). On the contrary, virtue is of a good odor: hence the love of spiritual brethren is also compared to ointment, which runs down upon the beard, the beard of Aaron, and upon the border of his garment (Ps 132 [133]). And the bridegroom says to the bride: Your odor is sweet, and your face is comely (Song 2:14). And how the corpse of the devil is spiritually trampled, that saying of the Apostle teaches us: God will crush Satan under your feet swiftly (Rom 16:20). And the words of the Lord, who says: I will blot them out like the mud of the streets (Ps 17 [18]:43). This one, because he has destroyed the land entrusted to him, and slaughtered the people committed to him, by no means keeping them alive for God but preparing them as companions of his burial, therefore the seed of the most wicked will not be, nor will it be named, forever. But according to the Seventy it has this sense: O Lucifer, who rose in the morning, who, when you had the works of virtue and of light, were the garment of God, and of whom it could be said: Clothed with light as with a garment (Ps 103 [104]:2): because you have slain many with the sword who went down to hell, and have been polluted with their blood, you will by no means be called the garment of God, but a garment mixed with gore, not stained and polluted, so that you might seem to have something clean, but wholly bloodied with blood. And this is to be noted, that it is said to the devil: as a garment mixed with blood will not be clean, so neither will you be clean. Where then are those who grant the devil penance, and say that he can be made clean? Nor do we on that account at once concede the heresy which asserts that there are diverse natures, and that there is one which never receives healing. For this garment, with which God was once clothed, is not unclean of itself, and was not so made by God; but because it is mixed with blood, and has wholly polluted itself by its own fault, and with evils added to it, it will not be clean. And it will not be clean for this reason: because it has destroyed the land of the Lord and slain his people, destroying and slaying the land of Judea and the land of confession and all the saints: and therefore it will not remain forever. Hence also in the Gospel it is said: Depart into the eternal fire, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels (Matt 25:41). And just as the saint is the garment of God, and a new garment, and is clothed with the tunic of salvation and of gladness, saying: Let my soul exult in the Lord: for he has clothed me with the garment of salvation, and has surrounded me with the tunic of gladness (Isa 61:10), so on the contrary the sinner, who bears the image of the old and earthly man, deserves to hear: Behold, all of you will grow old like a garment, and the moth will devour you (Isa 50:9). But he who has advanced in wickedness, and has been unwilling to cleanse his oldness by newness, will be compared not to an old garment but to the rag of a menstruous woman, saying: We have gone astray, and we have all become unclean; all our righteousness is like the rag of a menstruous woman.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.19":["But Nebuchadnezzar has been cast out of his tomb, and not even in death has he rested from torments. And he has been cast out like corrupt matter, which in Hebrew is called NESER (נצר), which Aquila translated ἰχῶρα, namely putrefaction and filth, which Symmachus translated ἔκτρωμα, that is, an untimely birth, the Seventy a dead thing, Theodotion a shoot: although this very word above (Isa 11:1), where we read of Christ: A rod will come forth from the root of Jesse, and a flower will rise up from its root, the Seventy translated flower, Theodotion shoot, Aquila ἀκρέμονα, that is, sucker. Properly, however, Neser means sucker, which grows at the roots of trees and, torn off by farmers, is thrown away as useless; hence we too translated useless stock. Therefore Nebuchadnezzar, like a useless stock prepared for burning, or polluted like corrupt matter, and wrapped up with those who were slain by him with the sword and went down to the foundations of the pit, will be dragged down into the depth, and will not have fellowship in burial even with those whom he slew. For the punishment of the author of an offense is one thing, and that of him who was compelled by the author another. This is the sword by which very many have been wounded and slain, of which we read in the seventh psalm: Unless you are converted, he will brandish his sword; he has bent his bow and made it ready. And in it he has prepared the instruments of death: he has made his arrows for those who burn (Ps 7:13, 14), namely for those who have received into their heart the fiery darts of the devil. But according to the Seventy, the corpse of Nebuchadnezzar, polluted and pierced through, will lie with very many slain on the mountains of pride, and will be led down to hell. And Symmachus translated foundations of the pit in place of stones of the pit, to signify in other words the depth and the Tartarus of hell.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.20-21":["The seed of the most wicked will not be named forever. Prepare his sons for slaughter in the iniquity of their fathers: they will not rise up, nor inherit the land, nor fill the face of the world with cities. All the histories agree that, after Belshazzar the grandson of Nebuchadnezzar was slain and Darius succeeded to the kingdom of the Chaldeans, no one of Nebuchadnezzar's stock reigned thereafter. This, then, is what Scripture foretells: that Babylon will come to such devastation that not even of the royal seed will anyone remain, but on account of the father's impiety all his offspring will be wiped out. And for what we translated in the last part of the sentence, nor fill the face of the world with cities, there is written in the Hebrew, for cities, ARIM (ערים), which we can also translate as adversaries; so that the sense is: no one of your seed will be raised up as an adversary who desires to restore the kingdom. According to the Seventy interpreters, who said: Most wicked seed, prepare your sons for slaughter because of the sins of your father, what sense it has I am unable to know.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Seed of the most wicked, prepare your sons for slaughter for the iniquities of their fathers: they will not rise up nor inherit the land, nor fill the face of the world with cities. And I will rise up against them, says the Lord of hosts: and I will destroy the name of Babylon and its remnant, and its offshoot and offspring, says the Lord of hosts. LXX: Most wicked seed, prepare your sons for slaughter for the sin of your father: that they may not rise up and possess the land, and fill the land with cities. And I will rise up against them, says the Lord Sabaoth: and I will destroy their name and remnant and seed. For most wicked seed, which the Seventy translated, in the Hebrew is written ZERA MRIM (זרע מרעים), which the others translated seed of the most wicked. Not that the seed itself is evil of itself: for God made all things good (Gen 1); but that from those who are most wicked by their own will an evil seed has sprung, which comes about by will, not by nature. Hence we read in Daniel too: Seed of Canaan, and not of Judah (Dan 13:56). And of good sons it is said by the Apostle: In Christ Jesus through the Gospel I have begotten you (1 Cor 4:15). And in the Gospel: As many as received him, to them he gave power to become sons of God (John 1:12). For everyone who commits sin is of the devil. To this seed, therefore, it is commanded that it prepare for slaughter its sons, namely all most wicked thoughts and evil works, which were born of ungodly fathers, who beyond doubt signify the hostile powers. These most wicked sons are slaughtered in the iniquities of their fathers for this reason: that they may no longer rise up and possess the land, which is to be possessed and filled by the saints, so that cities of the Lord may be built in it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.20":["For you have destroyed your own land, you have slain your own people. According to the Seventy interpreters, who said: Because you have destroyed my land, and slain my people, there is no doubt what they mean. For Nebuchadnezzar slew and destroyed the land of Judea and its people Israel. According to the Hebrew the understanding is difficult: how he destroyed his own land and slew his own people; unless perhaps it is to be taken in this sense: those whom God had given you for chastening, you have utterly destroyed. Or otherwise: the ancient kingdom of the Assyrians, through your great pride and your lifting up your neck against God, has been utterly destroyed. For had you behaved humbly and known your own measure, the Assyrian and the Babylonian would be reigning to this day. Or in this way: you were so cruel toward foreigners that in your fury you oppressed even the peoples subject to you.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.21-22":["And since the victory of men is not perfect: For unless the Lord guards the city, those who guard it will keep watch in vain (Ps 126 [127]:1): therefore the Lord himself will rise up against the sons of the most wicked, and will destroy out of confusion their name and remnant, and every offshoot and offspring, so that they may no longer sprout in the cities of the Lord. We read in the Gospel (John 8) that the devil is a liar from the beginning, and his father, that is, of the lie; which many not understanding wish the father of the devil to be the dragon who reigns in the sea, whom the Hebrews call Leviathan. And they think this suits the present passage according to the Seventy translators, who said: for the sins of your father: whereas in the Hebrew ABOTHAM (אבתם) plainly signifies not of your father but of their fathers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.22-23":["And I will rise up against them, says the Lord of hosts: and I will destroy the name of Babylon, and the remnant, and the offshoot, and the progeny, says the Lord. And I will make it a possession of the hedgehog, and pools of water: and I will sweep it with a broom, grinding it away, says the Lord of hosts. That Babylon was most mighty, and that, set foursquare on the plains, it extended sixteen thousand paces from corner to corner of the wall, that is, sixty-four thousand in all around its circuit, is reported by Herodotus and many others who have written Greek histories. And its citadel, that is, the Capitol of that city, is the tower which was built after the flood, and is said to reach four thousand paces in height, narrowing little by little from broad to narrow, so that the overhanging weight may be borne more easily by the broader parts. They describe marble temples there, golden statues, streets gleaming with stones and gold, and many other things that seem almost incredible. We have recounted all this to show that before the wrath of God all power is dust, and is to be compared to embers and ashes. If it were permitted to go into the barbarian nations and see the last traces of so great a city, we would see the possession of the hedgehog and the pools of water, and that what is now sung by the voice of Isaiah has truly been fulfilled: I will sweep it with a broom, grinding it away; for apart from the walls of baked brick, which are repaired after very many years in order to shut in the beasts, the whole space in between is a wilderness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.23":["And I will make it a possession of the hedgehog, and pools of waters: and I will sweep it with a broom, grinding it, says the Lord of hosts. LXX: And I will make Babylonia desert, says the Lord, so that hedgehogs dwell in it, and it will come to nothing. And I will make it a gulf of mud, for destruction. When the Lord of hosts has destroyed the name of Babylon, and its remnant, and its offshoot, and all its offspring, he will not be content with its destruction unless he gives it as a possession to the hedgehog, and as pools of waters, and sweeps it, not lightly and casually, but grinding, so that nothing of its old filth remains in it. In the Acts of the Apostles it is written (Acts 10 and 11) that in that linen vessel which, let down by four corners, hung from heaven, all kinds of four-footed beasts and creeping things and birds were contained; which the Apostle afterward explaining says: God has shown me that I should call no man unclean. The characters of men, therefore, were shown in the various animals, just as the Pharisees and Sadducees are called a brood of vipers because of their wickedness, and Herod is called a fox because of his deceits (Luke 3 and 13); and the luxurious and those prone to pleasure are called horses, mad after women (Jer 5): and, Do not become like the horse and the mule, in which there is no understanding (Ps 31 [32]). On the contrary, the innocent are called doves and sheep. Therefore, according to the teaching of the Lord the Savior, who called the cares of this world and the seduction of riches thorns, the hedgehog seems to me to be he who, according to the Apostle, rejoices in the uncertainty of riches (1 Tim 6), and trusts that he is armed, not with the armor of God, but with the thorns and sins of this world, to whom that word of the Gospel is most rightly said: Fool, this night your soul will be taken from you; and what you have prepared, whose will it be (Luke 12:20)? Such inhabitants has Babylon, deserted of virtues, where there is no well-watered field to bear the fruits of various seeds, but barren pools, slimy and muddy, in which animals that delight in mire creep. For this reason the most merciful Lord swept it, grinding most vehemently, and as with a kind of drag-net cleansed it down to the bare ground, so that the Babylonian seeds might perish and it might be inhabited by hedgehogs alone. When we see anyone sunk in the mud of riches, and, as the Seventy translated, in the gulf, that is, in a deep whirlpool, and surrounded as it were by a miry pool, let us not hesitate to call him a hedgehog, an inhabitant of deserted Babylon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.24-25":["The Lord of hosts has sworn, saying: If it is not so, that as I have thought, so it will be, and as I have pondered in my mind, so it will come to pass, that I will crush the Assyrian in my land, and tread him down upon my mountains: and his yoke will be taken from them, and his burden will be removed from their shoulder. He returns to the present, that is, to Sennacherib king of the Assyrians, who laid waste Samaria and Judea and, Jerusalem excepted, destroyed everything round about; and he joins things near at hand to things that are to come long afterward, so that the fear hanging over them may be removed: because those who heard could say: We are enduring a siege now, and this man promises things that will come many ages later. This, then, is the order of the prophecy: although Babylon is to be destroyed after very many years, and the whole seedbed of the stock of the Assyrians and of Chaldea is to be utterly destroyed, nevertheless, so that you may not fear the captivity close at hand, the Lord swears (who is to be believed even when he does not swear) that his judgment is not mistaken, and that what he has conceived in his mind will not be made void. And he speaks this with human feeling, namely that he who cannot be deceived is not deceived: I will crush, he says, the Assyrian in my land, and upon my mountains I will tread him down. For in one night one hundred and eighty-five thousand of the army of the Assyrians were destroyed, the Angel laying them waste (2 Kgs 19). And from those who were besieged in Jerusalem his yoke will be taken away, and the heavy dominion that hung over all, and the weight by which they were pressed down, will rather be thrust back upon himself. For when the king of the Assyrians fled into his own land, Hezekiah went out free with the remnant of the people.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The Lord of hosts has sworn, saying: Surely as I have thought, so shall it be, and as I have purposed in my mind, so shall it come to pass: that I will crush the Assyrian in my land, and on my mountains I will trample him. And his yoke will be taken from them, and his burden will be removed from their shoulder. After the hedgehog has dwelt in deserted Babylon, and in place of well-watered and fertile fields pools have covered everything, so that nothing of the former seed and abundance remains in Babylon, the oath of the Lord will be fulfilled, and what he purposed in his mind will come to pass: that the Assyrian is crushed in his land and trampled on his mountains. For the reckless enemy hastens to seize not only the land of God and all its lower places, but also those who in the service of God have so advanced in virtues that they are compared to mountains, and it is said of them: Its foundations are on the holy mountains (Ps 86 [87]:1). And note the propriety. In the land he is crushed; on the mountains of God he is trampled by the Lord himself. For when all enemies have been put under the feet of Christ, so that the last, death, is destroyed, then the most heavy yoke of the Assyrian, with which he had formerly weighed them down, will be taken from the saints, and the burden, or, as the Seventy translated, κῦδος, that is, glory, will be removed from their shoulder, so that, the yoke of the Assyrians having been taken away, they may see rest, that it is good, and the land, that it is most fruitful, and may put their necks under the yoke of Christ for labor, and become men who till the soil. Hence also Issachar, which means there is a reward, received his name from the virtues. And in the Prophet we read: There is a reward for those who serve the Lord (Jer 31:16); and in another place: Behold the Lord, and his reward is with him (Isa 40:10), who will render to each according to his work. And that the Assyrians, that is, the hostile powers, are crushed and trampled, that word of the Gospel also teaches: Behold, I have given you power to tread upon serpents and scorpions, and upon all the power of the enemy (Luke 10:19). And the words of the Apostle bear witness: God will crush Satan under your feet swiftly (Rom 16:20).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.26-27":["This is the counsel that I have devised upon the whole earth, and this is the hand stretched out upon all the nations. For the Lord of hosts has decreed, and who will be able to weaken it? And his hand is stretched out, and who will turn it back? In this place some think that the prophecy is general, against the whole world, and that the devastation of Babylon and of the Assyrian city is a type of the consummation of the world. We by no means contradict them, so long as we know that here the whole land of the Assyrians is properly signified, and that all the nations allied with the Assyrian king are pointed out. Whatever has been decreed by the Lord is prevented by no one's power. And his hand, stretched out and ready to strike, no one will be able to hold back.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.26":["This is the counsel which he has purposed upon the whole earth, and this is the hand stretched out upon all the nations. For the Lord of hosts has decreed, and who will be able to weaken it? And his hand is stretched out, and who will turn it back? For the whole earth the Seventy translated the whole world, at the end of the burden, or vision, against Babylon. What lay hidden is opened: that God purposed his counsel upon the whole earth, that is, the whole world, and not only against the land of the Chaldeans and the Assyrian and Chaldean king; and that his hand is stretched out, or raised, upon all the nations, and not upon the one Babylonian nation. From this it is shown that all the things which have been said pertain not specifically to one province, but generally to the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.27":["And what is said: Who will be able to weaken it, and who will turn it back? we ought to take not as of something difficult, as that is read: Who is wise, and will understand these things (Ps 106 [107]:43)? And: Who, do you think, is the faithful and prudent steward (Luke 12:42)? and other things like these; but as of something impossible. For no one will be able to weaken the counsel of the Lord, and to turn back his hand, stretched out or lifted on high, from striking.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.28":["In the year in which King Ahaz died, this burden came. We read that four kings of Judea are placed at the head of the Prophet's title: Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah, each succeeding the other in order and by descent; of whom we read above that Uzziah had died, as Isaiah relates: In the year in which King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up. From this we understand that both the Vision of the Lord seated and the things enjoined upon the Prophet, up to the place where it is written (Isa 6): Like a terebinth and like an oak that spreads out its branches: the holy seed will be its shoot, were prophesied under King Jotham (ibid., 7). The third to succeed is Ahaz, son of Jotham, son of Uzziah king of Judah, under whom Rezin king of Syria and Pekah son of Remaliah, king of Israel, went up to Jerusalem to make war against it, and the rest that Scripture records. We know, therefore, that all that is placed in between, up to the present place which we are now striving to expound, was prophesied under Ahaz; and when he died, the fourth, that is, the last, Hezekiah, succeeded, under whom the whole book is woven to the end.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In the year in which King Ahaz died, this burden came. Do not rejoice, all you Philistia, because the rod of him who struck you has been broken. For out of the root of the serpent will come forth a basilisk, and its seed swallowing the bird. And the firstborn of the poor will be fed, and the poor will rest confidently, and I will make your root perish with famine, and I will slay your remnant. LXX: In the year in which King Ahaz died this word came: Do not rejoice, all you foreigners, for the yoke of him who struck you has been broken. For out of the seed of the serpent will come forth the offspring of asps, and out of their offspring will come forth winged serpents: and the poor will be fed through him, and poor men will rest in peace. But he will slay your seed with famine, and will kill your remnant. When Ahaz, who is interpreted κατάσχεσις, that is, holding, or possession, whom we read was a most ungodly king, had died, this weight, or word, comes upon the Philistines. For while he lived and reigned among sinners, neither a weight against the foreigners nor the word of God could come to the Prophet. Indeed I remember having said this above too, when, Uzziah having died, Isaiah saw the Lord sitting upon a throne high and lifted up. And in Exodus we read (Exod 2:23) that after the king of Egypt died, the children of Israel groaned from their works, and cried out, and their cry went up from their works, and the Lord heard their groaning. For unless what was said were mystical, they ought rather to have cried out while he was reigning, when they were slaving at mud and bricks.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.29":["Do not rejoice, all you Philistia, because the rod of him who struck you has been broken: for out of the root of the serpent will come forth a regulus, and its seed swallowing the bird. What we say is natural: when the king of a hostile nation dies, his enemies always rejoice, expecting from the new state of things civil wars and seditions and inexperience in ruling. When Ahaz the sinner died, therefore, who had walked in the ways of the kings of Israel and had been joined to them by kinship, we understand that the Philistines, from close by, rejoiced and mocked Israel, because, having lost a king of mature years, they were subject to the young Hezekiah. Now by Philistines, as we said above, he means the Palestinians, whom the common edition writes as foreigners, although this is the term not for one nation but for all foreign nations. Do not, he says, rejoice, O Philistia, do not mock my people because the rod of Ahaz who struck you has been broken; because the staff which used to strike you seems crushed by death; because the serpent has been slain. For in his place a more harmful one will be born, Hezekiah the regulus, which the Greeks call the basilisk, who will kill you with his gaze and slay you with the breath of his mouth. For just as no bird can pass the gaze of the regulus unharmed, but, however far off it may be, is sucked into its mouth, so you too will perish wholly at the sight of King Hezekiah. And he has kept the metaphor beautifully: for since he had named a serpent and a regulus, he says that birds are devoured by its mouth and breath. And that none of the kings of Judah struck the Philistines as Hezekiah ravaged them, hear the book of Kings: He struck the Philistines as far as Gaza, and as far as its borders: from the tower of the watchmen to the fortified cities (2 Kgs 18:8). And for what we have translated swallowing the bird, where in the Hebrew is written SARAPH MOPHETH (שרף מעופת), it can be interpreted a flying serpent: so that the sense is: from the root of the serpent a regulus will be born, and its fruit, that is, the regulus's, a flying serpent, so that you understand a flying dragon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","It is commanded, therefore, to the Philistines, whom they now call Palestinians, and whom the Seventy always translate ἀλλόφυλοι, that is, foreigners; and it is said to them that they should not rejoice nor be glad because they have broken the rod, or the yoke, of him who struck them (Heb 12:6). For whom the Lord loves he rebukes, and he chastises every son whom he receives. And God instructs us as sons when he visits our iniquities with a rod and our sins with scourges, so as not to take away his mercy from us (Ps 88 [89]). This is the rod of which it is written in another Psalm: Your rod and your staff, they have comforted me (Ps 22 [23]:4). This is the rod, and this the yoke, which the Savior wishes to lay upon the necks of all, so that, having put off the yoke of Nebuchadnezzar, they may bear the yoke of Christ. But if anyone throws it off and breaks it, at once out of the seed or root of the serpent, who is the crooked serpent, will come forth the basilisk and asps, and out of the asps will go forth flying serpents, or those that swallow birds. For when the yoke of God has been thrown off and the discipline of the Lord broken, first the seed of the serpent takes root in our thoughts; secondly, from the most evil seed is born the basilisk, which is the king of serpents and is said to kill men by its breath and its look; or the offspring of asps, of which it is said in the Psalm: The poison of asps is under their lips (Ps 13 [14]:3; and 139 [140]:4), which are the various sins, and they burst forth from the most evil treasury of thoughts into evil works. And when they have reigned in foreign men, who are estranged from God, at once winged serpents come forth; so that it is not enough for them to have thought and done what is evil, unless they also seek a defense of their evil works and fashion various heresies. I think the winged serpents are those who exalt and lift themselves up against the knowledge of God and set their mouth against heaven: or those who swallow birds, that is, who with poisoned mouth devour in their jaws all who desire to fly and rise to the heights. And this, for the present, concerning the wicked.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.30":["And the firstborn of the poor will feed, and the poor will rest in confidence. When the regulus has struck you, and the flying dragon has laid waste your borders, you will by no means lie in wait for Judah, nor terrify my poor little people with your treachery; but, hard pressed by your own straits, you will weep over your calamity. And the humble and the poor, who trusted not in riches and in power but in my name, will rest in secure peace, and will dread the onslaught of no enemy.\n\nAnd I will make your root perish by famine, and I will slay your remnant. He speaks entirely in figures. And the sense is that, while the people of God rest in confidence, the root of the Philistines is dried up, and all its remnant will be consumed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But those who have not broken the rod and yoke of him who struck them, but have submitted their neck to the Lord, and are poor in spirit, will be fed, and will say: The Lord feeds me, and I shall lack nothing (Ps 22 [23]:1). And they will hear from the Lord: In most rich pastures I will feed them (Ezek 34; John 10); and they will go in and go out and find pasture. And the poor will be fed through him who strikes them in order to amend them: and they will rest in peace, or, with a careful shepherd, they will live confidently and will rest with Lazarus in the bosom of Abraham (Luke 16). But those who have broken the yoke and rod of him who struck them will suffer perpetual famine, so that they are not nourished by the word of God: but all their remnant will perish, lest anything sprout from the evil seed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.31-32":["Howl, O gate; cry out, O city: all Philistia is laid low. For smoke comes from the North, and there is none who escapes its column. And what will be answered to the messengers of the nation? That the Lord has founded Zion, and in him the poor of his people will hope. For messengers, that is, Angels, which Symmachus alone translated, all have translated kings, deceived by the ambiguity of the word, because, except for the one letter ALEPH, which is added in the word for Angels, kings and Angels are called by the same name among the Hebrews, that is, MALACHE (Kings מלכי, Angels מלאכי). In the book of Hebrew Names I have found that Philisthiim means falling by the cup. Those, therefore, who have been made drunk with the cup of Babylon and have drunk the wine in which is debauchery, and, among the other vices, have not obtained the kingdom of God because of drunkenness, according to the Apostle (Eph 5), are commanded that their gate should howl and their city cry out. The gate I take to be the mouth of the heretics, with which they blaspheme; the city, the soul, which is a treasury of evil thoughts. This ought to howl and lament, because it is wholly laid low and cast down to the ground, and there is nothing in it of sound understanding and of the wisdom of God. Why does the gate howl? for what cause does the city cry out? because all Philistia is laid low. And why it is laid low the following verse shows: Smoke comes from the North, and there is none who escapes its column. This smoke is raised by the burning darts of the devil; it is harmful to the eyes and contrary to light, and it rises from the North, from which in Jeremiah the pot is set on fire (Jer 1:14), and from which evils blaze forth upon all the inhabitants of the earth, who cannot say: I am a sojourner and a stranger, as all my fathers were (Ps 118 [119]:19); but they dwell in the earth. Hence in Proverbs too it is read: The North is a harsh wind (Prov 25:23; Sir 43). But by another name it is called the right-hand wind; for although of itself it is harsh and left-handed, and with a most hard neck will not receive the yoke of God, it is called right-handed by those who put sweet for bitter and bitter for sweet; who put darkness for light and light for darkness. And there is no one who escapes the column of this smoke; for no one is without sin, not even if his life be of a single day (Job 25).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.31":["Howl, O gate, cry out, O city, all Philistia is laid low: for smoke will come from the north, and there is none who escapes its column. He says the gate for those who are in the gate, and the city for the inhabitants of the city. And he directs his speech to the cities of the Palestinians, that they should wail and mourn as Sennacherib comes and lays everything waste like a torrent. For under King Hezekiah the Assyrian came, and among the other nations laid waste the Philistines. To them Jeremiah speaks: Behold, waters will rise up from the north, and they will be like an overflowing torrent: and they will cover the land and all that fills it, the city and its inhabitants (Jer 47:2). For from the north came the Assyrian, and those who went forth from its heat, subjugating Nineveh and the other nations. At the time when these things were being sung, the smoke was rising on high, that is, a rumor was spreading among the peoples, through the whole range of the nations, that the Phoenicians too and the Palestinians were to be laid waste.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.14.32":["What will be answered to the messengers of the nation? That the Lord has founded Zion, and in her the poor of his people will hope. Because he had said, There is none who escapes its column, Judah too seemed to be included in the general statement. If, he says, the messengers of the Assyrian nation ask why Judah alone has escaped, answer them that the Lord has founded Zion, and has himself protected the humble people by his power. For messengers (מלאכי) the Seventy translated kings (מלכי), misled by an error over a single letter, ALEPH.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And when Philistia has fallen, and its smoke has penetrated all things, so that no one can escape it, what will be said to the Angels who preside over the several nations, who wonder and desire to know why Zion alone, set on a watchtower and on the heights, has escaped the bitterness of this smoke? What, then, will be said to them? Surely what follows: That the Lord has founded it, and he himself is its foundation. And he has founded it upon the foundation of prudence, righteousness, fortitude and temperance, under which names Christ is understood, of whom the Apostle also says: For no one can lay another foundation besides that which has been laid, Jesus Christ (1 Cor 3:11). But he who is foolish, and speaks foolish things, and whose heart understands vain things, builds his house upon the sand, which has no foundation. In this Zion, therefore, which has been founded by the Lord, the poor, or the meek and humble of his people, will hope, of whom it is said: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land (Matt 5:4): and who have heard the Lord saying: Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart (Matt 11:29). They have been humbled before glory, and have heard the apostle Peter saying (1 Pet 5:6): Humble yourselves under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in the time of visitation. And these are the poor of whom we read above: The firstborn of the poor will be fed, and the poor will rest confidently.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.1":["The burden of Moab: because in the night Ar was laid waste, Moab has fallen silent: because in the night the wall was laid waste, Moab has fallen silent. Let it suffice to have spoken once about burden and weight. I give only this brief reminder: that sad things always follow a burden, whereas a vision is followed either by glad things at once, or by glad things at the end after the sad. Moab is a province of the Arabs, in which was Balak son of Beor (Num 22), who hired the soothsayer Balaam from Mesopotamia to curse Israel; where also the people were initiated into Baal-peor (ibid., 25). Its metropolis is the city AR (ער), which today is called Areopolis, a name compounded from the Hebrew and the Greek tongue; not, as many suppose, because it is the city of ἄρεος, that is, of Mars. And Ar also means ἀντίδικος, that is, adversary. How great its power was, Jeremiah testifies (Jer 48:2, 7ff.), saying: There is no more exultation in Moab. And again: You have trusted in your fortifications and in your treasures. And again: Moab has been fruitful from his youth, and has rested on his lees, and has not been poured from vessel to vessel, and has not gone into exile; and therefore his taste has remained in him, and his scent has not been changed. And in another place (ibid., 17): How has the strong rod been broken, the glorious staff? And a little later: We have heard of the pride of Moab; he is exceedingly proud. His loftiness, and arrogance, and pride, and the haughtiness of his heart I know, says the Lord, and his boasting. Just as, therefore, the Prophet prophesied against Babylon and the Philistines because they had oppressed the people of the Jews, so now the devastation of the Moabites, that is, of Arabia, by the Assyrians and the Babylonians is foretold. For they were laid waste by both nations, at the time when Sennacherib led Israel captive, and when Nebuchadnezzar overthrew Jerusalem (2 Kgs 17 and 25). For at the coming of each enemy he had mocked Ephraim and Judah, as Jeremiah says: Moab will strike his hand in his vomit, and he too will become a derision. For Israel was a derision to you, as though you had found him among thieves (Jer 48:26-27). Because of your words, therefore, which you have spoken against him, you will be led captive. And when he says: In the night AR was laid waste, Moab has fallen silent, it is a fitting beginning for the invective, that he who was conceived at night from his father's incest should be laid waste in darkness. Unless perhaps we take night for the greatness of the terror; and it is to be believed that, because the city trusted in its walls, it was overcome by stratagems and mines. I have heard a certain man of Areopolis say, and indeed the whole city is witness to it, that in a great earthquake in my infancy, when the seas passed over the shores of the whole world, the walls of this city collapsed on that same night.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The burden of Moab. LXX: The word against the land of Moab. Symmachus and Theodotion: The taking up of Moab. Just as circumcision is both carnal and spiritual, and of the spiritual it is said by the Apostle: For we are the circumcision, who serve God in the spirit, and glory in the Lord, and put no confidence in the flesh (Phil 3:3); and again, to distinguish the spiritual Israel, it is said of the carnal: Behold Israel according to the flesh (1 Cor 10:18): and, You Gentiles in the flesh (Eph 2:11); so Moab is to be taken spiritually, which means from the father, or paternal water, conceived of incest and drunkenness, because he would seem to have been begotten in a manner in the absence, or rather without the knowledge, of the father. In many places of the Scriptures we read of Moab, and especially in the volume of Numbers, when Balak, king of the Moabites, invited Balaam the soothsayer to curse, who among other things prophesied also this mystical word against Moab: A star will rise out of Jacob, and a man will rise up out of Israel, and will strike the princes of Moab (Num 24:17).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Because by night Ar has been laid waste, Moab has fallen silent; because by night the wall has been laid waste, Moab has fallen silent. LXX: By night the land of Moab perished, for by night the wall of the land of Moab perished. For AR (ער), which Theodotion alone set down thus, as it is read in the Hebrew, Aquila and Symmachus translated city, not considering that between the Hebrew letters AIN and RES it does not have the letter JOD, which, if it were there, would rightly be read as city. Worldly wisdom, of which the Lord speaks through the Prophet: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the prudence of the prudent I will reject (Obad 8; 1 Cor 1:19), because it has as its author the mind, which is begotten by God's creating, seems indeed to be born of the father, which is what Moab means; but because it is adulterous and an adversary to the people of God, it is begotten of incest, of a cave and of night. Hence it perished by night, namely in everlasting error. The Egyptians too in the Red Sea were overwhelmed by the waves at the morning watch, which indicates the time of night (Exod 14). And Lot, while the Sodomites were perishing by night, came to Zoar, and the sun rose for him (Gen 19). Understanding this, the blessed Apostle writes of the saints and the perfect (1 Thess 5:6, 7): We are not of the night nor of darkness, but we are sons of the day. For those who sleep sleep by night, and those who are drunk are drunk by night. But let us who are of the day keep watch, clothed with the breastplate of faith and love. And because he had left the night and had already begun to be a son of the day, he says to believers: The night has gone before, and the day has drawn near; let us walk honestly as in the day, not in revelings and drunkenness, not in chambering and wantonness (Rom 13:12). And when the land of Moab perishes by night, its wall too, which was built by the art of dialectic, was laid waste and destroyed in the same night, and fell silent in eternal silence. Moreover Ar, which means ἀντίδικος, that is, adversary, shows this: that this wisdom, which is the adversary of God, has been overcome by the Church's preaching fighting against it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.2":["The house has gone up, and Dibon, to the high places to lament over Nebo, and over Medeba Moab will howl. Do not let it be irksome to you that I walk along the road of the history; for you yourself wished me to do this. Now what is understood is the royal house; and the city of Dibon goes up to the idols set on the heights: over Nebo and over Medeba, noble cities, the whole province will howl. For in Nebo was consecrated the idol Chemosh, which by another name is called Baal-peor.\n\nOn all its heads baldness, every beard will be shaved. Among the ancients the shaving of the beard and the head was a sign of mourning. By these things, therefore, the greatness of the grief is shown, as Jeremiah cries out the same things against Moab: Every head baldness, and every beard will be shaved, and on all hands a binding, and on every back haircloth (Jer 48:37).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The house has gone up, and Dimon, to the high places, to lament. LXX: Be sorrowful within yourselves, for Dimon will perish, where the altar is: thither you will go up to weep. The whole house of hostile wisdom, and Dibon, which means their flowing, goes up to the high places in which it had grown proud: not to sacrifice victims, but to lament the things in which it had formerly sinned. And truly falsehood passes like a river, and flows by, and can never stand firm in a stable dwelling. But the word of God, which is compacted, is stable. Hence the manna too, which is seen like ice upon the face of the earth, does not flow away, but stands firm. Moreover, according to the Seventy, the Moabites are commanded to grieve and lament, not over others (for this belongs to the perfect) but over themselves, because their Dibon too, that is, their composed discourse, which flowed like a torrent, is to perish: in which they had, as it were, the altar of their consecration, and all worldly riches. Hence it is said in the psalm too: If riches abound, do not set your heart upon them. And the beginning of salvation is to understand and weep over one's own sins.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Over Nebo and over Medeba Moab will howl: on all its heads baldness, every beard will be shaved. In its crossroads they are girded with sackcloth: and upon its roofs and in its streets everyone howls: he will go down in weeping. And Heshbon and Elealeh will cry out: their voice has been heard as far as Jahaz: for this the armed men of Moab will howl, his soul will howl to itself. My heart will cry out to Moab: its bars as far as Zoar, a heifer of three years: for by the ascent of Luhith he will go up weeping, and on the way of Horonaim they will raise a cry of crushing: for the waters of Nimrim will be desolate: because the grass has withered, the shoot has failed, all greenness has perished. According to the greatness of the work is also their visitation: they will lead them to the brook of the willows: for the cry has gone round the border of Moab: its howling as far as Eglaim, and its cry as far as the well of Elim: because the waters of Dimon are filled with blood. For I will set upon Dimon additions, for those who have fled from Moab, a lion, and for the remnant of the land. Since the whole of this prophecy has almost a single sense, lest I tear it apart by setting it forth piecemeal, I have set it all down together, and I shall run through in brief discourse what seems to me, according to the anagogy, to be meant in each part. Nebo means sitting or prophecy; Medeba, from the forest; Heshbon, thoughts; Elealeh, ascent; Jahaz, deed or command; Zoar, small; Luhith, cheeks; Horonaim, hole of grief; Nimrim, leopards or transgressors; Eglaim, calves or mounds of sand; Elim, rams or strong ones; Dimon, sufficient grief. Since, then, every doctrine contrary to the truth, which is born without the inspiration of God from human sense in the darkness of error, has been laid waste by night, and its arguments, which are understood as walls, have been destroyed by the Church's preaching, so that they have fallen silent in eternal silence, insomuch that their whole faction has been turned to penance and tears, over Nebo, that is, the prophecy and the sitting, that is, their teachers, and over Medeba, where there are no fruitful trees but a barren forest in which wild beasts dwell, of which it is written in the twenty-eighth psalm: And he will uncover the thickets of the forest: there will be howling and lamentation, and all the ornaments of eloquence will be taken from their heads, so that they remain naked and unsightly. And if they seemed to have anything of manliness in the beard, shaved by the churchman, it is shown to be effeminate and weak.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.3":["In its crossroads they are girded with sackcloth; on its roofs and in its streets all howling will go down into weeping. The tears will not be private; public laments will resound for a public grief; neither matrons nor virgins, nor the tender age of children, nor the broken steps of old men will be kept within doors: the extremity of captivity will know neither shame nor weakness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In their crossroads too, that is, in the byways of errors, while each one according to his own will fashions what he wishes, he is girded with the sackcloth of penance; and upon the roofs and housetops, on which at first they believed themselves to be lofty, and in the streets (because they do not enter by the narrow way that leads to life, but by the broad way that leads to death), there will be howling. And they will by no means go up into pride, but will go down into weeping.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.4":["Heshbon and Elealeh will cry out. These are names of cities of the province of Moab, of which Heshbon was once the city of the king of the Amorites, about which the same Jeremiah says: Fire has gone forth from Heshbon, and flame from the midst of Sihon. Now Heshbon means thought, and so Jeremiah, alluding to the name: Against Heshbon, he says, they have thought evil things (Jer 48:2).\n\nEven to Jahaz their voice has been heard. The city of Jahaz overlooks the Dead Sea, where the border of the province of the Moabites lies. This, then, indicates that the howling of the province will resound to its farthest borders. Hence Jeremiah too says: From the cry of Heshbon, up to Elealeh and Jahaz they have uttered their voice (Jer 48:34).\n\nOver this the lightly armed of Moab will howl, its soul will howl for itself. The Hebrew word ELUSE (חלוצי), for which Aquila translated ἐξώμους, that is, lightly armed and with shoulders bared, and Symmachus girded, some think is to be understood not of men but as the name of a city, which today is called Eluza and is situated in the region of Moabitis. But we can also say this: that the whole strength of the fighting men has collapsed, and the whole land has resounded with their weeping for one another. If, however, ELUSE is understood, as Aquila would have it, as with shoulders bared, this sense is suggested to us: that all have bared their breasts to beat them in mourning.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Then they will understand all their thoughts to be vain, which is the meaning of Heshbon, and the ascent of pride to be empty, which is what Elealeh sounds, so that their voice is heard as far as the evil works which they have done, and the command which they thought was God's, condemning themselves by their own confession. Therefore the bars, that is, those who have been firm in Moab and have understood their error, will howl, and will begin to have hope of progress, when their soul has howled to itself.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.5":["My heart will cry out to Moab. The Prophet speaks with the feeling of one who grieves, either because the enemies too, upon whom so many evils come, are God's creatures, or because they were to be crushed by such great calamities that they become pitiable even to their enemies. Jeremiah says the very same: Therefore my heart will sound for Moab like a flute.\n\nIts bars, as far as Segor, a heifer of three years. And Jeremiah: From Segor, he says, as far as Horonaim, a heifer of three years. Of this city we have spoken in the books of Hebrew Questions as well, and now we note briefly that it is the fifth city after Sodom and Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboiim, which, being small, was preserved at the prayers of Lot. And it is called Bala, that is, swallowed up, the Hebrews relating that it was overthrown by a third earthquake. It is the city which today in the Syrian tongue is called Zoora, in the Hebrew Segor: in both, the little one. We can take the heifer of three years as signifying full age. For as the thirtieth year is the strongest in men, so the third is in cattle and beasts of burden. Understand the bars, too, as borders and strength, because Segor is situated at the borders of the Moabites, dividing the land of the Philistines from them.\n\nFor by the ascent of Luhith he will go up weeping. And Jeremiah: By the ascent of Luhith, weeping, he will go up in tears. We understand it to be the slope of the road that leads to the Assyrians, and by this captivity is signified.\n\nAnd on the road to Horonaim they will raise a cry of crushing. Again Jeremiah: The voice of a cry from Horonaim, devastation and great crushing. It would take long if I wished to speak about each one, since it is plain that these are the names either of cities in Moab or of places which those who are carried away into exile are leaving.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Hence the Prophet, with the feeling of one who suffers with them, speaks to those whose soul was howling to itself, and says: My heart will cry out to Moab, that I may call them to penance. And their bars and all the supports which they seemed to have in their heresies will come as far as Zoar, that is, as far as the little one: and they will be shown to be not strong but fragile. And this Zoar, that is, small penance, if it perseveres, will lead them to perfect salvation: which is what the heifer of three years signifies, according to what we read in Genesis, where Abraham is commanded to offer a heifer, a ram and a goat of three years, namely a perfect sacrifice, so that he might deserve to be the heir of the Lord (Gen 15). And when they have done penance, they will climb to higher things by Luhith, that is, by the tears of the cheeks. And by this, as it were, hole and entrance of grief, they will raise the cry of contrition to the Lord, so that they can say: A sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit: a contrite and humbled heart God will not despise (Ps 50 [51]:19).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.6":["For the waters of Nimrim will be deserted. This town lies above the Dead Sea, with salt waters, and barren for that very reason. And either he has alluded to the name, or what had not been so before came about after the devastation: that even the waters were turned to bitterness.\n\nFor the grass has withered, the shoot has failed, all greenness has perished. It is not, as some think, that all the grass truly dried up because of the barren waters of Nimrim; rather, Scripture is speaking by metaphor. And the sense is: in all Moab there will be the salt and bitter waters of Nimrim; as no grass springs up there, so drought will overtake the whole province, that is, from Segor to Horonaim, from border to border. Jeremiah says the very same: The waters of Nimrim will be very bad (Jer 48:34).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And this will come about because the waters of Nimrim, that is, the doctrine of the heretics, which is compared to leopards and transgressors, will be desolate and will be brought to nothing. They are leopards whose varieties and spots, in Jeremiah, are not changed (Jer 13), and apostates, of whom we read in the Psalms: I have counted all the sinners of the earth transgressors (Ps 118 [119]:119). Every grass too, and shoot, and whatever seemed green in their discourse, has withered.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.7":["According to the greatness of the work, so also their visitation: they will lead them to the torrent of the willows. For willows we read in the Hebrew ARABIM (ערבים), which can also be understood as Arabs, and can be read ORBIM (עורבים), that is, a village situated on their borders, whose inhabitants are said by many to have provided food for Elijah on Mount Horeb: a name which, because of its ambiguity, is also translated as ravens, and as the West, and as level places. And the sense is: the visitation will be according to the greatness of the disease. Take visitation here not as a remedy but as a blow. I will visit, he says, their iniquities with the rod, and their sins with stripes (Ps 88 [89]:33). The torrent of the willows understand as the rivers of Babylonia, of which David says: On the willows in the midst of it we hung up our instruments (Ps 136 [137]:2); or else as the valley of Arabia through which one passes to the Assyrians.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And they have been visited by God according to the greatness of their sins, so that him whom they had not perceived through benefits they might come to know through scourges. Accordingly they will be led to the valley or brook of the willows, so that no fruit may remain in them. For they say this is the nature of the seed of these trees: that whoever drinks it in a cup is without children. Hence the saints too, who because of their sins have come to be in the confusion of this world, hang their instruments on the willows of the rivers of the Babylonians (Ps 136 [137]).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.8":["For the cry will go round the border of Moab, its howling as far as Eglaim, and its cry as far as the well of Elim. Jeremiah too writes almost all the same things. And these are cities and places of the Moabites, in which the crying and wailing of the captive people is described.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","A cry goes round all the borders of the Moabites, either of those who call them to repentance, or of those who lament for their error: so that through their howling they may be able to offer the calves of the lips, and come as far as the fountain of the Lord's rams, or of the strong: because Elim signifies both.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.15.9":["For the waters of Dibon are filled with blood. Where formerly there was luxuriance because of the watered fields and the ever-flowing springs, there, because of the multitude of the slain, streams of blood will flow.\n\nFor I will set additions upon Dibon: for those who flee from Moab, a lion, and for the remnant of the land. Lest anyone think it a fault of the scribe and, in wishing to correct an error, make one, the one city is written both with the letter M and with the letter B: of these, Dimon is interpreted as silence, Dibon as flowing. Both names having been given it because of the waters that flow silently, to this day this little town is called indifferently both Dimon and Dibon. And as for his saying: I will set additions upon Dibon, since it seemed to raise the question what these might be, he showed it in the following line, saying: For those who flee from Moab, a lion, and for the remnant of the land: so that even those who have slipped away in flight are consumed by beasts. Although by metaphor we can also understand the lion as the king of the enemies, so that no one can escape his power, as though it were a roaring.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And the waters of Dimon, which means sufficient sorrow, or grief, which had polluted many by their sprinkling, will be shown to have been to the deceived peoples not for salvation but for blood. Hence the prophetic word promises that upon the flowing tears, which is the meaning of Dibon, they will redouble not one grief but many additions of grief: so that, after they have done full penance and have fled from Moab, they may have as their prince the lion, God, whom the Seventy called ariel, which means lion of God: whose edition I have forborne to discuss in this place, because it disagrees in very many points with the Hebrew truth, and from what we have translated its sense too can be understood.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.1":["Send forth the Lamb, the ruler of the earth, from the rock of the desert to the mountain of the daughter of Zion. What we are interpreting is not history but prophecy. And every prophecy is wrapped in enigmas, and with abrupt sentences passes, while speaking of one thing, to another: lest, if Scripture kept to order, it should be not prophecy but narrative. And the sense is: O Moab, upon whom the lion is about to rage, and from whom not even a remnant will be able to be saved, have this consolation: from you will go forth the spotless Lamb who takes away the sins of the world, who will rule over the whole world. From the rock of the desert, that is, from Ruth, who, widowed by the death of her husband, bore Obed by Boaz, and from Obed came Jesse, and from Jesse David, and from David Christ. And the mountain of the daughter of Zion we shall interpret either as the city of Jerusalem itself, or, according to the sacred understanding, as the Church, which is set upon the summit of the virtues.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Send forth the lamb, the ruler of the earth, from the rock of the desert, to the mountain of the daughter of Zion. And as a bird fleeing, and as young ones flying from the nest, so will the daughters of Moab be at the crossing of the Arnon. Enter into counsel, call a council: make your shadow like night at midday: hide those who flee, and do not betray the wanderers. My fugitives will dwell with you; Moab, be their hiding place from the face of the destroyer. For the dust is at an end, the wretch is consumed; he has failed who trampled the earth. And a throne will be prepared in mercy, and one will sit upon it in truth in the tabernacle of David, judging and seeking judgment, and swiftly rendering what is just. What we have translated from the Hebrew, send forth the lamb, the ruler of the earth, can be read thus: send forth the lamb to the ruler of the earth, in the sense, namely, that the lamb itself is not the ruler of the earth, as we interpreted according to the history, but that the lamb is to be sacrificed to the ruler of the earth. This lamb, then, which either is itself the ruler of the earth or is sacrificed to the ruler of the earth, is of the nation of the Moabites, and of those who have fled from Moab and have deserved to have the lion as their prince. And it signifies Ruth, from whom Christ was begotten (Matt 1:5), whom he calls the rock of the desert, because according to the precept of God the Moabites and Ammonites do not enter the Church of God even to the tenth generation and forever (Deut 23).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.2":["And it will be: as a bird fleeing, and young ones flying away from the nest, so will the daughters of Moab be at the crossing of the Arnon. He returns to the order he had begun: When I have set, he says, a most savage lion upon those who flee from Moab, and upon the remnant of the land, to break their limbs and bones, then they will fly away in terror, and all the daughters, that is, the villages and cities of the province of Moab, will migrate at the crossing of the Arnon, which is the border of the Amorites and the Moabites. And by putting crossing here too, he signifies captivity.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But he who has fled from the desert of Moab (to return from the prophecy to the anagogy), and, despising falsehood, has stood on the mountain of truth, will be like a bird fleeing; and like young ones flying from the nest, lest they be devoured by the Moabite serpents. Thus, he says, will all the daughters, that is, the Moabite souls, be at the crossing of the Arnon, which means their illumination: when, having abandoned their errors, they have crossed over to the knowledge of the truth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.3-4":["Take counsel, call a council: make your shade like night at midday, hide the fugitives, and do not betray the wanderers. My refugees will dwell with you: Moab, be their hiding place from the face of the devastator; for the dust is at an end, the wretched one is finished, he who trampled the land has failed. For wretched one in the Hebrew one reads SOD (סד), which can also be understood as devastator. And he speaks to Moab, that it should find counsel for its safety, and, gathering its elders, convene a council for its salvation. Do you wish, he says, to be saved and to merit the mercy of God? In the clear light and the open flight of my people, be like night and shade; take in the fugitives, and do not betray the wanderers. And why he said this he adds at once: My refugees will dwell with you. For when Jerusalem has been laid waste, and all Judea, which borders on the Moabites, my people will migrate to you. Be therefore their hiding place, and do not fear the onslaught of the devastator, because he will quickly pass like dust; and the despoiler of the whole land, who trampled it and subjected it under his feet, will perish as a breeze blows. Some wrongly interpret this place of the Antichrist, so as to suppose that at that time the saints will cross over to the Arabs because of their nearness to the city of Jerusalem, and that the Arabs are now commanded not to betray those who flee to them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.3":["It is said therefore to Moab himself, or to him who has escaped from Moab: Do nothing without counsel (Prov 13): and do not be carried about by every wind of doctrine, but follow him who is the Angel of great counsel (Eph 4): and call a council, so that out of the wandering and straying you may make a Church of God. And your shade and tabernacle, in which you formerly thought you were resting, which belonged to night and darkness, set it at midday, that is, in the brightest light, in which you ought to hide those fleeing from error, and no longer betray those who were formerly wanderers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.4":["For, O Moab, my fugitives who had forsaken me, who had gone out of the Church and, abandoning the teaching of the Holy Spirit, were following their own sense; or those who had dwelt with you, when the destroyer, the devil, begins to persecute them, do you, turned with your whole mind to the fear of the Lord, offer them hiding places: and know that after the coming of the Lamb, who arose from the rock of the desert as ruler of the whole earth and came to the mountain of the daughter of Zion, all the power of the devil, which is compared to dust, is at an end. And the wretch is consumed who made many wretched: and he has failed who trampled the whole earth, namely those who were earthly.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.5":["And a throne will be prepared in mercy, and he will sit upon it in truth, in the tabernacle of David, judging and seeking judgment, and swiftly rendering what is just. The Hebrews interpret this place thus: When the Assyrian has been put to flight, Hezekiah, a righteous man, will reign over Judah and will hold the throne of David, judging in truth the people of God subject to him. Others understand it of Christ: when the dust of the Antichrist is at an end, and the devastator who trampled the whole earth has been taken away, Christ the king will come, who will sit in the tabernacle of David, and in the day of judgment will render to all according to their works. Nor is there any doubt that this passage prophesies of Christ. But we can understand the same thing of his first coming, and show it in the tabernacle of the Church: that throughout the whole land of Moab the trophies of the Churches as they rise bear witness to the dominion of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And when he has been consumed and brought to nothing and has utterly failed, a throne and an everlasting kingdom will be prepared. First in mercy (for we are all (Rom 3) under sin, and need the grace of God); and he will sit in the tabernacle of David, which had fallen and has been raised up, who after mercy will judge, and seek righteousness, and render to each according to his works. Let us consider from the beginning of the Burden, or of the word, of Moab, in which it is said: By night Ar of Moab has been laid waste, and the rest up to this place; and we shall see how by steps and in the order of penance Israelites are made from Moabites, and flee like birds and fly away like young ones from the nest, so as to cross over by the Arnon and dwell on the mountain of the daughter of Zion: and, all the power of the devil or of the Antichrist having been crushed, Christ reigns in them and sets his throne among those who have been saved by mercy and righteousness. For the Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son (John 5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.6-7":["We have heard of the pride of Moab, he is exceedingly proud: his pride, and his arrogance, and his indignation are greater than his strength. Therefore Moab will howl to Moab, all of it will howl: to those who rejoice over the walls of baked brick, tell of its wounds. Again he returns to the present, and accuses Moab of pride, because it was lifted up far more than its strength warranted; and because of this pride Moab will howl to Moab, that is, the people to the city, or the metropolis to the province, and the whole land will resound with howling over the walls of baked brick, of which Jeremiah too says: Therefore I will wail over Moab, and to all Moab I will cry out, to the men of the earthen wall, lamenting (Jer 48:31). And by these things he shows both the power of its former prosperity and the wounds of its sudden overthrow.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.6":["We have heard of the pride of Moab: he is exceedingly proud. His pride and his arrogance and his indignation are more than his strength. Therefore Moab will howl to Moab, all of him will howl: to those who rejoice over the wall of baked brick, speak of his wounds: for the suburbs of Heshbon are deserted, the vineyard of Sibmah. LXX: We have heard of the insolence of Moab: he is exceedingly insolent: his pride and his insolence and his fury are not like your divination: not so will Moab howl: for in Moab all will howl: but for the inhabitants of Deseth you will meditate, and you will not be confounded: the plains of Heshbon will mourn the vineyard of Sibmah. In how many obscurities this passage is wrapped according to the Seventy translators is evident from the fact that it can scarcely be read. Let us speak, therefore, according to the Hebrew. It is the custom of the Scriptures, after they have relieved the despair of the human mind with glad tidings, to deter again by threats the negligent and those unwilling to do penance, lest the goodness of God harden our heart. Of this let us set down only one example. In the hundred and forty-fourth psalm we read: The Lord is sweet to all, and his compassions are over all his works (Ps 144 [145]:9). And a little after: The Lord upholds all who fall, and raises up all who are dashed down. The eyes of all hope in you, O Lord, and you give them their food in due season. And when he had said first, The Lord keeps all who love him, lest he make the hearer negligent, he added: And all sinners he will destroy. Since, then, he prophesied that when the Antichrist and his parent the devil, who trampled the whole earth, have been consumed, a throne is to be prepared in mercy, and he who is to sit in the tabernacle of David, the prophet speaks in the person of the saints who have been saved from Moab and have learned his pride by their own experience: We have heard of the pride of Moab, or the insolence, as the Seventy translated. For which of the heretics is not proud? who, despising the simplicity of the Church, regards the men of the Church as brute animals, and is lifted up by such a swelling of pride and insolence that he arms his mouth against the Creator himself, disparaging his prophets as though on the authority of the testimony of the Gospel, in which the Savior says: All who came before me were thieves and robbers (John 10:8): so much so that he even calls Moses, the servant of God, a murderer, and disparages Joshua the son of Nun as a bloodthirsty man, who was of such holiness that at the command of his word the sun and moon stood still: and calls David, of whose seed Christ was born (Matt 1), a murderer and an adulterer, not regarding his penance and his meekness, by which the clemency of God is obtained. But although he is proud and arrogant, and exults in his fury, yet he dares more than his strength allows.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.7-8":["Therefore Moab will howl to Moab, that is, one to another: namely, all the varieties of the heretics and of worldly wisdom will bellow against one another when they are in torments (1 Kgs 6). For this reason, to those who have walls not built of squared stones, of which the Temple was built, and so polished that hammer and axe were not heard in the house of God, O you teachers of the Church, or you who have been saved from the error of Moab, announce their wounds, with which they have been wounded by the darts of the heretics. For all their thoughts, which is what Heshbon signifies, do not belong to the dwelling of the Lord's city, of which it is written: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God (Ps 45 [46]:5); but they are suburbs, so that they may be believed to belong to the city of the Lord: and these suburbs are deserted by the protection of God, or burnt by the divine fire, especially the vineyard of Sibmah, which means lifting up height, because it raises itself on high and tries to build the tower of its pride up to heaven. And what is set down in the Seventy, for the inhabitants of Deseth, is not found in the Hebrew; but in its place is read ARES (חרש), which signifies potsherd, or baked brick.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.8":["For the suburbs of Heshbon are deserted: the vine of Sibmah. Between Heshbon and Sibmah there are scarcely five hundred paces; and by the metaphor of the vine he signifies the devastation of the whole province.\n\nThe lords of the nations have cut down its shoots. He keeps the metaphor he had begun, showing that the kings of the nations who had laid Moab waste ravaged all its hamlets and fortresses.\n\nThey have reached as far as Jazer: they have wandered in the desert: its offshoots have been left behind: they have crossed the sea. Understand the shoots and offshoots as its people and its fugitives; and the crossing of the sea as the captivity into Babylon, about which we shall read later: The burden of the desert of the sea.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The lords of the nations have cut down its branches: they have reached as far as Jazer. They have wandered in the desert: its shoots have been left: they have crossed the sea. For this I will weep with the weeping of Jazer for the vineyard of Sibmah: I will drench you with my tears, Heshbon and Elealeh: for upon your vintage and upon your harvest the voice of the treaders has burst in. And gladness and exultation will be taken away from Carmel: and in the vineyards he will not exult nor shout for joy: he who was accustomed to tread will not tread wine in the press: I have taken away the voice of the treaders. The suburbs of Heshbon, of which we spoke above, are deserted, and the vineyard of Sibmah, which can be interpreted not only as lifting up height but also as some conversion: because in the region of Moab it seems in some part to wish to be converted to the service of the Lord. Of this vineyard of Sibmah, therefore, the lords of the nations, the Apostles and apostolic men, have utterly cut off the branches and shoots, lest from some heresies other heresies should be born and an infinite multitude of the straying should come to be. And they not only cut off the shoots of Sibmah, but reached as far as Jazer, which means their strength: that is, to all the strongest doctrines of the heretics, built by the art of dialectic, in which they seemed to have the strength of their error; and their sword raged so far that at last they wandered in the wilderness and had no one to slay. And though they cut off its branches, yet, through the fault of the most evil root, some shoots remained. But the lords of the nations crossed the sea, that is, the temptations of this world, of which we read in the psalm: I came into the depth of the sea, and the tempest swallowed me (Ps 68 [69]:3); and in another place: Those who go down to the sea in ships, doing business in many waters: they have seen the works of the Lord, and his wonders in the deep (Ps 106 [107]:23). These, then, crossed the sea, so as to behold the works of the Lord and his wonders in the deep of temptations, while they are delivered from them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.9":["Therefore I will lament with the weeping of Jazer, the vine of Sibmah: I will drench you with my tears, Heshbon and Elealeh. This is the voice of the Prophet lamenting, and attesting the greatness of the devastation by the greatness of his tears, and weeping for the vine of Jazer and of Sibmah, and for Heshbon and Elealeh, cities once most strong, cut down as the Assyrian pruned them.\n\nFor upon your vintage and upon your harvest the voice of the treaders has burst in. Understand the treaders of the grapes, gathering in the joy of the vintage, as the armies of the enemies; and that in the very season of joy the season of captivity has come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And so the prophetic word laments the strength of the heretics, that is, Jazer, and the vineyard of Sibmah, which exalts itself against the knowledge of God. And I will drench you with my tears, Heshbon, namely their thoughts, and Elealeh, by which they climb to the heights. And why does he lament Jazer and the vineyard of Sibmah, and drench Heshbon and Elealeh with his tears? So that, while he himself weeps, he may teach them also to weep. For, he says, upon your vintage and upon your harvest the voice of the treaders has burst in. For the vineyard of the Moabites, because of the nearness of the place, is such as is the vineyard of the Sodomites too, of which it is said: For their vine is of the vineyard of the Sodomites, and their shoot of Gomorrah (Deut 32:31). And in Psalm 77 (78) we read of the Egyptian vine which God struck with hail. The harvests of Moab too grow in the valleys which are called Rephaim: and its false vintage is of the vineyard of which it is said above: The lords of the nations have cut down its branches. For these crush the most bitter grapes and tread them with their feet, lest the poisons of the dragon be pressed out of them and all who drink be killed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.10":["And joy and exultation will be taken away from Carmel. It is an idiom of the Scriptures always to liken Carmel, a rich and wooded mountain which overlooks Ptolemais and on which Elijah prayed, to fertility and abundance; and by this it signifies that all joy and fertility are to be taken away from cities once most fruitful.\n\nAnd in the vineyards he will not exult, nor shout for joy. What is understood is the former vintager, that is, the husbandman of the province of Moabitis. Accordingly he immediately added:\n\nHe who used to tread will not tread wine in the press: I have taken away the voice of the treaders. By no means will the joyful vintager sing the vintage shout at the vintage; but everywhere hostile devastation and the clamor of the victors will arise.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The gladness and exultation of the heretics, in which they had formerly been accustomed to rejoice, will also be taken away: so that, after they have done penance, they may deserve to hear: Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted (Matt 5:5). And what he added, from Carmel, signifies this: not that the heretics truly have Carmel, that is, the knowledge of the spiritual circumcision, but that they falsely boast of having it. And when the vineyards have been cut down, and gladness and exultation taken away from the knowledge falsely so called, then there will be none of the former treaders to tread the grapes which he had trodden before: and their voice will be struck dumb in eternal silence.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.11-12":["Moreover, according to the Seventy, who translated: The inner parts of the prophet, like a wall renewed by God, and that he is put to shame, that is, Moab, and enters to the altars and to the things that are made by hand, we can discuss it thus, saying that the inward parts of the prophet are always renewed and made stronger by God, so that the adversaries are confounded, and understand the things which have been fabricated by human hand to be empty and of no profit for salvation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.11":["Therefore my belly will sound for Moab like a harp, and my inmost parts for the wall of baked brick. Lest anyone should think the Prophet's lament a sign of exultation, by which he rejoiced that the enemies of the people of Israel were captives, he says that it is out of affection, and out of the inmost grief of his heart, that he mourns the overthrow of a city once most mighty.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For this my belly will sound for Moab like a harp, and my inward parts for the wall of baked brick. And it will be, when it has appeared that Moab has labored on his high places, he will enter into his holy places to entreat, and he will not prevail. This is the word which the Lord spoke to Moab from that time. When the error of Moab has been destroyed, or rather its false gladness changed into mourning and tears, my belly, the prophet's, which is composed with musical art like a harp, and which, conceiving from the fear of God, has begotten many children, so that there is no string that does not give back its sound, will resound with the mourning of Moab doing penance: and all my inward parts for the wall of baked brick: for which Theodotion translated for the scattered wall. For all the defenses of the adversaries, in which they formerly trusted, will be destroyed and will fall. And these are the inward parts and the inner things of the prophet, of which David too spoke in the Psalm: Bless the Lord, O my soul, and all my inner parts his holy name (Ps 102 [103]:1). For just as a harp does not give forth a tuneful and well-composed sound if even one string is broken, so the spiritual belly of the prophet, if one string of the virtues is lacking in it, will not be able to sound forth a sweet melody, nor to cry out with all its inward parts to the brick wall. It is also the opinion of the philosophers that the virtues cleave to one another; and of the Apostle James, that he who lacks one lacks all the virtues.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.12":["And it will come to pass, when it has become apparent that Moab has labored over its high places, it will enter its sanctuaries to pray, and it will not prevail. It is the last misery not to have help even in those whom it has always worshiped. Deserted, he says, by your own strength, and with all your defenders slain, you will go to the idols, you will venerate the shrines, and you will find no help in those upon whom the devastation will come together with you.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And when Moab has understood that he has labored in vain at the doctrines which he formerly thought lofty, he will enter into his holy places, not those which are holy of themselves, but those which in his error he thought holy, and he will not be able to find help. Or surely, having abandoned the error of falsehood, he will try to enter into the holy places of the Church, to make them his own, and to pray and entreat, but he will not prevail. For we cannot take hold of perfect virtue as soon as we wish.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.13":["This is the word which the Lord spoke to Moab from that time. From what time, do you suppose? Clearly from the time when Moab was created. And the Lord says: The Moabites and the Ammonites shall not enter into the Church of God (Deut 23:3). Or we may understand from that time as of ancient time: that these things were decreed long ago by the divine sentence; not that the foreknowledge of God brought about the cause of the devastation, but that the future devastation was known beforehand to the majesty of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And what he adds: This is the word which the Lord spoke to Moab from that time, let us know that an epilogue has been given back to the proem in which he had said, the word against Moab, or the burden, so that what he had begun there he has completed here. And from that time is to be understood as from when he began to speak to him, so that all that he said is one word of God, that is, one sentence.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.16.14":["And now the Lord has spoken, saying: In three years, like the years of a hireling, the glory of Moab will be taken away, above all its great people, and it will be left small and slight, by no means many. This prophecy, as we said above, is directed against the Moabites after the death of Ahaz, in the reign of Hezekiah, under whom the ten tribes were led into captivity by Sennacherib king of the Assyrians. So, just as a hireling waits for the setting of the sun, and day and night looks for the end of his work so as to receive his fixed wage, so too after three years Moab will be destroyed when the Assyrian comes, and scarcely a few will be left in the land to inhabit the overthrown cities and to till the deserted fields. It can also be a prediction about the Babylonian captivity: that after Jerusalem had been taken and three years had passed, Moab was to be laid waste by the Chaldeans; or else that within the space of three years no rest was to be given them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And now the Lord has spoken, saying: In three years, like the years of a hireling, the glory of Moab will be taken away over all his great people, and he will be left small and slight, by no means many. The three years in which the glory of Moab will be taken away over all his great people, or great riches, as the Seventy translated, are to be understood mystically. For just as the mercy of the Lord is in weights and measure, so torments and punishments too have their measure, so that after three years have passed, in which he will be left small and slight and by no means many, he may then cease to be without glory. And this is to be noted, that according to the prophecy of Ezekiel, when days are counted for years to Israel (Ezek 4), that is, to the ten tribes, which had sinned more grievously, three hundred and ninety years are reckoned in punishments, as it is contained in the Hebrew, not a hundred and ninety, as the common edition has: and to Judah, in which was the Temple of God, forty years. For he who is small deserves mercy: but the mighty will suffer torments mightily (Wis 6). And the servant who knows the will of his lord and does not do it will be beaten with many stripes (Luke 12). Moab, therefore, because he was an outsider and not of the people of God, but was converted after leaving his error, will be left small and slight and without glory, not for many years, but for three only. These are the years of which we read elsewhere too: Remember the days of the age (Isa 46), and again: I thought upon the days of old, and I had the eternal years in mind (Ps 76 [77]:6). For if Israel according to the flesh served the shadow and the patterns, and every solemnity of theirs was a type of things to come, why should not the years of the present time also prefigure the times to come? of which we read in another place too: What will you do in the days of the assembly, and in the days of the festival of the Lord? And what is joined to it, Like the years of a hireling (Hos 9:5), shows this: that false doctrine does everything for gain and profit. For every hireling, and he who is not the shepherd, whose own the sheep are not, when he sees the wolf coming, flees, because he is a hireling and the sheep do not belong to him (John 10). For this reason I think that the stranger and the hireling do not eat of the holy things, nor are servants partakers of the holy things. For it is not for love of the Lord but for wages that they do all things, who devour the houses of widows and lead off flocks of their own, to be clothed with their wool and to milk their milk (Matt 23). What we have said of hireling doctrine, let us refer to the rest as well. If I give alms in order to be glorified by men, I have received my reward and am to be called a hireling. If I pretend to be chaste, and there is something else in my conscience, I have not the glory of a hireling but the punishments of a sinner. And in the comparison of two evils, it is a lighter evil to sin openly than to feign and counterfeit holiness. But in this too Moab has made progress: that he who formerly had many companions in his error is afterward reduced to a few; or he who formerly was great in the riches of sins is, after penance, small and poor in malice.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.1-3":["Some think that this prophecy is the same as the one we read in Jeremiah: Damascus is dissolved, it has turned to flight, trembling has seized it, anguish and pains have held it like a woman in labor (Jer 49:24, 27). And again: I will kindle a fire in the wall of Damascus, and it will devour the ramparts of Ben-hadad. But it must be known that Jeremiah describes the Babylonian captivity of the city of Damascus, that is, of the few whom the king of the Assyrians had left in it; whereas Isaiah announces the captivity by the Assyrians that is close at hand. Others suppose that the Roman captivity is foretold, since the people of the Jews too was taken captive, and Damascus, over which Aretas ruled, endured a like servitude: so that all that is written about it is to be transferred to the time of Christ and to the mysteries of the Apostles.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.1":["The burden of Damascus: Behold, Damascus will cease to be a city, and it will be like a heap of stones in ruin. The cities of Aroer, abandoned, will be for flocks, and they will rest there, and there will be none to frighten them. And help will cease from Ephraim, and the kingdom from Damascus, and the remnant of Syria will be like the glory of the sons of Israel, says the Lord of hosts. After Babylon and the Philistines and Moab, the discourse turns to Damascus, that is, Aram, which itself was once a royal city too, and held the primacy in all Syria: Antioch and Laodicea and Apamea were not yet flourishing, cities which we know to have grown after Alexander and the empire of the Macedonians. Because, then, it always gave help to the ten tribes against Judah, as the history of Kings and Chronicles relates, he signifies that devastation by the Assyrians is drawing near to them too, as the king of the Assyrians says: I have taken Arabia, and Damascus, and Samaria. For as I have taken them, and all kingdoms, I will take you too. And in the volume of Kings we read: The king of the Assyrians went up to Damascus, and took it, and carried it away to Cyrene (2 Kgs 16:9). He also slew Rezin, who was king of Damascus: all of which it suffered while Hezekiah reigned in Jerusalem. Behold, he says, Damascus will cease to be a city: already captivity is near, already the Assyrian has set his army in motion. And it will be like a heap of stones in ruin, so that only broken walls and traces of its former power are to be seen in the vastness of the ruins.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The burden of Damascus. Symmachus and Theodotion: The taking up of Damascus; the LXX: The word against Damascus, adding something of their own, as they did at the beginning of Moab. We first read the name Damascus in Genesis: he was Abraham's home-born servant before Isaac, and was thought to be his heir, had not the son of Sarah been begotten by promise (Gen 15). Now it means either kiss of blood, or drinking blood, or blood of sackcloth, all of which suit the people of the heathen, who before the faith of Christ were friends of blood and cruelty, and did works worthy of lamentation and of sackcloth. In the book of Days (2 Chr 24) the history relates that when the year was completed the army of Syria went up against Joash king of Judah, and came into Jerusalem, and slew all the princes of the people, and sent all the spoil to the king of Damascus; it had come with few men, and God had delivered into their hand a very great multitude, because they had forsaken the Lord God of their fathers. This must be considered according to the anagogy and as a type of things to come, whether we may refer it to the time of the Lord's advent. For after the end of the acceptable year, in which the Gospel was preached by the Savior, the army of the nations went up from Damascus with few men against Judah and Jerusalem, who had forsaken the Lord; and they carried off all their riches of the Law and the Prophets, and sent them to the king of Damascus: namely, churchmen and men of the Gospel teaching, who, in comparison with the whole world, still unbelieving at that time, and with the Jews scattered through the whole earth, were few; and yet the Lord delivered Jerusalem into their hands, because they had forsaken the Son of God, who had been foretold beforehand through the prophets (Acts 9). Therefore I think that for this reason Saul too, who afterwards received the name of Paul from his virtue, went to Damascus because he was a zealot of the Law, and wished to fight against the believers from the nations; and that, overcome there, he followed those who lived in Damascus, so that from there he might go up again to Jerusalem to do battle with the Jews. Nor should it seem contradictory to anyone if in the book of Paralipomenon glad things are foretold of Damascus, and now in Isaiah sad things; for of Israel itself, which is surely the portion of God, now adverse things, now prosperous things are announced. And as it is said to Israel according to the flesh and to the nations: You nations according to the flesh, who are called uncircumcision by that which is called the Circumcision in the flesh; so, on the contrary, there is an Israel according to the spirit, and there are nations according to the spirit, and among the other nations there is a Damascus according to the spirit, lest we rest content with Jewish fables alone.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Behold, Damascus shall cease to be a city, and shall be as a heap of stones in ruin. The cities of Aroer shall be left for flocks, and they shall rest there, and there shall be none to make them afraid; and help shall cease from Ephraim, and the kingdom from Damascus; and the remnant of Syria shall be as the glory of the children of Israel, says the Lord of hosts. As was shown in the title, by Damascus is signified the calling of the nations, which loved or drank blood; and once they have believed in Christ, they will cease to be cities of their former way of life, and will be as a heap of stones in ruin. For as heaps of stones that are scattered over the fields are carried together into one mound, so out of all the nations a heap of believers has been gathered at the ruin of the people of the Jews, they falling and we rising.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.2":["The cities of Aroer, abandoned, will be for flocks. Aroer means tamarisk, which is properly a tree that grows in the wilderness and in salty soil; and by this the devastation is shown. They will rest there: the flocks is understood; and there will be none to frighten them. For the solitude will be so great that not even a robber lying in wait is to be feared.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The forsaken cities of AROER (ערוער), that is, of the tamarisk, shall also be for the flocks of the Church, so that we may inhabit those which the Jews forsook; or else, idolatry being destroyed, the Gospel shall be built up. This too we see fulfilled in our own times: the Serapeum at Alexandria and the temple of Marnas at Gaza have risen up as churches of the Lord, and the cities of Aroer have been made ready for the flocks of the Gospel. Aroer, that is, tamarisks, grow in desert places, according to what is written in the curse of the man who trusts in man, and whose heart departs from the Lord: He shall be, it says, like the tamarisk in the desert, and shall not see good when it comes; but he shall dwell in dryness, in the desert, in a land of salt and uninhabitable (Jer 17:6). Others again say that from this tree, by the arts of sorcerers, μίστρα, that is, hatreds, are stirred up. Therefore those who were formerly in the desert of the Gentiles, and in hatred of the Christian name, shall serve the flocks of Christ, which shall rest in them, and there shall be none to make them afraid, because the Lord shall dwell among them, and with the Shepherd present they will not be able to fear the wolf.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.3":["And help will cease from Ephraim: by no means will the ten tribes have help in it against Judah. And the kingdom from Damascus: ἀπὸ κοινοῦ [from the common or popular edition] will cease is understood. But when he says that the kingdom ceases and rests, he does not signify a perpetual devastation, but takes away for the present the power by which it formerly reigned in all Syria. And the remnant of Syria will be like the glory of the sons of Israel, says the Lord of hosts. As, he says, the ten tribes were destroyed when the Assyrian ravaged them, and all their glory was led into captivity, so the few who remain in Damascus will imitate them, and will have the glory of their allied nation. Take glory as said by irony, for ignominy. And all this will happen because the Lord has spoken, whose words cannot be made void.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Then the help of God shall cease from Ephraim, by whom in this place are understood the Scribes and Pharisees, according to the prophecy of Hosea, who names Ephraim those who are opposed to the people of God (Hos 5 and 8). And the kingdom shall cease from Damascus, so that sin, and the prince of sin, the devil, may no longer reign in Damascus, which formerly loved blood; but the remnant of Syria, that is, those from the nations who have believed, are to be as the children of Israel formerly were, the kingdom of God having been taken from them and given to the nations which bring forth its fruit, as the Lord God of hosts has spoken through all his prophets.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.4":["And it will come to pass in that day, the glory of Jacob will be made thin, and the fat of his flesh will waste away. When, he says, Damascus has been taken and has ceased to be a city, and such glory has crowned it as crowned Israel, that is, the ten tribes, then all the defense, and the fattest flesh, and the refuge of Jacob will waste away: for by no means will he have allies to join with him in laying Jerusalem waste. We have read above that Rezin king of Syria and Pekah son of Remaliah, king of Israel, went up to Jerusalem to make war against it, and that it was announced to the house of David: Syria has rested upon Ephraim; of whom the Prophet speaks to Ahaz: Do not fear, and let not your heart be afraid of these two tails of smoking firebrands, in the wrath of the fury of Rezin king of Syria and of the son of Remaliah, because Syria, Ephraim and the son of Remaliah have taken most evil counsel against you.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And it shall come to pass in that day that the glory of Jacob shall be made thin, and the fatness of his flesh shall waste away. And it shall be as one gathering in the harvest what remains, and his arm shall glean the ears, and it shall be as one seeking ears in the valley of Rephaim. And there shall be left in it as a cluster of grapes, and as the shaking of an olive tree, two or three olives at the top of a branch, or four or five at its summits, its fruit, says the Lord God of Israel. After the remnant of Syria has become as the glory of the children of Israel once was, and through their trespass salvation has been given to the nations, then all the glory of the Jews, by which they were glorious in the whole world, shall be made thin, and the fatness of their flesh shall waste away, since they have no prophets, no signs and mighty works, no present help of God, no dignity of the priesthood; but the whole body of their nation shall wither and be brought to nothing.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.5-6":["And it will be like one gathering in the harvest what has remained, and his arm will gather the ears: and it will be like one seeking ears in the valley of Rephaim. And there will be left in it as it were a cluster, and as at the shaking of an olive tree two or three olives at the top of a branch, or four or five on its topmost boughs, its fruit, says the Lord God of Israel. Those who understand the present devastation of Damascus as under the Roman empire maintain that these words signify the Apostles: that just as a few ears and olives are wont to remain in the field or on the trees, so the remnant of Israel is saved; especially since there follows: In that day man will bow down to his maker, and his eyes will look to the Holy One of Israel, that is, to Christ. The two olives, and three, and four, and five they interpret as fourteen Apostles: that is, the twelve who were chosen, and a thirteenth, James, who is called the brother of the Lord, and also Paul the Apostle, the vessel of election (Acts 9:15). But those who think that what was said was fulfilled in the times of the Assyrians would have it understood thus: that under the Assyrian captivity Damascus was not entirely destroyed, but, when some had been carried away to Cyrene, another part of the tillers of the land was left behind, which itself was afterwards destroyed when the Babylonian laid it waste; until under the Macedonians and the Ptolemies it was restored again, and at the coming of Christ it was indeed a city, but not of such power as it had been before. And the sense is: as few will remain in Damascus as the few ears that commonly remain after the reapers, which the poor are wont to glean in the very broad and very rich valley of Rephaim; or the few olives on the olive tree that have escaped the diligence of the gatherer.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.5":["And since it is said of the calling of the nations: The harvest is great, but the labourers are few (Matt 9), those poor ones shall glean the remnants of the harvest, which were saved through the Apostles, and the scantiest ears, not from the mountains and lofty places, but in the valley of Rephaim, that is, in the lowliness of the letter. And consider that Rephaim, which means giants, signifies the Pharisees and Scribes, as Ephraim did above. Indeed, for the valley of Rephaim the Seventy translated the hard valley, to express the hardness of the Jewish heart.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.6":["Out of their lowliness those who shall be saved are chosen like ears among the stubble and like clusters at the vintage; and as the shaking of an olive tree, two or three olives, or four or five. For when the smiting of the people of the Jews came, that olive tree of the people of Israel, which under Moses had six hundred thousand armed men (Num 26), and under David, when Joab numbered them, a countless people (2 Sam 24), could scarcely offer a few fruits to the Lord the Savior: two olives, Paul and Barnabas; and three olives, Peter and James and John (Acts 13), who both beheld the Lord transfigured on the mountain and were found worthy to go with the Lord to the daughter of the ruler of the synagogue (Matt 17). The four and five olives make up the remaining nine Apostles, among whom Matthias took the place of Judas the betrayer; and these, according to a diversity of graces unknown to us, are divided into four and five olives, so that they might show in themselves the number of the Gospels and the volumes of the Law, as preachers of both Testaments (Acts 1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.7-8":["In that day man will bow down to his maker, and his eyes will look to the Holy One of Israel. And he will not bow down to the altars which his hands have made, and which his fingers have wrought: and they will not look to the groves and the shrines. They think that this was fulfilled in the times of Christ, when, the kingdom of Damascus having been destroyed, the everlasting empire of the Savior succeeded, and the error of idolatry was diminished. The intention of these interpreters is indeed pious, but it does not keep the order of the history. We, however, say that, when Damascus had been overthrown and the ten tribes had been led away to the Assyrians, those who were left of the tribes that belonged to Israel turned, at Hezekiah's letters, to the worship of God, and came to the temple at Jerusalem, as the history of Chronicles relates (2 Chr 30). When Damascus has been destroyed, therefore, men will turn to their maker, that is, to him who created them, and their eyes will look by no means to the idols which they had made in Bethel and in Dan, but to God, despising the shrines and altars which their fingers had wrought.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In that day man shall bow down to his maker, and his eyes shall look to the Holy One of Israel. And he shall not bow down to the altars which his hands have made, and which his fingers have wrought; they shall not look to the groves and the shrines. At that time, when from the people of Israel, to whom it was formerly said, The Lord your God has called you a fruitful olive tree (Jer 11:16), and of whom we read in the Psalms, Your children like young olive plants round about your table (Ps 127 [128]:3), the Lord in his hunger shall scarcely have found, for the drought of spiritual grace, two olives, and three, or four, or five, the fullness of the nations shall come in, and shall bow down not to idols made with hands but to its God, and looking to the Holy One of Israel they shall despise altars and groves and shrines, knowing what is written: Every plant which the heavenly Father has not planted shall be rooted up (Matt 15:13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.9":["In that day the cities of its strength will be abandoned like ploughs, and the crops that were abandoned before the face of the sons of Israel, and it will be deserted. He does not say this against Damascus, but against the ten tribes, which were called Israel: that just as, at the coming of the people of God out of Egypt, all the nations that inhabited the land of promise, struck with sudden terror, abandoned their ploughs and the heaps of grain and their work half done, seeking safety in their feet, so the land of Israel too should remain desolate for a long time. Hence I wonder that for ploughs and heaps of grain Aquila chose to translate potsherd and Emir; Symmachus, forest and Amir; the Seventy, the Amorites and the Hivites. Theodotion alone set down the Hebrew word, ARS ET EMIR: which among them is read more correctly HORES (חרש) ET AMIR (והאמיר), that is, ploughshares and heaps of crops.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In that day the cities of his strength shall be forsaken like ploughs and standing corn, which were forsaken before the face of the children of Israel. And it shall be desolate, because you have forgotten God your Savior, and have not remembered your strong helper. LXX: In that day your cities shall be forsaken, as the Amorites and the Hivites forsook them before the face of the children of Israel. And they shall be desolate, because you have forsaken God your Savior, and have not remembered the Lord your helper. As under Moses and Joshua the son of Nun the Amorites and the Hivites, and the other nations that dwelt in the land of promise, forsook their ploughshares and standing corn and the heaps in the fields and fled, lest they be seized by their enemies, so the land of Judea and all its strongest cities were forsaken by their inhabitants when the Romans laid Judea waste and besieged Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.10-11":["Therefore you shall plant a faithful plantation, and shall sow a strange seed. In the day of your planting wild grapes, and in the morning your seed shall flower: the harvest is taken away in the day of inheritance, and he shall grieve grievously. LXX: Therefore you shall plant an unfaithful plantation and an unfaithful seed, and in the day on which you plant you shall go astray. But if you sow in the morning, it shall flower for harvest in the day of inheritance, when, as the father of a man, he gives an inheritance to his sons. Where we, following Aquila and Symmachus and the Seventy, have translated in the day of inheritance, which in Hebrew is BIOM NEHELA (ביום נחלה), one can read in the Hebrew in the worst day. And where Aquila and Theodotion translated and man shall grieve, we, taught by the Hebrews, instead of man, which in their tongue is ENOS, have rendered ANUS (אנוש), that is, grievously. Of the ambiguity of this word we shall treat more fully, if the span of this life allows, in Jeremiah, where according to the Seventy it is said: And he is a man, and who shall know him? Let me say, then, what I set out to say. Because you have forgotten, land of Judea, God your Savior, and have not remembered him who always gave you strength, therefore you shall indeed plant a faithful plantation, or as Aquila and Theodotion translated, a beautiful one, or as Symmachus, a good one, preaching one God; but you shall sow a strange seed, not receiving the Father because you do not receive the Son. For whoever believes in the Father believes also in the Son. And because you have sown the strange seed of blasphemy against Jesus in the synagogues of Satan, therefore you shall not gather the grape at the vintage, but the wild grape. And when your seed shall seem to flower, and to have some shadow of piety, while you draw men away from idols, yet when you come to gather the fruits in the day of inheritance you shall grieve grievously, seeing the people of the nations preferred to you. Whence the Apostle also says: I have great sorrow and unceasing grief in my heart. For I myself wished to be anathema from Christ for my brethren, who are my kinsmen according to the flesh, who are Israelites, to whom belong the adoption of sons, and the giving of the law, and the glory, and the covenant, and the promises; whose are the fathers and so on (Rom 9:2ff.). This not only Paul but every saint says, wishing that, together with the branches of the wild olive, the root too may be saved. Now that inheritance is the one we obtain from the Lord, of which the Apostle says: Now there are divisions of graces, but the same Spirit; and there are divisions of ministries, but the same Lord; and there are divisions of operations, but the same God, who works all in all. Someone may wonder why, where Aquila and Theodotion and Symmachus translated beautiful and good plantations, we have said a faithful plantation. The Hebrew word NEEMANIM (נאמנים), if it is written with the letter ALEPH, sounds πιστοὺς, that is, faithful; but if with AIN, and pronounced NEAMENIM נעמנים, it signifies beautiful. Hence Naomi too, whose name is written with this letter, says in Ruth (Ruth 1:20): Do not call me NOEMI (נעמי), that is, beautiful, but call me bitter.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.10":["Because you have forgotten God your Savior, and have not remembered your strong helper. These things, he says, you will suffer, O Israel, because you have abandoned God your Savior, who freed you from Egypt, who subjected hostile nations to you: and you have not remembered your helper.\n\nTherefore you will plant a faithful planting, and you will sow a foreign shoot. This is to be read more pointedly and as irony. Because, he says, you have forgotten God your Savior, and have not remembered your strong helper: will you on that account have to plant a faithful planting, and not rather sow a foreign shoot, for the enemy to snatch away? Or certainly thus: my sons, who were begotten from the stock of my people, you have established in the land for this purpose, that you might make them foreign and most wicked.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And an apostrophe is made to the land of Judea itself, that is, to those who dwelt in it: You have suffered all these things because you have forgotten God your Savior, which means Jesus, whose coming to you the Law and the Prophets continually announced; and you have not remembered your strong helper, who was always your succour. The cause, then, of the desolate cities of Judea is forgetfulness of the Savior, who had said at the beginning of this Prophet: Israel has not known me, and my people has not understood.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.11":["In the day of your planting, wild grapes, and in the morning your seed will flower. The harvest has been taken away in the day of inheritance, and it will grieve grievously. These, he says, are the fruits of your works that you will reap: your vine of Sorek will degenerate into wild grapes, your seed will hold out hope in the blade; but when ripeness has come, it will be reaped by another. And then you will grieve grievously, when you lose what you hoped for and almost already held.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.12-14":["Woe to the multitude of many peoples, like the multitude of the roaring sea: and the tumult of crowds, like the sound of many waters: the peoples will roar like the sound of flooding waters: and he will rebuke him, and he will flee far off: and he will be snatched away like the dust of the mountains before the wind, and like a whirlwind before the storm. At eventide: and behold, confusion in the morning, and he will not endure. This is the portion of those who have laid us waste, and the lot of those who plunder us. Those who suppose that the earlier captivity of Damascus was brought on by the Romans refer this also that is written: Man will bow down to his maker, and his eyes will look to the Holy One of Israel, to the times of Christ and of the Apostles. And what follows as well: You will plant a faithful planting, and you will sow a foreign shoot: in the day of your planting, wild grapes, and the rest, they take to concern the unbelief of the Jews. And this passage which we have now set out they interpret of the heathen, who persecute the Church. What follows too, He will rebuke him, and he will flee far off, they take of the devil, showing by a tropological interpretation the devastation of the persecutors and of the demons. But we follow the order we have begun, and cover historical foundations with a historical roof. Woe, he says, to all the nations that have made war against my people, whose onslaught was so great that it must be likened to the waves of the sea. But when they have come raging and have flooded my land, then their prince Sennacherib will flee from him, rebuked; and as dust is scattered when the wind snatches it, and a whirlwind is rolled on high in a storm, so he will indeed approach Jerusalem, besieging it, but he will be struck by the Angel; he will come in the morning, and will see the army of his power destroyed. And this will be the portion of those who have laid us waste. The Prophet says this either in the person of the people, or joining himself to his nation (2 Kgs 19:35-36). Afterwards we read: The Angel of the Lord went out, and struck in the camp of the Assyrians one hundred and eighty-five thousand: and they arose in the morning, and behold, all were the corpses of the dead. And Sennacherib king of the Assyrians went out, and departed, and returned, and dwelt in Nineveh.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.12":["Woe to the multitude of many peoples; like the multitude of the roaring sea, and the tumult of the crowds, like the sound of many waters, they shall roar. The peoples shall roar like the multitude of overflowing waters; and he shall rebuke him, and he shall flee far off, and he shall be carried away like the dust of the mountains before the wind, and like a whirlwind before the tempest. At eventide, behold, trouble; in the morning, and he shall not stand. This is the portion of those who have wasted us, and the lot of those who plunder us. Above, under the name of Damascus, we read of the calling of the nations, and the casting off of the Jews, and, through the Apostles and in the Apostles, the election of the few of the Jews who believed. And since, in comparison with the whole world and all the nations, only a small part believed of the Christian people, of whom it was said above: And the remnant of Syria shall be as the glory of the children of Israel (for many (Matt 22:14) are called, but few are chosen); and: Not all have faith (2 Thess 3:2): now there follows in due order, not a lament, but a woe to those nations which had refused to believe and persecuted the Christian people. And they are compared to the masses of the waves and to the raging sea, which, as far as in it lies, longs to overwhelm and seize everything.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.13":["For the peoples shall roar in the spectacles of theatrical licence, and in the cruelty of the amphitheatre, and in the frenzies of the circus, like the sound of overflowing waters, when with a voice concordant in impiety they blaspheme and cry that the Christians be thrown to the lions and the beasts, and other things of the kind. But while they rage like the sea, the Lord shall rebuke the author of their sedition and drive him away from his people. And as dust stirred up on the mountains is carried off the more violently the higher it is, and as a whirlwind that rises from the earth is borne aloft by a sudden tempest, so he too shall be carried off and separated from the people of God and shall flee, lest he be banished to the abyss.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.17.14":["And when the day of consummation comes, which is what evening means, then there shall be trouble for him as he recognizes his own sins. And at morning time, that is, on the day of the resurrection, he shall not stand, which the Seventy translated shall not be. But if he shall not be, what will they answer who grant the devil repentance and, as far as in them lies, promise him the summit of an archangel? This is the portion of those who have wasted us, and the lot of those who plunder us. These words are spoken either by the Christian people, or by the prophet in the person of the believing people: that their persecutors, who oppressed the saints of God with exiles, prisons and confiscations of goods, shall have eternal destruction and shall possess everlasting punishments.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.18.1-3":["But according to the Seventy the wings of the heretics are bewailed, which flit like ships over the sea of this world and surpass the impiety of the heathen, whom the Scripture now calls Ethiopians; and it is said to their teaching and speech: You who send books on the sea of this world, the hostages of your perversity, and letters to deceive those who will read them. Swift messengers go to a lofty nation, and to a foreign and most wicked people. For none of the churchmen has as much zeal for good as the heretics have for evil, and they think they gain a profit if they deceive others, and, being lost themselves, destroy the rest. Now the nation is called lofty on account of its pride, and foreign and most wicked because it is estranged from God. Truly a nation without hope, and trodden down, whose rivers throughout the whole earth imitate the dwelling of the Church, so that they may find themselves a lodging and a region in which to lift up the sign of their teaching, in which to sound the trumpet of the Scriptures.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.18.1":["Woe to the land, the cymbal of wings, which is beyond the rivers of Ethiopia. The Hebrew word SELSEL (צלצל), which Symmachus interpreted as sound and Theodotion as birds, we have rendered as cymbal. Aquila translated double shadow. But it must be known that a shadow is called SEL (צל), and here the syllable itself is doubled. From this we can understand the saying thus: Woe to the land that promises help in the shadow of its wings. And whereas Scripture says: He who dwells in the help of the Most High will abide in the protection of the God of heaven (Ps 90 [91]:1), for which in the Hebrew is written, He will abide in the shadow of the Almighty, this land, boasting that it has the very likeness of God, promises, though itself set in dangers, to give support to others. And it signifies either No, the city of Egypt which is now called Alexandria, or Egypt, on which Jerusalem always leaned as on a shaken reed, which, when it breaks, pierces the hand of the one who leans on it. And here the order is most beautiful: that just as in the former Vision the prophetic word threatened Damascus, because the ten tribes had help in it, asking God's mercy, so now too devastation is foretold to Egypt, for the sake of whose help the invocation of God was neglected. For this is the land which is beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, that is, beyond the streams of the river Nile, which no one doubts flows from Ethiopia into Egypt. Egypt can be called the cymbal of wings because of the swiftness of its crops: for the swift and rapid flight of birds gives back the sound of a cymbal. Some, from what follows: Who sends envoys by sea, and in vessels of papyrus upon the waters, understand the Roman empire, and refer the whole history to the times of Vespasian and Titus, by whom Jerusalem was overthrown. But it does not accord with our faith that the Lord should threaten the Roman empire for having overthrown an impious nation, and then say that gifts are to be brought to Mount Zion; unless perhaps we take these things too spiritually, of the Church.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Woe to the land, the cymbal of wings, which is beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, which sends ambassadors by the sea, and in vessels of papyrus upon the waters: go, swift Angels, to a nation torn away and rent asunder, to a terrible people, after which there is no other, to a nation waiting, waiting and trodden down, whose land the rivers have plundered. All you inhabitants of the world, who dwell on the earth, when the sign has been lifted up on the mountains you shall see, and you shall hear the blast of the trumpet. LXX: Woe to the land of the wings of ships beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, you who send hostages by the sea, and letters of papyrus upon the water. For swift messengers shall go to a lofty nation, and to a foreign and most wicked people, which is beyond it: a nation without hope, and trodden down; now the rivers of the whole earth as a region that is inhabited: their region shall be inhabited, as if a sign were lifted up from the mountain, and the voice of the trumpet were heard. I have set down both editions in a most obscure prophecy, so that nothing may seem to be lacking to those who wish to understand what is written. And at the same time I greatly marvel at those who think that our faith and Christian hope are content with simplicity, because it is written: The commandment of God is clear, enlightening the eyes (Ps 18 [19]:9), and that we ought to seek nothing more than that what is commanded be done; whereas the whole of Scripture, and the Prophets especially, are wrapped in the mysteries of things to come precisely in order to rouse us to understanding, and to what is said in the Gospel: Ask, and you shall receive; seek, and you shall find; knock, and it shall be opened to you. Since, then, I have expounded it in the fifth book according to the history and what is contained in the Hebrew, I shall now set out what seems to me according to the anagogy. A prudent reader may perhaps ask what is meant, in the Vision or Burden of Damascus, by the cymbal of wings and the other things that follow. After it had been said of the calling of the nations, of the casting off of the Jews, and of the election of those who believed through the Apostles, and afterwards of the multitude of the nations and the persecutors, who were compared to the waves of the sea, it followed that the prophetic word should also announce the heresies which have troubled the Church and lay it waste to this day, which, while the householder slept, oversowed tares in the field of the Church (Matt 13). And they are called a cymbal, as not having the love of God, according to that saying of the Apostle: If I speak with the tongues of men and of Angels, but have not love, I have become as sounding brass or a tinkling cymbal (1 Cor 13:1). And they are called not only a cymbal clashing with a harsh sound, but, because of the lightness of heretical speech running off in different directions, a cymbal of wings, or according to the Seventy, wings of ships, since, promising great merchandise, they sail on the waves of this world. By the wings of ships understand the sails by which they are lifted and drawn along. And he fittingly said wings of ships, for every heretic promises lofty things, and though he boasts that he has wings, he nevertheless sticks fast in the salt waves, and does not go far from land, and in mid-course suddenly suffers shipwreck. Hence Aquila translated, instead of cymbal, shadow of wings, for they do not have wings, but a likeness of wings. And what he adds, which is beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, signifies that the heretics surpass all in their impiety. For example: Epicurus says that there is no providence, and that pleasure is the highest good. In comparison with him Marcion is more wicked, and all the heretics who tear the Old Testament to pieces. For while they admit providence, they accuse the Creator, and assert that he erred in very many of his works and did not make them as he ought to have made them: for to what benefit of men, they ask, do serpents, scorpions, crocodiles, and fleas, bugs and gnats belong?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.18.2":["Who sends envoys by sea, and in vessels of papyrus upon the waters. Go, swift messengers, to a nation convulsed and torn apart, to a terrible people, after whom there is no other, to a nation waiting and trampled down, whose land the rivers have plundered. Among the Hebrews Egypt, and the Egyptian, and the Egyptians are all called by one word, MESRAIM (מצרים). Let this be said lest anyone stick at the word when he finds the masculine in place of the feminine, that is, the man in place of the land; for now too it is said: Who sends envoys by sea, that is, Mesraim herself, the Egyptian himself: because from Alexandria, which was then, as we have said, called No, envoys were sent to Jerusalem, and, in vessels of papyrus, that is, in letters, or else in ships, promising them their help, they said: Go swiftly to the Jews' convulsed nation, torn by the attack of the Assyrians: to a people once terrible, which enjoyed the command of God, to whose power no other is to be compared: to a nation which always waited for the help of God, and nevertheless is trampled down by men: whose land the rivers, that is, various kings, have ravaged. Others, however, think that the apostrophe is directed to the Lord, and that the sense is: O God, who send the Prophets into the sea of this world, and, admonishing the people through letters as if by men under sail, command your messengers: Go swiftly to my nation, torn and convulsed, to a most mighty people, which was once a terror to all the nations round about, which always waited for the help of God, and, because of the greatness of its sins, does not deserve to receive what it hopes for: whose land the kings of various nations have laid waste, and the rest that follows. Eusebius of Caesarea, promising a historical interpretation in his title, wanders off into various senses; and when I read his books, I found something very different from what he promised in the heading. For wherever the history fails him, he passes over to allegory, and joins together things so far apart that I marvel at his combining stone and iron into one body by a new architecture of speech. I have given this brief warning lest anyone think that we have borrowed what we say from his springs; for in the present passage too he says that the prophecy is directed against the Jews and Jerusalem, because at the beginning of the Christian faith they sent letters to all the nations that they should not accept the passion of Christ, and sent them as far as Ethiopia and the Western region, and filled the whole world with the spreading of this blasphemy.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","These, then, are beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, and they send ambassadors on the sea of this world, that is, their disciples, who, carrying their volumes, are rightly said to carry vessels of papyrus, that is, of paper, upon the waters, which are quickly blotted out. For as books by water and damp are soon dimmed, blotted out and perish, so their speech and teaching, though at the beginning it seemed to possess some strength, passes away and slips by. And so it is said to them ironically: O Angels of the heretics, go swiftly. These the blessed apostle Paul calls false apostles, deceitful workers, who transform themselves into Apostles of Christ (2 Cor 11). And go to a nation torn away and rent asunder: torn away from God and mangled by the bites of the heretics. To a terrible people; for nothing is more terrible than blasphemy, which sets its mouth on high. After which there is no other people: for every sin, compared with blasphemy, is lighter. A nation waiting, waiting and trodden down. For all the heretics promise themselves heavenly things and pledge great things, and yet they are trodden down by demons. Whose land the rivers have plundered, rivers which have their waters not from heaven but from the earth. For what corner is there, what remotest wilderness of the earth, which the turbid speech of the heretics does not reach?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.18.3":["All you inhabitants of the world who dwell on the earth, when the signal has been raised on the mountains, you will see, and you will hear the blast of the trumpet. All you nations round about, he says, when you hear my command like a signal raised on the mountains, and my order like the blast of a trumpet resounding on high, then you will see what I have commanded.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And so all who inhabit and dwell in the world lift up their eyes to the sign of the heretics, which is lifted up as it were on the mountains, and hear the blast of the trumpet, that is, of their teaching. So much according to the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.18.4":["For thus says the Lord to me: I will rest, and I will consider in my place. What is it that the Lord spoke to the Prophet? What follows: until the things I have commanded come about, I will rest in my seat: as the Jews think, in the Temple; as we think, in the heavens. And I will consider, he says, what end of things is coming.\n\nAs the noonday light is clear, and as a cloud of dew in the day of harvest. Just as in the whole day nothing is clearer than noon, when the sun glows from mid-heaven and lights up the whole world alike; and just as in the heat and scorching warmth, when the naked reaper is baked and his panting attests the greatness of his labor, the coolness of the dew is most welcome, if the morning moisture makes the dry stalks easy to cut: so my word, which I will consider in my place, will come welcome to all who believe in me.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For thus says the Lord to me: I will rest, and I will consider in my place, as the noonday light is clear, and as a cloud of dew in the day of harvest. For before the harvest it has all blossomed, and an unripe perfection shall bud, and its twigs shall be cut off with sickles; and what is left shall be cut away and shaken off. And they shall be left together to the birds of the mountains and to the beasts of the earth, and the birds shall be upon it all summer long, and all the beasts of the earth shall winter upon it. At that time a gift shall be brought to the Lord of hosts from a people torn away and rent asunder, from a terrible people, after which there was no other, from a nation waiting, waiting and trodden down, whose land the rivers have plundered, to the place of the name of the Lord of hosts, Mount Zion. God rests and contemplates in his place, or according to Aquila, in his firmament, that is, in the Church, of which the apostle Paul says: The pillar and firmament of truth (1 Tim 3:15). And he contemplates the things that are done in the Church, and as the noonday light is clear, so he surveys all things, according to what is said in the eighteenth Psalm, according to the anagogy, of the sun of righteousness: Nor is there anyone who can hide himself from its heat (Ps 18 [19]:7). And as a cloud of dew in the day of harvest and in the burning summer is most welcome, so the Lord refreshes the dwellers of his Church, and in his presence all things flourish.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.18.5":["For before the harvest it has wholly blossomed, and an unripe perfection has budded, and its little branches will be cut off with pruning hooks, and what is left will be cut away and shaken off. And they will be left together to the birds of the mountains and to the beasts of the earth, and the birds will be upon it all summer long, and all the beasts of the earth will winter upon it. Because he had spoken of noon, and had mentioned beforehand a cloud of dew in summer and harvest, and had taken his metaphor from husbandry, he keeps it in the rest, describing the pride of Egypt and the devastation of its people, and the corpses throughout the whole province which are to be devoured by birds. For as crops that burst forth before ripeness quickly perish, and, budding before the fullness of their time comes, are useless, so, he says, the peoples of Egypt will be cut off with pruning hooks like useless branches, and all the offshoots will be stripped bare.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And before the time of the consummation comes, since now we know in part and prophesy in part, many will be found perfect, of whom the Apostle says: Let as many of us as are perfect be of this mind (1 Cor 13; Phil 3:15). But the twigs that are useless shall be cut off with sickles, as the Savior says in the Gospel: I am the true vine, and my Father is the husbandman. Every branch in me that does not bear fruit, he will take it away; and every one that bears fruit he will prune, that it may bear more fruit (John 15:2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.18.6":["And lest you should think he speaks of a vine and not of men, he turns the metaphor into the truth of history: And they will be left, he says, together to the birds of the mountains, and to the beasts of the earth. For birds and beasts devour not the cut branches of trees, but corpses. Let us read Ezekiel more fully, where he prophesies against Pharaoh and against Egypt, and we shall find all these things written most plainly (Ezek 29). And when he says: The birds will be upon it all summer long, and all the beasts will winter upon it, he either truly signifies the multitude of the slain, or shows through the same metaphor that it is to be laid waste by all the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And those that have been cut off shall be left to the birds of the mountains and the beasts of the earth: to the birds which ravage what is sown by the wayside, and to the beasts to which the soul that does not confess God is delivered; so that whoever has been cut off and cast away by the Lord, and separated from his body, which is the Church, becomes, in summer as in winter, that is, in prosperity and adversity, the dwelling of birds and beasts.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.18.7":["At that time a gift will be brought to the Lord of hosts from a people torn away and torn apart, from a terrible people, after whom there has been no other: from a nation waiting, waiting and trampled down, whose land the rivers have plundered, to the place of the name of the Lord of hosts, Mount Zion. After the devastation of Egypt and the destruction of its empire, Israel will by no means trust in the vanity of its shadow, but will return to the Lord, and will bring its gifts to Mount Zion, that is, to his temple, entreating him alone, whose protection is true and eternal. And we understand this to have been done under Zerubbabel and Joshua, and Ezra and Nehemiah. The Seventy, for what we have said, waiting, waiting, where in the Hebrew is written hoping, hoping, translated to the contrary ἀνέλπιστον, that is, not hoping. And for this reason they gave Eusebius occasion to think that this is to be understood of the nations, which have neither hope, nor the Testament of God, nor the Prophets, rather than of the Jews: that gifts were afterwards to be sent by them to the Church, which is set upon a watchtower, and spiritual victims offered.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But as those who are useless and unfruitful in the Church are cut off and cast out, lest a little leaven corrupt the whole lump, so on the contrary it may come about that those who had been deceived by heretical error, torn away from the Lord and rent, terrible on account of blasphemy, vainly awaiting lies, trodden down by demons, and scattered by the rivers in various directions, when they remember their God and forsake their most wicked masters, may offer a gift to the Lord of hosts nowhere else but on Mount Zion, and in the watchtower, which is interpreted as the Church. We speak more briefly because we have already said much in the book of the historical explanation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.1":["The burden of Egypt. Behold, the Lord will ascend upon a light cloud, and will enter Egypt, and the images of Egypt will be moved at his presence, and the heart of Egypt will waste away in its midst. It is the custom of the Scriptures to append plain things to obscure ones, and to bring out in open speech what they had first said in enigmas. Hence in the present place too, because he had threatened Egypt: Woe to the land that overshadows with wings, which is beyond the rivers of Ethiopia, and the rest that the prophetic discourse wove together, he now makes the understanding plainer, and speaks with threats to Egypt itself: that not through angels but the Lord himself comes upon a light cloud, that is, a swift one, and enters Egypt, and the idols of Egypt tremble, and the heart of the strong wastes away, and the prophecy of Ezekiel is fulfilled: I will destroy the images, and I will make the idols cease from Memphis (Ezek 30:13). Some refer this whole prophecy to the times of the Savior, when he entered upon a light cloud, that is, the human body which he had taken from the Virgin, weighed down by no seed of human intercourse; or that he was carried by a light cloud, that is, by the virginal body, and at his entry all the demons trembled, and then came the first ruin of the idols, unable to bear the presence of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The burden of Egypt. Symmachus and Theodotion: The taking up of Egypt. LXX: The vision of Egypt. Where in the Hebrew is written MASSA MESRAIM (משא מצרים), which we have translated The burden or weight of Egypt, for which Aquila rendered ἄρμα Αἰγύπτου, we may say that because the prophet takes up and carries the yoke of the Lord, he proved worthy to behold, or to bear, the prophecy of Egypt. Hence I marvel that the Seventy, both in Babylon translated Vision for Weight, and in Philistia and Moab and Damascus Word, and now in Egypt Vision, when in the Hebrew in all these places what is set down is not Word and Vision but MASSA (משא), that is, Burden and weight. And as for Symmachus and Theodotion always translating λῆμμα, that is, a taking up, we ought to know that the prophet received spiritual grace from the Lord to know the mysteries of Egypt, or to behold them with the eyes of the mind, as the Seventy translated. But this Egypt is not the one the Jews suppose, as Josephus thought, who relates in the books of the Antiquities that the priest Onias fulfilled the oracle of Isaiah by building in the district of Heliopolis, which the Egyptians call a νομὸν, a temple after the likeness of the Temple of God, and an altar. For suppose we grant that these things were said of Onias and the altar he built in Egypt: what will be the five cities in Egypt that speak the Canaanite tongue, of which one according to the Seventy is called ἀσεδὲκ, and according to the other translators the city ARS or ARES, or the city of the sun? And who will be this savior who was sent to the Egyptians to save them, so that the Egyptians might know the Lord? When did they worship him with sacrifices and gifts, and pay their vows? When did the Lord strike Egypt and heal it? At what time shall we be able to show it happened that there was a way from Egypt to the Assyrians, and that the Assyrians entered Egypt, and the Egyptians the Assyrians, and that the Egyptians served Assur? When was Israel the third in Egypt, and among the Assyrians as a blessing in the midst of the earth, so that the Lord should say: Blessed is my people of Egypt, and the work of my hands the Assyrian? It is plain that these things do not belong to that Egypt which the Jews suppose. And so this place which we inhabit, and the world which lies in the evil one, we may call Egypt, especially because MESRAIM (מצרים), which is called Egypt, means ἐκθλίβουσα, that is, afflicting, or reducing to straits. Yet we must know that many things of the Burden, or Vision, of Egypt belong to the province of Egypt, which is inhabited and seen to this day. But both in this and in other places of the Scriptures very many things are set down which could not stand according to the history, so that by the necessity of the matter we are compelled to seek a higher understanding.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Behold, the Lord shall go up upon a light cloud, and shall enter into Egypt, and the idols of Egypt shall be moved at his presence, and the heart of Egypt shall melt in the midst of it. And I will make the Egyptians clash against the Egyptians, and a man shall fight against his brother, and a man against his friend, city against city, kingdom against kingdom. And the spirit of Egypt shall be broken in its bowels, and I will cast down its counsel; and they shall consult their idols and their diviners, and those with familiar spirits, and the soothsayers. And I will deliver Egypt into the hands of cruel lords, and a strong king shall rule over them, says the Lord God of hosts. Most of the prophecy of Egypt, which Isaiah took up or saw, I touched upon in the historical explanation; so now, according to tropology, only the chief points are to be gathered. The Lord went up upon a light cloud, the body of the holy Virgin Mary, which was weighed down by no burden of human seed; or certainly his own body, which was conceived of the Holy Spirit. And he entered into the Egypt of this world, and at once all the idols of Egypt were moved, so that divinations and the whole fraud of idolatry, which held possession of a deceived world, felt itself broken; so much so that the magi from the East, taught by the demons, or understanding from the prophecy of Balaam (Num 24) that the Son of God was born who would destroy all the power of their art, came to Bethlehem, and, the star showing the way, worshipped the child (Matt 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And in Isaiah we read that the Lord came into Egypt on a light cloud. Egypt is taken as the world, into which the Lord is recorded to have descended in the flesh, which, brought forth from a virginal womb, was weighed down by no weight of human seed and of sins.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","\"Behold, the Lord comes into Egypt upon a light cloud.\" By the light cloud we ought to understand either, properly, the body of the Savior, because it was light and weighed down by no sin; or certainly by the light cloud we ought to understand holy Mary, weighed down by no human seed. Behold, the Lord comes into the Egypt of this world upon a light cloud, the Virgin.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77","\"The Lord comes into Egypt upon a light cloud.\" See what he says. The Lord comes, the Lord and Savior, into this Egypt, in which we are: into the place of darkness, where Pharaoh is, the Lord comes. He comes only upon a light cloud. What is this light cloud? It seems to me to be holy Mary, weighed down by no human seed. This light cloud came into the world, and carries with it the Creator of the world. And what is said there in Isaiah? \"The Lord comes into Egypt upon a light cloud: and the idols of Egypt shall be shattered.\" The Lord comes, and the idols of Egypt were shaken and struck and destroyed. This cloud destroyed the Serapeum in Alexandria; not the emperor, a mortal man, but this cloud which came into Egypt.\n— Homily 24, on Psalm 96","This he says also in Isaiah: \"The Lord shall come into Egypt on a light cloud,\" that is, in holy Mary. On a light cloud, because he was not weighed down by human seed.\n— Homily 73, on Psalm 96 (Alternate Series)"],"ISA.19.2":["And I will make Egyptians rush against Egyptians, and a man will fight against his brother, and a man against his friend, city against city, and kingdom against kingdom. When the Lord has entered Egypt and destroys a most powerful nation by his presence, the first victory will be that Egyptians fall out with Egyptians and fight one another with rebellious hatred: which, it is clear, came about in the times of the Assyrians and of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, some sitting still and others resisting. And that he foretells these things of the Babylonian captivity, Jeremiah is witness, saying: Egypt is a choice and beautiful heifer; a goader will come to her from the North. And again: The daughter of Egypt has been put to shame, and delivered into the hand of the people of the North (Jer 46:20, 24). Ezekiel too, agreeing with equal authority of prophecy: And I will make the multitude of Egypt cease, he says, by the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon. And again: I will strengthen the arms of the king of Babylon, and I will put my sword into his hand, and I will break the arms of Pharaoh. And they will know that I am the Lord, when I have put my sword into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he has stretched it out over the land of Egypt, and I will scatter Egypt among the nations (Ezek 30:10, 24ff.). But if we refer these things to the times of the Savior, let us take this example from the Gospel: Do not think that I have come to send peace upon the earth: I have not come to send peace, but a sword. For I have come to set a man against his neighbor, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law, and a man's enemies will be those of his own household (Matt 10:34ff.). And again in another place: Two will be divided against three, and three against two: father against son, and mother against daughter, and daughter-in-law against mother-in-law (Luke 12:52-53).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Then the whole heart of Egypt melted, and Egyptians rose against Egyptians, according to what the Lord says in the Gospel: Do not think that I have come to send peace upon the earth; I have not come to send peace, but a sword. For I have come to set a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law (Matt 10:34ff.). At that time this too was fulfilled: A man's enemies are those of his own household (Mic 7:6). And a man shall fight against his brother, and a man against his friend. For in one house they were divided, two against three and three against two, and father was divided against Son and son against father, city against city and kingdom against kingdom: those who did not believe against those who believed, or else those who believed, longing to save their neighbours.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.3":["And the spirit of Egypt will be broken in its inward parts, and I will cast down its counsel: and they will consult their images and their diviners, and those with pythonic spirits, and the soothsayers. When sedition has arisen in Egypt, some desiring to serve the Babylonian, others unwilling to submit their necks to his yoke; or else some believing in Christ, and others resisting, the spirit of Egypt will be broken and torn apart, since not all wish the same things, and all their counsel will be brought to nothing. Then they will go to their images; and they will ask the diviners, and the soothsayers, and the seers, and those schooled in the magic arts, why these things have happened.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And the spirit of Egypt was broken in its bowels, so that they were no longer of one mind; but, separated against themselves by the spiritual sword, they came to know that all their counsel had been cast down; and nonetheless, remaining in their former error, those who had refused to receive the truth of the faith consulted their idols and their diviners, and those with familiar spirits, and the soothsayers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.4":["And I will deliver Egypt into the hand of cruel masters, and a mighty king will rule over them, says the Lord God of hosts. We follow a twofold understanding: either of the Chaldean times, when Egypt was conquered by Nebuchadnezzar; or of the Roman empire, when, after Antony and Cleopatra had been overcome, Augustus Caesar subjugated Egypt. That the Babylonians were cruel all Scripture testifies: they did not spare little ones, and wounded them with their darts, and had no pity on pregnant women. And that the Roman kingdom was most mighty the Scripture of Daniel also testifies (Dan 7), setting forth the fourth beast, which has teeth and claws of iron.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And when they had done this, the Lord delivered them into the hand of cruel lords, according to that saying of the Apostle: Whom I have delivered to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme (1 Tim 1:20), so that, oppressed by that hardest servitude, they may return to the most merciful Lord. And fittingly he calls the demons cruel lords, for nothing is crueller than they. The strong king also, who is their lord, is plainly understood as the devil, whom the Lord in the Gospel too calls the strong man; and when he is bound and overpowered, the vessels of his house and the spoil are plundered.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.5-6":["And the water shall dry up from the sea, and the river shall be laid waste and dried up, and the rivers shall fail; the streams of the embankments shall be diminished and dried up. The reed and the rush shall wither; the bed of the stream shall be laid bare from its source, and every watered sowing shall be dried up; it shall wither and shall not be. And the fishers shall mourn, and all who cast a hook into the river shall lament, and those who spread a net upon the face of the water, and they shall wither away. And those who work flax shall be confounded, plaiting and weaving fine things, and its watered places shall be drooping, all who made pools to catch fish. Foolish are the princes of Tanis; the wise counsellors of Pharaoh have given foolish counsel. When the strong king, the harsh one, has ruled in Egypt, all the learning and beauty of worldly eloquence shall dry up, and the devil himself, the source of all the rivers, from whom all lies spring, shall be laid waste, so that the other rivers and streams, which used to be filled from the turbid waters of the Nile, shall fail. The reed and the rush too shall wither from excessive drought. For rush the Seventy translated papyrus, from which paper is made, adding of their own green achi, which is not found in the Hebrew. And when I asked learned men what this word signified, I heard that the Egyptians in their own tongue call by this name everything green that grows in a marsh. Now the reed, according to tropology, is empty speech, having nothing solid in itself; and the papyrus is that which seems indeed to have pith and is not empty, and yet is fragile and quickly withers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.5-7":["And the water will dry up from the sea, and the river will be desolated and dried up, and the streams will fail; the channels of the embankments will be diminished and dried up. The reed and the rush will wither, the bed of the stream will be laid bare from its source, and every watered sowing will be dried up, will wither, and will be no more. It is natural that, when captivity has come through the wrath of God, pestilence should follow upon his indignation, and all the elements together should rage against those who have offended God. Hence it is written in another Prophet (Jer 12) that both the birds in the air and the fish in the waters fail, so that all things are withdrawn from human use. This we say if we choose to take it simply of a drought of the river Nile and its streams. But if by metaphor: in the river we understand the kingdom, and in the streams its leaders; and in the greenery, the rush and the papyrus the whole abundance of Egypt, so that through these things the riches of Egypt are described, in which Egypt is most fertile. Let us read Ezekiel, where King Pharaoh is described as a great dragon dwelling in the rivers, and he says: The river is mine, and I made myself. And he hears: I will put a bridle in your jaws, and I will make the fish of your rivers stick to your scales, and I will draw you out of the midst of your rivers, and all your fish will cling to your scales, and I will cast you into the desert (Ezek 29:3-4). At the coming of Christ, however, all these things are to be understood τροπικῶς, in keeping with what we read above: The Lord will make the sea of Egypt desert. And again: The Lord will lay his hand upon the violent river of Egypt, and will strike it in seven valleys, so that one may pass over it with shod feet: namely, that all the error of the Egyptian waters, and the evil arts by which they deluded the peoples subject to them, are dried up at the coming of Christ. And as for his saying: The water will dry up from the sea, we can also take it historically: that what is signified is not the great sea, but Lake Mareotis, because Scripture calls all gatherings of waters seas. These things can also be understood as said hyperbolically. And what follows: The bed of the stream will be laid bare from its source, describes the stream and the source drying up together.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.7-8":["All the streams too, once the head of the streams has dried up, shall be dried up, and whatever was formerly watered by the Egyptian waters shall be dried up and shall not be; so that the fishers of Egypt shall mourn, who are utterly opposed to the fishers of the Lord, and those shall lament who used to cast a hook into the river and spread a net upon the face of the water. Those who deceive people one at a time cast a hook into the turbid waters; but those who deceive many at once, speaking publicly in the synagogues of Satan and leading away whole flocks of the peoples, cast a net upon the Egyptian waters.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.8-10":["And the fishermen will mourn, and all who cast a hook into the river will lament, and those who spread a net upon the face of the water will waste away. And those who work flax will be confounded, plaiting and weaving fine things: and its watered places will be withered: all who made pools for catching fish. Take this too in two ways: that when Egypt has been laid waste and the whole province dried up with drought, the fishermen mourn, and those who used to cast a hook into the river lament, and those who worked nets and dragnets and wove little vessels of various kinds out of rush, that is, the princes and those of royal stock, and the rulers; and that at the coming of Christ all the fishermen of a perverse kind, who, contrary to the apostolic discipline, caught men for perdition, and with a foolish wisdom wove nets and dragnets in which to ensnare the lost, were confounded, and that there is no such fisherman, or a rare one, in the land of Egypt. We see this fulfilled in fact, in that the trophies of the Churches are rising, and throughout Egypt the idols have fallen.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.9":["Those too shall be confounded who used to work flax to make the vestments of the priests, plaiting it and weaving fine things, which belongs properly to the art of the dialecticians. For fine things the Seventy translated byssus, which itself also is woven into the vestments of the priests.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.10":["And what follows, And its watered places shall be drooping, all who made pools to catch fish, signifies that all the snares of the fishers of Egypt shall be destroyed and perish. Where we, following the sense, have translated pools made to catch fish, both in the Hebrew and in all the translators souls are set in place of fish, so that we may be drawn from the history to tropology: namely, that these fishers who made pools and pits made them for this purpose, to ensnare souls in them. It should be noted that for pools the Seventy translated ζύθον, which is a kind of drink made from grain and water, and is commonly called sabaium in the provinces of Dalmatia and Pannonia, in the heathen and barbarian speech. The Egyptians use this most of all, so that they give those who drink not pure waters but turbid ones, like dregs mixed in; so that by a drink of this kind is shown the teaching of heretical depravity.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.11-13":["The princes of Tanis are foolish; the wise counselors of Pharaoh have given foolish counsel: how will you say to Pharaoh: I am the son of the wise, the son of ancient kings? Where now are your wise men? Let them announce to you and show what the Lord of hosts has purposed concerning Egypt. The princes of Tanis have become fools, the princes of Memphis have withered: they have deceived Egypt, the corner of its peoples. That Tanis was the metropolis of Egypt the Psalmist also declares, because Moses worked there the many signs which are described in Exodus: He set his signs in Egypt, and his wonders in the plain of Tanis (Ps 77 [78]:43). That Memphis too was given over to the magic arts, the traces of its old error show to the present time. And this is briefly indicated: that when the Babylonian devastation came, all the counsels of the Magi and of those who promised knowledge of the future were convicted of folly; and that at the coming of Christ all were brought to nothing, since the seers of Egypt could find no counsel how to suppress the Christian doctrine. It is an idiom of the Scriptures to put corner for kingdom, because it holds the peoples together, and is, as it were, the strongest part of the whole house. Hence Christ too, who holds together the walls of the two peoples, is called the corner stone (Eph 2). And his adding: How will you say to Pharaoh, I am the son of the wise, the son of ancient kings, signifies that the Egyptians feign heroes and gods as the founders of their race: Horus, and Isis, and Osiris, and Typhon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.11":["Then the princes of Tanis shall be foolish, which means a lowly command. For all the heretics teach a lowliness contrary to loftiness, and drag men down to the underworld, and are princes of a lowly and abject command. The counsellors too of Pharaoh, who is the king of Egypt, and is rightly rendered scatterer and divided, and separated into various parts, shall be convicted of having given foolish counsel, when the Lord destroys the wisdom of the wise and rejects the understanding of the prudent (1 Cor 1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","How do you say to Pharaoh: I am the son of the wise, the son of ancient kings? Where now are your wise men? Let them tell you, and declare what the Lord of hosts has purposed concerning Egypt. The princes of Tanis have become fools, the princes of Memphis have withered; they have deceived Egypt, the corner of its peoples. The Lord has mingled in its midst a spirit of giddiness, and they have made Egypt err in all its work, as a drunken man errs and vomits. And there shall be no work for Egypt which may make the head and the tail, bending and curbing. The heretics are accustomed to say to their king, or Pharaoh: We are sons of the wise, who handed down the Apostolic teaching to us from the beginning; we are sons of ancient kings, who are called the kings of the philosophers, and we have the knowledge of the Scriptures joined to worldly wisdom.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.12":["These he now asks, or the very king of the heretics, where are his wise men who despised the simplicity of the Church; and he compels them to answer what the Lord Sabaoth has purposed concerning the Egypt of this world, and what he will do at the consummation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.13":["The princes of Tanis, who held the lowly command of the heretics, are proved to be fools. All the princes of Memphis have withered, who were tainted with the boasting of eloquence and of the mouth. For Memphis means mouth or from the mouth, and μεταφορικῶς signifies speech. And what follows: They have deceived Egypt, the corner of its peoples, or according to the Seventy: they shall deceive Egypt by tribes, signifies that the kingdom of worldly wisdom is shown to be foolish, and the princes of the several doctrines, who are interpreted as the tribes, are convicted of having had foolish teachers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.14-15":["The Lord has mingled in its midst a spirit of giddiness: and they have made Egypt err in all its work, as a drunken man errs and vomits: and Egypt will have no work to do, head and tail, bending and bridling. Let us speak first of the translation, and afterwards we shall discuss what is written. For spirit of giddiness one can also translate spirit of error. In what we have translated, bending and bridling, we can also say bent and wanton, so that we understand the old man and the boy. For we, while translating what is written in haste, misled by its ambiguity, rendered the Hebrew word AGMON (אגמון) as bridling, which Aquila translated more expressively στρεβλοῦντα, that is, one who does nothing rightly but everything perversely, so as to signify a boy. The sense, then, is: The princes of Tanis have become fools, and the wise counselors have given Pharaoh foolish counsel, and the princes of Memphis have withered, and they have deceived Egypt, the corner of the peoples, because the Lord has mingled in them a spirit of error and giddiness, so that they do not know what they are saying, and make Egypt err. And as a drunken man, vomiting, brings up what he had taken in, and does not know where he is, but lies out of his mind: so Egypt will have no work or counsel that has either a head or an end, or that suits either the old or the young, of whom the former, through excess of age, are foolish and rave, and the latter, through wantonness and infancy, do not know what they are doing. And whether you wish to take this of the Babylonian devastation or of the coming of Christ, both according to the letter and according to the spiritual understanding, it will hold together.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For the Lord has mingled for them a spirit of giddiness, or of errors, according to what is written: And as they did not see fit to have God in their knowledge, God delivered them up to a reprobate mind (Rom 1:28). And as he who is holy can say with Isaiah, We shall make the spirit of your salvation upon the earth, so he who is a sinner will make a spirit of error, that is, a spirit of wickedness, according to what we read also in Jeremiah: Your own transgression shall reprove you, and your wickedness shall rebuke you (Jer 2:19). But if the heretic who does not receive the Old Testament is scandalized that the Lord is said to mingle a spirit of error or of giddiness, let him hear what is written in the Apostle, that is, in the New Testament: God delivered them up in the desires of their heart to uncleanness (Rom 1:24). And again: For this reason God delivered them up to passions of ignominy. And again: God delivered them up to a reprobate mind, to do those things which are not fitting. Now they are delivered up in the desires of their heart because they changed the glory of the incorruptible God into the likeness of an image of corruptible man, and of birds, and of four-footed beasts, and of creeping things. This we read not only in the Epistle to the Romans, but also in that to the Thessalonians concerning Antichrist: Because they did not receive the love of the truth, that they might be saved, God shall send them the working of error, that they may believe a lie, and that all may be judged who did not believe the truth, but took pleasure in the lie (2 Thess 2:10); which I think the apostle Paul took from the present reading of Isaiah, in which he says: The Lord has mingled for them a spirit of error, and they have made Egypt err in all its works, as, he says, a drunken man errs and vomits, one who is drunk with vices. Of such drunkards Joel speaks: Woe to those who are drunk without wine. And not only drunk, but also vomiting the fury of dragons and the incurable fury of asps; so that, after they have vomited up wine of this kind, they may understand their drunkenness, and recognize that as long as they were drunk they had neither beginning nor end, that is, neither head nor tail, but were an animal maimed at both ends. For beginning and end, as both Symmachus and the Seventy translated, Theodotion, after his custom, set down the Hebrew words themselves, CHAPHPHE (כפה) and AGMON (אגמון), which Aquila translated bent and perverse: wishing old men to be understood by bent, and by perverse wanton boys, who do everything perversely; and hence the sense is that in Egypt not only head and tail are lacking, but also old men and boys, that is, both beginning and end.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.16-17":["In that day Egypt will be like women, and they will be astonished and afraid before the shaking of the hand of the Lord of hosts, which he will shake over it. And the land of Judah will be a festival to Egypt: everyone who remembers it will tremble before the counsel of the Lord of hosts, which he has devised against it. I think it better even to censure one's own error than, while blushing to confess one's inexperience, to persist in error. In what I translated, And the land of Judah will be a festival to Egypt, for festival one reads in the Hebrew AGGA (חגה), which can be interpreted both as festival (whence Haggai too is rendered festive) and as fear; this Aquila translated more expressively γύρωσιν, as when someone, afraid and trembling, casts his eyes about and dreads the approaching enemy. If, then, we choose to take it in a good sense, that the remembrance of Judah is a joy to Egypt, it is rightly called a festival. But if, as I judge, festival is turned into fear, let us understand dread or terror: that when Nebuchadnezzar has come, and all the hands of the brave men have been loosened like those of women, even the name of Judah is a terror to Egypt, because, in wishing to bring it help, it has suffered such great evils. Concerning our own times no one doubts that, in comparison with the Christians, all the heathen are like women, holding feeble opinions, and that whatever they say is turned into folly, while they are astonished at so great a conversion of the nation, and marvel at and understand the hand of the Lord; and whoever of the Gentiles remembers the Christian name confesses with fear the weakness of idolatry.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.16":["In that day Egypt shall be like women, and shall be amazed, and shall fear before the shaking of the hand of the Lord of hosts, which he shall shake over it. And the land of Judah shall be a festival to Egypt; everyone who remembers it shall tremble before the counsel of the Lord of hosts, which he has purposed concerning it. At that time (for that, as we have often said, is what day signifies), when the Lord has mingled a spirit of error and giddiness, so that Egypt vomits up the wine of dragons and the incurable fury of asps, understanding its error and its former drunkenness, Egypt shall fear like a woman: not with the chance fear that is wont to befall men, whom Egypt does not love but strangles and kills, but with the fear of women, whom alone Pharaoh wishes to live. And it shall fear the shaking, or lifting up, of the hand of the Lord, by which punishments are signified, which he will shake and lift up to strike Egypt.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.17":["Then the land of Judah, that is, the knowledge of the Scriptures, the Law and the Prophets, the Gospels and the epistles of the Apostles, shall be a festival to Egypt, if it comes to know them; or a fear, if by comparison with their teaching and truth it understands that it has held a lie. Everyone who remembers this land shall tremble with that trembling which leads to life: For the beginning of wisdom is the fear of the Lord (Prov 9:10). And we can take this not only of the consummation of the world, but of the present time as well: that every heretic fears a churchman learned in heavenly teachings, and is terrified at the remembrance of him. And he shall fear and tremble at the counsel of the Lord, which he has purposed concerning the Egypt of this world. We run through each point briefly, so as to pass on to the rest.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.18":["In that day there will be five cities in the land of Egypt speaking the language of Canaan, and swearing by the Lord of hosts: one will be called the city of the sun. For city of the sun the Seventy, meaning I know not what, translated asedec, which one of our writers interprets as city of righteousness; and, led astray by error, because according to the Hebrew ARES (חרס) is written, he thinks it means earth, which is written with other letters. Symmachus, then, translated better: one will be called the city of the sun; for ARES, an ambiguous word, means both potsherd and sun, because both are parched and dry. Onias, not understanding this place, built a temple in Egypt in the town of Ἡλιοπόλεως. Read the Histories of Josephus (Josephus, book 12, chapter 9). Others would have Ares, that is, ὄστρακον, that is, potsherd, understood as the city of Ostracine, and the other cities near Rhinocorura and Casium, which, it is clear, speak to this day in Egypt the Canaanite tongue, that is, Syriac; and they think that Syrians and Arabs from the neighborhood were transported into that land by Nebuchadnezzar. Further, those who expound the prophecy of the coming of Christ and of the Roman empire take the five cities either as the law of the Lord, which was first translated in Alexandria, or as the five orders of the Church: bishops, presbyters, deacons, the faithful, catechumens; or certainly as the spiritual understanding of the law, of which the Apostle too says: I would speak five words in the Churches with my understanding, rather than ten thousand words in a tongue (1 Cor 14); and they take it that one of the five cities is called the city of the sun, namely of righteousness, in whose wings is healing.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In that day there shall be five cities in the land of Egypt speaking the Canaanite tongue, and swearing by the Lord of hosts: one shall be called the city of the sun. The hand of the Lord lifted up or shaken over Egypt is of great profit, so that the land of Judah becomes a fear to it, and everyone who remembers it trembles. At that time five cities in the land of Egypt shall speak the tongue of Canaan, which most of our writers understand as the five senses: sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch. When we look on a woman to lust after her, our sight speaks the Egyptian tongue. When we hear a judgment of blood, though the Lord says, You shall not receive a vain report (Exod 23:1, LXX), our hearing speaks the Egyptian tongue. When, according to the prophet, we live in delights, and lie upon beds of ivory, and are anointed with the chief ointments, our smell speaks the Egyptian tongue. When our belly is our god (Phil 3), our taste speaks the Egyptian tongue. If we do not hear what the Apostle says, It is good for a man not to touch a woman (1 Cor 7), but on the contrary are joined to a harlot, our touch speaks the Egyptian tongue. But if, on the contrary, we lift up our eyes and see that the fields are already white for harvest, and are no longer bent toward the earth, but, like the woman in the Gospel who for eighteen years could not look up at heaven (Luke 13), lift up our eyes and say, To you have I lifted up my eyes, who dwell in the heavens (Ps 122 [123]:1), our eye and sight speaks the Canaanite tongue. If we circumcise our ears and hear the Lord saying, He who has ears to hear, let him hear (Luke 8:8), our hearing speaks the Canaanite tongue. Whoever can say to the bridegroom, We run after you in the fragrance of your ointments (Song 1:3), and, We are the good fragrance of Christ in every place (2 Cor 2:15), his smell speaks the Canaanite tongue. Taste too is taken in a good sense, of him who eats the bread that comes down from heaven, the living bread and not the dead, and who hears that saying, Taste and see how sweet the Lord is (Ps 33 [34]:9): at once it speaks the Canaanite tongue. And there is a spiritual touch also, of which the apostle John says, Our hands have handled of the Word of life (1 John 1:1); and he has it who touches Jesus by faith, so that the Savior can say of him, Someone has touched me, for I know that power has gone out from me (Luke 8:46). We have learned how great the good things are which the uplifted hand of the Lord bestows; let us ask why the five cities of Egypt speak not the Hebrew tongue but the Canaanite tongue. To this we shall try to answer thus. Hebrew sounds περάτην, that is, one who passes over, who passes from place to place. Therefore we too, though we be saints, as long as we are in Egypt and live in the darkness of this world, cannot speak the Hebrew tongue, but the Canaanite tongue, which is midway between the Egyptian and the Hebrew, and for the most part borders on the Hebrew. Canaan means as it were a moving, or as it were answering. When, therefore, we withdraw from Egypt and wish to go out from the power of Pharaoh, so that our land and our confession are fearsome to Egypt, then we are moved and as it were answer to the will of the Lord; and yet, because we are still in the present world, we cannot yet speak the Hebrew tongue. And what follows, five cities swear by the Lord of hosts, signifies that even while placed here we call to mind not demons but almighty God. Of these five cities, the names of the other four being passed over in silence, one is called the city of the sun, which seems to me to refer to sight. For as a city needs the sun and the moon in order to be seen, so we need the sun of righteousness in order that our eyes may be enlightened.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.19-21":["In that day there shall be an altar of the Lord in the midst of the land of Egypt, and a pillar of the Lord at its border. And it shall be for a sign and for a witness to the Lord of hosts in the land of Egypt. For they shall cry to the Lord because of the oppressor, and he shall send them a Savior and a defender to deliver them; and the Lord shall be known by Egypt. In sequence to what he had said above, In that day there shall be five cities in the land of Egypt speaking the Canaanite tongue, and swearing by the Lord of hosts, there now follows: There shall be an altar of the Lord in the midst of the land of Egypt, which Onias, according to Josephus (Antiquities, book 13, ch. 6), misunderstanding it, tried to fulfil. And the title of the Lord containing his passion, on which was written in Hebrew, Greek and Latin letters, Jesus of Nazareth, king of the Jews (John 19), for a sign of the Cross, and for a witness to all the nations, which are now called Egypt. And when persecution has grown at the hands of those who afflict the Christian name, then they shall cry in their hearts: Abba, Father (Rom 8). And the Lord of hosts shall send a Savior, that is, Jesus, and a Judge, or defender, to deliver them, so that, delivered, they may know the Lord, and themselves be known by the Lord; and where sin abounded, grace may abound the more (Rom 5). Now one altar of Egypt, that is, of this world, is spoken of, so that we may know that all the altars which are raised up against the altar of the Church are not the Lord's. In the book of the Historical Explanation we said that, up to the end of the vision of Egypt, since the prophecy was manifest, all things are to be referred to Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.19":["In that day there will be an altar of the Lord in the midst of the land of Egypt, and an inscription to the Lord at its border. And it will be for a sign and for a testimony to the Lord of hosts in the land of Egypt. For they will cry to the Lord because of the oppressor, and he will send them a Savior and a defender to free them. And the Lord will be known by Egypt, and the Egyptians will know the Lord in that day, and will worship him with victims and gifts, and will vow vows to the Lord and pay them. From this place to the end of the Egyptian Vision, or Burden, both the Jews and our writers would have it understood of the coming of Christ; but they put off their hopes into the future, while we hold them as already accomplished. Take day for time: although Josephus maintains that these things were done under Onias, who, fleeing into Egypt with a very great crowd of Jews, built a temple and an altar, and with vain rashness tried to fulfill the prophecy of Christ. But one altar is spoken of in the way that there is one faith, and one baptism, and one Church. And an inscription to the Lord at its border: there is no doubt that this signifies the Gospel and the writings of the Apostles. For just as above, according to the tropological understanding, the land of Judah, fearful or festal to Egypt, is understood as the Old Testament, so too the inscription at the borders of Egypt is shown to be the history of the Gospels.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.20":["Next he adds: And it will be for a sign and for a testimony, namely of the Lord's passion. Then those who have believed, when Egyptians rush against Egyptians, and a man fights against his brother, and city battles against city, when the time of persecution has broken upon them, will implore the mercy of the Lord, and at once the Savior will come, that is, Jesus, for that is what it means in our language.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.21":["And the Lord will be known by the Egyptians, and they will know him, whether the persecutors who have been overcome, or the believers set free by his present help. And they will worship him with victims and gifts, and will vow vows to the Lord and pay them. Let the Jews answer: It is commanded in the Law that no altar be made except in the one place which the Lord God has chosen, and that victims be sacrificed only by priests of the Levitical race (Deut 26). Behold, Isaiah teaches openly that the Egyptians know the Lord and worship him, and offer victims and gifts, and vow vows and pay them. If the Egyptians have a priesthood, the testimony of Paul is fulfilled in them as well, who says: When the priesthood is changed, there must of necessity be a change of the law (Heb 7:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And the Egyptians shall know the Lord in that day, and shall worship him with sacrifices and gifts, and they shall vow vows to the Lord and pay them. And the Lord shall strike Egypt with a plague, and shall heal it; and they shall return to the Lord, and he shall be appeased toward them, and shall heal them. After the Egyptians have come to know the Lord, they shall worship him with spiritual sacrifices and gifts; and they shall vow vows to the Lord and pay them, and shall say with David: A sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit (Ps 50 [51]:19), and: The lifting up of my hands is an evening sacrifice (Ps 140 [141]:2); when, believing in the Nazarene, they themselves have become Nazirites, drinking neither wine nor strong drink (John 19), nor the vinegar which was offered to the Lord to drink, nor whatever is made from the grape of Sodom. And when they have paid their vows with Abel, and God has had regard to them, the elder brother Cain, that is, the people of the Circumcision, will envy, and will shed Christian blood, which shall cry out to the Lord (Gen 4); and therefore he shall go out from the face of God, saying of the Savior: Crucify, crucify him (Luke 23:21), and: We have no king but Caesar (John 19:15). He who is holy in body and spirit offers a vow and pays it to the Lord. Zacchaeus too offered a vow, who promised half of his substance to the poor (Luke 19).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.22":["And the Lord will strike Egypt with a blow, and will heal it: and they will turn to the Lord, and he will be appeased toward them, and will heal them. For whom the Lord loves he rebukes, and he chastises every son whom he receives. Persecution serves not for the denial of believers, but for their proving and their crown.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","It is asked: if a savior and defender was sent to the Egyptians to deliver them from their straits, how is it now said, The Lord shall strike Egypt with a plague? But let us consider what follows: And he shall heal it. For whom the Lord loves he chastens (Heb 12). And the Savior himself in Psalm 68 (69) says to the Father: For they have persecuted him whom you struck, and they have added to the pain of my wounds (Ps 68 [69]:27). If, then, he did not spare his own Son, but delivered him up for us, that by his bruise and wounds we might be healed (Rom 8), the Lord delivered up the martyrs too to suffering, but he will heal them again in the resurrection, so that by their wounds the faith of believers may be confirmed. Hence it is said also to Job: Do you think that I spoke to you otherwise than that you might appear righteous? For he himself causes pain and restores to former health; and he visits his servants with the rod, so as not to take away his mercy from them. Hence the daughters and daughters-in-law who have sinned greatly, and have spread their feet to everyone who passed by, are not visited or chastened, for the Lord says: I will not visit your daughters when they commit fornication, nor your daughters-in-law when they commit adultery (Hos 4:14). The Lord, then, strikes the Egyptians, not with fire, not with the sword, but with the rod. For what son is there whom his father does not train? So that, after they have been healed, they may return to the Lord, and he may be appeased toward them, and heal them once more. For we always need the mercy of God, and there is no end to his clemency.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.23":["In that day there will be a road from Egypt to the Assyrians, and the Assyrian will enter Egypt, and the Egyptian the Assyrians: and the Egyptians will serve Assyria. Before the coming of Christ each nation had its own king, and no one could go from one nation to another; but in the Roman empire all things have been made one. Let the learned reader turn over the ancient histories, and he will find that the whole region from the Euphrates to the Tigris, and all between, belonged to the Assyrians. Those, therefore, whom the ancients called Assyrians we now call Syrians, naming the whole from the part. And when it is said: The Egyptians will serve the Syrians, it is to be taken either thus: that Roman legions furnished with Syrian soldiers guard Egypt; or that the two nations trade with each other, and the cities of Syria receive the abundance of Egypt, just as, conversely, Egypt is watered with the goods of Palestine and Phoenicia. Some of our writers wrongly refer these things to the thousand years, and in the Jewish manner declare that they will come to pass at the consummation of the world, when the Antichrist, coming from the Assyrians, has taken possession of Egypt and Ethiopia.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In that day there shall be a way from Egypt to the Assyrians, and the Assyrian shall enter into Egypt, and the Egyptian into the Assyrians; and the Egyptians shall serve the Assyrians. As we have shown that the other things which go before befell the Egyptians in a good sense, namely, five cities in their land speaking the Canaanite tongue and swearing by the Lord, and the altar of the Lord set in the midst of the land of Egypt, and the pillar, and the witness, and the sign, and the savior who should deliver them; and that the Lord should be known by the Egyptians, and they themselves should know him; and that they should offer sacrifices and gifts, and pay their vows, and, being struck, be healed, and return to the Lord, and he be appeased toward them, and they be healed again: so this too which follows, that the Egyptians serve the Assyrians, is to be taken in a good sense. For the Apostle too serves believers, that he may gain them (1 Cor 9). And Esau is made subject to his brother Jacob (Gen 33), that he may become a sharer in his blessings. Therefore those from the nations who are saved first, and have the altar of the Lord within them, will by their service save those who persist in hardness; and by mingling and fellowship with them they will themselves go to the Assyrians, in order to lead the Assyrians to Egypt, and so that afterwards they may be able to reach the people of Israel. For this reason, I think, the believing wife too serves the unbelieving husband, so that little by little she may draw him from Egypt and from the Assyrians to Judea.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.24-25":["In that day Israel shall be the third to the Egyptian and the Assyrian, a blessing in the midst of the earth, which the Lord of hosts has blessed, saying: Blessed is my people of Egypt, and the work of my hands the Assyrian; but my inheritance is Israel. Israel shall be the third in Egypt and among the Assyrians, that he may mingle the whole lump with the leaven of his blessing, and that those who formerly were at hostile variance with one another may be joined together by this clasp of blessing; and that Egypt may be the people of God, and the Assyrian the work of his hands, but Israel his inheritance. The Egyptian is blessed by the Lord, because he is blessed through fellowship with Israel. And the Assyrian is the work of his hands, because in him he has shown his clemency. But Israel alone can say: The Lord is my portion (Lam 3:24), he who beholds God with the mind, and is called his inheritance.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.24":["In that day Israel will be the third with the Egyptian and the Assyrian, a blessing in the midst of the earth, which the Lord of hosts has blessed, saying: Blessed is my people of Egypt, and the work of my hands to the Assyrian: but my inheritance is Israel. By no means will Israel be crushed in the middle between Egypt and the Assyrian, now with the Antiochi and Demetrii dragging the kingdom of Judah to themselves, now with the Ptolemies claiming its possession for themselves; but it too, established under the Roman empire, and through this under the empire of Christ, will be of the same condition as Egypt and the Assyrians, and will be blessed in all the earth. For out of Zion will go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.19.25":["Then it will be said by the Lord: Blessed is my people of Egypt: when, led not by Moses but by Christ the Lord, countless thousands of men have filled the deserts and, with Pharaoh drowned, have said in the desert: Let us sing to the Lord, for he has been gloriously magnified: horse and rider he has cast into the sea (Exod 15:1). Then also the work of the hands of the Lord will be among the Assyrians; for these nations above all, Egypt and Mesopotamia, flourish with swarms of monks, and vie with one another in equal piety. And the inheritance of Christ is Israel, that is, the places of his nativity, and of his cross, and of his resurrection and ascension, to which men flock from the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.20.1-2":["And this is to be noted, that before Ashdod is taken the Prophet is clothed in sackcloth and has his feet shod, so that he may indeed lament those who have been wounded by the fiery darts of the devil and serve lust, but may nonetheless himself go shod, so as to be able to tread upon serpents and scorpions, and walk safely through the desert of this world, in which there are serpents and scorpions and thirst for good things. But after Ashdod has been taken, as a type of the captivity of Egypt and the exile of Ethiopia, he walks barefoot and naked. For in the holy Land, to which he hastens to go, he could neither stand nor walk clothed in sackcloth and with his feet covered with skin, for the Lord says: Loose the shoe from your feet, for the place on which you stand is holy ground (Exod 3:5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.20.1":["In the year in which Tartan came into Azotus, when Sargon king of the Assyrians had sent him, and he had fought against Azotus and taken it. At that time the Lord spoke by the hand of Isaiah son of Amos, saying: Go and loose the sackcloth from your loins, and take your shoes from your feet: and he did so, walking naked and barefoot. And the Lord said: As my servant Isaiah has walked naked and barefoot, a sign and portent of three years will be upon Egypt and upon Ethiopia. So will the king of the Assyrians drive off the captivity of Egypt and the exile of Ethiopia, young and old, naked and barefoot, with buttocks uncovered, to the shame of Egypt. And they will be afraid and ashamed of Ethiopia their hope, and of Egypt their glory. And the inhabitant of this island will say in that day: Behold, this was our hope, those to whom we fled for help, to free us from the face of the king of the Assyrians; and how shall we be able to escape? We have set down the whole content of this passage so that we may discuss each point part by part. Azotus, which in Hebrew is called ESDOD (אשדוד), was a most powerful city of Palestine, one of the five cities: Sargon king of the Assyrians, who is called by seven names, sent the commander of his army, whose name was Tartan, and took it and held it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In the year that Tartan came into Ashdod, when Sargon the king of the Assyrians had sent him, and he had fought against Ashdod and had taken it, at that time the Lord spoke by the hand of Isaiah the son of Amoz, saying: Go, and loose the sackcloth from your loins, and take your shoes off your feet. And he did so, walking naked and barefoot. And the Lord said: As my servant Isaiah has walked naked and barefoot, it shall be a sign and portent of three years upon Egypt and upon Ethiopia. So shall the king of the Assyrians lead away the captivity of Egypt and the exile of Ethiopia, young and old, naked and barefoot, with buttocks uncovered, to the shame of Egypt. And they shall be afraid and ashamed of Ethiopia their hope, and of Egypt their glory. And the inhabitant of this isle shall say in that day: Was this our hope, to whom we fled for help, that they might deliver us from the face of the king of the Assyrians? And how shall we be able to escape? For Tartan the Seventy translated Thanatan, and for Sargon, Arna: what these mean we cannot know. For we cannot invent false etymologies for false names. Now these are not Hebrew names but Assyrian, and of them we have learned that they sound thus: Tartan, he gave a tower, or superfluous, or putting far off; and Sargon prince of the garden. This king of Assyria, whom we read of above as the great mind, has very many generals, of whom one is Tartan, lifted up in pride, and going far in wickedness, and greater than the rest; and he is sent to attack Ashdod, which in Hebrew is called ASDOD (אשדוד), and means fire of generation; and the general of the king of Assyria overcomes those who serve generation and lust. And the king of the Assyrians, Sargon, prince of gardens, is fittingly said to be given over to pleasure and luxury. Indeed Ahab king of Israel too desired to turn the vineyard of Naboth into a garden (1 Kgs 21); and Naboth, understanding this according to the laws of tropology, chose to die rather than do it, lest his father's inheritance and ancient possession be turned into the delights of an impious king.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.20.2-3":["Now the Prophet is commanded to lay aside the sackcloth and the shoes and walk naked and barefoot, and to be a sign and portent to the Egyptians and the Ethiopians, who persecuted the people of God and were made low on account of their pride. For Egypt means persecuting or afflicting; Ethiopians, lowly and cast down; for everyone who exalts himself shall be humbled (Luke 14:11). These are to be led into captivity and to suffer torments of three years (Exod 12), of which years we read in the Psalms: I thought upon the days of old, and I had the eternal years in mind (Ps 76 [77]:6). Let us not think these are small punishments, but punishments drawn out over long ages.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.20.2-4":["At the time, then, when the neighboring city was taken, Isaiah is commanded to lay aside his tunic of sackcloth (for this was the garb of the prophet lamenting the sins of the people), and the shoes from his feet, which the Seventy call caligae, and to go about naked and barefoot, presenting a type of the captivity of the Egyptians and of the Ethiopians, who had been a help to the Egyptians: that just as Isaiah walked naked and displayed his unsightliness to the onlookers with bare buttocks, so all Egypt and Ethiopia, when the Assyrian ravaged them, should become naked, and nothing at all should remain in the land. And let no one think this contrary to the prosperity promised above to the Egyptians: for there, after the evils, future blessedness is foretold; whereas here the present captivity is narrated, in which, having destroyed Israel and Damascus and the Philistines, he went straight on to Egypt, and overcame them and the Ethiopians alike. And lest this seem doubtful to anyone, let us set down the testimony of Isaiah himself: And Rabshakeh returned, and found the king of the Assyrians fighting against Libnah: for he had heard that he had set out from Lachish: and he heard men saying of Tirhakah king of Ethiopia: He has come out to fight against you (2 Kgs 19:8-9). And at the same time we learn the obedience of the prophets: that a man of noble birth (for the Hebrews relate that Isaiah was the father-in-law of Manasseh son of Hezekiah king of Judah) did not blush to go about naked, but, judging nothing more honorable than the commands of God, laid aside the sackcloth, and when it was thrown off he was naked, having had before only one tunic, and that of haircloth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.20.3":["And when it is said: A sign and portent of three years will be upon Egypt and upon Ethiopia, this signifies that Egypt and Ethiopia were to be laid waste by the Assyrians for three years.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.20.4-6":["And in that very captivity and exile of Egypt shall go the young and the old, who have been hardened in wickedness and have come to the vices of full age: naked, so that all their crimes may be laid bare (for nothing (Luke 8:17) is hidden that shall not be revealed), and barefoot, because they will not be able to eat the Passover of the Lord; for whoever eats it has his loins girded, and holds a staff in his hand, and stands with shod feet, lest, passing through the wilderness of this world, he be bitten by serpents (Exod 12). Then their buttocks, through which dung is voided, shall be uncovered, and all the shame of Egypt shall be shown, so that those shall be confounded who had their hope in Egypt and in Ethiopia, and shall see their glory changed into confusion; so much so that the inhabitant of this isle, that is, of the world, who is not a stranger and a sojourner, but longed to have a perpetual possession of the world, shall say in his confusion: Is this Egypt, and is this Ethiopia, whose help we hoped for, that they might deliver us from the prince of this world? How then shall we be able to escape, when those in whom we had our hope are taken captive?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.20.5-6":["Then, he says, the inhabitant of this island will say, that is, Jerusalem, which is beaten by the waves of the neighboring nations: Was this our hope? And did we flee to these to give us help, who could not rescue themselves from the evil of captivity? This, then, is the order of the providence of God, who disposes the whole race of men by his ineffable judgment. Against the wrath of God Israel hoped in Damascus: let the city be destroyed which, against his will, gives help to the impious. Judah hoped in the Egyptians: let Egypt too be destroyed. The Egyptians trusted in the Ethiopians: let the Ethiopians too be conquered by the Assyrians. The Assyrians grew proud, thinking the victory to be not God's but their own strength's: let them too be conquered by the Babylonians. Babylon lifted up its head against God: let it too be overcome by the Medes and Persians. The Persians and Medes in part persecuted the people of God, and the most savage ram tossed all the peoples toward the East and the West: let the he-goat Alexander come and trample him under his feet. And let him who was lifted up beyond measure perish by poison, and his kingdom be divided into parts; and when it has long been dashed against itself, let it be ravaged as the Roman conquers. The Roman himself has torn the flesh of the saints with iron teeth and claws and with bloody mouth: let a stone be cut from the mountain without hands, and let it crush like potsherds the kingdom that was first most mighty and of iron, and then brittle and weak (Dan 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.1-2":["As whirlwinds come from the southwest, it comes from the desert, from a terrible land. A hard vision has been announced to me. The voice of the Babylonian people in fear is brought in, or of Babylon itself, because it hears that the Medes and Elamites are preparing an army against it and are coming from the wilderness; and it sets forth an example by way of comparison. As, it says, a violent storm usually comes from the southwest wind, so devastation comes upon me from the wilderness, from a terrible land, whose very name I cannot hear without dread. A hard vision has been announced to me: for what is harder than captivity at hand?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.1-3":["And rightly, according to the Hebrew, whirlwinds and tempests come from the desert and from a terrible land, where God is not the inhabitant, and where all things are earthly; and whoever is unbelieving does a thing in keeping with his faithlessness, and he who is a spoiler lays waste. Hence he speaks boldly against his adversaries: Go up, Elam; besiege, O Mede: I have made all the groaning of the desert of the sea, and of the terrible land, and of the hardest vision that has been told, to cease. For my loins, doing penance, are full no longer of pleasure, as before, but of pain; and I shall no longer say: My loins are filled with illusions (Ps 37 [38]:7). For anguish and tribulation have taken hold of me, as it is wont to hold a woman in labour. The common edition and the Hebrew differ greatly from each other in this place; therefore we shall touch briefly on both, lest we leave the φιλεγκλημῶσιν some little occasion of carping at us.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.1":["The burden of the desert of the sea. If I did not read in what follows in this chapter: Babylon, my beloved, has become a wonder to me; and again: Fallen, fallen is Babylon, and all the graven images of her gods are broken on the ground; and above: Go up, Elam, and besiege, O Mede, I would be in doubt what this Burden is that is laid upon the desert of the sea. It is plain, therefore, that Babylon is called the desert of the sea, as Jeremiah says in the person of God: And I will make her sea desert, and I will dry up her land, and Babylon will become heaps of sand (Jer 51:36-37). And it is called sea because of the multitude of its inhabitants. Hence above, too, the multitude of Egypt was compared to the sea: The Lord will make the sea of Egypt desert. And that the Medes and Persians fought against Babylon and overthrew it, we read in the aforesaid Jeremiah: Sharpen the arrows, fill the quivers. The Lord will rouse the spirit of the kings of the Medes, and his mind is against Babylon, to destroy it, for it is the vengeance of the Lord, the vengeance of his Temple. Upon the walls of Babylon lift up a signal, strengthen the watch, set up watchmen, prepare ambushes: for the Lord has purposed, and has done all that he spoke against the inhabitants of Babylon. And again: Lift up a signal in the land, sound the trumpet among the nations, sanctify the nations against her, proclaim against her to the kings of Ararat, Minni, and Ashkenaz, number Tapsar against her, bring up the horse like the stinging locust. Sanctify the nations against her, the kings of Media, its leaders and all its magistrates, and all the land of its dominion. And the land will be shaken and troubled, because the thought of the Lord has kept watch against Babylon, to make the land of Babylon desert and uninhabitable (Jer 51:11ff.).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The burden of the desert of the sea. As whirlwinds come from the south-west, it comes from the desert, from a terrible land. A hard vision has been told to me: he who is unbelieving deals faithlessly, and he who is a spoiler lays waste. Go up, Elam; besiege, O Mede: I have made all its groaning to cease. Therefore my loins are filled with pain; anguish has taken hold of me, like the anguish of a woman in labour. LXX: The vision of the desert of the sea. As a tempest passes through from the desert, coming from the desert. From a terrible land a hard vision has been announced to me: the transgressor transgresses, and the wicked man does wickedly. Against me come the Elamites and the ambassadors of the Persians: now I shall groan, and comfort myself; therefore my loins are filled with anguish, pains have seized me, as a woman in labour. What we think according to the History we have said briefly; now let us gather the chief points of tropology. The vision, or weight, is seen against the sea of this world; and it is seen by the Prophet with how many temptations this world is filled. That the world is called the sea (to pass over many things), I am content with a single testimony of the Psalm: They who go down to the sea in ships, doing business in many waters, these have seen the works of the Lord, and his wonders in the deep (Ps 106 [107]:23). For those who do the work of God in this world, and say with the Prophet, I have come into the depth of the sea, see his wonders in the deep; and, delivered from temptations and straits, they say that they have heard a terrible and hard vision. Now this very tempest comes from the desert, in which the Lord too was tempted (Matt 4), and Israel lay open to the bites of serpents and the stings of scorpions (Num 21).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.2":["He who is unbelieving acts faithlessly: and he who is a despoiler lays waste. In the Hebrew it can be read thus: You who slay, slay; and you who lay waste, lay waste, so that the prophetic utterance is directed to the Elamite and the Mede himself, urging him to accomplish what he has begun, to go up fearlessly, to besiege the most mighty city. But if it is read as we have translated, as though spoken of a third person, he who is unbelieving acts faithlessly: and he who is a despoiler lays waste, it is to be joined with what precedes, in which Babylon declares that a hard vision has been announced to it.\n\nGo up, Elam, besiege, O Mede: I have made all its groaning cease. Do not fear, he says, O Persian and Mede, the multitude of Babylon; do not dread its past power: I have made to cease all its groaning and the weight with which it used to oppress you before; either because no one now groans and grieves, oppressed by the power of Babylon; or because Babylon itself is oppressed by such great evils that it does not even have freedom to groan.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And when it has come, it passes through and goes by; and then he who endures it understands that none transgresses but the transgressor, and none does wickedly but the wicked. That we are overwhelmed, then, by the waves of the sea, and that a savage tempest overpowers us, is our own fault, who were transgressors and wicked before the tempest. And what he says, Against me come the Elamites and the ambassadors of the Persians, has this sense: Elamites means despisers; Persians, tempters. Let those come, then, who are wont to despise, to scorn and to tempt: but I shall groan, and my groaning will be my consolation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.3-4":["Therefore my loins are filled with pain: anguish has possessed me, like the anguish of a woman giving birth: I fell down when I heard, I was troubled when I saw. My heart has withered, darkness has stupefied me. Babylon, my beloved, has been made a wonder to me. The Prophets are obscure for this reason, that the persons in them change very often. So now too the voice of Isaiah is brought in, lamenting Babylon in the prophetic spirit: that such great evils are to come upon it that he who tells of them, overcome by fear, cannot burst out into words, seeing the blood of so great a multitude shed; and, moved by the feeling of pity (for he is a man speaking of men), he grieves no less than a woman is wont to howl in childbirth; but, troubled and trembling, and falling to the ground, with his eyes darkened, he does not know what he is saying. And for what we have rendered: Babylon, my beloved, in the Hebrew one reads NESEPH ESCI (נשף השקי); and it is the very word which we set down at the beginning of the Babylonian Burden, upon a misty mountain: for there Neseph is written for misty or dark. And this city is properly so called because of its height and the summit of its pride raised up to heaven.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.3-5":["I fell down when I heard, I was troubled when I saw; my heart withered, darkness stupefied me; Babylon my beloved has been made a wonder to me. Set the table, behold in the watchtower those who eat and drink: rise up, princes, seize the shield. LXX: I did wickedly so as not to hear, I hastened so as not to see; my heart goes astray, iniquity has overflowed upon me; my soul stands fast in fear. Prepare the table, watch the watchtower: eat, drink; rise up, princes, prepare the shields. At the hearing and at the hardest vision which the Prophet saw coming from the desert of the sea, he says that he fell down and was troubled, and that with his eyes almost darkened and his mind stupefied he did not know what he was seeing. For that Babylon (for which Aquila and Theodotion translated darkness, to signify this world, which lies in the evil one (1 John 5), and whose princes, according to the apostle Paul (Eph 6), are the rulers of this darkness), which was once dear either to the Prophet or to God, has become a wonder in its overthrow. Hence the Prophet is commanded, eating at the table of the Lord and filled with his food, to contemplate more carefully what is to come upon the world; and through him it is said to all believers that, eating and drinking the body and blood of the Lord, they should be turned into princes of the Church, and hear with the Apostles, Rise up, and seize the shield of faith from the armour of the apostle Paul (Eph 6), with which they may quench the fiery darts of the devil. This according to the Hebrew and the interpretation begun concerning the world. Let us pass on to the edition of the Seventy, which differs greatly from the foregoing. The Prophet reproves himself, or rather in his own person confesses the error of others, who, following the letter that kills, despise the spirit that gives life; and he says that he did wickedly so as not to hear the law spiritually, but on the contrary hastened not to see the mysteries of God, and did not say with David: Unveil my eyes, and I shall consider the wonders of your law (Ps 118 [119]:18). Therefore his heart goes astray, and, filled with Jewish superstition, stands fast not in the love of God but in fear, so that he has the spirit of bondage in fear, and by no means the spirit of adoption, in which we cry, Abba, Father (Rom 8). Hence he is commanded to come to the table of spiritual food, and that all who follow his example should eat and drink at it; and that, having scorned the old error, those who lay in the letter should rise up in the spirit and become princes, and say with the Prophet: Lord, you have crowned us as with the shield of your good will (Ps 5:13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.3":["But my loins too are filled with anguish, and pains have taken hold of me as of a woman in labour, so that from the fear of the Lord I may conceive and bring forth, and make the spirit of his salvation upon the earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.5":["Set the table, watch in the watchtower those eating and drinking: arise, princes, seize the shield. This place is taken in two ways. O Medes and Elamites, to whom I said above: Go up, Elam, besiege, O Mede, take food, prepare yourselves for the coming battle, lest, when the time of fighting has come, weariness hold you back. And when you have eaten and drunk, arise, seize your arms, vanquish the Babylonian. And his saying, watch in the watchtower, is read in this sense: look out diligently for what is to come. It can also be understood thus: O Babylon, prepare a table and a banquet for Belshazzar your king, son of Evil-merodach, grandson of Nebuchadnezzar, and see what happens after the meal, at which he will drink toasts to harlots and concubines from the vessels of the Lord. O princes, who recline at table with the king (and he means the Medes and Persians), arise and seize your arms to kill the king. We learn this more fully in the volume of Daniel (Dan 5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.6-7":["For thus the Lord said to me: Go and set a watchman, and let him declare whatever he sees. And he saw a chariot of two horsemen, a rider on an ass, and a rider on a camel; and he gazed diligently with much attention, and a lion cried out. LXX: For thus the Lord said to me: Go and set for yourself a watchman, and whatever you see, declare it. And I saw two horsemen riding, a rider on an ass, and a rider on a camel; I heard much hearing, and I called Uriah to the watchtower. The Prophet is bidden to set a watchman in his heart, and to gaze more carefully at what is to come upon the world; and he saw two horsemen, a rider on an ass and a rider on a camel. These someone has interpreted so as to call the rider on the ass, according to the Gospel reading (Matt 21) and the prophecy of Zechariah (Zech 9), Christ; and on the contrary the rider on the camel the opposing power, on account of the foulness of that crooked animal. Others again refer the two riders, of the letter and of the spirit, to the two Testaments.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.6-8":["For thus the Lord said to me: Go and set a watchman, and let him announce whatever he sees. And he saw a chariot of two horsemen, a rider on an ass, and a rider on a camel. And he gazed diligently with much attention, and the lion cried out: Upon the watchtower of the Lord I stand continually by day, and upon my watch I stand whole nights. Behold, there comes this rider, a man of the two-horsed chariot; and he answered and said: Fallen, fallen is Babylon, and all the graven images of her gods are broken on the ground. My threshing, and son of my threshing floor, what I have heard from the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, I have announced to you. He gives the reasons for the fear above: why anguish possessed him like a woman in labor, and he fell down on hearing, and was terrified and surrounded with dark horror. Thus, he says, the Lord said to me: Go and appoint a watchman, to foretell the future to you in the prophetic spirit; and when I had set him in the watchtower, he saw a two-horse chariot coming, and a charioteer upon it, and an ass and a camel drawing the chariot. And he signifies Cyrus king of the Persians and of the Medes, coming with a small and a great empire. For before Cyrus the Persians were obscure, and were held of no account among the nations; the Medes were always most mighty. This one rider, then, supported by the army of two nations, comes against Babylon: and that watchman who had been set to look out for what was to come cried out like a lion; or else the prophet himself is called a lion.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.8":["And because there was doubt about the person of the watchman who had seen Cyrus coming up, he himself explains it more plainly, and says: Upon the watchtower of the Lord I stand continually by day, declaring himself to be the watchman of the Lord, who, always set in the prophetic office, and obeying the command of the Lord by day and by night, speaks whatever he commands.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But what the Seventy interpreters meant in this place, in translating uriam for lion, which in Hebrew is called ARIA (אריה), I do not well understand, especially since the aforementioned priest Urias, who is called to bear witness, is written with other letters.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And for what is said in the Hebrew, ARIE (אריה), for which Aquila and Symmachus translated lion and lioness, the Seventy, meaning I know not what, set down οὐρίαν, which a certain man thinks is to be interpreted light of the Lord, though light of the Lord, which is not found here, is written with other letters, and lion, which is read here, with others. And he would have this watchman, whom the Prophet is bidden to set in his heart, be called Uriah, and by the interpretation of the name he refers it to the understanding of Christ, so that, with him dwelling in us, we may be able to see what is to come. This indeed could also be fitted to the lion, since Jacob (Gen 49) and Balaam (Num 23 and 2) name the lion under the mystery of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","I am upon the watchtower of the Lord, standing continually by day, and I am upon my guard, standing whole nights. Behold, this man comes, the rider of a chariot of horsemen, and he answered and said: Fallen, fallen is Babylon, and all the graven images of her gods are broken to the ground. My threshing, and the sons of my floor, what I have heard from the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, I have declared to you. LXX: The Lord said: I stood all day, and upon the camp I stood all night; and behold, he comes, the rider of a chariot, and answering he said: Fallen, fallen is Babylon, and all her idols and the things made with hands are broken to the ground. Hear, you who are left and are grieving; hear what I have heard from the Lord Sabaoth; the God of Israel has declared it to us. The Prophet stands on the watchtower of the Lord, and, set in his light, looks forward to what is to come. And this is the guard he keeps, this the office entrusted to him: to see, in the darkness of this world, what is to come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.9":["What, then, did the lion cry out? Behold, there comes this rider, a man of the two-horsed chariot: there is no doubt that Cyrus is indicated. And the lion himself, who had cried out above, answered and said: Fallen, fallen is Babylon, and all the graven images of her gods are broken on the ground. For when the rider of the two-horse chariot laid it waste, Babylon was destroyed forever, and the temple of Bel and all the idols were leveled to the ground.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Behold, he says, this man comes, signifying by what follows that he saw the Lord the Savior coming, seated on a chariot of two, and yoking to one car two horses, or rather two animals, the ass and the camel. And this rider of the chariot answered the Prophet, who longed and desired to hear his words, and said: Fallen, fallen is Babylon, the confusion of the whole world; it fell at my coming, when I took a human body, and it will utterly collapse at the consummation of the world. And all her graven images are broken to the ground. For which the Seventy translated idols and things made with hands, signifying the scripture of the heretics and the various errors of heresies, who are makers of idols and worship what they have fashioned out of their own heart.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.10":["And in what follows: My threshing, and son of my threshing floor, he makes an apostrophe to Jerusalem, and to the Temple which stands on the threshing floor of Ornan, and says to her: O Jerusalem, and you people, son of my Temple, let not what I have said seem incredible to you: for they are not my words but the Lord's, and it is his words that have resounded through my mouth. Some think, but wrongly, that this is said not to Jerusalem but to Babylon, of which he said above as well: Babylon, my beloved, has been made a wonder to me; and that the sense is: You who have crushed me will yourself be crushed too, not by my word, but by the power of the Lord, which foretells to you that these things will come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And what follows, in the person of the Prophet saying, My threshing, and the sons of my floor, what I have heard from the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, I have declared to you, has this sense according to the Hebrew: O people, who are to be stored in my granaries, whom I have therefore threshed in various straits, that I might shake the chaff from you, and the purest wheat might be stored in my granaries: what I have heard from the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, is to come upon the whole world, this I have declared to all of you who are in the world. Others again say that the person is still the earlier one, namely the Lord the Savior, and that he himself speaks to the Apostles: What I have heard from the Father I have declared to you (John 15:15). And since in place of this the Seventy read, hear, you who are left and are grieving, this seems to me the sense according to their translation: O Apostles, of whom Isaiah writes, Unless the Lord had left us seed, we should have been as Sodom, and should have been like Gomorrah (Isa 1:9), whose remnant the Apostle too, writing to the Romans, teaches has been saved (Rom 9): you who were left of the people of the Jews that you might be saved, and grieve over the perdition of your nation, of which we read elsewhere also, There is sorrow in my heart, and continual grief for my brethren, who are Israelites according to the flesh (Rom 9:2, 3): these things which I have heard from God the Father I declare to you, which the God of Israel foretells to you as things to come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.11-12":["The burden of Duma. He calls to me out of Seir: Watchman, what of the night? Watchman, what of the night? And the watchman said: The morning comes, and the night: if you seek, seek; turn, come. For Duma the Seventy put Idumaea. But Duma is not the whole province of Edom, but a certain region of it which lies toward the south, and is twenty miles distant from the city of Palestine which today is called Eleutheropolis: near it are the mountains of Seir, which took their name from their founder; for Seir means shaggy and hairy, which Esau was. In the prophet Obadiah we have discussed this nation more fully: there it was necessary to go over the ancient history, and to draw in as testimony the same Visions of Ezekiel and Jeremiah, and especially the prophecy against Mount Seir (Ezek 25 and 35; Jer 49); and that saying from the Psalms: Into Edom I will stretch out my shoe; and elsewhere: The tents of the Edomites and the Ishmaelites, Moab and the Hagarenes, Gebal and Ammon and Amalek (Ps 59 [60]:10; Ps 82 [83]:7-8). Amos also says: For three crimes of Edom, and for four, I will not turn him back, because he pursued his brother with the sword, and violated his mercy (Amos 1:11), or, as Symmachus translated, his bowels: because he dared to fight against kindred peoples, and to be at variance with them in hostile hatred. Now the region of Esau was in the region of Duma, that is, in the mountains of Seir. We believe that this, as a metropolis, in keeping with the order of the preceding Visions, was conquered by the Assyrians or by Nebuchadnezzar, and that, mindful of its former stock, since it was begotten of the line of Abraham and Isaac, it implored the help of God, and under the compulsion of necessity begged for his clemency. Now, therefore, the Lord recounts: He who is besieged in Seir and surrounded by enemies implores my help, and says: O watchman of Israel, who guard your people with an everlasting watch, and, as it were in the night, keep vigil while they sleep, lest the enemy break in, why do you not protect us also, who are of his seed, with like clemency? To them I reply, the watchman and keeper of whom it is written: He who guards Israel will not slumber nor sleep (Ps 120 [121]:4). Morning comes for my people, and night for the nation of the Edomites; to them I will give light, you I will leave in darkness. Or certainly thus: the light has come, the night being past; if you call upon my help, and are of the stock of my servant Abraham, do not seek me only in your necessities, but turn to me with your whole mind. Come, and I will receive you as penitents. Let this be said according to the history. For the rest, because of the similarity of the letter, and because RES and DALETH differ little from each other, some of the Hebrews read Roma for Duma, wishing the prophecy to be directed against the Roman empire, by the frivolous persuasion by which they always think that the Romans are pointed out under the name of Edom: but Duma means silence.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The Lord therefore speaks to the choir of the Apostles and commands it: Call to me those who are of Seir, so that the multitude of the nations may serve me, which, after the likeness of Esau, has nothing in itself soft and smooth and sleek, but is shaggy, wild, intractable. And you, O Apostles, after you have called the nations to me out of Seir, guard the ramparts of the Church, lest the enemy break in easily; lest the lion, roaring and going about, seeking an entrance by which he may get in, tear and scatter the flock shut up in the Church. And the multitude of the Church answered: Not only in prosperity but in adversity, that is, both by day and by night, I will keep your commandments, O God. To her God says: If you truly seek me, show by your works that you seek me; and let it not suffice you to have sought once, but seek always him whom you have found; and to hold him more perfectly, forget your people and your father's house, and, having forsaken the error of the Gentiles, abide with me in the Church. This we would say according to the Seventy, who put in the title the vision of Idumea, that is, of the earthly, to show that those are called who formerly served earthly works. Further, according to Aquila, who set down Dumah, that is, silence, or likeness, we can understand it thus: that the multitude of the nations is roused to the likeness of the people of Israel, and that where formerly there was silence of the law of God, there the cry of confession should be, and the wild olive be grafted into the good olive. We read also in the parable of the Gospel that servants were sent to call good and bad, and to fill the householder's banquet with whomsoever they found, because the first guests refused to come (Matt 22). The Church too can relate that the Lord cries aloud to her out of Seir, that is, out of earthly places, and calls her forth to salvation; and she may say to the Lord himself: O watchman, why, rising by night, do you dwell in darkness? Why are you, without sin, in the flesh of sin? For what reason did you will to take a human body? The watchman answered, that is, the Samaritan, who in the Gospel carried the wounded man on his shoulders to the inn (Luke 10): The morning comes, and the night. And the sense is: For the multitude of the nations the sun of righteousness has risen, and for the Jews darkness has come; according to what is said by the Lord: For the judgment of this world I have come, that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind (John 9:39). And the watchman himself, who had said, The morning comes, and the night, speaks to the multitude of the nations: If you seek me, seek more earnestly. Turn to me, you returning sons, and I will heal your bruises; and come to me. The passages are difficult, and since according to the history they are by no means plain, we are compelled to follow various opinions according to the ἀναγωγὴν.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.11":["The Jews dream in vain that this prophecy is made against the city of Rome and the Roman empire; and that in what is written in Isaiah, The burden of Dumah, with the stroke of a letter slightly changed, res can be read in place of deleth, and it sounds Rome, since the letter vau in their language is taken both for u and for o.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","The burden of Dumah: he calls to me out of Seir: Watchman, what of the night? Watchman, what of the night? The watchman said: The morning comes, and the night. If you seek, seek; turn, and come. LXX: The vision of Idumea: he calls to me out of Seir: Guard the ramparts; I keep guard morning and night; if you seek, seek, and dwell with me. The Hebrew word ELAI (אלי), which all have translated to me, if you choose to read it ELI, means my God or my strong one. And what we have rendered he calls or he summons, that is, καλεῖ, may, by the ambiguity of the Hebrew and the Greek speech, be read call! or summon!; and the sense is: God, who is my watchman, calls me day and night to repentance, so that, leaving Seir, which means shaggy and hairy, I may return and dwell with him. Dumah sounds either likeness or silence; but Idumea is rendered earthly.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.13-17":["The burden in Arabia. In the forest at evening you will sleep, in the paths of Dodanim. Meeting the thirsty, bring water: you who dwell in the land of the south, meet the fugitive with bread. For they have fled from the face of the swords, from the face of the threatening sword, from the face of the bent bow, from the face of grievous battle. For thus says the Lord to me: Yet in one year, as in the year of a hireling: and all the glory of Kedar will be taken away, and the remnant of the number of the mighty archers of the sons of Kedar will be diminished: for the Lord God of Israel has spoken. As I sought, and long turned over with deliberation, what Arabia this was to which the prophetic word is directed, whether the Moabites, or the Ammonites and Edomites, and all the other regions which are now called Arabia, what follows in this same Vision gave me occasion to see that the Ishmaelites must be understood: All the glory of Kedar will be taken away, and the remnant of the number of the mighty archers of the sons of Kedar will be diminished (Isa 21:16-17). The book of Genesis teaches that from Ishmael were born Kedar and the Hagarenes, who by a perverted name are called Saracens. These dwell throughout the whole wilderness; of them, I think, the poet too speaks: And the Barcaei, roaming far and wide (Virgil, Aeneid 4); and the aforesaid volume: He will dwell over against the face of all his brothers (Gen 16:12): because a very broad desert stretches from India as far as Mauretania and the Atlantic Ocean. This, I think, is what the title in Jeremiah expresses: To Kedar and to the kingdoms of Hazor, which Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon struck (Jer 49:28); and at once there follows: Thus says the Lord: Arise and go up to Kedar, and lay waste the sons of the East: they will take their tents and their flocks; they will carry off for themselves their skins and all their vessels and their camels (ibid., 29); and again: For Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon has taken counsel against you, he has devised plans against you. Arise and go up against a nation at ease, dwelling confidently, says the Lord: no doors, no bars for them: they dwell alone. And their camels will be for plunder, and the multitude of their cattle for a prey. And I will scatter to every wind those who have the hair of their head cut round: and from all their borders I will bring destruction upon them, says the Lord: and Hazor will be a dwelling of dragons, deserted forever; no man will remain there, nor will a son of man inhabit it (ibid., 30ff.). I have set down the whole testimony of the prophecy from Jeremiah, so that you may understand without doubt what Kedar is. And consider how he has described exactly the nation of the Ishmaelites, that is, of the Saracens: because they dwell in tents; they hold whatever abodes the night drives them to; they have herds, and flocks, and droves of camels; they have neither doors nor bars, for they do not live in cities but dwell in the wilderness. These too, then, were destroyed by the Babylonians, in that they overthrew to the ground the city of Hazor, which was the metropolis of that nation in the desert; and yet, though their droves of camels and sheep were taken, and their skins and tents divided by lot, the destruction of that whole nation is not signified, because on dromedary camels they are wont to flee a hundred miles and more in a single day through the vast wilderness. All the glory of Kedar, he says, will be taken away; and when the number of the archers has been diminished, since they excel above all in that art of fighting, the rest who have fled will remain.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.13":["Since we have understood what Kedar is, and what Arabia, and what Hazor, let us see what the prophet Isaiah says: In the forest at evening you will sleep, in the paths of Dodanim. The word ARAB (ערב), as we have often said already, is used for evening, and Arabia, and raven, and plain, and the West. And for what we, following the Seventy, have translated you will sleep, one can render you will stay or you will dwell, that is, αὐλιθήσεσθε, which in Hebrew is THALINU (תלנו). DODANIM (דדנים) too is rendered as kinsmen and relatives. So it is now foretold to the Jews who have been able to escape the Babylonian siege, and to cross from the devastation of the whole province to the neighboring desert, that they will dwell in the wilderness of Arabia, on the road that leads to their brothers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The burden in Arabia. This is not found in the edition of the Seventy Translators; but what follows, In the forest at evening you shall sleep, in the paths of Dodanim, is joined to the preceding vision, so that it reads, if you seek, seek, and dwell with me in the forest. Arabia in our tongue sounds evening, which is the beginning of night and of darkness; and everyone who has the beginning of sins dwells in the evening. But he who comes to the height of them stands in the middle of the night. Hence in Egypt too the firstborn are slain at midnight (Exod 12). And the apostle Peter denied the Lord three times before the cock crew, which is understood as midnight (Matt 26); but after the night was far spent and the day began to draw near, when the darkness of midnight had been overcome and the cock, herald of the light, sounded, he wept bitterly and understood his sin, and at that time he could say: In the evening weeping shall linger, and in the morning gladness (Ps 29 [30]:6). So much meanwhile on the present place. But the name of Arabia, that is, of evening and of the West, receives different meanings in other places of the Scriptures.\n\nIn the forest at evening you shall sleep, in the paths of Dodanim. LXX: In the forest at evening you shall sleep, in the way of Dedan. Those who have begun to be in wickedness, and are entering on the way of sins, do not sleep or linger in sown fields and fallow lands, nor in meadows and cornfields, which the Savior teaches are white for harvest, nor among fruit-bearing trees, but in fruitless forests, where there are brambles and thorns and where beasts dwell. Of such a forest we read also in the volume of Kings, that the wood or forest devoured more than were slain by the sword, when Absalom rose up as a rebel against his father (2 Sam 18). And rightly, since evening is the beginning of evils, they are said to dwell in the way and in the paths, and in the way of Dedan, which means judgments. For as many kinds of sins as they have, so many sentences of judgment do they deserve. But Dedan can also be interpreted great judgment.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.14-15":["Coming to meet the thirsty, bring water, you who inhabit the land of the South; meet the fugitive with bread. For they have fled from the face of the swords, from the face of the threatening sword, from the face of the bent bow, from the face of the grievous battle. LXX: To meet the thirsty bring water, you who dwell in the region of Teman; meet those who flee with bread, because of the multitude of the slain, because of the throng of those who go astray, because of the multitude of the sword, and because of the multitude of the bent bows, and because of the multitude of those who fall in war. You who are in Teman, which means South and perfection, and who dwell in that place of which it is written, God shall come from the South (Hab 3:3), and who are consummated and perfect, and have within you the light of the knowledge of the Scriptures: meet those who flee from Arabia and from the forest with water and bread, and do not wait until they come to you, but imitate that father of the Gospel parable who ran to meet his returning son. And he indeed gave the robe and offered the ring (Luke 15); but you, offer water and bread to those who flee, so that those who were weary and spent may be sustained by your mercy and pass the more quickly to your dwellings. For they have fled the swords of the heretics, the teaching of the Gentiles, the blasphemies of the Jews. And because they have seen many slain by their darts, and very many fallen in battle, they themselves long to be delivered by your help. This place may properly be understood as directed against those who, giving themselves to leisure and sloth, are content with their own salvation and do not stretch out a hand to the penitent and converted.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.14":["And again the discourse turns to the Ishmaelites, and urges them to mercy: Run, and go to meet your brothers, weary and in peril from burning thirst, and bring them water: for so great is the heat of the sun that, unless you come to their aid, they will perish in the wilderness; and bring not only water but bread too to the fugitives, so that your clemency may relieve those whom the siege has worn out.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Moreover, that place in which we have translated: You who dwell in the land of the south, meet the fugitive with bread, and have read it in the imperative mood, as if in the person of the Lord, they affirm can be read in the Hebrew thus: Those who dwell in the land of the south met the fugitive with bread; so that, when God had said to them, Meeting the thirsty, bring water, they with hostile mind brought bread without water, to increase their thirst with food.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.15":["And at the same time he gives the reason why he commands this to be done, saying: They have fled the Babylonian sword, they have fled the bows of the Elamites, they have fled the threatening battle.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.16-17":["And do not despise the wretched: your captivity too will come quickly. For as the hireling's year is swift, and he thinks all labor short until he reaches the wage he longs for, so all the glory of the sons of Kedar will be taken from you, and your arrows will be broken, and a small number of fighters will remain.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For thus says the Lord to me: Yet within one year, as in the year of a hireling, all the glory of Kedar shall be taken away, and the remnant of the number of the mighty archers of the children of Kedar shall be diminished. For the Lord God of Israel has spoken. I tell you to meet with water and bread those who flee from the forest and from Arabia because the Lord has promised that these things shall be. As the year of a hireling passes quickly, who every day looks for nothing but the wage of his labour, or certainly is always at work and toil in order to receive the wage of his labour, so all the glory of Kedar, which means darkness, of which we read in the Psalm too, I have dwelt with those who dwell in Kedar (Ps 119 [120]:5), shall swiftly be taken away; and the whole number of the archers, that is, of the various doctrines, which used to wound those placed in the forest of Arabia, shall be brought to nothing, once those fleeing from the forest have been delivered, and have received the water of saving baptism, and have eaten the heavenly bread. For the Lord of all, and properly the God of Israel, that is, of those who behold God with the mind, has spoken that these things shall be.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.21.16":["Some, from the words yet in one year, and all the glory of Kedar will be taken away, would have it that what is foretold is not the Babylonian captivity, of which Jeremiah too speaks, but that of the Assyrians, who, one year after the devastation of Judea, pursued the Saracens far and wide.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.1-2":["What is it with you too, that you also have gone up, all of you, onto the housetops, full of clamor, a crowded city, an exultant town? A Hebrew used to tell me that the present Vision does not belong to the time when Nebuchadnezzar took Jerusalem and led Zedekiah, bound and deprived of his eyes, to Babylon, but to the times of Sennacherib, when Shebna the high priest betrayed a great part of the city, and only Zion, that is, the citadel and the Temple, and the nobles remained, after the example of the city of Rome, which, when the Gauls burst in, preserved its patricians and the flower of its youth in the citadel. But we can also speak of it as of the Babylonian captivity; although Eusebius refers everything to the coming of Christ, and thinks it was fulfilled in the times of Vespasian and Titus. Let us then pick out each point, touching briefly on the threefold exposition. What is it with you too? What do you have, he says, O Zion, that you too have gone up, all of you, onto the housetops? When he says, you too, he shows that the others had gone up before. Have you also been reckoned among the nations, that you are besieged by enemies and climb onto the housetops, full of the howling and lamentation of wretched women, you who were once a royal city?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.1":["The burden of the valley of Vision. The Seventy, although it is not found in the Hebrew, translated more plainly, the word of the valley of Zion. For this city is the seedbed of the prophets, in which the Temple was built and the Visions of the Lord were multiplied. Because, then, it is placed among the other nations and reckoned as one of many, it is by no means called a mountain, according to that prophetic saying: Its foundations are on the holy mountains: the Lord loves the gates of Zion above all the tents of Jacob (Ps 86 [87]:1); but a valley, because it has been humbled. Jeremiah says this very thing in other words, when he receives the cup full of wine and gives it to all the nations, and last of all holds it out to Jerusalem, that it may drink and vomit and fall and go mad: signifying that it too was to be laid waste by the Babylonians (Jer 25). The history of Kings and of Jeremiah tells this more fully. From this we understand that God is equally the creator of all, and disposes all things by the same judgment, according to what he himself says through Amos: Are you not to me as the sons of the Ethiopians, O sons of Israel? says the Lord. Did I not bring Israel up out of the land of Egypt, and the Palestinians from Cappadocia, and the Syrians from Cyrene? Behold, the eyes of the Lord are upon the sinful kingdom (Amos 9:7). Lest the Jews should think that they had a privilege of merit because they had been led out of Egypt, he says that the other nations too had been transferred to other lands by his command.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The burden of the valley of vision. LXX: The word of the valley of Zion. In the book of Hebrew Names we have set down that Zion means watchtower, which, set on high places, beholds from afar the things that are coming. Since, then, Zion, according to the laws of tropology, is referred to the Church, as in that saying of the second Psalm in the person of the Lord the Savior, But I have been appointed king by him over Zion, his holy mountain (Ps 2:6); and, Those who trust in the Lord are as Mount Zion (Ps 124 [125]:1); and, The Lord loves the gates of Zion above all the tabernacles of Jacob (Ps 86 [87]:2); and more plainly the Apostle: You have come, he says, to Mount Zion and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem (Heb 12:22): we ask why in the present vision it is called the valley of Zion. And by the very sequence of the words we are drawn to a spiritual understanding: that we should know that all the princes of perverse doctrines, who have fallen from the loftiness of the sense of the holy Scriptures and have rolled down to low things, dwell in the valley of Zion. Something of this kind I think Solomon too says in the Proverbs: The eye that mocks its father and despises the old age of its mother, let the ravens of the valleys dig it out, and the young eagles eat it (Prov 30:17). For as soon as the mind of the heretics has mocked the Creator, the Father, and despised the old age of the Church, its mother, it is dug out by foul and unclean birds, which are referred to the opposing powers. Nor can such men say, I have lifted up my eyes to the mountains, whence help shall come to me (Ps 120 [121]:1); but, like brute beasts, they are bowed down to earthly things.\n\nWhat is the matter with you also, that you too have gone up, all of you, onto the housetops? LXX: What has happened to you now, that you have all gone up onto vain housetops? That which, according to the Seventy translators, is asked in the volume of Kings, what is meant when Elisha says (2 Kgs 2:14), Where is the God of Elijah APHPHO (אפא), is set down more plainly in the present place: where the Seventy translated now, the Hebrew has APHPHO, which we have translated now also; and Aquila, wishing to preserve the Hebrew idiom, set down καίπερτοι, a conjunction which the Latin tongue cannot render. And when he says, What is the matter with you also?, he asks why she too goes up among the others, and, with the reputation of lofty things, abides in low ones. And the sense is: Since the Philosophers are puffed up, and all worldly wisdom, disputing about lofty things, despises the simplicity of the Church, why do you too pursue high things? These the Seventy, σημαντικώτερον, translated δώματα μάταια, that is, vain housetops, to show that there is also another housetop, from which the Savior forbids us to come down (Matt 24), which is not, however, a vain housetop. Indeed the apostle Peter too went up onto the housetop at the sixth hour of prayer (Acts 10). But now, to show the great variety of heresies, he has named not one housetop but very many housetops.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","We do not wish to be valleys of Zion, but mountains of Zion. For Zion has its valleys, it has also its plains. Whoever is a sinner is a valley of Zion, he is not a mountain of Zion. Someone may say: You are speaking from your own opinion. Let us call Isaiah as a witness, at the time when Zion had sinned, after many visions, where it is said, A vision against Idumaea, a vision against Moab, a vision against Edom and the sons of Ammon, at the last is placed: 'A vision of the valley of Zion'. For because it had descended from the height of faith, therefore it collapsed from a mountain into a valley.\n— Homily 45, on Psalm 132"],"ISA.22.2-3":["Your slain are not slain by the sword, nor dead in war. All your princes have fled together, and have been harshly bound. All who were found have been bound together: they have fled far away. If you refer it to the times of Sennacherib, when the city was partly taken, they are rightly said not to have been conquered by the sword nor slain in war, but by treachery, some fleeing from the city, and others, whom the enemy overpowered, bound in chains. But if to the Babylonian captivity, which is also the truer view, we shall say that they were overcome not by battle but by siege. And if you wish to understand it tropologically, with Eusebius, of the coming of Christ, you will say that they were slain not by the sword but by unbelief, and that all their princes withdrew from God and were bound with the cords of sins, and that there was none of the Pharisees who was not bound in the nets of the devil.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.2":["Full of clamour, a crowded city, an exulting town. LXX: The city is filled with those who cry out, an exulting city. The teaching of the heretics consists not in sense but in much speaking and clamour. Hence, on account of the multitude of those deceived, it is called a crowded city, and exulting on account of its pride. For being puffed up they swell, and boast that they have found holier things.\n\nYour slain are not slain by the sword, nor dead in battle. LXX: Your wounded are not wounded by the sword, nor are your dead dead in battle. The greatest part is overthrown by the deceptions of the heretics without any struggle or debate, and this is a very great crowd. The slain, then, or as the Seventy translated, the wounded of the valley of Zion are not slain and wounded by the sword, but have gone over to the heretics of their own will; and in comparison with those who are overcome after battle, he is the more unhappily wounded and slain who has surrendered of his own accord. Just as in martyrdom too, he who yielded after torments is liable to a lighter penalty than he who denied Christ without any necessity and without the pain of tortures.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.3":["All your princes have fled together, and have been harshly bound. In this place we have followed the translation of the Seventy, because in sense it is not far from the Hebrew. But to translate word for word from the Hebrew, it reads thus among them: All your chief men have departed together, they have been bound by the bow; and the other translators also followed this edition. For all the princes of the heretics have departed from the Church of Christ to the synagogue of Satan, and have gone over together, discordant in faithlessness, concordant in desertion; and they have been bound by the bow, of which it is written in the Psalm: Behold, the sinners have bent the bow, they have made ready their arrows in the quiver, to shoot in the dark at the upright of heart (Ps 10 [11]:2), and to send the fiery darts of the devil, which both wound and bind. And therefore they have been harshly bound, because they have stopped their ears like deaf asps, and stopping their ears, which will not hear the voices of the charmers, nor of the sorcerer charming wisely. For this reason the apostle Paul too commands that a heretic be left to his perversity after one admonition (Titus 3), because he is perverted and condemned by his own judgment. For they went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would surely have remained with us (1 John 2:19). Of these fugitive princes the prince is he whom the same Isaiah calls the fleeing dragon, the crooked serpent, who, when he has sent deadly words into the ears of the deceived, binds them harshly and does not suffer them to escape from his bonds. Delivered from these, the saint rejoiced in the Psalm: My soul has been rescued like a sparrow from the snare of the fowlers (Ps 123 [124]:7). And in another place, rejoicing that he has escaped, he says: You are my upholder and my refuge. My God, I will hope in him. For he has delivered me from the snare of the fowlers, and from the harsh word (Ps 90 [91]:2-3), or the turbulent word, which properly signifies the teaching of the heretics.\n\nAll who were found in you are bound together; they have fled far off. LXX: And the strong in you have fled far away. The Prophet's speech is still against the valley of Zion, whose inhabitants have gone up onto vain housetops and have cried out with a confused clamour, and have been wounded without battle; all of whose princes have fled and been harshly bound, and those who were strong among them have fled further away. For the more prudent a man is in heretical perversity, the further he withdraws from the Lord. And as for what he says according to the Hebrew, All were found in you, we ought to note this: that the heretics too say they have found those whom they have deceived; but their finding is perdition. Accordingly they are bound together and flee far off. Nor am I pleased with that exposition which holds that, according to the diversity of the heresies, the distances of those who flee are also diverse, since the holy Scripture says (Luke 11:23) that all who have been found by the heretics are bound together and have fled very far, the Lord saying: He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather with me scatters. Hence it is said of Moses (Exod 24): Moses alone drew near to God, but the rest did not draw near. For to his saints God is a God who draws near, and not from afar, says the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.4":["Therefore I said: Depart from me, I will weep bitterly: do not press to console me over the devastation of the daughter of my people. If at the devastation of Babylon the Prophet weeps with the feeling of a mourner, and says: Anguish has possessed me, like the anguish of a woman giving birth: I fell down when I heard, I was troubled when I saw, my heart has withered, darkness has stupefied me: Babylon my beloved has been made a wonder to me, how much more now, at the ruin of his own city, can he accept no consolation, giving himself wholly to lamentation!\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Therefore I said: Depart from me, I will weep bitterly; do not press to comfort me over the devastation of the daughter of my people. For it is a day of slaughter and of trampling and of weeping from the Lord God of hosts in the valley of vision, searching the wall, and magnificent upon the mountain. LXX: Therefore I said: Let me alone, I will weep bitterly; do not strive to comfort me over the breaking of the daughter of my race; for it is a day of tumult and of perdition and of trampling, and error from the Lord God Sabaoth in the valley of Zion; they err from the least to the greatest, they err upon the mountains. Samuel too mourned for Saul (1 Sam 15), and the Lord and Savior wept over Jerusalem (Luke 19; 2 Cor 12:21); and the Apostle writes to the Corinthians: Lest, when I come to you again, God humble me, and I mourn for many who sinned before and have not done penance. Hence, with the feeling of one who suffers with them, he says to others also: Who is weak, and I am not weak? Who is scandalized, and I do not burn? (2 Cor 11:29). So the Prophet too, seeing the people that was once of vision climbing the vain housetops and living in clamour, and all its princes fled and bound with the chains of sins, bursts into tears, and drives away those who would console him, and testifies that he weeps with most bitter weeping, not over the sons but over the daughter of his people, which has lost the dignity of manhood.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.5-6":["Searching the wall, and magnificent upon the mountain: and Elam took up the quiver, the chariot of a horseman, and the shield laid bare the wall. The army of Babylon is described entering the city, occupying the temple, and proudly driving through the streets in four-horse chariots. And that the shield, which is wont to cover other things, laid bare the gilded doorposts and the walls clad with marble facing, sounds elegantly in the Hebrew, and the sense is most beautiful: that the shield protected no one, but laid bare, since all the riches were torn out by the strength of the soldiers. If, however, the Elamites are set in the battle, Elam being a city of the Assyrians, it cannot be referred to the times of the Roman overthrow, unless perhaps we interpret everything allegorically.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.5":["For it is a day of slaughter, and of trampling, and of weeping, for the Lord God of hosts in the Valley of vision. He gives the reasons, according to the threefold understanding, why he said: Depart from me, I will weep bitterly: because Zion has been trampled down, once the mountain of visions and now the valley of weeping.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For the day of judgment and slaughter and trampling will by no means be Jerusalem, which means vision of peace, but of its ancient name Jebus, which means trampling; a day of weeping too, or of error, as the Seventy translated, from the Lord Sabaoth: not that weeping and error come from the Lord, but through the occasion of the holy Scriptures, which the Lord gave to be read, an occasion of error arises for those who can say: Why have you made us err from your way? (Isa 63:17). And in another place: The Lord has mingled for them a spirit of error (ibid. 29:10): so that they should stand not on the mountain of Vision but in the valley of Zion. And this very day, which arises from the Lord of hosts in the valley of Vision to show the works of each, searches the wall of the heretics, which they have built against the Church like a most solid rampart; and it searches the magnificent and glorious one, that is, their teachers, who boast that they stand upon Christ the mountain, of which we read elsewhere: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord, and to the house of the God of Jacob, and he will declare to us his way (Isa 2:3). Further, as for what the Seventy translated, they err from the least to the greatest, they err upon the mountains, we ought to understand this: that those who are greater are in greater wickedness, and yet that all err from the least to the greatest, and err upon the mountains: Moses, Jeremiah and the other Prophets, the Evangelists and the Apostles. And though they dwell in the valley, they err in a wondrous way upon the mountains.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.6-9":["These Elamites, according to the edition of the Seventy, have not one quiver but several; and they are riders of men, on whose souls they ride; and there is a very great multitude preparing itself for battle against the Church, so that all its valleys are filled; and the horsemen stop up their senses, and by their combat challenge to the contest the churchman, who is interpreted Judah; and when he brings forward the testimonies of the Scriptures and refutes all lies, the heretics will see the houses of the city of David, and will come to know all the things formerly secret: how in the citadel of David, which means strong of hand, that is, of Christ, are all the doctrines of truth. The places are obscure, not only according to the history but according to the ἀναγωγὴν. Those, then, whom our views displease ought to bring forward their own, so that we may acquiesce in their explanation, if it is true.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.6":["And Elam took up the quiver, the chariot of a man, a horseman, and the shield laid bare the wall. And your choice valleys shall be full of chariots, and the horsemen shall set their seats in the gate. And the covering of Judah shall be uncovered, and you shall see in that day the armoury of the house of the forest; and you shall see the breaches of the city of David, that they are multiplied. LXX: But the Elamites took quivers, riders, men upon horses, and a gathering of fighters; and your choice valleys shall be filled with chariots, and horsemen shall throng your gates; and they shall uncover the gates of Judah, and they shall look on that day into the choice houses of the city; and they shall uncover the hidden things of the houses of the citadel of David, and they shall see that they are many. Elam, which means their ascent, or the Elamites, as the Seventy translated, whom we render despisers, took up the quiver to shoot in the dark at the upright of heart; they are horsemen of men in a chariot, to lay bare with their shield and their assault the wall of Zion and the strongholds of the Church.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.7-9":["And your choice valleys will be full of chariots, and the horsemen will set their seats at the gate. And the covering of Judah will be uncovered, and you will see in that day the armory of the house of the forest, and you will see the breaches of the city of David, that they have been multiplied. He plainly describes the captivity which took place in the eleventh year of King Zedekiah, of which Jeremiah too speaks: Behold, I will call together all the families of the kingdoms of the North, says the Lord, and they will come, and each will set his throne at the entrance of the gates of Jerusalem, and upon all its walls round about (Jer 1:15). And lest we should think this foretold in word and not fulfilled in deed, the same Jeremiah says: In the eleventh year of Zedekiah, in the fourth month, on the fifth of the month, the city was opened, and all the princes of the king of Babylon entered, and sat in the middle gate (Jer 39:2). Then, therefore, a countless army was poured out round about the city of Jerusalem, and the Holy of holies was opened, and the armory, which was planted with a grove, was thrown open by the enemy's ravaging, and, the walls having been breached, the enemy entered through all parts of the city.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.7":["And your choice valleys, he says, and your lowly doctrines, O valley of Zion, shall be full of chariots, those which the Lord sank with Pharaoh, whose horsemen and riders shall set their seats in your gates, so as not to let those besieged and shut up in you go out.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.8-9":["Then almighty God, through churchmen, shall uncover the coverings of Judah and all the secrets of those who stand in the confession of the faith; and then, O valley of Zion, in that day and in the clear light of truth, you shall see the whole armour of the Apostle, and of the house of the forest, in which the throng of the nations stands; as it is sung also in Psalm 131 (132): Behold, we have heard of it in Ephrathah; we have found it in the fields of the forest (Ps 131 [132]:6). And when the covering and secret of Judah, which formerly was hidden on account of the mystery, has been uncovered, and you have seen the armour of the Church, then you will understand the schisms of the city of David, which you have multiplied in order to gather to yourself deceived peoples.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.9-11":["And you gathered the waters of the lower pool, and you numbered the houses of Jerusalem, and you destroyed houses to fortify the wall. And you made a reservoir between the two walls, and the water of the old pool: and you did not look up to him who had made it, and you did not see its maker from afar. He relates how they prepared the city for the siege: they transferred the waters of the lower pool, which could be captured, to the upper fortification; they destroyed houses and built walls; family by family they reckoned up how much water each house should receive; they made a cistern between the two walls, and restored the old pool to store the waters; and they did not rather call upon the help of God, who is the founder of the city and the Lord of the pool; as if he had said in other words: You have placed your trust in man, and not in God, who is the maker of man. Some, according to mystical senses, understand the old pool as the shadow of the Law, and the reservoir built between two walls, namely the New and the Old Testament, as built by the traditions and commandments of the Pharisees, one that cannot hold water: and that they did not look to the Son of God, nor believe in the presence of him whom they had not seen of old, because they had always been unbelieving toward the precepts of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And you gathered the waters of the lower pool, and you numbered the houses of Jerusalem, and you pulled down houses to fortify the wall. And you made a reservoir between the two walls, and the water of the old pool; and you did not look to him who had made it, and you did not see its maker from afar. LXX: And they turned the water of the ancient pool into the city, and they pulled down the houses of Jerusalem to strengthen the walls of the city; and you made yourselves water between the two walls within the ancient pool; and you did not look to him who made it from the beginning, and you did not see its creator. You who will see, or do see, the breaches of the city of David, that is, of the Church, which have been multiplied throughout the whole world; who have gathered the waters of your pool and teaching, not the upper but the lower, and have numbered, or pulled down, the houses of Jerusalem in order to fortify your wall: you have not dug wells which might have living and eternal waters, but broken cisterns, which cannot hold water. And you have made them between the two walls of the New and the Old Testament, and have neglected the water of the old pool, and have not considered God the giver of the law, and have utterly neglected the maker of the world. This according to the Hebrew. But according to the Seventy, the heretics considered that they were many, and trusting in this multitude they turned the teaching of the Old Testament away from the city of God, which is the Church, and pulled down the houses of Jerusalem in order to build assemblies of the wicked; and while they reject the Old Testament, they have not followed the New either, because the New is strengthened by the testimonies of the Old Testament. Therefore between the two walls, the old and the new, they have made themselves new waters, which they think are more inward and contain greater mysteries than are contained in the Old Testament; and they did not look to God, who is the founder of the Old Testament, and would not see its maker even from afar.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.12-14":["And the Lord God of hosts will call in that day to weeping, and to lamentation, and to baldness, and to the girding of sackcloth. And behold, joy and gladness, slaying calves and slaughtering rams, eating flesh and drinking wine: let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die. And it has been revealed in my ears by the Lord of hosts: If this iniquity will be forgiven you until you die, says the Lord God of hosts. When the captivity was at hand and Jerusalem besieged, while sword, famine and thirst pressed hard upon the city, Jeremiah called the people to repentance (Jer 34); and on the contrary the kings and princes, and the wretched multitude, with the despair of men perishing, gave themselves over to feasting. But nothing so offends God as a stiff neck after sins, and contempt born of despair. This he says through Amos too: For three and for four impieties I will not turn him back: because he both devised it and did it, and did not repent, and moreover taught evil. Hence now too it is said: If this iniquity will be forgiven you until you die (Amos 1:4). The allegorical interpreters think this was foretold of the passion of Christ: that God called the unhappy people to repentance even after the cross of the Lord Savior, and nevertheless they gave themselves over to despair and pleasure. For the beginning of this vision, where he says: Full of clamor, a crowded city, an exultant town, they refer to that time when the people, stirred up by the Pharisees, resounded with one voice against Jesus: Crucify, crucify such a one: we have no king but Caesar (John 19:6, 7). The Apostle too made use of the example of this testimony, writing to the Corinthians about the resurrection: If the dead do not rise, let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die (1 Cor 15:32).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.12":["And in that day the Lord God of hosts shall call to weeping and to mourning, to baldness and to the girding of sackcloth; and behold joy and gladness, killing calves and slaughtering rams, eating flesh and drinking wine: let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die. And it was revealed in my ears by the Lord of hosts: Surely this iniquity shall not be forgiven you until you die, says the Lord God of hosts. Daily the Lord God of hosts calls to repentance the heretics, who, despising the water of the old pool between the two walls, have dug themselves a reservoir and gathered the water of the lower pool, which has its waters not from heaven but from the earth; and he calls them to weeping, for blessed are those who weep, for they shall laugh (Matt 5:5); and to mourning, lest afterwards they hear: We have lamented to you, and you have not mourned (ibid. 11:17); and to baldness, that they may shave off all the works of death, and remove from their body whatever does not live in the body; and to the girding of sackcloth, lest afterwards, according to Jerusalem, they be girded with a rope instead of a girdle.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.13-14":["But they, on the contrary, instead of weeping and mourning, baldness and haircloth, had joy and gladness, and, as if all things ended with death, killing calves and slaughtering rams, to eat flesh and drink wine, they spoke the words of blasphemers: While we are in the present world, let us abound in delights; for tomorrow, that is, the time to come, will be without sensation. While they were saying these things, the Lord heard the words of blasphemy, and threatened that he would not forgive this iniquity until they either die to sin and vices, or are dragged down to the underworld with their offences. This indeed can be understood not only of the heretics but of every sinner who, neglecting his sins, becomes worse through despair, and, ensnared by pleasures, joins blasphemy to sin, so as to think that all the sensation of the living ends with death, saying that saying of Epicurus: \"After death there is nothing, and death itself is nothing.\"\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.15-16":["The Prophet, then, is commanded to go in to the high priest, who dwelt, according to the Seventy, in the pastophorium; according to Aquila, in the tabernacle; for which Theodotion, following the Hebrew truth, says: Go in to this Socen, which can be interpreted fair and upright (so that, namely, κατὰ ἀντίφρασιν, unjust and crooked is to be understood), to the high priest of the temple, and you will say to him: You who dwell in the house of God, whose crimes run riot through the whole city, why are you secure in your works, as though you did not fear captivity? Have you hewn yourself a tomb in the rock, and built so proudly that your ambition should follow you even in death?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.15-21":["Thus says the Lord God of hosts: Go, enter in to him who dwells in the tabernacle, to Shebna, who is over the Temple, and you will say to him: What are you doing here? or as whom are you here, that you have hewn out a tomb for yourself here? You have hewn out a monument on high, a tabernacle for yourself carefully in the rock. Behold, the Lord will cause you to be carried away, as a cock is carried away, and he will lift you up like a cloak. Crowning, he will crown you with tribulation; like a ball he will throw you into a broad and spacious land: there you will die, and there will be the chariots of your glory, the shame of the house of your Lord. And I will drive you out from your station, and depose you from your ministry. And it will come to pass in that day, I will call my servant Eliakim son of Hilkiah. And I will clothe him with your tunic, and strengthen him with your girdle, and I will give your power into his hand: and he will be like a father to the inhabitants of Jerusalem and to the house of Judah. And I will lay the key of the house of David upon his shoulder, and he will open, and there will be none to shut: and he will shut, and there will be none to open. And I will fix him as a peg in a sure place, and he will be for a throne of glory to his father's house. And they will hang upon him all the glory of his father's house, vessels of various kinds: every small vessel, from the vessels of bowls even to every vessel of the musicians. In that day, says the Lord of hosts, the peg that had been fixed in the sure place will be removed, and it will be broken and fall, and what had hung upon it will perish, because the Lord has spoken. We said above that Shebna was the high priest who betrayed the city to the Assyrians. But since this belongs to Hebrew tradition, and Scripture does not say it, let us understand him as proud, swollen and given to pleasure, trampling the peoples under his feet; and that, because he had done all the things the Prophet describes, his priesthood was transferred to Eliakim son of Hilkiah, so that, with him cast out, a new high priest might be made. From this we understand that pride, which offends God, is to be shunned with all zeal, and that it cannot be safe even with the privilege of the high priesthood. This is the Shebna (as some think) and the Eliakim of whom the same Prophet wrote further on: The king of the Assyrians sent Rabshakeh from Lachish to Jerusalem, to King Hezekiah, with a heavy force: and he stood by the aqueduct of the upper pool, on the road of the fuller's field; and there went out to him Eliakim son of Hilkiah, who was over the house, and Shebna the scribe, and Joah son of Asaph, the recorder (Isa 36:2ff.).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.15-25":["Those who take everything according to ἀναγωγὴν would have the priesthood of the Jews fall in the high priesthood of Shebna, and the mysteries of the Gospel worship shown in the succession of Eliakim, which means God rising again: so that what follows: In that day, says the Lord of hosts, the peg that had been fixed in the sure place will be removed, and it will be broken and fall, pertains to the ruin of the former people. And no one doubts, both according to the history and according to the allegory, that, because he had said peg, the metaphor is kept in the hanging up of vessels of different kinds; and that when the former have been broken, others are hung up.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.15":["Thus says the Lord God of hosts: Go, enter in to him who dwells in the tabernacle, to Shebna who is over the temple, and you shall say to him: What are you doing here, or as whom are you here? For you have hewn yourself a tomb here; you have hewn a memorial on high carefully, a tabernacle for yourself in the rock. Behold, the Lord will cause you to be carried away as a cock is carried away, and he will lift you up like a garment. Crowning, he will crown you with tribulation; like a ball he will cast you into a land wide and spacious: there you shall die, and there shall be the chariot of your glory, the shame of the house of your Lord. And I will drive you from your station, and depose you from your ministry. And it shall come to pass in that day that I will call my servant Eliakim the son of Hilkiah, and I will clothe him with your tunic, and strengthen him with your girdle, and I will give your power into his hand; and he shall be as a father to those who dwell in Jerusalem, and to the house of Judah. And I will lay the key of the house of David upon his shoulder; and he shall open, and there shall be none to shut; and he shall shut, and there shall be none to open. And I will fasten him as a peg in a sure place, and he shall be for a throne of the house of his father. And they shall hang upon him all the glory of his father's house, divers kinds of vessels, every small vessel, from the vessels of bowls even to every vessel of the musicians. In that day, says the Lord of hosts, the peg that was fastened in the sure place shall be taken away, and shall be broken and fall; and what hung upon it shall perish, for the Lord has spoken. LXX: Thus says the Lord Sabaoth: Go to the pastophorion, to Somnas the treasurer, and you shall say to him: What are you here? Or what have you here? For you have hewn yourself a tomb, and have made yourself a memorial on high, and have marked out for yourself a tabernacle in the earth? Behold, the Lord Sabaoth will cast out and crush a man, and will take away your robe and your glorious crown, and will cast you into a great land, and without measure, and there you shall die; and he will set your excellent chariot in shame, and the house of your prince for trampling. And you shall be removed from your stewardship and from your rank. And it shall come to pass in that day that I will call my servant Eliakim the son of Hilkiah, and I will clothe him with your robe, and will give him your crown with strength, and will give your stewardship into his hands; and he shall be as a father to the inhabitants of Jerusalem and of Judah, and I will give him the glory of the house of David, and he shall rule, and there shall be none to gainsay. And I will lay the key of the house of David upon his shoulder; and he shall open, and there shall be none to shut; and he shall shut, and there shall be none to open. And I will establish him as a prince in a sure place, and for a throne of glory of his father's house; and every glorious one in his father's house shall trust in him, from the least to the greatest. Every small vessel, from the vessels aganoth; and they shall hang upon him. In that day thus says the Lord Sabaoth: The man who was established in a sure place shall be moved, and shall be taken away and shall fall, and the glory that was upon him shall perish, for the Lord has spoken. The prophet Isaiah is commanded to go in to him who dwells in the tabernacle, which in Hebrew is called SOCHEN (סכן), to Shebna who is over the temple, or as the Seventy translated, ταμίαν, that is, the treasurer and steward of the treasures of the house of God. Now Sochen is interpreted either tabernacle or pastophorion, that is, the chamber in which the one set over the temple dwells. And who else is it that dwelt in the tabernacle of the Jews, and in what was once the house of God, but the word and reasoning of the Jewish law, which was occupied with the reading of the Old Testament?\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And what almost escaped us in the haste of dictation: Shebna means turn now, or conversion. It is said, therefore, to the prince of the Jews, that he should turn from the Law to the Gospel, and, forsaking the images of the victims, pass over to the truth of the spiritual sacrifice.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.16":["To this tradition and teaching, then, it is said: What are you doing here? Why do you wish to build yourself a house in the letter that kills, and to fix your tabernacle, which has no foundation, in the firmness of the rock, which ought to be called not so much a tabernacle and a house as a tomb and a memorial?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.17-18":["Hear what the Lord's sentence upon you is: as a cock is carried off on the shoulder of the one carrying it, and men each lift their cloak onto the shoulder, so your removal into captivity will be easy. You will have a crown, not of the golden plate and of the sanctification of the Lord, but of tribulation and anguish. For as a ball, if it is thrown down a slope, cannot stand still but rolls on in swift course, so you too will be led away to a most broad land, which we may understand as either Egypt or the plains of Babylonia, and there you will die; and there will be the chariots of your glory, all the powers and riches on which you now lean as you go about. There you will feel the shame of the house of your Lord: because you will suffer these things on account of your evil works and the violation of the Temple of your God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.17":["And I tell you this because you ought not to toil at hewing a tomb, since the Lord will carry away your priesthood, as GEBER (גבר) is wont to be carried, which all have translated man. But the Hebrew who instructed us in the reading of the Old Testament rendered it cock. As a cock, he says, is carried from one place to another on the shoulder of a porter, so the Lord will lightly carry you away from your place.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.18":["And you who once had the crown of the high priest, and the sanctification on the plate of gold on which the name of God was written (Exod 28), shall be crowned with tribulation and distress. And as a ball, if it is thrown down a sloping and spacious place, cannot stand still but rolls on without end, so all your people shall be scattered to the ends of the world. And there you shall die with your letter, and all your glory, and the chariots in which you were formerly borne in renown; and the splendour of the house of God shall be turned into shame.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.19-21":["I will drag you down from your rank and cast you out of the office of high priest, and in your place I will clothe Eliakim my servant, son of Hilkiah, with your garments, and adorn him with your girdle, just as Eleazar was clothed with the garments of his father Aaron. To him I will give the authority of the Temple, so that he may have the people, not subject to him as a slave, but loving him as a son.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.19":["For the Lord shall take you away from your station and from your ministry, that is, from the ceremonies of the victims, so that what is written to the Hebrews may be fulfilled: For when the priesthood is changed, there must of necessity be a change of the law also (Heb 7:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.20-21":["And when you have been cast out of your ministry, I will call my servant Eliakim, to whom I have said in another place also: It is a great thing for you to be called my servant (Isa 49:6, LXX). Eliakim means God rising again, or the resurrection of God. This God rising again, then, who is the son of Hilkiah, that is, of the portion of the Lord, shall receive your place, and shall be clothed with your robe and strengthened with your girdle, so that what you had in the letter he may possess in the spirit; and he shall be the father of those who dwell in Jerusalem, that is, in the vision of peace, which is interpreted as the Church, and to the house of Judah, where the true confession of faith is. Hence he himself says to the Apostles: Little children, yet a little while I am with you (John 13:33). And to another: Son, your sins are forgiven you (Matt 9:2). And to another woman: Daughter, your faith has made you whole (Luke 7:50).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.22":["He will receive the key of my house, and carry it on his shoulder; he will have authority over all the ceremonies.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","I will give him, he says, the key of the house of David, he who opens and no one shuts, who shuts and no one opens (Rev 3). And this very key shall be upon his shoulder, that is, in his passion, according to what is written in another place: Whose government is upon his shoulder (Isa 9:6). For what he has unlocked by his passion cannot be shut; and what he has shut in the ceremonies of the Jews shall be opened by no one else.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.23-24":["And as, when a peg is fixed in the wall of the Temple and set in a very firm place, all the vessels of the priests and Levites are hung upon it, both those needed in the sacrifices and those of the musicians of every kind, so the whole people will hang upon the authority of Eliakim my servant.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For I will fasten him as a peg in a sure place, where the assembly of the Faithful is; hence too those who believe in Christ are called the faithful. And he shall be for a throne of glory of his father's house, that is, of the Church; and they shall hang upon him all the glory of his father's house. Hence it is written in the Gospel also: All the people hung upon him (Luke 19). And this indeed was done not only at that time, but is fulfilled to this day, so that there hang upon him, as it were, divers vessels of God: wisdom and righteousness and all the things by which Christ is called. Divers kinds of vessels: for these Aquila translated SASAIM (צאצאים) and SEPHOTH (הצפעית), which Symmachus rendered grandsons and mixed ones, so that the Apostles and all believers, that is, the sons of sons, and those mixed from all the nations, may hang upon him. From the vessels of bowls, for which Theodotion set down AGANOTH (אגנות): for both small and great shall believe in the Lord. And I think the bowls are the Apostles, full of living waters, of whom it is said: Bless God from the fountains of Israel (Ps 67 [68]:27), in which bowls wisdom has mingled her wine. And every vessel, he says, of the musicians, of those who at all times resound in the praises of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.22.25":["And as for his saying: In that day the peg that had been fixed in the sure place will be removed, and it will be broken and fall, and what had hung upon it will perish, because the Lord has spoken, many refer it to Shebna: that when Eliakim had been fixed as a peg, the former peg, which had been fixed, collapsed. But since there follows: And what had hung upon it will perish, which by no means happened when Shebna was cast down, we understand this to be said: that when Shebna was deposed, Eliakim received the high priesthood, the dignity of whose priesthood the final captivity overthrew.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","What follows seems contrary to our sense: how this peg, which had been fastened in a sure place, is taken away and broken, and what had hung upon it falls and perishes, and this happens because the Lord has spoken. This can be resolved thus, if we read that saying of the Gospel, that in the last days the love of many shall grow cold (Matt 24), and the Lord himself says: Do you think the Son of man, when he comes, will find faith upon the earth? (Luke 18:8). It is not, then, the peg that will be broken and fall and perish, which it is impious to say; but the peg will be taken away from the sure place, that is, from the Church, through the impiety that daily grows up, and those who before hung upon him by faith will afterwards through unbelief be broken and fall and perish. And this shall happen in the last days, because the Lord has spoken. For peg, which in Hebrew is called JATHED (יתד), and is rendered alike by all, the Seventy alone translated above prince, and here man.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.1":["The burden of Tyre. Howl, you ships of the sea, for the house is laid waste from which they were wont to come: from the land of Cethim it has been revealed to them. Be silent, you who dwell in the island, the merchandise of Sidon. The prophet Ezekiel sets forth more plainly and more fully both the overthrow of Tyre and the causes of the overthrow, saying: Son of man, because Tyre has said of Jerusalem: Aha, the gates of the peoples are broken, she has turned to me, I shall be filled, she is deserted. Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I am against you, O Tyre, and I will cause many nations to come up against you, as the sea comes up in waves, and I will scatter the walls of Tyre, and destroy its towers, and I will scrape its dust from it (Ezek 26:2ff.). And who these many nations are, which will flood over Tyre like the surging sea, he teaches by name in what follows: Behold, I will bring upon Tyre Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon from the North, a king of kings, with horses and chariots and horsemen, and a great company and people. Your daughters that are in the field he will slay with the sword; and he will surround you with siegeworks, and will heap up a mound round about, and will raise the shield against you, and will set mantlets and battering rams against your walls, and will destroy your towers with his weapons, and the rest that follows to the end of the vision. For in another prophecy too, which is seen to be against the Egyptians, the same Scripture weaves in: Son of man, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon has made his army serve a great service against Tyre: every head made bald, and every shoulder rubbed bare, and no wage has been paid to him, nor to his army, for Tyre (Ezek 29). By this he signifies that a mound was heaped up over a long time by the army of Babylon, so that he tried beforehand to do what Alexander did afterwards, joining the island to the mainland (Quintus Curtius, book 4). Just as, therefore, we have read above God's threats against Babylon and the Philistines, and Moab, and Egypt, and the Edomites and Ishmaelites, because they mocked the captivity of his people, so now too a prophecy is woven against Tyre, hostile and mocking at the overthrow of Jerusalem: that it too is to be destroyed by the same enemy. We have read the histories of the Greeks, and especially of those who describe the wars of the Assyrian nation; and there we find that after the captivity of Jerusalem the Palestinians, and the Arabs, and the Damascenes, and after them the Egyptians were overthrown. And that these nations, and Tyre above all, always attacked Israel and rejoiced at its overthrow, the prophet Amos explains at the opening of his volume (Amos 1), and the Psalmist, briefly singing praises to God, declares: O God, who will be like you? Do not keep silent, nor be restrained, O God. For behold, your enemies have made a noise: and those who hate you have lifted up the head. They have devised malicious counsel against your people, and have plotted against your holy ones. They have said: Come, and let us destroy them from being a nation, and let the name of Israel be remembered no more. For they have plotted with one mind, together they have made a covenant against you: the tents of the Edomites, and the Ishmaelites, Moab and the Hagarenes, Gebal and Ammon and Amalek, the foreigners with the inhabitants of Tyre (Ps 82 [83]:1ff.). From all these we learn that the weight of God's wrath came upon Tyre the harlot, who is described here under the figure of a prostitute, and in Ezekiel under the metaphor of a ship (Ezek 27). Howl, he says, you ships of the sea. For this we read in the Seventy of Carthage; and in the Hebrew there is THARSIS (תרשיש), about which I have argued both on the prophet Jonah and in a certain letter. And because Carthage is a colony of the Tyrians, we can in the present place take Tharsis not only as the sea in general but also as Carthage: that ships no longer come to Tyre from Africa, nor from the land of Cethim, which some interpret as Cyprus: for to this day there is among them the city of Citium, from which came Zeno too, the heresiarch of the Stoic sect; although many of our writers, and especially the beginning of Maccabees, consider Cethim to be the islands of Italy and Macedonia. For Scripture records this, that Alexander king of the Macedonians went out from the land of Cethim.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The word of Tyre. What we thought about the burden, or word and taking up, of Tyre according to the Hebrews, we have said above in the book of the historical explanation of the ten Visions. Now we shall run briefly through the whole prophecy against Tyre according to the ἀναγωγὴν and the edition of the Seventy. Tyre is called SOR (צר) in the Hebrew tongue, and is rendered into our speech as distress. Every soul, then, that is taken up with vices and evil thoughts can be called sor.\n\nHowl, you ships of Carthage, for they have perished, and shall come no more. From the land of the Kittim she has been led captive. To whom are the inhabitants of the isle become like? The merchants of Phoenicia, crossing the sea in many waters, the seed of merchants like harvests brought in, the merchants of the nations. For Carthage, in the Hebrew is written THARSIS (תרשיש), which all have translated alike. And Tharsis means contemplation or searching out of joy. Kittim too, which in Hebrew is called CHETIM (כתים), means sea congealing; and for Phoenicia, Sidon is read among them. Those, then, are rebuked whom a throng of evil thoughts surrounds, and who, wishing, as the Apostle says, to become rich, fall into temptations and snares of the devil, and many harmful desires, which drag men down into the deep (1 Tim 6). And they are told that they must howl, knowing that all the trading of this world will perish, and the contemplation of joy and gladness be turned into mourning and tears. For this sea can be sailed no longer, but all things will freeze; and the colonies of the Tyrians will be led captive to punishment.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.2":["And in naming it an island he does not lie: for afterwards it was made part of the mainland by Nebuchadnezzar or by Alexander, when many mounds were heaped up in the narrow strait for its capture. He also calls it the merchandise of Sidon, in keeping with the following line, in which he says: Be ashamed, O Sidon. For the histories relate that Tyre is a colony of Sidon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Those crossing the sea have filled you; in many waters the seed of the Nile, the harvest of the river is its produce. That Tyre delights in trade and is the emporium of the whole world, both present times prove and Ezekiel describes at length. And that merchants cross the seas in carrying their goods, there is no one who doubts; nor does the city itself have such great wealth from the land of its own region, which is very narrow and hemmed in by the borders of Galilee and of Damascus, as it has from the shipping of goods.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For those who dwell in this isle are like no others than merchants, or carriers, who are carried about by every wind of doctrine and pass from one vice to another. And we dwell in an isle as long as we are beaten by the temptations of this world, and on every side our isle and little boat is struck by the waves of the sea. Now the merchants of this isle are from Sidon, which means huntress, and in which dwell very many hunters, of whom it is written: He will deliver you from the snare of the hunters (Ps 90 [91]:3). And in another place the saint rejoices that he has been delivered from their snares, saying: Our soul has been rescued like a sparrow from the snare of the hunters (Ps 123 [124]:7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.3":["The whole Nile and the fertility of Egypt pour into Tyre from close at hand. For Nile, which we read among the Hebrews as SIOR (שיחר), the Seventy and Theodotion interpreted merchants; this word means turbid, and by this signifies the waters of the Nile, as Jeremiah testifies: What have you to do with Egypt, that you should drink the waters of Geon? (Jer 2:18): for which in the Hebrew there is Sior, that is, turbid.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And all the merchants of the nations are compared to crops which quickly wither, or to the harvests of the river, which have their rain not from heaven but from the earth; and therefore they will perish with the nations. Where we read the seed of merchants, in the Hebrew is written the seed of sior, by which the Nile is understood, because it has turbid waters with which the crops of Egypt are watered.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.4":["Be ashamed, O Sidon: for the sea has spoken, the strength of the sea, saying: I have not travailed, and I have not brought forth, and I have not nourished young men, nor brought up virgins. I said above that Tyre is a colony of the Sidonians. Hear, then, O mother, all the seas crying out together against your daughter, and in a manner uttering a voice, though by nature they cannot speak: In vain have I brought riches together; to no purpose have I gathered wealth from the whole world into Tyre: she that was rich, she that was luxurious, and once rejoiced in a multitude of peoples, in which a throng of mortals was born, a troop of children, swarms of youth, whose streets rang with the games of virgins and infants, of young men and girls, has now been reduced to solitude. It is customary to make προσωποποιΐας out of mute things.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Be ashamed, O Sidon, said the sea; and the strength of the sea said: I have not travailed, nor brought forth, nor nourished young men, nor brought up virgins. And when it is heard in Egypt, pain shall seize them over Tyre. The sea of this world, in which dwell small animals with great, and the dragon which God formed to be mocked, seeing that those who had formerly been taken by the demons have been taken by the hunters of Christ, who hunt from every mountain and hill, speaks to the opposing power of the hunters, which Sidon means, and says to it: Be ashamed, O Sidon; for I have not travailed nor brought forth, nor nourished the young men taken by you, nor brought up the virgins whose folly is condemned in the Gospel (Matt 25). For my travail and childbearing, and my nourishing and bringing up of virgins, have been blotted out by the travail of the Apostles of Christ, one of whom said: My little children, of whom I travail again until Christ be formed in you (Gal 4:19). And: I gave you milk to drink, not solid food (1 Cor 3:2). And again: I wish to present you all as a chaste virgin to Christ (2 Cor 11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.5":["When it has been heard in Egypt, they will grieve when they hear of Tyre. This is plain: that the Egyptians, after they have heard that a most mighty and neighboring nation has been destroyed by a long siege, know that destruction is drawing near to them too.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","When Egypt hears this, which in Hebrew is called MESRAIM (מצרים), which means ἐκθλίβουσα, that is, afflicting and constraining, it too will be terrified, understanding that it too will suffer the same things that Tyre has suffered. And while this is referred to all vices, it can also be perceived in the diversity of the heresies, when, one heresy being taken, another trembles, and in the confusion of one the other perceives that it is to be taken. And as it is said of Sodom: For if the signs had been done in them which were done in Capernaum, they would have remained until this day; and it is added: But it shall be more tolerable for Sodom in the day of judgment than for you: so we learn of Tyre and Sidon, the Savior saying: But it shall be lighter for Tyre and Sidon in the day of judgment than for that city (Matt 11:21ff.). Hence too the Canaanite woman, who understood that her daughter, that is, her soul, was grievously vexed by a demon, is said to have come out of the borders of Tyre and Sidon to pray to Christ. For she could not otherwise have gone to meet the Savior, unless she had left the borders of Tyre and Sidon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.6-7":["Go to Carthage; howl, you who dwell in this isle. Is not this your disgrace from the beginning, before she was delivered up? Her feet shall lead her far off to sojourn. What follows, Her feet shall lead her far off to sojourn, has been added from the Hebrew, and is marked beforehand with an asterisk, that is, with shining stars. O Tyrians, you who dwell in distress and abide in an isle, exposed on every side to the waves of temptations, depart from it, and go to Carthage, that is, Tharsis, and hasten to true joy, mourning your old sins and the ancient disgrace which you either inflicted on others or suffered from them. And I command this because you see Tyre, your city, about to move from its seats and purpose to other borders, when, having laid aside their old error, they have bowed their necks to the Gospel of the Savior; so that those who formerly dwelt in distress may depart far off and become sojourners and dwellers in the teaching of the Lord the Savior.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.6":["Cross the seas, howl, you who dwell in the island. Again, for sea and Tharsis the Seventy translated Carthage. We read in the histories of the Assyrians that the besieged Tyrians, when they saw no hope of escape, embarked on ships and fled to Carthage, or to other islands of the Ionian and Aegean sea. Hence too it is said in Ezekiel: No wage has been paid to him, nor to his army, for Tyre (Ezek 29): because all the wealth of the city had been carried away, and all the nobles had left it empty.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.7":["Is not this yours, which gloried from the days of old in its antiquity? He convicts Tyre of pride, because it gloried in the antiquity of its founding and did not look to God, but, recalling the names of its fathers, thought itself eternal.\n\nIts own feet will lead it far away to sojourn abroad. He means those who had remained in the city and were led captive to Babylon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.8":["Who has devised this against Tyre, once crowned? Read Ezekiel again (Ezek 27), and you will learn from the lament over its prince how great the glory of Tyre was. He calls it crowned because, as a king among a great multitude of men raises his head adorned with a diadem, so Tyre, gleaming and lofty in riches, and shining with gold and gems and silk and purple, was esteemed a queen among all nations.\n\nWhose merchants are princes, its traders the renowned of the earth. We marvel that the envoy of Pyrrhus once said of the city of Rome: I have seen a city of kings. Behold, long before those times the merchants and traders of Tyre are described as princes and renowned men: so that by this the greatness of its opulence is shown, since a merchant of Tyre could be a king in the other's words.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Who has purposed these things against Tyre? Is she less, or does she not prevail? Her merchants are princes of Canaan, the renowned of the earth. The Lord Sabaoth has purposed to dissolve all the insolence of those who glory, and to dishonour all that is renowned upon the earth. The word Canaan has been added from the edition of Theodotion; for it Aquila translated merchants. The Holy Spirit therefore asks, or rather by the question affirms, that the Lord has purposed these things against the princes of Tyre, whose whole industry is to seek wealth by trading. And as in the Gospel we read of dealers in pearls (Matt 13), who, having sold all, long to buy one pearl, and who surely are to be called renowned merchants, not of earth but of heaven, so, on the contrary, the merchants of Tyre, that is, of tribulation and distress, are to be called the renowned of the earth, because whatever they do looks to the earth; and princes of Canaan, which means tossed about and shaken. For they do not set their feet upon the rock, nor can it be said of them, The rock is a refuge for the hedgehogs (Ps 39 [40]; 103 [104]:18), or for the hares; but rather they endure what the righteous man almost suffered: But my feet were almost moved, my steps had well nigh slipped (Ps 72 [73]:2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.9":["The Lord of hosts has devised this, to pull down the pride of all glory, and to bring into ignominy all the renowned of the earth. Because above, in the manner of one asking, he had said: Who has devised this against Tyre, once crowned?, now he himself answers: It is not that the threads of the fates have been spun thus, as fools think; it is not that the wheel of fortune has run; but it has been accomplished by the judgment of God and by his will, who resists the proud and gives grace to the humble (Jas 4); and he reminds the arrogant of their condition, so that through misery they may learn the power of him whose clemency they had refused to feel through his benefits.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The Lord of hosts, then, purposed this counsel: to dissolve the most wicked bonds of the merchants, or rather to scatter the compacted injury through which all who are subject to Tyre grow proud. And as for what he added, and to dishonour all that is glorious upon the earth, the Lord is not the author of dishonour, but shows to be shameful what lies open to shame of itself. This indeed we read in Leviticus also concerning the Priests: With defilement he has defiled him (Lev 21), the priest, no doubt: not that he is the author of the defilement, but that he shows him to be defiled who formerly seemed clean to very many.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.10-11":["Work your land; for ships no longer come from Carthage, and your hand no longer prevails, which provokes kings on the sea. The edition of the Seventy differs greatly in this place from the other translators and from the Hebrew itself; but let us pursue our purpose. Above he had said: Go to Carthage; howl, you who dwell in the isle. Now he says the opposite: since ships no longer come from Carthage, work your land. For it profited Tyre that the foreign ships perished, so that she is compelled to work her own land, of which it is said in the Proverbs: He who works his land shall be filled with bread (Prov 12:11): so that she may live not from the uncertain hazard of the waves and of shipwrecks, but from the fruits of her own work, of which it is sung in the psalms to the righteous man: You shall eat the labours of your hands (Ps 127 [128]:2). It is written in Hosea that the ways of the harlot were hedged by the Lord with thorns, so that she could not follow her lovers, in order that, driven by this necessity, she might return to her first husband. And this we often observe in the affairs of the world too: many who, through prosperity and abundance of all things, could not perceive the Lord, come to understand him through want, and are converted to the works of righteousness, after their hand no longer prevails to enjoy the felicity of this world, the hand which formerly provoked kings on the sea, or according to Symmachus, troubled them; kings whose heart is in the hand of God. And would that we too, scorning the trade of this sea, might work our land, and by no means wait for the ships of Carthage, or the ships of Tyre which used to go to Carthage, lest we be made subject to the power of the dragon who rules in the sea! But let us fix a steady step upon the land, or rather, hastening to heavenly things, let us work our land, so that, sowing here, we may reap there. Our hand too, which formerly was busied with the affairs of the world, and through power and felicity could move even kings, that is, the saints, from their standing, let it become feeble in the affairs of the sea, so that it may be strong in the work of its own land.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.10":["Pass through your land like a river, daughter of the sea: there is no girdle for you any more. As, he says, a river, or, as the Hebrew has it more expressively, a stream, for it is called IOR (יאר), is easily crossed on foot, so you too, led into captivity, will pass through your land, O daughter of the sea, either because you are an island, or because it was written of you above: The sea has spoken, the strength of the sea: I have not travailed, and I have not brought forth. And what sense his next words have, there is no girdle for you any more, Symmachus teaches more openly: you will no longer be able to resist, that is, you will not have the strength, nor your loins girded for fighting, so as to be able to go out against your adversaries.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.11-12":["The Lord Sabaoth has commanded concerning Canaan to destroy its strength, and they shall say: You shall no more do injury and iniquity to the virgin daughter of Sidon. That Tyre and Sidon are in the land of Canaan we have shown above also, setting forth the testimony of the Gospel, in which we read that the Canaanite woman, or Syrophoenician, came out of the borders of Tyre and Sidon and met the Lord the Savior. Now every soul that is set amid the waves of the world and is carried about by every wind of doctrine is to be called Canaan, which means as it were a tossing or a shaking. Hence it is said also to the adulterous old man: Seed of Canaan and not of Judah, beauty has deceived you (Dan 13). It profits Tyre and its distresses, then, that the Canaanite strength should perish, so that it may be said to its inhabitants that they can no longer do injuries and oppress by iniquity the virgin daughter of Sidon. Whoever is taken by the devil in the turmoil of various vices, and is delivered over to the outrages of shame, so as to pollute the body with pleasures and foulness, he is the son or daughter of Sidon. And this is to be known: that Sabaoth is not found in the Hebrew, and on the contrary virgin has been added from the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.11":["He has stretched out his hand over the sea, he has troubled kingdoms. Who stretched out his hand? From the very tenor of the discourse there is no doubt that it is God, of whom he said above: The Lord of hosts has devised this, to pull down the pride of all glory. He stretched out his hand, then, over all the seas, signifying the world and the earth, and troubled all the nations, among which you too, O Tyre, were troubled; or else over the sea, in which you are properly situated.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The Lord has given command against Canaan, to crush its strong ones. He shows what the sea is over which the Lord stretched out his hand, saying: He has given command against Canaan. For Tyre was founded in the land of Canaan. Read the Scriptures, and especially the Gospel (Mark 7), where the Syrophoenician woman, pleading for her daughter, comes out of the land of Canaan, and is called a Canaanite woman. And at the same time, because he had said: He has stretched out his hand, lest you should believe that the Lord works with limbs and by bodily function, he added, he has given command, so that for God to have done is to have commanded.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.12-13":["If you go to the Kittim, not even there shall there be rest for you; and if to the land of the Chaldeans, it too has been laid waste by the Assyrians. Siim founded it; they set up its ramparts, they raised its tower: its wall has fallen. What follows has been added from the edition of Theodotion under asterisks: Siim founded it; they set up its ramparts; they raised its tower; and joined without asterisks: its wall has fallen. The Kittim are interpreted a stroke consummated or perfect; the Chaldeans in this place, as it were breasts; the Assyrians, reproving. The etymology of Siim we too have not been able to find, and the other translators rendered it by the very name with which it is written among the Hebrews. It is said, therefore, to Tyre, that although she strive to go to the Kittim and avoid the stroke of her distresses, she cannot find perfect rest even there. And again, if she desires to go to the Chaldeans and enjoy their fertility and the abundance of all things, she will find them too desolate, the Assyrians reproving their barrenness; just as the Apostle also delivered sinners to Satan (1 Tim 1), that they might learn not to blaspheme: they are delivered to the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved. And Siim laid the foundations of the Chaldeans, by whom I understand the most wicked demons, who raised both the ramparts and the proudest towers of the Chaldean city against the knowledge of the Lord. But all their building has fallen into ruins; for there follows, its wall has fallen. For unless the Lord builds the house, they have laboured in vain who build it (Ps 126 [127]:1). We often see in the world some men pass from one pursuit to another: for example, those who have fared badly in soldiering pass over to trade; and again advocates take up the arms of warriors. They change their occupation in order to change their misfortune; and nonetheless, by the dispensation of God, for those whom he deigns to save everything turns out contrary to their striving, so that through want and miseries they are compelled to have confidence not in themselves but in their Creator.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.12":["And he said: You will no more glory, suffering calumny, O virgin daughter of Sidon; rise, cross over to Cethim: there too there will be no rest for you. God, who devised to pull down the pride of all glory, who gave command against Canaan to crush its strong ones, himself says: You will by no means glory any longer, nor trust in your own power. O virgin daughter of Sidon, that is, colony of the Sidonians, you will indeed flee in ships to the islands of the West, or to Cyprus, and the other lands of Macedonia and Greece; but there too, with God against you, you will not be able to find rest. Besides, everyone who is a wanderer and a stranger in the world, and of uncertain abode, is always in straits, continually in grief.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.13-14":["Behold the land of the Chaldeans, such a people there was not: Assyria founded it for captivity; they carried away its mighty men, they undermined its houses, they made it a ruin. Howl, you ships of the sea, for your strength has been laid waste. Because he had said above: Its own feet will lead it far away to sojourn abroad; and again, Pass through your land like a river; and because the people of Tyre was twofold, those who had fled and crossed over to Cethim, and those who had remained in the city, he speaks to both. Of those who had fled he said above: Rise, cross over to Cethim, there too there will be no rest for you. Of those who had remained and were led into captivity: Behold, he says, the inhabitants of the land of the Chaldeans, whose power no peoples had before, and which was founded by the Assyrian, have carried away the mighty men of Tyre. They undermined not only the walls but all the dwellings of the city, and made it a ruin. Since, then, some have fled and others have been captured, howl, you ships, whether of the sea or of Carthage; for your mart and your colony has been destroyed. And at the same time consider how he has praised the Chaldeans. He did not say, such a people will not be hereafter: for the kingdom of the Romans is more powerful and harsher; but, there was not before. He who denied what went before conceded what follows.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.14":["Furthermore, the Hebrews claim that Tarshish generally represents the sea, as in the psalms: \"With a violent wind, you will destroy the ships of Tarshish,\" that is, the sea, and in Isaiah: \"Wail, ships of Tarshish.\" I recall speaking about this several years ago in a letter to Marcella. The prophet, therefore, was not seeking to flee to a specific place, but he was hastening to continue toward wherever it was the sea would take him. Indeed, a terrified fugitive is rightly more interested in seizing the first opportunity to sail than he is in selecting a place of refuge. This also we are able to say: he who thought that \"God is known in Judea\" only and that \"his name is great in Israel\" only, once he felt him in the waves of the sea, confessed and said, \"I am a Hebrew, and I fear the Lord of heaven who made the sea and the dry land.\" But if he made the sea and the dry land, how can you who abandoned the dry land think it possible to avoid the Creator of the sea in the midst of the sea? At the same time, the salvation and conversion of the sailors taught him that the great multitude at Nineveh could also be saved by confessing like he did.We are able to say of our Lord and Savior that he left his native homeland, assumed flesh and, in a manner of speaking, fled from heaven and came to Tarshish. That is, [he came] to the sea of this world, about which it is said elsewhere: \"This is the sea, great and vast, where there are creatures without number and animals both small and large. Ships navigate there with the dragon whom you formed to play in it.\"\n— Commentary on Jonah, Chapter 1, Verse 3a","Howl, you ships of Carthage, for your strength has perished. The ships of Carthage, that is, of Tharsis, are bidden to howl; for they have not, as above, perished or shall not come, but their strength has perished. For Tharsis, according to another interpretation, is rendered into our tongue consummation of six, or of gladness. Now we read that this world was made in six days, and according to the traditions of the Church it will afterwards be consummated. All the goods of this world, then, and the whole labour of mortals, are compared to the uncertain course of ships, because they will quickly perish, and all the strength of those who sail will be dissolved. Hence it is written in Solomon also: The wise man has gone up to strong cities, and has destroyed their fortifications (Prov 21:22). For whatever is composed by the art of the heretics, of worldly wisdom, and of opposing doctrines, the churchman destroys, and teaches that it is subject to his feet.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.15-18":["And it shall come to pass in that day that Tyre shall be forsaken seventy years, as the time of one king, as the time of a man. And after seventy years Tyre shall be as the song of a harlot. Take a harp, go about, harlot city given over to forgetfulness. Play well on the harp, sing much, that you may be remembered. And after seventy years God will visit Tyre, and she shall be restored again to her former state; and her trade shall be with all the kingdoms of the world upon the face of the earth. And her trade and her hire shall be holy to the Lord; but it shall not be gathered for themselves, but for those who dwell before the Lord. All her trade, to eat and drink and be filled in common store, a memorial before the Lord. What is said, a memorial before the Lord, and upon the face of the earth, and as the time of a man, is not found in the Hebrew, but has been added in the Greek. Now Tyre, once straitened, is let go for seventy years, so that, the time of desolation fulfilled, she may turn her once harlot's song into the praises of God, and, taking up the harp, may have all its strings sounding in concord; so that, after she has played well in concord and sung much, there may be a remembrance before God of her who on account of fornication had been given over to forgetfulness; and that she may be restored to her former state, and have the riches of the kingdoms of the whole world, and that the wages of her labour may by no means be gathered for the Tyrians, but for those who dwell in the sight of the Lord, and that they may eat and drink and be filled with all the good things which have been brought together by the labour of all for the gladness of the banquet. The divine word exhorts Sidon and Tyre to repentance, and promises that her labours and wages are to be sanctified to the Lord. Who among sinners would not have hope of salvation, if only he sings well, and all the strings of the virtues, which once had been slackened, are tuned to the praises of the Lord? We read in Psalm 44 (45), which pertains especially to the union of the bridegroom and the bride, that is, to God the Savior and to the mysteries of the Church, that it is said among other things: The daughters of Tyre with gifts, the rich among the people shall entreat your face (Ps 44 [45]:13). And the bridegroom himself speaks again to the Tyrian bride: Hear, O daughter, and see, and incline your ear; and forget your people and your father's house, for the king shall desire your beauty (ibid. 11, 12). Hence in the description of her beauty too it is added: The queen stood at your right hand, in a vesture of gold, surrounded with variety (ibid. 10). And again: All the glory of the king's daughter is within (ibid. 14). And if the king desired the beauty of the penitent Tyrian woman, who has the ornaments of various virtues, how much more shall her hire and her trade belong not to those who remain in Tyre but to those who dwell in the sight of the Lord! These, after they have done penance, shall hear from the Lord the Savior: Eat, my friends, and drink, and be inebriated, my dearly beloved (Song 5:1). And what it is to eat and drink and be filled with the common store of all the virtues, the faithful reader understands. That the temple was desolate for seventy years Jeremiah (Jer 29), Daniel (Dan 9) and Zechariah (Zech 7) teach. And in Ezekiel (Ezek 16) we read of Sodom that it shall be restored to its former state, and of Egypt that, after the desolation of the land of Egypt and the drying up of the seven rivers, it shall recover its old fertility. Now the number seven and seventy, which is made up either of single days or of seven decades, signifies a perfect and consummated repentance; so that Tyre rightly, the time of repentance fulfilled, returns to her former state. Of this harlot, I think, that saying too in the Proverbs of Solomon is signified in mystical speech: Do not heed a most wicked woman; for honey drips from the lips of a harlot woman, which for a short while fattens your throat, but afterwards you will find her more bitter than gall (Prov 5:2-4). For she looks out through the window of her house into the streets (Matt 7), because wide and spacious is the way that leads to death; and whatever foolish young man she sees (for the wise she does not dare to tempt) passing by the corners, those who have lost the straight line, she speaks to him in the darkness and in the gloom, and under the appearance of pleasures leads him like a victim to death. If she is converted, and sings well, and fulfils the time of years of perfect repentance, she shall eat and drink and be filled. Let Novatian hear this, and be silent.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.15":["And it will come to pass in that day, you will be in oblivion, O Tyre, for seventy years, like the days of one king. From this place to the end of this prophecy prosperous things are announced to Tyre: that it ought to repent; that it is to be built again; that the overthrow of Tyre is to last only seventy years, the years during which the temple of God too was desolate: so that the city that mocked at the ruins of another should also match the time of that one's ruins. The seventy years of one king many think to be said of Nebuchadnezzar, the years during which the kingdom of the Assyrian nation continued in Jerusalem. Others think it is said in general: that he who has had a long time of reigning cannot go beyond seventy years.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.16":["But after seventy years it will be for Tyre like the song of a harlot. Take a harp, go about the city, harlot given over to oblivion; sing well, repeat the song, that you may be remembered. Because you have committed fornication with very many nations, and your former lovers have despised you, desolate and naked and filthy with the squalor of captivity, take up the harp now, redouble your songs, go about the city, lament your former brothel, and wash away with tears the traces of your old error, so that you may call forth the mercy of God upon you.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.17":["And it will come to pass after seventy years, the Lord will visit Tyre, and will bring it back to its wages, and again it will commit fornication with all the kingdoms of the earth upon the face of the earth. That all these things were fulfilled according to the history is shown by the concordant writings of the Greeks and Phoenicians: that after seventy years it was restored and returned to its former power, and became the mart of all the nations; and these, I think, are called fornications: so that, as he who commits fornication enters the brothel and pays the wage of debauchery, so the trader, flocking in from the whole world, has filled up the beauty of the city as of a harlot.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.23.18":["And its merchandise and its wages will be sanctified to the Lord: they will not be hidden away nor laid up. For its merchandise will be for those who dwell before the Lord, that they may eat to the full and be clothed even to old age. We find that these things have not yet been done according to the history; unless perhaps it is to be thought that after the building of Jerusalem and the restoration of Tyre the cities were friendly to each other, and Tyre often sent gifts to the temple of God: as we read in Ezra too (Neh 13:16), that he drove off the Tyrians who were selling fish on the sabbath, and did not allow them to enter the city: and from this one item of trade one must judge of the rest as well. The Jews put off their vain hopes into the future, declaring that these things are to be fulfilled after the Antichrist, in the thousand years. Nor is it surprising if those invent such things who, having spurned the truth of Christ, are to receive the Antichrist, the instrument of the devil, since even judaizing Christians contend that these things were said of the blessedness of the thousand years. But I, just as in the vision of Babylon I understand the time of the consummation under the type of its overthrow, and in Egypt refer to the time of Christ the altar of the Lord set up when the idols were destroyed, and in the vision of Moab recognize the throne of the Savior set up in its land, and in the visions of Damascus and all the others have no doubt that they are mysteries of our own times, so also in the vision of Tyre, which is the last, I interpret it of the time of Christian felicity: that after it was built and recovered its former state, and every merchant entered its harbor according to the old custom, their merchandise and their labor too are dedicated to the Lord. Let us behold the Churches of Christ built in Tyre; let us consider the wealth of all, that it is not laid up nor treasured, but given to those who dwell before the Lord, who, serving the altar, partake of the altar (1 Cor 9). And the Tyrians minister, not for riches, nor for heaping up the wealth of the priests, but for supplying the necessities of life: so that, according to the Apostle, having food and clothing, we may be content with these (1 Tim 6). And note that he did not say that the merchandise and wages of Tyre sanctified to the Lord are to be given to those who dwell in Jerusalem, as the Jews think, but to those who are before the Lord, who serve him. For so too the Lord has ordained, that those who preach the Gospel should live by the Gospel (1 Cor 9). And to serve the Lord and to dwell before him is a matter not of place but of merit.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.1":["Behold, the Lord will scatter the earth, and will lay it bare, and will afflict its face, and will disperse its inhabitants; and as the people is, so will the priest be; and as the servant, so his master; as the handmaid, so her mistress; as the buyer, so he who sells; as the lender, so he who receives the loan; as he who demands repayment, so he who owes. With scattering the earth will be scattered, and with plundering it will be despoiled: for the Lord has spoken this word. After the particular rebuke of the individual nations, Judea, Babylon, the Philistines, Moab, Damascus, Israel, Egypt, the desert of the sea, Edom and Arabia, the valley of vision, and at the last Tyre, in the explanation of which we have said what we could, the prophetic discourse now describes what the whole world is going to suffer at the consummation, and the prophecy is by no means about the individual nations, but about all of them alike. And first, what torments the impious are going to suffer; and how, according to the Gospel and the Apostle, heaven and earth and the fashion of this world pass away (Matt 24; 1 Cor 7), and sinners are led down into hell (Ps 30 [31]), of whom it is written: They shall go into the lowest parts of the earth, they shall be delivered into the hands of the sword, they shall be the portions of foxes (Ps 62 [63]:10). Then, because according to the quality of their merits there are many mansions with the Father, it is told how the saints are caught up in the clouds to meet the Lord in the air, and are to be with him always (John 14; 1 Thess 4). For the earth scattered, the Seventy translated the world corrupted; and for its face afflicted, that is, the earth's, the same translated, and he will uncover its face, so that the dead may come forth from their tombs; or he will lay it bare, so that all its works may be brought out into public view, and its inhabitants be dispersed into different places, destined for rewards or for punishments.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.2":["Then there will be no difference between noble and base, priest and layman, servant and master, handmaid and mistress, rich and poor, the lender and him who is pressed by debt, buyer and seller. For all will stand on equal terms before the judgment seat of Christ, and there will be no respect of persons with God (Rom 14; Col 3). Of this Job too speaks in almost the same words: The small and the great are there, and the servant who does not fear his master (Job 3:19). And the Savior in the Gospel, to whom all judgment has been committed, bears witness in full discourse.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.3":["The earth, therefore, will be scattered, and all earthly works will be reduced to nothing, so that, the image of the χοϊκοῦ having been abolished, the image of the heavenly may remain. For the first man was from the earth, earthly, and the second from heaven, heavenly: as is the earthly, such also are the earthly; and as is the heavenly, such also are the heavenly; so that, as we have borne the image of the earthly, we may bear also the image of the heavenly (1 Cor 15:47-49). Hence the same Apostle says: Flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God (ibid., 50). Not that, as the heretics say, the nature of bodies perishes, but that this corruptible puts on incorruption, and this mortal puts on immortality. And all these things will come to pass, because what is to come the Lord has spoken through the prophets.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.4-5":["The earth has mourned and has wasted away, and has been weakened; the world has wasted away, the height of the people of the earth has been weakened, and the earth has been slain by its inhabitants. LXX: The earth has mourned, the world has been corrupted, the lofty of the earth have mourned, but the earth has acted impiously because of its inhabitants. God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble (Jas 4:6). Hence the first sentence is against those who are lofty on the earth, that all things which are now strong may be weakened because of those who have mingled blood with blood, and, in the manner of the blood of Abel, have made the gore of the slain cry out to God (Gen 4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.5":["Because they have transgressed the law, they have changed the ordinance, they have broken the everlasting covenant. Because of this a curse will devour the earth, and its inhabitants will sin; therefore its tillers will go mad, and few men will be left. LXX: Because they have transgressed the law, they have changed the precepts, the eternal testament. Because of this a curse will devour the earth, because its inhabitants have sinned; therefore the inhabitants of the earth will be poor, and few men will be left. Let the Jews hear, who boast that they alone have received the law of the Lord, that first all the nations and the whole world received the natural law, and that the law was given afterwards through Moses for this reason, because the first law had been broken. Of it the Apostle speaks: For when the Gentiles, who do not have the law, do by nature the things that are of the law, these, not having the law, are a law to themselves: who show the work of the law written in their hearts (Rom 2:14). Those, then, who have kept these laws will obtain rewards; but those who have neglected them will endure what the prophetic discourse now threatens.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.6":["And as in the beginning God blessed every creature that he had made (Gen 1), so at the consummation of the world he will curse those who are earthly, and who were not sojourners but inhabitants of the earth, and sinned in it; who, forgetting their condition, have raged against one another in mutual fury. And few will remain who have the heavenly image; or, according to the Seventy, the inhabitants of the earth will be poor, because they have lost their spiritual riches.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.7-9":["The vintage has mourned, the vine has been weakened, all who were glad of heart have groaned. The joy of the timbrels has ceased, the sound of those who rejoice has been stilled, the sweetness of the harp has fallen silent; they shall not drink wine with song, the drink will be bitter to those who drink it. The city of vanity has been crushed, every house has been shut, with no one entering. There will be an outcry over wine in the streets; all gladness has been forsaken, the joy of the earth has been carried away. And desolation has been left in the city, and calamity will overwhelm the gates: for these things will be in the midst of the earth, in the midst of the peoples. At the consummation of the world, the remembrance of past delights will be the matter of torments. Hence also that rich man clothed in purple at his banquet, who had received his good things in his life, lifting up his eyes from hell, sees Lazarus at rest (Luke 16). And the Lord, rebuking the rich, the luxurious and those who laugh, says in the Gospel: Woe to you rich, for you have received your consolation. Woe to you who are now filled, for you shall hunger. Woe to you who now laugh, for you shall mourn and weep (ibid., 24, 25). When, therefore, the resurrection of the dead has come, and the day of judgment has arrived, then will mourn the wine and the vintage of which Moses speaks: Their vine is of the vine of Sodom, and their shoot of Gomorrah. Their grape is a grape of gall, their cluster is one of bitterness. Their wine is the fury of dragons, and the incurable fury of asps (Deut 32:33). Then every drink, or, as it is said in Hebrew, sicera, that is, drunkenness, which overturns the state of the mind and does not allow men to keep watch, will be changed into bitterness: it counterfeits honey for a time to those who use it, and in the end is found more bitter than gall. Then all the sweetness of those who rejoice, and the sound of timbrels and harp, will be turned into lamentation and groaning. Let us press this testimony upon those who at banquets are wanton not only with gluttony and drunkenness but also with their ears, so that through all the senses the strength of the soul grows soft.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.10":["The city of vanity will be crushed, or every city, or the spiritual Babylon, which sits clothed in purple upon seven hills, whose punishments we read in the Apocalypse of John (Rev 18). And he did well to say the city of vanity. For if it is said of heaven and earth and of all things that are earthly: Vanity of vanities, and all is vanity (Eccl 1:2), how much more must this be said of one city, which is a small part of the whole world! Then the houses whose ceilings are now gilded, and whose walls, while the poor die of cold without roof or hut, are clothed with veneers of marble and shine with the gleam of sawn ivory, will remain empty.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.11-12":["There will be an outcry in the streets over wine, not in the strait and narrow way that leads to life, but in the broad and spacious one that leads to death (Matt 7): over the wine and drunkenness of the error of those who have slept their sleep, and all the men of riches have found nothing in their hands (Ps 75 [76]). For the joy of the earth has been carried away into heaven, and desolation has been left in the once most crowded city, and the calamity of its inhabitants will overwhelm the gates of the busy streets, through which the throngs of the peoples used to stream in.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.13-14":["According to the Seventy, all that we have understood of the saints can be referred to the impious: that when the vintage is finished, then they cry out, set in punishments. But those who have escaped the number of the impious rejoice in the glory of the Lord, and the waters of the peoples of this world are troubled.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.13":["And that we might know plainly that the destruction of the whole world is being announced, he added: These things will be in the midst of the earth, in the midst of the nations, or of the peoples.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","As if a few olives that are left were shaken from the olive tree, and the grape clusters when the vintage is finished. These will lift up their voice and give praise; when the Lord has been glorified, they will neigh from the sea: therefore glorify the Lord in teachings, in the islands of the sea the name of the Lord God of Israel. How far the translation of the Seventy differs in this place from the Hebrew truth, the following words will show. LXX: As if someone were to shake an olive tree, so they will shake them; and if the vintage ceases, these will cry out with clamor. But those who have been left upon the earth will rejoice together with the glory of the Lord; the water of the sea will be troubled; therefore the glory of the Lord will be in the islands of the sea, the name of the Lord will be glorious, of the Lord God of Israel. When few men have been left, when the curse has devoured the earth, and there is desolation in the city, and all these things have happened in the midst of the earth, in the midst of the peoples and nations, so great will be the fewness of the saints, of whom the Lord speaks in the Gospel: Many are called, but few chosen (Matt 20:16); and so violent the oppression of the righteous, that even the elect of God, if it were possible, would be tempted; and their fewness is compared to the very rare berries of olives, of which, when they have been shaken down and gathered, scarcely a few remain at the tops of the branches; and to the way that, when the vintage is finished, the poor, driven by need, are accustomed to go round the empty vines and gather a few grapes.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.14":["These, then, who remain, and after the vintage and the pressing of the world have been able to escape the hands of the persecuting Antichrist, or the burning of the impending punishment, will lift up their voices on high and will praise God. When the Lord comes in the glory of his Father with the holy angels, and they see him reigning in majesty, then they will neigh in the likeness of horses, exulting in the greatness of their joy, and they will neigh from the sea of this world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.15":["Therefore you who are now instructed in the Holy Scriptures, and know that such great joy and such great rewards are reserved for you, glorify the Lord in teachings, hearing that which is written: He who glories, let him glory in the Lord (2 Cor 10:17). Not at all in the land of Judea, but in the islands, that is, in the Churches of this sea and world, in which is blessed and praised the name of the Lord God of him who was once Israel, or, of the man who sees God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.16-18":["The transgressors have transgressed, and with the transgression of transgressors they have transgressed. Terror and the pit and the snare are upon you, who are an inhabitant of the earth; and it shall be that he who flees from the voice of terror shall fall into the pit, and he who extricates himself from the pit shall be held by the snare. LXX: Woe to the transgressors who transgress the law. Fear and the pit and the snare are upon you who inhabit the earth. And it shall be that he who flees fear shall fall into the pit, and he who comes out of the pit shall be caught by the snare. This is the cause of my mourning and groaning, on account of which I said a second time, my secret is mine, my secret is mine: because all have transgressed the law of God, and the Lord's punishment is by no means put off, nor is it foretold as future, but it is imminent, and holds the inhabitants of the earth captive. And when they think they have escaped, they will fall from one thing into another, and wherever they turn they will not evade the impending wrath of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.16":["From the ends of the earth we have heard praises, the glory of the Righteous One; and I said: My secret is mine, my secret is mine: woe is me. LXX: From the wings of the earth we have heard portents: hope for the righteous; and they will say ※ my mystery is mine ⁚. This, which is said, my mystery is mine, is not found in the Seventy, but has been added in the Greek from the translation of Theodotion. Again, for what they put as woe, so as to join it to the following verse, in the Hebrew is said OI LI (אוי לי), which properly means woe is me. Also for ends, which we have set down for the sake of explaining the sense more openly, the Hebrew has MECCHENAPH (מכנף), which means wing, not end. These, then, of whom it was said above: They will lift up their voice and give praise, when they have neighed from the sea, and have glorified the Lord in teachings, and have seen the name of the Lord God of Israel praised in the islands of the sea, then will sing with harmonious voice and say: From the wings of the earth, that is, from the prophets and from the saints of the Lord, who, having taken the wings of a dove, hasten to the kingdoms of the heavens, we have heard his praises proclaimed; and that the glory or hope of the Righteous One is not vain, but all things are fulfilled in deed. So, while the saints say these things, and neigh from the sea, and lift up their voice and give praise, the Prophet speaks to himself: When, he says, I had heard these things, and perceived that the prophecy of the Prophets was to be fulfilled in the overthrow of the world, I said to myself with the inward feeling of my heart: I cannot tell all that I see. My tongue cleaves to my throat, my voice is shut in by grief. Woe is me, what a train of punishments passes before my eyes! I see as present the things that are to come. But those err who think this must be understood as spoken in the person of God, not following the order of the matter. And I wonder in what sense, for psalms and praises, which in the Hebrew reads ZEMROTH (זמרות), the Seventy translated portents, unless perhaps it is a sign and a portent that, the people of the Jews being shut out, the once unbelieving multitude of the nations is saved.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.18":["For the floodgates from on high have been opened, and the foundations of the earth will be shaken. With breaking the earth will be broken, with crushing the earth will be crushed, with shaking the earth will be shaken. With tossing the earth will be tossed like a drunken man, and it will be removed like a tent of one night, and its iniquity will weigh it down, and it will fall, and will not add to rise again. Therefore no one will escape the terror and the snare and the pit of the Lord, because the floodgates from on high, or, as the Seventy translated more plainly, the windows of heaven have been opened, so that the Lord might look down upon all the sins of men, which before, because he did not punish them, he seemed to sinners not to know. But after he has seen all the works of mortals through the opened windows, the foundations of the earth have been shaken, according to what is written in another place of the gaze of God: Who looks upon the earth, and will make it tremble (Ps 103 [104]:32).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.19-20":["Then the earth will be broken and crushed, and shaken and tossed in the likeness of a drunken man: not that the earth itself is reduced to dust and to nothing, but that all earthly things pass away, and another manner of life succeeds. And as a drunken man does not know what he is doing, but, his sinews growing dull with drunkenness, neither foot nor mind stands to its duty: so the whole earth, that is, all men who dwell on the earth, will be drunk with the greatness of the evils and the punishments, and will be stupefied at all that they see. And as a tent or pavilion of one night is moved from place to place, and the place of the former tent is abandoned by the traveler, so that no trace remains of the past lodging: so the fashion of this world will pass away, and the earth will be deserted which has been weighed down by its iniquity, whose weight and most grievous burden is described in Zechariah: Who sat upon a talent of lead (Zech 5:7). And it will fall, he says, and will not add to rise again. Not that the resurrection of men and of all who dwelt on the earth is denied, but that there will by no means be an earthly manner of life and the state of the former life, since human bodies rise again for this purpose, that souls may be clothed with the same bodies that they had laid aside before, and may receive from God either the good or the evil that they did upon the earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.21":["And this is the sense of the whole passage: The windows of heaven have been opened, so that, as the Lord looked out upon earthly sins, every fashion of earthly works might pass away and fall, and by no means rise again to its former state. In that day, that is, in the day of judgment, the Lord will visit upon the host, or upon the adornment, of heaven on high, so that he may judge not only earthly but also heavenly things. And what the adornment or host of heaven is, let us learn from the writing of Moses: Beware lest, looking up to heaven and seeing the sun and the moon, the stars and all the adornment of heaven, you be deceived and worship them (Deut 4:19). And the Lord will visit, according to the idiom of the Scriptures, the host and army of heaven as one sick and in need of the knife and cautery, according to this: I will visit their sins with a rod, and their iniquities with scourges (Ps 88 [89]:33). For in what follows too we read: My sword has been made drunk in heaven (below, Isa 34:5); and in Job: The stars are not clean in his sight. And: Against his angels he has found something perverse (Job 25:5). He will visit also upon the kings and princes of the earth, the rulers of this darkness, and the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places. Of these princes, who preside over the various provinces, it is written also in Daniel: The prince of the kingdom of the Persians came out to meet me, and the prince of the kingdom of the Medes, and the prince of the kingdom of the Greeks (Dan 10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.22":["These princes, then, who did not keep their rank, the Lord will gather in the day of judgment, bound together as in one bundle, and will cast them into the pit of hell, so that in them too may be fulfilled what is written of the impious: He opened a pit and dug it, and he fell into the ditch which he made (Ps 7:16). And they will be shut up in prison, according to what the Lord says: Depart into the eternal fire, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels (Matt 25:41). But what follows, And after many days they will be visited, seems to applaud my friends who grant repentance to the devil and the demons, in that after long times they are visited by the Lord. But let them consider that the divine Scripture did not say openly, They will be visited by the Lord, or, they will be visited by the angels, but absolutely, they will be visited. From this ambiguity of the word both remedy and correction can be understood: that after the righteous have received their rewards, those are visited in perpetual punishments. Yet it must be known that human frailty cannot know the judgment of God, nor pass sentence on the greatness and measure of the punishments, which has been left to the Lord's decision.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.24.23":["And it will be in that day: the Lord will visit upon the host of heaven on high, and upon the kings of the earth who are upon the earth. And they will be gathered together in the gathering of one bundle into the pit, and they will be shut up there in prison, and after many days they will be visited. And the moon will blush, and the sun will be confounded, when the Lord of hosts reigns on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem, and has been glorified in the sight of his elders. For what we have translated, and the moon will blush, and the sun will be confounded, the Seventy translated, the brick will melt, and the wall will fall; what the cause of the error is, the following words will show. The sun in the Hebrew language is called by three names: SEMES (שמש), and HAMMA (המה), which means heat, and HERES (חרס), which signifies ὄστρακον, that is, potsherd or dryness. MAOR (מאר), however, which in Greek is called φωστήρ and in Latin luminary, is common to the sun and the moon. Again, the moon is called JAREE (ירה), which in Greek is called μήνη, because it makes a month by a circuit of thirty days, and LABANA (לבנה), that is, white or bright. In the present place, then, for LABANA, that is, moon, the Seventy translated brick, which in Hebrew is called LEBENA, deceived by the ambiguity of the word. Again, for HAMMA, that is, heat, by which is understood the sun, they put wall, which in Hebrew is called HOMA (הומה).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Then the moon will blush, and the sun will be confounded, according to what the Apostle says: For the creation itself groans and travails (Rom 8:22), seeing that men who enjoyed its light have done nothing worthy of the goodness of God, who makes his sun rise upon the righteous and the unrighteous (Matt 5): as if a steward and bailiff, when the master comes, were to see the household subjected to various torments and not having fulfilled his precepts at all. Of this same passage the Savior teaches more fully in the Gospel: The sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light, and the stars will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be moved, when the sign of the Son of man has appeared in heaven, and all the tribes of the earth have beaten their breasts, and have seen the Son of man coming with the clouds of heaven in power and much glory (Matt 24:29, 30). We have learned of the overthrow of the earth, the visitation of the host of heaven, the gathering of the kings and princes into one bundle, and their thrusting down into the pit, and the keeping of the prison, and the visitation of those shut up after a long time, the blushing of the moon and the confusion of the sun. After all these things the Lord of hosts will reign on Mount Zion and in the heavenly Jerusalem, of which it is written also in the Epistle to the Hebrews: And in the sight of his elders he will be glorified (Heb 12). Such an elder was Abraham, who died in a good old age and was gathered to his fathers (Gen 25). Such were the elders whom Moses too is commanded to choose, whom he knows to be elders (Num 11): for the gray hair of a man is his wisdom (Wis 4:8); who imitate the Ancient of days, whose hair is described as white, so that length of age may be shown (Dan 7:9). This can also be understood of the ecclesiastical rank, if only they do not destroy the dignity by their works.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.1-5":["O Lord, you are my God: I will exalt you, I will confess to your name, for you have done wonders, ancient faithful designs: amen. For you have made the city a heap, the strong city a ruin, the house of strangers, so that it is no city, and will never be built for ever. Because of this a strong people will praise you, the city of mighty nations will fear you. For you have become strength to the poor, strength to the needy in his tribulation, a hope from the whirlwind, a shade from the heat: for the spirit of the mighty is like a whirlwind driving against a wall. Like heat in thirst, you will bring low the tumult of strangers; and as with scorching heat under a cloud, you will make the offshoot of the strong wither. LXX: O Lord my God, I will glorify you; I will praise your name, for you have done wonderful things, an ancient true counsel, so be it. For you have made cities a heap, strong cities, that their foundations might fall. The cities of the impious will not be built for ever. Therefore the people of the poor will bless you, and the cities of men who suffer iniquity will bless you. For you have been a helper to every lowly city, and a protection to those who are sad because of want; you will deliver them from most wicked men: a shade of the thirsty, and the spirit of men who suffer iniquity, like faint-hearted men thirsting in Zion, from the impious men to whom you have delivered us. The exposition of this place is twofold. The Jews think it is the voice of the saints and of the believing people, when God has done against the whole world the things said above, and the prophecies of all the prophets have been fulfilled; and the overthrown city they interpret as Rome, which is to be utterly destroyed; and the strong people, which praises the Lord, and to which the Lord has become strength in its tribulation and distress, they refer to Israel, which has been freed from the persecution of the nations as from most burning heat and thirst.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.1":["But others, both better and more correctly, understand it as said in the person of the prophet, giving thanks to the Father for the passion of the Lord Savior, because he has done wonders and has fulfilled his ancient designs in truth, when those standing at the right hand will hear: Come, blessed of my Father, possess the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world (Matt 25:34). Understanding this, Paul too said: As he chose us in him before the foundation of the world, that we should be holy and spotless (Eph 1:4); and, desiring that what he prophesies should come to pass, he adds the Hebrew word AMEN (אמן), for which the Seventy translated so be it. And the Lord in the Gospel often uses this word: Amen, amen, that is, truly, truly I say to you (John 6:54).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.2":["And why he praises and confesses to the name of the Lord, and what these wonders are, and the ancient designs which he showed true in deed, follows: For you have made the city a heap, the strong city a ruin, the house of strangers, so that it is no city, and will never be built for ever. The once strong city is understood as Jerusalem, which has become the house of strangers; of whom the Savior says in the psalm: Strange children have lied to me, strange children have grown old and have limped in their paths (Ps 17 [18]:46). This city, when it has been destroyed, will never be built for ever, so that the dreams of a thousand-year kingdom and of a golden and jeweled Jerusalem may be laid to rest.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.3":["And when Jerusalem has been destroyed for its impiety, a strong people will praise the Lord. And who the strong people is, the following verse shows: The city of mighty nations will fear you. While those blaspheme, the people of the nations will fear you. For the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Prov 9:10). And a strong people will praise you, and the city of mighty nations will fear you, that is, the Church gathered from the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.4":["For you have become strength to the poor, to your Christ, of whom we read also in the Psalms: Blessed is he who understands concerning the needy and the poor (Ps 40 [41]:1). And in Zechariah, according to the Hebrew truth, the poor man, that is, EBION (אביון), is described as sitting upon the colt of an ass (Zech 9). Strength to the needy, in the tribulation of his passion, a hope from the whirlwind of the cross, and a shade from the heat, when he said: Father, into your hands I commend my spirit (Luke 23:46). For as when the wind is dashed against a wall and passes by, so the whirlwind of the blaspheming Jews could not harm him.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.5":["And to use another comparison: as an offshoot is scorched by most grievous heat and droops, so you will make the tumult and clamor of the strangers, that is, of those who have become strangers to you, wither and perish. According to the Seventy translators, I could not find in this place, I do not say the sense, but the order and sequence of the words. And for that place where we have translated, like heat in thirst, for which in the Hebrew is written BASAION (בציון), which among them means pathless place or thirst: why they turned pathless place and in thirst into in Zion, the error is plain, from the likeness of the words SAION and SION (Both ציון), since it is marked with the same letters.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.6-8":["After the passion of the Lord, then, when he has freed him from thirst and heat and whirlwind, the Lord will make, not at all for the people of the Jews, but for all nations, on Mount Zion a fat feast, burnt offerings full of marrow, and the wine of a vintage refined from the lees, so that he may cast down and cause to be swallowed up the face of death and of the bond by which all peoples were bound; and he will break the net of death, and the web that had caught all nations. And according to the Apostle, death will be swallowed up for ever (1 Cor 15). And the Lord will take away the tear from every face, when, death being overcome, the kingdom of Christ has come, and the reproach of the human race, which had been made to the image of the Creator, has escaped the slavery of the devil and of death. Nor is it surprising if according to Symmachus death is called a ruler, since the blessed Apostle said: Death reigned from Adam until Moses, even over those who had not sinned, in the likeness of the transgression of Adam (Rom 5:14). The Ruler of all peoples, or the face of darkness upon all peoples, and the web that was woven over all nations, some wish to be understood as the Antichrist, who is to be consumed on the Mount of Olives, as we also said on the last vision of Daniel. According to the Seventy, a feast of gladness is prepared for all nations on Mount Zion, in which they will drink the wine which the Lord promised that he would drink with his saints in the kingdom of his Father (Matt 26 and Luke 22); and they will be anointed with ointment, so that, reborn in Christ, they may be made a new people; whence it is said: Deliver all these things to the nations, which Israel once celebrated in type and image. For this is the counsel of the Lord, that all things should be transferred to the nations, because death has been swallowed up (1 Cor 15), and every tear wiped away, and the reproach of the whole earth blotted out, as the rule of Christ succeeds.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.7":["And the Lord of hosts will make for all peoples on this mountain a feast of fat things, a feast of the vintage: of fat things full of marrow, of a vintage refined from the lees. And he will cast down on this mountain the face of the bond that was bound upon all peoples, and the web that he wove over all nations. And he will cast down death for ever, and the Lord God will take away the tear from every face, and the reproach of his people he will take away from all the earth: for the Lord has spoken. LXX: And the Lord Sabaoth will make for all nations upon this mountain: they will drink gladness, they will drink wine. They will be anointed with ointment on this mountain. Deliver all these things to the nations, for this counsel is upon all nations: death, prevailing, has devoured. And again: The Lord God has taken away every tear from every face, and the reproach of his people he has taken away from all the earth. For the mouth of the Lord has spoken. For what we have rendered, the face of the bond that was bound upon all peoples, Aquila translated, the face of darkness upon all peoples. And whereas Aquila said darkness twice, Theodotion named darkness once, the rest alike. For this Symmachus translated, the face of the Ruler who rules over all peoples. And what the Seventy meant to say for this place, Deliver all these things to the nations, is plain to the reader: that they set down not the words of Scripture but their own sense, because all the mysteries of the Law and of the Temple were to be transferred to the Churches of the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.9-10":["And they will say in that day: Behold, this is our God; we have waited for him, and he will save us: this is the Lord, we have waited patiently for him; we will exult and be glad in his salvation. For the hand of the Lord will rest on this mountain; and Moab will be threshed under him as straw is crushed in the wagon. And he will stretch out his hands under him, as a swimmer stretches them out to swim, and his glory will be brought low with the dashing of his hands. And the fortifications of your lofty walls will fall, and will be brought low and pulled down to the ground, even to the dust. When death has been swallowed up for ever, the people of God which has been freed from the hand of death will say to the Lord: Behold, this is our God, whom the unbelieving thought only a man; and we have waited for him, that is, we have believed his words, that he will fulfill his promises, and he will save us. Therefore, snatched by his help from the jaws of death, we will exult and be glad in him; and his hand and his power will rest on this mountain, of which we read above: When the Lord of hosts reigns on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem, and has been glorified in the sight of his elders (Isa 24:23). And Moab, which means from the father, will be crushed as straw is wont to be crushed by the wagon. He speaks according to the custom of Palestine and of many provinces of the East, which, for lack of meadows and hay, prepare straw for the feeding of animals. Now there are iron-shod carts, with wheels turning in the middle in the manner of saws, which crush the stubble and break it up into straw. As, then, straw is crushed by iron-shod wagons, so Moab will be crushed under him, whether under the power of God or in himself, so that nothing whole remains in him.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.10-12":["So the prophetic discourse, although it weaves its prophecy generally about the consummation of the world, yet, so as not to seem to neglect present things entirely, names Moab, which was an enemy of Israel, so much so that it made them commit fornication with the Midianites and be consecrated to the idol Baal-peor, which means Priapus; against which Jeremiah speaks: Chemosh will go forth into captivity, his priests and his princes together (Jer 48:7); and again: Moab will be confounded in Chemosh, as the house of Israel was confounded in Bethel (ibid., 13), and other things like these. And from one idol and the demon who presided over this idol, he indicates that all the adverse powers are to be brought low and led down into Tartarus, and crushed like dust. But if this is how it will be, where is the repentance of the devil?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.11":["And as one who swims is wont to stretch out his whole body, so he will be dashed from his power to the ground, and in falling will make a noise.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.25.12":["And all the fortifications of his lofty men, or of his walls, as is contained in the Hebrew, will fall and be brought low, and will be pulled down to the ground, broken to the dust.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.1":["In that day this song will be sung in the land of Judah: The city of our strength, the Savior, a wall and an outer wall will be set in it. LXX: In that day they will sing this song in the land of Judea: Behold a strong city, and our salvation will set a wall and a surrounding wall. When Moab has been brought low and pulled down to the ground, even to the dust, and all enemies laid beneath the feet of Christ, then this song will be sung in the land of Judah, or Judea, each of which means confession: so that, as we have understood Zion and Jerusalem as the heavenly city, so we may also understand the region of this city as the heavenly confession. Indeed, the saints, unwilling to sing the song of Judea in a foreign land, say: How shall we sing a song to the Lord in a foreign land? (Ps 136 [137]:4). I think that this is the song which in another place too the saints are commanded: Sing to the Lord a new song (Ps 95 [96]:1). And this song will be what follows: The city of our strength, the Savior (Matt 5). Which is this city? The one which, set on a mountain, cannot be hid. Of which it is written also in another place: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God (Ps 45 [46]:4); and again: Glorious things are spoken of you, city of God (Ps 86 [87]:2). The founder of this city is he of whom the Father speaks: he has built my city; nay rather, the city of our strength is the Savior, that is, Jesus. And a wall and an outer wall will be set in it: the wall of good works, and the outer wall of right faith, so that it may be enclosed by a double fortification. For it is not enough to have the wall of faith, unless faith itself is confirmed by good works. This wall and this outer wall, or surrounding wall, is built of living stones, which, according to the prophet, are rolled upon the earth. For what we have rendered, outer wall, Symmachus translated bulwark: so that the walls themselves are girded with fortifications, and with rampart and ditch and other walls, which in the building of camps they are accustomed to call breastworks.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.2-4":["After the words of the people and the answer of the Lord, and again the voice of the people, the Prophet speaks to the believers: You have hoped, or hope in the Lord, for everlasting ages, and the rest that follows. According to the Seventy, he enters the gates of the Lord who keeps righteousness in good works, and keeps or embraces the truth in the truth of faith, so that through good works and faith he may come to the peace which surpasses all understanding, and may merit to receive that peace; because he has believed in God, who is the eternal rewarder of good works (Phil 4). Hence also in another place it is written: You have desired wisdom: keep the commandments, and the Lord will grant it to you (Sir 1:33).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.2":["Open the gates, and let the righteous nation enter, keeping the truth. The old error has gone; you will keep peace: peace, because we have hoped in you. You have hoped in the Lord for everlasting ages. LXX: Open the gates, let the people enter which keeps righteousness, and keeps the truth, laying hold of the truth, and keeping peace: peace, because they have hoped in you, O Lord, for ever. This whole song, which the saints are to sing in the land of confession and praise, suddenly changes persons, and is woven as if by question and answer. The people of God had said: The city of our strength, the Savior, a wall and an outer wall will be set in it. The Lord answered, or rather commanded, not those who had said this, but the angels who presided over the gates of the Lord's city, that they should open the gates, and let the righteous nation enter through them, keeping the truth, or, as is said in the Hebrew, EMMUNIM (אמונים), which in our language is rendered faiths, in the plural number, not the singular. Which are the gates that are opened by the angels, so that there may enter, not the people of the Jews, which has been cast off, but the righteous nation, which from faith has received the name of the faithful? Surely those of which the holy man speaks: Open to me the gates of righteousness; having entered them, I will confess to the Lord (Ps 117 [118]:19). But no one will be able to enter these gates except one who has been freed from the gates of death, as the Psalmist said: You who lift me up from the gates of death, that I may declare all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion (Ps 9:15). For when we have been snatched from the gates of death, then we shall be able to sing all the praises of the Lord in the gates of the daughter of Zion. And as I consider the gates of death to be sins, of which it is said to Peter: The gates of hell shall not prevail against you (Matt 16:18), so I consider the gates of righteousness to be all the works of the virtues; and he who has entered them will find one gate, of which it is said: This is the gate of the Lord, the righteous shall enter by it (Ps 117 [118]:20). And as through many pearls one comes to the one pearl, so through many ways and gates we come to him who says that he is the way and the gate, through which we enter to the Father.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.3":["After the word of God, the people answered in Hebrew JESER SAMUCH (יצרסמך), which Aquila and Symmachus translated alike, πλᾶσμα ἐπηρεισμένον, that is, our error has been taken away, or our thought has been made firm, which before wavered between you and the idols, so that we are by no means carried about by every wind of doctrine, but believe with our whole mind in you, the Lord Savior. For this, so that the sense might be made plainer, we translated, the old error has gone. And because our thought has been made firm, therefore you will keep for us the peace which you promised to the Apostles, saying: My peace I give you, my peace I leave you (John 14:27); and not once only but a second time, so that the recompense which is promised with a double word may be secure, according to what the Apostle too said: Rejoice, again I say rejoice (Phil 4:4). And this he obtains of whom it is said in Leviticus: Man, man, of the children of Israel (Lev 17); and in the book of Numbers: Man, man, whose wife has defiled his bed (Num 5:12): so that the double man and the double husband may obtain a double peace. And we merit peace, they say, because we trust in you with our whole mind.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.4":["In the Lord God, the strong one, for ever: for he will bow down those who dwell on high; he will bring low the lofty city, he will bring it low even to the ground; he will pull it down even to the dust. The foot will trample it, the feet of the poor, the steps of the needy. LXX: O great eternal God, who have brought low and pulled down those who dwell on high, you will destroy strong cities, and will bring them down even to the pavement, and the feet of the meek and the lowly will trample them. These things too the Prophet speaks, who answered from that place where he had said above, Hope in the Lord for everlasting ages; and he joins to it what we have now set forth: In the Lord God, the strong one, for ever, and the rest. For the Lord God, the strong one, the Hebrew has three names, IA (יה) and ADONAI (יהוה) and SUR (צור), of which one signifies invisible, another ineffable, another mighty; the first of them is placed as the last syllable in ALLELUIA (הללו יה). And let the diligent reader observe this, that we sometimes divide the text in setting out a testimony, because the edition of the Seventy produces one sense, and the translation rendered word for word from the Hebrew another. The Prophet says, then: Hope in the Lord for everlasting ages: in the Lord God, the strong one, for ever, whose help is everlasting.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.5-6":["And because it is said in the words of the Savior, Everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted (Luke 14:11), we can refer this not only to men but also to the adverse powers. According to the Seventy, the Prophet sings praises to God because he brings low all the proud, and razes the fortifications of all cities to the ground, and the feet of the saints, the meek and the lowly trample them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.5":["For he will bow down those who dwell on high, because everyone who exalts himself will be humbled; those who boasted that they had Abraham as their father, and said in insult of the Lord: We were not born of fornication (John 8:41). He will bring low the lofty city: as the Jews think, Rome; as we prove to be more correct, Jerusalem, which killed the Prophets and stoned those who were sent to it, and at the last killed the son of the householder, so that, the heir being slain, the inheritance might perish (Luke 13). Nor is it called a city, which in Hebrew is said IR (עיר), but CARIA (כריה), which Aquila translated πολίχνην, which we can call either little city or hamlet or small town; and Jerusalem is frequently called by this name in the Scriptures. And he did well to set down a double humbling, He will bring low, he will bring it low: first under the Babylonians, when the temple was destroyed; the second time under Titus and Vespasian, whose ruin remains until the end.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.6":["The foot will trample it, and he repeats and joins the feet of the poor, without doubt of Christ, of whom we have said above too: He has become strength to the poor, strength to the needy in his tribulation. The steps of the needy, namely of the Apostles, who, imitating the poverty of the Lord, obtained also the privilege of his power; who, not being received, shook off upon it the dust of their feet.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.7":["The path of the righteous is straight, straight is the track of the righteous for walking; and in the path of your judgments, O Lord, we have waited for you; your name and your memorial are in the desire of the soul: my soul has desired you in the night. LXX: The way of the righteous is straight, the way of the righteous has been made straight, and prepared. For the way of the Lord is judgment; we have hoped in your name, and in the remembrance which our soul desires. The Prophet is still speaking of Christ, of whom he had said above: The foot will trample it, the feet of the poor. The path of this righteous one, then, is straight, or, to coin a new word, straightnesses, which the Greeks call εὐθύτητας, and which we can call more in the Latin manner equities; and in Hebrew they are called MESSARIM (משרים). In the one path of Christ, then, all righteousnesses are found; and for that reason he trod and wore it with his own foot, so that whoever wishes to walk along it may walk with an unstumbling course.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.8":["In this path of the Lord's judgments the saints have waited for him, and have hoped in him, because hope does not confound. And they have had his name and memorial in the desire of their soul, saying: My soul has longed to desire your judgments at all times (Ps 118 [119]:20); and again: My soul desires, and has fainted for your salvation (ibid. 81). And he who has the name of the Lord in his desire desires nothing else. And this must be noted, that the desire for the Lord is not in the flesh but in the soul, according to what we read in another psalm: My soul has thirsted for God, the strong, the living (Ps 41 [42]:2). For the flesh lusts against the spirit, and the spirit against the flesh (Gal 5). For these are opposed to one another, so that we do not do the things we wish.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.9-10":["According to the Seventy, the inhabitants of the earth are commanded to learn righteousness. For every man seems righteous to himself; but God knows the hearts of all, who will render to each according to his works (Prov 21). And in another place of the same book it is said: There are ways of a man which seem straight; but their ends look into the depth of hell (Prov 14:12). Hence we ought to learn righteousness, and not trust in our own judgment. For there is a righteous man who perishes in his righteousness (Eccl 7); not because he is righteous, but because he seems to himself to be righteous. But if Christ has been made for us by God wisdom and righteousness, sanctification and redemption (1 Cor 1), those who are commanded to know righteousness are commanded this: to learn and know Christ. And I command you, he says, to learn righteousness for this reason, because the impious has been taken away, and his kingdom destroyed, who, as long as he reigned on the earth, could neither know righteousness nor do truth; of which it is written elsewhere that grace and truth came through Jesus Christ (John 1:17). And because the impious does not do truth on the earth, he will be taken away: for he does not deserve to see the Lord reigning.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.9":["And what follows, In the night, according to the Seventy is joined to the following passage, according to the Hebrew to the former one. And he can say, My soul has desired you in the night, who says confidently with the Psalmist: Every night I will wash my bed, I will water my couch with tears (Ps 6:7). Night and darkness can also be taken for tribulation and distress. Hence in another psalm too the Prophet sings of the security of the righteous: The sun will not burn you by day, nor the moon by night (Ps 120 [121]:6), that is, neither in prosperity nor in adversity will you ever be moved from your place.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But also with my spirit within my breast, from the morning I will watch for you. LXX: From the night my spirit rises early to you, O God: for your judgments are a light upon the earth. We wish both to follow the Hebrew and not to pass over the common edition entirely, and by this necessity of things we are compelled to seek different understandings in a different order and wording. So what is said, from the night, is, according to the Seventy, as we said, the beginning of this passage; according to the Hebrew, the end of the preceding one; although even according to the Seventy it can be taken at the end of the preceding testimony, so that the sense is: My soul desires you in the night; and afterwards it begins: in the morning my spirit rises to you, O God. And it rises in the morning for this reason, because your precepts are a light upon the earth. For, keeping your commandments, and illumined by the light of those of which it is said, The precept of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes (Ps 18 [19]:9), I cannot sleep, but desiring you at all times, I rise to you with my spirit. And this must be observed, that while still placed in the night we desire the Lord with the soul; but after our spirit within our breast has moved itself toward God with the whole mind, let us watch for him in the morning: and, to speak more plainly, night and desire are joined to the soul, but morning and watching to the spirit. Further, he watches for God with the spirit within his breast who can say: Out of the depths I have cried to you, O Lord (Ps 129 [130]:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","When you have done your judgments on the earth, the inhabitants of the world will learn righteousness. Let us have mercy on the impious, and he will not learn righteousness; in the land of the saints he has done wickedly, and he will not see the glory of the Lord. LXX: Learn righteousness, you who dwell upon the earth. For the impious has ceased, and he will not learn righteousness upon the earth; he will not do truth; let the impious be taken away, that he may not see the glory of the Lord. Let us speak first according to the Hebrew, and when we have chosen to express the sense of the Prophet, then let us pass to the Seventy translators. As long as you do not exercise your judgments upon the earth, and neither repay good to the good nor evil to the evil, your righteousness, O Lord God, is unknown on the earth. But when on the day of judgment you have rendered to each what he deserves, according to the quality of his works, then your righteousness will be known in the world, which before seemed unjust among the unbelieving, so that one of the saints too said: But my feet were almost moved, my steps had well-nigh slipped: for I was zealous over the wicked, seeing the peace of sinners (Ps 72 [73]:2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.10":["To which the Lord answered: Let us have mercy on the impious; which all, except the Seventy, translated alike. And the sense is: rather let the impious obtain mercy, and learn my clemency, while he too is saved. And again, to the Lord as he speaks, the Prophet answered in the person of human impatience: And he will not learn righteousness. And the sense is: and how will he be able to know your righteousness, if he has experienced only clemency? And he gives the reasons why he wishes him to learn the righteousness of God: because he has acted wickedly in the land of the saints, and has fought continually against your saints, he ought to feel torments. And again the Lord, tempering the sentence, says: And let him not see, or he will not see the glory of the Lord. And the sense is: it is enough for him, in place of all punishment, that he will not see me reigning with my saints in my majesty. Some wish the impious one, that is, RESA (רשע), to be understood as the devil, of whom it is written in the ninth psalm: You have rebuked the nations, and the impious has perished; you have blotted out their name for ever and for ever and ever. The swords of the enemy have failed unto the end, and their cities you have destroyed (Ps 9:6). But let us take the impious generally either for the sinner or for one who does not have the worship of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","O Lord, let your hand be exalted, and let them not see: let them see, and let the zeal of the people be confounded, and let fire devour your enemies. LXX: O Lord, your arm is lofty, and they did not know; but knowing they will be confounded: zeal will seize the untaught people, and now fire will eat the adversaries. What he had said above, and he will not see the glory of the Lord, can be understood thus: You ask, O Prophet, that I should not have mercy on the impious, lest, if I have had mercy on him, he who has acted wickedly in the land of the saints should begin not to know righteousness; I answer you: will he then not see the glory of the Lord? Will he then not behold my triumphs, he who rather ought to see me reigning, so that he may know how great a good he lacks? And this is to be read more pointedly, with the voice of one asking a question.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.11":["To which the Prophet answered: O Lord, let your hand be exalted, and be stretched out to strike, so that the impious may not see you, and may not enjoy the light of your glory, not even for repentance. To whom the Lord answered: Let them rather see and be confounded, either the zeal of the people or those zealous for the people, and let fire devour and consume your adversaries, that is, those of the people of the saints. And it is the fire of repentance, which tortures their hearts because they have lost such a Lord. This can also be understood of the Jews, who did not know Christ, the arm of the Lord, and when they have seen and recognized him whom they had fastened to the gibbet, will be confounded. Then the untaught people, not knowing the law of God, will be roused by the goads of zeal, when it sees that the nations have succeeded to its place, and burns with the fire of regret, or with the pain of punishment, when it hears that which is written: Depart into the eternal fire, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels (Matt 25:41).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.12":["O Lord, you will give us peace: for you have wrought all our works for us. LXX: O Lord our God, give us peace: for you have rendered all things to us. It must be noted that after he has rendered to us our works, he is going to give peace, and how they set out the reasons why they ask for peace. For they say that for all the works which they have wrought upon the earth they have endured torments, and that it is just that after punishments and tortures they should obtain mercy. Or otherwise: Because the consummation of the world has come, and all that you spoke through the Prophets has been fulfilled in deed, and you have rendered all that you promised, grant us peace, which surpasses all understanding.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.13":["O Lord our God, lords have possessed us without you: only in you may we remember your name. LXX: Lord our God, possess us: O Lord, besides you we know no other, we call upon your name. For this reason we seek mercy and peace, which is to be granted after all things, because lords have possessed us without you, namely the idols, or the demons who sit by the idols; and we ask nothing else but that, after very many errors, we may be worthy to remember your name. According to the Seventy, those who said, Lord God, possess us, pray for this, that after peace has been restored to them they may become the possession of God. This indeed we read also of Wisdom, which according to the Hebrew speaks in Proverbs: God possessed me, the beginning of his ways (Prov 8:22), although some copies wrongly have creature for possession. Then it follows: But before all the hills he begot me. For how can begetting be fitted to a creature, when it rather suits possession? It is written in Deuteronomy: Is not he your father, who possessed you, and made you, and created you? (Deut 32:6). And this must be considered, that he did not say, the Lord, or God, possessed you, and made you, and created you; but father, so that the clemency of the name might soften the severity of the power. And what follows, O Lord, besides you we know no other, does not exclude the Son, but joins him to the Father; for he did not say \"We know no other,\" but \"Besides you we know no other.\" And since the Son says, I am in the Father and the Father in me, we do not know the Son outside the Father (John 14:10), because we know him in the Father. And indeed we name his name too, saying in the Lord's Prayer: Our Father, who art in heaven (Matt 6:9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.14":["Let the dying not live; let the giants not rise again. Therefore you have visited and crushed them, and have destroyed all memory of them. LXX: For the dead will not see life, nor will physicians raise them up. Therefore you have brought on, and destroyed, and taken away every male of theirs. Symmachus, in his manner, more plainly: The dead will not give life; the giants will not raise up. Therefore you have visited and crushed them, and have utterly destroyed all memory of them. Let us speak first according to the Seventy. The question seems difficult, how the dead do not see life. It is solved thus: they do not see life so long as they are dead. As if we were to say, a blind man does not see the light so long as he is blind; but if he has recovered his health, he will look upon the light: so too he who is dead in iniquity and sins cannot live before he is given life by righteousness and the virtues, through him who says, I am the life (John 14). Hence God is also called the God of the living, not of the dead (Matt 22). For the soul that sins, it shall die (Ezek 18:4). We read in the Epistle written to the Hebrews: Not laying again the foundation of repentance from dead works (Heb 6:1). But if sins are called dead works, why should not, on the contrary, the virtues be called living works? And what follows, Nor will physicians raise them up, its sense is plain: the fables of the poets are condemned, who boast that Virbius was raised up by Aesculapius. And this is to be said not only of the dead, but of every infirmity: that without the mercy of God the art of healing avails nothing. But how? Unless the Lord builds the house, they have labored in vain who build it: unless the Lord guards the city, he has watched in vain who guards it (Ps 126 [127]:1, 2). So unless the Lord has cured the sickness, the physicians labor in vain who desire to heal the sick. Unless the Lord has guarded health, they guard it in vain who even publish precepts for guarding health in their own books; and one must always learn, not only in the health of the body but also in that of the soul: Bless the Lord, O my soul, who heals all your sicknesses (Ps 102 [103]:1, 3). Further, those who persist dead in sin, and can recover the health of the soul by no art of medicine, will be utterly destroyed and taken away by the Lord, and whatever is strong in them, which is called the male, will be wholly removed. Hence Pharaoh too does not wish to kill the female sex, which is frail in itself and can easily perish, but every male, which, if it has grown up and come to manly age, is killed with difficulty (Exod 1). According to Symmachus, the dead will not give life, because, being dead in sin, they cannot give life to others, nor is praise seemly in the mouth of a sinner. And the giants, that is, RAPHAIM (רפאים), will not raise up others, they who themselves, according to the book of Genesis, are called the falling. And the Lord visits them, so that all memory both of the dead and of the giants may be blotted out. For he alone it is who raises the dead, of whom it is said: As the Father raises the dead and gives them life, so the Son also gives life to whom he will (John 5:21). We can call the dead the images of dead men, and the giants the demons who sit by their images. Nor should it trouble us why the Seventy translated male and the other translators memory, since among the Hebrews both are written with the same three letters, ZAI (ז), and CHAPH (כ), and RES (ר). But when we say memorial, it is read ZACHAR; when male, ZOCHOR. And they think that Saul was deceived by this ambiguity of the word when he fought against Amalek and killed every male of them (1 Sam 15). For when God commanded that he should blot out all memory of Amalek under heaven, he, for memory, led astray not so much by error as by the desire for plunder, interpreted males, not knowing that saying of the Apostle: Do not err, God is not mocked (Gal 6:7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.15":["You have been indulgent to the nation, O Lord, you have been indulgent to the nation. Have you been glorified? You have removed far all the ends of the earth. LXX: Add evils to them, O Lord, add evils to the glorious of the earth: you have made far off all the ends of the earth. The evils which the Seventy put twice are not found in the Hebrew; but because he had said above, Therefore you have brought on and destroyed, and taken away every male of theirs, following the same sense they added of their own, evils: so that those who are glorious on the earth may be crushed by double evils. Further, according to the Hebrews the sense is far otherwise, and in agreement with the earlier discussion. The Lord had said: Let us have mercy on the impious. The Prophet had answered: And where is your righteousness? especially since he has wrought such great evils against your saints. To which the Lord: And he will not see, he says, the glory of the Lord. Again the Prophet: Let your hand be exalted to strike, and let them not see your glory, which they do not deserve to behold. To which the Lord: Let them see rather and be confounded. Again the Prophet: O Lord, give us peace, and possess us, who remember your name. But let the impious and the proud not live, nor rise again in glory, but crush all memory of them. And he gives the reasons why he desires them to perish: You have been indulgent to the nation, O Lord, you have been indulgent to the nation; have you been glorified? And the sense is: You have often had mercy on the nations, that is, on the human race, and have exercised toward them an incredible clemency; have they known you? Have they glorified your name? Have they not, on the contrary, withdrawn far from you? For security breeds negligence, and negligence contempt.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.16":["O Lord, in distress they have sought you; in the tribulation of murmuring your teaching was to them. LXX: O Lord, in tribulation I remembered you; in small tribulation your teaching was to us. Because, being indulgent, you have often been despised and not glorified, but on the contrary all have withdrawn from the knowledge of you, therefore, O Lord, strike them, so that in distress they may seek you, and in the tribulation of murmuring your teaching may be to them: when so great a weight of evils presses upon them that they do not even dare to cry out confidently, but swallow their pain in silence. According to the Seventy, the Prophet remembers the Lord in tribulation, according to what is said in the psalm: Out of tribulation I called upon the Lord, and he heard me in a broad place (Ps 117 [118]:5). And in another place: To the Lord I cried when I was troubled, and he heard me (Ps 119 [120]:1). Hence the Apostle says: We are troubled, but not distressed; we suffer persecution, but are not forsaken (2 Cor 4:8). And in another place: The sufferings of this time are not worthy to be compared with the glory to come, which will be revealed in us (Rom 8:18). But if a small tribulation teaches, and amends, and corrects, how much more a great one, when we are reminded of our condition and remember the power of God!\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.17-18":["As a woman who has conceived, when she draws near to the birth, cries out in pain in her pangs: so have we become before your face, O Lord. We have conceived, and as it were travailed, and have brought forth spirit. LXX: And as a woman in travail, when she draws near to the birth, cries out in her pain: so have we become ÷ to your beloved ⁚ because of fear of you, O Lord; we have received in the womb, and have travailed, and have brought forth the spirit of your salvation, which we have made upon the earth. As a woman drawing near to the birth is compelled by pain to cry out, so we seek you in distress, and before the face of the dread of you we have conceived, and travailed, and brought forth, not children of the flesh, but of the spirit: so that we may believe in you with our whole mind, and learn through torments him whom we did not perceive through benefits. What the Seventy added, so have we become to your beloved, for which the others translated, so have we become before your face, O Lord, is to be marked beforehand with an obelus. But we can take the beloved of the Lord as Christ, for fear of whom we conceive, and travail, and bring forth, and make the spirit of salvation upon the earth. An apostolic man can say this too, when he instructs the peoples and imitates the apostle Paul: My little children, with whom I travail again until Christ be formed in you (Gal 4:19). Or is it to be doubted that the apostle Paul made the spirit of salvation upon the earth, who preached the Gospel from Jerusalem as far as Illyricum (Rom 15), and as a wise architect laid the foundation, besides which no one can lay another, which is Christ Jesus (1 Cor 3)? Whether, then, we read, because of fear of you, O Lord, we have received in the womb, or according to the Hebrew, before your face, O Lord, we have conceived, and have received in the womb, both pertain to this: that from the fear and remembrance of the Lord we conceive the word of God, and our heart is illumined as we say: The light of your countenance has been signed upon us, O Lord (Ps 4:7). And, show your face, and we shall be saved (Ps 79 [80]:4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.18":["Salvations we have not wrought on the earth; therefore the inhabitants of the world have not fallen. LXX: We shall not fall, but the inhabitants of the earth will fall. A different translation must needs have a different sense too. According to the Hebrew this is said: Because we have done nothing worthy of your mercy, therefore the impious have not fallen, but to this day prevail and possess the earth. But the Seventy assert this: that while the saints make the spirit of salvation upon the earth, those who are inhabitants of the earth fall; although between world, which in Hebrew is called THEBEL (תבל) and in Greek οἰκουμένη, and earth there is much difference. Whoever, then, have given themselves to dwelling on the earth, and have been grounded with a fixed root in earthly works, will fall; and they will not fall who sit in the world and rest in the Church, which is the dwelling place of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For they have crushed, or cut asunder, with iron carts the women with child of Gilead. These are the women with child of whom the Lord speaks in the Gospel: Woe to those who are with child, and to those who give suck in those days. The women with child are the souls of believers, who at the beginning of faith can say: From fear of you, Lord, we have conceived and been in labour; and if they have not been cut up and pierced with iron by the carts and the iron and toothed wheels, of which the psalmist says: The sons of men, their teeth are weapons and arrows, they can also say what follows: And we have brought forth; we have made the spirit of your salvation upon the earth. And consider at the same time that they cannot cut up men of mature age, but only those who are still in the womb, or who cannot take solid food and are still nourished with the milk of infancy.\n— Commentary on Amos","I speak to you a wondrous thing, but a true one. Our Zion, in which at times there are foreigners, and Tyre, and the Ethiopian: that watchtower, that harlot, that Rahab, that Babylon, she who spread apart her feet to everyone passing by, according to Ezekiel: that harlot, if she wills, suddenly becomes a virgin. She becomes a virgin, and conceives the Son of God, and gives birth. 'From the fear of you, O Lord, we conceived and were in labor; we brought forth the spirit of your salvation upon the earth.' See, then, that the harlot conceives from God and gives birth, and brings forth the Savior. We brought forth the spirit of your salvation upon the earth. Therefore our soul, that Rahab, that harlot, can conceive and bring forth the Savior.\n— Homily 18, on Psalm 86"],"ISA.26.19":["Let your dead live, let my slain rise again: awake and give praise, you who dwell in the dust, for your dew is a dew of lights, and the land of the giants you will pull down to ruin. LXX: The dead will rise again, and they will rise again from the tombs, and those who are in the earth will rejoice. For your dew is their healing, but the land of the impious will fall. While the saints travail and bring forth the spirit, and the inhabitants of the earth fall, because they have not wrought salvations on the earth, those whom the Apostle calls the dead in Christ, and who have been slain for the name of the Lord, will rise again in glory (1 Thess 4). And because their death is sleep, they are said, not, as according to the Seventy, to rise again, but to awaken and to wake up. Hence Lazarus too, who was to be awakened, is called sleeping by the Lord (John 11). All the martyrs, then, and the holy men who shed their blood for Christ, and whose whole life was a martyrdom, will rise again and awaken, and will praise God their Creator, they who now dwell in the dust, of whom it is written in Daniel: Many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth will rise again, some to everlasting life, and some to reproach and everlasting confusion (Dan 12:2). And in John the Evangelist we read: The hour will come, and now is, when those who are in the tombs will hear the voice of the Son of God, and those who hear will live, and those who have done good will come forth to the resurrection of life, and those who have done evil to the resurrection of judgment (John 5:28, 29). For the dew of the Lord, surpassing, as in the fables of the poets, all the Paeonian herbs, will give life to the bodies of the dead. And as, when seeds have been cast into the earth, the dew little by little makes the plants grow and come to the fruits of their kind, so the dew of the Lord, which is put for mercy, will be a dew of very many lights, which in Hebrew is called OROTH (אורות). But the land, that is, the bodies of the Raphaim, namely of the giants and the impious, the Lord will pull down into everlasting punishments. Indeed, for RAPHAIM (רפאים) the Seventy alone translated the impious. And because we read above, The dead will not see life, nor will physicians raise them up, for which Aquila and Symmachus translated Raphaim and giants, we ask what the cause of the error is, that for the Hebrew RAPHAIM some put giants, others physicians. If the Hebrew word RAPHAIM has VAU following its first letter RES, it is read ROPHAIM (רופאים) and signifies physicians; but if it is written without the letter VAU, it is read RAPHAIM, and is translated giants. And at the same time, because he had said above, The dead will not see life, so as to show more plainly that it was not said there of those dead by the law of nature and the separation of soul and body, but of those who are dead in sin, he now on the contrary says to God: Your dead will live, who have been slain for your sake; who are not called dead absolutely, as the Seventy translated, but, according to the Hebrew, where it is said JEJU METHECA (יהיו מתיך), your dead.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But the heavens too shall give their dew, the heavens of which it is written in Psalm 18 (19): The heavens tell the glory of God, and the firmament proclaims the works of his hands. Of these heavens it is said in the song of Deuteronomy: Rejoice, O heavens, with him, that is, with the Lord and Savior, whom he foreknew and predestined to be conformed to the image of his Son; it is he who speaks with Moses: Let my speech be awaited like the rain, and let my words come down like the dew. For those who were dead in sins shall rise again, and those who lay in whitewashed sepulchres, which are full of dead men's bones, shall be raised up, and those who dwell in the earth shall rejoice. And what the cause of the joy is, the following words show: For the dew which is from you is their healing.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ISA.26.20-21":["Go, my people, enter into your chambers, shut your doors upon you: hide yourself a little for a moment, until the indignation passes. For behold, the Lord will come forth from his place, to visit the iniquity of the inhabitant of the earth against him; and the earth will disclose its blood, and will no longer cover its slain. LXX: Go, my people, enter into your chambers, shut your door: hide yourself a little, however little, until the wrath of the Lord passes. For behold, the Lord from the holy place will bring wrath upon the inhabitants of the earth, and the earth will disclose its blood, and will no longer hide the slain. Above he had said of the saints, The dead will rise again, and those who are in the tombs will rise again; for the dew from you is their healing; and on the contrary of the impious, but the land of the impious will fall. Now he speaks to the saints: because resurrection has been promised to you, until the wrath of God rages against sinners and the impious, enter into your tombs and hide yourselves, for it is a short time until the indignation of God passes. For indeed the Lord comes forth from his place, because the Lord is merciful and compassionate, and the most clement father is compelled to strike his negligent children, and in a manner to be changed from his own judgment, so that he may visit and bring his wrath upon the inhabitants of the earth, of whom it is said in Hosea: Cursing and lying and adultery and theft have been poured out upon the inhabitants of the earth (Hos 4:2). And in the Apocalypse we read three times, Woe upon the inhabitants of the earth (Rev 8:13). But the righteous, although they are seen on the earth, yet their citizenship is in the heavens; they can say, I am a stranger on the earth, and a sojourner, as all my fathers were (Ps 38 [39]:13), and they enjoy the dwelling of the Most High, of whom the holy man speaks: He who dwells in the help of the Most High will abide in the protection of the God of heaven (Ps 90 [91]:1). Then the earth will disclose its blood, of which God speaks to Cain: The voice of your brother's blood cries to me from the earth, which has opened its mouth to receive your brother's blood from your hand (Gen 4:10, 11). This can also be understood of the Martyrs, who shed their blood for Christ and cry out beneath the altar of God: How long, O Lord, do you not avenge our blood on those who dwell upon the earth? (Rev 6:10). Of them Moses too says in the Song: The blood of his sons will be vindicated and avenged, and he will repay vengeance to his enemies (Deut 32:43). This blood the earth which had received it will disclose, and it will by no means cover the slain of the Lord, but will bring them out into public view for the condemnation of those who killed the Martyrs. Let this be understood of the simple resurrection.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.20":["But according to the anagogy, the people of God is commanded to enter into its chambers, or storerooms, for ταμεῖα means both: to shut, according to the precept of the Gospel, the door of its chamber (Matt 6), and to say with the Prophet: Set a guard, O Lord, on my mouth, and a fortified door to my lips (Ps 140 [141]:3); and to hide itself a little, however little, until the wrath of the Lord passes, so that it does nothing for the sake of glory, but enjoys the good of conscience and awaits God alone as judge. And the storerooms which are to be shut and hidden by those who have become rich in works and words are prudence, temperance, fortitude, justice, so that the left hand may not know what the right hand does, so that we may enjoy the riches of the Law and the Prophets and the Gospel. And that all the days of our life are short and very small, Jacob, exceeding a hundred years, says: Small and very evil are my days (Gen 47:9). And the wrath of the Lord that is to pass is that which those store up for themselves who are unwilling to do penance; after it has passed, the storerooms will by no means be shut any longer, but what is written will be fulfilled: Nothing is hidden that will not be revealed, and secret that will not be made manifest (Luke 8:17).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.26.21":["And what follows, For behold, the Lord from the holy place will bring his wrath, signifies this: that the wrath of God begins from the saints, or that all his vengeance is just and holy, not coming down from disturbance of mind, as is usual among men, but from zeal to correct. And I consider the earth of the inhabitants to be that of which it is written: Let the earth hear the words of my mouth (Deut 32:1). And: Give ear, O earth (Isa 1:2). And again: Earth, earth, hear the word of the Lord (Jer 22:29). For as those who dwell on the earth, so those who are in the flesh cannot please God (Rom 8). And earth in this place signifies the soul that lives carnally. And it will disclose its blood, if it has caused anyone to stumble, and deserves to hear with Cain: The voice of your brother's blood cries to me from the earth, which has opened its mouth to receive your brother's blood (Gen 4:10, 11). All blood, then, will be required on the day of judgment, nor will the earth hide its blood; and the slain whom it has killed, whether willingly or through negligence, it will bring out into the open.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.1":["In that day the Lord will visit with his hard and great and strong sword upon Leviathan the bar serpent, and upon Leviathan the twisting serpent, and he will slay the whale that is in the sea. LXX: In that day God will bring the holy and great and strong sword upon the dragon, the fleeing serpent, upon the dragon, the twisting serpent, and he will slay the dragon that is in the sea. The Hebrews hand down that the devil, that is, the slanderer, which is a Greek name, is called in Hebrew SATAN (שטן), that is, adversary. Hence in Zechariah too it is said: Satan, that is, the adversary, stood at his right hand to oppose him (Zech 3:1). And he is also called BELIAL (בליעל), that is, apostate, transgressor, and without yoke. Hence the Apostle too: What fellowship, he says, has Christ with Belial? (2 Cor 6:15). And wherever the Seventy put sons of pestilence, in the Hebrew is written sons of Belial. Hence also that which is sung in the psalm about the mystery of the Savior, The son of iniquity shall not proceed to harm him (Ps 88 [89]:23), in the Hebrew is said the son of Belial. And he is called by other names too, as is written in another psalm: You shall walk upon the asp and the basilisk, and you shall trample the lion and the dragon (Ps 90 [91]:13). This dragon is properly called in the Hebrew language LEVIATHAN (לויתן). He is the great whale of whom it is told in mystical language in Job that he is to be caught by Christ: Who is going to catch the great whale (Job 40:20); for there too Leviathan is put for whale; and again: But you will draw the dragon with a hook, you will put a halter about his nostrils; and immediately: This is the beginning of the Lord's creation, which was made to be mocked by his angels. And in the psalm: This is the great and wide sea: there are creeping things without number, small animals with great. This dragon which you have formed to mock him (Ps 103 [104]:25, 26). Of him John too writes in the Apocalypse: There was a battle in heaven: Michael and his angels fought with the dragon, and the dragon fought, and his angels, and they did not prevail, nor was a place found for them in heaven (Rev 12:7ff.). And: The great dragon was cast out, the ancient serpent, who is called the devil and Satan, who makes the whole world go astray; and he was cast down to the earth, and his angels with him; and there it is said: The accuser of our brethren has been cast out, who accused them before our God (ibid., 12:10). And it must be noted that in the psalm and in Job the dragon, that is, Leviathan, is said to have been made for this purpose, that he might be mocked by the angels. Hence also the Apostles receive power to tread upon serpents and scorpions, and upon all the power of the enemy (Luke 10). Because, then, from the place where it is written, Behold, the Lord will scatter the earth and will lay it bare (above, Isa 24:1), or, according to the Seventy, Behold, the Lord will destroy the world and will make it desolate, up to the present passage, judgment has been foretold against the world at the consummation of the world, and the last enemy, death, will be destroyed (1 Cor 15): therefore the final sentence is against the devil, that the holy and great and strong sword be brought upon him, or, according to the Hebrew and the other translators, the hard sword. For it is not said CADESA (קדשה), as the Seventy thought, which, if it were so, would signify holy; but CASA (קשה), which is properly translated hard. Hence also Kish, the father of Saul, is interpreted hard. And the holy, or hard, sword, on account of the sense of him who suffers it, some of our writers understand as the Word of God, of which the Apostle says: For the word of God is living and effective, and sharper than any two-edged sword (Heb 4:12). Hence in another place too we read that a two-edged sword goes out of the mouth of the Savior (Rev 19). And when at the end of the world the holy, or hard, and great and strong sword has been brought against Leviathan, who at the beginning of Genesis is called the most cunning serpent above all the beasts (Gen 3:1) that were on the earth, he will flee who was never accustomed to flee, not knowing that which is written: Whither shall I go from your spirit, or whither shall I flee from your face? (Ps 138 [139]:7). A certain poet in his Gigantomachy jested finely about Enceladus: Whither do you flee, Enceladus? Whatever shores you reach, you will always be under God. And this Leviathan, the fleeing serpent, is called in the Hebrew BARI (ברי), which Aquila translated bar, Symmachus shutting in, Theodotion mighty. I think he is called bar, or the one who shuts, because he has shut many in his prison and subjected them to his own power; and he has nothing straight in him, and for that reason is called twisting, nor can he imitate the rod of the Lord, of which it is written: A rod of uprightness is the rod of your kingdom (Ps 44 [45]). Him the Lord will slay with the breath of his mouth (2 Thess 2), him who was once the inhabitant of the sea, of its salty and bitter waves. Let those who say that the devil will do penance and obtain pardon explain to us how they take what is written: And he will slay the dragon that is in the sea, or the whale. For in the second place in the present passage he is not called Leviathan in the Hebrew, but THANNIN (תנין), which properly means whale. Hence the Hebrews suppose that Leviathan dwells under the earth and in the ether, but Thanniim in the sea; which is a Jewish fable. And what is said at the end of this passage, in that day, Eusebius joins to the former chapter, lest the following prophecy be fitted to this time. But the Hebrews and the other expositors join it to the following, which we are now about to set forth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.2-3":["In that day the vineyard of pure wine will sing to him. I the Lord, who keep it, will suddenly give it to drink: lest perhaps it be visited, I keep it night and day. LXX: In that day the good vineyard is his desire, that he may be its prince. I am a strong city, a city which is besieged: in vain will I give it drink. For they will be taken by night, and by day the wall will fall. The Hebrew in this place differs much from the edition of the Seventy; therefore let us discuss the particulars as we have set them out. The vineyard of which Isaiah speaks above, A vineyard was made for the beloved on a horn, in a fertile place: how it ought to be understood, let us learn from his own teaching: For the vineyard of the Lord Sabaoth is the house of Israel (Isa 5:1); of which it is sung in the psalm: You brought a vine out of Egypt, and so on (Ps 79 [80]:9). To this vineyard too he gives the cup of purest wine to drink through Jeremiah (Jer 25). For when he had sent him to give drink to all the nations, and the Prophet had willingly offered himself for this office, he is commanded first to make Jerusalem drunk. Hence he says: You have deceived me, O Lord, and I was deceived (Jer 20:7). Jerusalem, then, will drink, and an unmixed draught is given it to drink, so that it may learn lamentation and weeping. And the Lord says that he has guarded it for a long time and has given it a place for repentance, and because it would not be converted, it is to be made drunk suddenly. For he says that he has done this by days and by nights, so that it might always be kept by his help. But the Seventy call the same vineyard beautiful, in which were the Law and the Prophets, the priesthood and the high priesthood, and the knowledge of God, as Scripture says: God is known in Judea, his name is great in Israel (Ps 75 [76]:1). Others think that, according to their edition, it pertains to the Church, than which nothing is more beautiful, and of which it has been said: Glorious things are spoken of you, city of God (Ps 86 [87]:3): which is the princess of her mother, and says in the Song of Songs: The sons of my mother have fought against me (Song 1:5). And she says: I am a firm city, a city which is besieged. And he did well to say is besieged, not is taken by storm. And immediately he adds concerning the Synagogue, which has been turned from head into tail: In vain do I give it the drink of my teachings, for it will be taken in the darkness of its error. And because it did not receive the open light, its wall has fallen by day, that is, all that it thought its help; and there will be none of the adversaries who does not take it, which we ought to understand of the adverse powers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.4-5":["Indignation is not in me: who will make me a thorn and a brier in battle? I will march upon it; I will burn it up together. Or rather shall I hold fast my strength? It will make peace for me, peace it will make for me. LXX: There is none that has not laid hold of it. Who will set me as keeper of stubble in a field? Because of this adversary I have driven it back. Therefore the Lord has done all that he arranged: I have been burned; its inhabitants will say, we will make peace with him, we will make peace with him. According to the Hebrew, this is the sense: I, who by days and nights have always preserved my vineyard, lest the boar out of the wood should lay it waste, lest the beasts should devour it, have I no indignation, and do I not know how to strike the sinner, and to render to each what he deserves? Where Aquila put thorn and brier, in the Hebrew is written SAMIR (שמיר) and SAITH (ושית), which signify adamant and places full of brambles. Hence he says: Who will teach me to be hard, and to overcome my clemency, and to advance savage in battle and contest, so that I may march upon the vineyard which I kept before, and burn that which I had hedged with my wall? Or rather will my strength bring this about, that I defer my wrath, and save by the clemency of the Gospel those who were by no means saved by the authority of the Law? And according to the Hebrew it is to be read ἐμφατικῶς: Who will make me hard and cruel, so that I overcome my nature? For this indeed is signified by the desert and by the thorns: that I should crush, as in battle, and burn that which I have always guarded with my diligence. Or rather shall I hold fast my strength, which without doubt is Christ, and of which we read, Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God (1 Cor 1:24), so that it may make peace for me and reconcile the world? According to the Seventy, this is the sense, if we take it in the person of the Church: I am a firm city, a city surrounded by many enemies, I who in vain gave cups to my enemy the Synagogue. For it will be taken by night, and its wall will fall, and there will be none of the adverse powers which does not lay hold of it. Again I will speak: What does it profit me to guard her, who has stubble in her, not grain, who is so uncultivated that she is full of briers and thorns, whom I wished to save? But because she deals with me as an enemy, I have withdrawn from her. And the Apostles, born in me and from me, said: It was necessary that the word of God should be spoken first to you; but since you have rejected it and judged yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the Gentiles (Acts 13:46). Therefore the Lord will do what he threatened, so that all in her burn, surrounded by Roman fire. Or certainly they burn with vices and sins, so that they cannot quench the burning darts of the devil. For all are adulterers, their hearts like an oven. And those who formerly dwelt in her, after they have cried out, The city is taken and burned, will leave her, and, reconciling the world to God, will say: Let us make peace with him, let us make peace with him, that is, with Christ, always writing in their Epistles: Grace be with you and peace from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Lord (1 Cor 1:3). Of whom we read in this same Prophet: How beautiful are the feet of those who bring good tidings of peace! (Isa 52:7). Some refer this place to the Churches: that they are indeed guarded by God, but many in them bear no fruit; and for that reason they are set on fire by the burning zeal of a good teacher, so that they cry out and confess their error, and afterwards make peace with God, being truly called sons of Jacob.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.6":["Those who come forth from the root of Jacob, Israel will blossom and bud, and they will fill the face of the world with seed. LXX: Those who come are the sons of Jacob: Israel will bud and blossom, and its fruit will fill the world. After the Apostles, preaching the Gospel in the whole world, have said, We will make peace with Christ, we will make peace with him, those who were of the seed of Jacob on high, and have attained to the apostolic dignity, will be called sons of Jacob. Then Israel will bud and blossom, seeing that the whole world has been filled with the teaching of its sons and has borne most abundant fruits, which while it remained in Judea it had not borne.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.7":["Has he struck him according to the stroke of him who struck him? Or as he killed his slain, so was he killed? This place is understood in two ways: either against Jerusalem, so as to say that it was not struck by God as it itself struck Christ and his Apostles; or against the multitude of the nations, in that, while they persecuted and shed Christian blood, the Apostles and apostolic men nonetheless took care of their salvation and reconciled them to God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.8":["In measure against measure, when it has been cast off, you will judge it. LXX: Quarreling and reproaching, he will send them away. According to the Hebrew, this is the sense: As Jerusalem did, so will it receive, and according to the measure with which it measured, it will be measured back to it. And it will receive the measure heaped up when the time of judgment has come and God has cast it off. According to the Seventy, what is said depends on what precedes. For Israel will not be struck as it struck, nor killed as it killed: Israel, which quarreled against the Apostles, and reproached its teachers, and commanded them not to speak in the name of Christ. Therefore the Lord will cast them off and drive them out of his flock.\n\nHe has meditated in his hard spirit through the day of heat. LXX: Was it not you who meditated with a hard spirit to kill them, with a spirit of fury? According to the Hebrew this is said: with the measure with which it measured, Jerusalem will receive; therefore God in his hard and vehement spirit has meditated, or spoken, against it in the day of heat, that is, in the time of persecution, when the day of indignation and punishments is more burning. According to the Seventy, it is said to Jerusalem itself, or to Israel: Was it not you who, in your most hard and cruel spirit, and in the fury of your blasphemies, desired to kill the Apostles and teachers of the Lord?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.9":["Therefore by these things iniquity will be forgiven to the house of Jacob, and this is all the fruit, that his sin may be taken away: because he has made all the stones of the altar like stones of ash dashed to pieces; the groves and the shrines will not stand. LXX: Therefore the iniquity of Jacob will be taken away, and this will be his blessing, when I have taken away his sin, when he has made all the stones of the altars crushed like ash ground small, and their trees will not endure, nor the idols. He gives the reasons why, after the Jews have laid hands on the Lord, they may obtain pardon if they are willing to do penance, so that the prayer of the Savior may be fulfilled: Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do (Luke 23:34). Therefore, he says, iniquity will be forgiven to the house of Jacob, and its sin will be taken away, so that it may merit the blessing of God, which had called down a curse upon itself, saying: His blood be upon us and upon our children (Matt 27:25); because through the Apostles of the stock of Israel the Gospel will be sown in the whole world, and idolatry will be destroyed, and the altars will be ground down to dust, the groves will be cut down, the shrines will fall, and the knowledge of the one God under the mystery of the Trinity will be preached.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.10":["For the fortified city will be desolate; the beautiful one will be left, and abandoned as a desert. There the calf will feed, and there he will lie down and consume its topmost shoots. LXX: Their idols will be cut down like a grove, and the flock dwelling far off will be abandoned like a forsaken flock, and it will be for a long time for pasture, and there the flocks will rest, and after a long time there will not be in it anything green, because it has been dried up. Jerusalem, the city once firm and fortified, because it did not receive the son of the householder, but said, Come, let us kill him, for this is the heir, and the inheritance will be ours (Matt 21:38), will be desolate. And she who was once beautiful, of whom it is said in Ezekiel too, You ate fine flour and honey and oil, and you became exceedingly beautiful (Ezek 16:13), and in whom dwelt he of whom it is written, Beautiful in form above the sons of men (Ps 44 [45]:3), will be left and abandoned as a desert, as the Lord says to the Apostles: Arise, let us go hence (John 14:31). There the calf will feed, the Roman army, of which it is said in another place under the name of the boar: The boar out of the wood will lay it waste, and the solitary wild beast has fed upon it (Ps 79 [80]:14). And there he will lie down and consume its topmost shoots, under the metaphor of a vineyard and its shoots, so that nothing green, nothing of the tendrils, remains in it, but the enemy consumes all. According to the Seventy, because they did not receive the good shepherd, therefore they will be like a forsaken flock, and will lie open to the bites of the beasts; and nothing green will remain in them, because drought will possess all things.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.11":["In the drought of its harvest the women coming and teaching it will be crushed. For it is not a wise people: therefore he who made it will not have mercy on it, and he who formed it will not spare it. LXX: Women coming from the spectacle, come: for it is not a people having understanding; therefore he who made them will not have mercy on them, and he who formed them will not spare. What is said, In the drought of its harvest they will be crushed, for which the Seventy translated, there will not be in it anything green, because it has dried up, according to the Hebrew is coupled with what follows, according to the Seventy with the preceding sense. Let us speak first, then, according to the Hebrew. When the time of the drought and the harvest of Jerusalem, and, to speak more openly, of its devastation, has come, the throng of the synagogues will run together from the whole world to lament Jerusalem and console her evils. Or he is speaking openly of the women who, with bared breasts, strike their arms till they bleed; and the prophecy of the Lord will be fulfilled: Daughters of Jerusalem, do not weep for me, but lament over yourselves and over your children (Luke 23:28). And it is a great unhappiness for the people to learn songs of lamentation from women; just as it was a reproach to the people of Israel when, in the book of Judges (Judg 4), the Lord wrought salvation by the hand of the woman Deborah, and at the time of the approaching captivity, while the men were silent, the woman Huldah prophesied (2 Kgs 22). But for this reason the women will be crushed by the long journey, worn out by weakness, hunger and squalor, and they will teach the wretched people, because it is not a wise people, nor has it understood its Creator, who, neglected and despised by them, will not have mercy on his handiwork and will not spare his creature. According to the Seventy, it is said of Mary Magdalene, and the other Mary, and the rest of the women, who first saw the Lord rising again, and held his feet, and merited to hear from him: Do not fear: go, tell my brethren to go into Galilee, and there they will see me (Matt 28:10). Of these women, long before they were born, the prophetic discourse foretells, and calls them from the spectacle of the Lord's Passion and Resurrection, so that they may preach the Gospel, and, according to the Hebrew, teach Jerusalem, or the land of Israel, that this is the Lord and God. For since the people of Israel did not have wisdom at that time, when the Lord suffered, and the prophetic oracle was fulfilled, Save me, O Lord, for the holy one has failed (Ps 11 [12]:1), and, All have turned aside, together they have become unprofitable: there is none who does good, there is not even one (Ps 13 [14]:4), the women are called from the spectacle, so that they may announce to the Apostles what they themselves have seen. But Israel, of which it was said (above, Isa 1:3), Israel has not known me, and my people has not understood, provoked its most clement Creator and maker to bitterness, so that he by no means has mercy on it. These things are indeed said piously; but how they agree with the rest, and are fitted to the times of the consummation of the world, is a difficult interpretation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.12":["And it will be in that day: the Lord will strike from the channel of the river to the torrent of Egypt. LXX: And it will be in that day: the Lord will shut in from the trench of the river to Rhinocorura. Had he not added, in that day, by which we are taught that what he is about to say is to be coupled with what precedes, we could have expounded a sense proper to this passage; but now everything is to be referred to what precedes. Since the fortified city will be desolate, and the once beautiful one will be left as a desert, and there the calf will lie down and consume the shoots of the vineyard, and all things will be dried up, since it is not a people having understanding, and because of its folly it has obtained no mercy from its Creator: therefore the Lord will strike, or shut in, from the channel or stream of the river to the torrent of Egypt, so that in all Judea, which was once the land of promise, no word of teaching may be found, nothing of the knowledge of the Scriptures; of whom the Apostle too speaks: That we may not give heed to Jewish fables and commandments of men who turn away from the truth (Titus 1:14); and again: For there are many both insubordinate and vain talkers and deceivers, whom one must refute, especially of the circumcision (ibid., 10). And the channel, or stream, of the river, according to the history, we can call the Euphrates, as it is written also in the seventy-first psalm: He will have dominion from sea to sea, and from the river to the end of the world (Ps 71 [72]:8). Others think it is the Jordan. And this must be noted, that in the borders of Judea it is called river, in the territories of Egypt torrent, which has turbid waters and not perpetual ones. For the torrent of Egypt, the Seventy translated Rhinocorura, which is a town on the border of Egypt and Palestine, expressing not so much the words of the Scriptures as the sense of the words. And what we have said, will strike, for which the Seventy translated συμφράξει, that is, will shut in, Aquila and Theodotion translated ῥαβδήσει, which can be understood either as will strike with a rod or will count under the rod the number of his flock, so that it may be taken not in a bad but in a good sense.\n\nAnd you will be gathered one by one, O children of Israel. LXX: But you, gather one by one the children of Israel. O children of Israel, for which Symmachus translated house of Israel: when your adversaries have been struck from the stream of the river to the torrent of Egypt, that is, from the Euphrates to the Nile, you yourselves will be gathered to the faith of the Lord one by one, because the throng of the Jews did not believe; by which he signifies that few of the Jews will believe in the Lord Savior. Or certainly, O Apostles and apostolic men, since the multitude of the Jews does not believe, bring back from the whole world as many as you can, like sick sheep, to the folds of the Lord, and gather them with the people of the nations, so that that may be fulfilled which the Apostle Paul and Barnabas say to the Jews: It was necessary that the word of God should be announced first to you; but since you have judged yourselves unworthy of salvation, behold, we turn to the Gentiles (Acts 13:46).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.27.13":["And it will be in that day: a blast will be sounded on a great trumpet; and those who had been lost from the land of the Assyrians, and those who had been cast out into the land of Egypt, will come, and they will worship the Lord on the holy mountain in Jerusalem. LXX: And it will be in that day: they will sound with a great trumpet; and those who had perished in the region of the Assyrians, and those who had perished in Egypt, will come, and they will worship the Lord upon the holy mountain in Jerusalem. In this place the Jews promise themselves empty hopes: that at the consummation of the world, when the Antichrist, their ἠλειμμένος, as it is said, has come, the dispersed people will be gathered from the Assyrians and from the land of Egypt, and will come to Jerusalem, and, the temple having been built, will worship the Lord their God. This cannot stand at all according to the letter. For not from the Assyrians and from Egypt only, but from the whole world, those who are to believe in Christ will be called. This, therefore, is what it signifies: that at the last trumpet, according to the Apostle Paul (1 Cor 15), all who had perished among the Assyrians and in Egypt come to the Lord. Nor did he say all the children of Israel, but all who had perished, by which he signifies the multitude of the nations: that those bound to idolatry and to the magic arts and to philosophy come to the faith of Christ and worship him in the Church. And the great trumpet can be understood as the word of the Gospel, of which we read also in this same Prophet: Go up upon a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion: lift up your voice, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem (below, Isa 40:9). The holy mountain, then, and Jerusalem is that of which we have often spoken: You have come to Mount Zion and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and many thousands of angels, and the Church of the firstborn, who are enrolled in the heavens (Heb 12:22). Thus far it has been spoken of the consummation of the world, from the place at which we began to expound, Behold, the Lord will scatter the earth and will lay it bare (above, Isa 24:1), which is contained in the present volume. Now, with Christ helping, nay rather inspiring us, let us pass on to the ninth, which will hold the beginning of another prophecy.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.1-4":["Woe to the crown of pride, to the drunkards of Ephraim, and to the falling flower of the glory of its exultation, who were on the summit of the most fertile valley, going astray from wine. Behold, the strong and mighty one of the Lord, like the onrush of hail, a whirlwind that shatters; like the onrush of many waters overflowing and sent forth upon a spacious land. With feet shall the crown of pride of the drunkards of Ephraim be trodden down. And the falling flower of the glory of its exultation, which is upon the summit of the valley of the fat, shall be like an early fruit before the ripeness of autumn, which, when the beholder has looked upon it, as soon as he has taken it in his hand, he will devour it. LXX: Woe to the crown of insolence, the hirelings of Ephraim: a flower falling from glory upon the summit of the fat mountain, you who are drunk without wine. Behold, the wrath of the Lord is strong and harsh, like hail that is borne headlong, having no shelter, which falls violently like a multitude of waters dragging the soil along and making room for itself: with hands and feet shall the crown of insolence, the hirelings of Ephraim, be trodden down. And the flower that falls from the hope of glory on the top of the lofty mountain shall be like an early fig, which whoever sees it will long to devour before he takes it in his hand. Let us speak first according to the history, then according to tropology, and lastly according to the prophetic prediction. The divine word speaks against the ten tribes who reigned in Samaria and who, on account of Jeroboam, who was of the tribe of Ephraim, were called Ephraim. And it calls them a crown of pride, because, compared with the two tribes that were called Judah, they were loftier both in number and in strength. And it calls them the drunkards of Ephraim, since they did not understand their Creator, but in place of the Lord worshiped the golden calves in Dan and Bethel. These were once in the flower of the Lord, and in glory, when they were ruled by David and Solomon and, among the twelve tribes, worshiped God in the temple of Jerusalem; they were on the summit of the most fertile valley, which in Hebrew is called GE SEMANIM (גיא שמנים). This signifies the place where the Lord was betrayed; above the summit of this valley the temple of the Lord is situated. These men were made drunk with the wine of error and madness, which Jeroboam mixed for them. Therefore the Lord threatens them with punishment: that, as a whirlwind of hail shatters all things, and the onrush of overflowing waters sweeps away whatever it finds in its path, so they would be destroyed by the army of the Assyrians, and whatever remained would be carried off to the mountains or cities of Media. He compares the glory of the ten tribes to a crown of varied flowers, which had such beauty that, just as someone who, before summer and autumn come, sees a πρόδρομον fig on the tree, devours it as soon as he has taken it in his hand, so too the Assyrian, when he saw the ten tribes, would lay them waste and devour them, and leave nothing at all of the former people in Samaria. Let this have been said briefly according to the history. Let us pass to allegory. According to our exposition of the prophet Hosea, in which we referred Ephraim and Joseph and Samaria and the ten tribes, which were cut off from the body of the twelve tribes and abandoned the temple of the Lord, to the heretics, these are truly, according to the Septuagint edition, a crown of insolence: they blaspheme the Lord, and do everything for the sake of payment, and have fallen from the glory of the Lord; nor do they follow the slenderness of the manna and the humility of the Church, but they dwell on the most fertile mountain, drunk without wine. Therefore the strong and harsh wrath of the Lord, which is going to punish them, is compared to headlong hail, which falls not on roofs but on the heads of mortals, and to the floods of many waters, which drag along whatever they find in their way. This crown of insolence, the hirelings, are called Ephraim, who, according to the Apostle, for the sake of base gain have fallen from the flower and the hope and the glory of their former faith, and dwell in pride, and are the sweetest food of the devil, who devours them daily (1 Pet 5). According to the prophecy we can say this: that he calls the scribes and Pharisees, who blasphemed the Lord, a crown of insolence. And they were called the hirelings of Ephraim on account of Judas, who, of the tribe of Ephraim and of Iscarioth, a village of that same tribe, sold the Lord for a price; he truly, the flower of apostolic glory, fell upon the most fertile mountain, of which we think it was said: Jacob ate and drank and was filled, and grew fat, and the beloved kicked (Deut 32:15). Or, according to the Hebrew: upon the valley of the fat, that is, Gessemanim: in which the name of the place is also signified in which Judas betrayed the Lord. And it is called the fat valley, or the valley of the fat, on account of its fertility, and on account of the scribes and Pharisees who seized the Lord there: of whom it is written in the psalm: Fat bulls have besieged me (Ps 21 [22]:13). This valley of the fat, that is, Gessemanim, is named a second time in this chapter; and I wonder how the Seventy first called it a fat mountain and afterward a lofty mountain. The betrayer was drunk not with wine but with avarice and the incurable fury of asps, and with the food of the devil, who entered into him after the morsel (John 13), and he was utterly devoured, because his prayer was turned into sin, and not even his repentance had the fruit of salvation. The Hebrew word SACCHORE (שכורי) is ambiguous, and means either drunkards or hirelings. Hence Issachar too means there is a reward; and SACHAR (שכר) is μέθυσμα, that is, drunkenness. And the rest translated drunkards: the Seventy alone translated hirelings.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.5-8":["In that day the Lord of hosts shall be a crown of glory and a garland of exultation to the remnant of his people, and a spirit of judgment to him who sits upon judgment, and strength to those returning from war to the gate. But these also have not known because of wine, and have gone astray because of drunkenness; the priest and the prophet have not known: they are swallowed up by wine because of drunkenness, they have gone astray in drunkenness: they have not known the seer, they have been ignorant of judgment, or wonderfully. For all the tables are filled with vomit and filth, so that there was no more room. LXX: In that day the Lord Sabaoth shall be a crown of hope, which is woven of glory, for the remnant of the people. They shall be left in a spirit of judgment upon judgment, and strength forbidding to kill. For these have been deceived by wine: they go astray because of strong drink; the priest and the prophet have lost their minds because of strong drink; they are swallowed up because of wine, they have staggered, that is, φάσμα; a curse shall eat this counsel: for this counsel is for the sake of avarice. After the whole land of Samaria, that is, of the ten tribes, has been destroyed by the Assyrian's flood, and the crown of pride of the drunkards of Ephraim has been trodden underfoot and devoured like an early fig, then the Lord himself shall be a crown of victory to the remnant of the people of Israel, that is, of the two tribes, Judah and Benjamin; and a spirit of judgment to him who sits upon judgment, doubtless to Hezekiah king of Judah; and the strength of those who, when the whole region had been ravaged, returning from battle, shut themselves up in the city. This we shall read in this same prophet (below, Isa 37), when one hundred and eighty-five thousand armed men of the army of the Assyrians were destroyed by the stroke of an angel. But these also, that is, Judah and Benjamin, were made drunk with the wine of idolatry, and, despising the worship of the temple, venerated the images of demons, and did not know the Lord, the seer, who beholds all things. For all their tables, and their whole worship, were filled with vomit and filth, so that not only in the temple but on the tops of the mountains and in wooded places they filled everything with the filth of idolatry, and the Lord had no place of habitation among them. Let this have been said according to the letter. Further, according to tropology, let us follow the former understanding, and not leave the Seventy translators untouched. For when the heretics, who have climbed the most fertile mountain of pride, have been devoured by the mouth of the devil, the Lord shall be a crown of glory to those who dwell in the Church and who, compared with the multitude of the many who go astray, are few in number. And he shall be in a spirit of judgment upon judgment, because the Lord will wash away the filth of the sons and daughters of Zion by the spirit of judgment. But if anyone has blood, he will be purged not by the spirit of judgment but by the fire of burning; and he shall be strength to the people, and shall forbid it to be killed by those who are drunk with the wine of dragons and have gone astray because of strong drink. We have often said that wine is what is made from vines; but strong drink is every drink that can intoxicate and overturn the state of the mind, which Aquila properly translated drunkenness, whether it is made from wheat or barley or millet, or from the juice of fruits, or from the fruit of palms, or from any other kind. Therefore the priests and prophets of the heretics have lost their minds because of strong drink, and are swallowed up because of wine, such as were Prisca and Maximilla and their chief Montanus: and they did not know what they were saying. They are made drunk with wine when they misunderstand and pervert the holy Scriptures; with strong drink, when they misuse worldly wisdom and the snares of the dialecticians, which are to be called not so much bonds as phantasms, that is, certain shadows and images that quickly perish and dissolve. A curse shall possess the counsel of such men, who suppose that godliness is gain, and do everything for the sake of avarice. Of this phantasm the Apostle too spoke: Giving heed to spirits of error and doctrines of demons, speaking lies in hypocrisy (1 Tim 4:1-2). The third explanation is that, after the Lord has overthrown the scribes and Pharisees, and their associate Judas the betrayer, who had been a thief from the beginning, for he himself carried the purse of the money for the poor (John 12 and 13), he then becomes a hope and a crown of glory to those of the Jews who believed in the Lord; doubtless he means the apostles, whom he reserved for the preaching of the Gospel and did not allow at once to shed their blood for Christ. For all the scribes and Pharisees were made drunk with the wine and strong drink of which we spoke above, the priests as well as the false prophets. But their snares and plots came to nothing, since both Judas himself, who betrayed him, did it for money, and the priests, who corrupted the betrayer with money, did it out of fear of their own rejection. For he himself, making a scourge of cords, cast out of the temple those who sold oxen and sheep, and by his own authority overturned the seats of those who sold doves and the tables of the money-changers, saying to them: It is written: My Father's house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a house of trade, or a den of robbers (Matt 21:13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.8":["According to the anagogy, that Hebrew text in which we said: For all their tables are filled with vomit and filth, so that there is no more room, can be referred to the heretics, and to the scribes and Pharisees, in this way: that we say all their teaching and all their mysteries are full of vomit and filth, since they do not digest the food of the holy Scriptures nor make it life-giving to the whole body, but void it unripe and stinking, so that God finds no place in them. But I wonder what Theodotion meant, that for the Hebrew word called CISOA (קיאצאה), for which Aquila translated vomit of filth and Symmachus simply vomit, he should say ἐμετὸν δυσαλίας, that is, vomit of dysalia, a word I have not been able to find read anywhere among the Greeks, unless perhaps he coined a new name for a new thing. Indeed among the Hebrews too it is a compound, since vomit is called CI, and SOA filthy. Whatever, then, causes nausea and vomiting can be called δυσαλία.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.9-13":["Whom shall he teach knowledge, and whom shall he make to understand what is heard? Those weaned from the milk, those drawn from the breasts: for command, command again; command, command again; wait, wait again; wait, wait again; a little there, a little there. For with speech of lip and with another tongue he will speak to this people, to whom he said: This is my rest; refresh the weary, and this is my refreshment, and they would not hear. And the word of the Lord shall be to them: command, command again; command, command again; wait, wait again; wait, wait again; a little there, a little there; that they may go and fall backward, and be broken, and be snared, and be taken. LXX: To whom shall we announce evils, and to whom shall we announce tidings? those who are weaned from the milk, who are drawn from the breast. Expect tribulation upon tribulation; wait for hope upon hope; yet a little, yet a little: because of the mockery of the lips, because of another tongue with which they will speak to this people, saying to them: This is the rest for the hungry, and this the crushing, and they would not hear. And the word of the Lord shall be to them: Tribulation upon tribulation: expect, expect; hope upon hope: yet a little, yet a little; that they may go and fall backward, and be broken, and be endangered, and be taken. Who, he says, is worthy of the teaching of the Lord, who, by the words of the Savior, who says: He who has ears to hear, let him hear (Matt 11:15), understands with the heart what he has taken in with the ears? Who these are the following verse shows: Weaned from the milk, drawn from the breast: those who are no longer fed with the milk of infancy but eat solid food; who are drawn from the breast with Isaac, for which joy Abraham made a great feast (Gen 21); these deserve to hear the mysteries of the Lord and to understand what the priest and the prophet do not know, drunk and swallowed up by wine, who went astray and did not know the seer, because all their tables were filled with vomit and filth. These, when the prophets announced what was to come and threatened torments unless they did what had been commanded, used to say in mockery: Command, command again; command, command again, that is, give orders, give orders, command what we ought to do. And since they abused the patience of God, who defers his wrath in order to show mercy, they used also to say this, jesting, in the person of the prophets: Wait a little while, wait a little, the things we have foretold will come. All this they said among the people, because they did not believe the words of God; and at once the prophet adds: God will no longer speak to you in these words, to command what you ought to do and that you should wait for what is to come, but he will speak to you in present fury, he who had earlier said to the people: This is my rest, refresh the weary: I have labored a long time, in nothing have I found rest. The foxes have holes, and the birds of the sky nests; but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head (Matt 8; Luke 9). And this is my refreshment, that I may at some time rest in you. They would not hear, and despised my warnings; wherefore what they used to say to the prophets in jest: Command, command again; command, command again: wait, wait again; wait, wait again: a little there, a little there, mocking my patience, since they thought I was threatening things I would never do: let them feel it fulfilled in fact, so that they go to destruction and fall backward with an incurable ὀπισθοτόνῳ; and may never advance to the things before, nor be able to say with the Apostle: Forgetting the things that are behind, we stretch forward to those that are before (Phil 3:13); but may they be broken and snared, and be taken either by the Babylonian or by the Roman army. Where we have said: Command, command again; command, command again: wait, wait again; wait, wait again; a little there, a little there, in the Hebrew it is written thus: SAU LASAU, SAU LASAU; CAU LACAU, CAU LACAU: ZER SAM, ZER SAM; and a most unclean heresy is accustomed to misuse these words among simple and deceived people, to cause terror by the novelty of the expressions: namely, that whoever has learned these words and remembers them in their intercourse will pass without any doubt into the kingdom of heaven. We read in the Apostle: In other tongues and with other lips I will speak to this people, and not even so will they hear me, says the Lord (1 Cor 14:21). This seems to me taken from the present chapter according to the Hebrew; and we have observed this in the Old Testament (apart from a few testimonies which Luke alone uses, who had a greater knowledge of the Greek language): wherever anything is cited from the old instrument, they set it down not according to the Septuagint but according to the Hebrew, following no one's translation, but rendering the Hebrew sense in their own language. Symmachus, Theodotion and the Seventy understood this place differently, and because it is long to speak of them all, let us run briefly through the Seventy translators, who are read in the churches. The people of the Jews having been rejected, the priests and prophets who were drunk with strong drink and went astray, and the curse having eaten up the counsel they formed for the sake of avarice, to whom shall we announce the tribulations to come for Christ's sake? to whom the evils in return for which the crown of virtues is prepared? Surely to those who are weaned from the milk, who are drawn from the breast: doubtless he means the apostles: Expect tribulation upon tribulation. He is speaking to the company of the apostles and of all believers, that each should prepare himself not for one tribulation but for many, so that when he has been afflicted and pressed down he may hope again, and have hope upon hope. And if what has been promised delays a little, let him not be unbelieving: for a little, a very little, and what has been promised will come. For tribulation works patience; patience, proof; proof, hope; and hope does not confound (Rom 5:3-5). And this very tribulation will be doubled by the detraction of lips, and by the blasphemies of the persecutors, with which they rage with rabid mouth against the people of God. Further, the apostles and apostolic men will speak to the people of the Jews, saying: This is the rest for the hungry for righteousness, and this the crushing and the narrowness that leads to life. When they preached this, the ungodly would not hear. Hence what was said to the people of God: Endure tribulation, endure tribulation; expect hope, expect hope: yet a little, yet a little, will be turned into punishment for those who would not hear the word of the Lord, so that they fall and go backward, and run into the peril of siege and death, and are taken with no end of miseries. That the Seventy alone translated the Hebrew word DEA, which all rendered knowledge, as evils is an evident error. For the first letter, DALETH or RES, are distinguished by a small stroke. If, then, it is read DEA (דעה), it means knowledge; if REA (רעה), malice: not from the evil that is the contrary of good, but from distress.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.14-15":["Therefore hear the word of the Lord, you scornful men, who rule over my people that is in Jerusalem. For you have said: We have struck a covenant with death, and with hell we have made a pact: when the overflowing scourge passes through, it shall not come upon us, because we have made a lie our hope, and by a lie we are protected. LXX: Therefore hear the word of the Lord, you afflicted men and princes of this people that is in Jerusalem: Because you have said: We have made a testament with hell, and a pact with death: if the passing tempest rushes on, it shall not come upon us, since we have made a lie our hope, and by a lie we shall be protected. What we said above, that the princes of the Jews used to say to the prophets with mockery: Command, command again; wait, wait again, and other things like these, by which it is shown that they in no way believed the words of the prophets but held their prophecy in contempt, the present chapter shows, in which they are called scornful men. These are the scribes and Pharisees, who rule over the people of God in Jerusalem, whom the Seventy call afflicted men and princes of the people of Jerusalem. For they said, not in word but in deed: We are like the other nations too: we have a pact and a covenant with hell and with death: we have despaired of salvation once for all. Surely the captivity will come a long time hence, since you say: Yet a little, yet a little: wait, wait again. So when we are dead we shall not feel this scourge of captivity and this tempest. For once for all we have believed a lie, that is, we have had hope in God and in his Law in vain. And therefore we are protected by a lie, because, being dead, we have escaped the threatening captivity. The Hebrew word SOT (שוט) Aquila and Symmachus translated scourge, the Seventy tempest; CHASAB (כזב) too all translated lie, in which the Jews hoped, whose father, according to the Evangelist John, is the devil.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.16":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, I will lay in the foundations of Zion a tried stone, a corner stone, precious, founded with a foundation: he who believes, let him not hasten. And I will set judgment in the weight, and righteousness in the measure: and hail shall overthrow the hope of falsehood, and waters shall overflow the protection. And your covenant with death shall be blotted out, and your pact with hell shall not stand; when the overflowing scourge passes through, you shall be trodden down by it. When it passes through, it shall take you away: for in the morning, at daybreak, it shall pass through, in the day and in the night, and vexation alone shall give understanding to the hearing: For the bed is straitened, so that one must fall out, and a short cloak cannot cover both. LXX: Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I will put into the foundations of Zion a precious stone, chosen, a corner stone, honorable, in its foundations; and he who believes shall not be confounded. And I will set judgment in hope, and my mercy in the weight; and those who trust vainly in a lie, since the tempest shall not pass through us, and shall not take from us the testament of death, also your hope in hell shall not remain. If the coming tempest passes through, you shall be trodden down by it: when it passes through, it shall take you in the morning, in the morning it shall pass through: by day and by night there shall be a most evil hope. Learn to hear, you who are in tribulation: We cannot fight, and we ourselves are too weak to be gathered together. I had said to you, he says: Hear the word of the Lord, you scornful men, or afflicted princes of my people, and do not have a covenant with death nor a pact with hell, you who, despising my precepts, have made a lie your hope, and, boasting, or rather despairing, say: by a lie we shall be protected. Therefore the Lord, who is merciful and compassionate, patient and abounding in mercy (Ps 144 [145]), says that, against your will, he will lay in the foundations of Zion a stone chosen and tried and precious and a corner stone. Of this the Apostle too speaks: As a wise architect I have laid the foundation (1 Cor 3:10); and again: For no one can lay another foundation than that which is laid, which is Christ Jesus (ibid., 11). This stone is truly, and a second time, called a stone, just as in Leviticus man, man is said twice; and a corner stone, because it joined together the peoples of the Circumcision and of the Gentiles, of which it is said in the psalm too: The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner (Ps 117 [118]:22). These are the builders and masons who are now called scornful men and princes of the people that is in Jerusalem. Of this stone we read in Daniel too, that it was cut from the mountain without hands and filled the whole world (Dan 2): because the dispensation of the divine offspring took a human body, and the fullness of the Godhead dwelt in it bodily. Upon this stone, which by another name is called the rock, Christ built the Church, and, according to the Hebrew, founded it with a firm foundation; in which whoever believes shall not be confounded, or, according to the Hebrew, let him not hasten: that is, lest the coming of Christ should seem slow to him. For if he delays, according to Habakkuk, let no one despair: because he will surely come, and will fulfill his promises (Hab 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.17-18":["And the protection under which you thought you would be safe, a mighty tempest and a multitude of waters shall destroy, so that the friendship and covenant you had with death and with hell, that is, with the devil, shall perish forever. And the scourge, or tempest, of which you had said: When the overflowing scourge passes through, it shall not come upon us, shall come, and you shall be trodden down by it, that is, you shall suffer all the torments which, in your despairing mind, you thought you would never suffer.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.17":["God promises also to set in it the judgment of the weight: For the Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son (John 5:22). And righteousness, or mercy, in the measure, so that he may render to each according to his works, and temper righteousness and mercy each by the other, according to what we read in the psalms too: Mercy and truth have met each other: righteousness and peace have kissed (Ps 84 [85]:11). Your hope also, he says, and your lie, that is, the devil, the father of every lie, the hail of my punishments shall overthrow.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.19-20":["Further, as for what is read in the Seventy: Learn to hear, you who are afflicted: we cannot fight, and we ourselves are too weak to be gathered together, what sense it has, and how it is connected with what goes before, I am entirely ignorant. Unless perhaps the divine word is speaking to the princes of the people and exhorting them to have hope in God and not in death and hell, and to learn to hear the oracles of the prophets; and they answer that, for the weakness of their strength, they cannot fight against the opposing powers, nor be gathered together among the people of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.19":["For it will always rush upon you, and in prosperity as in adversity you will feel the wrath of the Lord, and cruel death will lay you waste. What shall I say of the punishments? The mere dread of punishments and the fear of torments will correct you unto salvation, and will make you understand your evils. And when you have been vexed, then you will know that my prophets spoke the truth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.20":["And what follows: For the bed is straitened, so that one must fall out, and a short cloak cannot cover both, has the sense we read in the Apostle: You cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons. You cannot be partakers of the table of the Lord and of the table of idols (1 Cor 10:20-21); and elsewhere: What participation has righteousness with iniquity? What fellowship has light with darkness? What agreement has Christ with Belial? What part has the believer with the unbeliever? What consent has the temple of God with idols (2 Cor 6:14-15)? He speaks under the metaphor of a most chaste husband, who says to an adulterous wife: One little bed cannot hold me and the adulterer with you, and a short cloak cannot cover both the husband and the paramour. O Jerusalem, then, to whom he speaks in Ezekiel too under the person of a fornicating wife (Ezek 16:21), and whom at the beginning of Hosea he calls a harlot and an adulteress (Hos 3), if you wish to be joined in my embraces, cast away your idols: if you serve idols, you cannot have me. This according to the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And the glory of the God of Israel was there, not because it took delight in such a neighbor, but so that by its presence it might destroy the idol of jealousy and the temple; hence the overthrow of the city and of the temple also follows after a short time; and it is written in Isaiah that a narrow bed can by no means hold two, and a short cloak cannot cover both, Scripture signifying that saying of the Apostle: What agreement has Christ with Belial? The temple of God with an idol?\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"ISA.28.21":["For as on the mountain of divisions the Lord shall stand, as in the valley of Gibeon he shall be angry, that he may do his work, his strange work, that he may perform his work, his work is foreign to him. And now do not mock, lest perhaps your bonds be tightened. For I have heard from the Lord God of hosts a consummation and a cutting short upon the whole earth. LXX: As a mountain of the ungodly the Lord shall rise up, and he shall be in the valley of Gibeon; with fury he shall do his works, a work of bitterness, and his fury shall deal as with strangers: and his bitterness is strange. And do not you rejoice, lest your bonds be strengthened, since I have heard from the Lord God Sabaoth things completed and cut short, which he will do upon all the earth. The Lord promised that he would lay a precious stone in the foundations of Zion, so that hail might overthrow the lie and the hope of the ungodly, and a mighty tempest might blot out the covenant with death and the pact with hell. Since the scornful princes would not receive it, just as once, when David reigned, the Lord cut down his adversaries against the Philistines on the mountain of divisions, which in Hebrew was called PHARASIM (פרצים), from which the place also took its name; and as in the valley of Gibeon, under Joshua the leader, when, in the confidence of God dwelling in him, he said: Let the sun stand still at Gibeon, and the moon over against the valley of Aijalon (Josh 10:12), and the sun stood still for the space of one day, and many of the foreigners perished: so the Lord shall be angry against the ungodly and the scornful, that he may do his work. For it is not the Lord's work to destroy those whom he created, but to do a strange work, which pertains to cruelty rather than to mercy. And again he repeats the very same thing in other words: that he may perform his work. It is not his work to punish sinners, but foreign and strange to him that he who is the Savior should punish.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to the Seventy, the Lord himself is said to be about to rise like a mountain of the ungodly and to be in the valley of Gibeon, that he may do his works, which are all one work of bitterness: and this should by no means seem blasphemous. For it does not say that the Lord will be a mountain of the ungodly, but like a mountain, which seems most heavy to the ungodly and those who bear it. Just as a negligent son and a sick man may think a father and a physician cruel if they restore them to health with blows and with the cautery of discipline. For the Lord will rise up and will be in the valley of Gibeon on account of those who, though for their sins they stand in a low place, are lifted up into pride by the swelling of their mind. For Gibeon means hill: that he may do his works, which are works of bitterness, when he is compelled to change his clemency and, instead of sweet, becomes bitter.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.22":["Since, then, the Lord is going to rise up from his patience and will by no means spare, just as he did not spare on Mount Pharasim and in the valley of Gibeon, I warn you, O scornful men, by no means to laugh at my prophets, nor to think that what they announce will not come, lest, if you persist in mocking, the bonds of your sins be tightened (For each one is bound fast by the cords of his own sins (Prov 5:22), or the time of captivity come upon you. For what the Lord was postponing in time, the bonds, the captivity and the punishments, or the last day of judgment, he is now about to fulfill in fact, and to consummate and cut short. Therefore I, the prophet, announce to you the things I have learned from the Lord God almighty are to come upon the whole earth, so that you may forestall the impending wrath by repentance.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Therefore you who are one day to suffer these things, do not now rejoice with that joy with which the rich man too rejoiced, clothed in purple at his banquet and neglecting the poor man Lazarus (Luke 16), lest perhaps your bonds become stronger. For what the Lord is going to do, and to fulfill his sentence in fact, this I have both heard and announced to you in full. And as for what he added: Upon all the earth, according to the history understand the borders of Judea; according to the anagogy, of the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.23":["Give ear and hear my voice: attend and hear my speech. Will the plowman plow all day that he may sow, will he break up and hoe his ground? When he has leveled its surface, will he not sow gith, and scatter cumin, and put wheat in rows, and barley, and millet, and vetch in their borders? And he will instruct him in judgment; his God will teach him. For gith shall not be threshed with saws, nor shall the wheel of the cart go round over the cumin; but gith shall be beaten out with a rod, and cumin with a staff: but bread shall be crushed. Yet the thresher shall not thresh it forever, nor shall the wheel of the cart vex it, nor shall he crush it with its hoofs. And this has gone forth from the God of hosts, that he might make his counsel wonderful and magnify righteousness. LXX: Give ear and hear my voice: attend and hear my words. Will he who plows plow all day, or will he prepare the seed before he prepares the ground? When he has leveled its surface, will he not then sow gith and cumin, and again sow wheat, and barley, and millet, and spelt in your borders, and you shall be instructed by the judgment of God, and shall rejoice? For gith is not cleaned with harshness, nor does the wheel of the cart go round over the cumin; but gith is beaten out with a rod, and cumin is eaten with bread. For I will not be angry with you forever, nor shall the voice of my bitterness trample you down; and these portents have gone forth from the Lord. Take counsel, exalt a vain consolation. He is now speaking also to the same men to whom he had said above: Hear the word of God, you scornful men, who rule over my people that is in Jerusalem; and he commands them to hear his voice and attend diligently to his speech.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.24-25":["Will the farmer, he says, always plow so that he may cast the seed? Will he not first break up the ground and turn over the furrows with the plowshare, and break the lumps lying there with rake and hoe, so that when he has leveled the surface of the earth and first softened the hard fields, he may then scatter gith or cumin, and sow wheat, and barley, and millet, and spelt in their borders, according to the variety of the land and of the seasons: for all things are not sown at once. For spelt, which the Greeks call ζέαν, some understand vetch.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.24-29":["God dispenses the human race in various ways: now he punishes, now he has mercy; now he rebukes, now he defends; that is, now he plows, now he sows, now he reaps the ripe fruits, and when they are reaped he threshes them on the floors, and governs his world as he will. Gith and cumin, that is, all the nations that did not receive the knowledge of him nor have the precepts of the Law, he corrects with a rod and a staff; but the wheat, that is, the people of the Jews, he will torment with great punishments. For to whom more is entrusted, from him more will be required. And the servant who knows the will of his lord and does not do it shall be beaten with many stripes (Luke 12:47); and in another place it is written: The mighty shall suffer torments mightily (Wis 6:7). Yet he will not torment them forever. For it is one thing to be ungodly, another to be a sinner. What we have interpreted of the nations and the Jews, others expound of the people and the priests: that the unlearned common people in the day of judgment will be chastised like gith and cumin with a rod and a staff, but the priests, who had the key of knowledge, will be tormented with great punishments; and that this happens by the judgment of the Lord, who shows in all things his wonderful counsel and the truth of his righteousness, so that from those who have received more, more is required.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.26":["And God by his judgment instructs the farmer himself, that is, the sower, by nature, and teaches him to know what cultivation to apply to which seed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.27":["Then, when the time of reaping has come, gith and cumin, which are the weaker seeds, are not ground by the wheels of the carts, which, made of iron like saws, are driven round and dragged over the reaped crops; but they are beaten out with a rod and a staff, which are commonly called flails.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Where we have translated: gith shall be beaten out with a rod, and cumin with a staff, the Seventy, meaning I know not what, translated: but cumin is eaten with bread. Indeed the old Greek translators too, in rendering the Hebrew, were silent about this expression, perhaps because they had nothing to say.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.28-29":["And that passage in which we, according to the Hebrew, have put: Yet the thresher shall not thresh it forever, nor shall the wheel of the cart vex it, nor shall he crush it with its hoofs, the Seventy translated not word for word but according to the sense: For I will not be angry with you forever, nor shall the voice of my bitterness trample you down: showing that refreshment is to come to sinners after torments, and that these things have gone forth from the Lord as portents and wonders. Hence sinners who are afterward to be punished are commanded to take counsel and exalt their consolation, by no means a vain one, as was added by the Seventy, but consolation absolutely. For God would never command them to exalt their vain consolation, which would be of no profit to them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.28":["But bread, that is, the wheat from which bread is made, is ground by iron wheels, and all its stalk is crushed into chaff. Yet it is not always vexed and ground, nor at all times crushed by the hoofs of the wheels; for which in the Hebrew it says by their horses: so that, since he had spoken of the hoofs of the wheels, he might keep the metaphor in the rest. Some would have it that, because he named hoofs and horses, herds of mares are meant, which are usually let into the threshing floors to tread out the grain; but Scripture could not say what the province of Judea did not have.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.28.29":["But this, that is, that gith and cumin are beaten out with a rod and a staff, while wheat and barley and spelt, and perhaps millet too, are ground by iron wheels, not forever, is the judgment of God, who in all things shows his wonderful counsel and displays the greatness of his righteousness in all. These things we have said παραφραστικῶς, so that we may more easily understand the sense for which they are said.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.1-8":["I know that I have read that Ariel means my light of God, which is far otherwise. For here the first syllable is written with ALEPH and RES (אר): but light, which in Hebrew is called OR, has between ALEPH and RES the middle letter VAU, which is not found in the present name. And they refer all that is now said against Ariel to the heretics, who reckon their own doctrine to be the light of God, and who are to be overcome by the true David: and all their solemnities are to be taken away, and their present joy changed into future sorrow; and God commands them to do penance, lest they begin to eat with the Moabites and be like the Gentiles. For he himself will overcome Ariel, and will encompass all their strength and riches with his army. And with towers, that is, with the teachers of the Church, he will bring their words low to the earth, so that they will by no means set their mouth against heaven, but will be written in the earth and speak from the earth, and become like dust shaken off by a wheel; and all the riches of the ungodly will be compared to a point and a moment of time, when he visits them in his majesty, descending in whirlwind and tempest and the fire of punishment; and they will understand that all their riches, and the pomp of their words, and the twists of their arguments, are like the dream of one who eats and drinks in vain: they who have made war against Jerusalem, the vision of peace, or against Ariel, the mightiest lion, and lastly against Mount Zion, upon which the city of the Church is set and cannot be hidden.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.1":["Woe to Ariel, Ariel, the city which David besieged. Year has been added to year, the solemnities have rolled round. I will surround Ariel with a rampart, and it shall be sad and mourning; and it shall be to me like Ariel. And I will set as it were a ring round about you, and I will cast up a mound against you, and set up fortifications for your siege. You shall be brought low, you shall speak from the earth, and your speech shall be heard from the ground, and your voice shall be like that of a python from the earth, and from the ground your speech shall mutter. And the multitude of those who winnow you shall be like fine dust, and the multitude of those who have prevailed against you like passing ash. And it shall be suddenly, at once: it shall be visited by the Lord of hosts with thunder and the shaking of the earth, and with the great noise of whirlwind and tempest, and of the flame of devouring fire. And the multitude of all the nations that have fought against Ariel, and all who have made war and besieged and prevailed against it, shall be like the dream of a night vision. As the hungry man dreams and eats, but when he has awakened his soul is empty; and as the thirsty man dreams and drinks, but after he has awakened he is still faint and thirsty, and his soul is empty: so shall be the multitude of all the nations that have fought against Mount Zion. LXX: Woe to Ariel, Ariel, the city which David besieged. Gather the produce, eat year upon year, for you shall eat with Moab: for I will press Ariel hard, and its strength and its riches shall be mine, and I will encompass you like David: and I will throw up a rampart round about you, and set towers around you, and your words shall be brought low to the earth, and your speeches shall sink into the earth. And your voice shall be like that of those who speak from the earth, and down to the pavement your voice shall grow weak. And the riches of the ungodly shall be like dust from a wheel, and the multitude of those who have oppressed you like ash that is swept away; and it shall be in an instant, suddenly, from the Lord Sabaoth. For there shall be a visitation with thunder, and shaking, and a great noise, a mighty tempest, and a flame of devouring fire. And the riches of all the nations that have made war against Ariel, and all who have fought against Jerusalem, and all who have gathered against it and afflicted it, shall be like one who sees a dream by night. And they shall be like those who hunger in their sleep and eat, and when they rise their dream is vain; and like one who thirsts in a dream and drinks, and when he rises he will still thirst, and his soul has hoped in vain: so shall be the riches of all the nations that have made war against Mount Zion. Where we have translated Woe, in the Hebrew is written OI (הוי), which among them is sometimes used in the vocative case, so that it does not lament Ariel but calls it; although in the present place it is to be taken as a lament. Ariel too means lion of God; and for city, which Aquila translated πολίχνην, that is, little town or little village, they read in the Hebrew CARIATH (קרית), which properly signifies village, and in the Syriac language is called Cartha; whence the village of the woods too is called CARIATH JARIM (קרית יערים). Indeed above too (cf. Isa 1:21), where we read: How has the faithful city Zion become a harlot?, for city Cariath is written, that is, village: which, to express Aquila's translation word for word, we can call citylet. Therefore Ariel, that is, lion of God, is the name given to Jerusalem, once most strong; or, as others think, to the temple and altar of God that was in Jerusalem. And what follows: Which David besieged, for which Symmachus translated camp of David, and Theodotion circumvallation of David, in the Hebrew reads HANA (חנה), which the most learned of the Hebrews held to signify dwelling place. If, then, we read: which David besieged, let us refer it to that time when David took the citadel of Zion, while the blind and the lame resisted, and Joab first climbed the heights of the buildings (1 Chr 11). But if according to Symmachus and Theodotion, it is to be understood that David restored and fortified it; year has been added to year, or taken away, as Aquila translated; and the solemnities have rolled round. For when the temple was overthrown and the Jewish worship taken away, all their festivity perished.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","At the beginning of this chapter too, where we have said: Year has been added to year, or taken away, they translated: gather the fruits, or produce, year upon year: eat, for you shall eat with Moab. And the sense is: before the acceptable year of the Lord, of his preaching, comes, or rather the two years, of which we read in the Canticle of Habakkuk according to the Hebrew: In the midst of two times you shall be known (Hab 3), sow for yourselves in tears, that you may reap in joy (Ps 125 [126]). It is written in the Gospel according to John that the Lord came to Jerusalem at three Passovers, which make two years (John 2:13). And what follows: For you shall eat with Moab, is not in the Hebrew. Of this we can say that, unless they gather for themselves the fruits of repentance, they will begin to eat with those who do not enter the Church of the Lord forever. The other points in which they seem to differ are plain, and from what we have expounded their interpretation is easy.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.2":["And the Lord says that he will surround Ariel with a rampart, with the Babylonian army, and that it will be sad and mourning when it has been destroyed by them. And again, under Jeshua son of Jozadak the high priest, and Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel, and Ezra and Nehemiah, when Haggai and Zechariah prophesied, it will be like Ariel, in that it will have the likeness of the ancient temple, but not have its magnificence and adornment.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.3":["A second time also the Lord threatens that he will surround Ariel with a ring, and cast up a mound against it, and set up fortifications for its siege, and that what he himself says, lamenting Jerusalem in the Gospel, will be fulfilled: If you knew the things that are for your peace, for days shall come upon you, and your enemies shall surround you with a rampart, and besiege you, and you shall be brought low to the ground (Luke 19:42ff.). And Jerusalem shall be trodden down by the nations until the time of the nations is consummated.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.4":["For in other words the prophet now says the same thing: that, brought low, it speaks from the earth, and its speech is heard from the ground; and its voice is like that of a python from the earth, and from the ground it mutters like sparrows; so that by these words he signifies the νεκυομαντείαν of the magicians, by which they are said to call up souls and to hear the thin voices of shades, or rather of demons. Indeed for python Aquila translated magician, which in Hebrew is called CHEB (כאב), for which the Seventy translated those speaking from the earth. By these words it is indicated that the ruin of the temple will remain until the consummation of the world, so that, having collapsed into ashes, it will never be raised again.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.5":["And so great an army of Roman power will encompass you, O Ariel, that it will be compared to countless dust, like ash flying through the air. Hence he compares to dust and ash not the weakness of those who made war against them, but their multitude, which is equal to the countless sands.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.6":["And this will be suddenly, at once, so that in the midst of peace sudden wars arise under Nero, and the Lord of hosts visits Jerusalem with thunder, and with the shaking of the earth, and with the whirlwind of tempest, and with the flame of devouring fire, by which he signifies that the temple was to be burned.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.7-8":["And the Romans, who, when the Jews had been overcome and Jerusalem overthrown under Titus and Vespasian, offered to the Capitol spoils from the vessels that once were God's, and thought that what they had done was the work of their own valor and of the power of their gods, not of the wrath of God, will possess all their riches as in a dream and in a vision of the night. And as one who is hungry thinks, while sleeping, that he is eating in his dreams, and one who is thirsty, his throat parched with thirst, drinks rivers, and when he has awakened his thirst becomes fiercer, having been mocked by an empty drink: so the multitude of all the nations which, subject to Roman power, fought against Mount Zion, will have their riches as in a shadow and a cloud and a dream of the night, riches which they will leave behind by an early death. In the place where we have put: all who have made war and besieged and prevailed against it, the Seventy translated: and all who have fought against Jerusalem, which is not in the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.8":["But that we may know that waters and cups signify teaching, the Lord speaks to the Samaritan woman: Everyone who drinks of this water will thirst again; but whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst for ever. Therefore whoever drinks of the waters of the Samaritan woman, that is, of the heretics, will always thirst and will not be able to cool the burning of a parched throat, as Isaiah cries aloud about this very thing: As one who is thirsty dreams that he drinks, and when he has risen he still thirsts, and his soul has hoped in vain; so shall all the nations be that fight against Jerusalem. For truly whoever drinks of the waters of the heretics, and fights against Jerusalem, the Church of God, drinks in his sleep, and his soul is mocked by empty images. And when he thinks himself satisfied, then he will have the beginning of thirst.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"ISA.29.9-10":["Be stupefied and wonder: waver and stagger, be drunk, and not with wine; reel, and not with drunkenness. For the Lord has mingled for you a spirit of deep sleep: he will close your eyes; your prophets and your princes, who see visions, he will cover. And the vision of all shall be to you like the words of a sealed book, which, when they give it to one who knows letters, they will say: Read this, and he will answer: I cannot, for it is sealed. And the book shall be given to one who does not know letters, and it shall be said to him: Read; and he will answer: I do not know letters. For spirit of deep sleep the Seventy translated compunction; Theodotion ecstasy of mind; Aquila καταφορὰν, that is, heavy sleep; which in Hebrew is called THARDEMA (תרדמה), and which Scripture records was sent by God upon Adam (Gen 2), when the woman was made from the rib of his side. And Jonah snored in this same sleep in the ship (Jonah 1). And the whole prophecy after the overthrow of Jerusalem and the temple, that is, of Ariel, is against the scribes and Pharisees, who, having the key of knowledge, neither enter themselves nor allow others who wish to enter. And they are commanded to be made drunk with stupor and wonder, or rather, according to the Septuagint, with the dissolution of the whole body and ecstasy of mind, and to reel and waver: staggering not with wine nor strong drink, but with the spirit of deep sleep, or of the Lord's compunction, so that, understanding their evil, they may at some time do penance and say with the prophet: I was turned in my weakness, while the thorn is fastened in me (Ps 31 [32]:4). Know, then, O scribes and Pharisees, who are the princes of the Jews, that the Lord is repaying you in kind. For when you heard the Lord the Savior, you would not understand, and you closed your eyes lest you should see him, and made your ears heavy lest you should hear. Therefore he too will close your eyes, which are the prophets, through whom you used to see the knowledge of God. Or he will close your eyes and those of the prophets, who are your princes. For the Law and the prophets were until John the Baptist (Luke 16): so that while you do not see, those may see of whom the later passage (cf. v. 18) testifies: And in that day the deaf shall hear the words of the book, and the eyes of the blind shall see and exult. For the Lord came for judgment, that the Jews who see might not see, and that those who were blind, namely the people of the nations, might see and contemplate the Lord (John 9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.11-12":["Since, therefore, the teachers of the Jews to this present day cannot read and open the seals and lay open the mysteries of the Scriptures, if you give that book to the unlearned people, who are devoured by their own teachers, they will confess that they do not know letters and therefore cannot read. And of two evils it is much lighter to confess ignorance of the Law than to boast of wisdom and not be able to know what is said.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.11":["Hence he significantly did not say that the vision of all the prophets is sealed to all who read; but to you, he says, it shall be sealed, to whom I now speak, to whom I foretell that these things will come; or he will close forever the eyes of your princes, who, according to the Septuagint, boasted that they saw things hidden and secret. And all holy Scripture, he says, shall be to you closed and sealed, so that you, who suppose you know the letters of the Law and the oracles of the prophets, and meditate day and night with unwearied mouth on the volumes of the Scriptures, may not understand what you read; as it is written in the Apocalypse of John: Who is worthy to open the book and to loose its seals (Rev 5:2)? And when no one had been found to open the seals, he says that he wept, and that at last he was granted a word of God to him: Do not weep: behold, the lion of the tribe of Judah, the root of David, has prevailed to open the book and to loose its seals. The lion of the tribe of Judah is the Lord Jesus Christ, who loosed the seals of the book, not, properly, of one book, as many think, the psalms of David, but of all the Scriptures, which were written by one Holy Spirit; and therefore they are called one book. Of this Ezekiel testifies in mystical speech (Ezek 2) that it was written within and without: in the sense and in the letter. Of this the Savior too speaks in the psalms: At the head of the book it is written of me (Ps 39 [40]:8); not of Jeremiah, not of Isaiah, but in all holy Scripture, which is called one book.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.13":["Let us humor the Jews for a little while, and listen patiently to the absurdities of those among them who are called wise; and then we shall understand what are the Jewish fables, of men who, without the authority of Scripture, without any support of reason, invent certain fabulous animals; of whom Isaiah prophesied, saying: \"This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me. But they worship me in vain, teaching the doctrines and commandments of men\" (Isa. 29:13). Approving this testimony in the Gospel, the Savior convicts them of having preferred the commandments of men to the Law of God. \"For God says: Honor father and mother\"; but they handed down: \"That whoever says to father and mother, Whatever gift is from me will profit you, shall not honor his father and his mother\" (Matt. 15:3 ff.).\n— Commentary on Titus","And the Lord said: Because this people draws near with its mouth, and glorifies me with its lips, but its heart is far from me, and they have feared me by the commandment of men and by doctrines; therefore, behold, I will proceed to cause wonder in this people by a great and astounding marvel: for wisdom shall perish from its wise men, and the understanding of its prudent men shall be hidden. LXX: And the Lord says: This people draws near to me with its mouth, and honors me with its lips, but its heart is far from me: in vain do they worship me, teaching the precepts and doctrines of men. Therefore, behold, I will proceed to remove this people: for I will remove them, and I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and hide the understanding of the prudent. The Lord used this testimony against the Pharisees in the Gospel of Matthew, saying: Why do you also transgress the commandment of God for the sake of your traditions (Matt 15:3). And again: Hypocrites, well did Isaiah prophesy of you, saying: This people honors me with its lips, but its heart is far from me. And in vain do they worship me, teaching the doctrines and precepts of men (ibid., 6-9). In this we ought to note what we have often pointed out: that the Evangelists and Apostles did not translate word for word, nor follow the authority of the Seventy translators, whose edition was already being read at that time, but, as Hebrews and men instructed in the Law, used their own words without loss to the sense. The people of the Jews draws near to God with its mouth and lips, because it boasts that it holds to the worship of the one God and rejects idols; but its heart is far from him, because it does not receive the Lord Jesus Christ. For he who does not receive the Son does not receive the Father. And at the same time let us learn how a person draws near to God, or becomes far from him. He speaks through Jeremiah too: I am a God who draws near, and not from afar, says the Lord (Jer 23:23 LXX). We draw near to God with the mind, not with the body; as we read of Moses too: Moses alone drew near to God; but the others did not draw near (Exod 24:2). For he prayed to God in spirit and in truth, and approached him with understanding and spirit. But these, of whom it is written that they are taken from the womb and instructed from boyhood to old age, and meditate day and night on the Law of God, do not draw near to God, because they accept the traditions of the Pharisees and scribes, which make them far from God; and they are whitened sepulchres, which outwardly appear beautiful but within are full of dead men's bones: they chose Barabbas and rejected the Lord (Matt 23:27).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.14":["Therefore he threatens, according to the Seventy, that he will proceed, not to remove them to the Assyrians and Babylonians, but to scatter them through the whole world. According to the Hebrew, he says that he will cause great wonder and an astounding marvel to the people of the Jews, so as to destroy the wisdom of those who did not receive the power of God and the wisdom of God, and to hide and conceal the understanding of the prudent: that is, lest they find him who is contained in the letter. From this place the Apostle Paul, writing to the Corinthians, set down a testimony: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the prudent I will reject (1 Cor 1:19): setting out the same sense in other words; not that God is an enemy of wisdom, of which he is the father, and through whom all wisdom among men exists; but he set it down with an addition, as it is in the Hebrew: of the wise of the people and of its prudent, that is, of the people of the Jews. And truly God worked a great marvel in the people after the coming of the Lord the Savior, so that at one and the same time Jerusalem burned, and the temple fell, and all the knowledge of the teachers was taken away; fulfilling what was said above (cf. Isa 3:1ff.): Behold, the Ruler, the Lord Sabaoth, shall take away from Jerusalem and from Judah the strong man and the strong woman: all the strength of bread and all the strength of water; the giant and the man of war, and the judge, and the prophet, and the diviner, and the elder, and the captain of fifty, and the wonderful counselor, and the wise architect, and the prudent hearer, and the rest that the prophetic word wove together.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.15":["Woe to you who are deep of heart, to hide counsel from the Lord, whose works are in darkness, and they say: Who sees us, and who knows us? This thought of yours is perverse, as if the clay should think against the potter, and the work say to its maker: You did not make me; and the thing formed say to him who formed it: You do not understand. LXX: Woe to those who make deep counsel, and their works shall be in darkness, and they say: Who sees us? who shall know us, and what we are doing? shall you not be reckoned as the potter's clay? Shall the thing made say to its maker: You did not make me wisely; or the thing formed to him who formed it: You did not make me? Against the same men of whom he had said above: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the prudent I will reject, or, as the prophet writes, hide, the word of God is now also directed: those who are wise in themselves and prudent in their own sight. Of whom that can most rightly be understood: They have failed, searching with searching (Ps 63 [64]); since it is written of the wisdom of God: For if anyone be perfect among the sons of men without your wisdom, he shall be reckoned as nothing (Wis 9). They think God does not know their counsels because their works are in darkness, and they say: Who sees us? and who knows us? And the wise of this world, and the teachers of the heretics, and the Pharisees of the Jews say this: that no one understands them; not remembering what is said to God: Darkness shall not be dark to you: and the night shall be lit up as the day (Ps 138 [139]:12). And: As is his darkness, so also is his light (Gen 3). Deceived by this error, Adam and Eve, hearing the sound of the feet of God walking in paradise, hid themselves under the tree in which was the knowledge of good and evil; and Cain too, saying: If you cast me out today from your face, I shall be hidden (Gen 4:14), with like folly thought that God did not know. And through Amos it is said of the ungodly and sinners: If they hide themselves from my eyes in the depth of the sea, there I will command the serpent, and it shall bite them (Amos 9:3). Hence, however wise someone may be and of the most ardent talent, if he does not have the wisdom and teaching of God, let us say of him: Unless the Lord builds the house, they labor in vain who build it. Unless the Lord guards the city, he who guards it watches in vain (Ps 126 [127]:1). And let us make a saying like it: Unless the Lord helps knowledge, he labors in vain who thinks himself wise. Unless the Lord keeps our heart with all watchfulness, he watches in vain who thinks it can be kept by his own diligence.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.16":["This thought of yours, he says, is perverse: that you should think the maker does not know what he has made, and the Creator is ignorant of his creature; as if the clay and the work should say to the potter and its maker: You did not make me, or you did not make me well, nor do you understand your handiwork. The Apostle uses this testimony in other words in writing to the Romans, where he discusses the deep knowledge of God and seeks to refute the false charge of men: O man, who are you to answer back to God? Does the thing formed say to him who formed it: Why have you made me thus? Or has not the potter power over the same clay to make one vessel unto honor and another unto dishonor (Rom 9:20-21). In all these that rule must always be observed: the Evangelists and Apostles translated from the Hebrew into Greek as seemed good to them, without loss to the sense.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.17":["Is it not yet a little while, and a short time, and Lebanon shall be turned into Charmel, and Charmel shall be reckoned as a forest? And in that day the deaf shall hear the words of the book, and out of darkness and gloom the eyes of the blind shall see. And the meek shall increase their joy in the Lord, and the poor among men shall exult in the Holy One of Israel: for he who prevailed has failed. The scorner is consumed, and all who watched for iniquity have been cut down: who made men sin by a word, and tripped up him who reproved in the gate, and turned aside in vain from the righteous. Let the Jews answer, and the friends of the bare history alone, who seek not fruit on the tree but only the leaves and the shade of words, which quickly withers and perishes: how at the coming of the Lord the Savior (since from the times of Isaiah to the dispensation of the flesh he assumed is, compared with eternity, a short and little space of time) Lebanon, a mountain of Phoenicia, was turned and transferred into Mount Carmel, which in Hebrew is called CHERMEL (כרמל), and lies on the border of Palestine and Phoenicia, overlooking Ptolemais (though another mountain too is called Carmel in the holy Scriptures, where lived Nabal the Carmelite (1 Sam 25), a foolish and wicked man, who had as wife Abigail, who on her husband's death married David; for while he lived she could not take David as husband, lest she be called an adulteress if she were joined to another man); and how Charmel is reckoned as a forest and as unfruitful trees? Since they cannot say this, let them hear that Lebanon, which means whitening, refers to the people of the nations, which, washed in the Lord and its former filth purged away, ascending wholly clean, leans upon the Savior in the person of the Church; and of it it is said in the Song of Songs: Who is this who comes up whitened, leaning upon her kinsman (Song 8:5); and that this people is transferred into Charmel, that is, into the knowledge of circumcision, so as to recognize the mysteries of the spiritual and true circumcision, and to be in the place once held by the people of the Jews; while those who would not receive Christ are reckoned as a forest and as barren trees, which cannot praise the Lord with the fruit-bearing trees.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.18":["When Lebanon has been changed into Charmel, and Charmel into a forest, then those who before were deaf and could not hear the words of the prophetic book (of which we said above that all holy Scripture is called one book, as the Preacher says (Eccl 12:11): The words of the wise are as goads, and as nails fixed deep, which are given from the councils by one shepherd) shall hear, and shall speak, those to whom the Savior will say: Ephphetha, which means be opened (Mark 7). And where we translated: out of darkness and gloom the eyes of the blind shall see, whose interpretation is easy, Aquila and Theodotion and Symmachus translated: The eyes of the blind shall see darkness and gloom: to show that the mysteries of Christ were to be laid open to the people of the nations, which before was blind, according to what is said: Dark water in the clouds of the air (Ps 17 [18]:12). And in the same psalm it is written of God: He made darkness his hiding place (ibid., 12). And in Proverbs we read: A wise man hearing these things will be wiser, and the prudent will possess government; and he will understand a parable and dark speech, the sayings of the wise and riddles (Prov 1:5-6). Indeed Moses too, that he might understand and see God (Exod 20), entered into the cloud and into the thick darkness, of which it is said in the Psalm: Thick darkness under his feet (Ps 17 [18]:10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.19":["When those who were before blind have either beheld all the mystical things, or have lifted their eyes from blindness and darkness to see, so that the true light may rise for those who sit in darkness and the shadow of death: then the meek, of whom it is written: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the earth (Matt 5:4); or the poor, whose prince says in the psalms: This poor man cried, and the Lord heard him (Ps 33 [34]:7); and: who shall judge the poor of the people, and shall save the sons of the poor (Ps 71 [72]:4), of whom it is said to the Jews: You have despised the counsel of the poor man: these shall increase their joy in the Lord, so that he who glories may glory in the Lord (2 Cor 10); and the poor among men, or those despaired of and despised by men, who before had no knowledge of the Law, shall exult in the Holy One of Israel, who says: Be holy, for I am holy (Lev 11:44).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.20-21":["And there is every exultation, because he who prevailed has failed. The scorner is consumed, or the arrogant and proud one, who had said: By strength I will do it, and by wisdom; I will remove the bounds of the nations and lay waste their strength; who mocked all, so much so that the saint too said: My loins are filled with illusions (Ps 37 [38]:8). And all who watched for iniquity have been cut down, or destroyed, whose destruction, because of their evil watchings, does not slumber: who made the whole race of men sin by a word of blasphemy, so that they denied the Word of God and set their mouth against heaven. He means the demons, who, wicked themselves, made all men sin in speech, blaspheming with various dogmas; and as far as in them lay they tripped up him who reproved in the gate and in judgment, and made him turn aside from righteousness, who was in the gates of the daughter of Zion, lifted up from the gates of death; and who rebuked those who hated him who reproved in the gates. What we have understood of the devil and his angels, the Nazarenes think was said against the scribes and Pharisees: that the δευτερωταί have failed, who before mocked the people with most wicked traditions, and watched day and night to deceive the simple, who made men sin against the Word of God, so that they denied Christ the Son of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.22-24":["Therefore thus says the Lord to the house of Jacob, he who redeemed Abraham: Jacob shall not now be confounded, nor shall his face now blush: but when he sees his sons, the works of my hands, in his midst sanctifying my name: and they shall sanctify the Holy One of Jacob, and shall proclaim the God of Israel; and those who err in spirit shall know understanding, and the mutterers shall learn the Law. LXX: Therefore thus says the Lord concerning the house of Jacob, which he separated from Abraham: Jacob shall not now be confounded, nor shall he now change his face; but when he sees his sons, my works: for my sake they shall sanctify my name, and they shall sanctify the Holy One of Jacob, and shall fear the God of Israel; and those who err in spirit shall know understanding, and the mutterers shall learn obedience; and what follows, ÷ and the stammering tongues shall learn to speak peace ⁚, is to be marked with an obelus. When, he says, the deaf have heard the words of the book, and the eyes of the blind have seen, and Lebanon has been turned into Charmel, and Charmel reckoned as a forest, so that the meek and the poor rejoice in the Lord, and the proud man and the scorner fail: then Jacob shall be confounded, who now, for the time being, is not confounded. He means the times of Isaiah. And his face shall blush, so that blushing and confusion may be an occasion of salvation, especially when he sees his sons, that is, the Apostles and apostolic men, who were of the race of the Jews, performing the mighty works of the Lord in the midst of the nations, and proclaiming the name of Christ to the Gentiles, and saying: Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name (Matt 6:9). For they will sanctify the Holy One who sprang from Jacob, and will teach the God of Israel among the nations, so that, having abandoned idols, they say: How false are the idols our fathers possessed, and there is none among them who gives rain; and then those who now err in spirit shall know the understanding of God, and those who once murmured against the Lord in the wilderness shall learn the Law, of which before they had no knowledge. For if they had received Moses, they would have received the Lord Jesus Christ too, since Moses spoke of him.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.29.22":["What at the beginning of the chapter we translated according to the Hebrew: Thus says the Lord to the house of Jacob, he who redeemed once Abraham, and brought him out of the Chaldeans, and led him into the land of promise (Gen 11), is perfectly plain. But what the Seventy translated: Thus says the Lord concerning the house of Jacob, which he separated from Abraham, can be understood thus: that he separated the scribes and Pharisees, who blasphemed the Lord, from Abraham, who saw the day of the Lord and rejoiced (John 8). For if they had been sons of Abraham, they would have done the works of Abraham. And the Apostle argues that those are to be called sons of Abraham (Heb 11) who have the likeness of Abraham's faith, that is, the people of the nations, which was called the son of Abraham not from works but from faith.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.1-5":["Some think this place was written against the ten tribes in Samaria, because, seeking help from the Egyptians, they were taken captive by the Assyrians. According to tropology: all who, despising the religion of God, return to their vomit, and, having lost the name of sons, are called most shameless dogs, take counsel not through the Lord, and strike a covenant not through the spirit of the Lord, and add sins to sins: so that, overcome by flattering vices, they also accept the perversity of false doctrines, and go down into the darkness of Egypt, seeking the help of Pharaoh, who reigns in Egypt, whose protection leads to disgrace and to everlasting reproach. For his princes are in Tanis, that is, in a humble and lowly command, most evil messengers, who labor in vain over a people that will not be able to profit them. For as the salvation of their disciples helps holy teachers, so the perdition of those seduced destroys the patriarchs of those who have fled to the help of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.1":["Woe, you deserting sons, says the Lord: that you should take counsel, and not from me; and weave a web, and not by my Spirit; that you might add sin upon sin. You who walk to go down into Egypt, and have not asked my mouth, hoping for help in the strength of Pharaoh, and having confidence in the shadow of Egypt. And the strength of Pharaoh shall be to you for confusion, and the confidence in the shadow of Egypt for disgrace. For your princes shall be in Tanis, and your messengers have come as far as Hanes. All are confounded over a people that will not be able to profit them: they were not for help nor for any use, but for confusion and for reproach. LXX: Woe, you transgressing sons, says the Lord. You have taken counsel, and not through me; and a pact, not through my spirit, that you might add sins to sins: you who go to go down into Egypt, and have not asked me, that you may receive help from Pharaoh and be protected by the Egyptians. The protection of Pharaoh shall be to you for confusion, and to those who trust in Egypt, a reproach. For there are in Tanis princes, most evil messengers. In vain shall they labor for a people that will not profit them for help, nor for use, but for confusion and reproach. After the prophecy against Ariel, and the other things that the preceding discourse set forth up to this place, there now begins another prophecy, which is shown to have been fulfilled after the hundred and fifty years that lay between Isaiah and Jeremiah. For it foretells that history which we read in the volume of Jeremiah (Jer 41), when, Jerusalem having been overthrown and all its wealth and princes carried off to Babylon, Gedaliah son of Ahikam was set over those of royal stock who had remained in Judea; and when he had been slain by treachery together with his Chaldean companions, all the captains of the warriors, and Johanan son of Kareah, and Jezaniah son of Hoshaiah, and the rest of the common people from the least to the greatest, came to Jeremiah the prophet and said to him: Let our prayer fall before you, and pray for us to the Lord your God for all this remnant. For we are left a few out of many, as your eyes behold us; and let the Lord your God show us the way by which we should go, and the thing that we should do (Jer 42:2-3). And when Jeremiah, after ten days, had answered from the word of the Lord: Thus says the Lord God of Israel: If you remain quietly in this land, I will build you up and not pull you down, I will plant you and not pluck you up, for I am appeased concerning the evil that I have done to you. Do not be afraid of the face of the king of Babylon, whom you fearfully dread (ibid., 10-11). And a little after: But if you say: We will not dwell in this land; and set your face to enter Egypt, and go in to dwell there, the sword which you dread shall overtake you there, and the famine about which you are anxious shall cleave to you in Egypt, and there you shall die (ibid., 42:13, 15, 16). The proud men answered, saying to Jeremiah: You are speaking a lie: the Lord our God has not sent you, saying: Do not enter into Egypt to dwell there; but Baruch son of Neriah is inciting you against us, to deliver us into the hands of the Chaldeans, to kill us and to have us carried away to Babylon (Jer 43:2-3). At length all the princes of the people, gathering the remnant with the women and little ones and the king's daughters, not hearing the voice of the Lord, entered Egypt, taking Jeremiah and Baruch with them, and came to Tahpanhes, so that Jeremiah, as a sign of the captivity to come, prophesied against the disobedient people the things contained in his book. What, therefore, was to happen afterward is foretold many years before; and those are called deserting and transgressing sons who, forsaking the counsel of God which they had received through Jeremiah, followed their own will, and wove a web, not by the spirit of God, which sounded to them from the mouth of Jeremiah. He put the weaving of a web μεταφορικῶς, to show an evil counsel; and they did this so as to increase their former sins with the sin of contention and pride.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.2-3":["You who go down, he says, into Egypt, and have not asked my mouth. Not that they did not ask, but that they would not hear the counsel of the prophet, hoping for help in the strength of Pharaoh, and having confidence in the protection, or shadow, of Egypt. For the strength of Pharaoh, which is put twice in this place, the Hebrew has written MAOZ (מעוז). We have noted this so that, where we read in the last vision of Daniel (Dan 11) the God MAOZIM (מעוזים), we may understand not, as Porphyry dreams, the God of the village of Modim, but the mighty and strong God. Pharaoh among the Egyptians is the name of the royal power; and each is called by a particular name as well, as Pharaoh Neco and Pharaoh Hophra: as if we were to set Caesars and Augusti before the proper names of kings. And the strength of Pharaoh, he says, shall be to you for confusion, and the confidence in the shadow of Egypt for disgrace. The same Jeremiah writes that in Tahpanhes, a city of Egypt, he buried stones in the gate of Pharaoh's house, and said to the men of Judah: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Behold, I will send and take Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, my servant, and I will set his throne upon these stones which I have hidden, and he shall set his throne upon them. And he shall come and strike the land of Egypt: such as are for death, to death; and such as are for captivity, to captivity; and such as are for the sword, to the sword: and he shall kindle a fire in the shrines of the gods of Egypt, and burn them, and carry them away captive. And he shall wrap himself in the land of Egypt as a shepherd wraps himself in his cloak, and he shall go forth from there in peace (Jer 43:10-12). For when the Egyptians had been overcome, the Jews too who had fled to the Egyptians were taken captive.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.4-5":["And what follows: For your princes shall be in Tanis, and your messengers have come as far as Hanes, is to be read with mockery and ἐμφατικῶς: that, despising the counsel of God, they sent their princes to Tanis, a city of Egypt, in which was the royal house of Pharaoh, and where under Moses very many signs and portents were performed, as the Psalmist says: Who did wonders in the land of Egypt, in the plain of Tanis (Ps 77 [78]:43). And the same Isaiah says against Egypt: Where now are your wise men? Let them tell you and say what the Lord Sabaoth has purposed against Egypt. The princes of Tanis have failed (Isa 19:12-13); and he records that they failed at that time when the Lord entered Egypt upon a light cloud. We do not read of Hanes, a city of Egypt, anywhere else; but from his saying: Your messengers have come as far as Hanes, we understand that it is the farthest city of Egypt, toward the Ethiopians and the Blemmyes; for which the Seventy translated: Most evil messengers shall labor in vain, who trusted in the people of Egypt, which could not profit them, and they became an everlasting reproach.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.6":["The burden of the beasts of the South. For this the Hebrew says MASSA BEEMOTH NEGEB (משא בהמות נגב), which the Seventy translated: The vision of the four-footed beasts in the desert. The foregoing exposition, which we have woven with a very full unfolding of the history, is the interpretation of this place: that, according to prophetic custom, the burden, that is, the weight and load of torments and punishments, came not only upon Babylon and Philistia, and Moab, and Damascus, and Egypt, and the desert of the sea, and also Edom and Arabia, and the valley of Zion, and lastly Tyre, but also upon the beasts of the South, which the Seventy translated four-footed beasts in the desert. He means the tribe of Judah, which, situated in the southern part, borders on the wilderness; and those who, rejecting the prophecy of Jeremiah, when Jerusalem had been taken, would not dwell in Judea, but fled through the desert to the Egyptians. And rightly are they called beasts of the South, or four-footed beasts in the desert, because they rejected the knowledge of God, and, despising his command, fled to the images of Egypt, having hope in Pharaoh; and whoever leans on him is like one who rests upon a reed, which, when broken, wounds the hand of him who leans on it. Of such beasts the sons of Korah too spoke in the psalm: Man, when he was in honor, did not understand; he has been compared to the senseless beasts, and has become like them (Ps 48 [49]:13). And what follows: This way of theirs is a stumbling block to them, can rightly be understood of the present chapter: that the way of the desert was their ruin. I know that I have read that the beasts of the South and the four-footed beasts in the desert, who, leaving the borders of Judea, fled to the darkness of Egypt, refer to the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places, and the rulers of this darkness, who through their longing for Egypt lost all their riches and former wealth. So he may have said. We, according to tropology, so as to discuss what we have not yet set out, say that all who, having deserted the Creator, have given themselves to the errors of the world are four-footed beasts in the desert of this world, from which they hope for help in vain, since they have abandoned the true help of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","In a land of tribulation and anguish, the lioness and the lion: from them the viper and the flying basilisk: carrying their riches on the shoulders of beasts of burden, and their treasures on the hump of camels, to a people that will not be able to profit them: Egypt shall help in vain and to no purpose. LXX: In tribulation and anguish, the lion and the lion's whelp: thence asps and the offspring of flying asps: who carried their riches on asses and camels to a nation that will not profit them: the Egyptians will profit you emptily and vainly. For lion the Hebrew has written LEIS (ליש); and for flying basilisk, which the Seventy translated offspring of flying asps, in the Hebrew it is called ἐμπρηστὴς and flying, which we can call the burner, and in Hebrew it is named SARAPH (שרף). Again, in the place where we translated carrying on the shoulders of beasts of burden, for beasts of burden all alike translated πώλους, that is, the foals of asses. The land of tribulation and anguish signifies the vast waste of the wilderness, through which the remnant of Jerusalem, with Johanan son of Kareah and the daughters of King Zedekiah, taking with them all they could have, fled over to Egypt. Understand the lioness and the lion's whelp μεταφορικῶς as Jerusalem and its people, as Balaam says in Numbers: Behold, the people shall rise up as a lioness, and shall be lifted up as a lion: it shall not lie down until it devours the prey and drinks the blood of the slain (Num 23:24). In Ezekiel too it is written: Take up a lamentation for the prince of Israel, and say: Why did your mother, a lioness, lie down among lions, and rear her whelps in the midst of young lions (Ezek 19:1-2)? And lest we should think that holy Scripture speaks truly of a lioness and a lion, it speaks against the nature of things, saying that from a lioness and a lion was born a viper and a flying basilisk, or the flying offspring of asps: namely, that from evil parents worse children were begotten, of whom the Gospel too spoke: Brood of vipers, who showed you to flee from the wrath to come (Matt 3:7)? And to the land of Israel it is said: You are a land on which it has not rained, nor has a shower come upon you in the day of wrath; whose princes in the midst of it are like roaring lions, seizing the prey, devouring souls by their might (Ezek 22:24-25). These offspring of vipers, or of flying asps, that is, the princes of the people and the whole crowd of the ungodly, went to a people that could not profit them, as Jeremiah says (Jer 44), that the Lord would deliver Pharaoh Hophra king of Egypt into the hand of his enemies and into the hands of those who sought his life, and that all the men of Judah who were in the land of Egypt would be consumed by sword and famine to utter destruction; so much so that the Hebrews hand down that, before Nebuchadnezzar took Egypt, Jeremiah and Baruch escaped the impending captivity by death. A certain writer expounded the lioness and the lion's whelp as the heavenly Jerusalem and the people deceived from it; and the flying basilisk, and the vipers, and the offspring of asps, as that crooked serpent of which we read above (above, Isa 14 and 27), and of which the Savior spoke in the Gospel: I saw Satan falling like lightning from heaven (Luke 10:18): who, called a dragon in the Apocalypse (Rev 12), drew down with him to the earth a third part of the stars, which by the perversity of their judgment lost their former riches, abandoning them in the Egypt of this world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.7":["Therefore I have cried out concerning this: it is only pride: be still. For which the Seventy translators, meaning I know not what, translated: Announce these things, since this consolation of yours is vain. Further, Symmachus translated ταραχαί εἰσιν κατοικεῖν, that is, there are tumults, that they may dwell. The Hebrew word REEB (רהב) Aquila translated ὅρμημα, that is, onset and pride; Symmachus disturbance; the Seventy vanity; Theodotion breadth; it is written with three letters, RES and HE and BETH, and it is the very word read in the Psalms: I will be mindful of Rahab and of Babylon, who know me (Ps 86 [87]:4). And the sense is: Since in the Egyptians there is vain help, I have commanded her, that is, Jerusalem, or I have cried out, that the Egyptians had only the name of a kingdom, and pride without strength; and that Israel ought to sit or dwell in its own land, and not seek help in vain from the weak.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.8":["Now go in and write for them on boxwood, and inscribe it carefully in a book: and it shall be in the last day for a testimony forever. The Hebrews say that, since in the prophets there is one spirit, and all times are joined together with the Lord, Jeremiah is commanded, and it is God's charge, that, having entered Egypt, he should write for them on boxwood, which is a wood that does not rot; or, as Symmachus translated, ἐπὶ πτυχὶ, that is, on a very broad tablet. For them, doubtless for the Jews: and that he should set it out carefully in a book, so that there may be no difficulty in reading it, so that when the prophecy has been fulfilled in fact, they may then understand that the word of the prophets is true. We run over the plain things so that we may linger on the more obscure.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.9-11":["For it is a people provoking to anger, and lying sons, sons unwilling to hear the law of God. Who say to the seers: Do not see; and to those who look: Do not look out for us the things that are right: speak to us pleasing things; see errors for us. Take the way away from me: turn the path aside from me: let the Holy One of Israel cease from before our face. It is plain that they provoked the Lord to anger, unwilling to hear the words of Jeremiah (Jer 43). They said to him: Do not see for us what we do not want: do not forbid us the way of Egypt, nor preach the things that are right; but say what pleases us, that we may go on into Egypt, which heaps sins upon sins. Why do you press upon us what we do not willingly hear? why do you show the way by which we do not wish to go? Why do you so often repeat in our ears: Thus says the Lord, the Holy One of Israel? Let this preaching cease from us. According to tropology, all heretics are called lying sons. For they went out from us, but they were not of us (1 John 2); they do not hear the Law of God with the ears of the inner man, and they say to the teachers of the Churches: Do not see for us the things that are right, nor threaten the flames of Gehenna; but promise us the kingdom of heaven, so that after luxury and delights paradise may be opened to us. Why do you show me the way of the Lord, by which I do not willingly go? Why do you repeat the name of the Holy One of Israel, or the word of Israel, which does not penetrate the secret places of my breast? This indeed is said also to the people of the Church when it is negligent, if it turns away from the severity of its teachers and favors flatterers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.12-14":["Therefore thus says the Lord, the Holy One of Israel: Because you have rejected this word, and have hoped in false accusation and tumult, and have leaned upon it: therefore this iniquity shall be to you like a breach that falls, and is sought for in a high wall: since suddenly, when it is not hoped for, its crushing shall come. And it shall be broken in pieces as a potter's jar is shattered with a very mighty crushing: and there shall not be found among its fragments a sherd in which a little fire may be carried from the burning, or a little water drawn from the pit. Because, he says, you said above: Let the Holy One of Israel cease from before our face, or, as the Seventy translated, take away from us the word of Israel, therefore thus says the Lord, the Holy One of Israel, and he thrusts himself upon you against your will, so that him whom you neglected when he warned you, you may feel when he punishes. You had confidence in the false accusation and lying of the Egyptians, and leaned upon their tumult, or contradiction and pride, as Symmachus and Theodotion translated; therefore this iniquity, or sin, will turn against you, so that you will be like the suddenly falling wall of a most strongly fortified city that has been taken, whose ruin has come all at once: for so the Seventy translated. And the sense according to the Hebrew is: As the breach of a very high wall that has drawn after it a long ruin can hardly be repaired and recover its former beauty, so sudden crushing will come upon you. And to use another comparison: As a potter's vessel, if it is shattered with a very mighty crushing, is broken into pieces so that scarcely a tiny sherd remains among the fragments in which a little fire may be carried, or a little water drawn from a puddle and a pit, so you, when you have gone into Egypt, with Nebuchadnezzar pursuing you there, will perish to utter destruction. The heretics too, and every doctrine contrary to the truth, trust in false accusation and lying, and, according to the Seventy, mutter against their Creator. Therefore the city of their ungodliness, which Cain built, will be destroyed; sudden perdition will come upon it, when it has been taken by churchmen, and so destroyed and broken in pieces that nothing remains in it that could rekindle a dead fire, or offer even a little muddy water to thirsting peoples.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.15-17":["For thus says the Lord God, the Holy One of Israel: If you return and are quiet, you shall be saved: in silence and in hope shall be your strength; and you would not, and you said: By no means; but we will flee to horses: therefore you shall flee; and we will mount upon swift ones: therefore swift shall they be who pursue you. A thousand men from before the terror of one: and from before the terror of five you shall flee. LXX: Thus says the Lord God, the Holy One of Israel: When you have returned and groaned, then you shall be saved, and you shall know where you were: because you trusted in vain things, your strength has become vain; and you would not hear, but you said: We will flee upon horses: therefore you shall flee; and we will be upon light riders: therefore light shall they be who pursue you. A thousand shall flee at the voice of one, and at the voice of five many shall flee. The Holy One, he says, whose name I had kept silent above, is himself the Lord God, who speaks to you both through me and through Jeremiah (Jer 12): If you do penance, and abandon either your vices or the error of your evil counsel, and remain in Judea, fearing not the onset of the Babylonians but my precepts, you shall be saved. Be quiet and hope in the Lord, and take hold of the strength of faith in my promises, you who, despising the precepts of life, said in despair: It shall by no means be as you say; but we will flee to the horses of the Egyptians, and go to them with quick and swift step. Since, then, you have said this, you shall indeed flee and enter Egypt at a rapid pace; but swifter will be the Babylonians, who will pursue you even to Egypt, and such terror and dread will seize Egypt that a thousand Egyptians will not be able to withstand one Chaldean, and a very great multitude of fugitives will not withstand five enemies; according to what we read in Deuteronomy: The Lord will cause you to fall before your enemies. You shall go out against them by one way, and flee before them by seven ways, and you shall be scattered through all the kingdoms of the earth (Deut 28:25, 32). According to the anagogy and the edition of the Seventy, the divine word exhorts all sinners to repentance, and especially the heretics: that when they have returned from their vices and groaned, and done penance, they may be saved; and then they will understand where they were before, and that they hoped in vain things to no purpose. And while he exhorts them to repentance, they on the contrary trust in false teachers and in the uncertainty of the world; and, unwilling to hear the words of God, they long for the horses of Egypt and delight in their swiftness, horses which the Lord forbade to be multiplied (Deut 17). Therefore churchmen will swiftly pursue them, and a thousand men, a disorderly crowd of fugitives, will not be able to withstand the voice of one warrior, or of five fighters (which we should refer to the mind and to the five senses).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.17":["Until you are left like the mast of a ship on the top of a mountain, and like a signal upon a hill. Jeremiah too writes that, when those who had fled into Egypt had been slain by sword and famine, a few remained who returned to Judea (Jer 44). And as, when a ship has been broken and its framework dissolved, only the tree that is called the mast remains, and is set up as a signal on the summit of a mountain or on a lofty hill, so, as a signal and a trace, scarcely one or two would remain, to show the power of God. And then, he says, all the remnant of Judah, who now are entering the land of Egypt to dwell there, will know, when they have returned to the land of Judea, whose word is fulfilled, mine or theirs.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.18":["Therefore the Lord waits, that he may have mercy on you, and therefore he shall be exalted in sparing you, because the Lord is a God of judgment: blessed are all who wait for him. Great is the clemency of God, that he waits for our repentance, and, until we are converted from our vices, holds back his mighty hand, so as not to be compelled to strike. And he has mercy and spares for this reason, that his mercy may be exalted, and the goodness of the Creator made known to all. Or certainly, according to what he says in the Gospel (John 12:32): When I am lifted up, I will draw all things to myself, he is exalted on the cross for this reason, that he may spare all. For he is the God of judgment; and blessed are all who wait for the Lord, who waits for their conversion. But whose this conversion is, and what it is, the following words will show.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.19":["For the people of Zion shall dwell in Jerusalem. The Jews refer this to the times of Cyrus, when the people returned from Babylon to Judea under Zerubbabel and Jeshua the high priest. But we, as we have often said already, refer all the promises that exceed the modest measure of that time to the coming of Christ, at which the people once captive, set free by the passion of the Lord, dwelt in Zion and Jerusalem, that is, in the watchtower and the vision of peace, that is, in the Church. Further, as to the word of holiness added in the Seventy, who said: for a holy people dwells in Zion, we can interpret it thus: we say that no one dwells in Zion except one who is holy, and who hears the Lord saying: Be holy, for I too am holy (Lev 11:44).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Weeping, you shall by no means weep; showing mercy, he will have mercy on you: at the voice of your cry, as soon as he hears, he will answer you. When you have returned to Zion and dwelt in Jerusalem, you shall by no means weep as you had wept before, but your weeping shall be turned into joy: For blessed are those who weep, for they shall laugh (Luke 6:21). And after you have cried out and said to the Lord: I cried with my whole heart, hear me, O Lord (Ps 118 [119]:145); and again: I cried, have mercy on me, and I will keep your commandments (Ps 85 [86]); and elsewhere: I waited in the morning and cried out (Ps 118 [119]:147); and your voice has been so clear that it pierces the heavens: at once the Lord will answer you, and you will be like Moses, of whom it is written: Moses spoke, and the Lord answered him (Exod 19:19).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.20-21":["And he will no longer make your teacher fly away from you, and your eyes shall see your instructor, and your ears shall hear the word of one admonishing behind your back: This is the way, walk in it, neither to the right nor to the left. LXX: And those who deceive you shall no longer come near you, because your eyes shall see those who led you astray, and your ears shall hear behind you the words of those who deceive, who said: This is the way, let us walk in it, whether to the right or to the left. In this place the Seventy's edition and the Hebrew disagree greatly. First, then, we shall treat of the common edition, and afterward follow the order of the truth. When the eyes of the heart have understood and discerned the truth, and have recognized with the whole reason of the mind those who had deceived them before, and with trained ears have perceived the words of the deceivers behind their back (for they always hasten to deceive the blind part of the body, according to what we read in the psalm: That they may shoot in the dark at the upright of heart (Ps 10 [11]:2)), who assume to themselves such authority that, whether they teach things on the right or on the left, that is, whether good or evil, they do not wish their disciples to examine them by reason, but to follow them as their forerunners: then those who before deceived will no longer be able to approach them, once they perceive that they have been seen through. Further, according to the Hebrew, the explanation is both easy and true. For when the Lord has given believers scant bread and short water, he will no longer make him who teaches man knowledge fly away from them; but their eyes will always see their instructor, and their ears will hear the word of one admonishing behind their back and saying: This is the right way, walk in it, neither to the right nor to the left; according to what is read elsewhere: We will turn aside neither to the right nor to the left; we will go by the king's highway (Num 20:17). For on either side whatever is beyond measure is in vice. And of the right side it is said: Do not be overly righteous (Eccl 7:17). And of the left, there is no doubt that it is placed on the side of the goats and of those who perish.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.20":["And the Lord will give you scant bread and short water. If we refer these things to the times of Zerubbabel, the interpretation is easy: that under him there was no perfect joy, as David says: When the Lord turned back the captivity of Zion, we became like those comforted: so that they received not full and perfect comfort, but a likeness of comfort. But if to the coming of the Savior, then by the scant bread and short water, according to the Apostle Paul and this same prophet Isaiah, the word of the Gospel is foretold, which, in place of the long-drawn observances and commandments of the Law, summed up all things in one word: You shall love your neighbor as yourself. For the Lord has made a word completed and cut short upon the earth (Gal 5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.22":["And you shall defile the plates of your graven images of silver, and the garment of your molten image of gold, and you shall scatter them like the uncleanness of a menstruous woman: Go out, you shall say to it. LXX: And you shall defile the silvered and gilded idols: you shall break them in pieces and scatter them like the water of a menstruous woman, and you shall cast them away like dung. When you have understood the truth, and the deceivers have not come near you, but your eyes have seen your instructor and your ears have always heard: This is the way, walk in it, neither to the right nor to the left: then you will break in pieces and scatter all the errors and idols and likenesses of the truth which an artful tongue had composed in the splendor of eloquence, which is what silver means, and in the reasoning of wisdom, which is what gold signifies; and you will judge them so unclean that you will compare them to the most filthy blood of a menstruous woman, which the Seventy, in other words, translated as the water of a menstruous woman.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.23-24":["In that day the lamb shall feed in your possession at large. And your bulls and the foals of asses that work the ground shall eat mingled mash, as on the threshing floor it has been winnowed. LXX: And in that day your cattle shall feed in a rich and spacious place. Your bulls and your oxen that work the ground shall eat chaff mixed with winnowed barley. In the abundance of all things, when the heavenly rain comes down, and that bread which came down from heaven, which whoever eats will never hunger, the lambs shall feed in a most spacious place, those who follow the Lamb wherever he goes and who are always on the right hand. The bulls are the leaders of the flock, of whom we read: The assembly of bulls among the cows of the peoples (Ps 67 [68]:31); and the foals of asses, sitting on which the Lord entered Jerusalem (Matt 21), who work the ground, shall eat from the threshing floor in such a way that it has been winnowed, namely, so that no chaff be mixed in their food; of which chaff it is written in Jeremiah: What has the chaff to do with the wheat, says the Lord (Jer 23)? and which in the Gospel is burned with unquenchable fire (Luke 3). Further, according to the Seventy, the cattle, which are not yet full of reason and wisdom, and one of whom says to God: I have become like a beast before you (Ps 72 [73]:23), shall feed in a most rich and spacious place, which does not bring forth thorns and thistles, but, like the land of Israel, flows with milk and honey and gives full freedom to those who feed there. And the bulls and oxen that work the ground, that is, the Apostles and apostolic men, of whom the apostle Paul too interprets what is written: You shall not muzzle the ox that treads out the grain; and: Is it for oxen that God cares (1 Cor 9:9)? but surely he speaks of those who work their own ground, and of whom he likewise says: We are God's husbandry, God's building (1 Cor 3:9); for the Father is the husbandman, and Christ the vine (John 15): these shall eat chaff mixed with winnowed barley. We read that Isaac sowed barley and, because he was among the foreigners, received a hundredfold harvest of barley (Gen 26). And Hosea hired an adulterous woman for a cor and a half cor of barley (Hos 3). The Savior too had filled with barley loaves five thousand men who still served the senses of the body and followed the Law of Moses (John 6). In another place, breaking the seven loaves of the Law and crushing them into pieces, he filled with wheaten loaves four thousand men who followed the number of the Gospel (Matt 15). Peoples of this kind eat a part of the chaff too, since in certain things they follow the letter; and yet through the chaff and the barley they gradually advance, so as to pass on to the wheat.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.23":["And rain shall be given to your seed wherever you sow in the land, and the bread of the fruits of the land shall be most abundant and rich. LXX: Then there shall be rain for the seed of your land, and the bread of the fruits of your land shall be plentiful and rich. It is written in the volume of Kings (1 Kgs 18) that after the false prophets had been slain by Elijah, rain was given to the land of Israel; and that the bread of Elisha, and afterward of the Savior, was most abundant and most rich, and had such fullness that many thousands of men were filled from it (2 Kgs 4; John 6). So also in the present place, rain will not be given to the spiritual seed, nor the most abundant and richest bread of the land, until the silvered and gilded idols have been broken in pieces and reckoned as a dunghill. For unless vices depart, virtues do not enter.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.25":["And there shall be upon every high mountain and upon every lifted-up hill streams of running waters in the day of the slaughter of many, when the towers have fallen. In this place the Jews refer the many slain and the falling towers to the power of the Roman empire, of which the Apostle too speaks (2 Thess 2:7): Only let him who holds now hold, until he be taken out of the way: namely, that at that time the blessedness of the people of Israel will be so great that not only the valleys and plains but all the mountains and hills will be watered with flowing waters. But let us understand by the mountains and hills those who are lifted on high by virtues, who hunger and thirst for righteousness (Matt 5), whom the Lord invites to drink. For whoever drinks of his waters will never thirst (John 4). Hence in the psalm too we read: Bless the Lord from the fountains of Israel (Ps 67 [68]:27). And in the Gospel it is said that whoever drinks of the waters of Jesus, rivers of living water shall flow from his belly (John 7). And the holy man says to God: For with you is the fountain of life (Ps 35 [36]:10), from which goes forth a most pure river, of which Scripture again makes mention: The stream of the river gladdens the city of God (Ps 45 [46]:5). And in another place: The river of God is filled with waters (Ps 64 [65]:10): that is, he who speaks through Jeremiah: They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water (Jer 2:13). This will happen when very many have been slain, or have perished: for many are called, but few chosen (Matt 20). And when the towers have fallen, whether the powers of the demons, or all the proud and arrogant and great in this world; of whom it is said in the psalms too: I saw the ungodly man lifted up and exalted like the cedars of Lebanon, and I passed by, and behold, he was not; I sought him, and his place was not found (Ps 36 [37]:35-36). Such towers those desired to build (Gen 11) who moved their feet from the East, whose tongues were confounded in Babylon; and those too on whom the tower of Siloam fell (Luke 13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.26":["And the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun, and the light of the sun shall be sevenfold, as the light of seven days, in the day when the Lord binds up the wound of his people and heals the stroke of its blow. LXX: And the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun, and the light of the sun sevenfold, in the day when the Lord heals the crushing of his people and cures the pain of your blow. I wonder how in the present place the same Seventy translated moon and sun, following the Hebrew, when above they translated the Hebrew words LABANA (לבנה) and HAMMA (חמה), which Aquila renders white and heat, and by which he signifies the moon and the sun, as brick and wall, in the place where it is written: And the moon shall blush, and the sun shall be confounded; for which they translated: the bricks shall melt, and the wall shall fall. Hence the suspicion comes to me that they did not err from the beginning, but were gradually corrupted by the fault of copyists. For it cannot be that those who in this place translated the same words well should have erred in the earlier passage. In the day, then, of the slaughter of many, when the arrogant and proud have fallen, and those who set their mouth against heaven have learned that they are earth, the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun: when the Lord gives a new heaven and a new earth, and the fashion of this world passes away, so that the moon and the sun may obtain the rewards of their labor and their course. For the expectation of the creature waits for the revelation of the sons of God, because the creature itself also will be set free from the bondage of corruption into the liberty of the glory of the sons of God (Rom 8:19); although some, understanding them as instruments of light, contend that they are without sense. The moon, then, will receive the brightness of the sun. Nor is it a wonder to think this of a most bright element, to which the rule of the night has been given, since it is written of the saints too: The righteous shall shine like the sun (Matt 13:43). And the sun will receive a sevenfold light, as was the light of seven days when the world was created in the beginning (although the Seventy did not translate seven days), when the Lord binds up the wound of his people, or heals the crushing of his people: when what is written will be fulfilled: Sorrow and mourning and sighing shall flee away (Isa 35:10): when, the fullness of the nations coming in, all Israel shall be saved; or certainly when the vengeance comes of those whose souls cry out beneath the altar: How long will you not avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth (Rev 6:10)? And consider this too: that he did not say, when he heals the crushing of his people Israel, or Jacob, but absolutely, of his people, so as to signify all who serve God. Some refer this place, and all that is contained in this chapter of promises, to the heavenly Jerusalem and to the return of its people, when what is written will be fulfilled: Heaven and earth shall pass away (Matt 24:35). Others refer it to the times of Elijah, and say that he is the one of whom it is written above: Your eyes shall see your instructor, and your ears shall hear the word of one admonishing behind your back: that then, according to the fables of the poets and the golden age of Saturn, streams of milk will flow from the mountains and hills, and the purest honey will drip from the leaves of the trees. Those who accept these things will also, by a Jewish error, take up the fable of the thousand years and the earthly kingdom of the Savior, not understanding that the Apocalypse of John, under the surface of the letter, weaves together the inmost mysteries of the Church.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.27-29":["Behold, the name of the Lord comes from afar: his fury is burning and heavy to bear. His lips are filled with indignation, and his tongue is like a devouring fire. His spirit is like an overflowing torrent reaching to the middle of the neck, to destroy the nations unto nothing, and the bridle of error that was in the jaws of the peoples. You shall have a song, like the night of a hallowed solemnity, and gladness of heart, like one who goes with the pipe to enter into the mountain of the Lord, to the Mighty One of Israel. LXX: Behold, the name of the Lord comes after a long time: his fury burning with glory, the utterance of his lips, an utterance full of wrath, and the wrath of his fury shall devour like fire; and his spirit, like water in a valley, dragging, shall come up to the neck, and shall be divided to confound the nations for their vain error, and error shall be cast away, and it shall take them in their sight. Must you always rejoice and enter continually into my holy places, as though celebrating feasts and rejoicing, that you may enter with the pipe to the mountain of the Lord, to the God of Israel? Let us speak first according to the Hebrew. What is said here hangs on what precedes. The prophetic word had rebuked those who, scorning the help of God, were fleeing to the Egyptians for fear of the Babylonians, and it threatened those who went down that they would die there. And again, after the punishments, it promises that those who were willing to listen to him would not only dwell in Jerusalem under Zerubbabel, Ezra and Nehemiah, but it pledges a greater blessedness to all who believe the word of God at the consummation of the world, when rivers of waters will run over all the mountains and hills, and, when many have been slain, the towers will fall. The moon too and the sun will receive a brighter light, when the Lord binds up and heals the wound of his people; although some contend, ὑπερβολικῶς, that these things were fulfilled in the land of Judea in the times of Cyrus, who released the people from captivity. Since, then, rewards were promised to the good and obedient, now, on the contrary, punishments are announced to the wicked and the scorners: that the Lord will fulfill his purpose, and will come after a long time to punish sinners, and will pronounce sentence upon all, and with the breath of his mouth will slay the impious one, whom he calls the bridle of the peoples: not one by which he might govern those subject to him, but one by which he might drag those subjected to him to ruin. He also sets down the likeness of a torrent flooding up to the neck, to testify that the end of all things has come. For as a torrent chokes the one up to whose neck it has reached, so the judgment of God will let no one go away unpunished. And when he has destroyed the bridle that was in the jaws of all the nations, together with the nations subject to it, and has brought it to nothing, then, he says, you, the saints, who obey my commands, shall have a song, like the night of the hallowed solemnity when you went out of Egypt, and in the solemnity of the Passover threw off the yoke of Egyptian slavery, saying at the Red Sea, when Pharaoh had been drowned: Let us sing to the Lord, for he is gloriously magnified (Exod 15:1); and such gladness of heart that you will imitate those who, bringing first fruits to the temple and carrying gifts into the winepresses of God, go with pipes, showing the joys of their heart in song. These things according to the Hebrew I have touched on briefly.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.27":["But in both editions it must be noted that it is not the Lord, but the name of the Lord, that comes after a long time, of which it is said in the Psalms: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord; the Lord is God, and he has shone upon us (Ps 117 [118]:26). And he himself says in the Gospel: I have come in the name of my Father, and you have not received me (John 5:43). And it is well said that he comes after a long time, since human impatience says: How long, O Lord, will you forget me unto the end? How long do you turn your face away from me? (Ps 12 [13]:1). His burning fury also comes with glory, so that him whom we despised in humility we may fear in majesty. This very thing is written in the psalms too: God manifestly will come, our God, and will not keep silence. Fire will burn in his sight, and round about him a mighty tempest (Ps 49 [50]:3, 4). For he himself says in the Gospel (Luke 12:49): I have come to cast fire upon the earth, and how I wish that it may burn! Again, in another psalm we read: The voice of the Lord cutting off the flame of fire (Ps 28 [29]:7), so that the seizing flame may consume whatever there is in the world of hay, wood and stubble. Hence God is also said to be a consuming fire (Deut 4). And as for what follows: The wrath of his fury shall devour like fire, most of our writers interpret the wrath of the Lord's fury as the devil, to whom we are handed over for punishment, who, according to the book of Samuel, moved David to number the people of God (2 Sam 24). And the Apostle says: to hand such a one over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved (1 Cor 5:5). But this fury itself, and the wrath of the Lord's fury, will do nothing by its own will, but only what it has been commanded. Hence there follows: The utterance of his lips, an utterance full of wrath.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.28":["His spirit too, like water in a valley, dragging, will come up to the neck, to make punishments overflow upon sinners. It will be divided according to the quality of their deserts, to destroy and confound the nations which vain error had led astray, so that they may understand that they have been overthrown.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.29":["Hence it is said to men of this kind, who prevailed in the power of this world and took delight in their own error, that they will not always be doing this. And some of our writers think that this passage especially, and the whole tenor of the chapter, is spoken against the heretics and all the doctrines that are contrary to the truth: that when the time of judgment has come, they shall by no means enter his holy places and his mountain, that is, the Church of the Lord, lest under the name of religion they gather riches and abandon themselves to luxury, as though celebrating the feasts of the Lord. The Jews understand these things of the nations Gog and Magog, which they think will come from the North, that is, from the regions of Scythia, of whom Ezekiel speaks more fully (Ezek 38-39).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.30":["And the Lord will make the glory of his voice heard, and will show the terror of his arm in the threatening of his fury and in the flame of devouring fire: he will dash them down with whirlwind and with hailstone. For at the voice of the Lord Assyria, struck with the rod, will tremble, and the passing of the rod will be founded, which the Lord will make to rest upon him with timbrels and with harps, and in chief battles he will overthrow them. For Topheth has been prepared from yesterday, prepared by the king, deep and wide; its fuel is fire and much wood: the breath of the Lord like a torrent of sulphur kindling it. LXX: And the Lord will make the glory of his voice heard, and will show the fury of his arm with fury and wrath: and with a devouring flame of fire he will strike with lightning vehemently, and like water and hail coming down with violence. For at the voice of the Lord the Assyrians will be overcome by the stroke with which he will strike them, and there will be round about him that from which he had the hope of help, in which he trusted; they themselves with timbrels and harp will fight against him by a change. For you will be deceived before the days: is a kingdom prepared for you too? A deep valley, wood laid, fire and the wood of many; the fury of the Lord, like a valley kindled with sulphur. I could have shown readers in passing what seems right to me according to the Hebrew; but what am I to do about the zeal of certain people, who declare that unless I also discuss the edition of the Seventy Translators, I shall have an imperfect work? I shall therefore follow the order of discussion I have begun. The voice of the Lord and his command will become known to all, and the strength of his arm will be plain to everyone, when the time of retribution has come, and it will be brought down with the greatness of flame and whirlwind and hail, and with the weight of stones. Of this, as we have said, Ezekiel writes at full length in his prophecy of Gog and Magog (Ezek 38 and 39).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.31-33":["Further, what is said according to the Seventy: At the voice of the Lord the Assyrians will be overcome by the stroke, when he has struck them, and there will be round about him that from which he had the hope of help, in which he trusted; they themselves with timbrels and harp will fight against him by a change, signifies that those who were once subject to the devil rise up against him, and fight against him with joy and gladness by a sudden change, when, understanding their error, they destroy him by whom they had been deceived. And it is said to him that from the beginning he deceived himself, thinking his kingdom everlasting, he for whom Gehenna and eternal punishments are prepared.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.31":["At the command of his voice Assyria, struck with the rod, will tremble: every impious man, every imitator of the hostile nation; not that on the day of judgment the Assyrian alone is to be struck, but that by the Assyrian we should understand the devil.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.32":["Then there follows: And the passing of the rod will be founded, which the Lord will make to rest upon him. And the sense is: the rod will not strike him and then be lifted again, and, in the manner of those who flog, make an end of beating; but he will make it remain in his punishments as if founded and fixed with a deep root. And if it is understood in this way, where will the repentance of the devil be? Especially since it is said to sinners: Go into the eternal fire, which God has prepared for the devil and his angels (Matt 25:41). With timbrels and with harps, and in chief battles, he will overthrow them: that is, the Lord will overthrow the demons and all the impious, to the joy of all.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.30.33":["For from yesterday, that is, from time past, Topheth, that is, a broad and spacious Gehenna, has been prepared by the king, the Lord, to burn them with eternal fires. Its nourishment and kindling is fire and much wood, that is, perpetual flame and the punishments of sinners. And since the prophetic word had foretold this as of a furnace and an oven of burning fire, it keeps the metaphor, so that we may know that it is kindled by the breath and spirit and will of the Lord, with sulphur mixed in, which rouses the flames, to make the torments more bitter.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.31.1-3":["These things, indeed, we can also understand of those who, in a time of persecution and of tribulation and distress, do not trust in the Lord, but in the help of the Egyptians, that is, of worldly men; not knowing that Abraham was endangered in Egypt, and that the people of God served with clay and bricks in that region, which is spiritually called the iron furnace. Hence in the Apocalypse of John too we read: The place in which the Lord was crucified is spiritually called Sodom and Egypt (Rev 11:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.31.1":["Woe to those who go down into Egypt for help, hoping in horses, and having confidence in chariots, because they are many, and in horsemen, because they are exceedingly strong, and have not trusted in the Holy One of Israel, and have not sought the Lord. But he, being wise, has brought evil, and has not taken away his words; and he will rise up against the house of the most wicked, and against the help of those who work iniquity. Egypt is man and not God, and their horses are flesh and not spirit: and the Lord will incline his hand, and the helper will collapse, and he to whom help is given will fall, and all of them together will be consumed. After the burden of the beasts of the South, and of those who carried their riches into Egypt to a people that could not profit them, who hoped for help in the strength of Pharaoh and had confidence in the shadow of Egypt, the prophetic word had spoken, under the figure of the return to Jerusalem and of the release of the captivity under Cyrus, of the consummation of the age and of the perfect blessedness of the righteous: now it returns to the same people to whom it had spoken above, that they should not go down into Egypt. And this is the difference between the present passage and the earlier one: there the tribe of Judah is commanded not to go down into Egypt; here it is prophesied that, scorning the command of the Lord, they will go down, and that by Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon Israel and the Egyptians alike, the fugitives and their helpers alike, will be slaughtered together. Of this Jeremiah speaks most fully (Jer 42). Woe, then, to those who go down into Egypt and, for fear of the Chaldeans, scorn the precepts of the Lord, hoping in idolaters, while they boast that they hold the worship of God; and, trusting in the help of horses, they do not know that it is written: A horse is a deceitful thing for safety (Ps 32 [33]:17); and they have confidence in four-horse chariots and carriages and horsemen, and do not remember that Song: Horse and rider he has cast into the sea (Exod 15:1). And they have not trusted in the Holy One of Israel, who was promising his help through Jeremiah to those who remained in the land of Judea. And they have not sought the Lord: not that they did not seek him, but that, having sought him, they scorned his words. Hence it is also said of the wicked priests: Who devour my people, they have not called upon the Lord (Ps 52 [53]:5, 6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.31.2":["But he, being wise, that is, the Lord, brought evil upon them, and the worst, namely Nebuchadnezzar; or evil in the absolute sense, signifying a thing, not a person; and he did not take away his words, which he had spoken through the prophets. Wherefore he will rise up against the tribe of Judah, the house of the most wicked, and against the help of the Egyptians, who work iniquity.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.31.3":["For Egypt, or the Egyptian, as the Seventy translated, is man and not God, and their horses are flesh and not spirit. Hence it is also written in Jeremiah: Cursed is the man who has his hope in man, and has made firm the flesh of his arm, and whose heart has departed from the Lord. And he will be like a tamarisk in the desert, which will not see when good things come (Jer 17:5, 6). And in another place it is written: Vain is the salvation of man (Ps 59 [60]:13), or, as it stands better in the Hebrew, in man. And when the Lord stretches out his hand to punish, or inclines it, under the metaphor of a charioteer, and slackens the reins of the running horses, the Egyptian helper will collapse, and Israel, to whom help was being given.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.31.4":["For thus says the Lord to me: as when a lion roars, and a lion's whelp, over its prey, and a multitude of shepherds comes against it, it will not dread their voice, nor be afraid at their multitude: so will the Lord of hosts come down to fight upon Mount Zion and upon its hill. Like birds flying, so will the Lord of hosts protect Jerusalem, protecting and delivering, passing over and saving. In plain passages we set down one edition, especially where there is no difference of sense. When the Israelites, and the Egyptians in whose help they trusted, have been slain in Egypt, the captives will return to Jerusalem in the reign of Cyrus. And by two likenesses the strength of the Lord, who had promised that he would be their helper, is shown. As a lion and a lion's whelp, when hungry, if it sees a flock of sheep, is not frightened by any voice of the shepherds, and, conscious of its own strength, despises their multitude: so the Lord of hosts will fight, not against Mount Zion and its hill, but upon Mount Zion, against its adversaries.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.31.5":["Let us state the other comparison as well: as birds, to defend their young, fly over their nests, and if they see a serpent or a man, or other birds approaching their chicks, forget their own weakness and fight with beak and claws, and redouble the grief of their breast with chattering cries: so the Lord will protect Jerusalem, and will deliver it, passing over, and will save it. For passing over, in the Hebrew it is PHASE; in the three translators, besides the Seventy, ὑπερβαίνων is set down. From this it is plain that Pascha, that is, the Phase of the Lord, signifies not passion but passage. And that the Lord, in the likeness of birds, was a protector over Jerusalem, he himself testifies in the Gospel: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone those who were sent to you (Matt 23:37): how often I wished to gather your children, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not? And in Deuteronomy it is said of the Lord: He spread his wings, and took them up, and carried them on his shoulders (Deut 32:11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.31.6-9":["Turn back, as you had withdrawn into the depth, sons of Israel. For in that day a man will cast away the idols of his silver and the idols of his gold, which your hands have made for you for sin. And Assyria will fall by a sword not of a man, and a sword not of a human being will devour him: and he will flee, not from the face of the sword, and his young men will be tributaries. And his strength will pass away from terror, and his princes will flee in fear, said the Lord, whose fire is in Zion, and his furnace in Jerusalem. LXX: Turn back, as you had withdrawn into the depth, sons of Israel. For in that day a man will cast away the idols of his silver and the idols of his gold, which your hands have made for you for sin. And Assyria will fall by a sword not of a man, and a sword not of a human being will devour him, and he will flee, not from the face of the sword; his young men will be overcome: for they will be surrounded by rock as by a rampart, and will be conquered; and whoever flees will be taken. Thus says the Lord: Blessed is he who has seed in Zion, and kinsmen in Jerusalem. While the Lord fights upon Mount Zion against its adversaries, and protects it like birds: Turn back, O sons of Israel; or, as Symmachus translated: Repent, you who by deep counsel and sin have withdrawn from the Lord. For if you do this, casting away the idols of gold and silver which were to you for sin, on account of which your city was taken, Assyria, for fear of whom you now flee, will fall, not by the sword of men, nor by the strength of an army, but by the power of God. And he means the Angel by whom one hundred and eighty-five thousand Assyrians were destroyed in one night. The Assyrian king himself will also flee, not from the sword of men, but from the wrath of God: so that his young and strong men become tributaries to the Medes, of whom it is said above, against Babylon: Behold, I will stir up the Medes against you. And the strength of the Assyrian will pass away and go by through the terror of the Lord, and all his princes will tremble. For the Lord has said it and promised it, for whom to have said is to have done; he who has fire in Zion and a furnace in Jerusalem, so that the flame going out from Jerusalem may consume his adversaries like stubble and wood. Another interprets the words: In that day a man will cast away the idols of his silver, and the rest, in such a way as to testify that after the return from the Babylonian captivity until the coming of Christ the sons of Israel never worshipped idols; and that the Assyrian too is signified in the present place as one to be overcome not by the Angel but by the Medes, because a most ancient and once powerful kingdom, after offending the Lord, was destroyed and served its conquerors. The Nazarenes understand this passage thus: O sons of Israel, who by most wicked counsel denied the Son of God, return to him and to his Apostles. For if you do this, you will cast away all the idols which were formerly to you for sin, and the devil will fall before you, not by your own strength, but by the mercy of God: and his young men, who once fought for him, will be tributaries of the Church, and all his strength and his rock will pass away: the philosophers too, and every perverse doctrine, will turn their backs at the sign of the cross. For it is the sentence of the Lord that this should come to pass, whose fire, or light, is in Zion, and his oven in Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.31.9":["Fire and light are written in Hebrew with the same letters, ALEPH, VAU, RES; if it is read UR, it means fire; if OR, light. This is said because for fire, which Symmachus alone translated, Aquila and Theodotion translated light. But what is meant by what we read in the Septuagint: They will be surrounded by rock as by a rampart, and will be conquered: and whoever flees will be taken, I am unable to know. Unless perhaps we can say this: that the young men of the Assyrian king, who are to be conquered and taken, are surrounded by rock, that is, by the strength of the Lord, as a city is surrounded by a rampart. Further, what follows: Blessed is he who has seed in Zion, and kinsmen in Jerusalem, we can interpret so as to say that it fits the sense of the present passage, in that the divine word exhorts those fleeing into Egypt to return to Jerusalem and beget sons. To this city so great a blessedness is promised that it is defended and protected by the help of the Lord; and in another place it is written of it: The glory of this latter house will be greater than that of the former (Hag 2:10). This can also be referred to the Church, the vision of peace and the watchtower, of which it is written: Glorious things are spoken of you, city of God (Ps 86 [87]:2). And elsewhere: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God (ibid. 45 [46]:3). For in her is the fire and the furnace, to devour sinners and to consume wood, hay and stubble; or the light and the oven, so that the brightness of the righteous and the punishment of sinners may be shown.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.1-5":["These things are according to the Hebrew, from which the Seventy disagree in most places not only in words but also in senses. For when the righteous king has reigned, and his princes have ruled with judgment, there will be a man hiding his words, a man who by nature is God, and he will hide his words, speaking to them in parables (Matt 20:21, 23, 24, and 25). And he will hide them as from running water, that is, from the people of the Jews, which, like water, is borne and drawn this way and that at the will of its teachers. This man, hiding his words among the unbelieving, will appear in Zion, that is, in the Church, like a glorious river running down in a thirsty land. For he will appear to the people of the Gentiles, which before suffered thirst for the truth; and the river of God will water the thirsty fields, the river of which it is written: The rush of the river makes glad the city of God (Ps 45 [46]:4). And in another place: The river of God is filled with waters (Ps 64 [65]:10). For he himself says in the Gospel: He who thirsts, let him come to me and drink. He who believes in me, as the Scripture says, out of his belly will flow rivers of living water (John 7:38). Then they will by no means have confidence in their teachers, but will offer their ears to hear the Lord; and both with heart and with tongue they will confess the peace of the Lord, forsaking the foolish princes and their ministers, who imposed silence on those who believed in Jesus.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.1":["Behold, a king will reign with righteousness, and princes will rule in judgment. And a man will be like one who is hidden from the wind and conceals himself from the tempest: like streams of waters in thirst, and the shade of a jutting rock in a desert land. The eyes of those who see will not be dimmed, and the ears of those who hear will listen diligently. And the heart of the foolish will understand knowledge, and the tongue of stammerers will speak quickly and plainly. He who is senseless will no longer be called a prince, nor will the deceitful man be called great. For the fool will speak foolish things, and his heart will work iniquity, to accomplish pretense, and to speak deceitfully to the Lord, and to empty the soul of the hungry, and to take away drink from the thirsty. The vessels of the deceitful man are most evil: for he has devised schemes to destroy the meek with the word of a lie, when the poor man spoke judgment. But the prince will think the things that are worthy of a prince, and he himself will stand over the leaders. LXX: For behold, a righteous king will reign, and princes will rule with judgment. And there will be a man hiding his words, and he will be hidden as from water that is borne along: and he will appear in Zion like a running river, glorious in a thirsty land; and they will by no means be trusting in men, but they will give their ears to hear, and the heart of the weak will attend to hearing, and the tongues of the stammering will quickly learn to speak peace: and they will no longer tell the fool to be a prince, nor will your ministers any longer say, Be silent. For the fool will speak foolish things, and his heart will understand vain things, to accomplish iniquity and to speak error to the Lord, to scatter the hungry souls and to make the thirsty souls empty. For the counsel of the most wicked will think unjust things: to slay the humble with unjust words, and to scatter the words of the humble in judgment. But the pious have thought wisdom, and this counsel abides. According to the Seventy Translators, who said: For behold, a righteous king will reign, and princes will rule with judgment, what follows must be joined to what precedes, namely to the words with which the previous section ended: Blessed is he who has seed in Zion, and kinsmen in Jerusalem. According to the Hebrew, the former prophecy, concerning those who were going down into Egypt, is finished, and now begins another oracle, namely concerning the coming of Christ and of his Apostles. For this king will reign righteously, not respecting persons in judgment, and his princes will rule with truth, considering causes, not men.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.2":["And whoever is under his shelter will be as safe in tribulations and distresses, and in the tempest of this world, as one who, fleeing wind and whirlwind, hides himself in a safe place; and as one who finds the purest springs in the desert; and as one who, in fierce heat, when the blaze of the sun scorches everything, rests under a jutting rock. Where we said: like streams of waters in thirst, the Seventy translated: and he will appear in Zion like a running river. For the Hebrew word about which we argued above, BASAION (בציון), which means in thirst, or in dryness, and in a pathless place, the Seventy and Theodotion translated in Zion, reading SION, which is written with the same letters, for SAION, that is, thirst.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.3-4":["When, therefore, Christ has reigned, and his princes have ruled in judgment, the eyes of those who believe will not be dimmed, and the ears of those who hear, which before were deaf, will attend diligently, and the heart of those once foolish will understand knowledge, and the tongue of the stammering and the mute, which could not utter Christ, will confess the Lord with full and open speech.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.5":["He who is foolish will no longer be called a prince (1 Cor 1). For God has made foolish the wisdom of this world. Nor will the deceitful and perverse teacher be called great among the peoples: namely the scribes and Pharisees, to whom the Lord said: Fools and blind, which is greater, the gold or the temple that sanctifies the gold? (Matt 23:17).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.6-7":["What follows contains nearly the same sense as the Hebrew, and can be fitted to the heretics. For these speak a lie against the Lord, and pervert the hungry and thirsty souls, and make them go away empty; so that even if they have anything good by nature, they lose it through the fault of their teachers, whose whole counsel is to destroy the humble with unjust words and to scatter the words of the humble in judgment. In which it must likewise be noted that whoever can be deceived by them is humble, and clings to the earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.6":["For the fool will speak foolish things, for which in the Hebrew it is written: NABAL NABALA IDABBER (נבל נבלה ידבר). We say this in order to explain the words of Abigail, who says of Nabal the Carmelite: According to his name he is foolish (1 Sam 25:25). For truly the fool will speak foolish things, and his heart will accomplish iniquity: he who feared where there was no fear, and practiced folly in place of wisdom, saying: If this man were from God, he would not break the sabbath (John 9:16). This the master of the synagogues does to this day, to accomplish pretense, for which in the Hebrew is written ONEPH (חנף), that is, ὑπόκρισις. Hence it is said again and again to the Pharisees: Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites (Matt 23:14). And he will speak, he says, deceitfully to the Lord: Master, we know that you are from God, and that you have no concern for men; is it lawful to give tribute to Caesar, or not? (ibid. 22:16, 17). They empty the souls of the hungry people, and take away drink from the thirsty crowds, neither entering themselves nor allowing others to enter.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.7":["For all the vessels and weapons of the deceitful teacher and prince are most evil; he contrives snares to destroy the simple with the word of a lie, saying to the deceived people: Search and see, that a prophet does not arise from Galilee (John 7:52). When the poor man spoke to them judgment: he who, though he was rich, became poor for our sake (2 Cor 8). For truly Christ spoke judgment and righteousness to them, saying: If I do not do the works of my Father, do not believe me: but if I do them, even if you will not believe me, believe the works (John 10:37).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.8":["This poor man who speaks judgment, the prince and Lord, will think the things that are worthy of a prince, saying: I came only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 15:24). And he will be anxious for the unbelieving, desiring to save those who do not believe; and he will stand over his leaders, the Apostles, to one of whom he says: But you, a man of one mind with me, my leader and my familiar friend (Ps 54 [55]:14).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.9-10":["After the calling of the nations, when Christ the king has ruled with righteousness, and his princes, the Apostles and apostolic men, have presided over the believers with judgment, the prophetic word will be directed to the wealthy women, whom we must take either as the cities of Judea, or as the synagogues of that time, or, as most think, as the matrons of the rich men of the former people of the Jews, to whom, as to women lying prostrate after their ruin, it is said: Rise; and they are nonetheless called confident daughters, or hoping; and they are commanded to hear the words of the Lord, and to remember the days and the year, of which we shall speak in what follows, as the Savior himself says: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me: he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to proclaim release to the captives and sight to the blind, to call the acceptable year of the Lord (Luke 4:18, 19); and that this very remembrance may become for them, according to the Seventy, grief with hope, so that they may lament that they denied the Lord, and may have hope of salvation if they repent. For he says that the vintage is ended, and that after the last devastation, which happened under Vespasian and Titus and Hadrian, no other captivity will come, nor will clusters remain among the peoples to be gathered afterward.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.11-12":["Hence they are summoned to lamentation, and are commanded to bare their breasts and to gird their loins, because the country once desirable, and the fruitful vine, of which it is written: I planted you a fruitful vine, wholly true: how have you turned into the bitterness of a strange vine? (Jer 2:21), has been destroyed by the ravaging Roman.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.13":["Over the ground, he says, of my people thorns and briers, or grass will come up. And the sense is: if thorns and briers have filled the land of Judea, which is the land of promise, how much more the other cities, which are full of joy, and which that saying of the Gospel will overtake: Woe to you who rejoice now, for you will weep (Luke 6:25)!\n— Commentary on Isaiah","What was said above according to the LXX: Over the land of my people thorns and grass will come up, can be referred both to the heretics and to all the simple among believers, who do not understand Holy Scripture as befits its majesty. Hence we have fitted each to each: that the land of the people of God brings forth thorns for the heretics, and grass for all the unskilled in the Church.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.14":["Wealthy women, rise and hear my voice: confident daughters, take in my speech with your ears. After days and a year you too will be troubled, you confident ones: for the vintage is ended; the gathering will come no more. Be astonished, you wealthy women; be troubled, you confident ones: strip yourselves and be confounded. For which Symmachus translated be made naked; there follows: Gird your loins, lament upon your breasts, over the desirable country, over the fruitful vine: thorns and briers will come up over the ground of my people: how much more over all the houses of joy of the exulting city? For the house has been forsaken; the multitude of the city has been left; darkness and groping have come upon the caves for ever: the joy of wild asses, pastures of flocks. Until the spirit is poured out upon us from on high: and the desert will be in Carmel, and Carmel will be counted as a forest. And judgment will dwell in the wilderness, and righteousness will sit in Carmel. And the work of righteousness will be peace, and the service of righteousness silence, and security for ever. And my people will sit in the beauty of peace, and in the tabernacles of confidence, and in wealthy rest. But hail in the descent of the forest, and the city will be brought low with lowliness. Blessed are you who sow upon all waters, sending forth the foot of the ox and the ass. The LXX, for the darkness and groping which have come upon the caves for ever, translated and your villages will be caves for ever: what in the Hebrew is called OPHEL (עפל) and BEEN (בחן), which the Hebrews think were two towers in Jerusalem, lofty and very strong, called by these names. The first of them means darkness, or cloud, because it raised its head up to the clouds; the other, proving and firmness, or, as Symmachus renders it, inquiry: because the eyes were deceived in gazing at its summit.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","For the Lord, that is, my temple, has been forsaken, as I said to the Apostles: Rise, let us go hence (John 14:31); and to the unbelieving: Your house will be left to you desolate (Luke 13:35). Palpable darkness has taken possession of the inner sanctuaries of the Temple and its secret mysteries, and they have become no longer storerooms for the vessels of the Lord, but caves for ever. For they had heard from the Lord Savior (Matt 21:13): The house of my Father shall be called a house of prayer: but you have made it a den of robbers. The joy, he says, of wild asses, pastures of flocks. This is to be understood either according to the letter, in that all things are deserted; or spiritually, in that, Israel having been driven out, savage men who have no knowledge of God dwell in Judea.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.15-16":["And this is to be until the spirit is poured out upon us from on high, which the Savior, ascending to the Father, promised to believers, saying: Behold, I go, and I will send you the Paraclete, the Spirit of truth (John 16). And again: Until you receive power from on high (Luke 24:49). And what he had said above: Yet a little while, and Lebanon will be in Carmel, and Carmel will be counted as a forest: and in that day the deaf will hear the words of the book, and out of darkness and gloom the eyes of the blind will see (above, Isa 29), he now repeats in other words: that the wilderness of the nations is turned into the riches of Israel, and Israel is counted among the nations: at which time judgment will have dwelt in the desert, the Lord and Savior, to whom the Father has given all judgment (John 3). And righteousness will rest in Carmel, of which it was said above: And the desert will be in Carmel: that judgment and righteousness dwell in the desert, and rest in Carmel, which before was called the desert.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.17-18":["The work of righteousness too will be peace, which, according to the Apostle, surpasses all understanding (Phil 4). And the service of righteousness silence, so that they may worship the Lord not with the much speaking of the Jews but with the brevity of faith; and may rest secure in eternal peace, and in their tabernacles may be the riches of which the Apostle spoke (1 Cor 1:5): I give thanks to my God through Jesus Christ, because in everything you have been made rich in him, in all speech and in all knowledge.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.18-19":["And when the Christian people sits, or dwells, in beauty, or, as the Seventy translated, in the city of peace, which is without doubt in the Church, then hail and tempest and the raging wrath of the Lord will come down upon the forest, of which it was said above: And Carmel will be counted as a forest; and the city of Jerusalem will be brought low, and, according to another Scripture, will speak from the earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.32.20":["Do we ask where we have read of the ox and the ass together? In Deuteronomy it is written: You shall not plough with a calf and an ass together (Deut 22:10). And in this same Isaiah: Blessed is he who sows upon every water, where the ox and the ass tread (Isa 32:20). Ebion ploughs with the ox and the ass together, worthy, for the lowliness of his understanding, of the poverty of his name; for he so receives the Gospel as not to abandon the ceremonies of the Jewish superstitions, which went before in shadow and image. But blessed is he who sows in the words of the Scriptures, of the old as well as of the new Instrument, and treads the waters of the letter that kills, so as to reap the fruit of the life-giving spirit.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","At the end of the section too they translated thus: Blessed are those who sow upon every water, where the ox and the ass tread.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Since these things are so, and we have learned from the prophetic oracle how great the goods are that the Church will possess, and how great the evils that Jerusalem will suffer: blessed are you, Apostles, and you other teachers, who sow upon all the waters of Holy Scripture, in which the ox and the ass tread. The ox is a clean animal, on account of the election of the fathers; the ass unclean, on account of the former idolatry of the Gentiles; so that the Church of the Lord may be gathered both from the Circumcision and from the Uncircumcision.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.1":["Woe to you who plunder: will you not yourself be plundered? And you who despise: will you not yourself be despised? When you have finished plundering, you will be plundered: when, wearied, you have ceased to scorn, you will be scorned. LXX: Woe to those who make you wretched: for no one makes you wretched. And those who reject you do not reject you: those who reject will be taken, and will be handed over, and like a moth in a garment so will they be overcome. He had spoken of the Apostles and of the teachers of the churches: Blessed are those who sow upon every water, where the ox and the ass tread: now he directs a curse at their persecutors, nay, at their prince, who possesses the hearts of the impious: that whatever they have done they may receive back, and their present power may be the material of future torments. Hence according to the LXX it is said: Woe to those who make you wretched: for no one makes you wretched. And the sense is: as far as lies in them, when they persecute you and afflict you with various tortures, they think you wretched once you have been killed and tortured. But no one can make you wretched, for they have power not over the soul, but over the body. And he who rejects you does not reject you, but him who sent you, according to what the Savior says to the disciples: He who rejects you rejects me: and he who rejects me rejects him who sent me (Luke 10:16). The persecutors, therefore, will be taken, and will be handed over to eternal fires. And as a moth consumes a garment, so will the perpetual flame consume them. This is also written at the end of this prophecy: Their worm will not die, and their fire will not be quenched.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.2-6":["O Lord, have mercy on us: for we have waited for you: be their arm in the morning, and our salvation in the time of tribulation. At the voice of the Angel the peoples fled, at your exaltation the nations were scattered. And your spoils will be gathered as the young locust is collected, as when the ditches are full of it. The Lord is magnified, for he has dwelt on high: he has filled Zion with judgment and righteousness. And there will be faith in your times: the riches of salvation are wisdom and knowledge: the fear of the Lord is itself his treasure. LXX: O Lord, have mercy on us, for we trust in you: the seed of the unbelieving has come to perdition, but our salvation is in the time of tribulation: because of the voice of fear the peoples were astonished at the fear of you, and the nations were scattered. But now your spoils will be gathered, small and great: as if someone were gathering locusts, so will they mock you: holy is God who dwells on high. Zion is filled with judgment and righteousness; they will be handed over in the law; in treasures is our salvation: wisdom has come, and discipline, and piety from the Lord; these are the treasures of righteousness. The Hebrews think that the preceding section, in which we read: Woe to you who plunder: will you not yourself be plundered? and the rest to the end, was spoken against Sennacherib king of the Assyrians: that after he had overthrown the cities of the ten tribes, which were called Israel, and had also taken the cities of Judea, except Jerusalem, he himself would be overcome, and his army would be destroyed when the Angel struck. Hence they contend that the things contained in the present section are now spoken in the person of the people giving thanks to God and saying: O Lord, have mercy on us, we have waited for you: that he himself was the arm and the strength of the people hemmed in by the siege, in the morning time, and their salvation in need and distress. For at the voice of the Angel, which Symmachus rendered just as it is written in the Hebrew, AMUN (המון), and whom the Hebrews hold to be Gabriel and to have the etymology of people, the Assyrian fled, and at the exaltation of God the nations that were with him were scattered this way and that. As they fled, the spoils were gathered by the Jews, as a multitude of young locusts and of locusts is usually gathered when they have been heaped up in ditches. In their victory the Lord was magnified, and Zion was filled with judgment and righteousness, and the faith of the besieged people was proven; and they had all their riches in wisdom, and in the knowledge of God and the fear of the Lord, who alone was their treasure. These things they may say according to the history, striving by every means to overthrow the mysteries of Christ and of his Apostles. But we, after the blessedness of the Apostles, of whom it was said above (cf. Isa 32:20): Blessed are those who sow upon every water, where the ox and the ass tread, and after the lamentation and wailing of those who persecuted them, of whom it is said in what follows: Woe to those who make you wretched; but no one makes you wretched: and as a moth in a garment, so will they be worn away, prove that these things are spoken in the person of the same Apostles and of all believers. And the sense is: Lord, we have hoped in your help, and we trust in you: the seed of the unbelieving has perished for ever, and our salvation has appeared in the time of tribulation. For when you gave help, the whole multitude of the nations was scattered, and became a prey and a mockery to the victors. And this came about because the holy Lord dwells on high, and Zion is filled with judgment and righteousness, of which it was written above: Judgment and righteousness will rest in the desert. This, then, is Zion, which before was called the desert; and whoever dwells in her, to him will be handed over the law of the Gospel, in whose treasure is our salvation, and the wisdom of believers, and discipline and piety, or faith, which properly belongs to Christians, and the fear of the Lord, in which the treasures of wisdom are contained.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.7-8":["Behold, those who see will cry outside: the Angels of peace will weep bitterly. The ways are laid waste, the one who passed along the path has ceased, the covenant has been made void, he has cast off the cities, he has not regarded men. The earth has mourned and languished, Lebanon is confounded and has become deaf, and Sharon has become like a desert, and Bashan and Carmel are shaken. Now I will rise up, says the Lord: now I will be exalted, now I will be lifted up. You will conceive burning heat, you will bring forth stubble; your spirit, like fire, will devour you. And the peoples will be like ashes from a burning: thorns gathered together will be burned with fire. The Hebrew word ARELLAM (אראלם), for which Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated I will appear to them, dividing the last syllable and reading ARE LAHEM (ארא להם), the Hebrews think signifies Angels, and that this is a prophecy, after the calling of the nations and the riches of the Church, of the overthrow of the Temple: that the Angels will weep, no longer dwelling within, but going outside; and those who before were messengers of peace will weep bitterly. Or the Apostles themselves, who had been sent to announce peace to Jerusalem, to which the Lord said: If you too had known the things that belong to your peace (Luke 19:42), and to whom the Lord appeared, to strengthen them by his sight, will weep for Jerusalem, because it did not receive their preaching, because the ways are laid waste, and the one who passed along the righteous path has ceased, according to what is said in the Lamentations of Jeremiah: The ways of Zion mourn, because there are none who come to the solemnity (Lam 1:4). The covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob has also been made void: He has cast off the cities of Judea: he has not regarded men, who by their own fault chose to be beasts.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.9":["The earth has mourned and languished for those who dwelt in the land (Gen 15 and 22). Lebanon is confounded and has become deaf: without doubt it signifies the Temple, of which we read in Zechariah: Open your gates, O Lebanon, that fire may devour your cedars (Zech 11:1). Or Jerusalem, which in Ezekiel is called Lebanon, when the Prophet says: A great eagle with great wings, which has the design of entering into Lebanon (Ezek 17:3); which the divine word afterward interprets: when, it says, Nebuchadnezzar came to Jerusalem; plainly explaining the Eagle as the king of Babylon, and Lebanon as Jerusalem. Sharon has become like a desert, and Bashan and Carmel are shaken. For Bashan, the LXX translated Galilee, the province in place of one place of the province. Sharon, moreover, is the name of the whole region around Joppa and Lydda, in which very broad and fertile plains stretch out. Bashan too is a region beyond the Jordan, which the two and a half tribes possessed, and which means most fruitful and fat (Deut 3); and Carmel, of which we spoke above. The once fertile places of Judea, then, will be turned into a wilderness, by which it is shown μεταφορικῶς that all the opulence of the Jews is to be exchanged for want and poverty. Since, then, they would not receive the word of the Apostles, Lebanon is confounded, and Sharon is turned into a wilderness, and Bashan and Carmel are shaken.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.10":["Therefore the Lord says that he will rise up, either from his very great patience, or from the underworld, and will be exalted among the nations, and lifted up on the cross.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.11":["And he turns in apostrophe to the Jews themselves: You will conceive burning heat, and you will bring forth stubble, and your spirit will kindle a fire for you, so that the devouring flame may consume what was conceived and brought forth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.12":["And the peoples of the Jews, he says, will be like ashes from a burning, showing the greatness of their misfortune by the remnants of the embers. For their thorns, which have been gathered together by the multitude of their sins, will be devoured by the burning; which most refer to the last captivity and overthrow of Jerusalem. Others affirm that it will come about more fully and perfectly at the time of judgment.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.13":["Hear, you who are far off, what I have done, and you who are near, know my strength. The sinners in Zion are terrified, trembling has seized the hypocrites. Who of you can dwell with devouring fire? Who of you will dwell with everlasting burnings? He who walks in acts of righteousness, and speaks truths, who casts away avarice from calumny, and shakes his hands free of every bribe, who stops his ears lest he hear blood, and shuts his eyes lest he see evil. He will dwell on high; fortresses of rocks are his height: bread has been given him, his waters are faithful. His eyes will see the king in his beauty: they will behold the land from afar. Your heart will meditate fear. Where is the scribe? Where is he who weighs the words of the law? Where is the teacher of little ones? You will not see the imprudent people, the people of deep speech, so that you cannot understand the eloquence of its tongue, in which there is no wisdom. LXX: Those who are far off will hear what they have done: those who draw near will know my strength. The lawless who were in Zion have departed; trembling will seize the impious. Who will announce to you that fire is burning? Who will announce to you the eternal place? He who walks in righteousness, and speaks the right way, and hates impiety and iniquity, and shakes his hands free of gifts, and stops his ears so as not to hear the judgment of blood, and shuts his eyes so that they may not see iniquity, he will dwell in a lofty cave of a very strong rock. Bread will be given him, and his water will be faithful: your eyes will see the king with glory, they will see the land from afar; your soul will meditate fear. Where are the grammarians? Where are the counselors? Where is he who numbers those who nourish both the small and the great people? To whom he did not give counsel: nor did he know his deep voice, so that the despised people did not hear, and there is no understanding for the one who hears. Since the ways are laid waste, the one who passed along the paths has ceased, the covenant with the people of the Jews has been made void, God has cast off their cities and has not counted them among men; therefore you who are far off, hear what I have done, and draw near, that you may know my strength.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.14":["The sinners, or the impious, who were in Zion are terrified, or have departed, and trembling has seized the hypocrites, to whom the Lord said in the Gospel: Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites (Matt 23:14)! Who of you can dwell with him who is a devouring fire? Who will dwell with everlasting burnings? Or according to the LXX: Who will announce to you that the eternal fire is burning, which God has prepared for the devil and his angels?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.15":["Who, then, will be able to dwell with God, or to announce the punishments to come? Everyone who is such as the prophetic word describes: who walks in many acts of righteousness, and loves acts of righteousness not once but always, and speaks truths not on one occasion but continually; who casts away avarice, which is the mother of calumny. For money is not heaped up for one man except with another's loss and harm. And he shakes his hands free of every bribe; for bribes blind the eyes even of the wise. He stops his ears lest he hear the judgment of blood. All iniquity and oppression and injustice is a judgment of blood: and though one does not kill with the sword, yet he slays by his will. And he shuts his eyes lest he see evil. Happy the conscience that neither hears nor beholds evil.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.16":["He, then, who is such will dwell on high, that is, in the kingdom of heaven, or in a lofty cave of a very strong rock, in Christ Jesus: the rock that followed the people of Israel, that they might drink from it and be protected by its strength. Bread will be given him, and his waters are faithful: which the fables of the Gentiles understand as ambrosia and nectar. But we shall interpret the bread and the most faithful waters as the law of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.17":["He, then, who is such will behold Christ the king in his brightness, of whom it is said above (cf. Isa 32:1): Behold, a righteous king will reign, and princes will rule in judgment. And his eyes will see from afar the land which they had long desired, the land of the meek and gentle, of which it is written in the Gospel: Blessed are the meek, for they will possess the land (Matt 5:4); or, set in heaven, they will look down on earthly things.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.18":["Your heart will meditate fear: so that when you have attained all things, you may say with the Apostle: I who am not worthy to be called an Apostle, because I persecuted the Church of God (1 Cor 15:9). For the beginning of wisdom is the fear of the Lord. Where is the scribe? Where is he who weighs the words of the law? Where is the teacher of little ones? (Prov 9:1). Where, he says, are the scribes and Pharisees, who, weighing the words of the Law in the balance, deceived the unhappy people? whom the Scripture now calls little ones: little in sense, little in understanding.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.19":["And when the eyes of the believer have beheld the king in his majesty, and his heart has meditated fear, then he will not see the imprudent people, the people of the Jews, or of the philosophers and orators of the world, who applaud themselves for secular learning and eloquence, of whom he now says: A people of deep speech, so that you cannot understand the eloquence of its tongue; whose whole adornment is in words, who have only the leaves and the shade of speech, and do not possess the fruit of truth. Then there follows: In which there is no wisdom; of whom it is also said in another place: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the prudent I will reject (1 Cor 1:19). Why? Because God has made foolish the wisdom of this world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.20-22":["Look upon Zion, the city of our solemnity: your eyes will see Jerusalem, a wealthy habitation: a tabernacle that can by no means be moved, nor will its pegs be taken away for ever, and none of its cords will be broken. For only there is our Lord magnificent: a place of rivers, streams very broad and open; no ship with oars will pass through it, nor will a great trireme cross it. For the Lord is our judge, the Lord is our lawgiver, the Lord is our king; he himself will save us. Your cords are loosened, and will not hold: so will your mast be, that you cannot spread out the ensign. Then the spoils of many preys will be divided: the lame will seize the plunder. Nor will the neighbor say, I am faint: the people that dwells in it, iniquity will be taken away from it. LXX: Behold Zion, the city, our salvation: your eyes will see Jerusalem, a rich city, tabernacles that will not be moved: nor will the stakes of its tabernacle be taken away for ever, and its cords will not be torn apart: for the name of the Lord is great to you: your place will be rivers and streams very broad and spacious: no ship driven by oars will go in through it: for my God is great: the Lord will not pass me by, our judge, the Lord our prince, the Lord our king, the Lord himself will save us. Your cords are broken because they did not hold: your mast is bent, so that it lets the sails go: it will not raise the ensign until it is given over to devastation. Many lame, therefore, will take spoils: and they will not say, I shall labor; the people that dwells in them, their sin will be forgiven them. O righteous one, to whom it was said above: your heart will meditate fear; and, the imprudent people you will not see; and who had heard before, your eyes will see the king in his beauty: look upon Zion, the city of our solemnity, see the Church of Christ, in which is the true solemnity: your eyes will behold the vision of peace, and riches beyond hope, which eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor have they entered into the heart of man, and the tabernacle that can by no means be moved. For the former tabernacle, which the people of the Jews had, was moved and taken away. Nor will its pegs be moved for ever, and all its cords will be firm; so that the Lord dwells in it, which is the place of all the rivers and running streams, through which no one of the opposing side will be able to sail: nor a great trireme, which is interpreted as the devil, will be able to cross it: because the Lord himself is our judge, and prince, and king, and Savior, and under his protection we shall fear the snares of no one. These things are said of Zion, the city of our solemnity, and of Jerusalem, the richest habitation, which the Lord himself will surround and fortify with his rivers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.33.23-24":["But now he speaks to the earthly Jerusalem: that the cords of its tabernacle are loosened, and could not hold up the tent, and its mast, on which the most beautiful sails once hung, has fallen with its ropes broken, so that it is useless not only for sailing but also for an ensign. And after their ship has been broken up and the tabernacle scattered, so that the cords are torn apart on both sides, the plunder will be divided among the victors: who will be so weak in their own strength that they are called lame; and nonetheless, since the wrath of God gives them strength, they will feel no weakness, nor will they say: I have labored. For whoever is among them, iniquity and sin will be taken away from him, because he has carried out the will of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.1":["Come near, you nations, and hear, and you peoples, attend: let the earth hear, and its fullness, the world and all its offspring. For the indignation of the Lord is upon all the nations, and his fury upon all their host: he has slain them and given them over to slaughter. Their slain will be cast out, and from their corpses a stench will rise: the mountains will melt with their blood. And all the host of the heavens will waste away: the heavens will be rolled back (or folded together) like a book: and all their host will fall away, as a leaf falls from the vine and from the fig tree, for my sword has been made drunk in heaven. Behold, it will come down upon Idumea, and upon the people of my slaughter, to judgment. The sword of the Lord is filled with blood, it is fattened with fat: with the blood of lambs and goats, with the blood of rams full of marrow; for the Lord has a victim in Bozrah, and a great slaughter in the land of Edom. And the unicorns will come down with them, and the bulls with the mighty. Their land will be made drunk with their blood, and their ground with the fat of the fat ones. After the overthrow of Jerusalem and the shipwreck of the once most solid ship, and the plundering of all its furnishings, it speaks of the destruction of all the nations, and of the consummation of the world, which is on the future day of judgment. Hence all the nations and peoples, the earth and its fullness, the world and all its offspring, both the places that are inhabited and those that are uninhabitable through excess of cold and of heat, are commanded to hear, and to learn with all trembling of mind what is to come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.2-4":["For the indignation of the Lord is described as coming not upon the one nation of Judea alone, and upon the Assyrians and Chaldeans, the Egyptians, the Moabites and the Ammonites and the Philistines, but against all the nations, and upon all their host, or, as the LXX translated, their number, to slay them and to make the stench of rotting bodies rise on high, signifying the sins of all the nations; so that the mountains are filled with their gore and filth and blood: all the lofty powers and the Angels who presided over the several nations; and all the host or strength of the heavens wastes away: the heavens are rolled back, or folded together, like a book, and all their host and army, and, as the LXX translated, the stars will fall away, in the likeness of leaves which, as the cold draws near, fall withered and shrivelled from the vine and the fig tree. Which indeed the Savior also says in the Gospel: The stars will fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens will be moved, and then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven (Matt 24:29, 30). And this must be considered, that he does not say that the heavens perish, but that they are rolled back, or folded together, like a book: so that after all sins have been laid open and read through, the heavens which before were open are folded together, so that the offenses of many are no longer written in them. Of books of this kind Daniel speaks in his volume: The judgment sat, and the books were opened (Dan 7:10), in which the works of each had been written down. Most think that the stars fall according to the Apocalypse of John (Rev 6 and 8), and according to what is written elsewhere: All the stars, burning, will be dissolved, and heaven and earth will pass away (Luke 10:2). For the form of this world passes away. Some think that this is said of the stars that glitter in the heaven, so that the whole is shown in the part: namely, that through the ruin of the stars the destruction of the heavens too is shown (1 Cor 7). Others, however, think that those stars will fall of which the Apostle Paul also writes: Our struggle is not against blood and flesh, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of this darkness, against the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places (Eph 6:12). Nor is it surprising if the demons who dwell in the air are called heavenly, since the Scripture also names the birds of heaven, which certainly fly not in heaven but in the air. For Satan too transforms himself into an Angel of light (2 Cor 11), pretending to be a star. And the Savior saw him falling like lightning from heaven (Luke 10). And tropologically it is said of him as of a great star: How has Lucifer fallen, who rose in the morning? (Isa 14:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.5":["All these things will come about because his sword has been made drunk in heaven: the punishment and sentence and vengeance upon sinners which is pronounced by the Lord. This sword and blade is sharpened against the impious in Ezekiel (Ezek 21), and, when very many have been slain, is commanded to go back into its sheath. And when it has been made drunk and filled in heaven, that is, in the air, which by the usage of the Scriptures is called heaven, then it will come down to Idumea too, that is, to earthly things: so that after the punishment of the demons the souls of men too may be judged. For Idumea in our language means earthly.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And because God did not spare the angels who sinned, who by their own fault lost their heavenly dwelling, therefore he himself says through Isaiah: My sword has been made drunk in heaven.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ISA.34.6-7":["And it is filled with blood and fattened with the fat of lambs and goats, and of rams full of marrow, and of bulls: so as to signify that princes and people alike are to be punished. For the Lord has a victim in Bozrah, and a great slaughter in the land of Edom. Of this Bozrah and Idumea the same Prophet also testifies in what follows (cf. Isa 63:1), saying: Who is this who comes from Edom, with his garments red from Bozrah? And some think that, because Bosor means flesh, the torments of all in the flesh are shown by the Lord's victim in Bozrah: they fall into a pious error. For in the present place it is written not with the letter SIN, which is set in BOSOR (בשר), that is, flesh, but with SADE, and it is called BOSRA (בצרה), which according to Joshua and Jeremiah is found not in Edom, that is, Idumea, but in the land of Moab (Jer 48). And Bosra in our language means fortified and surrounded, or strengthened: to teach that the world has been made firm by the will of the Lord, according to what is sung in the psalm: He has founded it upon the seas, and established it upon the rivers (Ps 23 [24]:2); and of the firmness of the earth it is said in the person of God: I have made firm its pillars (Ps 74 [75]:3). And when the Lord's victim has been in Bozrah, and his slaughter in Idumea, the unicorns will come down with the mighty bulls, namely the kings and princes of the earth; and all things will be filled with slaughter and blood, and with the fat of those once rich and mighty. By these words, which, after the manner of human speech, strike terror into the hearers, torments are shown against all princes and mighty men, and against peoples and small ones. By Bosra, that is, fortified and strengthened, and by Edom, or Dumah, and Idumea, the teachers of the Jews want Rome to be understood, and all the things contained in the following section to be said of it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.8-17":["What we have said is prophesied of Jerusalem, and what the Jews suspect is said of the Roman empire, some refer to the whole world, so as not to seem to disagree with what precedes.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.8":["For it is the day of the Lord's vengeance, the year of the recompenses of the judgment of Zion. And its torrents will be turned into pitch, and its ground into sulphur: and its land will become burning pitch. Night and day it will not be quenched, for ever: its smoke will go up: from generation to generation it will be desolate; for ages of ages there will be none passing through it. And the pelican and the hedgehog will possess it, and the ibis and the raven will dwell in it, and the measuring line will be stretched over it, to bring it to nothing, and the plummet unto desolation. Its nobles will not be there: they will rather call upon the king, and all its princes will be as nothing. And thorns will spring up in its house and nettles, and the thornbush in its fortresses: and it will be the lair of dragons and the pasture of ostriches. And demons will meet with onocentaurs, and the hairy one will cry out one to another: there will lie down the lamia, and will find rest for herself. There the hedgehog has had its hole, and has reared its young, and has dug round about, and cherished them in its shade: thither the kites have gathered, one to another. Search diligently in the book of the Lord, and read: not one of them was missing, one did not seek the other: for what proceeds from my mouth, he has commanded, and his spirit, he himself has gathered them. And he himself has cast the lot for them and his hand has divided it to them by measure; they will possess it for ever: from generation to generation they will dwell in it. The Hebrews, as we said above, contend that these things were prophesied of the Roman empire, and that the devastation of a once most powerful kingdom is foretold in vengeance for Zion; and most of our writers think that this, according to the letter, is also written in the Apocalypse of John. But we, from what is said, the day of the Lord's vengeance, the year of the recompenses of the judgment of Zion, judge this to be the year of which the Savior himself speaks: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to proclaim release to the captives, and to the blind that they may see, to call the acceptable year of the Lord, and the day of recompense for our God: to comfort all who mourn, and to give glory to those who lament for Zion (Luke 4:18, 19; Isa 61:1-3); and of which it was said above: Wealthy women, rise (Isa 32:9), and remember the days of the year in grief with hope;\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.9-11":["and therefore we judge that after the general consummation of the whole world the Scripture returns to Jerusalem, to which it was speaking at that time, and describes its devastation at full length: namely, that after the time of the Roman siege has come, all things will be filled with pitch and sulphur and burning flames, and its smoke will remain for ever, and the pelican and the hedgehog and the ibis and the raven will dwell there, animals which are accustomed to dwell in desert places; and that this will come about because the line and the plummet of the Lord, that is, his sentence, cannot be changed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.11-14":["But those who follow tropology affirm that, once the people of the Jews has been driven out, idolaters and those who serve various superstitions will dwell in Jerusalem under the names of beasts and monsters; and that these are the pelicans and hedgehogs, the ibis and the raven, the dragons and ostriches, and the onocentaurs, and the demons, and the hairy ones, and the lamia, which in Hebrew is called LILITH (לילית), and was translated lamia by Symmachus alone, and which some of the Hebrews suspect to be ἐριννὴν, that is, a fury. And indeed, if we consider that colonies brought from various nations were settled in Jerusalem, and that according to the rite of their own provinces the several households worshipped the monstrous forms of their own demons, we shall affirm that all these dwelt in Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.12-13":["Its nobles, that is, the Apostles and the believers, will not be there, nor will they be joined to the number of the lost, but will rather call upon Christ the king. But all its princes, namely the scribes and Pharisees, will be brought to nothing, and in the once most adorned houses thorns and nettles and the thornbush will spring up. And it will be the lair of dragons and the pasture of ostriches, which are themselves also signs of utter desolation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.14":["And there will meet one another in it, according to the LXX, various phantoms of demons, or, as all the others translated according to the Hebrew, SIIM (ציים) and IIM (איים), onocentaurs and hairy ones and the lamia, which the fables of the Gentiles and the fictions of the poets describe.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.15-17":["And what the LXX translated: There the stags met one another, and saw one another's faces: they passed by in number, not one of them perished, nor did one seek the other. For the Lord commanded them, and his spirit gathered them: and he himself gave them lots, and his hand will divide for them, that they may feed for ever, and possess it from generation to generation, and rest in it: this we shall interpret allegorically, to teach that the stags, that is, the Apostles and all holy teachers, of whom it is written: As the stag longs for the springs of waters, so my soul longs for you, O God (Ps 41 [42]:1); and elsewhere: The voice of the Lord perfecting the stags (Ps 28 [29]:9); and again: Let the stag of friendship and the foal of your graces speak with you (Prov 5); and in Job: But you have watched the months of the stags, and you will bring forth their young (Job 39:2); and in the Song of Songs it is said: My kinsman is like a roe, or a young stag upon the mountains of spices (Song 2:9); met one another in Jerusalem and saw each other's faces, and passed through it and left it, and went on to various provinces, because the Lord commanded them: Go and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit (Matt 28:19); and that his spirit gathered them, and gave them lots and divided them, so that one went to the Indians, another to the Spains, another to Illyricum, another to Greece, and each rested in the province of his own Gospel and teaching.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.15":["The hedgehog too rears its young there, and has a most secure lodging: thither the kites are gathered, a most rapacious bird, which in Hebrew is called DAJOTH (דיות), or, as the LXX translated, stags, of which we shall speak below.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.34.16-17":["Among these things the Prophet speaks to his hearers: O men who hear me speaking, what I announce as to come will all be fulfilled in deed. For they are written in the book of the Lord, and his sentence is fixed, and not even one will be void. For what proceeds from my mouth, he has commanded; that is, I indeed speak, but the words are the Lord's, and by his spirit whatever has been said will be accomplished. By his decree and measure each thing will be fulfilled, and they will not abandon their order unto everlasting generations. Let these things be said according to the Hebrew and the historical explanation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.1":["The desert and the pathless land will be glad, and the wilderness will exult, and will flower like the lily. Budding, it will bud forth, and will exult, joyful and praising. The glory of Lebanon has been given to it, the beauty of Carmel and Sharon. They will see the glory of the Lord, and the beauty of our God. LXX: Rejoice, thirsty desert, and let the wilderness exult, and let it flower like the lily. The deserts of the Jordan will flower and exult: the glory of Lebanon has been given to it, and the honor of Carmel: and my people will see the glory of the Lord, and the height of our God. Since Jerusalem has been turned into pitch, and its everlasting smoke has gone up, and the pelican and the hedgehog, the ibis and the raven, dragons and ostriches, demons and onocentaurs, the lamia and the hairy ones dwell in it, and the sentence of the Lord has been fulfilled upon it: Your house will be left to you desolate (Luke 13:35): therefore she who before had been deserted, of whom it is said in the psalm: The voice of the Lord shaking the wilderness, and the Lord will shake the desert of Kadesh. The voice of the Lord preparing the stags; and he will lay bare the thickets of the forests (Ps 28 [29]:8, 9), will be changed into an abundance of all things; and, as the stags fight, the beasts which formerly possessed all the forests of the nations will be driven out of them, so that what is said in this same Prophet may be fulfilled: Rejoice, barren one, who do not bear: break forth and cry, you who do not travail; for more are the children of the desolate than of her who has a husband (Isa 54:1). She who before was thirsty, or pathless, not having life-giving waters, and through whom the Lord did not walk, will now flower into a lily, or, as Aquila expressed it more significantly, καλύκωσις, which we can call a swelling rose whose petals have not yet opened. And she will flower, so as to declare with the Apostle: We are the good fragrance of Christ in every place (2 Cor 2:15); and that saying of the Song of Songs: Flowers have appeared in the land: the mandrakes have given their fragrance (Song 7:12, 13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.2":["And what is set down in the LXX, and the deserts of the Jordan will exult, is indeed not found in the Hebrew; but we can say that by the river Jordan John's baptism of repentance is shown, which the Lord sealed and confirmed by his own washing. And since this is said figuratively of the wilderness, which refers to the nations, and in which John was, the Jordan can fittingly be joined to it, so that through the desert of the nations we may come to the baptism of the Savior. And what follows, the glory of Lebanon has been given to it, and the beauty of Carmel and Sharon, we must take according to the earlier explanation, in which we said that by Lebanon the Temple or Jerusalem is understood, as Zechariah says: Open your gates, O Lebanon (Zech 11:1); and Ezekiel: A great eagle with great wings, full of talons, which has the design of entering into Lebanon and Carmel (Ezek 17:3); and that Carmel signifies the former people, of which it was said above: And the desert will be in Carmel, and Carmel will be counted as a forest (Isa 32:15); and that Sharon has the same meaning, since the Scripture says: Sharon has become like a desert. All the brightness, then, the worship of God, and the knowledge of circumcision, and the most fruitful and level places, which are called Sharon, for which Symmachus translated plains, will be given to the once deserted Church, and its inhabitants will see the glory of the Lord, and the beauty, or height, of our God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.3-4":["Strengthen the slackened hands, and make firm the feeble knees. Say to the faint-hearted (or to the fearful), Be strengthened, and do not fear; behold, your God will bring the vengeance of recompense: God himself will come and will save you (or, as the LXX translated, us). Then the eyes of the blind will be opened, and the ears of the deaf will be unstopped (or will hear). Then the lame man will leap like a stag, and the tongue of the dumb will be opened, for waters have broken out in the desert, and torrents in the wilderness, and what was dry ground will become a pool (or marshes), and the thirsty land springs of water. In the lairs in which dragons dwelt before, the green of reed and rush will spring up (for which the LXX translated: There will be the gladness of birds, and folds of flocks). And there will be a path there, and a way (or a clean way), and it will be called the holy way; the polluted will not pass through it. And this will be for us a straight way, so that fools may not go astray in it (or, as the LXX translated, nor will there be an unclean way there, but those who have been scattered will pass through it, and will not go astray). There will be no lion there, he says, and no evil beast will go up by it, nor will one be found there, but those who have been set free will walk in it. And the redeemed of the Lord will return, and will come to Zion with praise, and everlasting gladness will be upon their head. They will obtain joy and gladness, and sorrow and groaning will flee away. We have mingled both editions, lest, by setting out each one, the size of the books be stretched, which already exceeds the measure of brevity. The Apostles, of whom it was said above: They will see the glory of the Lord and the beauty of our God, are commanded to strengthen the hands that had grown slack among the nations, and to make firm the feeble knees; so that those who before could not do the work of God with slackened hands, and had a withered right hand, may stretch it out to good works; and those who long limped among idols with various errors may walk with firm step upon the truth; and that, strengthening the faint-hearted or timid by faith in the Lord, they may make them not fear, and the fear of the one God may drive out the fears of all error. And the cause of their security and constancy is that Christ is to come, to whom the Father has given all judgment, and he will render to each according to his works (John 5). He himself will come and will save you, you to whom it is said, Do not fear; or us, so that the Apostles may say that salvation is common to them with the believers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.5-6":["Then the eyes of the blind will be opened, and the ears of the deaf will be unstopped. Then the lame man will leap like a stag, and the tongue of the dumb will be opened. And although this was fulfilled by the greatness of signs, when the Lord said to the disciples of John who had been sent to him: Go and report to John what you have heard and seen: the blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, the deaf hear, the dead rise again, the poor have the gospel preached to them (Luke 7:22); yet it is fulfilled daily among the nations, when those who before were blind, and stumbled against wood and stones, behold the light of truth. And those who with deaf ears could not hear the words of the Scriptures now rejoice at the precepts of God; when those who before were lame, and did not keep the right way, leap like stags, imitating their teachers; and the tongue of the dumb is opened, whose mouths Satan had shut, so that they could not confess the one Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.6-7":["And for this reason the eyes will be opened, the ears will hear, the lame will leap, and the tongue of the dumb will be opened: because in the once deserted Church the waters of saving baptism have broken out, or burst forth, and torrents and streams in the wilderness, namely the various spiritual graces; and what was dry ground has been turned into a pool and marshes, so that it is not only free from the burning of thirst, but is navigable and well watered, and has very many springs, which the stag longs for, and of which whoever has drunk can bless the Lord, according to what is written: Bless the Lord from the springs of Israel (Ps 67 [68]:27).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.7":["In the lairs of the Gentile souls, in which dragons dwelt before, there will be the reed and the rush, with which the faith of the Lord may be written, and on which limbs once weary may rest; or there will be the gladness of birds, and folds of flocks: so that they may take the wings of a dove, and, leaving lowly things, hasten to the heights, and be able to say with the Psalmist: The Lord shepherds me, and I shall lack nothing: in a place of pasture, there he has set me: beside the waters of refreshment he has brought me up.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.8":["There will be a path there, and a most clean way, which will be called holy, and which says of itself: I am the way (John 14:6), through which he who is polluted will not be able to pass. Hence it is also said in the psalm: Blessed are the undefiled in the way (Ps 118 [119]:1). And this way, that is, our God, will be for us so straight and level and plain that it has no error: and fools and the senseless may be able to walk in it, to whom Wisdom speaks in Proverbs: If anyone is a little one, let him come to me. And to the unwise she said: Come, eat my bread, and drink the wine which I have mixed for you. Leave childishness, and live, and walk in the ways of prudence (Prov 9:4-6). For God has chosen the foolish things of the world (1 Cor 1): the prince of which fools speaks in the psalm: O God, you know my foolishness. And the foolishness of God is wiser than men (Ps 68 [69]:6). Hence the LXX translated: And those who had been scattered (1 Cor 2), and separated from the fellowship of the Lord, will by no means go astray.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.9-10":["There follows: There will be no lion there: our adversary the devil, who goes about roaring, seeking how he may enter the sheepfold of the Lord (1 Pet 5). And the evil beasts, his attendants, will not go up by it. For the track of a serpent cannot be found upon a rock. But those will walk in it who have been set free from the chains of sins, and redeemed by the blood of the Savior, and have repented, and have come to Zion, of which we have often spoken: You have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem (Heb 12:22); lest after the Jewish fashion we seek a golden Zion and a jewelled Jerusalem, which, according to the prophecy of Daniel, has been dissolved into everlasting ashes (Dan 9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.35.10":["And the everlasting gladness of those who praise the Lord will be upon their head, so that after they have conquered the world they may be able to say with the Apostle and with the Prophet: I have finished the course, I have kept the faith; for the rest, there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness (2 Tim 4:7, 8); and, O Lord, as with the shield of your good will you have crowned us (Ps 5:13). Then, as joy and gladness take their place, sorrow and groaning will flee away, when he comes out of Zion who will deliver. All these things we, following the apostle Paul, interpret of the first coming of the Savior: but the Jews, and our Judaizers, refer them to the second, on the strength of a single little verse, They will return and come to Zion with praise, longing for the blood of victims, and the servitude of all the nations, and the beauty of wives.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.36.1-4":["And it came to pass in the fourteenth year of king Hezekiah, Sennacherib king of the Assyrians came up against all the fortified cities of Judah and took them. And the king of the Assyrians sent Rabshakeh from Lachish to Jerusalem to king Hezekiah with a heavy hand, and he stood at the aqueduct of the upper pool, in the way of the fuller's field. And there went out to him Eliakim son of Hilkiah, who was over the house, and Shebna the scribe, and Joah son of Asaph, the recorder; and Rabshakeh said to them: Say to Hezekiah: Thus says the great king, the king of the Assyrians: What is this confidence in which you trust? Or with what counsel and strength are you planning to rebel? In whom do you have confidence, that you have withdrawn from me? Behold, you are trusting in this broken staff of reed, in Egypt: on which if a man leans, it will enter into his hand and pierce it: so is Pharaoh king of Egypt to all who trust in him. But if you answer me, We trust in the Lord our God, is it not he whose high places and altars Hezekiah has taken away, and has said to Judah and Jerusalem: You shall worship before this altar? And now deliver yourself to my lord the king of the Assyrians, and I will give you two thousand horses, and you will not be able on your part to furnish riders for them. And how will you withstand the face of the judge of a single place, one of the lesser servants of my lord? But if you trust in Egypt, and in chariots, and in horsemen: and now, have I come up without the Lord against this land to destroy it? The Lord said to me: Go up against this land, and destroy it. The history is plain and needs no interpretation; and these same things are related, written more fully, in the volumes of Kings and of Chronicles: that in the third year of Hoshea son of Elah, king of Israel, Hezekiah son of Ahaz king of Judah began to reign. He was twenty-five years old, it says, when he began to reign, and he reigned twenty-nine years in Jerusalem, and he did what was good before the Lord, according to all that David his father had done. And a little after: He hoped in the Lord God of Israel, and after him there was none like him among all the kings of Judah, nor among those who were before him: he clung to the Lord and did not depart from his footsteps, and he did his commandments which the Lord had commanded Moses: whence also the Lord was with him, and in all things to which he went forth he conducted himself wisely (2 Kgs 18:2, 7). And he rebelled against the king of the Assyrians and did not serve him: in the sixth year of whose reign Shalmaneser king of the Assyrians took Samaria and the ten tribes which were called Israel, and carried them away to the Assyrians, and settled them in Halah and in Habor, the rivers of Gozan, in the cities of the Medes (2 Kgs 17 and 18). And after seven years, that is, in the fourteenth year of Hezekiah, Sennacherib king of the Assyrians entered Judea and besieged its fortified cities, wishing to take them. And when he was besieging Lachish, Hezekiah sent messengers to him, saying: I have sinned; withdraw from me, and whatever you lay upon me I will bear. And when he had paid three hundred talents of silver and thirty of gold at the king's command, having broken off the doors of the Temple of the Lord and stripped off its plates, which he himself had fastened on, the king of the Assyrians sent Tartan and Rabshakeh from Lachish to king Hezekiah with a strong hand to Jerusalem: and when they had gone up, they came to Jerusalem and stood by the aqueduct of the upper pool, which is in the way of the fuller's field, and called the king to them. And there went out to them Eliakim son of Hilkiah, the overseer of the house, and Shebna the scribe, and Joah son of Asaph, the recorder; and Rabshakeh said to them: Speak to Hezekiah: Thus said the great king, the king of the Assyrians, and the rest that is contained in the history. In this the arrogance of Rabshakeh is to be considered: that, like some opposing power, he imitates the custom of the Prophets, so that what they are accustomed to put in their prologues, Thus says the Lord, to show the authority and greatness of the speaker, this man now said: Thus says the great king, the king of the Assyrians. And there went out to him Eliakim son of Hilkiah, who was overseer of the house, and Shebna the scribe, and Joah son of Asaph, the recorder. This is the Eliakim son of Hilkiah of whom we read above, in the Vision of the valley of Zion (cf. Isa 22:20, 21): I will call my servant Eliakim son of Hilkiah, and I will clothe him with your tunic, and strengthen him with your girdle, and give your power into his hand, and he shall be as a father to the inhabitants of Jerusalem and to the house of Judah, and the rest. Now these things are said to Shebna, who before him was overseer of the Temple, of whom it is written in the same vision: Go and enter in to him who dwells in the tabernacle, to Shebna the overseer of the Temple (ibid., 15); and the Hebrews relate that he, terrified by Rabshakeh's threats, surrendered to the Assyrians and betrayed the lower part of Jerusalem to the enemy, so that, apart from the citadel of Zion and the Temple, nothing else remained that the Assyrian did not hold. Hence those err who think that the Shebna who now goes out with Eliakim and Joah to Rabshakeh is the same as the one above. For there Shebna is set down as overseer of the Temple, who is said to be destined for capture by the Assyrians; but here Shebna is a scribe, that is, γραμματεὶς, which in Hebrew is called SOPHER סופר, and he is ὁμώνυμος with the former. As for Rabshakeh, who spoke in the Hebrew tongue, the Jews suppose him to have been a son of the prophet Isaiah, who was himself also a traitor; and that the other son of Isaiah, who was left, was called Jasub, which in our language means left. Others, again, think that he was a Samaritan, and that for this reason he knew the Hebrew speech and blasphemed the Lord so boldly and impiously.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.36.1":["I have read in someone's Commentary that this Sennacherib is the same who also took Samaria, which is altogether false. For the sacred history relates that first Pul king of the Assyrians, under Menahem king of Israel, laid waste the ten tribes; second, that Tiglath-pileser came to Samaria under Pekah son of Remaliah of Israel; third, that Shalmaneser under Hoshea king of Israel took all Samaria (2 Kgs 15, 17); fourth, that it was Sargon who stormed Ashdod (Isa 20); fifth, Esarhaddon, who, when Israel had been carried away, sent the Samaritans into the land of Judea as guards (2 Kgs 17); sixth, Sennacherib, who under Hezekiah king of Judah, having taken Lachish and the other cities of Judea, besieged Jerusalem (2 Kgs 18). Others, however, think that one and the same king is called by many names.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.36.6-10":["Let us then consider the words of Rabshakeh; and first, what he says: You are trusting in this broken staff of reed, in Egypt, is false: for no history relates that Hezekiah sent to the Egyptians and asked for Pharaoh's help. And what he adds: If you answer me, We trust in the Lord our God, is true. But again he joins a lie to the truth: that Hezekiah had taken away His high places and altars. For he had done this not against God but for God, so that, idolatry and the old error being destroyed, he might command God to be worshiped in Jerusalem, where his Temple was; although we read that by a very bad custom the people sacrificed victims to God on the mountains and hills, even when the altar had already been built. And his promising two thousand horses, wishing to show the fewness of the besieged, for which Hezekiah could not furnish riders, does not come from the weakness of the people of the Jews, as if it lacked the skill of riding, but from the observance of the commandments of God, who through Moses had commanded concerning the king of Israel: He shall not multiply horses for himself, and he shall not have very many wives (Deut 17:16, 17). But if, he says, you cannot withstand me, the servant of Sennacherib, who am the least of his servants, how will you withstand the so great might of the king? And to what he had said, If you answer me, We trust in the Lord God, he replied cunningly and shrewdly that he had come not by his own will but by the Lord's commands: The Lord said to me: Go up against this land and destroy it. And the argument is: certainly I could not have come here without the Lord's will. But since I have come and taken many cities, and only part of Jerusalem remains untouched, it is clear that I came by his will.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.36.6":["Of a reed staff of this kind Rabshakeh too vainly reproaches Hezekiah king of Judah, saying: Behold, trusting in this reed rod, and a broken one, upon Egypt; whoever leans on it, it goes into his hand: so is Pharaoh king of Egypt and all who trust in him; and he indeed was lying about a righteous king in recalling these things — he who is interpreted as 'much with a kiss': for he was drunk from the golden cup of Babylon, and therefore he was thrusting his own things upon the people, while it was confessing the Lord —; here, however, Pharaoh is reproached for having been to the house of Israel a rod or reed staff, promising them help that was vain and fragile and soon to be shattered.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"ISA.36.11-15":["And Eliakim and Shebna and Joah said to Rabshakeh: Speak to your servants in the Syrian tongue, for we understand it: do not speak to us in the Jewish tongue in the ears of the people who are on the wall. And Rabshakeh said to them: Has my lord sent me to your lord and to you to speak all these words, and not rather to the men who sit on the wall, that they may eat their own dung and drink the urine of their feet with you? And Rabshakeh stood and cried out with a loud voice in the Jewish tongue, and said: Hear the words of the great king, the king of the Assyrians. Thus says the king: Let not Hezekiah deceive you, for he will not be able to rescue you; and let not Hezekiah give you confidence in the Lord, saying: The Lord will surely deliver us; this city will not be given into the hand of the king of the Assyrians. Do not listen to Hezekiah. For thus says the king of the Assyrians. The accusation of Rabshakeh is a testimony to Hezekiah: that when all the cities of Judea had been taken he trusted in the Lord and said to the people: Do not fear, nor be afraid of the king of the Assyrians and of all the multitude that is with him; for there are many more with us than with him. With him is an arm of flesh; with us is the Lord our God, who is our helper and fights for us. And the people, it says, were strengthened by such words of Hezekiah king of Judah. Hence Rabshakeh wishes to destroy what the other had built up, and he speaks to the people: Let not Hezekiah deceive you; and let him not give you confidence in the Lord God. And the humble entreaty of Eliakim and Shebna and Joah, Speak to your servants in the Syrian tongue, for we understand it; and do not speak to us in the Jewish tongue in the ears of the people who are on the wall, has this sense: What need is there for the people to be stirred by false terrors, and for you to boast of a vain strength? Speak a language which the people do not understand. For we have knowledge of your language, and we know the Syrian speech, which is common to both. To which Rabshakeh arrogantly: Has, he says, my lord sent me to your lord and to you, and not rather to the men who sit on the wall? And again, increasing the terror with a threat, that they may eat, he says, their own dung, and drink the urine of their feet with you? By this he shows that they will be taken by hunger and want and thirst. And at the same time he joins enticement to fear, so that those whom he had not conquered by terror he might deceive by promises and persuasion, saying, in the words of the king of the Assyrians:\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.36.16-17":["Make a blessing with me, and come out to me, and eat each one of his vine, and each one of his fig tree, and drink each one the water of his cistern: until I come and take you away to a land that is like your land: a land of grain and wine, a land of bread and vineyards. Let not Hezekiah trouble you, saying: The Lord will deliver us. Have the gods of the nations delivered each one his land from the hand of the king of the Assyrians? For which in the volume of Kings one reads: Do with me what is profitable, and come out to me (2 Kgs 18:31). So the sense is the same. Do, he says, what may benefit you and turn to your blessing. Or he says this: Bless the Assyrian king and praise him, and confess him your lord, so that you may obtain rewards; and until I return from Egypt, or, having taken Libnah, come back, dwell in your city and enjoy your goods. But afterward I will come and transfer you to a land which is like your land, of grain, wine and olives. And he does not say the name of the region, because he could not find one equal to the land of promise; but he promises a likeness. For this is what each one desires: the land in which he was born. Some think that Media was promised them, which had a likeness to the land of Judea both in situation and in its crops. And what he adds:\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.36.19-20":["Where is the God of Hamath and Arpad? Where is the God of Sepharvaim? Have they delivered Samaria out of my hand? Who is there among all the gods of these lands that has rescued his land out of my hand, that the Lord should rescue Jerusalem out of my hand? And they were silent and answered him not a word. For the king had commanded them, saying: Do not answer him. This shows that Samaria served all these gods, and was taken for that reason. But if, he says, with so many gods presiding, we easily overcame the ten tribes, how much more easily shall we conquer you, nay, Jerusalem alone, with one God as its guardian?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.36.21":["And all the people kept silent and answered him nothing; for they had received the king's command not to answer him. Truly righteous was Hezekiah, doing all things faithfully and everything with counsel. And he had commanded that the blaspheming Assyrian not be answered for this reason: lest he provoke him to greater blasphemies. Hence it is written: Do not kindle the coals of the sinner (Sir 8:13); and in the psalm we read: When the sinner stood against me, I was dumb, and was humbled, and kept silent from good things (Ps 38 [39]:2-3); and again: Set, O Lord, a guard to my mouth, and a fortified door to my lips: incline not my heart to words of malice (Ps 140 [141]:3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.36.22":["And Eliakim son of Hilkiah, who was over the house, and Shebna the scribe, and Joah son of Asaph, the recorder, came in to Hezekiah with their garments torn, and reported to him the words of Rabshakeh. And it came to pass, when king Hezekiah had heard, he tore his garments, and was covered with sackcloth, and went into the house of God. And he sent Eliakim, who was over the house, and Shebna the scribe, and the elders of the priests covered with sackcloth, to Isaiah, the son of the prophet Amos, and they said to him: Thus says Hezekiah: This day is a day of tribulation and of rebuke and of blasphemy, for the children have come to the birth, and there is no strength to bring forth. If by any means the Lord your God may hear the words of Rabshakeh, whom the king of the Assyrians his master sent to blaspheme the living God and to reproach him with words which the Lord your God has heard. Lift up therefore a prayer for the remnant that is found. And the servants of king Hezekiah came to Isaiah; and Isaiah said to them: Thus shall you say to your master: Thus says the Lord: Do not be afraid before the words that you have heard, with which the servants of the king of the Assyrians have blasphemed me. Behold, I will give him a spirit, and he shall hear a report, and shall return to his own land, and I will make him fall by the sword in his own land. Leaving aside what is plain, let us discuss only those things in which a hidden sense lies. They tear their garments because they hear Rabshakeh blaspheming. The king himself also tears his garments, because he believed it was owing to his own sins and the people's that Rabshakeh had come up to the gate of Jerusalem and had spoken such things against the Lord. Hence the high priest, because he believed the Savior had blasphemed, tore his garments (Matt 26); and Paul and Barnabas, when the Lycaonians offered them the worship due to God, tore their garments (Acts 14). In place of royal attire, then, he was covered with sackcloth; and going from the palace to the Temple, he sent Eliakim the high priest, and Shebna the scribe, and the elders of the priests to Isaiah, the son of the prophet Amos. In this the humility and prudence of the king is to be considered. He himself goes to the Temple; the princes of the people and the elders of the priests, covered not with priestly stoles but with haircloth, he sent to Isaiah, the son of the prophet Amos. For which in the volume of Kings one reads: He himself was covered with sackcloth and entered the house of the Lord, and he sent Eliakim the overseer of the house, and Shebna the scribe, and the elders of the priests covered with sackcloth, to Isaiah the prophet, the son of Amos (2 Kgs 19:1, 2). Here, because Isaiah was writing the history about himself, he did not call himself a Prophet but the son of a Prophet; there, however, because the writer of the history was another, he writes that he himself was a Prophet. This we read also of Matthew the Evangelist: that Matthew himself called himself a publican (Matt 9), while the other Evangelists were silent about the name of publican and set down only the apostolic dignity; and that in the pairings of the Apostles he is second in his own list and first in the others'.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.3":["And they said, it says, to him: Thus says Hezekiah, not 'the king', not swelling with the title of empire: This day is a day of tribulation and of rebuke and of blasphemy: of our tribulation, of God's rebuke, of the enemies' blasphemy. And he sets down the likeness of a woman in labor and in pain, who has come up to the birth and cannot bring forth, nor say: From fear of you, O Lord, we have conceived, and have been in pain, and have brought forth the spirit of salvation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.4":["There follows: If by any means the Lord your God may hear the words of Rabshakeh (Isa 26:18 LXX). For we do not dare to call the Lord of all our Lord, since through his anger we suffer so much; but we call him your Lord. And we have this confidence of vengeance: that the living God is blasphemed by a worshiper of dead idols. And they have reproached with words which the Lord your God has heard. Lift up therefore a prayer, our prayer, which lies prostrate: not for the whole people, which has already perished, but for the remnant, which is besieged.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.5-7":["And when the servants of king Hezekiah had come to Isaiah: again he was silent about the name of Prophet, in order to keep the humility he had begun. And Isaiah anticipated them: for by the same spirit by which he knew things to come, he had heard the king too, though absent; and he tells them what they should answer to their master, humbled in this confidence of conscience. Say, he says, to your master, who is your lord; for my Lord says this: Do not fear the words with which not you but I have been blasphemed. Nor do I tell all that I am going to do to the king of the Assyrians, lest I seem to boast of my power; but only that a spirit is to be given him, not of God but an adverse one. And having heard a report, let him return to his own land and fall in it by the sword: so that Hezekiah might hear both things he desired together, that he was to be freed from siege and danger, and that the hostile and angry king would die in his own land. If anyone asks why in a book of Prophecy the history written in the volumes of Kings and Chronicles seems to be mixed in, let him consider that prophecy is coupled with the history, as much concerning the liberation of the city as concerning the destruction of the Assyrian, and the turning back of the sun by ten hours, and the prolonging of life by a space of fifteen years, which belongs both to prophecy and to history.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.8-12":["And Rabshakeh returned and found the king of the Assyrians fighting against Libnah: for he had heard that he had departed from Lachish. And he heard concerning Tirhakah king of Ethiopia, people saying: He has come out to fight against you. And when he heard it, he sent messengers to Hezekiah, saying: Thus shall you say to Hezekiah king of Judah, speaking: Let not your God, in whom you trust, deceive you, saying: Jerusalem will not be given into the hand of the king of the Assyrians. Behold, you have heard all that the kings of the Assyrians have done to all the lands which they overthrew, and will you be able to be delivered? Have the gods of the nations rescued them, whom my fathers overthrew, Gozan and Haran, and Rezeph, and the sons of Eden who were in Telassar? Where is the king of Hamath, and the king of Arpad, and the king of the city of Sepharvaim, Ana and Ava? In the same words and with the same sense it is written in the volume of Kings and of Chronicles that Rabshakeh, according to the will of the Lord, abandoned the siege of Jerusalem and went on to his master, who, as he had learned, was going to attack Libnah, Lachish having been either abandoned or taken. Sennacherib himself too, hearing that Tirhakah king of the Ethiopians was bringing war against him, went to meet him as he came, and nonetheless sends messengers and letters to Hezekiah, so that those whom he had not yet taken by force he might terrify by words. And just as he had said to the people, Let not Hezekiah deceive you (2 Kgs 18:29), so he speaks the same blasphemy to the king: Let not your God deceive you (2 Kgs 19:10). And he sets out the examples of his forefathers: that just as their gods could not deliver the other lands out of their hands, so neither would Jerusalem be delivered.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.9":["We pass quickly over the plain things in order to dwell on the doubtful ones. That Sennacherib king of the Assyrians fought against the Egyptians and besieged Pelusium, and that, when the mounds had already been built to take the city, Tirhakah king of the Ethiopians came to its help, and that in one night near Jerusalem one hundred and eighty-five thousand of the Assyrian army fell by pestilence, is related by Herodotus, and most fully by Berosus, the writer of Chaldean history, whose credibility must be sought from their own books.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.13":["In the enumeration of the other nations he sets down ANA (הנע) and AVA (ועוה), which the Seventy ran together, saying anavegava; and the conjunction and, that is, vau, between the two nations Ana and Ava, they set down in the Hebrew tongue, so that to the ignorant it seems one nation or city.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.14-17":["And Hezekiah took the books from the hand of the messengers and read them; and he went up into the house of the Lord and spread them out before the Lord, and prayed to the Lord, saying: O Lord of hosts, God of Israel, who sit upon the Cherubim, you alone are God of all the kingdoms of the earth: for you made heaven and earth. Incline, O Lord, your ear and hear; open, O Lord, your eyes and see, and hear all the words of Sennacherib which he sent to blaspheme the living God. For truly, O Lord, the kings of the Assyrians have made desolate the lands and their regions, and have given their gods to the fire: for they were not gods, but works of men's hands, wood and stones; and they broke them in pieces. And now, O Lord our God, save us from his hand, and let all the kingdoms of the earth know that you alone are God. Against the blasphemies of king Sennacherib Hezekiah seized his accustomed weapons. And again he goes to the Temple and spreads out his letters before the Lord. Before, he had been silent: for, terrified by fear of the Lord, he did not dare to open his mouth in the Temple or to pour out free prayers to God. But now, because he had already heard Isaiah saying: Do not be afraid before the words that you have heard, with which the servants of the king of the Assyrians have blasphemed me, and the rest, he boldly entreats the Lord and asserts that God alone is living; whereas the idols we understand as images of the dead.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.18-19":["And what he adds: For truly, O Lord, the kings of the Assyrians have made desolate the lands and their regions, and have given their gods to the fire: for they were not gods, but works of men's hands, wood and stones; and they broke them in pieces, is proved by many histories, which write that the kings of the Persians came into Greece and overthrew and plundered the temples of the Greeks.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.20":["And he asks for vengeance, so that through his occasion all kingdoms may know that he alone is God, who can deliver his own from danger.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.21-22":["And Isaiah son of Amos sent to Hezekiah, saying: Thus says the Lord God of Israel: As to what you have asked me concerning Sennacherib king of the Assyrians, this is the word which the Lord has spoken concerning him: The virgin daughter of Zion has despised you and mocked you; behind you the daughter of Jerusalem has shaken her head. Whom have you reproached, and whom have you blasphemed, and against whom have you raised your voice and lifted up the height of your eyes? Against the Holy One of Israel? By the hand of your servants you have reproached the Lord and have said: With the multitude of my chariots I have gone up to the height of the mountains, the ridges of Lebanon, and I will cut down its lofty cedars and its choice fir trees, and I will enter the height of its summit, the forest of its Carmel. I dug, and drank water, and dried up with the sole of my foot all the streams of the embankments. Because Hezekiah had entreated the Lord so boldly and did not send to Isaiah as he had sent before, the Prophet himself does not go to him, but sends messengers to tell him in the words of God: Concerning Sennacherib, against whom you pray, this is the Lord's sentence: The virgin of Zion and daughter of Jerusalem (who is called virgin and daughter for this reason, that while all the nations worship the images of dead men, she alone preserves the chastity of the religion of God and the worship of the one Godhead) has mocked you and despised you; and she who, lest she provoke you to greater blasphemy, had not answered you when you were present, has shaken her head behind you as you go away, sure of vengeance, secure about punishment.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.23-24":["And she has said this: Not against me, but against the Lord you have been proud; and not you yourself, but through your servants, so that the arrogance of the blasphemer might be the greater. For you said that with the multitude of your chariots you would climb the height of the mountains and the ridges of Lebanon, and cut down its lofty cedars and fir trees. This we ought to take either μεταφορικῶς of all the nations and their princes, or of Jerusalem, which is what Lebanon means, so that we refer its cedars and firs to all its powerful and noble men, and the height of its summit and the forest of Carmel to the Temple. For he himself had said above: Have you not heard what the kings of the Assyrians have done to all the lands which they overthrew? Therefore neither will you be able to be delivered.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.25":["And what he adds: I dug and drank water, and dried up with the sole of my foot all the streams of the embankments, can be understood according to the history: that because of the multitude of his army he dried up all the streams, so that he was compelled to dig wells for himself; according to the metaphor: that he laid waste with his army all the peoples, who are sometimes described under the name of waters. For which the Seventy alone translated: and I have set a bridge, and I have made desolate the waters, and every gathering of waters: that is, that no nation was impassable to him, but he trod with his foot upon all the waters of the peoples.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.26-27":["Have you not heard what I did to it long ago? From the days of old I formed it, and now I have brought them, and it has come to be for the uprooting of the hills that fight together, and of fortified cities. Their inhabitants, with shortened hand, trembled and were confounded: they became like the hay of the field, and the grass of the pasture, and the herb of the housetops, which withered before it ripened. Your dwelling, and your going out, and your coming in I have known, and your madness against me, when you raged against me. Your pride has come up into my ears; I will therefore put a ring in your nostrils, and a bridle in your lips, and I will lead you back by the way by which you came. These things are to be understood as spoken in the person of God against the words of the Assyrian: that the Lord answered his blasphemy thus: Do you not know that these things which you have done, you did by my will, and that I foretold them as to come, and commanded that they be done through you? So what I decreed long ago has been fulfilled in its time: that the hills, that is, the princes who formerly fought among themselves, and the most fortified cities, when I drew back my hand and did not furnish my accustomed help, should be uprooted and tremble and perish, and be compared not to the olive and the vine and fruitful trees, but to hay and grass and the herbs of the housetops, which are a hindrance to the crops and wither before they come to maturity.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.28":["So I knew beforehand your sitting, and your going out, and your coming in, and I spoke of the madness with which you were going to rage against me, through the Prophets as they prophesied (above, on Isa 14:13): through whom I had long ago known that you would say: I will ascend into heaven; above the stars of heaven I will set my throne, and I will be like the Most High.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.29":["So your fury and your pride have reached my ears, and I will by no means bear with you any longer, so that you may understand that what you were able to do, you were able to do not by your own strength but by my decision. For the impious nations and the unfruitful trees deserved to be cut down and to fall through you, as through my axe and saw. So I will put a ring, or a muzzle, in your nostrils, to bind fast your blaspheming mouth, so that you may by no means dare to speak such things any more; and I will put a bridle on your lips, to tame your ferocity and lead you back to the Assyrians. This figure Scripture also uses in the Psalms against the impious: With bridle and muzzle bind fast the jaws of those who do not draw near to you (Ps 31 [32]:9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.30":["But this shall be a sign to you: Eat this year what springs up of itself, and in the second year feed on fruits; but in the third year sow and reap, and plant vineyards, and eat their fruit. And what has been saved of the house of Judah, and what is left, shall send down root downward and bear fruit upward: for out of Jerusalem shall go forth a remnant, and salvation from mount Zion. The zeal of the Lord of hosts shall do this. All these things the Prophet speaks to Hezekiah through messengers: what Sennacherib said, what the Lord answered him. Now he addresses his speech to the king himself, lest perhaps he doubt that the things spoken will come. And it was for this reason above all that the Prophets had credit for their words among the people: because they told not only of things that were to come many ages later, but also of things to be fulfilled at once and after no great space of time; and that within two years both the Assyrian king would perish and security would be restored to the city of Jerusalem. This, he says, will be the sign of the things to come that I foretell: that this year you will eat what springs up of itself; or, according to the Seventy, what you sowed before. And in the second year, according to Symmachus, feed on fruits; or, according to the same Seventy, on what has sprouted from the former crops and from seed falling to the ground. But in the third year, the Assyrian now put to flight and the siege lifted, sow and reap, and plant vineyards, and eat their fruits.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.31-32":["For the remnant of this small city, which is now walled in by a hostile army and does not believe it will escape, will receive such abundance and happiness in all things that, like a tree founded on a deep root, they will be filled with the thickest fruits. For out of Jerusalem and out of mount Zion a remnant shall go forth and fill the land of Judea, not by its own merit but by the mercy of God, nay by the zeal with which he was jealous for his people against the impious.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.33-35":["Therefore thus says the Lord concerning the king of the Assyrians: He shall not enter this city, and he shall not shoot an arrow there, nor shall a shield seize it, and he shall not cast up a mound around it. By the way by which he shall come, by it he shall return, and he shall not enter this city, says the Lord; and I will protect this city and save it for my own sake and for the sake of David my servant. He returns to his subject, and after the hope of things to come he shakes off the present fear. For there was not as much gladness over the things he had promised would come long afterward as there was anxiety about those that were imminent. And when he says that the Assyrian will withdraw, and will not raise a shield against Jerusalem nor shoot darts, nor is the city to be walled in with siege works and a mound, and that the adversary will return by the way he came, and the city will be freed from the present siege, and at the end adds: for my own sake and for the sake of David my servant, this signifies that they are preserved not by their own merit but by God's clemency, nay by the memory of their father David. In this they are reminded both of their own negligence and of his faith and righteousness: that God so loves righteousness that he protects even the descendants of holy men, not by their own merit but by the virtue of their forefathers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.36":["And the Angel of God went out and struck in the camp of the Assyrians one hundred and eighty-five thousand; and they arose in the morning, and behold, all were corpses of the dead. And Sennacherib king of the Assyrians went out and departed and returned, and dwelt in Nineveh. And it came to pass, while he was worshiping in the temple of Nisroch his god, Adrammelech and Sharezer his sons struck him with the sword; and they fled into the land of Ararat; and Esarhaddon his son reigned in his place. One hundred and eighty-five thousand of the bravest men are slain by one Angel in one night, and without wounds on the slain a cruel death runs through them, driving the souls out of the bodies by the will of God. Concerning which one reads in Chronicles: And the Lord sent an Angel, who struck every strong man and warrior and the commander of the army of the king of the Assyrians; and he returned with disgrace to his own land (2 Chr 32:21). He was preserved for this reason, that he might know the power of God and restrain his blaspheming mouth, and become a witness of that majesty which a little before he had despised. As for what he added, And they arose in the morning, let us take it either of the Israelites or of the rest of his army: although it is written in the volume of Kings that when the king himself had risen at daybreak, he saw all the bodies of the dead (2 Kgs 19).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.37.37-38":["Pharaoh too is preserved through the ten plagues of Egypt, so as to perish last: which this man also suffered. For when he had returned to Nineveh, the chief city of his kingdom, and was worshiping in the temple of Nisroch his god, as though bringing back a victory over his enemies, and was entering the shrine of his idol triumphant and giving thanks, the despiser of the true God is slaughtered in the sanctuary of a false deity: and he perishes not by the Angel's sword, which would have been a fate shared with many, but by the parricide of his sons. And when they had fled into the land of Ararat, which is understood as Armenia, Esarhaddon succeeded to his father's place, who, as Scripture testifies, sent inhabitants to Samaria, so that the land should not remain uncultivated. Ararat is a level region in Armenia, through which the Araxes flows, of incredible fertility, at the roots of mount Taurus, which extends as far as there. So the Ark too, in which Noah was delivered with his children when the Flood ceased, was carried not to the mountains of Armenia in general, which is called Ararat, but to the highest mountains of Taurus, which overhang the plains of Ararat.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.1":["In those days Hezekiah was sick unto death; and Isaiah son of Amos, the Prophet, came in to him and said to him: Thus says the Lord: Set your house in order, for you shall die and not live. And Hezekiah turned his face to the wall, and prayed to the Lord, and said: I beseech you, O Lord, remember, I pray, how I have walked before you in truth and with a perfect heart, and have done what is good in your eyes: and Hezekiah wept with a great weeping. Whom the Lord loves he rebukes, and he chastises every son whom he receives (Prov 3). Lest the heart of Hezekiah be lifted up after his incredible triumphs and the victory won out of the midst of captivity, he is visited with infirmity of body and hears that he is going to die, so that, turning to the Lord, he may bend his sentence. This indeed we read also in the prophet Jonah, and in the threats against David, which are said to be coming and were not done: not because God changes his sentence, but because he calls mankind to the knowledge of himself. For the Lord repents over evils.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.2":["And Hezekiah turned his face to the wall because he could not go to the Temple: to the wall of the Temple, that is, next to which Solomon had built the palace. Or simply to the wall, lest he seem to display his tears to those sitting by. Or certainly, according to Jeremiah, to his heart; who calls CIR (קיר), that is, wall, the heart: so that he entreated the Lord with his whole mind.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.3":["And he says: I beseech you, O Lord, remember, I pray, how I have walked before you in truth and with a perfect heart, and have done what is good in your eyes. And hearing that he is going to die, he does not pray for life and many years, but leaves to God's judgment what he wishes to grant. For he knew that Solomon had pleased God for this reason, that he did not ask for more years of life; but as one about to go to the Lord, he recounts his works, how he walked before him in truth and with a perfect heart. Happy the conscience that in time of affliction remembers its good works: For blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God (Matt 5:8). And what of its being written elsewhere: Who shall boast that he has a pure heart (Prov 20:9)? This is solved thus: perfection of heart is ascribed to him here because he destroyed the idols, overturned the vessels of the temple of Baal, broke the bronze serpent in pieces, and did the other things which Scripture records. And he wept with a great weeping because of the Lord's promise to David, which he saw would perish in his death. For at that time Hezekiah had no sons; for after his death Manasseh began to reign in Judea when he was twelve years old. From which it is clear that Manasseh was begotten after the third year of the life granted to him. So all this weeping is because he despaired that the Christ would be born of his seed. Others assert that even holy men are terrified by death, because of the uncertainty of judgment and their ignorance of God's sentence as to what abode they will have. And at the same time the question of fate is solved, and of the chains of necessity and of causes: that the day of death is by no means fixed beforehand for each person, but that by the will of God and by causes unknown to mortals one either lives or dies; especially since the necessity of death, once set, is here postponed, and we have read that very many were raised up after death.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.4-6":["And the word of the Lord came to Isaiah, saying: Go and say to Hezekiah: Thus says the Lord, the God of David your father: I have heard your prayer, I have seen your tears. Behold, I will add to your days fifteen years, and I will deliver you and this city out of the hand of the king of the Assyrians, and I will protect it. And this shall be a sign to you from the Lord, that the Lord will do this word which he has spoken: Behold, I will make the shadow of the lines, by which it had gone down on the dial of Ahaz in the sun, go back ten lines: and the sun went back ten lines by the steps by which it had gone down. Here, as in a prophecy, the history is related in reversed order, which in the volume of Kings is read in better sequence. While Hezekiah was weeping with a great weeping, before Isaiah had gone out of the middle part of the court, the word of the Lord came to him, saying: Go back and say to Hezekiah, the leader of my people: I have heard your prayer and seen your tear: Behold, I have healed you. And Hezekiah said to Isaiah: What will be the sign that the Lord will heal me (2 Kgs 20:4 and 5)? To him the Prophet answered: This shall be the sign from the Lord that the Lord will do the word which he has spoken: Do you wish the shadow to advance so many lines, or to go back as many steps? And Hezekiah said to him: It is easy for the shadow to increase by ten lines, and I do not wish this to be done; but that it go back ten steps. And when, at Isaiah's calling upon the power of the Lord, the sign had been accomplished, Isaiah commanded that a lump of fresh figs be brought: and when they had brought it and laid it upon his wound, he was cured. The Prophet returns to the king at the Lord's command, so that he who had struck might himself heal; and Hezekiah is called the leader of his people, and son of David, whose works he followed, for he had done what was right according to all that David his father did: and his prayer is heard and his tears are seen, because he had walked before the Lord in truth and with a perfect heart, and had wept with a great weeping, and had done what was pleasing in his eyes. Fifteen years are added to his life, which he had not asked for, and besides, the security of the kingdom during his lifetime is promised. But if, as some think, to live in the body is a condemnation, and, according to what is said: Return, my soul, to your rest (Ps 114 [116]:7), and in another place: Bring my soul out of prison (Ps 141 [142]:8), death is to be desired, that we may be freed from prison: how does the Lord now grant it as a benefit that one who was about to be freed should live yet fifteen years in prison?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.7-8":["And a sign is given, that the sun should go back ten steps, which we, following Symmachus, have rendered as lines and dial; for he understood the steps as lines, to make the sense clearer to readers. Or else the steps had been built by mechanical art in such a way that the shadow, descending through each one, marked off the spaces of the hours. This sign was a type both of the present time and of the future: that as the sun went back to its beginning, so Hezekiah's life too returned to years already unwoven; and that for us, who live in the hebdomad and the ogdoad, the spaces of life are prolonged through the resurrection of Christ. The guides to the holy Places in this province are accustomed to show, within the enclosure of the Temple, the steps of the house of Hezekiah, or of Ahaz, down which the sun descended. But I will never believe that the house, I do not say of Ahaz, who was an impious king, but of any righteous king whatever, was in the Temple of God: since Solomon is said, among other things, to have offended God for this reason, that he built Millo on high, from which, walking in the tower of his palace, he used to look down on the court of the Temple (1 Kgs 9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.9":["The writing of Hezekiah king of Judah, when he had been sick and had recovered from his infirmity. LXX: The prayer of Hezekiah king of Judah, when he was sick and rose up from his infirmity. I wonder how the Seventy alone put prayer for writing, since prayer is called THEPHELLATH (תפלת), and not MACHTHAB (מכתב), which is what is written in the present place; otherwise prayer would fittingly be said if it concerned the present time and not the past. For it is clear that after his health was restored, rising from his infirmity, he wrote these things down; from which it follows that it is not a prayer but a thanksgiving for the benefit he had received.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.10":["I said: In the half of my days I shall go to the gates of hell: I sought the remainder of my years. I said: I shall not see the Lord God in the land of the living: I shall not behold man any more, and the dweller of rest. My generation has ceased: it is taken away and rolled up from me like a shepherds' tent. My life is cut off as by a weaver: while I was yet beginning, he cut me off: from morning to evening you will make an end of me. I hoped until morning: like a lion, so he has broken all my bones. From morning to evening you will make an end of me. LXX: I said: In the height of my days I shall go to the gates of hell: I will leave the remaining years. I said: I shall by no means any more see the salvation of God in the land of the living, nor shall I see man any more with the inhabitants: I have failed from my kindred. It has gone out and departed from me, as one takes down a tent who had pitched it: like a web my spirit has departed, when it is cut off by the weaver. In that day I was given up until morning: like a lion, so he has broken all my bones. From day to night I was given up. He relates what he thought silently in the time of pressing distress and imminent sickness. I said, he says, in my heart, in the half of my days, or, as Aquila and Symmachus and Theodotion translated, in the infirmity and silence of my days; for which the Seventy translated height, because of the likeness of a letter, reading RAME (רמי) for DAME (דמי): although some have rendered DAME as blood, so that the sense is, In the blood of my days, when my blood and my death were being sought. So in despair I said: I shall go to the gates of hell, either by the common law of nature, or those gates from which the Psalmist sings that he was freed: You who lift me up from the gates of death, that I may announce all your praises in the gates of the daughter of Zion (Ps 9:15). These I think are the gates of hell which do not prevail against Peter (Matt 16), because he slept in the fullness of his days. The saints fill up their days, as Abraham did, who died full of days in a good old age (Gen 25). But sinners and the impious die in the half of their days, of whom the Psalmist also speaks: Men of blood and deceitful men shall not live out half their days (Ps 54 [55]:25). For they do not fulfill the works of virtue, nor are they eager to amend their faults by penance. Hence in the middle of life's course and in the darkness of their errors they will be led to Tartarus. I sought, he says, the remainder of my years, thinking that he would live no longer.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.11-12":["There follows: I shall not behold man any more, and the dweller of rest. This we had formerly joined to the following verse because of the ambiguity of the word; for the Hebrew word HOLED, if it is read or written ELED (חלד), means rest; if EDEL (חדל), it means setting. So he fears that he may not dwell in rest with the saints and the men of God, that he may not see the Lord in the land of the living, that his generation may be dissolved like a tent, that like a web it may be cut off at the very beginning of the light, and that the Christ may by no means arise from his seed. And that our body is called a tent the Apostle too teaches, saying: We who are in this tent groan, being burdened (2 Cor 5:4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.11":["I said: I shall not see the Lord, the Lord in the land of the living. For Lord and Lord, IA (יה) is set twice in the Hebrew, which sounds in the last syllable of ALLELUIA (הללויה); for which the Seventy translated: I shall by no means any more see the salvation of God in the land of the living. Of which it is written in another place: I will please the Lord in the region of the living (Ps 114 [116]:9). And again: I will please the Lord in the light of the living (Ps 55 [56]:9). The region of the Saints, then, is the very one that is called the light of the living. For he is not the God of the dead but of the living (Matt 22). And this is all that he fears: that, led down to hell, he may by no means deserve to behold the salvation of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.12-13":["From morning, he says, to evening you will make an end of me: I hoped until morning: which Job too says he endured in his distress and in the torments of his body (Job 4), when in the day he awaited the night, and in darkness looked for the light, thinking that with the change of the times his torments could change. That this is true he knows who burns with high fevers, whose inner fire, like a lion, consumes all his bones, and who, for the greatness of his pain, does not think he will live any longer.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.14-15":["Like the young of a swallow, so will I cry: I will meditate like a dove: my eyes are worn thin looking upward. O Lord, I suffer violence; answer for me: what shall I say, or what will he answer me, since he himself has done it? LXX: Like a swallow, so will I cry; like a dove, so will I meditate. For my eyes have failed with looking to the height of heaven, to the Lord, who has rescued me and taken away the pain of my soul, and he himself has done it. Imminent death and the pressing pain of sickness, like a lion, were breaking all the bones of my body; but I, like the swallow and the dove, joined days and nights with weeping and groaning; and from God alone, who could help, with my eyes lifted on high, I awaited aid. And I said to him: I suffer more than my deserts require; but even if I have erred in anything, I will turn to the better; do you answer for me: For it is not of him who wills nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy (Rom 9:16). And again he turns back into himself: What shall I say, or what shall I plead against you, my maker? Or what will he answer me, who himself has done what he willed? Whatever he has decreed, then, must be endured. For young of a swallow, or swallow, as the Seventy translated, in the Hebrew is written SUS AGOR (סוס עגור), which Aquila translated the horse Agor; Theodotion, SIS AGUR (סיס עגור); for the middle vowel-letter, if vau is put between two Samec, is read sus and means horse; if jod, it is read sis and is called swallow. Symmachus translated thus: Like a shut-in swallow, so will I sing. This word agor is also read in Jeremiah, where it is written: The kite in heaven has known its time: the turtledove and the swallow and the stork have kept their times (Jer 8:7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.15":["I will reckon up all my years in the bitterness of my soul. O Lord, if one lives thus, and if in such things is the life of my spirit, you will rebuke me and you will give me life. Behold, in peace my bitterness is most bitter. But you have rescued my soul, that it should not perish: you have cast all my sins behind your back. For hell will not confess to you, nor will death praise you: those who go down into the pit will not await your truth. The living, the living, he will confess to you, as I also do today. The father will make your truth known to the children. O Lord, save me, and we will sing our psalms all the days of our life in the house of the Lord. LXX: O Lord, concerning it, it was announced to you: and you have raised up my spirit, and, having comforted me, you have given me life. Behold, in peace is my bitterness: for you have delivered my soul, that it should not perish, and you have cast all my sins behind me. For in hell they will not praise you, nor will the dead bless you. Nor will those who are in hell hope for your mercy: the living will bless you, as I also do. For from today I will make sons, who will announce your righteousness, O Lord of my salvation, and I will not cease blessing you with the psaltery all the days of my life before the house of God. Nothing belonging to mortals is long-lasting, and all the happiness of the world is lost even while it is held. For when the time of tribulation has come, all that is past is no help to the one who endures it. Hence the foolish opinion of Epicurus, who asserts that present evils are softened by the remembrance of past goods. So Hezekiah says that he reckons up all the years of his reign, and of his past happiness, as he thought it, in his present bitterness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.16-17":["And because he is now secure and no longer suffers what he relates, he philosophizes on the human state and says: O Lord, if one lives thus: if we have been born in such a condition, you have rebuked me, but you have given me life, and you have granted peace, the Assyrian being put to flight; but my peace was more bitter to me than all bitterness, because, when tranquillity was restored to the peoples and the city was secure, I alone entered the threshold of death. But you have rescued my soul, that it should not perish, either for the present life or for the future. For you have cast all my sins behind your back, lest I should look upon them in sadness, but that I might contemplate your mercy.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.18":["For hell and death will not confess, nor will they praise you, according to what is written: But in hell who will confess to you (Ps 6:6)? And confession in this place is taken not for penance but for glory and praise, as we read also in the Gospel: I will confess to you, O Lord, Father of heaven and earth (Matt 11:25). Those who go down into the pit, he says, will not await your truth: better than mercy in the Seventy. For he who is in hell does not await the truth of judgment but the mercy of God: especially since the Savior descended into hell to free the prisoners from the lower world. For pit the same translators more plainly rendered hell.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.19":["The living, the living, he will confess to you, as I also do today. Here too confession is put for praise. For he does not confess his crimes, but gives thanks to God: and praise is not beautiful in the mouth of a sinner (Sir 15). And while hell and death do not confess nor praise God, on the contrary life and the living glorify the Lord. And what follows: The father will make your truth known to the children, signifies what is said in Deuteronomy: Ask your father, and he will declare it to you; your elders, and they will tell you (Deut 32:7), so that through the successions and each generation the clemency of God may be proclaimed to posterity. For which the Seventy set: For from today I will make sons, who will announce your righteousness. And the causal conjunction joins what follows to what precedes: namely, that the living, and he himself living, bless God for this reason, that from this day he would make sons who would announce his truth, which certainly was not in his own power. For those children were not promised him through the Prophet; only the present life was granted. Especially since he begot a most impious son, Manasseh, who filled Jerusalem with blood from gate to gate, and did not bless but cursed God, persecuting his saints. We can therefore say this according to the Seventy: that he did not say I will make sons, but παιδία, which we understand either as little ones, or boys, or little infants, and descendants, so that from the fact that he himself obtained mercy, all posterity to come, knowing this, might praise his incredible clemency.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.20":["Save me then, O Lord; and all of us who believe in you and have been delivered by your help will sing to you in the Temple all the days of our life.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.38.21-22":["And Isaiah commanded that they take a lump of figs and plaster it upon the wound, and he would be healed. And Hezekiah said: What will be the sign that I shall go up into the house of the Lord? This is to be read before the prayer of Hezekiah, or Writing, which we have now interpreted: for the plaster was laid on the wound, and the sign of future health was asked by him, before he gave thanks to the Lord, which he is said to have done when healed. The Hebrews say that the word SIIN (שחין), which the Seventy passed over, means ulcer, not wound. For Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion also translated ἕλκος, by which they wish the royal disease to be understood, to which whatever is sweet, whether taken as food or applied to the body, is thought to be contrary. So, that the power of God might be shown, health was restored through things harmful and contrary. Others suppose SIIN to be not ulcer but abscess: when the swelling body is filled with ripened and putrefying pus. And according to the art of physicians, all matter is drawn out to the surface of the skin by dried and pounded figs: and hence a medicine established by use and experience is not to be despised, since God too made this one. So the prayer and thanksgiving is written up to this point. As for the sign that was given, the earlier order of the history has narrated it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.39.1":["At that time Merodach-baladan, son of Baladan, king of Babylon, sent books and gifts to Hezekiah: for he had heard that he had been sick and had recovered. And Hezekiah was glad over them, and showed them the storehouse of spices, and of silver, and of gold, and of perfumes, and of the best ointment, and all the storerooms of his furnishings, and all that was found in his treasuries: there was not a word that Hezekiah did not show them in his house and in all his dominion. Above we read that in the fourteenth year of king Hezekiah, Sennacherib king of the Assyrians came up against all the fortified cities of Judah and took them, and afterward besieged Lachish, passed on to Libnah, and sent part of his army to Jerusalem; and that, when one hundred and eighty-five thousand of his army had been slain by the Angel, he himself fled to Nineveh and was killed by his sons in the shrine of his god, and Esarhaddon his son reigned in his place; that Hezekiah fell sick and recovered his health at the Prophet's message; that an incredible sign was done, so that the sun went back by the space of ten hours toward its rising, and the day became almost double. Now we read that at that time, that is, in the same year in which all these things were done, Merodach-baladan, son of Baladan, king of Babylon, sent books and gifts to Hezekiah; not Esarhaddon, who had succeeded his father Sennacherib in the kingdom among the Assyrians, about whose death or life Scripture is silent. From which it is clear that the kingdom of the Assyrians was then one thing, and that of the Babylonians another. Indeed, the Assyrians took Samaria, that is, the ten tribes; but Judah and Jerusalem, we read, were taken later by the Chaldeans, whose king was Nebuchadnezzar. And because among them there is the observation of the heavenly bodies, and the course of the stars is known by long practice and exercise, as is shown also at the Lord's nativity, they understood that the sun had gone back and the spaces of the day had been doubled, in the service of him whom they thought the only God. And when they inquired into the causes and reason of this miracle, as the report flew through all the nations, they learned that because of the sickness of the king of Judah even the course of the brightest sign had been changed. That this is not my opinion but the faith of holy Scripture, the words of Chronicles testify, which say after other things: This is Hezekiah who stopped the upper spring of the waters of Gihon, and turned them down to the west of the city of David. In all his works he accomplished prosperously what he wished; but in the embassy of the princes of Babylon, who had been sent to him to inquire about the portent that had happened on the earth, God left him, that he might be tested, and that all that was in his heart might be made known (2 Chr 32:30, 31). And he was left to temptation for this reason, because after so great a victory, and the sun's going back, and the congratulation of a most powerful kingdom, his heart was lifted up. Indeed, in the same volume it is written: Many brought victims and sacrifices to the Lord in Jerusalem, and gifts to Hezekiah king of Judah: and he was exalted before all the nations. In those days he was sick unto death, and he prayed to the Lord; and he heard him and gave him a sign; but he did not make return according to the benefits he had received: for his heart was lifted up, and there was wrath against him, and against Judah and Jerusalem (2 Chr 32:23, 24). And again holy Scripture says that the lifting up of his heart was softened by penance, adding: And afterward he was humbled because his heart had been lifted up, both he and the inhabitants of Jerusalem: and therefore the wrath of the Lord did not come upon them in the days of Hezekiah.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.39.2":["He was glad, then, at the coming of the envoys of Merodach, whom the Hebrews suppose to have been the father of Nebuchadnezzar, and at the offering of gifts and the congratulation on his recovery. And he showed them, according to the Seventy, the house NECHOTHA (נכתה), for which Symmachus translated of his spices; and the treasuries of silver, and of gold, and of perfumes, and of the best ointment, which in the Hebrew is written of good oil; and all the treasuries of the vessels of his gaza, or, as is read there, of his vessels. Now in the language of the Persians riches are called gaza; nor is it a Hebrew word, but a Barbarian one. There was not, he says, a word (which according to Hebrew usage is frequently taken for thing) that he did not show them in his house and in all his dominion. Hence the most just wrath of God, because he showed not only his own treasures and those of the palace but also those of the Temple, which certainly was within his dominion, and from whose doors he had already earlier taken the plates of gold.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.39.3":["Then Isaiah the Prophet came in to king Hezekiah and said to him: What did these men say, and from where did they come to you? And Hezekiah said: From a far country they came to me, from Babylon. And he said: What did they see in your house? And Hezekiah said: They saw all that is in my house: there was not a word, or thing, that I did not show them in my treasuries. And Isaiah said to Hezekiah: Hear the word of the Lord of hosts. Behold, the day will come, and all that is in your house, and what your fathers have stored up until this day, shall be carried away to Babylon: nothing shall be left, said the Lord. And of your sons who shall come forth from you, whom you shall beget, they shall take away: and they shall be eunuchs in the palace of the king of Babylon. And Hezekiah said to Isaiah: Good is the word of the Lord which he has spoken. And he said: Only let there be peace and truth in my days. The Hebrews relate that Hezekiah fell sick for this reason, because after the unheard-of victory of the Jews and the destruction of the Assyrian king he did not sing praises to the Lord, as Moses sang when Pharaoh was drowned (Exod 15), and Deborah when Sisera was killed (Judg 4), and Hannah when Samuel was born (1 Sam 2); and that hence he was reminded of his frailty. And again, after the health of his body and the greatness of the sign, another occasion of pride was offered him, which, as a prudent man and a worshiper of God, he ought to have avoided; nor ought he to have shown to foreigners his riches, which he possessed by God's gift. From which, according to the laws of tropology too, we learn that pearls are not to be cast before swine, nor what is holy given to dogs (Matt 7). For he who is of a faithful spirit hides matters; and whoever does not do this, all his strength is unnerved, and his posterity perishes, and, its manly vigor lost, is reduced to womanly softness (Prov 11:13-15). So Isaiah goes in to the king and, as though not knowing, inquires: What did these men say, and from where did they come? He asks two things: what they said, and from where they came. The king answered one, passing over the other, which is to be read with ἐμφάσει and a lifted brow: From a far country they came to me, from Babylon: meaning that the farther the land from which they came, the more glorious is he on whose account they came. And they came, he says, to me, when he ought to have said: they came to glorify God for the greatness of the sign; from Babylon, which is the most powerful city in the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.39.4":["And again Isaiah: What, he says, did they see in your house? And he answered with part of the truth: that they saw everything in his house, and there was nothing that he did not show them in his treasuries. But he is silent about the other thing, for which he feared offense: that he showed them all that he had in his dominion; no doubt the furnishings of the Temple as well.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.39.5-7":["For these reasons Isaiah pronounces the sentence in the word of God: Hear the word of the Lord of hosts: The time will come when all these things which are in your house, acquired not by your labor but by your fathers', will be carried over to Babylon, and of your seed eunuchs will be made in the royal court.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.39.7":["From this the Hebrews hold that Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah, who were of the royal seed, were made eunuchs; that they were in the service of king Nebuchadnezzar there is no doubt.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.39.8":["So Hezekiah said: Good is the word of the Lord which he has spoken. In this he is reproached by the Hebrews for not imitating the goodness of Moses, who spoke to the Lord: Either forgive them this offense; or if you do not, blot me out of your book which you have written (Exod 32:32). Hence the apostle Paul too wishes to be anathema from Christ for his brethren who are Israelites (Rom 9); and they say that for this reason Hezekiah was not approved by the words of God, who in what follows says: Comfort, comfort, O my people, says your God, so that those for whom he had not prayed might be comforted by the Lord's clemency.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.1-2":["Comfort, comfort, O my people, says your God: Speak to the heart of Jerusalem, and call to her; for her evil is fulfilled, her iniquity is forgiven. She has received from the hand of the Lord double for all her sins. LXX: Comfort, comfort my people, says God; ÷ O priests ⁚ speak to the heart of Jerusalem: comfort her: for her humiliation is filled up. Her sin is loosed, for she has received from the hand of the Lord double her sins. According to the other translators, others are commanded to comfort the people of God and Jerusalem alike; according to the Hebrew, the people itself is commanded to comfort, and to speak to the heart of Jerusalem, and to call to her. To speak to the heart of Jerusalem is an idiom of the Scriptures. For he who speaks to one who mourns, and comforts with soothing words, is said to speak to the heart. Let Shechem son of Hamor teach us, who, after he had violated Dinah, spoke to her heart and comforted her (Gen 34). And wherever you find anything similar, it has this sense. And the cause of the comfort is the remission of sins; and the cause of the remission, that she has received from the hand of the Lord double for all her sins. For he who knows the will of his Lord and sins will be beaten with many stripes (Luke 12). And everyone who has the Holy Spirit dwelling in him, whom the Savior promised to the Apostles, saying: I will ask my Father, and he will give you another comforter, to be with you for ever, the Spirit of truth (John 14:16); and again: But when the comforter, the Holy Spirit, has come, whom my Father will send in my name, he will teach you all things (ibid., 26); and again: When the comforter has come, whom I shall send you from the Father, the Spirit of truth who proceeds from the Father, he will bear witness of me (John 15:26); and: It is to your advantage that I go: for if I do not go away, the comforter will not come to you (John 16:7); he is a comforter, and he is now commanded to comfort the people of God. Hence the Apostle Paul too said to the believers: Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies and God of all comfort, who comforts us in all our tribulation, so that we may be able to comfort those who are in any tribulation, through the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God: for as the sufferings of Christ abound in us, so through Christ our comfort also will abound (2 Cor 1:1-6); and again: And our hope for you is firm: knowing that as you are partakers of the sufferings, so you will be also of the comfort (2 Cor 1:7). But who this people is that is comforted through the Apostles and churchmen, not Israel and Jacob and Judah, as Scripture records in other places, but the people of God, the prophet Zechariah testifies, saying: Rejoice and be glad, daughter of Zion: for I will come and dwell in the midst of you, says the Lord. And many nations shall flee to the Lord in that day, and shall be his people, and they shall dwell in the midst of you, and they shall know that the Lord almighty has sent me to you (Zech 2:10, 11). By this testimony it is clearly shown that very many nations are to be turned into the people of God. And these things the Lord says, sent by the Lord whose name is Almighty. And this is to be noted, that our sins are not loosed unless we have received them from the hand of the Lord. Nor is it the same for sins to be loosed and to be forgiven. For he to whom they are forgiven does not need loosing, as he hears in the Gospel: Take heart, son, your sins are forgiven you (Matt 9:2). But he for whom they are loosed, for him they are loosed for this reason, that they have been purged and paid off through punishments. According to the history, Jerusalem received from the hand of the Lord double her sins: once from the Babylonians, the second time from the Romans. And what is added by the Seventy, O priests, is to be marked beforehand with an obelus.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.3-5":["The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight in the wilderness the paths of our God. Every valley shall be exalted, and every mountain and hill shall be brought low: and the crooked shall become straight, and the rough ways plain; and the glory of the Lord shall be revealed, and all flesh together shall see that the mouth of the Lord has spoken. LXX: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord: make straight the paths of our God. Every valley shall be filled, and every mountain and hill shall be brought low; and the crooked shall become straight, and the rough ways plains. And the glory of the Lord shall appear, and all flesh shall see the salvation of God, for the Lord has spoken. Mindful of this voice, the Scribes and Pharisees and the princes of the Jews, when they heard that John was preaching in the wilderness a baptism of penance and teaching the people, send men to ask him whether he was the Christ, or Elijah, or the Prophet; and when he had answered that he was none of these, they ask again: Tell us then, who are you? that we may give an answer to those who sent us. What do you say of yourself? And he answered: I am the voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord; as Isaiah the Prophet said (John 1:22, 23). In this it is to be noticed that the straight ways of the Lord and the paths of our God, the filling of the valleys, and the lowering of the mountains and hills, and the correction of the crooked, and the level plains made of rough places and rocks, and the glory of the Lord and the salvation of our God, are proclaimed not in Jerusalem but in the wilderness of the Church and in the deserted multitude of the nations, of which we read above (cf. Isa 35:1): Rejoice, thirsting desert: let the wilderness exult and flower like the lily. For this multitude was deserted of the knowledge of God, and was held by idols: low in confession, lifted up in pride, rough and intractable in savagery. But after the glory of the Lord appeared, and all flesh saw the salvation of God, all things were suddenly changed and the way of the Lord prepared, so that the glory of God appeared in the wilderness: when the Lord was baptized in the Jordan, and the heavens were opened, and the Holy Spirit descended in the form of a dove and remained on him; and the voice of the Father thundering from above was heard: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased: hear him (Matt 3:17). And all flesh saw the salvation of God. It was called flesh for this reason, that before it did not have the Holy Spirit. Of it the Lord also says: My spirit shall not remain in these men, because they are flesh (Gen 6:3). But that flesh will see the salvation of God of which the same Lord spoke through Joel: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and they shall prophesy (Joel 2:28). This was cried out not only at that time by John, who, as the forerunner and precursor of the Word of God, is rightly called a voice (Matt 3); but to this day in the desert of the Gentiles the teachers of the Churches cry out: that we should make straight ways and paths for God in our hearts, and be filled with virtues, and be bowed down by humility; that we should change the crooked into straight and turn the rough into smooth: and so deserve to see the glory of the Lord and the salvation of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.3":["For this is he who was spoken of through the prophet Isaiah, saying: The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. He was preparing the souls of believers, in which the Lord was going to walk, so that he, the pure one, might walk in most pure ways, saying: I will dwell in them and walk among them, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people. Porphyry compares this passage with the beginning of the evangelist Mark, in which it is written: The beginning of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God; as it is written in the prophet Isaiah: Behold, I send my angel before your face, who shall prepare your way; the voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight. For since the testimony is woven together from Malachi and Isaiah, he asks how we can think the example was taken as from Isaiah alone. To him churchmen have answered most fully. We, however, think either that the name of Isaiah was added through the fault of copyists, which we can prove in other places as well, or at any rate that one body has been made out of different testimonies of the Scriptures. Read Psalm 13 (14) and you will find this same thing.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.40.6-8":["The voice of one saying: Cry. And I said: What shall I cry? All flesh is grass, and all its glory is like the flower of the field. The grass is withered, the flower has fallen; because the spirit of God has blown upon it: truly the people is grass. The grass is withered, the flower has fallen; but the word of our Lord shall stand for ever. LXX: The voice of one saying: Cry: and I said: What shall I cry? All flesh is grass, and all the glory of man is like the flower of grass. The grass has dried up, and the flower has fallen ※ because the spirit of God has blown upon it ※ truly the people is grass ※ the grass is withered, the flower has fallen ⁚ but the word of our God abides for ever: What is marked with asterisks, because the spirit of God has blown upon it: truly the people is grass: the grass has dried up, the flower has fallen, has been added from the Hebrew and the edition of Theodotion. From which it is clear that it was either passed over by the Seventy or gradually lost by the fault of copyists, since both the former and the following verse end in flower. Above we read, the Prophet saying: And I heard the voice of the Lord saying: Whom shall I send, and who will go to this people? And I said: Behold, here am I, send me; and he said: Go and say to this people: With the ear you shall hear and not understand: and seeing you shall see and not see, and the rest. Having suffered hard things for that preaching, now, when the Lord's voice says Cry, fearing the like, he asks what he ought to cry; and beginning from the general, All flesh is grass, and all its glory is like the flower of grass, he comes to the particular, so as to say none the less of the people: Truly the people is grass. And indeed, if anyone looks at the frailty of the flesh, how from moment to moment we grow and decline and do not remain in the same state, and how the very time in which we speak, dictate and write flies away from the portion of our life, he will not hesitate to call flesh grass, and its glory like the flower of grass, or the meadows of the fields. He who was lately an infant is suddenly a boy; he who was a boy, suddenly a young man; and up to old age he is changed by imperceptible stages; and he perceives himself an old man before he wonders that he is no longer young. A beautiful woman who drew flocks of young men after her is shrunk with a furrowed brow; and she who before was an object of love is afterward one of disgust. This the distinguished orator among the Greeks also writes: that the beauty of the body either fails with time or is consumed by sickness. So the flesh has withered and its beauty has fallen, because the spirit of the fury and sentence of God has blown upon it (to return from the general discussion to the order of Scripture): the flesh of him who bears the image of the earthly and serves vices and luxury; and it is grass and a passing flower. But he who has and keeps the image of the heavenly, he is the flesh that sees the salvation of the Lord, that is renewed daily unto knowledge according to the image of the Creator, and, receiving an incorruptible and immortal body, changes its glory, not its nature. But the word of our Lord, and those who are joined to the word, abide for ever.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.9":["At the same time this too must be considered, that today also, so long as we explain the Scriptures according to the letter to little ones and sucklings, and to those in whose hearts Christ never grows up nor advances in age and wisdom and grace with God and men, we are praised, looked up to, held in admiration. But when we have begun a little to call them on to pass over to greater things, from being our heralds they become enemies, and they would rather follow the Jews than the apostles, who, withdrawing from the teaching and traditions of the Pharisees, entered into Christ himself, the propitiatory, and the perfection of the Law; nor do they deign to accept the divine word, which bids the teachers of the Church to climb to loftier doctrines, to raise the voice with all their strength, and not to fear the clamor of the little ones barking around them, saying: \"Go up on a lofty mountain, you who bring good tidings to Sion. Lift up your voice in strength, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem. Lift it up, do not fear\" (Isa. 40:9).\n— Commentary on Galatians","Go up on a high mountain, O woman who brings good tidings to Zion: lift up your voice with strength, O woman who brings good tidings to Jerusalem: lift it up, do not fear; say to the cities of Judah: Behold your God: behold, the Lord God will come in strength, and his arm shall rule. Behold, his reward is with him, and his work before him. Like a shepherd he will feed his flock: in his arm he will gather the lambs, and in his bosom he will lift them up: he himself will carry the ewes with young. LXX: Go up on a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion: lift up your voice with strength, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem: lift it up, do not fear: say to the cities of Judah: Behold your God, behold the Lord God will come with power, and his arm with dominion: behold, his reward is with him, and his work in his sight. Like a shepherd he will feed his people, and in his arm he will gather the lambs: and he will comfort those that are with young. The choir of the Apostles is commanded to climb the heights for the preaching to all flesh which is to see the salvation of God, and, being about to speak of great things, to abide on the heights. Moreover, the Hebrew and the other translators put it in the feminine gender, so as to say: O woman who brings good tidings to Zion, and O woman who brings good tidings to Jerusalem. In the Greek this word is ambiguous, so that we can take it either of her who announces or of her to whom it is announced. Whether, then, Zion and Jerusalem announce the word of God and the salvation of the Lord, For out of Zion has gone forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem (Isa 2:3), or it is announced to them through the Apostles, they must climb the heights and pass over to the mountains. And in a wonderful way, although Zion itself is a mountain, as Scripture says, Mount Zion, in which you have dwelt (Ps 73 [74]:2), she is commanded to ascend another, higher mountain, by which the prince of Tyre was wounded. And because there were to be many things opposed to the teaching of the Apostles, and they were to be led and set before governors and tribunals, there is joined: lift it up, do not fear: say to the cities of Judah, that is, to the Synagogues and peoples of the Jews, of whom the Lord said: I have not come except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 15:24). And the apostle Paul: To you, he says, the word of God had to be announced first (Acts 13:46). And what is it that they are commanded to say? Behold your God, whom you were always expecting:\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The Hebrews assert, and there is no doubt about this matter among them, that the Holy Spirit is called in their tongue by the feminine gender, that is, RUA CODSA (רוח קדשה). And what is said in Psalm 67 (68): The Lord will give the word to those who bring good tidings with great power, they understand thus: The Lord will give the word to those women who bring good tidings with great power (Ps 122 [123]:3): namely, to those souls which have obtained the Holy Spirit. And also the verse: As the eyes of a maidservant are on the hands of her mistress: they interpret the soul as the maidservant and the Holy Spirit as the mistress. But also in the Gospel written according to the Hebrews, which the Nazarenes read, the Lord says: just now my mother, the Holy Spirit, took me. No one ought to be scandalized in this matter, that spirit is said among the Hebrews in the feminine gender, while in our language it is called in the masculine gender, and in the Greek speech in the neuter. For in the Godhead there is no sex. And therefore in the three principal languages in which the title of the Lord's passion was written, it is called by three genders, so that we may know that what is diverse is of no gender.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.10":["Behold, the Lord God will come in strength, whom you despised when he came in humility. And his strength will rule, who before had taken the form of a servant, made obedient to the Father unto death (Phil 2). Behold, his reward is with him, and his work before him (Isa 40 and 62). According to what he himself says in the Gospel: For the Son of man is to come in the glory of his Father, and he will render to each according to his work (Matt 16:27).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.11":["Like a shepherd he will feed his flock. He who afterward is to come in majesty first takes the form of a shepherd, and says of himself: I am the good shepherd, and I know my sheep, and mine know me, and I lay down my life for my sheep (John 10:14, 15). Of him the Father speaks in Zechariah: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered (Zech 13:7). In his arm, he says, he will gather the lambs; not the bulls and rams and he-goats and great sheep, whom he threatens through Ezekiel (Ezek 34) because they feed on the milk and are clothed with the wool and crush the weak of the flock, but the lambs, still tender and of an unformed infancy in Christ, who have lately been reborn in baptism, of whom the Lord himself spoke to Peter: Feed my lambs (John 21:15). Hence also in the same Ezekiel it is written: I will raise up over them one shepherd, and he shall feed them, my servant David; and he shall be their shepherd, and I the Lord will be their God, and David a prince in their midst. I the Lord have spoken, and I will make with David a covenant of peace (Ezek 34:23-25). In this it is to be considered that, long after the time of David, the gluttonous shepherds being rejected, he says he will raise up our Lord, who is of the stock of David, to gather the lambs and cherish them in his bosom, and himself to carry on his shoulder the young of the sheep, or the ewes with young; as we read in the Gospel that he carried back to the fold on his shoulders the sheep that had strayed and stayed behind from its accustomed flock (Luke 15). We can call the ewes with young the Apostles and apostolic men and all the teachers of the Church, who are in labor with the salvation of very many, and say with the Apostle: My little children, with whom I am again in labor until Christ be formed in you (Gal 4:19).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.12":["Who has measured the waters in his fist, and weighed heaven with his palm? Who has poised the mass of the earth with three fingers, and weighed the mountains in a weight, and the hills in a balance? Who has helped the spirit of the Lord, or who was his counselor and showed him? With whom did he take counsel, and who instructed him, and taught him the path of righteousness, and schooled him in knowledge, and showed him the way of understanding? Behold, the nations are like a drop of a bucket, and are counted as the tipping of a balance: behold, the islands are like a little dust. And Lebanon is not enough for burning, and its beasts will not be enough for a burnt offering. All the nations are before him as if they were not, and they are counted to him as nothing and void. LXX: Who has measured the water with his hand, and heaven with his palm, and all the earth with his fist? Who has set the mountains in a weight, and the rocks in a balance? Who has known the mind of the Lord, or who was his counselor? Who taught him, whose counsel did he receive, and who instructed him? Or who showed him judgment, and who showed him the way of understanding? If all the nations are counted like a drop of a bucket, and as the tipping of a balance, they are counted also as spittle. Lebanon is not enough for burning, and all the four-footed beasts are not enough for a burnt offering, and all the nations are as nothing, and are counted to him as nothing. Lest anyone should think the calling of the nations difficult, and that all flesh should see the salvation of God, that those who bring good tidings to Zion should be commanded to climb a high mountain, and that the Lord himself should come in strength and render to each according to his work, and like a shepherd cherish the lambs in his bosom and himself carry the ewes with young, his greatness is described: that nothing is impossible to him; and that he who founded all things and is the Creator of all can accomplish also these things, which are small in comparison with those. In calling it fist and palm, he uses the words and measures of human custom, so that we may learn the power of God through our words. Where the Seventy translated: Who has measured the water with his hand? or, as we rendered: Who has measured the waters in his fist? Aquila translated: Who has measured the waters with his smallest finger? For this is what λιχὰς means: that, namely, not with the whole hand but with the little finger, which is commonly called the taster, the whole vastness of the waters is weighed; while σπιθαμὴ, that is, the palm, signifies the hand stretched out from the thumb to the tip of the last finger. But if we close the hand, it becomes a fist: so that through the palm and the fist we may know the outstretched heavens and the globe of the earth. For fist, which the Seventy translated in the grasping of the earth, in the Hebrew is written SALIS (שליש), which Symmachus translated τρίτον, Aquila τρίσωμον: and we, to make it plainer, rendered it three fingers; namely, that he weighed the mass of the earth and the heights of the mountains and hills as if with three little fingers and with the small tipping of a balance: by which the majesty of God and the power of the Creator is shown.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.13-14":["Who, he says, has helped the spirit of the Lord, or who was his counselor and showed him? etc. For which Symmachus translated: Who has prepared the spirit of the Lord, and who has shown him the man of his counsel? With whom did he take counsel, and who gave him understanding, and taught him the way of judgment, and instructed him in knowledge, and showed him the road of prudence? By this he shows more plainly that the spirit was prepared, or established, the one of whom one reads in the Apostle: Now the Lord is the Spirit. And: Upon whom the Spirit of God rested, the Spirit of wisdom and understanding, and the rest. Who says in what follows: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me: because he has anointed me (Isa 61:1). For he himself is the spirit of the Lord and the man of his counsel, in whom dwelt all the fullness of the Godhead bodily (Col 2). With him he took counsel, of whom we spoke above (cf. Isa 9:6): Wonderful, Counselor. And in Proverbs it is written: God founded the earth by wisdom, and prepared the heavens by understanding (Prov 3:19). Moreover, the Seventy, who said: Who has known the mind of the Lord, and who was his counselor? wish it to be understood that the mind and reason and sense of God, through whom all things were made and without whom nothing was made, is he of whom it is sung in the Psalms: By the word of the Lord the heavens were established, and all their power by the spirit of his mouth (Ps 32 [33]:6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.15":["All the nations that did not know their Creator, or the whole race of mortals, are in comparison with God like a drop of a bucket, and like the tipping of a balance, which with a light weight inclines to one side. And just as, if a small drop flows from a bucket, it is disregarded by the one who carries it, so the whole multitude of the nations, compared with the heavenly ministries and the multitude of the Angels, is counted as nothing. The islands too are counted as spittle, or, as Symmachus and Theodotion have it, setting down the Hebrew word itself, as DOC (דק), which falls off: for which Aquila translated λεπτὸν βαλλόμενον. And the Hebrews say that this word signifies the finest dust, which, snatched up by the wind, is often thrown into the eyes and is felt rather than seen. The tiniest and almost invisible particles of dust, then, are called by this word: what perhaps Democritus with his Epicurus calls atoms. And there are many words which are read in the Greek just as they are set down in the Hebrew, because of the difficulty of translating, and because of the poverty of both the Greek and the Latin speech in comparison with the Hebrew language.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.16":["And at the same time, to draw men away little by little from idolatry, he takes away the ceremonies of victims, and teaches that all the wood of Lebanon and the herds that graze on it could not suffice for his burnt offerings.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.17":["But if all the nations are in his sight as if they were not, and are counted as nothing and void (and among all the nations is Israel too), then Israel too is as if it were not, and is counted as nothing and void. This we say so that its pride may be broken, and they may know that they are like the other nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.18":["To whom then have you likened God, or what image will you set up for him? Has the smith cast a graven image, or has the goldsmith shaped it with gold, and the silversmith with plates of silver? The wise craftsman has chosen strong and incorruptible wood: and he will seek how to set up an idol that will not be moved. Having described the greatness of God and shown his power in part, the nations and islands being counted as a drop of a bucket and the tipping of a balance, and as dust, and the ceremonies of victims being rejected, he teaches in what follows why all the nations are as nothing in his sight and are counted as void: To whom have you likened God, or what image will you set up for him, who is spirit, and is in all things, and runs everywhere, and holds the earth as in his fist?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.19-20":["And at the same time he mocks the folly of the nations, that a craftsman or bronze-smith, or goldsmith or silversmith, should make a god for themselves, and fasten it together with plates and nails and set it firmly, lest it be pushed over by the blasts of the winds. And what he added: the wise craftsman has chosen strong and incorruptible wood, in the Hebrew is said AMSUCHAN (המסכן); which is a kind of incorruptible wood from which idols are especially made. He says these things so that, idols being rejected, he may prepare the way for the teaching of the Gospel, and all crooked things may be directed into straight; the valleys exalted and the hills brought low; and the glory of the Lord revealed, so that all flesh may see the salvation of God. According to tropology we can say that the chiefs of the heretics are rebuked, who fashion various idols from their own heart: whether by the charm of eloquence, which silver signifies; or by the splendor of gold, which is referred to the understanding; or by incorruptible wood, which are the baser doctrines: and these are thought perpetual by those who fashion them, and are strengthened by the dialectical art, so that they may not be moved and fall, but stand on a solid root.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.21-22":["Will you not know? Will you not hear? Has it not been announced to you from the beginning? Have you not understood the foundations of the earth? He who sits upon the circle of the earth, and its inhabitants are like locusts; who stretches out the heavens like nothing, and spreads them out like a tent to dwell in; who makes the searchers of secrets as though they were not; he has made the judges of the earth as void. Indeed, their stock was neither planted, nor sown, nor rooted in the earth: suddenly he blew upon them and they withered, and the whirlwind will carry them off like stubble. And to whom have you likened me and made me equal? says the Holy One. Lift up your eyes on high, and see who has created these things: who brings out their host by number, and calls them all by name. Because of the greatness of his might and strength and power, not one was left behind. LXX: Will you not know? Will you not hear? Has it not been announced to you from the beginning? Have you not known the foundations of the earth? He who holds the circle of the earth, and its inhabitants as locusts; who has set up the heaven as a vault, and stretched it out as a tent to dwell in; who makes princes reign as nothing, and has made the earth as nothing. For they shall not be planted, nor sown, nor shall their root be made firm in the earth. He blew upon them, and they were dried up, and the tempest will take them up like stubble. Now therefore to whom have you likened me? Shall I be made equal to them? said the Holy One. Lift up your eyes on high, and see who has shown all these things. He who brings out his array by number, he will call them all by name, from much glory and in the strength of might: nothing is hidden from you. From the beginning, he says, I taught you by the natural law, and afterward through Moses I testified by the written Law that idols were nothing, and that the Creator of the world was himself God, who had founded so great a mass of earth upon the seas and set it upon the rivers, so that the heaviest element hangs over thin waters at the will of God, who like a king sits upon the circle of the earth: from which some contend that it is like a point and a globe; and its inhabitants are like locusts. For if we consider the various nations in the whole world, from Ocean to Ocean, that is, from the Indian sea to the British, and from the Atlantic to the frozen North, where the waters congeal and beautiful amber grows solid, we see the whole race of men in between dwelling like locusts. Why then is earth and ashes proud? Since the heaven, or rather, to use the authority of the Scriptures, the heavens, are stretched out like a vault; or, as it stands in the Hebrew, like DOC, of which we spoke above, for which the Seventy there translated spittle: and the one word they rendered now spittle, now vault, that is, arch; and since he stretches out their so great breadth above like a tent and a pavilion, so as to cover men like a roof and make them dwell as in a most spacious house: why should we wonder if the small bodies of men are counted like locusts and tiny creatures? Again, those who in this place too contend that the heaven hangs over the earth as a ἡμικύκλιον, and that the heaven is in the likeness of a sphere, misuse the word vault, as though the middle part of a sphere covered the earth: whereas in the Hebrew we have read not vault but DOC, that is, the finest dust. For which the spittle that is spat upon the ground, and is mixed with dust, and perishes, shows that the whole greatness of bodies is to be counted as nothing.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.23":["And he who stretched out the heavens and spread them out, so that either the multitudes of Angels might dwell above or men might abide beneath, and made, as it were, a great house for rational creatures, he himself according to the character of the times appoints princes, or searchers of secrets, so that they are as though they were not; and he has made the judges of the earth as void. For which the Seventy translated: and has made the earth as nothing; since also at the beginning of Genesis, where it is written: But the earth was invisible and unformed (Gen 1:2), the others translated: But the earth was void and nothing. How many kings Greek and Barbarian and Roman history recounts! Where is that countless army of Xerxes? Where the multitude of Israel in the desert? Where the incredible power of kings? Why should I speak of ancient things? Present examples teach us that princes are as nothing, and that the judges of the earth are counted as void.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.24":["These princes and judges of the earth (or, as others suspect, of heaven) were neither sown nor planted nor made firm with a solid root, and suddenly, at God's command, they are carried off and perish, as stubble is snatched away by whirlwind and tempest; according to what is written: And I passed by, and he was not; and I sought him, and his place was not found (Ps 36 [37]:36).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.25":["Since, then, so great is the power and majesty of the Creator, to what likeness do you compare God? And do you not rather understand the maker from the greatness of his creatures?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.26":["If you do not believe words, at least believe your eyes; and from the service of the heavens and of all the elements consider the power of the Lord. Who brings out their host by number, that is, of the heavens; and calls them all by name: understand, the stars. Of which it is also sung in the Psalms: Who numbers the multitude of the stars, and calls them all by name (Ps 146 [147]:4). Or let us interpret the host of heaven as the Angels and all the armies of the heavens, of whom Daniel also speaks: Thousands of thousands ministered to him, and ten times a hundred thousand stood before him (Dan 7:10). Hence he is also called the Lord Sabaoth, which in our language is said the Lord of the army and of hosts, or of powers. And he brings out the host of the heavens by number, so that the sun and the moon and the other stars, which Abraham could not number, are numbered by him and serve in the office assigned them (Gen 15): while the sun completes the same course of heaven in one year, Lucifer and Vesper in two years, the moon in each month, and all the stars accomplish it in fixed times; and some of them are called wandering, and we perceive their unequal motions with the eyes, not the mind, and we do not so much understand as wonder. For the greatness of God's might makes all things serve in their order. Or, according to the Seventy, because of the multitude of his glory and the power of his strength, nothing can be hidden from him: but by the majesty of the Creator he knows the ways and reasons and courses of all things.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.27":["Why do you say, Jacob, and speak, Israel: My way is hidden from the Lord, and my judgment will pass away from my God ? Do you not know, or have you not heard? The everlasting God, the Lord who created the ends of the earth, will not fail, nor will he labor, nor is there any searching out of his wisdom. He gives strength to the weary, and to those who have no might he multiplies strength. Boys will fail and will labor, and young men will fall in weakness. But those who hope in the Lord will change their strength, they will take on wings like the eagle, they will run and not labor, they will walk and not fail. LXX: For do not say, Jacob, and why have you spoken, Israel: My way is hidden from God, and my God has taken away my judgment and has departed? And now do you not know, and have you not heard? The eternal God, the God who created the ends of the earth, will not hunger, nor will he labor, nor is there any finding out of his understanding. He gives strength to the hungry, and sorrow to those who do not grieve. For young men will hunger, and youths will labor, and the chosen will be weak. But those who wait for God will change their strength. They will take on wings like eagles; they will run and not labor; they will go on and not hunger. Since the greatness of Almighty God is so great that nothing escapes him, and all things are governed by his will, why do you say, Jacob, that is, the two tribes which were called Judah, and speak, Israel, the other ten tribes in Samaria, you who have already been led captive to the Assyrians: My way is hidden from the Lord, and my judgment will pass away from my God? And the sense is: You say, earthly things do not concern God, nor does he consider what each one of us does; hence we too are unjustly oppressed by our enemies, and, according to Habakkuk, we are destroyed like beasts and fishes, without any ruler (Hab 1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.28":["To them God answered: Do you not know, and have the words of the preceding Scriptures not taught you? or, according to the Seventy, are you ignorant, because you have not heard, that the eternal God and Creator of all, the Lord, knows all things, holds all things together, and orders all things by his majesty? Nor does he ever fail or labor, so that he should not understand your judgment, and your ways should be hidden from him. Nor is there, he says, any searching out of his wisdom; from which place I think that saying of the Apostle was taken: Incomprehensible are his judgments, and unsearchable his ways (Rom 11:33); or, as the Seventy translated, he will not hunger, and he will not labor. For where there is food, there is often hunger, if you take the food away; and where there is hunger, there is also labor. But since these things are not in God, why do you attach human passions to him?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.29":["Rather, he himself gives strength to the hungry and the weary, and to those who seem to be as nothing in the world he multiplies power and might; or, according to the Seventy, he gives sorrow to those who do not grieve; for there is a sadness that leads to death, and there is a sadness that leads to life. Hence to those who have an impenitent heart he gives sadness, so that they may understand their sins.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.30":["And because many rejoice in the health of the body, and think youth and boyhood to be perpetual, he adds and says that the flowering age quickly falls, and robust bodies wither.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.40.31":["But those who have their trust not in their own strength but in God, and always await his mercy, change their strength and go from strength to strength, and take on wings like the eagle, and hear: Your youth will be renewed like the eagle's (Ps 102 [103]:5); they run to the Lord and do not labor in their desire for him; they walk and never fail. We have often said that the old age of eagles grows green again by the changing of their feathers, and that they alone can look at the radiance of the sun and gaze with flashing eyes upon the splendor of its rays, and that by this test they prove their young, whether they are true-born. So too the saints grow young again, and, having put on an immortal body, do not feel the labor of mortals, but are caught up in the clouds to meet Christ, and, according to the Seventy, never hunger, because they have the Lord present as their food.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.1":["Let the islands be silent before me, and let the nations change their strength; let them approach, and then let them speak; let us draw near together to judgment. Who raised up the Righteous One from the East, called him to follow him? He will give the nations before him, and he will hold kings. He will give them as dust to his sword, as stubble swept off by the wind to his bow. He will pursue them; he will pass on in peace; the path will not appear in his feet. Who has wrought and done these things, calling the generations from the beginning? I the Lord, I am the first and the last. The islands saw and were afraid; the ends of the earth were astounded; they drew near and came. Each will help his neighbor, and will say to his brother Be strong. The coppersmith strengthened the one who was forging at that time, striking with the hammer, saying, It is good for gluing; and he strengthened it with nails, so that it should not be moved. LXX: Be renewed toward me, you islands, for the princes will change their strength; let them draw near and speak together; then let them declare judgment. Who raised up righteousness from the East, called it to his feet, and it will go? He will give before the nations, and will cast kings into amazement, and will give their swords into the earth, and their bows like stubble cast away; and he will pursue them; the way of his feet will pass over in peace. Who has wrought and done these things? He will call it, he who calls it from the beginning of the generations. I God am first, and for the things that are to come I am. The nations saw and were afraid; the ends of the earth were astounded and drew near; they came together, each judging to help his neighbor and his brother; and he will say: The craftsman and coppersmith has prevailed, striking with the hammer, at the same time drawing out; at one time indeed he says, The soldering is good. They strengthened them; they will fix them with nails; and they will not be moved. The nations, that is, the islands, which are beaten by the false and bitter waves of this world, are bidden to listen and to close their mouths, and to know what was said to Israel: Hear, Israel, and be silent; and to change their strength, lest through their former weakness they be unable to hear the word of God, so that they may first approach the Lord, and not be content with their own salvation, but teach others what they have learned, and dispute with the Lord whether the God of all has kept what is just.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.2-4":["Some of the Hebrews think it is said of Abraham, who was called righteous from the East, that is, from the Chaldeans, because he alone was found righteous; and he followed him, going out of his native land into a land he did not know; and God delivered into his hand the kings who had come against Sodom and Gomorrah, and before his sword and bow made them like stubble and dust; and he pursued them and returned in peace, and did not feel the course of the long journey; and this he did not by his own strength but by the mercy of the Lord, who from the beginning knew that these things would come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.2-7":["What we have interpreted of the person of Christ, and the calling of the nations, and the preaching of the Gospel, and the condemnation of idols, some refer to Cyrus king of the Persians: that God raised him up from the East against the Babylonians and made him obey his command, laying many nations low before him and subjecting all things to his sword and bow; and they refer what follows to his person, namely that their idols, made by human effort and called into being by the coppersmith's art, did not profit the Babylonians.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.2":["And he so questions them and challenges them to answer, as to teach by questioning what they do not know: Who raised up the righteous one from the East, or righteousness? For he is not the God of the Jews only, but also of the nations, who called Christ the Lord, the Savior, who was made for us wisdom from God, righteousness and sanctification and redemption (1 Cor 1); in whom, according to the same Apostle, the righteousness of God is revealed (ibid.). And he called him to follow him in all things and to do the works of the Father, and to fulfill that which he says: O God, I have willed to do your will (Ps 39 [40]:9). In his sight kings and nations will bow their necks, and to his sword and arrows the adverse powers will be made subject like stubble and dust.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.3":["He will pursue them, namely the kings and princes of the several nations; and he will pass through in peace, calling all to peace, so that they may be reconciled to God. The path will not appear in his feet, that is, he will not feel the labor of the way, nor any weariness of human weakness, but will drink of the brook in the way, and therefore will lift up his head (Ps 109 [110]).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.4":["Who, he says, has wrought and done these things? Who raised up the righteous one, or righteousness? Who delivered nations and kings to him? Who subjected all things to his sword and bow? Surely he who from the beginning of the world foretold that these things would come, who is the Founder of all. And because he had said, with the feeling of one inquiring, Who raised up the righteous one from the East, and so on, while all were silent he answered himself: I am the Lord, I am the first and the last. It is he who speaks also in the Apocalypse of John: I am the alpha and the ω, the beginning and the end (Rev 1:8; and 22:13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.5-7":["Saw, he says, the islands, that is, the nations round about, and were terrified by his power: and the ends of the earth: namely Shem the son of Noah, who at the furthest time of the lands had escaped from the Flood with his father and his brothers, and had been preserved until that time (Gen 14). Him they want to be understood as Melchizedek, and that he came to meet Abraham as he returned from the battle, and received him, and blessed him, and strengthened him with the blessings of God; and that he is the coppersmith who forged Abraham and with the hammer of his art brought him out into better things, and said to him: It is good that you be glued to the fear of the Lord and joined to him. And he strengthened him, or made him solid, with the nails of the Lord's precepts, so that he should not be moved but should abide in his fear,\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.5":["The islands saw, that is, the nations, or the Churches gathered from the nations, which endure the storms of this world; and they feared the Lord: For the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Prov 9:10). All the ends of the lands trembled at the words of the Apostles, drawing near and coming together to the Gospel of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.6-7":["And when they saw that they themselves were saved, they fulfilled in deed what they had heard above: let them approach, and then let them speak, so that they helped their neighbors and wished to strengthen their brothers in the Lord, and said to them: Depart from idols, despise the ancient images of demons, which a human hand fashioned, which were produced by the beating hammer, which were joined with glue, which were made fast with nails so that they should not be moved, and which, when they stand, cannot walk.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.8":["And you, Israel my servant, Jacob whom I have chosen, seed of Abraham my friend. In whom I took hold of you from the ends of the earth, and called you from its far places, and said to you: You are my servant; I have chosen you and have not cast you off. Do not fear, for I am with you; do not turn aside, for I am your God; I have strengthened you and have helped you and the right hand of my righteous one has upheld you. Behold, all who fight against you will be confounded and will blush; they will be as though they were not; and the men who contradict you will perish. You will seek them and will not find them; the men who rebel against you will be as though they were not; and the men who war against you, as a thing consumed. For I the Lord your God have taken hold of your hand, saying to you: Do not fear, I have helped you. Do not fear, worm Jacob, you who are dead out of Israel. I have helped you, says the Lord, and your redeemer is the Holy One of Israel. I have made you like a new threshing wagon, having sawing teeth. You will thresh the mountains and crush them, and you will make the hills like dust. You will winnow them, and the wind will carry them off, and the whirlwind will scatter them; and you will exult in the Lord, you will rejoice in the Holy One of Israel. LXX: But you, Israel my child, Jacob whom I have chosen, seed of Abraham whom I loved, whom I took up from the ends of the earth, and from its heights I called you, and said to you: You are my child; I have chosen you and have not forsaken you. Do not fear, for I am with you, lest you go astray; for I am your God, who have strengthened you, and I have helped you, and have made you firm with my righteous right hand. Behold, all your adversaries will be confounded and will blush; for they will be as though they were not; and all your enemies will perish. You will seek them and will not find them, the men who will rage against you. For they will be as though they were not, and those who fight against you will not be. For I am the Lord your God, who hold your right hand, who say to you: Do not fear, Jacob, little Israel. I have helped you, says God, I who redeem you, the Holy One of Israel. Behold, I have made you like the new wheels of a threshing wagon, in the manner of saws; and you will thresh the mountains and crush the hills, and you will make them like dust and winnow them, and the wind will carry them off, and the tempest will scatter them. But you will rejoice in the Lord and in the holy ones of Israel. That there is a fleshly Jacob and Israel and a spiritual one, the Apostle Paul teaches: Behold Israel according to the flesh (1 Cor 10). From this we understand that there is also another according to the spirit; and to the fleshly Israel it is said: If you were sons of Abraham, you would do the works of Abraham. To them also it is said above (cf. Isa 40:27): Why do you say, Jacob, and speak, Israel: My way is hidden from the Lord, and my judgment has passed away from my God? Now, on the contrary, God speaks to the spiritual Israel, which received the coming of its Lord, which he calls first servant, then chosen, and finally the seed of Abraham. For first we received the spirit of bondage in fear (Rom 8); and afterward, being chosen, we are joined in friendship with God. So after the calling of the nations, when the islands saw and feared, the ends of the earth were astounded, drew near and came, the remnant according to the election of grace is called, of whom the Gospel also writes: These twelve Jesus chose, whom he also named Apostles (Luke 6); who, after the bondage of the Law as tutor, were chosen in the Gospel, and were found worthy to be the seed of Abraham the friend of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.9":["For in him he took hold of them from the end of the earth, and from its far-off borders he called Israel out of all the nations, gathering first the people of the Jews, of whom the Apostle Paul also says: It was necessary that the word be preached first to you; but since you turn us away, we go to the nations (Acts 13:46).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.10":["To you, therefore, who are both my servant and my chosen one, who are to preach among the nations and to suffer very many persecutions, I say: Do not fear, for I am with you, to whom I speak in the Gospel: Behold, I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the age (Matt 28:20). Do not go astray, nor turn aside from the truth, nor deceive yourself with a false persuasion that by your own strength, unskilled and naked, you will penetrate the forest of the nations and call the fiercest peoples to gentleness. It is I who have strengthened you and have helped you; and the right hand of my righteous one has upheld you, or protected you, that is, of the Lord the Savior; or my right hand, as the Seventy translated.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.11-12":["For behold, your adversaries, the people of the Jews, and all who fight against you, and your persecutors, will be turned into nothing, and you will seek your adversaries and will not find them. And the reason is given why he does not find his adversaries: Because they will be as though they were not. These adversaries are said to rage against the Apostles, or, according to the Hebrew, to be consumed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.13-14":["And for this reason you ought not to fear, because I have taken hold of your hand, I who say to you: Do not fear, I have helped you; do not fear, worm Jacob, you who are dead out of Israel, or, as the Seventy have it, very few out of Israel. What we have set down, I have helped you: do not fear, worm Jacob, is not found in the Seventy. Now the worm, which in Hebrew is called THOLATH (תולעת), is the name given, for its lowliness and contempt, to the choir of the Apostles, imitating the Savior, who speaks in the psalm: I am a worm, and no man, a reproach of men, and the outcast of the people (Ps 21 [22]:7). For as a worm penetrates the earth, so the apostolic word penetrated the cities of the nations, and entered first into the hardest hearts. And rightly they are called few out of Israel, because in comparison with the whole world very few of the Jewish nation believed, to whom the Lord says in the Gospel: Do not fear, little flock (Luke 12:32). Or, according to the Hebrew, dead out of Israel, who say with the Apostle: I die daily (1 Cor 15:31); and elsewhere: I am crucified with Christ. Yet I live, not I; but Christ lives in me (Gal 2:20).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.15-16":["And what follows, I have made you like a new threshing wagon, having sawing teeth, signifies this: that the preaching of the Gospel crushes the adverse powers and the spiritual wickedness that lifts itself up against the knowledge of God, which, according to the variety of their pride, are called mountains and hills; and when these are carried off and scattered after the manner of a whirlwind, let Israel exult and rejoice in the Lord, the Holy One of Israel. We can also say that the churchman, having the sawing teeth of the Gospel preaching on a new wagon, which works not in the oldness of the letter but in the newness of the spirit, crushes the hardest hearts of unbelievers, separating the wheat from the chaff and crushing the mountains and hills, that is, all the chiefs of the heretics, so that, crushed and ground down, they are reduced to dust and are taught that they are nothing, so that those who had been wrongly gathered together may be divided for their own good.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.17":["The needy and the poor seek water, and there is none; their tongue is parched with thirst. I the Lord will hear them; I the God of Israel will not forsake them. I will open rivers on the sloping hills, and fountains in the midst of the plains. I will make the desert into a pool of waters, and the trackless land into streams of waters. I will put in the wilderness the cedar and the thorn, and the myrtle, and the olive tree. I will set in the desert the fir, the elm and the box together, that they may see and know and consider and understand together that the hand of the Lord has done this, and the Holy One of Israel has created it. LXX: And the poor and the needy will exult; for they will seek water, and there will be none; their tongue has dried up for thirst. I the Lord God will hear them, the God of Israel, and I will not forsake them; but I will open rivers on the mountains, and fountains in the midst of the plains; I will make the desert into marshes, and the thirsty land into watercourses. I will put in a land without water the cedar, and the box, and the myrtle, and the cypress, and the poplar, that they may see and know and think and understand together that the hand of the Lord has done these things, and the Holy One of Israel has shown it. The people of the nations, poor and meager, which did not have the knowledge of the truth, seeks wholesome waters through various teachers and the diverse doctrines of the philosophers, and does not find them, because there are none; their tongue is parched with thirst, without the Law and the Prophets. For, according to the faith of the Gospel, it had spent all its substance on physicians (Luke 8), and could not be freed from the gore of idolatry and the blood of victims.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.18":["Therefore the Lord, the God of Israel, did not wholly forsake them, nor let them perish for ever, but opened rivers on the sloping hills, or on the mountains, and fountains in the midst of the plains. These rivers took their beginning from that river which is read of in the Psalms: The rush of the river gladdens the city of God (Ps 45 [46]:4); and in another place: The river of God is filled with waters. These waters and fountains flowed from that fountain who speaks through Jeremiah: They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water (Jer 2:13). And above in this same Prophet it is said: They will drink waters from the fountains of the Savior. Of these also it is sung in the Psalms: Bless the Lord God from the fountains of Israel (Ps 67 [68]:27). And he made, he says, the desert into a pool of waters, and the trackless land into streams of waters. Of these waters the Savior too spoke in the Gospel in mystical language (John 4:13-14): Whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will not thirst for ever; but the water that I shall give him will become in him a fountain of water springing up into eternal life. And again: If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink. Whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him, out of his belly will flow rivers of living water (ibid. 7:37-39). But this, says the Evangelist, he spoke of the Holy Spirit, whom those who believed in him were to receive.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.19-20":["Since, then, the multitude of the Church, once a desert, had been watered with life-giving waters, therefore, according to the Seventy, there spring up in it the cedar, and the box, and the myrtle, and the cypress, and the poplar; according to the Hebrew and the other translators, the cedar, the setta, and the myrtle, and the olive tree, and the fir, and the elm, and the box together. These varieties of trees signify the diversity of spiritual grace. And because the nature of the others is known to all, let us discuss only the Hebrew SETTA (שטה), which Theodotion translated thorn. It is a kind of tree growing in the desert, resembling the white thorn, of which all the wood of the ark and the furnishings of the tabernacle were made, which are called Settim (Exod 37): a wood that does not rot and is very light, and that surpasses all woods in solidity and beauty, as much in strength as in sheen. The cedar, too, and the cypress and the myrtle are of the finest fragrance and do not rot. The elm also and the poplar, or the box, are either joined to vines to wed them, or are most fit for various works. And all these are set together in the wilderness, so that not even one string of the Lord's lyre, and no power of the graces of the Church, may seem to be lacking: so that all may understand, and together know in their mind, that the hand of the Lord has accomplished all these things, so that in the dryness of the nations streams of virtues should be found, and in a land once deserted and full of salt the cedar and the cypress and other trees should spring up, whose loftiness and crown hastening toward heavenly things the olive tree should illuminate, which is the nourishment of light and the rest of those who labor.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.21":["Bring your judgment near, says the Lord; bring forward whatever you may have, said the king of Jacob. Let them approach and tell us whatever things are to come; tell the former things that have been, and we will set our heart on them and we will know their last end; and show us the things that are to come. Declare the things that are to come hereafter, and we will know that you are gods. Do good also, or evil, if you can, and let us speak and see together. Behold, you are of nothing: and your work is of that which is not; an abomination is he who has chosen you. LXX: Your judgment is near, says the Lord God; your counsels have come and drawn near, says the king of Jacob. Let them draw near and declare to us the things that are to come; or tell the former things that were, and we will apply our mind, and we will know what the last things are. Tell us also the things to come, and declare the things that are to come at the last, and we will know that you are gods. Do good and evil, and we will marvel, and we will see together whence you are and whence your work is: from a land of abominations they have chosen you. As we said above that there are two Jacobs and two Israels, of those who do not believe and of those who believe in the Lord the Savior, so, when the nations have been called, and rivers and fountains have burst forth in the once desert Church, and it is wonderfully leafy with a variety of trees, all of them full of abundance, those of the nations who refused to believe in Christ are summoned to judgment, to answer why they refused to see and know and consider and understand that the hand of the Lord has done these things and the Holy One of Israel has created all things. Let your idols approach, he says, which you think most mighty. Or bring forward whatever you may have in yourselves, and lay open your counsels: on what ground, with what mind, you refused to receive the manifest truth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.22-23":["And at the same time an apostrophe is made to the idols themselves, which are without sense and possess the faculty neither of hearing nor of answering. Tell us, he says, either things past or things to come, and show your power by the outcome of events. Now this signifies that after the coming of Christ all the idols fell silent. Where is Delphic Apollo, and Loxias, and the Delian, and the Clarian, and the other idols that promised knowledge of the future, which deceived the most powerful kings? Why could they foretell nothing of Christ, nothing of his Apostles, nothing of the ruin and abolition of their temples? If, then, they could not foretell their own destruction, how could they announce what concerned others, whether evil or good? But if anyone says that many things were foretold by idols, it must be known that they always joined falsehood to truth, and so tempered their sentences that whether something good or something evil happened, either could be understood. Such is that oracle to Pyrrhus king of the Epirotes: I say that you, son of Aeacus, the Romans can conquer. And to Croesus: Croesus, having crossed the Halys, will destroy the greatest kingdoms. There is also another sign that idols are not gods: that they can do neither good nor evil. Not that idols, or the demons that sit by idols, have not often done evil; but that, unless power has been granted to them by the Lord, they cannot do it. Indeed, in the Gospel they beg to have power over the herd of swine (Matt 8). And in Job we read that without the Lord's command he [the devil] could not destroy even the beasts and possessions of the holy man (Job 1). Nor is this to be wondered at in God's case, since even the Apostle Paul delivers some to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme (1 Tim 1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.23-24":["Or else it must be said that mute images of men, without sense, can do neither good nor evil, nor know things past nor things to come, because they are of nothing and of that which is not, and it is not so much they as he who worships them that is worthy of abomination. For the idols of the nations are silver and gold, the works of men's hands. They have a mouth, and will not speak (Ps 113 [115]:13). How then can they know and announce things to come, or relate things past, and do good or evil, when they have neither the sense of men nor that of brute animals? Nor does it matter of what material they seem to be made, since all are earthly.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.25-26":["The Hebrews interpret the one raised up from the North and coming from the rising of the sun as the Assyrian and the Persian, who came to Jerusalem and trod down all its princes; and none of the idols which they worshiped knew that this would come, but the Lord alone, who punished them for their sins.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.25":["I have raised up from the North, and he will come from the rising of the sun; he will call upon my name, and he will bring on rulers as mud, and as a potter treading the ground. Who has announced it from the beginning, that we may know; and from the first, that we may say: You are righteous? There is none announcing, nor foretelling, nor hearing your words. The first will say to Zion: Behold, they are here; and to Jerusalem I will give a bearer of good tidings. And I looked, and there was not even any of these who would enter into counsel, and, when asked, would answer a word. Behold, all are unrighteous, and their works are vain; their images are wind and emptiness. LXX: But I have raised up him who is from the North, and him who is from the rising of the sun; they will be called by my name. Let princes come, and as the clay of the potter, and as a potter treading clay, so you will be trodden down. For who will announce to you the things that are from the beginning, that we may know the things that are in the future, and we will say that it is true? There is none who foretells , nor who hears your words. I will give a beginning to Zion, and I will comfort Jerusalem on the way; for of the nations there is none, and of their images there was none who announced; and if I ask them, Where are you from? they will not answer me; for they are your makers, and they lead you astray in vain. He still speaks against the idols, and against those who after the coming of Christ refused to forsake them. And while they remain in error, he says that he himself has raised up the people of the nations from the North. For from the North evils will blaze forth upon all the inhabitants of the earth. And to Jeremiah it is said: What do you see, Jeremiah? And he answered: A pot set on fire, and its face from the face of the North (Jer 1:13). But they are raised up from the North, so that they may believe in him who has come from the rising of the sun, of whom we read above: Who raised up the righteous one from the East, or righteousness? And in another place: Behold a man, the Dayspring is his name (Zech 6:12). He will call them by my name, so that, forsaking their images, they may worship the one God. For he himself says in the Gospel: I have come in the name of my Father, and you did not receive me; another will come in his own name, and you will receive him (John 5:43). He will tread down the princes and rulers and all the pride of the world, and they will be made subject to him as clay to the potter, so that from them he may make one vessel for honor and another for dishonor (Rom 9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.26":["These things, which, according to prophetic custom, I say that I have done as though they were already past, or, according to Symmachus, which I promise as future, none of the demons and idols can foretell. Hence all the oracles fell silent, because there is no one who announces, no one who hears, while the once brutish hearts of the Gentiles have understood their error and forsaken false prophecies.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.27":["And the Lord will speak first to Zion, that is, to his Church, and will say to her: Behold, here are your sons, whom I have given to you through faith in me. And he means the Apostles, who preached the Gospel in the whole world. Zion and Jerusalem differ in name; but as there is one city, so also there is one Church.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.41.28-29":["Let all the nations, therefore, which follow the error of idols, be questioned by us, so that they may answer whence they are. And this will be the answer of them all: that all follow vanity and the winds, and worship the works of their own hands. Whatever we have said of the idols and the nations, let us refer to the doctrines of perversity, and to their chiefs, the heresiarchs: that while Christ announces the peace of the Church and shows them the way of truth, they follow error and are likened to wind and whirlwind.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.1":["Behold my servant, I will uphold him; my chosen one, in him my soul is well pleased. I have put my spirit upon him; he will bring forth judgment to the nations. He will not cry out, nor will he accept a person, nor will his voice be heard outside. A bruised reed he will not break, and smoking flax he will not quench; in truth he will bring forth judgment. He will not be sad nor troubled, until he sets judgment in the earth; and the islands will wait for his law. LXX: Jacob my child, I will take him up; Israel my chosen one, my soul has received him. I have put my spirit upon him; he will bring forth judgment to the nations; he will not cry out, nor will he let go, nor will his voice be heard outside. A broken reed he will not break, and smoking flax he will not quench; but in truth he will bring forth judgment. He will shine and will not be broken, until he sets judgment upon the earth; and in his name the nations will hope. Jacob and Israel are not found in the present passage, which the Evangelist Matthew too did not set down, following the Hebrew truth. We say this to the shame of those who despise our work. This has been discussed more fully in the little Commentaries on Matthew, and in the book which we recently wrote to Algasia. Meanwhile it must be noted that where it is written of the choir of the Apostles, they are called both Jacob and Israel and the seed of Abraham, to whom, as to men and servants, it is said accordingly: Do not fear, worm Jacob, and Israel small in number. But where the prophecy is of Christ, it is read without Jacob and Israel: Behold my servant, I will uphold him; my chosen one, in him my soul is well pleased. And in what follows: I have given you as a covenant of the race, as a light of the nations, to open the eyes of the blind, and to bring the bound out of their chains, and those who dwell in darkness out of the prison house. Nor is it to be wondered at if he is called a servant, made of a woman, made under the law (Gal 4); he who, though he was in the form of God, humbled himself, taking the form of a servant, and was found in fashion as a man (Phil 2). In him the soul of God was well pleased, seeing that all virtues were in him; nay rather, that he himself was the power and wisdom of God. I have put, he says, my spirit upon him: the Spirit who descended in the form of a dove. He will bring forth judgment to the nations, which before they did not know.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Behold my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved, and the rest. This is said through the prophet Isaiah in the person of the Father: I will put my Spirit upon him. The Spirit is put not upon the Word of God and upon the Only-begotten who came forth from the bosom of the Father, but upon him of whom it was said: Behold my servant.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.42.2":["He will not cry out: for he will be gentle and meek; and he will not accept a person in judgment, or he will not lift up (it is understood) his voice on high. Or, according to Symmachus, he will not be deceived, understanding all the snares of the devil; or, according to the Seventy, he will not let go, namely the people of the Jews, calling them to repentance. Nor will his voice be heard outside: for he did not preach the Gospel outside Galilee and Judea among the other nations. And if we read that he was in the borders of Tyre and Sidon (Matt 15), or in the region of Caesarea Philippi, which is now called Paneas, still it must be known that it is not written that he entered those cities themselves.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Nor will anyone hear his voice in the streets. Wide indeed and spacious is the way that leads to perdition, and many enter by it. Who are the many? Those who do not hear the voice of the Savior, because they are not on the narrow way but on the spacious one.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.42.3":["A bruised reed, or a broken one, he will not break. For he will be easy to appease toward all, and will give pardon to sinners, saying to the woman: Take heart, daughter, your sins are forgiven you (Luke 7:48). And smoking flax, or, as the others translated, dim and dark, he will not quench: those who were near to being quenched were preserved by the clemency of the Lord (which we have discussed in the works mentioned above concerning the Jews and the nations); but he will judge all things with truth, in no way fearing the scribes and Pharisees, whom he boldly called hypocrites (Matt 7, 15, 22, 23 throughout, and Luke 6, 12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","A battered reed he will not break, and smoldering flax he will not put out. He who does not hold out a hand to a sinner and does not carry the burden of his brother, he breaks the battered reed. And he who despises the small spark of faith in little ones, he puts out the smoldering flax. Christ did neither of these things; for he had come for this, to save what had perished.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.42.4":["But what follows, He will shine and will not be broken, until he sets judgment upon the earth, the Evangelist Matthew did not set down (Matt 12); or else the words in the middle, between judgment and judgment, were removed by a scribe's error. And it signifies that, rising from the dead, he enlightened all, and was not broken by death, until he set judgment upon the earth, he who said in the Gospel: For judgment I came into this world, that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind (John 9:39). For this we translated: He will not be sad, nor troubled, but he will keep an evenness of countenance at all times: what the philosophers falsely boast of Socrates, that he was never sadder or happier than usual. For this Aquila and Theodotion translated: He will not darken, and he will not run, until he sets judgment in the earth. And the sense is: he will deter no one by sadness of countenance, nor will he hasten to punishment, he who has reserved the truth of judgment for the last time. And what follows, and the islands will wait for his law, the Seventy set down more plainly: in his name the nations will hope; these are called islands in the Scriptures because they lie open on every side to the inroads of persecutors. And by his law let us understand not that which was given through Moses, but the Gospel: For out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem (above, Isa 2). Of this law Jeremiah too prophesies: Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and I will make a new covenant, not according to the covenant which I once made with their fathers (Jer 31:31-32).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.5":["Thus says the Lord God, who creates the heavens and stretches them out, who makes firm the earth and the things that spring from it, who gives breath to the people that is upon it, and spirit to those who tread on it: I the Lord have called you in righteousness, and have taken hold of your hand, and have kept you, and have given you as a covenant of the people, as a light of the nations, that you might open the eyes of the blind, and bring the bound out of confinement, out of the prison house those who sit in darkness. I am the Lord, that is my name; my glory I will not give to another, nor my praise to graven images. The former things, behold, they have come; new things too I declare; before they spring up, I will make you hear of them: It was uncertain who had said before: Behold my servant, I will uphold him; my chosen one, in him my soul is well pleased. I have put my spirit upon him; he will bring forth judgment to the nations. For after the condemnation of the idols the speech had burst out suddenly: Behold my servant, and the rest, which we interpreted as spoken in the person of the Father concerning Christ. So, lest he leave it ambiguous who had said the foregoing, he adds and says: Thus says the Lord God, who creates the heavens and stretches them out. Or, as the Seventy translated: Who made the heaven and fixed it. The Creator and Lord of the universe, therefore, himself promised the coming of his Christ, he who not only stretched out the heavens and fixed them, but also made the earth firm with its lofty mass, and all things that spring from it: so that his invisible things may be clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made (Rom 1), his eternal power also and divinity. He gave breath to all who dwell on the earth, by the first law of mortals: for God breathed into the face of Adam the spirit of life, and he became a living soul (Gen 2). But spirit he gave properly to those who tread on the earth and subject the wisdom of the flesh to their own power.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.6-7":["He, then, whose praises are so great, spoke these things to him whose law the islands and the nations await: I the Lord have called you in righteousness: of whom he himself speaks in the Gospel: Righteous Father, the world does not know you (John 17:25); so that he might be the God not only of the Jews but also of the nations. And I have taken hold of your hand, because whatever the Son works, the Father works. And I have kept you, you who yourself preserve all things. And I have given you as a covenant of the people of Israel, to whom I had promised beforehand that you would come. And as a light of the nations, which sat in darkness and in the shadow of death, that you might open the eyes of the blind, who before did not see God. And bring the bound out of confinement (Prov 5); for each one is bound fast by the cords of his own sins. And in the Gospel the Lord loosed him whom Satan had bound (Matt 8). Out of the prison house those who sit in darkness, those who were living in the night and gloom of error.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.8":["And what he adds, I am the Lord, that is my name; my glory I will not give to another, does not exclude the Son; for when he says in the Gospel, Father, glorify me with the glory which I had with you before the world was (John 17:5), the Father himself answered: I have both glorified it, and will glorify it. For he did not say: My glory I will give to no one, for if he had said that, he would have excluded the Son too; but my glory I will not give to another, besides you, to whom I have given it, to whom I will give it. Hence I greatly admire the propriety of holy Scripture, in that all the translators, who are accustomed to disagree in many other places, translated the word AHER (אחר) with one voice another. And that we may know that by this sentence not the Son but the idols are excluded, the following words bear witness: Nor my praise to graven images. For which the Seventy have: nor my powers to images. For since Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God, he contains in himself all the powers of the Father.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.9":["There follows: The former things, behold, they have come. And the sense is: What I have spoken, what I have promised through Moses and the Prophets, all has been fulfilled. But now I announce to you the Gospel, the calling of the nations, the passion of Christ, the newness of faith: so that as you see the former things fulfilled in fact, so you may also believe that the things I now promise will come,\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.10":["Sing to God a new song, his praise from the ends of the earth, you who go down to the sea, and its fullness, the islands and their inhabitants. Let the desert and its cities lift up; Kedar will dwell in houses; give praise, you inhabitants of Petra; from the top of the mountains they will cry out. They will give glory to the Lord, and will declare his praise in the islands. The Lord will go forth as a mighty one, as a man of war he will stir up zeal; he will shout and cry aloud; he will prevail over his enemies. I have always held my peace, I have kept silent, I have been patient; like a woman giving birth I will speak; I will scatter and swallow up at once. I will make the mountains and hills desolate, and I will dry up all their grass; and I will turn the rivers into islands, and I will dry up the pools. And I will lead out the blind into a way that they do not know, and in paths of which they are ignorant I will make them walk. I will turn darkness before them into light, and crooked things into straight. These words I have done for them, and I have not forsaken them. They have turned back; let them be confounded with confusion, who trust in a graven image, who say to a molten image: You are our gods. LXX: Sing to the Lord a new song; his dominion is glorified, and his name from the top of the earth; those who go down to the sea and sail it, the islands and their inhabitants. Rejoice, O desert, and its villages, the farmsteads and the inhabitants of Kedar. Those who dwell in Petra will rejoice; from the top of the mountains they will cry out. They will give glory to God; they will declare his powers in the islands. The Lord God of powers will go forth and will crush war; he will stir up zeal, and will cry out over his enemies with strength. I have held my peace from the beginning; shall I always hold my peace and endure? Like a woman giving birth I have been patient; I will burst forth and will dry up at once; I will lay waste mountains and hills, and I will dry up all their hay. And I will turn the rivers into islands, and I will dry up the marshes. And I will lead the blind by a way that they do not know, and I will make them tread paths of which they have been ignorant; I will turn darkness into light for them, and crooked things into straight. These words I will do for them, and I will not forsake them; but they have turned back. Be confounded with confusion, you who trust in graven images, who say to molten images: You are our gods. He who had said before, The former things, behold, they have come; new things too I declare; before they spring up, I will make you hear of them, and had promised that he would tell what they did not know, declares in the following discourse what those new things are, commanding the Apostles and apostolic men to sing a new song, in no way in the oldness of the letter, but in the newness of the spirit; and not only in the old Instrument, but in the new; and that his praise should reach even to the ends of the earth. For his going forth is from the highest heaven, and his circuit even to its height (Ps 18 [19]). The sound of the Apostles went out into all the earth, and their words to the ends of the world. Or, according to the Seventy: Glorify his name in the ends of the earth: so that in the whole world the name of Christ may be announced to the nations. And who these are who are to sing the new song, the following words declare: You who go down, he says, to the sea, and sail it, or the fullness of the sea. For Jesus, seeing the Apostles on the shore by the sea of Gennesaret mending their nets, called them and sent them into the great sea (Luke 5), so that from fishers of fish he might make them fishers of men; and they preached the Gospel from Jerusalem as far as Illyricum and the Spains, capturing in a short time even the might of the city of Rome itself. Or certainly they went down to the sea and sailed it, enduring the storms and persecutions of this world. By the islands too and their inhabitants understand either the diversity of the nations or the multitude of the Churches.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.11-12":["Let, he says, the desert and its cities lift up their voice, of which we have also spoken above. Or let the desert rejoice, and its villages, and Kedar, which was once an uninhabitable region beyond Arabia, of the Saracens; and the inhabitants of Petra, which itself is also a city of Palestine. And this signifies that the people of the nations, formerly a desert as regards the knowledge of God and bound by the errors of idolatry, is turned to the praises of the Lord. Or, because Kedar means darkness, and, according to the Apostle (1 Cor 10), the rock is Christ, all believers are commanded that those who were before in darkness, and now believe in the Lord the Savior, should cry aloud from the top of the mountains and openly preach Christ, those to whom it was said also above (cf. Isa 40:9): Go up on a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion; lift up your voice with strength, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem. And I will set his glory in the islands, of which we spoke before.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.13":["And the prophetic word describes the glorious coming of the Savior, of which the Apostle Paul also speaks: According to the illumination of the glory of the great God and our Savior Jesus Christ (2 Cor 4; 2 Tim 1:10); and compares him to a most mighty man, who is about to fight against his adversaries and to stir up zeal. Of this it was prophesied also in the Song of Deuteronomy: They have moved me to jealousy with that which was not God, and have provoked me with their idols; and I will move them to jealousy with a nation that is not, and with a most foolish nation I will provoke them (Deut 32:21).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.14-15":["He will also shout and cry aloud against his enemies, openly rebuking their unbelief and declaring with a loud cry: I have held my peace, I have always kept silent, I have been patient, or, as the Seventy translated: I have held my peace; shall I always hold my peace (Eccl 3:7)? So that what is said through Solomon may be fulfilled: A time to keep silence, and a time to speak. And the sense is: For a long time I have often borne you when you sinned; but because I had kept silent before, I will no longer keep silent. And as a woman giving birth pours forth the infant into the light, and makes open what was before held shut in her womb, so I will now bring forth my pain and the forbearance which I always had over your crimes, and I will scatter your counsels; and at one time I will swallow up the whole nation, and all the pride of your mountains and the swelling of your hills. And the grass, of which it was said above, Truly the people is grass, that is, princes as much as the common herd, I will reduce to a desert. This was added in the Seventy, which had passed over these words in silence, from the edition of Theodotion (see above, Isa 40:7). And when I have dried you up and destroyed you from head to foot, then I will make the rivers of my teaching run in the islands of the nations, and I will reduce your pools or marshes to dryness, so that among the nations there may be the knowledge of the Scriptures, and among you the dryness of teaching.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.16":["And I will lead the blind by the way which before they did not know, of whom we also read above: I have given you as a covenant of the people, as a light of the nations, that you might open the eyes of the blind. These will be led by the way of which Christ speaks: I am the way, or by the way of the knowledge of God; and I will make them walk by the paths of the Prophets. Then their darkness will be changed into light, and crooked things will be turned into straight, so that they may understand what they read, and with the eyes of the heart behold the clear light of Christ in the Old Testament. And at the same time he adds: These words which I have done, or I will do for them: no longer promising things to come, but rendering what I had promised before.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.17":["But while I say these things, the people of the Jews has turned back, so as not to believe him who promises, and to be confounded in its errors, and to neglect the pledge of God, having before believed in idols. Or, when the fullness of the nations has come in, then the people of Israel too will be converted (Rom 11), and will repent of its error, through which it had before served idols. Or certainly, after the calling of the nations, he returns to the beginning, so as to say that all the nations which refused to believe the Gospel are to be confounded in their idols.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.18-19":["You deaf, hear, and you blind, look, that you may see. Who is blind but my servant? and deaf but he to whom I have sent my messengers? Who is blind but he who has been sold? and who is blind but the servant of the Lord? You who see many things, will you not keep them? you who have open ears, will you not hear? And the Lord willed to sanctify him, and to magnify the law and exalt it. But this is a people plundered and laid waste; all are the snare of young men, and they are hidden in prison houses; they have become a prey, and there is none to rescue; a plunder, and there is none to say: Give back. Who is there among you who will hear this, who will attend and listen for the time to come? Who gave Jacob up to plunder, and Israel to those who lay waste? Was it not the Lord himself, against whom we have sinned? And they would not walk in his ways, and they did not hear his law. And he poured out upon him the indignation of his fury, and strong war; and he burned him round about, and he did not know it; and he set him on fire, and he did not understand. LXX: You deaf, hear, and you blind, look up, that you may see. Who is blind but my children, and deaf but those who rule over them? Who is blind but he who has received; and the servants of God have been blinded. You have often seen, and have not kept watch; your ears are open, and you have not heard. The Lord God willed that he should be justified, and should magnify praise. And I saw, and the people was laid waste and plundered. For there is a snare in the chambers everywhere, and in the houses together, in which they hid them. They became a plunder, and there was none to rescue the prey, and there was none to say: Give back. Who is there among you who will hear these things and know the things to come? Who gave Jacob up to plunder, and Israel to those who lay waste? Was it not God, against whom they sinned? And you would not walk in his ways, nor hear his law. And he brought upon them the anger of his fury, and war prevailed against them, and those who burned them round about; and each one did not know, nor did they lay it to heart. Lest anyone should think that what is said, You deaf, hear, and you blind, look, that you may see, belongs to the people of the nations, which before was deaf and blind (as the Jews strive to prove by a foolish interpretation), the prophetic word itself shows who ought to be understood as the deaf and the blind. Who, he says, is blind but my servant, and deaf but he to whom I have sent my messengers? There is no doubt that he means the Prophets. Who is blind but he who has been sold by his sins? Who is blind but he who formerly was the servant of the Lord?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.20":["To him it is said: O Israel, you who see many things, and have very many Prophets through whom to know the commandments of God, will you not keep what has been commanded you? You who have open ears, will you not hear what is said? Of whom we also read above: Hearing you will hear, and will not understand; and seeing you will look, and will not see; for the heart of this people has been made fat (Isa 6:9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.21":["The Lord, he says, willed to sanctify him, and to magnify his Law, and to exalt and raise up the people lying prostrate.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.22":["But he refused to do the will of God, and therefore he was plundered and laid waste by his adversaries, whom we ought to understand either as demons or as enemies. All are the snare of young men, and they are hidden in prison houses. Or, as the Seventy translated, There is a snare in the chambers everywhere, and in the houses together, in which they hid them: signifying the scribes and Pharisees, who deceived the wretched common people and everywhere laid snares against the Lord the Savior and his Apostles (Luke 11), having the key of knowledge, neither entering themselves, and forbidding others who wished to enter. And beautifully he called their breasts, in which the worst thoughts dwelt, prisons. Therefore they became a prey and a plunder, and there was none to free them and to speak a word on their behalf.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.23-24":["And at the same time the Prophet exhorts them that, if all cannot hear, at least a few may know the things to come and understand from whom they have suffered these things, and may recognize the causes of their devastation, they who would neither hear nor do what had been commanded in the Law.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.42.25":["Therefore the Lord poured out upon them all his anger and an indignation full of fury, so that he who before had kept silent would no longer hold his peace, and would chastise them not with words but with punishments and torments; and a most mighty war would seize them, which they could in no way escape, showing the ferocity of the Romans. And though he has been burned on every side and has no soundness in him, yet he does not understand the cause of his punishment: that he has been punished because he did not receive the Son of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.1":["And now thus says the Lord who creates you, Jacob, and forms you, Israel: Do not fear, for I have redeemed you, and I have called you by your name: you are mine. When you pass through the water , I will be with you, and the rivers will not cover you. When you walk into the fire, you will not be burned, and the flame will not burn in you; for I am the Lord your God, the Holy One of Israel, your Savior; I have given Egypt as your propitiation, Ethiopia and Saba for you. Since you have become honorable in my eyes and glorious, I have loved you, and I will give men for you, and peoples for your life. Do not fear, for I am with you; from the East I will bring your seed, and from the West I will gather you. I will say to the North: Give; and to the South: Do not hold back; bring my sons from afar, and my daughters from the end of the earth. And everyone who calls upon my name, I have created him for my glory, and I have formed him, and I have made him. Bring forth the people that is blind and has eyes, that is deaf and has ears. All the nations are gathered together, and the tribes are assembled. Who among you will declare this, and will make us hear the former things? Let them bring their witnesses and be justified, and let them hear and say: Truly you are my witnesses, says the Lord, and my servant whom I have chosen, that you may know and believe me, and understand that I myself am he. Before me no god was formed, and after me there will be none. I am, I am the Lord, and besides me there is no savior. I have declared, and I have saved; I have made it heard, and there was no stranger among you. You are my witnesses, says the Lord, and I am God, and from the beginning I myself am, and there is none who can deliver from my hand; I will work, and who will turn it back? LXX: And now thus says the Lord who made you, Jacob, and created you, Israel: Do not fear, for I have redeemed you; I have called you by your name, you are mine; and if you pass through water, I am with you, and the rivers will not overwhelm you; and if you pass through fire, you will not be burned; the flame will not consume you; for I am the Lord your God, the Holy One of Israel, your savior. I have made Egypt your exchange, and Ethiopia and Syene for you. Since you have become honorable, and have been glorified in my sight, I also have loved you, and I will give men for you, and princes for your head. Do not fear, for I am with you; from the East I will bring your seed, and from the West I will gather you. I will say to the North: Bring; and to the Southwest: Do not hold back; bring my sons from a far land, and my daughters from the ends of the earth, all who are called by my name; for in my glory I have created him, and I have fashioned him, and I have made him; and I have brought forth the blind people, who have eyes and are blind, and their ears are deaf. All the nations are gathered together, and their princes have assembled. Who will declare these things? or who will declare to you the things that are from the beginning? Let them bring their witnesses, and be justified, and let them hear and speak true things. Be witnesses for me, and I am a witness, says the Lord God, and my child whom I have chosen, that you may know and believe and understand that I am. Before me there was no other god, and after me there will be none. I am God, and besides me there is no Savior. I have declared, and I have saved; I have reproached, and there was no stranger among you. You are my witnesses, and I am the Lord God from the beginning, and there is none who can deliver out of my hands. I will do it, and who will turn it back? The Hebrews interpret these things of the second coming of the Savior, when, after the fullness of the nations, all Israel is to be saved. But we in no way think that the promise is made to those to whom it was said above: Who is blind but my children, and who are deaf but those who rule over them? and the servants of God have been blinded. And again: The people has been laid waste and plundered, and things like these. Finally he adds: He poured out upon him the indignation of his fury, and strong war; and he burned him round about, and he did not know it; and he set him on fire, and he did not understand. Therefore, as we said above that there are two Jacobs and two Israels, one fleshly and the other spiritual, of those who refused to believe in the Savior and of those who received the Son of God, so this too is now to be understood: that the choir of the Apostles is summoned, and the first Church of Christ gathered from the people of the Jews, to understand its Creator and Maker, who is the founder both of its soul and of its body; and not to fear persecutors, because it has been redeemed by the blood of Christ, who has called it by his name and, out of intimacy, calls it in a special way his own people; but to preach without hesitation and to dread no dangers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.2":["And what he adds, waters and rivers, and fire and flames, describes the onslaught and fury of the persecutors, who begrudge the salvation of the nations and do not want the word of the Gospel to be preached. The Jews, however, would have the Egyptians understood by the waters, the Babylonians by the rivers, the Macedonians by the fire, the Romans by the flame.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.3":["And he commands them not to fear for this reason, that the Lord God, their Savior and the Holy One of Israel, is with them, who has made them preach to Egypt, Ethiopia and Syene, and to the furthest bounds of the world. For Syene the other translators put SABA (סבא), whence came the queen of the South, who came to hear the wisdom of Solomon (1 Kgs 10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.4":["Therefore the choir of the Apostles has become honorable in the sight of God and beloved by him; and the salvation of all the men and peoples who have been called through them to the Gospel will also be reckoned to the salvation of their own souls.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.5-7":["Hence he adds and says: Do not fear, I am with you, I who say to you in the Gospel: Behold, I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the age (Matt 28:20). For from the East and from the West I will bring your sons, to rest in the bosom of Abraham; and from all parts of the world a people of my sons and daughters will be gathered, who either call upon my name or are called by my name, so that they are called Christians, to the glory of their founder.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.8-9":["And what follows, Bring forth the people that is blind and has eyes, that is deaf and has ears, many think is said of the people of the nations, who through the teaching of the Apostles began to hear and to see. But let us understand this too of scattered Israel, which was called through the Apostles and believed first; to whom Paul also says: It was necessary that the word of God be preached first to you (Acts 13:46). And the Lord himself in the Gospel: Go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 10:6). Then follows: All the nations are gathered together, and the tribes are assembled, that is, together with the people that believed from among the Jews, so that the calling may be twofold, of the circumcision and of the uncircumcision. And what is said, Bring forth, is a word addressed to the Son. Or, according to the Seventy, the Father speaks of the Son, that he has brought forth his blind and deaf people, and joined them to the nations, and made of them many princes of the Church. And the prophet marvels that no one among the nations could foreknow these things, nor know the counsels of God, but only his people, which had received the Law and had the prophets: For God is known in Judea; in Israel his name is great (Ps 75 [76]:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.10":["Or else God himself is the witness and performer of his own words, and so is his child and servant whom he has chosen. There is no doubt that he means Christ, to whom he says also above: It is a great thing for you to be called my child. And all these things will take place so that the truth may be preached to the world, and that those whose mind was before brutish and senseless may know and believe and understand that besides the one God there is no other God, neither before nor after. For the founder of the times never has a beginning, since this very word, ever, belongs to time. And as he has now said, I am a witness, says the Lord God, and the child whom I have chosen, the Lord saying this same thing in the Gospel: The testimony of two men is true. I am he who bears witness of myself, and the Father who sent me (John 8:17-18), so too we must think of his divinity: that there is no God but he who speaks and his child whom he has chosen. And beautifully, besides God the Father there is no other God, because Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God (1 Cor 1), who says in the Gospel: I am in the Father, and the Father is in me (John 14:11). For as the one Lord Christ does not take from the Father his being Lord, so the one God the Father does not take from the Son his being God, he who in the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God; he was in the beginning with God (John 1:1-2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And according to what he commands in another place: Be my witnesses, and I am a witness, says the Lord God, and the servant whom I have chosen, the Father with the Son and the Holy Spirit is a witness for us, so that in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may stand. And it seems to me that the sentence stands rather in this way, and that the truth is confirmed under these three witnesses, rather than through the letter. For there were two witnesses against Susanna, and against the Lord Savior himself, and yet the word did not stand in their mouth. Against Naboth, too, almost the whole city bore witness, and the agreement of most wicked witnesses had not the firmness of truth but the conspiracy of a crime.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah"],"ISA.43.11":["And what follows, And besides me there is no Savior, shows that the Son saves all things in the Father. Of him the same prophet testifies: And the Lord will send them a Savior, who will save them (Isa 19). For God, who is wise and strong, cannot be without his own wisdom and power.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.12-13":["Since, then, I foretold from the beginning that these things would come, there will be no one who can refuse my will, nor indeed make void what I shall have done. These things are said indeed to Jacob and Israel, but they refer to the apostolic choir and to all of the people of the Jews who have been willing to believe in Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.14-15":["Thus says the Lord, your redeemer, the Holy One of Israel: For your sake I have sent to Babylon, and I have pulled down all the bars, and the Chaldeans glorying in their ships. I am the Lord, your Holy One, the Creator, Israel, your King. LXX: Thus says the Lord God, who redeemed you, the Holy One of Israel: For your sake I will send to Babylon, and I will rouse up all the fugitives, and the Chaldeans will be bound in ships. I am the Lord God, the Holy One of Israel, who have shown Israel your king. According to the Hebrew, the prophetic word is still directed to Israel: that their redeemer, the Lord and the Holy One of Israel, has spoken these things to them: For your sake, you who preach the Gospel with me, to whom I said above, Do not fear, for I am with you, who are witnesses of my will, and who announce my child to an unbelieving world as a second witness, I have sent my Son into Babylon and the confusion of this world. And I have pulled down all its bars, which in Hebrew are called BARIHIM באריחים, and which Theodotion translated strong ones. And the Chaldeans, he says (understand ἀπὸ κοινοῦ I have pulled down), who gloried in their ships: namely those who, like ships, were tossed about among the idols. Of the Chaldeans no one doubts that they signify demons. I the Lord have foretold these things that were to come, I who am your Creator and the king of Israel. But according to the Seventy the sense is far different: I the Lord, who have delivered you from dangers, and the Holy One of Israel: for your sake I will send to Babylon the king of the Medes and Persians, and I will make its inhabitants flee; and the Chaldeans who had taken you captive will be carried in bonds across the Caspian Sea to other nations. I the Lord have decreed these things that are to come, I who have shown that Israel would be the king of believers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.16-17":["Thus says the Lord, who made a way in the sea, and a path in the rushing waters; who brought forth the chariot and the horse, the army and the strong man: together they have fallen asleep, and they will not rise again; they are crushed like flax, they are quenched. Do not remember the former things, and do not look upon the things of old. Behold, I am doing new things; now they will spring forth; surely you will know them. I will make a way in the desert, and rivers in the trackless land. The beast of the field will glorify me, the dragons and the ostriches; for I have given waters in the desert, rivers in the trackless land, to give drink to my people, my chosen one. This people I have formed for myself; they will tell my praise. LXX: For thus says the Lord, who gives a way in the sea, and a path in the mighty water; who brings forth chariots and horses and a most mighty multitude. But they have fallen asleep, and they will not rise again; they are quenched like quenched flax. Do not remember the former things, and do not consider the old things. Behold, I will do new things, which will now spring forth, and you will know them; and I will make a way in the desert, and rivers in the waterless land. The beasts of the field will bless me, the sirens and the daughters of the ostriches, for I have given water in the desert, and rivers in the waterless land, to give drink to my chosen race, my people, whom I have acquired, that it may tell of my powers. The Lord, who destroyed and cast down Babylon, and pulled down its mightiest from their power, and made captives of all the Chaldeans who were sailing prosperously on the waves of this world, he himself found a way in the rushing waters of the Red Sea, so that his people, freed from Egypt, might pass over. Or, he who made a way in the Red Sea, he himself also found a path in the rushing waters of the river Jordan, so that both the going out from Egypt and the entrance into the land of promise might have a miracle. He sank into the deep the chariots and horses and the whole army of Pharaoh, who fell asleep in perpetual sleep. They were crushed and quenched like flax, in a brief space of time, in a point and a moment. For flax, not yet caught by the fire, is at once quenched because of the lightness of its substance, and dissolves into ash.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.18-19":["Therefore I command you this: that among my signs and wonders, by which Babylon, the mightiest city, was overthrown, and by which a way was opened for my people in the Red Sea and in the Jordan, you should in no way remember the old things, since in the Gospel I am going to do far greater things, in comparison with which past things ought to be passed over in silence. For no longer will I find a way in the Red Sea, but in the desert of the whole world. Nor will one river or fountain burst forth from the rock, but many rivers, to refresh not bodies, as before, but thirsting souls; and that will be fulfilled which we read above: You will drink waters from the fountains of the Savior (cf. Isa 12:3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.20-21":["Then what never was done will be done: all the beasts and dragons and ostriches that dwelt in the wilderness of the nations, and were like beasts in the blood of idolatry and the savagery of their manners, will glorify me and praise me together. For dragons, which Theodotion alone, as it is written in the Hebrew, called THANNIM (תנים), the others translated Sirens: monstrous creatures which by their sweet and deadly song cast sailors headlong to be torn apart by Scylla's dogs. And this signifies that those who were formerly given over to pleasure and luxury are turned to the service of the Lord; although it is better to understand dragons, since ostriches are joined to them, so that, since he was speaking once of the beasts of the wilderness, he set down these animals, which are at home in the desert. They will praise me, he says, and will glorify me, the beasts of the field, and the dragons, and the ostriches, because I have given waters in the desert of the nations, and rivers in the dryness of the peoples, so that my people might drink, which I have chosen for myself, or my chosen race, and the people which I have acquired with my blood, that it might tell my praises and my powers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.22":["You have not called upon me, Jacob, nor have you labored in me, Israel. You have not offered me the ram of your burnt offering, and with your victims you have not glorified me. I have not made you serve with offering, nor have I given you labor with incense. You have not bought me sweet cane with silver, and with the fat of your victims you have not made me drunk. But you have made me serve in your sins; you have given me labor in your iniquities. LXX: Not now have I called you, Jacob, nor have I made you labor, Israel. You have not brought me the sheep of your burnt offering, nor have you magnified me with your victims; nor have I made you serve with sacrifices, nor have I given you labor with incense; nor have you bought me incense with silver, nor have I desired the fat of your sacrifices; but in your sins I have defended you, and in your iniquities. While the beasts of the field, the dragons and the ostriches, praise me, you, Jacob, in no way wished to call upon me, nor afterward to labor so as to amend your sin by repentance.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.23-24":["And as for your thinking that you have offered me victims and sacrificed rams, know that they were not accepted by me, because they were shared by me and the idols. And he repeats that sense of which he had spoken above: To what purpose is the multitude of your victims to me, says the Lord? I am full of the burnt offering of rams, and of the fat of lambs, and the blood of bulls and goats I do not want. For I have not, he says, required anything precious from you, so as to make you labor in preparing it. I have not exacted offerings from you, nor sought frankincense, nor sweet cane, nor incense and spices of various kinds, with whose smoke you might make me drunk and sate me. But obedience, which is above sacrifice, and the sacrifice of which David says in the Psalms, A sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit; a contrite and humbled heart God does not despise (Ps 50 [51]:19): these I wanted, these I sought. But you have made me serve and labor in your sins, so that I was compelled to say: I have labored in bearing; and I can no longer bear you. Hence God says the same thing also through Hosea: What shall I do to you, Ephraim? Shall I protect you, Israel (Hos 11:8)? What shall I do to you? Shall I make you like Admah, and like Zeboiim? But what the Seventy set down, but in your sins I have defended you, and in your iniquities, can be joined to what precedes thus: that it is God's labor and weariness to defend sinners.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.25":["I am, I am he who blots out your iniquities for my own sake, and I will not remember your sins. Put me in remembrance, that we may be judged together; tell, if you have anything, that you may be justified. Your first father sinned, and your interpreters have transgressed against me. And I have defiled the holy princes; I have given Jacob to slaughter, and Israel to blasphemy. LXX: I am, I am he who blots out your iniquities, and I will not remember them. But you, remember, and let us be judged. Tell your iniquities first, that you may be justified. Your fathers sinned, and your princes have acted unjustly against me, and the princes have defiled my holy things. And I have given Jacob up to destruction, and Israel to reproach. You, he says, Jacob and Israel, have made me labor in your sins, and I could scarcely carry the burdens of your iniquities; you whom I in no way call my servants or my children, but address by the bare names of Jacob and Israel, so as to expose and reprove you as sinners. But I, for my own sake, because I am merciful and patient and of many mercies, will blot out all your iniquities in the sprinkling and the blood of the new Testament; I will blot out the old bond that was written against you; and I will no longer remember your sins, which, if you are willing to believe, I am going to forgive you in baptism.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.26":["Put me, then, in remembrance: if you have anything righteous to answer me, I will gladly receive it, that we may be judged together, and you may convict me of not having done for you what I ought to have done. This sense we read more fully in Micah: My people, what have I done to you, and in what have I troubled you? Answer me: for I brought you up out of the land of Egypt, and freed you from the house of bondage, and sent before your face Moses and Aaron and Miriam. And in the fiftieth Psalm David says to God: That you may be justified in your words, and may prevail when you are judged (Ps 50 [51]:5). Tell, then, if you have anything, that you may be justified. And the sense is: I will not speak first against you, lest you say you have been overwhelmed by a multitude of words; but if you have anything righteous, speak for yourself, so that you may be seen to endure undeservedly the things you suffer.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to the Seventy, who added on their own, Tell your iniquities first, that you may be justified, God calls them to repentance, so that they may understand their crimes and sins and obtain pardon. For it is written also in another place: The righteous man is his own accuser at the beginning of his speech (Prov 18:17).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.43.27-28":["And that you may know that I have mercy on you not for your merit but for my own clemency, I will go back to your fathers and forebears, so that you may understand that you were begotten of sinners: Your first father sinned in the wilderness, namely the whole people of Israel. Or else the author of your race, Abraham, is convicted of having sinned when, as the Lord promised the land of promise to his seed, he answered: Whereby shall I know that I shall possess it? And your interpreters, he says, have transgressed against me (Gen 15:8): Aaron and Moses at the water of contradiction, who spoke as mediators between me and you (Exod 17). And that we may understand that this is not a forced exposition, there follows: And I have defiled the holy princes, of whom it is also sung in the psalm: Their judges have been swallowed up beside the rock (Ps 140 [141]:6). These he says he has defiled for this reason, that they did not enter the land of promise. And I have given Jacob to slaughter, and Israel to blasphemy, so that none of those who had come out of Egypt, except two, entered Judea, but their carcasses lay in the wilderness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And their princes and fathers are said to have violated the holy things of the Lord, not serving the Law of God, but seeking the traditions and commandments of men; on account of whom Jacob perished, and Israel was given to reproach, cast out of his own province, an exile and a stranger throughout the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.1":["And now hear, Jacob my servant, and Israel, whom I have chosen. Thus says the Lord, who makes you and forms you from the womb, your helper: Do not fear, my servant Jacob, and you, Most Upright, whom I have chosen. For I will pour out waters upon the thirsty, and streams upon the dry ground; I will pour out my spirit upon your seed, and my blessing upon your offspring. And they will spring up among the grass, like willows beside flowing waters. This one will say, I am the Lord's; and that one will call in the name of Jacob; and this one will write with his hand, To the Lord; and he will be likened in the name of Israel. LXX: But now hear, Jacob my child, and Israel whom I have chosen. I am the Lord God, who made you and fashioned you from the womb; you will yet have help. Do not fear, my child Jacob, and beloved Israel whom I have chosen; yet I will give water in thirst to those who walk in the waterless land; I will put my spirit upon your seed, and my blessings upon your sons. And they will spring up like grass in the midst of water, and like the willow beside flowing waters. This one will say, I am God's; this one will cry out in the name of Jacob. And another will write on his hand, I am God's, and will cry out in the name of Israel. Where the people of the Jews is accused for its unbelief and blasphemy, it is called by the bare words, without any dignity of name: You have not called upon me, Jacob, nor have you labored in me, Israel; and again: I have given Jacob to slaughter, and Israel to blasphemy (above, Isa 43:22, 28). But where he speaks to the choir of the Apostles, which is from the Jews, the privileges of names are also added: Hear, Jacob my servant, and Israel whom I have chosen: so that servitude comes first, election second.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.2":["Thus says the Lord, your maker and former, who helped you from the womb (Gen 25), so that while still in your mother's womb you seized your brother's heel; or who at the beginning of the newborn Church preserved you from the persecutors. Do not fear their cruelty, Jacob my servant, and Most Upright, whom I have chosen. He calls Israel by another name; for the Hebrew word ISURUN (ישרון) the others translated εὐθύτατον, or εὐθῆ, that is, most upright and upright; the Seventy alone most beloved, adding Israel of their own. For properly, according to the Hebrews and the faithfulness of the letters, ISRAEL (ישראל) means upright of God; whereas a man seeing God lies not in the letters but in the sound of the word. Hence also the book of Genesis is called εὐθεῖς, that is, of the righteous: Abraham, Isaac and Israel.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.3":["Do not fear, then, Jacob and Israel, because I will pour out waters upon the thirsty and the dry ground, of which it has often been said: And I will pour out, or I will put my spirit upon your seed, and my blessing upon your offspring, which will be born again of water and the Holy Spirit in baptism. This the Savior also promised in the Gospel, saying: If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink. And at once it is added: But this he said of the Holy Spirit, whom those who believed were to receive (John 7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.4":["He also compares those who are born again in baptism to green grass, and to the willow which springs up beside flowing waters and, against the nature of things, bears fruit, though before it was barren, or rather its seed, taken as food, makes people barren. This indeed is read in the first psalm: And he will be like a tree which is planted by the streams of waters, which will give its fruit in its season, and its leaf will not fall. Others understand by the grass and the willows springing up anew a twofold calling: so that the people of the nations is in the grass, and in the willows those of Israel who believed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.5":["And at the same time he describes the variety of believers: One will say, I am the Lord's, he who trusts that the works of righteousness are in him; another will call (understand: sinners to repentance) in the name of Jacob, so that they too may supplant vices and sins. Another will write with his hand, I am God's; or, as the Seventy translated, he will write on his hand, I am God's, so that he glories in being a soldier in the new service of Christ's warfare. And he will be likened in the name of Israel. For not all from Israel, but a great part from the multitude of the nations will be likened in the name of Israel, so as to receive the Law and the Prophets and all the graces of the Holy Spirit which were promised to the people of Israel.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.6":["Thus says the Lord, the King of Israel, and his redeemer, the Lord of hosts: I am the first and I am the last, and besides me there is no God. Who is like me? Let him call and declare, and set out the order for me, since I established the ancient people; let them declare to them the things to come and the things that shall be. Do not fear, nor be troubled: from that time I have made you hear, and I have declared; you are my witnesses. Is there a God besides me, and a former whom I have not known? The makers of an idol are all nothing, and their most beloved things will not profit them. They themselves are their witnesses, that they do not see nor understand, so that they may be confounded. Who has formed a god, and cast a graven image that is profitable for nothing? Behold, all its partners will be confounded; for the craftsmen are of men; they will all assemble, they will stand, and they will be afraid, and they will be confounded together. The ironsmith has worked with the file; with coals and with hammers he has formed it, and he has worked with the arm of his strength. He will hunger and faint, he will not drink water, and he will grow weary. The carpenter has stretched out the rule he has formed it with the plane; he has made it with the corner tools, and turned it with the compass; and he has made the image of a man, like a handsome man dwelling in a house. He cut down cedars, he took the holm oak and the oak that had stood among the trees of the forest. He planted a pine, which the rain nourished; and it became fuel for men; he took from them and was warmed; he kindled it and baked bread. But of the rest he has made a god, and has worshiped it; he has made a graven image, and has bowed down before it. Half of it he burned in the fire, and on half of it he ate flesh. He cooked a stew and was filled, and was warmed, and said: Aha! I am warm, I have seen the fire. But the rest of it he made into a god and a graven image for himself; he bows down before it and worships it, and prays to it, saying: Deliver me, for you are my god. They have not known, nor understood; for they have forgotten, so that their eyes may not see, so that they may not understand with their heart. They do not consider in their mind, nor know, nor perceive, so as to say: Half of it I burned in the fire, and on its coals I baked bread; I cooked flesh and ate, and of the rest of it shall I make an idol? Shall I fall down before the trunk of a tree? Part of it is ash; a foolish heart has worshiped it; and he has not delivered his soul, nor will he say: Perhaps there is a lie in my right hand. After the preaching of the Apostles, the calling of the nations, the coming of the Savior, and the outpouring of the Holy Spirit, whom he promised he would give to all believers, when, according to the variety of their merits, one will say, I am the Lord's; another will call in the name of Jacob; another will write with his hand that he is the Lord's, and, though sprung from the unbelieving crowd of the Gentiles, will be likened in the name of Israel, so that, forsaking idols, he may be a worshiper of the one God, another περικοπὴ begins, which we have set out whole at once, so as not to divide the continuity of a single sense. And because the Seventy in this passage, except for a few words, do not differ from the Hebrew, we have been content with our own edition, and when it has been understood, the others too will be known. The discourse is against the idolaters of that time when the prophet Isaiah was announcing things to come to the peoples, and it reproves those who, despising the religion of Almighty God, bow down to wooden images and worship the works of their own hands. Let us run through it, then, point by point. Thus says the Lord, the King of Israel, which is to believe in me, and his redeemer, which will receive the coming of my Son: the Lord of hosts, and of powers, and the Almighty. For this is what SABAOTH (צבאות) means in Hebrew. I am the first, and I am the last, I am the Α and the Ω; and besides me there is no God, because the child whom I have chosen is God in me. Of him I said above: Behold my child, whom I have chosen; my chosen one, whom my soul has received; he will bring forth judgment to the nations; and in his name the nations will hope. Nor does he say that he is alone, but that besides his own power and wisdom there is no outside God; and condemning the opinion of many gods, and images, he says: Who, he says, is like me?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.7":["Let him call the things that are not as though they were, and set out the order of my creation, I who have balanced all things by reason, since I made man upon the earth. Nor do I want this only, but I also ask for knowledge of things to come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.8":["Hence you, Israel, whose king and redeemer I am, do not fear the images, which you learned on Mount Sinai are nothing. Or is there perhaps another Creator, whom I have not known? Or besides this is there another world, to show the power of an unknown God?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.9-11":["And not only the things that are made, but those also who make them, will be held as nothing. And when the time of vengeance has come, the works of their hands will in no way be able to free them; blind and senseless, they confound their own craftsmen.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.9-20":["And the prophetic discourse on the mockery of idols is woven more fully, but these things are easy to understand and do not call for a long-winded, nay, a superfluous exposition. On this Flaccus too writes in a Satire, deriding the images of the nations (Satires, book 1, 8): Once I was a fig-tree trunk, a useless log, when the craftsman, uncertain whether to make a stool or a Priapus, preferred that I be a god: thence I am a god, of thieves and birds the greatest terror. . . . . . . . . . . . . And whatever has been said of idols can be referred also to the chiefs of the heresies, who with a crafty heart put together the images of their doctrines and lies, and venerate what they know to have been feigned by themselves. Nor is their own error enough for them, unless they have deceived all the simple into worshiping them. They think gain to be godliness, and devour the houses of widows (1 Tim 6), and, abusing the ignorance of the common people, with the art of dialectic, as with adze and auger, file and plane, they form their god, and forge it with the hammer, and gild it with the charm of rhetorical speech: whose god is the belly, and whose glory is in their shame (Phil 3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.12-17":["For who could believe that a god is formed by adze, file and auger and hammer? And that images are either cast in the coals, or by rule, plane and corner tools and compass suddenly rise up into gods? Especially since the cheapness of the art is shown by the hunger and thirst of the craftsman. For a wooden statue is made, expressing the human form, and the more beautiful it is, the more august a god it is thought. It is set in a shrine and shut up in an eternal prison, the thing that grew for a long time in the forests and, according to the variety of trees, was a cedar or a holm oak or an oak or a pine. And in a wondrous manner its chips and shavings are thrown into the fire to warm the god's craftsman and to cook various stews; but the other part is formed into a god, so that, when the work is finished, its maker may worship it and invoke the help of his own work.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.18-20":["Nor does he understand or consider, nay, he does not see with the eyes either of the flesh or of the mind, that that cannot be God of which a part has been burned in the fire, nor can the divine majesty be made by the hand of a man.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.21":["Remember these things, Jacob and Israel, for you are my servant; I have formed you, you are my servant, Israel; do not forget me. I have blotted out your iniquities like a cloud, and your sins like a mist. Return to me, for I have redeemed you. Praise, O heavens, for the Lord has done it; shout for joy, you ends of the earth; resound with praise, you mountains, you forest and every tree in it; for the Lord has redeemed Jacob, and Israel will glory. LXX: Remember these things, Jacob and Israel, for you are my child; I have formed you as my child; and you, Israel, do not forget me. For behold, I have blotted out your iniquities like a cloud, and your sins like darkness. Return to me, and I will redeem you. Rejoice, O heavens, for God has had mercy on Israel. Sound the trumpet, you foundations of the earth; cry out with gladness, you mountains, you hills and all the trees that are in them; for God has redeemed Jacob, and Israel will be glorious. Since these things are so, and you know that idols are figments of men, O Jacob, and Israel my child, do not forget your Creator, nor do yourself the injury of bowing down to the work of your own hands.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.22":["And since these are to be utterly destroyed at the coming of my child whom I have chosen, therefore know with all the eagerness of your mind that, as a cloud and darkness and mist is either dissolved by the heat of the sun or thinned out by a sweeping wind, so I will cause your iniquities to be dissolved, and all the sins with which you had before offended me. Only return to me and do penance, for you are to be redeemed with precious blood.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.23":["If you do this, know that heaven and earth rejoice in your salvation, and all the elements sing together; or the angels who dwell in heaven, and the other powers by which the foundations of the earth are borne; or certainly the Apostles and Prophets, of whom the Apostle also spoke: Built upon the foundation of the Apostles and Prophets (Eph 2:20). The mountains also and the forests, or the hills, which, according to the variety of their virtues, have held the first, the middle and the last places, are bidden to shout for joy and to sound the trumpet, knowing that the Lord has redeemed Jacob, and that he rejoices in the conversion of Israel; or that Israel himself, turned from error, becomes glorious.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.24":["Thus says the Lord, your redeemer, and your former from the womb: I am the Lord who makes all things, who alone stretches out the heavens, who establishes the earth, and there is none with me; who makes void the signs of the diviners, and turns the soothsayers to madness; who turns the wise backward, and makes their knowledge foolish; who raises up the word of his servant, and fulfills the counsel of his messengers; who say to Jerusalem: You will be inhabited , and to the cities of Judah: You will be built, and I will raise up its deserts; who say to the deep: Be desolate, and I will dry up your rivers; who say to Cyrus: You are my shepherd, and you will fulfill all my will; who say to Jerusalem: You will be built; and to the temple: You will be founded. LXX: Thus says the Lord who redeemed you, and who forms you from the womb: I am the Lord who accomplish all things; I alone stretched out the heaven, and made firm the earth. Who else has scattered the signs of the pythons, and divinations from the heart? I who turn the prudent backward, and make their counsel foolish; and I establish the word of my child, and prove true the counsel of his angels; who say to Jerusalem: You will be inhabited; and to the cities of Judah: You will be built, and its deserts will spring up; who say to the abyss: You will be made desolate, and I will dry up your rivers; who say to Cyrus that he should be wise, and do all my wishes; who say to Jerusalem: You will be built; and I will lay the foundations of my holy house. When the idols and the iniquities of Israel have been destroyed and its sins blotted out, when the whole creation will rejoice together, and, according to the faith of the Gospel (Luke 15), the angels in heaven will rejoice over the repentance of sinners, the power of God is described: that it is in no way a great thing for him to redeem Jacob and to restore an upright heart in Israel, whom he formed from the womb. And if this should seem no small thing to anyone, let it not be difficult for him who alone stretched out the heavens; according to what is read elsewhere: Who stretches out the heaven like a skin (Ps 103 [104]:2). Not that the Son is excluded from the stretching out of the heavens, For all things were made through him, and without him nothing was made that was made (John 1:3); but that, as we have often said, the idols are excluded by this sentence. For in the Proverbs of Solomon too it is said in the person of Christ, the power of God and the wisdom of God: When he was preparing the heaven, I was with him (Prov 8:27). For he spoke, and they were made; he commanded, and they were created (Ps 148; Ps 32 [33]:9); and: By the word of the Lord the heavens were made firm, and all their power by the spirit of his mouth (Ps 32 [33]:6). We press these points frequently, so that no occasion may be left to the heretics for blaspheming Christ. In the establishing of the earth too, when he was making its foundations solid, there was no one with God except him who was in him.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.25":["He, then, being so great and such, when the time of the foreordained mystery has come, that all idols may be destroyed and the knowledge of God alone may be preached in the world, will destroy and overturn all the prophecies of the diviners, and the signs and portents of the pythons and soothsayers, by which they have deceived the human race; and he will show to be foolish the wisdom of the philosophers, which itself also is the greatest part of error, since by human thoughts they are in no way shown to have comprehended the wisdom of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.26":["He will also fulfill in deed the word of his child, or servant, of whom we spoke above, and the counsel of his messengers, namely of the Apostles and all the teachers, who will announce the will of their master to the nations. He, then, who is to do such great things, whose power I have described in a brief discourse, now says also to Jerusalem, which was to be destroyed by the Babylonians, before it is destroyed, that it will again be inhabited by peoples; and to the cities of Judea, that they will be restored; he who will also raise up its solitudes, so that all may be filled with inhabitants.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.27":["He, then, who says to Jerusalem and to Judea and its deserts that it will be inhabited and built and raised up, says to the deep or abyss, that is, to Babylon, Be desolate, and I will dry up your rivers, all the power of its kings. Or, because he had spoken of the deep and the abyss, rightly by metaphor he set down rivers too, of which the Psalmist also sings: By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat and wept (Ps 136 [137]:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.44.28":["And he who restores Jerusalem and destroys Babylon also says to Cyrus, king of the Persians, who first destroyed Babylon and the Chaldeans: You are my shepherd, or, as the Seventy translated, that he should be wise. The cause of this difference is plain. For if we read the Hebrew word ROI with the letter RES (רעי), it is understood as my shepherd; if with DALETH (דעי), as knowing or my understanding one; and the likeness of these letters is distinguished by a small stroke, and for this reason they are often confused. It is written at the beginning of Ezra that at the edict of Cyrus, king of the Persians and Medes, the captivity of the people of Israel was loosed, and those who wished to return were sent back to Jerusalem under Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel, and Jeshua son of Jozadak, the high priest. For the Lord inspired him to do his will and to fulfill his commands. And significantly he added, who say to Jerusalem: You will be built; and to the Temple: You will be founded. For under Cyrus it was ordered that Jerusalem and the Temple be built, but while he lived only the foundations of the Temple were laid. For the rest, under Darius, in his second year, when Haggai and Zechariah prophesied, the Temple began to be built.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.1-3":["His right hand he took hold of and held, so that no one could resist his strength. Let us read Xenophon's history of the elder Cyrus in eight books, and we shall see the prophecy of Isaiah fulfilled. For what city was not opened to him? What city did not bow beneath him the backs of its kings? What walls, before impregnable, were not overthrown by his siege? Hence God makes an apostrophe to Cyrus himself: I have given you the treasures and the hidden wealth of all the cities, so that you, who before worshiped idols, might perceive the one God through his benefits, especially since you know that your name was foretold long before you were born. Josephus too relates this in the eleventh book of the Jewish Antiquities: that Cyrus read that Isaiah had prophesied of him by his very name, and that for this reason he loved the Jews very much as God's intimates.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.1-7":["According to tropology we can say these things also of the churchman, to whom God has given speech and wisdom, so that by his disputation he may overturn all the sects contrary to the truth, as holy Scripture records of Stephen too (Acts 6), that no one could resist his wisdom; and so that he may subject kings, that is, the patriarchs of the several heresies, to his power, and may open and break what before seemed shut fast by the art of dialectic, and may bring into the open the secrets of the heretics, overcoming and refuting them, so that they may come to know the secrets of Christ, in whom are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge (Col 2). Such a man God calls by his own name, because he is the defender of his child Jacob and of his chosen Israel. Him he takes up and likens to his own word; and he must beware lest he think that what he speaks is his own, but must refer all to the glory of the giver, lest he too deserve to hear: I girded you, and you have not known me. For when, equipped with the armor of the Apostle, he has taught all that there is no other God but one, who is the God of Jacob and Israel, Marcion will be confounded, who understands two gods, one good and the other just, one the founder of things invisible, the other of things visible; of whom the former makes light, the latter darkness, the one peace, the other evil: whereas the one and the same God has founded both according to the diversity of merits.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.1":["Thus says the Lord to my anointed, Cyrus, whose right hand I have taken hold of, to subdue nations before his face, and I will turn the backs of kings, and I will open doors before him, and the gates will not be shut. I will go before you, and I will humble the glorious ones of the earth; I will break the gates of bronze, and I will shatter the bars of iron. And I will give you hidden treasures and the secrets of hidden places, that you may know that I am the Lord, who call your name, the God of Israel. For the sake of my servant Jacob, and Israel my chosen, I have also called you by your name; I have likened you, and you have not known me. I am the Lord, and there is none besides; apart from me there is no God; I girded you, and you have not known me; that they may know, those who are from the rising of the sun and those who are from the west, that besides me there is none. I am the Lord, and there is no other. I form light and create darkness; I make peace and create evil. I am the Lord who does all these things. I know that in this passage very many, not only of the Latins but of the Greeks, err badly, thinking that it is written: Thus says the Lord to my anointed, the Lord; so that it may be understood according to what we read elsewhere: The Lord rained from the Lord (Gen 19:24); and: The Lord said to my Lord (Ps 109 [110]:1). For it says not κυρίῳ, which means Lord, but Cyrus, who in Hebrew is called CHORES (כרש), the king of the Persians, who overcame Babylon and the Chaldeans; and, joined with the Medes, he is read of above as the driver of a two-horse chariot, that is, of the camel and the ass. He was called Christ, that is, the anointed of the Lord, because this was the mark of royal power among the Hebrews; so that, as among us the diadem and the purple are given to emperors alone, so among the Hebrews those who were to reign were anointed with ointment. Hence Saul too is called the Lord's anointed (1 Sam 6). And in the Psalms we read: Do not touch my anointed ones, and do no harm to my prophets (Ps 104 [105]:15).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.4-6":["And these things, he says, I have granted you for the sake of Jacob my servant, and Israel my chosen, and I have called you by your name, as I called Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and foretold you long before, as I did Isaac and Josiah: lest you should be thought to be that Christ to whom you have been likened, and whose type and image you went before. But you have not known me, that is, you have worshiped images, not God. I girded you with strength; I made you victor over many nations, and you have not known your helper. In this place I cannot sufficiently marvel at the folly of readers, that they refer these things to Christ, through whom the world was reconciled to God. I, he says, am the Lord, and there is none besides apart from me. For besides my Word and Reason, and my power and wisdom, which is in me, there is no other God. And I have done these things so that the whole world, from the East and the West, may acknowledge that there is no other God besides me. I am the Lord, and there is no other. For the Father is in the Son, and the Son in the Father, who says in the Gospel: I and the Father are one (John 10:30). And in the volume of Ezra it is set down that Cyrus wrote to all the nations that there is no God but the God of Israel alone. Or it is to be understood thus: that the release of the captives and God's clemency toward his people made God better known to all the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.7":["I, then, who gave you strength, and am the only God, myself make things contrary to one another, light and darkness, that is, day and night; peace and evil, that is, leisure and war; by which he signifies both that he was angry with his people, when it endured the darkness of captivity and the evils of bondage, and again that he had mercy on it, when, sent back to their homeland, they received peace and joy. For as darkness is contrary to light, so war is contrary to peace. Hence let the heresy be confounded which thinks God the founder of evils; since here evil is set not as the contrary of good, but for affliction and war, according to what is written in the Gospel: Sufficient for the day is its own evil (Matt 6:34).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But the evil which the Lord does in the city is not the contrary of virtue, but affliction and torment, of which we read: Sufficient to the day is its own evil, that is, tribulation and distress. And in the prophet we read: I am God, who made light and darkness, who make peace and create evils. For just as darkness is contrary to light and day, so war too is contrary to peace; it is not evil in itself, but seems to be evil to those who endure it. And that we may know that in the holy Scriptures evil is sometimes taken not as the contrary of virtue but as affliction, punishments and distress, let us take one more example, from Jonah: And God saw their works, that they had turned from their most wicked ways, and he repented over the evil which he had spoken of doing to them. The evil there is surely the overthrow of the Ninevites, which God had threatened through the prophet, and not the evil contrary to virtue, which belongs to sin, on account of which he was threatening torments to sinners.\n— Commentary on Amos"],"ISA.45.8":["Drop down dew, heavens, from above, and let the clouds rain the righteous one: let the earth be opened, and let it bud forth the Savior, and let righteousness spring up together: I the Lord have created him. LXX: Let the heaven rejoice from above, and let the clouds sprinkle righteousness: let the earth bud forth, and let mercy spring up, and let it bud forth righteousness together: I am the Lord who created you. The interpretation of this passage is twofold. For some think that it clings to what was said above, and that, when King Cyrus sets the captives free, heaven and earth rejoice: μετωνυμικῶς, that is to say, those who dwell in heaven and on earth. Others separate it from what precedes and want it to be the proper beginning of this chapter, and a prophecy of the Lord's coming: that command is given to the clouds, of which it is written above (cf. Isa 5:6): I will command the clouds not to rain rain upon it, that is, upon the vineyard of Israel; and to which the truth of God reaches, that they should rain upon the world the righteous one, or righteousness; and that the earth should be opened and bud forth the Savior. Of it is sung in the Psalms: Truth has sprung from the earth, and righteousness has looked down from heaven (Ps 84 [85]:12); or, according to the Seventy: The earth has budded forth mercy and righteousness alike, so that sinners may obtain mercy and the righteous their rewards. And as for what follows: I the Lord have created him, or, I am the Lord who created you, he will not be scandalized at the word 'creature' who has read that he is a worm and a servant, and that he sprang from the earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Or else, according to the LXX, who said: He has risen from his holy clouds, understand the patriarchs, prophets and apostles, whom the Lord commanded not to rain rain upon Israel. These are the clouds of which it is written: And let the clouds sprinkle justice; for these bodily clouds, into which the denser air is gathered, cannot rain justice.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ISA.45.9-11":["Woe to him who contradicts his maker: a potsherd of the Samian earth. Does the clay say to its potter, What are you making? and, Your work is without hands? Woe to him who says to a father, What are you begetting? and to a woman, What are you bringing to birth? Thus says the Lord, the Holy One of Israel, his fashioner: Ask me about things to come concerning my sons, and concerning the work of my hands give charge to me. I made the earth, and I created man upon it: my hands stretched out the heavens, and I gave command to all their host. I have raised him up to righteousness, and I will direct all his ways. He will build my city, and he will let my captivity go, not for a price nor for gifts, says the Lord of hosts. LXX: What better have I made than the potter's clay? Will the ploughman plough the earth? Will the clay say to the potter, What are you making? because you do not work and have no hands? Woe to him who says to a father, What are you begetting? and to a mother, What are you bringing to birth? For thus says the Lord God, the Holy One of Israel, who made the things to come: Ask me about my sons, and concerning the works of my hands give charge to me. I made the earth, and man upon it. I with my hand made firm the heaven: I gave command to all the stars. I raised him up with righteousness as king: all his ways are straight. He will build my city, and will turn back the captivity of my people: not with a price nor with gifts, said the Lord God Sabaoth. Those who wish to refer what is said to the person of Cyrus interpret this passage thus: When I promise great things, that for the sake of my people's return to Judea I will lay all kingdoms beneath Cyrus, woe to him who is unbelieving and does not think that the things I say will come, as if clay and a broken potsherd should cavil at its potter, why it was made, or why it was made so; and a work should speak against the hands of its craftsman; and a son should cavil at his father and mother, why he was poured out onto the earth by the law of nature. Since therefore I, the Lord, the Holy One of Israel, have fashioned Cyrus, and he was begotten at my command, it is superfluous for you to doubt about things to come; rather you ought to know that I dispose of my sons, the people of Israel, and of the work of my hands, not at your discretion but by my will: so that I, who formerly created darkness and evil for them, now grant light and peace.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But one who refers the understanding so begun to Christ frames the words of his explanation thus: Woe to those who contradict God and do not think that Christ will come; as if clay and a potsherd from among potsherds should contradict its potter. Woe to him who says to the Almighty Father, Why do you beget a Son? and to the woman, holy Mary, What are you bringing to birth? Of whom the Apostle too writes (Gal 4): That Christ was made of a woman, and made under the law. These things, then, says the Lord, the Holy One of Israel, who fashioned the Savior in the virginal womb, saying through Gabriel: The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you (Luke 1:35): and the holy thing that will be born in you will be called the Son of God: let the potsherd question me and search out the secrets of things to come. And let it instruct me how I ought to regenerate the adopted sons who are to believe in my Son. The evangelist John too says: As many as received him, to them he gave power to become sons of God (John 1:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.9":["But what the Seventy mean, who at the beginning of this chapter translated: What better have I made than the potter's clay? Will he who ploughs plough the earth? I am not able to know: unless perhaps I follow the edition of Theodotion, who put in place of this: Woe to him who contends against his maker, ploughing those who plough the earth: namely, that he wounds the hearts of mortals unto penitence, and, after the manner of furrows, breaks them up and overturns them. But this too is a frivolous interpretation. Further, that God, our Creator and maker, is called a potter, the apostle Paul too says in the Epistle to the Romans: Does the thing formed say to him who formed it: Why have you made me thus? Has not the potter power over the clay, to make from the same lump one vessel for honor and another for dishonor (Rom 9:20)? And in Jeremiah it is written at greater length, who relates, after other things: I went down into the potter's house, and behold, he was making a work on the wheel, and the vessel that he was making was spoiled in his hands. And again he made from the same clay another vessel, as it pleased in his sight. And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Can I not, like this potter, make you, house of Israel, says the Lord? Behold, as clay in the hand of the potter, so are you in my hands, house of Israel (Jer 18:3, 4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.12-13":["For I am God, who did not create the earth for emptiness, that it should be deserted and squalid, but that men should dwell in it. I stretched out, or made firm, the heavens, that they might be the dwelling place of the angels. And I distinguished them with the variety of the stars, and commanded each in what order to run, and to complete the different spans of days and months and years. I, then, who made heaven and earth, what great thing is it if I have created one king to keep my commands, and if I direct all his ways? For he will order that my city Jerusalem be built, and that the captives return to Judea. Not for a price, nor for gifts, but because of my will, says the Lord God of hosts.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But if I made the earth to be inhabited by men, and stretched out the heavens above, and adorned them with the variety of the stars, so that worshipers of God might dwell on the earth, what wonder is it if I have sent my Son, a righteous king, into the world, or raised him up from the underworld, all of whose ways are straight? For he committed no sin, nor was guile found in his mouth (1 Pet 2:22). He it is who is to build my city upon the rock, against which the gates of hell shall not prevail (Matt 16); and which, set on a mountain, cannot be hidden (ibid., 5); and to let go free all who were formerly bound with demonic chains, not for a price nor for gifts: for we have been saved freely, the Apostles hearing and doing: Freely you have received, freely give (Matt 10:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.12":["And what is written: I gave command to all the stars, has given some an occasion to hold that the stars are rational and have soul and sense. For, they say, he would not command any but beings who understand: not remembering that it is written in Jonah: The Lord commanded the scorching wind (Jonah 4:8). And again: He commanded the morning worm (ibid., 7). And that in the Gospel the Savior rebuked the winds and the sea (Luke 8), in which it is plain that there is neither sense nor reason.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.13":["Some refer these things to Zerubbabel, who led the captives out of Babylon, and built the city, and constructed the temple, while Haggai and Zechariah prophesied and said: The hands of Zerubbabel have founded this house, and his hands will complete it (Zech 4:9). But we say more rightly and more truly that neither did Cyrus build the city, which was constructed afterwards under Nehemiah, nor were all his ways made straight, he to whom it was said above: I took you and girded you, and you did not know me; and that all that is said exceeds the station of Zerubbabel, who was under the nod and power of the Medes and Persians. For Zerubbabel neither constructed the city, nor let the captivity go, nor can he be called king who, living under other kings, lacked this name.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.14-15":["Thus says the Lord: The labor of Egypt, and the merchandise of Ethiopia, and the Sabaim, men of stature, will pass over to you, and will be yours: they will walk behind you, they will go bound in manacles: and they will worship you, and make supplication to you: only in you is God, and there is no God apart from you. Truly you are a hidden God, the God of Israel, the Savior. They are confounded and have blushed, all of them: together the makers of errors have gone off into confusion. Israel is saved in the Lord with an eternal salvation: you will not be confounded, and you will not blush, for ever and ever. LXX: Thus says the Lord Sabaoth: Egypt has labored, and the merchandise of the Ethiopians and of the Sabaim: lofty men will pass over to you, and they will be your servants, and they will follow you bound in manacles, and they will pass over to you, and will worship you, and will pray in you: because God is in you, and there is no God apart from you. For you are God, and we did not know it: the God of Israel, the Savior: let all his adversaries be confounded and blush, and let them walk in confusion. ÷ Be renewed unto me, islands ⁚ Israel is saved by the Lord with an eternal salvation: they will not be confounded nor blush for ever. In this passage too, those who follow the history say that Egypt, and the Ethiopians, and the nations of the Sabaim, which are beyond Ethiopia, served Cyrus, and that the furthest nations were subjected to him; and that from his wondrous victory they understood that the Lord was in him, and that there was no other God besides him who dwelt in him. But how what follows: Truly you are a hidden God, the God of Israel, the Savior, can fit the person of Cyrus, I do not understand. Unless perhaps they use the edition of Theodotion, who translated: In you is the strong one, and there is no other God besides him: therefore you are the strong, hidden God of Israel, the Savior. Whithersoever they turn, they will not be able to escape the snares of the truth. For grant that God is in Cyrus, and that there is no other God besides him who is in Cyrus: how will it be fitting to say to the person of Cyrus, Truly you are a hidden God, the God of Israel, the Savior? Therefore the God in whom God is, is more rightly and more truly understood as our Lord Jesus Christ, who says in the Gospel: I and the Father are one (John 10:30). He is called a hidden God because of the mystery of the body he assumed, and the God of Israel, Savior, which means Jesus. For he, according to the angel Gabriel, will save his people (Luke 1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The Hebrews strive with foolish contention to assert that, up to the place where we read: Only in you is God, and there is no God apart from you, it is said either to Jerusalem or to Cyrus; but that what follows: Truly you are a hidden God, the God of Israel, the Savior, is suddenly an apostrophe made to the almighty God; though it is plain even to fools that the texture of the discourse is one, and that a sense which is joined together in the very order and reasoning of the narration cannot be divided.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.14":["That Egypt, and the Ethiopians, and the Sabaim, lofty and exalted men, served him, no one doubts, since one sees the world subjected to him, and perceives that, under the name of a few nations which dwell at the furthest ends of the earth, all the hinges of the heavens and all the shores of the earth are to believe in him. Hence it is beautifully said, as to men laboring in the error of idolatry, that the labor of Egypt ceases. For no nation was so given over to idolatry, and venerated such innumerable monstrosities, as Egypt, of which we read above (Isa 19:1): Behold, the Lord will ascend upon a light cloud, and will enter Egypt, and the idols of Egypt will be moved at his presence, and the heart of Egypt will melt in its midst.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.16-17":["They are confounded and have blushed, all together, namely the Scribes and Pharisees. And the makers of error have gone off into confusion, who spread a lie through the whole world, saying that he had been stolen away by the Apostles (Matt 28). But Israel, saved in the Lord with an eternal salvation, is understood as the choir of the Apostles, and those who believed through the Apostles. Hence it is said to them: You will not be confounded nor will you blush, not only in the present age but also in the one to come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.16":["Further, what is added in the Seventy: Be renewed unto me, islands, we can expound thus: we may say that the Churches gathered from the nations are renewed in Christ, and are called islands because they endure the rage and the storms of persecutors, and, founded upon the rock, are shaken by no mass of whirlwinds.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.18":["For thus says the Lord, who creates the heavens, God himself who forms the earth and makes it, he himself its fashioner: he did not create it in vain; that it might be inhabited he formed it: I am the Lord, and there is no other. I have not spoken in secret, in a dark place of the earth. I did not say to the seed of Jacob: Seek me in vain. I am the Lord who speaks righteousness, who declares right things. Gather together, and come, and draw near together, you who have been saved from among the nations: they have not known, who lift up the wood of their graven image, and pray to a god that does not save. Declare, and come, and take counsel together: who has caused this to be heard from the beginning, has foretold it from that time? Is it not I the Lord, and there is no God besides me? A righteous God and one who saves, there is none besides me. Be converted to me, and you will be saved, all the ends of the earth: for I am God, and there is no other. By myself have I sworn: the word of righteousness will go forth from my mouth, and will not return: for to me every knee shall bow, and every tongue shall swear. Therefore he will say in the Lord: Mine are righteousnesses and dominion: to him they will come, and all who resist him will be confounded. In the Lord all the seed of Israel will be justified and will be praised: LXX: Thus says the Lord who made the heaven: this God who showed the earth and made it, he himself prepared it: he did not make it for emptiness, but formed it to be inhabited. I am the Lord, and there is no other. I have not spoken in secret, nor in a dark place of the earth. I did not say to the seed of Jacob, Seek vanity: I am the Lord who speak righteousness, I declare truth. Gather together and come, and take counsel together, you who are being saved from the nations; they have not known, who carry the wood of their graven image: they pray to gods that do not save. If they declare, let them draw near, that they may know together who has made these things heard from the beginning; then it was declared to you. Am I not the Lord God, and there is no other besides me: a righteous one and a savior, there is none besides me. Be converted to me, and you will be saved from the ends of the earth. I am God, and there is no other. By myself I swear: unless righteousness shall go forth from my mouth: my words will not be turned back, for to me every knee will bend, and every tongue will swear and will confess to God, saying: Righteousness and glory will come to him, and all who separate themselves from the Lord will be confounded: all the seed of the sons of Israel will be justified and will be glorified in God. Having called Egypt, and Ethiopia, and the Sabaim, lofty men, by which the salvation of all the barbarian nations and the conversion of the whole world to God is shown, God shows his righteousness: that he is the Lord not of the Jews only, but also of the nations. For he, the maker of heaven and earth, is equally the God of all, and he created the earth for no other reason than to be the dwelling place of men, who should worship and understand their Creator, and despise all idols.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.19":["For on Mount Sinai too, from its lofty summit, he spoke these things in the hearing of the peoples: You shall have no strange gods besides me, nor shall you make yourself an idol (Exod 20:3, 4). But it is better that we believe this was said of the preaching of the Gospel: for Moses spoke in a hidden wilderness to one people only. But the sound of the Apostles went out into the whole world, and their words reached to the ends of the earth (Ps 18 [19]). I did not say, he says, to the seed of Jacob, Seek me in vain. For I promised them the kingdoms of heaven, and I spoke to them first: I came only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 15:24). And therefore I spoke righteousness and declared right things, or truth, so that, leaving behind the images and ceremonies of the Law, they might follow the truth of the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.20":["But because they would not believe, and judged themselves unworthy of salvation, therefore I say to the nations: Gather together from the whole world; and come and draw near to me together, you who have been saved from among the nations. By this he shows that not all the nations will believe at once, but gradually and in parts. Finally he rebukes those who remained in their former error, saying: They have not known, who lift up the wood of their graven image, and pray to a god that does not save. And the sense is: They did not understand my words, weighed down by the burden of their idols, and hoping in those in whom there is no salvation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.21":["Hence the Apostles are commanded to proclaim the truth in season and out of season (2 Tim 4), and to enter on counsel for the salvation of the nations. And this, that is, that very many from the nations should be gathered and come and be saved, God spoke from the beginning, and the mouths of all the Prophets sang it, who spoke by the word of the Lord, besides whom there is no other. For the Son is God not apart from him, but in him.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.22":["And beautifully he joins: A righteous God, not of one nation at all, but of the whole world, to which he says: Be converted to me, and you will be saved, all the ends of the earth; fulfilling what the Father promised the Son: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession (Ps 2:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.23":["And he swears by himself that the sentence of his mouth, and the word he has once uttered concerning the salvation of the nations, will by no means be made void; but that the promise of him who said above: Be converted to me, and you will be saved, all the ends of the earth, will be fulfilled in deed. And he swears, according to the Apostle (Heb 6), so that through two immovable things, in which it is impossible for God to lie, we may have firm consolation; but he also swears this: that, idols abandoned, every knee should bow to him, of those in heaven, on earth, and under the earth, and every tongue of mortals should swear by him (Phil 2). In this the Christian people is plainly signified. For it is the custom of the Church to bend the knee to Christ: which the Jews, displaying the pride of their mind, do not do at all. And every tongue too of all the barbarian nations confesses God, not in synagogues but in the Churches of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.45.24-25":["And every tongue that confesses Christ will speak in the Lord, and will say: Mine are righteousnesses, and mine is the dominion, not the Jewish people's. To him all the nations will come, and those who formerly resisted his Gospel will be confounded; and all the seed of Israel will be justified and praised, whose preaching and most fertile sowing has borne the richest fruits throughout the whole world. Or, according to the Seventy, every tongue that swears by and confesses God will say that the righteousness and glory of the whole world comes to him, and that the Jews, who separate themselves from him, are confounded. But those who have sprung from the stock of the sons of Israel, and who have sprouted from the seed of the Apostles, and have believed in Christ, may they have righteousness and everlasting glory.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.46.1-2":["These idols, he says, which cannot save those who carry them, are nothing else but burdens for the priests, weighing them down even to weariness. And when captivity comes, they are the first to be led captive, for the value of the metals of which they are made, and they cannot set free their own soul, or that of those who carry them. Not that dumb idols, which are without sensation, have a soul and some sense of pain; but that soul and members are spoken of καταχρηστικῶς in things that are without sense and members. Otherwise, too, we read in Proverbs: In the hand of the tongue are death and life. Or else this must be said: that the heaviest burden among the nations was the error of idolatry, which weighed its worshipers down to the earth, and could not save them, and made their souls captives of the devil and of demons.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.46.1":["Bel is broken, Nebo is crushed: their idols are made over to beasts and cattle, your burdens with heavy weight even to weariness. They have wasted away and are crushed together: they could not save the one who carried them, and their soul shall go into captivity. LXX: Bel has fallen, Dagon is crushed: their graven images are made into beasts and cattle; you carry them bound together like a burden for one who labors, and faints, and hungers, and has no strength besides, who will not be able to save from war: but they themselves have been led captive. After the calling of the nations, and the election of believers from Israel, he testifies that the idols have collapsed. Bel has fallen, has fallen, or is broken: whom the Greeks call Belus, the Latins Saturn. So great was his worship among the ancients that they sacrificed to him not only human victims, captives and mortals of low birth, but even their own children. Nebo, too, is itself an idol, which means prophecy and divination, which he signifies fell silent throughout the whole world after the truth of the Gospel. Or, according to the Seventy, Dagon, which, however, is not found in the Hebrew. And it is the idol of Ascalon, of Gaza and of the other cities of the Philistines. And he passes from the particular to the general: Their idols are made over to beasts and cattle. Not that the idols of the Gentiles were exposed as prey to beasts and cattle: but that the religion of the nations consists in images of beasts and of brute animals, which above all in Egypt were consecrated to divine worship. Of which Virgil says (Aen. 8.698): Monsters of gods of every kind, and barking Anubis.\n\nFor most of their towns too have names from beasts and cattle: κυνῶν from dog, λέων from lion; θμοῦϊς, in the Egyptian tongue, from the he-goat; λύκων from wolf; to say nothing of the dreadful and horrible onion, and the noise of the puffed-up belly, which is the religion of Pelusium.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.46.3-4":["Hear me, house of Jacob, and all the remnant of the house of Israel, who are carried from my womb, who are borne from my matrix. Even to old age I myself, and even to gray hairs I will carry you: I have made, and I will bear: I will carry, and I will save. To whom have you likened me, and made me equal, and compared me, and made me like? You who lavish gold from the purse, and weigh silver on the scales, hiring a goldsmith to make a god: and they fall down and worship. They carry it on their shoulders, bearing it and setting it in its place, and it will stand, and will not move from its place. But when they cry to it, it will not hear: it will not save them from tribulation. LXX: Hear me, house of Jacob, and all the remnant of Israel, who are carried from the womb, and are instructed from a little child even to old age. I am, and until you grow old I am: I sustain you: I have made, and I will carry: I will take you up, and I will save you. To whom have you likened me? See, consider, you who err and lavish gold from the purse, and weigh out silver on the scales, and hire a goldsmith: they have made the works of their hands, and bowing down they have worshiped: they carry it on their shoulders, and go. But if they set it in its place, it stays, and will not move: and whoever cries to it, it will not hear him, and will not deliver him from evils. He by no means calls him Jacob, nor Israel, which itself, we explained above, is said in reproach of the people when it is without the addition of servant, and child, and chosen: but, much more meanly, house of Jacob, and remnant of Israel, because of the nearness of flesh and blood, and as it were the dregs of the remnant of Israel. And he teaches them that they were carried by God out of Egypt like children and sucklings, as it were from a mother's womb and the matrix of one with child. Not that the ineffable and incomprehensible majesty of God has either a womb or a matrix, or feet and hands and the other members of the body; but that we may learn God's affection through our own words. Otherwise, in Psalm 109 (110) too, this same thing is sung in the person of God. For in the place where the Seventy translated, from the womb before the morning star I begot you, the Hebrew has written MEREHEM מרחם, which means from the matrix. But in the present passage it is written not only from the womb and from the matrix, that is, MEBETEN מבטן and MEREHEM מרחם, but MENNI מני, a preposition which signifies from my womb, or from my matrix. And the sense is: I who begot you from infancy, and bore you in my womb and matrix, will myself protect you even to old age, not mine but yours; so as to teach them that they are to be saved by the divine mercy. For the Creator of all spares his creature, and the good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep (John 10). But he who is a hireling, whose own the sheep are not, flees when he sees the wolf. Since, then, I made and begot children, I will bear them and I myself will carry them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.46.3-7":["According to the Seventy, who said: Who are carried from the womb, and are instructed from infancy even to old age, this signifies that they meditate on the law of God day and night in vain, having no knowledge of God, but venerating images of men and beasts: so much so that they need prophetic rebuke, through which God speaks to them: To whom have you likened me, and made me equal? and the rest: that they lavished gold and silver, and, hiring a sculptor, made idols, and worshiped the works of their own hands, which are carried on shoulders, and, fixed and set firm, cannot move themselves, nor profit those by whom they are worshiped. We pass quickly over what is plain, so that by the mercy of Christ we may unlock what is closed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.46.8":["Remember this, and be grounded, and return, transgressors, to heart. Recall the former age, for I am God, and there is no God beyond, nor is there any like me. Declaring from the start the last things, and from the beginning things not yet done, saying: My counsel shall stand, and all my will shall be done. Calling a bird from the East, and from a far land the man of my will; and I have spoken, and I will bring it about; I have created, and I will do it. LXX: Remember these things, and groan: repent, you who err. Return in heart, and remember the former things from of old; for I am God, and there is no other besides me. I who declare beforehand the last things before they happen and are fulfilled, and I have said, all my will shall stand, and all that I have planned I will do. I who call a bird from the East, and from a far land, those concerning whom I have planned: I have spoken, and I have brought it about: I have created and I have done it. Because I myself begot you, I myself carried you, and from infancy even to old age you are saved not by your merit but by my compassion: forsake the idols you have made, and return to the worship of the one God. Repent, groan for the error that held you; nay, be grounded, lest the sudden whirlwind of idolatry overturn you again: and return to heart, that is, to your mind, you who, venerating images, dashed yourselves like madmen against wood and stones.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.46.9-10":["Consider from the beginning of the world that there is no God besides me, and that no other could know things to come, except me, who announce through the Prophets what I am going to do: so that, when I have fulfilled what was foretold, I may prove my divinity by divination. For I now say that the mystery which in times past was unknown to all generations, or rather my counsel, will stand; so that, when you see it brought to pass, you may know no God but him who knew that these things would be, nay, commanded them to be done.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.46.11":["I am he who calls a bird from the East: as the Hebrews think, Cyrus king of the Persians, or Darius prince of the Medes; and from a far land the man of my will, to fulfill all my will against Babylon and the Chaldeans. Or, as we prove to be true, the Lord Savior, of whom Balaam too prophesies: A star shall rise out of Jacob, and a man out of Israel (Num 24:17), whose name is the East (Zech 6), whom the Magi coming from the East worshiped. For he it is who speaks in the Psalms: O God, I have willed to do your will (Ps 39 [40]:9), of whom the Father spoke: and he proved his pledge by deed. In place of what we have rendered from the Hebrew, the man of my will, the Seventy put, those concerning whom I have planned. According to them, then, by the birds called from the East we can understand the ministries of the angels, which run to and fro through the whole world at the Lord's command; who are ministering spirits, and are sent for the salvation of believers. Of whom it is also sung in the psalm: You who make your angels spirits, and your ministers a burning fire (Ps 103 [104]:4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.46.12-13":["Hear me, you hard of heart, who are far from righteousness. I have brought my righteousness near: it shall not be far off, and my salvation shall not delay: I will give salvation in Zion, and in Israel my glory. LXX: Hear me, you who have lost heart, who are far from righteousness. I have brought near my righteousness, and the salvation that is from me I will not make to tarry. I have given salvation in Zion, and to Israel glory. Those to whom he had said above: Hear me, house of Jacob, and all the remnant of the house of Israel, and again: Return, transgressors, to heart, even now he calls, according to the Hebrew, hard of heart because of their unbelief, and, according to the Seventy, men who have lost heart and mind. Following this, the most learned man Stephen the martyr, worthy of his name, says in the assembly of the Jews: Stiff-necked and uncircumcised in hearts and ears, you have always resisted the Holy Spirit, as your fathers did (Acts 7:51). These, then, are far from the righteousness of God, because they did not believe in it; which God, in his clemency, made to be near, and wishes by no means to tarry or to be far off in coming to the earth. For he has given Zion his salvation, and Israel his glory. Let this be said of the prophecy of things to come, and of the coming of the Lord Savior. But according to the history, salvation is given to Zion, and glory to Israel, because God made his righteousness to be near, so that he called a bird from the East, and from a far land the man of his will, to avenge the wrongs of Israel and of overthrown Jerusalem, and, with the Medes and Persians victorious, to destroy Babylon and the Chaldeans, as the following words of the Prophet testify.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.1-2":["Go down, sit in the dust, virgin daughter of Babylon: sit on the ground, there is no throne for the daughter of the Chaldeans, for no more shall you be called soft and tender. Take the millstone, and grind meal: lay bare your shame, uncover your shoulder, reveal your legs, pass over the rivers. Your disgrace shall be revealed, and your reproach shall be seen: I will take vengeance, and no man shall withstand me. LXX: Go down, sit upon the ground, virgin daughter of Babylon, sit on the ground. There is no throne for the daughter of the Chaldeans, for no more at all shall you be called soft and tender. Take the millstone, grind meal. Reveal your covering, lay bare your gray hairs, uncover your shins, pass over the rivers: your disgrace shall be revealed, your reproaches shall appear. What is just I will take from you, no more at all will I hand you over to men. As in Ezekiel the adornment of Tyre, which is given over to trading, is set forth under the figure of a ship and all its equipment (Ezek 26), and the king of Egypt, because of the abundance of waters, is called a dragon, and his scales, and the rush and papyrus and little fishes are described, and the likeness of harlots and of a brothel bears witness to Jerusalem's fornication with idols: so in the present passage the servitude of Babylon is shown under the person of a captive woman who was once a queen: and she is told to come down from the pride of her kingdom and sit in the dust. And she is called a virgin and a daughter: either because all of us men are God's creation, and Babylon is not, as the heretics hold, of a damnable nature; or because of the luxury and adornment of a city once most powerful, which, when she had grown old and was near her setting, boasted that she was a little virgin and a girl. Although from what is written according to the Seventy, daughter of Babylon, some interpret it not of Babylon itself but of the city of Rome, which in the Apocalypse of John (Rev 14) and in the Epistle of Peter (1 Pet 5) is specifically called Babylon, and they maintain that all that is now said to Babylon fits its ruin, against which the bird and the righteousness of God are to be called: so that, after Zion, that is, the Church, has been saved, she may perish for ever. It is said, then, to Babylon, queen and daughter of the Chaldeans (for she was founded by the Chaldeans), that she will no more at all be called soft and tender and overflowing with delights, she who was carried in the hands of all the nations, so that she scarcely pressed the soles of her feet to the ground: and she is commanded to take the millstone and grind meal, which is a sign of harsh captivity and the utmost servitude; so that she who was once a queen afterwards serves at the work of grinding meal.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.2-3":["But because there follows: Lay bare your shame, the millstone too is understood figuratively by the Hebrews, namely that, after the manner of a harlot, she lies open to the lust of the victors. And what is written in the book of Judges about Samson (Judg 16), that he was condemned by the Philistines to the mill, they wish to signify this: that, being the strongest of men, he was compelled, for the sake of offspring, to do this with foreign women. In the place where we have translated, lay bare your shame, for which the Seventy translated, reveal your covering, Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself, SAMTHECH (צמתך); Aquila SEMMATHECH; Symmachus τὸ σιωπηλόν σου: which we can express as your silent part, because it ought to be kept silent for modesty. This indeed we also read in the Song of Songs, where the beauty of the bride is described: at the end he adds: Apart from your silent part (Song 4): the translators being unwilling to render a word which in holy Scripture would sound shameful. And rightly he uses immodest names against Babylon (though there is nothing shameful in calling a member of the human body by its own name), she who is commanded to bare her breasts, and to uncover her legs and thighs, and to go into captivity, and to have her disgrace seen, and to lie open to everlasting reproach. And the Lord says he has done this to take vengeance on her who oppressed his people, and that he will hear no intercessor on her behalf who might try to soften the Lord's anger by coming before him. And he means the angel who presides over the Babylonian nation, who says with the other angels: We have cared for Babylon, and she is not healed. And as for what the Seventy translated, what is just I will take from you, no more at all will I hand you over to men, Babylon is understood: or certainly, that which is just, and taken away from Babylon. The Stoics argue that many things shameful in reality are, by the depraved custom of men, decent in words, such as parricide, adultery, homicide, incest, and other things like these. And again, that things decent in reality seem shameful in their names, such as begetting children, relieving the swelling of the belly by breaking wind, easing the bowels of excrement, loosing the bladder by pouring out urine: and finally, that we cannot, as from ruta we say rutula, so make a ὑποκοριστικὸν of menta. Therefore SEMMATHEC (צמתך), which Aquila put, as we have said, is the name for the private parts of a woman. Its etymology among them sounds your thirsty one; so as to show the insatiable lust of Babylon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.4":["Our Redeemer, the Lord of hosts is his name, the Holy One of Israel. Sit silent, and go into darkness, daughter of the Chaldeans; for no more shall you be called mistress of kingdoms. I was angry with my people, I defiled my inheritance, and I gave them into your hand: you showed them no mercies. Upon the old man you made your yoke exceedingly heavy, and you said, For ever I shall be mistress: you did not lay these things to heart, nor did you remember your latter end. LXX: He who delivered you has said, the Lord Sabaoth, his name is the Holy One of Israel. Sit pierced with grief, enter into darkness, daughter of the Chaldeans: no more at all shall you be called the strength of the kingdom: I was angry against my people, I defiled my inheritance. I gave them into your hand, but you gave them no mercy: on the old man you made the yoke exceedingly heavy, and you said, For ever I shall be mistress: you did not understand these things in your heart, nor did you remember the last things. According to the Seventy, the first verse is joined to what precedes, so that the sense is: And these things will he do who delivered you, the Lord Sabaoth, his name is the Holy One of Israel. But according to the Hebrew, the Prophet speaks in the person of the people, saying that the Lord of hosts, whose name is the Holy One of Israel, has done these things against Babylon.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.5":["And again the discourse is directed to Babylon itself. Sit silent, or pierced with grief, and remember your crimes. Enter into darkness: because for confusion and disgrace you cannot bear the light: no more at all shall you be called mistress, not of one kingdom, nor even of all kingdoms.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.6-7":["And at the same time, because a hidden question arose, why God should be angry against the Chaldeans whom he himself had sent to take Israel, he answered that, being angry against his people, he had wished to chastise them, not to destroy them; to scourge them, not to kill them. But they had given free rein to their own cruelty, and had laid on more stripes than God's vengeance demanded: and it is a great sign of Babylonian cruelty that they did not spare even old men, whose age is venerable even among enemies. And this too is a sign of pride, that, deceived by present prosperity, she did not consider the uncertainties of the future. We ought therefore always, in prosperity, to beware of what is to come, and not to oppress those handed over to us, who are being instructed for this purpose, that they may become better.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.8-9":["And now hear these things, you delicate one, who dwell confidently, who say in your heart: I am, and there is none besides me any more: I shall not sit as a widow, and I shall not know barrenness. And these two things shall come upon you suddenly in one day, barrenness and widowhood: all things have come upon you, because of the multitude of your sorceries, and because of the hardness of your enchanters, exceedingly. And you trusted in your wickedness, and you said: There is none who sees me: your wisdom and your knowledge, these have deceived you, and you said in your heart: I am, and besides me there is no other. Evil shall come upon you, and you shall not know its rising, and calamity shall rush upon you which you shall not be able to expiate, misery shall come upon you suddenly which you shall not know. Two things will come upon Babylon together, barrenness and widowhood, so that she has neither sons, that is, peoples subject to her, nor a husband, whom we can understand as the king: which she suddenly endured while she did not expect them. For she could not suppose that the Persians, of no strength before, would, in the reign of Cyrus, overcome her and subject her to their power.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.9-11":["What you have suffered, he says, is not only because of your pride and the abundance of all wealth and your delights, but also because of the multitude of your sorceries, and of your enchanters, in whom you trusted. And evil shall come upon you which you did not know before, and whose rising you did not know. Or, as the Seventy translated: Destruction shall come upon you, and you shall not know it: a pit, and you shall fall into it: so that you who were preparing the evil of captivity for all the nations may yourself fall into the pit that you prepared. What is plain we pass over in brief speech.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.12-13":["Stand with your enchanters, and with the multitude of your sorceries, in which you have labored from your youth, if perhaps anything may profit you, or if you can become stronger. You have failed in the multitude of your counsels: let the augurs of the heaven stand and save you, who gazed at the stars and reckoned the months, that from them they might announce to you things to come. Behold, they have become as stubble, fire has burned them: they shall not deliver their souls from the hand of the flame: there are no coals to warm them, nor a hearth to sit by: so have they become to you, all those things in which you had labored: your traders from your youth have gone astray, each in his own way: there is none to save you. LXX: Stand now in your enchantments and in your many sorceries, which you learned from your youth, if they can profit you: and you have labored in your counsels: let the astrologers of the heaven, who gaze at the stars, stand and save you: let them announce to you what is to come upon you. Behold, they shall all be burned as brushwood in the fire: and they shall not rescue their soul from the flame; because you have coals of fire, you shall sit upon them. These shall be a help to you. You have labored in your exchange from your youth: man has gone astray in himself: but for you there shall be no salvation. That Babylon and all Chaldea pursued the arts of enchanters and augurs, and soothsayers and Gazarenes, whom we call haruspices, is proved by the reading of Daniel the Prophet, who records that the kings of Babylon did everything on their counsel. And in place of what we have translated, following Symmachus and Theodotion: Let the augurs of the heaven stand and save you, the Seventy translated more plainly, Let the astrologers of the heaven stand and save you; who are commonly called mathematicians, and think that human affairs are ruled by the course of the stars and the motion of the heavenly bodies. Hence the Magi too came from the East, saying they had seen the Lord's star, either from the knowledge of their art, or from the prophecy of Balaam, their own Prophet, who had said in Numbers: A star shall rise out of Jacob, and a man from Israel (Num 24:17).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.13-14":["Let these, then, who reckon the months and count up the years, and, weighing the moments of the hours, promise knowledge of the future, tell you what the Lord has planned against you; and, since they are silent about what is to come, the Prophet answered: Behold, they have become as stubble, fire has devoured them; so that those who promised salvation to others did not know their own punishments: and there is no doubt that, when the city was burning, the devouring flame consumed its inhabitants. And what follows: There are no coals to warm them, nor a hearth to sit by, the Hebrews explain thus: They have no knowledge of warmth, nor an enlightening sense that could dispel their darkness and drive out the cold of error. For which the Seventy, meaning I know not what, translated: You have coals of fire, you shall sit upon them: these shall be a help to you; unless perhaps we can say this, that fire and burning are much more useful to Babylon than the magi and Gazarenes, the astrologers and enchanters, were. For the one calls them to repentance through penalties and punishments; the others lead them through error to pride.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.14-15":["Let us ask those who assert that there are different natures whether Babylon is of an evil nature or a good one. If they say of an evil one, which there is no doubt they will answer, how is she called to repentance, and told: Sit pierced with grief, enter into darkness, daughter of the Chaldeans? And then, after the enumeration of her sins and crimes: You have coals of fire, you shall sit upon them: these shall be a help to you? And what is meant by what is added according to the same Seventy: You have labored in exchange from your youth? What is this exchange? Surely from good to evil. From which it is plain that men are good by nature, and become evil by will. Finally it is added: Man has gone astray in himself, not by nature, but by the choice of the mind.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.47.15":["All her labor and her traders, whom we understand as the magi, achieved this: that each went astray in his own way; and, himself lost, could not offer salvation to another.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.1-2":["Hear these things, house of Jacob, you who are called by the name of Israel, and have come forth from the waters of Judah: who swore in the name of the Lord, and make mention of the God of Israel, not in truth nor in righteousness. For they are called after the holy city: and upon the Lord of Israel they are established: the Lord of hosts is his name. These things I foretold would come upon the Chaldeans and Babylonians, and what I have spoken through my Prophets must be fulfilled in deed. But you, house of Jacob, and you who are called by the name of Israel, and who have come forth from the water of Judah, and the rest that follows, hear more attentively what I am about to say. And it is to be noted that he by no means calls them Jacob, but the house of Jacob; nor Israel, since they are falsely called by this name, not having the work of the name. And from the waters, he says, of Judah you have come forth, σεμνοτερῶς calling it waters in place of seed, so as to show that they are by no means sons of the virtues of the Patriarchs, but of their flesh. And rightly he called them the waters of Judah, because that tribe alone was still then remaining in the land of Judea; and at that time the royal seed of David was being preserved. Who, he says, swore in the name of the Lord, not that you might honor the Lord; but that, having taken up his name, you might do him wrong, while you want him to be the witness of your lie, and you rest in the holy city, and lean upon the Lord of Israel; so that you may boast that you are inhabitants of the city of Jerusalem, and have the privilege of the Lord Sabaoth, while you take up the empty names of Jacob, of Israel and of the holy city, and of the almighty God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.3-10":["Most of our writers, to touch briefly on a few things according to the Seventy Translators, hold that the coming of Christ is prophesied: that he comes suddenly, unhoped for, and shows his presence to a most hard people, whose ears God never opened, because its heart had grown fat, and it heard heavily with its ears. And that, as soon as the Lord was poured forth from the virginal womb, it was called transgressor and wicked, seeking to kill him. And as for what he joins: For my name's sake I will show you my fury, and my glorious things I will bring upon you, he draws on the sense of the Apostle, or rather the apostle Paul (Rom 1) takes his testimony from this passage, that the wrath of God is revealed to terrify those who sin, and afterwards glory is offered to those who have turned: Behold, he says, I have sold you, not for money, but I have sold you in your sins, and I have rescued you from the furnace of poverty. For which reason Solomon too (Prov 30) does not wish to have riches or poverty, but asks only for necessaries, lest either by the former his heart be lifted up into pride, or in the latter he be compelled to do what he does not wish and, pressed by want, to blaspheme God. Hence the Apostle too says: Having food and clothing, he says, let us be content with these (1 Tim 6:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.3-5":["The former things I declared from of old, and they went forth from my mouth, and I made them heard: suddenly I worked, and they came to pass. For I knew that you are hard, and your neck is a sinew of iron, and your brow is bronze. I foretold it to you from of old: before they came, I made it known to you, lest perhaps you should say: My idols have done these things, and my graven images and my molten images have commanded them. What you have heard, see it all: but you have not declared it. I have made you hear new things from that time: and things kept in store which you do not know: they are created now, and not from of old: and before the day, and you did not hear them, lest perhaps you should say: Behold, I knew them. You have neither heard, nor known, nor from of old was your ear opened: for I know that you will deal very treacherously, and I have called you a transgressor from the womb. For my name's sake I will put my fury far off: and with my praise I will bridle you, lest you perish. Behold, I have refined you, but not as silver: I have chosen you in the furnace of poverty. For my own sake, for my own sake I will do it, that I be not blasphemed: and my glory I will not give to another. LXX: The former things I declared still: and they went forth from my mouth: and it was made heard: suddenly I did them, and they came to pass, I know that you are hard: and your neck is a sinew of iron, and your brow is bronze: and I declared it to you long ago, before they came upon you. I made it heard to you: lest perhaps you should say that idols have done it for me, that graven and molten images have commanded me. You have heard all things, and you have not known: but I have made you hear new things, those which are now to be. And you did not say, They are being done now, and not long ago: and not in former days. Do not say either, I knew them: you neither know, nor understand, nor from the beginning did I open your ears. For I know that you will deal very treacherously: and you will be called wicked even from the womb. For my name's sake I will show you my fury: and I will bring my glorious things upon you, that I may not kill you. Behold, I have sold you, not for silver: but I have rescued you from the furnace of poverty: for my own sake I will do it, because my name is profaned: and my glory I will not give to another. I foretell to you that the Babylonians are to be overcome by the Medes and Persians, and I will suddenly do what I have threatened, lest, when the things foretold have come, you should think they happened either by the nod of the gods you worship or by chance. Nor do I boast of knowledge of the future, but I speak because of your unbelief, you whose heart I knew from the beginning to be unbelieving, and your neck of iron, and your brow of bronze.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.6-7":["Behold, you have heard all the things that are to come, and yet you hide the truth in silence. Nor do I recount the past things by which my power has often been proved: how I led the people out of Egypt, drowned the Egyptians in the Red Sea, handed over the land of promise, subjugated various nations to you. But I announce the new things that I am going to do against Babylon, so that the shameless lie of your mouth may be refuted, you who claim to know what you do not know.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.8":["For from the beginning you have been a transgressor of my commandments; and God called you a transgressor from the womb, when, set free from Egypt, you were, as it were, conceived in my womb, and brought up, and instructed. You longed for the ox's head of Egypt, saying: These are your gods, Israel, who brought you out of the land of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.9":["Not for your merit, then, but by my mercy I have put off my fury, lest you should perish utterly, and for the praise of my name I will bridle you, so that, like a beast of burden and a horse in bridles, you may follow me against your will.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.10":["Behold, I have refined you, that is, I have tested you, as silver is smelted. Or, I wished to test you not in riches but in the furnace of poverty. From this it is shown that most men are tempted both by riches and by poverty, if they either badly abuse the former or by no means bear want with virtue.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.11":["For my own sake, then, I will do it, lest my name be blasphemed among the nations, and they think that you were overcome not by my anger but by the help of their idols. And what he adds, my glory I will not give to another, signifies this: lest the idols be thought to have crushed the people of God. Or certainly, when he says, I will not give to another, he shows that he has already given it to another; for 'to another' is said to distinguish it from the former.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.12":["Hear me, Jacob, and Israel, whom I call. I myself, I am the first, and I am the last. My hand also founded the earth, and my right hand measured the heavens: I called them, and they will stand together. Gather together, all of you, and hear: who among them has declared these things? The Lord has loved him: he will do his will in Babylon, and his arm upon the Chaldeans. I, I have spoken and called him: I have brought him, and his way is made straight. Draw near to me, and hear these things: not from the beginning have I spoken in secret: from the time before it came to be, there I was. And now the Lord God has sent me, and his spirit. LXX: Hear me, Jacob and Israel, whom I call. I am the first, and I am for ever: and my hand founded the earth, and my right hand made firm the heaven. I will call them, and they will stand together: and all will be gathered, and will hear: Who has announced these things to them? Loving you, I have done your will upon Babylon: to take away the seed of the Chaldeans. I have spoken and I have called. I have brought him and have made his way prosper. Bring them to me, and hear these things: Not from the beginning have I spoken in secret: when it was being done, there I was. And now the Lord God has sent me, and his spirit. To those to whom he had already said before: Hear these things, house of Jacob, you who are called by the name of Israel, and have come forth from the waters of Judah, he now speaks: Hear me, Jacob and Israel, whom I call. For many are called, and few chosen (Matt 22). Hence he names them not chosen, because they had not yet received the Savior; but called. I am, he says, α and ω, the first and the last, who live, and was dead (Rev 22); so that you may refer life to the first, and the last to him who died. Who emptied himself, taking the form of a servant, and became obedient to the Father; he humbled himself even to death, even the death of the cross (Phil 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.13":["My hand founded the earth. Hence in Proverbs too he says: God by his wisdom founded the earth, and his right hand measured (Prov 3:19), or made firm the heavens, or the heaven, as the Seventy translated. And he calls the heavens, that they may obey his command and tell forth his glory. But if the heavens obey the will of the Lord and run in their order, why does earth and ashes glory (Sir 10), and not know its own frailty?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.14-15":["Gather together, all of you, and hear: either heavens, or the whole creation, or the whole multitude of Israel. What are the things they are bidden to hear? That the Lord has loved him: there is no doubt that he means Cyrus and Darius, who did the will of the Lord against Babylon, and exercised his arm upon the Chaldeans. And he himself spoke and called him by his name, and brought him, and his way was made straight, so that none dared to resist his might.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But according to Symmachus, who translated: Who has announced these things to him? whom the Lord has loved, who does his will in Babylon: and according to the Seventy, to take away the seed of the Chaldeans, it is referred to the person of the Lord: who is truly beloved by the Father, and who did all the will of the Father, and who overthrew in Babylon, that is, in the confusion of this world, all the seed of the Chaldeans, who are interpreted as demons. He himself spoke, and heard the Son, and brought him, who says to believers: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened (Matt 11:28).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.16":["Hence he summons them to draw near and hear, and, as the Lord foretells it, to know that the king of the Persians and Medes will come, to overthrow Babylon and destroy the Chaldeans. And to announce these things, the Prophet says that he has been sent by the Lord and by his spirit. This is according to the Hebrews and their opinion.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And hear these things which from the beginning I have spoken in secret, that is, through the riddles and mysteries of the Prophets, which had been unknown to all past generations. When all things were being made by the Father, he himself was with him, who rejoiced with him, who even now says: I, who was always with the Father, and in the Father, and was never without the Father, even now speak (John 14): and according to the frailty of the flesh I assumed, I say that the Lord God has sent me, and his spirit: and in one short verse the mystery of the Trinity is shown to us.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.17-18":["Thus says the Lord, your redeemer, the Holy One of Israel: I am the Lord your God, who teach you profitable things, who guide you in the way in which you walk. Would that you had heeded my commandments; your peace would have been like a river, and your righteousness like the whirlpools of the sea. And your seed would have been like the sand, and the offspring of your womb like its pebbles: they would not have perished, and his name would not have been worn away from before my face. Since he has promised Israel things to come, he gives the reasons why he afflicted them before: if they avoid these, they will by no means suffer the like. If, he says, you had heeded my commandments, as the Seventy translated: or certainly, with the feeling of one who wishes, would that you had heeded my commandments: if you had done them, your peace would have been like rivers, and your righteousness like the whirlpools of the sea: signifying abundance and plenty of all things.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.19":["And as for what follows: and your seed would have been like the sand, and the offspring of your womb like its pebbles: it seems indeed to hold good in the people of the Jews, who to this present day sprout sons and grandsons like little worms; but how is this to be taken as a promise, when they have neither peace nor righteousness? For either he is angry with them, or appeased. If angry, how is his seed multiplied daily? If appeased, how are they in servitude, and do not possess peace and righteousness? From this it is plain that he now speaks of the Apostolic seed, of which we also read above (cf. Isa 1): Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us a seed, we should have been like Sodom. Since this is by no means seen fulfilled at that time, it is fulfilled in the coming of Christ: and the seed of Israel remains before his face.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.20-22":["But those who, more truly and more rightly, refer these things to the Savior's coming (cf. below, Isa 61:1), of which it is said: He has sent me to announce good news to the poor, to preach release to the captives (Luke 4:18, 19), understand it to be an exhortation of those who announce the Gospel of the Lord Savior himself, that we should go forth from Babylon, that is, from the confusion of this world, and flee the Chaldeans, of whom it has often been spoken. For the Lord redeemed his servant Jacob with his most precious blood, and led him through the desert of the world, and split water from the rock. Concerning which the Apostle too says: And the rock was Christ (1 Cor 10:4). And the divine word is split, and divided into many parts, so that what we cannot receive whole we may take in parts. And lest this preaching should be thought to be spoken to all the seed of Jacob, and not only to those who were to believe through the Apostles, there is added and joined: There is no peace for the wicked, says the Lord: that is, to those who remained in their former error; who did not deserve to drink from the rock. Whose side, to speak in a new way, wounded by the lance, flowed with water and blood (John 19), consecrating for us baptism and martyrdom.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.20":["Go forth from Babylon, flee from the Chaldeans: declare it with a voice of exultation, make this heard, carry it to the end of the earth, and say: The Lord has redeemed his servant Jacob. They did not thirst in the desert when he led them out; he brought forth water from the rock for them, and he split the rock, and the waters flowed. There is no peace for the wicked, says the Lord. Those who understood the place above, where it is written: I, I have spoken, and called him: I have brought him, and his way is made straight, of Cyrus and Darius, refer these things too to his times, when the people went forth from Babylon, and fled from the Chaldeans, and was redeemed by the Lord its God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.21":["This also that is said: They did not thirst in the desert when he led them out, he brought forth water from the rock for them; and he split the rock, and the waters flowed, although they cannot show it fulfilled according to the history (for under Zerubbabel and Ezra they did not come through a desert, nor did a split rock furnish them waters, as is related to have happened to those going out of Egypt), they nevertheless maintain was fulfilled hyperbolically, in the likeness of the former happiness, when they came into Judea through the desert of the nations, and were set free from captivity.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.48.22":["And so that we may know, they say, that it was prophesied not of Christ but of Cyrus, there is joined: There is no peace for the wicked, says the Lord: and the sense is: Perfect happiness will not be except under Christ, which is reserved for the last time.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.1":["Hear, islands, and give heed, peoples from afar: the Lord called me from the womb, from the belly of my mother he remembered my name. And he made my mouth like a sharp sword: in the shadow of his hand he protected me. And he made me like a chosen arrow: in his quiver he hid me; and he said to me: You are my servant, Israel, for in you I will glory. And I said: I have labored in vain, without cause and vainly I have spent my strength: therefore my judgment is with the Lord, and my work with my God. LXX: Hear me, islands, and give heed, nations. After a long time it shall stand, says the Lord. From the belly he called me, and from the womb of my mother he called my name. And he made my mouth like a sharp sword, and under the protection of his hand he hid me. He made me like a chosen arrow, and in his quiver he hid me. And he said to me: You are my servant, Israel: and in you I will be glorified. And I said, I have labored without cause, in vain, and I have given my strength for nothing. Therefore my judgment is with the Lord, and my labor before my God. I know that both these things and those further on that we are going to speak of belong to the knowledge or understanding of a single chapter, and that all must be taken in the person of Christ. But I did not wish, by setting everything out together, to burden the reader's mind, and to confound by its magnitude what can more easily be said in parts. Hence I have also set down both editions, so that what seems obscure in the one may be unlocked by the reading of the other. After the calling of the remnant of Israel, then, and the casting off of the people that remained in unbelief, of whom he had said: There is no peace for the wicked, says the Lord (Isa 48:22), he passes over to the Churches gathered from the nations, and speaks to them under the name of islands. These lie open to the ambushes of persecutors as to the waves of the sea, and, with shipwreck raging on every side, are beaten rather than moved. And lest anyone think that our exposition is forced, and that what is said belongs not to the nations but to the Synagogues of the Jewish people, there follows: And give heed, peoples, or nations, from afar, that is, from the furthest ends of the earth. Or, as the Seventy translated, after a long time it shall stand, that is, not at this time in which they are spoken; but they will come to pass after many times. The Lord, he says, called me from the womb, and from the belly of my mother he remembered my name. Which for the present seems obscure to the hearers, but afterwards will become known to all the nations, when Gabriel will have said to Joseph of the virgin birth: And you shall call his name Jesus: for he will save his people (Matt 1:21).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.2":["He also made his mouth like a sharp sword, so that with the spirit of his mouth he may slay the wicked one. Of which sword he himself also speaks in the Gospel: I came not to send peace upon the earth, but a sword, separating the evil from the good: For I came to divide a man against his father, and a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law (Matt 10:34, 35). And in the shadow, he says, of his hand he protected me, so that the lowliness of the flesh might be covered by the power of the divinity, as the angel announced to the Virgin: The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you (Luke 1:35). He made me, he says, like a chosen arrow, in his quiver he hid me. When he says, a chosen arrow, he shows that God has very many arrows, but not chosen ones: which arrows are the Prophets and the Apostles, who run through the whole world. Of whom it is sung in another place too: Your arrows are sharp, O most mighty; peoples will fall beneath you (Ps 44 [45]:6); and again: The sharp arrows of the mighty, with desolating coals (Ps 119 [120]:4). But Christ, out of many arrows and very many sons, is the one chosen arrow, and the Only-begotten Son: whom he hid in his quiver, that is, in a human body, so that the fullness of the divinity might dwell in him bodily. And rare is the faith of believers: to whom it is also said above (Isa 45:15): You are a hidden God, and we did not know it. Receiving a wound from this arrow, the bride too speaks in the Song of Songs: I am wounded with love.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to tropology this place can be explained thus: Judah is stretched as a bow when the Lord and Savior is sent by the Father into this world, he who is himself both the bow and the archer and the arrow. The bow, as in the present place. The archer, as in Psalm 44 (45): Your arrows are sharp, most mighty; and when someone has been wounded by them, he says: I am wounded with love. And the arrow is he himself who speaks through Isaiah: He has set me as a chosen arrow, and in his quiver he has hidden me. The chosen arrow is the Word of God; the quiver in which the arrow is hidden is the dispensation of the flesh he assumed.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ISA.49.3":["And he said to me: You are my servant, Israel: for in you I will be glorified (Song 4:9). A servant, because, though he was in the form of God, he deigned to take the form of a servant (Phil 2), and Israel, because he was born of the seed of the Jews. And, what can be understood of no other of the servants, there is joined: For in you I will glory. For he himself also says in the Gospel, Father, glorify your name (John 12:28). He it is who in the psalm says to the Son: Arise, my glory, and arise, psaltery and harp (Ps 56 [57]:9), that is, the choir of all the virtues.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.4":["And when the Father said to me these things that I have related, I answered him: How are you glorified in me, Father, since I have labored in vain, and could not call back to you a great part of the Jewish people? And all these things are said to show the free will of man. For it is God's to call, and ours to believe: nor, if we do not believe, is God at once powerless; but he leaves his power to our choice, so that the will of the righteous may obtain its reward. Since, then, they would not believe in you through me, my judgment is with you, that I have done all things that I ought to have done for them, saying in the Gospel: I have glorified you on the earth, having completed the work which you gave me to do (John 17:4); and again: I have manifested your name to men. And my work, or my labor and pain (for this is what πόνος signifies), is in your sight. For in the Gospel too he wept over Jerusalem (Luke 19), and in the psalm, because of the multitude of unbelievers, he says that in a certain way he suffered in vain: What profit is there in my blood, while I go down to corruption (Ps 29 [30]:10)? And according to the Hebrew, hanging on the cross, he says: Far from my salvation are the words of my lamentations (Ps 21 [22]:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.5-6":["And now says the Lord, who formed me from the womb to be his servant, that I might bring Jacob back to him, and Israel will not be gathered: and I am glorified in the eyes of the Lord, and my God has become my strength. And he said: It is a small thing that you should be my servant to raise up the tribes of Jacob, and to turn back the dregs of Israel. I have given you as a light of the nations; that you may be my salvation to the end of the earth. LXX: And now thus says the Lord, who formed me from the womb to be his servant, that I might gather Jacob to him, and Israel: I shall be gathered and glorified before the Lord, and my God shall be my strength. And he said to me: It is a great thing for you to be called my child, and to raise up the tribes of Jacob, and to turn back the dispersion of Israel. Behold, I have set you for a covenant of the race, for a light of the nations, that you may be salvation to the end of the earth. When I said, I have labored in vain, without cause and vainly I have spent my strength, because the Jews would not believe, and my judgment is with the Lord, and my work, which with his help I have fulfilled, is with the Lord: the Lord, who formed me from the womb to be his servant, answered me. By this he shows that he is called servant who was formed from the womb. He also says in the psalm: From the belly of my mother you are my God (Ps 21 [22]:11). What, then, does the Lord say to him? That he should bring back to him Jacob, who had wandered off, who, having forsaken his Creator, was serving idols. Hence he himself says to the disciples: Do not go into the way of the Gentiles, and do not enter the cities of the Samaritans; but go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 10:5); and in another place: I came only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 15:24). This, then, was the will of the Father: that the wicked vinedressers should receive the Son who was sent, and render the fruits of the vineyard; but they killed him, saying: Come, let us kill him, and the inheritance will be ours (Matt 21:38); and this is what he now says: And Israel will not be gathered, that is, will not return to the Lord. And I greatly wonder how the common edition has overturned, by a different interpretation, a very strong testimony against the faithlessness of the Jews, saying: I shall be gathered and glorified before the Lord: since Theodotion and Symmachus agree with our interpretation. About Aquila, however, I do not wonder, that a man most learned in the Hebrew language, and rendering word for word, has in this place either feigned ignorance or been deceived by the perverse exposition of the Pharisees, since he chose to translate, and Israel will be gathered to him, that is, to God. For the Hebrew word LO in this place is not written with LAMED and VAV (לו), which, if it were, would signify to him or to that one, but with LAMED and ALEPH (לא), which properly sounds not. Since, then, Jacob was not brought back to God, nor Israel gathered, therefore, since they did not believe, the Son says: I am glorified in the eyes of the Lord. For in me the whole world has believed: and my God has become my strength, who also comforted me in my sadness over the casting off of my people; and said to me: It is a small thing if you serve me to raise up the tribes of Jacob, which fell through their own fault; and to turn back the dregs, or remnant, of Israel. For this is what the Hebrew word NESURE (נצורי) sounds. For in their place I have given you as a light of all the nations, to illumine the whole world, and to make my salvation, through which all are saved, reach to the ends of the earth. And what is read in the Seventy, I shall be gathered, and glorified before the Lord, can be understood thus: that the Lord has been gathered together with the believers. And as for what follows: He said to me: It is a great thing for you to be called my child, let us refer 'great' to the man and to the child, who is small in comparison with God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.6":["Nor is it any wonder that men, holy though they are, are nobly called servants of God, since through the prophet Isaiah the Father speaks to the Son: \"It is a great thing for you to be called my child (puer)\" (Isa. 49:6), which in Greek is said: μέγα σοί ἐστι τοῦ κληθῆναί σε παῖδά μου. Now because \"child\" (puer), that is, παῖς, can according to the Greeks signify both servant (famulus) and son, we inquired in the Hebrew, and found written not \"my son\" but \"my servant\", that is, ABDI (עבדי). Hence also the prophet Obadiah (Abdias), whose name means \"servant of the Lord\", received his name from his service to God. If it disturbs anyone that the Lord and Savior, who is the founder of the universe, is called a servant of God, he will not be disturbed if he hears him speaking to the apostles: \"Whoever wishes to be greater among you, let him be the servant of all\"; and: \"The Son of man came, not to be served, but to serve\" (Matt. 20:27, 28); and, lest he should seem to teach this by words alone, he showed it by example. For taking a towel, he girded himself, and filling a basin with water, he washed the feet of the disciples (John 13). It is therefore not impious to believe that he who had taken the form of a servant did those things which belong to a servant, so that he may be said to have served his Father's will, since he himself served his own servants.\n— Commentary on Titus"],"ISA.49.7":["Thus says the Lord, the redeemer of Israel, his Holy One, to the contemptible soul, to the abhorred nation, to the servant of lords. Kings shall see, and princes shall rise up, and they shall worship for the Lord's sake, for he is faithful: and the Holy One of Israel, who has chosen you. LXX: Thus says the Lord who delivered you, the God of Israel. Sanctify him who despises his own soul, who is an abomination to the nations, who is the servant of princes. Kings shall see him, and princes shall rise up, and they shall worship him for the Lord's sake: for the Holy One of Israel is faithful, and he has chosen you. In place of what we have said: to the contemptible soul, to the abhorred nation, to the servant of lords, Theodotion translated: to him who despises his soul, who is an abomination to the nation, who is the servant of princes: which plainly fits the person of Christ. For he, the good shepherd, laid down his soul for his sheep (John 10), and despised it, he who is an abomination to the nation of the Jews, who curse him three times every day in their synagogues under the name of the Nazarenes. He was the servant of princes, and so humble that he stood before Annas and Caiaphas, and was sent to Pilate and Herod to be crucified. With this interpretation Aquila agreed, and in part the Seventy, although they changed and weakened the sense in this, that for nation they translated nations, and for servant servants. Others, however, think that this is said to the nation of the Jews, which despised its own soul, and is an abomination to the whole world, and serves princes, of whom it is written: Who devour my people as they eat bread (Ps 13 [14]:4). But the interpretation concerning Christ is better. What, then, does the Father, once the redeemer and the Holy One of Israel, say to the Son? Kings shall see, and princes shall rise up, and they shall worship, when he comes in the glory of the Father with his angels, and sits on the throne of his glory, judging the living and the dead: then all will worship him for the sake of the Lord his Father, who chose him. Or it is to be understood thus: Kings, whose heart is in the hand of God, and the princes of the Church of God, will worship you: for the Lord is faithful, the Holy One of Israel, who has chosen you. And all these things he refers to him who despised his own soul, who is abhorred by the nation, who is the servant of princes.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.8-9":["And he kept him, or formed him, when death had been overcome, and gave him as a covenant of the people of the Jews, that is, of those among them who were willing to believe: that he might raise up the land, which lay in the errors of idolatry, and possess the scattered, or deserted, inheritances, which did not have God as their inhabitant, and might say to those who were in bonds, Go forth, those who were held fast by the bonds of sins, since each one is bound fast with the cords of his own sins (Prov 5); and to those who were in darkness, Be revealed: those who sat in darkness and in the shadow of death, and could not see the light. And after they have been converted, and have beheld the clear light of Christ, they shall feed in the ways and in the paths of the holy Scriptures, and shall say: The Lord feeds me, and I shall lack nothing; in a place of pasture there he has set me: he has brought me up beside the waters of refreshment (Ps 22 [23]:2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.8":["Thus says the Lord: In a pleasing time I have heard you, and in the day of salvation I have helped you. And I have kept you, and given you as a covenant of the people, that you might raise up the land, and possess the scattered inheritances: that you might say to those who are bound: Go forth; and to those who are in darkness: Be revealed. In the ways they shall feed, and in all the plains shall be their pastures. They shall not hunger nor thirst, and the heat and the sun shall not strike them: for he who has compassion on them shall rule them, and shall give them drink at the springs of waters. And I will make all my mountains a way, and my paths shall be raised up. Behold, these shall come from afar, and behold, those from the North and the sea, and these from the land of the South. Praise, O heavens, and exult, O earth: shout praise for joy, O mountains, for the Lord has comforted his people, and will have mercy on his poor. LXX: Thus says the Lord: In an acceptable time I have heard you, and in the day of salvation I have helped you. And I have formed you, and given you as a covenant of the nations, that you might establish the land, and possess the deserted inheritances. And that you might say to those who are in bonds, Go forth, and to those who are in darkness, Be revealed: in all the ways they shall feed, and in all the paths shall be their pastures. They shall not hunger nor thirst, nor shall the heat nor the sun strike them; but he who has mercy on them shall comfort them, and shall lead them by the springs of waters. And I will make every mountain a way, and every path a pasture for them. Behold, these shall come from afar, these from the North and the sea: and others from the land of the Persians. Rejoice, O heavens, and let the earth exult, let the mountains break forth into gladness: for God has had mercy on his people, and has comforted the lowly of his people. The Apostle Paul used this testimony in the second Epistle to the Corinthians, saying: In an acceptable time I have heard you: and in the day of salvation I have helped you. Behold, now is the acceptable time, etc. (2 Cor 6:2). If, then, the vessel of election refers what is said to the understanding of the first coming, let us too follow the footsteps of his exposition, and, like little children, press our letters upon the lines our teacher has traced. The pleasing and acceptable time, and the day of salvation, is the passion and resurrection of the Savior, when he prayed on the cross: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me (Matt 27:46)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.10":["And he who has been fed and nourished in such ways and paths shall neither hunger nor thirst, nor feel the heat of the sun: and in him what is written will be fulfilled: The sun shall not burn you by day, nor the moon by night (Ps 120 [121]:6). So that he feels neither the adversities nor the prosperities of this world. Since the Lord is merciful and compassionate, he himself will comfort them and rule them, and lead them to the springs of waters. Or, he will give them drink at the springs, of which it is written: Bless the Lord from the springs of Israel (Ps 67 [68]:27). And in another place: Draw waters from the springs of the Savior (Isa 12:3). These springs are in the Old Testament and the New.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.11":["And all the stumbling-blocks that could hinder the steps of believers the Lord will turn into level ground for them, and he will bring low the high places and raise up the low, that they may have a level and open road.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.12":["And who these are for whom the way is prepared, he sets out more plainly: Behold, these shall come from afar: and behold, those from the North and the sea, and these from the land of the South. Showing the four regions of the world, East and North, West and South, he put afar for the East: for the southern region, in the Hebrew is read SINIM (סינים), which the Seventy translated of the Persians. The rest expressed it as it is read in the Hebrew, sinim, which we have translated from the South: suspecting this, that Mount Sinai is situated in the southern part, according to the Prophet Habakkuk: God will come from the South, and the holy one from Mount Paran, shady and thick (Hab 3). But if by sinim, as the Seventy translated, we understand the Persians, who are situated toward the East, we shall be able to refer what is said above, Behold, these shall come from afar, to the South.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.13":["And the heavens and the earth are commanded, either the powers that dwell in heaven and on earth, or angels and men, to sing together the praises of God. And those who are set on the height of the virtues are to testify to the gladness of their mind with jubilation and exultation. For the Lord has comforted his people, those of the Jews who were willing to believe. And he has had mercy on his poor and lowly ones: or on all his people, who were called to him from East and West, North and South, having neither Law nor Prophets nor spiritual riches, but, deserted, poor and lowly, lay subject to all the demons.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.14":["And Zion said: The Lord has forsaken me, and the Lord has forgotten me. Can a woman forget her infant, so as not to have pity on the son of her womb? And if she should forget, I will not forget you. Behold, I have engraved you on my hands: your walls are always before my eyes. Your builders have come: and those who destroyed you and scattered you shall go forth from you. Lift up your eyes round about, and see, all these are gathered together, they have come to you. As I live, says the Lord, you shall be clothed with all these as with an ornament, and you shall put them about you like a bride, for your deserts and your solitudes, and the land of your ruin, shall now be too narrow for the inhabitants, and those who swallowed you up shall be chased far away. Still they shall say in your ears, the sons of your barrenness: The place is too narrow for me, make room for me to dwell. And you shall say in your heart: Who has begotten these for me? I was barren and not bearing, carried away and captive: and who has brought these up? I was left destitute and alone: and these, where were they? LXX: But Zion said: The Lord has forsaken me, and God has forgotten me. Will a woman forget her little one, so as not to have pity on the offspring of her womb? But if a woman should forget even these, yet I will not forget you, says the Lord. Behold, upon my hands I have painted your walls: and you are always before me. And quickly you shall be built by those by whom you had been destroyed: and those who scattered you shall go forth from you. Lift up your eyes round about, and see: all these are gathered together, they have come to you. As I live, says the Lord, you shall be clothed with all these as with an ornament, and you shall put them about you like a bride's necklace: for your deserts, and the places that were scattered, and those that fell down. Now you shall be straitened because of the inhabitants: and those who had humbled you shall be far from you. For your sons, whom you lost, shall say in your ears: The place is too narrow for me: make me a place to dwell. And you shall say in your heart: Who has begotten these for me? But I was childless and a widow, and who has brought these up for me? I was left alone, and these, where were they? We have often recalled that Jerusalem and Zion are to be understood in four ways in the holy Scriptures. In one way, according to the Jews, the city over which the Lord laments in the Gospel: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the Prophets, and stone those who are sent to you (Matt 23:37). And in another place: When you see Jerusalem surrounded by an army, then know that its desolation has drawn near (Luke 21:20). Second, it is the congregation of the Saints, who, set in the peace of the Lord and on the watchtower of the virtues, are rightly called Zion, of which it is said: Its foundations are on the holy mountains: the Lord loves the gates of Zion above all the tents of Jacob (Ps 86 [87]:1). For neither were the foundations of the Jewish Zion, which we see destroyed, loved by the Lord, nor could what is loved by the Lord be destroyed. Third, the multitude of the Angels, Dominions and Powers, and all that is appointed to the ministry of God, is called Jerusalem. Of this Jerusalem the Apostle too was speaking: But the Jerusalem which is above is free, which is the mother of us all (Gal 4:26). And in another place: But you have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem (Heb 12:22). Fourth, that is called Jerusalem which the Jews, and our own Judaizers, following the Apocalypse of John, which they do not understand, think is to be set down from heaven, golden and jeweled (Rev 21), whose bounds and boundless breadth they think are described also in the last part of Ezekiel. Since these things are so, we must now consider more carefully which of the four Zions has said: The Lord has forsaken me, and the Lord has forgotten me. And there is no doubt that it is the congregation of the Saints, which formerly was among the Jews and has been forsaken by the Lord, that recalls these things, and complains with a tearful voice that it has been deserted and left destitute of the Lord's help.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.15":["To it God answered, using a likeness from nature: If it can happen that a mother forgets her infant, and is not moved by pity toward the offspring of her own womb, then I too will forget you. I will say something more: even if she should forget, overcoming the laws of nature by hardness of mind, yet I will not forget my creature, and I will always hold the souls of the Saints in my heart.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.16-17":["For know, you who think yourself utterly forsaken, that you are engraved and painted on my hands, and that your walls remain continually before my eyes. From this we learn that Jerusalem is by no means to be sought in the region of Palestine, which is the worst of the whole province, and rough with rocky mountains, and suffers a dearth of water, so that it uses the rains of heaven, and consoles the scarcity of springs by the construction of cisterns; but in the hands of God, to whom it is said: Your builders have hastened. Or, according to the Seventy: Quickly you shall be built by those by whom you had been destroyed. For destroyed by the Jews, it was built by the Jews. Deserted because of the guilt of the Scribes and Pharisees, it was gathered together at the preaching of the Apostles of Christ, both from the Jews and from the nations. There follows: And those who destroyed you and scattered you shall go forth from you: the most wicked teachers; so that you may by no means follow the commandments and traditions of men (Matt 15), but the law of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.18":["And she is told to lift up her eyes round about, and see the sons who had been gathered to her. Of whom the Lord too spoke: Lift up your eyes, and see that the fields are already white for harvest (John 4:35). And to make us secure: As I live, says the Lord (which, according to the Old Testament, is said by the custom of swearing), you shall be clothed with all these as with an ornament, and you shall put them about you, as a bride puts a necklace about herself. Happy is he who is of such merit and such virtue that he is called an ornament of the Church. I think, moreover, that these signify the various spiritual graces by which the bride's array is adorned. Of her too it is sung in Psalm 44 (45): The queen stood at your right hand in gilded clothing, surrounded with variety (Ps 44 [45]:10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.19":["For what had before been deserted, and had fallen into solitudes and ruins, will, at the coming of the Gospel of Christ, be restored, and will have so great a multitude of inhabitants that it cannot hold them. Provided only that the persecutors be chased far away, or those of whom we spoke above: Those who destroyed you and scattered you shall go forth from you.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.20":["And the sons of your barrenness, whom you thought you had utterly lost, and by them to have been widowed, shall say in your ears: The place is too narrow for me in the synagogues, make room for me in the Churches, to dwell more widely, so that I may not be crushed by the blasphemies of the Jews, so that your breadth may hold the whole world together with me.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.21":["But you, unable to utter with your mouth the greatness of your joy, will ponder silently in your heart, and will say: Who has begotten these for me? I was barren and a widow, deserted and captive among the people of the Jews; I had ceased to have sons; for a long time I had not given birth. After Haggai and Zechariah and Malachi I had seen no other Prophets until John the Baptist; and how is it that I, who had been alone and destitute of a husband's help, have now begun to have so many sons? And so that we may know that Zion is built from both peoples upon the rock and foundation of Christ, Paul says to believers: Built upon the foundation of the Apostles and Prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief cornerstone. (Eph 2:20.) From this it is plain that the one foundation of the Apostles and Prophets is our Lord Jesus Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.22":["Thus says the Lord God: Behold, I lift up my hand to the nations, and to the peoples I will raise up my standard: and they shall bring your sons in their arms, and carry your daughters upon their shoulders. And kings shall be your foster fathers, and queens your nurses. With face bowed to the ground they shall worship you, and lick the dust of your feet: and you shall know that I am the Lord, in whom those who wait for him shall not be confounded. LXX: Thus said the Lord God: Behold, I raise my hand over the nations, and over the islands I will lift up my standard, and they shall bring your sons in their bosom, and carry your daughters on their shoulders. And kings shall be your foster fathers: and princesses your nurses: on the face of the earth they shall worship you, and lick the dust of your feet; and you shall know that I am the Lord, and those who wait for me shall not be confounded. To what the Church had said: I was barren and captive, who has begotten these for me? I was left destitute and alone, and these, where were they? the Lord answered: Do you wonder why these things have come about? You will by no means wonder when you hear that I have lifted up my hand to the nations. Of this hand the Saint too says: You have formed me, and laid your hand upon me (Ps 138 [139]:4). And the Savior himself: No one can snatch out of my Father's hand (John 10:29). And the Apostle Peter: Humble yourselves under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in the time of visitation (1 Pet 5:6). This hand lifted up to the nations is the very one of which this same Prophet speaks here: There shall be the root of Jesse, who shall rise up to be the prince of the nations: in him the nations shall hope (Isa 11:10). And not only will he lift up his hand to the nations; but he will also raise up his standard among the peoples. There is no doubt that this is the banner of the cross, so that what is written may be fulfilled: The earth is full of his praises. And again: In all the earth his name is wonderful (Ps 8:1). Then they shall bring the sons of Zion in their arms, or in their bosom, and carry her daughters upon their shoulders. Such too was Lazarus, and all the saints who rest in the bosom of Abraham (Luke 16), and the souls of believers, to whom Paul the Apostle said: I gave you milk to drink (1 Cor 3:2). And elsewhere: My little children, with whom I am again in labor until Christ be formed in you (Gal 4:19). And in another place: As a nurse cherishes her children: so, longing for you, we desire to impart to you not only the Gospel of Christ, but also our own souls (1 Thess 2:8). Otherwise it is ridiculous, in the Jewish manner, for full-grown sons and daughters to be carried in arms and on shoulders.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.23":["But the kings who are foster fathers, and the queens and princesses who are nurses, plainly point to the Apostles and to Apostolic men: such as Abraham too was, to whom it is said: You are a king from God among us (Gen 23:6); and whose heart is in the hand of God (Prov 21): who say to believers: As newborn babes, rational and without guile, desire milk (1 Pet 2:2). The princess also, that is, ἄρχουσα, which is the sound of Sarah's name, and the queens, or the queen, of whom it is sung in Psalm 44 (45): The queen stood at your right hand in gilded clothing, nourishes Christ's little ones daily, and leads them to perfect age: and every age, sex and rank will worship Zion for the sake of him who dwells in her. For if Christ is the head of the Church, the head is worshiped in the body. And if it is said to certain people: Exalt the Lord our God, and worship the footstool of his feet (not that the footstool is to be worshiped; but that the majesty of his feet is indicated), why should not the Church be worshiped, which embraces the whole body of Christ, so that what is written in Zephaniah may be fulfilled: They shall worship him, each from his own place: all the islands of the nations (Zeph 2:11), so that they by no means come to Jerusalem three times a year in the Jewish manner, but, worshiping the Lord in their own place, possess Jerusalem. And as for what follows: and they shall lick the dust of your feet, this signifies that kings and princes, whatever of earthly work has clung to the feet of the Church, wipe and lick it away by their word. Hence the Apostles too are commanded to shake off the dust of their feet. And it is said to Peter that he who has once been washed needs only to wash his feet. (Matt 10; John 13). Through all these things the Church learns that there is no other Lord besides him, and those who have waited for him will possess eternal glory.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.24-25":["Shall the prey be taken from the strong? or can what has been captured by the mighty be safe? For thus says the Lord: Indeed even the captivity shall be taken from the strong: and what has been carried off by the mighty shall be saved. But those who have judged you, I will judge: and your sons I will save. And I will feed your enemies with their own flesh, and they shall be drunk with their own blood as with new wine: and all flesh shall know that I am the Lord who saves you, and your redeemer, the strong one of Jacob. LXX: Shall anyone take spoils from a giant? And if anyone leads a captive away unjustly, he shall be saved. Thus says the Lord: If anyone captures a giant, he shall take spoils, and he who takes from the strong shall be saved. But I will judge my judgment. And I will rescue my sons, and those who afflicted you shall eat their own flesh, and shall drink their own blood like new wine, and shall be drunk: and all flesh shall know that I am the Lord who rescued you, and the help of the strength of Jacob. Our Lord and Savior, who had spoken earlier through Isaiah, himself repeats the same sense in the Gospel: how can anyone enter the house of a strong man and plunder his goods, unless he first binds the strong man, and then he will plunder his house (Mark 3)? The strong man and giant is the devil, who had subjugated all the nations to his rule, daring to say to the Savior: All these things have been delivered to me, and I will give them to you, if you will fall down and worship me (Matt 4:9). For the world lies in the evil one (1 John 5), and none of the righteous could conquer him: nor, according to Amos, take the prey from the lion's mouth (Amos 3). The question having been set, then, that no one could conquer the strong man and the giant, nor save what had been seized by him, the Lord answered that all would be accomplished by his coming: namely, that the nations formerly captured would be taken from the giant, and all his furniture and his whole household be divided among the Apostles. So is fulfilled what is written: The king of the powers of the beloved, and to the beauty of the house to divide the spoils. For he it is who, ascending on high, led captivity captive, he gave gifts among men (Ps 67 [68]:13, 19; Eph 4): that is, those who had formerly been taken captive unto death he received unto life. Hence Symmachus translated more plainly: But even the captivity of the strong one shall be taken away, and the plunder of the terrible one shall be safe. He wishes the strong and terrible one to be understood as the devil, who has been overcome by the power of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.49.25-26":["Therefore the Lord, judging those who had oppressed Zion, or judging her judgment, will set free her sons who had been captured by the giant, and will save them, and he will feed the adverse powers with their own flesh, and make them drunk as with new wine, so that they may be glutted not by the slaughter of others but by their own death. And those who are flesh, because they have lost the spirit of God, are to feed on their own flesh. This indeed the Saint also says in the Psalms: When those who afflicted me drew near to eat my flesh (Ps 26 [27]:2). For it is not the spirit, which is incorporeal, that is eaten, but the flesh, which lies open to the bites of fierce beasts. And then Zion will learn, nay, all flesh which will see the salvation of God, that her Redeemer and Savior is he who wrestled with Jacob, or who was the helper of the strength of Jacob: so that in his passion, lifted up on high, he blessed those who crucified him: Father, forgive them: for they do not know what they are doing (Luke 23:34).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.1":["Thus says the Lord: What is this bill of divorce of your mother, with which I put her away? Or who is my creditor, to whom I sold you? Behold, for your iniquities you were sold, and for your crimes I put away your mother. For I came, and there was no man: I called, and there was none to hear. LXX: Thus says the Lord: What kind of bill of divorce of your mother is it, with which I put her away? Or to which creditor and exactor of mine did I sell you? Behold, for your sins you were sold, and for your iniquities I put away your mother, for I came, and there was no human being: I called, and there was none to obey. After the calling of the nations, and the kings and princes, the foster fathers and nurses, and the captivity of the one formerly strong and mighty, whose prey has been distributed to the Apostles, and after the madness of the demons, who have been glutted with their own flesh and made drunk with their own blood, when all flesh has known that the Redeemer and the strong one is himself the God of Jacob, he speaks to the people of the Jews concerning Zion, who had said before: The Lord has forsaken me, and the Lord has forgotten me. Do you think that your mother, the earthly Jerusalem, has been cast off by my harshness, and that I, of an unbending mind, gave her a bill of divorce, and do you not rather understand, what is true, that she withdrew from me by her own fault? For when I said to her: Cease to act wickedly, learn to do good, she would not hear me; but turned against me a shoulder that drew away: hence I said to her sons: Woe to them, for they have withdrawn from me. Manifest are those who have acted wickedly against me. And to her herself: Your departure will instruct you, and your wickedness will rebuke you; and you will know that it is evil for you that you have forsaken me. But perhaps you cannot show a bill of divorce, and some creditor of mine, demanding money, while I had nothing to pay, takes you in compensation for the debt? It is not so: but I will show you why I have forsaken your mother with her sons. Your crimes and sins have sold you to the demons, so that, ensnared by the pleasures of the present age, both you forsook your parent and she her husband. Hence I could no longer keep your adulterous mother, but, as she wished to go, I let her. And that each one is sold by his own sins, since, left to our own choice, we are led by our will either to good or to evil, Paul the apostle also teaches: But I am carnal, sold under sin (Rom 7:14). For he who commits sin is the servant of sin (John 8). And as the avaricious and the robbers are servants of mammon, so every sin lords it over sinners. To whom it is said: Let not sin reign in your mortal body (Rom 6:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.2-3":["Is my hand shortened, and made very small, so that it cannot redeem? Or is there no power in me to deliver? Behold, at my rebuke I will make the sea a desert, I will turn the rivers into dry land: the fish shall rot for lack of water, they shall die of thirst. I will clothe the heavens with darkness, and make sackcloth their covering. LXX: Is my hand not able to deliver, or is it not strong enough to rescue? Behold, by my threat I will make the sea a desert, and make the rivers deserts, and their fish shall dry up, because there is no water, and they shall die of thirst. And I will clothe the heaven with darkness, and make its covering as haircloth. Against those who thought that the Lord had not been able to free his people from captivity, he sets out a reason and the fullest examples: that he who made the Red Sea passable for his people (Exod 14), dried up the streams of the Jordan, and, when the rivers in Egypt dried up, turned the fish to rottenness (Exod 7), and who for three days in Egypt made a darkness that could be felt, so that the heaven seemed covered as with sackcloth and darkness, could surely also free his people from danger. Or, because he had said above: I came, and there was no human being: I called, and there was none to hear, we can say this: that he who is the performer of such great signs, and makes heaven and earth and the seas serve his nod, could also himself have escaped the cross, as he says in the Gospel: Or do you think that I cannot ask my Father, and he would give me at once more than twelve legions of angels (Matt 26:53)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.2":["And so that you may know that your mother was not repudiated by me, but withdrew by her own will, after many benefits I took a human body, and spoke no more through the Prophets, but in person. I came, and there was no man, or human being. For all, abandoning the image of man and of a human being, took on the images of beasts and serpents. Hence it is said to Herod because of his malice: Go and tell that fox (Luke 13:32). And to the Pharisees: Brood of vipers (Matt 23:33). And to the lustful: They have become horses mad after females (Jer 5:8). And of the pleasure-lovers: Do not cast your pearls before swine. And to the shameless: Nor give what is holy to dogs (Matt 7:6). And in common of them all: The vision of the four-footed beasts that were in the desert (Isa 30). The Lord therefore came, and did not find a man. For man, when he was in honor, did not understand: he was compared to senseless cattle, and became like them (Ps 48 [49]:13). I called them, he says, as a rational animal, and said: Incline your ear to the words of my mouth (Ps 77 [78]), and my people did not hear my voice. I cried out, and said: If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink (John 7:37). And in another place: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened (Matt 11:28). And there was none to hear; hence I said to them in the Gospel: You have neither seen the form of God, nor heard his voice, because you do not have his word abiding in you (John 5:37, 38).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to the anagogy, at the rebuke of the Lord the sea becomes a desert when all the bitterness of this world is dried up, and the rivers are made desolate, of which the spiritual dragon in Egypt said: The rivers are mine, and I made them (Ezek 29:9). And of which we read in another place: What have you to do with the way of the Assyrians, to drink the waters of the rivers (Jer 2:18)? The fish too, when the net has been cast into the sea, shall rot, those which have been separated from the good fish.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.3":["And as for what follows: I will clothe the heaven with darkness, and its covering shall be as haircloth, either let us understand by heaven all that is above us, as the birds which are in the air are called heavenly, and the adverse powers, which run to and fro between heaven and earth, are said to be heavenly. Or certainly the heaven is clothed with darkness when it is covered with clouds. According to what is written: Who covers the heaven with clouds, and gives rain to the earth (Ps 146 [147]:8). And in threatening drought God says: I will make the heaven bronze, and the earth iron (Deut 28:23). Not that the nature of the elements is changed, but that by bronze and iron the greatness of the punishments is shown. The philosophers say that the clouds are not raised up more than ten stadia from the earth, and hide the brightness of the sun. It is not, then, the heaven that is wrapped in sackcloth, but, the light of heaven being shut off, the air that is beneath is darkened by the darkness of the cloud. We can interpret the heavens wrapped in darkness and covered with sackcloth thus: that we say all are under sin, and that the saints too need the mercy of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.4-7":["The Lord has given me a learned tongue, that I may know how to sustain by a word him who is weary. He rouses in the morning, in the morning he rouses my ear, that I may hear him as a master. The Lord God has opened my ear: and I do not gainsay; I have not gone backward. I gave my body to those who struck, and my cheeks to those who plucked them. I did not turn away my face from those who rebuked and spat. The Lord God is my helper, therefore I am not confounded: therefore I have set my face like a very hard rock, and I know that I shall not be confounded. LXX: The Lord gives me a tongue of discipline, that I may know when I ought to speak a word. He set me in the morning, he added to me an ear for hearing; and the discipline of the Lord opens my ears. But I do not refuse, nor do I gainsay. I gave my back to the scourges, and my cheeks to blows. But I did not turn away my face from the shame of spittings. And the Lord is my helper, therefore I am not confounded: but I have set my face like a very strong rock, and I shall know that I shall not be confounded. The Jews, separating this little chapter from what goes before, want to refer it to the person of Isaiah: that he says he received from the Lord the word by which to sustain the weary and wandering people and call it back to salvation; and that, after the manner of little children, who are taught in the morning hours, he hears from the Holy Spirit what he should speak; and that he did not gainsay his command, but when the Lord asked, Whom shall I send, and who will go to this people? he answered him: Here am I, send me (Isa 6:8). And because he had said, Hear the word of the Lord, you rulers of Sodom: give ear to the Law of our God, you people of Gomorrah (Isa 1:10), he suffered such hard things that he was exposed not only to insulting words but also to the pains of blows. Yet, through his awareness of the God who commanded him, he was not at all terrified; but, according to what is said to Ezekiel, Behold, I have made your face stronger than their faces, and your forehead harder than their foreheads; like adamant and flint I have made your face (Ezek 3:8, 9), he beat down all their assaults. So let those say who strive by every means to overturn the prophecies about Christ and to twist them by a crooked interpretation into a perverse understanding: as if, should these things too have been written of Isaiah, they could take away the other testimonies about Christ, which are so plain that they pour their clear light into the eyes of all. To the person of the Lord, then, with whom the previous book also ended, these things too are to be referred: that according to the dispensation of the body he assumed he was taught, and received a tongue of discipline, that he might know when he ought to speak and when to be silent. Indeed, he who was silent in his passion now speaks through the Apostles and apostolic men in the whole world. And it belongs to great knowledge to give one's fellow servants their food in due season, and to consider the persons of the hearers. Hence the Apostle Paul too speaks to those who did not accept the faith of the Prophets with testimonies from their own authors: as some of your own poets have said: For we are also his offspring (Acts 17:28), meaning Aratus. And again, from the Comic poet: Evil conversations corrupt good morals (1 Cor 15:33); and the hexameter verse of Epimenides: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, idle bellies (Titus 1:12). If these do not keep the order and measure of the meter in translation, it must be known that in Greek they run on metrical feet. And he did this because he had received a tongue of discipline, that he might know when he ought to speak a word. To him an ear was added by grace, which he did not have by nature: so that we may understand that by no means are the ears of the body to be taken here, but those of the mind, of which the Lord also spoke in the Gospel: He who has ears to hear, let him hear (Luke 8:8). This discipline and instruction opened his ears, that he might pass on the knowledge of the Father even to us; he who did not gainsay him, but became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross (Phil 2). So that he set his body, or his back, to the blows; and scourges cut the breast that held God, and he did not turn his cheeks away from the slaps. That he endured this from the servant of the chief of the Priests is not in doubt, so that the crowds of the Jews too mocked him, vying with one another. Struck and spat upon, he did not blush, but was led as a lamb to the sacrifice; and as a sheep before its shearer he did not open his mouth. And that the Son, according to the mystery of the body he took up, heard from the Father what he should speak, we learn more fully in the Gospel, in which he himself says: And the Father who sent me has given me a commandment, what I should say and what I should speak. And again: As I hear, so I judge (John 8:28; 5:30).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.8":["Near is he who justifies me: who gainsays me? Let us stand together. Who is my adversary? Let him come near to me. Behold, the Lord God is my helper: who is he that will condemn me? Behold, they shall all be worn away like a garment, the moth shall eat them. LXX: He who is near justifies me: who is he that is judged with me, let him resist me together? And who is he that enters into judgment against me? Let him draw near to me. Behold, the Lord is my helper, who will afflict me? Behold, all of you shall grow old like a garment, and like a moth it shall eat you. If anyone, he says, thinks that I was justly given over to the cross and had committed some sin, let him resist me. Who is he that is judged with me, so that he may be overcome not by the power of my majesty but by reason? Now a man is judged with the Lord not by the authority of one who reigns but by comparison of virtues: as the Apostles also judge the twelve tribes of Israel, and the Ninevites and the queen of Sheba judge the people of the Jews. In this sense it is said to Jerusalem: Sodom is justified by you (Ezek 16:52).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.9":["Since, then, no one can be justified in the presence of the Lord, all shall grow old like a garment (Ps 101 [102]). And what grows old is near to perishing. And like a moth, he says, it shall eat them: namely the conscience of sins, and jealousy over the nations that have been saved. Of this it is said at the end of this volume: Their worm shall not die. And in Micah, against the perverse princes: I will take away their goods like a moth that eats them up, and walks upon the rule in the day of watching (Mic 7:4 LXX). And in Proverbs: An understanding heart is a moth of the bones (Prov 14:30 LXX). For moth in the present place Symmachus translated rust, Aquila worm.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.10":["Who among you fears the Lord, hearing the voice of his servant? He who has walked in darkness and has no light, let him hope in the name of the Lord, and lean upon his God. LXX: Who is there among you that fears the Lord, let him hearken to the voice of his child: those who walk in darkness have no light. Trust in the name of the Lord, and lean upon God. You scourged me, you spat upon my face: yet I call my persecutors to repentance. For I do not will the death of the sinful people, but only that it turn back and live (Ezek 33). And I say: who is there among you that fears the Lord and hears the voice of the servant, or child, that is, of his Son? Of these the one belongs to the flesh he assumed, the other to his nature. Nor let us think it a small thing to fear the Lord, according to what is said in Proverbs: The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Prov 9:10). For perfect love casts out fear, because fear has punishment; and he who fears is not perfect in love. But here fear is put for timidity, and εὐλαβείᾳ, of which we also read elsewhere: Blessed is he who fears all things (Prov 28:14), on account of εὐλάβειαν, that is, on account of timidity: those whom people commonly call God-fearing in the religion of God. For the Lord protects their path, and guards the way of the God-fearing. Of this fear it is written: The fear of the Lord surpasses all things. And elsewhere: There is no want for those who fear him (Sir 25:14; Ps 33 [34]:10). Indeed, it is only after many steps of virtue that one arrives at such fear of the Lord. For the divine word speaks in the person of a master and father, instructing a disciple and a son: If you call upon wisdom, and give your voice to understanding, and if you seek her like money, and search her out like treasures: then you shall understand the fear of the Lord, and find the knowledge of God (Prov 2:3). This is the fear that keeps the souls of the saints whole, chaste and pure, of which it is said: The fear of the Lord is holy, enduring for ever and ever. To it the divine oracles exhort us: Fear the Lord, and give him glory (Rev 14:7). Let him, then, who so fears the Lord hear the voice of his servant, or child, who walked in darkness, and there was no light for him; who had no form nor glory, but in the likeness of sinful flesh took the form of a servant, that he might overcome sin in the flesh. Of him it is said that he should hope in the name of the Lord and lean upon his God, so that whatever belongs to human frailty he may strengthen and sustain by the divine majesty. This is according to the Hebrew. But according to the Seventy it is said of sinners, who would not obey the voice of his Son, and walk in the darkness of error, and therefore have no light. For it could not be that wisdom should enter into a perverse soul. They neither knew nor understood, and therefore they walk in darkness. For they did not do the works and commandments of the Lord, nor sow righteousness for themselves, nor gather the vintage of the vine, so as to kindle for themselves the light of knowledge. For he who desires wisdom, let him keep the commandments, and the Lord will grant it to him. Hence it is also said to God: Your precepts are light. And elsewhere: The commandment of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes (Ps 18 [19]:9). And the ungodly are commanded to trust in the Lord, and, falling through their sins, to lean upon his help and be sustained by it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.50.11":["Behold, all of you who kindle a fire, girded with flames, walk in the light of your fire, and in the flames that you have kindled: by my hand this has been done to you; you shall sleep in sorrows. LXX: Behold, all of you kindle a fire and strengthen the flame; walk in the light of your fire, and in the flame that you have kindled; because of me these things have been done to you. You shall sleep in grief. Exhortation profits nothing, nor is there hope of salvation after crimes. All have turned aside, together they have become useless (Ps 13 [14]); all kindle a fire for themselves and strengthen the flame. For they have in themselves hay, wood, stubble, thorns and thistles and darnel, which are to be handed over to the eternal burning. Hence it is also written in Proverbs: In much wood the fire thrives (Prov 25:21). But if a little fire lays hold of great material, how much will a great flame lay waste? It is said also in the Epistle that is ascribed to the Hebrews, of the earth that, bringing forth thorns and thistles, is reprobate and near to a curse, and its end is burning (Heb 6:8). Therefore those who are girded and surrounded with flames, and have strengthened the burning for themselves, are called to salvation, and it is said to them: Walk in the light of your fire, and in the flames that you have kindled, that in punishments and torments they may learn the power of God and return to salvation. And it signifies the devastation of the Jewish people, by which they were handed over to the Romans, and they bear the yoke of captivity to this day. For by the hand of Christ all these things were done, and they shall sleep in grief and sorrows, because neither through benefits nor through torments would they receive the Son of God. Hence he speaks to them: Go into the eternal fire, which God has prepared for the devil and his angels (Matt 25:41). This is what the Apostle also says: For wrath has come upon you to the end (1 Thess 2:16). From this little chapter we learn that each one kindles a fire for himself according to the quality of his sin. And just as, of people staying in the same place and, if one may say so, in the same bed, some are healthy while others burn with the heat of fevers, feeling different torments from the diversity of their humors and phlegm: so the fire that is kindled by sinners has its fuel in sins and in iniquity, of which it is written: Iniquity shall burn like a fire, and shall be devoured by burning like dry grass (Isa 9:18).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Moreover, what we have set down according to the Seventy: A man who is estranged from me and sets his thoughts in his heart, and the torment (or punishment) of his iniquity before his face, agrees with that testimony which is written in Isaiah: Walk in the light of your fire, and in the flame which you have kindled; for each one kindles for himself the blazing of flames and prepares punishments for himself, while he is unwilling to correct his errors by repentance, but remains in errors that deserve the burning of flames.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"ISA.51.1-3":["Hear me, you who pursue what is just, and seek the Lord. Look to the rock from which you were hewn, and to the cavern of the pit from which you were cut out. Look to Abraham your father, and to Sarah who bore you: for I called him alone, and blessed him, and multiplied him. The Lord therefore will comfort Zion, and will comfort all her ruins, and will make her desert like delights, and her wilderness like the garden of the Lord. Joy and gladness shall be found in her, thanksgiving and the voice of praise. LXX: Hear, you who pursue what is just, and seek the Lord. Look to the strong rock which you hewed out, and to the hole of the pit which you dug. Look to Abraham your father, and to Sarah who bore you, for he was one, and I called him, and blessed him, and loved him, and multiplied him: and now I will comfort you, Zion. And I have comforted all her deserts like a paradise, and the things that are toward the west like the paradise of the Lord: they shall find exultation and gladness in her, confession and the voice of praise. While those who kindled a fire for themselves and made the flames most strong sleep in grief and everlasting sorrow, you who pursue righteousness and seek the Lord (and he means the choir of the Apostles and of those who believed through the Apostles), look to the rock, that is, to Abraham your father, from whom you were hewn, and to the cavern of the pit from which you were cut out, that is, to Sarah who bore you. And consider this: that when he was a hundred years old and one, and had a barren wife, I multiplied his sons like the stars of heaven, so that their multitude surpassed number. If, then, from one man so many thousands of peoples were born, what great thing is it for me to restore the ruins of Zion, and to change her deserts into a paradise of pleasure, and into the garden of the Lord which God planted in Eden toward the East; and that there should be found in her, in place of the various trees, joy and gladness, confession and the voice of praise? The Jews refer this to the times of Zerubbabel, because after the devastation of Babylon Zion was restored, the Temple built, and the ancient religion given back. But just as above (Isa 49:22, 23) it is said to Zion: They shall bring your sons in their arms, and carry your daughters upon their shoulders. And kings shall be your foster fathers, and queens your nurses; and: Behold, these shall come from afar, and those from the north and the sea, and these from the land of the south; when Zion shall say: The place is too narrow for me: make room for me that I may dwell: things which exclude the meanness of those times, and are not known to have been fulfilled in the earthly Jerusalem: so here too the Lord speaks to those who pursue what is just, according to what is said elsewhere: Seek peace, and pursue it (Ps 33 [34]:15); and again: Pursuing hospitality (Rom 12:13). And they seek the Lord, so that one may infer things present from things former: since far more difficult things have already been done, let them not doubt that these things which are promised will come to pass. Further, according to the Seventy it is said to the pursuers of righteousness that they should look to the most strong rock which they hewed out, and the hole of the pit which they dug, that is, to the Lord the Savior, of whom the Apostle speaks: And the rock was Christ (1 Cor 10:4). And elsewhere it is written: He set my feet upon a rock (Ps 39 [40]:3). And again: On a rock he exalted me. For they pierced his side with a lance, and from it came out blood and water; and they dug his hands and feet, as the Lord the Savior himself says: They dug my hands and my feet, they numbered all my bones (Ps 21 [22]:18). We can call the hewn rock the tomb of the Savior, in which he was laid, and, rising from the dead, he begot innumerable sons. And he was called Abraham, that is, father of many nations. Just as Sarah, at first barren, is understood as the Church, which by another name is called Zion, whom the Lord has comforted, and whose deserts he has made like a paradise. For more are the children of the desolate than of her who has a husband. And what is said, And the things that are toward the West I will make into the paradise of the Lord, or into the garden of God, signifies that on those sitting in darkness and the shadow of death a sudden light has arisen. For paradise, in whose place we rendered delights, the Hebrew has EDEN (עדן), of which it is written at the beginning of Genesis. And this is to be noted, that in Zion, which is compared to the paradise of God, there ought to be nothing else but joy and gladness, confession and the voice of praise, so that what the saints will do in heaven with the Angels of God, this they may practice on earth too in the unceasing praise of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.4":["Attend to me, my people, and my tribe, hear me: for a law shall go forth from me, and my judgment shall rest for a light of the peoples. Near is my righteous one, my savior has gone forth: and my arms shall judge the peoples. The islands shall wait for me, and shall wait upon my arm. LXX: Hear me, hear me, my people, and kings, attend to me: for a law shall go forth from me, and my judgment for a light of the nations. My righteousness draws near swiftly, and my salvation shall go forth like a light: and in my arm shall the nations hope. The islands shall wait for me, and in my arm they shall hope. Once in the Hebrew it is said, Attend; twice according to the Seventy, hear me, hear me: to teach us that we ought to hear both with the ears of the body and with the sense of the soul. And the multitude of the nations, which is the people of God, is called to listen carefully to what is said; of which Zechariah says: Many nations, he says, shall flee to the Lord, and shall be his people (Zech 2:11). Or, as some will have it, the remnant of believers from Israel is called the people; and the tribe, or nations, are those who believed from the multitude of the nations, as Moses says to the nations in the Song of Deuteronomy: Rejoice, you nations, with his people (Deut 32:43). For tribe, as we have translated, Theodotion translated race, Symmachus nation, the Seventy kings. For we are the tribe, and the nation, and the royal and priestly race of the Lord, such as Abraham also was, who was called a king, and the other saints, of whom it is written: Do not touch my anointed ones (Ps 104 [105]:15). What is it that they are bidden to hear? For a law, he says, shall go forth from me, and my judgment for a light of the peoples, or of the nations. Here is shown the spiritual law of the Gospel, which shall go forth from Zion, not that of Moses, which was given of old on Sinai; and my judgment shall go forth for a light of the nations, by which he established and decreed that all nations are to be saved.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.5":["And lest perhaps we should think that what he promised would come after a long time, he adds: near is my righteous one, or righteousness. For Christ has been made for us by the Father wisdom and redemption (1 Cor 1), holiness and righteousness, and all the names by which virtue is called. And it is beautifully said that righteousness will go forth, so that not one nation only but the whole world may be saved. And that the Son of God, who was sent by the Father, is called Savior, or salvation, which in Hebrew is said Jesus, Simeon declares, holding the child in his arms: Now you dismiss your servant, O Lord, for my eyes have seen your salvation, which you have prepared before the face of all peoples, a light for the revelation of the nations (Luke 2:29ff.). And as for what follows, And my arms shall judge the peoples, or according to the Seventy, and in my arm shall the nations hope: it signifies either that all are judged by his power, or that all the nations will believe in Christ, who is the arm and strength of God. It is said also in another place: Your arm is with might. Let your hand be strengthened, and your right hand exalted (Ps 88 [89]:14). And again: Sing to the Lord a new song: his right hand will save for him, and his holy arm (Ps 97 [98]:1, 2). For the right hand and arm of the Lord is he himself who saved for himself those who were formerly lost, so that none should perish of those whom the Father had given him (John 17). And that the islands are said to be either the souls of the Saints, which in the persecutions of this world have been made solid by a firm faith in God, or the multitude of the Churches from the nations, we have often explained. And just as by the arm of the Lord we can understand the Savior, so by his arms which judge the peoples we can understand all the saints, in whom God will judge the world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.6":["Lift up your eyes to the heavens, and look at the earth beneath: for the heavens shall melt away like smoke, and the earth shall be worn away like a garment, and its inhabitants shall perish like these. But my salvation shall be for ever, and my righteousness shall not fail. LXX: Lift up your eyes to heaven, and look upon the earth beneath, for heaven has been made firm like smoke. But the earth shall grow old like a garment, and its inhabitants shall die like these. But my salvation shall be for ever, and my righteousness shall not fail. This is what the Lord says in the Gospel: Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away (Matt 24:35). And David sings in the psalm: In the beginning, O Lord, you founded the earth, and the heavens are the works of your hands. They shall perish, but you remain; and they shall all grow old like a garment; and like a cloak you shall roll them up, and they shall be changed (Ps 101 [102]:26). From this it is shown that the perishing of the heavens signifies not destruction but a change for the better. Of these it is said: There shall be a new heaven and a new earth, which I will make to remain in my sight (Rev 21:2). For if it is written of the saints, We shall all indeed sleep, but we shall not all be changed (1 Cor 15:51), and in the titles of four psalms we read, For those who shall be changed: how much more is this to be believed of heaven and of the sun and the stars, when the moon shall receive the light of the sun, and the sun shall glow with sevenfold brightness? Hence the whole creation too groans and travails, awaiting the revelation of the sons of God, that it may be changed for the better (Rom 8). And not only the souls of men, which are immortal, but their bodies too are to be changed into a glorified substance. For this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal put on immortality (1 Cor 15:53). And since heaven and earth grow old, it follows that the things contained within the circle of heaven, and men, who are the inhabitants of the earth, also die like these: not unto their destruction, but unto the abolishing of their old baseness and the renewal of the glory to come; when the righteous shall shine like the sun (Matt 13:43), and, the past and old things departing, all things shall have been made new. Some take perishing and growing old for abolition and death, according to what we read in the catholic Epistle: The heavens that now are, and the earth, have been reserved for fire by the same word. And again: The elements, burning, shall be dissolved (2 Pet 3:7). This is indeed also the opinion of the philosophers of the world, that all the things we see will perish by fire. Hence the Apostle too: Passes away, he says, the fashion of this world, while we look not at the things that are seen but at the things that are not seen. For the things that are seen are temporal, but the things that are not seen are eternal (1 Cor 7:32). Something of the kind the apostle John also writes: The world passes away, and its desire (1 John 2:17). And in the Hebrew it is said, The heavens shall melt away like smoke; or, according to Aquila and Symmachus, they shall be ground to nothing, and be crumbled and vanish in the manner of salt; of whom the former said ἡλίσθησαν, the latter ἁλίσουσιν, which plainly draws its etymology from the crumbling and dissolving of salt. In place of this I wonder what the Seventy meant to say, heaven has been made firm like smoke: for if firmness is taken for strength, how is what is firm compared to smoke? Unless perhaps we can say this: that all the firmness and strength and might of the heavens is made equal to the emptiest wind and to smoke, which dissolves into the air, according to Ecclesiastes: Vanity of vanities, and all is vanity (Eccl 1:2). And this must be said: if heaven and earth shall perish and grow old, by what consequence should its inhabitants die and perish like these, when we know that souls are everlasting, and that bodies will rise again? From this it is plain that heaven and earth do not perish and are not reduced to nothing, but are changed for the better.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.7":["Hear me, you who know what is just, people: my law is in their heart. Do not fear the reproach of men, and do not be afraid of their blasphemies. For as a garment, so shall the worm eat them; and as wool, so shall the moth devour them. But my salvation shall be for ever, and my righteousness to generations of generations. LXX: Hear me, you who know judgment, my people, of whom my law is in their heart. Do not fear the reproach of men, and do not be overcome by their contempt. For like a garment they shall be consumed by time, and like wool they shall be eaten by the moth. But my righteousness shall be for ever, and my salvation to generations of generations. He who had said above, A law shall go forth from me, and my judgment for a light of the nations, now speaks to the same people, who know his judgment and have his law in their hearts, that they may do all things with judgment, and have the law which the Lord promises through Jeremiah, saying (Jer 31:31ff.): I will establish a new covenant, not according to the covenant that I made with their fathers; but I will establish a covenant, putting my laws in their minds, and upon their heart I will write them; and I will be their God, and they shall be my people: so that they may live by no means according to the letter but according to the spirit, restoring the natural law in their hearts, of which the Apostle also writes: For when the nations, which do not have the law, do by nature the things that are of the law, these, not having the law, are a law to themselves; who show the work of the law written in their hearts (Rom 2:14). Of this law it is signified in the psalm: The mouth of the righteous shall meditate wisdom, and his tongue shall speak judgment. The law of his God is in his heart, and his steps shall not be supplanted (Ps 36 [37]:30, 31). And because the prophetic spirit knew that many persecutions would come upon believers, he exhorts them to fortitude, nay rather to contempt of those who are going to persecute them. Which the Lord also said in the Gospel: Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul; but rather fear him who can destroy both soul and body in Gehenna (Matt 10:28). And in another place: Rejoice when they speak all evil against you, lying (ibid. 5:11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.8":["For as a garment is consumed by the worm, or by time, and as wool is devoured by the moth, so all the reproach of the persecutors will pass away with the persecutors. Hence the Apostles too gloried that they had been counted worthy to suffer insults for the Lord (Acts 5). And they gloried because the salvation and righteousness of the Lord, which had promised them victory and rewards, that is, the Lord and Savior, abides for ever, or in each generation, of the former people and of the later, who received his coming; of whom it is also said above: Near is my righteous one, my savior has gone forth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.9-11":["Arise, arise; put on strength, O arm of the Lord. Arise as in the days of old, in the generations of ages. Was it not you who struck the proud one, who wounded the dragon? Was it not you who dried up the sea, the water of the mighty deep, who made the depth of the sea a way, that the freed might pass over? And now those who have been redeemed by the Lord shall return and come into Zion praising; and everlasting gladness shall be upon their heads: they shall hold joy and gladness; sorrow and groaning shall flee. LXX: Rise up, rise up ÷ Jerusalem ⁚ and put on the strength of your arm. Rise up as in the beginning of the day, as an everlasting generation. Are you not she who cut down the breadth, who undid the dragon? Are you not she who made the sea a desert, the many waters of the deep; who made the depth of the sea a way of passage for those who had been freed and redeemed? For by the Lord they shall be led back, and shall come into Zion with gladness and everlasting exultation. For on their head praise and gladness shall take hold of them. Sorrow and grief and groaning shall flee. The name Jerusalem, which was added here by the Seventy, is neither found in the Hebrew nor did any of the three Translators put it; hence it is to be marked beforehand with an obelus, and so the following little chapter is to be joined with the sense of the preceding. The Lord had exhorted those who believe in him, saying: Do not fear the reproach of men, and do not be afraid of their blasphemies. And he had promised them, as their help, his salvation and everlasting righteousness, which is none other than Christ, the arm of the Lord, of whom he had said above: And in my arm shall the nations hope. Hence the people speak to the arm of the Lord, and pray for his coming, and implore the promised help, saying: Arise, arise: put on strength, O arm of the Lord. Arise as in the days of old, and exert all your strength, so that you who through many ages delivered your Saints from dangers may protect us too by your strength. For you are he who struck the proud one, who wounded the dragon, namely Pharaoh king of Egypt, who in Ezekiel too is called the great dragon (Ezek 29). You dried up the Red Sea, so that through the most mighty waters and the depths of the sea your people might find a way and escape the pursuing Egyptians. You, then, who did these things, now too lead back into Zion those redeemed and freed by your blood, into the heavenly Jerusalem, or into the Church which you have prepared for yourself by your blood: and when they are in it, they shall rejoice with everlasting gladness, and shall say: Lord, as with the shield of your good will you have crowned us (Ps 5:13). For they shall have joy and gladness, sorrow and groaning fleeing away. This is according to the Hebrew. But according to the Seventy, Jerusalem, that is, the sinful soul, is called to put on the strength of her arm, and to take up her former works, as she had been before she fell, when she dwelt in the day and in the light. For you are she, he says, who overcame the broad and spacious way that leads to death, and broke up the dragon, the crooked serpent, of which we also read in the Psalms: You crushed the heads of the dragons in the waters (Ps 73 [74]:13). Remember your former strength, that you trod down the sea of this world and made it a desert, and found a way in the midst of the waves. Hence the Apostle Peter too passed over the sea of this world to the Lord, and he who was walking by faith began to sink through unbelief, except that he was held up by the right hand of the Lord. As, then, the former victory was granted with the Lord's help, so also to those who return after repentance and hear, Shall he who falls not rise? says the Lord (Jer 8:4), and, Turn to me, you sons that turn, and I will heal your bruises (ibid. 3), he himself will stretch out his hand, and lead them back into Zion, the watchtower and summit of virtues, with joy and everlasting gladness, and will set praise and gladness upon their head. For the eyes of the wise man are in his head; and they shall have this crown and be protected by this diadem, so that they always rejoice and praise the Lord, because joys have taken the place of sorrow, grief and groaning.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.12-13":["I, I myself will comfort you. Who are you, that you should have feared a mortal man, and the son of man, who shall wither like grass? And you have forgotten God your maker, who stretched out the heavens and founded the earth; and you have been in dread continually all the day at the face of the fury of him who afflicted you and had made ready to destroy. Where now is the fury of the afflicter? He shall come quickly, walking, to open, and he will not kill unto utter destruction, nor shall his bread fail. But I am the Lord your God, who trouble the sea, and its waves swell: the Lord of hosts is my name. I have put my words in your mouth, and in the shadow of my hand I have protected you, that you may plant the heavens and found the earth, and say to Zion: You are my people. LXX: I am, I am he who comforts you. Know who you were, and that you feared a mortal man, and the son of man, who have withered like grass; and you have forgotten God your maker, who made heaven and founded the earth; and you feared always, all the days, the face of the fury of him who afflicted you, as he planned to take you away; and now where is the fury of him who afflicts you? For when you have been saved, he shall not stand nor remain ※ and he will not kill unto corruption, and his bread shall not fail ⁚ for I am your God who trouble the sea and make its waves resound. The Lord Sabaoth is my name. I will put my words in your mouth, and in the shadow of my hand I will protect you: by which I set up heaven and founded the earth; and Zion shall say, You are my people. The people of believers had prayed to the arm of the Lord, who himself is the power of God and the wisdom of God, to arise and give them help, so that, with him fighting for the saints, they might possess joy and gladness, sorrow and groaning being put to flight. So either the arm of the Lord or the Lord himself answered: I am, I am; and not at all through the Prophets, but I myself will comfort you: the God of mercies and the Father of all consolation. And I wonder how, when I said above, Do not fear the reproach of men, and do not be afraid of their blasphemies, you feared the rage of the persecutors, and did not know that they are mortal, who like grass suddenly wither and perish. And surely at the time when you feared them, you forgot the Lord your maker. For if the memory of me had always dwelt in your heart, you would never have feared men, who are to be dissolved into ashes. And whom did you forget? God, who hung the heavens by incredible power, and founded the earth as a stable mass. And you feared your adversaries not for a short while, which could in some measure have been granted you, but continually and all the day, so that, though you had the light of faith, the darkness of dread possessed you, and dread of him who thought you would perish. Where then now is the pride of the persecutors? Where is the worldly power with which they raged against you with rabid mouth?\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to the Seventy it is said to every believing soul that, though created in the image and likeness of God, she did not know her own dignity, but feared man and the son of man, who can kill only the body, and did not know that she is immortal, nor say with the Prophet: The Lord is my light and my savior, whom shall I fear? The Lord is the protector of my life, of whom shall I be afraid (Ps 26 [27]:1, 2)? And again: The Lord is my helper, I will not fear what man may do to me. The Lord is my helper, and I shall look down upon my enemies (Ps 117 [118]:6, 7). And what man is, is shown by another verse: In the Lord I will hope: I will not fear what flesh may do to me (Ps 55 [56]:5). Hence it is now said too: You feared a mortal man, and the son of man, who have withered like grass. For all flesh is grass, and all its glory like the flower of grass (Isa 40); and, terrified by the power of the judge, you forgot that he himself was your protector, who made heaven and earth and all the elements we see; or those heavens that bear the image of the heavenly one, and that earth which multiplies the Lord's seed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.14-16":["Symmachus, in the place where we said, He shall come quickly, walking, to open, and he will not kill unto utter destruction, translated thus: Quickly the underworld shall be opened, and he shall not die unto corruption, Christ being understood, who speaks in the fifteenth psalm: You will not leave my soul in the underworld, nor will you give your holy one to see corruption. In that place too where we, according to the Hebrew and Aquila, rendered, I have put my words in your mouth, and in the shadow of my hand I have protected you, that you may plant the heavens, and found the earth, and say to Zion: you are my people, he translated thus: I will put my words in your mouth, and in the shadow of my hand I will protect you, by which I planted heaven and founded the earth, and that I may say to Zion: you are my people.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.14":["My son will come quickly, walking and trampling your adversaries, to open to you the way of victory; or to unbar the underworld; who will not kill unto utter destruction, but wills to save those who turn. Then too his bread, which, as the Gospel proves, means doctrine, shall never fail, but shall always lie open to those who wish to eat.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And what is said above, And he will not kill unto corruption, and his bread shall not fail, was added from Theodotion's edition out of the Hebrew.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.15-16":["And at the same time he makes an apostrophe to the Son, whom he promises will come quickly, and whose bread he says is everlasting: that he himself, according to the dispensation of the flesh he assumed, is his Lord God, who makes the sea troubled and its waves swell, so that the pride of the persecutors is puffed up against his servants, and then, with the Lord's help, grows calm again. And he says that he has put his words in his mouth. For whatever the Son speaks are the words of the Father; and that he protected him in the shadow of his hand; and that he was protected for this reason, that he might plant new heavens and found a new earth, and say to Zion, that is, to the Church: You are my people. Therefore Zion is nothing other than the people of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.15":["He troubles the sea and makes its waves resound, he to whom it is also sung in the Psalms: You rule the strength of the sea, and the tossing of its waves you calm (Ps 88 [89]:10). In Jeremiah too it is written: Will you not fear me, says the Lord, and will you not tremble before my face, who set the sand as a boundary for the sea, an everlasting decree which it shall not pass (Jer 5:22)? The seas were troubled when the Lord's dragnet drew out a multitude of fish (Matt 13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.16":["And the Lord put his words in the mouth of the believer, and protected him in the shadow of his hand. He also says in the Gospel: When they hand you over, do not think how or what you are to speak; for what you are to speak will be given you in that hour. For it is not you who speak, but the spirit of your Father who speaks in you (Matt 10:19, 20). And in another place he says to the righteous man: Open wide your mouth, and I will fill it (Ps 80 [81]:12). And again: Open your mouth to the word of God: For the Lord will give the word to those who preach the good news with great power. Hence God speaks to the inner man of Jeremiah, after he touched his mouth: Behold, I have put my words in your mouth (Jer 1:9). He could sing with the Psalmist: He put a new song in my mouth, a hymn to our God (Ps 39 [40]:4). And who has wrought all these things but the Lord and Savior, who made heaven and earth, and says to Zion, You are my people? Which properly suits the Church gathered from the nations. And in Hosea he promises to believers, saying: I will call not my people, my people; and he shall say to me: You are my God (Hos 2:24).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.17":["Lift yourself up, lift yourself up, arise, Jerusalem, who have drunk from the hand of the Lord the cup of his wrath: you have drunk to the bottom of the cup of stupor, and have drained it to the dregs. There is none to sustain her, of all the sons whom she has borne; and there is none to take her by the hand, of all the sons whom she has brought up. Two things there are that have befallen you; who shall be grieved over you? devastation and destruction, and famine, and the sword: who shall comfort you? LXX: Rise up, rise up, lift yourself up, Jerusalem, who have drunk from the hand of the Lord the cup of his fury. For the cup of ruin, the κόνδυ of fury, you have drunk off and emptied: and there was none to comfort you of all your sons whom you bore; and there was none to take you by the hand, nor of all your sons whom you raised up. These two things are against you: who shall be grieved with you? ruin and destruction, famine and the sword: who shall comfort you? That Jerusalem and Zion are δυώνυμον I have often taught: of which Zion, which means watchtower, because it is set upon a mountain, is called the citadel; while the rest of the city is called Jerusalem, which was formerly called Jebus and Salem. Her the Prophet now exhorts to arise, she who had earlier fallen by denial, saying in the passion of the Lord: Crucify, crucify such a one: we have no king but Caesar (John 19:15); and to do penance, and to feel through the evils of captivity why she offended her Creator. Physicians are accustomed to give to a nauseated stomach a most bitter antidote, which took its name from its taste, so that it may vomit up the harmful humors and be able to pass on into the bowel the food cooked and digested, which the great quantity of phlegm did not allow to be digested. So Jerusalem too, who drank from the cup of the Lord's fury, and from his κόνδυ, which Symmachus translated bowl, and which, according to the book of Genesis, Joseph ordered to be hidden in the sack of his brother Benjamin (Gen 44), is bidden to arise from her drunkenness, because she drank it, and emptied it, and drained it to the dregs: which the three expressed by a single word, Ἐξεστράγγισας. This is the cup of which we read in the Psalms: A cup in the hand of the Lord, full of pure wine mixed. And he has poured from this into that; yet its dregs are not emptied out: all the sinners of the earth shall drink (Ps 74 [75]:9, 10). Of it God also speaks to Jeremiah: Take the cup of pure wine from my hand, and you shall make all the nations drink of it, to whom I shall send you. And they shall drink and vomit, and go mad before the face of the sword which I shall send among them (Jer 25:15, 16). And when he says that he gave it to drink to the other nations and to Jerusalem and the cities of Judea, he adds: Thus says the Lord Almighty, the God of Israel: Drink and be drunk, and vomit, and fall before the face of the sword which I shall send among you (Jer 25:27). And it is to be noted that this cup of the Lord's fury is his sword, which is sent into the midst of sinners. Here a question arises: how, after the drinking, the drunkenness and vomiting and the fall, it is said in Jeremiah that Jerusalem cannot arise, and now he speaks to her through Isaiah: Lift yourself up, lift yourself up, arise, Jerusalem. It is resolved thus: as long as someone drinks the cup, and is drunk, and raves, and vomits, and falls, he cannot arise; for he has not yet drained the cup of the Lord, nor come to the dregs, so as to have drunk it to the bottom. But now he speaks to Jerusalem in the past tense: who have drunk from the hand of the Lord the cup of his fury, and not, who are drinking.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","We can understand these things according to the anagogy also of the sinful soul, which, not wishing to drink the cup of the Lord's fury, says in the psalm: O Lord, do not rebuke me in your fury, nor chastise me in your wrath (Ps 6:1). But if she has drunk it, it is good for her to feel her punishments, and to hear the Lord saying: When the wrath of my fury has passed, I will heal again. And elsewhere: Shall he who falls not rise again, says the Lord (Jer 8:4)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.18":["And at the same time we must consider that from then on there was no prophet, no Apostle in Judea to comfort her, and take her by the hand, and lift her up as she lay. From this it is plain that these things are said of the time after the last captivity: otherwise, history relates that in Babylon and after Babylon she had Ezekiel and the other Prophets.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.19":["And as for what he says, Two things there are that have befallen you, or these two things are against you: who shall be grieved over you?, and in place of two he names four, devastation and destruction, famine and the sword: like this is what is sung in the Psalter: Once God has spoken, these two things I have heard: that power belongs to God, and to you, O Lord, mercy: for you will render to each according to his works (Ps 61 [62]:12, 13). For there too God speaks once, that he is almighty, and the Prophet hears two things, that his omnipotence prevails on both sides, so that he both grants mercy to the penitent and renders to those who persist in sin the punishments they deserve. In the same way in this other place two things befell Jerusalem, each of which has two parts. For destruction follows ruin, or devastation; and death follows famine and the sword.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.20":["Your sons have been cast out, they have slept at the head of all the ways, like an ensnared oryx: full of the indignation of the Lord, of the rebuke of your God. LXX: Your sons, in want and sleeping at the head of all the ways, like a half-cooked beet, full of the fury of the Lord, and failing from the Lord God. For half-cooked beet the other translators rendered an oryx caught and ensnared, which in Hebrew is called THO (תוא), a kind of beast born in the desert, which is placed among the clean animals in Leviticus and Deuteronomy. For this the Seventy supposed the Syriac word thoreth, which means beet. From the name let us pass to the sense. Your sons, like an oryx caught in the snares of the hunters, have slept at the crossroads and in the streets, and, laying their naked limbs upon the naked ground, have shown by their own miseries the indignation of the Lord and the rebuke of their God. Further, according to the Seventy: those who would not have spiritual riches in every word, and knowledge, and good works, but were poor who do not endure threatening, shall by no means dwell in houses that are built of virtues, but at the beginnings of the ways and at their outlets, touching all things and leaving all things. They are rightly said to sleep that sleep of which it is written: They have slept their sleep, and found nothing (Ps 75 [76]:6); with which sleep the king of Assyria lulled them. And they are compared to a half-cooked beet, which is a kind of vegetable most cheap and most fragile. Of such vegetables I think it is said in the Psalms: Do not be jealous of evildoers, nor envy those who work iniquity. For like grass they shall swiftly wither, and like green herbs they shall quickly fall (Ps 36 [37]:1, 2). For it is the food of the sick. He who is once and for all unbelieving is called a raw beet. But he who, content with simple faith, does works of righteousness without reasoning and the truth of doctrines, can be called a cooked beet. Further, he who wavers in the middle between vices and virtues, and approaches the service of God with a double heart, is most rightly called a half-cooked beet; to him too God speaks in the Apocalypse of John: Would that you were either hot or cold: but now, because you are lukewarm, I will vomit you out (Rev 3:15, 16). These, lukewarm and sleeping, are full of the fury of the Lord, and dissolved, or failing, through the Lord God: not that the Lord, who did not make death and does not delight in the destruction of the living, is the cause of their dissolution; but in that those who have sinned under the Law are judged through the Law, which works the wrath of God for those who are its transgressors.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.21-23":["But to come, according to the Seventy, to the spiritual understanding: it is said to the soul humbled by vices and drunk with passions, that she may know that she has the Lord as her judge, and that she will render account for all things. And if she has been turned to better things, the cup of ruin is to be taken from her hands, and the bowl of the Lord's fury, which held punishments, of which Ezekiel also says to Jerusalem: You shall drink the cup of your sister Samaria, deep and wide, that you may be drunk (Ezek 23:32), is to be handed over into the hands of those who had humbled her. There is no doubt that he signifies the adverse powers, which said to her soul: Bow down, that we may pass over. Here it is likewise to be noted that they did not bend her down, nor use force so that she who was formerly upright should be bowed to the ground, but left it to her own choice. And she of her own will laid down her neck, or her back, or her whole body, not inside but outside, for those who trampled her. Something of the kind we also read in the Gospel (Luke 13): that for eighteen years Satan had bent a woman down, whom the Lord raised up to her former stature, so that she could say: I have lifted up my eyes to the mountains, whence my help shall come (Ps 120 [121]:1); and: To you I have lifted up my eyes, who dwell in heaven (Ps 122 [123]:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.21":["Therefore hear this, poor little one, and drunk, but not with wine. Thus says your ruler, the Lord, and your God, who fought for his people: Behold, I have taken from your hand the cup of stupor, the bottom of the cup of my indignation: you shall not drink it again any more. And I will put it in the hand of those who humbled you, and said to your soul: Bow down, that we may pass over; and you made your body like the ground, and like a road for those passing over. LXX: Therefore hear, you who are humbled and drunk, but not with wine. Thus says the Lord God who judges his people: Behold, I have taken from your hand the cup of ruin, the κόνδυ of my fury: and you shall not drink it again any more: and I will give it into the hands of those who unjustly oppressed you and humbled you; who said to your soul, Bend down that we may pass over; and you made your middle parts level with the ground for those passing outside. O Jerusalem, to whom I said, Lift yourself up, lift yourself up, arise, and do penance, because you have drunk from the hand of the Lord the cup of his fury and drained it to the dregs; and your sons, who are scattered through the whole world, and led away captive, have lain in the streets and at the heads of the ways: know that you are poor and humbled, and drunk, not with wine, but with the fury of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.51.22-23":["Therefore, if you do penance, and, lifted up, arise, you will know that the cup of stupor and of ruin, or according to Symmachus and Theodotion, of tearing and of commotion, is to be taken from your hand; and that you will no more drink the condy, for which in this place too Symmachus translated bowl; but that it is to be handed over to your adversaries, who said to your soul, Bow down, that we may pass over: and when they said it, you bent down of your own will. And you made your body like the ground, or your back; or according to the Seventy, your middle parts and your necks for those passing outside. Let this be said according to the history: that Jerusalem, if she is willing to lift herself up and arise, shall by no means drink the cup of the Lord's fury, nor suffer any more what she endured before.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.1":["Arise, arise, put on your strength, Zion: put on the garments of your glory, Jerusalem, city of the Holy One: for the uncircumcised and the unclean shall no more pass through you. LXX: Rise up, rise up, ÷ Zion: ⁚ put on your strength, Zion, and put on your glory, Jerusalem, holy city. No more shall the uncircumcised and the unclean pass through you. Here again the name Zion, as above, was added by the Seventy, and therefore it has been transfixed with the spit. To her, then, to whom he had said above (Isa 51:17), Lift yourself up, lift yourself up, arise, Jerusalem, he now says: Arise, arise, put on your strength, Zion. And who Zion is, the following verse shows: Put on the garments of your glory, Jerusalem. We have said this to prove that Jerusalem and Zion are one city. And she is commanded to put off her garments of mourning and to put on those which she had before she drank from the hand of the Lord the cup of fury. And she is called the city of the sanctuary, for this is what CODES (קדש) signifies, on account of the temple which was built in her; or of the Holy One, on account of the knowledge of God; or holy, because she alone in the whole world had received the law. Hence too, after the resurrection of the Savior, the bodies of the dead appeared in the holy city, which surely, on account of its blasphemy and the hands laid on the Lord, could not be holy (Matt 27). And what is promised, that if she rises after her ruin and puts on her strength and glory, the uncircumcised and unclean shall no more pass through her, signifies what the Apostle Paul also says: What participation has righteousness with iniquity? What fellowship has light with darkness? What agreement has Christ with Belial? What part has the believer with the unbeliever? What accord has the temple of God with idols (2 Cor 6:14, 15)? All these things too, as we began to explain, are referred to the state of the soul. If through penance she has recovered her former strength, let her be called the dwelling of the Holy One, and become a Temple of God, and let no uncircumcised and unclean λογισμὸς pass through her. Of these it has been said: From my hidden sins cleanse me, O Lord, and from those of others spare your servant. If they have not had dominion over me, then I shall be spotless, and shall be cleansed from the greatest offense (Ps 18 [19]:13, 14). But all the things that are promised to Zion and Jerusalem are not said, as the Jews dream, of her stones and ashes and embers, that she may be restored to her former state; but of the people of Jerusalem, which killed the Prophets and stoned those who were sent to it (Matt 23), and at the last laid hands even on the Son of God. That people, falling in the passion of Christ, was raised up in his resurrection, when many thousands of the Jews believed and the remnant was saved. The same we can say of the Church also, which is the vision of peace and the watchtower: if she has fallen into heresy, she is commanded to go out and take up the ornaments of her former faith. And if she has been raised up, the rewards of holiness and continence are promised to her, so that the uncircumcised and the unclean shall by no means pass through her. This can be referred not to the cutting of the flesh and the foreskin, but to the uncleanness or cleanness of works, so that we call uncircumcised and unclean those who serve the pleasure and lust of the body. Indeed, Jeremiah speaks to those circumcised in the flesh, not in the spirit: All the nations are uncircumcised in the flesh; but the sons of Israel are uncircumcised in their hearts (Jer 9:26). And the blessed Apostle, disputing of virginity and continence and marriage, threw in, in the midst: Was anyone called circumcised? Let him not draw on a foreskin. Was he called in foreskin? Let him not be circumcised (1 Cor 7:18). This seems to me to say in other words: Has he been called without a wife, and believed? let him not take a wife. Or on the contrary: having a wife, has he believed in Christ? let him by no means make a divorce. And he keeps this sense not only in circumcision and foreskin, but in freedom and slavery, so as to call virgins and the continent free, and slaves those who render the debt to a wife. For it is not in our power to draw on a foreskin after circumcision, as those in the book of Maccabees are said to have made foreskins for themselves: which is said of the sons being born, and not of the fathers. Or he who has been called free is rather the slave of Christ (ibid. 7:22), since in the baptism of Christ there is no difference of Jew, pagan, Greek and Barbarian, man and woman, free and slave.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.2-3":["Shake yourself from the dust: arise, sit down, Jerusalem: loose the bonds of your neck, captive daughter of Zion. For thus says the Lord: You were sold for nothing, and you shall be redeemed without silver. LXX: Shake off the dust, and rise up, Jerusalem. Loose the bond of your neck, captive daughter of Zion. For thus says the Lord: You were sold for nothing, and you shall be redeemed without money. That the Prophet's word is by no means addressed to Jerusalem, that is, to the ruins of her stones, and her ashes and embers, but to the people that dwells in her, and that it is called daughter on account of the effeminacy of its spirit, the following verse shows, in which he says: Loose the bonds of your neck, captive daughter of Zion. For truly captive is the people of the Jews, which to this day bears the yoke of Nebuchadnezzar, and is bound fast by the cords of its sins and blasphemies; which was sold for nothing, and did nothing worthy for which it could be redeemed. To it also it is said above: Behold, for your sins you were sold, and for your iniquities I put away your mother. And he gives the reasons why they were sold, why they were cast off: Because, he says, I came, and there was no man; I called, and there was none to hear. From this it is plain that they were handed over to error and to demons because they had not heard him crying: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened (Matt 11:28). But those who are willing to believe shall be redeemed, by no means with silver and money, but with the precious blood of Christ, so that they hear through the Apostles: Grace to you and peace (Rom 1:7). For not because of merits, but because of grace and faith in Christ, we have been reconciled to God. It is said also to the soul which, polluted by the filth of vices, had lost the whiteness of her former way of life, that she should shake off, with the Apostles, the dust that has clung to her feet (Matt 10). For it could not be that she who, prostrate, had laid down her neck for those passing outside, and had joined her middle parts to the earth, and had said, My soul is humbled in the dust, my belly cleaves to the earth (Ps 43 [44]:25), should not have taken on the image of the earthly. From this the Apostle calls us back, saying: As we have borne the image of the earthly, so let us bear also the image of the heavenly (1 Cor 15:49). Hence those who are in the flesh cannot please God (Rom 8). Not that the nature of the flesh is condemned, whose maker is God, and in which very many have pleased God and reign with Christ; but that the works of the flesh are rejected, of which the same Apostle speaks: But I am carnal, sold under sin (Rom 7:14). Indeed, to men of this kind he says: But where there is jealousy and rivalry among you, are you not carnal, and do you not walk according to man (1 Cor 3:3)? And on the contrary to the saints: But you are not in the flesh, since the Spirit of God dwells in you. The dust, then, is shaken off, of which it is written, Shall the dust confess to you, or declare your truth (Ps 29 [30]:10), so that the bonds of our neck may be loosed, and we may by no means hear, your neck is an iron sinew, but may deserve to hear with the bride: How beautiful are your cheeks, like the turtledove's: your neck like necklaces (Song 1:9). And again: I have put bracelets on your hands, and a necklace on your neck; so that, freed from the heavy burden and receiving back our former ornaments, we may cease to be captives: redeemed by him who came to proclaim release to the captives, and of whom it is written (above, Isa 45:13): He shall build my city, and bring back the captivity of my people, not for a price nor for gifts. With this sense the Apostle Peter also agrees: Knowing that you were redeemed from your vain way of life handed down from your fathers, not with corruptible things, silver and gold, but with the blood of the spotless lamb (1 Pet 1:18).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.4-5":["According to the Seventy, God says to Israel that it went down into Egypt of its own will, as Moses says in Deuteronomy: With seventy souls your fathers went down into Egypt (Deut 10:22); and they were led away captive violently by the Assyrians. Hence it is said to them: And now what are you here? What are you doing in the land of Judea, you who after the murder of the Prophets laid hands on the Son of God? Or else God speaks to the angelic powers and the Angels who presided over the Temple: What are you doing here, why do you not leave the blaspheming people? As Josephus also relates (Antiquities of the Jews, Book 6, ch. 12): when the gates of the Temple were suddenly opened and unbarred of their own accord, gates which many men could scarcely shut, a voice came forth from the inner sanctuary of the Temple, of those saying: Let us pass over from these seats. Here we must consider the exactness of the words: that they did not say, Let us depart, but, Let us pass over, namely to the people of the nations. Hence too the veil of the Temple was torn in two from top to bottom, so that all the ceremonies of the Jews were laid open, and at that time was fulfilled what is said in this same Prophet: Out of Zion shall go forth the law, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem. And he shall judge among many nations, even far off (Isa 2:3, 4). For their sound has gone out into all the earth, and their words to the ends of the world (Ps 18 [19]). And in every place incense is offered to God, and a pure sacrifice (Mal 1), since the prophetic word was fulfilled: All the ends of the earth shall remember and return to the Lord: and all the families of the nations shall worship in his sight; for the kingdom is the Lord's, and he shall rule over the nations (Ps 21 [22]:28ff.). Hence it is said to the princes of the Jews: Wonder, and howl, since you are the cause of the people's ruin. Further, according to the anagogy we can say this: that the people of God goes down of its own will into the Egypt of this world when it is a lover of pleasures rather than of God, and does not hear that prophetic word: Woe to those who go down into Egypt for help (Isa 31:1). When it has been beguiled by vices, and has dwelt in a place of waters and rivers, which does not have the dryness of chastity, then it shall be handed over violently to the Assyrians, so that they may rule over it, who afterwards may accuse and convict it in its sin. For they are both enemies and avengers, of whom he speaks to Israel: And now what have you to do with the way of Egypt, to drink the water of Gihon; and what have you to do with the way of the Assyrians, to drink the water of the rivers (Jer 2:18)? He, then, who has gone down into Egypt, and has slipped from the height of Jerusalem to lower places, and going to Jericho, another Egypt, has received many wounds from robbers, to him it is said: And now what have you here? Why do you pretend to be in the land and in the Church of God, you who with eagerness and all desire went down into Egypt, and are possessed by the Assyrians, and have been taken captive; and you ought rather to howl and mourn, because on account of your vices and sins the name of God is blasphemed among the nations? To such it is said in Ezekiel: You have defiled my name among the nations (Ezek 36:20). And as the Lord says to his disciples in the Gospel, Let your light shine before men, that they may see your good works and glorify your Father who is in heaven (Matt 5:16), so on the contrary, when we have done evil works, the name of God is blasphemed among the nations because of us.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.4":["For thus says the Lord God: My people went down into Egypt at the beginning, to sojourn there; and Assyria oppressed him without any cause. And now what have I here, says the Lord? For my people has been taken away for nothing: its rulers act unjustly, says the Lord, and continually all the day my name is blasphemed. Therefore my people shall know my name in that day: for I myself who spoke, behold, I am here. LXX: For thus says the Lord: My people went down into Egypt formerly, to sojourn there; and they were violently led away to the Assyrians; and now what shall be here, says the Lord? Because my people has been taken away for nothing, wonder, and howl. Thus says the Lord: Because of you my name is always blasphemed among the nations. Therefore my people shall know my name in that day: for I myself who spoke am here. He rebukes the people of the Jews and foretells what is to come: that the people which of its own will went down in Jacob to the Egyptians, and in a time of need and famine sojourned in the land of Goshen (Gen 47), afterwards suffered oppression from the Assyrians, whom it had harmed in nothing, and was carried away in captivity into Babylon (2 Kgs 17:23).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.5":["Hence he adds: And now what have I here, says the Lord? And the sense is: I have nothing left for which I should remain in this region, from which my people has been taken away for nothing, and sold for its sins, and caught like an oryx in a net, either by the forces of the Romans or by the snares of the devil, in which it is held bound to this day. And that they should suffer these things, their rulers and teachers acted unjustly; who, according to Symmachus and Theodotion, shall howl, according to Aquila, shall weep, when they have been handed over to torments. For they are the ones who stirred up the people against the Savior, so that they cried with one voice: Crucify, crucify such a one (John 19:15). Of them he had already said before: The Lord himself will come with the elders of the people and with its princes. But why have you burned my vineyard, and the plunder of the poor is in your houses (Isa 3:14)? Wherefore according to the Seventy he says to them: Because of you my name is always blasphemed among the nations. And it must be known that among the nations is not found in the Hebrew, but simply, my name is continually blasphemed, so that \"in your synagogues\" is understood: who day and night blaspheme the Savior, and, under the name of Nazarenes, as I have often said, heap curses on the Christians three times a day.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.6":["While they, then, blaspheme and curse the Lord, his people, of whom much has already been said, that is, the Christian people, shall know the name of him who is to come in the name of the Father. And it shall know it because he himself who formerly spoke through the Prophets will instruct the peoples in person.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Therefore he who has the knowledge of the name of God, and is not ignorant that he was created in his image and likeness, will dwell in light. And he will be in the day which Abraham rejoiced to have seen (John 8), of which holy David also speaks: This is the day which the Lord has made, let us exult and rejoice in it (Ps 117 [118]:24). For those who have lit for themselves the light of knowledge, and have the everlasting light which has been promised to believers, The Lord shall be to you an everlasting light (Isa 60:20), walk honestly in the day, and are sons of light and of the day; and they know him who says to Moses, Go, say to the sons of Israel: He who is has sent me (Exod 3:14); and him whom they had known before in the saints they will know to be present to themselves too.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.7":["How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him who announces and proclaims peace, and announces good, who proclaims salvation, and says to Zion: There shall reign your God. The voice of your watchmen: they have lifted up their voice; together they shall praise, for they shall see eye to eye, when the Lord has turned Zion back. LXX: As an hour upon the mountains, so are the feet of him who brings good news of the hearing of peace, who brings good news of good things: for I will make my salvation heard, saying: Zion, your God shall reign. The voice of those who guard you has been lifted up, and with the voice together they shall rejoice, for eyes shall see to eyes, when the Lord has had mercy on Zion. Consequently, of him who had said above, I myself who spoke, behold, I am here, the Prophet now testifies that he himself preached the Gospel upon the mountains, that is, upon the Apostles, of whom it is written: Draw near to the everlasting mountains (Mic 2:9 LXX); and whose doctrine is the illumination of God. Hence it is said to him in the Psalms: You give light wonderfully from the everlasting mountains (Ps 75 [76]:5). He announced and proclaimed peace to those who were far off, that is, to the nations, and to those who were near, that is, to the Jews, reconciling the world to God; of him it is sung in the psalm under the name of Solomon: In his days righteousness shall arise, and abundance of peace, until the moon be taken away (Ps 71 [72]:7). And in this same Prophet, of the child who is born and of the son who is given to us, whose government is upon his shoulder, and who shall be called the Angel of great counsel, it is said after the rest (above, Isa 9:7): And of his peace there shall be no end. For he is our peace, who has pacified all things through the blood of his cross, in heaven and on earth; who said to the Apostles: My peace I give to you, my peace I leave with you (John 14:27). And he announced good things to us, not those which the Philosophers call indifferent, but those which are truly good, which the Father gives to those who ask him, that is, all the graces of the Holy Spirit. Indeed, another Evangelist writes in the same place: How much more will your Father who is in heaven give the Holy Spirit to those who ask him (Luke 11:13)? These are the good things which the Lord promises to believers: Hear me, and you shall eat good things, and your soul shall delight in good things; and he announced to all not only good things but salvation, which he himself gave, who says to Zion, that is, to the Church: There shall reign your God. Hence the Apostle too says to the Saints: Let not sin reign in your mortal body, so that you obey its desires (Rom 6:12). And writing of sinners: Death, he says, reigned from Adam to Moses (Rom 5:14).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","What the Seventy translated, as ὥρα, that is, an hour upon the mountains: so the feet of him who brings good news of the hearing of peace, and the rest, Paul, following the sense of the Hebrew Truth, sets down in the Epistle to the Romans: How beautiful are the feet of those who bring good news of good things, who bring good news of peace (Rom 10:15): wishing the Apostles to be understood, whose feet the Lord washed (John 13), that they might be clean and beautiful for preaching, and might run through the whole world, and in a short time fill the world with the doctrine of Christ. Now ὥρα, that is, hour, according to the Seventy and the ambiguity of the Greek word, signifies either time or beauty. If time, this will fit it: In an opportune time I have heard you, and in the day of salvation I have been your helper (Isa 49:8). Hence the Apostle adds: Behold, now is the acceptable time; behold, now is the day of salvation (2 Cor 6:2). For at the opportune time he shed his blood for all, when all had turned aside, together they had become useless; there was none who did good, there was not even one (Ps 13 [14]): that he might taste death for all, because all had sinned and were in need of the glory of God (Rom 3). But if we refer beauty to what is said in the psalm, Beautiful in form above the sons of men (Ps 44 [45]:3) (for what is more beautiful than that the form of a servant should become the form of God, and sit and reign with Christ in the heavenly places?), ὤρα can, according to the breadth of the Greek language, also be called care and solicitude, according to what the Saint says: You will multiply me in my soul, in your strength (Ps 137 [138]:4). For you will multiply me, in the Greek it says πολυωρήσεις με, that is, you will hold me worthy of much care and solicitude. And elsewhere: According to your height ἐπολυώρησας the sons of men (Ps 11 [12]:9): which in other words is said in Greek, πολλῆς ὤρας, τοῦτ' ἔστι, φροντίδος ἠξίωσας. But these things are superfluous; and the feet of Christ or of the Apostles are rather to be taken as beautiful, as all except the Seventy translated alike, Paul approving their interpretation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.8":["And what follows, The voice of your watchmen, or of your guards, signifies the Apostles, of whom God speaks to the Church in another place too (below, Isa 62:6): Upon your walls I have set guards, who shall never be silent, remembering the Lord, who will lift up their voice, discoursing of lofty things. Hence it is said to them (above, Isa 40:9): Go up upon a high mountain, you who bring good news to Zion. Lift up your voice with strength, you who bring good news to Jerusalem. These will praise God with one voice, and see with eyes to eyes; which the Apostle says in other words, Face to face (1 Cor 13); when the Saint sings, My eyes are ever toward the Lord (Ps 24 [25]:15), and, To you I have lifted up my eyes, who dwell in heaven (Ps 122 [123]:1). And the Lord will answer him: For the eyes of the Lord are upon the righteous, and his ears toward their prayer (Ps 33 [34]:16).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.9":["Rejoice and praise together, deserts of Jerusalem, for the Lord has comforted his people: he has redeemed Jerusalem. The Lord has prepared his holy arm in the eyes of all the nations: and all the ends of the earth shall see the salvation of our God. LXX: Let the deserts of Jerusalem break forth into gladness together, for the Lord has had mercy on her, and has rescued Jerusalem. The Lord will reveal his holy arm in the sight of all the nations. And all the ends of the earth shall see the salvation of our God. When the people of the Jews had been led into captivity and the city burned, there was in Jerusalem either a rare inhabitant or none. But after he who formerly spoke in the Prophets, and was in the beginning with God, God the Word, dwelt among us and was made flesh, the deserts of Jerusalem were restored; and he came of whom it is written: He shall build my city, and bring back the captivity of my people: so that she is by no means lamented by Jeremiah, How does the city sit alone that was full of peoples: she has become like a widow, she who was multiplied among the nations (Lam 1:1), but hears David singing: When the Lord turned back the captivity of Zion, we became like those comforted (Ps 125 [126]:1, 4). And a little after: We became glad.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.10":["And so that we may know that these things are by no means said of the people of the Jews, but of all who were to believe in the Lord through the apostles, he sets it down and says: he who comforted her, or had mercy on her, and who rescued or redeemed her, he himself prepared or revealed his holy arm in the sight of all the nations: and all the ends of the earth shall see the salvation of our God. From this it is plain that, once the spiritual Jerusalem, that is, the Church, which had been abandoned by the Jews, was built up through the Apostles, the arm of the Lord is revealed to all the nations, and all the ends of the earth see his salvation. This is understood in two ways. For either the Father reveals his arm to all the nations, or the Son reveals his strength, of which it is written: For power went out from him and healed all (Luke 6:19). And again: I perceived that power went out from me, which healed the woman with the issue of blood (Mark 5:30). And that the Son of God is called the right hand and arm of the Father, there are many testimonies, of which we shall mention a few: Will save for him his right hand, and his holy arm (Ps 97 [98]:2). And elsewhere: In my arm shall the nations hope (Isa 51:5). Of him Jacob says: He shall be the expectation of the nations (Gen 49:10). And Psalm 88 (89): Your arm is with might. With this arm the Lord led the people of Israel out of the land of Egypt. Of this he spoke to the prince of the Apostles: Blessed are you, Simon Bar-Jona, for flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, but my Father who is in heaven (Matt 16:17). And the Apostle Paul of himself (Gal 1:15): When, he says, it pleased God, who separated me from my mother's womb, to reveal his Son in me. And that he joined all the ends of the earth to all the nations who are to see the salvation of God shows what is also said in another place: Turn to me from the ends of the earth, and you shall be saved (Isa 45:22); and through Jeremiah: To you the nations shall come from the ends of the earth (Jer 16:19); and again: All the families of the nations shall remember and turn to the Lord; for the kingdom is the Lord's, and he shall rule over the nations (Ps 21 [22]:28ff.): so that not only the variety of the individual nations but all the hinges of the world too are foretold as about to believe in Christ; according to what he himself says: But when this Gospel has been preached in the whole world, then shall the end come (Matt 24:14). Others will have the heights and extremities of the earth to be understood as those who, standing not in the middle of the earth but at its extremities, like the rims of wheels, forsake lowly things and hasten to the heights.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.11-12":["Depart, depart, go out from there, do not touch what is polluted: go out from the midst of her, be cleansed, you who carry the vessels of the Lord. For you shall not go out in tumult, nor shall you hasten in flight. For the Lord will go before you, and the God of Israel will gather you. LXX: Depart, depart, go forth from there, and do not touch what is unclean: go out from the midst of her: be separated, you who carry the vessels of the Lord, for you shall not go out with tumult, nor go with flight: but the Lord will go before you, and the God of Israel who gathers you. This too the Jews try to expound thus: Go out from Babylon, and abandon their idols. Go out from the midst of her, and bring back into the temple the vessels which Nebuchadnezzar had carried off when Jerusalem was taken (2 Kgs 15), now that Cyrus releases the captives under Zerubbabel and Ezra (Ezra 1); and do not go out of Babylon as you fled out of Egypt before, with tumult and fear, but with peace and with the goodwill of the king of the Persians and Medes, in whom the will of the Lord appeared, who protected and gathered you. Others interpret what we have said of Babylon as said of the Roman kingdom: that all these things are to be fulfilled at the coming of Christ, who is to set them free.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.11":["But we, having heard above, How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him who announces and proclaims peace, and, The Lord will reveal his arm in the sight of all the nations, and, all the ends of the earth shall see the salvation of our God, understand this by no means of the Jews, but of the choir of the Apostles and of all the Saints. They are commanded to depart from Jerusalem and to preach the Gospel in the whole world, as the Lord the Savior says: Go and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit (Matt 28:19): so that they may by no means stay with the blaspheming Jews, for whose slaughter the Roman army was being made ready, but may leave the polluted, and that those who carry the vessels of the Lord may be separated from them and be cleansed. For they are the Temple of the Holy Spirit (1 Cor 3), and the vessels of gold and silver of a great house. Such was the Apostle Paul, who is called a vessel of election (Acts 9); for he had prepared himself to be a precious vessel, fit for the ministry of God. Or surely this must be said: that the vessels of the Lord are the spiritual armor of God, of which the Apostle Paul also spoke: Put on the armor of God (Eph 6:11); and he lists it piece by piece: the breastplate of righteousness, and the shield of faith, and the helmet of salvation, and the sword of the spirit, which means the word of God. To these he adds the belt of truth, and the feet shod in the preparation of the Gospel of peace. And in another place: Laying aside, then, the works of darkness, let us put on the arms of light (Rom 13:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.12":["There follows: You shall not go out in tumult, nor shall you hasten in flight. For they departed from Jerusalem not as the vanquished but as victors: so that they who, preaching the Gospel of God daily in the Temple, had brought many thousands of the Jews under the faith of Christ, might subdue the world too to his Gospel. For they had the Lord going before them, to gather them, the God of Israel, that is, to make one flock out of the whole world, so that what the Lord says to the Father in the Gospel might be fulfilled (John 17:21): Grant that, as I and you are one, so these too may be one in us: so that, with the same mind and the same judgment, forsaking the vices that fight one another and are contrary, they might lay hold of the one and only virtue. For vices and passions do not follow one another, as is said of the virtues, in which there are neither ὑπερβολαὶ nor ἐλλείψεις, that is, neither more nor less, but all things are tempered. In the vices, by contrast, all things are contrary: as panic to boldness, superstition to impiety, luxury to thrift.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.13-14":["Behold, my servant shall understand; he shall be exalted, and lifted up, and shall be very high. As many were astonished over you, so shall his appearance be without glory among men, and his form among the sons of men. He shall sprinkle many nations: over him kings shall shut their mouth: for those to whom it had not been told of him have seen; and those who had not heard have beheld. LXX: Behold, my child shall understand, and shall be exalted, and shall be greatly glorified. As many shall be astonished over you, so shall your appearance be in glory from men, and your glory from the sons of men: so shall many nations marvel over him, and kings shall shut their mouth, for those to whom it has not been announced of him shall see, and those who have not heard shall understand. So that no ambiguity may be left to readers as to who it is that said, I who spoke, behold, I am here, and what the holy arm of the Lord is, which has been revealed to all the nations, God the almighty Father teaches plainly: Behold, my servant shall understand, or my child, of the difference between which we have spoken above. And he shall understand not as the Word of God and wisdom, but as servant and child. He, though he was in the form of God, deigned to take the form of a servant, becoming obedient to the Father unto death, even the death of the cross (Phil 2). Wherefore he exalted him, and gave him the name which is above every name. He speaks in the psalm: I will bless the Lord, who has given me understanding (Ps 15 [16]:7); and of him David sings: Who made the heavens in understanding (Ps 135 [136]:5). For he himself is wisdom and understanding, he who as a child advanced in age and wisdom; of whom Peter too speaks: The God of our fathers has glorified his Son Jesus, whom you indeed handed over, and denied before the face of Pilate, when he wished to release him. But you denied the holy and righteous one (Acts 3:13, 14); of whom too we showed that it was written above: I am witness, says the Lord, and the child whom I have chosen: over him very many shall be astonished (Isa 43:10), when they have seen his signs. And hence the wonder will be greater, that his appearance will be without glory among men: not that it signifies ugliness of form, but that he came in humility and poverty. He, though he was rich, became poor for us; and he said to believers: Learn from me, for I am meek and humble of heart (Matt 11:29); of whom Clement too, the apostolic man, who governed the Roman Church after Peter, writes to the Corinthians: The scepter of God, the Lord Jesus Christ, did not come in the boasting of pride, though he could do all things, but in humility. So much so that, when struck by the servant of the priest, he answered: If I have spoken evil, convict me of the sin; but if well, why do you strike me (John 18)? though he had twelve thousand Angels who would obey his nod.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.52.15":["He shall sprinkle many nations, cleansing them with his blood and consecrating them in baptism to the service of God. Over him kings shall shut their mouth, and the princes of the world, all of whose wisdom has been overthrown by the preaching of the cross; and those who had not had the Law and the Prophets, and to whom nothing had been announced of him, they themselves shall see and understand. Of them the Savior also speaks: Blessed are those who have not seen and have believed (John 20:29). In comparison with them the hardness of the Jews is reproved, who, seeing and hearing, fulfilled in themselves the prophecy of Isaiah, who says: Hearing you shall hear, and shall not understand: and seeing you shall look, and shall not see. For the heart of this people has been made fat, and with their ears they have heard heavily (Isa 6:9, 10).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.1":["Who has believed our report? and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed? And he shall go up like a sapling before him, and like a root out of a thirsty land: there is no form in him, nor comeliness: and we saw him, and there was no appearance, and we desired him. Despised and the lowest of men, a man of sorrows, and knowing infirmity; and his face was as it were hidden, and despised: whence we did not esteem him. Truly he bore our sicknesses, and our sorrows he carried: and we thought him as it were a leper, and struck by God, and humbled. LXX: Lord, who has believed our report, and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed? We announced as a little child before him, as a root in a thirsty land. There is no form in him nor glory, and we saw him, and he had no form nor comeliness: but his form was without honor, and failing beyond the sons of men. A man in a stroke, and knowing how to bear infirmity, for his face is turned away, despised and not esteemed. He carries our sins, and suffers pain for us, and we esteemed him to be in pain, and in stroke, and in affliction. After the words of the Father, by which he had announced to the world that his Son would come, and before the scandal of the cross, of which he was going to say, His appearance shall be without glory, and his form beyond the sons of men, he had put first the glory of the resurrection, He shall be exalted and lifted up, and shall be very high, so that the glory of the resurrection might go before the humility of the cross: now the choir of the Prophets answers that it has fulfilled its office and, as far as lay in it, has announced to all his arm and power. And in saying, Who has believed our report, and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed?, it signifies how rare believers from among the Jews are.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","At the beginning of the chapter, in which according to the Seventy it is said, Lord, who has believed our report, and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed (Rom 10), a testimony which the apostle Paul also uses to the Romans, expounding it of the Lord's passion, Lord is not found in the Hebrew, but was added to make clear the person to whom it is said.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.2":["And as for what follows, He shall go up like a sapling before him, for which the Seventy translated, We announced as a little child before him: for sapling Symmachus translated branch, to show the man assumed, who came forth from the virginal womb. Of him he adds: Like a root out of a thirsty land. For thirsty Aquila translated pathless, to show the privilege of virginity: that he was created without any human seed from an earth previously untrodden. This is he of whom we read above too: A rod shall come forth from the root of Jesse, and a flower shall go up from his root (Isa 11:1), so as to signify his birth and his going up in the world. But if he had no form nor glory, but his form was ignoble and failing beyond the sons of men, or, as it stands in the Hebrew, despised and the lowest of men, how is it said in the Psalms: Gird your sword upon your thigh, O most mighty, in your beauty and comeliness (Ps 44 [45]:4)? This is easily resolved. He was despised and ignoble when he hung on the cross and, made a curse for us, bore our sins, and said to the Father: God, my God, why have you forsaken me (Matt 27:46)? But he was glorious and comely in appearance when at his passion the earth trembled, the rocks were split, and, as the sun fled, the elements feared an eternal night. Of him the bride too says in the Song of Songs: My kinsman is white and ruddy, chosen out of thousands (Song 5:10). White in the fullness and purity of his virtues, ruddy in his passion, of which we shall read later: Who is this who comes up from Edom, tawny are his garments from Bozrah; chosen out of thousands in the resurrection, so that he who was the firstborn of all creation might become the firstborn from the dead.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.3":["And what he adds, A man in pain, and knowing how to bear infirmity, or a man of sorrows, and knowing infirmity, shows the true body of a man and a true soul, he who, knowing how to bear infirmities, overcame them all by his divinity. And his face was hidden and despised, so that the divine power was concealed by the human body; of whom it was said above: You are a hidden God, and we did not know.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.4":["He truly bore our sicknesses and sins, and suffers pain for us, not in seeming, that is, τὸ δοκεῖν, as an old and a new heresy suppose; but he was truly crucified. He truly suffered pain, saying in the Gospel: My soul is sorrowful even unto death (Matt 26:38). And: Now my soul is troubled (John 12:27). And we thought him unclean, or in pain, as the Seventy translated; for which Aquila and Symmachus put leprous, Theodotion scourged. This, in other words, by Hebrew idiom is understood as leprosy, according to what is written in the psalm: And the scourge shall not come near your tent (Ps 90 [91]:10). And the sense is: We thought him struck by God for his own sins, he who was humbled for our sake and crucified with robbers. Where Symmachus translated Ἐν ἁφῇ ὄντα, that is, in leprosy, Aquila put ἁφημένον, that is, leprous; which many, not understanding, take to mean abandoned, and others read καθήμενον, that is, sitting.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.5":["But he was wounded for our iniquities, he was bruised for our crimes. The discipline of our peace was upon him, and by his bruise we have been healed. All we like sheep have gone astray, each one in his own way has turned aside: and the Lord has laid on him the iniquity of us all. He was offered because he himself willed it, and he did not open his mouth. LXX: But he was wounded for our sins, and was weakened for our iniquities. The discipline of our peace was upon him: by his bruise we have been healed. All like sheep have gone astray, man has gone astray in his own way. And the Lord handed him over for our iniquities, and he, because of affliction, did not open his mouth. We, he says, thought him a man of sorrows, or, according to the Seventy, to be in pain, and struck by God, and bearing his own sins. But he was wounded for our iniquities, saying in the psalm: They dug my hands and feet (Ps 21 [22]:18), so that by his wound he might heal our wounds; and he was bruised, or weakened for our crimes, so that, made a curse for us, he might free us from the curse. For cursed is every man who hangs on a tree (Deut 21; Gal 3). Hence the discipline of our peace is upon him. For what we ought to have endured for our crimes, he suffered for us, pacifying through the blood of his cross both the things that are on earth and the things that are in heaven. For he is our peace, who made both one, and broke down the middle wall of partition, dissolving the enmity in his flesh, and by his bruise we have been healed (Eph 2). From this it is plain that, just as his scourged and torn body bore the marks of injury in weals and bruises, so his soul too truly suffered pain for us, lest it be believed that in Christ there was truth in part and falsehood in part (Rom 3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.6":["All, he says, like sheep have gone astray, and we have needed the mercy of God, saying in the psalm: I have gone astray like a lost sheep (Ps 118 [119]:176); the sheep which in the parable of the Gospel was carried back on the shoulders of the good and careful Shepherd (Matt 18). And who these sheep are, the following verse shows: Man has gone astray in his own way; or each one has turned aside in his own way, to follow his own error, and, forsaking the right way, to hold differing opinions about the Crucified. The Lord, however, has laid on him the iniquity of us all, or handed him over for our sins, so that what we could not bear through the weakness of our strength, he might carry for us, he who was offered because he himself willed it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.7":["For he endured the cross not by necessity but by will, saying in the Gospel: The cup which the Father has given me, shall I not drink it (John 18:11)? And to Peter, who was scandalized at the name of the cross, because he did not know the mystery and trembled with human fear: Get behind me, Satan, you are a scandal to me, for you do not savor the things that are of God, but those that are of men (Matt 16:23). Otherwise, if he had not been offered by his own will, he who could point out and foretell the traitor, and said to the Apostles, All of you will be scandalized in me this night, could have avoided those who had been sent against him; but he went to meet them fearlessly, and offered himself of his own accord, saying: Whom do you seek (John 18:4, 6)? And at once they fell backward; for they could not bear the voice of God present before them. And beautifully he added: And he did not open his mouth. When Pilate said, Do you not speak to me?, he would not answer. Or according to the Seventy: Afflicted, he did not open his mouth. Or according to Symmachus and Theodotion, hearing, he did not open his mouth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","As a sheep he shall be led to the slaughter, and as a lamb before its shearer he shall be dumb, and he shall not open his mouth. From distress and from judgment he was taken away. Who shall declare his generation? for he was cut off from the land of the living: for the crime of my people he struck them. And he shall give the ungodly for burial, and the rich man for his death: because he did no iniquity, nor was there deceit in his mouth. And the Lord willed to bruise him in infirmity. LXX: As a sheep he was led to the sacrifice, and as a lamb dumb before its shearer, so he did not open his mouth. In humiliation his judgment was taken away. Who shall declare his generation, for his life is taken from the earth? By the iniquities of my people he was led to death; and I will give the worst men for his burial, and the rich for his death. For he did no iniquity, nor was there deceit in his mouth: and the Lord wills to cleanse him from the stroke. This testimony, when in the Acts of the Apostles the Eunuch of queen Candace was riding in his chariot (Acts 8ff.) and, reading it, did not understand it, he understood when Philip interpreted it of the passion and name of the Savior; and at once, baptized in the blood of the lamb of whom he was reading, he deserved to be called a man, and was sent as an Apostle to the nation of the Ethiopians. He, then, who was offered to Pilate because he himself willed it, and did not answer, so that, condemned, he might mount the gibbet for us, was himself led as a sheep to the slaughter, and as a lamb before its shearer he was dumb. For Christ our passover has been sacrificed (1 Cor 5), whom John the Baptist pointed out, saying: Behold the lamb of God, behold him who takes away the sins of the world (John 1:29). He too is often mentioned in the Apocalypse of John the Evangelist as the lamb that was slain (Rev 5). He speaks of himself in Jeremiah (Jer 11): But I was like an innocent lamb, led to the sacrifice, and I did not know. For though he knew no sin, he was made sin for us (2 Cor 5). And as a lamb, when it is led to the sacrifice, does not resist, so he suffered by his will, that he might destroy him who had the dominion of death (Heb 2), humbling himself unto death, even the death of the cross (Phil 2). This is the lamb in whose figure a lamb was sacrificed, whose blood, smeared on the doorposts, put to flight the destroyer of Egypt (Exod 12); who not only redeemed us with his blood, but also covered us with his wool, so that he might warm with his garment us who were freezing with unbelief, and that we might hear the Apostle saying to us: As many of you as have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ (Gal 3:27). And in another place: Put on Christ Jesus (Rom 13:14).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.8":["And as for what follows, From distress and from judgment he was taken away, or as the Seventy translated, in humiliation his judgment was taken away: it signifies either that from tribulation and judgment he ascended victorious to the Father; or that he, the judge of all, did not find the truth of judgment, but without any fault was condemned by the sedition of the Jews and the voice of Pilate. Hence the Prophet marvels that the God of all handed himself over to suffering. Of which Paul speaks: For if they had believed, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory (1 Cor 2:8). There follows: Who shall declare his generation? This is understood in two ways: for either it is to be taken of his divinity, that it is impossible to know the mysteries of the divine birth, of which he himself speaks in Proverbs, Before all the hills he begot me (Prov 8:25), according to what we read elsewhere, Who has known the mind of the Lord, or who has been his counselor (Rom 11:34)? that is, no one; or of the Virgin's giving birth, that it can be explained only with difficulty. Indeed, when it was said to Mary by the Angel, You shall conceive and bear a son, she answered: How shall this be to me, since I do not know a man? To whom the Angel in turn: The Holy Spirit, he says, shall come upon you, and the power of the Most High shall overshadow you (Luke 1:31, 34, 35); so that the mysteries of this birth are told only by the Angel or by the Evangelist. Its narrator is most rare, according to that saying: Who is wise, and he will understand these things? understanding, and he will know them (Hos 14:10)? But if a prudent reader answers in silent thought: And how is it written, No one knows the Son but the Father, and no one knows the Father but the Son, and he to whom the Son wills to reveal him? and he who knows the Father and the Son can surely also declare the mystery of the generation: let him hear that it is one thing to know and another to utter, because often what we conceive in the mind we cannot unfold in speech. Therefore the saints too can know the mystery of the divine birth in the body by faith rather than tell it. Otherwise the Apostle too, caught up into the third heaven and into paradise, heard words which a human tongue can by no means utter (2 Cor 12). But the Spirit too intercedes for us with unutterable groanings. Of him, then, whose generation either no one or hardly anyone can declare, the life was taken away from the earth, so that he might live not on earth but in heaven. Or he was cut off from the land of the living, so that there might be fulfilled of him what is written in the Apocalypse of John: I am the first and the last: I who was living, and was dead: and behold, I live for ever (Rev 1:18); so that after the life he lived on earth, dead to the earth, he might live in heaven for ever. And what is joined to this, For the crime of my people he struck them, or, according to the Seventy, by the iniquities of my people he was led to death, has a double sense. For either by his death he struck the persecutors and the criminals of his people; or because of the greatness of the sins of the people which he always held as his own peculiar people, he was led to death, so that by his death he might call them back to life.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","The book of the generation of Jesus Christ. In Isaiah we read: His generation, who shall declare it? Let us not think, then, that the Gospel is contrary to the prophet, so that what he said was impossible to utter, this one begins to narrate; for there it is said of the generation of the divinity, here of the incarnation. And he began from the things of the flesh, so that through the man we might begin to learn of God.\n— Commentary on Matthew","It is said in Isaiah concerning the Lord Savior, \"Who shall declare his generation?\" When it is said, \"Who?\" every creature is excluded. For he did not say, \"Which of men shall declare his generation?\" but generally, \"Who?\" — that is, not an angel, not an archangel, not a cherub, not a seraph. For if that which is spoken of was hidden in God from the heavenly creatures, what wonder if it seems unknown to us who were made from clay?\n— Homily 69, on Psalm 91 (Alternate Series)"],"ISA.53.9":["He gave the ungodly for his burial, and the rich man for his death; or the worst men for his burial, and the rich for his death: signifying both peoples, so that in the evil and the worst, who before had no knowledge of God (Rom 9), the multitude of the nations is shown; in the rich, to whom belonged the Testament and the Lawgiving and the Prophets, the Jewish people is pointed out. Therefore the Lord suffered and was buried for this reason, that he might gather to himself a Church from both peoples. Or this must be said: that the Scribes and Pharisees, and Sadducees, the priests and high priests, who formerly reigned among the people and abounded in excessive wealth, God handed over to the Romans after the Lord's passion, and subjected to everlasting servitude. He for whose burial and death the ungodly and the rich were handed over did no iniquity, nor was deceit found in his mouth. This can be understood of no man at all, that he sinned neither in deed nor in word, as Scripture says, No one is clean from filth, not even if his life has been of a single day, and, all like sheep have gone astray, each one has turned aside in his own way (Job 25), except of him who bore our sins, and suffers pain for us, and was wounded for our iniquities, and afflicted for our crimes, by whose bruise we have been healed. Expounding this testimony about him, Peter the prince of the Apostles also says: For to this you were called, because Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example, that you should follow in his steps; who did no sin, nor was deceit found in his mouth; who when he was cursed did not curse, and when he suffered did not threaten (1 Pet 2:21ff.).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.10":["And the Lord willed to cleanse him from the stroke which he had received when pierced by the soldier's lance. Or to bruise him in infirmity and wound; of which he himself also said: For they persecuted him whom you struck (Ps 68 [69]:27). And through Zechariah God says: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered (Zech 13:7). Therefore his suffering was not of necessity, but of the will of the Father and of his own, he who said to him: O God, to do your will, I have willed it (Ps 39 [40]:9). Of which we also read above: He was offered because he himself willed it.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","If he lays down his soul for sin, he shall see a long-lived seed, and the will of the Lord shall be directed in his hand. Because there has labored his soul, he shall see, and be satisfied: by his knowledge he himself, the righteous one, shall justify my servants who are many, and he himself shall bear their iniquities. LXX: If you give an offering for sin, your soul shall see a long-lived seed: and the Lord wills to take away his soul from pain, to show him light, and to form understanding; to justify the righteous one who serves many well: and he himself shall bear their sins. According to the Hebrew this is the sense: If he whom the Lord willed to bruise, or to cleanse, lays down his soul for sin, he shall see a long-lived seed, which he sowed in good ground. Of which it is written in the Gospel: The sower went out to sow; and again: The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field (Matt 13:3). And the will of the Lord shall be directed in his hand, so that whatever the Father willed may be fulfilled by his powers, as he says to the Father: I have kept in your name those whom you gave me. I have guarded them, and none of them has perished except the son of perdition (John 17:12).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","According to the Seventy this is said: O you for whose sins the Son of God was led to death, you who, the worst men and rich in evil, were given for his burial and death: if you are willing to do penance, and to offer for your sins a sacrifice pleasing to God, a contrite spirit, your soul shall see a seed of long time, the Lord the Savior himself. Of whom it is sung in Psalm 88 (89): His seed endures for ever, and his throne like the sun in my sight. And again: I will establish his seed for ever and ever, and his throne like the days of heaven. Which in other words Gabriel says to the Virgin: Behold, you shall conceive in your womb and bear a son, who shall be called the Son of the Most High. And the Lord shall give him the throne of David his father, and he shall reign over the house of Jacob for ever; and of his kingdom there shall be no end (Luke 1:31, 32). For the Lord wills to take away from pain the soul of him who had said, My soul is sorrowful even unto death (Matt 26:38), so that the shame of the cross may be tempered by the glory of the resurrection.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.11":["And he shall see an everlasting seed, and the will of the Father shall be directed in his hand, because his soul labored for a long time, finding no rest among the Jews, and saying in the Gospel: Foxes have holes, and the birds of heaven nests; but the Son of man has nowhere to lay his head (Matt 8:20). And in this same Prophet: I have labored, bearing it (Isa 1:14). Because, then, he labored, he shall see the Churches rise up in the whole world, and shall be satisfied with their faith. Indeed, when, hungry and thirsty, he had sat down by the well of Jacob (John 4) at midday under the scorching sun, he would not use the food that had been bought, because he was already satisfied with the faith of the Samaritan woman and of the city of Shechem coming out to him. In this sense he said among the eight beatitudes: Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness (Matt 5:6). By his knowledge, that is, by his doctrine, he himself, the righteous one, who did no sin, nor was deceit found in his mouth, and the servant of the Father, who had taken the form of a servant and had served the will of the Lord, shall justify many who believe from the whole world. And he himself shall bear their iniquities, which they could not bear, and by whose weight they were crushed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And to show him light, so that he may see all enlightened through him. And to form with understanding, him being understood, upon whom the spirit of wisdom and understanding shall have come down. And to justify the righteous one, who has served many well; for he came not to be served but to serve (Matt 20), washing away in Peter's feet (John 13) the sins of all the Apostles. He appeared in the flesh, was justified in the spirit. Of him Judas the traitor too confesses: I have sinned, betraying righteous blood (Matt 27:4). And Pilate's wife: Have nothing to do with that righteous man: for I have suffered many things today in dreams because of him. And it is to be noted that he was not justified so that from being unrighteous he might become righteous; but the righteous one is justified, not so that he might begin to be what he was not, but so that what he was might appear to all. This righteous one suffered for the unrighteous, that he might offer us all to God. Of him it is said to the Jews: And you denied the holy and righteous one, and asked that a murderer be granted to you (Acts 3:14). And their sins, he says, he himself bore, as a physician bears the sicknesses of the sick; of him the healthy have no need, but those who are ill (Luke 5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.53.12":["Therefore I will allot him very many, and he shall divide the spoils of the strong: because he handed over his soul to death, and was reckoned with the wicked: and he bore the sins of many, and prayed for the transgressors. LXX: Therefore he shall possess many, and shall divide the spoils of the strong: because his soul was defiled in death, and he was reckoned with the unrighteous, and he took up the sins of many, and for their iniquities he was handed over. He gives the reasons why after his passion Christ obtains many rewards. Because, he says, he suffered, and did all the things which the preceding discourse described, and himself bore the iniquities of many: therefore I will allot him very many, so that, in place of Jacob, the portion of the Lord, and Israel, the line of his inheritance, they may believe in him, coming from the East and the West, and recline in the kingdom of God with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob (Matt 8): when that is fulfilled which is written: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations as your inheritance, and the ends of the earth as your possession (Ps 2:8). Of which we also read in this same Prophet: There shall be the root of Jesse, and he who shall rise to rule the nations, in him the nations shall hope (Isa 11:10). These very many were once held by the strong; and under the figure of the ass and the colt they had many masters, to whom the Apostles said: The Lord has need of them (Matt 21:3). These were strong before Christ took up a human body and, having overcome the strong man, plundered his house. So he handed over and divided the prey of the strong to his Apostles, so that Peter, James and John became the princes of the circumcised people, and Paul and Barnabas were sent to the nations, separated in place, not in mind, and stood under one Lord in different battle lines, so that by the victory of both columns they might raise a triumph to the Savior. Hence those who say that the dispensational contention between Peter and Paul (Acts 15) was truly a quarrel and a struggle, so as to satisfy the blaspheming Porphyry, and who assert that the ceremonies of the old Law are to be kept in the Church of Christ by those of the stock of believing Israel, ought also to expect a golden Jerusalem in the thousand years, so as to sacrifice victims and be circumcised, so as to sit on the sabbath, sleep, be filled, get drunk, and rise up to play: a play which offends God. We have said this on account of what is now prophesied: And he shall divide the spoils of the strong (Isa 53:12). According to what is written in another place: When the heavenly one divided kings in it. And again: The king of the hosts of the beloved, and the beauty of the house, to divide the spoils (Ps 67 [68]:13). Indeed, of the apostle Paul, who was of the tribe of Benjamin, it is said according to the Hebrew: Benjamin a ravening wolf: in the morning he shall eat, and at evening he shall divide the spoils (Gen 49:27). Of these too we have already spoken before: They shall rejoice in your sight, as those who rejoice at harvest, and as those who divide the spoils (Isa 9:3), dividing among themselves the Churches of Christ in the whole world. Therefore he shall receive very many nations, he who came to proclaim release to the captives, and led captive the captivity formerly held by the devil and the demons, and gave it as a gift to men and to believers, because he handed over his soul to death and was reckoned with the wicked, or unrighteous (1 Cor 9). For if his Apostle became to those who were without law as one without law, though he was not without law but was under the law of Christ, why should not Christ too have been reckoned with the unrighteous, that he might redeem the unrighteous from sin and become all things to all, that he might save all? For he bore our sins in his body (1 Pet 2), fastening them to the wood of the cross, that he might blot out the handwriting which we had made to the devil and his angels, written by the hands of the soul, that is, by its works. Of these the apostle Paul speaks: And you, when you were dead in sins and in the desires of the flesh, he made alive with Christ, forgiving us all sins. And blotting out the handwriting that was against us, which was contrary to us, he took it out of the way, fastening it to the cross: despoiling the principalities, he put the powers to shame, triumphing over them confidently (Eph 1:2; Col 2:14, 15). And the unrighteous with whom he was reckoned, the Evangelist Mark understands as the robbers, writing: And they crucified with him two robbers, one on the right, and the other on the left. And the Scripture was fulfilled which says: And he was reckoned with the unrighteous (Mark 15:27). This can also be understood more deeply, as the Lord says of himself: I am reckoned with those who go down into the pit: I have become like a man without help, free among the dead (Ps 87 [88]:4). For he was truly reckoned among sinners and the unrighteous, so that he might go down to the underworld, which in many places of the Scriptures is called the pit, and set free those bound in prison. He was handed over for our sins, and rose again for our justification (Rom 4). And he was of such clemency that he prayed on the cross for the transgressors, nay rather for his persecutors, and said: Father, forgive them: for they do not know what they do (Luke 23:34).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.1":["For it is written: Rejoice, barren one, you who do not bear; break forth and cry out, you who do not travail: for many more are the children of the desolate than of her who has a husband (Isa. 54:1.)\n\nThe synagogue had the Law as her husband; and, according also to the prophecy of Hannah, she was once fruitful in children (1 Sam. 1). But the barren Church, without Christ as husband, without any address of the Bridegroom's word, lay long in the desert. But after the synagogue received the bill of divorce into her hands, and turned all her husband's ornaments into the ornament of an idol, then the husband, his former girdle rotting, wove another belt for his loins, another loincloth out of the Gentiles; and she, as soon as she was joined to her husband, conceived and gave birth. And in Isaac the Lord cries out through the prophet: \"Is a nation born all at once?\" (Isa. 66:8): when in a single day, in the Acts of the Apostles, three thousand and five thousand men believed (Acts 3 ff.). Nor do I think it necessary for us to speak of the Christian multitude and of the fewness of the Jews, since the banners of the cross shine forth in the whole world, and a Jew scarcely appears in the cities, rare and conspicuous.\n— Commentary on Galatians","I know that I have often said, Eustochium, that the Apostles and Evangelists, wherever they set down testimonies from the Old Instrument, were accustomed, if there is no difference between the Hebrew and the Septuagint, to use either their own words or those of the Seventy Translators; but if the sense is one thing in the Hebrew and another in the old edition, they follow the Hebrew rather than the Seventy Translators. In short, just as we have shown that they set down many things from the Hebrew which are not found in the Seventy, so let our rivals show some testimonies taken from the Seventy which are not in the books of the Hebrews, and the dispute is finished. We have said this because the present passage, though it is one in sense, differs in words. With its explanation the fifteenth book on Isaiah begins.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Praise, O barren one, who do not bear; sing out praise and neigh, you who were not in labor: for more are the sons of the deserted one than of her who had a husband, says the Lord. LXX: Rejoice, O barren one, who do not bear: break forth and cry out, you who are not in labor: for more are the sons of the deserted one than of her who has a husband. For the Lord has said. Symmachus translated this passage thus: Rejoice, O barren one, who have not borne. Be glad in exultation, and neigh, you who have not been in labor: For more are the sons of the scattered one than of her who was under a husband. From him Theodotion and Aquila do not differ, apart from a few words. After the nativity of the Savior, and the course of His life and mighty works, the passion of the cross, and the glory of the resurrection, when, laying down His soul, He saw a long-lived seed, and in His knowledge He, the righteous one, justified very many, and divided the spoils of the strong, and prayed for the transgressors, giving room for repentance, the Prophet passes to the calling of the nations, and describes in full discourse those who were to believe in Him. This passage the apostle Paul too refers, under the name of Sarah and Isaac, to the Church (Gal 4:27ff.): that the former people, from Mount Sinai and Hagar, is in bondage with her children, but the later one is free, of whom Isaiah says: Rejoice, O barren one, who do not bear: break forth and cry out, you who are not in labor: for more are the sons of the deserted one than of her who has a husband; and immediately: But we, he says, brethren, are sons of the promise according to Isaac. But as then he who was born according to the flesh persecuted him who was according to the spirit, so it is now also. But what does the Scripture say? Cast out the bondwoman and her son. For the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with the son of the free woman. But we, brethren, are not sons of the bondwoman, but of the free: with which freedom Christ has endowed us (Gal 4:27ff.). If, then, the vessel of election, taking from Isaiah the testimony which we now have in hand, referred it to the sons of the promise, and to the Church gathered from the nations, or from both peoples, which had been deserted and abandoned among the Jews, which had not had God as her husband, nor had received the Law or the Prophets, we are compelled by that very reasoning to follow the footsteps of our predecessor, and to call her the deserted one of whom we read above (cf. Isa 35): Rejoice, O deserted one, and the rest like these. Of her too it is written in Jeremiah, in the person of God: She who bore seven has been made empty, and her soul has fainted. Her sun has set while it was yet midday (Jer 15:9). And in the volume of Samuel: The barren has borne seven, and she who had many sons has been weakened (1 Sam 2:5). And in the Psalms: Who makes the barren woman dwell in a house, a joyful mother of sons (Ps 112 [113]:9). Now the synagogue is said to have borne seven sons because of the mystery of the week and of the sabbath, to which the former people had been bound. Or else, for seven, many are to be understood, according to an ambiguity of the Hebrew speech, by which both sabbath and many are signified; of this more has been said in the book of Hebrew Questions which we wrote on Genesis. She, then, as long as she had God, the divine word, for her husband and was joined to the Law, bore very many sons to God; but when she received the bill of divorce, and would not answer her husband when he called, she heard: You are your mother's daughter, who have forsaken your husband (Ezek 16:45). And again: You have not called me as Lord, nor as father and guide of your virginity (Jer 3:4): therefore she is lamented in this same Prophet: How has the faithful city become a harlot, Zion, full of judgment: in which righteousness slept in her, but now robbers (Isa 1:21). But this too is to be noted, that when he says: More are the sons of the deserted one than of her who has a husband, the synagogue is not wholly excluded from childbearing, but the multitude of the nations is preferred to her. For she too, in the Apostles and through the Apostles, first bore a people from the Jews. Hence the two chief of the Apostles divided between them the hosts of those who believed in Christ, of the Circumcision and of the Gentiles, so that from both peoples they might build up Jerusalem, formerly deserted and poor. And that the Hebrew has put neighing for gladness signifies the greatness of joy, in the likeness of a horse neighing for victory; of which more is written in Job (Job 39). This passage and the rest that follow the Jews and our own judaizers refer to Jerusalem, which they say is to be restored in a kingdom of a thousand years, and they say she is the same who formerly had a husband and afterward ceased to have one, and that after the divorce she will have many more sons than she had before under a husband. Yet the comparison is plainly set out of two women: of her who had a husband and was put away, and of her who was always deserted and without a husband. Nor is it to be wondered at in the Jews, whose eyes and ears are closed, if they do not see the open truth. Of the Christians I do not know what to say, who, though the Apostle says, which things are allegorical, and refers Sarah and Hagar to the two Testaments, the old and the new, give their hands to the Jews out of desire for earthly pleasures in the thousand years.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.2-3":["Enlarge the place of your tent, and stretch out the skins of your tabernacles: do not spare: make your cords long, and make your stakes firm. For you shall penetrate to the right and to the left, and your seed shall inherit the nations, and shall inhabit the deserted cities. LXX: Enlarge the place of your tabernacle, and fix the skins of your curtains, do not spare. Lengthen your cords, and strengthen your stakes; yet more to the right, and to the left spread out, and your seed shall possess the nations, and you shall make the deserted cities to be inhabited. To her to whom he had said: Praise, O barren one, who do not bear; break the bonds, by which you were formerly held bound, and cry out in confession of the Lord, you who had no children, it is now commanded, in the likeness of the tabernacle of Moses which he once had (Exod 36) in the desert, that she enlarge her tent and stretch out the skins; and make her cords longer, and drive deep and strengthen the stakes by which every tent is shaped, lest it be scattered by the blasts of the winds. And let her penetrate to the right and to the left, and by no means imitate the narrowness of the Jewish tabernacle, which enclosed a hundred cubits of length and fifty of breadth, nor be shut in by the smallness of the Temple, which had sixty cubits in length and twenty in breadth; but let her not cease to take up room to the right and to the left (Exod 27). And lest we think this is said, according to the frivolous contention of the Hebrews, of Zion, which is to be restored by the Lord to its ancient state, he sets out more plainly what lay hidden: And your seed shall inherit the nations. Of this we read in the Gospel also: He who sows went out to sow (Matt 13:3); and again: Did you not sow good seed in your field (ibid., 27)? This seed will also make the deserted cities to be inhabited, so that Churches of the nations rise up throughout the whole world. Or certainly the seed is to be called that of the Apostles and the remnant of the Jewish people, of which it is said in this same Prophet: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us seed, we should have been as Sodom, and should have been like Gomorrah (Isa 1:9). And the Apostle: A remnant, he says, has been saved (Rom 11:5). This concerns the greatness of the Churches, which, in place of the one land of Judea, and that a very narrow one, will spread their bounds through the whole world. Let us come to the spiritual understanding. He who is in a tabernacle does not possess a firm and perpetual dwelling; but he always changes places, and hastens on to what lies beyond, saying in the psalm: I will pass over into the place of the wonderful tabernacle (Ps 41 [42]:5), forgetting the things that are past and stretching himself forward to what is to come, until he attains the prize of the heavenly calling. Of this tabernacle we read in another place also: How beloved are your tabernacles, O Lord of powers! my soul longs and faints for the courts of the Lord (Ps 83 [84]:1). And afterward he adds: Blessed are they who dwell in your house; for ages of ages they shall praise you (ibid., 6). For the end of the tabernacles is the possession of an eternal house, which does not change its foundations, nor is moved from one place into another. For those who are planted in the house of the Lord have first flowered in His courts, so that they may come from flowers to fruit, and may be able to say: But I am like a fruitful olive tree in the house of God (Ps 51 [52]:10). In short, the holy man, hastening to pass beyond the tabernacles and longing to see the house of God, says that he has one wish, never to go out of the house of God: One thing I have asked of the Lord, this will I seek: that I may dwell in the house of the Lord all the days of my life (Ps 26 [27]:4). The tabernacle, then, must be enlarged, and the curtains and skins stretched out, and the cords of manifold and varied discourses drawn out further, and the stakes made firm by reason on the right and on the left, so that the seed of the word, that is, of the doctrine of God, may be able to possess the nations, and to make habitable the cities which he received who had doubled the mina. Now the right and the left in the holy Scriptures are taken in a good sense when we understand them both according to the spirit and according to the letter, through the arms of righteousness on the right and on the left (2 Cor 6), so that the humbler understanding may hold to the ordering of life and the examples of our forebears, and the spiritual and sublime one may carry us over from present things to things to come. This is what the Lord also said to the Pharisees when they questioned Him: Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's (Matt 22:21): that we should both be subject to the powers of this world, which not unjustly bear the sword for vengeance on those who have done evil, and this is understood as the left; and that we should render to God the things that are God's, so as to fear no one else but Him who has power over both soul and body, and this is taken as the right (Matt 10). Finally, it is said of the wisdom of God, to which nothing precious can be compared, that in her right hand she has length of life and very many years, but in her left riches and glory; so that those riches are to be understood which consist in knowledge in the present and in good works, and the glory which he receives through whose works God is glorified among the nations; while length of life and very many years signify eternal life, which, disregarding present things, hastens toward what is to come.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.4-5":["Do not fear, for you shall not be confounded, nor shall you blush: for you shall not be ashamed, because you shall forget the confusion of your youth, and shall no more remember the reproach of your widowhood. For He who made you shall rule over you, the Lord of hosts is His name: and your redeemer, the Holy One of Israel, shall be called the God of all the earth. LXX: Do not fear, because you have been confounded: nor be confounded, because you have been reproached: for you shall forget the eternal confusion, and of the reproach of your widowhood you shall not be mindful: for the Lord who made you, the Lord Sabaoth is His name: and He who delivered you, the God of Israel, shall be called of all the earth. A question arises: if it is said to the Church gathered from the nations: Rejoice, O barren one, who do not bear; and: More are the sons of the deserted one than of her who has a husband, meaning that she who afterward bore more sons had not had a husband, and that she who formerly had a husband was turned to barrenness, how is it now said to her who had no husband: You shall no more remember your widowhood, and you shall forget the confusion of your youth? From this the Jews wish it to be understood that all that is said is said to Jerusalem, which, deserted by God, is to be restored by Him again. They will easily be repelled when they are reminded that it is said in the person of the Lord in Zechariah: And I took to myself two rods: the one I called Beauty, and the other I called Cord: and I fed the flock (Zech 11:7). Of this we have spoken more fully in its place, and now it will be spoken of in part. The two rods are the two peoples, of the nations and of the Jews; of these the first to be called was the throng of the Gentiles, which received the natural law fixed in their hearts, about which Paul, writing to the Romans, argues most forcefully (Rom 1); and nothing is more beautiful than this rod, that every creature should be called equally to the worship of its Creator. But the second, that is, the rod of the people of the Jews, was called Cord: which, after the offense of the nations, is called the portion of the Lord, and Israel the cord of His inheritance (Deut 32). Finally, after Israel was called in Abraham, the Lord says: I took my rod which was called Beauty, and I cut it off, to make void my covenant which I struck with all the nations (Zech 11:10). Therefore at the coming of Christ it is said to the rod that had been cut off: Do not fear, nor blush, nor flood your face with shame. For you shall by no means be confounded any longer, as you had been confounded before, nor shall you be mindful of the confusion of your youth, and you shall not remember your widowhood, through which you were abandoned by God: for your maker Himself shall rule over you, whose name is the Almighty, who reigns not in the one nation of Judea but in the whole world. Finally, there follows: He who had made you has Himself redeemed you with His blood, and He shall be called the God of all the earth: that is to say, of all who dwell on the earth. From this it is plainly evident that this is by no means said to Jerusalem, which never ruled over the whole world, but to the Church of Christ, whose inheritance is the possession of the world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.6-8":["For the Lord has called you as a woman forsaken and mourning in spirit: and as a wife cast off from her youth, says your God. For a moment, for a little while, I forsook you: and with great mercies I will gather you. In a moment of indignation I hid my face from you for a little while: and with everlasting mercy I have had pity on you, says your redeemer, the Lord. LXX: Not as a woman forsaken and faint-hearted has the Lord called you, nor as a woman who is hated from her youth, says your God. For a little time I left you: and with great mercy I will have pity on you. In a little wrath I turned away my face from you: and with everlasting mercy I will have pity on you: says the Lord who delivered you. Here the friends of the Jews debase the forsaken woman and the wife cast off from her youth, whom the Lord forsook for a moment and for a little while, saying that she is Jerusalem; and that He, hiding His face for a little while, took her up again in everlasting mercies and changed her past grief into joy. This is according to the Hebrew. But according to the Seventy he says that He did not call her as a forsaken woman and faint-hearted, nor as a wife who had been held in hatred from her youth; but that He left her for a little while and turned away His face for this reason, that He might have pity on her forever. If, then, the Jews and our judaizers say that Israel was forsaken for a little while, so that at the coming of Christ God might have pity on it, and interpret 'a little' by comparison with the whole of eternity, why do they not grant us too to call that a little time during which the nations were forsaken, so that they, cast off in the middle period, who in their youth had been God's, might afterward in their old age obtain eternal mercy? Especially since, in the time of Israel's calling, the throng of the Gentiles was never shut out, but a door of return always lay open to them through the proselytes; so that, as we seem to have been shut out for a little while by their calling, so by their perpetual exclusion a return to God is granted to us. But we have spoken of a perpetual exclusion if they do not repent. Otherwise the apostle Paul says: God has shut up all things under sin, that He may have mercy on all (Gal 3:22). What we have interpreted of the Church gathered from both peoples, and the Jews take of Jerusalem, those who follow tropology alone, and in the most difficult places flee the questions that arise by an excursion of free disputation, refer to the sinful soul, which has been cast off by God, not out of hatred but by dispensation, so that, pressed by the weight of evils, she may return to her former husband, and, having lost her substance, may not despair of the father's clemency (Luke 15). Is it not great mercy to run to meet the returning son, to hold out the ring and the robe and a kiss, and, after the likeness of another parable, to say to the envious brother: Friend, if I am good, why is your eye evil (Matt 20:15)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.9-10":["As in the days of Noah, so is this to me, to whom I swore that I would no more bring the waters of Noah upon the earth: so I have sworn not to be angry with you and not to rebuke you. For the mountains shall be moved, and the hills shall tremble: but my mercy shall not depart from you, and the covenant of my peace shall not be moved, says the Lord who has compassion on you. LXX: From the water which was under Noah this is to me: as I swore to him at that time that I would by no means any more be angry with the earth over you, nor in threatening you remove the mountains: nor shall your hills be removed. So neither shall my mercy fail, nor shall the covenant of my peace be taken away, says the Lord who is merciful to you. So that the congregation of the saints may believe the mercy of the Lord to be everlasting, and that we were forsaken for a moment and briefly for this reason, that they might be joined in friendship with God by an eternal covenant, he sets forth the examples of the forebears, saying: As, when the whole world was sinning, after all the earth had corrupted the way of the Lord, the flood was brought in, and together with all the authors of sins all sins were blotted out, and in one man, Noah, the human race was preserved; to whom I swore that a flood would by no means be brought on the lands, and my pledge has been kept until now and will never be made void (Gen 8 and 9): so I swear to my Church, which I have redeemed for myself with my blood, that I will by no means be angry with those on whom I have had pity, nor will my clemency be changed by any harshness of rebuke. For the mountains and hills will more easily be moved than my sentence will be changed. In accordance with which He also says in the Gospel: Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away (Matt 24:35). And this, he says, is my mercy: that the covenant of peace, by which the world has been reconciled to me, is kept not by the merit of those to whom it was given, but by my clemency. According to the Seventy the sense is confused, and everything is so disordered that what is being said can hardly be understood: not that I do not know what a most prudent man has said on this passage, but that it does not satisfy my mind. For he posits a tropological flood, which he interprets of the baptism of the Savior, gathering very many examples, such as this: The Lord makes the flood to be inhabited (Ps 28 [29]:10). And again: The Lord is sweet to those who wait for Him in the day of tribulation: and knowing those who fear Him, in the flood of the journey He makes an end (Nah 1:7, LXX): namely, that He blotted out all sins in baptism, saying in another place: I am, I am He who blots out your iniquities (Isa 43:25). For all had turned aside, together they had become unprofitable (Ps 13 [14]:3). There was none who did mercy or truth, nor was there knowledge of God upon the earth. Cursing and lying and murder and adultery and theft had seized everything, and they had mingled blood with blood. Whence He speaks through the Prophet: Woe is me! for the devout man has perished from the earth. There is none who does right among men; all are judged in my blood. Each afflicts his neighbor with affliction, and they prepare their hands for evil (Mic 7:2, LXX); and the rest like these. Among them is this: No one is clean from filth, not even if his life upon the earth be of one day (Job 14:4). Hence he holds that the Lord made the flood, who according to the apostle Peter was put to death in the flesh and made alive in the spirit (1 Pet 3), and preached to the spirits set in prison, when the patience of God waited in the days of Noah, bringing the flood upon the ungodly; after whose pattern water cleanses us, not washing away the filth of the flesh, but as the questioning of a good conscience toward God. And the mountains and hills which are not moved, and in a flood of this kind had been shaken, he wants to be understood as the saints, once the everlasting covenant has been received, who in the former flood had been moved and had left their firmness. He says that the mountains are also the demons and the adverse powers, who saw the daughters of men, that they were fair, and, wounded by the dart of love, took for themselves wives of all whom they chose, and lost their former strength; and they will by no means be in this flood (Gen 6). This is what he may have said; its explanation I leave to the reader's judgment.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.11-12":["Poor little one, torn by the tempest, without consolation. Behold, I will lay your stones in order: and I will found you on sapphires: and I will make your battlements of jasper, and your gates of carved stones, and all your borders of desirable stones: All your sons taught by the Lord, and a multitude of peace for your sons, and in righteousness you shall be founded. LXX: Lowly and unstable, without consolation. Behold, I prepare for you the carbuncle as your stone, and sapphire as your foundations: and I will make your battlements jasper, and your gates stones of crystal, and your walls choice stones: and all your sons disciples of God, and great peace shall be for your sons, and in righteousness you shall be built. Where we have said: I will lay your stones in order, in the Hebrew is written BAPHPHUCH (בפך), which all except the Seventy have translated alike: I will lay your stones in stibium, in the likeness of an adorned woman who paints her eyes with stibium, so as to signify the beauty of the city. And where we, following the Seventy, have said jasper, the Hebrew has written CHODCHOD (כדכד), which Symmachus alone translated χαλκηδόνιον. For crystal also, in whose place ECDA (אקדה) is read among the Hebrews, Symmachus and Theodotion put of carving, that is, γλυφῆς, and Aquila τρυπανισμοῦ: a word that conveys the sense of bored and engraved gems. We have spoken of the difference of translation; let us come to the sense. He is still speaking to the Church, formerly lowly and poor, which had neither the Law nor the Prophets nor the word of God; torn by the tempest or unstable, which had endured many whirlwinds of the world and wavered among the various errors of idols; which had no comforter, and in vain spent all her substance on physicians: that He Himself will come, He Himself will descend, and build on earth the heavenly Jerusalem, which in the Apocalypse of John is called the bride and wife of the Lamb, having light like a precious stone, like jasper and crystal, and a great wall, and twelve gates inscribed with the names of the tribes of Israel, of which three were on the East, and three on the North, and three on the South, and three on the West (Rev 21); and the wall rested on twelve foundations, its whole building of jasper stone, and the several foundations of the walls had each its own stone: the first jasper, the second sapphire, the third chalcedony, the fourth emerald, the fifth sardonyx, the sixth sardius, the seventh chrysolite, the eighth beryl, the ninth topaz, the tenth chrysoprase, the eleventh jacinth, the twelfth amethyst. Reading this, we cry out and say: O the depth of the wisdom and knowledge of God, how inscrutable are His judgments, and His ways past finding out! For who has known the mind of the Lord, or who has been His counselor (Rom 11:33, 34)? And again: Who is wise, and he will understand these things; understanding, and he will know them (Hos 14:10)? Let them answer, the lovers of the letter alone, which kills, who in the thousand years make ready exquisite foods for gluttony and luxury, whose God is the belly and whose glory is in their shame (Phil 3); who after the second coming of the Savior in glory hope for marriages, and little children of a hundred years, and the outrage of circumcision, and the blood of victims, and a perpetual sabbath; who say with Israel, in a perverse way: Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall reign: let them answer what this heavenly Jerusalem is to which it is now said: Behold, I will lay your stones in order; or according to the Seventy: Behold, I prepare for you the carbuncle, your stone: so that the whole city is full of carbuncles, and has sapphire foundations, and battlements of jasper or chalcedony, and gates of crystal or carved in relief, and a wall of precious stones round about; and all her sons have no men as teachers, but God, and are called disciples of God; and there is in her perpetual peace and the building up of righteousness. From this it is clear that under the occasion of righteousness, which is the name of a virtue, we ought to seek the other virtues too for the building up of the Church, and not follow Jewish ravings. For let them explain what it is that is said of wisdom in Proverbs: She is more precious than all the most precious stones (Prov 3:15). For if Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God (1 Cor 1), it is foolish for Christ to be compared to senseless stones. And again we read of the judgments of God: The judgments of the Lord are right, justified in themselves, desirable above gold and very precious stone (Ps 18 [19]:10, 11). From this it is plain that the stone compared with the other stones is the one of which it is said above in this same Prophet, in the person of God: Behold, I will lay in the foundations of Zion a corner stone, a chosen stone, precious: and he who believes in it shall not be confounded (Isa 28:16). This stone the builders rejected (Ps 117 [118]), namely the Scribes and Pharisees and the chief men of the Jews, and it has become the head of the corner (Matt 21). Of it the Apostle Peter also says in his discourse: To us therefore who believe in the corner stone, precious and chosen, there is honor; but to unbelievers it is a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense (1 Pet 2:7, 8). And he too speaks to the chief priests in the Acts of the Apostles: This is the chosen stone, precious, which you despised and rejected (Acts 4:11); which has become the head of the corner, and holds together the two peoples, of the nations and of Israel; which built the city whose builder and maker is God: of which the Apostle also writes to the Corinthians: You are God's building. And: As a wise architect I have laid the foundation, and another builds upon it. But let each one see how he builds. For no one can lay another foundation than that which has been laid, Jesus Christ (1 Cor 3:9, 10). And if anyone builds on this foundation gold and precious stones, wood, hay, stubble, each one's work will be made manifest. Of this foundation he speaks in another Epistle also: Built upon the foundation of the Apostles and Prophets, Jesus Christ Himself being the corner stone (Eph 2:20); and again: In whom you are all being built as living stones into a holy priesthood, to offer spiritual sacrifices (1 Pet 2:5). Of these stones it is said mystically: Holy stones are rolled upon the earth (Zech 9:16), with which Christ builds the Church upon the rock, saying in the Gospel: Upon this rock I will build my Church (Matt 16:18). He who has deserved to enter this city says joyfully to the Lord: As we have heard, so have we seen in the city of the Lord of powers, in the city of our God: God has founded it forever (Ps 47 [48]:9). Concerning the founder of this city it is said in another place also: He shall build my city, and shall bring back the captivity of my people (Isa 45:13). As for the nature of the twelve stones and gems, this is not the time to speak, since very many both of the Greeks and of the Latins have written of it. Of these I will name only two: the bishop Epiphanius, a man of holy and venerable memory, who has left us a notable volume of his genius and learning, which he entitled περὶ λίθων; and Pliny the Second, at once orator and philosopher among the Latins, who in his most beautiful work of natural history filled the thirty-seventh book, which is also the last, with a treatment of stones and gems. These twelve stones are written in order in Exodus and in Ezekiel, that is, ἐν τῷ λογείῳ of the high priest, and in the crown and diadem of the prince of Tyre. Let us speak first of Exodus: four rows of stones were woven in (Exod 28). The first row had the sardius, topaz, emerald; the second row, carbuncle, sapphire, jasper; the third row, ligure, agate, amethyst; the fourth row, chrysolite, beryl, onyx, set in gold; and they were inscribed with the names of the twelve tribes of the sons of Israel. In which it is to be noted that the second row of stones is also set down in the present Scripture: carbuncle, sapphire and jasper. For we do not yet hold what is perfect, nor have we come to the first things, because now we see through a mirror in an enigma. Further, in Ezekiel we find it written thus: You are the seal of likeness and the crown of beauty; you were in the delights of the paradise of God. You were clothed with every first stone: sardius, topaz and emerald, carbuncle and sapphire and jasper, silver also, and gold and ligure, and agate and amethyst, and chrysolite and beryl and onyx; and with gold you filled your treasuries and your storehouses within you. From the day you were created, I set you with the Cherubim on my holy mountain; you were in the midst of the fiery stones, spotless in your days, from the time you were created until your iniquities were found in you (Ezek 28:12ff.). For who is so foolish and of so senseless a mind as to think that the prince of Tyre, whoever he believes him to be, set in the paradise of God, and created among the Cherubim, and dwelling among the fiery stones (which we understand without doubt as the Angels and heavenly Powers), was one adorned with earthly stones and having the likeness and the seal? Of the nature of all the stones, then, and of each one in part, this is not the time to speak; for not everything is always to be said. Now let us discourse only of the carbuncle, the sapphire and the jasper. The carbuncle which is prepared, or laid in order, seems to me the fiery word of doctrine, which, putting to flight the error of darkness, illuminates the hearts of believers. This is the one which one of the Seraphim took, grasped with tongs, to purify the lips of Isaiah (Isa 6); which is born, according to the faith of Genesis, in the land of Havilah, where there is the best gold (Gen 2), and the carbuncle stone and the leek-green. Further, the sapphire, which is placed in the foundations, has the likeness of heaven and of the air above us: and it is such that it can say that line of Aristophanes with Socrates: Ἀεροβατῶ καὶ περιφρονῶ τὸν ἥλιον; which we can turn into the Latin tongue: I tread the air and look down upon the sun (See Suidas under περιφρονεῖν). Or with the apostle Paul: But our conversation is in heaven (Phil 3:20). The Scripture of Ezekiel also records that the place where the throne of God is has the likeness of sapphire, and that the glory of the Lord consists in this color, which bears the image of what is above the heavens. And the battlements of the Lord's city, that is, the ramparts of the walls, are strengthened with jasper: those who can destroy and refute every height that lifts itself up against the knowledge of God, and subject falsehood to truth (Ezek 1; 1 Cor 15). He, then, who is most strong in disputing and strengthened by the testimonies of the holy Scriptures, he is a battlement of the Church. There are many kinds of jasper: one has the likeness of emerald, which is found at the springs of the river Thermodon, and is called Grammatias, by which they assert that all phantoms are put to flight. Another is greener than the sea, and tinged as if with flowers; they report that this is born on Mount Ida in Phrygia and in its deepest caves. Another, again, is found among the Iberians and the Hyrcanians and by the Caspian Sea, and especially by the lake Neusis. There is also another jasper like snow and the foam of the sea waves, and mildly reddish as though with blood mixed in. We have said this so that we may recognize all the spiritual graces in the battlements of the Church; he who has them puts vain fears to flight, and can say with the bride: My kinsman is white and ruddy (Song 5:10). And the gates of this city are of crystal stone, which is carved in various ways; than this stone nothing is purer. In short, in the most violent frosts of the Alps, and in caves inaccessible to the sun, waters are said to congeal into crystal: to the touch it is stone, to the sight water. By this it is shown that those who are at the doors of the Church ought to be stained by no filth, but to be of the purest faith, and to say with the prophet: From your commandments I have understanding (Ps 118 [119]:104), and to hear this: Blessed are the clean of heart, for they shall see God (Matt 5:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.12-14":["And the walls of the city, or the borders and περίβολος, are built of choice stones, by which we can understand the remaining stones, and that all her sons are taught, or are disciples of God: a testimony the Lord uses in the Gospel of John, saying: No one can come to me unless my Father who sent me draws him, and everyone who has heard and learned from the Father comes to me (John 6:44, 45). And shortly after: It is written in the prophets: They shall all be taught by God. He also speaks through Jeremiah: Putting my laws in their minds, and I will write them on their heart; and they shall no more teach each his neighbor and his brother, saying: Know the Lord. For all shall know me, from the least to the greatest. For I will be merciful to their iniquities, and their sins I will not remember (Jer 31:33, 34). And the teaching of the disciples of God has a multitude of peace, which was left to them by the Lord; and the building of the most beautiful city is completed in righteousness, so that He is the God not of one nation but of the whole world, calling to His faith slaves and free, Greeks and Barbarians, rich and poor, noble and lowly, men and women, little children and old men, and all things in the world which seem to be contrary (John 14). We have gone beyond the measure of brevity, which is useful in all that has to be said, since we seek the city of God not, with the Hebrews and our Half-Jews, on earth, but in the heavens: the city which, set on Christ the mountain, cannot be hidden.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.14":["Withdraw far from calumny, for you shall not fear; and from terror, for it shall not come near you. LXX: Withdraw from the unjust man, and you shall not fear, and trembling shall not come near you. A most beautiful order. He had consoled the poor and lowly one, promising her spiritual graces. Now he teaches what she ought to do if she does not wish to dread the assaults of her adversaries. And the sense is: You do not wish to fear your enemies? Do what I say: Withdraw far from calumny, or from iniquity, for all iniquity and plunder is born of calumny; and you shall not fear, and trembling and terror shall not come near you, so that you fear not men but God, saying with Moses: I am trembling and fearful (Deut 9:19); and with one of Job's friends: Horror and trembling came upon me, and shook my bones greatly (Job 4:14); and with the prophet: At the voice of my prayer trembling entered into my bones (Hab 3:16); and: Upon whom does the Lord rest, but upon the humble and quiet, and him who trembles at His words (Isa 66:2)? But the trembling of the ungodly is another, which is born of the fear of punishment; of it is written: Trembling seized the ungodly (Ps 47 [48]:7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.15":["Behold, the sojourner shall come who was not with me; he who was once a stranger to you shall be joined to you. LXX: Behold, proselytes shall come to you through me, and they shall be your settlers, and shall flee to you for refuge. These things too the Jews think are said to Jerusalem: that many of the nations will be proselytes and will take up the law of Moses and its ceremonies. We, according to the interpretation we have begun, refer this to the Church, which has been gathered through the apostles from both peoples, which has neither spot nor wrinkle, which is free, and the mother of all believers (Eph 5; Gal 4). To gather these proselytes and strangers the Lord sent His disciples, saying: Teach all nations (Matt 28:19), so that they might flee to the Gospel and take up the new Law, and that those who were once dwellers in idolatry might become settlers of the Church. Of them it is said in the psalms: The Lord makes the blind wise. Or, as is read in the Latin codices: The Lord gives light to the blind, the Lord loves strangers (Ps 145 [146]:8, 9): so that after the blind have received the light of truth, and the foolish have learned wisdom, then, passing over to the Church, they may be loved by the Lord, and hear through the prophet: I will call not my people, my people (Hos 2:24); and when this people enters the Temple of God, jealousy seizes the unlearned people.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.16-17":["Further, according to the Seventy this seems to me the sense: I, your Creator, have not made you as the devil, that worst of smiths, forges vessels of iniquity with an unclean spirit and brings them forth to perdition; and he ought to know that vessels of this kind do not have a prosperous course, but are broken in mid-career. What the vessels are he shows in the following words: And every voice that shall rise against you in judgment, you shall overcome them all. The vessels of iniquity have a voice, and this voice rises against the Church when the heretics set their mouth on high and their tongue passes through the earth. And what follows, And they shall be liable to you in it, we have marked beforehand with an obelus, because it seems to have been added not so much by the Seventy as by bad copyists. And when the Church has overcome every voice that rises against her, then those who serve and obey the Lord will have an inheritance, an eternal possession; of which it is said in Jeremiah in other words: There is a reward for your works (Jer 31:16). And the dwellers of the Church will be righteous to the Lord. For everyone who does righteousness is born of God; and to them the Lord says: Be holy, for I am holy (Lev 20:26).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.16":["Behold, I have created the smith who blows the coals in the fire, and brings forth a vessel for his work: and I have created the slayer to destroy. Every vessel that is fashioned against you shall not prosper: and every tongue that resists you in judgment you shall judge. This is the inheritance of the servants of the Lord: and their righteousness is with me, says the Lord. LXX: Behold, I have created you, not as a coppersmith blowing the coals in the fire and bringing forth a vessel for work. But I have created you not for perdition, to destroy: every vessel that is made against you shall not prosper: and every voice shall rise against you in judgment: you shall overcome them all ÷ and they shall be liable to you in it ⁚ There is an inheritance for those who serve the Lord, and you shall be righteous to me, says the Lord. Let us speak first according to the Hebrew: If you wish not to fear, and that dread be far from you, do what the preceding discourse has related; for I am He through whom you are to have proselytes. I am He who created the smith who blows the coals in the fire, that is, the devil, the craftsman of all evils, not by necessity of nature but by the choice of his mind; who has kindled fires and will bring forth vessels against you, such as were Simon and Elymas the magicians, who resisted the apostles Peter and Paul (Acts 13). I have created the slayer of those who are going to be unbelievers: not that I am the cause of their perdition, but that the adversary is created for battle, so as to be perdition to the vanquished and a cause of rewards to the victors.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.54.17":["And all who have been fashioned against you by the blowing smith shall not prosper, but shall feel both present punishments and those to come. And what before seemed hidden is said more plainly in what follows: And every tongue that resists you in judgment you shall judge, destroying the wisdom of the wise and rejecting the prudence of the prudent. And all the leaders of the heretics, and the teachers of the Jews, and the philosophers of the world, whom that smith had forged, you will condemn by your judgment; as the Queen of Sheba also condemned the unbelievers, and the Ninevites; and on the contrary Sodom will be justified by comparison with a worse Jerusalem (Matt 12). After this he adds what the rewards to come are, lest they think the contest is in vain and ends with the present death. This is the inheritance of the servants of the Lord, that is, the kingdom of heaven, and life everlasting, and the repayment of labors, the things which eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor have they entered into the heart of man, which God has prepared for those who love Him (1 Cor 2). And if for those who love Him, then also for those who serve Him, who have no other Lord but God. And this is the righteousness of those who are with God, says the Lord: that present tribulation should be compensated by future joy.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.1":["All you who thirst, come to the waters, and you who have no money, hasten, buy and eat: come, buy without silver and without any exchange wine and milk. Why do you weigh out silver not for bread, and your labor not for fullness? LXX: You who thirst, go to the water, and as many as have no money, go, buy, and eat, and walk, and buy without money and without price wine and fat. Why do you buy with money, and your labor not for fullness? The Hebrew word OI (הוי), of which I had said above, in the prophecy against Ariel (cf. Isa 29), that it was ambiguous, and signified either the interjection of one calling or woe, set here at the beginning of the passage, is read not with the feeling of one lamenting but of one calling. Since, then, he had said that every vessel fashioned against the Church is to be broken, and that every voice and tongue that had armed itself against the knowledge of God is to be overcome, he summons the believers to the river of God, which is filled with waters, and whose stream gladdens the city of God, that they may drink waters from the fountains of the Savior, who says to the Samaritan woman: If you knew the gift of God, and who it is that says to you, Give me to drink, you would have asked Him, and He would have given you water. The water that I shall give him shall become in him a fountain of water springing up into life eternal (John 4:10, 14). Of these waters He cried out in the Temple: If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink. He who drinks of the water that I shall give him, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water (John 7:37, 38), signifying the Holy Spirit, whom those who believed were to receive, and of whom the Prophet sounded forth in mystical speech: My soul has thirsted for the living God (Ps 41 [42]:2); and again: With you is the fountain of life (Ps 35 [36]:10). He Himself spoke of Himself: They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and have dug for themselves broken cisterns, which cannot hold waters (Jer 2:13). These waters the clouds sprinkle, to which the truth of God reaches, as it is written: And the clouds sprinkle righteousness (Isa 45:8, LXX). And the thirsty are commanded by no means to drink the muddy waters of Sihor and the swelling floods of the Assyrians, but to go to the waters of Siloam, which go with silence; and not to fear poverty of speech if perhaps they have no silver (Isa 45; Jer 2), but to hear the Apostle saying: By grace you have been saved (Eph 2:8); and the Lord saying to the disciples: Freely you have received, freely give (Matt 10:8). And in a wonderful manner they buy waters without money; and they do not drink them, but eat them. For He is both water and the bread which came down from heaven (John 6). Therefore what is read in some copies, Buy and drink, was changed by unskilled copyists, who thought it more consistent if the waters were drunk rather than eaten. But there is also a most wicked money, or silver, which the Scripture rejects, saying: Money that is given with deceit shall be reckoned as a potsherd (Prov 26:23, LXX); and in another place: Your silver is reprobate (Jer 6:30). And there is a silver which is compared to the utterances of God: The utterances of the Lord are pure utterances: silver tried in the fire of earth, purified sevenfold (Ps 11 [12]:7). Spurning, then, that silver and those moneys with which we cannot buy the waters of the Lord, let us go to Him who, holding the cup of the Sacrament, said to the disciples: Take and drink; this is my blood, which shall be shed for you for the remission of sins (Matt 26:27, 28). This wine Wisdom too mixed in her bowl, summoning to drink all the foolish of the world and those who do not have the wisdom of the world; and so that we may buy not only wine but also milk, which signifies the innocence of little children: a custom and type which is kept to this day in the Churches of the West, that wine and milk are given to those reborn in Christ. Of this milk Paul also said: I gave you milk to drink, not solid food (1 Cor 3:2). And Peter: As newborn little children, desire the rational milk (1 Pet 2:2). Hence Moses too, understanding the wine and the milk of the passion of Christ, testifies in mystical speech: His eyes are lovely with wine, and his teeth white with milk (Gen 49:12). For milk in the present passage the Seventy translated fat; of which holy David says in the psalm: Let my soul be filled as with fat and fatness (Ps 62 [63]:6); and in another place: He fed them with the fat of wheat, and with honey from the rock He satisfied them (Ps 80 [81]:17). These fats denote nothing other than the mystical flesh, to which the Lord exhorted the disciples, saying: Unless you eat my flesh and drink my blood, you shall not have life in you (John 6:54). Hence also He was betrayed in Gethsemane, which means valley of fat, or most fertile.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.2":["And at the same time he rebukes those who follow worldly wisdom and the perverse and feigned doctrines of the heretics, and the traditions of the Pharisees, and all knowledge falsely so called which is lifted up against God, and who at great prices and with unceasing labor weigh out silver for a discipline in which there is no bread, and sweat for foods in which there is no fullness. From this it is shown that the wisdom to be pursued is the one which lies not in the leaves and flower of words but in the marrow and fruits of meanings; which does not fly past the ear but refreshes the mind. To learn it we do not cross the seas, nor do we need great expense, but the word is near, in our mouth and in our heart.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Hear, you who hear me, and eat what is good, and your soul shall delight in fatness. Incline your ear, and come to me: hear, and your soul shall live. And I will strike with you an everlasting covenant: the faithful mercies of David. LXX: Hear me, and eat good things, and your soul shall delight in good things. Attend with your ears, and follow my ways: and hear me, and your soul shall live in good things. And I will establish for you an eternal testament, the faithful holy things of David. Lest anyone think that the hearing to which the divine word exhorts those who are to hear is of the flesh and not of the mind, it promises them good things not of the flesh but of the soul. For if, he says, you hear me, you shall eat the good things of the land (Isa 1:19). Or the good: He who says, I am the good shepherd (John 10:11). And your soul shall delight in good things and in fatness. Therefore the good things promised to the soul are not riches, and health of body, and the dignities of the world, which even the philosophers call indifferent, that is, neither good nor evil, and which vary according to the quality of those who use them; but those are to be believed good to which God exhorts us: Turn aside from evil, and do good (Ps 36 [37]:27). But if honesty and the virtues are called the good things of the soul, then evils too are to be taken to be not poverty, and infirmity of body, and low birth, but all the vices, which are truly evil. In short, Abraham did not have good things because he was rich, but because he used his riches well (Gen 13). And Lazarus, who afterward rested in his bosom, did not endure evils because he suffered the torments of diseases together with want; rather, through what were thought evils in the world, he obtained true goods. Hence too that rich man clad in purple received his good things in his lifetime, which were good to him who thought them good (Luke 16); and of Lazarus it is not said, conversely: He received his evil things in his lifetime; but: he received evil things in his lifetime, which seemed evil not to him who suffered them but to others. An example of both is given us by blessed Job, who was overcome neither by the goods nor by the evils of the world, but bore all things with equal firmness of mind (Job 2). Wherefore Solomon prays to God: Give me neither riches nor poverty. But appoint for me what is necessary and sufficient for my living, lest, being full, I become a liar and say: Who sees me? or, being in want, I steal and forswear the name of the Lord (Prov 30:8, 9). And if he prays for this, to have neither riches nor poverty but only the necessaries of living, of which the Apostle also says: Having food and clothing, with these we are content (1 Tim 6), it is clear that riches and want, health and sickness, pleasure and torments, are neither good nor evil, but become good and evil according to the difference of those who bear them. Therefore it is not, as the χιλιαστὰς would have it, abundance of wealth, and dainty foods, and fatness of body, pheasants and stuffed turtledoves, mead, unmixed wine, the beauty of wives, swarms of children, that the Lord promises to the soul, but those delights to which He summons us mystically, saying: Delight, or take your delight in the Lord, and He will give you the petitions of your heart (Ps 36 [37]:4); and elsewhere: I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living (Ps 26 [27]:13); and in another psalm: Bless the Lord, O my soul, and all that is within me His holy name; who fills your desire with good things (Ps 102 [103]:1 and 5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.3":["Finally he adds: Hear me, and your soul shall live (Isa 55:3). The promise of all good things is life everlasting. If you are willing to hear, and your soul lives forever, I will strike with you an everlasting covenant, the faithful mercies of David. Of these the same Psalmist sang: The mercies of the Lord I will sing forever. From generation to generation I will proclaim your truth (Ps 88 [89]:1, 2). And so that we may know what these mercies are, he shows in the following words: Once I have sworn by my holy one: if I lie to David, his seed shall remain forever, and his throne as the sun in my sight, and as the full moon forever, and a faithful witness in heaven. He is called faithful for this reason, that He has fulfilled His promises. For these the Seventy translated the faithful holy things of David (Acts 13:34), which can be taken as firm and strong; as is this: All His commandments are faithful, confirmed for ever and ever (Ps 110 [111]:8). And in another place: God is faithful, and there is no iniquity in Him (Deut 32:4). And the apostle Paul: If we deny Him, he says, He remains faithful: He cannot deny Himself (2 Tim 2:13). And in another place, writing to Timothy: A faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptance (1 Tim 1:15). And this covenant which the Lord promises will not be brief and for one time, as was that of the people of the Jews, but will remain forever, so that the true David may come, and in the Gospel may be fulfilled what was promised in the person of God: I have found David my servant; with holy mercy I have anointed him (Ps 88 [89]:21); whose hand He set in the sea, and his right hand in the rivers. Him, according to Ezekiel, when David had already been asleep for many ages, He calls His servant and shepherd, saying: I will raise up for you one shepherd, my servant David (Ezek 34:23).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.4-5":["Behold, I have given him as a witness to the peoples, a leader and teacher to the nations. Behold, you shall call a nation which you did not know: and nations which have not known you shall run to you, because of the Lord your God and the Holy One of Israel, because He has glorified you. LXX: Behold, I have given him as a testimony among the nations, a prince and commander to the nations: nations which did not know you shall call upon you: and peoples who were ignorant of you shall flee to you for refuge: because of the Lord your God, the Holy One of Israel, who has glorified you. He had summoned the people of the Jews to believe, to incline their ear and receive the everlasting covenant which the Lord had promised to David, and to Abraham and his seed, saying: In your seed the nations shall be blessed (Gen 22:18). The apostle Paul, explaining this: He did not say, he says, in seeds, but, in seed, which is Christ (Gal 3:16). And because He knew that, while they did not believe, the peoples of the nations would believe, He passes over to the nations, and says that He has sent His Son as a witness or testimony of all the nations, to announce His precepts and commandments to the peoples; who says of Himself: This Gospel must be preached in the whole world, for a testimony to all the nations (Matt 24:14). Finally, He refutes the proud Pilate with this word: For this I was born, to bear witness to the truth (John 18:37). Of Him the apostle Paul writes to Timothy: One mediator of God and men, the man Christ Jesus, who gave Himself a redemption for all, a testimony in its proper times: in which I have been appointed a herald and an Apostle (1 Tim 2:5). All that is said, then, is to be referred to Him who was led as a sheep to the slaughter, and as a lamb before the shearer did not open His mouth. Of Him it is said above: We saw Him, and He had neither form nor comeliness (Isa 53:2); and: He Himself bore our sins, and suffers for us. To Him have fled, or run, those who before did not know Him, saying: Our God is refuge and strength (Ps 45 [46]:1); and again: Lord, you have become our refuge from generation to generation (Ps 89 [90]:1). Of their faith we read above also: Those to whom it was not preached concerning Him shall see: and those who had not heard shall understand. He is the witness of all that the Father promised and bestowed on the world, whose mystery Paul gathers up in a brief word to the Ephesians: The God, he says, of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of glory (Eph 1:17); of whom it is written now as well: Because of the Lord your God, and the Holy One of Israel, who has glorified you, and that with the glory which He had before the world was made. Of it the Prophet also testifies: The glory of God shall appear (Heb 1), He who is the brightness of glory and the form of His substance, when every tongue shall confess that the Lord Jesus is in the glory of the Father (Phil 2). He answered the Son, when He asked for the glory which He had had before: I have both glorified it, and will glorify it. Of which the apostle John also says: We saw, he says, His glory, the glory as of the only-begotten of the Father, full of grace and truth (John 12:28; 1:14). Therefore, when he speaks of Him who in the beginning was with God, God the Word, He is called the Father of glory; but when of Him who says in the Gospel, Why do you persecute me, a man who speaks the truth to you (John 8:40)? He is called the God of our Lord Jesus Christ. Not that there is one and another (which is the very bad error of many), but because one and the same Son of God speaks now with the feelings of the glory of His divinity, now with those of our nature, which He deigned to take up.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.6":["Seek the Lord while He can be found: call upon Him while He is near. Let the ungodly man forsake his way, and the unrighteous man his thoughts, and let him return to the Lord, and He will have mercy on him, and to our God: for He is abundant in forgiving. LXX: Seek the Lord: and when you have found Him, call upon Him. And after He has drawn near to you, let the ungodly man forsake his ways and the unrighteous man his thoughts and return to the Lord: and he shall obtain mercy: for He will abundantly forgive your sins. Since, then, as we have already said before, you would not receive the everlasting covenant and the faithful mercies of David, which, when you were unwilling to take them up, the throng of the Gentiles took up, I the Prophet warn you, my own people, and solemnly charge you: while there is time, do penance. Turn to Him who now speaks to you through the Prophets, who afterward will speak in person. Seek Him while He can be found, while you are in the body, while room for repentance is given; and seek Him not by place but by faith. How God is to be sought is said more fully in another place: Think of the Lord in goodness, and seek Him in simplicity of heart: for He is found by those who do not tempt Him, and appears to those who are not unbelieving toward Him (Wis 1:1, 2). Hence we too, knowing the word spoken of sinners, Those who withdraw far from you shall perish, let us say to the Lord: Whither shall I go from your spirit, and whither shall I flee from your face? If I ascend into heaven, you are there: if I descend into hell, you are present (Ps 138 [139]:7, 8). And let us call upon Him while He is near, lest through our vices and sins He withdraw far away. For He draws near to those who draw near to Him, and joyfully runs to meet the son returning after a long time. Wherefore the holy man sings in the psalm: But for me it is good to cleave to God (Ps 72 [73]:28). And Moses alone drew near to the Lord (Exod 20). And through Jeremiah God says: I am a God who draws near, and not from afar (Jer 23:23): drawing near to those who draw near to me by faith, and withdrawing far from those who depart far from me by unbelief. Because of this it is said to believers: Draw near to God, and He will draw near to you. And lest we should think this could suffice, he at once adds: Resist the devil, and he will flee from you (Jas 4:8). Of him it had been said above: Your adversary the devil, like a lion, going about, seeks whom he may devour. Resist him, strong in faith (1 Pet 5:8, 9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.7":["Nor is it enough to seek the Lord, and to find Him while it is the time of repentance, and to call upon Him while He is near, unless the ungodly man forsakes his former ways and the old thoughts by which he had turned aside from the Lord. For then we shall return to the Lord, who will have mercy on us, and to the most clement father, who is abundant in mercies and ready to forgive, when we have forsaken our former thoughts and ways, so that afterward we may deserve to hear: Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven, and whose sins are covered (Ps 31 [32]:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.8":["For my thoughts are not your thoughts: nor are your ways my ways, says the Lord. For as the heavens are exalted above the earth, so are my ways exalted above your ways, and my thoughts above your thoughts. LXX: For my thoughts are not as your thoughts: nor are your ways as my ways, says the Lord. But as far as heaven is distant from the earth, so far is my way distant from your ways, and your thoughts from my thought. Do not think, he says, that what I promise is difficult, and let it not seem incredible to you that the ungodly and the unrighteous can be saved, whether the people of the Jews or all those among the nations who did not know God: consider this, that there is a great difference between my counsels and yours, and as great a diversity of will as of nature (Prov 19). For many are the thoughts in the heart of a man, but the counsel of the Lord abides forever. You, as men, often repenting of a promise, have overturned your old will with a new will. For the Lord scatters the counsels of the nations, and rejects the thoughts of the peoples, and makes void the wills of princes (Ps 32 [33]); but the thoughts of His heart are from generation to generation, and whatever He has decreed cannot be changed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.9":["Do you wish to know the difference between my counsel and yours? As far as heaven is distant in place from the earth, and the dwelling of the Angels is one thing and that of men another, so far are my thoughts separated from your counsels. For of my thoughts it is said: His judgments are inscrutable, and His ways past finding out (Rom 11:33). But of yours: They devised a counsel which they could not establish (Ps 20 [21]:12). And in another place: Whatever counsel you have entered upon shall be scattered, and whatever you have spoken shall not remain among you (Isa 8:10). Things that are clear need no interpretation; therefore they are touched on briefly rather than discussed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.10-11":["And as the rain and the snow come down from heaven, and will not return thither again; but they soak the earth, and water it, and make it spring up, and give seed to the sower, and bread to the eater: so shall my word be which goes forth from my mouth; it shall not return to me empty, but it shall do whatever I willed, and shall prosper in the things for which I sent it. LXX: As if the rain or the snow should come down from heaven, and not return until it soaks the earth, and it bring forth and spring up, and give seed to the sower and bread for eating: so shall my word be which has gone forth from my mouth: it shall not return until it accomplishes what I willed, and I will make its ways and my precepts prosperous. What is said hangs on what went before, and briefly the sense is this: let the people of the nations not be incredulous that after such great crimes the ungodly man is suddenly saved. For my thoughts are not as the thoughts of men, and as far as heaven is distant from the earth, so far are my thoughts separated from the thoughts of men. For I am most clement and abundant in forgiving. Do you wish to receive another likeness also? As the rain and the snow come down from heaven, and will not return thither again, but soak the earth, and water it, and make various seeds sprout, so that from full harvests bread is produced for the use of men: so the word of my promise, which I have once pledged and which has gone forth from my mouth, will not be void, but all will be fulfilled in deed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.10":["Or certainly this is to be said, that the word of the Gospel's teaching is called rain, and the showers which the spiritual clouds pour upon the good earth, the clouds to which the truth of God reaches. This rain and these showers Moses promises in the Song of Deuteronomy (Deut 32:1, 2): Let the earth hear the words of my mouth: let my speech be awaited as the rain, and let my words come down as the dew; so that those who have sown in tears may reap in joy (Ps 125 [126]). And let those who have sown in righteousness and in the spirit reap the fruit of life everlasting, and receive the bread of the Gospel's teaching, of which it is written in Proverbs and in Ecclesiastes: Open your eyes, and be filled with bread (Prov 20:13); and again: Cast your bread upon the water, for in the multitude of your days you will find it (Eccl 11:1). For it is not to be believed that those who eat are commanded that, to eat this bread by which bodies are nourished, they must open their eyes and so be filled with the bread which in Habakkuk the poor man eats in secret (Hab 3). Rather, he exhorts us to the bread of the teaching of God, which we cannot eat unless we have opened the eyes of our heart. Of this Paul too often writes to those who are nourished by the words of faith and truth (2 Cor 9). And the teacher is commanded to cast the bread of his teaching upon every water, and to pour spiritual grace upon all, and to know that if he does what has been commanded, he will receive rewards in the last time. And it would seem unjust enough that, while he who gives alms makes for himself friends of the mammon of iniquity, who may receive him into the eternal tabernacles (Luke 16), he who bestows spiritual foods, and gives his fellow servants their rations in due season, should not find them after many ages, which Ecclesiastes calls the multitude of days (Eccl 11).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.11":["According to the anagogy the understanding is twofold: the word of the Lord is either He of whom it is written: In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God (John 1:1), who will not return to Him empty unless He has done the Father's will, and fulfilled all the things for which He had been embodied, and reconciled the world to God. He is said to proceed from the mouth, and from the womb and belly: not that God has these members, but that we may learn the nature of the Lord through our own words.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.12":["For you shall go out in gladness, and be led forth in peace. The mountains and the hills shall sing praise before you, and all the trees of the country shall clap their hands. Instead of the saliunca the fir shall come up, and instead of the nettle the myrtle shall grow. And the Lord shall be named for an everlasting sign, which shall not be taken away. LXX: In gladness you shall go out, and in joy you shall be led forth. The mountains and the hills shall leap, awaiting you with joy, and all the trees of the field shall applaud with their branches. And instead of the στοιβὴ (that is, the most worthless stalks) the cypress shall come up: and instead of the conyza the myrtle shall come up. And the Lord shall be for a name and for an everlasting sign, and it shall not fail. My word, he says, shall not return empty, but after it has fulfilled all that I willed, and done my will on earth, then it will come to me; and that will be fulfilled which is written: The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies the footstool of your feet (Ps 109 [110]:1, 2). For you shall go out in gladness from the blood of idolatry, and you shall be led forth in peace, so that you may hear from the Apostle: Grace to you and peace (Rom 1:7). Or, you shall be led forth in joy, so that after the shadow of the Law you may learn the truth of the Gospel. For the mountains and hills, by which we can understand the Angels and the souls of the Saints, which are called mountains and hills according to the variety of their virtues, will rejoice over those who repent, and will show the gladness of their mind by leaping. This the Lord also says in the Gospel: The Angels in heaven will rejoice over one sinner who does penance (Luke 15:7). All the trees of the field also shall clap with the hand, or with their branches: those which are planted beside the running waters, which shall give their fruit in its season, and their leaf shall not fall off (Ps 1). One such tree spoke in the psalm: But I am like a fruitful olive tree in the house of the Lord (Ps 51 [52]:10). Let us ask those who follow the simple history alone and eat the flesh of the lamb boiled whether trees clap with their branches and applaud with the hand, and in what sense that which is said of the rivers is to be taken: The rivers shall clap with the hand (Ps 97 [98]:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.55.13":["And not only shall the mountains and hills leap and sing, and all the trees of the field which the Lord has blessed clap with their branches and applaud with their hands, but the στοιβή too and the κονύζη, or the saliunca and the nettle, shall be turned into the fir and the myrtle and the cypress. Στοιβή, according to Symmachus and the Seventy, is written in the Hebrew NESUS (נעצוץ), which Aquila and Theodotion interpreted as κονύζην. Now κονύζη is a most worthless and bitter herb, of a very foul smell. And where the Seventy translated κονύζη, the word which in the Hebrew is SARPHOD (סרפד), Symmachus rendered nettle. However the proper sense of the names stands, this is to be said: that evil things are turned into good, and virtues spring up in place of vices, that is, righteousness in place of iniquity, fortitude in place of rashness, temperance in place of luxury, prudence in place of folly. Let us give examples of our forebears: Matthew and Zacchaeus and the publicans were saliunca, and στοιβή, and useless stalks, and κονύζη of bitter taste and most loathsome smell, saying: My scars have rotted and are corrupted, because of my folly (Ps 37 [38]:6). These, suddenly changed into Apostles, became cypress and fir and myrtle, of the best fragrance and needful for various works. Paul too, the persecutor of the Church, when he heard from the Lord, Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me? It is hard to kick against the goad (Acts 9:4, 5), was a nettle, having the stings of persecution. But when he preached the Gospel in the whole world and could say, We are the good odor of Christ (2 Cor 2:15), he is rightly called a cypress and a myrtle. Harlots and publicans go before the Pharisees into the kingdom of God, and the thief passes from the cross into paradise (Matt 21). Therefore what is said in the Gospel, A good tree cannot bear evil fruits (Luke 6:43; Matt 7:18), by no means refers to a property of nature, as the heretics would have it, but to the choice of the mind. Finally it is added: Either make the tree good, and its fruits good. From this it is clear that each one by his own will makes his soul a good or an evil tree, whose fruits vary. There follows: And the Lord shall be for a name and for an everlasting sign, which shall not fail. To those who have been changed from evil into good the Lord will be for a name and for an eternal sign, so that from His name they are called Christians, and are branded with the brand of His cross. Of this sign Simeon spoke, holding the little child in his arms: This one shall be for the fall and for the rising again of many, and for a sign which shall be spoken against (Luke 2:34); and of it was said above: The Lord shall give you a sign (Isa 7:14); and the holy man sings in the psalm: Make with me, O Lord, a sign for good (Ps 85 [86]:17). And He Himself who is the sign says: When you see, He says, the sign of the Son of Man: a sign which shall not fail, nor be changed by any end, but shall pass from the present way of life into the future.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.1":["Thus says the Lord: Keep judgment, and do righteousness: for my salvation is near to come, and my righteousness to be revealed. For righteousness the Seventy translated mercy; the rest alike. The prophecy about the nations being ended, which at the coming of the word of God are to be changed from saliunca and nettle into cypress and myrtle, Isaiah speaks to the hearers of that time, that they should do all things that are right and prepare themselves for the coming of the Savior, because He Himself is the righteousness and mercy of God. For if the thoughts of the Saints are judgments, and we ought to have senses trained for discerning good and evil, why should we not at all times keep judgment, so as not to despise the person of the poor man in judgment, nor be frightened by the power of the rich, but judge the great as we judge the small, knowing, according to Moses, that judgment belongs to the Lord, who judges those who judge (Deut 1)? In accordance with which we also read in the psalm: God has stood in the synagogue of gods: and in the midst He judges the gods (Ps 81 [82]:1). To what is now said, Keep judgment, and do righteousness, this is like: Blessed are they who keep judgment and do righteousness at all times (Ps 105 [106]:3), so that they may pursue justly what is just. And yet under the name of righteousness the whole field seems to me to be signified: that he who has done one righteousness is said to have fulfilled all the virtues, which follow one another and cling to each other, so that he who has one has all, and he who lacks one lacks all. Something of the kind the fourteenth psalm also sounds: He who walks without blemish and works righteousness (Ps 14 [15]:2). And elsewhere it is written: Learn righteousness, you who dwell upon the earth (Isa 26:9). And that the Savior, who has been made for us righteousness and holiness and redemption, is Himself the mercy of God (1 Cor 1), the words of the saints testify: And God sent His mercy and His truth (Ps 56 [57]:4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.2":["Blessed is the man who does this, and the son of man who lays hold of it: keeping the sabbath, so as not to profane it: keeping his hands, so as not to do any evil. LXX: Blessed is the man who shall do these things, and the man who holds them fast, and keeps the sabbaths, so as not to profane them, and guards his hands, so as not to do iniquity. He who can say with the apostle Paul: When I was a little child, I spoke as a little child; I understood as a little child, I thought as a little child; when I became a man, I destroyed the things that belong to a little child (1 Cor 13:11): he obtains the present blessedness, forgetting the things that are past and stretching himself forward to what is to come, until he attains to the unity of the faith and the knowledge of the Son of God, to the perfect man, to the measure of the age of the fullness of Christ; so that that psalm can be fitted to him: Blessed is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the ungodly (Ps 1:1). This man, then, and son of the inner man, of whom it is said more than once in Leviticus, Man, man (Lev 17:8), is blessed in this, that he first does, and then lays hold of, these things: namely judgment and righteousness and the salvation of the Lord, which is near and is to be revealed to all the nations; so that he not only does what is commanded but holds it with a tight hand; and keeps the sabbath, so as not to profane it. And what the sabbath is which he has commanded to be kept the following verse shows: Keeping his hands, so as not to do any evil. For it profits nothing to sit still on the sabbath, or to sleep, and to gape after feasts; rather, it profits if one, doing good, rests from evil, and keeps a continual sabbath, that is, a rest, from iniquity, and does only those things that pertain to the salvation of the soul, and does not set his hand to any servile work. For he who commits sin is the slave of sin (John 8). But we have been called into freedom, with which freedom Christ has endowed us (Gal 4), so that we may by no means work for the food that perishes; but, cleaving to the Lord, let us say with the Prophet: But for me it is good to cleave to God (Ps 72 [73]:28). Let us become one spirit with Him, and let us fulfill the dedicated sabbaths, lest we be of the six days in which the world was made, to which the Apostles did not belong, to whom the Lord said: If you were of this world, the world would love what is its own. But now you are not of this world, because I have chosen you, and therefore the world hates you (John 15:19).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.3":["And let not the son of the stranger who cleaves to the Lord say: The Lord will divide me from His people with a separation: and let not the eunuch say: Behold, I am a dry tree. LXX: Let not the foreigner who has been joined to the Lord say: Do you think the Lord will separate me from His people? and let not the eunuch say: For I am a dry tree. Those who understand this passage in a lowly way refer what is said to proselytes from the nations and to eunuchs in the literal sense: that both strangers, if they have taken up the law of the Lord and are circumcised, and eunuchs, such as was that eunuch of Queen Candace (Acts 8), who could not be idle even on a journey, and, while he sought an interpreter of his reading, found Christ whom he was seeking, are not strangers to the salvation of God. And this, they say, is said against the Jews, who boast of the nobility of their race, and say that they are sons of Abraham, and think those blessed who have seed in Zion and kinsfolk in Jerusalem (John 8). But we understand that the same people whom we interpreted above (above, on Isa 55) through the saliunca and the nettle and the κονύζην and the στοιβήν as turned into the fir and the cypress and the myrtle are now summoned to the faith of the Gospel: that they ought not to despair if they cleave to the Lord, nor think themselves separated from the people of God. For all who have been baptized in Christ have put on Christ (Gal 3:27). There is neither Jew nor Gentile, Circumcision nor Uncircumcision; to whom it is commanded in the Song of Deuteronomy: Rejoice, you nations, with His people, that is, of God, who had as His former people the Jews (Deut 32:43). These, coming from the East and the West, will rest in the bosom of Abraham. This is what John the Baptist also said: And do not say, We have Abraham as our father. For I say to you: God is able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham (Matt 3:9). And as among the proselytes there is no difference, but both men and women are called equally to salvation, so too among the eunuchs who have castrated themselves for the kingdom of heaven both sexes are included: those who have put to death their members which are upon the earth, fornication, uncleanness, passion, evil desire, until all attain to the perfect man and say with the Apostle: We know no one according to the flesh. And if we have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know Him no longer (2 Cor 5:16). Therefore by eunuchs are by no means to be understood those whom the fiery poet describes, saying (Lucan, Book 10): And the unhappy youth too, maimed by the steel, and cut away from manhood........ But those of whom the Lord speaks in the Gospel: Who have castrated themselves for the kingdom of heaven (Matt 19:12); such were the Apostles too, to whom, when they wondered and said because of the difficulty of the thing, Who then can be saved?, the Savior answered: He who can take it, let him take it. Hence the Apostle too testifies that concerning eunuchs of this kind, that is, concerning virgins, he has no commandment of the Lord, but gives counsel as one who has obtained mercy from the Lord, wishing all to be as himself: For the time, he says, is short: it remains that those who have wives be as though they had none (1 Cor 7:29). For he who has been called free from the bondage and duty of marriage is truly the slave of Christ.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.4-5":["For thus says the Lord to the eunuchs: If they keep my sabbaths, and choose the things I have willed, and hold fast my covenant, I will give them in my house and within my walls a place, and a name better than sons and daughters: an everlasting name I will give them, which shall not perish. LXX: Thus says the Lord to the eunuchs: Those who keep my sabbaths, and choose the things that I will, and hold fast my testament, I will give them in my house and within my wall a renowned place, better than sons and daughters: an everlasting name I will give them, and it shall not fail. He had set forth two, proselytes and eunuchs. He speaks first of the eunuchs, and gives them precepts for living, and promises them the rewards to come; and after them he comes to the proselytes, promising them also like things. He speaks, then, to the eunuchs, who in despair said, Behold, I am a withered tree (Exod 23), that they should not think themselves subject to the curse spoken to the barren: Cursed is the barren one who does not raise up seed in Israel (Deut 7). If, he says, they keep my sabbaths, and choose out of my precepts those things which I have willed, and not those which I have conceded because of the weakness of the hearers, and hold fast my covenant or testament with all fidelity, I will give them in my house, and in my Temple, and within the walls of my most strongly fortified city, a place, and a name better than sons and daughters, which shall be blotted out by no forgetfulness. Who the eunuchs are we have said above: those who are anxious for the things that are God's; to whom the Wisdom also speaks which is inscribed with the title of Solomon: Blessed is the barren woman who is undefiled, who has not known the bed in transgression; she shall have fruit in the visitation of souls. And the eunuch who has not wrought iniquity with his hand, nor devised evil things against the Lord: for the chosen grace of his faith shall be given him, and a most delightful portion in the temple of the Lord (Wis 3:13, 14). This barren woman is fruitful in virginity; this eunuch does violence to the kingdom of heaven and takes it by force. He is a keeper of the sabbaths, so as never to do the works of marriage (1 Cor 7). He has chosen what the Lord willed, so as to offer more than is commanded: so as to consider not the indulgence of His Apostle but his will. He holds fast the everlasting covenant of the Lord, so as not to be free for prayer for a time and then return again to the same thing, but he knows that he will receive in the house of the Lord, which is His Church, the best place. For there are many mansions with the Father (John 14). And he who has been a eunuch and has done all the things that are written will have the best place within His walls, namely, so as to become a tower of the Lord and to stand in the priestly rank, and instead of sons of the flesh to have many spiritual sons. That such a eunuch was the Evangelist John, whom Jesus loved most, the histories of the Church relate: he who reclined on the breast of Jesus (John 13); who, while Peter walked more slowly, ran to the Lord borne up on the wings of virginity (John 20); who, plunging himself into the secrets of the divine nativity, dared to say what all the ages had not known: In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God: He was in the beginning with God (John 1:2). Away, then, with all the cavilling of the Jews, and let it cease to make itself ridiculous at the opening of the kingdom of heaven to half-men, since chastity lies not in the weakness of the body but in the will of the mind.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.6":["And the sons of the stranger who cleave to the Lord, to worship Him and to love His name, so as to be His servants: everyone who keeps the sabbath so as not to profane it, and holds fast my covenant, I will bring them to my holy mountain, and I will make them joyful in my house of prayer. LXX: And to the foreigners who have been added to the Lord to serve Him, and to love His name, so as to be His servants ÷ and handmaids ⁚ and all who keep my sabbaths so as not to violate them, and hold fast my testament, I will bring them into my holy mountain, and I will make them joyful in my house of prayer. After the eunuchs he passes to the foreigners whom he had named before, and to them also he promises rewards: that if they serve Him, and pass from servitude to the love of His name, they shall be His servants; such as was the Apostle Paul too, who writes at the beginning of his Epistles: Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ (Rom 1:1); and Moses, the servant of God (Heb 3). And that and handmaids has been added in the Seventy we have marked beforehand with an obelus. For it cannot be that in spiritual gifts there should be any difference of sex, since in Christ Jesus there is neither male nor female, but in Him we are all one (John 17). Of the sabbath and the everlasting covenant we have spoken above.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.7":["Whoever, then, does these things, God will bring him to His holy mountain and fill him with gladness in His house of prayer. The holy mountain is either the doctrines of truth and the confession of the Trinity, or the Lord Himself, to whom in the last time (according to this same Isaiah and the prophet Micah) very many nations shall flow together (Isa 2; Mic 4). And the house of prayer is the Church, which is spread throughout the whole world, and not the Temple of the Jews, which was confined within the very narrow bounds of the land of Judea.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Their holocausts and their victims shall please me upon my altar: for my house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples, says the Lord God, who gathers the scattered of Israel: I will yet gather to him his gathered ones. All you beasts of the field, come to devour, all you beasts of the forest. LXX: Their holocausts and victims shall be acceptable upon my altar. For my house shall be called a house of prayer for all nations, says the Lord, who gathers the scattered of Israel: for I will gather to him a congregation. All you wild beasts, come, eat, all you beasts of the forest. The difference between sacrifices and holocausts we learn more fully in Leviticus. Holocausts are those which are burned whole upon the altar; victims and sacrifices are those of which part is offered to the altar and part is given to the priests. It is not to be believed, then, that the sacrifices of the foreigners and the eunuchs are required according to the Jewish rite. But we ought to know this: that the eunuchs offer holocausts, since they consecrate themselves wholly to God; and the foreigners victims, since they are free for prayer for a time; of which God says: The sacrifice of praise has glorified me; and in another place: Offer to God the sacrifice of praise (Ps 49 [50]:14). Of these victims and holocausts the Lord speaks through Hosea: I desire mercy and not sacrifice: the knowledge of God more than holocausts, which are offered upon the altar of the Lord (Hos 6:6); the altar which John in the Apocalypse testifies that he saw in heaven, under which were the souls of the martyrs (Rev 6); from which one of the Seraphim, grasping a coal with tongs, brought it to purify the lips of Isaiah (Isa 6:6). This is the altar, and this the tabernacle, to whose likeness all the things made in Exodus are referred, by which spiritual victims have been offered to God (Exod 38). Otherwise, concerning bodily sacrifices and holocausts, the same God testifies above: To what purpose do I want the multitude of your victims? says the Lord. I am full of the holocaust of rams, and of the fat of lambs, and the blood of bulls and goats I do not want. For my house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples. Not for the one nation of Judea, nor in the one place of Jerusalem and its city, but in the whole world; by no means of bulls and goats and rams, but of prayer. This testimony the Lord made use of when He spoke against those who, under the name of the Holy Spirit, sold the gifts of doves in the Temple, and sat in the chairs of pestilence, and had the tables of the money-changers (Matt 21 and John 2), doing all things for the sake of shameful gain, not knowing what is written: Freely you have received, freely give (Matt 10:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.8":["These things the Lord promised would be, He who gathers the scattered of Israel; to whom the Apostle Peter also writes an Epistle; of whom the Evangelist says: This he did not say of himself, but, being high priest that year, he prophesied that Jesus was to die for the nation, and not for the nation only, but that He might gather into one the sons of God who were scattered (John 11:51, 52); and that might be fulfilled which was said: The congregation of the peoples shall surround you: for its sake return on high (Ps 7:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.9":["And when the remnant of Israel had been gathered through the Apostles, and those who before had been scattered had been brought into one flock, all the beasts are summoned to come and devour Israel, namely those who would not believe, of whom the Apostle says: For the end shall come upon them (Phil 3). And the Lord in the Gospel: But when you see Jerusalem surrounded by an army, then know that its consummation has come (Luke 21:20). Or this is to be said: that they were handed over to be devoured by the beasts which the Prophet abhors, saying: Do not deliver to the beasts the souls that confess to you (Ps 73 [74]:19). Of these beasts, tropologically, which devoured a certain portion of the Lord, we have read above also (cf. Isa 9): Syria from the rising of the sun, and the Greeks from the West, who devour Israel with their whole mouth. And in Jeremiah: Israel is a wandering sheep, the lions have driven it out: first the king of Assyria devoured it, and last the king of Babylon shall break its bones (Jer 50:17). This too can be said: that together with the people of Israel the nations, formerly ravening, are summoned to faith, so that, leaving the forests which slew very many of Absalom's army, they may come to the Church, and eat the heavenly bread, and be filled with the flesh of the Lamb.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.10-11":["Nor were they content with this end, they who slept in guarding the Lord's flock, and could not bark, and loved their couch, by which the pleasure of the body is indicated; but among their own, shameless of soul, they have not known fullness, they who are never sated with their error; who devour the people of the Lord as a meal of bread, and the houses of widows, and serve gluttony and lust. For they cannot say: The commandment of the Lord is bright, enlightening the eyes (Ps 18 [19]:9), nor know what is written: A man's wisdom enlightens his face (Prov 17:24). And they are dumb dogs against the enemies, of whom it is written: Do not give what is holy to the dogs (Matt 7:6). And sleeping dogs, who do not know the precept: Do not give sleep to your eyes, nor slumber to your eyelids (Ps 131 [132]); and again: Watch, for you do not know at what hour your Lord will come (Matt 24:42). And therefore, giving place to the devil, they are most shameless dogs, which are never sated. For they vomit what they have eaten, and return to their vomit (Prov 26). Of them the Apostle Peter also speaks: There has happened to them that true proverb: The dog returned to his vomit, and the sow that was washed to her wallowing in the mire (2 Pet 2:22).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.10-12":["If this is said of the princes of the Jews, because of whom the people of the Lord was devoured by the beasts, let us avoid the examples of the worse; let us not be drunk with wine, in which is luxury, nor be overwhelmed by pleasures; let us not be dumb in speaking and ravening dogs against our adversaries; but let us follow the ways of God rather than our own, and hear the Scripture warning us: Think of the Lord in goodness (Wis 1:1). And again: Understanding is best for those who practice it. And if we have been dogs, let us not despair of salvation, hearing the words of the Canaanite woman saying to the Lord: Yes, Lord, for the little dogs also eat of the crumbs that fall from their masters' table (Matt 15:27). For this reason she obtained mercy, and heard from the Lord: O woman, great is your faith: be it done to you as you will. For she could say with the Prophet: I have run the way of your commandments (Ps 118 [119]:32). And again: Lead me in the right way.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.10":["His watchmen are all blind, they have all not known: dumb dogs, not able to bark, seeing vain things, sleeping, and loving dreams. And the most shameless dogs have not known fullness: the shepherds themselves have not known understanding: all have turned aside into their own way, each to his own avarice, from the highest to the last. Come, let us take wine, and be filled with drunkenness, and it shall be as today, so also tomorrow, and much more. LXX: See how they have all been blinded: they have all not known, dumb dogs, they will not be able to bark: dreaming of a bed, loving slumbers. And dogs shameless of soul have not known fullness. And they are evil, not knowing understanding: they have all followed their own ways. Each one alike from its summit. Come and let us take wine, and be drunk, and let tomorrow be such a day, great, exceedingly more ⁚. So that all the beasts of the forest may come and devour those who have been gathered, he says, or so that the throngs of the nations may succeed into the place of the Jews, their watchmen are the cause, namely the Scribes and the Pharisees: because they were all blind and did not know the Lord the Savior, nor would they see the present light. To them the Lord said: Fools and blind, which is greater, the gold, or the altar that sanctifies the gold (Matt 23:17)? and again: Blind guides, straining out a gnat and swallowing a camel (ibid., 24); and: The blind leading the blind, both fall into the pit (Matt 15:14). And that teachers are called watchmen we have read above also. For in the place where, according to the Seventy, it is said: The voice of your guardians has been lifted up (Isa 52:8), in the Hebrew is written: The voice of your watchmen. Of these was Ezekiel, to whom God says: Son of man, I have made you a watchman for the house of Israel (Ezek 3:17). But those watchmen were not blind; indeed, they were called seers. But these of whom the Prophet now speaks are called not only blind watchmen but also dumb dogs, not able to bark. For those who ought to have guarded the Lord's flock, and driven off the wolves, and barked for the Lord, love dreams and delight in demonic visions. And the sense is: they cannot speak the truth, but all lies.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.11-12":["And what follows: Each to his own avarice, from the highest to the last: come, let us take wine, and be filled with drunkenness, and it shall be as today, so also tomorrow, and much more, is not found in the Seventy Translators, but has been added from the Hebrew and is marked beforehand with shining stars. In short, none of the Church's interpreters has expounded these little verses, but they leap over them and pass them by, as if a pit lay open in the middle of the road. And what he says is this: since, turning aside from the way of the Lord, they have followed their own paths, therefore they burn with avarice from the highest to the last, from the princes to the common people, so that, after they have begun to beat their fellow servants, and to eat and drink with the drunken, they say: Come, let us take wine, and be filled with drunkenness. Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die. And it shall be as today, so also tomorrow, and much more (1 Cor 15:32). This is what he said above: They have not known fullness: they are not sated with past pleasure, but prepare future delights, much greater than those they had enjoyed before.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.56.11":["That these watchmen and dogs are the same as the shepherds the following words show: The shepherds themselves have not known understanding. For the Hebrew word ROIM (רעים), which is written with four letters, res and ain and jod and mem, if it is read roim, means shepherds; if raim, the worst. We have said this to show the causes of the varying interpretation. For all have sought their own things, and not the things that are God's, nor have they walked in the way of the Lord (Phil 2), of which it is written: Seek which is the good way, and walk in it (Jer 6:16). But according to what is read in the volume of Judges: There was no king in Israel; each one did what seemed right to himself (Judg 17:6), and followed his own errors.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.1-2":["The Jews think that both this and the rest that follow are to be understood either generally of all the righteous, whose blood Manasseh shed, and filled Jerusalem from gate to gate; or else that Isaiah prophesies of his own death, because he was to be sawn by Manasseh with a wooden saw, which is a most certain tradition among them. Hence most of our own writers also refer what is set down in the Epistle to the Hebrews concerning the passion of the Saints, They were sawn asunder (Heb 11:37), to the passion of Isaiah.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.1":["The righteous man has perished, and there is none who reflects on it in his heart: and men of mercy are gathered, for there is none who understands: for the righteous man has been gathered away from the face of malice. Let peace come: let him rest in his bed, who walks in his uprightness. LXX: See how the righteous man has perished, and no one takes it to heart: and righteous men are taken away, and no one considers. For the righteous man has been taken away from the face of iniquity: his burial shall be in peace: it has been taken away from the midst. Because of the blind watchmen and the dumb dogs, who are themselves the shepherds, not knowing understanding, who are not sated with present pleasures but are always preparing themselves for future ones, the righteous man has perished, of whom Pilate's wife says: Have nothing to do with that righteous man (Matt 27:19). And Pilate, having washed his hands: I am innocent, he says, of the blood of this righteous man. Herein it is to be considered that the word of perdition, about which the heretics often raise a cavil, that it signifies destruction and abolition forever, is applied to Christ, whose perdition certainly shows the greatness of the persecution, and not an end of substance. And there is no one, he says, who reflects on it in his heart, or lays it up. For it could not be that the blind and dumb, seeing vain things and loving dreams, and ignorant of understanding and wisdom, should think of the things that are God's. And what follows, And men of mercy, or righteous men, are gathered and taken away, signifies the Apostles, who are slain by the ungodly and gathered by the Lord. And he gives the reasons why the righteous man was slain and taken away, saying: For the righteous man has been gathered away from the face of malice, so that he might not see the evils of the world; or, because of the malice of men, whose sins He Himself bore, He ascended as victor to the Father.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.2":["And as for what is said according to the Hebrew, Let peace come, let him rest in his bed: let him walk in his uprightness, the sense indeed is clear, but the sequence of the words, which stands in the idiom of its own language, seems disordered in ours. And what he says is this: Let the peace of the Righteous One come, which, ascending to the Father, He left to the Apostles, saying: My peace I give to you, my peace I leave to you (John 14:27). And when the peace of Christ, which surpasses all understanding, has come, His Apostles will rest in their beds, and their death will rest. By this he shows that the martyrs do not perish but conquer, and rest in an eternal seat. But He Himself whose peace it is, and whose Apostles rest in their beds, walks in His uprightness, ascending as victor to the Father by a straight road. Further, what is read in the Seventy, The righteous man has been taken away from the face of malice, his burial shall be in peace, it has been taken away from the midst, is all referred to Christ, without mingling in the person of the Apostles: His burial is in peace, and has been taken away from the midst. For His flesh did not see corruption, nor did He remain in the sepulcher, He who is free among the dead, as the Angel says to the women: Jesus whom you seek is not here: Come and see the place where the Lord was laid (Matt 28:5, 6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.3":["But draw near hither, you sons of the sorceress, seed of the adulterer and of the harlot. Against whom have you made sport? Against whom have you opened wide the mouth and thrust out the tongue? LXX: But draw near hither, you lawless sons, seed of adulterers and of a harlot. In what have you made sport? Against whom have you opened your mouth, and against whom have you thrust out your tongue? The righteous man having been taken away, whose burial is in peace, or rather having been taken out of the midst, you who are sons of iniquity, draw near to me and hear what I shall say (John 8). For just as he who commits iniquity is a slave of iniquity, so he who, like Judas the traitor, is a son of perdition, can be called a son of iniquity. Indeed it is written of the Lord the Savior: And the son of iniquity shall not proceed to afflict him (Ps 88 [89]:23). For of iniquity, or of the lawless, which the Seventy translated, Theodotion set down the Hebrew word itself, ONENA (עננה), which we, following Symmachus, have rendered of the sorceress, because, that is, Jerusalem, who is the mother of those who hear, was always given over to idolatry. Therefore he calls them the seed of the adulterer, or of adulterers (of whom it is said: And they committed adultery with wood and stone [Jer 3:9]), and of a harlot; and without doubt he means the same woman of whom we read above: How has the faithful city Zion become a harlot? (Isa 1:21).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.4-5":["Are you not wicked sons, a lying seed, you who take comfort in gods under every leafy tree, sacrificing little children in the torrents under the overhanging rocks? LXX: Are you not sons of perdition, a lawless seed, you who call upon idols under leafy trees, sacrificing your sons in the valleys in the midst of the rock? You, he says, have done these things which the preceding discourse has related. You who are wicked sons, or sons of perdition, such as was Judas the traitor, who was called the son of perdition (John 17); and a lawless seed, or a lying seed and a seed of falsehood, who delight in gods under every leafy tree, and sacrifice your sons in the torrents (Acts 8). Of this the history of Kings and of Chronicles also tells, that they sacrificed their sons to gods and consecrated them in the fire (2 Kgs 16 and 17). That Ahab king of Israel and Manasseh king of Judah did this is not in doubt; they went on from the murder of their children to the blood of the Prophets. Of this also it says in Hosea: Sacrifice men, he says, for the calves have failed (Hos 13:2); or, as it is written in the Hebrew: Sacrificing men, you adore calves. And in the psalms it is written more fully: They were mingled among the nations and learned their works, and served their graven images, and it became a stumbling block to them. And they sacrificed their sons and their daughters to demons, and they shed innocent blood, or harmless: the blood of their sons and of their daughters, and they sacrificed to the graven images of Canaan. And the land was stained with bloods, and was polluted in their works (Ps 105 [106]:35ff.). Since, then, the history is plain, that the slayers of Christ are the sons of those who did these things, a question arises against those who will have it that there are different natures, one lost and evil, which cannot be saved, and another good, which cannot perish: how are sons called sons of perdition? For if, as they think, the sons of perdition are of a most evil nature, how was that found which had first been lost? Indeed in the parables of the penitent, both the sheep lost out of a hundred sheep and the drachma that had perished out of ten are recovered, and the lost son is found, of whom the father had said to the elder son: This your brother had perished, and is found; he was dead, and has come to life again (Luke 15:32). For nothing ever perishes unless it was first safe, and nothing ever dies unless it had lived before. Therefore these who are now called sons of perdition, or of iniquity and wickedness, forsook the Lord by their own fault, and from sons of the Lord began to be sons of perdition, as this same Prophet says to them: You have forsaken the Lord and provoked the Holy One of Israel to anger (Isa 1:4). We can also take these things, according to tropology, of the heretics, who are sons of perdition and a most evil seed, or a seed of falsehood. For they are liars from the beginning, like the devil, who is its father, who is the father of every lie. These call those whom they have deceived to idols, or to the images of their dogmas, under leafy and shady trees, promising them pleasures and delights, or concealing their impurities. Hence Adam too, after he had sinned, hid himself in paradise under a tree, so as not to be open to the sight of God (Gen 3). Nor is there any doubt that sons of perdition of this kind, and a lawless seed, have very many sons, whomever they have deceived, and that they slay them in deep valleys and in the abyss of impiety under overhanging rocks, which always threaten to fall; and these, because of the diversity of falsehood and the variety of perverse dogmas, are called rocks in the plural. But we have one rock, which always follows the people of God, from which the people of Israel once drank, when it enjoyed the familiar friendship of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.4":["Against whom, he says, have you made sport, spitting in his face and plucking his beard; and against whom have you opened wide and opened your mouth, and thrust out your tongue, saying to him: You are a Samaritan and have a demon (John 8:48)? And again: This man does not cast out demons except by Beelzebub, the prince of demons (Matt 12:24). And afterward, at the passion: Crucify, crucify such a one (Luke 23:21). And again: His blood be on us and on our children (Matt 27:25). And elsewhere: Ah, you who destroy the Temple and in three days rebuild it! He saved others; himself he cannot save. Let him come down now from the cross, and we believe him (ibid. 40).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.6":["In the parts of the torrent is your portion; this is your lot, and to them you have poured out a libation, you have offered a sacrifice. Shall I not be angry over these things? LXX: This is your portion, this is your lot; and to them you have poured out libations, and to these you have prepared victims. Shall I not then be angry over these things? The things said fit the times of Isaiah. For all the mountains, valleys and torrents were full of the worship of demons, of which Jeremiah speaks: that the fathers and sons gather wood and kindle the fire, to make chavonas for the host of heaven, while the women knead and mix fat with fine flour, in order to provoke the Lord to anger (Jer 7). And no one doubts that he means cakes, or little pastries prepared by the hand of an artisan; for that is what chavonas means in our tongue. Of these Moses, speaking in the person of the Lord with a prophetic spirit, sang beforehand in the Canticle of Deuteronomy: They provoked me with strange gods, and with their abominations they stirred me to anger; they sacrificed to demons and not to God (Deut 32:16, 17); and this they did by their own will, because it lies in our choice to choose good or evil. Thus to those who have offered themselves to God by virtues it is said: He chose for us his inheritance, the beauty of Jacob, which he loved (Ps 46 [47]:5). And in another place: The Lord's portion was made his people Jacob, Israel the cord of his inheritance (Deut 32:9). And in the Acts of the Apostles it is related that very many gave themselves to the portion and lot of Paul and Silas. Those, then, who have been the portion and lot of the Lord, like the Levites, will have the Lord as their portion and will be able to say with David: The Lord is my portion (Ps 72 [73]:26). These things we can also take of the person of the heretics. For they, having abandoned the worship of God, venerate the images of their errors, and offer victims to them and pour out libations, doing in secret things that it is shameful even to speak of, and leading captive silly women laden with sins, who are led away by various desires, always learning and never arriving at the knowledge of the truth (2 Tim 3). Since, then, the impious do these things in both kinds of impiety, is it not just that the anger of God should be provoked against them?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.7-8":["Upon a high and lofty mountain you have set your bed, and there you went up to sacrifice victims. And behind the door and back of the post you have set your memorial. LXX likewise. That Israel sacrificed victims to the gods of the nations on high mountains and hills, and like a most shameless harlot spread her feet for all the demons, sacred history relates; and there was no place that was not defiled by the filth of idolatry, so much so that they set idols behind the doors of their houses, which they call the household Lares, and poured out the blood of their souls in public as much as in private. With this error and most evil custom of antiquity the cities of many provinces are afflicted; and Rome herself, the mistress of the world, venerates with wax tapers and lamps, in every tenement block and every house, the image of Tutela, whom they call by that name for the protection of their dwellings, so that, as they enter and as they leave their homes, they may be ever reminded of their inveterate error. The heretics too, whose heart is lifted up in pride, and who, despising the humility of the Church, promise themselves lofty things, go up the most lofty mountains of their dogmas, and there prostitute their bed to demons. And because, according to the Prophet, they have turned backward, imitating the wife of Lot (Gen 19), they are changed into a pillar of salt, having the appearance of the Church's seasoning and none of its savor at all; they are cast out of doors, and are good for nothing except to be trodden under the feet of all. Therefore the Lord warns in the Gospel that he who holds the handle of the plow should not look behind him (Luke 9).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.8-9":["According to the Seventy, in the things in which they disagree, the sense seems to me this: Did you think, harlot, that if you had departed from me and joined yourself to your lovers, you would find something more? To her God also speaks through Ezekiel: To all harlots wages are given, but you have given wages, and in you this perverse thing has been done (Ezek 16:33). For not only did you receive nothing from your lovers, but you even lost the things you had received from your husband. What follows, You have enlarged your bed, and you have made a covenant with them, is not found in the Seventy. This woman loved those who sleep with her, the blind watchmen and the dumb dogs, who in their sleep see dreams of vanity. And she multiplied her fornication, so as to have intercourse not once with one, but often and with very many. For this reason she made many depart far from her: the Angels who were her guardians, or the holy men who withdrew from the company of such a harlot. This we can also say of the heretics, who, having forsaken God, follow their own errors and think they have something more, when by following falsehoods they have lost the truth, and have multiplied their fornication, not in one dogma but in many, and have made the Angels who preside over the Churches depart from them; who, not content with fornication near at hand, send envoys beyond their borders to the dogmas of the gentiles and the errors of barbarous nations, in order to deceive their hearers with novelty of words.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.8":["For beside me you have uncovered yourself and received the adulterer; you have enlarged your bed, and you have made a covenant with them; you have loved their couch with open hand; and you adorned yourself for the king with ointment, and you have multiplied your perfumes. You sent your envoys far off, and you were humbled even to hell. LXX: You thought that if you had departed from me you would have something more. You have loved those who sleep with you, and you have multiplied your fornication with them, and you have made many to be far from you, and you have sent envoys beyond your borders, and you were humbled even to hell. Let us discuss the Hebrew first, which in this place differs much from the Seventy. To her of whom he had said above, Upon a high and lofty mountain you have set your bed, and whom he had rebuked as a harlot, prostituted at the entrance of houses and in dark places behind the doors, so that whoever wished to enter the dwelling might have the pleasure set out before his eyes: this same woman he now rebukes and refutes as an adulterous wife, because, while sleeping with her husband, she secretly received the adulterer, and uncovered the coverlet, or rather enlarged her bed, and made a pact, as though drawing up the instruments of a dowry with the adulterers. He says this to show that they worshiped idols not only in the fields and in houses, but also set up the image of Baal in the Temple, which Ezekiel too says he saw when he had dug through the wall. You have loved, he says, their couch with open hand (Ezek 8), so that you did not blush at your sin, nor desire to hide the shame of your fornication, but sinned with all freedom.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.9":["And you adorned yourself for the king with ointment, and you have multiplied your idols. And the sense is: You sought various ornaments, so as to entice the adulterers both by sight and by scent. What is said, you adorned yourself for the king with ointment, is understood in two ways: either for God the king, while you adorn yourself with the things that he had given and prostitute yourself to idols; or certainly for the king who is the idol of the Ammonites, who is called Moloch, which in our tongue means king, and whom according to the Seventy we read in other places as Melchom (Zeph 1). And you sent your envoys far off, so that you might have a zeal for fornication not only with the idols of the neighboring nations, but also with those of nations set far away. He means the gods of Babylon and of Egypt. And you were humbled, he says, even to hell: not with the humility that is praised, but with that by which Amnon had humbled his sister Tamar (2 Sam 13). And truly it is a great humiliation, or rather a hurling down even to hell, to be hurled from the light and summit of chastity into the darkness of the brothel, or rather into the abyss of lusts. To her it is said in another place: You shall be brought down even to hell.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.10":["In the multitude of your way you have labored, and you did not say: I will rest. You have found the life of your hand; therefore you have not asked me. LXX: In your many journeys you have labored, and you did not say: I will leave off. Strengthened, you have done these things; therefore you have not asked me. He who walks in the one royal way does not labor, the way about which God commanded through Moses: You shall walk in the royal way, and you shall not turn aside to the right or to the left (Deut 5:32). There is one way of truth, which says in the Gospel: I am the way, the truth and the life (John 14:6); and many ways of falsehood, in which Jerusalem is now accused of having walked. God, knowing the difference between these ways, speaks above to those who stray: Your ways are not as my ways (Isa 55:8); and they, confessing to the Lord, say: You have made our ways turn aside from your ways (Ps 43 [44]:19). Having come to know, then, the one royal way, let us see which are the ways to the right and to the left, by which we are forbidden to go. The royal way is the tempered one, having in itself neither more nor less. For example, the straight and royal way is prudence. We turn aside to the right if we are wiser than we ought to be wise, and love cunning instead of prudence; for the serpent was more prudent than all the beasts in paradise (Gen 3), and the sons of darkness are more prudent than the sons of light (Luke 16). And we turn off to the left when we are foolish and are less wise than is needful; of whom it is said: The fool has said in his heart, There is no God (Ps 13 [14]:1). Piety too and true religion is a royal way. He turns aside to the right who is superstitious, and deserves to hear: Be not overly righteous (Eccl 7:17); to the left, he who neglects the worship of God and is numbered among the kids and the he-goats. Liberality too and stewardship is a very great virtue, from which he turns aside to the right who is stingy and does not give what is needful, not only to others but not even to himself; to the left, he who devours his substance with harlots and says with Israel: Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die (Isa 22:13). Fortitude also and constancy is a royal way, from which he turns aside to the right who is rash and stubborn, to the left he who is fearful and timid. Hence the holy man, desiring to walk by the straight way, prays: Lead me, Lord, in the straight way (Ps 138 [139]:24). And in another place: Make known to me, Lord, the way in which I should walk, for to you I have lifted up my eyes (Ps 142 [143]:8). Of a way of this kind Scripture also speaks elsewhere: Seek the good way and walk in it, and you shall find purification for your souls (Jer 6:16). Jerusalem, then, was humbled even to hell for this reason, that she labored in many ways and did not say: I will amend my error by repentance; but on the contrary: I will be strengthened in what I have begun, and I will not heed the sentence of him who warns: Turn your foot away from the rough way, and your throat from thirst (Jer 2:25). Then it follows: But she answered, ἀνδρίσομαι, that is, I will act manfully, which is now said with another word: I will be strengthened. And because she has done the things that the preceding discourse related, therefore she has not entreated the Lord, having confidence in her own strength rather than in God. Further, what is set down according to the Hebrew, You have found the life of your hand; therefore you have not asked, has this sense and meaning: Because you have abounded in all things and overflowed with riches, therefore you have neglected the Lord. Such riches Solomon too refuses to have, lest he forget God (Prov 30 and 31). And to Sodom it is said through Ezekiel that because she was filled with bread, she therefore grew proud (Ezek 16). Yet not only riches but poverty too tests a man. Hence it is written above: I have tested you in the furnace of poverty (Isa 48), in which Lazarus too was tested, the poor man who endured want and infirmity together (Luke 16).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.11":["For whom have you been anxious and feared, that you have lied, and have not remembered me, nor thought of me in your heart? For I am silent and as one not seeing, and you have forgotten me. LXX: Whom have you feared and been terrified of, and you have lied to me, and have not remembered me? Nor have you set me in your mind and in your heart. And I, seeing you, will despise you, and you have not feared me. Because, with your barns full, you have confidence in riches and would not ask me, therefore you have feared a great many. For you could not say: The Lord is my helper, and I will look down on my enemies (Ps 117 [118]:7); and again: The Lord is my light and my savior; whom shall I fear? (Ps 26 [27]:1). And even if at some time you wished to say this against the conscience of your mind, you lied. For how could you call upon me, you who had no memory of me, and never thought on the precepts of him who says: Honor the Lord, and you shall be strengthened, and apart from him fear no one (Prov 7:1)? For I am he who, according to Symmachus and Aquila, always kept silent about your sins, and passed over with dissimulation whatever crimes you committed, as though I did not see them, so that you might return to me, if not out of fear of me, at least because of my patience; I who question the sons of men not with my eyes but with my eyelids. But you on the contrary have forgotten me, and have deserved to hear: You have forgotten the law of your God, and I will forget your sons (Hos 4:6). And again: They walked perversely against me, and I will walk against them in perverse fury. The mind, which in the Seventy is joined with the heart, is not written in the Hebrew, but has been added as a kind of interpretation of what ought to be understood by the heart in the Scriptures.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.12":["I will declare your righteousness, and your works shall not profit you. When you cry out, let those whom you have gathered deliver you. LXX: I will declare your righteousness and your evils, which shall not profit you. When you cry out, let them rescue you in your tribulation. I who always kept silent, and passed over your sins as though I did not see them, will by no means keep silent any longer, but will say what I already said before: I have kept silent; shall I always keep silent? And I will declare your righteousness and your works. This is to be read as irony, as if someone were to say to a man caught in crimes: See your good works!\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.13-14":["And the wind shall carry them all away, a breeze shall take them off. But he who has confidence in me shall inherit the land and shall possess my holy mountain. And I will say: Make a way, provide a road, turn aside from the path, take away the stumbling blocks from the way of my people. LXX: For the wind shall take all these away, and the tempest shall carry them off. But those who hope in me shall possess the land and shall obtain my holy mountain by inheritance, and they shall say: Make clean the ways before his face, and take away the stumbling blocks from the way of my people. The Jews assert that these things are prophesied of the Babylonians: that when these have been overcome by the power of God, the people return to Judea and possess Mount Zion, and all the hindrances of the journey are taken away, and they suffer the ambushes of no nation, because the Lord has made the way level for his people. And they contend that these things were fulfilled under Zerubbabel and Ezra.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.13":["Therefore, if ever a time of tribulation comes upon you, and you begin to stretch out your hands not to the idols you have worshiped but to heaven, and to implore the help of God, let those hear you and deliver you from danger whom you worshiped in your security. God speaks to the same woman through Jeremiah also: Where are your gods which you had made? Let them rise and save you in the time of your evils (Jer 2:28). Where the Seventy translated, Let them rescue you in your tribulation, and we have said, Let those whom you have gathered deliver you, Symmachus translated: Let your synagogues rescue you. He therefore speaks properly to the Jews, that the crowd of their synagogues will not be able to deliver them in the time of siege.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","But we, joining what is said to what goes before, assert this: that the wind and the tempest take away all the idols which could not deliver them, and they are like the dust which the wind drives from the face of the earth; but that those who have had confidence in the Lord will possess the land of which we shall read in this same Prophet: He will lift you up to the good things of the land. From this it is clear that this land is not below but above, to which the saint will climb as the Lord lifts him up, the land of which we have often spoken: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land (Matt 5:4); and I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living (Ps 26 [27]:13). The holy mountain of God is to be understood as that of which the writer of the Epistle to the Hebrews speaks: You have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and to many thousands of Angels, and to the Church of the firstborn who are written in the heavens (Heb 12:22). We can also understand the land and the mountain of the Lord which the saints are going to possess as the holy Scriptures, which the Lord threatens shall be taken away from the Jews, saying: The kingdom of God shall be taken away from you and given to a nation that produces its fruits (Matt 21:43), so that they may find in them that mountain of which we read in this same Prophet (Isa 2) and in Micah: In the last days the mountain of the Lord shall be manifest (Mic 4:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.14":["And the Lord himself commanded the Apostles and all the teachers of the Church to resolve by their interpretation whatever seemed harsh and difficult, and to provide a way for understanding, and to take all stumbling blocks out of the midst, so that the people of the Lord may understand what it reads without any hindrance and may advance in the fear of God. Of these ways John the Baptist too said: Prepare the ways of the Lord, etc. (Matt 3:3). Where we have put, in the person of God, and I will say, the Seventy put and they shall say: namely those who shall possess the land and obtain the holy mountain of God by inheritance. Nor will they be content with their own salvation, but will also call forth the other peoples to teaching. Symmachus interpreted this place thus: But he who trusts in me shall possess the land, and shall obtain my holy mountain by inheritance, and shall say: Make a straight way, and so on; because he who has possessed the land and the mountain himself instructs the others to prepare a way for the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.15":["For thus says the high and lofty one who inhabits eternity, and holy is his name: dwelling in the high and holy place, and with the contrite and humble spirit, to revive the spirit of the humble and to revive the heart of the contrite. LXX: Thus says the Most High, who dwells in the heights for ever, Holy among the holy is his name, the Most High resting among the holy, and giving patience to the fainthearted and life to those who are contrite of heart. The prophetic discourse had begun against the people of the Jews, whose teachers he called blind watchmen and dumb dogs and shepherds who know no understanding, and lawless sons, and the seed of adulterers and of a harlot. After this he came to Jerusalem, whom he reproaches for her fornication, because she enlarged her bed for her lovers, and received them with open hand, and adorned herself for all the idols, and sent envoys far off, so as to enjoy foreign lovers too. This rebuke finished, he comes to those who trust in the Lord, to whom he had promised the land and the mountain as a possession, and for whose return he had ordered the road to be prepared: doubtless to the Apostles, at whose preaching men of both peoples believed. Hence he begins with the praises of God, and in the following discourse bears witness to what he has promised concerning the return of his people. For thus says the Lord, high and lofty, who dwells in the heights, and Holy among the Holy, not by place but by the merit of those in whom he dwells, one of whom says in the psalm: I will exalt you, Lord, for you have upheld me (Ps 29 [30]:2); and again: Be exalted, Lord, you who judge the earth (Ps 93 [94]:2). Not that God is lifted up by human words, but, according to what is said elsewhere, The Lord has become my salvation (Ps 117 [118]:21), he becomes high even for the humble. He resists the proud and gives grace to the humble (Jas 4), so that he may lift them up and dwell in the heights. For the Lord is high, and looks upon the lowly (Ps 137 [138]:6). And in the Gospel the Angels cry out: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace (Luke 2:14). And again in the Psalms: Praise the Lord from the heavens; praise him in the heights (Ps 148:1). And not only does the high one dwell in the heights, but the holy one also among the holy, he who says in another place: Be holy, for I am holy (Lev 11:44). And just as wisdom does not enter a perverse soul, nor dwell in a body subject to sins (Wis 1), so the holy one dwells among the holy; and wherever there is filth, purity cannot dwell, as the saint says to the Lord: But you dwell in the holy place, the praise of Israel (Ps 21 [22]:4). Hence we too dare to say in prayer: Our Father, who are in heaven, hallowed be your name. And in the second psalm we read: He who dwells in the heavens shall laugh at them, and the Lord shall deride them (Ps 2:4). For Christ does not dwell in those who are earthly, but in those who are above the heavens, who tell the glory of the Lord, of whom it is said: May he hear him from his holy heaven (Ps 19 [20]:7). And again: The heaven of heaven is the Lord's (Ps 113 [115]:16). With this sense Moses too, the servant of God, agrees in like words: Behold, the heaven and the heaven of heaven belong to the Lord your God (Deut 10). We must certainly not take this in the Jewish manner, as though God, who is diffused everywhere and by whom all things are contained, were shut in any place and dwelt in heaven alone; rather, let us rightly understand the heights and the heavens as the Saints and the Powers. For he it is who dwells in heaven, of whom it is written in the Gospel: No one has ascended into heaven except he who descended from heaven, the Son of man who is in heaven (John 3:13). He rests among the holy and dwells in the humble and in those who are contrite in spirit, of whom it is said: A sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit; a contrite and humbled heart God does not despise (Ps 50 [51]:19). He gives patience to the fainthearted, so that those set in straits may endure tribulation in the hope of things to come. He gives life, namely to those who were dead through sin. Of him it is said: With you is the fountain of life (Ps 35 [36]:10). He it is who says in the Gospel: I am the way, the truth and the life (John 14:6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.16":["I will not contend for ever, nor will I be angry to the end; for the spirit shall go forth from my face, and I will make the breaths. LXX: I will not take vengeance on you for ever, nor will I always be angry with you. For the spirit shall go forth from me, and I have made every breath. The Lord who dwells in the heights and looks upon the lowly, whose name is holy, and who rests among the holy, who upholds the afflicted and grants life to the oppressed (Ps 112 [113]), has spoken thus: I will not always be angry, nor will I be indignant without end. I strike in order to amend; I kill in order to give life. For I have pity on my creature, and those whom I myself have made I will not allow to perish for ever. My spirit too, which shall go forth from me, or which, according to the Hebrew, Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion, surrounds all things (for this is what περιειληθήσεται and περιβαλλεῖ signify), is the quickener of all things. And the breath, or in the plural the breaths (for this is what NASAMOTH נשמות means), I have made, of which it is written elsewhere: Every breath, or everything that breathes, praises the Lord (Ps 150:6). Of this spirit and breath it is written at the beginning of Genesis: God breathed in the breath of life, and man became a living soul. Job says something of the kind: The spirit of the Lord which is in my nostrils, and the breath of the Almighty which teaches me (Job 27:3). Since, then, the spirit goes forth from me, and all things are quickened and live by my breath and inspiration, it is not just that those who are upheld by my breath and spirit should perish for ever. Some of our writers say that this is the spirit by which the whole world is breathed upon and governed, and through which all things have knowledge of God; the distinguished poet, writing of it after the Stoics, says (Virgil, Aeneid 6): In the beginning, the heaven and the earth and the watery plains, the shining globe of the moon and the Titanian stars, a spirit nourishes within; and mind, poured through the limbs, moves the whole mass and mingles itself with the great body; and so on. Others, however, understand the Holy Spirit, who in the beginning was borne over the waters and gave life to all things; who goes forth from the Father, and because of the fellowship of nature is sent by the Son, who says: It is expedient for you that I go. For if I do not go, the Paraclete will not come to you; but if I go, I will send him to you (John 16:7). Of him again he says: When the Paraclete comes, whom I will send you from the Father, the Spirit of truth, who goes forth from the Father (John 15:26). Let it scandalize no one if the Spirit is said to go forth from the Father, since the Son too said of himself: I went forth from the Father and have come; for I did not go forth of myself, but he sent me (John 16:28). Of this Spirit he spoke: I will ask the Father, and he will give you another Paraclete, to be with you always, the Spirit of truth (John 14:16). This is the Spirit of whom Moses also writes: Who will grant that all the people of the Lord be prophets, and that the Lord give his Spirit upon them? (Num 11:29). And Joel, in the person of God: I will pour out of my Spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy (Joel 2:28). And Zechariah: But receive my words and my ordinances, which I command by my Spirit to my servants the Prophets (Zech 1:6). And again: I will pour out upon Jerusalem the Spirit of grace and of mercy (Zech 12:10). And many other things from both Testaments, which it would take long to repeat now. This alone we say, that the same Prophet has spoken of this Spirit and breath together: Thus says the Lord God, who made the heaven; and a little after: Who gives breath to his people that is upon it, and spirit to those who tread on it (Isa 42:5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.17-21":["Some understand this place specifically of the Savior: that because of the sins of the world he was grieved for a little while, saying: My soul is sorrowful even unto death (Matt 26:38); and that the Father struck him, he who speaks through Zechariah: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered (Zech 13:7); and that he himself said of himself in the sixty-eighth psalm: For they persecuted him whom you struck (Ps 68 [69]:27); and that God turned his face away from him, so that for a little while he took the form of a servant and walked sad, mourning the sins of the people, lamenting and bewailing Jerusalem; and that the Father, who saw his ways, therefore healed him who was free among the dead, and his flesh did not see corruption, so that he who was struck on the cross might be healed in the resurrection. And I comforted him, he says, giving him the whole world in place of the one nation of the Jews. And those who mourn for him, understood, I comforted; and there is no doubt that he means the Apostles. And he fulfilled the fruit of his lips, he who had said to him: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession (Ps 2:8): peace upon peace, reconciling the world to God, so that he might call both peoples to himself and be healed by the Father. But the impious, of whom we have spoken above, he compares to the sea, which can never rest, but even in the greatest calm rolls with its waves breaking on the shores, and its end is mire and trampling.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.17":["Because of the iniquity of his covetousness I was angry, and I struck him; I hid, and I was indignant; and he walked mourning in the way of his heart. I have seen his ways, and I healed him; and I brought him back, and restored consolations to him and to those who mourn for him. I created the fruit of the lips, peace, peace to him who is far off and to him who is near, said the Lord, and I healed him. But the impious are like the raging sea which cannot rest, and its waves overflow into trampling and mire. There is no peace, said God, for the impious. LXX: Because of sin I grieved him for a little while, and I struck him; and I turned my face away from him, and he was grieved and walked sad in his ways. I have seen his ways, and I healed him; and I comforted him, and gave him true consolation, peace to those who are far off and to those who are near, says the Lord, and I will heal them. But those who are lawless shall be tossed like waves, and shall not be able to rest. There is no joy for the impious, says the Lord God. After the casting off of the people of the Jews and of those who refused to believe in the Savior, he had promised those who have confidence in him the land of the meek and the mountain of the saints, and had commanded the Apostles: Make a way for the returning people, and take all stumbling blocks out of the midst. And lest this seem incredible, he sets forth his power: that, being high and great, he can easily grant pardon to the penitent, and that it is just for the Creator to have pity on his creature. Because of this he adds that he was angry and grieved his people, and that he did so because they had sinned and had the covetousness of iniquity, and were not content with one iniquity, but always added sins to sins. And I struck, he says, him for a short while, in order to heal him, and I hid, understood, my face, so that he might long for me the more and say: Show your face, and we shall be saved (Ps 79 [80]:4). And I was indignant; and he, perceiving this, went about sad, saying: All the day I went about sad (Ps 37 [38]:7). And he walked mourning, or turned, in the way of his heart, so as to do penance not before the faces of men but in his heart.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.18":["Therefore, seeing the ways of his conversion, I healed the wounds with which I had struck him before. And I brought back to me him whom I had first cast out, and restored to him true consolation; for many are the false consolations by which the human race is deceived. Or: I comforted those who mourn for him, saying in the Gospel: Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted (Matt 5:5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.19":["What follows, I created the fruit of the lips, has this sense: I have fulfilled what I had promised, saying: As I live, says the Lord, I would rather the repentance of the sinner than his death (Ezek 33:11); or: I have granted all that I had promised before. And what is the Lord's promise? My peace I give you; my peace I leave you (John 14:27). And this is what he now says: Peace upon peace; not to one nation only, but to the whole world: namely to those who were far off and to those who were near, that is, first to the nations, and afterward to those of Israel who were willing to believe. Of these the Apostle too says: To you indeed it was necessary that the word of God be preached; but since you judge yourselves unworthy of salvation, behold, we turn to the nations (Acts 13:46). He, writing to the Ephesians and discussing most fully the people of the Circumcision and of the Gentiles, adds: But now in Christ Jesus you who once were far off have been brought near in the Blood of Christ. For he is our peace, who made both one, and broke down the middle wall of partition, dissolving the enmity in his flesh, and so on (Eph 2:13, 14); and again: And coming, he announced peace to you who were far off, and peace to those who were near (ibid., 17). For through him we both have access in one spirit to the Father: giving peace upon peace to those who had been far off and to those who were near, both those who had not had the Law and the Prophets and those who had received them before, he received them and healed them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.57.20-21":["But the impious, or lawless, shall be tossed like the raging sea, and shall never be able to rest. Although this can be understood of every impious man, yet it refers properly to the people of the Jews, who cried out with one voice and persevered in their impiety, saying: Crucify, crucify such a one. We have no king but Caesar (John 19:15). As, then, the raging sea cannot rest, but its waves overflow and roll up on the shores and are sucked back again, and breaking upon themselves make a mire that is trampled underfoot, so the impious will be subject to eternal trampling; they have no joy, and they rest in no peace, because they persevere in being impious.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.1":["Cry, cease not; lift up your voice like a trumpet, and declare to my people their crimes, and to the house of Jacob their sins. LXX: Cry with strength, and spare not; lift up your voice like a trumpet, and declare to the people their sins, and to the house of Jacob their iniquities. The preceding chapter finished, in which peace is announced to the righteous and no rest to the impious, God commands the prophet Isaiah to cry with strength, and to lift up his voice like a trumpet, and not to fear to tell the sins and crimes of the people of Israel, by no means afraid of death, nor of the threats and abuse of the impious, but rather considering the Lord who commands him. And because they have deaf ears and can scarcely hear, according to Symmachus and Aquila he is to turn his throat into a horn, which in Hebrew is called SOPHAR (שופר), and which they interpreted as κερατίνην, that is, of horn. Above, indeed, where it was said to the Apostles, Go up onto a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion; lift up your voice with strength, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem; lift it up, do not fear (Isa 40:9), neither horn nor trumpet is taken up, but they are ordered to go up the mountain. Here, however, because those to whom the message must be announced were sinners and lingered below, the Prophet is not ordered to go up onto a mountain, but only to lift up his voice like a trumpet and announce to them the wars to come. Besides, of the day of judgment, when the resurrection of the dead and the last trumpet are spoken of, it is written: For the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall rise incorrupt; so that, as they are to be judged at the sound of the trumpet, so Israel, dead in sin, may hear his crimes. And at the same time he is called the people of God, so that he may learn what kind of father he has lost, who calls even a sinner his people.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.2":["For they seek me from day to day, and wish to know my ways, as a nation that has done righteousness and has not forsaken the judgment of its God. LXX likewise. This properly fits the Jews, who run every day to the synagogues and meditate on the law of God, desiring to know what Abraham, Isaac and Jacob did, and what the rest of the saints did, and, turning over the books of the Prophets and of Moses by heart, chant the divine commandments. To them this will most rightly be applied: The wicked will seek me and will not find me. For thus it is written: Everyone who seeks finds (Matt 7); whoever does not find is proved to have sought wrongly. For the Lord is found by those who seek him in goodness, and is shown to those who are not unbelieving toward him. Since, then, they have forsaken the righteousness and the judgment of God, by which names Christ is shown, as the psalmist says, O God, give your judgment to the king, and your righteousness to the king's son (Ps 71 [72]:1), for Christ has become for us redemption, holiness and righteousness (1 Cor 1), in vain do they boast of their knowledge of the law, since the saint glories not in the knowledge of the Scriptures but in works, saying: I have run the way of your commandments, when you enlarged my heart (Ps 118 [119]:32).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","They ask of me the judgments of righteousness, and they wish to draw near to God. Why have we fasted, and you have not looked? Why have we humbled our souls, and you have not known? Behold, in the day of your fast shall be found your own will, and you have exacted again from all your debtors. Behold, you fast for lawsuits and contentions, and you strike with the fist wickedly. LXX: They now ask of me righteous judgment, and they desire to draw near to God, saying: Why have we fasted, and you have not seen? Why have we humbled our souls, and you have not known? For in the days of your fasts your own wills are found, and you afflict all who are subject to you, fasting for lawsuits and quarrels, and you strike the lowly with your fists. This is another rashness of the Jews: as though in the confidence of a good conscience, they demand righteous judgment and imitate the words of the saints, who say: Judge me, Lord, for I have walked in my innocence (Ps 25 [26]:1, 2); and again: Prove me, Lord, and try me; burn my reins and my heart; and in another place: Judge my cause and redeem me (Ps 118 [119]:154). And they desire to draw near to God, whereas this is not a matter of place but of affection, and not of an idle mind but of laborious work. In short, what it is to draw near to God, Scripture teaches, saying: Seek the Lord; and when he has drawn near to you, let the impious man forsake his ways and the unrighteous man his thoughts. For the Lord draws near to those who draw near to him, and to those who righteously pursue what is righteous and can say: But for me it is good to cling to God (Ps 72 [73]:28). For if Almighty God is the Father of truth and righteousness, whoever is a liar and unrighteous cannot draw near to God, of whom it is written: The malicious shall not dwell beside you, nor shall the unrighteous abide before your eyes (Ps 5:6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.3":["Why have we fasted, they say, and you have not looked? Why have we humbled our souls, and you have not known? They accuse the Lord of unrighteousness, because he does not regard their good works, and they press upon God only the hunger of the belly, without the work of the virtues: not eating the foods which God created for the faithful and for those who know the truth, to be taken with thanksgiving, but those of which it is written in Proverbs: For these are fed with the foods of impiety, and are drunk with the wine of iniquity (Prov 4:17). Glutted with feasts of this kind, the Pharisee, among his other words of pride, boasted that he fasted twice in the week (Luke 18); he had not drunk the wine of the vine of Sorek, but of the vine of Sodom, whose wine is the fury of dragons and the incurable fury of asps, and their grape is a grape of gall. Them God rebukes through the Prophet: They eat the sins of my people (Hos 4:8); and again: Why have you kept silent about their impiety, and gathered the vintage of iniquities, and eaten the grain of falsehood? (Hos 10:13). Since, then, they had put a question to God, desiring to know why they had fasted and humbled their souls and God had not looked on them, forgetting the ancient history, why he did not look on the gifts of Cain, who offered rightly but did not divide rightly with his brother (Gen 4), not seeing the charity of God, as Scripture says, You shall love the Lord your God with your whole heart, and your neighbor as yourself (Matt 22:39): therefore God answered that he does not reject fasts but the works done during fasts, saying: In the days of your fasts your own wills are found, so that you do not God's will but your own wills, which Paul, writing to the Ephesians, rejects: According to the prince of the power of the air, the spirit that now works in the sons of unbelief, among whom we all too once lived in the desires of our flesh, doing the will of the flesh and of the thoughts; and we were by nature sons of wrath, as the rest also (Eph 2:2, 3). What follows according to the Seventy, And you goad all who are subject to you, or you afflict, Theodotion and Symmachus translated better, and we have followed them in this place: And you exact again from all your debtors. By this he shows that it is not without danger to dun a poor man who is unable to pay, and not to give back to a debtor shivering with cold the cloak you have taken in place of a pledge, lest his cry come to God. For he who has pity on the poor lends to God (Prov 19); and on the contrary he who duns one who has nothing does violence to God. And that we may know that this is the sense in the present place, we are going to read further on in this same chapter: Loose the bonds of impiety, undo the bundles that weigh down. Doubtless he means bonds of debt.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.4":["For what does it profit for the face to grow pale with fasts, and to fast for lawsuits and quarrels, when Scripture says, He who loves sins rejoices in quarrels (Prov 17:19)? The servant of the Lord must not quarrel, but be gentle (2 Tim 2), and imitate him who says: Learn of me, for I am meek and humble of heart (Matt 11:29), so that the humility be not affected but of the heart, seeking not the glory of men but the conscience of the soul. You fast, he says, and strike the lowly with your fists, and beat them with slaps (1 Tim 3). Hence a bishop must not be a striker. For how can he rein in lust who cannot restrain either his hand or his tongue? Fasts of this kind the Lord rebukes at the beginning of this prophet too, saying: Fasting and idleness, new moons and sabbaths and your other solemnities my soul hates (Isa 1:13). Hence it is commanded through Joel: Sanctify a fast, proclaim a healing (Joel 2:15), so that we may fast from vices, and, by good works and the healing of our sins, our fasts may become holy. Then it follows: Let the bridegroom go forth from his chamber, and the bride from her bridal room, so that in the time of fasting we may be free for prayer, lest our inner man feed on the flesh of the dragon, which was given as food to the peoples of the Ethiopians. And we say these things not as rejecting fasts, through which Daniel too, the man of desires, came to know the future (Dan 9), and the Ninevites appeased the wrath of God (Jonah 3), and Elijah (1 Kgs 19) and Moses (Exod 34), in a hunger of forty days, were filled with the familiar friendship of God, and the Lord himself (Matt 4) fasted as many days in the desert, so as to leave us the solemn days of fasts; but because it does not profit to carry an empty belly and to do other things that displease God. And, to pass over the rest, there are those who disfigure their faces so as to appear to men to be fasting, who, obtaining glory in the present, will be shut out of the kingdom of God, because they have received their reward (Matt 6). Hence the Apostle too says that, even if he hands over his body to martyrdom so as to burn, or to glory (for both are found in the copies), without the charity of God (1 Cor 13), which is in the conscience of the heart, he sheds his blood in vain.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Do not fast as you have until this day, so that your cry may be heard on high. Is this such a fast as I have chosen, for a man to afflict his soul for a day? Is it to twist his head round like a ring, and to spread sackcloth and ashes? Will you call this a fast and a day acceptable to the Lord? LXX: Why do you fast to me as today, so that your voice may be heard in clamor? I have not chosen such a fast, and a day for a man to humble his soul. Not even if you bend your neck like a ring and spread under you haircloth and ashes, not even so shall you call it an acceptable fast. Lest he seem to reject the fast which he himself had commanded, he teaches how one should fast; or rather, before teaching what they should pursue, he instructs them in what they ought to avoid. So that your cry, he says, may be heard on high: do not bring a clamor to prayer, praying in the synagogues and at the corners of the streets so as to be seen by men (Matt 6), and lifting up your voice on high, and proclaiming your fasts and works with the Pharisee, who is condemned by the Lord's judgment (Luke 18). For this reason the Lord, rejecting the prayer of the Pharisees, teaches his disciples how they ought to pray (Matt 6): with the chamber of their breast shut, so that they may speak to God alone, who can hear the silence of the heart; that they may cry in their hearts, Abba, Father (Rom 8:15); and that they may not disfigure their faces, but wash them with water and anoint their head with oil. Of this John the Evangelist too writes: And you have an anointing from the holy one (1 John 2:20); when they have been anointed with it, they too become holy.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.5":["For God does not seek only affliction and the humbling of the soul through injury to the body, that a man should twist his body like a ring, and bow his neck, and go about sad, according to what is said in the Psalms: I am afflicted and bowed down to the end; all the day I went about saddened (Ps 37 [38]:7); nor that one should be clothed in sackcloth and sleep in ashes, as we read of David and of Ahab (2 Sam 3; 1 Kgs 21); but that along with these he should do the things that follow. Otherwise the Apostle too says that he fasted often (2 Cor 6 and 11), and the Lord teaches that his disciples will fast when the bridegroom has been taken from them (Matt 9); and in the Psalms the penitent says: I have eaten ashes like bread, and mingled my drink with weeping (Ps 101 [102]:10); And when they were troublesome to me, I put on haircloth (Ps 34 [35]:13). From this we learn that the things the Lord wills must be done first, and the others not passed over.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.6-7":["According to tropology we can say this: Let him who fasts from all evils, and wishes his fast to be regarded, not only turn away from evil but also do good, so as to loose every bond of iniquity by which the simple among believers have been bound through the frauds of the heretics; and the στραγγαλιὰς, the obligations of violent treatises, which the Prophet too points out in the psalm, saying: But those who turn aside into obligations the Lord will lead away with the workers of iniquity (Ps 124 [125]:5). For all who are bound with the chains of the heretics turn aside from the Lord, and are like an ox led to the sacrifice; and they choose and exchange falsehood for the truth. Such men, then, who are broken in judgment, let the churchman release and set free; and let him undo and break every writing of the heretics, of whom this same Prophet says: Woe to those who write iniquity; for writers write malice, robbing the judgment of the poor of my people (Isa 10:1, 2), so that they may by no means hold the simple entangled in their snares. And when he has done this, let him break the bread of the Church's doctrine to the hungry, as the Lord too did, giving the broken loaves to the Apostles; he left these to them in seven baskets and twelve hampers, by no means whole as they were held in the Law, but crumbled and broken in the Gospel, so that they might have something to bestow on the poor (Matt 15). Those whom you see not having the warmth of faith, but shivering outside the Church in the cold of unbelief, needy and strangers, bring, he says, into the house of the Church, and cover them with the cloak of incorruption, so that, clothed with the tunic of Christ, they may by no means remain in the tombs, as we read of him who, possessed by a legion of demons, lived naked among the memorials of the dead. And when, he says, you have done this, and your liberality has been laid open to all, do not, in particular, despise those of the household of the faith.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.6":["Is not this rather the fast that I have chosen? Loose the bonds of impiety, undo the bundles that weigh down; let those who are broken go free, and break every burden. Break your bread to the hungry, and bring the needy and the wanderers into the house. When you see the naked, cover him, and do not despise your own flesh. LXX: Is not such a fast what I have chosen, says the Lord? But loose every bond of iniquity. Undo the obligations of violent contracts. Let the broken go in release, and tear up every unjust writing. Break your bread to the hungry, and bring the poor without a roof into your house. If you see the naked, cover him, and do not despise those of the household of your seed. After teaching what kind of fast he rejects, he shows what kind he gladly accepts. Loose, he says, the bonds of impiety; undo the bundles that weigh down; or, as the Seventy translated more plainly, the obligations of violent contracts, which our translators, deceived by the ambiguity of the word, that is, συναλλαγμάτων, rendered as exchanges instead of bonds of debt. He means the bundles of papers in which the frauds of moneylenders are contained and by which the poor are oppressed with debt, which in all cities is the chief cause of sedition. Hence the Roman people too occupied the neighboring hills, and hence the New Tables, which the Greeks call χρέων ἀποκοπάς. The Prophet, then, does not command that no one should exact what is owed, especially what he lent justly and justly asks back; otherwise he would be a champion of tribunician sedition. But where the contract is unjust, where the poor are oppressed by fraud, there the bundles of contracts and all the bonds of iniquity must be broken. Or certainly this must be said, since the discourse is about fasts, and a fast involves the affliction and humbling of the soul, while the affliction of the body begs for the pardon of sins: the divine Scripture teaches that we should forgive our debtors, so that the heavenly Father too may forgive us our debts (Mark 11). The old history relates that in the seventh year of release, or in the fiftieth, which is the true Jubilee, all possessions return to their owners, and their former freedom is restored to slaves, and all the debts, which people commonly call contracts, become void (Lev 25 and 27). But if this is commanded in the old Law (Exod 21), how much more in the Gospel, where (Matt 5) all good things are doubled, and we are by no means told to take an eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth, but to offer the cheek to him who strikes us! And so that we may know that what we said above, You exact again from all your debtors, is written of creditors, something is added more plainly about debtors: Let those who are broken go free, that is, those who are broken by poverty, whom want has afflicted: do not let their children go begging. And break every burden by which they are weighed down. For this the Seventy translated more plainly, and tear up every unjust writing; which Symmachus translated: And break every false contract.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.7":["But it could happen that someone would say: I have no debtors; what must I do for my fast to be accepted? It follows: Break your bread to the hungry. Not many loaves, lest you plead poverty, but one loaf; and not even the whole of it, but a part of the loaf, which you would have eaten if you were not fasting, so that your fast may not be a gain for your purse but a filling of your soul. And he fittingly added your, lest you make alms out of plunder: For the ransom of a man's soul is his own riches (Prov 13:8); and in another place: Honor the Lord from your righteous labors (Prov 3:9). Surely, if you have no bread, and the multitude of the hungry is very great, give something from which you suffer no loss. A thing in which there is no expense: the poor without a roof bring into your house; or, as it is in the Hebrew, into the house, so that, if you have no house of your own, you may bring them into the little lodging which you hold either on rent or by favor. If you see the naked, cover him. This the Lord too said in the Gospel: He who has two tunics, let him give the other to him who has none (Luke 3:11). For he did not order one tunic to be cut and divided, which many do for the sake of popular favor, but the second not to be kept back; increasing the precepts of righteousness, so that he said that even a cup of cold water held out has its rewards. And do not despise, he says, your own flesh. For every man is our flesh; and according to the Gospel parable of the man who was going down from Jerusalem to Jericho and was wounded by robbers, he who did him good is called his neighbor (Luke 10). Or certainly, according to the Seventy, who said, And do not despise those of the household of your seed, let us understand those of the household of the seed as those of whom the Apostle too teaches that alms must be given to all, but above all to those of the household of the faith (Gal 6). For they are our flesh and our seed, who are begotten with us of one parent. To them the Savior also stretched out his hand, saying: These are my mother and my brothers, who do the will of my Father (Mark 3:34, 35). And so that we may know that there is one Lord of both the New and the Old Instrument, the Lord says the same things in the Gospel of those who will stand on the right hand on the day of judgment: Come, blessed of my Father, possess the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world. For I was hungry and you gave me to eat; I was thirsty and you gave me to drink; I was sick and in prison, and you came to me; a stranger, and you brought me into your house; and naked, and you clothed me (Matt 25:34ff.). And when they say in humility that they have done nothing for the Lord the Savior, he answers: As long as you did it to one of the least of my brothers, you did it to me.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.8":["Then your light shall break forth like the morning, and your health shall quickly arise, and your righteousness shall go before your face, and the glory of the Lord shall gather you up. Then you shall call, and the Lord will hear; you shall cry, and he will say: Here I am. LXX: Then your morning light shall break forth, and your healings shall quickly arise, and your righteousness shall go before you in your sight, and the glory of God shall surround you. Then you shall cry, and God will hear you. While you are still speaking, he will say: Here I am. When you have not done the things which the preceding discourse related, and have done these of which we have now spoken, then your fast will be acceptable, and your morning light will break forth: the sun of righteousness, namely, of whom the prophet Malachi speaks in the person of God the Father: For you who fear my name the sun of righteousness shall rise, and healing in his wings (Mal 4:2). He who is called the sun there is called the light here. For as the morning light dispels the darkness, so the light of knowledge and truth puts all errors to flight. And your healings, he says, shall quickly arise. For this Aquila, rendering word for word, put: And the scar of your wound shall quickly close over; so that, that is, the wounds of our sins may be closed at the rising of the sun of righteousness, the scar quickly forming over them. Where the Seventy translated τὰ ἰάματά σου, that is, your healings, the Latin translators, led by the likeness of the word ἱμάτια, that is, garments, put that instead. Hence many, deceived by the falseness of the translation, use this testimony to prove the resurrection of the body, wanting the body to be taken as the garment of the soul, which is to arise on the day of resurrection. And your righteousness, he says, shall go before you in your sight (Gen 30:33 LXX), according to what Jacob says: My righteousness will hear me tomorrow; and what is written will be fulfilled: Behold the man and his works. And the glory of the Lord shall surround you, as with a most beautiful garment, so that you may be glorified before God and before men.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.9":["Then you shall call, and the Lord will hear; you shall cry, and he will say: Here I am, not by voice but by deed, not by an answer but by benefits. This is proved according to the letter in the Gospel too, when the leper said to the Lord, If you will, you can make me clean, and the Lord answered, saying: I will; be clean.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","If you take away the chain from your midst, and cease to stretch out the finger and to speak what does not profit; when you pour out your soul to the hungry, and fill the afflicted soul, then your light shall rise in the darkness, and your darkness shall be as the noonday. LXX: If you take away from you the bond, and the ordination, and the word of murmuring, and give your bread to the hungry from your soul, and satisfy the humbled soul, then your light shall rise in the darkness, and your darkness shall be as the noonday. The Hebrew word MOTA (מוטה), which in Jeremiah is translated iron collar (Jer 28), is read twice in the present chapter. For in the place where, above, according to the Seventy, we said, Undo the obligations of violent exchanges, let us know that for the one word MOTA they put violent contracts, for which Aquila translated error, Symmachus turning aside, Theodotion κλοιὸν, that is, collar. Again, in the present place, where we have rendered, If you take away the chain from your midst, for chain the Seventy translated συνδεσμὸν, that is, binding together, or bond; Aquila, as above, interpreted it error; Symmachus, going over to Theodotion's view, put collar. Let this be said about the variety of interpretation. For the rest, the sense is joined with what precedes thus: The morning light will rise for you, and your healings will quickly arise, and your righteousness will go before you, and the glory of the Lord will crown you; and he will hear you at once when you entreat him, and will show that he is present. Only on this condition: that to the works mentioned above you also add these, that you take away from yourself the bond and the chain with which your soul is bound. Of this Peter too spoke to Simon: For I see that you are in the gall of bitterness and in the bond of iniquity (Acts 8:23). And Paul grieved, seeing the city of the Athenians bound to idolatry. For each one is bound fast by the cords of his own sins (Prov 5). For these bonds of the soul David too prayed in the psalm: Cleanse me, Lord, from my hidden sins, and from those of others spare your servant (Ps 18 [19]:13). It is hard to find one who is not bound by these chains. Rarely is a soul found that does not have this most heavy collar put around its neck, weighing it down to earthly works, so that it looks not to heaven but to the earth. This is related of the woman in the Gospel too, whom Satan had bound for eighteen years, so that, bent over, she could not see the things of heaven (Luke 13). If, then, he says, you take this chain out of the midst of your heart, and scatter bonds of this kind, and the χειροτονίαν, for which the three other translators with one voice translated the outstretched finger, so that not only do you not think evil yourself and not do it, but you do not even disparage your neighbor and point out each person as if with your finger, and speak what does not profit your soul, considering not your own evils but the errors of others: then you will receive what the following discourse adds. Very many of our writers understand χειροτονίαν, that is, the ordination of clerics, which is accomplished not only by the invocation of the voice but by the imposition of the hand (lest, namely, as we have laughed to see in some places, a clandestine invocation of the voice should ordain clerics without their knowing it), in such a way as to take up the testimony of Paul writing to Timothy: Lay hands hastily on no one, and do not share in the sins of others (1 Tim 5:22). For it is no light sin to cast pearls before swine and to give what is holy to dogs (Matt 7), and to bestow ordination to the clergy not on the holy and those most learned in the law of God, but on one's own hangers-on and on the ministers of base services, and, what is more disgraceful than these, at the prayers of silly women. Here we must consider the wisdom of the Apostle, in whom Christ was speaking, who, to show the danger of ordination, joined to it the torments of those who sin: And do not share in the sins of others. As, then, in the ordination of wicked men he who appoints such men is a partaker of their sins, so in the ordination of saints he who chose good men is a partaker of their righteousness. It follows: And the word of murmuring, understood ἀπὸ κοινοῦ: if you take it away from you. Now the word of murmuring is when God speaks against us, Let the murmuring of this people cease from me, and it shall not die; and we refer our sins to God, to make excuses in sins, and say: The heat of the body overcame me, the incitements of youth conquered me, I was created such by God, poverty forced me to theft.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.10":["So if we do not do the things that have been said, and do the things that are to be said, so as to give to the hungry not, as above, our bread, but our soul, so as to help him in whatever ways we can, and to give not as if out of sadness and necessity but from the soul, receiving rather than giving a benefit, for God loves a cheerful giver (2 Cor 9:7); so as not to refresh the hungry or afflicted soul in part but to satisfy it, and to grieve with those who grieve and mourn with those who mourn (Rom 12): then our light will rise in the darkness, he who says: I have come as light into the world, so that everyone who believes in me may not remain in darkness, but may have the light of life (John 12:46). For the lamp of our body is the eye: if it is single, our whole body will be full of light. But if what ought to be light has been turned into darkness, then the whole body will be dark, and it will be said to us: If the light that is in you is darkness, how great is that darkness itself! (Matt 6:23). Let us consider by what great virtues we may come to the light of God, so that the darkness may be turned into noonday, and we may say with the bride: Where do you pasture, where do you lie down, at noonday? (Song 1:6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.11":["And the Lord will give you rest always, and will fill your soul with splendors, and will deliver your bones. And you shall be like a watered garden, and like a fountain of waters whose waters shall not fail. LXX: And your God shall be with you always, and you shall be filled as your soul desires, and your bones shall grow fat. And they shall be like a drunken garden, and like a fountain for which the water shall not fail. What has been added in the Alexandrian copies at the beginning of this chapter, And still my praise shall be in you always, and at the end, and your bones shall spring up like grass, and shall grow fat, and they shall possess by inheritance unto generation and generations, is not found in the Hebrew, nor even in the corrected and true copies of the Seventy; hence it must be marked beforehand with an obelus. When the darkness, he says, of your breast has been dissolved by the rising of the light, and noonday has arisen in place of a palpable night, then you will always have God dwelling in you, to fill your soul with splendors. Of these we read in the psalm: With you is the principality in the day of your power, in the splendors of the saints; from the womb before the daystar I have begotten you (Ps 109 [110]:3). Or: you will be satisfied with all that your soul desires, because you yourself have satisfied the hungry soul, and you will be able to say: Bless the Lord, O my soul, who fills your desire with good things (Ps 102 [103]:5); and your bones will be delivered from destruction and from everlasting fire. Or: they will grow fat, so as to recover their former stoutness, having been wasted by the famine of the word of the Lord. This indeed we can understand according to the letter of the resurrection of the bodies which are to fall, and also of the virtues of the soul, which the saint signifies when he says in the psalm: All my bones shall say, Lord, who is like you? (Ps 34 [35]:10). Of these it is also written in Proverbs: A good report makes the bones fat (Prov 15:30). And to those to whom rewards are promised: There shall be health for your body, and healing for your bones (Prov 16:24). Of these again it is written: The Lord keeps all their bones; not one of them shall be broken (Ps 33 [34]:21). On the contrary, he who is a sinner, and has lost the strength and virtue of the soul, laments and says: Our bones are scattered beside hell (Ps 140 [141]:7). For we cannot believe that the bones of sinners are not in hell but beside hell. And a good report makes the bones fat (Prov 15:30), which has nothing to do with the fattening of the body. It follows: And you shall be, or it shall be, that is, either you or your soul, like a watered garden; of which it is written: A fountain went forth and watered the whole face of paradise (Gen 2:6), where in the Hebrew garden is read. This fountain we can call no other than him to whom it is said, With you is the fountain of life (Ps 35 [36]:10), who unceasingly waters his Church and the hearts of all believers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.12":["And the deserts of ages shall be built up in you; you shall raise up the foundations of generation and generation; and there shall be called in you the builder of hedges, turning the paths into rest. LXX: And the places desert from of old shall be built up for you, and your foundations shall be everlasting for generations and generations; and you shall be called the builder of the wall, and you shall make the paths in the midst to rest. Not only, he says, will your light rise in the darkness, and your darkness be like noonday, and the Lord give you rest always, and you be like a watered garden, and the rest, but there shall be built up in you the things that had been desert for a long time; and the foundations of your dwellings will be laid to endure through many generations; so that either you yourself will be called the builder of hedges and walls, or one will arise in you who will be the builder of hedges and will turn the paths into rest, or, according to the Seventy, will make the tracks in the midst to rest. The Jews, and the friends only of the letter that kills, refer these things to the restoration of the cities of Palestine; and they contend either that they were done under Zerubbabel and Ezra and Nehemiah, or that they will be in the last time: that for the restoration of Jerusalem and of the cities round about, very deep foundations are to be laid, and walls built so high that none of the enemy can enter, and the entry of all foes into them is barred. But we, following the order of the explanation we have begun, say that the things that had been desert among the Jews are built up in the Church, not for a short time but for ever, and that its foundations are to be raised up from both peoples, that is, in two generations. Hence the Apostle said: As a wise architect I have laid the foundation; another builds upon it; but let each one see what he builds upon it (1 Cor 3:10); and in another place: We are God's husbandry, God's building; and again: Built upon the foundation of the Apostles and Prophets (Eph 2:20); and that she, or he who was born in her, is to be called the builder of hedges. Of him we read in the psalm: A man was born in her, and the Most High himself has founded her (Ps 86 [87]:5). These hedges and walls (for this is what φραγμοῦς signifies in the Greek tongue) Scripture records as being set around a field and vineyards: You brought a vine out of Egypt; you cast out the nations and planted it; and a little after: Why have you broken down its wall, and all who pass by the way gather its vintage? (Ps 79 [80]:9 and 13). And in this same Prophet: A vineyard was made for the beloved; describing it in beautiful language, he adds: I built a tower and surrounded it with a wall (Eccl 2:4). Whoever breaks it down, according to Ecclesiastes, a serpent will bite him. It was surrounded for this purpose, that the approach of all beasts into the vineyard of God might be barred. This is the crooked serpent who deceived Eve in paradise; because she had broken down the precepts of God, she was therefore open to his bites, and heard from the Lord: You shall watch for his head, and he shall watch for your heel (Gen 3:15). In the place where we, lest we seem to innovate in anything, interpreted according to the Seventy, because the testimony is widely known, and you shall be called the builder of hedges, in the Hebrew is read and there shall be called in you GODER PHERES גדר פרץ, which Aquila interpreted περιφράκτης διακοπῆς, which we can render according to the sense as one who sets himself against the raging anger of God. Indeed, Symmachus translated setting a wall against the one who strikes (1 Sam 7), such as were Moses and Aaron and Samuel, who withstood the anger of the Lord and, as though by building a wall, set a limit to his indignation. This is also said to Jeremiah: that he should not run to meet the Lord, nor wish to resist his anger, and so shut off his indignation by prayers as by a kind of wall (Jer 7); and to Moses, as though the Lord were being held back by him: Let me go, he says, and I will strike this people (Exod 32:10). A builder, then, of walls, ramparts and hedges of this kind will turn the paths into rest, so that the wrath of God may by no means rage, but the Lord may be appeased toward them, and all the paths of indignation may come to rest.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.13":["If you turn away your foot from the sabbath, from doing your own will on my holy day, and call the sabbath delightful, and the holy of the Lord glorious, and glorify him while you do not make your own ways, and your own will is not found to speak a word. LXX: If you turn away your foot on the sabbaths, so as not to do your own wills on the holy day; and you shall call the sabbaths delightful, holy to the Lord; you shall not lift your foot to work, nor speak a word ÷ in anger from your mouth ⁚. To the one to whom he had said above, Is not this rather the fast that I have chosen: loose the bond of impiety, undo the bundles that weigh down, and the rest, he now too promises rewards, if he is willing to do the things that follow (Exod 20): namely, that he restrain his foot on the sabbaths, and not do his own wills, nor stain the holy day of the Lord with his wills. It is commanded in the Law that on the sabbaths we should do no servile work, kindle no fire, sit in one place, and do only those works that belong to the salvation of the soul. If we take this according to the letter, it cannot be fulfilled at all. For who can do this: sitting the whole day and night on the sabbath, not go away from one place, or rather not even move slightly, since if he did he would be a transgressor of the law? From one commandment, then, which is impossible according to the letter, we are compelled to understand the rest spiritually as well: that we should not do servile work and lose the freedom of the soul, for he who commits sin is the slave of sin (John 8); that we should not carry a burden on the sabbath, such as he carried who says: My iniquities have gone up over my head; like a heavy burden they have weighed heavily upon me (Ps 37 [38]:5); that the iniquity which sits upon the talent of lead should have no power in us; that the pleasure and lust of the body should not set us on fire (Zech 5), for all adulterers, their hearts are like an oven (Hos 7); and the fiery darts of the devil are there to shoot in the dark at the upright of heart (Ps 10 [11]). We are commanded too that, having taken hold of the plow, we should not look behind us; that, having climbed to the height of the roofs, we should by no means wish to come down to take the garments of the world (Luke 9); but that we should hear with Moses, But you, stand here with me (Exod 34:2); and sit with the Lord, weary on the way of this world, at the well of the Samaritan woman, thirsting for the conversion and salvation of those who stray (John 4); that we should hear the Apostle warning, Be firm and immovable (1 Cor 15:58), lest we suffer what Judas the traitor suffered, who lost the place of his ministry and became a transgressor; and let us always remember that sentence: If the spirit of him who has power rises up against you, do not leave your place (Eccl 10:4). He who rests thus on the sabbath washes his hands among the innocent, and does not move his feet to do his own wills: he keeps the delightful sabbaths of the Lord. This sabbath rest, according to the Epistle written to the Hebrews, is promised to us in the heavens, and glory is prepared (Heb 4): so long as we do not make our own ways on the sabbath, nor is our own will found, to speak a word; that is, that we sin neither in deed nor in word. Otherwise, if these things are forbidden only on the sabbath, then on the other six days freedom to sin is granted us. And since it is foolish to believe this, it remains that the sabbath, which means rest, must be sanctified by the faithful at all times, while they do the wills not of the flesh but of the soul. What is added according to the Seventy, in anger from your mouth, is not found in the Hebrew. To this sabbath, to be celebrated with all festivity, the Savior calls us in the Gospel, saying: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will give you rest (Matt 11:28); for this is what ἀναπαύσω ὑμᾶς signifies: that, laying down the burdens of sins, we may keep sabbath in Christ and say: He has set my feet upon a rock (Ps 39 [40]:3), and may avoid what the saint says he almost suffered: But my feet were almost moved (Ps 72 [73]:2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.58.14":["Then you shall delight upon the Lord, and I will lift you up above the heights of the earth, and I will feed you with the inheritance of Jacob your father. For the mouth of the Lord has spoken. LXX: And you shall be confident upon the Lord, and he will lift you up above the good things of the earth, and he will feed you with the inheritance of Jacob your father. For the mouth of the Lord has spoken. When you have called the sabbaths delightful, and have not moved your foot on the sabbath to do servile work, nor spoken a word, that is, have always kept silent and fulfilled what is commanded, Keep your tongue from evil, and let your lips speak no deceit (Ps 33 [34]:14), then you will delight in the Lord, and will see fulfilled in yourself: Delight in the Lord, and he will give you the petitions of your heart (Ps 36 [37]:4). Or: you will have confidence in the Lord, according to what is written: He who trusts in the Lord is blessed; and in Jeremiah: Blessed is the man who trusts in the Lord, and the Lord shall be his hope (Jer 17:7); and elsewhere: It is good to trust in the Lord rather than to trust in man (Ps 117 [118]:8). And the Lord will lift you up and raise you above the heights and good things of the earth. For the land of the meek and the land of the living is not below but above. Thus it is said to Lot as he fled from the valley of Sodom and Gomorrah: Save yourself on the mountain (Gen 19); and after he had reached it, the sun rose for him in Zoar. Of this land the Lord said: Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the land (Matt 5:4); and many other things which we have often used, among which is this: Honor your father and your mother, and you shall be long-lived upon the land which the Lord your God will give you (Exod 20:12). According to the letter this cannot stand at all; for many honor their parents and die soon, and other men, parricides, live a long time. But so that we may know more surely that this land is in the heights, let us briefly recall the thirty-sixth psalm, in which it is written: The meek shall possess the land, and shall feast in the abundance of peace. After this it adds: Wait for the Lord and keep his ways, and he will lift you up to possess the land (Ps 36 [37]:11); of which it is sung in another place: The righteous shall possess the land, and shall dwell upon it for ever and ever (ibid., 34). This too is impossible: for if the earth shall pass away, this one certainly which we inhabit, how shall the righteous dwell upon it for ever and ever? From this we learn that there is a land which is set in the heights. After this it adds: And I will feed you, or the Lord will feed you, with the inheritance of Jacob your father. For as he who has the faith of Abraham is called a son of Abraham, so he who supplants vices and sins is called a son of Jacob; and whose virtue anyone imitates, his son he is also called. The good things of the land set in the heights, with which the son of Jacob is satisfied, must be taken as those which eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor have they entered into the heart of man (1 Cor 2). Of them it is written in the Epistle to the Hebrews: For those who say these things show that they seek a homeland; and if indeed they were seeking that from which they had come out, they had time to return to it. But now they desire a better homeland, that is, the heavenly Jerusalem (Heb 11:14ff.). From this we learn that the fable of the thousand years, in which marriages are promised again, and foods, and the conduct of an earthly life, is to be rejected. For if in the resurrection they will neither marry nor be given in marriage, but will be like the Angels, because they are sons of the resurrection (Matt 22); and the Apostle speaks of the belly and foods: Food for the belly, and the belly for foods; but God will destroy both the one and the other (1 Cor 6:13): how shall we seek again, in an incorrupt, spiritual and immortal body, the vices of our former mortal and corrupt flesh? And we say this not as denying the substance of the glorified body, but as taking away entirely the former works in those who are like the Angels. Otherwise, even in this body of ours we imitate the angelic virtues by fasts and continence and perpetual chastity and charity, and yet we do not lose the substance of our bodies. That we may believe all these things to be future, let us know that they have been promised by God, whose mouth has spoken; so that, as in the hands we understand work, and in the feet walking, and in the belly generation, and in the ears and eyes hearing and sight, so in the mouth we may understand the speech of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.1":["Behold, the hand of the Lord is not shortened, that it cannot save, nor is his ear made heavy, that it cannot hear; but your iniquities have divided between you and your God, and your sins have hidden his face from you, so that he would not hear. LXX: Is the hand of the Lord not able to save, or is his ear made heavy, that it cannot hear? But your sins separate between you and God, and because of your sins he has turned his face away from you, so as not to have mercy. The things which I have now said I will do, You shall be confident in the Lord, and he will lift you up to the good things of the land, and will feed you with the inheritance of Jacob your father, I could have fulfilled before as well. For my hand is not shortened and drawn in, so that I cannot stretch it out to set my people free; nor do I have deaf ears, so that I cannot hear. From this it is shown that the ears of the Lord, which hear the righteous and do not hear sinners, are by no means like our ears, which hear equally the voice uttered, whether of righteousness or of unrighteousness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.2":["But the sins, he says, of yours, like a wall set up in the midst, divide you and God. Hence the Savior too in his passion made both one (Eph 2), and, dissolving the middle wall of partition, destroyed the enmity in his own flesh, so that those whom the wall of sins had divided the blood of Christ might join together. He opened the gate of paradise, which had been shut for a long time (Gen 3), and quenched the fiery sword with his own blood, so that the thief might hear: Today you shall be with me in paradise (Luke 23:43). What follows, And because of your sins he has turned his face away from you, so that he would not hear, or so as not to have mercy, shows this: that he could not bear the stench of their sins and their iniquities, but turned away his face, so as not to see their foulness and be compelled at once to strike. Hence the sinner, longing to see the face of the Lord, says in the psalm: How long, Lord, will you forget me to the end? How long will you turn your face away from me? (Ps 12 [13]:1). And elsewhere: Show your face, and we shall be saved (Ps 79 [80]:4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.3-4":["Their lips too and their tongue speak iniquity and falsehood, so that instead of Christ they promise themselves the Antichrist; and there is no one among the people who calls for righteousness and judges truth, or, as it is in the Hebrew, faith, that is, who believes in Christ; but they trust in nothing and speak vanities. Hence the Apostle commands that we should not give heed to Jewish fables and the commandments of men who turn away from the truth (Titus 1). For truly they trust in nothing and follow vanities; in which the Lord's prophecy is fulfilled: I have come in the name of my Father, and you have not received me; if another comes in his own name, him you will receive (John 5:43).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.3":["For your hands are defiled with blood, and your fingers with iniquity; your lips have spoken falsehood, and your tongue utters iniquity. There is none who calls for righteousness, nor is there any who judges truly; but they trust in nothing and speak vanities. LXX: For your hands are defiled with blood, and your fingers with sins. Your lips are defiled with iniquity, and your tongue meditates unrighteousness. No one speaks righteous things, nor is there righteous judgment. They trust in vain things and speak empty things, iniquities and sins. What he named above in general, he now sets out in its parts. And we must note that he by no means charges them with the idolatry in which they were held in the times of Isaiah, but with the shedding of blood, of which he had already spoken before: The righteous man perishes, and there is none who considers it. And righteous men are taken away, and no one understands in his heart. For the righteous man has been taken away from the face of iniquity; his burial shall be in peace, and he shall be taken from the midst (Isa 57:1, 2). And although they themselves did not lay hands on the Lord the Savior, yet by crying with one voice of impiety, His blood be on us and on our children (Matt 27:25), they are guilty of his death and have defiled hands. Of them the same Prophet testifies, saying: If you lift up your hands to me, I will not hear. Your hands are full of blood (Isa 1:15).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.4":["They have conceived toil and brought forth iniquity; they have broken the eggs of asps and woven the webs of the spider. He who eats of their eggs shall die, and what has been warmed shall break out into a basilisk. Their webs shall not serve for a garment, nor shall they be covered with their works. LXX: For they are in labor with pain and beget iniquity. They have broken the eggs of asps and woven the web of the spider. And he who wishes to eat of their eggs, breaking one, will find it rotten, and in it a basilisk. Their web shall not serve for a garment, nor shall they be covered with their works. Those who await the coming of the Antichrist, and cannot say, From your fear, Lord, we have conceived, and been in labor, and brought forth; we have wrought the spirit of your salvation upon the earth (Isa 26:18), rightly conceive pain and bring forth iniquity, so that the one whom they seek with much labor they may receive in iniquity. Of their father it is written: He conceived pain and brought forth iniquity (Ps 7:15).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.5-6":["Some explain this place thus: The eggs of asps they have split, or broken: that is, they abandoned all the biting words of the Prophets, and wove for themselves the smooth speeches of the false prophets; and whoever eats of them will be struck by the serpent, and will understand that the words of falsehood have profited him nothing.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.5":["These break and burst the eggs of asps, so as to take their poisons into their own minds; to them it is said in the Gospel: Serpents, brood of vipers (Matt 3:7). And he fittingly set down the eggs of asps and serpents, which are said to produce eggs first. And because they not only retain the poisons of malice in their hearts, but also meditate on the law of the Lord day and night, muttering old wives' fables, therefore it is said: And they weave the webs of the spider, with which they catch flies and gnats and small creatures; but if anything strong comes against them, it flies through as though through empty air. He who eats, he says, of their eggs shall die, that is, he who yields to their counsels, as Abel yielded to Cain when he said, Let us go out into the field (Gen 4:8), will at once be killed; or, as the Seventy translated: He who wishes to eat of their eggs, crushing one, will find it rotten, and in it a basilisk. For he who, deceived at first sight, thinks them the eggs of hens and other birds, which are harmless to eat, if he breaks one before he eats, will at once perceive a most foul stench and stink, and in what has been warmed he will find a basilisk; or, according to Symmachus and Theodotion, an asp. Aquila put a viper, for which in the Hebrew is read EPHEE (אפעה). He, then, who, hearing the Jewish traditions, wishes to prepare himself for the feasts of the thousand years, and, entangled in the delights of the promises, to stretch out his hand to the egg: if before he eats, that is, before he yields to deadly words, he is willing first to consider what is said, and to examine each word and handle its reasoning, he will at once find that in them the Antichrist is being prepared. According to the Hebrew, then, he who eats of their eggs shall die. But he who, according to the Seventy, is willing to break them first, and to look more carefully and see what lies hidden, will find a most foul stench, and the serpent, the prince of all, the devil, lying hidden in their traditions.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.6":["And because he had said above, They weave the webs of the spider, he explains that the weaving of these webs does no good. Their labor, he says, and all their doctrine does not make the garment of Christ, nor a cloak by which the nakedness of the soul may be covered; but they sweat with fruitless labor, as this same Prophet says: This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me. And in vain do they worship me, teaching the doctrines and precepts of men (Isa 29:13); following these, they neglect the law of God. And so that we may know that works are signified by the web, he added: Nor shall they be covered with their works.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Their works are useless works, and the work of iniquity is in their hands. Their feet run to evil, and they hasten to shed innocent blood. Their thoughts are useless thoughts; desolation and destruction are in their ways. The way of peace they have not known, and there is no judgment in their steps. Their paths have become crooked to them; everyone who treads in them does not know peace. LXX: For their works are works of iniquity, and their feet run to malice; swift to shed harmless blood; and their thoughts are the thoughts of fools. Destruction and misery are in their ways, and the way of peace they have not known, and there is no judgment in their ways. For their paths are perverse, which they pass along, and they do not know peace. Therefore they are not covered with their works, because their works are works of iniquity, which the Apostle calls unfruitful, that is, having no fruits (Eph 5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.7-8":["What follows, Their thoughts are the thoughts of fools; destruction and misery are in their ways, and the way of peace they have not known, and, above, Their feet are swift to shed blood, the Apostle set down in Romans (ch. 3). Many, not knowing this, think it was taken from the thirteenth psalm, in which the verses were added in the common edition and are not found in the Hebrew; about this we spoke more fully at the beginning of this volume.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.7":["And their feet run to evil and hasten to shed harmless blood (Prov 1). For they had this custom in the slaying of the Prophets too. To them Stephen said: For which of the Prophets did your fathers not persecute? (Acts 7:52). And the Lord in the Gospel: Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the Prophets and stone those who are sent to you (Matt 23:37); and again: All the righteous blood that has been shed upon the earth shall come upon you, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah the son of Barachiah, whom you killed between the Temple and the altar (ibid., 35). Hence it is said to them above as well: For your hands are defiled with blood, and your tongue speaks iniquity (Isa 59:3). And through this practice of cruelty and blasphemy they came to the slaying of the Lord, just as Judas too came by the way of avarice to murder, or rather to sacrilege joined with avarice.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.8":["Rightly did they not know the way of peace, who refused to receive its author. For he is our peace, who says to the Apostles: My peace I give you, my peace I leave you (John 14:27); and to Jerusalem: If you knew the things that are for your peace; for the days shall come upon you, and your enemies shall surround you with a rampart, and besiege you, and dash your children to the ground (Luke 19:42, 43). And those who have not received peace consequently have no judgment in their ways either: namely him of whom it was said above, Behold my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved whom my soul has loved; he shall bring forth judgment to the nations (Isa 42:1). It follows: Their ways are perverse, not by nature, but by their own will. For whatever is perverted and bent is twisted from straight into crooked. The whole doctrine of the Pharisees is a subversion of the truth; whoever walks by it not only does not find peace, but does not even know it, so as to know what he ought to seek.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.9":["Because of this judgment is far removed from us, and righteousness will not overtake us. We waited for light, and behold, darkness; for brightness, and we have walked in darkness. We have groped for the wall like the blind, and we have felt about as if without eyes; we have stumbled at noon as in darkness; in dark places as dead men. We shall all roar like bears, and like doves we shall moan in meditation. We waited for judgment, and there shall be none; for salvation, and it is far removed from us. LXX: Therefore judgment has departed from them, and righteousness does not overtake them. While they waited for light, darkness came upon them. Waiting for it, they shall walk in darkness; they shall grope for the wall like the blind, and shall feel about as though having no eyes. They shall fall at noon as at midnight; like dying men they shall groan; like a bear and like a dove they shall walk together. We waited for judgment, and there is none; salvation has departed far from us. After the accusation of the people of the Jews, the Prophet answers in their person what they ought to say as penitents, if indeed they wish to recover health after their wounds. And I wonder why the Seventy framed the speech as though the Prophet were speaking about them, and not they themselves answering the Prophet's words, when in what follows, overcome by the truth, they did this very thing, saying: We waited for judgment, and there is none; salvation has departed far from us. For our iniquity is great in your sight, and our sins have withstood us, and the rest. In the present time, then, all things are fulfilled. Judgment, which has been announced to the nations, is far removed from the Jews; and righteousness, which has been laid hold of by the nations, will not overtake them. They waited for the light, saying, Send forth your light and your truth (Ps 42 [43]:3), which had been promised them through the Prophets, and they have been seized by the darkness of error. They waited for brightness, and they walked in darkness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.10":["So they grope at the holy Scriptures like the blind feeling for a wall, seeking in them only the words and the leaves, not the sense and the fruit which is held in the letters. Then he adds and says: We have stumbled, or we shall fall at noon as in darkness; in dark places, as dead men. For while the sun of righteousness flashes its brightness over the whole world, they linger in darkness, namely as men dead in sin.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.11":["We shall all roar, he says, like bears, and like doves we shall moan in meditation; so that they are at once cruel and wretched: fierce and savage toward the lowly and those subject to them, fearful and trembling toward those who are stronger, whom they dread as doves dread hawks. We read in another place too: Like a she-bear robbed of her cubs; and in Daniel the bloody kingdom, in which there are three rows of teeth, is compared to a most ferocious she-bear (2 Sam 17:8; Dan 7). And like doves we shall moan in meditation, without sense and understanding of the Scriptures, meditating only on the words, which they chant by heart. But just as the doves of which it is written, Ephraim is like a dove that has no sense (Hos 7:11), and as in the Gospel the simplicity of doves is set against the malice of serpents (Matt 10), so in the present place simplicity without prudence, which is close to folly, is shown in the mere meditation of words. They will wait for judgment, and there will be none; for salvation, understood, they will wait, and there will be none, because it has gone far away to the nations. Here it must be noted that everything is said in the future tense, so as to indicate what the Jews are now enduring.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.12":["Our iniquities are multiplied before you, and our sins have answered against us; for our crimes are with us, and we have known our iniquities: to sin and to lie against the Lord; and we have turned away so as not to go behind our Lord, so as to speak calumny; and we have conceived transgression, and have spoken from the heart words of falsehood. And judgment is turned backward, and righteousness has stood far off, for truth has fallen in the street, and equity could not enter. And truth has been given over to forgetfulness, and he who departed from evil lay open to be a prey. LXX: For our iniquity is great in your sight, and our sins have withstood us. For our iniquities are in us, and we have known our unrighteousnesses. We have acted impiously, and we have lied, and we have departed from our God. We have spoken unjust things and been disobedient. We have been in labor with and meditated from our hearts unjust words. And we have departed from judgment, and righteousness has stood far off, for truth has been consumed in their ways, and they could not pass by the straight road. And truth has been taken away, and they turned aside their mind so as not to understand. He gives the reasons why they roared like bears and meditated like doves, and waited for a judgment that was not there, and for a salvation that passed over to the nations: because multiplied, they say, are our iniquities in your sight, from which you had turned away your face for a long time, so as not to see them, so as not to strike. And our sins have answered against us, so that we have received what we deserved. And our crimes are with us, and we have known our iniquities, which, while we committed them for a long time, we thought to be righteousness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.13":["And what are the iniquities? To sin and to lie against God; or, as Aquila translated according to the Hebrew, to deny God: which refers to the Savior. And we have forsaken, they say, our God, saying: We know that God spoke to Moses; but as for this man, we do not know where he is from (John 9:29); so as to speak calumny: If this man were from God, he would not break the sabbath (ibid., 16); and: By Beelzebub the prince of demons he casts out demons (Luke 11:15). And transgression, which in Greek is called more expressively ἀπόστασις, when someone denies God and is convicted of prevarication. We have conceived and spoken from the heart words of falsehood, despising the law of God and following the traditions of men, which they call δευτερώσεις, and which we feigned in our heart:\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.14":["And judgment is turned backward, and righteousness has stood far off. For what share has righteousness with iniquity? What agreement has Christ with Belial? Righteousness has taken its stand among the nations and has departed from us, because truth has fallen in the street. For wide and spacious is the way that leads to death (Matt 7), because they would not enter the narrow way in which truth abides.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.15-16":["And the Lord saw, and it appeared evil in his eyes, that there is no judgment; and he saw that there is no man, and he was at a loss, because there is none to meet him. And his own arm will save for him, and his righteousness itself has strengthened him. He has put on righteousness as a breastplate, and a helmet of salvation upon his head. He has put on the garments of vengeance, and is covered as with a cloak of zeal. As for vengeance, as for the repayment of indignation to his foes, and recompense to his enemies, he will repay recompense to the islands. LXX: The Lord saw, and it did not please him, because there was no judgment. And he saw, and there was no man. And he considered, and there was none to defend. And he avenged them with his arm, and upheld with mercy. And he put on righteousness as a breastplate, and set the helmet of salvation on his head. And he was wrapped in the garment of vengeance, and in a cloak, as one repaying vengeance, reproach to his adversaries. Up to this point the prophetic discourse has been woven in the person of the people; now the prophet speaks in his own person. When they said these things, the Lord saw that they made a show of repentance with the mouth and did not practice it in the heart, and it did not please him. For he sought in them the truth of judgment, which had been transferred to the nations, and did not find it. He longed for some righteous man who could meet him in his anger, and did not find one; he who had said above as well: I came, and there was no man; I called, and there was none to hear (Isa 50:2). And because, while they persisted in their crime, not even one righteous man was found, but all had turned aside, together they had become useless, and there is none who does good, there is not even one (Ps 13 [14]), he strengthened him with his arm and righteousness, or mercy, so that those who wish to be converted from error may be preserved not by their own merit but by the clemency of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.15":["And truth has been given over to forgetfulness, of which it is written: Truth has sprung from the earth, and righteousness has looked down from heaven (Ps 84 [85]:12). Here we must observe that truth is named often, so that the person of Christ might be shown; forsaking him, they followed falsehood. And he who departed from evil lay open to be a prey. Although this seems obscure, it can be explained thus: when we were fashioning falsehood from our own heart and forsaking the law of God, righteousness stood far off, and truth fell in the streets, and equity could not enter in to us, and the Son of God, who is the truth, was given over to forgetfulness; so much so that whoever wished to depart from the traditions of the Jews at once lay open to plots and persecutions, so that they cast out of the synagogues the man blind from birth who had received his sight (John 9). And after the resurrection of the Lord the Savior they persecuted and stripped all who believed in him, to whom the Churches from the whole world of the nations sent ministrations, for them to distribute through the hands of the Apostles.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.17-18":["Then he put on the breastplate of righteousness, and the helmet of salvation, and the garments of vengeance, and was wrapped in the cloak of zeal; and thus armed he went forth to war, to render vengeance to his enemies, doubtless meaning the Jews who persevered in their blasphemies, and recompense to his foes, when the Roman army surrounded them; and in their victory the Lord is shown to have fought. Paul used this testimony when writing to the Ephesians, wishing us to put on the armor of Christ, by which we may resist the fiery darts of the devil (Eph 6). And what is not found in the Seventy Translators, He will repay recompense to the islands, signifies the cities of Judea, which the Roman army laid waste. For above too (Isa 20) the Lord speaks through the Prophet to the inhabitants of the island, that is, of Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.19":["And those from the West shall fear the name of the Lord, and those from the rising of the sun his glory, when he comes like a violent river which the spirit of the Lord drives on; and the redeemer shall come to Zion, and to those who return from iniquity in Jacob, says the Lord. This is my covenant with them, says the Lord: My spirit which is in you, and my words which I have put in your mouth, shall not depart from your mouth, nor from the mouth of the seed of your seed, says the Lord, from now and for ever. LXX: And those from the West shall fear the name of the Lord, and those from the rising of the sun the glorious name. For the wrath of the Lord shall come like a violent river, ÷ it shall come with fury ⁚ and he who delivers shall come from Zion. And he shall turn away impieties from Jacob, and this shall be my testament to them, says the Lord. My spirit which is in you, and my words which I have given in your mouth, shall not fail from your mouth, nor from the mouth of your seed, says the Lord, from now and for ever. After the Lord has put on the breastplate of righteousness, and the helmet of salvation, and the garments of vengeance, and the cloak of zeal for the fight, and has come to the contest and to vengeance, to repay his foes and render to his enemies, and to overthrow their islands, that is, their cities and villages, then the foreigners who are to come from the West and the East to rest in the bosom of Abraham (Matt 8), of whom it was also said above (cf. Isa 49:12), Behold, these shall come from afar, some from the West, and others from the North, and others from the land of the Persians, will fear the Lord with that fear which is the beginning of wisdom (Sir 1). Of this we read in many places, of which let us set down a few: Blessed is the man who fears the Lord (Ps 111 [112]:1); and: There is no want for those who fear him (Ps 33 [34]:10); and: The fear of the Lord is discipline and wisdom (Prov 15:33); and: Come, children, hear me; I will teach you the fear of the Lord (Ps 33 [34]:12); and: Blessed is the man who fears the Lord (Ps 127 [128]:4); and: The fear of the Lord adds days (Prov 10:27). That, once Israel has been rejected, the crowd of the nations takes its place, the prophet Malachi teaches more fully, in whom it is said to the Jews: I have no pleasure in you, says the Lord Almighty, and I will not accept a sacrifice from your hands (Mal 1:10, 11); and again of the multitude of the nations: For from the rising of the sun to its setting my name has been glorified among the nations. The author of this blessedness is he who will come like a violent river which the spirit of the Lord drives on; or, as Aquila translated, like a narrow river, the spirit of the Lord its seal; or Theodotion, like an assailing river, the spirit of the Lord is sealed. In this testimony, then, as the Seventy translated it, like a violent river the wrath of the Lord shall come with fury, the last part is not found in the Hebrew. For wrath and fury are not to be set among the promises of God, since in the other things that follow there is the blessedness of things to come, and the threat and punishment of sinners. According to Aquila and Theodotion, the spirit of the Lord which is sealed in Christ confirms the example which is read in John the Evangelist: For him God the Father has sealed (John 6:27); of whom it has already been said before: A rod shall come forth from the root of Jesse, and a flower shall rise up from its root, and the Spirit of the Lord shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and fortitude, the spirit of knowledge and piety, and the spirit of the fear of the Lord has filled him (Isa 11:1ff.). Hence we too say: The light of your countenance, Lord, is sealed upon us (Ps 4:7). And in Ezekiel the foreheads of the men who groan are sealed with the impression of THAV, the Hebrew letter which is the last among them (Ezek 9). But if we wish to know how the Holy Spirit is a violent river, let us read again the Acts of the Apostles, in which it is written: And when they were gathered together, suddenly there came a sound from heaven as of a violent wind, and it filled the whole house where they were sitting. And there appeared to them tongues parted as of fire, and it sat upon each of them; and they were all filled with the Holy Spirit (Acts 2:2, 3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.20-21":["Paul, explaining this place carefully, writes to the Romans that there is no distinction of Jew and Greek, but the same Lord of all, rich toward all who call upon him (Rom 10); and that the nations did not believe in such a way that Israel was wholly driven out. For I too, he says, am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin. God has not cast off his people whom he foreknew (Rom 11:1, 2). And a little after, when he had set down the example of Elijah, complaining to the Lord that he alone was left, and hearing from him that the Lord had left for himself seven thousand men who had not bent the knee before Baal (1 Kgs 19), he added: So then at this present time also a remnant has been saved according to the election of grace. And if by grace, it is no longer of works; otherwise grace is no longer grace. What then? What Israel was seeking, this it has obtained; but the election has obtained it, and the rest have been blinded. And they, if they do not remain in unbelief, will be grafted in. For God is able, he says, to graft them in again (Rom 11:5ff.): not against nature from the wild olive, as the multitude of the nations was grafted in, but according to nature into the good olive, that is, into their own. And at the end he set down, so as to arrive at the present chapter: For I would not have you ignorant, brothers, of this mystery, lest you be wise in your own eyes: that blindness has befallen Israel in part, until the fullness of the nations should come in, and so all Israel should be saved, as it is written: He who delivers shall come from Zion, and shall turn away impieties from Jacob; and this is my testament to them, when I take away their sins (Rom 11:25ff.). We have pursued these things at greater length so that we may understand that whatever promises we have read, and are going to speak of, concerning Zion and Jerusalem, are said not generally to all the Jews, but specially to those of Israel who were chosen in the Apostles and through the Apostles.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.20":["It follows: The redeemer of Zion shall come, and of those who return from iniquity in Jacob, says the Lord. For this the Seventy translated: He who delivers shall come from Zion, and shall turn away impieties from Jacob. If, then, he who turns away the impieties from Jacob will come from Zion, we understand this: that a man was born in Zion, and the Most High founded her, he who turns away the crimes of Jacob. But if the redeemer shall come to Zion, and to those who return from iniquity in Jacob, says the Lord, this is the sense: Christ will come, who will redeem Zion with his blood; or, according to the Hebrew idiom, he who is the kinsman of Zion and was begotten of the stock of Israel; for this is what GOEL (גואל), that is, ἀγχιστεὺς, means. And lest we think that all of Zion is redeemed and freed from its crimes, Zion which is bloodied with the blood of the Lord, he added significantly: to those who return from iniquity, if they are willing to do penance, in whom the Lord's prayer may be fulfilled: Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do (Luke 23:34).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.59.21":["Theirs, then, is the redemption in Zion, and theirs the kinsman, who from Jacob have received him; and to them he promises, saying: This is my covenant with them, or pact, as all the others translated, or testament, as the Seventy put it. What the covenant, pact and testament is, the following verse shows: My spirit, he says, which is in you, and my words which I have put in your mouth, shall not depart from your mouth, nor from the mouth of your seed, from now and for ever. This is said either to Isaiah, as it seems to me, or to the Lord, as very many think. With reference to Isaiah, then, the order is connected thus: This is the everlasting pact of the Gospel: that my spirit which is in you, and my words which I have put in your mouth, by which you foretell things to come, shall not depart from your mouth, nor from that of your sons and grandsons and your seed, so that every generation in succession is indicated: namely, that the grace of the Prophets comes upon the Apostles, and through those of Israel who are to believe through the Apostles. What he adds, from now and for ever, agrees with that testimony: Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away (Matt 24:35).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.1-2":["Further, the name Jerusalem and of the nations, which the Seventy set down here, is not found in the Hebrew, and must be marked beforehand with an obelus, against those who assert that everything said here is said to Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.1-3":["He also cries out to Jerusalem in another place: Be enlightened, be enlightened, O Jerusalem; for your light has come upon you, and the glory of your God has risen upon you. For behold, darkness and gloom shall cover the earth over the nations, but over you the Lord shall appear, and the glory of your God has risen upon you. And kings shall walk in your light, and the nations in your splendor. If gloom and darkness shall cover the earth over the nations, how in turn shall the nations walk in the splendor of the Lord? But those nations will be in darkness which the cold and frost have gripped; and those will walk in the light of the Lord which have followed the kings, the apostles and prophets, of whom it is written: The heart of the king is in the hand of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"],"ISA.60.1":["Arise, be enlightened; for your light has come, and the glory of the Lord has risen upon you. For behold, darkness will cover the earth, and thick gloom the peoples; but upon you the Lord will rise, and his glory will be seen in you. And the nations will walk in your light, and kings in the brightness of your rising. LXX: Be enlightened, be enlightened, ÷ Jerusalem ⁚ for your light has come, and the glory of the Lord has risen upon you. Behold, darkness will cover the earth, and thick gloom upon the nations. But upon you the Lord will appear, and his glory will be seen in you. And the nations will walk in your light, and kings in your brightness. What we thought about the restoration of Zion and Jerusalem, and about everything that is promised to her by the prophetic oracle, we said more fully at the end of the preceding book, where we explained what that little verse meant: The redeemer will come to Zion, and to those who return from iniquity in Jacob. Now we must touch briefly on what most people think of this passage, so that, once the error has been seen through, we may more easily take up the truth. The Jews and our half-Jews, who expect a golden and jewelled Jerusalem from heaven, contend that these things will come to pass in the kingdom of a thousand years, when all the nations are to serve Israel, and the camels of Midian and Ephah, coming from Sheba, will bring gold and incense, and all the sheep of Kedar will be gathered, and the rams of Nebaioth will come to be sacrificed upon the altar of the Temple that will have been built. They say too that from the islands, and above all in the ships of Tarshish, her daughters will fly like doves, bringing riches of gold and silver; that the walls of Jerusalem will be built by foreigners, with the kings of the nations set over them; that the gates of the city will always stand open, so that by day and by night the riches of Jerusalem and the victims may be brought in; and that everything that lies desolate will be rebuilt with cypress and pine and cedar cut in Lebanon, above all the Temple of the Lord, in which there will be everlasting gladness; so that she will suck the milk of the nations and eat the riches of kings, and there will be such an abundance of all things that they will have gold for bronze, silver for iron, bronze for wood, iron for stones. Her princes too, they say, will enjoy everlasting peace, and bishops will preside over the peoples in righteousness, and her gates will be carved in relief. And what is greater than these, instead of the sun and the moon the Lord himself will shine with everlasting light; and for one man she will have a thousand very strong men, and for little ones she will possess very mighty nations. So say those who desire earthly pleasures, and seek beauty in wives and a great number of children, whose god is their belly and whose glory is in their shame (Phil 3); and whoever follows their error confesses himself, under the Christian name, to be like the Jews. Others, however, assert that all these things were promised to the Jews in a carnal sense, if they had received him who says in the Gospel: I am the light of the world (John 8:12), which enlightens every man coming into the world; so that, just as victims were granted to the people of Israel, not because they were good in themselves, but so that they might not be offered to demons, so too the Lord promises these things to the gluttonous Jews, who seek nothing but the pleasures of the body, so that at least for the sake of their carnal desires and an abundance of wealth they might receive the Son of God; and since they did not receive him, the promises too, they say, became void. Thus to the Canaanite woman pleading for her daughter: I did not come, he says, except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 15:24). And to his disciples: Do not go away into the way of the nations, and do not enter the cities of the Samaritans; go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 10:5, 6). For this reason the Apostles too first proclaimed the Lord in the synagogues, and when these did not receive the Gospel they said to them: It was necessary that the word should be preached to you; but since you have not received salvation, behold, we turn to the nations (Acts 13:46). For the light came into the world, and the Jews loved darkness rather. Hence the Lord too, when he had wept over Jerusalem, added: If you also had known the things that make for your peace (Luke 19:42). And since she did not receive it, he went on: But now a day will come upon you, and your enemies will surround you with a rampart, and hem you in, and bring you down to the ground together with your children (ibid., 43). But let us, following the former sense, believe that all of this is said to the Church, which was first gathered from the Jewish people, and which through the Apostles passed on to the nations the light that had risen upon her. To her it is said: Arise, be enlightened, so that she who fell in the unbelievers may rise in the faithful; she who fell in the synagogues may rise in the churches; and, once she has risen, may be enlightened, so that she may have no darkness of error at all. For your light, he says, has come, the light that all the Prophets promised, which you were ever awaiting. And the glory of the Lord, which was once upon the tabernacle and his Temple, has risen upon you; of which it is said: Glorious things have been said of you, city of God (Ps 86 [87]:3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.2":["For behold, darkness will cover the earth, that is, those who savor earthly things; and thick gloom the peoples, or, as we read in the Hebrew, the tribes: which refers properly to the Jews, of whom it is written in another psalm: For there the tribes went up, the tribes of the Lord, the testimony of Israel (Ps 121 [122]:4). But upon you the Lord will rise, the sun of righteousness, and his glory will be seen in you, of which it is written: And we saw his glory, the glory as of the only-begotten of the Father, full of grace and truth (John 1:14).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.3":["And the nations will walk in your light. We all shall walk in the light of the Apostles, which shines in the world, and the darkness did not comprehend it. And kings, he says, in the brightness of your rising, when you were first born in Christ. This is fulfilled both spiritually and carnally: so that kings whose heart is in the hand of the Lord, and in whose mortal body sin does not reign (Prov 21), may walk in the brightness of the newborn Church, or in him who has risen in the Church, and may bow their necks to the faith of Christ, the true king (Rom 6). And we see this fulfilled daily, when the Roman princes, the error of idolatry and the fury of persecution being removed, pass over to the faith and tranquillity of Christ. There are some who await in a time to come all these things which we relate as partly fulfilled and wholly to be fulfilled from the first coming of the Savior until the consummation of the world, when, as the fullness of the nations comes in, all Israel is to be saved (Rom 11). Their opinion is by no means to be rejected, provided we know that these things are to be fulfilled spiritually and not carnally.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.4":["Lift up your eyes round about and see: all these have gathered and come to you. Your sons will come from afar, and your daughters will suck at your side. LXX: Lift up your eyes round about, and see all your sons gathered. All your sons have come from afar, and your daughters will be carried upon the shoulders. It is said to the Church, Zion, which was first gathered through the Apostles; of whom we also read in the Acts of the Apostles that devout men from the whole world were in Jerusalem, who received the word of God, and in the tongues of their own and of foreign nations either heard others speaking or themselves spoke to the rest (Acts 2). And they are commanded to lift up their eyes round about, which the Lord too commanded the Apostles, saying: Lift up your eyes and see, for the crops are already white for the harvest (John 4:35). For out of Zion, and by no means out of Mount Sinai, the law will go forth, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem. And she is commanded to lift up her eyes and see her sons gathered, who come from afar. To her it is said in another place too: Rejoice greatly, daughter of Zion, proclaim, daughter of Jerusalem: Behold, I come, and I will dwell in your midst, says the Lord (Zeph 3:17); and: Many nations will come to the Lord, and they will be my people, and I will be their God (Zech 2:11). Now we are the sons who come to the Lord from afar, once strangers to the Testament of God and to his promises, having no hope, and without God in the world. But what does the Apostle say? You who were once far off have now been brought near (Eph 2:13). And what follows, And your daughters will suck at your side, signifies that souls that are sucklings in Christ, and in the infancy of baptism, of whom the apostle Peter too speaks, Like newborn babes, desire the rational milk without guile (1 Pet 2:2), suck the milk of the apostles. To such babes and sucklings Paul also spoke: My little children, with whom I am again in labor until Christ be formed in you (Gal 4:19). And in another place: As a nurse cherishing her children, so, longing for you, we desire to impart to you not only the Gospel of Christ but our own souls as well (1 Thess 2:7, 8). But what is said instead of this in the Seventy, your daughters will be carried upon the shoulders, must be considered more carefully. For the sons, who are strong, come from afar by themselves and are gathered to the faith of the Lord. But the daughters, who are weaker and, through the frailty of their sex, have not yet come to the perfect man, are carried on the shoulders of the Apostles, so as to be brought into the bosom of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.5":["Then you will see and overflow, and your heart will marvel and be enlarged, when the multitude of the sea has been turned to you, and the strength of the nations has come to you. LXX: Then you will see, and you will fear, and you will be astonished in heart: because the riches of the sea, and of the nations, and of the peoples will be transferred to you. When you have lifted up your eyes and beheld your sons and daughters either coming swiftly by themselves or carried on the shoulders of the saints, then you will rejoice, and like rivers you will overflow with sudden waters, and your heart will marvel and be astonished, nay rather will be enlarged, as it hears the Apostle: My mouth is open to you, O Corinthians. And again: Be enlarged, and we too (2 Cor 6:11); lest through narrowness of heart you should be unable to have as your guest Christ, who says in the Gospel: I and the Father will come and will make our dwelling with him (John 14:23). But what is added in the Seventy, and you will fear, is not found in the Hebrew; unless perhaps, after the greatness of the joy, fear crept in, lest she lose so great a good. Or is it not joy to see riches, and the multitude of the sea being transferred and turned to oneself, and the strength of the nations coming to oneself, so that whatever is in the world and on the face of the earth is one's own; and, the faith of the nations being strengthened, to say: I can do all things in him who strengthens me, Jesus Christ (Phil 4:13)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.6-7":["A flood of camels will cover you, the dromedaries of Midian and Ephah. All from Sheba will come, bringing gold and incense, and proclaiming praise to the Lord. All the flock of Kedar will be gathered to you; the rams of Nebaioth will minister to you; they will be offered upon my altar of propitiation, and I will glorify the house of my majesty. LXX: And herds of camels will come to you, and the camels of Midian and Gephar will cover you: all from Sheba will come, bringing gold and incense, and they will proclaim the salvation of the Lord. All the sheep of Kedar will be gathered to you, the rams of Nabaeoth will come, and they will be offered as propitiatory upon my altar; and the house of my prayer will be glorified. After the riches of the sea and the strength of the nations, herds of camels too, and the dromedaries of Midian and Ephah, are promised to Jerusalem, all of which will come from Sheba carrying gold and incense, and, what is greater than these, proclaiming the salvation of the Lord. All the sheep of Kedar too will be gathered to the Church, and the rams of Nebaioth will come, or, as is written in the Hebrew, will minister, and will be offered, or will offer victims upon the altar of the Lord, so that his house may become renowned. Midian and Ephah are regions beyond Arabia, fertile in camels, and the whole province is called Sheba, whence came also the queen of Sheba, who came to hear the wisdom of Solomon, she too bringing gold and incense, carrying much to the peaceable king and receiving greater things from him (1 Kgs 10). Kedar is the region of the Saracens, who in Scripture are called Ishmaelites. And Nebaioth is one of the sons of Ishmael, from whose names the desert is called, which, poor in crops, is full of flocks. Through the familiar names of barbarian nations, then, that are neighbors to Israel, the conversion of the whole world is proclaimed. For in this place Midian means iniquity; Ephah, loosed, or pouring out; Sheba, conversion, or captivity; Kedar, darkness; Nebaioth, prophecies. So herds of camels, loosed from the bonds of iniquity and pouring out their souls to God, will cover Jerusalem with gifts, and all will come from captivity and in their conversion, bringing the gold of faith and the incense of sacrifice. And through these gifts, by no means content with their own salvation, they will advance so far as to proclaim the salvation of God to others as well. That rich man in the Gospel who, like a camel, carried the burdens of riches, and was a camel, would not listen to the Lord's counsels nor be unloaded, so that, the pack cast off, he might fly to heaven on the wings of a dove; therefore he went away sad. And of a camel of this kind the Savior speaks: It is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle than for a rich man into the kingdom of heaven (Matt 19:24). He set forth the difficulty of the thing, not its impossibility. Indeed your mother Paula, of holy memory, and your brother Pammachius passed through the eye of a needle, that is, through the strait and narrow way that leads to life, into the kingdom of heaven, leaving behind with its burdens the broad way that leads to Tartarus. Nay, whatever they had they carried into the gifts of the Lord, fulfilling what is written: The ransom of a man's soul is his own riches (Prov 13:8). For the things that are impossible with men are possible with God (Matt 19). Having among their gifts the chief things, gold in understanding and incense of the sweetest odor, and saying: Let my prayer be directed as incense in your sight, and: We are the good odor of Christ in every place (2 Cor 2:15), they proclaim daily the salvation of the Lord by the example of their virtue, so that all the sheep of Kedar may be gathered in the Church and pass from the darkness of error to the light. And let the rams of the Prophets, of whom it is sung in the twenty-eighth psalm, Bring to the Lord, sons of God, bring to the Lord the young of rams, come and be offered, or, according to Theodotion, offer themselves as a sacrifice to the Lord, and become propitiatory victims, so that the Church of Christ may be glorified. Of sheep of this kind the Savior spoke to his disciples: Go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 10:6). And again: My sheep hear my voice (John 10:27). And more fully in Ezekiel: Behold, I will seek my sheep and visit them, as a shepherd seeks his flock. Thus says the Lord: I will seek the lost, and bring back the straying, and bind up the broken, and strengthen the weak, and guard the strong, and I will feed them with judgment (Ezek 34:11, 12). And so that we might know who the sheep were, he puts it more plainly: And they will know that I am the Lord their God, and they are my people, the house of Israel, says the Lord God; and you are my sheep, and you are all the sheep of my flock, and I am the Lord your God (ibid., 27, 28). If anyone among the nations is rich, then, let him be saved as a camel, not without gifts and offerings, with which he may proclaim the word of the Lord. If anyone is of the simplicity of sheep and of the authority of rams, let him go up, or be offered upon the altar of the Lord by those who have the power, so that his house may be glorified. But what we noted as written in the Hebrew, The rams of Nebaioth will minister to you, and they will offer upon my altar of propitiation, is properly understood of those who, chosen from the nations for the priesthood, are ministers of the Savior. And if anyone is contentious, and contends that these things were promised in a carnal sense, let us answer him: We have no such custom, nor has the Church of God (1 Cor 11:16). And let us say this too: that even if they were promised carnally to the Jews, still they were promised under a condition, namely that if they had received their light, which had been sent to them, then these things also would follow; that is, that through the desire of gold and an abundance of wealth and of carnal things, by whose allurements this nation was always captivated, they might receive the Son of God sent to them; and since they did not receive him, all was taken away, and the inheritance was given back spiritually to those who received him.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.6":["And again I say to you: It is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven. By this saying it is shown that it is not difficult but impossible. For if, as a camel cannot enter through the eye of a needle, so a rich man cannot enter the kingdom of heaven, none of the rich will be saved. But if we read in Isaiah how the camels of Midian and Ephah come to Jerusalem with gifts and offerings, and how those who were formerly bent and twisted by the perversity of vices enter the gates of Jerusalem, we shall see how these camels too, to which the rich are compared, when they have laid down the heavy burden of sins and the crookedness of the whole body, can enter by the narrow and strait way that leads to life.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"ISA.60.8-9":["Who are these who fly like clouds, and like doves to their windows? For the islands wait for me, and the ships of the sea in the beginning, that I may bring your sons from afar, their silver and their gold with them, to the name of the Lord your God, and to the Holy One of Israel, because he has glorified you. LXX: Who are these who fly like clouds, and like doves with their young to me? The islands have waited for me, and the ships of Tarshish among the first, to bring your sons from afar, and their silver and gold with them, for the sake of the holy name of the Lord, and because the Holy One of Israel has been glorified. Since the Lord entered Egypt upon a light cloud, and commanded the clouds of the Prophets, to whom the truth of God has come, not to rain showers upon Israel, the Church, first gathered from the people of the Circumcision, marvels that a crowd of nations is flying to her from the whole world, and, having taken the wings of the Holy Spirit, is hastening, according to Symmachus and Theodotion, to their windows, according to Aquila to their floodgates, in order to enter the Church. Or that teachers with their disciples, that is, doves with their young, are flying to the Church from the islands of the nations, which, according to the oracles of the Prophets, will wait for the Lord. The ships of THARSIS (תרשיש) too, that is, of the sea, of which we spoke more fully in the vision of Tyre, will bring the sons of the Church in the beginning of faith, carrying gold and silver. For with the heart one believes unto righteousness, and with the mouth confession is made unto salvation (Rom 10:10). Of this gold and silver it is written in the sixty-seventh psalm: The wings of a dove covered with silver, and her hinder parts in the gleam of gold (Ps 67 [68]:14). And in the seventy-first psalm: The kings of Tarshish and the islands will offer gifts, and the kings of the Arabians and of Sheba will bring presents (Ps 71 [72]:10). All these things are brought to the name of the Lord God and to the Holy One of Israel, who has glorified him.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.10-11":["Or certainly this must be said: that she who was once afflicted and handed over to captivity in the people of the Jews he reconciles to himself in the calling of the nations, so that her gates are always open, and are shut neither by day nor by night, and stand continually open to those who desire to be saved; that is, that entrance to her is not denied to those who wish to believe, whether in joy or in tribulation. And let the strength, or the riches, of the nations be brought in to her, and let their kings minister to her, or be led to her as captives. This you will understand when you see the most eloquent men led to the faith of Christ, and the wisdom of the wise made foolish, and the prudence of the prudent rejected (1 Cor 1), so that the foolishness of God is wiser than men.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.10":["And the sons of strangers will build your walls, and their kings will minister to you. For in my indignation I struck you, and in my reconciliation I have had mercy on you. And your gates will be open continually; they will not be shut by day or by night, so that the strength of the nations may be brought to you, and their kings be led in. For the nation and the kingdom that will not serve you will perish, and the nations will be laid waste with desolation. LXX: And foreigners will build your walls, and their kings will stand by you. For because of my wrath I struck you, and because of mercy I have loved you. And your gates will be open always; they will not be shut by day or by night, so that they may bring in to you the strength of the nations, and their kings who are to be led in. For the nations and the kings that will not serve you will perish; the nations will be made desolate with desolation. Among the other things with which the Church is enriched and the city of the Savior is built, foreigners too and the sons of strangers build her walls, so that the enemy cannot enter nor faithlessness find a place. Foreigners and strangers properly signify the people of the nations, who truly built up the Church of Christ, so much so that their kings and princes minister to her, or stand by her. This is taken either carnally or spiritually. If carnally, we see the Roman Caesars bow their necks to the yoke of Christ, build churches at public expense, and issue decrees of law against the persecutions of the heathen and the snares of heretics. If spiritually, whoever among them are princes in continence, eloquence and holiness, and subjugate the servitude of the flesh to the rule of the soul, these minister to her and stand by her, or give aid to her whom he often forsakes because of her negligence, or strikes with the rod of persecutors, so that he may again love her for his own mercy's sake.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.12":["But the nation and their kings who refuse to render the Church a good and profitable service, so as to pass over into apostolic dignity, will perish with the perdition that is prepared for the ungodly; and whatever was in them who refused to have God as their guest will be reduced to desolation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.13":["The glory of Lebanon will come to you, the fir and the box and the pine together, to adorn the place of my sanctification; and I will glorify the place of my feet. And the sons of those who humbled you will come to you bowed down, and all who disparaged you will worship the footprints of your feet, and they will call you the city of the Lord, the Zion of the Holy One of Israel. LXX: The glory of Lebanon will come to you, in cypress and pine and cedar together, so that my holy place may be glorified, ÷|÷ and I will glorify the place of my feet ⁚ And the sons of those who humbled you will go to you in fear, ÷ and provoked you ⁚ and all who provoked you will worship the footprints of your feet; and you will be called the city of the Lord, Zion, of the Holy One of Israel. Much is lacking in the Seventy, which I have set down from the Hebrew under asterisks, and what they added I have marked beforehand with an obelus. Lebanon is a mountain of Phoenicia, planted with lofty trees, which the Psalmist describes when he says: I saw the ungodly exalted above measure and lifted up like the cedars of Lebanon (Ps 36 [37]:35). And in another place: The Lord will break the cedars of Lebanon (Ps 28 [29]:5). And many other things which I pass over for the sake of brevity. From this mountain Hiram king of Tyre once sent timber to Solomon at Joppa, for building the Temple of God (1 Kgs 5). From it too Scripture now promises that the fir and the box and the pine, or, according to the Seventy, the cypress and the pine and the cedar, or, according to Aquila, the fir, thaadaor, and thaassur, or, according to Theodotion, Brais, Thadaar, and Theassur, are to be cut together, so that the temple of Zion may be built (2 Chr 2). If that is so, where will the golden and jewelled Jerusalem be? Where the wife of the Lamb? Where the twelve gates adorned with a variety of gems? Unless perhaps she will have jewelled walls and foundations, and the Temple, which ought to be more richly adorned, will be built of wood! For this reason we are compelled to take everything spiritually: that the fir, the cypress, the pine and the cedar, once lofty trees of Lebanon, have glorified the Temple of God and the place of his feet, and made it renowned. And not to draw out the sense in a long discourse: do not the holy man and most eloquent martyr Cyprian, and Hilary, the confessor of our own time, once lofty trees in the world, seem to you to have built the Church of God?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.14":["And what follows, And the sons of those who humbled you will come to you bowed down, or returning, and all who had disparaged you will worship the footprints of your feet, we ought to understand of those who are Christians not by will but by necessity, and who bow with fearful minds through dread of offending those who reign. Or certainly that those who were formerly persecutors afterwards believed: such as was the apostle Paul too, who persecuted the Church of God and was afterwards called a vessel of election (Acts 9). When these things have been so fulfilled that the fullness of the nations enters in, then all Israel will be saved. And she will truly be called the city of the Lord, the Zion of the Holy One of Israel, who stands upon the watchtower and has been gathered from both peoples.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.15":["Because you were forsaken and hated, and there was none who passed through I will make you the pride of the ages, a joy from generation to generation; and you will suck the milk of the nations, and you will be suckled at the breast of kings. And you will know that I am the Lord who saves you, and your redeemer, the Mighty One of Jacob. LXX: Because you were forsaken and hated, and there was none who helped, I will make you an everlasting exultation, a joy of generations unto generations. And you will suck the milk of the nations, and you will eat the riches of kings. And you will know that I am the Lord who has saved you, and who has rescued you, the God of Jacob. She who before had been forsaken and hated, when the branches in you were broken off because they had not borne fruit, branches which were broken off for this reason, that there was no one who passed through and offered help there, of whom it is said in the psalms, And those who passed by did not say: The blessing of the Lord be upon you (Ps 128 [129]:8): therefore I will make you an everlasting pride, or an exultation and joy of two generations, other branches from the wild olive of the nations being grafted in place of the former branches, which contrary to the nature of the pattern will bear fruit, not of the former bitterness but of the sweetness which they have drawn from the root.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.16":["You will suck too the milk of the nations, and you will be suckled at the breast of kings. What sense this passage has we explained more fully above, when discussing the little verse, Your sons will come from afar, and your daughters will suck at your side. Or, according to the Seventy, you will eat the riches of kings. These riches, according to the Hebrew truth, are the breasts of kings and teachers, by which the infancy of those born in Christ is instructed and nourished. When you have sucked these and have come to solid food, so that you also eat the riches of such kings, then you will know that I am your Savior, who redeemed you with my blood, or the mighty God of Jacob.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.17-18":["I will make, he says, your princes in peace, and your bishops in righteousness. For which it is written in the Hebrew: I will make your visitation peace, and your overseers righteousness. Here is the admirable majesty of holy Scripture, that it named the future princes of the Church bishops, all of whose visitation is in peace, and the title of whose dignity is in righteousness: so that they do not respect persons in judgment, and nothing unjust is heard in the land of the Church, nor destruction and unhappiness in her borders. For wherever there is iniquity, and righteousness is not kept, and peace is lost, all these things follow.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.17":["For bronze I will bring gold, for iron I will bring silver, and for wood bronze, and for stones iron; and I will make your visitation peace, and your overseers righteousness. Iniquity will no more be heard in your land, devastation and destruction in your borders; and salvation will occupy your walls, and praise your gates. LXX: For bronze I will bring you gold, and for iron I will give you silver, and for wood I will grant you bronze, and for stones iron. And I will give your princes in peace, and your bishops in righteousness; and iniquity will no more be heard in your land, nor destruction and misery in your borders; but your walls will be called salvation, and your gates carving. The same thing, according to the nature of its position, is a genus to one, a species to another. And so as not to seem to say anything about the arts of rhetoric and dialectic, I shall give common examples which can instruct the simple reader. My son, who in relation to me is a species and has me as his genus, is a genus to his own son. I shall give another: what is greater to one is smaller to another. A centurion is greater than a common soldier, smaller than a tribune. Five are few compared with ten, many compared with one. So in the restoration of the spiritual Jerusalem, wood will be turned into bronze, and stones will be changed into iron; that is, men once brutish and senseless will be changed into those materials which provide some use to the city. And the bronze and iron themselves, through the increase of virtues, will be changed into gold and silver, so that they have not merely the appearance of usefulness but value as well and beauty. What gold and silver signify in the holy Scriptures we have often said.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.18":["But instead of all these, he says, salvation will occupy your walls, or the Savior, which in the Hebrew reads JESUS, which properly sounds as the name of the Lord. For he himself is the strength of the walls of the Church, which has her gates in the praise of the Lord, so that those who enter her may first learn to praise the Lord and to confess his name. But as for the Seventy having translated γλύμμα, that is, carving, instead of praise, we can say this: that the gates of the Church, through which we enter her, ought to be engraved with all the virtues.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.19-20":["You will no longer have the sun to give light by day, nor will the brightness of the moon illumine you; but the Lord will be to you an everlasting light, and your God will be your glory. Your sun will no more go down, and your moon will not wane, for the Lord will be an everlasting light, and the days of your mourning will be ended. LXX: And you will no longer have the sun for the light of day, nor will the rising of the moon give you light by night; but the Lord will be to you an eternal light, and God your glory. For the sun will not go down for you, and the moon will not fail you. And the Lord will be an everlasting light, and the days of your mourning will be ended. From this passage we are compelled to refer everything that has been said and is still to be said to the last time, when, as heaven and earth pass away, the office of the sun and the moon will cease, and the Lord himself will be a perpetual light; so that what the χιλιασταὶ assert is to be fulfilled carnally, we believe will come to pass spiritually, differing from them in the nature of the promises, not in the time. To them this must briefly be answered: if for false prophets and sinners the sun goes down at noon, and on the contrary for those who fear the Lord the sun of righteousness rises, why should he not always rise for the saints who says, I am the light of the world (John 8:12), who shines in the darkness, and the darkness did not comprehend him (John 1)? For the sun will not burn us by day, nor the moon by night (Ps 120 [121]:6), because we have the Lord as a perpetual light, and the days of our mourning will be ended; we shall by no means be lamenting over the overthrow of Jerusalem, but rejoicing at the building up of the Church. For blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted. Blessed are those who weep, for they shall laugh. Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled (Matt 5). So that, once they have been filled with the flesh of the Lord, they may pour forth the word of the Lord and tell their works to the king (Ps 44 [45]).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.21":["And your people will all be righteous; they will inherit the land for ever, the shoot of my planting, the work of my hand, for glorifying. The least will become a thousand, and the little one a very mighty nation. I the Lord will do this suddenly in its time. LXX: Your people, all righteous, will possess the land for ever, keeping the planting, the works of their hands, for glory. He who is small will become thousands, and he who is least a great nation. I the Lord will gather them in time. When the days of mourning are finished and completed, when sorrow will be turned into joy, the people of Zion will be all righteous, not for a short time but for ever; and because it is righteous, it will possess the land of the gentle. Nor is it to be wondered at if, to receive the good things of the promise, it holds the land of the meek and of the living, for which the Prophet sighs, saying: I believe that I shall see the good things of the Lord in the land of the living (Ps 26 [27]:13), since it is the shoot of the Lord's planting and the work of his hands for glorifying God. For every planting, he says, which my heavenly Father has not planted will be rooted up (Matt 15). Or, according to the Seventy, the people that keeps the planting of God and the works of the Lord's hands will be preserved in the glory of the Creator. Of the good planting God speaks through Jeremiah: I planted you a good vine, wholly true, which in Isaiah is called the vine of Sorek (Isa 5). And because Paul, conscious of the virtues of Christ speaking in him, could say, Be imitators of me, as I also am of Christ (1 Cor 11:1), he therefore said to the Corinthians: I planted, Apollos watered, but the Lord gave the increase (1 Cor 3:6). For those planted in the house of the Lord will flourish in his courts (Ps 91 [92]).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.60.22":["Then he who was a little one will become thousands, hearing from the Lord: Have authority over five or ten cities (Luke 19:17, 19), so that he is called a χιλίαρχος. And he who had said with the Apostle, To me, the least of all the saints, this grace was given (Eph 3:8), will be prince of a great nation in the heavens, when at the appointed time the Lord sends his angels, and they gather to him all the saints from the height of heaven to its summit (Matt 24): not only from Israel but also from the people of the nations, signifying whom he said: And I have other sheep which are not of this fold, and them also I must gather, that there may be one flock and one shepherd (John 10:16). And he will do this suddenly, so that when everything has been despaired of, then they may be gathered into a very mighty nation. And although we see these things being fulfilled in part daily in the Church, yet they will be more fully fulfilled at the consummation of the world and at the second coming of the Savior.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.1":["The Spirit of the Lord God is upon me, because the Lord has anointed me; he has sent me to bring good news to the meek, to heal the broken in heart, and to preach indulgence to the captives, and opening to those who are shut in; and to proclaim a year of favor to the Lord, and a day of vengeance to our God; to comfort all who mourn; to appoint for those who mourn in Zion, and to give them a crown instead of ashes, the oil of joy instead of mourning, a mantle of praise instead of a spirit of sorrow. LXX: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to heal the broken in heart, to preach remission to the captives and sight to the blind, to call an acceptable year to the Lord, and a day of retribution, to comfort all who mourn, to give to those who mourn in Zion, to give them glory instead of ashes, the anointing of gladness to those who mourn, a garment of glory instead of a spirit of sorrow. He who said above, I the Lord will gather them in time, or, according to the Hebrew, I the Lord will do this suddenly in its time, says next, The Spirit of the Lord God is upon me: not that the Lord God has a Lord God, but that, according to the dispensation of the flesh he assumed, he speaks the things that are lowly. To him the Psalmist had already said: You have loved righteousness and hated iniquity; therefore God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of exultation above your fellows (Ps 44 [45]:8). For when fellows are named, understand the nature of the flesh, since God has no fellows of his substance. And because the anointing was spiritual, and by no means of a human body, as it was among the priests of the Jews, he is therefore said to have been anointed above his fellows, that is, the other saints. His anointing was fulfilled at the time when he was baptized in the Jordan, and the Holy Spirit came down upon him in the form of a dove and remained on him (John 1). Of him this same Prophet also said: A rod will come forth from the root of Jesse, and a flower will rise up from its root, and the spirit of God will rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and strength, the spirit of knowledge and godliness (Isa 11:1, 2). Hence the Savior too, after he was baptized in the Jordan, when he had come to Nazareth, where he had been brought up, entered their synagogue on the sabbath day, according to custom; and when he had stood up to read, the book of the prophet Isaiah was given to him, and on opening it he found written: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor, to preach remission to the captives and sight to the blind, to set the broken at liberty, to preach the acceptable year of the Lord. Then he rolled up the book, gave it back to the attendant, and sat down; and the eyes of all who were in the synagogue were fixed on him, and he began to say to them: Today this Scripture has been fulfilled in your ears; and all bore witness to him, and marveled at the words of grace that came forth from his mouth. If, then, the prophecy was fulfilled at that time, how do some refer what is said here to the times of the consummation? Unless perhaps we can say this: that what was then fulfilled in part is to be fulfilled more fully when the whole people of God will be righteous. For now we know in part and we prophesy in part; but when that which is perfect has come, the things that are in part will be done away (1 Cor 13). He was anointed, then, with the Holy Spirit to bring good news to the poor, or the meek, saying to them in the Gospel: Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven (Matt 5:3). And: Blessed are the gentle, for they shall possess the land (ibid., 4). And he was sent to heal those who are broken in heart (Luke 4), and who say: A broken and humbled heart God does not despise (Ps 50 [51]:19). Or, according to Symmachus and Theodotion, to bind up the wounds of sinners, to preach remission to the captives, sight to the blind, or opening to those who are shut in, which Symmachus translated more plainly, release to those in chains. Of whom, or rather to whom, it is said above: Behold, I have set you as a light of the nations, to open the eyes of the blind, to lead out the bound from their chains, and from the prison house those who sit in darkness (Isa 42:6, 7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.2-3":["And he comforted all who mourn, saying: Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted (Matt 5:5), so that he might appoint for those who mourn in Zion, and give them glory and a crown instead of ashes. Among these was the apostle Paul too, who mourned for Zion and said: For I have sorrow and continual grief (Rom 9:2); and again: I could wish that I myself were anathema from Christ for my brethren and kinsmen according to the flesh, who are Israelites (ibid., 3). And therefore those who mourned and lamented received the oil of joy instead of ashes, seeing that very many of the Jews had believed and were receiving a most pure robe instead of the garment of mourning.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.2":["By the acceptable year and the day of retribution understand the whole time of his preaching, during which he lived in the flesh. This the apostle Paul too interprets of the first coming of the Savior, saying: Behold, now is the acceptable time; behold, now is the day of salvation (2 Cor 6:2). Of this we spoke more fully above (cf. Isa 60). But if retribution is taken not in the rewards of the good but in the punishments of sinners, in accordance with its being called the day of vengeance, it must be understood of the Jewish people, upon whom, after his passion, the wrath of God came at once.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.3-4":["And in her they will be called the mighty ones of righteousness, the planting of the Lord for glorifying. And they will build the places deserted from of old, and raise up the ancient ruins, and restore the cities deserted and scattered from generation to generation. And strangers will stand and feed your flocks, and the sons of foreigners will be your farmers and vinedressers. LXX: And they will be called generations of righteousness, the planting of the Lord for glory. And they will build the everlasting desert places; those that had formerly been made desolate will be raised up, and they will renew the deserted cities, desolate for generations; and foreigners will come and feed your sheep, and ploughmen and vinedressers of other nations. After both the Apostles and apostolic men have received, instead of ashes and a spirit of sorrow, the oil of joy and the mantle, or, according to the Seventy, the robe of glory and praise, then they will be called generations of righteousness, the glorious planting of the Lord; or, according to the Hebrew ELE (אילי), with both syllables lengthened, mighty ones, and of the righteousness of God, or the planting of the Lord for glorifying; so that, when they have been glorified, or have themselves glorified the Lord, they may build the cities deserted from of old, and raise up the ancient ruins, both of the people of the Jews and of all the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.5":["They will have not only the skill of building and restoring cities, but they will also be the best of shepherds, so that, the old shepherds being driven away, to whom God had said through Ezekiel, O shepherds of Israel, do shepherds feed themselves, and not rather the sheep (Ezek 34:2)?, they may hear with the apostle Peter: Feed my sheep (John 21:17). And in a wonderful manner they will pass from masons and shepherds to farmers, that is, to ploughmen and vinedressers, so that they may say with the Apostle: We are God's building, God's husbandry (1 Cor 3:9). Thus the Savior asks the scribes and Pharisees, the vinedressers and farmers of the Jews, what he will do to the wicked vinedressers and farmers; and when they answered, He will destroy the wicked wickedly, and give the vineyard to other farmers (Matt 21:41), he went on: The kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to a nation producing its fruits (ibid., 43). The matter needs no interpretation. For how few of the princes of the churches are from the Jews, and not from foreigners and men of outside nations! Formerly serving idols, and strangers to the testament of God and aliens from his promises, having no hope and without God in the world, they now preside over the churches, and with the plough of faith they tame the hard and formerly untamed hearts of the Gentiles to bear fruit, so that they may multiply the seed of the Lord's doctrine with an abundance of good works.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.6":["But you will be called the priests of the Lord; it will be said to you, ministers of our God. You will eat the strength of the nations, and you will pride yourselves in their glory. Instead of your double confusion and shame they will praise their portion; therefore in their land they will possess double things; everlasting gladness will be theirs; for I am the Lord who loves judgment, and hates robbery in burnt offering. LXX: But you will be called the priests of the Lord, ministers of our God. You will eat the strength of the nations, and in their riches you will be admirable. Thus they will possess the land a second time, and everlasting joy will be upon their head. For I am the Lord who love righteousness and hate robberies from iniquity. The builders of the deserted cities and the shepherds of the flocks, who are themselves the ploughmen and vinedressers, that is, the sons of foreigners, are themselves also the priests of God, to whom the Prophet now says: But you will be called the priests of the Lord, and it will be said to you, ministers of our God: there is no doubt that he means the princes of the churches. Or certainly it is to be understood of the Apostles, so that the order is: When masons, shepherds, ploughmen and vinedressers from the nations have been set over the churches, you, of whom it was said, A remnant will be saved (Rom 9:27), and, Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us a seed, we should have been as Sodom, and should have been like Gomorrah (Isa 1:9), will be called priests and ministers of God, such as the sons of David also were, of whom Scripture says: And the sons of David were priests of God (2 Sam 8:18). These will eat the strength of the nations, and in their riches they will be admirable. For the crown of fathers is the glory of their children, and the progress of the people is the feast of the priests (Prov 17). Of riches of this kind Paul wrote to the Corinthians: I give thanks to my God through Jesus Christ that in everything you have been made rich in him, in all speech and in all knowledge, even as the testimony of Christ has been confirmed in you, so that you lack nothing in any grace (1 Cor 1:4ff.). The strength of the nations is the triumph of the Martyrs, and we are proud in their glory, not with that pride which lies in vice, which God resists, that he may give grace to the humble, but with that which is taken for power and glory (Jas 4). Hence Moses too had a horned face (Exod 34), who could say: In you we shall toss our enemies with the horn (Ps 43 [44]:6). And for the pride of glory Aquila translated καὶ ἐν δόξῃ αὐτῶν πορφύρᾳ ἐνδύσεσθαι, that is, you will be clothed in purple, to show the badge of royal splendor.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.7":["And what follows, Instead of double confusion and instead of shame they will praise their portion, which is not found in the Seventy, I think should be explained thus: Because you had a double confusion, both over the people of the Jews, which had departed from God, and over the nations, which served idols, you will see them, converted to the fear of God, praising their portion: without doubt the Lord, of whom the holy man also spoke: My portion is the Lord (Ps 72 [73]:26). No one can say this except one who has no other portion. Because, then, you had over them a double confusion and the shame of their sin, over which they themselves did not blush, therefore in their land, that is, in the land of the gentle and of the living, they will possess double things, since they will have believed both in the things present and in the things to come. And they will have everlasting gladness. For which we read in the Seventy, thus they will possess the land a second time. And everlasting joy will be upon their head, so that those who had possessed the land within the very narrow borders of Judea afterwards possess the whole world. Of this land the Father speaks to the Savior: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession (Ps 2:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.8":["This the Lord has granted, who loves the truth of judgment and hates robbery in burnt offering. For which the Seventy translated robbery from iniquity, as though there were some robbery that did not consist of iniquity. What he says, then, is this: God loves the poverty of the righteous more than the gifts of the rich, which come from robberies and iniquity.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And I will give their work in truth, and I will strike an everlasting covenant with them. And their seed will be known among the nations, and their offspring in the midst of the peoples. All who see them will recognize them, that these are the seed which the Lord has blessed. LXX: And I will give their labor to the righteous, and I will set an eternal testament with them. And their seed will be known among the nations, and their descendants in the midst of the peoples. Everyone who sees them will recognize them, that these are the seed blessed by God. God, who loves righteousness and judgment and detests violent burnt offerings (for whatever comes from robbery he reckons as the hire of a harlot and the price of a dog), will himself give the labor of those who will possess the land a second time, and whom he will have crowned with a double gladness, to the righteous, or, as is better in the Hebrew, in truth: so that it is no longer, as in the Law, the shadow of the truth, but the truth itself. And he will strike an everlasting covenant, not such as he had given to Moses, which has passed away, but the covenant of the Gospel, of which Christ speaks: Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away (Matt 24:35).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.9":["And then the apostolic seed will be known among the nations, and all future generations will receive the sowing of God's doctrine; by no means saying that in which the people of the circumcision formerly erred, saying: For what else does God seek but seed? Everyone who sees them will recognize at first sight that they are the seed which the Lord has blessed. For who would not recognize the people of God from their manner of life, their gentleness, continence, hospitality and all their virtues? And who, on the contrary, would not detest the bloody hands of Israel, against whom the Prophet prays, saying: Fill their faces with shame, O Lord (Ps 82 [83]:17)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.10":["Rejoicing I will rejoice in the Lord, and there has exulted my soul in my God: for he has clothed me with the garments of salvation, and with the vesture of righteousness he has encompassed me, as a bridegroom adorned with a crown, and as a bride decked with her jewels. For as the earth brings forth its shoot, and as a garden makes its seed spring up, so the Lord God will make righteousness and praise spring up before all the nations. LXX: With joy they will be glad in the Lord; let my soul exult in the Lord. For he has clothed me with the garment of salvation, and has put about me the tunic of gladness. As a bridegroom with a mitre, and as a bride he has adorned me with a woman's finery, and as the earth putting forth its flower, and as a garden makes its seeds spring up, so the Lord God brings forth righteousness and praise in the sight of all the nations. The beginning of the passage according to the Seventy, who said, With joy they will be glad in the Lord, is joined to the end of the preceding passage. But according to the Hebrews it is the opening of another section, in which the Church is brought in answering the words of Christ: Rejoicing I will rejoice in the Lord, and my soul will exult in my God: by no means in the fathers, as the Jews gloried, saying, We are the seed of Abraham, and have never been slaves to anyone (John 8:33), but in God, as Scripture says: Now the multitude of the believers had one heart and one soul (Acts 4:32). And she gives the reasons for her gladness: For he has clothed me with the garment of salvation, and with the vesture, or tunic, of righteousness and gladness, which in Hebrew is called MAIL (מעיל), he has adorned and encompassed me. For as many of us as have been baptized in Christ put on Christ, and have the tunic of righteousness, who has been made for us holiness, righteousness and redemption (Gal 1). And she sets forth a likeness of two companies in the Church, the perfect and the beginners (1 Cor 1). The perfect she compares to the beauty of the bridegroom; the beginners she likens to the adornment of the bride. Paul was perfect, who, like a bridegroom adorned with a crown and to be crowned, or, as Aquila translated, ἱερατευμένος στεφάνῳ, which in our language is bearing a crown as a priest, said by the authority of Christ speaking in him: I have fought the good fight, I have finished the course, I have kept the faith; for the rest, there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness (2 Tim 4:7). And in another place: As many of us, then, as are perfect, let us be thus minded (Phil 3:15). But he was a beginner in comparison with fullness, when he spoke in the person of beginners: When I was a little child, I spoke as a little child, I understood as a little child, I thought as a little child (1 Cor 13:11). And again: We see in part and we know in part, until that which is perfect comes (ibid., 9). And therefore he is likened to the adornment of the bride, who is adorned with a woman's finery, or, as the others translated, with vessels, or with her jewels.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.61.11":["And she sets forth examples of another comparison, of which the first refers to the bridegroom, the next to the bride. As the earth brings forth its shoot and is watered by the rains of heaven, and as a garden makes its seed spring up, which longs for the waters of springs and rivers, so, he says, the Lord will make righteousness and gladness spring up before all the nations: by no means before Israel, so as to strike down the haughtiness of the Jews, but before all the nations, which are gathered in the Church.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.1":["For Zion's sake I will not be silent, and for Jerusalem's sake I will not rest, until her righteous one goes forth as brightness, and her savior is kindled as a lamp. And the nations will see your righteous one, and all kings your renowned one, and a new name will be called for you, which the mouth of the Lord has named. And you will be a crown of glory in the hand of the Lord, and a diadem of the kingdom in the hand of your God. LXX: For Zion's sake I will not be silent, and for Jerusalem's sake I will not leave off, until her righteousness goes forth as light, and her salvation burns as a lamp. And the nations will see your righteousness, and all kings your glory, and he will call you by a new name, which the mouth of the Lord will name. And you will be a crown of beauty in the hand of the Lord, and a diadem of the kingdom in the hand of your God. The Lord and Savior had said, The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me (Luke 4:18), up to the place where it is written, All who see them will recognize them, that these are the seed which the Lord has blessed (Isa 61:1). After this promise the Church answered, Rejoicing I will rejoice in the Lord, she who in the third of the Psalms of the Steps, in the person of the repentant people, sang joyfully: I rejoiced at the things that were said to me: we shall go into the house of the Lord (Ps 121 [122]:1). Now the person of the prophet is brought in, saying: For Zion's sake I will not be silent, and for Jerusalem's sake I will not rest. By day and by night, he says, I will not close my mouth, nor will my prayer ever be silent; but I will cry out so long, and join prayers to prayers, until he who was promised comes and enlightens the whole world with his brightness. Who it is whom he seeks, whose coming he longs for, he puts more plainly: Until her righteous one goes forth as brightness, and her savior is kindled as a lamp. Or, according to the Seventy: Until her righteousness goes forth as light, and her salvation burns as a lamp: the light which said in the Gospel, I am the light of the world (John 8:12); which, when it has been kindled in Zion and in Jerusalem, will by no means shine in Judea alone, but it will be said to her: The light that is in you has been kindled; the light that went forth from the Father has begun to burn within your borders, and it will enlighten all the nations (Matt 6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.2":["And all kings will see your renowned one, O Jerusalem and Zion: he who was begotten of your stock, who, lifted up in you on the gibbet, drew all men to himself; so that the nations see his righteousness, by which the Creator of all has had mercy on the nations, and kings his glory, by which he was glorified on the cross and subjected all kingdoms to his rule. Indeed she will no longer be called Jerusalem and Zion, but she will receive a new name, which the Lord will have given her, saying to the apostle Peter: You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my Church, and the gates of hell will not prevail against it (Matt 16:18). This title he derived from the name of the Lord, so that she is called the Lord's. And her people will no longer be called by the old name, Israel, but by a new one, that is, Christian.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.3":["And she will be as a crown of beauty in the hand of the Lord, and as a diadem of the kingdom in the hand of her God, when the throng of believers has crowned her, and the diadem of empire, which the Martyrs have adorned with the variety of their gems, is in the hand of God, that he may crown his Son with their victories. Hence the apostle Paul too said to the saints: My joy and my crown.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.4":["You will no longer be called Forsaken, and your land will no more be called Desolate; but you will be called My-will-is-in-her, and your land Inhabited, for the Lord has been well pleased in you, and your land will be inhabited. LXX: And you will no longer be called Forsaken, and your land will not be called Deserted any more. For you will be called My will, and your land Inhabited. Instead of Zion and Jerusalem you will be called the Church and the Lord's; instead of Jews they will be called Christians. Nor will you be content with this end of names; but you who before were called forsaken, which in Hebrew is AZUBA (עזובה), will be called EPESI-BA (חפצי־בה), which means my will is in her; and your land, which before was called SEMEMA (שממה), desolate or deserted, will afterwards be called BULA (בעולה), which Aquila translated ἐσχημένη, Symmachus and Theodotion συνωκισμένη, the Seventy οἰκουμένη, all of which signify inhabited and possessed. It is a custom of the Hebrews always to give names to things from what befalls them: as Abram, who was first called high father, when he heard the promise, And in your seed all the nations will be blessed (Gen 12:3), was named father of many nations, that is, Abraham. And above, a name is given to the Lord Savior: Quickly take the spoils, swiftly plunder (Isa 8:1). The sons of Zebedee too, one of whom could utter the voice of thunder (Mark 3), In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God (John 1:1), were called BANEREEM (בני רעם), which means sons of thunder. But what follows, For the Lord has been well pleased in you, and your land will be inhabited, has been passed over by the Seventy; and it gives the reasons why she is called EPHSI (חפצי) and why she is called BULA (בעולה): because the Lord has been well pleased in Zion, and has made habitable her land, which before had been deserted through the error of the Jews. Or we may refer this same thing to the Church, which, possessed before by idols, had been deserted by God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.5":["For the young man will dwell with the virgin, and your sons will dwell in you. And the bridegroom will rejoice over the bride; your God will rejoice over you. LXX: And as a young man dwelling with a virgin, so will your sons dwell. And it will be that as a bridegroom is glad over a bride, so the Lord will be glad over you. The Apostle too says: Husbands, love your wives, as Christ also loved the Church (Eph 5:25). And when in another place he had set down the example, For this reason a man will leave his father and mother, and will cleave to his wife, and the two will be in one flesh, he added: This is a great mystery; but I speak in Christ and in the Church (Eph 5:31, 32). If, then, because of the shortness of the time, which is straitened, husbands ought so to have their wives as though they had them not, how much more holy will the union between the bridegroom and the bride be? This is the bridegroom of whom it is sung in the eighteenth psalm: And he as a bridegroom coming forth from his chamber (Ps 18 [19]:6). And this is the bride who is written of quite often in the Song of Songs, who has neither wrinkle nor spot (Song 4); whom Paul longs to present as a chaste virgin to one husband, so that she may be holy in body and spirit (1 Cor 2). Of her also, under the title of the beloved, the forty-fourth psalm sings: The queen stood at your right hand in gilded clothing, surrounded with variety (verse 10). As, then, the bridegroom rejoices in the bride, and the young man with the virgin, examples in which there is a holy union, so the Lord will be glad in her whose names have been changed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.6":["Upon your walls, Jerusalem, I have set watchmen; all the day and all the night they will never be silent. You who remember the Lord, do not be silent, and give him no silence, until he establishes, and until he makes Jerusalem a praise in the earth. LXX: And upon your walls, Jerusalem, I have set watchmen, all the day and all the night, who will never be silent, remembering the Lord: for there is none like you if he corrects, and he will make Jerusalem a joy upon the earth. The Prophet had said, For Zion's sake I will not be silent, and for Jerusalem's sake I will not rest, and the rest, up to the place where the passage has now ended: The bridegroom will rejoice over the bride; your God will rejoice over you. And he had promised that he would pray by day and by night that the Savior and Righteous One who had been promised might come and, like a lamp, light up the whole world. After this is brought in the person of God speaking to Jerusalem, that is, to the Church of the firstborn, and to her that was built in the Apostles and through the Apostles: Upon your walls I have set watchmen, whom we can take to be either the angels, or the Apostles and all her princes and teachers. They guard the walls of the Church, so that our adversary the devil, who as a roaring lion goes about and seeks how he may enter (1 Pet 5), may not find an occasion for laying waste the Lord's flock. These watchmen ought not to be silent by day or by night, neither in glad times nor in sad, but ought always to entreat the Lord's mercy, so that his flock and the walls of Jerusalem may be guarded and fortified by his help. Hence the word is directed to these same watchmen and teachers: O you who remember the Lord, and weary his mercy with your prayers day and night, take care that prayer is never silent in your mouth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.7":["Give him no silence, understand: the Lord; but be always troublesome, in season, out of season, and imitate the woman who importuned the hard judge. For if he laid aside the fierceness of his mind because of constant entreaty, how much more will the heavenly Father give good things to those who ask him? And you ought to ask for as long as it takes until Jerusalem, which fell in the Jews and is become an example and a curse, is turned into the praise of the whole world. And I wonder what the Seventy meant, instead of what is in the Hebrew, give him no silence, until he establishes and makes Jerusalem a praise in the earth, by saying what does not cohere in sense with what precedes: For there is none like you, if he corrects, and makes Jerusalem a joy upon the earth. Unless perhaps we fit it together with artful speech, so that it is said to the watchmen of the Church that there will be none like those who by their preaching have brought it about that Jerusalem is corrected and becomes the joy of the whole earth.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.8-9":["The Lord has sworn by his right hand and by the arm of his strength: If I give your wheat any more as food to your enemies, and if the sons of strangers drink your wine, for which you have labored! For those who gather it will eat it and praise the Lord, and those who bring it in will drink it in my holy courts. LXX: The Lord has sworn by his right hand and by the strength of his arm: If I give my wheat any more to be food for your enemies, and if the sons of strangers drink any more your wine for which you have labored! But those who have gathered will eat it and praise the Lord, and those who have gathered will drink it in my holy courts. Almighty God, who had said to the Church, Upon your walls I have set watchmen, who will never be silent, and whom I bid never to cease praying until they obtain what they pray for, now swears by his right hand and the arm of his strength. Of this we have often said that it is our Lord and Savior, who according to the Apostle is the power of God and the wisdom of God (1 Cor 1). And he swears that the wheat and wine of Jerusalem will by no means go to be food for enemies, nor will foreigners enjoy her labor; but whoever have sown in tears will reap in joy, and those who have harvested, and, the chaff cast away, have gathered the pure grain into the barns, will themselves eat the labors of their hands, and extol the Lord with everlasting praise, and drink the wine in his holy courts. By these we understand either the many mansions with the Father, if we take it of the future blessedness in the kingdom of heaven, or certainly the churches spread throughout the whole world, in which we are planted, and afterwards shall flourish in the house of the Lord. And when he says, I will by no means give your wheat any more to your enemies, and foreigners will not drink your wine for which you have labored, he shows that the labor of the Jews and all their works were formerly possessed by demons, when they wavered between God and idols, when Elijah said to them: How long do you limp on both feet? If the Lord is God, follow him (1 Kgs 18:21). And according to the typical history which is narrated in the book of Judges, the Midianites came and laid waste its fruits as far as Gaza, so that the food of men was turned into the fodder of brute beasts (Judg 6). Now this is the wheat and this the wine which they will not eat except praising the Lord, and will not drink except in his holy courts; of which the Lord said in his passion: Amen, amen, I say to you, I will not drink of the fruit of this vine until I drink it new in the kingdom of my Father (Mark 14:25). These things are fulfilled in part in the Church, when the Lord says to his disciples: Drink, my friends, and be inebriated, brethren, for wine gladdens the heart of man (Ps 103 [104]). And it is drunk in full light and at midday by Joseph with his brothers (Gen 43). And it will be fulfilled more fully when the earth is inebriated with the blessings of the Lord. The wheat too, from which the heavenly bread is made, is that of which the Lord speaks: My flesh is truly food. And again of the wine: And my blood is truly drink (John 6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.10-11":["And lest the Jews should think this was said of themselves, there is joined to it: Lift up a signal to the nations, and to the peoples of the Gentiles, so that it is not the narrow confines of the one land of Judea that are called to salvation, but the message is made heard at the ends of the earth, so that the whole world may hear of the passion of the Creator of all.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.10":["Pass through, pass through the gates, prepare the way for the people, make the road level, and pick out the stones; lift up a signal to the peoples. Behold, the Lord has made it heard at the ends of the earth: say to the daughter of Zion, Behold, your Savior comes; behold, his reward is with him, and his work before him. And they will call them a holy people, redeemed by the Lord. But you will be called a city sought out, and not forsaken. LXX: Go through my gates, and make a way for my people, and throw the stones off the way. Lift up a signal to the nations; for behold, the Lord has made it heard to the end of the earth. Say to the daughter of Zion: Behold, your Savior comes, having his reward with him, and his work before his face; and he will call it a holy people, redeemed by the Lord. But you will be called a city sought after, and not forsaken. He commanded the watchmen of the walls of Jerusalem, whom he had commanded before not to be silent and never to cease praying, to pass through the gates of Jerusalem and make a way for the people. This the half-Jews contend will come to pass in the last time, when, after the fullness of the nations, Israel is to be saved and return to the Lord. Others think it is to be fulfilled in the kingdom of heaven, when there will be true gladness, and each will receive according to his merit, and they will by no means see the Lord in humility, but will perceive him in the guise of a judge, when he comes upon the clouds with a multitude of angels to render to each according to his work. But we, following the explanation we have begun, can understand these things of the first coming as well, so as to say that the Apostles and apostolic men are commanded to go in and pass through the gates against which hell does not prevail, and to remove every hindrance from the way, so that the people may enter the Church of the Savior without any stumbling block. Hence John too cried in the wilderness: I am the voice of one crying in the desert, as Isaiah said: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight; every valley will be filled, and every mountain and hill will be brought low (John 1:23; cf. Isa 40:3, 4). And the same sense is now expressed in other words. The churchman chooses the stones, who softens all hardness from the heart of believers; of whom the same Baptist said: God is able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham (Matt 3:9). Or, according to the Seventy, he throws the stones off the way who separates the unbelieving from the believers.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Although before we had thorns and thistles, although stones, it is said to us in Isaiah: \"And cast the stones out of the way.\" And He says this for the reason, that when He wishes to walk about in our way, He may not stub His foot. The stones which are in the way, and are commanded to be cast out, are our sins. Christ does not walk in our heart, if there be any sin: at once He stumbles against the stones.\n— Homily 17, on Psalm 84"],"ISA.62.11":["Say, he says, to the daughter of Zion. She is called the daughter of Zion either because she first arose from the Jews, she of whom it says in the Song of Songs, The sons of my mother have fought against me (Song 1:5); or certainly she is called daughter because she received from God the name of adoption. For as many as received him, to them he gave power to become sons of God (John 1). And what is it that the masters and teachers are bidden to announce to the daughter of Zion? Behold, your Savior comes, who in the Hebrew is called Jesus. Hence Gabriel too, to Joseph: And you will call, he says, his name Jesus, for he will save his people from their sins (Matt 1:21). This Savior is the judge of all believers, to render to each according to his works: to the righteous rewards, to sinners everlasting punishments (Matt 16).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.62.12":["And he will call, he says, them, the Lord and Savior himself. Or, according to the Hebrew, they will call them, the Apostles and apostolic men, a holy people, and redeemed by the Lord, who have been redeemed by the blood of Christ. The city itself too will by no means be called forsaken, as she had been called before, whether among the Jews because of their trafficking, or among the nations because of their idolatry, but sought after, as it is said in Hebrew DRUSA (דרושת); so that, according to the growth and variety of their virtues, they may always receive new names.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.1-3":["Furthermore, because Idumea means both earthly and, on account of the color red, can also be understood as bloody, for this reason the Savior, carrying back to the Father the victory over the world, when the angels were crying out: Open your gates, O princes, and the king of glory shall enter in. And in Isaiah, astonished, they were asking: Who is this who comes up from Edom, with garments tawny from Bozrah, so beautiful in a white robe? And one spoke to him face to face: Why is your clothing red, and your garments like those of men who tread in the winepress? He himself speaks in triumph, displaying the palms of his cross: I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there is no man with me.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","That your foot may be dipped in blood. Because his own foot was dipped in his own blood. \"I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there was no man with me.\" And again: \"Who is this who ascends from Edom? his garments red from Bosor.\" Edom is interpreted \"blood,\" Bosor \"flesh\": it signifies our Lord himself crucified.\n— Homily 7, on Psalm 67"],"ISA.63.1":["Who is this who comes from Edom, with dyed garments from Bozrah? This one, beautiful in his robe, marching in the greatness of his strength. LXX: Who is this who comes from Edom, with tawny garments from Bosor? So beautiful in his robe, violence with strength. This whole passage, from the place where according to the Seventy it is said, Be enlightened, be enlightened, Jerusalem, for your light has come, and the glory of the Lord has risen upon you (Isa 60:1), up to the present passage, where it is said, Who is this who comes from Edom, with dyed garments from Bozrah?, many of our people refer to the end of the world, in which they contend these things are to be fulfilled, whether carnally or spiritually (for the opinion of most is divided). But we, since we read above in this same section too, The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to bring good news to the poor, which the Lord and Savior, having read the volume of Isaiah in the synagogue of the Jews, showed to be fulfilled in himself, saying, Today this Scripture has been fulfilled in your ears (Luke 4:21); and since it is now said of the same one that after his passion he ascended to the Father bloodied, we are compelled by necessity to take everything that is said of the first coming of the Savior. For it cannot be, as most of our people strive to make it, that things joined together in the works should be divided in time. Nor do we deny that it is a task of the greatest toil to join all these things together, and so to show that they are to be fulfilled spiritually at the consummation as to teach that they have already been fulfilled in Christ both according to the flesh and according to the spirit. Since, then, the Savior has come to the daughter of Zion, whose reward is with him and his work before him; and the righteous one has gone forth as brightness, and the Savior has been kindled as a lamp; and the bridegroom has rejoiced over his bride the Church, nay rather her God has exulted over her, who is himself both bridegroom and Lord: therefore, according to the assumption of the flesh and the passion of the cross, it is said to him: That your foot may be dipped in blood (Ps 67 [68]:24). And under the name of Judah it is prophesied in Genesis: Judah, your brothers will praise you; your hands will be on the necks of your enemies (Gen 49:8ff.), the sons of your father will worship you. Judah is a lion's whelp; to the prey, my son, you have gone up; resting, you have couched as a lion, and as a lioness: who will rouse him (Gen 49:8, 9)? And again: Binding his foal to the vineyard, and his she-ass, O my son, to the vine, he will wash his robe in wine, and his cloak in the blood of the grape (ibid., 11). This is he whom the angelic powers, seeing him ascend bloodied to the Father, command the other angels, and hear from them: Lift up your gates, O princes, and be lifted up, O everlasting gates, and the king of glory will come in. Who is this king of glory? The Lord strong and mighty, the Lord mighty in battle (Ps 23 [24]:7, 8). And again: The Lord of hosts, he is the king of glory (ibid., 10). We have set this down beforehand to show that the present passage too agrees with the other testimonies. Hence it is said in the Song of Songs too: My kinsman is ruddy and white (Song 5:10): ruddy in the passion, white in the resurrection. The angels, then, terrified by the strangeness of the thing (for the mystery of the passion and resurrection of Christ, according to the apostle Paul (1 Cor 2), had been unknown to all past generations), ask: Who is this who comes bloodied from the land of Edom? For EDOM (אדם) is expressed in our language as both earthly and bloody. With dyed garments from Bozrah? Many, falling into a pious error, think this is to be understood of the Lord's flesh, that is, BASAR (בשר); but if this word signified flesh, it would be written with the letter SIN in the middle, whereas now it is written with SADE, and means strong and fortified. This we take either of Jerusalem (Ps 59 [60]:11), which formerly was surrounded with very strong walls, in which the Lord suffered; or of hell, of which it is written in the fifty-ninth and in the hundred and seventh psalm: Who will lead me into the fortified city (Ps 107 [108]:11)? in which the souls of the dead are shut up, and which is surrounded with very strong guards. It must be known also that according to the history BOSRA (בצרה) is not in Edom but in Moab. There follows: So beautiful in his robe, marching in the greatness of his strength. Of him the forty-fourth psalm too sings: Beautiful in comeliness above the sons of men. Gird your sword upon your thigh, O most mighty. In your comeliness and your beauty set out, proceed prosperously, and reign (Ps 44 [45]:3-5). For which the Seventy translated, violence with strength. What sense this has, let the prudent reader inquire.\n\nI who speak righteousness, and am a champion to save. LXX: I who speak righteousness, and the judgment of salvation. The Lord answered the angels who asked: Do you ask who I am, who ascend bloodied to the heavens, and that the blood with which I am sprinkled has given me not unsightliness but beauty? I am he to whom the Father has given all judgment (John 5). Of him the Psalmist too said: O God, give your judgment to the king, and your righteousness to the king's son (Ps 71 [72]:1). I who speak righteousness, so as to repay evil to the evil and good to the good; who came to fight against the adverse powers, and to preach remission to the captives, and to free the bound from prison, so that the adversaries may feel punishment and the captives liberty.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.2":["Why then is your clothing red, and your garments like those of men treading the winepress? LXX: Why are your garments red, and your clothing like that of men treading the winepress, full and trodden down? Where it now says red, the Hebrew reads EDOM (אדום). So above too, where Edom is written, it is not the name of a place but a word for blood. And again the angels ask and say: We have learned that you are he who speaks righteousness, and that the salvation of all has been placed in your judgment. Now we wish to know why your garments are as if dyed with new wine; or what cause there was for you to stain with blood the tunic that was woven from the top and cannot be torn, and that had from the virgin's womb such whiteness as no fuller on earth could make? For mercy befits you more than cruelty, whiteness more than gore. To them the Lord answered, not with one little verse as before, but with many words, so as to teach everything to those who did not know, lest they be compelled to ask again.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.3":["I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there is no man with me. I have trodden them in my fury, and trampled them in my wrath; and their blood has been sprinkled upon my garments, and I have polluted all my clothing For the day of vengeance is in my heart; the year of my redemption has come. I looked round, and there was no helper; I sought, and there was none to give aid. And my own arm saved for me, and my indignation itself helped me. And I trampled the peoples in my fury, and made them drunk in my indignation, and brought down their strength to the earth. The Seventy, instead of what we have said, I have trodden the winepress alone, translated full and trodden down, which is rather to be read with the preceding passage than taken as the beginning of the following one. The rest they translated thus: And of the nations there is no man with me; and I trampled them in my fury, and broke them like earth, and brought down their blood to the earth, and I have defiled all my garments. For the day of retribution has come upon them, and the year of redemption is at hand. I looked, and there was no helper; I considered, and there was none to give aid; and my arm rescued them, and my fury came on, and I trampled them in my wrath, and brought down their blood to the earth. For the winepress, which in Hebrew is called GETH (גת), Theodotion set down the Hebrew word itself, PHURA (פורה). But Symmachus is better in this place, and we have followed him. For the word PHURA is ambiguous, and generally signifies both winepress and flask. Something must be said, then, about the winepress: that according to the custom of holy Scripture it is sometimes put for the vengeance and punishment of sinners, sometimes for the gathering of new fruits. It is put for penalties and torments when Jeremiah, lamenting the overthrow of Jerusalem in Lamentations, says: The Lord has trodden the winepress for the virgin daughter of Judah; therefore I weep (Lam 1:15, 16). In a good sense the eighth and the eighty-third psalms are inscribed 'for the winepresses'. Of these we shall speak in their own places, if life goes with us, the Lord granting it. This winepress, in which both punishments for the evil and rewards for the good are trodden out by the Savior, he alone trod, and he had no helper. For neither angel nor archangel, Thrones, Dominions, nor any of the heavenly powers assumed a human body, and suffered for us, and trampled and crushed the adverse powers, but only he who speaks in the psalm: Save me, O Lord, for the holy one has failed (Ps 11 [12]:1); so much so that even the most confident apostle Peter, most firm in the truth of faith, fled terrified with fear, nay even denied the Lord (Matt 27). And what follows, And their blood has been sprinkled upon my garments, and I have polluted all my clothing, must by no means be understood as though we believed that demons and the adverse powers have blood; but everything is to be taken tropologically, when the most merciful God, in order to instruct his people and free them from the bonds of captivity, is compelled to strike the enemies.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Out of the whole world he chose twelve apostles: out of the twelve one departed, eleven remained. The cross came, ten fled: one Peter remained, one with the One. And that one fled, and would that he had fled: and he denies. Therefore let us say, the whole human race perished. Since it had perished, therefore the Lord, being crucified, said: I have trodden the winepress alone, and of the nations there was not a man with me. At that time the psalm was fulfilled: Save me, O Lord, for the holy one has failed. There is none who does good, there is none, even to one. He who had promised, saying If it be necessary for me to die, or to go into prison, I will not deny you, denied.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"ISA.63.4":["For the day of vengeance, he says, is in my heart; the year of my redemption has come. Of this we read above too (cf. Isa 61), in a good sense, to preach the acceptable year of the Lord and the day of retribution to our God; and now it is taken both in a bad and in a good sense. In a bad sense: For the day of vengeance is in my heart. In a good sense: The year of my redemption has come; so that at the time when the adversaries are punished, the people of God might be set free, nay rather redeemed by the precious blood of the Lamb, who in the Apocalypse of John is said to have been slain. Of this day of retribution Moses too prophesied by the Holy Spirit: And he will render vengeance to his enemies, and will repay those who hate him. I looked round, and there was no helper; I sought, and there was none to give aid (Deut 32:41).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.5-6":["He had also said in the psalm: And I looked for one who would grieve with me, and there was none, and for one who would comfort me, and I found none (Ps 68 [69]:21). For he, though he was in the form of God, did not count it robbery to be equal with God, but emptied himself (Phil 2), taking the form of a servant, and became obedient to the Father unto death, even the death of the cross; and therefore he gave him a name above every name, so that he might save for himself by his right hand those who believe in him, and his indignation, which was against the adversaries, might set the people of God free. Or he rescued the believers by his arm, and no longer put off his fury; and he brought down their blood to the earth, either that of the demons, τροπικῶς, or certainly the blood of the Jews; for which the other translators rendered contention; who strove with all zeal that they might let the bound people go.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.7":["I will remember the mercies of the Lord, the praise of the Lord for all that the Lord has rendered to us, and for the multitude of good things to the house of Israel, which he has bestowed on them according to his indulgence and according to the multitude of his mercies. LXX: I have remembered the mercy of the Lord; I will remember the mighty works of the Lord in all that he has rendered to us: the Lord, a good judge to the house of Israel, will repay us according to his mercy and according to the multitude of his righteousness. Now that the passage on the coming of the Savior has been completed, the first coming, as we judge, the second, as most suppose, in which is understood the voice of one judging, nay rather fighting and slaughtering the adversaries of himself and of his people, and in which he is described as bloodied and victorious, another περικοπή begins, in which the Prophet speaks in the person of the people, recounting God's benefits toward it and the hardness of its own heart, on account of which Jerusalem was given over to burning and Israel to captivity. This the Jews refer to the Babylonian times. But we have shown by order and by reason that it is said of the present time, in which they serve the Romans, and Israel having been shut out, the crowd of the nations has taken its place. Let us run, then, through each member of the discourse, and set them forth briefly, so that the exposition may not be rambling. Pressed by the weight of evils, I have hope in nothing else but in the mercy of God, who shows mercy to thousands of those who love him, and extends his mercy to those who know him. From this it is shown that the mercy of the Lord comes to the aid of those who love and know God. For it is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy (Rom 9). And unless the Lord builds the house and guards the city, he who builds and guards it watches in vain (Ps 126 [127]). For he himself will grant strength, so that we may do valiantly and be able to say: My strength and my help, and my refuge in the day of my evils (Jer 16). These evils in the present place we ought to take not for vices and sins but for distresses. And as for what follows according to the Seventy, I will remember the mighty works of the Lord in all that he has repaid me, for which the Hebrew contains the praises of the Lord, let us understand signs and miracles, for which 'mighty works' is often put, as there: My glory I will not give to another, nor my mighty works to graven images (Isa 42:8). And elsewhere: They will proclaim his mighty works in the islands, that is, either to the nations of the whole world, or to the churches, which are founded on a firm mass, and are struck rather than overthrown. Sometimes virtue is by no means taken for strength and miracles, but for good conduct, as in that saying of the Apostles, of Peter: Supply virtue in your knowledge (2 Pet 1:5); and of Paul: If there is any virtue, and if any praise (Phil 4:8). In accordance with which it is said in another place too: Better is barrenness with virtue. The first understanding of virtue befits God, the second men. And instead of what we have translated, For all that the Lord has rendered to us, which can be taken both in a good and in a bad sense, Symmachus set it down more plainly as a repayment of good things, saying: For all things in which he has done good to us, and for the multitude of goodness which he has shown toward the house of Israel. For which the Seventy translated: The Lord, a good judge to the house of Israel, has repaid us according to his mercy and according to the multitude of his righteousness. No one can say this except one who understands that he justly bears what he suffers. Thus in the seventy-second psalm, whose opening is, How good is the God of Israel to those who are upright in heart, the prophet speaks in the person of those who are troubled by the judgments of God: But my feet were almost moved, my steps had well nigh slipped, seeing the peace of sinners, and the rest, up to the end of the psalm. Hence the apostle Paul, who had known the Lord as a good and righteous judge, speaks with confidence: There is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the righteous judge will render to me; and not to me only, but also to all who love the coming of our Lord and Savior (2 Tim 4:8). Nor is there any difference, as the heretics would have it, between good and righteous, for the same Apostle writes: So the law is holy, and the commandment holy and righteous and good (Rom 7:12), so that goodness is understood as the genus and righteousness as the species. Wherefore he writes to the Romans: For scarcely will anyone die for a righteous man; for perhaps for a good man someone would dare to die (Rom 5:7).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.8-9":["And he said: Surely they are my people, sons who do not deny; and he became their Savior. In all their tribulation he was not troubled, and the Angel of his face saved them; in his love and in his indulgence he himself redeemed them, and he bore them and lifted them up all the days of the age. But they provoked him to anger and afflicted the spirit of his Holy One and he was turned to be their enemy, and he himself fought them down. LXX: And he said: Is it not my people? Sons will by no means reject. And he became their salvation out of all their tribulation; not an envoy nor an angel, but he himself saved them, because he loved them and spared them; he himself redeemed them, and took them up, and exalted them all the days of the age. But they did not believe, and they embittered his Holy Spirit; he was turned to be their enemy, he himself fought against them. Where we have translated, In all their tribulation he was not troubled, the word which in Hebrew is LO, and is an adverb of negation, for not, can also be read as he himself, so that the sense is: In all their tribulation he himself was troubled, that is, God; so that he himself bore not only our sins but also our tribulations. For he bears our infirmities and grieves for us. Further, the Seventy put down something else which is not found in the Hebrew, not an envoy nor an angel, but he himself saved them, of which we shall speak in its place. So God, who is a righteous judge to the house of Israel, and who tempers the severity of righteousness with the mercy of one who judges, said: I have begotten sons and exalted them, and they have spurned me. Yet because they are my people, and have once been named sons, they will not perish for ever; if they cease to spurn and deny me, they will find a Savior, because in all their tribulation he himself was troubled. Or, he was not troubled, so that he forsook them for a little while, and compelled them, stripped of his help, to pray. Or certainly, he by no means troubled them, but on the contrary, when others persecuted them, he was their helper, and sent his angel to free them from danger. Or, according to the Seventy, he did not wish to save his people through angels and prophets and other holy men, but he himself came down to the lost sheep of the house of Israel, to carry back the sick sheep on his shoulders, and to find the drachma that had been lost, and to run joyfully to meet the prodigal son as he returned. For this reason the bride says in the Song of Songs: Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth: not through the Patriarchs, Moses and the Prophets, but let him assume my body, let him dwell in my flesh, let the Word become flesh, and so let him kiss me, dwelling in me, that he may be Emmanuel. Not as an envoy, then, nor as an angel, but he himself will save those who have received salvation, not by the merit of their works but by the love of God. For God so loved the world that he gave his only-begotten Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.10":["But if the prudent reader should answer in silent thought, Why have many not been saved, if he himself saved them, and loved and spared his sons, and redeemed them with his blood, and took them up and exalted them once assumed?, a clear reason is given: But they did not believe, and they embittered his Holy Spirit, or the Spirit of his Holy One, which in Hebrew is CADESO (קדש). God, then, willed to save those who desired it, and he called them to salvation, so that their will might have its reward; but they would not believe. Otherwise it is written of John too: There was a man sent from God, whose name was John; he came to bear witness to the light, that all might believe through him (John 1:6). Nor is he at once at fault if many would not believe; but the will of the one who came was this, that all should believe and be saved. And if whoever has exasperated and afflicted the Holy Spirit, or the Spirit of his Holy One, that is, of Christ, exasperates God, it is because the Holy Spirit is of the same nature with the Father and the Son. Wherefore the Apostle commands: Do not grieve the Holy Spirit, in whom you have been sealed (Eph 4:30). This Holy Spirit withdraws from a body subject to sins, and wisdom does not enter a perverse soul (Wis 1). Hence David too, conscious of his sins, prayed that he might not lose the Holy Spirit, saying: And do not take your Holy Spirit from me (Ps 50 [51]:13). And so that we may know that everyone who exasperates the Holy Spirit offends God, and from a friend becomes an enemy and a foe, the apostle Peter speaks more expressively in the Acts of the Apostles: Why have you agreed to lie to the Holy Spirit? You have not lied to men but to God (Acts 5:9). This is the Holy Spirit of whom the Lord said in the Gospel: If you love me, keep my commandments; and I will ask the Father, and he will give you another Paraclete, to be with you for ever, the Spirit of truth (John 14:15, 16). Of him we find also in the Wisdom which is written under the name of Solomon: For the Holy Spirit of discipline will flee deceit, and will withdraw from foolish thoughts (Wis 1:5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.11-12":["And he remembered the days of the age, of Moses, of his people. Where is he who brought them up out of the sea with the shepherds of his flock? Where is he who put in its midst the Spirit of his Holy One? Who led Moses by the right hand with the arm of his majesty; who divided the waters before them, to make for himself an everlasting name; who led them through the depths like a horse in the desert that does not stumble. Like a beast going down into the plain, the Spirit of the Lord was its leader; so you led your people, to make for yourself a name of glory. LXX: And he remembered the ancient days, he who brought up from the land the shepherd of the sheep. Where is he who put the Holy Spirit in them? Who led Moses by the right hand, the arm of his glory. So he led his people through the mighty water in his sight, to make for himself an everlasting name. He led them through the deep like a horse through the wilderness, and they did not labor. And like cattle through the plain the Spirit came down from the Lord, and was their leader: so you led your people, to make for yourself a name of glory. The Lord, who from a protector became the adversary of the people of the Jews, who provoked his Holy Spirit to anger, and who fought them down, himself remembered the ancient days, when Moses prayed for them in the wilderness, saying: Either forgive them this offense, or if you do not, blot me out of the book which you have written (Exod 32:31, 32). Isaiah, then, remembering the ancient history, says: Where is that Moses who brought them up out of the Red Sea? Where is the shepherd of the sheep? Where is he who labored with the other shepherds of the Lord's flock? Who obtained the Spirit of God by his prayer and entreaties, and put it in the Lord's flock? Or certainly it is to be understood thus: Where is that mercy of the Lord with which he once had pity on his people, so as to grant them the grace of the Holy Spirit too; who led Moses his servant to the right-hand side, not to the left, with the arm of his majesty; who divided the waters before them, to make for himself an everlasting name, so that his power might be told even to the present day?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.13-14":["For he led his people through the boundless depths of the waters like a horse through the wilderness, and like cattle over the plain, and the Spirit of the Lord was its leader, that is, of the Lord's flock (Exod 14). By Spirit here we ought to understand the angel who was the leader of the people of Israel, according to what is written: Who makes his angels spirits, and his ministers a burning fire (Ps 103 [104]:4). And in the Epistle to the Hebrews: These are ministering spirits, sent forth to serve for the sake of those who are to possess salvation (Heb 1:14). Let us consider whether what is written in the Acts of the Apostles, The Spirit of the Lord caught away Philip, and the eunuch saw him no more (Acts 8:39), ought to be taken of an angel. There are some who maintain that the angel did these things in the Holy Spirit. We run through what is plain so as to dwell on what is more obscure.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.15":["Look down from heaven, and see from your holy dwelling and that of your glory. Where is your zeal and your strength? The multitude of your bowels and of your mercies toward me have held themselves back. For you are our father; and Abraham has not known us, and Israel has been ignorant of us. You, O Lord, are our father, our redeemer from of old is your name. LXX: Turn from heaven, and see from your holy house and that of your glory: where is your zeal and your strength? Where is the multitude of your mercies and of your compassions, with which you bore with us? For you are our father, because Abraham has not known us, and Israel has not known us. But you, O Lord our father, deliver us: from the beginning your name is upon us. You who granted such great things to the people as the preceding discourse has related, so that you even deemed us worthy of the fellowship of your Spirit, now too look down from heaven and see our works, if indeed they are worthy of you. Why do you turn away your face from us? Now heaven is called the holy dwelling and the house of his glory (Ps 43 [44]), according to that saying: Heaven is my throne, and the earth the footstool of my feet (Isa 66:1); and in another place: He who dwells in the heavens will laugh at them; and: To you I have lifted up my eyes, who dwell in heaven (Ps 122 [123]:1). Not that almighty God, who holds heaven in his palm and the earth in his fist, is shut in by any place, but that the things which are holier are called his place and dwelling. Thus Solomon, who built the house of God, says in prayer to him: The heavens of heavens and the earth do not suffice you (Sir 16:18). And in the Lord's Prayer it is said: Your will be done, as in heaven, so also on earth (Matt 6:10). Wherever, then, the will of God is done, that is his dwelling and the house of God. As for what follows, Where is your zeal and your strength?, let us discuss it by that testimony in which God, through Ezekiel, speaks to Jerusalem, who had followed her lovers: Now I will not be angry with you, and my zeal has departed from you (Ezek 16:42). And the sense is: Because we have sinned and you have begun to hate us, your zeal has departed from us, the zeal which does not depart when he speaks through Zechariah: I have been zealous for Zion and Jerusalem with great zeal (Zech 1:14). Hence he says too in what follows: And I will be angry over the nations that have gathered against her round about. When zeal departs, the strength of God departs too, and the affection of a father's bowels is overcome, while the incredible mercy of God is conquered by the greatness of our sins, so that he who could not bear to see me oppressed holds himself back from helping me.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.16":["For you, he says, are our father, you who are the creator of all. Abraham does not know us, and Israel is ignorant of us, because we have offended you, nor do they recognize as sons those whom they perceive not to be loved by their God. A sudden question arises: why did he set down Abraham and Israel, that is, Jacob, and leave the name of Isaac unspoken (Gen 32)? To this we shall answer that, when the beginning and the end are set down, the middle too is named. Or thus: Abraham was called to faith from the nations, and according to the character of his former and his subsequent condition received a change of name. Jacob too reached it only by much toil, to be called Israel. Hence too Abraham had three wives, and Jacob four; but Isaac kept his ancient name from beginning to end, and, signifying the chastity of the Church, was content with one wife. Those, then, who pray for a change from sinners to a better state take on the likeness of those to whom joy succeeded after sorrow. And this is all that they ask: that, since he is their father and has deigned to give them this name, he may not forget his sons, lest through them the name of God be blasphemed among the nations.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.17-18":["Return, he says, O Lord, or turn us back, for the sake of your servants, Abraham and Israel, who have not known us, or whom we believe to be among the people. For as if for nothing, and without any toil, our adversaries have possessed your holy people. Or, turn us back so that, according to the Seventy, we may possess a little of your holy mountain, since we cannot possess the whole of your mountain; so that when we have had a part we may come to its fullness and contemplate the glory of the Only-begotten from the Father, full of grace and truth (John 1); of which it is said in this same prophet: In the last days the mountain of the Lord will be manifest (Isa 2:2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.17":["Why have you made us stray, O Lord, from your ways? You have hardened our heart, so that we did not fear you. Return for the sake of your servants, the tribes of your inheritance. As if for nothing they have possessed your holy people; our enemies have trodden down your sanctification. We have become as in the beginning, when you did not rule over us, nor was your name called upon us. LXX: Why have you led us astray, O Lord, from your holy way? You have hardened our hearts, so that we did not fear you. Return for the sake of your servants, for the sake of the tribes of your inheritance, that we may possess a little of your holy mountain; our adversaries have trodden down your sanctuary; we have become as from the beginning, when you did not rule over us, nor was your name called upon us. The Epistle which Paul writes to the Corinthians, though it is written to the people of one city, according to the variety of its inhabitants, that is, of saints and sinners, now praises them, now corrects them, now teaches, now rebukes; it calls them to continence; it does not refuse marriage; it draws them back from idolatry; it instructs them about the resurrection; it stretches out a hand to second marriage, lest it give place to fornication. We have said this so that we may understand that the present passage too, which is woven entirely out of the prayer of the people entreating God, belongs either to the righteous or to sinners, and that they now praise the Lord, now raise a question with the Lord and refer their own fault to God. Hence after the rest they say this too: Why have you made us stray, O Lord, from your ways, or led us astray from your way? You have hardened our heart so that we did not fear you? Not that God is the cause of error and of hardness, but that his patience, as he waits for our salvation and does not correct those who do wrong, seems to be a cause of error and hardness. Hence, when he is vehemently angry with some, he withholds his hand from striking and says: I will not punish your daughters when they have committed fornication, nor your brides when they have committed adultery (Hos 4:14). For he scourges every son whom he receives, and he strikes in order to amend (Heb 12). Thus of those who have by no means lost the name of sons, but are drawn back to repentance through punishment, he says: I will visit their iniquities with the rod, and their sins with scourges; but my mercy I will not take from them (Ps 88 [89]:33). For there is a sorrow that leads to life, and there is a sorrow that leads to death. Hence the sinner too says in the psalm: You have made our paths turn aside from your way, and have humbled us in the place of affliction (Ps 43 [44]:19). And for forty years the people wander in the desert, so that they may not find their former way nor return to the Egyptians (Num 32). In Hosea too the paths of Jerusalem are hedged in and closed with thorns, so that she may not follow her lovers, and, compelled by necessity, may return to her former husband (Hos 2). And God is said to have hardened the heart of Pharaoh, so that he would not let his people go, and so that he might be afflicted with ten plagues (Exod 4). This question Paul discussed most carefully to the Romans (Rom 9), and we have touched on it in a certain work.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","As for what is set down above, Why have you made us stray (Isa 63:17)?, in what sense it is to be taken Jeremiah teaches most fully: You have led me astray, O Lord, and I was led astray; you took hold of me, and you prevailed (Jer 20:7). For while you promise me mercy, and like a merciful father you conceal your severity, and like a skilful physician you hide the sharpest knife so as not to frighten the sick man before you heal him, you have made me careless. For this reason God says of Jerusalem too: Behold, I will lead her astray, and I will make her like a desert, and set her like a land without water, and I will speak to her heart; and I will give her possessions from there, and the valley of Achor to open understanding (Hos 2:14, 15). Let us consider the order of each thing: he leads her astray, and makes her deserted and without water, so that she may suffer thirst for the virtues. After she has said, My soul has thirsted for you, how many ways my flesh for you (Ps 62 [63]:2), then he will speak to her heart and console her as she mourns. And he will give her a possession from there, that is, from the repentance and sorrow which works salvation. And the humility of her troubled heart (for this is what the valley of Achor means) opens her understanding, so that she may not be ignorant of her Creator.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.18":["Our enemies, he says, have trodden down your sanctuary. There is no doubt that he means the Temple, which the victorious Romans trod down.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.63.19":["And we have become as in the beginning, before we were called in Abraham, and while we were in Egypt, having no God, nor kings, nor princes, nor Prophets, nor the Law of the commandments of God (Hos 3); all of which was fulfilled after the passion of the Lord, and is being fulfilled today. For since they said, His blood be upon us and upon our children (Matt 27:25), an everlasting curse remains, and God does not rule over them, nor is his name called upon them, since they are by no means called the people of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.64.1-3":["O that you would rend the heavens and come down! At your presence the mountains would flow down. As the burning of fire they would melt away; the waters would burn with fire, so that your name might be made known to your enemies; at your presence the nations would be troubled. When you do wonders, we shall not endure them; you came down, and at your presence the mountains flowed down. LXX: If you open heaven, trembling will seize the mountains because of you; and they will melt as wax melts before the fire, and fire will burn up the adversaries; and your name will be manifest among the adversaries; at your presence the nations will be troubled. When you do glorious things, trembling will seize the mountains because of you. Since our enemies have trodden down your sanctuary, and we have become as in the beginning, when your name was not called upon us, therefore we entreat and say: O that you would rend the heavens and come down, and that you who are always promised would at some time fulfill your promises. This was said at the time when the Savior had not yet come, nor had yet assumed from the virgin's womb, out of our nature and substance, the man whom he would save; so that, as we have borne the image of the earthly, we may bear the image of the heavenly (1 Cor 15). If you did this, they say, and the heavens, or heaven, were opened, at the coming of your majesty the mountains would flow down, or trembling would seize the mountains, and they would be consumed as wax is consumed before the fire. To Ezekiel too the heavens were opened, and he saw a great vision (Ezek 1). And Moses as well, in the blessings of Deuteronomy, prays: May the Lord open to you his good treasure, the heaven, to give you blessing (Deut 28). And in the Gospel (Matt 3) John the Baptist is related to have seen the heavens opened, and the Holy Spirit coming down upon the Lord in the form of a dove. The mountains which at the coming of the Lord, of whom it is written, God is a consuming fire (Deut 4:24), will be consumed and melt like wax, are the adverse powers and all who raise themselves up against the knowledge of God. Of them it is sung in the ninety-sixth psalm too: He saw, and the earth was shaken. The mountains melted like wax at the presence of the Lord, at the presence of the God of the whole earth (Ps 96 [97]:4, 5). For if you came down and fulfilled your promises, the waters of the sea would melt away (according to Symmachus), and that fire would consume all their saltness. Of this it is written in another psalm too: As smoke vanishes, so let them vanish; as wax is consumed before the fire, so let sinners perish before the face of God (Ps 67 [68]:2). And this is to be noted, that when the waters of the sea have been consumed by the divine fire, then the name of the Lord Savior will be made known to his enemies; of whom it is said in the sixty-seventh psalm: The tongue of your dogs, from the enemies, by him; so that him whom they had not perceived through his benefits they may come to know through the overthrow of their city and their own captivity. And at his presence the nations will be troubled, or, as is said more expressively in the Hebrew, will be moved: so that those which before were immovable may come to salvation. And when he has done wonders, and shown in the Gospel the signs which he once showed in Egypt and in the wilderness, they will confess that they cannot bear the glory of his coming, or that trembling has seized the mountains. And beautifully, according to the Hebrew, as though those who above had entreated, O that you would rend the heavens and come down! At your presence the mountains would flow down, had been heard, they afterwards add: You came down: you, the Word, were made flesh and dwelt among us, truly Emmanuel, which means God with us. And therefore at your presence all the mountains flowed down, of which we spoke above. The Hebrews expound this passage thus: The fire will burn up the wicked just as the waters boil with the heat of fire; for by AMASIM (חמסים) they wish to be understood not consumption and melting, as the others translated, but the wicked.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.64.4-5":["From of old they have not heard, nor perceived with their ears; the eye has not seen, O God, besides you, what you have prepared for those who wait for you. You have met him who rejoices and does righteousness in your ways; they will remember you. LXX: From of old we have not heard, nor have our eyes seen a God besides you, and your works, which you will do for those who wait for mercy; for he meets those who do righteousness, and they will remember your ways. A paraphrase of this testimony the apostle Paul, as a Hebrew of the Hebrews, takes from the authentic books in the Epistle which he writes to the Corinthians, not rendering word for word, which he altogether disdains to do, but expressing the truth of the sense, which he uses to support what he wished (1 Cor 2). Let the ravings of the apocrypha, then, fall silent, which on the pretext of this testimony are thrust upon the churches of Christ. Of them it can truly be said that the devil sits in ambush with the rich in the apocrypha, to kill the innocent. And again: He lies in wait in the hidden place like a lion in his den; he lies in wait to seize the poor (Ps 9 [10]:8). For the Ascension of Isaiah and the Apocalypse of Elijah contain this testimony. And on this pretext, and through many things of this kind, silly women of the Spains, and above all of Lusitania, laden with sins, led away by various desires, always learning and never coming to the knowledge of the truth (2 Tim 3), were deceived into taking up the monstrosities of Basilides, of Balsamus and Thesaurus, of Barbelo too and Leusibora, and of the remaining names. Of these the apostolic man Irenaeus, bishop of Lyons and martyr, writes most carefully, explaining the origins of many heresies, and above all of the Gnostics, who through Mark the Egyptian deceived noble women, first of the Gauls about the Rhône, then of the Spains, mixing pleasure with fables and claiming for their ignorance the name of knowledge (Irenaeus, Against Heresies, book 1). What the people says in the present passage is this: that they have never come to know, either by ears or by eyes, what God prepares for his saints in the future; that he meets those who do righteousness and remember his ways. Further, according to the Seventy, they say that they have never known any other God besides him who is truly God, nor seen such and so great works of another, which he will do for those who wait for his mercy and do righteousness and remember his ways. And how is it written, No one has ever seen God (John 1:18), and again, No one sees my face and lives (Exod 33:20)? God, then, is by no means set forth in words, nor is he visible to the eyes; but he is seen by those of whom it is written: Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God (Matt 5:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.64.5-6":["Behold, you are angry, and we have sinned; in them we have always been, and we shall be saved. And we have become as one unclean all of us, all our righteousnesses as the cloth of a menstruous woman. And we have all fallen like a leaf, and our iniquities, like the wind, have carried us away. LXX: Behold, you are angry, and we have all sinned; therefore we have gone astray, and we have all become as unclean, all our righteousness as the cloth of a woman who sits apart; and we have fallen away like leaves because of our iniquities; as the wind will carry us away. The order is inverted. It is not because you are angry that we have sinned, but because we have sinned that you are angry. And because we have sinned, you are angry with us, O Lord; therefore we have gone astray and left the right way. Or, according to the Hebrew: we who have always been in sins shall be saved only by your mercy, we who of ourselves are unclean; and whatever righteousness we seem to have is compared to the cloth of a woman who sits apart, or of a menstruous woman. For this Theodotion set down the Hebrew word itself, EDDIM (עדים), which Symmachus translated κυουσῶν, that is, of women in childbirth; Aquila μαρτυριῶν, that is, of testimonies: when in the first marital union the blood proves a bride a virgin. And we have fallen away, he says, like leaves from the trees, which because of our iniquities are scattered by the blasts of the winds. Here it must be considered that the righteousness which is in the Law is called uncleanness in comparison with the purity of the Gospel. For what was formerly glorified has not been glorified, because of the surpassing glory. Hence the apostle Paul too, who had fulfilled everything according to the righteousness which is in the Law, says that he counted all things as loss, that he might gain Christ (Phil 3), and because of the surpassing knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ, for whose sake he reckons all things as dung, that he may gain Christ and be found in him, not having his own righteousness, which is from the Law, but the righteousness which is from God through the faith of Christ. For there is a righteous man who perishes in his righteousness (Eccl 7), if after the truth of the Gospel he wishes to practice the shadows of the Law, and not to pursue justly what is just. Hence, according to the most wise Solomon, every man seems righteous to himself when he lives in the Law; to whom the Lord says in the Gospel: You are those who justify yourselves before men (Luke 16:15). Fleeing this, Paul says: I am conscious of nothing against myself, yet I am not justified by this (1 Cor 4:4). But out of sin, the righteousness of God, of which the same Solomon speaks: Understand true righteousness. There is, then, another righteousness which has no truth, and this is the true righteousness, of which again it is said in the Gospel: Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness (Matt 5:6); which many prophets and righteous men desired to see, and did not see (John 8). But Abraham saw this righteousness and was glad, by no means following the letter that kills, but the spirit that gives life. Nor is it surprising to say this of men, when even the stars, compared with the righteousness of God, are not clean, and he has found out something perverse in his angels (Job 4:18; 15:15). If anyone, then, after the Gospel of Christ and the coming of the Son of God, observes the ceremonies of the Law, our tutor, let him hear the people confessing that all that righteousness is compared to a most filthy cloth; to which Esther too compares her diadem, which was the badge of royal power, and which she wore by no means willingly but of necessity: You know my necessity, that I detest the sign of my pride, which is upon my head in the days of my display; I abhor it like a menstruous cloth, and I do not wear it in the days of my quiet (Esth 14:16).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.64.7":["There is none who calls upon your name, who rises up and takes hold of you. You have hidden your face from us, and have dashed us down in the hand of our iniquity. LXX: And there is none who calls upon your name, and who, remembering, lays hold of you. For you have turned away your face from us, and have handed us over because of our iniquities. Because we have fallen away like leaves from the trees, which the wind scatters when they are dried up, therefore no one has been found who would call upon your name, and, rising from the ruin of his iniquities, would lay hold of you or take hold of you, and say with the bride: I have found him whom my soul sought; I will hold him and will not let him go (Song 3:4). The wind is put in the Scriptures in three ways: either for the greatness of temptations, or for perversity of doctrine, or simply for the wind by which the clouds are gathered and the air is driven together into clouds. Of temptations there is that saying of the Gospel: The rivers came, the winds blew, and beat upon that house, and they could not overthrow it, for it had been founded upon rock with a firm mass (Matt 7:27). Of perversity of doctrine: That we may not be carried about by every wind of doctrine in the wickedness of men (Eph 4:14). Of the wind simply: And they could not sail, for the wind was against them (Mark 6:48): not that this too cannot be understood tropologically, but that it only provides a likeness for the present sense. For when the Lord turns his face away from us, we are dashed down in the hand of our iniquity, or we are handed over to temptations because of our sins. From this we learn that iniquity too has hands, and so have all sins, which desire to hold us tightly bound. Hence too death and life are in the hand of the tongue (Prov 18); and the holy man prays: Let not the foot of pride come to me (Ps 35 [36]:12). But we, since the Lord has turned his face away from us, knowing what is written, Everyone who calls upon the name of the Lord will be saved (Joel 2:32), let us call upon his name and say: I have made my sin known to you, and I have not covered my iniquity. I said, I will declare my iniquity against myself to the Lord; and you forgave the impiety of my heart (Ps 31 [32]:5). And at once we shall have the Lord answering: I have blotted out your iniquities like a mist, and your sins like a cloud (Isa 44:22); and we shall say: Show your face, and we shall be saved (Ps 79 [80]:4), so that we may by no means be slaves of sin, but your servants. For everyone who commits sin is its slave (John 8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.64.8-9":["And now, O Lord, you are our father, and we are clay; and you are our fashioner, and we are all the works of your hands. Do not be angry, O Lord, exceedingly, and do not remember iniquity any longer. Behold, look: we are all your people. The city of your Holy One has become a desert; Zion has become a desert, Jerusalem desolate. The house of our sanctification and of our glory, where our fathers praised you, has been made a burning of fire, and all our desirable things have been turned into ruin. Will you hold yourself back over these things, O Lord, will you be silent, and afflict us exceedingly? LXX: And now, O Lord, you are our father; but we are clay, we are all the works of your hands. Do not be angry with us, O Lord, too much, and do not remember our sins in time. And now look, for we are all your people; the city of your Holy One has become a desert, Zion; Jerusalem as a wilderness, for a curse; the house of our sanctuary and glory, which our fathers blessed, has been burned with fire, and all our glorious things have fallen. And over all these things you have held back, O Lord, and you have been silent, and have humbled us exceedingly. If we consider our merits, we must despair. But if we consider your mercy, you who scourge every son whom you receive, we dare to pour out prayers (Heb 12). For you are our father, who deigned to say: Israel is my firstborn son (Exod 4:22). And although we are clay, and the works of your hands, and you are our fashioner, and the vessel cannot answer the potter as to why it was made thus or thus, yet, knowing that we are sons, we dare to say: Do not be angry, O Lord, exceedingly. We do not forbid wrath, nor do we seek any further your patience, through which we have stored up for ourselves wrath on the day of wrath. But this we ask: that you be not angry with us exceedingly, that in the time of vengeance and punishment you do not remember our iniquity, but rather look upon your people, which was once called your people.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.64.10-11":["We can refer these things to the Church, or to the soul of a holy man, which can rightly be called a watchtower and vision of peace when the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit have dwelt in it. But if, through our fault or that of the people, such a Zion has been forsaken by the Lord, it will at once lie open to the burning of the fiery arrows of the devil; for all who commit adultery, their hearts are like an oven. And when the coolness of chastity has been driven out, the flame of lust will rage in the temple of God, so that whatever in us was formerly glorious and renowned may collapse, be destroyed and perish. And then is fulfilled what is said in the Psalms: They have burned your sanctuary with fire; they have profaned on the earth the tabernacle of your name. This he alone can quench out of whose belly flow rivers of living water.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.64.10-12":["And have mercy on the city of your sanctuary, in which your name has been called upon. This city is called Zion and Jerusalem, which the rush of the river makes glad, and in which there was once the watchtower of the virtues and the vision of peace (Ps 43 [44]). Your house too, that is, the Temple of our sanctification and glory, in which our fathers praised you, has fallen into ruins and ashes, and has been destroyed by devastating fire; so that no victims are offered in it, no Passover is celebrated, nothing is done of the ceremonies which you commanded to be done; but all our desirable things have been turned into ruins. Since, then, this is how things stand, will you, O Lord, hold back your mercy over these things, and be silent toward the adversaries who blaspheme, and afflict and humble us, not in the usual manner but exceedingly? All this the Jews think was fulfilled in the times of the Assyrians and the Babylonians. But we, in accordance with what follows in the person of the Savior, I appeared to those who did not ask for me; I was found by those who did not seek me (Isa 65:1), refer everything to the time of the Roman victory, which Josephus, the writer of the Jewish history, sets forth in seven volumes, to which he gave the title The Capture of the Jews, that is, περὶ ἁλώσεως. And it is superfluous to discuss in words what lies open to the eyes, since all their desirable things have been turned into ruins, and the Temple, celebrated throughout the whole world, has become the dunghill of the new city, which was called by its founder Aelia, and has passed into a dwelling-place of owls; and in vain they say daily in their synagogues: Over all these things, O Lord, you will hold back, and you will afflict us and humble us exceedingly.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.1":["They sought me who before did not ask for me: they found, who did not seek me. I said: Behold me, behold me, to a nation that did not call upon my name. LXX: I appeared to those not seeking me: I was found by those who did not ask for me. I said: Behold me, in a nation that did not call upon my name. To the prayer of the people mingled with complaints, in which it had said: Why have you made us err, O Lord, from your way: you have hardened our hearts, so that we did not fear you (Isa 63:17)? there answers, as the Jews would have it, God the Father; as we hold, the Lord and Savior: So far am I from being harsh and cruel, I who prefer the repentance of the sinner to his death, and do not repel repentant sons, provided they have kept the dignity of their name, that I wish to save even strangers (Ezek 33). For those who did not seek me, and could not say: We have found him of whom Moses wrote in the Law, and the prophets, Jesus (John 1:45), to these very ones I appeared; of whom the Prophet also testified elsewhere: Those to whom nothing was announced concerning him will hear, and those who have not heard will understand (Isa 52:15). But you, who meditate on the law of God day and night, boast in vain of a repentance whose works you do not do. Hence the Evangelists and apostles, always wishing to strengthen the coming of the Lord with testimonies of the Old Testament, said: That what was spoken through the Prophet might be fulfilled (Matt 1:22). The centurion was not seeking the Lord, and he heard from the Lord: Not even in Israel have I found such faith (Luke 7:9; Matt 8:10). The Syrophoenician woman did not know the utterances of the Prophets, and she is praised by the voice of the Lord: O woman, great is your faith. The nobleman (John 4), who in Greek is called βασιλικὸς, and whom, from the royal court, we can more correctly render palatine, found healing not only for his son but for his whole house. And what is said according to the Hebrew: They sought me who before did not ask, refers to the same sense: that those who formerly had no knowledge of God afterwards seek the Lord and come to know him by revelation; he who was also revealed to Paul while he was persecuting the Church of believers (Acts 9). And he says to the apostle Peter: Blessed are you, Simon Barjona, for flesh and blood have not revealed it to you, but my Father who is in heaven (Matt 16:17). Him Moses too was seeking when he said to God: If I have found favor with you, show yourself to me openly, that I may see you (Exod 33:13). Looking for the reproach of Christ as greater riches than the treasures of Egypt, while he longed for the recompense of things to come, he contemplated in his mind the invisible God as if he saw him. Of whom it is also read in the Psalms: The hope of all the ends of the earth, and in the sea far off (Ps 64 [65]:6). And in Genesis: He himself shall be the expectation of the nations (Gen 49:10). And what follows: I said: Behold, I am: to a nation that did not call upon my name (Exod 3:14), agrees with that sense: He who is has sent me to you. Who, though he was in the form of God, did not think it robbery to be equal to God, etc. (Phil 2); but, calling forth the nations of the whole world, he made one nation of his name, that of the Christians.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.2":["I have spread out my hands all the day to an unbelieving people, who walk in a way not good, after their own thoughts. LXX: I have spread out my hands all the day to an unbelieving and contradicting people, who have not walked in the good way, but after their sins. This which was said above: I appeared to those who did not ask for me: I was found by those who did not seek me, the apostle Paul, writing to the Romans, when he had expounded it of the person of the nations, added: But to Israel he says: I have spread out my hands to an unbelieving and contradicting people (Rom 10:21); a people which, when it was overcome by the benefits of the Lord and saw signs beyond what a man can do, said: This man does not cast out demons except by Beelzebub, the prince of the demons (Luke 11:15). And again: You are a Samaritan and have a demon (John 8:48). And again: though you are a man, you make yourself God (John 10:33). And elsewhere: This man is not from God, for he does not keep the sabbath (John 9:16). And many other things, which it would take long to string together. Finally, when he on the cross stretched out his hands to the unbelieving people and said: Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do (Luke 23:34), they on the other side said: Ah! you who destroy the Temple and in three days build it up: let him come down now from the cross, and we believe him. He saved others, himself he cannot save (Matt 27:40). Of him Simeon too, holding the little child in his arms, prophesied: Behold, this one is set for the fall and for the rising of many, and for a sign that will be spoken against (Luke 2:34). At Rome the Jews say to Paul: Concerning this sect it is known to us that everywhere it is spoken against (Acts 28:22). We can also take the spread-out hands of the generosity of the giver, in that he denied them nothing when they asked, but at once restored health to the leper who asked (Matt 8), and the man blind from birth received eyes (John 9), and thousands of the people, hungry in the desert, besides children and women, were satisfied (Matt 14). The spread-out hands also signify the mercy of a parent eager to receive his children into his bosom. But they, on the contrary, walked in a way not good, and followed their own thoughts. To them the Lord speaks through the Prophet: Turn your foot away from the rough way (Prov 4:27). For they had loved strangers, and like a harlot had said: I will go after my lovers (Hos 2:5). And leaving the good way, which says in the Gospel: I am the way (John 14:6), they walked on the broad and spacious way that leads to death, and followed their own thoughts; and the Prophet, shunning these, entreats the Lord: From my hidden faults cleanse me, O Lord, and from those of others spare your servant. If they have not ruled over me, then I shall be spotless (Ps 18 [19]:13, 14). And the Apostle teaches that those who did not have the knowledge of God are handed over to a reprobate mind and to evil works, to do the things that are not fitting (Rom 1). But let us say with the voice of the Psalmist: Lead me in the right way (Ps 138 [139]:24).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.3":["A people that provokes me to anger, before my face always: who sacrifice in gardens, and offer sacrifice upon bricks (From Exod 20:4). LXX: This people that embitters me in my sight: they always sacrifice in gardens, and burn incense upon brick ÷ to demons that are not. ⁚ Though the Lord says: You shall not make for yourself an idol, nor a likeness of anything that is in heaven, and that is upon the earth. And though through Moses he rebukes those who do so: They provoked me with strange gods, and with their abominations they provoked me to bitterness. They sacrificed to demons, and not to God (Deut 32:16, 17); they, out of a zeal for sinning, as if to irritate and provoke God, not only did what was not lawful, but did it continually and always, sinning in the sight of the Lord, to whose eyes all things lie open, or in his Temple, sacrificing victims to idols. And so that nothing might be lacking to the sacrilege, they sacrificed in gardens and burned frankincense upon bricks, joining luxury to idolatry and pleasure to sacrifices; and instead of the one altar which by the law of God had been built of unhewn stones, they made baked bricks and the turf of the fields bloody with the blood of victims. Let this be said according to the letter: but according to the spiritual understanding, every heretic provokes the Lord to anger, and in the falsity of his doctrines offers unclean sacrifices, by no means firm and lasting forever, but like gardens green for a short time, making provision for the delights of the flesh. To him it is said: All flesh is grass, and all its glory as the flower of grass. The grass has withered, and the flower has fallen: but the word of the Lord, which is preached in the churches, abides forever (Isa 40:6, 7). Naboth, not wishing such gardens to be made out of his vineyard, poured out his blood to Ahab, that most impious king (1 Kgs 21). For it was not fitting that a father's inheritance should perish, and that, the vines being cut down which bear the fruit that gladdens the heart of man, the herbs of pleasures and vices should spring up in the place of virtues. There is reported a deservedly celebrated and praised saying of the wisest man among the Greeks, who calls all the pleasures of the world, and the pomp and luxury of the world swiftly passing away, the gardens of Adonis. The heretics also sacrifice upon stones, when they strive to make their errors and their studied lies firm by the dialectical art, and to build them up square, and to strengthen them by γραμμικαῖς, as they say, ἀνάγκαις, that is, by dust cut on a brick and marked out with lines. But what is read in the Seventy: to demons that are not, and is not found in the Hebrew, must be taken in this sense: that according to the letter and according to the spirit, neither do demons subsist, since they have already fallen away from God, who truly is, nor do the sects of the heretics, which hold no truth, but pass away and perish like shadows. Hence Esther too speaks to the Lord: Do not hand over your inheritance to those who are not (Esth 14:11). And the saint prays in the psalm: Forgive me, that I may be refreshed before I depart, and be no more (Ps 38 [39]:14). For he who, while he lives in this body, has not obtained pardon of his sins, and so departs from life, perishes to God and ceases to be, although he subsists to himself in punishments.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.4-5":["Who dwell in tombs, and sleep in the shrines of idols: who eat swine's flesh, and profane broth is in their vessels: who say, Depart from me, do not come near me, for you are unclean. LXX: In tombs and in caves they sleep for the sake of dreams: who eat swine's flesh and the broth of victims; all their vessels are profane: who say, Go far from me, do not come near me, for I am clean. There was no sacrilege that the people of Israel left out: not only sacrificing in gardens and kindling frankincense upon bricks, but also sitting, or dwelling, in tombs, and sleeping in the shrines of idols, where they used to lie upon the spread-out skins of victims, so as to learn the future from dreams. This the error of the heathen celebrates to this day in the temple of Aesculapius and in many others, which are nothing else but the tombs of the dead. Nor were they content with this end of impiety, but they also fed on the flesh of swine, which was forbidden by the law, and devoured with greedy throats yesterday's broth, which the Greeks call ἕωλον (Lev 12). Hence both they themselves and their vessels were unclean, and they grew daily to such a pitch of wantonness and pride that whoever was not led by a like error they reckoned unclean, and fled from his touch, as the Samaritans and the Jews do to us, and all the heretics, such as lately sprang up in Gaul under a hot-headed master, who, shunning the basilicas of the Martyrs, flee from us who celebrate prayers there according to custom, as though we were unclean. Yet it is not so much they who do this as the demons dwelling in them, who cannot bear the power and the scourges of the holy ashes. And which of the heretics does not rest in tombs, does not sleep in the caves of his masters? Such was Marcion, and Valentinus, and lately Eunomius, who bore witness to the uncleanness of his mind by his leprous flesh; who, leaving the light of the Holy Spirit and the upper room in which the Apostles stayed, linger in the darkness of the devil and love the caves which Jeremiah condemns in mystical speech: My inheritance has become to me as the cave of a hyena (Jer 12:8), which our people call a beast: an animal that always pursues corpses and lives on the juice and gore of dead bodies. Against these, who dwell in caves, Habakkuk too said with a voice of lament: Woe to him who gives his neighbor a drink of a turbid draught, and makes him drunk, that he may look upon his caves (Hab 2:15). Of these caves the Lord too was speaking: It is written, My Father's house shall be called a house of prayer: but you have made it a den of robbers (Matt 21:13), in which you kill the souls of those you deceive, so that you do not see in them the visions of which the same Savior says: I have multiplied visions, and in the hands of the Prophets I have been likened (Hos 12:10). And another says to him: You spoke, he says, in a vision to your sons (Ps 88 [89]:20); but you believe dreams and phantasms, which the Lord commanded us not to believe. These feed on the flesh of swine, of whom it is said: Do not cast your pearls before swine (Matt 7:6). And they eat profane broth, which is seasoned in their books with heretical art. All their vessels are unclean, that is, both their bodies and their teachings; and they come to such madness that whoever is not like them they detest as one lost.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.5":["These shall be a smoke in my fury, a fire burning all the day. Behold, it is written before me: I will not be silent, but I will render and repay into their bosom your iniquities, and the iniquities of your fathers together, says the Lord, who sacrificed upon the mountains and upon the hills, reproached me; and I will measure back their former work into their bosom. LXX: This is the smoke of my fury: a fire burns in it all the days. Behold, it is written in my sight: I will not be silent until I render and repay into their bosom their sins, and those of their fathers, says the Lord: who burned incense upon the mountains and upon the hills, reproached me; I will render their works into their bosom. Because of your works, and the incredible arrogance of your mind, in that, being unclean, you thought the clean unclean, you shall be smoke, a burning fire, which is kindled from my fury. But fury, forgetfulness, anger, repentance we ought to take in God in the same way as feet, hands, eyes, ears and the other members which the incorporeal and invisible God is said to have. Not that he is open to these passions, he who by the gift of his grace extinguishes them in us; but so that through our words we may understand God's disposition toward us. For anger, which is the desire for vengeance, is not defined in God as it is in men; it has its matter in our vices, not in the will of the Lord: for we treasure up for ourselves wrath in the day of wrath and of the revelation of the righteous judgment of God, so that a wise flame may consume our thistles and thorns, and the tares that we have brought in place of God's seed, and also the wood, hay and stubble that we have built upon Paul's foundation (1 Cor 3). Of this perpetual fire we read in the song of Moses too: A fire is kindled from my fury, and it shall burn down to hell below. It shall devour the earth and its produce: it shall burn the foundations of the mountains, and my arrows shall consume them (Deut 32:22). The sense of this testimony is that the fire and vengeance of God always burns for sinners, and pursues them even to the lower world. Such was that rich man too in the Gospel (Luke 16), clothed in purple, who, while Lazarus rested in Abraham's bosom, was tormented with eternal burnings. The same flame will also burn the earth, that is, our flesh, and its produce, that is, the desires of the flesh, and the foundations of the mountains that raise themselves against the knowledge of God. The darts of God will also destroy the mountains themselves, so that, humbled in punishments, they cease to be mountains.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.6":["Behold, he says, it is written before me. For all our sins lie open to the eyes of God, and are written in those books of which we read in Daniel: Thrones were set, and the books were opened (Dan 7:10). Of which it is said in another place: Let them be written upon the earth (Jer 17:13). And he will by no means keep silent any longer, he who had said before: I have kept silent: shall I always keep silent? says the Lord (Isa 42:14). But he will render to each what he has worked, into his bosom, that is, into the secret place of the heart, so that their own conscience may torment them. Of this the prophet Hosea too prophesies: Their princes shall fall by the sword because of the unruliness of their tongue (Hos 7:16). This is their own detraction in their bosom, which proceeds from the heart of each. Something similar is written in Proverbs: In their own bosom all things are rendered to the wicked (Prov 5:22). This bosom, because of the ruling part of the soul (Greek ἡγεμονικὸν), is called by another name the head: I have given their ways upon their heads (Ezek 9:10). And elsewhere: His sorrow shall be turned upon his head, and upon his crown his iniquity shall come down (Ps 7:17). But there is also a good bosom, which will enjoy the holiness of conscience, of which the Psalmist said: And my prayer shall be turned into my bosom (Ps 34 [35]:13). Bosom is also understood as affection and love toward anyone, as when the word is addressed to a husband: The wife who is in your bosom; and to a parent: They will bring your sons in their bosom (Luke 16). Hence all who have Abraham as their father, and have deserved to be like him in his virtues, rest in his bosom.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.7":["For we ought not to take as fathers, whose iniquities and sins are now repaid to us, those from whose seed our bodies were born. Otherwise Moses himself, who had said that the sins of the fathers are to be repaid to the sons to the third and fourth generation of those who hate God (Exod 20), interprets his own sentence, saying: The sons shall not die for the sins of the fathers: but each shall die in his own sin (Deut 24:16). Ezekiel approves the same sentence, that that parable is by no means to be spoken: The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the teeth of the sons are set on edge (Ezek 18:2), but that only his teeth are set on edge who has eaten, and that the sinning soul perishes, and does not pay for another's sins. From this we understand that those are to be taken as fathers, whether in the good sense or in the contrary, to whom each is added when he has departed from this life. Now Israel sacrificed upon the mountains and hills when it set up the golden calves in Bethel and Dan. And it reproached God over his precepts, when it neglected his ceremonies and gave the worship of God to demons: whose works are to be repaid into their bosom (Hos 4). And every heretic who despises the simplicity of the Church sacrifices on the mountains and reproaches God, when he despises his precepts.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.8":["Thus says the Lord: As if a grain is found in a cluster, and it is said: Do not destroy it, for it is a blessing; so will I do for the sake of my servants, that I may not destroy the whole. LXX: Thus says the Lord: As if a grape is found in a cluster, and it is said: Do not touch it, for there is a blessing in it: so will I do for the sake of my servant, that I may not destroy all. The Lord had said that Israel would perish, and that all its sins lay open to his sight, and that he would repay the works of each into their bosom. After this he brings in a likeness and an example by comparison, to teach that each perishes by his own sin, and that, even if the multitude of sinners be very great, one righteous man does not perish through the vice of all. If anyone, he says, in a cluster and bunch of grapes which could not come to ripeness and has borne sour fruit, or has been spoiled by some fault of the air and the soil, finds one grain unharmed, which still has hope of growing larger and coming to its usual ripeness, let him say to another: Do not touch it, but let it be, that it may grow, for it is a blessing of the Lord that, among so great a multitude of grapes, it alone escaped the drought: so, he says, out of the innumerable multitude of the Jews, which has offended God, if I find a few righteous, I will set them free from the destruction of the many. And this I will do for the sake of my servants Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, to whom the promise was made, or for the sake of those who serve me among very many sinners. Something of the kind we read in Genesis too, when righteous men to free the city from its sin are sought, little by little, from fifty down to ten (Gen 18), and Lot alone, as a righteous man, is snatched out of Sodom with his daughters (Gen 19). Of him it is written in the Catholic Epistle (2 Pet 2) that his soul was tormented as he saw abominations committed, and that he showed the judgment of his soul by the bearing of his body. Enoch too, alone among a very great multitude of sinners, was taken up to God (Gen 5). And Noah with his children the Flood, sent because all flesh was turning away from the service of the Lord, could not destroy (Gen 7). Abraham too was saved from the fire of the Chaldeans by the integrity of his faith (Gen 11). With this sense the words of Jeremiah also agree, spoken in the person of the Lord: As something warm in the desert, I found Israel with those slain by the sword (Jer 38:2 LXX; Vulg. 31:2). For when, he says, the whole world lay lifeless, wounded by the swords of idolatry, I found Israel in Abraham to have the warmth of faith, like a physician who after a battle, among the corpses of the dead, where he perceives that someone still has something of life in his beating veins, applies care to his wounds to restore him to health. For what is written ὡς θερμὸν, that is, as something warm, the Latin translator, deceived by the ambiguity of the word, rendered lupine, and in this most of the Greeks also err. And yet it should be known that in the Hebrew, for warmth, THODA (תודה) is written, which means grace: namely, that Israel was saved by the grace of God, and not by the merit of its own works.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.9":["And I will bring forth a seed out of Jacob, and out of Judah one possessing my mountains: and my elect shall inherit it, and my servants shall dwell there, and the plains shall be folds for flocks, and the valley of Achor a resting place for herds, for my people who have sought me. LXX: And I will bring forth a seed out of Jacob, and out of Judah, and it shall inherit my holy mountain, and my elect shall inherit, and my servants shall dwell there, and in the woodland there shall be sheepfolds for flocks, and the valley of Achor for the rest of herds, for my people who have sought me out. Him whom above he called the grain of the cluster, or the grape, or (as many would have it) the bunch, he now calls the seed of Jacob and Judah, who is to possess the mountains, or his mountain. Most understand the seed of Jacob and Judah as Christ, of whom it is said in Genesis: Judah, your brothers shall praise you (Gen 49:8), and the rest. For no one doubts that the Savior was born of the stock of Judah. But others would have the Apostles understood, of whom we have often said: The remnant shall be saved (Isa 1:9). And: Unless the Lord Sabaoth had left us a seed, we would have been as Sodom, and we would have been like Gomorrah (Rom 9:29). These possessed the mountain of the Lord by their awareness of Christ dwelling in them, saying: We have come to Mount Zion, and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem (Heb 12:22). Or his mountains, of which it is sung in the psalms: Mountains are round about it, and the Lord is round about his people (Ps 124 [125]:2). And: Its foundations are in the holy mountains (Ps 86 [87]:1). And the elect of the Lord will possess Zion, and his servants will dwell in it. Of whom it is written in the same volume: The seed of Abraham his servants, the sons of Jacob his elect (Ps 104 [105]:6). Whoever, then, is still a seed and has not been formed into a son, he is a servant of the Lord, to whom he says in the Gospel: I know that you are the seed of Abraham, but not yet sons (John 8). For if they were the seed of Abraham, they would surely do the works of Abraham. But he who is a son is also an elect of the Lord. Hence the elect possesses Jerusalem, and the servants dwell in it; and of the difference between sons and servants it is said: You have not received the spirit of slavery again unto fear, but the spirit of adoption (Rom 8:15).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.10":["And there shall be, he says, plains, or woodlands, as folds for flocks. For plains, SARON (שרון) is put in the Hebrew. The whole region around Lydda, Joppa and Jamnia is suited to pasturing flocks. Of it it is also written in the Acts of the Apostles: But the woodlands shall be turned into sheepfolds (Acts 9), according to what is sung in the psalm: The voice of the Lord perfecting the stags, and he will lay bare the woodlands (Ps 28 [29]:9), or the thickets of the forests, so that robbers may cease to lurk there, and beasts and venomous animals, and places once of ambush and blood may pass into churches of the Lord, and flocks may be pastured there by him who laid down his life for his sheep. Of whom it is written: He will shepherd us forever (Ps 47 [48]:15). This voice of the Lord, perfecting the stags and laying bare the thickets of the forests, cries out through John in the desert: Already the axe is laid to the root of the trees. Every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down and cast into the fire (Matt 3:10). And again: Every valley shall be filled (Luke 3:5); of which it is now also said: and the valley of Achor a resting place for herds, and for rest for my people who have sought me out. Of this valley we also read in the book of Joshua son of Nun that Achan was put to death there, who stole from the accursed thing and from the spoils of Jericho, and was put to death with all his house; and from the fact that he troubled the people, the very place where this happened received the name ACHOR (עכור), that is, of trouble and tumult (Josh 7). The valley of Achor, then, in which there was once a curse and punishment, will be for the rest of herds. Expounding this, Paul says: Is God concerned for oxen (1 Cor 9:9)? or does he not rather speak of us, since he who plows ought to plow in hope, and he who threshes on the floor ought to thresh in hope of sharing? Of this valley it is also written mystically in Hosea: I will speak to her heart, without doubt to Jerusalem's; and I will give her possessors from there, and the valley of Achor, to open her understanding (Hos 2:14). For this reason, then, the valley of Achor is handed over as a possession to the herds of the people of God, and the woodlands are turned into folds for flocks: that understanding may be opened, and the truth of the Lord be known.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.11-12":["And you who have forsaken the Lord, and have forgotten my holy mountain; who set a table for fortune, and pour libations upon it: I will number you for the sword, and you shall all fall in the slaughter. LXX: But you who have forsaken me, and have forgotten my holy mountain, and prepare a table for fortune, and fill a drink for the demon: I will hand you over to the sword: you shall all fall slain. In places once wooded and full of trouble, in the places of the Gentiles, there will be folds for the sheep, that is, the Churches of believers from the whole world. But you, O people of Israel, who have forsaken the Lord and provoked the Holy One of Israel to anger, who have forgotten his holy mountain, of which we have often spoken, whether the Lord Savior, who is the mountain of mountains and the holy one of all the holy, or Mount Zion and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem: you who do this and that, I will hand over to the sword, so that you may all fall slain together. What the sword is, we shall say later. You set, he says, a table for fortune, and pour libations upon it: or according to the Seventy: You prepare a table for fortune, and fill a cup for the demon, or κέρασμα, which all have likewise translated, that is, a mixed drink. Now there is in all cities, and especially in Egypt and in Alexandria, an old custom of idolatry, that on the last day of the year and of the month which is their last, they set out a table loaded with feasts of every kind, and a cup mixed with honeyed wine, taking an omen of the fertility either of the past year or of the coming one. And this the Israelites did, worshiping the monstrosities of all the images, and they offered not victims upon the altar, but poured out libations upon a table of this kind. And where the Seventy translated to the demon, the Hebrew has MENNI (מני), which Symmachus rendered without me: so that the sense is: You who prepare a table for fortune, and fill a drink without me; to teach that it is done not for him but for the demon. Now the sword by which they are slain is taken for punishment. For not all the people of Israel were handed over to the sword, since we see how many thousands of them are scattered over the whole world; but to punishments and torments, to captivity and the utmost servitude, according to what is said elsewhere: All the sinners of my people shall die by the sword; and in the Song of Deuteronomy: I will make my arrows drunk with blood, and my sword shall devour the flesh of the wounded (Deut 32:42). It can hardly be believed that the arrows of the Lord are made drunk with blood, and that his sword is sated with the flesh of the wounded. Job speaks of these arrows, which pierced him at every moment of the hours: The arrows of the Lord are in my body: their fury drinks up my spirit; for when I begin to speak, they goad me (Job 6:4). But according to tropology it must be said that all who desert the Church and forget the holy mountain of God, and give themselves over to spirits of error and doctrines of demons, prepare a table for fortune, believing that nothing is God's concern, but that all things are governed either by the course of the stars or by the changes of fortune: whom Paul rebukes, saying: You cannot partake of the table of the Lord and of the table of demons. You cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons (1 Cor 10:20, 21): for they will be handed over to eternal punishments, so that none of them can escape the slaughter and the ruin.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.12":["Because I called, and you did not answer; I spoke, and you did not hear: but you did evil in my eyes, and what I did not will, you chose. LXX: Because I called you, and you did not hear: I spoke, and you despised, and you did evil in my sight, and what I did not will you chose. You have been handed over to the sword, he says, because you have not only forsaken me and forgotten me and mixed a cup for fortune, but when I was Emmanuel, that is, God with us, of whom John too writes: The Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us (John 1:14), I called you, present among you: Return to me, returning sons (Jer 3:14). And: Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened (Matt 11:28); and you would not answer. I spoke in parables, and did all that I ought to do, and you did not hear me, nay, you even despised me. For I am he who said above as well: I came, and there was no man: I called, and there was none to obey (Isa 50:2). Nor was this impiety enough for you, to despise me when I was present, and to kill the heir sent to you; but you did evil in my sight, and chose what I did not will, and blasphemed the Son of God, so as to ask for yourselves Barabbas the robber, the author of murder and sedition; and you still dare to say: Why have you made us err from your way (Isa 63:17)? For if those who did not know me found me, and those who did not call upon me received me, what torments are you worthy of, who would not receive him who was sent to you and said: I have not come except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matt 15:24), but on top of that fastened him to the cross? We must consider what is meant by what he says: And what I did not will you chose; or what the will of the Son of God is, who says in the Gospel: Not everyone who says to me, Lord, Lord, shall enter the kingdom of heaven: but he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven (Matt 7:21). Of which God himself also says: I have willed to do your will (Ps 39 [40]:9). This is the will of which the Lord himself was speaking: I have found David the son of Jesse, a man after my own heart, who will do all my wills (Acts 13:22). Hence the saint too prays and says: Teach me to do your will, for you are my God (Ps 142 [143]:10). For all things are lawful, but not all things are expedient. And concerning virgins the Apostle relates that he has no command of the Lord, yet he wishes us to be as he himself is (1 Cor 6). By this he shows that it is by no means the indulgence of the master that is to be followed, but his will (1 Cor 7); and that we ought to choose the things that are expedient, not those that are lawful, as is that concerning second marriage: I wish the younger women to marry, to bear children, to be mothers of families (1 Tim 5:14). And he gives the reasons why he allows this: For already some have turned aside after Satan. Therefore second marriage is not his pure will, since it is cheapened by the comparison with fornication.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.13-14":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, my servants shall eat, and you shall be hungry. Behold, my servants shall drink, and you shall be thirsty. Behold, my servants shall rejoice, and you shall be put to shame. Behold, my servants shall give praise for exultation of heart, and you shall cry out for sorrow of heart, and for contrition of spirit you shall howl. LXX: Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, my servants shall eat, but you shall be hungry. Behold, my servants shall drink, but you shall be thirsty. Behold, my servants shall be glad, but you shall be put to shame. Behold, my servants shall exult in joy, but you shall cry out because of the sorrow of your heart, and because of the contrition of your spirit you shall howl. Not only shall you fall by the sword, you who have forsaken the Lord and done evil in his sight and would not hear him when he was present, but you shall see a great difference between yourselves and the multitude of the nations. For those who will be my servants shall eat and drink, they shall rejoice and give praise for exultation of heart. But you, on the contrary, shall be hungry and thirsty, you shall be put to shame, and for sorrow of heart and contrition of spirit you shall howl. All these things the χιλιασταὶ think are to be fulfilled in the thousand years, believing food and drink to be the kingdom of God, and not understanding what is written: Labor not for the food that perishes, but for the bread of life and truth (John 6:27), and desiring to eat the flesh of Christ and the fruit of the tree of life. Of this the Savior speaks: I am the bread that came down from heaven (John 6:33). And Ecclesiastes: Open your eyes, and be filled with loaves (Prov 20:13). And the Psalmist: I was young, he says, and have not grown old, and I have not seen the righteous forsaken, nor his seed seeking bread. All the day he is merciful and lends, and his seed shall be for a blessing (Ps 36 [37]:25). Of this bread and spiritual banquet he instructed the disciples: You who have continued with me in my trials shall eat at the table of my Father in the kingdom of heaven (Luke 22:28). But if we take it of simple bread, how shall we be able to explain this: The Lord will not kill the righteous soul with hunger (Prov 10:3). And again: The Lord knows the ways of the spotless, and their inheritance shall be forever. They shall not be put to shame in the evil time, and in the days of famine they shall be filled (Ps 36 [37]:18, 19). For how many saints die in persecution, worn out by hunger and want; how many righteous men are hungry, while the wicked are distended with surfeit! But the drink is to be taken as that which is drawn from the fountains of Israel; whoever drinks of it will have in himself a fountain of water springing up into eternal life (John 4; Luke 22). This the Savior too promises that he will drink with the Apostles in the kingdom of the Father: it gladdens the heart of man (Ps 103 [104]:14), so that those who drink can say: You have put gladness in my heart (Ps 4:7). Of this food and drink it is said among the eight beatitudes: Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness (Matt 5:6). And the mother of the Lord, full of the Holy Spirit, prophesied: He has filled the hungry with good things (Luke 1:53), those who before did not have the food of the Lord: and the rich he has sent away empty, those who were given for his burial, and who spat out him whom the Prophets had promised. Of these the Psalmist sings in another place too: The rich have been in want, and have hungered (Ps 33 [34]:11), that is, the people of the Jews. But those who seek the Lord, that is, the crowd of the Gentiles, shall not lack any good thing. And when those who serve Christ and believe in him have rejoiced and praised God for exultation and joy of heart, then those others will be put to shame, seeing others succeed to their place; and they will cry out for sorrow of heart, fulfilling what is written: There shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth (Matt 13:50): when the lambs of the nations and of the righteous stand at the right hand, and the goats of the Jews and of the wicked on the left: the one receiving eternal rewards, the other eternal punishments.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.14":["And the contrition of spirit for sorrow of heart is to be understood of the conscience of sins, according to what is written: Those who err in spirit shall know understanding (Isa 29:24). And: A contrite and humbled heart, O God, you will not despise (Ps 50 [51]:19). The spirit is crushed when it has been lifted up; as is written of a king hostile to Israel: The Lord hardened his spirit (2 Kgs 17:14). And of the prince of the Babylonians, when his heart was lifted up and his spirit hardened, so that he said in his pride: This is Babylon, and I have made it (Dan 4:27).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.15-16":["And you shall leave your name for an oath to my elect, and the Lord God shall slay you, and he shall call his servants by another name, in which he who is blessed upon the earth shall be blessed in God, amen: and he who swears in the earth shall swear in God, amen. LXX: For you shall leave your name for satiety to my elect. But you the Lord shall slay: and to those who serve me a new name shall be called, which shall be blessed upon the earth: for they shall bless the true God, and those who swear upon the earth shall swear by the true God. For satiety, which in Hebrew is called SABAA (שבועה), the others translated oath; this word has many meanings and varies with the difference of accents. For it is understood as oath, and fullness, and satiety, and many, and seven. Of this we spoke in the book on Genesis, and in this volume (cf. Isa 4), where seven women took hold of one man. Again, where the Seventy translated true, and in the Hebrew it is said AMEN (אמן), Aquila rendered πεπιστωμένως, that is, faithfully. And what he says is this: when others have succeeded to your place, your name will be an oath for my elect, so that they may hold you up as an example of evils and abhor suffering such things, and swear thus: may I not suffer these things which the people of the Jews suffered. Or your name will be for satiety: which is usually said of those whose recollection and memory is hateful, and who have come to the point of satiety and nausea. Or certainly it must be said that they leave their name to the elect of the Lord, so that the crowd of the nations may succeed in their place, and be called sons of Abraham and Israel. Of whom Paul speaks: Peace upon them, and upon the Israel of God (Gal 6:16). For not all who are of the Israelites are Israel, nor are all who are the seed of Abraham sons (Rom 9:7); to whom it is said: If you were sons of Abraham, you would do the works of Abraham (John 8:39). And because they are the seed of Abraham and not sons, of which difference we spoke above, John the Baptist therefore rebukes them, saying: And do not presume to say, We have Abraham as our father: for God is able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham (Matt 3:9). For how can they be his sons who have said to wood and stone, You have begotten me, since, on the contrary, those who are of faith are called sons of Abraham? But you, he says, the Lord shall slay, so that you are by no means called the circumcision, but the concision (Phil 3); so that you lack eternal life, so that you do not have him who says: I am the life (John 14:6). But his servants, he says, he will call by another name, or a new one, which will be celebrated in the whole world. And it will be blessed: so much so that he who has been called by that name is blessed in the Lord, and receives the seal of the true Circumcision, amen: which the Lord often uses in the Gospel to approve what has been said: Amen, amen I say to you (John 5:19). But the new or other name is none other than that which is derived from the name of Christ, so that the people of God is by no means called Jacob, and Judah, and Israel, and Ephraim, and Joseph, but Christian. For whoever swears in the earth will by no means swear by idols and false gods, but by God: and this sentence again is confirmed by the seal of amen. Further, according to the Seventy, who for amen rendered the true God, so that the true God is blessed, and those who swear upon the earth swear by the true God: we do not, according to the error of the Arians, refer this only to the person of God the Father, of whom it is written: That they may know you, the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom you have sent (John 17:3), but to the Son, who is himself also true God, as the evangelist John says: The Son of God has come, and has given us understanding, that we may know the true one, and may be in his true Son Jesus Christ (1 John 5:20). This is the true God and eternal life. For if the Savior says of himself: I am the truth (John 14:6), consequently the true God received his name from truth, so that he is by no means called God after the manner of false gods, but after the manner of the true God the Father, and is himself true God. Otherwise, if he is not true, he will be like an idol, which recoils upon the heads of those who deny that Christ is true God. And this is the new name which is written for him on the pebble in the Apocalypse (Chapter 2), which is blessed in the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.16-17":["For the former distresses have been given over to oblivion, and because they are hidden from my eyes. For behold, I create new heavens and a new earth, and the former things shall not be in memory: and it shall not come up upon the heart: but you shall rejoice and exult forever in the things that I create. LXX: For they shall forget the former tribulation, and it shall not come up upon their heart. For there shall be a new heaven and a new earth: and they shall not remember the former things, and they shall not come upon their heart; but they shall find joy and exultation in it. The cause of the gladness and of the confession of the true God is that an eternal oblivion of the former distresses will follow, so that they will by no means remember the idols and their ancient error, but will pass from darkness to light, so as to enjoy eternal blessedness. For they will forget their former evils, not by a forgetting of memory, but by the succession of good things, according to what is written: In a good day there is forgetfulness of evils (Sir 11:27). And elsewhere: The affliction of an hour brings forgetfulness of delights (ibid. 29): because those placed in distress by no means enjoy in their minds, according to the error of Epicurus, their former pleasures. Though this too may be said: that in the new heaven and in the new earth all memory of the former way of life will be blotted out, lest this very thing be a part of evils, to remember the former distress.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.17-18":["Now the new heaven and the new earth those who think that all things we see will perish interpret by the testimony of the Gospel: Heaven and earth shall pass away (Matt 24:35). And by that of the apostle Paul: For the things that are seen are temporal: but the things that are not seen are eternal (2 Cor 4:18). But those who consider newness to be a change for the better, and not the destruction of the elements, use this example: In the beginning, O Lord, you founded the earth, and the heavens are the works of your hands. They shall perish, but you remain, and they shall all grow old like a garment, and like a cloak you shall roll them up, and they shall be changed (Ps 101 [102]:26, 27). In this it is clearly shown that perdition and destruction mean not an abolition into nothing, but a change for the better. For neither does what is written in another place: The moon shall shine like the sun, and the sun shall receive sevenfold light (Isa 30:26), signify the destruction of the former things, but a change for the better. That this may be understood, let us set down examples from our own condition: when an infant has grown into a boy, and a boy into a youth, and a youth into a man, and a man into an old man, he by no means perishes through the several ages. For he is the same who was before; but he is changed little by little, and is said to have perished to his former age. Understanding this, the apostle Paul too said: For the fashion of this world passes away (1 Cor 7:31). Let us consider what he said: the fashion passes away, not the substance. Peter too signifies the same thing: This escapes them, willingly: that the heavens were from the beginning, and the earth out of water and through water subsists by the word of God; through which the former world, flooded by the deluge, perished. But the heavens and earth which are now are by the same principle kept for fire (2 Pet 3:5ff.); in what sense this is to be taken, he teaches afterwards: But we see new heavens and a new earth, and his promise (ibid. 13): he did not say, we shall see other heavens and another earth, but the old and ancient ones changed for the better. We can also say this: that those converted from idolatry, having deserted their ancient error, see new heavens and a new earth, by no means reckoning the elements to be gods, nor the things that are born of the earth. Now we confess that heaven and earth are the works of the hands of God: but at that time we venerated servants and the creature with the homage due to God; and David, perceiving this, sings in the psalm: I shall see the heavens, the works of your fingers (Ps 8:4): not that at that time, when he said this, he did not see the heavens; but that through the several increases of virtues, and the knowledge of things to come, he sees as new those which he already saw as old. And what is said at the end of this testimony: Which I create, was omitted by the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.17-25":["The eighteenth book on Isaiah, or rather the last, is dedicated to your name, O daughter Eustochium, and to that of your holy mother Paula, so that I may now recall with equal remembrance those whom I have looked up to with equal honor: especially since she too, while she lived, often asked for this work together with you, and your brother Pammachius, a most learned man, did not cease, both then and afterwards, to press me with frequent letters; and for me there is one and the same devotion toward friends present and absent, and toward men and women sleeping in Christ, that is, a love of souls, not of bodies. Nor am I unaware how great a diversity of opinions there is among men. I do not speak of the mystery of the Trinity, whose right confession is the ignorance of knowledge, but of the other doctrines of the Church: namely of the Resurrection, of the state of souls and of human flesh, of the Promises of things to come, how they ought to be taken, and in what way the Apocalypse of John is to be understood; for if we take it according to the letter, we must judaize; if we discuss it spiritually, as it was written, we shall seem to go against the opinions of many of the ancients: of the Latins, Tertullian, Victorinus, Lactantius; of the Greeks, to pass over the rest, I shall mention only Irenaeus, bishop of Lyons, against whom Dionysius, a most eloquent man, bishop of the Church of Alexandria, writes an elegant book, mocking the fable of the thousand years, and the golden and jeweled Jerusalem on earth, the restoration of the Temple, the blood of victims, the idleness of the sabbath, the outrage of circumcision, marriages, childbirths, the rearing of children, the delights of banquets, and the servitude of all the nations; and again wars, armies and triumphs, and the slaughter of the vanquished, and the death of the sinner a hundred years old. To him Apollinaris replied in two volumes, and not only the men of his own sect but also a very great multitude of ours follow him, in this part at least, so that with a foreboding mind I already see how many people's rage is to be stirred up against me. I do not begrudge them, if they love the earth so much that in the kingdom of Christ they desire earthly things, and after an abundance of food and the gorging of gullet and belly, seek the things that are beneath the belly. Of whom the apostle Paul says: Food, he says, for the belly, and the belly for foods: but God will destroy both it and them (1 Cor 6:13). And: The kingdom of God is not food and drink (Rom 14:17). And the Lord and Savior: You err, he says, not knowing the Scriptures nor the power of God. For in the resurrection they will neither marry nor be given in marriage, but they will be like the Angels (Matt 22:29, 30). Nor in saying this do I take away the reality of bodies, which I confess will rise incorrupt and immortal, so that they change their glory, not their substance. And so we must walk on the straight path, so as to turn aside neither to the left nor to the right, that is, so as to follow neither the Jewish error nor the heretical. Of these, the one sort, who are of the flesh, love flesh alone; the other, ungrateful for the benefits of God, refuse to have what Christ had both when he was born and when he rose. For when the Apostles thought him a spirit, or, according to the Gospel of the Hebrews which the Nazarenes read, an incorporeal demon, he said to them: Why are you troubled, and why do thoughts arise in your hearts? See my hands and feet, that it is I myself: Handle and see, for a spirit does not have flesh and bones, as you see that I have. And when he had said this, he showed them his hands and feet (Luke 24:38, 39). And again he spoke to Thomas when he doubted: Put your finger here, and see my hands; and stretch out your hand, and put it into my side; and do not be unbelieving, but believing (John 20:27). Finally, to prove the reality of his body, he is written to have taken food, as he had also ordered food to be given to the daughter of the ruler of the synagogue, whom he raised from the dead. And Lazarus, lest his resurrection be thought a phantom, is related to have sat at a banquet with the Savior (Mark 5; and Luke 13; John 12). Not that after the resurrection we shall eat and drink, as our Millenarians would have it, and that immortal and incorrupt bodies must be sustained by earthly nourishment: otherwise, where there is food, diseases follow; where there are diseases, a physician must be called in; where there are physicians, death often follows; and then again a resurrection, and a new way of life all over again; but so that the taking of food might prove the faith of the resurrection. These things we guard against with great care, and we speak with restraint, knowing that we must walk among serpents and scorpions, which bite and strike in secret (Ezek 2). Of whom Ecclesiastes too says: If a serpent, he says, bites in silence, he who disparages in secret has nothing less than it (Eccl 10:11); and his father David: Sitting, you spoke against your brother, and against your mother's son you laid a stumbling block. These things you did, and I was silent: you thought, wicked one, that I shall be like you. I will rebuke you, and set it before your face (Ps 49 [50]:20, 21); so that you may be shown to have yourself what you charge against others. But if the length of my explanations on the Prophets displeases anyone, let him hear plainly that I have said far less than the obscurity of the matter demands, and that anyone can make brief little commentaries for himself out of our fullness, which nevertheless will not suffice for a full understanding. For the Prophets do not narrate a simple history and the order of events; but all things are full of riddles, and one thing sounds in the words, another is held in the sense: so that the things you would have reckoned plain, and running on in an unhindered reading, are wrapped again in the obscurities of what follows. But it is time for me to put an end to the prophetic volume, in whose exposition, if I am longer than usual, allowance must be made for its final parts, which I did not wish to divide, lest the number of books be increased.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.18-19":["For behold, I create Jerusalem an exultation, and its people a joy: and I will exult in Jerusalem, and rejoice in my people. LXX: For behold, I make Jerusalem an exultation, and my people a gladness, and I will exult over Jerusalem, and be glad over my people. You ought, he says, to rejoice and exult with everlasting gladness in the creation of new heavens and a new earth, and not to remember the former things at all, lest any sadness should arise in you from the recollection of the old: for I will create not only new heavens and a new earth, but Jerusalem too in exultation, and its people in joy. Without doubt it will be a gladness for the whole earth, so that the city which, when old, was the joy of one nation, when new may be the gladness of all the nations. And so great will be the exultation and gladness of all over the city and people of God, that I too, its founder, will exult and rejoice over Jerusalem and over my people, who shall eat and drink and be glad and exult in it, and who have been called by the new name.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.19":["And there shall no more be heard in it the voice of weeping, and the voice of crying: there shall no longer be an infant of days, and an old man who does not fill out his days. For the child shall die a hundred years old, and the sinner a hundred years old shall be accursed. LXX: And there shall no more be heard in it the voice of weeping, and the voice of crying: nor shall there be there any more one untimely and an old man who does not fill out his time. For there shall be an infant a hundred years old, and he who dies a sinner at a hundred shall be accursed. For it will not be fitting that in the city of Jerusalem and in the people of God, which will receive the eternal creation of exultation and joy, the voice of weeping and crying should be heard any more, when sorrow, mourning and groaning have gone away. For contraries cannot exist together. And where there is exultation and joy, which is the fruit of the Holy Spirit, there weeping and lamentation cannot abide, which befits those who mourn and grieve, and above all crying, which Paul expels from the Church of believers, lest, like the Jews, we make a crying instead of judgment (Eph 4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.20":["In such a city there will not be different ages: an infant and an old man, small and great, who does not fill out his days; but as sons of the resurrection all will come to the perfect man, to the measure of the age of the fullness of Christ, so that no one will lack a span of years nor have more than enough, nor will it be that, while one does not yet have firm strength, another ceases to be what he was and withers in decrepit age; and all will come to the number of a hundred, as Abraham was, who at this age received the promise of his son Isaac (Gen 21). Of the praises of this number there is no need to say much, lest the discussion be superfluous. This only we say: that ten decades have equal sides, and possess the firmness of a square form. In the promises too, for the things we have spent, the Lord promises us them multiplied a hundredfold; and the seed falling on good ground has as its first number of most abundant yield the hundred. Finally Isaac too, sowing one seed of faith, received the works of his labor multiplied by this number. At that time, then, when all will be of one age, both the saint and the sinner will be perfected by a like resurrection, nor will they differ from one another in time; but one will be drawn to rewards, the other to punishments: and the sinner will be accursed in this, that he will suffer eternal punishments in an incorrupt body. That too which we read in the Apocalypse of John (Chapter 20), that after the resurrection small and great are brought into the presence of the judge, signifies differences not of ages but of merits. For he who is small, according to the sentence of Solomon, is worthy of mercy: but the mighty shall mightily suffer torments (Wis 6:7). With this sense the words of the Lord also agree: The servant who knows the will of his lord and does not do it shall be beaten with many stripes (Luke 12:47). But he who does not know, and has done things worthy of stripes, shall be beaten with few. The blessed apostle Paul, with Christ speaking in him, expounding the old Scripture, declares the patriarch Abraham to be the father not only of the circumcision but of the uncircumcision (Rom 4), that is, that two peoples were begotten from him; and that the one which had been born according to the flesh, a hundred years old, lies under a perpetual curse because of Christ, who was born of the stock of Abraham, and on whom it laid hands. The Gospels teach this figure (Mark 5), in which it is related that the woman with the issue of blood began to be ill at the time when the daughter of the ruler of the synagogue was born, and that when the one was healed the other at once died, within one and the same span of years: that the new people persists in youth, and the old dies in unbelief and is accursed. This we have said according to the Seventy translators, whose edition is spread through the whole world, lest in a most famous passage we should seem to take refuge in the citadel of the Hebrew language. Whether you understand this after the resurrection at the second coming of the Savior, or believe it after baptism in the first resurrection, it is not at odds with the faith of the Church. The Hebrews contend that these things will come to pass before the resurrection in a kingdom of a thousand years upon the earth, and that so long a span of future life is promised that a hundred years are counted as infancy; but that the sinner will die in the hundredth year of his age: namely, so that he may not enjoy the wealth he has gathered, but may know that he is accursed because of his sin. But if it is so, where will be the perfect blessedness, which is violated and corrupted by sin, and where sin is punished by an untimely death?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.21-22":["And they shall build houses, and dwell in them; and they shall plant vineyards, and eat their fruits. They shall not build, and another dwell: they shall not plant, and another eat. LXX likewise. Of these houses the prophetic word also makes promise in Psalm 68 (69), saying: For God will save Zion: and the cities of Judah shall be built, and they shall dwell there, and acquire it by inheritance, and the seed of his servants shall possess it: and those who love his name shall dwell in it. All this the Jews take carnally, that Jerusalem and the cities of Judea are to be restored to their former state. But if we grant them this, let them hear that this is promised not only to Jerusalem but also to Sodom, as Ezekiel says: Sodom shall be restored to its ancient state (Ezek 16:55). Therefore the houses in which those who build them are to dwell are to be understood either as the Virtues or as the various mansions with the Father, which he who has built them will possess forever. Such houses the midwives too, who feared God, are said in Exodus to have built for themselves: although it is written in the Hebrew that houses were built for them by God, because they feared him (Exod 1). And Jacob, because he was simple, or, as it is said in Greek, ἄπλαστος, that is, by no means feigned, and not such as those who are condemned in the Epistle of Peter, of whom he says: In covetousness they make merchandise of you with feigned words (2 Pet 2:3), therefore dwelt in a house which Esau, who delighted in wild beasts and forests, could not possess. Such a house the words of the Savior in the Gospel also describe: Everyone who comes to me, and hears my words, and does them, will be like a prudent man who built his house upon the rock (Matt 7:24), and the rest. Otherwise, according to the letter, many saints build houses and do not dwell in them, being taken away by travel abroad, or by another's plunder, or by death. Such was Job, who, rich in flesh and spirit, was brought down to such poverty that he sat on a dunghill outside the gate of the city (Job 2). On the contrary, that rich man in the Gospel, clothed in purple, built a house and dwelt in it, to whom it could most rightly be said: Fool, this night your soul shall be taken from you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be (Luke 12:20)? And he says not only, They shall build houses and dwell in them, but also, They shall plant vineyards, and they themselves shall eat their fruit. According to what is said in Micah: Each shall rest under his vine and under his fig tree; and there shall be none to terrify him (Mic 4:4). This is the vine that spoke in the Gospel: I am the vine, and you are the branches, and my Father is the husbandman (John 15:5). Every one that does not bear fruit shall be cut down, and cast into the fire (Matt 3:10). Its fruits are eaten and drunk, and gladden the heart of man, and inebriate the friends of the bridegroom, and are drunk daily in the kingdom of God. And he rests under the fig tree, and fears no one's snares, who enjoys the sweetness of the Holy Spirit and is filled with his fruits: love, joy, peace, faith, continence, patience. Of a planter of this kind it is said: He who plants a fig tree shall eat its fruits (Amos 9:14). He who has built such houses and planted such vineyards, of which the Apostle too speaks: I planted, Apollos watered, God gave the increase (1 Cor 3:6), shall eat the labors of his hands (Ps 127 [128]); and sowing in the spirit, from the spirit he shall reap eternal life (Gal 6): nor shall he be supplanted by the frauds of the devil and his henchmen.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.22":["For as the days of the tree, so shall be the days of my people, and the works of their hands shall wear out for my elect. They shall not labor in vain, nor bring forth in trouble: for it is the seed of the blessed of the Lord, and their grandchildren with them. And it shall be that before they cry out, I will hear: while they are still speaking, I will hear. The wolf and the lamb shall feed together, and the lion and the ox shall eat straw, and for the serpent dust shall be its bread. They shall not harm nor kill in all my holy mountain, says the Lord. LXX: For as the days of the tree of life, so the days of my people: the works of their labors my elect shall make old: they shall not labor in vain, nor bring forth sons for a curse: for it is a seed blessed by the Lord, and their grandchildren with them. And it shall be that before they cry out, I will hear them: while they are still speaking I will say, What is it? Then wolves and lambs shall feed together: and the lion like the ox shall eat straw, and the serpent earth as bread. They shall not harm nor destroy in my holy mountain, says the Lord. Eternal will be the dwelling and the planting of the righteous, so that the days of the life of my people are compared to the days of the life of the tree, of which it is said in the Psalms: The righteous shall flourish like the palm (Ps 91 [92]:13), daily triumphing over his adversaries and bearing before him the token of victory. But if we read according to the Seventy, who rendered the sense from the Hebrew rather than the words, the days of the tree of life, we understand the tree of life which is set in paradise. Lest Adam should stretch out his hand to it and live, he was cast out of paradise (Gen 3). To guard it were set the Cherubim, that is, a multitude of knowledge, and a fiery sword, to keep the way of the tree of life, lest Adam, placed in sin and not yet acknowledging his offenses, should eat of it and die the death of impenitence, despair and pride. What this tree of life is, Solomon explains more plainly, who, discoursing of the wisdom of God, says: It is a tree of life to all who come to it, and for those who lean upon it, it is as firm as upon the Lord (Prov 3:18); nor is there doubt that it signifies the Word of God, who is himself the way and wisdom, and says of himself: I am the life (John 11:25). And of whom the Prophet sings: In wisdom you have made all things: the earth is filled with your creation (Ps 103 [104]:24); and the apostle Paul: Christ the power of God, and the wisdom of God (1 Cor 1:24). The works of the people of God too shall not grow old, but shall be renewed daily, so that they walk not in the oldness of the letter, but in the newness of the spirit (Rom 7). So that, as through the disobedience of one man very many were made sinners, so through the obedience of one man many may be made righteous. Or certainly this must be said: that the works of those grow old whose house, founded upon the rock, abides forever, and who have built upon the foundation of Christ gold, silver, precious stones (1 Cor 3); and that, on the contrary, the works of those perish whose house, founded upon the sand, is overturned by a sudden storm, and who have built upon the foundation of Christ wood, hay, stubble. Or does it not seem to you that he makes his works old daily who, forgetting the things that are past, stretches forward to what is to come? Hence a Testament is called both old and new: not that the old perishes, but that no other succeeds the new. This too can be said according to the Hebrew: that the works of the people, and of those who believe in Christ, are the fruits of the Apostles, that is, of the elect of God, and are laid up in their treasuries. For many are called, and few are chosen (Matt 20).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.23":["These shall not labor in vain, as the Jews once labored; but they shall eat the labors of their hands. Nor shall they bring forth in trouble, or for a curse, or, as is put more expressly in the Hebrew, εἰς ἀνυπαρξίαν, which in their language is said LABALA (לבהלה), that is, so that they cease to be and have an end of their substance: for which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion translated haste: so that they may by no means hasten to believe without reason, but may imitate Nathanael, who was praised by the voice of the Lord: Behold, truly an Israelite, in whom there is no guile (John 1:47), who sought Christ by the authority of the Scriptures and longed to recognize him from the Prophets, saying: Can anything good come out of Nazareth? And the sense is: How do you bring me a Messiah from Galilee and Nazareth, whom I know to have been promised from Bethlehem of Judah? The Apostles, then, and apostolic men will so bring forth sons as to instruct them from the holy Scriptures, so that they do not imitate the curse of the Jews, but say with the Prophet: From your fear, O Lord, we have conceived in the womb, and have been in labor and have brought forth (Isa 26:18). For of these it had been said: Blessed is the offspring of your womb (Deut 28:4). Such are the sons of Abraham, who do his works: and in the old history they are called the sons of the Prophets (John 8): such too as the Apostles begot in the new Instrument (Acts 16): Paul begot Timothy, Luke and Titus and many others; Peter Mark the evangelist, and the rest others; whose seed is blessed and is blessed to this day, and the sons of sons remain. Of whom the Prophet says: Blessed is the man who fears the Lord; he will delight exceedingly in his commandments. His seed shall be mighty on the earth; the generation of the upright shall be blessed (Ps 111 [112]:1, 2). And elsewhere: Your sons like young olive plants round about your table. Behold, thus shall the man be blessed who fears the Lord (Ps 127 [128]:4, 5). On the contrary, of Judas the betrayer, and of all who are like him, it is said: Let his sons be orphans, and his wife a widow. Let his sons be shaken and carried off, and let them beg; let them be cast out of their houses (Ps 108 [109]:9, 10). For the end of an evil generation is very bad. They conceive sorrow and bring forth iniquity. Of whose prince it is written: Behold, he has travailed with injustice, he has conceived sorrow, and brought forth iniquity (Ps 7:15). But if this is said of the wicked, how is it that Samuel, a blessed and righteous man, begot very wicked sons, who did not walk in their father's way (1 Sam 8); and David, of whose seed Christ was born, begot two sons, Amnon and Absalom, of whom the one became the murderer of his brother, the other of his father (2 Sam 13, 15 and 16)? From all these things we learn that sons and grandchildren ought to be understood according to what we have said. According to which Paul too is in labor with sons until Christ be formed in them, and begets Onesimus in chains (Gal 4).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.24":["When these cry out, they will at once be heard, and while they are speaking the Lord will say, Here I am. All of which we see fulfilled even according to the letter in the Acts of the Apostles. For all the nations could not have believed in so short a time unless their faith had in some way been wrung from them by the miracles of signs. For when the Apostles and apostolic men spoke and cried out, the Lord answered with the greatness of his signs, so that Dorcas rose at the prayers of Peter (Acts 9), so that the father of Publius, on the island of Malta, was healed by the prayers of Paul of fevers and dysentery, which are diseases contrary to each other, and so that the whole world said that they were gods walking among men (Acts 28).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.65.25":["Then the wolf and the lamb fed together, Paul the persecutor and Ananias the disciple (Acts 9). He is the wolf of whom it is written: Benjamin, a ravening wolf, in the morning shall eat the prey, and at evening shall give food (Gen 49:27); or, as it is said in the Hebrew, shall divide the spoils. His teaching was the food of believers, and he triumphed over his vanquished adversaries in the whole world. But the lambs are to be believed to be all who in white garments follow the Lamb wherever he goes (Rev 14), whom the Lord handed over to Peter to feed, saying: Feed my lambs (John 21:16). The lion too like the ox shall eat straw, when most eloquent men, once powerful in the world, give themselves over to the rusticity of the Scriptures, so that they by no means feed on worldly eloquence, which like honey drips from the lips of a harlot, but follow the plainness and the straw of the history, until by much labor and diligence they deserve to come to the grain of the senses. Of this straw and grain Jeremiah speaks: What has straw to do with wheat, says the Lord (Jer 23:28)? And it must be considered that it is not the ox that is turned to fury, but the lion that is changed to gentleness. The serpent too, which watched for the heel of man, and whose head was watched for by man, will by no means be nourished by the destruction of others, but will eat earth, or dust, as bread (Gen 3). Or certainly it is to be understood thus: that the devil, who formerly fed on the deaths of men, eats only those who are dust and earth: by all of which is shown the change of the wicked into good, who will be harmless, having laid aside their ancient ferocity: not outside, but in the holy mountain of the Lord, that is, in the Church, and in the confession of that mountain by which the prince of Tyre was wounded, and which, cut out of the mountain without hands, grew into a great mountain and filled the world (Dan 2). Let us in this place ask the Jews, and all who under the Christian name still eat the straw of the Scriptures, which, separated from the wheat by the winnowing fan of the Lord, will be given over to the wind and the flames, what blessedness is to be reckoned in this: that in a kingdom of a thousand years, on Mount Zion, in Jerusalem, the city of Christ then present, and in a most august Temple, wolves and lambs, lions and oxen, serpents and men should eat together and dwell alike? and that they should be harmless only to those who have dwelt in the holy mountain of the Lord? From which we understand that all who are outside the mountain are to be slain. So the whole world will be stripped of wolves, lions, bears, leopards and serpents and the other beasts; and the immense woodlands, and the vast waste of the Egyptian desert, which is fertile in venomous creatures; and, as the height of happiness, the holy city will be the dwelling place not only of men but of beasts and serpents, so that, according to the earlier prophecy, the wolf dwells with the lamb, and the leopard with the kid, the calf and the lion, and sheep stay together with bears (Isa 11); and a little child leads them, and an infant from the breast puts his hand into the hole of the asp, and kills the basilisk in its den. And the cause of so great a happiness is that the whole earth has been filled with the knowledge of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.1-2":["Thus says the Lord: Heaven is my seat, and earth the footstool of my feet. What is this house that you will build for me, and what is this place of my rest? All these things my hand has made, and all these things have come to be, says the Lord. LXX likewise. Lest we should understand the holy mountain as Zion and be covered with the Jewish error, who think that Jerusalem is to be built up and that all the things the Lord promises are to be fulfilled there carnally, he takes this suspicion away from us, and sets down the testimony which Stephen, the first martyr in Christ, used against the contentiousness of the Jews (Acts 7). Solomon built him a house, without doubt for God; but the Most High does not dwell in things made with hands, as the Prophet says: Heaven is my throne: but earth is the footstool of my feet. And Paul in the same volume: God, who made the world and those who are in it, by no means dwells in temples made with hands (Acts 17:24). For if, like one sitting on a throne and reigning, heaven is his throne and earth the footstool of his feet, how shall he be shut up in a small place who fills all things, and in whom all things are? Hence Moses too: Do not say, he says, in your heart, He is far off: for God is in heaven above and upon the earth beneath: and there is no other God besides him (Deut 4:39). And the Psalmist: Where shall I go from your spirit, and where shall I flee from your face? If I go up into heaven, you are there: if I go down to hell, you are present (Ps 138 [139]:7). With him Jeremiah too agrees, speaking in the person of God: I am a God at hand, and not a God from afar. Can anyone hide in secret places, and I not see him? Do I not fill heaven and earth (Jer 23:23, 24)? For in him we all are and move (Acts 17). And he says this to refute the Jewish error, who think that the invisible, incorporeal and incomprehensible God can be shut up in the temple of Jerusalem. This indeed Solomon himself, the builder of the Temple, confesses at length in his prayer to the Lord (1 Kgs 8). And lest we think that the greatness of God is to be measured even by heaven and earth, we read of him in another place: Who holds heaven in his palm, and the earth in his fist (Isa 40:12). By these things God is shown to be both outside and within, both poured into and poured around, since he is not shut in by the throne that surrounds him, and he shuts in with his fist and his palm. He is the creator not only of heaven and earth, but also of the invisible Angels and Archangels, Dominions, Powers, and of all men, of whom the Apostle speaks (Col 1): all of which the hand of God has worked. Of this hand both Job and the Psalmist make mention: Your hand has made me and fashioned me (Job 10:18; Ps 118 [119]:73). For all things were made through him, and without him nothing was made. Of whom John says again: He was in the world, he says, and the world was made through him (John 1). For he spoke, and they were made; he commanded, and they were created (Ps 32 [33]). And this is what the Scripture of Genesis shows in mystical speech: God said, and God made (Gen 1). For by the Word of the Lord the heavens were made firm, and all their power by the spirit of his mouth. And there is no place of the Lord's rest except the one which the Prophet adds next.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.1":["The Lord is in his holy temple, the Lord's seat is in heaven. Before the bodily temple was built by Solomon, the prophet knows the spiritual temple of the Lord and his throne in heaven. Whereupon it is asked, how it is said elsewhere, \"Heaven is my throne.\" When the throne of the Lord is in heaven, his throne is encompassed by heaven, and the throne of the Lord is in a part of heaven; when heaven is the throne of the Lord, the whole heaven is turned into the throne of the Lord. From which, according to the ἀναγωγήν (higher spiritual sense, anagōgē), the Lord dwelling in his holy temple — that is, in the soul of the believer — dwells either in a part of it or in the whole. When we are still imperfect, and there is in us both something good and something evil, God dwells only in a part of our soul, that is, in heaven. But when we are consummate and perfect, we are wholly turned into a dwelling-place of God, and we are made heaven, his throne.\n— Homily 60, on Psalm 10 (Alternate Series)"],"ISA.66.2":["But to whom shall I look, except to the poor little one and the contrite of spirit, and the one who trembles at my words? He who sacrifices an ox is as one who kills a man. He who slaughters a sheep is as one who brains a dog. He who offers an oblation is as one who offers swine's blood. He who remembers frankincense is as one who blesses an idol. LXX: And upon whom shall I look, except the humble and quiet, and the one who trembles at my words? ÷ But the wicked man ⁚ who sacrifices a calf is as one who strikes down a man. He who sacrifices from the flock is as one who kills a dog. He who offers fine flour is as one who offers swine's blood. He who gives frankincense for a memorial is as a blasphemer. Once the altar and the earthly Temple, which the hand of man had built, have been taken away, the victims of the Jews too are rightly taken away, lest perhaps they should say: We are not so foolish as to think that God can be shut up in a place; but in a place set apart for sacrificing, we sacrifice to God the victims which are prescribed by the law. The inhabitant of heaven, then, or rather the Creator of all, who denies that he has a temple on earth, gladly takes up as his temple the man who is humble and quiet and trembles at his words, according to that saying of the Apostle: But you are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you. If anyone violates the temple of God, God will violate him. For the temple of God is holy, which you are (1 Cor 3:16, 17). Whoever, then, is humble and quiet and trembles at the words of God, him the Lord looks upon; and of him may be understood what is prophesied under the name of the earth: The earth trembled and was quiet, when God arose to judgment (Ps 75 [76]:9, 10). For its inhabitants, for whom the earth is named μετωνυμικῶς, considering the judgment of God, keep quiet from evil works; and sitting at home, rest in an eternal sabbath, so as not to stir themselves to the servile work of sin, as the builders of the tower once moved their feet from the East and left the rising of the true light. Nor did they hear what is said to Cain: You have sinned, be quiet (Gen 4). But according to the Proverbs of Solomon: He who hears God shall dwell confidently, and shall rest without fear from every evil (Prov 1:33). This is the humble and poor little one, contrite of spirit and trembling at the words of the Lord, of whom it is written in the Gospel: Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven (Matt 5:3). And elsewhere: Blessed is he who considers the needy and the poor (Ps 40 [41]:1). And again: The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me; he has sent me to preach good news to the poor (Isa 61:1).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.3-4":["All these things they have chosen in their ways, and in their abominations their soul has delighted. Therefore I too will choose their mockeries, and what they feared I will bring upon them. For I called, and there was none to answer: I spoke, and they did not hear: and they did evil in my eyes: and what I did not will, they chose. LXX: And they themselves have chosen their ways, and their soul has willed their abominations: and I will choose their mockeries, and repay them their sins. For I called them, and they did not hearken: I spoke, and they did not hear. And they did evil in my sight, and what I did not will they chose. To sacrifice an ox, to slaughter a ram, to offer sacrifice, to burn frankincense, which God regards as murder and the offering of a dog, and swine's blood and blasphemy, they themselves chose; and that they should worship such things was of their own choice; according to what he says through Ezekiel: I gave them statutes not good, and precepts not good (Ezek 20:25). And in their own ways, he says, they walked, not in the way of the Lord. And their soul has willed their abominations, so that they were lovers of themselves rather than lovers of God. For this reason, since they chose their own ways, and not him who says: I am the way (John 14:6), the Lord too chose their mockeries, or their mockers, who in Hebrew are called THALULE (תעללי), that is, ἐμπαῖκται, so as to set over them very wicked princes, and bring upon them likewise all their evils, or what they feared. And he gives the reasons why they were handed over to the Scribes and Pharisees, their mockers, of whom the first psalm sings according to the Hebrews: And he has not sat in the chair of the mockers: whom the Seventy call the pestilent: such as were the sons of Eli, sons of pestilence, for which in the Hebrew BELIAL (בליעל) is written, that is, of the devil. For, he says, I called, and there was none to answer: I spoke, and they did not hear: and they did evil in my eyes: and what I did not will, they chose. These verses he sets down a second time in the present place, and also above, where it is said: I will number you for the sword, and you shall all fall in the slaughter: for I called, and you did not answer (Isa 65:12), and the rest likewise. In what sense this testimony ought to be taken was said there.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.3":["And what follows in the common edition: But the wicked man, is not found in the Hebrew, but it simply joins on and says: He who sacrifices an ox is as one who kills a man. This is also written in another place: I desire mercy, and not sacrifice: the knowledge of God rather than holocaust (Hos 6:6). And through Malachi: All that I hated, you did: covering my altar with tears: is it fitting that I should look upon your sacrifice, and receive anything acceptable from your hands (Mal 2:13)? Let the Jews hear that God does not seek sacrifices, but the mind of those who offer. And he who slaughters, he says, a sheep is as one who brains a dog. Hence it is also written through Moses: You shall not offer the hire of a harlot, nor the price of a dog, in the house of your God. (Deut 23:18). And the dog and the harlot are fittingly joined, because each animal is prone to lust. And at the same time let us consider that he did not say: He who sacrifices a ram is as one who sacrifices a dog; but as one who kills, he says, a dog. This word is not used of sacrifices, but of things that are slaughtered unlawfully. He who offers an oblation and sacrifice is as one who offers swine's blood. This too is itself forbidden by the Law; such are the ceremonies of the Jews (Lev 11, and Deut 14). He who gives frankincense is as a blasphemer, or rather as one who blesses an idol. But this too may be the sense: After my Son, who came meek and poor, sitting upon the colt of an ass, I do not want flesh, I detest victims, I reject the sacrifices of the Jews and the shadows of the Law, because the truth of the Gospel is delightful to me (Zech 9, and Matt 21, and Luke 19, and John 12). But if this is said, that after the lightning of the Gospel the old religion ceases in the cloud, what will they answer who, believing from among the Jews, think that they can offer sacrifices carnally without harm to themselves?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.5":["Hear the word of the Lord, you who tremble at his speech. Your brothers who hate you, and cast you out for my name's sake, have said: Let the Lord be glorified, and we shall see in your gladness: but they shall be put to shame. LXX: Hear the word of the Lord, you who tremble at his speech. Say to your brothers who hate you and abhor you, that the name of the Lord may be glorified: and may it appear in their gladness: and they shall be put to shame. Those of whom he had already said before: Upon whom shall I look, except upon the humble and quiet, and the one who trembles at my words? he commands that, having despised the victims of the Jews and scorned all the swelling pride of the Scribes and Pharisees, they pass over to the worship of him whom those others would not hear when he called, and despised, and did evil in his sight. Nor is he content with this end of his precepts, but he enjoins on them the commandment of the Gospel (Luke 6): that they love their enemies, do good to those who hate them, and pray for their persecutors, and imitate the mercy of the Father, who makes his sun rise upon the just and the unjust: That seeing, he says, your works, they may glorify your Father who is in heaven (Matt 5:16). After the general understanding, this is commanded specially to the Apostles and to apostolic men: that they love the Jews, their persecutors, and hold those who abhor them in the place of brothers, saying with Peter: Men, brothers and fathers, hear; and with the apostle Paul: I have sadness, and continual sorrow in my heart, for my brothers who are Israelites (Rom 9:2). And the name of the Lord is glorified when men see the ferocity of persecutors broken by our patience, and the striking hand put to shame by the turning of the other cheek. To these it is commanded in the Gospel: Let your light shine before men, that seeing your good works they may glorify your Father who is in heaven (Matt 5:16); and in the Apocalypse of John: Fear God, and give him glory (Rev 14:7). Paul too writes to the Corinthians, calling them to continence: That you may glorify God in your body (1 Cor 6:20). And again: Whether you eat or drink, do all to the glory of God (1 Cor 10:31). Hence the Lord too did not curse in return those who cursed him (1 Pet 2), and prayed for his persecutors. But what he commands: Say to your brothers (Matt 28:10), is not to be taken simply; otherwise many call others brothers and do not keep love in their heart, of whom the Apostle writes: They profess that they know God, but in their works they deny him (Titus 1:16). Of these the Lord too was speaking: Not everyone who says to me: Lord, Lord, shall enter the kingdom of heaven: but he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven (Matt 7:21). And so that sentence can stand: No one says, Lord Jesus, except in the Holy Spirit (1 Cor 12:3). For though the heretics say Lord Jesus, and many in the resurrection will say: Lord, did we not prophesy in your name, and do many mighty works (Matt 7:22)? the Lord will answer them: Depart from me, I do not know you, workers of iniquity. Therefore saying is proved not by speech, which is easy, but by disposition and works. From these things we learn that the Lord of both Testaments is the same, who commands that if we see the beast of our enemy fall under its burden, we should not pass by, but lift it up with him, and if we find an ox or an ass straying, we should return it to him (Luke 15, Matt 18). So much according to the Seventy. Further, according to the Hebrew, this seems to me to be the sense: Hear, Apostles, hear, my disciples, you who tremble at the word of the Lord: I will tell what your brothers have said, who hate and separate you and reckon you strangers, not because of your evils but because of my name; who think unclean all those of their nation who believe in me, and say: Depart from me, for you are unclean. What, then, is it that he records them to have said: Let the Lord be glorified, and we shall see in your gladness? The sense of this little verse is this: Why do you bring in to us a humble God? why one crucified, and a man of sorrows, who knows how to bear infirmities? We want to behold him reigning in his majesty (as you say): we will look up to him triumphing in his glory too; humble and cast down we cannot see him. And at once he adds: But they shall be put to shame; understood: those who say such things, who do not understand the mysteries of the Scriptures, and will feel through their own evils the power of him whom they had despised for his humility.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.6":["A voice of uproar from the city, a voice from the Temple, the voice of the Lord rendering recompense to his enemies. For uproar the LXX put crying, and the rest likewise. We wish to know what the confusion of the Jews is, who said: Let the Lord be glorified, that we may see your gladness, and may behold the triumphs of your king with our eyes, by no means in empty promises. A voice, he says, of crying from the city: without doubt it signifies Jerusalem surrounded by the Roman army and divided within into three parts by sedition, when one held the Temple and possessed all that had formerly been holy, fighting outside against the enemy and inside against his fellow citizens. At that time, both in the city and in the Temple, there was heard the howling of priests and Levites as well as of the common rabble, of women and children, when the Lord rendered recompense to his enemies, fulfilling the threat in which he had said: Your house shall be left to you desolate (Luke 13:35); and that prophecy: I have forsaken my house. When the Angels presiding over the temple said with one voice: Let us pass out of these abodes. Of these things not only Josephus, the writer of the Jewish history, but many ages before the Psalmist testifies, saying: I have seen iniquity and contradiction in the city (Ps 54 [55]:10), which surrounded its walls all day and all night, so that the city was overthrown (Mic 3), and another prophecy was fulfilled: Zion shall be plowed like a field: and Jerusalem shall be left like a hut in a garden of cucumbers (Isa 1:8).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.7":["Before she was in labor, she brought forth: before her travail came, she brought forth a male. Who has ever heard such a thing? who has seen the like of this ? Shall the earth be in labor in one day: or shall a nation be brought forth at once? for Zion has been in labor and has brought forth her sons: Shall I, who make others bring forth, not bring forth myself, says the Lord? If I, who give generation to the rest, am barren, says the Lord your God. LXX: Before she who travails brings forth: before the pain of those in travail comes, she escapes, and she has brought forth a male. Who has heard such a thing, and who has seen the like? If the earth has travailed in one day; or if a nation has been born at once, because Zion has travailed and brought forth her little ones. But I have given this expectation, and you have not remembered me, says the Lord. Have I not made her who bears and the barren, says your God? While the crying resounded from Jerusalem and its Temple, when it was besieged and overthrown, and the enemies of Christ, who would not receive God as king, were receiving eternal punishments for their impiety and blasphemies, the Church gathered in the name of the Lord, of which it is said in the psalm: A man was born in her: and the most high himself has founded her (Ps 86 [87]:5), before she was in labor brought forth; before her travail came, she bore a male. For it was not over a long time, as the people of the Jews grew greater through Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and the twelve Patriarchs, and again through their children and grandchildren; but at the preaching of the Gospel the whole world at once conceived, and was in labor, and brought forth a male, whom Pharaoh and Herod tried to kill, and who, both in Moses and in Christ, was saved in Egypt. Finally, Abraham and Isaac had male children, and Jacob, the father of many sons, begot one daughter, for whose sake he suffered hard things (Gen 34). And if the daughters of Zelophehad, by the sentence of God, receive their father's inheritance (Num 27), this is to be considered: that their father, who begot no son, died in his own sin, and that Moses dared to judge nothing concerning them, but referred it to the Lord, who, lest they remain without means, commanded them to be joined to their kinsmen. The book of Genesis also relates (Chapter 6) that after men began to become many, a number that is always associated with vice, and daughters were born to them, they were taken, not by Angels, but by the sons of God, from whom the giants were born: or, as it is written in the Hebrew, ἐπιπίπτοντες, that is, those who fall upon. On the contrary, it is said to the saint: Your wife like an abundant vine on the sides of your house. Your sons like young olive plants round about your table (Ps 127 [128]:3). And again: May you see your sons' sons (ibid. 6).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.8-9":["Further, according to the LXX the sense is this: that at one time, at the preaching of the Gospels, one nation of the whole world should arise. Through many prophets I have often promised, and you have not remembered my promise, O city which is full of cries, O Temple which has been forsaken by the Lord, O people to whom I have rendered its recompense. Have I not, he says, made her who travails and the barren; she who before had been barren afterwards travailed and brought forth? Of her it is written in the psalm: Who makes the barren woman dwell in a house, a mother rejoicing in sons (Ps 112 [113]:9). Or certainly the order of things has been reversed: she who bore has become barren, and she who was once barren has brought forth very many, because this is the sentence of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.8":["Zion, then, that is, the remnant from Israel and the faith of the believing Apostles, brought forth a male, the Lord Savior, who was born at once in the whole world: something that no one has heard of, that no history relates, nor the teaching of anyone, that all the nations should believe in a short time, and that out of all the nations there should be made one nation of the Christians, of which Paul too speaks: If anyone is a new creature in Christ, the old things have passed away; behold, they have become new (2 Cor 5:17): according to what is written elsewhere: And all the families of the nations shall worship in his sight: for the kingdom is the Lord's, and he shall rule over the nations. For all the nations, he says, which you have made shall come and worship before you, and shall glorify your name (Ps 21 [22]:28ff.). Of him Jacob says: He is the expectation of the nations (Gen 49:10). And the Psalmist: The hope of all the ends of the earth (Ps 64 [65]:6). And the same Isaiah whom we are now expounding: There shall be the root of Jesse, and he who shall rise up to be prince of the nations; in him the nations shall hope (Isa 11:10); the Apostles fulfilling what was commanded: Teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit (Matt 28:19), so that a new people may be born, of which Psalm 21 (22) also sings: The heavens shall announce his righteousness to a people that shall be born, which the Lord has made (Ps 21 [22]:32). And again: A people that shall be created shall praise the Lord (Ps 101 [102]:19). And this people was created in one day, which the sun of righteousness illumines, as Scripture says: The Lord shall be to you an everlasting light (Isa 60:19). What is said: A nation shall arise at once, for Zion has been in labor and has brought forth her sons, we can also refer to that time when in one day three thousand and five thousand of the Jewish people believed (Acts 2). It is also said in the same book of the Acts of the Apostles that there were in Jerusalem men from all the nations under heaven, who heard them speaking the great works of God in various tongues (ibid.).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.9":["And what follows according to the LXX: But I have given this expectation, and you have not remembered me, says the Lord. Have I not made her who bears and the barren, said your God, is put more plainly in the Hebrew, with which the other translators also agree: Shall I, who make others bring forth, not bring forth myself, says the Lord? If I, who give generation to the rest, am barren, says the Lord your God; according to what is said elsewhere: He who planted the ear, shall he not hear? and he who formed the eye, does he not consider (Ps 93 [94]:9); namely, that he who created all men out of nothing can make out of all the nations one part of those who believe in him, the Church. Finally, in Psalm 28 (29), where we read: The voice of the Lord shaking the desert (Ps 28 [29]:8), in the Hebrew it is written: The voice of the Lord making the desert bring forth (According to the Hebrew), so that the Church, formerly a desert, may bring forth, and the stags may be perfected when the woodlands are opened and crushed.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.10":["Rejoice with Jerusalem, and exult in her, all you who love her. Rejoice with her with joy, all you who mourn over her, that you may suck and be filled from the breast of her consolation, that you may milk and abound with delights from her glory of every kind. LXX: Rejoice, Jerusalem, and make an assembly, all you who love her. Rejoice with joy, all you who mourn over her, that you may suck and be filled from the breast of her consolation, that, after you have sucked, you may be delighted from the entrance of her glory. It is commanded to the Apostles and to apostolic men, who love both Jerusalems, bewailing and mourning the one that has fallen, and awaiting with all desire the one that is to rise, that they rejoice with her and in her, who has been built of living stones, which roll upon the earth and, in the likeness of the wheels of the Cherubim, follow the spirit going before: not in those stones which have been dissolved into eternal ashes. Of these the Lord was speaking: Amen, amen I say to you, there shall not remain stone upon stone, until all these things are fulfilled (Matt 24:2). Rejoice, he says, with her with joy, all you who mourn her. Such were those whom we have shown above, the apostles Peter and Paul, and all who were awaiting the redemption of Israel: that they may suck and be filled at the breasts of her consolation.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.11":["For it could not be that she who had borne a male and had just given birth should lack an abundance of milk for the rearing of that nation and of the little ones who had been born at once, so that she might offer them two breasts, by no means, as formerly in Egypt, broken and lying low, but whole and standing in virginal beauty, those of the Old and New Instrument, to supply rational milk. Of these the bridegroom speaks to her: Your breasts are better than wine (Song 1:1). These were the ones of whom they specially said: Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted (Matt 5:5). And this is to be noted: that those who are little ones need the milk of consolation, and are still in a mortal place. But those who after the milk of consolation have advanced to solid food will abound with the delights of truth, and with the knowledge of glory of every kind, which in Hebrew is called ZIZ (זיז). For this the LXX translated entrance; Symmachus fatness; Theodotion multitude. We have said this in order to open an ambiguous word of Psalm 79 (80), in which it is written: The boar from the forest has laid it waste, and the solitary wild beast has fed upon it (Ps 79 [80]:14). For where in our codices and in the Greek one reads μόνιος ἄγριος, that is, the solitary wild beast, in the Hebrew ZIZ SADAI (זיז שדי) is written: which Aquila translated παντόδαπον χώρας, that is, every kind of thing of the region, to signify that Israel was laid waste not only by the Romans but by all the nations. And the glorious entrance of the Church is to be understood as that of which the apostle John writes: And in the twelve gates were twelve pearls, and the streets of the city of pure gold, like transparent glass (Rev 21:21). These pearls the Lord forbids to be cast before swine (Matt 7); and when the good merchant has found them in the Prophets and Apostles, he goes on to the one most precious pearl, not casting away the former ones, but seeking through them the more precious (Matt 13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.12":["For thus says the Lord: Behold, I turn upon her as it were a river of peace, and as an overflowing torrent the glory of the nations, which you shall suck: you shall be carried at the breast, and upon the knees they shall caress you. LXX: For thus says the Lord: Behold, I will turn toward them like a river of peace, and like a torrent overflowing the glory of the nations. Their little ones shall be carried on the shoulders, and upon the knees they shall be comforted. When Jerusalem has been built up by the Apostles and restored to her former state, after her sons and inhabitants have been comforted at her breasts and through the abundance of milk have come to the delights of truth, through which one comes to the glory of God: then the Lord will turn upon her the river of peace, of which we have often said: The river of God is filled with waters (Ps 64 [65]:10). And: The rush of the river gladdens the city of God (Ps 45 [46]:5); so that, with peace possessing all things, and the wars of the nations ceasing, of whom Scripture says: Scatter the nations that want wars (Ps 67 [68]:32), the torrent of the teachings of God may water the fields of believers. Then her sons, or little ones (as the LXX translated), shall be carried on the shoulders, and upon the knees they shall receive comfort. On the shoulders, of which it was also said in the earlier prophecy, and which Jacob invokes upon his son Issachar: For he put his shoulder under to labor (Gen 49:15), and he was called a husbandman. For it is by sweat and great labor that we come to an abundance of fruits. Hence it is said to Zion through Jeremiah too: Set your heart upon your shoulders (Jer 24:7), so that she may understand the Lord from his commandments, imitating him of whom it is written: Jesus began to do and to teach (Acts 1:1), so that faith may not be idle, but may run through works to the reward. What knees and bosom signify I have briefly explained above too, and now the example of Abraham can teach us, in whose bosom Lazarus rests, and all who come from the East and the West, who shall recline with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.13":["As one whom a mother caresses, so will I comfort you, and in Jerusalem you shall be comforted. You shall see, and your heart shall rejoice, and your bones shall sprout like grass. And the hand of the Lord shall be known to his servants, and he shall be indignant with his enemies. For servants the LXX put those who fear; for enemies, the unbelieving: the rest likewise. We learn the mercy of the Creator toward his creatures from the example of mothers, who, nursing their children with love in their bosom, surpass every affection. Finally, God, wishing to show how he loves those whom he has created: Shall, he says, a woman forget, so as not to have pity on the offspring of her womb? but even if she should forget, yet I will not forget you (Isa 49:15). The Gospel passage too has this sense, in which the Lord speaks to Jerusalem: How often have I wished to gather your sons, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you would not (Matt 23:37)? And Deuteronomy, in which something of the kind resounds: As the eagle protects her nest, and has longed for her young: spreading her wings, she has taken them up and carried them on her shoulders (Deut 32:11). Those who have written on the natures of living creatures say that in all wild beasts, beasts of burden, cattle and birds there is an inborn affection toward their offspring and young, but that the love of eagles is the greatest, which place their nests in high and inaccessible places, lest a snake harm the brood. And that an amethyst stone is found among its young, by which all poisons are overcome. If this is true, the affection of God toward his creatures is rightly compared to eagles, who protects his children with every guard, lest the dragon and ancient snake, the devil and Satan, creep up on the new brood, so that at the name of the stone which is laid in the foundations of Zion all the snares of the adversaries may be broken. And this Jerusalem, in which the sons shall be comforted by their mother and fondled on her knees, is the one of which the Apostle writes: But the Jerusalem that is above is free, which is the mother of us all (Gal 4:26). Filled with the milk of her consolation, he comforted the others who needed his words, saying: Blessed be God, the Father of mercies and the God of all consolation, who comforts us in all tribulation, so that we too may be able to comfort those who are in any tribulation, through the consolation with which we ourselves are comforted by God (2 Cor 1:3).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.14":["After these have been comforted, it will be said to them: And you shall see, and your heart shall rejoice, and your bones shall sprout like grass, or shall spring up, or according to Symmachus, shall flourish (John 16:22). And they shall see, without doubt, God, which is true gladness. Of these the Lord spoke: Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God (Matt 5:8). The vision of him is perfect joy, which dwells in the heart of full faith: and the sprouting of the bones follows, of which we have already spoken before. If the reader has forgotten this, let him go back to the earlier explanation. For it is better for him to read again what was written than for us to repeat what was said. And known, he says, shall be the hand of the Lord to his servants, or to those who fear him, and he shall threaten his enemies, or his unbelieving. Now the hand is taken either for power, in that God can do all the things that he promises, according to what Moses says in the person of God to Pharaoh: Now I will send forth my hand, and I will strike you, and I will kill your people, and you shall be blotted out from the earth (Exod 3:20). And again he himself to the same tyrant: Behold, the hand of the Lord shall be upon your cattle (Exod 9:4); of which the Psalmist said to God: Your hand slew the nations, and you planted them (Ps 43 [44]). Or certainly the hand of the Lord is to be understood as Christ, of whom we also read above: My hand has made all these things. This hand the Lord will make known to his servants and to those who fear him, and he will threaten his unbelieving ones and his enemies, so as to promise rewards to the one, and to threaten punishments to the other. In this the propriety of the words is to be considered: that he did not say, He will inflict upon his enemies, but he shall threaten, so that, deterred by the threat, they too may pass over to the service of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.15":["For behold, the Lord will come in fire: and his chariots like a whirlwind, to render his fury in indignation, and his rebuke in a flame of fire. For in fire the Lord is judged, and with his sword unto all flesh: and those slain by the Lord shall be multiplied. LXX: Behold, the Lord will come as fire, and his chariots as a tempest, to render his vengeance in fury: and his rebuke in a flame of fire. For in the fire of the Lord all the earth shall be judged: and by his sword all flesh shall fall: many shall be wounded by the Lord. The hand of the Lord, which will be made known to his servants and to those who fear him, itself threatens the unbelieving, or his enemies, that if they do not repent they shall endure what follows: For behold, he says, the Lord will come in fire, and his chariots like a whirlwind or tempest. These we ought to understand as the Angelic powers, when the Lord is to come in the glory of the Father with his Angels, to judge the living and the dead: not that the Lord is fire, but that to those who endure punishments he seems to be fire. And although Moses said (Deut 4:24), and the Apostle confirms the same (Heb 12:29): God is a consuming fire; yet the Savior, expressing the substance of the divinity, says: God is spirit (John 4:24). But between spirit and fire, according to bodily understanding, there is much difference. And if he is fire or spirit, how is he said to have eyes and ears and hands and feet and belly and the other members of the body, since spirit and fire do not have these? God, then, is called a consuming fire so that he may consume whatever vices are in us, hay, wood and stubble, and the thorns, that is, the cares of this world, which the unfruitful earth has borne instead of good seed, of which it is said to the Hebrews: But that which bears thorns and thistles is reprobate and near to a curse; whose end is burning (Heb 6:8). Of this fire the Lord too was speaking in the Gospel: I have come to cast fire upon the earth (Luke 12:49). And we read above: Iniquity shall burn like fire, and like dry grass it shall be consumed by fire (Isa 9:18), and all the tares which the enemy sowed over, while the householder slept (Matt 13). Of this it is also written in the Epistle of James: How small a fire kindles how great a matter (Jas 3:5)! And in Proverbs: With much wood the fire thrives (Prov 26:20). Although, in that it is a proverb, something else may be understood (Sir 28). This fire, I think, sat upon the tongues of the Apostles and of all believers, when they spoke in various tongues, and it put to flight all the darkness of error, and illuminated the hearts of those who received the word of the Lord (Acts 2). And what follows: His chariots like a tempest, or his four-horse chariots, we shall explain by examples from the Psalms, in which it is written: God will come openly, our God, and will not keep silent. Fire shall burn before him: and round about him a mighty tempest (Ps 49 [50]:3, 4). And elsewhere: The chariot of God is ten thousandfold, thousands of those who rejoice (Ps 67 [68]:18). With these chariots and horses Elisha was surrounded, which his servant could not see. Hence he says: O Lord, open the eyes of this servant, that he may see. And when his eyes were opened, it says, he saw chariots and four-horse chariots and horses round about (2 Kgs 6:17). In this fiery chariot Elijah too was snatched up to heaven, when Elisha cried: My father, my father, the chariot of Israel and its charioteer (2 Kgs 2:12). All these things are related, not that God has chariots or four-horse chariots, or is carried by the swiftness of horses, he of whom it is written elsewhere: Who walks upon the wings of the winds (Ps 103 [104]:3); but as he is said to sit on a throne when he assumes the bearing of one who judges, so when he comes to vengeance, to subdue his adversaries, he is shown in the bearing of a victor and one who triumphs. Of the fury of the Lord we have often said that it ought to be taken in the same way as his forgetfulness, and repentance, and sorrow, and the other affections which do not befit the majesty of God.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.16":["And so in this fire and fury, in these chariots and in the flame of fire all flesh shall be judged, or the Lord himself with his creatures, so that he may be seen to pass sentence not by power but by the truth of judgment, according to what is said at greater length in Micah and more briefly in Psalm 50 (51): That you may be justified in your words, and may prevail when you are judged (Ps 50 [51]:5). And the sword by which all flesh is to be judged is to be taken as that of which we have spoken before, and shall now speak in part: Unless you are converted, he will brandish his sword (Ps 7:13). This sword, the punisher of sinful flesh and of all vices, the Israelites complain had been given into the hands of Pharaoh; and they say to Moses and Aaron: May God see you and judge: for you have made our odor abominable before Pharaoh, so that a sword was given into his hand, and he might kill us (Exod 5:21). Of this sword it is also written in Amos: By the sword all the sinners of the people shall perish (Amos 9:10): though there are certainly other kinds of punishments as well. Whatever, then, pricks and strikes and tortures and torments is called a sword in the holy Scriptures; by which sword those shall be wounded, or slain, who have not done the will of God, and, having abandoned the light of truth, have wrapped themselves in the darkness of error. Of whom it is written in Jeremiah: If the Ethiopian will change his skin (Jer 13:23). And in Zephaniah: And you Ethiopians shall be wounded, or slain by my sword (Zeph 2:12). In what sense the whole content of this passage is to be taken, the Apostle explains at greater length, writing to the Romans: But according to your hardness and impenitent heart, you treasure up for yourself wrath in the day of wrath and of the revelation of the righteous judgment of God, who will render to each according to his works. To those indeed who, according to patience in good work, seek glory and honor and incorruption, eternal life. But to those who are of contention and are unbelieving toward the truth, but consent to iniquity, wrath and fury, tribulation and distress upon all men who work evil, the Jew first and the heathen (Rom 2:5ff.).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.17":["Those who were sanctified, and thought themselves clean in the gardens behind one within: who ate swine's flesh, and the abomination, and the mouse, shall be consumed together, says the Lord. LXX: Those who are sanctified and purified in the gardens and on the thresholds: eating pig's flesh, and abominations, and the mouse: they shall be consumed together, says the Lord. Symmachus and Theodotion translated this passage thus: You who are sanctified and cleansed in the gardens one after another, among those who eat swine's flesh, and the abomination, and the mouse, shall fail together, says the Lord, wishing to show that it is not those who are sanctified in the gardens one after another who themselves eat swine's flesh and the abomination and the mouse; but that those who are cleansed associate with those who eat the things that are forbidden. And the divine word rebukes the peoples of the Jews, or rather the Scribes and Pharisees, whom the Lord too rebuked, saying: You are those who justify yourselves before men: but God knows your hearts, for what is exalted among men is an abomination before God (Luke 16:15). Hence he rebuked them and said: Woe to you, Scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, who clean the outside of the cup and the dish, and within you are full of uncleanness (Matt 23:25). And again: You who are whitewashed tombs: but within you are full of the bones of the dead and of all filth (ibid. 27): who blessed with their mouth and cursed in their heart (Ps 61 [62]); who spoke peaceable things to the Lord and made their words softer than oil, but were themselves darts; of whom it was most rightly said: This people honors me with their lips: but their heart is far from me (Isa 29:13). Now God had commanded through Moses (Num 19) that if anyone had been overtaken in sin, he should be sprinkled with the ashes of the red heifer, and by other modes of expiation, through sacrifices and victims, return to the Temple; neglecting this, they built in places of delight and pleasure, that is, in the most pleasant gardens, either baptisteries or pools for watering their garden beds, thinking to wash away adulteries and all the foulness of lusts with plain water; to whom that saying will most rightly be applied: And they purge the night in the river (Horace). Nor let it seem unfitting to anyone if things that are done shamefully are set out rather plainly, for the correction of those who do not blush to commit such things as it is shameful even to speak of, while they cling to harlots in the manner of dogs, and males work shamefulness upon males, receiving in themselves the recompense of their sin. Those who do these works are, in will and in crimes, with those who eat swine's flesh and all the things that are forbidden by the Law, and the mouse, which we call the dormouse, or, according to the provinces of the East, μυωξοὺς. Both those, then, who eat these things, and these who practice every foulness, doing such things as perhaps even the heathen do not do, shall be consumed alike (Lev 11). According to tropology we can say: all who are lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God are sanctified in the gardens and on the thresholds, because they cannot enter the mysteries of truth, and they eat the foods of impiety, since they are not holy in body and spirit; nor do they eat the flesh of Jesus or drink his blood. Of which he himself speaks: He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood has eternal life (John 6:55). For Christ our pasch has been sacrificed (1 Cor 5). He is eaten not outside, but in one house and within.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.18":["The Lord comes, then, to gather the works and thoughts of each. From this we learn that not only works but thoughts too are to be judged on the day of judgment. For he who has looked on a woman to lust after her has already committed adultery with her in his heart (Matt 5). Of these it is said: Now their own thoughts have surrounded them: when the Lord will judge the hidden things of men according to the Gospel of Jesus Christ; so that what the Apostle writes may be proved by the truth of the judgment: their thoughts mutually accusing or excusing them, on the day when God will judge the hidden things of men (Rom 2:15): so that all our thoughts, gathered at one time, may be examined by the righteous judge, while our conscience either accuses us or makes satisfaction for the offense, whether the sins are more or the good works, and whether they are old or new, whether blotted out by repentance or renewed by fresh crimes, when the Lord says: I have given their ways upon their heads: and I will render them their thoughts (Hos 4:9). Who fashioned their hearts one by one, and understands all their works (Ps 32 [33]:15). Of whom we also read in another place: You alone know the hearts of men (2 Chr 6:30). For no one knows the things that are in a man except the spirit of the man that is in him (1 Cor 2). Hence God also speaks through Jeremiah: I am a God at hand, and not from afar, says the Lord: Shall a man hide himself in secret places, and I not know (Jer 23:23)? Finally it is said to the Scribes and to the Pharisees: You are those who justify yourselves before men: but God knows your hearts (Luke 16:15). In this it is to be considered that he did not say Father, according to the blasphemies of the heretics, lest he should seem to exclude the Son, but God, which is common to the Father and the Son. In the beginning indeed was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God: he was in the beginning with God (John 1:1, 2). Of whom the same John writes: But Jesus himself did not trust himself to them, because he knew all men, and had no need that anyone should bear witness to him of man: for he himself knew what was in man (John 2:24, 25). With this too agrees: But Jesus, knowing their thoughts (Luke 6:8). And in another place: But Jesus, knowing their malice (Matt 22:18). And lest there be any doubt that he knows all things, since the heretics raise a calumny about the Word of God, it is said at greater length in the Epistle written to the Hebrews: For the word of God is living, and evident, and sharper than any two-edged sword, and dividing even to the parting of soul and spirit, of joints also and marrow, and a judge of the thoughts and intents of the heart. And there is no creature invisible in his sight. But all things are naked and open to the eyes of him of whom we speak (Heb 4:12). And after all thoughts have been gathered into one, accusing us or defending us, then all the nations and tongues will be brought together into the midst with their thoughts. And according to the apostle Paul we read of tongues not only of men but also of Angels (1 Cor 13). From which it is given us to understand that all creatures are to be judged by the Lord, not only upon the earth, but in the air and in the heavenly places, according to what he himself had said in earlier passages: My sword has been made drunk in heaven, and it shall come down upon the earth (Isa 34:5).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.19":["But I come to gather their works and their thoughts together with all the nations and tongues: and they shall come and see my glory. And I will set a sign among them, and I will send of those who have been saved to the nations in the sea, to Africa, to Lydia, those who draw the arrow, to Italy and Greece, to the islands far off: to those who have not heard of me, and have not seen my glory. LXX: And I know their works and their thoughts, and I come to gather all the nations and all the tongues: and they shall come and see my glory, and I will let down a sign upon them: and I will send forth of those who have been saved to the nations, to Tharsis and Phud, and Lud and Mosoch and Thobel, and to Greece, and to the islands far off, who have not heard my name, nor seen my glory. Before I explain what the enumeration of so many nations signifies, something must be said briefly about the variety of the translations. Tharsis in the Hebrew language is the name for the sea; and also (as they say) a region of India: although Josephus, with a letter changed, thinks that Tarsus, a city of Cilicia, is named instead of Tharsis, to which Jonah, fleeing from Joppa, wished to go (Jonah 1). And Phud, or Phul, is Libya and all Africa as far as the Mauritanian sea, in which there is to this day a river called Phud, and the whole region around it is called Phutensis. And Lud they call the Lydians, whose colonists were the Etruscans, now called Tuscans, once most skilled in shooting arrows, and they are now called those who draw the arrow. For this one reads in the Hebrew MOSCHE CESETH (משכי קשת), which all, apart from the Seventy, likewise translated those who draw the bow: and the Seventy put μοσὸχ, rendering a name instead of a word. If this is accepted, it signifies the Cappadocians, whose metropolis is Mazaca, which afterwards received from Caesar Augustus the name of Caesarea. Wishing I know not what, they also in Genesis (Chapter 10) rendered as μοσὸχ the last son of Aram, that is, of the Syrians of Damascus, who is called Mes: for which we more correctly translate Maeonians. And Thubal, or Thobel, means either Italy or Iberia, that is, Spain, from the river Iberus; whence to this day a region of the Spains is called Celtiberia. Of whom Lucan finely says (Lucan, book 4): The Celts of the Gauls, mingling their name with the Iberians: whom we can call Gallo-Spaniards. And the Greeks, who in the Hebrew speech are called Javan, signify the Ionians: whence too the Greeks are called Iones, and the sea the Ionian. And this is to be considered: that he set down chiefly Eastern nations from Japheth, one of the sons of Noah: although in Tharsis and Phud he also remembered Shem and Ham, that is, the names of India and Africa, so as to show the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And all shall come to see the glory of God: and he will set a sign among them, which is shown at the beginning of Ezekiel under the interpretation of the Hebrew letter THAU (Ezek 9). He who has been stamped with this sign will escape the hand of the smiter. With this the doorposts of the houses in Egypt too were marked, when, as Egypt perished, Israel alone remained unharmed (Exod 12). Of this sign, which Ahaz king of Judah would not accept, the prophet Isaiah testifies: Therefore the Lord will give you a sign: Behold, a virgin shall conceive in the womb and bear (Isa 7:14). Hence the Prophet too entreats for what had been promised: Work with me a sign for good (Ps 85 [86]:17). And in another place: You have given to those who fear you a signal, that they might flee from the face of the bow (Ps 59 [60]:4). This the Lord, ascending to the Father, left us, or set upon our foreheads, so that we might freely say: The light of your countenance has been signed upon us, O Lord (Ps 4:7). But what follows: And I will send of those who have been saved to the nations: and he enumerates them one by one, seems to be contrary to our exposition. For if it is said of the day of judgment, how does he run back again to the first coming of the Savior, when the Apostles are sent to the nations and hear from the Lord: Go, teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit (Matt 28:19)? This is resolved thus: the day of judgment is proclaimed, or rather threatened, so that through fear of the punishment to come they may receive the coming of the Savior and the Gospel of the Apostles, who are to be sent first. And he finely said: And I will send of those who have been saved (Isa 66:18). For unless the Lord had left us a seed, we would have been as Sodom, and we would have been like Gomorrah. And the Apostle: A remnant, he says, has been saved (Rom 9:27). These are sent to the nations, to Tharsis, and Phud, and Lud, and Mosoch, and Thobel, and Greece, and the very many islands that are far off, and that have not heard the name of the Lord nor seen his glory. Of these we have spoken above according to the history (cf. Isa 12). Now the anagogy must be touched upon briefly. Tharsis means the search of joy: so that we may consider not the things that are present, but those that are to come; and may deserve to hear: Because you have been faithful in a few things, you shall enter into the joy of your Lord (Matt 25:21). Of which the apostle Peter too writes: Exult with joy unspeakable and glorious (1 Pet 1:8); so that we may praise God, saying: Our mouth has been filled with joy, and our tongue with exultation (Ps 125 [126]:2). And what follows, Phud, means the shutting out of the mouth: so that, shutting out all blasphemy, we may speak the things that are good and say: My mouth shall announce your righteousness, O Lord: all the day your salvation (Ps 70 [71]:15). And again: I have not hidden your truth in my heart, and I have spoken your salvation (Ps 39 [40]:11). Lud too is rendered in our language profit, and Mosoch stretching out, and Thubal, or Thobel, led to mourning, or turned, or all things: all of which agree with the calling of the nations, that they may pursue profit through the confession of their soul, and, forgetting the things past, stretch out to the things to come. And let them mourn their ancient sins, and be drawn to mourning and to the sorrow that leads to life. For blessed are those who mourn, for they shall laugh (Matt 5). And let all be turned to the Lord, so that they may afterwards pass over to grace, and know what was said by the Apostle: Glory, and honor, and peace to everyone who works good, to the Jew first and to the Greek (Rom 2:10): since he is the God not only of the Jews but also of the nations. Further, what is said: And to the islands afar, which have not heard my name, nor seen my glory, fits those of whom we read above: Those to whom nothing was announced concerning him will see: and those who have not heard will understand (Isa 52:15). And again: I appeared to those who did not ask for me: I was found by those who did not seek me (Isa 65:1). I said: Behold, here I am, to those who did not call upon my name. And we have often said that islands signify either the nations of the whole world or the Churches scattered through the whole world.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.20":["And they shall announce my glory among the nations, and they shall bring all your brothers from all the nations as a gift to the Lord, on horses, and in four-horse chariots, and in litters, and on mules, and in carriages, to my holy mountain Jerusalem, says the Lord. LXX: And they shall announce my glory among the nations: and they shall bring your brothers from all the nations as a gift to the Lord, with horses and wagons, in the lampenae of mules, with canopies, into the holy city Jerusalem, says the Lord. Those who have been saved and sent to the various nations, and to those who had not heard at first nor seen the glory of the Lord, will announce it to all the nations, and will bring the brothers of the Jewish people, whose remnant has been saved, as an offering to the Lord from all the nations: either those very ones who, despising the error of idolatry, have followed the knowledge of the true God, or those of the people of Israel who have believed throughout the whole world. To these the apostle Peter too writes (1 Pet 1). And they will bring them on horses, and in four-horse chariots, and in litters, and on mules, and in carriages. For carriages, which Symmachus alone rendered, whom we have followed in this place, Aquila, the Seventy and Theodotion translated mules. And where the Seventy put with canopies, which we can render sleeping-litters or basternae, the others with one voice translated φορεῖα, which we render as vehicles of various kinds; but where the Seventy and Theodotion said lampenae, for which Symmachus rendered litters, Aquila put σκεπαστὰ, which itself also signifies litters covered with skins. Let this be said about the variety of the translations. Now the horses, and four-horse chariots, and litters, and mules and carriages, and vehicles of different kinds we can understand as Angelic ministries, of which it is said elsewhere to God: Mount upon your horses, and your riding is salvation (Hab 3:8). On these horses, chariots and four-horse chariots Elijah was snatched up to heaven, and Elisha showed his servant, who did not know it, that he was surrounded and kept safe by them (2 Kgs 2). And Zechariah saw in the night: Behold, a man mounting a red horse: and he stood among two shady mountains: and behind him red horses, and starling-colored, and dappled, and white, and I said, he says, What are these, O Lord? And the Angel who spoke in me said to me: I will show you what these are. And the man who stood among the mountains answered and said to me: These are those whom the Lord has sent to walk through the earth (Zech 1:8-10). John too in the Apocalypse testifies that he saw these things: I saw heaven opened, and behold a white horse: and he who sat upon it was called faithful and true, and judging in righteousness, and a warrior. And his eyes were like a flame of fire: and upon his head were many diadems: having a name written which no one knows but himself; and he was clothed in a garment sprinkled with blood: and his name was called the Word of God: and the army in heaven followed him on white horses, clothed in white and most pure linen, and from his mouth went forth a sharp sword, that with it he might strike the nations (Rev 19:11ff.). The Lord and Savior sat upon a red horse when he took a human body; to whom it is said: Why are your garments red? And: Who is this who comes up from Edom, his garments tawny from Bozrah (Isa 63:2). And horses of various colors followed him: red in martyrdom, or starling-colored in flight, or dappled in virtues, or white in virginity. And he sat upon a white horse when after the resurrection he took an immortal and incorrupt body. And whoever followed him used white horses, that is, incorrupt and immortal bodies. It would be long if we wished to explain both testimonies: this only I will say, that the various vehicles by which men are brought to faith are Angels, or holy men who have advanced from men to Angels. And that each of us has Angels many Scriptures teach, among which is this: Do not despise one of these least ones: for their Angels daily see the face of the Father who is in heaven (Matt 18:10). And when the girl Rhoda announced the apostle Peter, others believed it was his Angel (Acts 12). But if this is said of the least, and of one man, how much more is it to be thought of all the saints, and especially of the Apostles, whose Angels daily see the face of the Father, according to what is written? The Angel of the Lord encamps round about those who fear him (Ps 33 [34]:8). And Jacob speaks of himself: The Angel who delivered me. These are they who ascend and descend upon the son of man. Those who are swift in faith are carried on horses; those who are manifold in grace, in four-horse chariots; those who need consolation, in covered litters and under canopies, so that they may deserve to hear: The sun shall not burn you by day, nor the moon by night (Ps 120 [121]:6). And mules we take in two ways in the holy Scriptures: either in respect of sterility and continence, such as David and Solomon sat upon, of whom the one means strong of hand, the other peaceable; or in the bad sense, of which it is said: Do not become like the horse and the mule, which have no understanding (Ps 31 [32]:9); over which Doeg was set (1 Sam 21). And the carriages, for which (as we said above) the Seventy translated covered vehicles, the rest simply vehicles, are to be understood as those of whom the Apostle speaks: Bear one another's burdens (Gal 6:2). Further, the lampenae are to be understood as the shining bodies of the saints, and souls illuminated by faith in the Lord, to whom it can be said: You are the light of the world (Matt 5:14). And all this equipage serves this purpose: that we may enter the holy city of God, or the holy mountain of the Lord, Jerusalem: not that which kills the Prophets and stones those sent to her; but the heavenly Jerusalem, of which we have often said: But the Jerusalem that is above is free, which is our mother (Gal 4:26). And again: But you have come to Mount Zion and the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem (Heb 12:22). This we can understand either of the present Church, which has been gathered throughout the world by the Apostles, or of the future, so that what the Apostle prophesied by the Holy Spirit may be fulfilled: We shall be caught up in the clouds to meet the Lord in the air, and so we shall always be with the Lord (1 Thess 4:16). The Jews, and the heirs of the Jewish error, the Ebionites, who took the name of poor from the lowliness of their understanding, and all who await the delights of the thousand years, understand the horses and four-horse chariots, and wagons, and litters, or basternae, and sleeping-litters, and male and female mules, and carriages, and vehicles of different kinds just as they are written. Namely, that at the consummation of the world, when Christ has come to reign at Jerusalem, and the Temple has been restored, and the Jewish victims have been sacrificed, the sons of Israel are to be brought back from the whole world, by no means on horses, but on mules of Numidia. And those who have been of senatorial rank and have held the place of princes are to come in carriages from the Britons, the Spaniards, the Gauls and the Morini, the farthest of men (Virgil, Aeneid 8, so calls the Morini), and from where the two-horned Rhine is divided, with all the nations, which will have been made ready for their service, coming out to meet them.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","As if the sons of Israel should bring an offering in a clean vessel into the house of the Lord. And I will take of them for priests and Levites, says the Lord. LXX: As if the sons of Israel should offer their victims to me with Psalms in the house of the Lord: and of them I will take priests and Levites, says the Lord. Those who have been sent to the nations, he says, and have announced my glory to them, will bring your brothers from all the nations as an offering to the Lord, with horses, and four-horse chariots, and litters, and mules, and carriages, and various vehicles, to the holy mountain Jerusalem, in the same way as the sons of Israel used, while their religion stood and the ceremonies of the Temple were kept, to offer victims with Psalms in the house of the Lord. Or, as it stands in the Hebrew, and all translated with one voice, in a clean vessel: which the sons of Israel who behold God with the mind offer even to this day in the House of God, which is the Church: spiritual victims, with the fruits and virtues of their soul, in clean vessels, that is, in holy bodies. Of these the Apostle writes: Do you not know that you are the temple of God, and the Holy Spirit dwells in you (1 Cor 3:16)?\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.21":["And I will take, he says, of them priests and Levites, says the Lord: so that those who have been saved may preach to the nations. One of these said: So let a man reckon us as ministers of Christ, and stewards of the mysteries of God (1 Cor 4:1). And Luke the evangelist: As they delivered to us, he says, who from the beginning saw and were ministers of the word. Of these we also read above: You shall be to me priests of the Lord (Isa 61:6). For as he is a Jew in secret who is circumcised in spirit (Rom 2), of whom it is written: We are the Circumcision, who serve God in spirit (Phil 3:3), and we offer spiritual victims pleasing to God, and sing psalms with the spirit and with the understanding: so too they are priests and Levites in secret who follow not the line of descent but the order of faith. Or certainly he does not speak of the Apostles and apostolic men, who were the princes of the Church from the people of the Jews, but of the nations enumerated above: from the sea, from Africa, from Libya, from Cappadocia, from Italy, from Greece, from all the islands, whose inhabitants at first had not heard the Lord nor seen his glory, and afterwards will be turned into priests, so that those who had been the tail may be the head, and those who were the head may be turned into the tail.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.22":["For as the new heavens and the new earth, which I make to stand before me, says the Lord: so shall your seed stand, and your name. And it shall be month from month, and sabbath from sabbath: all flesh shall come to worship before my face, says the Lord. LXX: For as the new heaven and the new earth, which I make to remain in my sight, says the Lord: so shall your seed stand and your name. And it shall be month from month, and sabbath from sabbath: all flesh shall come to worship the Lord in my sight in Jerusalem. When he says: I will take of them for priests and Levites, he shows that the old priesthood has been set aside, which was owed to the tribe of Levi, where there is no choice but the order of nature, and a line of family descending through posterity. For when the priesthood is changed, there must of necessity be a change of the Law as well, and the choice belongs to those on whom the priesthood is conferred by no means according to blood, but according to merits and virtues, who will come from the islands of the nations and will announce the glory of the Lord. And they shall be brought on horses, and in four-horse chariots, and in litters, and on mules, and in carriages. And as there will be a new heaven and a new earth, and a new Jerusalem, so that it is by no means said: Heaven and earth shall pass away (Matt 24:35). And again: The heaven shall be rolled up like a book: and the earth shall be scattered by corruption (Rev 6:14): so, with all things new, the people of God too will become new, as Scripture says: The old things have passed away: behold, all things have become new (Isa 43:19). And in another place: If anyone is a new creature in Christ (2 Cor 5:17). And there will be a new people too; for Christ is the firstfruits, then those who are Christ's, at his coming: and a new heaven and earth, of which it is said among the eight beatitudes: Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven. Blessed are the meek, for they shall possess the earth (Matt 5:3, 4). For every creature is now subject to vanity in hope, because of him who subjected it (Rom 8). But they shall be set free from the servitude of corruption into the glory of the sons of God, who from men will become his sons, and will stand always in his sight, and their name will remain continually, so that no other nation succeeds them, as happened in the case of the Jews; but they will remain forever.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.23":["And it shall be, he says, month from month, and sabbath from sabbath: so that out of carnal sabbaths and months there may come spiritual and delightful sabbaths, which sabbath-rest is reserved for the people of God. And a spiritual month, when the moon is filled from point to point and runs its course, so as to make a month, that is, the kalends, which in Greek are called νεομηνίαι, that is, the beginning of a new month; among whom the beginning of the kalends begins and ends not according to the course of the sun and the different lengths of the months, but according to the circuit of the moon. This is the moon of which it is said in praise of the true Solomon: And he shall remain with the sun and before the moon, for generations of generations (Ps 71 [72]:5). She is called the chosen one in the Song of Songs: Who is this who has come forth like the dawn, beautiful as the moon, and chosen as the sun, wonderful (Song 6:9)? The physicists, and those whose concern it is to discuss the heavenly bodies, say that the moon does not have its own light but is illuminated by the rays of the sun. For it is always filled and shines on that part of its orb by which it is nearer to the sun, and is not darkened by the shadow of the earth: which the poet too showed in one little verse (Virgil, Georgics 1): Nor does the moon rise beholden to her brother's rays.\n\nIf this is true, we too can say tropologically that the Church, which grows and wanes in peace and in persecutions, and, when oppressed by the darkness of temptations, takes up again her former light, has her splendor from the sun of righteousness, and that this is what is said: The moon shall shine like the sun (Isa 30:26); and that her righteous inhabitants shine like the sun in the kingdom of their father. And that through these months the seed of the Lord, which has been restored forever, comes to its solemnities, which the saint sounded forth with mystical mouth, saying: I had the eternal years in mind, and I meditated (Ps 76 [77]:6). Of the sabbath, which signifies rest, we have treated in full discourse (cf. Isa 56), in the prophecy of the eunuchs and strangers. And now it must be said briefly that those come at the new moons and on the sabbaths who, having passed beyond and left behind the six days in which the world was made, hasten to the seventh day, that is, the sabbath, in which there is true rest. About these solemnities the apostle Paul instructed believers, saying: For the law, having a shadow of the good things to come (Heb 10:1). And again: Let no one, then, judge you in food or in drink, or in respect of a festival day, or of a new moon, or of sabbaths, which are shadows of things to come (Col 2:16). But if these things went before in the shadow of good things to come, and in an image, the whole law is to be understood spiritually, of which the same Apostle said: We know that the law is spiritual (Rom 7:14), whose mysteries David too longed to know: Unveil my eyes, and I will consider the wonders of your law (Ps 118 [119]:18). For the things that are seen are temporal, but the things that are not seen are eternal (2 Cor 4). To run through all of these in a quick discussion does not belong to this time. For if eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor have they entered into the heart of man, the things that God has prepared for those who love him (1 Cor 2), how do we attempt to show the whole Law, like a description of the whole world, on a small tablet? And as for what follows: All flesh shall come to worship in my sight in Jerusalem, it should be known that in the Hebrew Jerusalem is not written, so that we may strike down the haughtiness of the Jews; but only in my sight, so that the word of the Lord may be fulfilled, who says in the Gospel: Amen, amen I say to you, the hour will come when neither on this mountain nor in Jerusalem will you worship the Father (John 4:21). And again: God is spirit. And those who worship him must worship in spirit and truth (ibid. 24). And that saying of the Apostle: Lifting up holy hands in every place (1 Tim 2:8). And all flesh signifies not the people of the Jews but the whole race of men, according to what was said above: All flesh shall see your salvation (Isa 40:5). And in Joel: I will pour out of my spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy (Joel 2:28). And in Zechariah: Let all flesh fear before the face of the Lord (Zech 2:13). And in the Psalms: To you all flesh shall come (Ps 64 [65]:2). Which is said in other words in another psalm: All the nations which you have made shall come and worship in your sight, and shall glorify your name, O Lord (Ps 85 [86]:9). Month from month, and sabbath from sabbath, in which all flesh shall come to worship God, the χιλιασταὶ, whom we can call Millenarians, interpret ridiculously: that those who are near shall come to Jerusalem every sabbath; those farther off, by months, that is, when the cycle of the kalends is completed; those very far off, every year, that is, on the days of the Pasch or of Tabernacles, according to what is said in Zechariah: Going up from year to year, to worship the Lord, the king of hosts, and to celebrate the feast of Tabernacles (Zech 14:16). And to offer a greater laugh to their hearers, what is written in the last part of the same prophet's volume: And there shall be no more a merchant in the house of the Lord of hosts in that day, they interpret thus: that traders will cease to exist in the thousand years, because all things will grow in all places, so that, namely, neither shall we need malabathrum leaf, amomum and pepper, nor shall the Indians want pennyroyal from us.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"ISA.66.24":["And they shall go forth, and see the corpses of the men who have transgressed against me. Their worm shall not die, and their fire shall not be quenched. And they shall be a sight to the full for all flesh. LXX likewise. All flesh that is to worship in the sight of the Lord, whether in the heavenly Jerusalem or in every place in which clean hands are lifted up, will go forth to see the corpses of the dead men who have transgressed against God. This can be understood either of the Jews, of whom it was said: I have begotten and exalted sons: but they have despised me; or of all who, having the knowledge of God in their hearts, have turned aside to vanity, so as to worship the creature rather than the Creator. And they will go forth not in place, but in understanding. For the corpses of the dead cannot be within with the Lord. But if all flesh is to worship the Lord, and, on the contrary, the corpses of the men who have transgressed against the Lord are to be handed over to eternal burnings, on both sides there will be a resurrection of real flesh. And the worm that shall not die, and the fire that shall not be quenched, are taken by most as the conscience of sins, which tortures those placed in punishments: because by their own vice and sin they have lost the good of the elect, according to what is said: I have been turned in misery, while the thorn is fastened in me (Ps 31 [32]:4). And in Proverbs: An understanding heart is a moth of the bones (Prov 25:20). And again, under the obelus: As a moth to a garment, and a worm to wood: so sorrow tortures the heart of a man (ibid.). Provided only that they do not deny the eternal punishments of transgressors and of those who deny the Lord, since the Lord says in the Gospel: Go into the eternal fire, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels (Matt 25:41). And in another place: Bind his hands and feet, and cast him into the outer darkness: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth (Matt 22:13). If we hear of hands and feet, and of outer darkness, which is the punishment of eyes that do not see the light of God; and of weeping, which itself too certainly belongs to the eyes; and of gnashing of teeth: I am amazed that some bring in after the resurrection an airy body, to be dissolved little by little into thin air, because the Lord, by the power of his majesty, came in to the Apostles when the doors were shut (John 20). He who certainly, even before the resurrection, walked upon the sea with a hovering step, and granted this very thing to the apostle Peter, so that he who walked by faith afterwards sank through unbelief; to whom it was said: Why did you doubt, you of little faith (Matt 14:31)? The fire too is to be taken in the same way as the worm: it burns as long as it has matter on which the devouring flame may feed. If anyone, then, has in his conscience the tares which the enemy sowed over while the householder slept, these too the fire will burn up, these the burning will devour (Matt 13). And before the eyes of all the Saints their punishments will be shown, who instead of gold and silver and precious stone have built upon the foundation of the Lord hay, wood, stubble, the fodder of everlasting fire. Further, those who want punishments to end at some time, and torments, though after long ages, still to have a limit, use these testimonies: When the fullness of the nations has come in, then all Israel shall be saved (Rom 11:25). And again: God has shut up all things under sin, that he may have mercy on all (Gal 3:22). And in another place the saint says: I will bear the anger of the Lord, because I have sinned against him; until he justifies my cause, and takes away my judgment, and brings me out into the light (Mic 7:9). And again: I will bless you, O Lord, because you were angry with me. You turned your face away from me, and you have had mercy on me (Isa 12:1). The Lord too speaks to the sinner: When the anger of my fury has passed, I will heal again (Ps 30 [31]:20). And this is what is said in another place: How great is the multitude of your goodness, O Lord, which you have hidden for those who fear you. All these they unfold, wishing to assert that after torments and tortures there will be refreshments to come, which must now be hidden from those for whom fear is useful: so that, while they dread punishments, they may cease to sin. This we ought to leave to the knowledge of God alone, whose not only mercies but torments too are in the balance, and who knows whom, how, and for how long he ought to judge. And let us say only what befits human frailty: O Lord, do not rebuke me in your fury, nor chasten me in your wrath (Ps 6:1; 37 [38]:1). And as we believe that the torments of the devil, and of all deniers and impious men who have said in their heart: There is no God (Ps 13 [14]:1), are eternal: so we hold that the sentence of the judge upon sinners and impious men who are nonetheless Christians, whose works are to be tried and purged in the fire, will be moderate and mixed with clemency (1 Cor 3:13).\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JER.1.1-3":["The words of Jeremiah, the son of Hilkiah, of the priests who were in Anathoth, in the land of Benjamin. Which word of the Lord came to him in the days of Josiah, the son of Amon, king of Judah, in the thirteenth year of his reign. And it came in the days of Jehoiakim, the son of Josiah, king of Judah, until the end of the eleventh year of Zedekiah, the son of Josiah, king of Judah, until the exile of Jerusalem in the fifth month. The other prophets, such as Isaiah, Hosea, Joel, were before the captivity of the ten tribes of Israel or of the two tribes of Judah and Benjamin; others after the captivity, such as Daniel, Haggai and Zechariah; but Jeremiah and Ezekiel wove their prophecy when the captivity was imminent, but one of them in the land of Judah, the other in Babylon. Jeremiah, while he was still a boy, began to prophesy in the thirteenth year of Josiah, the son of Amon, king of Judah, and he prophesied under his rule for nineteen years, and afterward under Jehoiakim his son for eleven years, and under Zedekiah, who was the last of the kings of Judah, for eleven years, until the fifth month, when Jerusalem was taken by the Babylonians. But the three months each of Jehoahaz and of Jeconiah — of whom the one was led into Egypt, the other with his mother into Babylon — are counted within the years mentioned above. And thus from the beginning of his prophecy until the captivity of Jerusalem, in which he himself was also taken, he prophesied for forty-one years, besides that time when he was led into Egypt. And there he prophesied in Tahpanhes, as is contained in this very volume. For 'the words of Jeremiah' the Seventy put: the word of God which came to Jeremiah, in this sense, namely, that 'the words of Jeremiah' are the word of the Lord. And he was of the family of the priests who dwelt to the north of Jerusalem at the third milestone, in the little village of Anathoth. And at the same time, wonderful is the clemency of the Lord, that, with the captivity already near and the Babylonian army besieging Jerusalem, he nonetheless calls the people to repentance, preferring to save them converted rather than to destroy them as transgressors. For 'exile', which all the others translated with one accord, the Seventy put 'captivity'. And after the beginning of the prophesying of Jeremiah, in the thirty-fifth year of his prophecy, Ezekiel began to prophesy in Babylon to those who had been taken captive with him.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.1":["The Prophet Jeremiah, for whom this prologue is written, was seen among the Hebrews to be certainly more rustic in style than Isaiah and Hosea and certain other prophets, but equal in meanings, which the same Spirit obviously prophesied. Furthermore, his simplicity of speech happened from the place in which he was born. For he was from Anathoth, which is up to today a village three miles distant from Jerusalem, a priest from priests and sanctified in his mother's womb, dedicating with her virginity a man of the Gospel to the Church of Christ. This boy began to prophesy the captivity of the city and Judea both not only by the Spirit, but also with eyes of flesh. The Assyrians had already transferred the ten tribes of Israel, and now colonies of gentiles had taken possession of their lands. For this reason he prophesied only in Judah and Benjamin, and he lamented the ruins of his city in a fourfold alphabet, which we have presented in the measure of the meter and in verses. Besides this, the order of visions, which is entirely confused among the Greeks and Latins, we have corrected to the original truth. And the Book of Baruch, his scribe, which is neither read nor found among the Hebrews, we have omitted, standing ready, because of these things, for all the curses from the jealous, to whom it is necessary for me to respond through a separate short work. And I suffer because you think this. Otherwise, for the benefit of the wicked, it was more proper to set a limit for their rage by my silence, rather than any new things written to provoke daily the insanity of the envious.\n— Prologue to Jeremiah","After the explanations of the twelve prophets, of Isaiah, of Daniel and of Ezekiel, I at last set my hand to Jeremiah, brother Eusebius, dedicating to you my short commentaries on him, so that you may join the man of the Gospel to Matthew the evangelist, whom I expounded many years ago, at your urging, in a compressed sense. And because the volume is very long and in most places is woven of a plain history, I advise your prudence of this: do not look in this work for a broad explanation, especially on those things which have already been said in the other prophets as well and which are clear to the understanding of themselves. And so I shall try to write by the hand of secretaries in such a way that nothing is lacking in the sense, though much is lacking in the words. I shall prepare for you the warp and the weft and the leashes; you yourself must make the most beautiful garment, so that you may be able not only to listen to us but also to teach others. But the little book of Baruch, which is commonly joined to the edition of the Seventy and is not found among the Hebrews, and the pseudepigraphon [falsely ascribed] letter of Jeremiah, I judged should by no means be expounded; rather, I judged that I should set in order the arrangement of Jeremiah, confused by the error of copyists, and should fill up from the Hebrew sources the many things that are lacking, so that you may hold a prophet new out of the old, and true in place of the corrupt and falsified, making light of the rage of the detractors, who cavil not only at the words but even at the syllables of our words, thinking that they know something in this, if they disparage the works of others. Just as lately an unlearned slanderer burst out, who thinks my commentaries on the letter of Paul to the Ephesians deserve censure, and, snoring in his excessive madness, does not understand the laws of commentaries, in which many opinions of various men are set down, with the names of the authors either suppressed or expressed, so that it is the reader's judgment to decide which he ought chiefly to choose; although in the first book of that same work I said in the preface that I would speak things either my own or another's, and that the commentaries themselves belong as much to the ancient writers as to us. Not seeing this, his forerunner Grunnius long ago strove to carp at it, and I answered him in two books. What this man brings forth as if his own has also already been cleared away when another was slandering, to pass over the volumes against Jovinian, in which he grieves that virginity has been preferred to marriage, and marriage to a second marriage, and a second marriage to many marriages. Nor does the utter dolt, weighed down with the porridge of the Scots, remember that we said in that very work: I do not condemn the twice married, nay, not even the thrice married, and, if it can be so, the eight times married; I will add something more: I receive even a fornicator who repents; whatever is equally lawful must be weighed in an equal scale. Let him read the apology for that same work, which many years ago Rome received with joy against his master, and then he will perceive that he blasphemes with another's voice, and that he is so unskilled that he does not even have curses of his own, but uses against us the rage even of enemies long since buried. But now the work proposed must be undertaken.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.5":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Before I formed you in the womb, I knew you; and before you came forth from the womb, I sanctified you, I gave you as a prophet to the nations. Not that Jeremiah existed before his conception, as heresy surmises, but that the Lord foreknew that he would be, the Lord to whom things not yet made are already made, according to that which the Apostle says: who called the things which were not as if they were. And that he is sanctified in the womb we must take according to that saying of the Apostle: but when it pleased him, who set me apart from my mother's womb and called me through his grace, to reveal his Son in me, that I might preach him among the nations. John the Baptist too is sanctified in the womb, and receives the Holy Spirit, and is moved in the womb, and speaks through his mother's mouth. And as for his saying: I gave you as a prophet to the nations, he wishes this to be understood, which we shall read later in the prophet himself, that he prophesied not only to Jerusalem but also to many nations round about. Some understand this place of the Savior, who was properly the prophet of the nations and through the apostles called all the nations. For he truly, before he was formed in the virginal womb and before he came forth from his mother's womb, was sanctified in the womb and known to the Father, inasmuch as he is always in the Father, and the Father always in him.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.6":["And I said: Ah, ah, ah, Lord God, behold, I do not know how to speak, for I am a boy. LXX: and I said, O Lord God, I do not know how to speak, for I am young. He shrinks from an office which, because of his age, he cannot sustain, with the same modesty with which Moses too says that he is of a thin and slender voice. But Moses is rebuked as one of great and robust age; to this one pardon is granted for his boyhood, which is adorned with modesty and shame.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.7-8":["And the Lord said to me: Do not say: I am a boy, for to all that I shall send you to, you shall go, and all things whatsoever I command you, you shall speak. Do not be afraid of their face, for I am with you to rescue you, says the Lord. 'Do not consider your age,' he says, '— for you have learned from another prophet who says: the gray hairs of a man are his wisdom —, let it only be of your will that you go forward; you will have me as companion, and with my help you will accomplish all things: open your mouth, and I will fill it. And do not consider the multitude of those to whom and against whom you are to speak, but me, who am with you to rescue you, says the Lord'. But the Lord delivers, not in the sense that the prophet is free of persecutions and distresses, since we read that he suffered many things, but so that by enduring he may overcome all things and not yield to distress.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.9":["And the Lord put forth his hand and touched my mouth, and the Lord said to me: Behold, I have given my words in your mouth. It is to be noted that here the hand of God is put forth to touch the mouth of the prophet, and it is said to him: behold, I have given my words in your mouth, whereas in Isaiah it is written: and one of the seraphim was sent to me, and in his hand he had a coal which he had taken with tongs from the altar, and he touched my mouth and said: behold, this has touched your lips, and it will take away your iniquities and cleanse your sins. For there, because he was of firm and mature age, and he himself simply confesses that he has unclean lips and dwells in the midst of a people having unclean lips, one of the seraphim is sent, to touch his mouth not with a hand but with tongs and a coal, and to take away his iniquities and cleanse his sins. But here the hand of God himself is put forth, through which he has wrought all things, and which in another place is called the arm, not to take away sins, which on account of his boyhood he had not committed in great number, but to bestow the grace of speaking. Further, Ezekiel devours a book written within and without, containing both divine mysteries and the simple history. Jeremiah's mouth is touched and the words of the Lord are bestowed, that he may receive confidence in preaching. And beautifully, according to the letter, a hand is put forth, so that, seeing the likeness of human limbs, he may not dread the touch of the hand.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.10":["And in the beginning of Jeremiah too something of the kind is written: Behold, I have put my words into your mouth; behold, I have set you today over nations and kingdoms, to root up and to undermine, and to destroy, and to build, and to plant. A very bad building is destroyed so that afterwards a good building may be put up; a wicked planting is rooted up so that a planting may be set in its place.\n— Commentary on Zephaniah","Behold, I have set you today over the nations and over the kingdoms, to pluck up and to destroy and to ruin and to scatter and to build and to plant. This, which we have added from the Hebrew, 'scatter' or 'put down', is not found in the LXX. And it must be considered that two joyful things follow four sad ones. For good things could not be built unless evil things had been destroyed, nor the best things planted unless the worst were rooted out. For every planting which the heavenly Father has not planted shall be rooted out, and the building which does not have its foundation upon the rock but has been raised upon the sand is undermined and destroyed by the word of God. And that which Jesus will consume with the spirit of his mouth and will destroy by the coming of his presence, namely every sacrilegious and perverse doctrine, he will ruin forever. Further, the things which are lifted up against the knowledge of God and trust in their own wisdom, which is foolishness with God, he will scatter and put down, so that lowly things may be built in their place, and in the place of the former things, which have been destroyed and plucked up, the things which accord with the truth of the Church may be raised and planted, and that may be fulfilled which the Apostle says: you are God's building, God's husbandry. Many understand this place of the person of Christ. For 'Jeremiah' means 'the exalted one of the Lord', who destroyed the kingdoms of the devil, which the devil had shown him on the height of the mountain, and who brought to ruin the adverse powers, blotting out on the cross the handwriting of errors. Of these the psalm too, after the truth of the history, speaks tropikōs [figuratively]: why have the nations raged and the peoples meditated vain things? The kings of the earth stood up, and the princes gathered together into one. In place of these, plucked up, destroyed, ruined and dragged down to lower things, the Church of God is built and planted. But concerning the person of Jeremiah there is no doubt; for we read in what follows that he takes in his hand a cup full of unmixed wine and is commanded to give drink to all the nations round about.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.11-12":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: What do you see, Jeremiah? And I said: I see a watching rod, and the Lord said to me: You have seen well, for I will watch over my word, to perform it. For 'watching rod' the LXX translated 'staff of nut-wood'. We must therefore take pains that the Latin reader may briefly understand the Hebrew etymologian [etymology]. 'Saced' is said for 'nut', while 'watch' or 'watchful' or 'to watch' is called 'soced'. Hence also in later passages the watching leopard is set down under this name. So from the word for 'nut', because of the likeness of the word, the name has played upon the sense of 'watchful'. This indeed is written also in Daniel according to Theodotion, where from the trees schinu and prinu, namely the holm oak and the mastic, cutting asunder and sawing asunder is decreed for the adulterous elders. Besides, at the beginning of Genesis too, from 'man', which is called 'is', woman is called 'issa', as it were a kind of virago, because she was taken from man. For 'staff of nut-wood' Aquila and Symmachus translated 'watching rod', but Theodotion 'of almond'. And the rod watches, considering all the sins of the people, so as to strike and rebuke the transgressors. Hence the Apostle too writes to those who sin: what do you wish? Shall I come to you with a rod, or in love and the spirit of gentleness? This is the rod or staff of which David too speaks: your rod and your staff, they have comforted me. And beautifully he put 'have comforted'; for the Lord rebukes to this end, that he may amend. And just as the nut or the almond has a very bitter rind and is enclosed in a very hard shell, so that, when the harsher and hard parts are stripped away, a very sweet fruit is found, so every rebuke and the labor of continence seems bitter indeed for the present, but brings forth very sweet fruits. Hence there is also that old saying: 'the roots of letters are bitter, the fruits sweet'. Some understand the 'watching rod' and 'of nut-wood' as the Lord, of whom Isaiah says: a rod shall come forth from the root of Jesse. Hence too the rod of Aaron, which was thought dead, is related to have blossomed in the resurrection of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.13":["And the word of the Lord came to me a second time, saying: What do you see? And I said: I see a pot set on fire, and its face is from the face of the north. And the Lord said to me: From the north evils shall be opened — or shall blaze up — upon all the inhabitants of the earth. By certain degrees torments are assigned to sinners, so that they may come little by little to salvation: those who will not be amended when the rod strikes are sent into the bronze pot set on fire, of which Ezekiel writes more fully, which is kindled from the face of the north, signifying the Babylonian king and the city of Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.14":["And beautifully it is added: from the north evils shall blaze up upon all the inhabitants of the earth — either of the land of Judea or surely of the whole earth —, of whom it is written in Revelation too: woe upon all the inhabitants of the earth. For the saints are not inhabitants of the earth but strangers and sojourners, of whom one says: I am a stranger upon the earth and a sojourner, as all my fathers were, and another: few and very evil are my days, in which I sojourn on the earth. Hence Peter too writes his catholic epistle to the strangers and sojourners of Pontus, Galatia and Cappadocia. And according to the mystical senses Solomon says: the north wind is a harsh wind, but by name it is called right-handed, that is, by those who have grown stiff with its cold and have lost the warmth of faith.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.15":["For behold, I will call together all the families of the kingdoms of the north, says the Lord, and they shall come, and each one shall set his throne at the entrance of the gates of Jerusalem, and upon all its walls round about, and upon all the cities of Judah. And I will speak my judgments with them concerning all the wickedness of those who have forsaken me and have poured libations to foreign gods and have worshiped the work of their own hands. No one doubts that many nations, and the kings of the several nations, were subject to the Babylonian king; who, when Jerusalem was besieged, set round about, and especially at the outlets of the gates, their throne and tents, so that none of those who were shut in could break out; and that not only Jerusalem but all the cities of Judea were hemmed in by a like siege.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.16":["'And when', he says, 'the city has been taken, then I will speak to them, that my judgments were right and that each one received what he deserved, not for the other vices to which the human condition is subject, but above all for idolatry, through which, forsaking me, they worshiped the works of their own hands'. Some take this place in a good sense, namely that those who have been boiled in the bronze pot through torments and purged will afterward be princes of Jerusalem, and that, after the Lord has had mercy on them, he will then reproach them because, having forsaken him, they venerated images. But this is a forced and perverse interpretation; let not an unlearned expounder raise a false charge.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.17":["You therefore gird up your loins, and arise, and speak to them all things which I command you. Job too is commanded to gird up his loins, and the apostles, with loins girded — which Elijah and John the Baptist mortified with girdles of skin —, to hold lamps in their hands, namely those of the preaching of the Gospel. Whoever therefore is to speak the words of God ought to gird up his loins, knowing that all the strength of the devil is in the loins, and that the righteous man says in the psalms: my loins are filled with illusions. And when he has girded his loins, let him hear that which is written: arise, you who sleep, and be lifted up, and Christ will enlighten you, so that, always watching and rising up from sleep, he may speak the things which God has commanded him.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Do not be afraid of their face; for I will not make you fear their countenance, or, as the Seventy and the other translators have: lest perhaps I make you fear. And the sense according to our translation is: 'do not be afraid of their face; for with me as your helper you will not be able to fear them'; according to the Seventy: 'do not be afraid of their face, have confidence in my command; for if you do not offer what you have, so as to cease to fear, I will forsake you and hand you over to fear, and I shall in a manner seem to make you fear, while I leave you to dread'. And this signifies that truth is always bitter, and that the multitude of men is not to be feared, who, when rebuked, do not bear correction but lay snares for him by whom they are corrected. And what follows according to the Seventy: for I am with you to deliver you, says the Lord, is not found in the Hebrew. And the sense is: 'I will rescue you, not in this, that no one lies in wait for you, but in this, that by enduring snares you do not transgress'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.1.18-19":["For I have given you today — or behold, I have set you on this day — as a fortified city, as an iron pillar and as a bronze wall over all the land: to the kings of Judah, to its princes and to the priests and to the people of the land. And they shall fight against you and shall not prevail, for I am with you, says the Lord, to deliver you. The divine word describes why the prophet ought not to fear. I, he says, have set or have given you today — that is, in the present life, while it is called today — as a very strong city, not as a single house, nor a tower or some walls, but as a whole city, which, set upon a mountain, cannot be hid, of which it is written: glorious things are spoken of you, city of God, and: I am a strong city, a city which is besieged. And as a pillar, he says, of iron, of which the Apostle writes: the pillar and ground of the truth. Hence Peter and John, who were reputed pillars of the Church, gave to Paul and Barnabas the right hands of fellowship. Nor is this enough, but as a wall, he says, of bronze, which is marred by no rust, nor perishes when beaten by rains, but becomes stronger with age. And you shall be such against kings and princes and people, not of any place whatever, but of the land, who savor earthly things and do not know heavenly things, who bear the image of the earthy and not of the heavenly; these, he says, shall fight against you and shall not prevail. Why, I ask? What is the cause of such great strength, that neither kings nor princes nor priests nor peoples prevail against one man? It follows: for I am with you, says the Lord, to deliver you. If ever the kings of Judah, which means 'confession', and its princes and priests and peoples, that is, bishops and presbyters and deacons, and the base and ignoble common crowd, should wish to rise up against a holy man, let him have firmness of faith and cease to fear, for with the Lord's help he will overcome.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Go and cry in the ears of Jerusalem, saying. This is not found in the Seventy, but was added under asterisks from the edition of Theodotion, who translated the Hebrew word 'carath', for which we said 'cry' or 'preach', as 'read'. For by its ambiguity it signifies reading and crying out and preaching. But the ears of Jerusalem we ought to take as the ears of its inhabitants.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Thus says the Lord: I have remembered you, pitying your youth and the love of your betrothal, when you followed me in the wilderness, in a land that is not sown. LXX: thus says the Lord: I have remembered the mercy of your youth and the love of your perfection. This is said more fully in Ezekiel, when the Lord joins Jerusalem to himself in marriage and, under the person of a wife, unites her to his embraces, or, to show a more ardent affection, calls her a girl and a young maiden and betrothed; for what we have not yet obtained, we desire the more to obtain. 'When', he says, 'you followed me in the solitude, and in the manner of betrothal gifts and a dowry I distributed to you the ornaments of the law and the necklaces of words'. And all this he refers not to her merit but to his own mercy, through which love too followed. This also, which we have set down: in the wilderness, in a land that is not sown, is not found in the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.3":["Israel is holy to the Lord, the firstfruits of his crops; all who devour him shall transgress; evils shall come upon them, says the Lord. When he calls Israel the firstfruits of the Lord's crops, he shows that the people gathered from the nations comes after the firstfruits, according to that which is written in another place: Remember your congregation, which you have possessed from the beginning. But firstfruits are owed to the priests and not to enemies. What follows: all who devour him shall transgress; evils shall come upon them, says the Lord, has this sense: just as those who devour the firstfruits, being in no way of priestly stock, are guilty of a crime, so those who defile Israel will be subjected to evils, according to that which the holy man says in Psalm 26 (27): While the harmful draw near against me to eat my flesh, those who trouble me, my enemies themselves have become weak and have fallen. For they will not be immune from punishment on the ground that they carry out God's sentence, and evils will come upon them; for it must needs be that offences come, but woe to him through whom offences come!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.4-5":["Hear the word of the Lord, house of Jacob and all the families of the house of Israel. Thus says the Lord: What iniquity did your fathers find in me, that they went far from me and walked after vanity and became vain? Another prophet too testifies to this sense: My people, what have I done to you, and how have I been troublesome to you? Answer me; for I brought you out of the land of Egypt and freed you from the house of servants. Now both names — Israel too — are set down, not according to the two and ten tribes, but for the whole people, since Jacob himself too was afterwards called Israel. And he traces the offense from the fathers, not so that the sins of the fathers are imputed to the sons, but so that the sons too, having the likeness of their fathers, are punished both for their own crime and for their parents'. We often read that God has mercy on sons for the sake of holy fathers. Now the fathers of the sinful people forsook God — and not briefly, but for a long time — and instead of God followed vanity, namely idols, which profit their worshipers nothing, and they became like them, according to that which is written: Let those who make them become like them, and all who trust in them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.6":["And they did not say: Where is the Lord, who made us go up from the land of Egypt, who led us across through the wilderness, through a land uninhabitable and pathless, through a land of thirst and the image of death, through a land in which no man walked nor did a human being dwell? For 'human being' the Seventy translated 'son of man', and for 'image of death' 'shadow of death' was added from Theodotion. Since this is plain according to the history, this must be considered according to anagōgēn [the higher, spiritual sense]: that as long as we are in this world and are being led out of Egypt, we go up little by little, and first we cross deserts and a land uninhabitable, which the holy man ought not to inhabit, and pathless, to show the difficulty of the journey; through a land of thirst, where we always desire greater things and are not content with things present, and the image or shadow of death — for we always stand in danger, and everywhere the devil sets his snares —, through a land in which no man walked, who is of the perfect age in Christ — for we shall all rise again to a perfect man, to the measure of the age of the fullness of Christ — ; nor does he who is a man of God, or a son of man, ever dwell in it, but he always hastens on to greater things. From this it is clear that perfection is not on the way, but at the end of the way and in the dwelling that is prepared for the saints in the heavenly places, and to whom it is said: you who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God. In vain, therefore, does the new heresy sprung from the old suppose that perfect victory is here, where there is battle and contest and an uncertain outcome of things to come.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.7":["And I brought you into the land of Carmel, that you might eat its fruit and its good things, and having entered you defiled my land and made my inheritance an abomination. 'For the toil of a very hard journey I gave you an abundance of all things'; for this is what 'Carmelus' signifies, which in Hebrew is called 'Chermel' and in our language sounds as 'knowledge of circumcision'. And as that people polluted and violated with idolatry the holy land, fertile in all things, so we, receiving the knowledge of the true circumcision, eat its fruit and, if negligence creeps in, defile the land of God and make his inheritance abominable.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.8":["The priests did not say: Where is the Lord? And those who held the law did not know me, and the shepherds transgressed against me, and the prophets prophesied by Baal and followed idols. After such great benefits they turned the privileges of their dignity into contempt, so that the priests did not seek the Lord, the teachers of the law, who ought to have taught others, did not know him, and the shepherds became transgressors through negligence, and the prophets, who discourse among the peoples, speak not for God but for an idol, and venerate their own fabrications. These words must be used against the teachers of our own order, who devour the people of God like bread for food and by their evil works do not call upon the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.9":["Therefore I will still contend with you in judgment, says the Lord, and with your sons I will dispute. Lest he seem to strike through power, he contends by reason as with equals, according to that which David sings and the Apostle adopts: that you may be justified in your words and may prevail when you are judged. And in setting down 'still', he testifies that he has often done so; and in adding 'with your sons', he shows a like obstinacy also in the sons of the wicked. And covertly he signifies that their sons too, at the coming of the Lord, followed the ancient denial of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.10-11":["Pass over to the islands of Kittim and see, and send to Kedar and consider earnestly, and see whether such a thing has been done: if a nation has changed gods — or its gods — ; and surely they are not gods. But my people has changed its glory into an idol or, for that from which nothing will profit it. He makes a comparison of a thing that is incomparable, and sets the true God beside lying ones. 'Go', he says, 'to the islands of Kittim', which we ought to take as those either of Italy or of the western regions, from the fact that the island of Cyprus, in which a city is called by this name, is near the land of Judea; from it too came Zeno, the chief of the Stoics. Kedar, however, is a region of the wilderness and of the Ishmaelites, whom they now call Saracens, against which a prophecy of this very prophet is woven in his final parts, and which David mentions, saying: I have dwelt with the inhabitants of Kedar; my soul has long been a sojourner. And the sense is: 'Either go to the west or send into the wilderness, and see whether any nation has done what you have done. For none of them has spurned its gods, nor, by comparison with golden ones, exchanged its wooden and stone ones; but following its ancient error it has kept what it had received from its ancestors; and this surely, although none of them is a god, but they are images fashioned by the hand of men. But my people has changed the truth for a lie and preferred to me an idol, which will not be able to profit them in the time of need'. We can say this also against those who follow vices with greater zeal than virtues, whom the Apostle admonishes, saying: I speak in human terms because of the weakness of your flesh. As you yielded your members to serve uncleanness and iniquity unto iniquity, so now yield your members to serve righteousness unto sanctification.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.12-13":["Be astonished, O heavens, at this, and, its gates, be utterly desolate, says the Lord; for my people has done two evils: they have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and have dug for themselves broken cisterns, which cannot hold waters. LXX: the heaven was astonished at this and shuddered beyond measure and exceedingly, the rest likewise. The heaven, to which it was said: Give heed, O heaven, and I will speak and: Hear, O heaven, and give ear, O earth, seeing the precepts of God trampled underfoot, shudders and cannot hide its astonishment. For every creature groans together and grieves together over the sins of men. Now the people of God has done two contrary things: first, that they forsook God, who is the fountain of life and gave the precept, saying: I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt; second, what is written in the same place: You shall have no gods in my sight; instead of which it followed demons, whom he calls broken cisterns because they cannot keep the commandments of God. And this must be noted, that the fountain is perpetual and has life-giving waters, but cisterns and pits are filled either from torrents or from the turbid waters of the earth and from rains. And he calls those the gates of the heavens of which it is also written in Psalm 23 (24): Lift up, O gates, your heads, and the king of glory shall enter; for which the Seventy translated: Lift up your gates, O princes; of these more will be said fully in its proper place. And let it trouble no one that Aquila and Symmachus translated 'heavens', but the Seventy and Theodotion 'heaven'. For the Hebrew 'samaim' is of common number, and both 'heavens' and 'heaven' are called by the same name, like Thebes, Athens, Salonae.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.13":["Between a well and a pit, that is, a cistern, there is this difference: a well has perpetual waters, flowing from a living spring; a cistern, which keeps water cool, holds waters that are external and brought in from outside. Hence God speaks through the prophet Jeremiah: They have forsaken me, the fountain of living water, and dug for themselves cisterns which cannot hold water.\n— Commentary on Hosea"],"JER.2.14-15":["Why then has he become a prey? Lions have roared over him and given their voice; they have made his land a wilderness, and his cities have been burned, and there is none who dwells in them. The divine word asks so that it may itself reply. And by lions he means the princes of Babylon, who made his land a wilderness and destroyed his cities by fire. Or at any rate, according to anagōgēn, let us understand the lions as the adverse powers or the leaders of the heretics, who, desolating the land of the Church, have laid waste all its cities with heretical fire, and with that fire of which it is written: All committing adultery, their hearts are like an oven. For they truly give their voice, and in this same prophet, under the person of the partridge, they cry out and gather what they did not bring forth, and they make riches not with judgment. But for this reason its cities have been laid waste and destroyed, because they do not have God as their inhabitant, as Scripture says: and there is none who inhabits them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.14":["Is Israel a slave, or one born in the house? I think it was from this passage that the Jews, puffed up with pride, said to the Savior: We are the seed of Abraham and have never been slaves to anyone; how do you say: You shall be free?, not knowing that everyone who commits sin is the slave of sin, and that each one serves him by whom he is overcome. Born, then, of Abraham, the friend of God, by their own vice they became as it were sons of Ham, to whom it was said: Cursed be Canaan; he shall be a slave to his brothers.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.16-17":["The sons also of Memphis and Tahpanhes have debauched you even to the crown of the head. Has not this been done to you because you forsook the Lord your God at the time when he was leading you along the way? This, which we said: at the time when he was leading you along the way, is not found in the Seventy. Now he names two very great cities of Egypt, Memphis and Tahpanhes, and says that their sons debauched Israel even to the crown of the head, in that sense in which Isaiah set it down: From the sole of the foot even to the crown of the head there is no soundness in him. And so great was the lust of the Egyptians, who are great of flesh, that they spared no member but debauched everything. According to the letter, refer it to the idols of the Egyptians; according to the spiritual understanding, to the teachers of perverse doctrine, who pollute the purity of the Church with their own filthiness. And these things will befall him for this reason, because he forsook the Lord his God, and that above all at the time when he ought to have followed him as his guide.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.18":["Woe to you, devil, or Antichrist, or perverse doctrine of the heretics, who with your teachings and your turbid potion make drunk the peoples of the deceived, and overturn their former faith, giving them a potion not from Siloam, not from the Jordan, not from the springs of Israel, but from the brook Kidron, and from the river of Egypt, of which Jeremiah says: What have you to do with the way of Egypt, that you should drink the water of Gihon? For this in the Hebrew is written sior, that is, turbid and muddy; for although the rivers of Egypt are believed to go out from the paradise of the Scriptures, yet because they are trampled by the feet of Pharaoh, they have lost their splendor, and, defiled by the Egyptian mud, have been turned into torrents, at having escaped which the holy one rejoices, saying: Our soul has passed through the torrent.\n— Commentary on Habakkuk","And now what do you want on the way of Egypt, to drink the water of Sior? And what have you to do with the way of the Assyrians, to drink the water of the river? For 'Sior' we translated 'turbid', which is what the Hebrew word signifies; for it the common edition has 'Geon'. Since, then, he had set down above the sons of Memphis and Tahpanhes, who debauched Israel even to the crown of the head, he now names Egypt itself more plainly. And no one doubts that the Nile has turbid waters, and that by the river of the Assyrians he signifies the Euphrates, as Scripture says that the land of promise extends from the torrent of Egypt even to the great river Euphrates. But those who have forsaken Christ, the fountain of life, and have dug for themselves the pits of the heretics, which cannot hold the waters of doctrines, must needs be subjected to the lions, who reduce their land to a wilderness and destroy all the churches, and be polluted even to the crown of the head, and drink turbid waters and the streams of the Assyrian river and of the north, whence evils blaze up upon the earth.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.19":["Your malice shall reprove you — or your transgression shall instruct you — and your turning away shall rebuke you. Know and see that it is an evil and bitter thing that you have forsaken the Lord your God and that the fear of me is not with you, says the Lord God of hosts. It must be noted that malice too, or transgression, after it has glutted the transgressor and, like the quails, has come to the point of nausea, instructs the one who does repentance; and he is commanded to see what he has forsaken, what he has followed, and how, spurning good and sweet things, he has chosen evil and bitter ones. And all this happened because he forsook the Lord his God, and the fear of him is not with him. For the beginning of wisdom is the fear of the Lord; and because he did not have it, he is handed over to evil and bitterness.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.20":["From of old you have broken my yoke — or your —, you have burst my bonds — or your — and said: I will not serve. For on every lofty hill and under every leafy tree you lay prostrate as a harlot or there you poured yourself out in fornication. He speaks to Israel as to a harlot, because she broke the marriage covenant and said: I will not serve — 'the Lord' or 'the husband' is understood —, but on every lofty hill and under every leafy tree lay prostrate to idolatry; for pleasant and lofty places are always dedicated to idols. This can also be said to him who, a Christian from the beginning and in part instructed in the sacred letters, afterwards, through desire for secular literature, which is signified in the hills, and for the pleasant things of eloquence, which are shown in the leafy trees, prostrates himself to demons, who, under the pretext of learning and lofty knowledge, pollute the souls of believers and make them spread their feet for every passer-by.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.21":["And they gave him wine mixed with gall to drink, and when he had tasted it he would not drink. God speaks to Jerusalem: I planted you a true vine; how have you turned into the bitterness of a foreign vine? A bitter vine makes bitter wine, which it gives the Lord Jesus to drink, so that what is written might be fulfilled: They gave gall for my food, and in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink. But what is said: when he had tasted it he would not drink, indicates that he tasted indeed the bitterness of death for us, but on the third day he rose again.\n— Commentary on Matthew","But I planted you a choice vineyard, wholly a true seed; how have you been turned for me into what is crooked, a strange vineyard? LXX: But I planted you a fruitful vineyard, wholly true; how have you been turned into bitterness, a strange vine? For the 'choice' or 'fruitful' vineyard the Hebrew has 'sorec', which is set down in the song of Isaiah. Now it is a kind of the finest vine, with which slip the Lord says he planted Israel too, and he marvels how the true seed and choice vineyard has been turned into bitterness and has therefore become a strange vineyard. And let no one be secure, if both the planting of the Lord and the true seed and the vineyard sorec is so changed by its own vice that through bitterness it withdraws from the Lord and becomes a strange vine. And in this the clemency of the Creator must be considered, that he who said in the Gospel: I am the true vine, granted also to his disciples and to the people believing in him to be a choice or true vine, if it wills to remain in that in which it has been planted.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.22":["If you wash yourself with nitre and multiply for yourself the herb borith, you are stained with your iniquity before me, says the Lord God. For 'herb borith', which we have rendered as it is set down in the Hebrew, the Seventy translated poan, to signify the fullers' herb, which, after the manner of the province of Palestine, grows in green and moist places and has the same power for washing away dirt as nitre does; but our 'nitre' and 'fullers' herb' is repentance. The preaching of the Church too, which reproves and rebukes and corrects the offender, has the likeness of a more biting nitre. He who is stained with the light dirt of sins is cleansed by lighter admonitions. But grave sins, which drag to death, can be washed away neither by nitre nor by the herb borith, but need heavier torments. For each one's work, of what sort it is, the fire will test, and in fire it will be revealed. And beautifully he added: you are stained with your iniquity in my sight, because, even if you seem clean to men, you are not clean to me, who know the consciences of each one. Hence too in another place it is said: no one living shall be justified in your sight.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.23-24":["A light runner unfolding her ways, a wild ass accustomed to the wilderness, in the desire of her soul has drawn in the wind of her love; none shall turn her away. All who seek her shall not fail; in her monthly courses they shall find her. LXX: toward evening her voice howled, she widened her ways over the waters of the wilderness, in the desire of her soul she was borne by the spirit, she was handed over. Who will turn her back? All who seek her shall not toil; in her humiliation they will find her. In this passage the Seventy's edition disagrees greatly with the Hebrew truth; yet each has its own sense. Because above she said: I am not defiled, and he was speaking as if to a woman who had behaved shamefully, he describes her fornication. 'As', he says, 'a light roe' — which we called 'runner' in the common gender, and which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion translated more expressively dromas kouphē [a light runner, in the feminine] — 'unfolds her ways and is carried swiftly to the pastures, and as a wild ass accustomed to the wilderness draws in, in the desire of her soul, the wind or spirit of her love' — for among the Hebrews both 'wind' and 'spirit' are called by one name, 'ruha' —, 'so too Israel, or Jerusalem, was borne with all her impetus toward the desire of lust and burned with every love of idols, and there is no one who can turn her from this impetus by his warnings, not that the inability of the prophets brought this about, but the perverse malice of her who desires'. Whoever, he says, seek her will not toil greatly; in her monthly courses and in her uncleanness they will find her, for which Aquila translated neomēnian, that is, 'the Kalends', Symmachus 'month', the Seventy and Theodotion 'humiliation'. But according to the Seventy this is the sense: the harlot Jerusalem, like that woman who is described in Proverbs, toward evening howled with her voice and provoked lovers to lust, opened the ways of her shamefulness, and spread her feet for every passer-by. And there was a place having the pleasantness of flowing waters, which becomes more delightful when there is solitude all around, so that no one sees her fornicating. In the desire, he says, of her soul epneumatophoreito [she was borne along by a spirit], or by a perverse spirit she was led, or she drew the refreshment of love, or at any rate she sang the songs of her shamefulness. She was handed over, he says, to her vices and to lust; no one will be able to turn her back. All who wish to find her will find her in the humiliation of her shamefulness, so that she can never be sated with the love of pleasure.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.23":["How do you say: I am not defiled, I have not gone after the Baals? See your ways in the vale or valley, which in Hebrew is called 'ge' and by the Seventy is translated polyandrion, which in our language can be called 'the tomb of a multitude'. 'In vain', he says, 'are you unwilling to confess your crimes, and do you boast of cleanness, you who are polluted with the filth of idolatry, and shamelessly deny that you worshiped the idol of the Baals. Look back at the vale of the sons of Hinnom, which is watered by the springs of Siloam, and there you will see the shrine of Baal, whom you venerated, having forsaken God'. And what is added: know what you have done, opens the closed eyes of her who denies, so that she may see what she blushes to look upon. According to tropology, let us refute by their works the shameless brow of those who will not confess their vices. For men of this kind do not walk in the strait and narrow way that leads to life, but in the broad and spacious one, by which very many enter, which leads to death. Hence too it was significantly named polyandrion, or else according to the history, because there a multitude of the people was slain and perished through the evil of idolatry.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.25":["Restrain your foot from nakedness and your throat from thirst. And you said: I have despaired, by no means will I do it; for I have loved strangers, and after them I will walk. LXX: turn back your foot from the rough way and your throat from thirst. She said: I will act manfully, because she loved strangers and walked after them. Those who are about to keep the Passover are commanded to have shoes on their feet. And the Apostle proclaims shod the feet of those who are prepared by the Gospel, lest, while they walk through the wilderness of this world, they lie open to venomous creatures, which ought to be trampled and crushed by the Gospel foot. We restrain the throat from thirst when we receive the precepts of the Savior, who says: Whoever thirsts, let him come to me and drink. She, out of despair of better things, denied that she would do what the Lord commanded, and set out the reason, saying: I have loved strangers, and I will follow them, thinking by a shameless confession to escape her crimes. But according to the Seventy, the way of sinners is rough, and it is turned by the Lord into a level way. Whoever follows heretics is branded by the verdict of these verses, because he has said: I have despaired — or, in an evil purpose, I will act manfully — and in my error I will be strengthened. And it is necessary that whoever follows a doctrine strange to the Church's should love strangers and follow their footsteps, either demons or the leaders of the heretics, because they are strangers to God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.26-27":["As a thief is confounded when he is caught, so are the house of Israel confounded, they and their kings, princes and priests and their prophets. Although the face of thieves is shameless and bold, yet it blushes when it has been caught in theft. And so Israel too, saying to wood: You are my father, and to stone: You have begotten me, so as to call those his parents whom he himself had fashioned, is confounded when he has been caught in his idolatry. And lest we think that he says this of the common people, he sets down kings and princes and priests and their prophets. Let us use this testimony if ever our princes, and those who are reckoned leaders in the Church, are caught in shameful sins.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.27":["They have turned to me the back and not the face. Those who cast the words of God behind them themselves turn their back against him and not their face. For when a master gives instruction, it is a sign of one who obeys if he listens with his head bowed before him; but if he turns his back, it is a sign of one who despises, as it is written in another place: and they turned to me a withdrawing shoulder. 'So far', says God, 'have they despised my precepts that they would not even hear, but showed the swelling of their mind by the gesture of their body'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","And in the time of their affliction they will say: Arise and deliver us. Those who did not perceive God through benefits, let them perceive him through torments.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.28":["Where are your gods, which you made for yourself? Let them arise and deliver you in the time of your affliction. It is a shameless request, in time of need and distress, to seek help from him whom they despised in peace. And it is to be read with the feeling of one rebuking: 'Let your gods, which you made for yourself, deliver you; so that, since God is the creator of men but man has made a god, necessity may prove what they can do whom you formerly worshiped when you were secure.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","According to the number of your cities were your gods, O Judah. The several cities worshiped either the same gods or single and different ones, so that they did not seem to have agreement even in impiety, but a superstition fighting against itself followed diverse error. And what follows: and according to the number of the streets of Jerusalem they sacrificed to Baal, was added by the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.29":["Why do you wish to contend with me in judgment? You have all forsaken me, says the Lord. Human perversity is prone to excuse itself, so that whatever men suffer deservedly they appear to suffer unjustly, and they refer their own fault to the judgment of God. 'In vain', therefore, he says, 'do you put forward complaints and plead that the judge is unjust, since what you suffer comes from your impiety.' And what follows: and you have all acted unjustly against me, was likewise added by the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.30-31":["Like a ravaging lion is your generation. LXX: like a ravaging lion, and you did not fear. 'The sword', he says, 'which devoured your prophets' — there is no doubt that he means the soothsayers of Baal and of the idols —, 'has laid everything waste like a lion, and yet your generation, which ought to have been amended by the slaying of a few, has persevered entire in crime'. But according to the Seventy this is the sense: 'Thus the sword of the Lord, which has displayed the sword of the adversaries, has devoured and torn your false prophets like a lion, which most greedily tears the prey it has found, and yet not even by the punishment of your prophets could you be turned to better things.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.30":["In vain have I struck your sons; they did not receive discipline, for which the Seventy set down: you did not receive. And the sense in the Hebrew is: 'They themselves, who were struck, were unwilling to receive discipline,' but in the Seventy: 'I struck your sons for this reason, that by their death you might be instructed. And lest perhaps you should say: You were unwilling to correct us when we sinned, learn from the blows dealt your sons that I desired to cure you with a harsher remedy.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Your sword has devoured your prophets. 'Not my prophets but yours, and by no means my sword but yours, has devoured them, the sword which you suffered for your sins'. But the Seventy do not have 'your', but translated simply: the sword has devoured your prophets, 'so that it may be shown to be either the enemy's sword or my sword, by which I have pierced your sins.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.31":["See the word of the Lord: Have I become a wilderness to Israel, or a late-yielding land? Why then has my people said: We have withdrawn, we will come to you no more? LXX: hear the word of the Lord: thus says the Lord: Have I become a wilderness to Israel, or a land full of thorns, because my people said: We will not serve, nor will we come to you? Moses too saw the voice of God, and John the apostle says that he saw and handled the word of God. And he marvels how the people of Israel held God as a wilderness, while it followed idols as though they were the throng of cities. A late-yielding land is one that does not receive the rains of doctrines nor the discipline of the Gospel; and full of thorns, because it had not been cultivated. The people, then, that was once God's is the more wicked in this, that it withdrew from the Lord and is unwilling to return again to its God. It is a great offense to be unwilling to appease him whom you have offended.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.32":["The band covers the breast, and holds that place in women which the Rational holds in the High Priests. Of this breast-band Jeremiah too spoke in mystical speech: If a bride can forget her adornment, or a virgin her breast-band, I too will forget you, says the Lord (Jer 2:32). The virgin, such as the Apostle desires to present us to Christ (2 Cor 11), so long as she is not corrupted in Egypt and her breasts, which are bound with the breast-band, are not broken, is joined to the bridegroom; and though daily she bears sons of the virtues, she by no means ceases to be a virgin. But if at some time she has spread her feet to every passer-by and followed her lovers, and, according to the prophecy of Hosea, the Lord has hedged in her ways and shut off her paths, she will return to her former husband and will hear: Put off the garments of mourning, and put on the garments of your glory (Hos 2).\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Will a virgin forget her ornament, a bride the band of her breast? But my people has forgotten me days without number. Through these words we learn that Christ is the bridegroom of the virgin Church, which has no spot or wrinkle. And if he himself is the bridegroom, the words are his, of whom John the Baptist also speaks: He who has the bride is the bridegroom. He, therefore, loses his ornament who withdraws from the Lord, and he loses the understanding of doctrines, which is signified in the breast. Hence also John the Evangelist reclines upon the breast of the Lord, and for the priests, among other things, the breast of the victims is set apart. But the greater the number of the times in which we forget God, the greater the penalty of the sin, because it could not be tamed even by the length of ages.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.33-34":["Why do you strive to show your way good, to seek love? You who moreover have also taught your wickednesses, your ways, and on your wings — or in your hands — has been found the blood of the souls of the poor and innocent. Not in pits did I find them, but in all these or under every oak. 'In vain', he says, 'do you desire to defend yourself by the art of words and to show your works as good, so as to merit love, you who besides have also taught others your ways and been an example of evil works to all. And on your wings indeed — or in your hands — has been found the blood of the innocent, whom you sacrificed to idols, or whose souls you destroyed by the likeness of sacrifices'. 'The poor' we set down from the Hebrew; they are not found in the Seventy. 'But these,' he says, 'poor and innocent ones I did not find slain in pits' — which usually happens through the ambushes of robbers —, 'but in all the things which I have mentioned above, or under the oak' — which in Hebrew is called 'ella,' which indeed also signifies 'these', so that the sense is: 'in all these, or under the oak and terebinth' —, 'under whose shade and leaves, as in pleasant places, you enjoyed the crimes of idolatry'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.35":["And you said: I am without sin and innocent, and therefore let your fury be turned away from me. Behold, I will contend with you in judgment, because you said: I have not sinned. How exceedingly vile you have become — or since you have greatly despised — repeating your ways! These words must be used against those who will not acknowledge their sins, but in the time of affliction and distress say that they suffer unjustly what they suffer, and provoke the wrath of God the more, since it is another and greater sin not to mourn what they have done, but to put forward vain excuses for their sins. In judgment, he says, I will contend with you because you said: I have not sinned, as though it were something greater than this sin to hold one thing in the conscience and to utter another in speech. Let the new heresy sprung from the old hear that it is the very greatest wrath of God to be unwilling to confess sin humbly, and instead to boast shamelessly of righteousness.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.2.36-37":["And you shall be confounded by Egypt, as you were confounded by Assyria; for from this one too you shall go out, and your hands shall be upon your head, since the Lord has crushed your confidence — or hope — and you shall have nothing prosperous in it. To avoid the onset of the Egyptians they fled for refuge to the Assyrians, whose protection was vain; for we read that they were conquered by the Egyptians. Again, to escape the wrath of the Assyrian they relied on the help of the Egyptians, who, as history relates, were overcome by the Assyrians. They are rebuked, therefore, because, abandoning hope in God, they rely on the help of men, which has all been crushed and so overthrown that they can find nothing of use in it. Hence he says: and from this one you shall go out, that is, from Egypt, as you went out from the Assyrians, and your hands shall be upon your head, and you will mourn that you vainly expected protection from the Egyptians. Let us recall the history, when Tamar, corrupted and violated by Amnon, her most wicked brother, placed her hands upon her head sprinkled with ash and so returned to her house.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.1":["It is commonly said — for which the Seventy translated only it is said — : if a man dismisses his wife, and she, departing from him, marries another man, will he return to her any more? Will she not be polluted and defiled, that woman — or that land — ? And you have committed fornication with many lovers or shepherds. For the word 'rehim', which is written with four letters, 'res, ain, ioth, mem', signifies both 'lovers' and 'shepherds'; and if we read 'rehim', it means 'lovers', if 'rohim', 'shepherds'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Yet return to me, says the Lord or and you used to return to me, says the Lord. In the Hebrew he even receives her as a penitent after her fornication and exhorts her to return to him; but in the Seventy he does not call her to repentance, but rebukes the shamelessness of the harlot, in that after adultery she dares to return to her husband. And as to what he says: and that woman will be defiled, for which in the Hebrew we read 'land', he leaves the example and speaks more plainly of the land of Israel, which is compared to an adulterous woman. Let us use this testimony against those who, abandoning faith in the Lord and entangled in the errors of the heretics, after many fornications and the deception of souls, pretend to return to the former truth, not in order to lay aside the poisons of their breast, but in order to instill them into others.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.2-3":["And you have polluted — or killed — the land with your fornications and in your evils. Therefore the drops of the rains have been withheld, and there was no late rain or and you had many shepherds for your stumbling. The land was killed or polluted on account of the killing of those who perished by the fornication of idolatry; hence the blessing of all things was taken away, so that they suffered a drought of the word of God. Or she had shepherds through whom she offended God, so that those who ought to have been teachers, to keep others from error, became the authors of impiety.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.2":["Lift up your eyes straight ahead and see where you have not been prostrated; you sat in the ways waiting for them like a robber in the wilderness or like a deserted crow. In place of 'robber' and 'crow' in the Hebrew 'arabe' is written, which can also signify 'Arabs', a nation given to brigandage, which to this day raids the borders of Palestine and besets the roads for those going down from Jerusalem to Jericho, a thing the Lord too recalls in the Gospel. 'Lift up, therefore, your eyes, O Jerusalem, and look around this way and that, and see where you have not been by fornication prostrated. For just as robbers are accustomed, toward evening and in deserted places, to lay ambushes for travelers, so you, like the harlot of Proverbs, sat in the ways toward evening, to kill by your intercourse the souls of those who commit fornication; on account of which the whole land is polluted with your fornications'. And significantly, according to anagōgēn [the higher, spiritual sense], those who promise that they will abandon heretical errors are commanded to lift their eyes straight ahead. For unless they begin to see what is straight, they cannot condemn their former crookedness.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.3":["The brow of a harlot woman has become yours, you were unwilling to blush. LXX: the face of a harlot has become yours, you have become without blushing toward all. Because he had said above: I have not sinned, and she had sinned the more by denying her crimes, therefore he now rebukes her as a brazen woman of excessive shamelessness, so that she is not borne with brazen face to one man and another only, but blushes before none. Let us use this word against the conventicle of the heretics, who glory in their errors.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.4":["Therefore at least from now on call me: My father, you are the guide of my virginity; will you be angry forever, or will you persevere to the end? Let the heretics blush, who are unwilling to be turned to better things or to go back to the Father, their creator, and let them hear: at least from now on call me: my father, you are the guide of my virginity. He himself has betrothed your soul to his embraces and teaches how it ought to pray and to do repentance. But the more merciful he is who shows the way of salvation after fornication, the more wretched is the harlot who is unwilling to receive healing after her wounds.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.5":["Behold, you have spoken and done evil things, and you were able. 'In place of words of repentance you have blasphemed with words of pride, and you have fulfilled your evil thought and shown your strength against your husband, so that you can do what you handled in speech.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.6-10":["And the Lord said to me in the days of King Josiah: Have you seen what averse Israel has done? She went off by herself upon every high mountain and under every leafy tree, and committed fornication there. And I said, when she had done all these things: Turn to me; and she did not return. And her sister, Judah the transgressor, saw that, because averse Israel had committed adultery, I had dismissed her and given her a bill of divorce; and the transgressor Judah, her sister, did not fear, but went and committed fornication, she too, and by the facility of her fornication she defiled the land and committed adultery with stone and wood. And in all these things her sister, Judah the transgressor, did not return to me with her whole heart, but in falsehood, says the Lord. The torments of some are the remedies of others; and when a murderer is punished, he himself indeed receives what he has done, but others are deterred from the crime. Thus, when the ten tribes, which were called Israel, had been captured by the Assyrians and carried over into Media, the two tribes, Judah and Benjamin, which ought to have feared the like and to have been turned to God with their whole mind, surpassed the crimes of the ten tribes and followed idols to such a degree that they set up a statue of Baal in the temple of God, which in Ezekiel is called the idol set up to provoke the zeal and jealousy of the Lord. Now he speaks under the figure of two sisters, because they were begotten of the one stock of Abraham and Isaac and Jacob, and he calls the first the averse one, the second the transgressor. For the former became wholly averse to God at once, when the golden calves were worshipped in Dan and Bethel; but the latter, with whom were the temple and the religion of the true God, by imitating her sister gradually withdrew from the Lord, and is therefore called the transgressor. But according to anagōgēn the prophecy concerns the heretics, who, while they imagine that they are pursuing with heretical subtlety the knowledge falsely so called, climb the mountain of pride and, beguiled by the pleasures of this flesh, display their fornication under every leafy and pleasant tree. And when they are handed over to the devil for the destruction of the flesh, it often happens that the house of 'Iuda', that is, of 'confession' and of the true faith, is by no means frightened by the example, but commits far greater things, and by the facility of its fornication defiles the land of the Church and commits adultery with stone and with wood, following those doctrines which are adverse to God. But if a man of the Church wishes to correct the one who errs, and to cut away the rotten flesh, and to draw back to repentance those who had followed falsehood, and they nonetheless, under the appearance of the Church's truth, follow their old error, it can be said of them: in all these things the transgressor Judah has not returned to me with her whole heart, but in falsehood. Now this prophecy is made in the times of Josiah, a righteous king, under whom Jeremiah also began to prophesy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.11":["And the Lord said to me: Averse Israel has justified her soul in comparison with the transgressor Judah. 'More righteous,' he says, 'is Israel in comparison with Judah, because the former perished at once, at the beginning, while the latter could have been corrected by the torments of the former'. Let the new heresy sprung from the old take note that Israel is said to be justified in comparison with a worse one. Nor is this surprising of sisters of one nation, since Sodom too, in comparison with Jerusalem, receives the name of righteousness, as the Lord says through Ezekiel: 'Sodom has been justified by you', and the publican becomes righteous in comparison with the Pharisee.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.12-13":["Go and cry — or read — these words toward the north, and you shall say: Return, averse Israel, says the Lord, and I will not turn away — or I will fix — my face from you — or upon you —, for holy — or merciful — am I, says the Lord, and I will not be angry forever. Yet know your iniquity, that you have transgressed against the Lord God — or you have acted impiously — and you have scattered — or poured out — your ways to strangers under every leafy tree, and you have not heard my voice, says the Lord. The Hebrew word 'carath' is understood both as 'call' or 'cry' and as 'read'; hence Aquila and Symmachus translated 'keep crying', the Seventy and Theodotion 'read'. Now the word is directed to the north and against Babylon and the Assyrians, to the ten and to the two tribes, and their return is proclaimed. 'And I will not turn away', he says, 'my face from you, or I will not fix my face against you, so that I may receive you not with the severity of a judge but with a countenance of mercy. For I am holy and merciful, so as no longer to recall your iniquity nor to remember that you withdrew from the Lord and that in his place idols delighted you, and that you committed fornication under every shady and leafy tree'. This indeed may fittingly be said also to the heretics, or to those who are negligent in the Church, who are daily called to repentance by men of the Church, and to whom this can properly be applied: 'and you have not heard my voice.' And every heretic dwells in the north and has lost the warmth of faith, and cannot hear that word of the Apostle: fervent in spirit; and because he has given himself over to pleasures, he has withdrawn from the Lord and scattered his ways among alien doctrines and followed pleasure. For no heresy is built except for the sake of the gullet and the belly, in order to seduce silly women laden with sins, always learning and never arriving at the knowledge of the truth, of whom it is truly said: who devour my people like food of bread, and whom Christ marks as devouring the houses of widows. 'And when,' he says, 'I have had mercy on you, do not think that you are righteous, but always remember your iniquity and know that you have committed fornication against the Lord, and bow down the neck of pride, so that you who offended through arrogance may appease God through humility'. But to that which we said above, and I will not fix my face upon you, that prophetic word is fitting: turn away your face from my sins and blot out all my iniquities.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.14-16":["Be converted, returning sons — or wandering and departing —, says the Lord, for I am your husband — or I will be lord over you —, and I will take you, one from a city and two from a kindred, and I will bring you into Zion, and I will give you shepherds according to my heart, and they will feed you with knowledge and doctrine. And when you have been multiplied and have increased in the land in those days, says the Lord, they will no longer say: the ark of the covenant — or testament — of the Lord; nor will it come up upon the heart, nor will they remember it, nor will it be visited, nor will it be made any more. The Jews think that this was fulfilled after the return from Babylon under Cyrus, king of the Persians, and Zerubbabel son of Shealtiel, even though not all returned; that this is what is signified by: I will take one from a city and two from a kindred. But it is better taken of the coming of Christ, when the remnant was saved, as the Apostle says and explains: unless the Lord of Sabaoth had left us seed, we would have been as Sodom and would have been like Gomorrah; then they were brought into Zion, of which it is written: glorious things are spoken of you, city of God, and shepherds were given according to his heart, the apostles and apostolic men, and they fed the multitude of believers not in Jewish ceremonies, but in the knowledge and doctrine of Christ, and with the preaching of the Gospel sown in the whole world. They will by no means have confidence in the ark of the Lord, which was the guardian of the Mosaic law, but they themselves will be the temple of God; nor, like the erring Nazarenes, will they serve sacrifices that have been abolished, but they will pursue a spiritual worship. Others, however, understand this of the end of the times, when, as the fullness of the Gentiles comes in, all Israel will be saved.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.17":["At that time they will call Jerusalem the throne of the Lord, and all the nations will be gathered to it in the name of the Lord in Jerusalem, and they will not walk after the depravity of their most wicked heart. The Lord will by no means sit upon the ark of the testament and the cherubim, to whom that people formerly used to say: you who sit upon the cherubim, show yourself, but all who believe with a perfect mind will be the throne of God. Or certainly the whole is better understood of the Church, when all the nations are gathered in the name of the Lord into 'Hierusalem', in which is the 'vision of peace', and by no means walk after the depravity of their most wicked heart, so as to do what they desire, nor follow their own errors, but say with the prophet: my soul has clung after you, your right hand has upheld me.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.18":["In those days the house of Judah will go to the house of Israel, and they will come together from the land of the north to the land which I gave to your fathers. This is properly fulfilled at the coming of Christ, when out of the twelve tribes together they believed the Gospel, and, leaving the land of the north and of the harshest cold and withdrawing from the dominion of the devil, then received the land of promise which he had promised to their fathers, Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. I recently published a small book on the land of promise.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.19":["But I said: how or I will set you among sons and give you a desirable land, a glorious inheritance of the hosts of the nations! And I said: You will call me father, and you will not cease to walk after me. In place of 'a glorious inheritance of the hosts of the nations', which the Seventy translated: a named inheritance of God almighty of the nations, Theodotion translated more significantly: a renowned inheritance of the strength of the mightiest one of the nations, signifying Christ, who is the leader and lord of all the nations that believe in his name and passion. He himself said to Israel: you will call me father, and: he who believes in me believes in the Father. He himself promised: 'I will set you among sons, that is, in the number of my sons who have believed in me from the people of the nations and to whom I have given a desirable land'. For as many as received him, he gave them power to become sons of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.20":["But as if a woman should despise her lover, so has the house of Israel despised me, says the Lord. It is the voice of Christ toward the people of the Jews, to whom he had said: I will set you among sons and give you a desirable land, and you will call me father and will not cease to walk after me. 'As,' he says, 'a woman despises not a husband but a lover, once she has been joined with him, when she sees him serving her lust and the law of nature reversed in her regard, by which she had once been subject to a husband, as the Lord says: and toward you shall be her turning, so the house of Israel, that is, the people of the Jews, has despised the Lord, the Savior, to its own ruin.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.21-22":["A voice in the ways — or on the lips — was heard, the weeping and wailing of the sons of Israel, because they have made their way wicked, they have forgotten the Lord their God. Be converted, returning sons, and I will heal your aversions — or bruises — , for which Symmachus translated conversions. God gladly receives the penitent, and runs to meet the son worn out with want and squalor, and at once clothes him in his former garments and restores glory to the one returning, provided only that he returns with weeping and wailing. For by his own fault he has made his way wicked and has forgotten the Lord, his God and Father; to such the prophetic word now speaks: be converted, returning sons. 'Whom I call sons for this reason, because, having understood your sins, you return with weeping and wailing to your parent. And when you', he says, 'have returned to the Lord, he will heal all your bruises or aversions, by which you had withdrawn from the Lord, or certainly your conversions'. For although we return to the Lord by our own will, yet unless he draws us and strengthens our desire with his protection, we shall not be able to be saved. Let us understand this both of the people of the Jews returning to the Lord and of the heretics who have forsaken the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.22-23":["Behold, we come to you; for you are the Lord our God. Truly the hills were liars, and the multitude — or strength — of the mountains; truly in the Lord our God is the salvation of Israel. Let the penitent say this, leaving behind all pride and the multitude or height of the mountains and hills through which he grew proud against God, and, prostrate in humility, let him speak: truly in the Lord our God is the salvation of Israel.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.24":["Confusion has devoured the labor of our fathers from our youth, their flocks and their herds, their sons and their daughters. All the labors of the heretics, of which it is written: they have failed, searching with searching, from the youth of those whom they had deceived, their sons and their daughters, who had advanced in heresy or were held only by luxury, our confusion has overwhelmed. Hence they add:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.3.25":["We will sleep in our confusion, and our shame will cover us, because we have sinned against our God, we and our fathers, from our youth up to this day, and we have not heard the voice of the Lord our God. Let Israel say this, who did not hear his Lord; let every heretic say this who repents; and yet it is a part of salvation to confess and to know one's sins. 'Tell,' he says, 'your iniquities first, that you may be justified'. For truly Israel forsook Christ, the Lord his God, and sinned against him not only at the time when he was seen in the flesh, but also before his coming. Hence they say: 'we and our fathers, from our youth up to this day, and we have not heard the voice of our God, who spoke to our fathers: if you believed Moses, you would believe me also; for he wrote of me'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.1":["If you return, Israel, says the Lord, to me, turn back, for which the Seventy translated: if Israel is turned, says the Lord, to me, he shall be turned. And the sense is: 'if he has returned to me, he will return from captivity', or otherwise: 'when he has offered what he has'. For to him who has, it shall be given; but from him who has not, even that which he seems to have shall be taken away. But according to the Hebrew the sense is this: 'if you return to me, Israel, and once for all desire salvation, and say that you have sinned and have not listened to the voice of your Lord, turn back fully; believe in him whom you denied, and then there will be full conversion'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","If you take away your stumbling blocks from before my face, you shall not be moved. When, therefore, we are moved and say: but my feet were almost moved, we do not suffer this through the weakness of nature, but because we set up stumbling blocks and our idols against the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.2":["And you shall swear: the Lord lives, in truth and in judgment and in righteousness, and the nations shall bless him and shall praise him. And how is it that the Gospel forbids us to swear? But here 'you shall swear' is said for confession and for the condemnation of the idols by which Israel used to swear. Accordingly the stumbling blocks are taken away and he swears by the Lord. And as for what is said: the Lord lives, in the Old Testament it is an oath for the condemnation of the dead, by whom all idolatry swears. At the same time it must be noted that an oath has these companions: truth, judgment and righteousness; if these are lacking, it will by no means be an oath, but perjury. 'And when,' he says, 'Israel has done this too, and through the apostles has become the teacher of the nations, then all the nations shall bless, or be blessed, in him, and shall praise him, because salvation has gone forth from Israel'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.3-4":["For thus says the Lord to the man of Judah and Jerusalem: break up fallow ground for yourselves, and do not sow upon thorns; be circumcised to the Lord and take away the foreskins of your hearts, men of Judah and inhabitants of Jerusalem, lest my indignation go forth like fire and be kindled, and there be none to quench it, because of the wickedness of the thoughts — or devices — of yours. For what we said: be circumcised to the Lord and take away the foreskin of your hearts, Symmachus put: be purified to the Lord and take away the wickednesses of your hearts, understanding circumcision as cleansing and foreskins as vice. And this is commanded to the men of Judah and Jerusalem who follow the true faith and dwell in the Church: that they should not sow upon thorns, which the word of the Gospel signifies, thorns that choke the seed of God, but should first make fallow ground, dig up all the brambles and take away the briars, so that clean fields may receive clean seeds. This is what is said in another place: do not cast your pearls before swine, and do not give what is holy to dogs. For how can a man hear the word of God and conceive the seeds and bear fruit, whose mind is full of the troubles of the world? And as for what follows: be circumcised to the Lord and take away the foreskins of your hearts, it is commanded to no one else but the man of Judah and the inhabitant of Jerusalem, that they should forsake the letter that kills and follow the Spirit that gives life. 'For if you do not do this,' he says, 'my indignation will go forth like fire and will be kindled, and there will be none to quench it'. And he therefore warns and foretells beforehand so as not to be compelled to do it, as we also prove in the case of the Ninevites, to whom the sentence was foretold so that they might turn aside the impending fury by repentance. And all these evils will come because of the wickedness of our thoughts or devices. Where are those who say that there is no sin in thoughts, when, according to the truth of the Gospel, all vices proceed from the heart?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.5-6":["Declare in Judah and make it heard in Jerusalem; speak, sound the trumpet in the land, cry aloud, say: gather together, and let us enter the fortified cities. Let 'Iudas' hear this, let 'Hierusalem' hear this, in which is the 'confession' of faith, and in which the 'peace' of Christ dwells, and to which it was said through Isaiah: go up onto a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion; lift up your voice, you who bring good tidings to Jerusalem; let it cry aloud and give command thus: 'let us enter the fortified cities — the wars of the heretics are rising: let the fortifications of Christ hold us — ; raise the standard of the cross on the watchtower and on the height of the Church; be strong, you who fear, do not stand still, but run to the help of Christ; for evil,' he says, 'I bring from the north, and great destruction, the true Nebuchadnezzar, who is permitted by me to exist in this world for this very reason, that your strength and victory may be proved'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.7":["The lion has come up from his lair, and the plunderer of the nations has raised himself up; he has gone forth from his place to make your land a desolation; your cities will be laid waste, remaining without inhabitant. This is, as we have said, the true Nebuchadnezzar, of whom Peter also speaks: our adversary the devil goes about like a roaring lion. And he comes up, it may be, from the abysses, into which he is to be bound again, and he begs not to be sent there, and the plunderer — or the devastator — of the nations has raised himself up, of whom it was said: he shall rule over all his enemies, and who boasts in the sight of the Lord: I have gone around the whole earth and trodden it down. For who is there whom the poisons of the devil do not touch, except him alone who can say: behold, the prince of this world comes and finds nothing in me? He often makes the whole land of the Church a desolation, so that those who have gone out from the Church fight against the Church, of whom John the evangelist also speaks: they went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would surely have remained with us. The cities of the land of Judea are laid waste and the conventicles of the heretics flourish; if anyone, therefore, is a supporter of the authors of perverse doctrines, of him it can be said: the lion has come up from his lair, and the plunderer of the nations has raised himself up, and the rest.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.8":["For this gird yourselves with sackcloth, lament and howl, for the wrath of the fury of the Lord has not turned away from us or, as the Seventy translated, from you. In no other way can we escape the lion and most savage beast unless we do repentance and turn to the Lord, not only in mind but also in deed. For as long as he lays waste the Church and the land of Judah, and ravages Jerusalem as well, the wrath of God is manifest.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.9":["And it shall be in that day, says the Lord: the heart of the king shall perish, and the heart of the princes, and the priests shall be astounded, and the prophets shall be dismayed. While the plunderer lays waste the Church of the Lord and the wrath of the Lord remains against us, all help will be useless. The heart of the king shall perish, whose heart ought to be in the hand of God, and the heart of the princes, who were thought to be wise; for God has made foolish the wisdom of the world, because through it they did not know God. The priests themselves too, who ought to teach the law of the Lord and to defend the peoples subject to them from the fury of the lion, made senseless by a kind of stupor, shall be turned to madness — for so the Seventy translated, expressing 'ecstasy of mind' for 'stupor' — and the prophets shall be dismayed, or, as Aquila translated the Hebrew word 'iethmau', they shall be out of their minds. For who would not go mad, who would not lose heart, when he sees the princes and kings and priests and prophets that were once his own under the lion?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.10":["And I said: alas, alas, alas, Lord God — for which the Seventy translated: O Lord God —, have you then deceived this people and Jerusalem, saying: you shall have peace? And behold, the sword has reached even to the soul. Because he had said above: at that time they shall call Jerusalem the throne of God, and all the nations shall be gathered to it in the name of the Lord in Jerusalem, and now he says: the heart of the king shall perish, and the heart of the princes, and the priests shall be astounded, and the prophets shall be dismayed, the prophet is troubled and thinks that God has lied to him, and does not understand that the former was promised for long ages after, while this would come about in the near time, in accordance with which the Apostle too says: has God rejected his people? Far from it. And the sword reaches even to the soul when nothing living is left in the soul. At the same time he shows this too: that unless the sword goes before, to cut away and purge the vices of the soul, peace and the promise do not follow.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.11-12":["At that time it shall be said to this people and to Jerusalem: a burning wind — or of error — in the ways that are in the desert, the ways of the daughter of my people, not for winnowing and cleansing. A full spirit shall come to me from these. When the sword has reached even to the soul and the threshing floor has been finished, then the burning wind will come from the desert, which will not cleanse and winnow it, so that, with the chaff scattered here and there, the grain may be laid up in the granaries; but a full spirit will come, not to the people at all, but to me, so that my wheat may be scattered. 'Wind' and 'spirit' are called among the Hebrews by the same name, 'ruha', and according to the nature of each passage we should take it either as 'wind' or as 'spirit'. Others expound this passage thus: that after the threshing floor has been cleansed, the remnant will be saved, whence it is also written: a spirit of fullness shall come to me, as the evangelist says: 'we have all received of his fullness, and we shall obtain the grace of the Holy Spirit'. But according to the history take the burning wind as Nebuchadnezzar, who consumes all things; according to tropology, as the adverse power, which, coming from the desert and from the wilderness, where there is no lodging of God, tries to overthrow his Church.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.12":["And now I — but I will speak my judgments with them. Aposiōpēsis [breaking off], after that line of Vergil: whom I — but it is better to calm the troubled waves. Being about to speak of good fortune, then, he restrains himself and joins sorrows to sorrows; for these are the judgments which God speaks with his people of old, so that they may know that they justly suffer what they suffer.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.13":["Behold, he shall come up like a cloud, and like a tempest his chariots; his horses are swifter than eagles. Woe to us, for we are laid waste! He sees things to come as present and describes the Babylonian army, the din of whose chariots and wheels is compared to a most savage tempest, and the swiftness of whose horses is likened to eagles. When the prophet had said this and had pointed out with his finger the enemies as though they were coming, the people groans and feels these things not as future but as already done, saying: woe to us, for we are laid waste! Refer this same thing to the Church: that daily the army of the true Nebuchadnezzar assails us, and the chariots of Pharaoh and all his cavalry outstrip the onrush of eagles. If the man of the Church understands this, believing that saying: 'when you have turned and groaned, then you shall be saved', he will say: woe to us, for we are laid waste!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.14":["Wash your heart from wickedness, Jerusalem, that you may be saved; how long shall harmful thoughts linger in you? To the people saying: woe to us, we are laid waste! the prophet answers, nay rather God through the prophet: wash your heart from wickedness, Jerusalem, with that water of which Isaiah too speaks: wash yourselves, be clean, the water of saving baptism, the water of repentance. And he speaks to the metropolis of the Jews, so that by the city the peoples may be understood: 'how long do you lie subject to wicked thoughts, which proceed from your heart?' And in the holy Scriptures we should take the heart for the soul and the understanding.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.15":["For the voice of one declaring from Dan, and of one making known an idol — or pain — from mount Ephraim. The divine word now speaks according to the lie of the land of Judea. For the tribe of Dan, near Mount Lebanon and the city which is now called Paneas, faces the north, from which Nebuchadnezzar is to come. And he describes an idol, whether Bel, or pain, or iniquity, as coming from mount Ephraim. For after the tribe of Dan comes the land of Ephraim, through which one comes to Jerusalem. 'Dan' means 'judgment', 'Ephraim' 'abundance'. The judgment of the Lord, therefore, will come upon the land that sins against the Lord, with all the abundance of punishment.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.16-17":["Say to the nations: behold, it has been heard in Jerusalem that guards are coming from a distant land; and they have given their voice over the cities of Judah; like guards of fields they have become over her round about, because she has provoked me to anger, says the Lord. He wants all the nations round about to know the sentence of God, and all to receive correction from the scourging of Jerusalem. 'It is told,' he says, 'by common report in Jerusalem that adversaries are coming from a distant land, and that the roar of a howling army is rising against her, who besiege the city so diligently and shut in the city with siegeworks, that you would think them not so much adversaries as guards of fields and vineyards. But all this has come about not by the strength of the enemies, but by the fault of Jerusalem, because she provoked God to anger'. For if the adverse powers have no power over swine, how much more is this true of men, and of men once of the city of God!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.18":["Your ways and your thoughts have done these things to you; this is your wickedness, because it is bitter, it has touched your heart. He makes an apostrophe to the city of Jerusalem: that her ways and thoughts, by which she sinned both in deed and in word, have made all that came upon her come, and that her wickedness, which is bitter in itself, has touched her heart and pierced the inmost parts of her soul. Whatever, therefore, befalls us befalls us through our own fault, who turn the sweet God into bitterness and force him to rage against his will.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.19-20":["My belly, my belly, I am in pain; the senses of my heart are troubled. I will not keep silent, for my soul has heard the sound of the trumpet, the clamor of battle. Destruction upon destruction has been called, and all the land is laid waste; suddenly my tents are laid waste, in a moment my skins. Where we, following Symmachus, have put 'are troubled', and in the Hebrew is written 'homae', the Seventy and Theodotion put maimassei, a word whose meaning I do not know to this day. Aquila, however, put ochlazei, which itself also means 'tumult'. Let this be said about the word, over which I know there is a great dispute among very many. And the voice is introduced of the prophet, and through the prophet of God speaking, that he grieves over the destruction of his people and his inner bowels are torn as a man's are, just as the Savior too grieved over the death of Lazarus and lamented Jerusalem, so as not to hide his grief in silence; and every blast of the trumpet and roar of the battles disturbs his feeling, while evils are heaped upon evils and the whole land of the two tribes is laid waste. 'While I was not expecting it,' he says, 'my tents of old and my skins were ravaged by the raging Babylonian, and my former lodgings passed into the plunder of the enemies'. And God says this same thing when he sees the tumults of sedition and discords in the Church, and the partridge crying in his assemblies, and the rest of God turned into wars. Whence it follows:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.21":["How long shall I see those fleeing, shall I hear the sound of the trumpet? Either those fleeing the Babylonian king, or those fleeing me and withdrawing from my service.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.22":["And the Lord himself through the prophet: The leaders of my people have not known me; they are foolish sons, and without understanding; they are wise to do evil, but to do good they have not known. They were indeed called my leaders, he says, and leaders of my people, but because they did not know me, and destroyed the name by their deeds, therefore they are foolish sons and without understanding; they have prudence only to this end, that they may subject the simple flock to themselves and trample it under their feet; but to do good and to rule the people they have not known.\n— Commentary on Micah","For my foolish people has not known me. They are unwise sons and senseless; they are wise to do evil, but to do good they have not known. The cause of the destruction, the devastation, the flight, the trumpet: that the people has become foolish, not by nature but by the bent of its will. And this very folly is proved from this, that they did not know God; and instead of wise sons they have become foolish sons and senseless. For what greater folly can there be than that, while the ox knows its owner and the ass its master's crib, Israel should not know the Lord and should despise him when present, whom it always desired to see? And as for what is added: they are wise to do evil, but to do good they have not known, here wisdom is to be taken for wickedness, in accordance with which the sons of this age too are wiser than the sons of light, and the steward of iniquity is said to have done certain things wisely, and the serpent in paradise is read to have been more prudent than all the beasts. That, then, is true wisdom which is joined to the fear of God; otherwise, where there are snares and evasion, it is to be called not wisdom but cunning and craftiness. For what we said: for my foolish people has not known me, the Seventy translated: for the princes of my people have not known me, so that the fault is rather the teachers' than that of the people, which does not have the knowledge of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.23-24":["I looked at the earth, and behold, it was empty and nothing, and at the heavens, and there was no light in them. I saw the mountains, and behold, they were moving, and all the hills were troubled. I gazed, and there was no man, and the bird of the sky had departed. I looked, and behold, Carmel was deserted, and all its cities were destroyed before the face of the Lord and before the face of the wrath of his fury. The prophet sees in the spirit the things that are to come, so that the people, hearing, may be terrified and, having done repentance, may by no means suffer what it dreads. The earth is empty, its inhabitant destroyed; the heavens have no light, since the people does not see for the greatness of its terror; the mountains themselves and the hills have unsafe hiding places, and by hyperbolēn [hyperbole] they seem to move and be troubled.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.25":["He gazed and looked around here and there, and not even a bird could be found; for even the mute elements feel the wrath of God, and the irrational animals dread it. That this is true the whole world now shows, in that, a multitude of men having been slain, the birds too, which are wont to follow the inhabitants, have gone away and perished.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.26":["Carmel itself too, which overhangs the great sea, planted with olives and thick with shrubs and vines, will come to such desolation that it will have the waste of a wilderness; all the cities too will become deserted. And the cause of all these evils is this: that through the fault of the sinning people the wrath of the Lord has been roused. Whatever we have said according to the history of Jerusalem and Judea, let us refer to the Church of God when it has offended God and has been laid waste either by vices or by persecution, so that where once there was a choir of virtues and joy, there a multitude of sins and sorrows dwells.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.27":["For thus says the Lord: all the land shall be deserted, but yet I will not make a full end; the earth shall mourn and the heavens above shall grieve, because I have spoken, I have purposed, and I have not repented, nor have I turned back from it. God's mercy being mixed with wrath, all the land is deserted, but a full end is not made, so that there may be those who understand his clemency.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.28":["The heaven too will look sad above, and the earth itself will mourn, because the sentence of the Lord has come right to its end, and he has not repented of the things he purposed and spoke. But repentance is ascribed to God when a sentence foretold is withdrawn and raging wrath does not come right to its end. He threatened through Jonah, and the multitude of tears and groans overcame the impending sword.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.29":["At the sound of the horseman and of the one shooting the arrow — or bending the bow — every city has fled — or region — ; they have gone into the heights and climbed the rocks — and what follows: and they have gone into the thickets or caves, was added by the Seventy —, all the cities are abandoned, and no man dwells in them. The divine word describes the army of the raging Babylonian: that for fear of it the whole people abandoned the city and climbed every height, and yet could not escape the wrath of the Lord. But whatever, as we said above, is understood in the history against Jerusalem, refer to the Church when it has offended God and has been handed over to adversaries, either in a time of persecution or, at any rate, through vices and sins.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.30":["But you, laid waste, what will you do? For 'laid waste', which in Hebrew is said 'sadud', which Aquila alone translated so, the others translated 'wretched' and 'pitiable', through her own fault, because she has offended the merciful God. Accordingly it follows:\n\nWhen you clothe yourself in scarlet and are adorned with a golden necklace and paint your eyes with antimony, in vain will you adorn yourself; your lovers have despised you, they will seek your soul. He speaks under the figure of an adulterous woman: 'once you have offended God and, as it were, left your husband, the Creator, in vain do you seek out ornaments; the demons, your lovers, have despised you, and they will seek not at all the uncleanness of debauchery, but the destruction of your soul'. This same thing is to be understood spiritually against those who have lost conjugal affection and the chastity of the true faith. 'If,' he says, 'you clothe yourself in scarlet — take up faith in the blood of Christ —, if you adorn yourself with a golden necklace — have meditation of the sense and of spiritual understanding — and paint your eyes with antimony — have zeal for the mysteries and for knowing the secrets of God —, in vain will you adorn yourself. For these things you had prepared for your lovers too, and therefore the narrow bed cannot hold both, nor does God accept the ornaments with which you formerly pleased your lovers'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.4.31":["For I have heard a voice as of a woman in labor, anguish — or groans — as of a woman in childbirth; the voice of the daughter of Zion, swooning and spreading out her hands: woe to me, for my soul has fainted because of the slain! He describes the city of Jerusalem wailing and crying out after the likeness of a woman in childbirth, that is, one who is bearing her first offspring. For as a woman giving birth, who has not yet experienced the pain of childbearing, swoons — she suffers anguish, she can scarcely breathe, and with her hands spread out she collapses —, so too the daughter of Zion, when she sees her children slain, bursts out into these words and says: woe to me, for my soul has fainted because of the slain! Two examples are compared in one passage: of a woman bearing children and of a woman mourning, so that whatever a woman suffers in childbirth or in the deaths of her children, Jerusalem may suffer in her peoples.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.1":["Go about the ways of Jerusalem, and look, and consider, and seek in its squares, whether you may find a man who does judgment and seeks faith, and I will be merciful to it. But if they even say: The Lord lives, this too they will swear falsely. Great is the love of righteousness: not that God should deliver the city for ten righteous men, as in Abraham's question and God's answer, but that if he finds a single one in Jerusalem, now on the very point of perishing, who does judgment and seeks faith — or, as Symmachus translated, truth —, God would nonetheless have mercy on Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.2":["And because it could happen that some would be found among the people who feigned the worship of God and swore by the Lord, he forestalls this: that God is in no way delighted by empty words, but by the truth of faith; and he says: 'I do not love them because they swear by me, and they swear in falsehood; but those whose hearts and lips agree'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.3":["Lord, your eyes look upon faith. You struck them, and they did not grieve; you crushed them, and they refused to receive discipline; they hardened their faces beyond rock, and they would not return. After the words of the Lord, by which he had given command, saying: Go about the ways of Jerusalem and the rest, the prophet speaks to the Lord: Lord, your eyes look upon faith, which in Hebrew is called 'emuna': not the works of the Jews, in which they exulted according to the ceremonies of the law, but the faith of Christians, through which we have been saved by grace. From this passage we learn that punishments are inflicted for this reason: that vices may be corrected. Accordingly he says: You struck them, and they did not grieve; you crushed them, and they refused to receive discipline. For Jerusalem is corrected through every torment and scourge; and over and above all these they did not even feel shame for their own vices, but, hardening the impudence of their brow like a rock, they would not be converted to better things.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.4-5":["But I said: Perhaps they are poor and foolish — or they could not — not knowing the way of the Lord, the judgment of their God. I will go therefore to the nobles and will speak to them; for they have known the way of the Lord, the judgment of their God. He speaks here of the poor and the nobles not in terms of want and riches; rather, he compares the people with the princes. And the sense is: 'Seeing the obstinacy of the unbelieving people, and that with hardened face it would not receive discipline, I was reasoning this out with myself: perhaps the ignoble common crowd cannot know the teaching of God and is therefore excusable, because through inexperience it is not able to know the commandments of God. I will go therefore to the priests and to those who preside over the people, and will speak to them; for they have known the will of the Lord and know the judgment of their God'. He says this in the language of one who doubts, according to that saying of the Gospel: I will send my son; perhaps they will reverence him, so that from the ambiguity of the statement and the suspense of the words the free will of man may be shown.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.5":["And behold, these all the more together have broken the yoke, have burst the bonds. Therefore a lion from the forest struck them, a wolf at evening laid them waste, a leopard watching over their cities. Everyone who goes out from them will be taken, because their transgressions are multiplied, their turnings-away are strengthened. 'Those whom I thought to be teachers were found to be worse than the disciples; and the greater the authority among the rich, the greater the insolence of their sins'. For they broke the yoke of the law, as the Apostle says: Now therefore why do you tempt God, to put a yoke upon the neck of the disciples, which neither our fathers nor we have been able to bear? But through the grace of the Lord Jesus we believe that we are saved, in the same way as they also; and they burst the bonds of the precepts of God, and not of the Pharisees, of whom it is said in the second Psalm: Let us burst their bonds asunder, and cast away their yoke from us.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.6":["Jeremiah also speaks of sinners: A lion from the forest struck them, and a wolf destroyed them even to their houses; and a leopard kept watch over their cities. All who go out from them will be taken. In this, note that no one is taken except one who has gone out from the cities of God.\n— Commentary on Nahum","Because, then, they did these things, a lion from the forest struck them, namely the Babylonian kingdom; a wolf at evening laid them waste, signifying the Medes and Persians, in place of which in the vision of Daniel a 'bear' is set down, in whose mouth there were three rows; a leopard watching over their cities, prefiguring the onslaught of Alexander and his swift course from the west all the way to India. He calls him a leopard on account of his variegation, and because he fought against the Medes and Persians with very many nations subject to him. And there were, he says, four heads on the beast, and power was given to it. And because he is not prophesying of the future, but is weaving the history of the past or of things now on the very point of coming, for that reason he is silent about the Roman empire, of which perhaps it is said: Everyone who goes out from them will be taken. And he gives the causes why they suffered these things: because their transgressions are multiplied and they persevered in their transgressions. Hence it is said: and their turnings-away are strengthened. What we set down at the beginning, that the Hebrew word 'sacad' signifies 'watchfulness', is shown in the present place; for where we said 'a leopard watching', in the Hebrew is written 'nemer sacad'. According to tropology: those who are thought great in the Church, because they break the yoke and burst the bonds, are for that reason handed over to the disgrace of passions, so that they do the things that are not fitting.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.7":["On what ground shall I be able to be merciful to you? Your sons have forsaken me and swear by those who are not gods. I filled them, and they committed adultery, and in the house of the harlot they ran riot. They have become to me horses, lovers toward the females, and stallions; each one neighed after the wife of his neighbor. Shall I not visit for these things? says the Lord, and on such a nation shall not my soul take vengeance? A Katalogos [a catalogue] of the sins of Jerusalem, while God says that he does not know on what ground he could have mercy on her. 'The sons', he says, 'of yours have forsaken me: by no means mine, but yours, because they swear by those who are not gods. I filled them, and they committed adultery'. Let those hear this who, brooding over the riches they have received from the Lord, are slaves to luxury.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.8":["They have become horses, lovers toward the females. For 'stallions' in the Hebrew is written 'mosechim', which all with one accord translated helkontes, that is, 'drawing', so that the size of the genitals may be shown, according to that saying of Ezekiel: like the flesh of asses is their flesh. This is what is written in another place: they were likened to senseless beasts and became like them. And at the same time he shows so great a madness of lust that he calls it not the appetite of pleasure but chremetismon, that is, 'neighing', and keeps the metaphoran [metaphor] of horses raging for lust.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.9":["'When you have done these things', he says, 'are you not deserving of a fitting visitation?' And note that here 'visitation' is put for penalty and punishments, according to what is written: I will visit their iniquities with the rod. And on such a nation shall not my soul take vengeance? After she has been bound by sins, she is no longer called the people of God, but a nation, from which the soul of God has withdrawn, according to what is written: your new moons and sabbaths and feast days my soul hates. But what in the Old Testament is said by way of affection, in the New is written as truth, as the Savior says: I have power to lay down my soul, and I have power to take it up.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.10-11":["Go up upon her walls — or bulwarks — and destroy, but do not make a full end! Take away her shoots — or props —, because they are not the Lord's! For with transgression the house of Israel and the house of Judah has transgressed against me, says the Lord. He commands the nations of which he had said above: a lion from the forest struck them, a wolf laid them waste, and a leopard in their cities, to go up upon the walls of Jerusalem, or its bulwarks, and destroy it, but not to make a full end, so that the remnant may be saved and there may be one to announce the glory of God among the nations; and he mingles clemency with severity. And as for what he added: take away her shoots or props, he orders all the aids to be removed which she lost by her own fault, because the house of Israel and the house of Judah transgressed against God, signifying the ten tribes and the two. Let the Church hear this: that the walls and bulwarks of those who have no hope in the Lord and transgress against him are quickly destroyed, but that nevertheless a full end is not made, on account of the clemency of the judge and not on account of the merits of the offenders.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.12-13":["They have denied the Lord and said: He is not — or these things are not — nor will evil come upon us; sword and famine we shall not see. The prophets have come to wind, and an answer — or a word — was not in them. These things therefore will come upon them. Because they have denied the Lord — or they have lied to the Lord — and said: 'He is not, by whose judgment all things come to pass, but all these things have happened by chance; and the things which the voices of the prophets threaten us with will not come, nor shall we see the sword, nor shall we endure the famine of a siege; and whatever the prophets spoke, they spoke to the wind, and all things have passed away void, nor did they have an answer, that is, an oracle, or the word of God was not in them', therefore they will endure what the following discourse describes. Let the Church hear this, when it is negligent and rejects the providence of God: that it will endure both sword and famine, unless it believes that the things which are spoken will come.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.14":["Thus says the Lord God of hosts: Because you have spoken this word, behold, I give my words in your mouth into fire, and this people into wood, and it will devour them. 'You said: The prophets have come to wind, and the things they threaten will not come; therefore, O prophet, I give my words in your mouth, which are to have the power of fire, and which are to turn this people into wood, so that the unbelieving may be burned up together by your discourse and prophecy.' So God is called a consuming fire, that he may consume in us whatever, upon the foundation of Christ, we have built of hay, wood, stubble.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.15":["Behold, I will bring upon you a nation from afar, O house of Israel, says the Lord, a strong nation, an ancient nation, a nation whose language you will not know, nor will you understand what it says. Its quiver is like an open sepulchre; all are mighty. And it will eat your crops and your bread, it will devour your sons and your daughters, it will eat your flock and your herds, it will eat your vine and your fig tree, and it will crush with the sword your fortified cities, in which you have confidence. Nevertheless in those days, he says, I will not make you into utter destruction. 'Not after a long time, nor, as you falsely believe, will the prophets speak to you into the wind; but even now I will bring upon you a nation of the Babylonians, which will come from afar, a strong nation' — for which in the Hebrew is written 'etham' —, 'an ancient nation' — over which Nimrod the giant once held dominion —, 'whose language you will not know or' — as is written in the Hebrew — 'nor will you understand what it says; for it is a solace in misfortunes if you have such enemies as you can entreat and as understand your prayers'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.16":["And what follows: its quiver is like an open sepulchre, which is not said in the edition of the Seventy, signifies the Babylonian armament. Nor is there any doubt that the kingdom of the Assyrians, the Babylonians, the Medes and the Persians is most skilled in archery.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.17-18":["And at the same time he describes the devastation of the land of Judea and the slaughter of many, the driving off of cattle, the overthrow of cities and of walls: that all things are taken by the hostile sword, and that yet amid such great evils he does not destroy them to extermination, but keeps a remnant safe, either of those who were led into Babylon and released for the cultivation of the fields of the land of Judea, or of those who, after the heat of persecution, kept the faith of the Lord either by flight or by confession.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.19":["But if you say: Why has the Lord our God done all these things to us? you shall say to them: As you have forsaken me and served a foreign god — or foreign gods — in your land, so you shall serve foreigners in a land not yours. Great is the folly of not knowing why they have suffered, when they have sinned so greatly; and brief is the answer to those who are in doubt: as you have served a foreign god — that is, Baal — or foreign gods of all the nations in the land of Judea, so you shall serve foreign gods in a land not yours, no doubt the Babylonians and Chaldea. 'For if a strange religion delights you, what need is there to take up an error from afar? Dwell with such people; nay rather, serve those whose gods you worship.' This can also be taken of heretics, of whom it is written: they went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would surely have remained with us: that the Lord casts out of the Church the heretics who for a long time under his name have venerated the images of their own lies, so that they may worship outside what they formerly venerated inside, and the chaff may be separated from the wheat.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.20-21":["Declare this to the house of Jacob and make it heard in Judah, saying: Hear, O foolish people, who have no heart, who having eyes do not see, and ears and do not hear! In many ways he draws sinners back to salvation, and he calls foolish the people that has abandoned the author of wisdom, and he compares them to the images of which it is written: They have eyes and do not see; they have ears and do not hear. Let those who make them become like them, and all who trust in them. Properly, however, he speaks to Judah and to the house of Jacob — for Israel had already for a long time been in exile among the Assyrians — and at the same time he gives us to understand that even without a precept we ought to understand by natural sense the things that are right.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.22":["Will you not then fear me, says the Lord, and will you not grieve before my face — or fear — ? I who set the sand as a boundary for the sea, an everlasting precept, which it will not pass. And they will be moved and will not be able — or it will be troubled and will not be able — and its waves will swell — or will sound — and will not pass over it. But to this people there has come to be an unbelieving and provoking heart, and they have withdrawn and gone away, and they have not said in their heart: Let us fear the Lord our God, who gives us the early and the late rain in its season, keeping for us the fullness of the yearly harvest! He recounts his benefits in order to convict the ungrateful. 'Me,' he says, 'you will not fear, I who have bestowed such great things on you? I do not ask for the love of the perfect, but for the fear of beginners, I who set the sand as a boundary for the sea, I who by my precept have reined in at the shores so mighty an element and the immense masses of the deeps, according to that which is written: He set a precept, and it will not pass.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.23-24":["Things that have no sense of hearing hear me and perceive me; and the people once mine, made foolish by its own fault, not only despises but even provokes its sweet God. They have withdrawn,' he says, 'from me, and turned their backs on me, and gone away with hurried step, nor did a silent conscience draw them back, so that they should say in their hearts: Let us fear him who gives us the early and the late rain! — through all of which I have shown good things, the fullness of the yearly harvest', for which Aquila's first edition and Symmachus translated 'weeks'; for in the Hebrew is written 'sabaoth', which, owing to the ambiguity of the word, signifies both 'weeks' and 'fullness'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.25":["Our iniquities have turned these things aside, and our sins have withheld good from us. Therefore, if at any time the sea both oversteps its boundaries and the rain is held back, by no means has the hand of the Lord been shortened, so as not to do these things; but our sins have turned aside these things as they were coming to us, so that they went on to others who had not sinned. 'And they have withheld,' he says, 'the good that was already coming to us, according to that which is written according to the letter: I will command the clouds that they rain no rain upon it'. But we can take the early and the late rain as the law and the Gospel, and as the various callings from the first hour to the eleventh, in which one reward of eternal life is promised to the laborers of the vineyard.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.26-27":["For among my people there have been found ungodly men lying in wait like fowlers, setting snares and traps to catch men! As a trap full of birds, so their houses are full of deceit. That the early and the late rain might turn aside from them and all good things might not come, the causes are added: because there have been found among his people 'ungodly men'. He did not say 'unjust' and 'sinners', as the new heresy would have it, but 'ungodly'. Ungodliness openly denies God; iniquity and sin, if it confesses its error, easily bends God to mercy. And for what we have said, lying in wait like fowlers, which is not found in the Seventy, Aquila and Symmachus translated the word 'iasir' as like the net of a fowler, because even he who among them seems good and upright lays snares like a fowler, while they hunt one another to death and fill their own houses with the losses and costs of others, so that that saying of the philosophers is fulfilled: Every rich man is either unjust or the heir of an unjust man. And would that these things were done only by those who seem to be outside, and whom the Lord judges, and were not fulfilled in our own little assemblies by those whom avarice, the root of all evils, possesses, so that we look not at the faces of those who come to us, but at their hands!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.27-29":["Therefore they have been magnified and enriched. They have grown fat and sleek, and they have passed over my words most wickedly. They have not judged the cause, the cause — or judgment — of the orphan they have not directed, and the judgment of the poor — or of the widow — they have not judged. Shall I not visit for these things? says the Lord, or shall my soul not take vengeance on a nation such as this? If I should wish to note one by one the things that have been left out in the edition of the Seventy, it will become long. 'Those who lay snares,' he says, 'and rejoice in the nakedness of others, therefore they have been magnified and enriched.' Because they did the things above, they have grown fat and sleek, according to that which is written: He grew fat and sleek, and the beloved kicked. And they have passed over my words, because in the consciousness of their riches they said that saying from the Gospel: Soul, you have many goods laid up for very many years; rest, eat, drink, feast! Moreover, they passed over to their own harm, and, despising all men, they did not set the judgment of God before their eyes. They despised the orphan and the poor, for which the Seventy said 'widows', which is not found in the Hebrew; for 'hebionim' properly denotes 'poor', not 'widows'. But what follows, Shall I not visit for these things? says the Lord, or shall my soul not take vengeance on a nation such as this?, has already been discussed above.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.5.30-31":["Astonishment and wonders have come to pass in the land: the prophets prophesied a lie — or wicked things — and the priests clapped with their hands, and my people loved such things! What then will you do at the last — or after these things — ? Above he had said: I will go to the chief men and will speak to them; perhaps they have known the way of the Lord. And behold, these rather have together broken the yoke, they have burst the bonds; now he describes who the chief men are, namely the prophets and the priests, some of whom foretell things to come, while others determine from the law the things to be done. 'And behold,' he says, 'while those men prophesied a lie, the priests clapped with their hands.' And so that it may be shown that the people too is not without fault, which lets itself be led away by such men, it is written: and my people loved such things: once mine, but after it loved them, it ceased to be mine. What then will they do when the last time of judgment has come, or the necessity of captivity? Hence there are astonishment and wonders, because neither among the princes nor among the people was anyone found who thought rightly.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.1":["Be strengthened, sons of Benjamin, in the midst of Jerusalem, and in Tekoa sound the trumpet, and over Beth-haccherem raise a signal — or a standard —, for evil has been seen — or has appeared — from the north, and great destruction! No one is unaware that Jerusalem is situated in the tribe of Benjamin. That Tekoa too is a small village situated on a mountain, twelve miles distant from Jerusalem, we see daily with our own eyes. Between these is another village, which in the Syriac and Hebrew tongue is named Bethacarma, and it too is set on a mountain. What he says, therefore, is this: 'Since from the north Nebuchadnezzar is now on the very point of coming and captivity is near and threatening, O inhabitants of Jerusalem, seize your arms and sound the trumpet in Tekoa and in Beth-haccherem raise the standard', he says, 'so that you may be able to resist your enemies!' Beniamin means 'son of the right hand', Thecua 'trumpet', Bethaccarem 'estate of the vineyard'. Let us refer all these things to the Church, so that, if she has transgressed and the onslaught of persecution has come, she may prepare herself to resist.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.2-3":["I have likened the daughter of Zion to a beautiful and delicate woman. To her shepherds will come with their flocks; they have pitched — or they will pitch — their tents against her round about; each one will pasture those who are under his hand. Sanctify — or Prepare — war against her; arise, and let us go up at noon! The beauty of Jerusalem is described, which is Zion itself, so that the one name denotes the whole city, the other the citadel of the city. For Sion means 'citadel', that is, watchtower, and she is compared to a beautiful woman; and as lovers come together to the one, so shepherds are said to come together to the other. And quite elegantly in the Hebrew the word that is written with four letters, 'res, ain, iod, mem', if it is read 'reim', means 'lovers', if 'roim', 'shepherds', so that either, according to the metaphoran [metaphor] of a beautiful woman, lovers are understood, or, according to the overthrow of the city, shepherds: of whom the former hasten to defile a harlot, the latter to besiege and overturn a city. In the shepherds and their flocks let us perceive the princes and the army of the Chaldeans. And they will pitch their tents round about in the siege of the city, and each one will pasture those who are under his hand, that is, his own squadrons and companies.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.4":["These princes or shepherds will say to their flocks: 'Sanctify against Jerusalem war, for it is the command of the Lord. Arise, and let us go up at noon: let us fight not by night and by ambush, but in full daylight; for no one can resist us'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Woe to us, for the day has declined, for longer have grown — or have failed — the shadows of evening. Arise, and let us go up by night, and let us destroy the houses — or foundations — of her! Those say: 'Arise, and let us go up at noon, and let us fight in clear daylight'; these reply: woe to us, for the shadows of evening have grown longer, according to that saying of Vergil: and now far off the rooftops of the farmhouses smoke, and greater shadows fall from the high mountains. And the sense is: 'If we suffer these things by day, what shall we suffer by night?'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.5":["And again those who had said above: 'Sanctify war against her and arise', now rouse themselves to battle, saying: 'Arise, and let us go up by night, so that our adversaries may know that the victory belongs not to the time of day but to strength, and let us destroy the houses, which are fenced in vain by the firmness of their walls'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.6":["For thus says the Lord of hosts: Cut down her tree — or her trees —, pour out — or heap up — a mound around Jerusalem! 'Therefore', they say, 'we are secure of victory, because it is the command of the Lord, who orders the Chaldeans: cut down the trees and heap up a mound for the siegeworks to come!' By these words it is shown, before it comes to pass, that the city was not to be taken at once by an encircling ring of troops, but by a long siege, as we read afterwards.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","This is the city of visitation — or the lying city —, all false accusation — or oppression — is in her midst. As a cistern makes cold — or a pit — its water, so she has made her wickedness cold. The Lord commands that the trees be cut down, that mounds be cast up round about, because the time of her visitation has come, that she may receive the due for her sins, the greatest of which is sykophantia, that she oppresses the innocent by false accusation.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.7-8":["Iniquity and devastation will be heard in her; before me continually are sickness and wound. Be instructed, Jerusalem, lest perhaps my soul depart from you, lest perhaps I make you a desert, an uninhabitable land! LXX: Ungodliness and misery will be heard in her before her face. Through every pain and scourge you will be instructed, Jerusalem, lest perhaps my soul depart from you, lest I make you a trackless land, which is not inhabited. Through these words we learn that the Lord scourges every son whom he receives; and for that reason Jerusalem is instructed by blows and torments, that she may be corrected, and that the soul of God may not depart from her and she be reduced to a wilderness. If ever, therefore, we lie under frequent distresses, let us recall for our consolation this little verse: through every pain and scourge you will be instructed, Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.7":["As, therefore, a cistern or pit makes its water cold, so in the midst of Jerusalem the wickedness that is in her has lost all the warmth of life. And this is to be noted, that the saints are called fervent in spirit, whereas evils are cold; whence that also is written, that in the last days, when iniquity has been multiplied, the love of many will grow cold. I think that this too means the same: I have been likened to those who go down into the pit. And let the Latin reader understand this, so that it may suffice to have said it once: that 'lacus' [pit] does not mean 'pool' according to the Greeks, but 'cistern', which in the Syriac and Hebrew tongue is called 'gubba'. In the present passage, however, for 'lacus', which all translated alike, in the Hebrew it is 'bor'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.9":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Even to the last cluster they will glean, as in a vineyard, the remnant of Israel; turn back your hand like a grape-gatherer to the basket! LXX: For thus says the Lord of powers: Glean, glean as in a vineyard the remnant of Israel; return like a grape-gatherer to his basket! Some take these words in a good sense, others in a bad. In the good sense: 'When Jerusalem has been laid waste, let the remnant be saved'. In the bad, thus: 'Let not even one cluster and small bunch of grapes remain in the vineyard; let everything be gathered; and whatever you find, throw into the basket like a grape-gatherer, so that, as he drags the clusters to the winepress, so you may drag the captives to Babylon'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.10":["To whom shall I speak, and whom shall I solemnly warn, that he may hear? Behold, their ears — or your ears — are uncircumcised, and they cannot hear! Behold, the word of the Lord has become to them a reproach, and they will not receive it! They cannot hear because they were unwilling to circumcise their ears; yet the inability is not free of punishment, since it comes down from contempt and unbelief. If anyone, therefore, does not receive the words of God and has no understanding of his precepts, he is of uncircumcised ears. And at the same time it is to be noted that circumcision in the Scriptures is spoken of in three kinds: in the foreskin, in the heart, and in the ears; whence the Lord too says: He who has ears to hear, let him hear. It is by our own will, therefore, that we do not receive the word of God; and for that reason it becomes to us a reproach, so that what had been given for salvation is turned by our own fault into punishment.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.11":["Therefore I am full of the fury of the Lord; I have toiled in holding it in. LXX: And I have filled up my fury and held it in, and I did not consume it. According to the Hebrew it is said in the person of the prophet, that he foresees the wrath of God that is coming, and is full of the fury and anger of the Lord, and can hold it in no longer, nor dares to entreat the Lord for sinners. According to the Seventy, however, a new sense is set down: that the Lord himself speaks and says: I have completed my fury, by striking the sinful people, and yet that he restrained it and did not pour it all out, so that a remnant might be saved. This seems to me to be self-contradictory; for if he completed his fury, how did he hold it in so as not to complete it?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Pour out — or He poured out — upon the little child outside and upon the council of young men together; for man with wife will be taken, the old man with him who is full of days. And their houses will pass to others, their fields and wives alike. Either the prophet in the spirit commands the coming Chaldean to pour out the fury of the Lord upon the little children and to spare not even their innocent age, or else he relates what has been done upon the council of young men together, who had seized their arms to fight back. For man with wife will be taken: names most sweet to one another will alike feel captivity, the old man with him who is full of days. Therefore old age is not the last age, but that of those who are 'full of days', whom in our own speech we call 'depositos' [given up for dead] or 'decrepitos' [decrepit].\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.12":["There follows: and their houses will pass to others through the evil of captivity, their fields and wives together, so that both wife and possession pass to the enemy. Whatever we understand according to the letter of Jerusalem, let us refer according to the spiritual understanding to the Church, if she has offended God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","For I will stretch out my hand over those who inhabit the earth, says the Lord. For from the least even to the greatest, all are devoted to avarice, and from prophet even to priest all practice deceit. And they were healing the breach of the daughter of my people with disgrace, saying: Peace, peace! And there was no peace. What in the Hebrew is said: I will stretch out my hand over sinners or the inhabitants of the earth, among the Seventy is always written 'I will lift up'; and each is the posture of one who strikes, according to what is written: and still the hand of the Lord is stretched out or lifted high. The inhabitants of the earth, moreover, are always in vice; whence in Revelation too it is said quite frequently: Woe to the inhabitants of the earth!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.13":["From the least even to the greatest, all are devoted to avarice, according to that saying of the Apostle: the root of all evils is avarice. And from prophet even to priest all practice deceit: the one by prophesying a lie, the other by perversely interpreting the law of God; for the prophet also says in another place: Seek the law from the priests.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.14":["'And though', he says, 'they were doing such things, they kept announcing prosperity of every kind to my people, and as it were desired to heal the wound and the disgrace of my daughter, saying: Peace, peace, when there was no peace at all'. This is properly to be understood of the priests and teachers who promise prosperity to the rich and to those whom they see set in the highest honor, and proclaim God to be merciful, rather preparing them for punishment and wrath.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.15":["Were they confounded because they committed abomination? Nay rather, they were not confounded with confusion, and they knew not how to blush. This is to be read more closely according to the Hebrew. 'And though', he says, 'they have done such things, were they at all confounded? Did they at all blush in their crimes? Nay rather, they increased their contempt, and they knew not how to blush' — and he put 'knew not' here for 'would not' — 'or else, through excessive contempt and the vice of an ingrained evil, they could not even understand'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Therefore they will fall among those who fall; in the time of their visitation they will collapse, says the Lord. 'Since', he says, 'they knew not how to blush, and had not only not the deed but not even the knowledge and the sense of repentance, therefore those who formerly stood will fall among them; they will collapse through their own vices and, when the time of their visitation and punishment has come, they will be joined to all who fall'. And it is a great ungodliness not only not to guard against sins but not even to be willing to understand them, and to make no distinction between good and evil works.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.16":["Thus says the Lord: Stand upon the ways and see, and ask about the ancient paths, which is the good way, and walk in it! And you will find refreshment — or purification — for your souls. And they said: We will not walk — or We will not go —. And I set watchmen over you: hear the voice of the trumpet! And they said: We will not hear. Therefore hear — or they heard —, O nations, and know, O congregation — or you who pasture flocks, or according to Symmachus: and know the testimony that is in them —, how great things I will do to them! Hear, O earth! The parable of the Gospel, if it is understood, will furnish the understanding of this passage; in it the good merchant is said to sell all his pearls, so that with their price he may buy the one most precious pearl: namely, that through the patriarchs and prophets we come to him who says: I am the way. We must therefore stand in the prophets, and contemplate most diligently, and ask about the ancient or everlasting paths — which are worn by the footsteps of many saints, which in Greek are more expressively called triboi —, which is the good way in the Gospel, and walk in it; and that way, when it has been found, affords refreshment or purification to the souls of believers. But they, on the contrary, answered: We will not walk in the way of the Gospel, the prophet saying this properly of the faithlessness of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","There are many ways, and many ways lead to one way. Indeed, it is also said in Jeremiah: 'Stand in the ways of the Lord, and ask for the eternal paths of the Lord, and you will find the way, and you will walk in it.' See what it says, Stand in the ways, and ask for the eternal paths of the Lord, and you will find the one way, and walk in it. See how through many ways we come to one way. This is also said in the gospel in other words, under another meaning of the parable, concerning that merchant who had many pearls, who sold them, and bought one most precious pearl. What it says is this: Stand in the ways, that is, have very many pearls. Stand in the ways, and ask for the eternal paths of the Lord. Beautifully it says eternal, lest we should think that at the coming of Christ the law and the prophets were brought to an end. But see what it says. Stand in the ways, in the law and the prophets. Stand in the ways; do not so believe in the one way that you abandon the many ways; but stand in the ways, and ask for the eternal paths of the Lord. Ask the paths of the Lord, what Jeremiah said, Isaiah, Ezekiel, what the rest of the prophets said. Ask the paths. It is better said in Greek τρίβους (tribous): for a path (semita) is so called from the fact that it is worn (trita) by the heel of those walking. And what is said is this: Ask the paths, that is τρίβους (tribous), which the foot of the Lord wore down: seeing that the Lord too walked in them. But when you have asked for the eternal paths of the Lord, and through those paths you have found the one way, which says 'I am the way and the life and the truth,' walk in it. See the mysteries of the scriptures: see in the simple words of the scriptures very many sacred meanings, in the ways and paths, in the law and the prophets. It did not say, walk, but, stand: do not abandon the law nor the prophets. Yet when you have found those ways, through these ways walk in this one way.\n— Homily 42, on Psalm 127"],"JER.6.17":["And at once he adds: 'And I set watchmen over you' — no doubt the choir of the apostles is indicated, according to Ezekiel: Son of man, I have given you as a watchman to the house of Israel —, 'and I commanded that you hear the voice of the trumpet, either the commands of the Gospel or the teaching of the apostles, according to that saying of Isaiah: Go up onto a high mountain, you who bring good tidings to Zion; lift up your voice like a trumpet, you who announce to Jerusalem'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.18":["They said: We will not hear, and when invited to the supper they would not come; therefore it is said: Hear, O nations! This the apostles too followed when they speak in Lycaonia: It was necessary that the word of God be spoken first to you; but since you thrust it away and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the Gentiles. And know, O congregation: by no means the Jewish one, but that of all nations; or, you who pasture flocks: the bishops and presbyters and the whole order of the Church; or, know the testimony that is in them; for the testimony of God is faithful, giving wisdom to little ones. To them the Lord also speaks: Fear not, little flock, and: Behold, I and my children, whom God has given me. 'Know, therefore, how great things I will do to the people that does not believe.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.19":["And in what is added: Hear, O earth, the whole world is called to hear, as we also read at the beginning of Isaiah: Hear, O heaven, and give ear, O earth: that is, what the Lord is going to do to the people of the Jews.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Behold, I will bring evils upon this people, the fruit of their thoughts — or of their turning away —, because they have not heard my words and have cast away my law. He calls 'evils' the punishments and penalties of those who suffer, not upon the Gentiles, who are called to the truth of the Gospel, but upon the people that answered: We will not hear. And it is going to receive the fruit of its thoughts or of its turning away, as David says: You shall eat the labors of your hands. And the cause is plain: because they did not hear the words of the Lord and cast off his law.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.20":["To what purpose do you bring me frankincense from Sheba, and sweet-smelling calamus from a distant land? Your burnt offerings are not accepted, and your victims have not pleased me. That frankincense comes from Sheba no one doubts; whence also that line of Vergil: and a hundred altars glow with Sabaean incense. And that 'calamus', which in Hebrew is called 'cane', for which the Seventy and Theodotion translated 'cinnamon', comes from a distant land, the prophetic word shows, so that we may understand India, from which many spices come by way of the Red Sea. This kind of spice physicians call kassian syringos [pipe cassia]. And the sense is: 'In vain do you bring me spices of sweetest odor for compounding the ointments that are prescribed in the law, and kindle burnt offerings, you who do not do my will in the law', according to what was said above: they have not heard my words and have cast away my law. This, moreover, properly fits those who offer sacrifices from robbery and the stripping of the wretched, and think they redeem their sins by alms from iniquity, though Scripture says: The ransom of a man's soul is his own riches, which are gathered not from iniquity but from labor and righteousness.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.21":["Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I will put ruins into this people, and they will fall in them — or weakness, and they will be weakened in it —, fathers and sons together, neighbor and kinsman, and they will perish. We see fulfilled all the things with which the Lord threatened that people; for daily they fall in their blasphemies, and they have nothing strong in them, but all is weakness among them. The sons follow the blasphemies of their fathers, and daily receive that imprecation: His blood be upon us and upon our children; and not only they themselves, but also their neighbors and kinsmen, all who follow the law and the prophets according to the letter that kills and not according to the spirit that gives life; and all alike will perish, because alike they have sinned.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.22-23":["Thus says the Lord: Behold, a people comes from the land of the north, and a great nation will rise from the ends of the earth. It will seize arrow and shield — or zibynnam [spear] —; it is cruel — or shameless — and will not have mercy; its voice will sound like the sea, and they will mount upon horses, prepared like a man for battle against you, daughter of Zion. This is properly prophesied of the Babylonians, who are to come against the people of Jerusalem; and the whole array of their armor and the onset of the combatants is described, so that, shaken by the terror of the voice, they may do repentance and appease a most merciful God. Finally, he gives occasion for entreaty when he says: against you, daughter of Zion. We may apply this testimony, in a transferred sense, to a time of persecution, when the whole rage of the devil is stirred up against us, and there is no mercy, and like the waves of a most violent sea they overwhelm those who resist.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.24":["We have heard the report of it; our hands have grown feeble; tribulation has seized us, pains as of a woman in labor. The people answers, whom the prophet, nay rather the Lord through the prophet, has threatened with the Babylonians: that before they come they have been overcome by fear, and cannot lift up their hands, and anguish has seized them as pain seizes a woman in labor; than which pain those women who have felt it affirm that nothing is more grievous.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.25":["Do not go out into the fields, and do not walk on the road, for the sword of the enemy, terror is all around! The Gospel teaches that one must not go out into the field nor come down from the height of the housetops, but must heed that word: Save yourself on the mountain, to which in Isaiah and Micah we are commanded to run and climb. According to the letter, however, it is commanded that they not go outside, that they not desert the walls, but that they protect themselves with strong fortifications.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.26":["Daughter of my people, gird yourself with sackcloth and sprinkle yourself with ashes; make for yourself the mourning for an only-begotten — or a beloved —, a bitter lament — or a pitiable one —, for suddenly the destroyer will come — or misery — upon us — or upon you — ! Since he had said above: Do not go out into the fields, and do not walk on the road, for the sword of the enemy, terror is all around, and had forbidden flight as well, he teaches what they ought to do: namely, that they be converted to repentance and have this as their firmest and safest armor. Where we have said: make for yourself the mourning for an only-begotten, for 'only-begotten' in the Hebrew is written 'iaid', which means 'solitary' rather than 'only-begotten'. For if it were 'beloved' or 'lovable', as the Seventy translated, 'ididia' would be set down, which is also the name God gave to Solomon. And nothing is more grievous than to lose one son, or an only son. And that which we have translated: for suddenly the destroyer will come or misery upon us, the Seventy rendered upon you, whereas God has spoken far more mercifully, testifying that whatever is to come upon his people comes upon himself as well. 'The destroyer', moreover, properly signifies either Nebuchadnezzar or the devil.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.27":["I have given you as a strong assayer among my people, and you will know and test their way. All these are princes turning aside — or disobedient —, walking deceitfully — or perversely —, bronze and iron; all of them are corrupted. The bellows has failed in the fire — which the Greeks more expressively call physētēra —, the lead is consumed, in vain has the smelter smelted — or the silversmith —; for their wickednesses have not been consumed. Call them reprobate silver, for the Lord has cast them away! The prophet is given to the unbelieving people as a 'strong' assayer, which in Hebrew is called 'mabsar', which means either 'fortified', according to Aquila, or 'shut in' and 'surrounded', according to Symmachus and the Seventy, like a most strongly fortified city, so that he may fear no ambushes of the people: 'so that, when you have tested', he says, 'and known the way of the transgressing people, you may then understand that silver mixed with bronze can by no means be purified'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.6.28-30":["For as lead is mixed with metals that are adulterated and debased, so that the foreign matter may be separated, and, if perhaps it has not been purified, all the lead is consumed and reduced to nothing, so all the eloquence of teaching and the prophetic word perishes in those who scorn to hear. Let us speak also of those who, like deaf asps, stop their ears so as not to hear the voices of the charmers. For in vain has the silversmith smelted or the smelter; for their wickednesses have not been consumed. Hence they are called not 'silver' but 'reprobate silver', for the Lord has cast them off. And the princes are those who withdraw from the Lord, or the disobedient, who walk perversely and deceitfully.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.2":["The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying: Stand in the gate of the house of the Lord and proclaim — or read — there this word, and say: Hear the word of the Lord, all Judah, you who enter by these gates to worship the Lord! This is not found in the edition of the Seventy, but has been added from Theodotion out of the Hebrew. Now the prophet is commanded to stand in the gate of the Lord, through which the multitude of the people enters to pray to the Lord, so that on this occasion they may be able to hear what the Lord commands. By this we understand the hardness of the people of the Jews, in that they held the prophets as liars and madmen, since it is through the occasion and the throng of the place that they are compelled to hear the words of the Lord, and not because they are the words of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.3":["Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Make good — or direct — your ways and your pursuits — or your devices — and I will dwell with you in this place or I will make you dwell in this place. The most merciful physician desires to heal the wounded with every remedy. But when he says: make good or correct your ways, he shows that they are perverse and have nothing good in them. And because it is natural that each person loves his native soil and holds nothing sweeter than his fatherland, he promises rewards to the obedient. 'I will dwell,' he says, 'with you, so that I may make you secure about your dwelling, or I will found you yourselves' — according to Symmachus — 'with a firm dwelling', who says: and I will establish you in this place.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.4":["Do not trust in lying words, saying: The temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord it is! For if you direct well — or direct — your ways and your pursuits, if you execute judgment between a man and his neighbor, if you do not practice false accusation against the stranger and the orphan and the widow — or do not oppress them — and do not shed innocent blood in this place, and do not walk after strange gods to your own harm, I will dwell with you — or I will make you dwell — in this place, in the land which I gave to your fathers from age to age. This, which the Seventy added at the beginning of this chapter: in words of falsehood, which will not profit you at all, is not found in the Hebrew. He commands both the people of the Jews then and us today, who seem to be established in the Church, not to have confidence in the splendor of buildings and in gilded ceilings and in walls clad with veneers of marble, and say: the temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord it is. For that is the temple of the Lord in which true faith dwells, holy conduct, and the choir of all the virtues.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.5-7":["Then he adds: 'if you make your ways straight, and your thought does not go off after error, and you follow righteousness and do not commit evil, and do not shed innocent blood by scandalizing every simple person, and do not walk after strange gods by worshipping perverse doctrines which you have feigned out of your own heart to your own harm, then either I will dwell with you in this place, which you call the temple of God, and in the land which I gave to your fathers, namely the apostles and apostolic men, or at any rate with a firm standing I will make you dwell from the beginning to the end.' This can also suit those virgins who boast of their chastity and display purity with a shameless face, while their conscience holds something else, and who do not know that definition of virginity given by the Apostle, that she be holy in body and in spirit. For what does purity of body profit when the mind has been debauched, if she does not have the other virtues which the prophetic word has described?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.8-10":["Behold, you trust in words of falsehood — or lying words —, which will not profit you: to steal, to kill, to commit adultery, to swear falsely, to pour libations to the Baals, and to go after strange gods whom you do not know. And you came and stood before me in this house, in which my name has been called upon, and you said: We are delivered — or we have ceased — because we have done all these abominations. The sins that follow show that they have confidence in the temple in vain. For what does it profit to cross boldly the threshold of the house of God, to stand with neck held high, and to have not only the heart but also the hands polluted with theft, murder, adultery, perjury, sacrilege, and the worship of those gods whom you do not know? No one doubts that this happens spiritually in the Church, when people, considering the prosperity of the present time, do not take account of their sins and think that they are hidden from God, because punishment does not follow at once; nay rather, they break out into such madness that they think themselves delivered because after their evil works they have even withdrawn from the worship of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.11":["Has this house — or my house —, in which my name has been called upon, become a den of robbers in your eyes? I, I am, I have seen, says the Lord. From this passage, I think, was taken what is in the Gospel: It is written: My Father's house shall be called a house of prayer; but you have made it a den of robbers, or, as is written in another Gospel, a house of trade. The Church of God is turned into a den of robbers when theft, murder, adultery, perjury, sacrilege, the invention of heresy and every crime are at home in it, when princes burn with the torches of avarice and a cheap, or at any rate not cheap, little cloak possesses what were once the riches of kings. Hence he adds: 'I, I am, I have seen, says the Lord; my eyes have beheld what you think hidden; the darkness of your treasuries does not escape my knowledge'. He who, though he was rich, became poor for our sake, now blushes at our riches and says: Woe to you rich, who have your consolation!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.12":["Go to my place in Shiloh, where my name dwelt from the beginning, and see what I did to it because of the wickedness of my people Israel! From the past he teaches the present, and to those who say: the temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord, the temple of the Lord and who rejoice in the gleam of a costly house he recalls the history of Shiloh, where the tabernacle of God first was, of which it is written in the psalm: and he rejected the tabernacle of Shiloh, so that, just as that place collapsed into ruins and ashes, so the temple too may fall, since it has been the dwelling of like sins. Therefore, just as Shiloh is an example for the temple, so is the temple for us, when the time of that testimony comes: Do you think the Son of Man, when he comes, will find faith on the earth?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.13":["And now, because you have done all these works, says the Lord, and I spoke to you, rising early and speaking, and you did not hear, and I called you and you did not answer, I will do to this house, in which my name has been called upon and in which you have confidence, and to the place which I gave to you and to your fathers, as I did to Shiloh, and I will cast you out from my face, as I cast out all your brothers, the whole seed of Ephraim. This which we have set down: rising early and speaking, is not found in the Seventy. Now God rises early, not because there is any time for him without daybreak, but because after the rest of the night, when the strength of the body has been restored, the soul of men is more vigorous and, in no way occupied with pleasures and the desire for food, is able to hear and to do what is said. Hence we also read this in the psalm: In the morning you will hear my voice; in the morning I will stand before you and will see; and in Isaiah: By night — or at daybreak — my spirit will rise to you, O God, for your precepts are light upon the earth. Hence also the apostle Paul calls them sons of light, and not of night nor of darkness, nor sleepers, as the others too have slept, who do not perceive the commandments of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.14-15":["Since, therefore, God called them, rising by night, so that he might free them too from darkness, he threatens them that he will do to the temple in Jerusalem things like those he did to the place Shiloh, where the tabernacle first was, so that like sins may be struck by an equal sentence. And just as the Lord cast out the seed of Ephraim, that is, the ten tribes, which were called Israel and had princes on account of Jeroboam son of Nebat, of the tribe of Ephraim, which was likewise called the tribe of Joseph, so he testifies that he will also cast away Jerusalem and the tribe of Judah with Benjamin. He cast away Shiloh, therefore, and he will cast away the temple too; he cast away the ten tribes, and he will cast away the two as well. Whatever is said to that people, let us understand it of ourselves too, if we do like things.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.16":["You, therefore, do not pray for this people, nor take up praise and prayer for them — or nor ask that they obtain mercy, — and do not withstand me, for I will not hear you. So that the prophet may not seem, in asking, to fail to obtain what he requests, God commands him not to intercede for a sinful people that does no repentance at all. But when he says: and do not resist me, he shows this, that the prayers of the saints can resist the wrath of God. Hence the Lord also speaks to Moses: Let me go, that I may strike this people, and I will make you into a great nation; and in the Psalms it is written: And Phinehas stood and appeased, and the shaking ceased, and it was reckoned to him for righteousness. Aaron too, seizing a censer, stood in the midst between the fire and the people about to burn, and the wrath of God ceased. And lest we think God cruel, since he does not even allow himself to be entreated, he gives the reasons why he does not hear, saying:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.17-19":["Do you not see what these are doing in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem? The sons gather wood and the fathers kindle the fire and the women sprinkle fat, to make cakes — or chauonas — for the queen — or the host — of heaven, and to pour libations to strange gods and to provoke me to anger. Is it me they provoke to anger? says the Lord, is it not themselves, to the confusion of their own face? 'Do you wish,' he says, 'to hear, prophet, why I said to you: do not pray for this people? They do these things that follow: both within and without and in the streets and in the outlets of Jerusalem the sons carry together wood and the fathers kindle the fire and the women sprinkle fat with flour to make chauonim' — which we have rendered 'cakes' or 'preparations', so that it shows every kind of sacrifice to the queen of heaven, whom we should take to be the moon, or at any rate to the host of heaven, so that we understand all the stars — 'and after this they pour libations to strange gods, not that these exist, but in that under their names they burn incense to demons, and they provoke me to anger by doing these things. Nor do the wretches understand that this contention does not harm me, whom anger never changes, but themselves, to the confusion of their own face and to everlasting disgrace.' Whatever, therefore, we do, we do not harm God, who can never be harmed, but we prepare destruction for ourselves, storing up wrath for the day of wrath. And he set down the different tasks of the sons and of the fathers and of the mothers, or wives, so that there may be no age that has dissented from the impiety.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.20":["Therefore thus says the Lord God: Behold, my fury and my indignation is kindled — or has dripped — upon this place, upon men and upon beasts and upon the trees of the region and upon the fruits of the earth, and it will be set alight and will not be quenched. He who had said above: Is it me they provoke to anger?, how does he now say: Behold, my fury and my indignation has dripped upon this place? And the sense is: 'I indeed am not angered by nature, but they act in such a way as to provoke me to anger, so that I seem to change my nature. Let them, therefore, feel me angry, which, so far as in them lies, they are trying to bring about.' And beautifully he does not say: 'has been poured out' my fury upon this place, but: has dripped, so as to signify a moderate punishment. But if in a drop of fury there is such harshness, what will happen if the whole downpour is poured out? But 'kindling' too can be understood thus: that what he long did not wish to do, he is compelled to do by the multitude of sins. And when God is angry, both men and the things that are men's will feel a like destruction. 'And it will be set alight', he says — no doubt the fury of the Lord — 'and will not be quenched, because the people do not do the things through which it could be quenched.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.21-23":["Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Add your burnt offerings to your victims and eat flesh, for I did not speak with your fathers, and I did not command them on the day that I led them out of the land of Egypt, concerning the matter of burnt offerings and victims, but this word I commanded them, saying: Hear my voice, and I will be your God and you will be my people, and walk in every way that I have commanded you, that it may be well with you. Having rejected their temple, he consequently rejects their sacrifices too, and indirectly reproves them because they sacrifice victims not out of reverence for him but out of desire for feasting. But when he says: I did not speak with your fathers, and I did not command them on the day that I led them out of the land of Egypt, about the matter of the burnt offering and of victims, it is clearly understood that he first gave the Decalogue, written on tablets of stone by the finger of God, and that after the offense of idolatry and the head of the calf he afterward commanded that it be done to himself rather than to demons, taking away the pure religion of the commandments of God and granting the blood of victims and the craving for flesh.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.24-25":["And they did not hear nor incline their ear, but they went off in the wills — or desires — and the depravity of their evil heart, and they became backward and not forward, from the day on which their fathers came out of the land of Egypt until this day. 'When I said: Hear my voice and I will be your God and the rest, they did not hear nor incline their ear, but they did the desires of their own heart, and against the judgment of the Apostle, who forgot what lay behind and stretched himself toward what lay before, they did the contrary, so as to desire the past and despise the future.' And when he says: from the day on which their fathers came out of the land of Egypt until this day, he says that they spent all the time in between in offense against the Lord. Hence the grace of the Gospel was also necessary, which saved them not by their own merit but by the mercy of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.25-26":["And I sent to you my servants, the prophets, by day, rising at daybreak and sending; and they did not hear me nor incline their ear, but they hardened their neck and wrought worse than their fathers. Just, therefore, is the wrath of the Lord, which has been kindled and has dripped upon a contemptuous and stiff-necked people that refuses to hear the words of God. But how God sends the prophets either by night or at daybreak and by day, we have said above.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.27-28":["And you will speak to them all these words, and they will not hear you; and you will call them, and they will not answer you. And you will say to them: This is the nation that has not heard the voice of the Lord its God nor received discipline; faith has perished and has been taken away from their mouth. 'Do not doubt,' he says, 'that they have hardened their neck and wrought worse than their fathers; behold, I give room for repentance, and I do not speak in order that these things may happen, but because they are going to happen, therefore I foretell them. At least now speak to them with my words, and yet they will not hear you; and you will call them, and they will not answer you; for they will be of such pride that, when you call them to hear, no one will deign to answer. And you will say to them: This is the nation that has not heard the voice of the Lord its God nor received discipline'. Beautifully, as I have already said before, he calls them not at all his people, but a nation. And although this was in part done in the time of the prophets too, and went before in shadow and in image, yet it is more fully fulfilled in Christ, when they refused to receive discipline and despised the voice of their Lord. Hence it is fittingly added: faith has perished, which belongs properly to Christians, and has been taken away from their mouth, namely every confession of the Son of God and of the faith.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.29":["Shear your hair and cast it away, and take up in the open — or upon the lips — a lament, for the Lord has cast off and forsaken the generation of his fury. We read that Job too, on hearing of the death of his sons and daughters, sheared his hair, and among the ancients this was the custom of all who mourned, to shear the locks; but now, on the contrary, to let the hair grow long is the sign of mourning. And all the prophet's lament and lamentation is taken up for this reason, for the Lord has cast off and forsaken the generation of his fury; no doubt he signifies the people of the Jews. And this is properly to be referred to the times of Christ, when faith perished and was taken away from the mouth of the people blaspheming the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.30-31":["For the sons of Judah have done evil in my sight, says the Lord; they have set their stumbling blocks in the house in which my name has been called upon, to pollute it, and they have built the high places — or the altar — of Topheth, which is in the valley of the son of Hinnom, to burn their sons and their daughters with fire, which I did not command nor think of in my heart. That the sons of Judah set up a statue of Baal in the temple of God we learn at the beginning of Ezekiel. Now 'high places', which in Hebrew are called 'bamath', or 'altar' of Topheth, which is in the valley of the sons of Hinnom, signifies that place which is watered by the springs of Siloam, and is pleasant and wooded, and to this day offers the delights of gardens. And this error of paganism took hold of all the provinces, so that they sacrificed victims at the heads of springs and in the loveliest groves, and the observance of every depraved superstition was kept. 'Tofeth' in the Hebrew tongue means 'breadth', and it is said to be written in the book of Joshua son of Nun about this place, which is in the valley of the sons of Hinnom and in Hebrew is called 'gehennom'; for 'ge' signifies pharanga, that is, 'valley', and 'hennom' signifies either a man's name or 'grace'. And the Hebrews hand down that 'gehennam' was named from this place, because, namely, the whole people of the Jews perished there by offending God, in which place they also consecrated their sons in fire to idols or offered them as a burnt offering, which he had not commanded them nor ordered by any sanction of the law. But if Jephthah offered his virgin daughter to God, it is not the sacrifice that pleases, but the mind of the one offering; for if a dog or an ass or any unclean animal whatever had first met the father as he returned from the slaughter of the enemy, he ought not to have offered it to God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.32-33":["Therefore, behold, the days will come, says the Lord, and it will no longer be called Topheth and the valley of the son of Hinnom, but the valley of killing, and they will bury in Topheth because there is no room. And the carcasses of this people will be food for the birds of heaven and the beasts of the earth, and there will be none to drive them away. He signifies the time of the siege, which they endured from the ninth year of King Zedekiah to the eleventh year, and that the valley itself will by no means be called 'gehennom', that is, 'valley of hennom', or 'of the sons of Hinnom', but, on account of the killing of very many, 'valley of slaughter'. And so great will the slaughter be that in the place formerly of worship there will be innumerable tombs, and those whom they cannot bury will be torn by birds and devoured by beasts, and there will be no one to drive them away, since he fears the like and is overcome by the duties of burying. We pass quickly over what is plain, so that, wherever there is occasion, we may linger over what is obscure. For the very size of the book can cause weariness to readers; how much more if it were discussed by us at greater length!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.7.34":["And I will make to cease from the cities of Judah and from the streets of Jerusalem the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, and the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride; for the land will be in desolation. When the place of idolatry has been turned into tombs, so that where they had offended God, there their corpses lie unburied, all gladness will be taken away from the city of Jerusalem that once was and from the other cities that were under its dominion, and all things will be filled with sorrow and groaning and desolation.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.1-3":["At that time, says the Lord, they shall cast out the bones of the king of Judah and the bones of his princes and the bones of the priests and the bones of the prophets and the bones of those who dwelt in Jerusalem, from their graves. And they shall spread them out to the sun and the moon and all the host of heaven, which they have loved and which they have served and after which they have walked and which they have sought and worshiped. They shall not be gathered and shall not be buried; they shall be for a dunghill upon the face of the earth. And all who remain of this most wicked family shall choose death rather than life, in all the places that are left, to which I have cast them out, says the Lord of hosts. All the things that the prophetic word describes we see to have happened in our own time, not to Jerusalem alone, which suffered these things from the Chaldeans and the Romans, but to the whole world, so that tears have dried up and everything has been filled with the bones of the dead. And because, according to ancient custom, they used to lay up gold and certain ornaments, whether of women or of men, in tombs, avarice broke these open too and dug them up, so as to expose them to the sky and the light. And therefore first the bones of the kings of Judah and of its princes, of the priests also and of the prophets and of all the people who had been in Jerusalem, were brought out of their tombs, lying open to the sun and the moon and to the sight of all the stars, so that they lay open to the gaze of those whom they had served after forsaking God, and, consumed, were dissolved into a dunghill and ashes; and if anyone of the multitude had been able to escape, to whatever places he came, he would prefer death to life and think destruction the remedy of an unhappy soul.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.4":["And you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord: Shall he who falls not rise again? And he who has turned away, shall he not return — or shall he not turn away — ? After such great evils he calls to repentance those who were able to remain, or before they come. He has threatened these things; he exhorts them to conversion and gives room for repentance. But what is written according to the Hebrew: and he who has turned away, shall he not turn away, signifies this: that he who has turned away from God, if he is willing to be converted to better things, can also turn the fury of God to better things, and withstand what is coming, and escape the blows by prayers.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.5":["Why then has this people in Jerusalem turned away with a contentious turning away? They have laid hold of falsehood and they would not return? 'The more I,' he says, 'called them to repentance, the more they withdrew from me, not so much from eagerness to sin as from eagerness to overcome me. For they have laid hold firmly of falsehood, whether idols or whatever perverse things are contrary to truth and righteousness, and they would not return' — he did not say 'they could not' —, 'but with all eagerness they have pursued wicked things'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.6":["I attended and listened; no one speaks what is good, there is none who does repentance over his sin, saying: What have I done? All have turned to their course, like a horse going with a rush to battle. Called back to repentance, they scorn to listen; nor is it enough for them to have offended against the Lord and to have spoken blasphemy, but all, like horses rushing in hot course to battle, do not reflect on their sin nor say: what have I done? By which we understand that these things are said either of the whole human race, because it is prone to vices, or of the time of the Savior, when all have turned aside, together they have become useless; there was none who did good, there was not even one. Hence he himself also cries out mystically: save me, O Lord, for the holy one has failed! But if these things are so, where are those who say that it lies within our will to be free of all sin? No one, he says, speaks what is good, because we shall render account even for an idle word on the day of judgment.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.7":["The kite in the heaven has known its time; the turtledove and the swallow and the stork have kept the time of their coming; but my people has not known the judgment of the Lord. For 'kite', which Symmachus translated, the Seventy and Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself, 'asida', Aquila 'herodiona' [heron]. And again for 'swallow' Symmachus translated 'cicada', which in Hebrew is called 'sis'. And for what we have put as 'stork', both Aquila and Symmachus translated 'agur', just as it is written in the Hebrew, for which the Seventy translated 'sparrows of the field'. And it is one and the same sense which is also set down at the beginning of Isaiah: the ox has known its owner and the ass the manger of its master, but Israel has not known me and my people has not understood; namely, that even small birds know their seasons and know when, hastening to warm places, they ought to escape the harshness of winter, and again at the beginning of spring come back to their accustomed regions. And here we ought to take 'heaven' for 'the air', which is above.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.8":["How do you say: We are wise, and the law of the Lord is with us? Truly the lying pen of the scribes has wrought falsehood. He speaks to the scribes and Pharisees, who boast of their knowledge of the law and, writing, write iniquity. And as for what he says:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.9":["The wise are confounded, terrified and taken, it is not that those who do these things are wise, but he calls them wise so that they may be condemned by their own judgment and, on the contrary, their wisdom may be proved to be folly, as Paul says: you who teach another, do you not teach yourself? And because they have cast away the word of the Lord, therefore there is no wisdom in them. In vain, then, do they boast of knowledge of the law who destroy their teaching by their works.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.10":["Therefore I will give their women — or wives — to strangers, their fields to heirs, because from the least to the greatest all are devoted to avarice, from the prophet to the priest all practice falsehood — or iniquity —. And they healed the breaking of the daughter of my people to ignominy, saying: Peace, peace, when there was not peace. They received the wages of their works, so that those who cast away the word of the Lord were themselves cast away by him. 'Their wives,' he says, 'and their possessions I will hand over to the enemy. And lest my sentence be thought cruel, let the hearer recognize the causes: from the least to the greatest all are devoted to avarice — for avarice is the root of all evils —, from the prophet to the priest. Those who ought to restrain others when they sin are the first to be given over to crimes, and all either do iniquitous things, while they plunder the goods of others, or at any rate practice falsehood, so that there is no truth in their mouth.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.11":["And after these things, like good physicians, they desired to heal the wounds of others with words, they who were themselves pierced through with the wounds of every shameful deed'. This we see daily in our own people too, as the Apostle says: you who preach that one must not steal, do you steal? and the rest. When these see sinners and rich men, they desire to heal the breaking of the daughter of the people of God, that is, of the Church, to the ignominy either of those who are deceived or of those who deceive others, so that they say to men covered with every crime: peace, peace, when there is no peace, while the war of their sins hangs over them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.12":["Were they confounded, because they committed abomination? Nay rather, they were not confounded with confusion, and they did not know how to blush. This is to be read Erōtēmatikōs [as a question], so that the sense is: 'Did they blush in their crimes, did they understand the abominations which they wrought?' 'By no means; but they burst out into such madness that they neither desired to amend their vices through confusion nor to confess their crimes in hope of pardon'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Therefore they shall fall among those who collapse; in the time of their visitation they shall collapse, says the Lord. These are the rewards of those who did not know how to blush: that those whose dignity was loftier than the peoples should be mingled in the ruins of the people. For from the least to the greatest all are devoted to avarice, and from the prophet to the priest all practice falsehood; and the time of visitation is the approaching captivity.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.13":["Gathering I will gather them, says the Lord; there is no grape on the vines and there are no figs on the fig tree; the leaf has fallen, and I have given them things that have passed by. What the time of visitation is, in which the transgressors shall collapse, he teaches more plainly, saying: 'Gathering I will gather them' — no doubt in Jerusalem —, 'so that they may be besieged by the Chaldeans for a long time and endure the evils of famine. When,' he says, 'the seasons pass, and autumn follows summer, and in winter the leaves of the trees fall, you will see everything from afar and from them you will take no food. There is no indeed grape on the vines for you, who do not take the fruit of the vines, and there are no figs on the fig tree for the besieged people, who sees the fruits of its own trees plundered by the enemy. The leaves,' he says, 'will fall when summer and autumn are past. For I have given them things which they would see pass by, so that with the greater grief they would lose the abundance of all things, which they were not permitted to touch'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.14-15":["Why do we sit still? Come together, and let us enter the fortified city — or the fortified cities — and let us be silent there — or let us be cast out there —, for our Lord has cast us off — or has made us silent — and has given us water of gall to drink; for we have sinned against the Lord. We waited for peace, and there was no good, for a time of healing, and behold, dread. The voice of the people is brought in, answering and confessing its vices and exhorting one another to enter the walled cities, or the one city Jerusalem; for the rest had already been taken. 'And let us be silent', he says, 'there, for our Lord has made us silent — for we have no confidence to pray —, or let us be cast away there or let us be cast out like dung. He himself has made us drink waters of gall, because we turned God, who is sweet, into bitterness. And the cause why we should drink such waters is plain: for we have sinned against the Lord and we waited for peace, we who had done no good work and thought that the time of remedy was at hand for us, when all things were filled with dread and terror'. A change of persons, especially in the prophets, makes the understanding difficult; if these are restored to their own places and causes and times, the things which seemed obscure will become plain.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.16":["From Dan was heard the snorting of his horses, at the sound of the neighings of his fighters — or of his cavalry — the whole land was shaken; and they came and devoured — or and they will come and will devour — the land and its fullness, the city and its inhabitants. So the people did not say, as the Seventy translated above: let us enter the fortified cities, but: the fortified city, so as to signify Jerusalem. And indeed he now also adds: the city and its inhabitants. Nebuchadnezzar is described coming with his army from Dan, through Phoenicia, at which place the river Jordan rises; and what the Seventy give as things to come, the Hebrew, in accordance with the truth of the event, records as already done.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.17":["For behold, I will send among you serpents, basilisks — or most wicked or, as the Seventy translated, deadly —, for which there is no charm; and they shall bite you, says the Lord, incurably, with the pain of your failing heart. Who those are who come from Dan, and whose snorting was heard, and who reduced the whole land to a wilderness, the same prophetic word shows under another figure, calling them 'serpents', 'most wicked' or 'deadly', and, as Aquila translated, 'basilisks', which are called in Hebrew 'sapphonim'; for which I do not understand what his second edition meant by saying 'watchmen', unless perhaps because of the similarity of the word. For which there is no, he says, charm. For in vain do they pour out prayers to God against the ancient serpent, the twisting snake, who have scorned the commandments of God. Let us use this passage against those who, scorning the sayings of the Savior, are handed over to the adverse powers.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.18":["My pain is above pain, within me my heart is mourning. For this, as we said above, the Seventy joined what is said to the preceding sentence, so as to put: and they shall bite you, says the Lord, incurably, with the pain of your failing heart. But in the Hebrew there is not so much 'pain', which in Greek is called odynē, as meidiama, which we can translate as the 'grimace' of a mouth contracted by pain and having the likeness of a smile. And these things are to be read Emphatikōs [emphatically] as from the person of God, lamenting the overthrow of Jerusalem and not bearing her miseries.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.19":["Behold the voice of the crying of the daughter of my people from a land far off! He describes the weeping and wailing of the city of Jerusalem when the enemy had entered.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Is the Lord not in Zion, or is her king not in her? Either the Lord himself is the king, or else 'Lord' refers to the Father, 'king' to the Son, according to that which is written under the name of Solomon: O God, give your judgment to the king, and your righteousness to the son of the king. But why the voice of crying is in Jerusalem and the crying itself comes from a land far off, the cause is manifest: because the Lord is not in her and her king has withdrawn from her.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Why then have they provoked me to anger with their graven images and with foreign vanities? 'But the Lord has withdrawn from them,' he says, 'because they provoked me, who was their Lord and king, to anger by the worship of idols'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.20":["The harvest is past, the summer is ended; and we are not saved. Again the people speaks, which was shut up in Jerusalem by a long siege, saying that the seasons have changed and the circle of the year has rolled round, and all their hope has been made void and has passed away.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.21":["Over the breaking of the daughter of my people I am broken and saddened, astonishment has taken hold of me. God answers that in the affliction of Jerusalem he himself appears afflicted, and, after the likeness of a man, he is broken by astonishment.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.8.22":["Is there no resin in Gilead, or is there no physician there? Why then has the scar of the daughter of my people not closed over? Not only in the present passage, but also in many other testimonies of the Scriptures we find the resin of Gilead put for repentance and medicine; and we find that God now wonders why the wounds of Jerusalem have by no means been healed and the scars have not yet drawn over the skin, because there are no prophets or priests by whose medicine they ought to be healed.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.1":["Who will give water to my head, and to my eyes a fountain of tears? And I will weep day and night for the slain of the daughter of my people. 'If I,' he says, 'were turned wholly into weeping, and there were not drops of tears at all but the abundance of a river, still I could not worthily weep for the slain of the daughter of my people. For so great are the evils that by their greatness they surpass all grief over them'. And this can be understood as much from the person of the prophet as from that of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.2-3":["For they are all adulterers, an assembly of transgressors, and they have stretched out their tongue like a bow of falsehood and not of truth; they have grown strong in the land, for they have gone forth from evil to evil, and they have not known me, says the Lord. Sinners pass from evil to evil when they exchange idol for idol and go over from sins to sins, or at any rate pass from the evil of the siege to captivity. And of the saints indeed it is said: they shall go from strength to strength, but of sinners: they have gone forth from evil to evil. And the cause of all their miseries is that they did not know the Lord, and that it is an assembly of men transgressing and arming their tongue like a stretched bow for blasphemy; and they have grown strong in the land, so that they deserve to hear: earth you are and into earth you shall go.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.2":["Who will give me in the wilderness a lodging place of wayfarers? And I will forsake my people and withdraw from them. LXX: who would give me in the wilderness a last dwelling, that I may leave my people and withdraw from them? 'It is better,' he says, 'to dwell in the farthest wilderness than to abide among such great crimes of men'. Hence the Savior also said in the Gospel: how long shall I bear with you? And in another place it is written: at that time he who understands shall sit and be silent, for it is a most evil time.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.4":["Let each one guard himself from his neighbor, and let him not have confidence in any brother of his, for every brother supplants with supplanting, and every friend walks deceitfully, and a man will mock his brother, and they will not speak the truth; for their tongue has learned to speak falsehood — or for they have taught their tongue to speak falsehood —. They have labored to act wickedly — or they have acted wickedly and have not ceased, so as to be converted —. Your dwelling is in the midst of deceit; in deceit — or usury upon usury and deceit in deceit; — they have refused — or they would not — to know me, says the Lord. This passage is to be used in a time of persecution and distress, when faith is either rare or none, when neither brother nor neighbor is to be trusted and his enemies are those of his household, when, according to the Gospel, the father will hand over the son and the son the father, and two are divided against three and three against two.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.5":["And what he adds: they have taught their tongue to speak falsehood or their tongue has learned to speak falsehood, shows that the habit of lying is in a manner turned into nature, and that they act with zeal so as to do wicked things.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.6":["And as for what follows: your dwelling is in the midst of deceit, in deceit, the word is properly directed to the prophet, that he dwells in the midst of a lying people — or, as the Seventy translated: usury upon usury and deceit upon deceit — and that they daily increase their crimes and by no means repent of their former deed, but heap new crimes upon past ones; doing these things, they act with all eagerness so as not to know the Lord, who commanded that these things should not be done.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.7":["Therefore thus says the Lord of hosts: Behold, I will melt — or I will test by fire — and try them; for what else shall I do in the face of the daughter of my people? Or for I will act in the face of the wickedness of the daughter of my people. Whenever we are subject to distresses, we receive evils from God and are tested by persecutions, so that whatever adulterated matter is in us may be smelted out by the heat of tribulations and miseries: for the silver of the Lord is tested by fire, proved in the earth, purified sevenfold.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.8":["Their tongue is a wounding arrow, it has spoken deceit; with his mouth he speaks peace with his friend and secretly lays snares for him. Shall I not visit upon these things, says the Lord, or on such a nation shall my soul not take vengeance? Every heretic who wounds the hearts of his hearers, of those who do not know what is written: keep your heart with all watchfulness, possesses a wounding arrow and speaks in deceit, and while with his mouth he promises peace to his neighbor, he secretly sets snares.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.9":["As for the following verses, in which he says: shall I not visit upon these things, says the Lord, or on such a nation shall my soul not take vengeance? He makes frequent use of them in this prophet, so that, when he has enumerated the evil works one by one, he infers that he does justly what he does.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.10":["Upon the mountains I will take up — or take up — weeping and lamentation, and over the beautiful places — or the paths — of the desert a wailing, because they are burned up — or they have failed — because there is no man — or human being — passing through. And they have not heard the voice of the possessor — or of substance —; from the bird of the heaven to the beasts they have migrated and withdrawn. When the Babylonian comes upon them and lays everything waste, the desolation of the province is prophesied: that there is wailing on the mountains, weeping in the desert or in the paths of the wilderness, that all things have failed and been burned up, and there is no one to walk through the land, all having been slain, and nothing has remained that can breathe and live. Hence for 'possessor' the Seventy translated 'of substance', which in Hebrew is called 'macne'; and 'substance' here is not taken for ousia, that is, 'essence', but for 'wealth' and 'riches'. And what he adds: from the bird of the heaven to the beast they have withdrawn and departed, shows this, as we have often said: that all things feel the wrath of God, and not only the birds of the air but also the fish of the water fail. According to tropology, weeping is taken up upon the mountains and lamentation over the beautiful places of the desert when the princes of the Church sin and nothing of the substance of God is found in her, nor is the voice of the Lord who possesses the Church heard through holy and apostolic men, and from the bird of the heaven to the beasts, that is, from those who can ascend on high down to the irrational and all the simpler ones, they have withdrawn from the assembly of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.11":["And I will give Jerusalem into heaps of sand — or into exile — and into lairs of dragons, and the cities of Judah I will give into desolation, because there is no inhabitant. When churchmen and all the teachers have failed, then Jerusalem is given into exile or into heaps of sand, so that heretical speech prevails in her and she is made a lair of dragons and her cities are reduced to a wilderness, and there is in her no dwelling of the divine word and of him who says: I will dwell and walk among them, and I will be their God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.12":["Who is the wise man who may understand this, and to whom the word of the mouth of the Lord may come, and who may declare it: why the land has perished and is burned up like a desert, because there is none who passes through? And the Lord said: Because they have forsaken my law, which I gave them, and have not heard my voice and have not walked in it, and have gone after the perversity of their heart and after the Baals, whom they learned from their fathers. The prophet asks whether he can find anyone of the wise in Jerusalem, and of those to whom the word of God comes and who can declare the will of the Lord and give the reasons why Judea has been reduced to a wilderness and, all having been slain, no one has remained to pass through it.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.13-14":["And he brings in the Lord answering and giving the reasons: because they had forsaken his law, which he had given them, and had not heard his voice nor done what had been commanded, but had gone after the perversity of their heart. Therefore we must trust not in our own will but in the Lord; for the heart in men is perverse, and from our heart come forth the worst thoughts. And after the Baals, he says, they have gone, whom they learned from their fathers. Bahal is the idol of the Sidonians and is of the singular number, but Bahalim of the plural number. Therefore neither the error of parents nor that of ancestors is to be followed, but the authority of the Scriptures and the command of God who teaches.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.15-16":["Therefore thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Behold, I will feed them, this people, with wormwood — or with distresses — and I will give them water of gall for drink, and I will scatter them among the nations which neither they nor their fathers have known, and I will send the sword after them until they are consumed. This can be prophesied both of the near time, when they were taken captive by the Chaldeans, and properly of this present time, when they have been scattered among the nations which neither they nor their fathers had known, and, divided through the whole world, have been fed with wormwood, or with necessities and distresses. And they have received water of gall for drink, which either signifies the greatness of their evils and the everlasting yoke of captivity, or else means that, through ignorance of the law of God, they are going to receive Antichrist instead of Christ. And the sword is sent after them, so that they are consumed even to destruction, or else a sword which divides them and does not allow them to be of one accord in evil, so that they perish in this, that they are evil.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.17-18":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Consider — or understand — and call the mourning women, and let them come, and send to those who are wise, and let them hasten — or let them speak —, let them make haste and take up a lamentation over us — or over you — and let our eyes — or your — bring down tears and our eyelids — or your — flow with waters, for a voice of lamentation has been heard from Zion or in Zion. On account of the coming captivity and overthrow of Jerusalem he bids the mourning women be called, who in times of grief are accustomed, with a tearful voice and beating their arms with their hands, to provoke the people to tears; for this custom remains to this day in Judea, that women with hair loosened and breasts bared, with a measured chant, stir everyone to weeping. And God joins himself to them with the feeling of one who suffers with them, or the prophet does, so as to say that whatever the people endures, he himself endures and feels. But as for what he adds: for a voice of lamentation has been heard from Zion, what this voice is follows at once.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.19":["How are we laid waste and confounded exceedingly? Because we have forsaken the land, for our tents have been thrown down — or we have cast away —. This is the voice of those lamenting Zion: how are we laid waste and confounded exceedingly? And at once they answer themselves and set out the causes of the devastation, saying: because we have forsaken the land through our own fault and sin, and our tents have been thrown down, which they once possessed as if passing by. Let the crowds of those who once believed say this too in persecution: that they have been laid waste and confounded for this reason, because they have forsaken the land of the Lord and deserted their tents.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.20-21":["Hear therefore, O women, the word of the Lord, and let your ear take up the speech of his mouth, and teach your daughters lamentation, and each woman her neighbor wailing, for death has come up through our windows, it has entered our houses, to destroy the little ones from outside, the young men from the streets! In the preceding section he had said: call the mourning women, and let them come, and send to those who are wise, and let them hasten; now he speaks as if to women present, to the condemnation of the priests and teachers and of all the men, so that, since these cease from teaching, the women may hear the word of the Lord and take up the words of his mouth and teach their daughters and their neighbors wailing and the causes of tears, for death, he says, has come up through our windows, it has entered our houses. Although this can also be understood spiritually, in that the death of sins enters through all the senses to the destruction of the soul, yet it can also be understood of the onslaught of the Babylonians: that so great is their strength and speed in fighting that they do not wait to unbar the doors, but climb up through the windows and roofs to lay waste the houses of Jerusalem. And the little ones perish who are outside and go out of Jerusalem, and the young men, to whom John also writes, who do not enter by the strait and narrow way that leads to life, but walk through the streets, of which it is written: how broad and spacious is the way that leads to death!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.21":["These are the windows of which Jeremiah also says: Death has entered through our windows; for the enemies desire to enter through all our senses, and to take the city of a good conscience, and to run about through our fortifications, and to destroy the houses which we have built for ourselves by good works. And they do all things entering through the windows like a thief, for they do not enter openly, that they may shoot in the dark at the upright of heart.\n— Commentary on Joel","For Jeremiah says: 'Death has entered through your windows.' Now we have five windows: that is, sight, hearing, taste, smell, and touch. If therefore I have looked at a woman to lust after her, I have already committed adultery with her in my heart; and death has entered through my window, that is, through sight. If the lyre or the organ or the reed-pipe as it were dissolves me, death has entered through my hearing. But if I touch something soft, and am unstrung by the softness of the body, death has entered through my touch, and the rest likewise.\n— Homily 51, on Psalm 140"],"JER.9.22":["Speak: Thus says the Lord: And the carcass of man shall fall — or the corpses of men — like dung upon the face of the region — or of the field — and like hay behind the back of the reaper, and there is none who gathers. The Hebrew word that is written with three letters, 'daleth, beth, res' — for it has no vowels in the middle —, if it is read, according to the context and the judgment of the reader, as 'dabar', means 'word'; if as 'deber', 'death'; if as 'dabber', 'speak'. Hence the Seventy and Theodotion joined it to the previous section, so as to say: to destroy the little ones from outside, the young men from the streets, by death. But Aquila and Symmachus translated lalēson, 'speak', so that God commands the prophet to speak what follows: thus says the Lord and the rest. And the sense is: when death has come up through our windows and has entered the houses of Jerusalem, and the little ones and the young men have perished from outside and from the streets, then their carcass, or the corpses of the dead, will be as it were for a dunghill upon the face of the earth and like stubble that is left behind the back of the reapers and, being useless, is not gathered. By these things he wishes to show that there will be so great a slaughter in Jerusalem and around the city that there will be no one to bury those who fall.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.23-24":["Thus says the Lord: Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, and let not the strong man glory in his strength, and let not the rich man glory in his riches; but let him who glories glory in this: to know — or to understand — and to know me, that I am the Lord, who do mercy and judgment and righteousness on the earth! For these things please me — or for in these is my will —, says the Lord. All the pride of men is taken away, while their wisdom and strength and wealth are reckoned as nothing, and this is the only glorying: that one should know and understand that he himself is the Lord, who does mercy and judgment and righteousness upon the earth; that all things are governed by the providence and righteousness of God, and that the things which seem to us to have no reason are full of righteousness and reason. For these things alone please God, and in these is his will. Where then are those who say that man is governed by his own will, and that the power of free will was so given that the mercy and righteousness of God were taken away? Hence also the Apostle, taking up this testimony, sets down the example: let him who glories, glory in the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.9.25-26":["Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and I will visit upon everyone who have the foreskin circumcised, upon Egypt and upon Judah and upon Edom and upon the sons of Ammon and upon Moab and upon all who are shorn about the hair, dwelling in the desert; for all the nations have the foreskin, but all the house of Israel are uncircumcised in heart. In part, the peoples of many nations, and especially of those that border on Judea and Palestine, are circumcised to this day, and above all the Egyptians and the Edomites, the Ammonites and the Moabites, and the whole region of the Saracens, which dwells in the wilderness and of whom it is said: upon all who are shorn about the hair, dwelling in the desert. Judah, therefore, who is mingled with the nations written above, ought not to glory because he does not have the foreskin but is circumcised by the law of God, since others too do these things who do not keep the commandments of the law and do not know the God of Israel. Nor is circumcision, which was given as a sign, of any profit unless the commandments of the Lord are fulfilled; just as the long hair also signifies a nation, not strength of body and the bravery of fighters. And what follows: all the nations have the foreskin, but all the house of Israel are uncircumcised in heart, has this sense: whereas, apart from the Egyptians, Edomites, Ammonites and Moabites, and the Ishmaelites dwelling in the wilderness, of whom for the most part a portion is circumcised, all the other nations in the whole world are uncircumcised in the flesh, the whole house of Israel is uncircumcised in heart, not in flesh; and this uncircumcision leads to death, for that one is of the flesh, this of the spirit.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.2-3":["Hear the word which the Lord has spoken concerning you, house of Israel! Thus says the Lord: Do not learn according to the ways of the nations, and do not be afraid of the signs of heaven, which the nations fear, for the laws of the peoples are vain! He speaks properly against those who venerate the heavenly bodies and suppose that the human race is ruled by those which have been set as signs of years, seasons, months and days, and that earthly things are governed by the causes of heavenly ones. And in saying: the laws or the statutes of the peoples are vain, he shows that all human wisdom is futile and has no usefulness in itself.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.3-5":["For he has cut wood from the forest, the work of the craftsman's hands with the adze; with silver and gold he has adorned it, with nails and hammers he has fastened it, that it may not come apart — or may not be moved —. They are fashioned after the likeness of a palm tree and will not speak; carried, they will be lifted, because they cannot walk. Do not therefore fear them, for they can do neither evil nor good. A description of the idols which the nations venerate. He has cut wood, he says, from the forest — the material of idols, then, is cheap and perishable — the work of the craftsman's hands — since the craftsman is mortal, mortal therefore are those things too which he fashions —; with silver and gold he has adorned it, so that by the gleam of both materials he may deceive the simple. And this error has passed down even to us, so that we think religion lies in riches. With nails and hammers he has fastened it, that it may not come apart or that it may not be moved. How great is the power of idols, which cannot stand by themselves unless they are fastened with nails and hammers? Fashioned after the likeness of a palm tree, they have the beauty of metals and are adorned by the art of painting, but they possess no usefulness that would yield any fruit to the craftsman. And they will not speak — for they have nothing living in them, those of whom it is written: they have a mouth and will not speak; they have ears and will not hear —; carried, they will be lifted. Stronger is he who carries than the things which are carried; nay rather, in him there is sense, in this a shape without sense. Do not therefore fear them, for they can do neither good nor evil. For many of the Gentiles are accustomed to worship some demons so that they may do no harm, and to entreat others so that they may grant benefits; whence also that line of Vergil: a black beast to Winter, a white one to the favoring Zephyrs. Whatever we have said of idols can be referred to all the doctrines which are contrary to the truth. For they too promise mighty things and fashion out of their own heart an image of vain worship; they boast great things, and to deceive all the simple they hold fast the gaze of the unskilled with thoughts golden, as it were, and with speech gleaming with the splendor of silver, and they are raised aloft by their inventors; in them there is no usefulness, and their worship belongs properly to the nations and to those who do not know God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.6-7":["There is none like you, O Lord; great are you, and great is your name in might. Who will not fear you, O king of the nations? For yours is the beauty; among all the wise of the nations and in all their kingdoms there is none like you. The wise and the foolish alike shall be proved; the teaching of their vanity is wood; silver wrapped — or drawn out — is brought from Tarshish, and gold from Uphaz, the work of the craftsman and of the hand of the coppersmith; blue and purple are their clothing; the work of craftsmen — or of the wise — are all these things. But the Lord is the true God; this is the living God and the everlasting king. At his indignation the earth will be shaken, and the nations will not endure his threatening. These words are not found in the Seventy, but in most copies they have been added from Theodotion's edition, and although according to the letter they seem clear, according to the anagōgēn [the higher, spiritual sense] they hold great difficulty. For none of those gods which are fashioned by heretical art is like the true God. All will fear him who is king of the nations; for the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom, and from it we advance to love. Yours, he says, is the beauty; in truth there is comeliness, in falsehood ugliness. Although the heretics seem to themselves to be wise according to the wisdom of the world, which is destroyed, yet in all the kingdoms in which they tear the Church apart there is none like you, as the divine word says: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the prudence of the prudent I will reject.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.8-9":["They are at once wise and foolish; their teaching, according to the quality of their talent, is either cheap and is compared to wood, or is like silver because of the charm of its eloquence; it is brought from Tarshish — either it is a region of India, as Josephus would have it, or else the whole sea is called Tarshish and has a likeness to the sky — and yet it is wrapped in the artifice of words; for if it were laid open, it could not deceive. And gold from Uphaz. Gold is called by seven names among the Hebrews, of which one is called 'ophaz', which we can call 'obryzum' [refined gold], so that it may gleam on the surface of the idols, which within is wood and of cheap material. They are covered with blue and purple, so that by their surface they may deceive the eyes, while they promise themselves the color of the heavens and the heavenly kingdoms; and yet all these things are works of the wise, who are thought wise in the world, but before God are foolish.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.10":["But the Lord our God is the true God — all those, therefore, are lies — and he himself is the living God — so those which are fashioned are dead — and the everlasting king — the shadows of the heretics prevail for a time, but in the long run they decay —; at his indignation the earth will be shaken — those who have earthly works and fashion earthly images — and the nations will not endure: by no means the people of the Lord, but the multitude of the nations, which cannot bear God's threatening.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.11":["Thus therefore you shall say to them: The gods who did not make the heavens and the earth, let them perish from the earth and from those things which are under heaven! These words are to be said to the false gods and to those which have been put together by artifice — for they made neither the heavens nor the earth —, words which the fellow workers of Christ, who are called God's and the Lord's, for the most part frame through the Church's teaching.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.12":["The Lord, who makes — or made — the earth by his might, prepares the world by his wisdom, and by his prudence stretches out the heavens. At his voice he gives a multitude of waters in the heaven, and lifts up — or brings out — the mists from the ends of the earth; he has made lightnings into rain, and brings out the wind — or the winds — from his treasuries. Every man has become foolish from knowledge; every craftsman is confounded in his carved image, for what he has cast is false, and there is no spirit in them. They are vain and works worthy of laughter; in the time of their visitation they will perish. Not like these is the portion of Jacob; for he who formed all things, he it is, and Israel is the rod of his inheritance; the Lord of hosts is his name. He who makes the earth by his might is God the Father. And he makes it by his might, that is, by the Lord the Savior; for Christ is the power of God and the wisdom of God. He himself is also the prudence in which he stretches out the heaven. For he himself spoke, and they were made; he commanded, and they were created, saying to the Son: Let us make man after our image and likeness!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.13":["At his voice he gives a multitude of waters in the heaven. For all the teaching of the Lord flows from heavenly things, as David says: A willing rain you will set apart, O God, for your inheritance, and it was weakened; but you have perfected it. And he lifts up — or brings out — the mists from the ends of the earth. The mists or clouds, which the Lord commanded not to rain a shower upon Israel, are brought out from the extremities of the earth; one of these mists said: For I think that God has displayed us apostles as last, as men destined to death, because we have been made a spectacle to this world, both to angels and to men. He has made lightnings into rain. For when the shower of teachings has come from heaven and has sated the parched hearts of men, then you will find flashes and the bright lightnings of wisdom. And he brings out the winds from his treasuries, in which are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.14":["Every man has become foolish from knowledge. Though it be Paul, though Peter, though Moses and Abraham, in comparison with God all their wisdom will be counted as nothing; whence also the foolishness of God is wiser than men. Every craftsman is confounded in his carved image, he who has fashioned images out of his own heart, and what he has cast is false. But if every man is foolish from knowledge, everything that he has made is foolish and false. And there is no spirit in them. It should be noted that in this passage too 'wind' and 'spirit' are called by one name among the Hebrews, 'ruha'; but he means the spirit of sanctification, which cannot be found in the minds of heretics.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.15":["They are vain and a work worthy of laughter. For who would not laugh when he has looked closely at the images of the heretics? For either what they say is rustic, and it is wood; or it is composed in fine speech, and it is found to be silver; or else it is feigned out of their own mind, and in vain they promise the image of gold. In the time of their visitation they will perish. Heresy prevails for a time, so that all the elect may become manifest and be proved; but when the visitation of God has come and his eye has looked upon foolish things, all things fall silent.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.16":["Not like these is the portion of Jacob, that is, of those who supplanted the Jews and daily destroy the heretics. But the portion of the saints is God, of whom the prophet says: The Lord is my portion. For he who formed all things, he it is, who made and molded all things, both in mind and in body, and Israel is the rod of his inheritance. Everyone who is 'upright in God' or 'discerns God with the mind' is the scepter of his inheritance, whose name is 'the Lord almighty' or 'of powers'; for this is what 'the Lord of hosts' means, which in the Hebrew is written 'the Lord sabaoth'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.17-18":["Gather out of the land your confusion, you who dwell under siege, for thus says the Lord: Behold, I will cast far away the inhabitants of the land at this turn, and I will afflict them, that they may be found. LXX: She has gathered from outside her substance, she who dwells in a stronghold, for thus says the Lord: Behold, I will supplant the inhabitants of this land and afflict them, that they may be found. Jerusalem is commanded to gather whatever substance she has outside into the most strongly fortified city and to prepare provisions for a long siege; for he is threatening no longer, as before, about a future and distant time, but now about the captivity close at hand, which is about to come at any moment. 'Gather,' he says, 'both from outside and from the land, that is, from the fields, your substance into confusion. For whatever you have is worthy of confusion; and although you prepare these things, learn the words of the Lord concerning them: Behold, I at this turn, that is, at this time, will cast or hurl as from a sling far away the inhabitants of the land, this one'. For this the Seventy translated: I will supplant and make fall; for this Hebrew word, that is 'cole', Aquila and Symmachus translated sphendonisō [I will sling], and the sense is: 'As from a sling I will hurl them with all force, and so I will cause them to be besieged, and so I will afflict and hem them in, that all may be found in the city and be unable to escape the evil'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.19":["Woe is me over my crushing, my most grievous wound! LXX: Woe over your crushing, your most grievous wound! According to the Hebrew, Jerusalem herself speaks, because she is grievously afflicted and bears an incurable wound; but according to the Seventy, the prophet speaks to Jerusalem and laments her over her crushing and her own wound.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","But I said: Truly this is my infirmity — or my wound — and I will bear it or it has seized me. Jerusalem herself speaks: 'Whatever I suffer, I suffer through my own fault; I understand my wound, which has seized me, or I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.20":["My tabernacle is established, all my cords are broken — or all my skins are torn apart —, my sons have gone out from me — or my sheep — and they are not; there is no one to stretch out my tent any more — or there is no more place for my tabernacle — and to raise up my skins. Jerusalem laments the overthrow of her city as so easy that you would think it not the razing of the walls and all the fortifications to the ground, but the taking down of a tabernacle and a tent. 'My tabernacle,' she says, 'that is, my dwelling, has suddenly been taken away. All my cords are broken — she keeps the metaphoran [metaphor] of the tabernacle — or all my skins are torn apart. My sons have gone out from me or my cattle'; this, added by the Seventy, does not stand according to the history; for in a long siege how could sheep and cattle have been carried off from Jerusalem, when, even if there had been any, famine had consumed them? 'And they do not', she says, 'subsist or they are not'; for they were not carried over into Chaldea, but a great part of them was killed and utterly destroyed. 'There is no one to stretch out my tent any more: there is no one to restore me and lay the foundations of my walls, which have been leveled to the ground'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.21":["For the shepherds have acted foolishly and have not sought the Lord; therefore they have not understood, and all their flock has been scattered. Through the metaphor of shepherds and sheep the guilt of the princes and the scattering of the people are described. For because the princes acted foolishly and did not seek the Lord, whom they ought to have sought with their whole heart, therefore they did not see the evils that were coming, or did not understand the Lord, and the whole multitude of Jerusalem has been scattered hither and thither.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.22":["A voice of hearing, behold, comes, and a great commotion from the land of the north, to make the cities of Judah a desolation and a dwelling of dragons — or a lair of ostriches and, as Symmachus translated, of sirens —; for this in the Hebrew 'thannim' is set down. The words of the prophet: 'Behold,' he says, 'the sound and roaring of the Babylonian as he comes is heard, and a great commotion, or earthquake, from the land of the north, to reduce all the cities of Judea to desolation, their inhabitants slain, and to make dragons and all venomous creatures dwell in place of men, or ostriches'; this animal too is at home in desolation, and is born and nourished in deserts; or else we can understand sirens, certain monsters and phantoms of demons. All these things, which both the past and the present discourse have described, let us refer to the times of the Church's persecution, when the tabernacles of the Lord are overthrown and every dwelling of the Church is reduced to desolation. And that all these things come to pass is the fault of the shepherds, who have acted foolishly and have not sought the Lord nor understood him; and therefore the whole flock has been scattered.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.23":["I know, O Lord, that his way is not man's, nor is it a man's to walk and to direct his steps. Let the new preachers blush, who say that each one is ruled by his own will, when this prophet too says: his way is not man's, and David sings in a lyric song: By the Lord shall a man's steps be directed, and he will greatly desire his way. Or else this is the sense: 'What we suffer from the Babylonians comes not from dread of them, but from our desert and from your indignation'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.10.24-25":["Chasten me, O Lord, but yet in judgment and not in your fury, lest perhaps you bring me to nothing! Pour out your indignation upon the nations which have not known you, and upon the provinces — or generations — which have not called upon your name, for they have devoured Jacob and consumed his place, and his beauty — or pastures — they have scattered or reduced to desolation. We read this passage in the Psalm: O Lord, rebuke me not in your fury, nor chasten me in your wrath! And the sense is: 'We deserve indeed all that we endure, and we deserve much greater things than we suffer; yet this I beseech, that you chasten me as a father, not as an adversary, that you amend me as a son and do not punish me as an enemy; for you chastise every son whom you receive, and through every pain and scourge you amend Jerusalem. But the enemies, who have not known you, and the provinces or regions, which have not called upon your name, ought by no means to feel your judgment, but your indignation. For you handed us over that we might be amended. But they made their yoke heavy; they did not spare the old man and they afflicted the little ones; they ate us up and utterly devoured us, and reduced Jerusalem to such desolation that they scattered everything of both public and private buildings and killed your people'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.1-2":["The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying: Hear the words of this pact — or covenant — and speak to the men of Judah and to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord, the God of Israel. It is not, indeed, set down in the heading at what time, or under which king and in which year of his, this prophecy was made; but we understand either that it is to be joined to the former prophecy and its time, or certainly that this word of the Lord came to the prophet some interval of time after the former prophecy. It is to be noted, moreover, that the word 'berith' Aquila and Symmachus always translated 'pact', the Seventy and Theodotion 'covenant'. Properly, however, the word is now directed to Jerusalem and to the men of the tribe of Judah.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.4":["Cursed be the man who will not hear the words of this pact, which I commanded your fathers in the day in which I brought them out of the land of Egypt, out of the iron furnace, saying: Hear my voice and do all that I command you, and you shall be my people and I will be your Lord. Not because of the privileges of race, not because of the injury of circumcision and the idleness of the sabbath, but because of obedience does God become the God of the people of Israel, and Israel his people. And here, indeed, he speaks as to servants, that they may please God; but in the Gospel the Lord says to the disciples: You, he says, are my friends, if you do what I command you. No longer do I call you servants, because the servant does not know what his lord does. But I have called you friends, because all that I have heard from my Father I have made known to you. And when they have become friends, from friends they pass over into sons; for as many as received him, to them he gave power to become sons of God. Hence he commands friends and sons: Be perfect, as your Father is perfect, enjoining likeness, not equality. And there it is obedience to the commandments, here likeness to God. And what he says: in the day in which I brought them out of the land of Egypt, out of the iron furnace, gives us to understand that the oven and the iron furnace, kindled, signify the greatness of tribulation and punishment, not some place of punishment made ready out of the material of iron.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.5":["That I may raise up the oath which I swore to your fathers, that I would give them a land flowing with milk and honey, as it is this day. The fathers are seen to receive when the sons receive, and the promise to Abraham is fulfilled in his posterity. A land moreover streaming with milk and honey we ought to take hyperbolically, for an abundance of all things, as in that line: let honey flow for them, and let the rough bramble bear amomum, and again: and he held back the wine running everywhere in streams; or else, tropologically, let us understand the land streaming with milk and honey as the Church of Christ, in which, as little ones and sucklings, we are brought up through faith, so that we may be able to take solid food.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","And I answered and said: Amen, O Lord, for which LXX: So be it, O Lord — for this is what 'amen' signifies — is what they translated. The Lord had said: I swore to your fathers that I would give them a land streaming with milk and honey, as is proved today by the facts themselves. The prophet, loving his people, takes up the voice of the Lord and longs that what has been given may be true and may endure forever. Hence he says: 'Truly, O Lord, you have fulfilled what you promised; or So be it, O Lord, that is: may what you have given remain always!'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.6":["And the Lord said to me: Cry out — or read — all these words in the cities of Judah and outside Jerusalem, saying: Hear the words of this pact and do them! The most merciful God frequently foretells what is to come, so that the mind that is hard to believe may at last be softened. And he foretells it both in the city of Jerusalem and outside in the countryside, so that those whose peril is common may have the same penitence.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.7-8":["For, testifying, I testified to your fathers in the day in which I brought them out of the land of Egypt, even to this day; rising early I testified and said: Hear my voice! And they did not hear nor incline their ear, but each one went away in the perversity of his evil heart; and I brought upon them all the words of this pact, which I commanded them to do. Up to this point it is not found in the LXX, and what follows: and they did not do it, was placed by them and joined to the end of the preceding passage, in which it is written: Hear the words of this pact and do them! And they did not do it. But what it means to rise early and frequently press the words of God upon them through the prophets, and to bring them out of Egypt, and often to admonish them and to say: Hear my voice, and that they went away after the perversity of their heart, and that afterwards he renders to them what they deserve to receive, we have already said before.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.9":["And the Lord said to me: A conspiracy has been found among the men of Judah and among the inhabitants of Jerusalem. They have turned back to the former iniquities of their fathers, who would not hear my words; and these, therefore, have gone after foreign gods to serve them. The house of Israel and the house of Judah have made void my pact, which I made with their fathers. For 'conspiracy', which we have translated following Symmachus, Aquila and the Seventy and Theodotion translated syndesmon, which we can call 'a binding-together'. Hence also Athaliah, when she discovered that a plot was being prepared against her in the temple, spoke with the same word: Conspiracy, conspiracy. Properly, however, Scripture employs this word when one strives toward wrongdoing not by a sudden and chance sin but by prepared plots and conspiracy, and when with like mind and one zeal they act so as to despise the commandments of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.10":["And he says that both fathers and sons, with one mind and an equal resolve, neglecting God, worshiped idols, namely both Israel and the house of Judah, that is, both the ten tribes and the two, whose rule was in Jerusalem, so that those who had one zeal in contempt of God may have an equal punishment in enduring captivity.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.11-12":["Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I will bring upon them evils from which they will not be able to go out; and they will cry to me and I will not hear them. And the cities of Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem will go and cry to the gods to whom they pour libations; and these will not save them in the time of their affliction. God does not hear in the time of need and distress, because they too would not hear the voice of the Lord. This Saul also suffered; for when, dreading the ranks of the Philistines, he had not deserved to receive the word of the Lord, he turned to the pythoness, so as to learn from idols what he ought to have wrung from the Lord by the insistence of prayer and of tears. Through these things we learn that, even if the Lord does not hear, we must by no means cease, nor flee for refuge to demons. They cannot help their worshipers; but we must flee to the help of the Lord, who, though angered, is quickly turned and changes his sentence, if those too with whom he has been angry are changed. And all that is now said pertains to the tribe of Judah and the city of Jerusalem, upon whom captivity is pressing.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.13":["For according to the number of your cities were your gods, O Judah, and according to the number of the ways of Jerusalem you have set up altars of confusion for pouring libations to the Baals. Let us read the books of Kings and of Chronicles, and we shall find that Judah and Jerusalem did far worse things than Israel, so that as many cities as they had, so many kinds of idols they also had, and as many as were the streets and street-corners in the city of Jerusalem, so many altars they had, to their own confusion, on which they sacrificed victims to the idols of the Baals.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.14":["You, therefore, do not pray for this people, and do not take up praise and prayer for them, for I will not hear in the time of their crying to me, in the time of their affliction! Jeremiah is commanded not to wish to entreat the Lord for those against whom the sentence has already been completed, lest his prayer should seem weak and should not be heard because of their own crimes. 'Do not', he says, 'pray for them, nor take up praise, striving, by recounting the clemency of the ancient history with which I have always had mercy on him, and by praising me, to change my sentence! For even if you do this, I will not hear those who are forced to ask me in the time of necessity'. From which we learn that it is in vain for anyone to ask on behalf of another, when the one for whom God is asked does not deserve to receive.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.15-16":["And the other half shall be split toward the west and the sea, with a very great chasm: this is the people of the circumcision, to whom God speaks through the prophet: Why has my beloved done abomination in my house? Shall vows and holy flesh take away your wickedness from you, or will you escape by these? This beloved did abomination in the house of God, so that she crucified the Son of God and heard: The Lord called your name a shady olive tree and a wooded beauty; and your branches became useless, which were broken off from the good root, so that we might be grafted in their place.\n— Commentary on Zechariah","What is it that my beloved has done many crimes in my house? Will holy flesh take away from you your wickednesses, in which you have gloried? A rich olive, beautiful, fruitful, comely, the Lord called your name; at the sound of a great speech a fire blazed up in it, and its thickets were burned up. He calls beloved and most dear the people of Judah, which set up idols in his temple and worshiped them, and thinks that in it it appeases the wrath of God if it sacrifices many offerings, and glories in the multitude of sacrifices, which cannot take away the wickednesses of sins. He compares Jerusalem, or the whole people of the Jews, to a beautiful and fruitful olive, which, exalted through pride, by no means acted humbly nor understood its creator and ruler, but, lifted up through pride and having spoken grandly, was set on fire by the fire of the Lord, so that its branches or thickets were burned up and reduced to nothing, that is, the whole people was destroyed by the sword of the adversaries. This sense is found in another place too, where it is said to Jerusalem: I planted you a fruit-bearing vine, wholly true; how have you been turned into bitterness, O alien vine? When its walls are torn down and the boar from the forest lays it waste and all the beasts devour its fruits, let us say this passage to the princes of the churches: What is it that my beloved has done many crimes in my house? Or else to the rich, who, while they plunder what belongs to others and do not take away the wickednesses of their heart, think that they merit the clemency of God: Will holy flesh take away from you your wickednesses, in which you have gloried? But now the names of those who make offerings are recited publicly, and the redemption of sins is turned into praise, nor do they remember that widow in the Gospel who, by casting two coins into the treasury, surpassed the gifts of all the rich.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.17":["And the Lord of hosts, who planted you, has spoken evil against you for the evils of the house of Israel and of the house of Judah, which they did to themselves to provoke me, pouring libations to the Baals. 'A rich olive,' he says, 'beautiful, fruitful, your Lord called you, and he planted you; but because at the sound of the great speech of God a flame came down upon you and consumed all your branches, therefore he who had planted you has now spoken evil against you, not out of the injustice of his sentence nor out of a sudden perversity of speech, but for the evils which the house of Israel and the house of Judah did to themselves and zealously carried out, pouring libations to the Baals, so as to provoke me to anger'. And although God can do what he wills, he gives his reasons, lest he should seem to act unjustly, according to that which is written: that you may be justified in your words and may overcome when you are judged.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.18-20":["But you, O Lord, have shown me and I have known; then you showed me their pursuits. And I was like a gentle lamb that is carried to be a victim, and I did not know that they had devised counsels against me: Let us put wood into his bread and crush him from the land of the living, and let his name be remembered no more! But you, O Lord of Sabaoth, who judge justly and test the reins and the heart, let me see your vengeance on them! For to you I have revealed my cause. This is the consensus of all the churches, that they understand these words to be spoken by Christ under the person of Jeremiah: that the Father showed him how he ought to speak, and showed him the pursuits of the Jews, and that he himself, like a lamb led to be a victim, did not open his mouth and did not know — 'sin' is understood, according to what is said by the Apostle: who, though he had not known sin, was made sin for us —, and that they said: Let us put wood into his bread — namely the cross into the body of the Savior; for he it is who says: I am the bread that came down from heaven, — and let us root him out — or crush him — from the land of the living! For this crime they devised in their mind, to blot out his name forever. But on the contrary, according to the mystery of the body he had assumed, the Son speaks to the Father and calls down his judgment, while he praises his righteousness and invokes God as the inspector of reins and heart, that he himself may render to the people what it deserves, and he says: let me see your vengeance on them — namely on those who persist in crime, and not on those who turn to repentance; of these he says on the cross: Father, forgive them; for they do not know what they do —, and he reveals to the Father and lays open his cause, that he was crucified through no fault of his own but by the crime of the people, saying: Behold, the prince of this world comes and finds nothing in me. The Jews and our Judaizers understand these words to be spoken in the person of Jeremiah, and they maintain that he endured these things from the people because of his prophecy of things to come and because of the impending evils of the captivity. But I do not know how they can prove that Jeremiah was crucified, since Scripture does not record it, unless perhaps they devised it and did not do it.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.11.21-23":["Therefore thus says the Lord to the men of Anathoth, who seek your life and say: You shall not prophesy in the name of the Lord, and you shall not die in our hands. Therefore thus says the Lord of hosts: Behold, I will visit upon them; the young men shall die by the sword, their sons and their daughters shall die by famine, and there shall be no remnant of them; for I will bring evil upon the men of Anathoth, the year of their visitation. This seems to run counter to the former view, by which we wish to prove that the things that were said were said in the person of Christ, and not in that of Jeremiah, who properly dwelt in the little village of Anathoth, which is three miles distant from Jerusalem. But if we understand the etymologian [etymology] of the town Anathoth, which means 'obedience', it will be clearly shown that by the men of Anathoth, who once obeyed the precepts of the Lord, all the Jews are meant, and especially the inhabitants of the city of Jerusalem, upon whom came the final sentence, that they should perish by the evils of the siege, by sword and famine and pestilence. But to free ourselves from all the trouble of interpretation, let us follow this rule: that all the prophets did most things as a type of the Lord the Savior, and that whatever was fulfilled in Jeremiah in the present time is prophesied of the Lord for the future.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.1-2":["Righteous indeed are you, O Lord, if I dispute with you — or for I will satisfy you — ; yet I will speak judgments to you: Why is it that the way of the wicked prospers, and it is well with all who transgress and act wickedly? You planted them and they have put forth root; they prosper — or they have begotten sons — and they bear fruit; you are near to their mouth and far from their reins. This disputation is indeed against all who act wickedly, and the meaning of Psalm 72 (73) is briefly summed up in it, in which the prophet says: How good is the God of Israel to those who are upright of heart! But my feet were almost moved, my steps had almost been poured out, because I was jealous of sinners, seeing the peace of sinners, and so on. But properly it is said against the heretics: though they are wicked, their way prospers, and they beget as sons those whom they have deceived in heresy, and they transgress and act wickedly, so that they despoil the Church, and, while they persist in the perversity of their opinion, they boast that they have been planted by God and have put forth root, have begotten sons and have borne fruit; and though they repeat the name of Christ again and again, they do not have God dwelling in them, according to that word of Isaiah: This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.3":["And you, O Lord, have known me, you have seen me and have tested my heart with you. Gather them like a flock to be victims, and sanctify them in the day of slaughter! 'It is no stumbling-block', he says, 'that the wicked, or all the heretics, flourish for a time; for you, O Lord, have known me and have seen me and have tested my heart with you'. Whom does God the Father know as he knows his Son? For no one knows the Son except the Father, and no one knows the Father except the Son, and he to whom the Son wills to reveal him. 'Although', he says, 'the heretics prosper, although they beget sons and bear fruit, and you are near to their mouth and far from their reins, that is, from their conscience, yet it is no small consolation that like cattle they are being fattened to be victims. You gather them into the city of Jerusalem, or into their own conventicles, so that like victims they may be slaughtered unto death, and may then be sanctified when they have been slain by the sword of the Church; for the slaying of the heretics is the salvation of those who had been deceived'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.4":["How long will the land mourn, and the grass of every region wither, because of the wickedness of those who dwell in it? Beast and bird are consumed, because they said: He will not see our last end or God will not see our ways. Whatever happens in the world, whether of good or of evil, does not happen without providence and by fortuitous chance, but by the judgment of God. The land is now barren, the grass withers: do you wish to know the reason? The wickednesses of its inhabitants bring this about, so that both the animals upon the earth and the birds of the sky are consumed, since all these things were created for the uses of men. They rose up to such blasphemy as to say that God is ignorant of their ways and does not know what each one is going to suffer. And when he says 'how long', he signifies that the wrath of God remains, because the mind of those who sin is not changed.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.5":["If, running with footmen, you have grown weary, how will you be able to contend with horses? If in a land of peace you have been confident, what will you do in the pride — or roaring — of the Jordan? 'If', he says, 'the frequent captivity inflicted by the neighboring nations has worn you out, by the Moabites and Ammonites, the Philistines and Edomites, what will you do in the face of the long captivity which is to lead you all the way to Chaldea?' And he compares footmen with horsemen, because in truth and according to the history all Persia and all Chaldea and the army of those regions delight in cavalry. But these nations which I have mentioned above, because of the difficulty of their terrain, are suited not so much to battle as to brigandage. And he keeps the metaphoran [metaphor] and says: 'If, running with footmen, you have failed from weariness, what will you do if you wish to match your running to horses? And if in your own land you had some confidence, what will you do when you have crossed the Jordan and endured its whirlpools?'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.6":["For even your brothers and the house of your father, they too have fought against you and have cried out after you with full voice; do not believe them when they speak good things to you! 'So far', he says, 'will you be covered by the most grievous floods of the Jordan, and so far will the multitude of horsemen coming against you from afar lay you waste, that your brothers too, the Edomites, and the house of your father, who were begotten of the stock of Lot, Moab and Ammon, will themselves, in the time of necessity and distress, fight against you and insult you. Therefore beware of being willing to believe them and of placing hope in kinship, on account of which they rage against you with greater hatred than enemies do'. This can also be taken of the Savior, that his brothers and the house of his father fought against him and cried out with full voice, saying: Crucify, crucify him; we have no king but Caesar.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.7-9":["Behold, your house will be left to you deserted. He had said this very thing long before, in the person of Jeremiah: I have left my house, I have abandoned my inheritance; my inheritance has become to me like the cave of a hyena. We prove that the house of the Jews, that is, that temple which before shone more majestically to the eyes, is deserted, because it lost Christ its inhabitant and, eager to snatch the inheritance, killed the heir.\n— Commentary on Matthew"],"JER.12.7":["I have left my house, I have abandoned my inheritance, I have given my beloved soul into the hand of its enemies. My inheritance has become to me like a lion in the forest; it has given forth its voice against me, therefore I have hated it. He who said in the Gospel: Rise, let us go hence, and again: your house shall be left to you deserted, here too, in the prophet, threatens the same things, and says that he has done what he is going to do. For the inheritance of the Lord is Israel, and the cord of his inheritance. And what he says: I have given my beloved soul into the hand of its enemies, is this: I have power to lay down my soul, and I have power to take it up again.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.8":["And the inheritance that was once the Lord's, the people of the Jews, became like a lion in the forest against him, when in the passion it cried out against him with one voice. And because it gave forth its voice against him, therefore he held it in hatred and cast it off, and she who was once beloved and most dear is now called hateful.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.9":["Is my inheritance to me a bird of many colors? Is it a bird dyed all over? Come, gather together, all you beasts of the earth, hasten to devour! LXX: Is my inheritance to me a hyena's cave? Or a cave round about it, upon it? Go, gather all the beasts of the field, and let them come to eat it! By the bird of many colors and dyed all over he means, according to the letter, the peacock. 'Such beauty', he says, 'did Israel have, and with such virtues was Jerusalem adorned, that there was nothing good that was not seen in her. Because, therefore, what was once my inheritance, that is, the people of Israel, has become to me like a lion in the forest and has given forth its voice against me, and I have detested it with all hatred, therefore come and gather together against it, all you beasts of the earth, the multitude of diverse nations, and devour her who has not known her Lord!' But if, as the Seventy and the other interpreters translated, it is read: Is my inheritance to me a hyena's cave?, let us refer it to the uncleanness of that nocturnal beast, which lives on the corpses of the dead and is wont to dig bodies out of tombs, and there is no uncleanness on which it does not feed. Such is Israel, offending its Lord and handed over to the bites of all the beasts.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.10":["Many shepherds have demolished — or have corrupted — my vineyard, they have trampled — or have polluted — my part, they have made my desirable portion a desert of solitude — or pathless —. They have made it — or it has been made — a ruin, and it has mourned over me or has perished. Let those hear these things who desire to be princes of the peoples, and who are to render account in the day of judgment not only for themselves but also for the flocks entrusted to them; for because of them the part of the Lord is trampled and polluted, so that where once was the lodging of God, there is the dwelling-place of beasts. Others, however, understand not the leaders of the people and the priests, but the princes of the enemies, who laid Jerusalem, that is, the vineyard of the Lord, in ruins.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.11-12":["And it has mourned over me; with desolation the whole land has been made desolate, because there is no one who considers in his heart. Upon all the ways of the desert the destroyers have come, because the sword of the Lord has devoured from the end of the earth to its end; there is no peace for all flesh. LXX: because of me with ruin the land has been ruined, and the rest. This, which we have set down: and it has mourned over me, is joined according to the Hebrew to the preceding verse, so that it reads: they have made it a ruin, that is, my inheritance, and it has mourned over me, bereft of my help. According to the Seventy, however, God says that because of him the land has been ruined and reduced to a solitude, because there is no one who considers in his heart, nor is there any peace for all flesh. For flesh cannot receive the peace of God; for the wisdom of the flesh is hostile to God, and those who are in the flesh cannot please God. Further, according to the Hebrew, all Judea has been made desolate for this reason, that there is no one who considers God in his heart, nor anyone left who can escape. For through all the ways of the wilderness the destroyers have come, that is, the hostile army, and the sword of the Lord has devoured from border to border, and there was no rest for those fleeing from the city. Hence it is said: there is no peace for all flesh.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.13":["They have sown wheat and reaped thorns; they have received an inheritance, and it will not profit them. LXX: You have sown wheat and reaped thorns; their clergy will not profit them. 'They expected better things', he says, 'and the worst came; they hoped for prosperity and suffered adversity; they received from the Lord an abundance of all things, which will not profit them'. According to the Seventy, however, all the heretics sow as it were wheat and reap thorns, while the Lord waited for them to bear fruit; and they did not do judgment, but a cry. This is said also to churchmen, who ruin the words of the Lord and his teaching by their evil manner of life, concerning whom it is added: their clergy will not profit them; for what can the name of bishop and of presbyter, or the rest of the ecclesiastical order, help them, when they are rather weighed down by their dignities, and the mighty suffer torments mightily, and the more has been entrusted to them, the more is required of them?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Be confounded at the fruits — or at your boasting — because of the wrath of the fury of the Lord — or at the reproach in the sight of the Lord —. Thus says the Lord against all my most wicked neighbors, who touch the inheritance which I distributed to my people Israel: Behold, I will pluck them up from their land, and I will pluck up — or cast out — the house of Judah from the midst of them. And when I have plucked them up, I will turn — or return — and have mercy on them and bring them back — or make them dwell —, each man in his inheritance and each man in his land. It is said to those whom their clergy and the ecclesiastical order will not profit, that they should be confounded at their boasting and at the reproach before the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.14-15":["And as to what he adds: against all my most wicked neighbors: according to the letter, the neighbors of the holy land are the Edomites, the Philistines, Moab and Ammon; but according to tropology, they are all the heretics, who are reckoned under the name of Christ and are neighbors rather than inhabitants of the holy land, who touch the inheritance of God and lay it waste; and it is said of them that they are removed from the midst of the land and that the house of Judah is taken out of their midst. These, when they have been plucked up and freed from the jaws of the heretics, will obtain the mercy of God and will be brought back into their inheritance and into their land.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.12.16-17":["And it shall be, if, being instructed, they learn the ways of my people, so as to swear in my name: The Lord lives, as they taught my people to swear by Baal, they shall be built up in the midst of my people; but if they do not hear, I will pluck up that nation with plucking up and destruction, says the Lord. If those brought over from heresy into the Church learn the ways of the people of God and swear in the name of the Lord, and not in the name of the idols which they fashioned out of their own heart, they will be built up by the Lord and will be part of his people. But if, having been brought over into the Church, they keep remnants of perverse dogmas and do not hear the words of the Lord, that nation will be plucked up from the midst of the people of God with a perpetual plucking up and destruction, so that no place of repentance whatever is left to them. These things we see daily and prove by the facts themselves: that the heretics feign the truth of the faith for this reason, that they may deceive all the simple, not that they themselves may be converted to the faith, but that they may draw the faithful into unbelief.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.1-11":["Thus says the Lord to me: Go and get yourself a loincloth — or girdle — of linen, and you shall put it upon your loins, and you shall not bring it into water — or it shall not pass through water —! And I got the loincloth according to the word of the Lord and put it about my loins. And the word of the Lord came to me a second time, saying: Take the loincloth — or girdle —, which you have got, which is about your loins, and arise, go to the Euphrates and hide it there in a cleft of the rock! And I went and hid it in the Euphrates, as the Lord had commanded me. And it came to pass after very many days, the Lord said to me: Arise, go to the Euphrates and take from there the loincloth — or girdle —, which I commanded you to hide there! And I went to the Euphrates and dug and took the loincloth from the place where I had hidden it; and behold, the loincloth — or girdle — had rotted, so that it was fit for no use. And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Thus says the Lord: So will I make the pride — or injury — of Judah rot, and the great pride of Jerusalem, and this most wicked people, who refuse to hear my words and walk in the perversity — or in the direction — of their most wicked heart, and have gone after strange gods, to serve them and to worship them; and they shall be like this loincloth, which is fit for no use. For as the loincloth clings to the loins of a man, so I glued to myself the whole house of Israel and the whole house of Judah, says the Lord, that it might be to me for a people and for a name and for praise and for glory; and they did not hear. The girdle or loincloth, which is joined to the loins of God, is the people of Israel, which, taken up from the earth in the likeness of flax and unwashed, had neither softness nor whiteness, and yet through his mercy clung to God. And when it had sinned — for linen and a loincloth of this kind is rational —, it was led across the Euphrates, that is, to the Assyrians, and there it is hidden, that is, in a manner swallowed up by the multitude of great and innumerable nations and reckoned as nothing. But after a long time the prophet himself, as a type of God, frees the people from captivity. And this people nevertheless, even after the return, did not keep the precepts of God, but, following strange gods, at the last even laid hands on the Son of God and wasted away in eternal perdition. Every holy man too is a loincloth of God, who, taken up from the earth and from the clay of the earth, is joined in fellowship with God and, in a manner, covers and girds with greater diligence the things in his Church that seem shameful, lest they lie open to the bites of the Gentiles and the heretics. This loincloth, if it touches water and crosses the streams of the Euphrates, so as to be soaked with the moisture of the Assyrian region, loses its former strength and rots and falls apart; and although it returns to the use of God, yet it cannot have its former beauty, not through the harshness of God but through its own fault, because they refuse to hear his words and walk in the perversity of their heart, or what seems right to them, this they do. But the divine word itself also explains why it set forth this likeness, saying: 'For as the loincloth clings to the loins of a human being, so I glued and made to cling to me the whole house of Israel and the whole house of Judah' — namely the ten and the two tribes —, 'that it might be to me for a people named and for praise and for glory; and for all these things they did not hear me, but followed their own vices'. Let him therefore beware who can say: But for me it is good to cling to God, lest perhaps through negligence he be separated from his loins and cross the Euphrates and be given into the power of the king of Assyria, and be lodged by no means in the most solid rock but in a cleft of a rock corrupted and vitiated, that is, in the filth and vices of the heretics, and come into such rottenness that he can no longer return to the use of the Lord and to being his girdle.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","The Lord has reigned, and he has clothed himself with the patriarchs and the prophets and the believing people. He has clothed himself in beauty: the patriarchs and the prophets were as it were the garment of Christ. That is the loincloth which is written of in Jeremiah: that is the loincloth which Jeremiah had about his loins. Do you wish to know that the saints are, as it were, a loincloth, the garment of God? Afterwards God himself speaks to Jeremiah: 'Just as you,' he says, 'placed that loincloth about your loins, so I too have joined my people to myself.' As, then, a garment is about a man, so the people is about God. But because this loincloth, because this beauty with which the Lord had been clothed, was exposed across the Euphrates in a cleft of the rock, and there rotted away, and was led off into captivity by the Assyrians—what does the Lord do? He is not naked, he cannot be without a loincloth, he cannot be without a garment: the former people having been lost, he makes himself a garment out of the people of the Gentiles.\n— Homily 26, on Psalm 98","Do you wish to know how we clothe the Lord? We read in Jeremiah: 'Take unto thee, he says, a girdle, and go, he says, to the Euphrates, and put it in a hole of the rock. And I did, he says, what the Lord had commanded me. And after some days I returned to the Euphrates, and behold, he says, the girdle had rotted, so that it was fit for no use. And the Lord spoke, he says, to me'. Attentively take heed. 'As, he says, thou hadst a girdle about thy loins, so also I had this people about me, saith the Lord'. Why have I said all this? To show that believers are the garment of Christ.\n— Homily 45, on Psalm 132"],"JER.13.12-14":["You shall say therefore to them — or to the people — this word: Thus says the Lord God of Israel: Every flask — or wineskin — shall be filled with wine. And they will say to you: Do we not know that every flask — or wineskin — is filled with wine? And you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord: Behold, I will fill all the inhabitants of this land, and the kings who sit of the stock — or the sons — of David upon his throne, and the priests and the prophets and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem with drunkenness, and I will scatter them, a man from his brother, and fathers and sons together, says the Lord; I will not spare — or I will not long for — and I will not yield nor have mercy, so as not to destroy them. The Hebrew word 'nebel' Aquila's first edition translated 'flask', the second 'nebel' itself, Symmachus 'mixing-bowl', the Seventy 'wineskin', Theodotion 'vessel'; and all this is filled not with oil, not with water, not with honey, not with milk, nor with any other matter of a liquid element, but with wine and drunkenness, showing that we are a fragile vessel, according to the Apostle, who says: But we have this treasure in earthen vessels, and that it cannot be that there should not be fulfilled in us what is written: For good does not dwell in my flesh, and again: For the good that I will, this I do not do, but the evil that I do not will, this I work, and then: Wretched man that I am, who will deliver me from the body of this death? And with this drunkenness, by which we forget the precepts of God, and with vices and sins, the whole human condition is filled, as the prophet says: No one living shall be justified in your sight — not by comparison with God, as the old and new heretics and the patrons of the heretics would have it, but with respect to his knowledge; for man sees in the face, God in the heart — and what sometimes seems clean to us is found filthy in his eyes: not only the ignoble crowd and the worthless rabble, but the kings of the churches of the stock or sons of David, who sit leaning back, with neck erect and paunch thrust out, upon his throne. The priests too themselves, the second rank in ecclesiastical honor, and the prophets, who seem to have knowledge of the Scriptures, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem are filled according to the variety of their sins, or Judah, as the Seventy added. And when they are drunk, they are scattered from fellowship with one another, and fathers are separated from sons and sons from fathers, so that they are defiled by diverse heresies and under the name of Christ fight one another with the sword and do battle against their mother who bore them, the Church. Hence he says: 'I will not long for them, but I will hold them in everlasting hatred; I will not spare and I will not yield nor have mercy, not out of cruelty of sentence but out of the truth of judgment. For those who slaughtered my peoples will themselves perish forever'. This can also be taken simply according to the history: that the kings and the priests and the prophets and all the people of Jerusalem were to be made drunk with the Babylonian cup and overwhelmed by the evils of captivity.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.15":["Hear and perceive with your ears, do not be lifted up, for the Lord has spoken! Because he had said above: Every wineskin shall be filled with wine, so that the kings too and the priests and the prophets and every inhabitant of Jerusalem should be filled with drunkenness, therefore he joins on and says: Hear and perceive with your ears, as much outwardly as inwardly, as much with the mind as with the body, and do not be lifted up through pride, considering your frailty and that there is no one who, according to the quality of sin, is free of this drunkenness. Hence he who lifts himself up against him through pride is destroyed and corrupted and is unworthy of the mercy of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.16-17":["'But if', he says, 'you refuse to hear me, secretly' — according to Aquila 'in darkness' — 'the soul will weep, mine' — or, according to the Seventy, 'yours' — 'before the face of pride, so that not even groaning and weeping may be free, lest the eyes of the victors be offended'. But we can also interpret this passage thus: the Savior speaks: 'Work while it is day; the night will come, when no one can work any longer'. Of this time there is also the prophecy of Isaiah: For the stars of heaven and Orion and all the adornment of heaven will not give light, and they will be darkened at the rising of the sun, and the moon will not give light. Zephaniah too agrees in the same words, saying: A day of tribulation and distress, a day of misery and perdition, a day of darkness and whirlwind, a day of cloud and gloom. Before, therefore, the time of judgment arrives and our feet stumble upon the dark mountains, namely the adverse powers that are set over torments and tortures, let us do repentance, lest, waiting for light, we be wrapped in the darkness of night; and let us know that, unless we do this, the soul either of God or of the prophet will weep before the face of our pride, who refuse to hear the words of God. Hence the prophet himself also says:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.16":["Give glory to the Lord our God, before it grows dark and before your feet stumble upon the misty — or dark — mountains! You will wait for light, and there will be the shadow of death, and they will be placed in darkness — or, according to the Hebrew: and he will set it in the shadow of death and in gloom —. But if you do not hear, in secret the soul will weep, mine — or yours — before the face of pride or injury. Those to whom the divine word had said: Hear and perceive with your ears and do not be lifted up, he now calls to repentance, that, before they are led to Babylon and their feet stumble upon the dark or misty mountains, they should give glory to God. Hence sinners too are often told: Give glory to God! And that Babylon and the whole region of the Chaldeans are called misty or dark mountains we read at the beginning of Isaiah, against Babylon, where it is written: Upon the misty mountain lift up a sign, which in Hebrew is called 'neseptha'. This therefore he commands: that, before they are led into captivity and feel the evils of servitude, they should do repentance, since, when they wait for light, they are to sit in darkness.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.17":["Weeping, Jerusalem shall weep — or weeping, it shall weep — and my eye shall shed a tear, and I cannot conceal my grief in silent groans; but the whole cause of the torments is that the flock of the Lord has been taken captive. Let us say to the Jews and to our own Judaizers, who follow only the simple and killing history: Unless you hear secretly, that is, in mystery or in darkness, which God has made his hiding place, and, according to Solomon, so as to understand a parable and a dark saying, the soul will weep, the prophet's or their own, before the face of pride, while through contumacy they resist God. Hence there will also be continual weeping and perpetual tears, because the flock of the Lord has been taken captive and corrupted by the true Nebuchadnezzar.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.18-19":["The cities of the south are shut up, that is, the tribe of Judah and Jerusalem, which, lying next to the desert, faces toward the south, and there is none who opens them, surrounded as they are by siege. All Judea has been carried away — or all Judah — with exile complete, or 'it has received what it deserved, and it has been fulfilled in it'; for so the Seventy translated. He raves in this place who understands the king as Christ and the mighty as angels or apostles, as if they were to take on the body of humility and sit in the dust and lose — either the king or the mighty — from their head the crown and the glory of strength. He also interprets the shut cities of the south as hell, as cities opened to no one, and says that all the glory of Judah was carried away when there was fulfilled in the passion: All have turned aside, together they have become useless; there is none who does good, there is not even one. The Hebrew word 'gebira' Aquila and Symmachus translated 'mistress' and 'lady'; the Seventy thought it was 'geburoth' and said 'the mighty'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.18":["Say to the king and to the mistress — or say to the king and to the mighty —: Humble yourselves, sit down, for there has come down — or there has been taken away — from your head the crown of your glory! The cities of the south are shut up, and there is none who opens; all Judea has been carried away — or all Judah has been carried away — with exile complete — or captivity —. The prophet is commanded to speak to King Jeconiah and to his mother, whom he calls lady and mistress or queen, that they should humble themselves and sit in the dust; for they have lost the royal dignity and are to be handed over to the Babylonian king.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.20-21":["Lift up your eyes and see those who are coming — or you who are coming — from the north! Where is the flock that was given to you, your glorious herd? What will you say when he visits you? For you teach them against yourself and instruct them to your own head. The inhabitants of Jerusalem are commanded to lift up their eyes and see the Chaldeans coming from the region of the north; and the city herself is questioned, and it is said to her: 'Where is the flock that was given to you, your glorious herd? Where is your people, which you had received from God? Where is that multitude, so great and glorious that you would have believed the crowd of a whole province gathered into one place? What will you say when he visits you, the Lord, with his rod, and hands you over to the Babylonian enemies, whom you yourself taught against you, either to your own head or from the beginning, so that you fled to their help and followed their idols — they who, under the pretext of your friendship, learned by what road they should come to you?' Let the negligent Church hear this, that she herself teaches her adversaries how they can seize her in a spiritual captivity and tear her herd with the cruelty of beasts.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.21-22":["Will not pains seize you like a woman in labor? But if you say in your heart: Why have these things come upon me? Because of the multitude of your iniquity your more shameful parts have been uncovered, your soles have been polluted — or have been dishonored —. 'While you do not know it, as a sudden childbirth seizes a woman, so a sudden captivity will seize you. But if you wish to complain and ask why you have been handed over to enemies, hear plainly that the multitude of your iniquity has done these things to you, so that, as of a harlot woman whose garments have been lifted, your ignominy has been uncovered and your fornications have been shown in public'. By these things we learn that, so long as sins are lesser, God acts patiently and waits for our repentance; but if we choose to join offenses to offenses and to make a heap of sins, our shameful parts will be uncovered and our soles will be shown to all, either in the present age or in the future. For there is nothing hidden that will not be made manifest, when that word of Daniel will be fulfilled: These shall rise to eternal life and those to reproach and to everlasting confusion.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.23":["If the Ethiopian can change his skin or the leopard its spots, you too will be able to do good, when you have learned evil. This testimony is used against the Church by those who desire to assert diverse natures, and they say that the blackness or the variegation of sins is so great that they cannot pass into whiteness and the beauty of a single color, not attending to what follows: and you will be able to do good, when you have learned evil. For whatever is learned is not of nature but of effort and of one's own will, which through excessive habit and love of sinning is in a manner turned into nature. But this, which is impossible for men, is possible for God: so that it is by no means the Ethiopian and the leopard who are seen to change their nature, but he who works in the Ethiopian and the leopard, as the Apostle says: I can do all things in him who strengthens me, Christ. Hence also in another place: I labored, he says, more than all of them; yet not I, but the grace of God which is in me, and: I live, now not I, but Christ lives in me. And again we read it written: What do you have that you did not receive? But if you received it, why do you glory as if you had not received it? For these reasons let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, nor the strong man in his strength, nor the rich man in his riches, nor the chaste man in his chastity, knowing that in all these things it is the power of Christ, not of those who glory in their virtues.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Chus in the Hebrew tongue is interpreted 'Ethiopian,' that is, black and dark, who has such a soul as he has a body, of whom Jeremiah says: 'If the Ethiopian will change his skin, or the leopard his spots.' So this Chusi signifies his black and Ethiopian enemy, Saul. And he says this: As the Ethiopian cannot change his skin, so this man too cannot change his character. Twice he came into my hands, I could have killed him. Whose blood I could have shed, I wished rather to overcome him by kindness; but his malice cannot be conquered. For just as the Ethiopian's skin cannot be changed, so too his malice cannot be changed.\n— Homily 3, on Psalm 7"],"JER.13.24":["And I will scatter them like stubble that is snatched away by the wind in the desert. This is your lot and the portion of your measure from me, says the Lord or and the portion of your disobedience against me. 'Because through excessive habit of evils they could not change their nature — not by the fault of the Creator, but by the pursuit of ingrained wickedness —, therefore I will scatter them like stubble snatched away by the wind into the wilderness, according to what is written elsewhere: like the dust which the wind casts from the face of the earth'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.25-27":["Because you have forgotten me and trusted — or hoped — in falsehood, therefore I too have bared — or will bare and will uncover — your thighs — and hinder parts — before your face, and your ignominy will appear, your adulteries and your neighing, the wickedness — or estrangement — of your fornication. The cause of the scattering of Jerusalem is that she has forgotten God and trusted or hoped in falsehoods. He who, apart from God, trusts in the things of the world forgets God. Hence his thighs or hinder parts are uncovered, so that he may see his own ignominy, and the things that ought to be behind may come to the front, and he himself may perceive what he has done, and his ignominy may appear not only to himself but to all. Your adulteries, he says, and your neighing shows not lust alone but the madness of lust, after the manner of mares that are eager for coupling, as in that line of Vergil: hippomanes, as the shepherds call it by a true name, a sticky venom drips from the groin. Let us ask Jesus that neither in the present age nor in the future he uncover our thighs and hinder parts, but that he blot out our iniquities and not let all our crimes appear.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.25":["And he makes an apostrophe to Jerusalem herself: that this is her lot and this the portion which she herself has chosen, a measure heaped up and full and running over, or the portion of her disobedience, by which she would not acquiesce to the Lord; for with what measure she has measured, it will be measured back to her.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.13.27":["Upon the hills in the field I have seen your abominations. Woe to you, Jerusalem! You will not be cleansed — or because you have not been cleansed — after me; how long yet? 'Not only in the midst of the city of Jerusalem but on every hill and in all regions I have seen your idols'; hence it is said to her: 'Woe to you, Jerusalem, because you have not been cleansed after me, so that, though you boasted that you followed my footsteps and paraded the confession of my name, yet you have never been purified, because you have forgotten me and hoped in falsehoods'. Hence he rebukes her and says: How long yet? And the sense is: 'How long shall I wait for you? How long shall I bear it? How long will you forget me to the end and despise my precepts?' He commits fornication on the hills and in the fields and is never cleansed, who, with neck erect through pride, is not humbled under the mighty hand of God but trusts in his own crimes and vices.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.1":["The word of the Lord which came to Jeremiah concerning the words of the drought. All things are in accord with the wrath of God; hence the sun also sets at noon upon sinners, and the moon and the other stars do not give their light, and we must suppose that in the time of the siege there were no rains, so that the besieged endured a dearth of water. For the city uses a single spring, that of Siloam, and this not a perennial one, and to the present day a dearth of rains causes a lack not only of crops but also of water to drink.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.2":["Judea has mourned — or Judah — and its gates have fallen — or have been made empty — and have been darkened — or have been made dark — upon the earth, and the cry of Jerusalem has gone up. Its elders sent their lesser ones — or younger ones — to the water; they came to draw — or to the wells — and did not find water; they brought back their vessels empty. They were confounded and afflicted — or they blushed — and covered their heads; because of the desolation of the land — or and the works of the land, because they have failed —, because no rain came — or there was no rain — upon the land, the farmers were confounded, they covered their heads. In the time of drought, when the multitude suffers a famine of hearing and learning the word of God, Judea mourns, 'boasting' that it formerly had the worship of God and the 'confession' of the true faith; and its gates are either emptied or fall, which we ought to refer to the senses, through which instruction is conceived by the soul. Then all things are darkened and wrapped in darkness, and in Jerusalem it is by no means reason and the word of teaching that reign, but clamor and confusion.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.3-4":["The elders also, who ought themselves to go to draw the waters, send the younger ones, in whom there are no gray hairs of wisdom, and therefore they come to the wells and do not find the waters which, as the history relates, the patriarchs found. They bring back their vessels empty, the younger ones, that is, not because there were no waters, but because they could not find them. They were confounded and afflicted — or they blushed — and covered their heads — because they could not say with the Apostle: 'But we all, with unveiled face, behold the glory of the Lord' — because of the desolation of the land or and the works of the land have failed, through which we advance to the knowledge of God. And the cause is plain: because no rain came upon the land. For the clouds were commanded not to rain a shower upon it. The farmers also, one of whom says: You are God's husbandry, God's building and: We are God's fellow workers, cover their head and are confounded, understanding that without the grace and help of God they strive in vain.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.5-6":["For the hind also — or the hinds — gave birth in the field — or they gave birth — and forsook — or they forsook —, because there was no grass. And the wild asses stood on the crags, they drew in the wind like dragons; their eyes failed, because there was no grass or hay. Great is the barrenness when even the hinds give birth in the field and forsake their young because there is no grass or hay, so that they, which by the scent of their nostrils draw serpents out of their holes and kill venomous creatures, do not enjoy the food of grace; and when the wild asses also, of which it is written: Who has sent out the wild ass free into the wilderness?, leaving the plains and the open country, stand on the crags and cannot run, and draw in the wind after the likeness of dragons, and their eyes fail and cannot see the clear light, since rational food has been withdrawn. This drought often happens in the churches, when both stags and wild asses are found among the peoples and waste away through a dearth of teachers: there are those who could learn, and there are none who could teach.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.7-8":["If our iniquities have answered — or have withstood — us, O Lord, act for your name's sake, for many are our turnings away — or our sins —. Against you we have sinned, O expectation of Israel, his Savior in the time of tribulation. If we are in doubt why the rains do not come down upon the land, why all things wither with dryness, let us hear: our iniquities have withstood us; 'therefore, O Lord, not according to our works but according to your holy name, overcome our many turnings away. For against you we have sinned, whom the secrets of the heart do not deceive, and we wait for you, who are the true hope and expectation of Israel and save him in the time of tribulation, according to that which is written: To the Lord I cried when I was in tribulation, and he heard me'. Let us too say, in the time of drought and dearth of waters: 'Against you we have sinned and have done evil before you; we wait for your coming, you who save Israel not by its own merit but by your clemency.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.8-9":["Why will you be like a settler in the land and like a traveler turning aside to lodge? Why will you be like a wandering man or a mighty one who cannot save? LXX: why have you become like a stranger in the land and like a native turning aside to lodge? Will you be like a man asleep and like a man who cannot save? The Jews understand this passage thus: 'Why do you separate yourself from your people and, like a traveler, for the refreshment of a single hour not care what lodging you use, but, being about to pass on to other places, not save your people and forsake the temple once renowned?' Our people, however, think that it is said of the future dispensation of Christ: that he was to be a pilgrim in the land and to use the hospitality of the earth for a short time, and, like a man passing through and strong, having left Israel, to make for the multitude of the Gentiles, so as to pass from place to place, from people to people, from the temple to the Church.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.9":["But what is said according to the Seventy: Will you be like a man asleep and like a man who cannot save? sets down a likeness, not the truth of the matter, according to that which is written: Arise, why do you sleep, O Lord?, not that the Lord sleeps, of whom it is said: He who keeps Israel will not slumber nor sleep, but that he seems to sleep to those whom he forsakes. Finally, in what follows too, it is not written: 'a man asleep', who cannot save, but: 'like a man'; in both, anthrōpopathōs [in human terms].\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","But you are in us, O Lord, and your name has been invoked upon us; do not forsake us or do not forget us! 'You who among the Jews will be like a stranger and a traveler and a wandering man, forsaking your ancient dwelling, dwell in us, and your name has been invoked upon us, so that we are called Christians; therefore do not forsake us nor forget us, to whom the mouths of all the prophets have sung of your future coming'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.10":["Thus says the Lord to this people: because it has loved — or they have loved — to move its feet and has not rested — or they have not spared — and it has not pleased the Lord — or and God was not well pleased in them —, now he will remember their iniquities and will visit their sins. When the people says: 'Why have you become like a stranger and a traveler and a wanderer, so as to forsake your dwelling?', the Lord answers the people that was once his own: 'Do you wish to know the reason? Listen to what is said: because the people has loved to move its feet and has not withdrawn them from the fetter of sins, or has not rested and could not stand, therefore I too have forsaken it, and I have no good pleasure in it'. He, then, who long delayed and through patience was unwilling to punish sinners, since they persisted in wickedness, will remember their iniquities and will visit the sins of those who are, as it were, sick and do not perceive God, so that they may cease to sin any more. But it should be noted in the holy Scriptures that it is always the feet of sinners that are moved, while to the saints it is said, with Moses: But you, stand here with me, and elsewhere it is written: Praise the Lord, you servants, who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.11-12":["And the Lord said to me: Do not pray for this people for good! When they fast, I will not hear their prayers, and if they offer burnt offerings and victims, I will not accept them, for by the sword and by famine and by pestilence I will consume them. It is foolish to pray for one who has sinned unto death, as John says: There is a sin unto death; I do not say that one should pray for that. All iniquity is sin, and there is a sin not unto death. Fasts and prayers and victims and burnt offerings profit only when we withdraw from vices and weep for our former sins; but if, persisting in our crimes, we think that we can buy the ears of God with vows and sacrifices, we greatly err, judging God to be unjust. For he who has once been destined for the sword and famine and pestilence cannot be rescued by any prayers. Hence the prophet too is told not to ask in vain for what he cannot obtain.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.13":["And I said: Ah, ah, ah, Lord God — or you who are, Lord God —, the prophets say to them: You shall not see the sword, and famine shall not be among you, but he will give you true peace in this place. And the Lord said to me: The prophets prophesy falsely in my name. I did not send them, and I did not command them, nor did I speak to them; a lying vision and deceitful divination and the seduction of their own heart they prophesy to you. Let the teachers hear this who promise prosperity to those who sin and persist in their vices, who say to the rich: 'You shall not see the sword of God's torments, and famine shall not be among you. For you will be filled with the words of God, and the Lord will give you most true peace in the place of the Church, or of Jerusalem'. But that, according to the Hebrew, he said three times: Ah, ah, ah, answers to what came before, where the Lord had threatened, saying: by the sword and by famine and by pestilence I will consume them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.14":["Since, then, the prophets, nay rather the false prophets, promised false things, the Lord therefore spoke through Jeremiah: 'Do not listen to the words of the false prophets, who were not sent by me but came of their own will! Hence they are by no means to be called prophets but diviners, they who speak seduction to the people'. For it is much better to amend sins through fear of punishments than, through hope of prosperity, to fall under the divine sentence.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.15-16":["Therefore thus says the Lord concerning the prophets who prophesy in my name, whom I did not send, saying: Sword and famine shall not be in this land: by sword and famine those prophets shall be consumed. And the peoples to whom they have prophesied shall be cast out in the streets of Jerusalem because of famine and sword, and there shall be none to bury them, they and their wives, their sons and their daughters, and I will pour out upon them their own evil or their own evils. Let the false prophets beware, who by promising prosperity trip up the people of God, lest they themselves perish and the deceived people be destroyed by a like ruin, and they lie in the streets of Jerusalem and, trampling on the precepts of the Lord, perish by famine and sword, and there be none to bury them nor to cover their disgrace with the dust of repentance. For the prophets themselves and the people, and their wives, and sons and daughters, and their whole generation will be on the dunghill without any undertaker. How many lie in the streets of Jerusalem! How many do we see unburied receiving their own evils, which they suffer as the Lord pours them out!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.17":["And you shall say this word to them: Let my eyes shed a tear — or let your eyes shed tears — through night and day, and let them not be silent — or let them not cease —, for the virgin daughter of my people has been crushed with a great crushing, with a most grievous blow, exceedingly. This passage is understood in two ways: either that God himself laments his people and his eyes do not cease to weep, or at any rate that he commands that the eyes of the peoples should flow with tears; and that what must be lamented is no light thing, since the virgin daughter of his people has been struck with the greatest crushing and an intolerable blow. Others think that these things are said in the person of the prophet.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.18":["If I go out into the fields, behold, those slain by the sword, and if I enter the city, behold, those wasted with famine — or the pain of famine —! The prophet also and the priest have gone off into a land which they did not know. A just cause for lamenting, that the virgin has been crushed, the daughter struck and the people destroyed. 'For if,' he says, 'I wish to go outside, I shall see the slain; if to enter the city, I shall behold those wasted and scarcely clinging to their bones through the necessity of famine'. And what wonder is it to say this of the common people and the ignoble crowd, when the prophets also and the priests, who prophesied prosperity to others and who ought to have opened the commandments of the law, themselves went into a land which they did not know and endured the evil of captivity? Let our prophets and priests hear this: that because of their negligence there is no security either within or without, since they both scandalize those who are without and allow those who are within to perish by famine, and those who were the authors of sinning become companions in the torments.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.19":["Have you utterly cast off Judah, or has your soul abhorred Zion? Why then have you struck us, so that there is no healing? We waited for peace, and there is no good, and for a time of healing, and behold, turmoil! The prophet marvels that the Lord has so suddenly cast off Judah and Jerusalem, that is, the kingdom of the two tribes, in which were the religion of God and the ceremonies of the temple, and has struck them with so great a blow that no medicine can be applied. 'We waited', he says, 'for peace and for a time of healing, and there is no good at all, but, on the contrary, turmoil, so that where formerly there had been the worship of God and tranquility, there all things were filled with seditions and the roar of the enemy'. If ever, therefore, our Zion too and our Judah is cast off and the soul of God abhors it, let us by no means marvel, but rather let us say what follows:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.20":["We have acknowledged, O Lord, our impieties, the iniquity of our fathers, because we have sinned against you. 'Both we', he says, 'and our fathers, with the same madness, neglected the precepts of God, and the measure of our forefathers has been filled up in us, so that whatever had been lacking in them was completed by our heap'. Hence it is also said of Judas: Let the iniquity of his fathers return to remembrance in the sight of the Lord, and let not the sin of his mother be blotted out! Let it be over against the Lord always, and let the memory of them perish from the earth!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.21":["Do not give us over to reproach for your name's sake, nor bring upon us the dishonor of the throne of your glory! Remember, do not make void your covenant with us! Let us consider the throne of God's glory to be not only the temple of Judea, which has often been destroyed, but every saint, in whom, according to that which is written: You have dashed his throne to the ground, it is dashed down and destroyed when he has offended God by a multitude of sins; but nevertheless he who has perished by his own fault is upheld by the clemency of the Lord, which is exchanged for the severity of the sentence if the Lord makes void his pact, by which he has promised that we shall be saved.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.14.22":["Are there among the graven images of the nations any that rain, or can the heavens give showers? Are you not the Lord God, whom we have awaited? For you have made all these things. After many and varied discourses he returns to the title of the prophecy, in which it is written: the word of the Lord which came to Jeremiah concerning the words of the drought. So what he says is this: 'Since the images of demons cannot rain, nor can the heavens of themselves give their shower, therefore you, O Lord our God, whom we have always awaited, to whom we have turned our hopes and vows, you give your rain! For all things are yours, and whatever is good cannot be given without you, whose it is'. Let us say this also against the heretics, who cannot bestow the rain of doctrines, and who, though they promise that they are heavens and boast that it is written of them: The heavens declare the glory of God, nevertheless cannot give the showers of doctrine. For it is God alone who instructs his people and bestows the diversities of grace on those who await him.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.1":["And the Lord said to me: if Moses and Samuel stand before me — or against me —, my soul is not toward this people. For we read that these men withstood the wrath of the Lord on behalf of the people and turned aside a sentence that was already impending. 'Even if', he says, 'they stand either in my sight or against me' — to one of whom God said: Let me alone, and I will strike this people —, 'yet I will not hear, because the crimes of the offending people are complete'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Cast — or send — them out from my face, and let them go forth! Sinners withdraw from God not in place but in will, although we read that both Adam and Cain were cast out from the face of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.2-3":["And if they say to you: where shall we go forth?, you will say to them: thus says the Lord: those for death, to death, and those for the sword, to the sword, and those for famine, to famine, and those for captivity, to captivity! And I will visit upon them four kinds, says the Lord: the sword for slaying, and dogs for tearing, and the birds of the sky and the beasts of the earth for devouring and scattering. The prophecy of Ezekiel also shows four plagues to which the people of the Jews was handed over: the sword, pestilence from famine, beasts, captivity. Among the beasts, moreover, understand the dogs and the birds as well, to which they were handed over for their bodies to be torn and devoured and scattered. For it could not happen that, once the Creator was neglected, the whole creation should not rise up against sinners.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.4":["And I will give them over to ferment — or commotion and distresses — to all the kingdoms of the earth because of Manasseh, son of Hezekiah, king of Judah, for all that he did in Jerusalem. We read in the book of Chronicles that Manasseh, after his captivity and repentance, returned to Jerusalem and reigned; but just as the merits of the saints descend to their posterity, as in the case of David and the rest, so the outrages of sinners reach their posterity, if their children and grandchildren have done similar things. But what he says: 'I will give them over to ferment or commotion and distresses of the whole earth', was both fulfilled in part under the Babylonians and is now being fulfilled in full, since the impious people imitated a most wicked king, one who filled Jerusalem from gate to gate with the blood of the righteous. From this we learn that peoples are very often destroyed by the crime of kings and princes and of those set over them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.5":["For who will have pity on you, Jerusalem? or who will grieve for you? or who will go to ask for your peace? For when God has been offended no one can intercede for the outrages of sinners, because a creature cannot be as merciful as the Maker, nor can a stranger spare outsiders as the Lord spares his own.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.6":["You have forsaken me, says the Lord; you have gone backward. The reason is given why no one should have pity on Jerusalem, or grieve for her, or entreat for her peace: that, although according to the Apostle she ought, forgetting what lies behind, to have stretched forward to what lies before, she on the contrary turned back and longed for the meats of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","And I will stretch out my hand over you and kill you; I have labored, being entreated — or entreating —, for which the Seventy translated: I will by no means forgive them any longer. An outstretched hand is the sign of one who strikes; the killing of sinners signifies wrath brought to completion. But what he added: I have labored, being entreated or entreating, has a twofold sense: that God has already grown faint from often forgiving them, and that he is weary from always calling them to salvation.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.7":["And I will scatter them with a winnowing fan in the gates of the land — or of my people —; I have killed and destroyed my people, and yet they have not turned back from their ways. 'What does it profit that I am entreated so often, when they do not turn back from their most wicked ways nor do repentance? For I scattered them as with a winnowing fan, to purge my threshing floor, and I scattered them in the gates of the land, so that in a manner they might tread the thresholds of hell, and I have killed and destroyed my people, so that, compelled by the necessity of evils, they might avoid the evils that were impending'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.8":["Her widows have been multiplied to me above the sand of the sea; I have brought upon them, upon the mother of the young man, a destroyer at noon; I have sent terror suddenly upon the cities. God desires to save sinners by various remedies, so that those who had despised him when he coaxed them may fear him when he threatens. The widows were multiplied above the sand of the sea when their husbands had been slain; the mothers, having lost their children, felt the destroyer not at night and through ambushes, but in broad daylight, to show the open force of a stronger adversary. 'I have sent', he says, 'upon the cities' — no doubt those of Judah and of the sinful people — 'terror suddenly, so that, the more sudden the evil was, the more difficult escape would be'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.9":["She is weakened — or has cast away or has been made empty —, who bore seven — or very many —, her soul has fainted; the sun has set for her while it was still day — or midday —; she is confounded and has blushed. And her remnant I will give to the sword in the sight of their enemies, says the Lord. We have often said that the Hebrew word 'saba' means either 'seven' or 'oath' or 'very many'. Hence the translation also differs, Aquila, the Seventy and Theodotion translating 'seven', Symmachus 'very many'. She, then, who was rich in children was suddenly made childless, and perished in broad daylight, and was confounded in her own desolation. 'But the remnant of the people', he says, 'I will hand over to the sword, so that no one may escape death and the wrath of God'. Others refer it to the synagogue, which was weakened so that the multitude of the Church might grow, according to what is written: The barren has borne seven — or very many — and she who had many children has been weakened. Hence also there has set for her the sun of righteousness, in whose wings is healing, and therefore she is covered with eternal confusion, losing her people by the spiritual sword.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.10-11":["In place of what we translated according to the Hebrew: all curse me, up to the place where it is written: in the time of tribulation against the enemy, I have found it written thus in the common edition: My strength has failed in those who curse me. So be it, Lord, with them being directed, if I did not stand by you in the time of their affliction and in the time of their tribulation for good against the enemy! And the sense is: 'My strength has failed in those who curse me; for they do not understand my power, which is made perfect in weakness, and the more they curse me, the more my strength fails in them'. And either the prophet or the Lord joins to this and says: So be it, Lord, with them being directed! That is: 'Let the curses which the enemies speak come upon me, and let them be directed into good, if in the time of their tribulation and distress, when the enemy was laying them waste and hastening to capture them, I did not stand in your sight and entreat you for them and say: Father, forgive them; for what they do, they do not know'. But we also often find Jeremiah in this book entreating for the people.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.10":["Woe is me, my mother, why did you bear me a man of strife — or of judgment —, a man of discord — or who am to be judged — in the whole earth? This can be understood synekdochikōs [by synecdoche] of Jeremiah, since he was judged not in the whole world but in the land of Judea. But it truly befits the Lord, the Savior, who says in the Gospel: For judgment I came into this world, that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind, of whom it is written: Behold, this one is set for the fall and the rising of many in Israel, and for a sign that will be contradicted. For which of the philosophers, which of the Gentiles, which of the heretics does not judge Christ, laying down laws for him concerning his birth and passion and resurrection and substance? Nor is it a wonder that Christ, according to the reality of the body he assumed, says: Woe is me, my mother, since in another place too what is said plainly suits his person: Woe is me, for I have become like one who gathers stubble at harvest, and like a cluster at the vintage, not having an ear of grain to eat the first-fruits! And lest we think that the lowliness of these groans is referred to the Word of God, who it is that laments follows at once: Woe is me, soul, for the reverent one has perished from the earth! — not that we divide the persons, as the impious do, but that one and the same Son of God speaks now according to the flesh, now according to the Word of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","I have not lent at interest, nor has anyone lent at interest to me; all curse me, for which LXX: I have not profited, nor has anyone profited me, Theodotion: I have not owed, nor has anyone owed me. The sense of all of these, in the person of Christ, is this: 'No one has offered himself who would worthily receive my money, nor has anyone lent at interest to me by making me his debtor in cherishing the saints and the poor. Or I have not profited, nor has anyone profited me; for no one has been willing to receive as much as I desired to bestow. Nor has anyone profited me; for the salvation of the creature is the gain of the Creator. Or certainly I have not owed, nor has anyone owed me; no one has given me as much as I desired to receive, nor has he made me his debtor in anything'. And what he adds: nor has anyone owed me, has this sense: 'For how could he owe me interest, who did not deign to accept a loan?' All, he says, curse me; for which of the heretics and of those who err does not curse Christ, by believing perverse things and blaspheming things more perverse?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.11":["The Lord says: if your remnant is not for good, if I did not meet you in the time of affliction and in the time of tribulation — and of distress — against the enemy. These words can also be taken in the person of Jeremiah, who was compelled to prophesy in a most evil time, with captivity already imminent, and to suffer hard things from an unbelieving people. To what he had said above: Woe is me, mother, why did you bear me a man who am to be judged and contended with by all the earth? and the rest, the Lord answers: 'Do not consider the present, but the future; for your remnant and your last things will be for good. Indeed even at present, when the enemies desired to crush you, I was at your side, and you were protected by my help'. This, moreover, can be referred as well to Jeremiah as, according to the dispensation of the flesh he assumed, to the Savior.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.12":["Shall iron be leagued with iron from the north, and bronze? Symmachus: Shall iron harm iron from the north, and bronze? LXX and Theodotion: If iron will know, and a covering of bronze? The cause of the variety is clear; for the word 'iare', which is written in the present place, owing to the ambiguity of its pronunciation means both 'friendship' and 'malice'; and if, in place of the letter 'res', which is similar to the letter 'daleth', 'daleth' is read, it signifies 'knowledge and understanding'. What is said is to be understood thus: 'Do not grieve that the people is your enemy; for when you announce hard things, one who is hard cannot love you': either the Babylonians, who come from the north and are the hardest iron, will not be able to be joined in friendship with this people, which is harder and untamed after the likeness of bronze; or the hardest iron, that is, the people of Israel, is unworthy of the knowledge of God, since it has come to such malice that it is encased in a harder metal, bronze.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.13-14":["Your riches and your treasures I will give up for plunder for nothing — or without price — for all your sins and in all your borders. And I will bring your enemies — or I will make you serve your enemies — from a land which you do not know, because a fire has been kindled in my fury; it will burn upon you. 'All your substance', he says, 'I will hand over to your enemies without any price, on account of the sins which you have committed in all your borders. Therefore I will bring your enemies or I will make you serve in the land of Chaldea, because my fire, once kindled in my fury, will burn in you and cannot be extinguished; for you have furnished the material for your own burning, so that my fire may consume your wood, which is in you, and hay and stubble'. And so the cause of the burning is not in the Lord, but in those who supplied fuel to the fire.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.15-16":["You know, Lord: remember me and visit me, and deliver me from those who persecute me! Do not take me up in your patience; know that I have borne reproach for you! Your words were found, and I ate them — or by those who reject your words; consume them — and your word became to me — or will be to me — a joy and the gladness of my heart, because your name has been called upon me, O Lord God of hosts! What we said: 'You know', is not found in the Seventy. Happy, moreover, is that conscience which bears reproach for God. Hence he says: 'Your words were found, which you were speaking by my mouth, and I ate them, that is: they were turned into food for me'; or, according to Symmachus: 'I received them, so that they were turned into gladness for me, though formerly they had been a reproach'. Hence the Babylonians too confess that what Jeremiah had foretold as future has been fulfilled. Or the sense is this: 'I felt distresses, I endured the miseries inflicted by a persecuting people, but nevertheless I rejoiced that I had obeyed your precepts, and for your name's sake I suffered hard things'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.17-18":["I did not sit in the council of those who make merry, and I gloried; — or I was afraid — before the face of your hand I sat alone, because you filled me with bitterness. Why has my pain become perpetual — or why are those who sadden me strengthened — and my desperate wound refused to be healed — or my wound is strong; whence shall I be healed —? It has become to me as a lie of faithless waters or as lying water that has no faithfulness. The Hebrews think that these words are spoken in the person of Jerusalem: that she sat alone and was filled with bitterness, and her pain became perpetual, and that, as waters pass away, so the words of the prophets, by which they promised themselves prosperity, passed away falsely. It is better, however, that we take these words to be spoken in the person of the prophet, in the words of a holy man, who did not sit in the council or the secret gathering of those who make merry, because he feared the hand of God impending over him, or rather gloried that he had no fellowship with the wicked. Alone, he says, I sat, according to what is written: I have not sat with the council of vanity, and with those who do iniquitous things I will not go in. I have hated the church of the malignant, and with the impious I will not sit; and in another place: I am solitary, until I pass over. 'Before the face', he says, 'of your hand I sat alone, while I fear you, while I always await your hand impending over me. In the council of those who make merry I refused to sit, but I swallowed down my bitterness, so that I might prepare joy for myself in the future. Nor did I have intervals in my pain, but I was weighed down by continual misery, so that I expected no remedies. For those who saddened me prevailed, and my wound became strong. But in this I had consolation, that it was like lying and passing water. For as passing waters are seen while they flow, and glide away, so also every assault of the enemies has passed away, with you helping me'. Would that the Lord might grant to us too not to sit in the council of those who make merry and of those who do not think of the things to come, nor to yield to adversities, but always to dread the sentence of God and to say with the prophet: I sat alone, because I am filled with bitterness! 'Let it rejoice, then, in the present time — by no means the council of the wise, but the hidden and secret company of those who make merry; for me let it be good to cleave to God, to put my hope in the Lord, to be sated with reproaches and to await the sentence of my judge, which, when the end has come, will show in deed that all sorrow and bitterness have receded like flowing waters.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.15.17":["Jeremiah was in captivity; but there were also many multitudes. And what does he say? I sat alone, because I was filled with bitterness. How were you alone in the city? But therefore alone, because I had no companion of my purpose.\n— Homily 54, on Psalm 143"],"JER.15.19-21":["Therefore thus says the Lord: if you turn, I will turn you, and you will stand before my face; and if you separate the precious from the vile, you will be as my mouth; they will turn to you, and you will not turn to them. And I will give you to this people for a wall of bronze, strong, and they will fight against you and will not prevail, because I am with you to save you and deliver you, says the Lord. And I will free you from the hand of the most wicked and redeem you from the hand of the strong or of the pestilent. It is clear that it was by no means Jerusalem but the prophet who said the preceding words. To him the Lord answers: 'If you turn the people from their sins, I too will turn you from tribulation into gladness, and you will stand before my face, as the angels stand in the sight of God, daily seeing his face, and if you separate the precious from the vile, you will be as my mouth. And do not think', he says, 'that there is no reward for a good work; if by your words you set apart any of my saints from the number of sinners, you will be as my mouth and will be joined to my precepts. For they ought to be your imitators, and not you theirs. And do not shrink back and say: Why has my pain become perpetual, and my wound strong or desperate, so that I despair of being able to be healed? For I will give you as a wall of bronze, and most strong, so that you may resist the adversary with all your strength and have me as your helper, and I will free you from the hand of the most wicked or the pestilent, and redeem you either by my blood or at present by my help'. Let us consider how great a reward the word of a teacher has, if it has been able to free anyone from error and lead him out of the number of sinners!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.2":["And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Do not take a wife, and let there be no sons and daughters for you in this place! For thus says the Lord concerning the sons and daughters who are begotten in this place, and concerning their mothers who bore them, and concerning their fathers, of whose stock they were born in this land: They shall die by the deaths of sicknesses; they shall not be lamented and they shall not be buried; they shall be as dung upon the face of the earth, and they shall be consumed by the sword and by famine, and their corpses shall be food for the birds of heaven and for the beasts of the earth. If, at the time of the impending captivity, the prophet is forbidden to marry a wife, so that he may not have tribulation of the flesh and, on top of his own grief, be tormented also by the miseries of his wife and children, how much more does the Apostle command, because the time has been shortened and the consummation is at hand, that even those who have wives should be as though they had none! Hence superfluous is the reproach of the new heretic against our teaching that second and third marriages do not derive from law but from indulgence. For it is one thing to do what is good in itself, another to be granted something lest you do worse. For he himself also states the reasons why he wishes young widows to marry, adding: for some of them have already turned back after Satan. And at the same time this teacher of continence and perpetual chastity, who blasphemously makes himself equal to God, praises third and fourth marriages, which I would call not so much marriages as consolations of miseries and the last plank of the shipwrecked; unless perhaps he grants indulgence to his Amazons, so that they may wage the wars of lust right up to decrepit old age.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.3-4":["But why the prophet is kept from marrying a wife, the reason is plain: because with the siege close at hand all were to perish by pestilence and sword and famine, and the number of the dying would be so great that the duty of burial would be overwhelmed, and the corpses would lie like dung to be torn by birds and beasts. And this too must be noted, that to waste away through sicknesses and long infirmity is the wrath of God. Hence Joram also, the son of Jehoshaphat, is consumed by infirmity, and the Apostle teaches that those who have violated holy things fall sick and waste away and die.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.5-8":["For thus says the Lord: Do not enter the house of feasting, nor go to lament, nor comfort them, for I have taken away my peace from this people, says the Lord, my mercy and compassions. And both great and small shall die in this land; they shall not be buried nor lamented, and they shall not cut themselves, nor shall baldness be made for them. And they shall not break bread among them for the mourner, to comfort him over the dead, and they shall not give them the drink of the cup to comfort them over their father and mother. And you shall not enter the house of feasting, to sit with them and eat and drink. The Apostle commands that with those who turn away from God not even food is to be taken; and further: do not even say hail to such! And the Savior forbids the apostles to greet anyone on the way; hence Elisha too forbids Gehazi to give greetings as he went to heal the boy. Now it is the custom to bring food to those who mourn and to prepare a feast, which the Greeks call perideipna, and which among our people are commonly named 'parentalia' [ancestral rites], because rites are performed for parents. Divine Scripture also says elsewhere: give wine to those who are in mourning, that is, so that they may forget their grief. The prophet is therefore commanded not to comfort anyone of the people, not to mix himself in the feasts of the enemies of God, not to perform rites over the funerals of the dead. For it is one thing to die by the common law of nature, another to be slain by the sentence of God. 'I have taken away', he says, 'my peace from this people, and they are unworthy of mercy, and I will spare no age, but both great and small shall perish alike, so that they shall lack even burial'. Nor shall they cut themselves, he says, nor shall baldness be made for them. This custom existed among the ancients and still persists today among some of the Jews, that in mourning they cut their arms and make baldness; which we read Job also did. Hence the prophet is told not to break bread among them nor to go in to comfort them over the dead, not to give drink, not to enter the house of feasting, and not to mix with those who have been made ready for God's sentence. But if this is said of those who mourn, what will become of the heretics, whose word spreads like a cancer, and who daily lay low in the Church the corpses of the deceived?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.9":["For thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Behold, I will take away from this place, before your eyes and in your days, the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride. And when you have announced to this people all these words, and they have said to you: Why has the Lord spoken all this great evil against us? What is our iniquity, and what is our sin that we have sinned against the Lord our God?, you shall say to them: Because your fathers forsook me, says the Lord, and went after strange gods and served them and worshiped them, and forsook me and did not keep my law. But you too have done worse than your fathers. When the Church has sinned, all joy and all gladness is taken away from her, of which the Apostle says: rejoice; again I say: rejoice; the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride, of whom it is written: he who has the bride is the bridegroom.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.10-11":["'But if', he says, 'the people asks you why it suffers these things, and seeks the causes of its miseries, you shall answer them: because your fathers forsook me, says the Lord, those who preside over you in the churches, and went after strange gods — whose god is the belly, and avarice and luxury, and their glory is in their shame — and served them — for by whatever one is overcome, of that he is also the slave — and worshiped them — for each one worships what he loves — and forsook me and did not keep my law'. It belongs properly to priests not only to teach the law but also to do it, so that they may teach the peoples subject to them and the flock entrusted to them not by words at all, but by examples.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.12-13":["For behold, each one walks after the depravity of his evil heart, so as not to hear me. And I will cast you out of this land into a land which you do not know, you and your fathers, and there you shall serve strange gods day and night, who will not give you rest. Once forsaken by the Lord, they do the things that are not fitting, so as to go after the desires of their evil heart, out of which come the worst thoughts; and for this reason they are separated from the Church, so as to go into a far country, which neither they nor their fathers had known before they sinned, so that there they may serve strange gods, who are not gods, but are thought to be so by the error of those who worship them. But in adding 'day and night', he shows the perpetual perseverance of sinners in wickedness, since both in the days they serve shameful deeds and in the nights lust. Who will not give, he says, you rest; there is no doubt that he means the false gods, of whom he said: and there you shall serve strange gods. Whatever, therefore, we sin, whatever we do by day and night and whatever evil works we commit, is the dominion of demons, who never give us rest and always drive us to add offenses to offenses and to make a heap of sins.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.12":["And lest they should perhaps say: 'it is an unjust sentence that the fathers eat the sour grape and the children's teeth are set on edge', he adds: but you too have done worse than your fathers, so that just punishments may be brought upon those who have sinned worse than their fathers.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.14-15":["Therefore, behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and it shall no longer be said: The Lord lives, who brought the sons of Israel out of the land of Egypt, but: The Lord lives, who brought the sons of Israel out of the land of the north and out of all the lands to which I cast them; and I will bring them back into their land, which I gave to their fathers. Plainly the future restoration of the people of Israel is foretold, and mercy after the captivity, which according to the letter was in part fulfilled under Zerubbabel and Jeshua the high priest and Ezra, and according to the spiritual understanding is described as to be fulfilled more truly and more perfectly in Christ. 'The time will come', he says, 'when it will no longer be said that the people was brought back from Egypt through Moses and Aaron, but that it was brought back from the land of the north when Cyrus, king of the Persians, released the captives'. And out of all, he says, the lands; which will be fulfilled not at all in the time of Cyrus, but at the very end, as the Apostle says: 'after the fullness of the nations has come in, then all Israel will be saved'. We can also say this of the persecutions which befell our people from the days of Nero, of whom the Apostle writes: and I was delivered from the mouth of the lion, down to the times of Maximinus: how the Lord had mercy on his people and brought them back into their land, no doubt into the Church, which he gave to their fathers, the apostles and apostolic men.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.16-18":["Behold, I will send many fishers, says the Lord, and they shall fish them; and after this I will send them many hunters, and they shall hunt them from every mountain and from every hill and from the caves of the rocks, for my eyes are upon all their ways. They are not hidden from my face, and their iniquity was not concealed from my eyes. And I will repay first their double iniquities and sins, with which they have defiled my land with the carcasses of their idols and have filled my inheritance with their abominations. The interpretation of this passage is diverse. For the Jews suppose that the Chaldeans are signified, who are described under the name of fishers, and afterwards the Romans, who are compared to hunters and who hunted the unhappy people from the mountains and hills and caves of the rocks. And the Lord says that he himself did this because he looked upon their ways and repaid their iniquities, with which they had defiled the land by venerating images, and had polluted his inheritance with the abominations of idols.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.16":["But our writers more rightly and better think that the prophet prophesied these things about the future. For since he had said above: I will bring them back into their land, which I gave to their fathers, he now shows how they are to be brought back: that first he sends the apostles, to whom the Savior said: come after me, and I will make you fishers of men, and afterwards hunters, whom we can take to be either churchmen or angels, who, once the time of the consummation has come, are to hunt all the saints from the mountains of lofty doctrines and from the hills of good works and from the caves of the rocks, the apostles and apostolic men. For not only is Christ the rock, but he also granted to the apostle Peter that he should be called rock; those who rest in his thoughts are rightly said to have been transferred from the rock.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.17-18":["And he shows that either the apostles or those who are afterwards to be hunted had sins and received their double iniquities. For the servant who knows the will of his lord and does not do it shall be beaten much. And it must be known that in the Hebrew 'first' is set down, and in the edition of the Seventy it is omitted. But when he says: I will repay first their double iniquities and sins, he intimates that after they have received evils, they will receive good things too. And these who are afterwards to be transferred have defiled the land of the Lord with the carcasses of their idols and have filled his inheritance with their abominations, so that the whole world may become subject to God and be preserved not at all by its own merit, but by his mercy. What we have set down here from the Hebrew: they are not hidden from my face, is not found in the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.19":["O Lord, my strength and my might and my refuge in the day of tribulation or of evils! All the strength of men without the power of God, which is Christ, is reckoned feeble and of no account; hence one must flee to God and say: Lord, you have become a refuge for us from generation to generation; and in another place: he who dwells in the help of the Most High shall abide in the protection of the God of heaven. And the day of tribulation or of evils is to be understood as that of which the Apostle also says: that he might rescue us from the present evil age, and: redeeming the time, because the days are evil.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","To you the nations shall come from the ends of the earth and shall say: Truly our fathers possessed a lie, vanity, which did not profit them. LXX: to you the nations shall come from the ends of the earth and shall say: How false are the idols our fathers possessed, and there is no profit in them! After Israel has been cast out and carried away by the fishers and hunters, the multitude of the nations is accordingly called to faith and confesses that it, or its fathers, had been engaged in their former error. And those who say: 'how false are the idols our fathers possessed, in which there is no profit!' confess that the things to which they are passing over are true and supported by every protection.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.20":["Shall a man make gods for himself, and they are not gods? This too the nations say, who have come to the Savior from the ends of the earth, rebuking the ignorance both of themselves and of their ancestors, by which they thought that gods are made by man, when it belongs to God to make men.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.16.21":["Therefore, behold, I will show them at this turn, I will show them my hand and my power, and they shall know that my name is the Lord. The hand of God, through which he has wrought all things, and the power, of which the Apostle said: 'Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God', is shown to the nations once the passion of the Son has been completed. And beautifully he said: 'I will show them at this turn; and I will show them plainly, and not as before in shadow and in image and in prophecy of things to come, so that, after they have come to know it, they may know my name and hear from the Son: Father, I have made your name manifest to men'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.1":["The sin of Judah is written with an iron stylus, with an adamantine nail, engraved upon the breadth of their heart — or on the breast of their heart — and on the horns of the altars — or altar-stones — of them. Concerning the nations which had been converted to the Lord, it was said above: behold, I will show them at this turn, I will show them my hand and my power; now he speaks of Israel, which has been cast off: the sin of Judah is written with an iron stylus, with an adamantine nail, and so on. Why this was passed over by the Seventy I do not know, unless perhaps they spared their own people, as it is evident that they did also in Isaiah: cease therefore from man, whose breath is in his nostrils, because he himself has been reckoned exalted; and many things of this kind, for which, if I wished to set them all out, there would be need, I will not say of a book, but of books. The sins of the nations have been blotted out, because, converted to the Lord from the ends of the earth, they have heard this: praise the Lord, all you nations, praise him, all you peoples, because his mercy has been confirmed upon us, and the truth of the Lord remains forever; and they are those concerning whom he had said to Moses: let me alone, that I may kill this people, and I will make you into a great nation. But the sin of Judah, indelible and, so to speak, to be abolished by no means, is written with an iron stylus, with an adamantine nail, which in Hebrew is called 'samir': not that there is any nail which is called 'samir', but because the adamant stone, which received this name from the fact that it is untamed and unbreakable, has such polish and smoothness that one can write on it with an iron stylus without any hindrance, so that the hard substance of iron writes on a harder tablet of adamant, and what is written lasts forever. For they themselves said: his blood be upon us and upon our children. Hence it is also engraved or carved on the horns of the altars — or altar-stones — of them, so that the works of sacrilege may endure in eternal memory. But if this is so, where is that which the doting old woman invents, that a man can be without sin if he wishes, and that the commandments of God are easy?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.2-4":["When their sons remember their altar-stones and their groves and leafy trees on the high mountains, sacrificing in the field, I will give your strength and all your treasures for plunder, your high places on account of sins in all your borders. And you shall be left alone, bereft of your inheritance, which I gave you, and I will make you serve your enemies in a land which you do not know, since you have kindled a fire in my fury; it will burn forever. These things too are not found in the Seventy, for the same reason, I think, that we stated above, namely, lest an eternal sentence should remain against them. You shall be left, he says, alone, bereft of your inheritance, which I gave you, and I will make you serve your enemies in a land which you do not know, either under the Babylonians or, as is truer, under the Romans. For they themselves kindled the fire and provoked the most merciful Lord to fury, and this fire of his fury will burn forever. I am ashamed of the contentiousness of our own people, who find fault with the Hebrew truth. The Jews read against themselves, and the Church does not know what is in her favor. Hence let us, who are the sons of the apostles, remember the iniquities of the former people, and we testify that they have suffered justly. But the 'high places', which in Hebrew are called 'bamoth', can also be taken against the heretics, who have set their mouth on high, and their tongue has passed over the earth, who have broken out into such madness that they alone have remained without the grace of the Holy Spirit and have lost the inheritance of the Lord, namely the former truth of the faith. Hence for them too an eternal burning is prepared, and servitude to the demons, who are their enemies and avengers.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.5":["Thus says the Lord: cursed is the man who trusts — or has hope — in man and makes flesh his arm, and whose heart departs from the Lord. For he will be like a tamarisk in the desert, and he will not see when good comes, but he will dwell in dryness in the desert, in a land of saltness and uninhabitable. If every man who trusts in man is cursed, and Paul of Samosata and Photinus, although they proclaim the Savior holy and exalted above all in virtues, nevertheless confess him to be a man, then they will be cursed, having hope in a man. But if it is objected to us that we too believe in him who says: but now you seek to kill me, a man who has spoken the truth to you, which I heard from God; this Abraham did not do. You do the works of your father, we shall answer with that saying of the Apostle: even if we once knew Christ according to the flesh, yet now we no longer know him. Then the same Apostle, at the beginning of his epistle, writes to the Galatians: Paul an apostle, not from men nor through man, but through Jesus Christ and God the Father, who raised him from the dead, and all the brethren who are with me. For if death has been swallowed up in victory, why should not the lowliness of the flesh, which was assumed for the sake of human salvation, have passed into the majesty of the divinity, so that he has made both one, and we do not adore a creature but the Creator, who is blessed forever? Cursed, therefore, is not only he who has hope in man, but he who makes flesh his arm, that is, his strength, and who thinks that whatever he has done belongs not to the mercy of the Lord but to his own power. For he who does this, his heart departs from the Lord, since he asserts that he can do what he cannot.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","\"For vain is the salvation of man.\" \"Cursed is the man who has his hope in man.\" There is no need to interpret this: this saying is plain to all. Someone hoped in an emperor, hoped in a prince, in a father, or a mother: in the course of some affair the spirit of the one in whom he hoped was suddenly taken away, and his salvation vanished. He did not say, \"vain is the salvation of that man in whom he hopes,\" but \"the salvation of him who hopes in man.\"\n— Homily 34, on Psalm 107"],"JER.17.6":["And he will be like a tamarisk — which in Hebrew is called 'aroher', or, as Symmachus translated, an unfruitful tree — in the wilderness, and he will not see when good things come, which the multitude of the nations is to see, but he will dwell in dryness in the desert. This is said of the people of the Jews, who dwell in the desert and bear no fruits and are in a land of saltness, which bears no fruits, and uninhabitable, which has neither God as its guest, nor the protection of angels, nor the grace of the Holy Spirit, nor the knowledge of teachers.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.7":["Blessed is the man who trusts in the Lord, and the Lord will be his confidence — or hope — ; and he will be like a tree that has been transplanted — or fruitful — upon the waters, that sends out its roots toward moisture; and it will not fear when the heat comes, and its leaf will be green — or its twigs in leaf — and in the time — or year — of dryness it will not be anxious — or will not fear — nor will it ever cease to bear fruit. Let that be said of the Jews and of the heretics, who have hope in man, namely in their Christ, whom they think will come not as the Son of God but as a mere man. On the contrary, the man of the Church, who trusts in the Lord, hears this: and know that the Lord himself is God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.8":["He trusts in the Lord and will be compared to that tree of which it is sung also in the first Psalm: and he will be like a tree that has been planted beside the courses of waters, which will give its fruit in its season; and its leaf will not fall off. Upon the waters, moreover: the grace of the Holy Spirit, its diverse gifts. That sends out its roots toward moisture: that it may receive fruitfulness from the Lord; but we can also say otherwise, that we have been transplanted from the Jewish dryness into the everlasting grace of baptism. And it will not, he says, fear when the heat comes — either the time of persecution or the day of judgment — ; and its leaf will be green — or there will be twigs in leaf on it —, so that it never fears dryness, but buds with the grace of all the virtues. And when the time or the year of dryness comes, it will not be afraid, when the Lord in anger commands the clouds not to rain a shower upon Israel. And what follows, nor will it cease to bear fruit, will be able to explain that passage which is written in Mark, that the Lord came to the fig tree and did not find fruit on it, because it was not yet the time, and cursed it, that it should not bear fruit forever. For he who trusts in the Lord, and whose confidence the Lord is, will not be afraid even in the time of the Jewish dryness, but will always bear fruits, believing in him who once died for us and dies no more and says: I am the life.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.9-10":["The heart of all is depraved and inscrutable; who will know it? I the Lord search the heart and prove the reins, who give to each one according to his way and according to the fruit of his devices. LXX: the heart is deep above all things, and it is man; who will know him? and so on in the same way. The Hebrew word 'enos' is written with four letters, 'aleph' and 'nun' and 'uau' and 'sin'; if, then, it is read 'enos', it means 'man'; but if 'anus', 'inscrutable' or 'desperate', because no one is able to find out the heart of men. Symmachus, however, translated this passage thus: inscrutable is the heart of all; but who is the man that can find it out? And our people are wont, with a good desire indeed but not according to knowledge, to use this passage against the Jews, to show that the Lord and Savior is a man according to the dispensation of the flesh that he assumed, and that no one can know the mystery of his birth, according to what is written: who will declare his generation?, except God alone, who searches out secrets and renders to each one according to his works. But it is better that we take it simply, that no one knows the secrets of the thoughts except God alone; for he had said above: cursed is the man who has hope in man, and on the contrary: blessed is the man who trusts in the Lord. Hence, lest we should think the judgment of men certain, he added that the hearts of nearly all are perverse, as the psalmist says: cleanse me from my hidden faults, O Lord, and from those of others spare your servant — no doubt, from thoughts — and in Genesis: but God, seeing that the wickedness of men was great on the earth, and that every thought of the heart was intent upon evil at all times, and again: for the sense and thought of the human heart are prone to evil from its youth. Through these we learn that God alone knows their thoughts. But if it is said of the Savior, but Jesus, seeing their thoughts, and no one can see thoughts except God alone, then Christ is God, who searches hearts and proves the reins and will render to each one according to his works.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.11":["The partridge has brooded — or has gathered —, what it did not bring forth — and, as the Seventy translated: the partridge cried out, it gathered what it did not bring forth —, he makes his riches not with judgment; in the half of his days he will leave them — or in the half of his days they will leave him — and at his end he will be a fool. The writers of natural history, of beasts and birds as well as of trees and plants, whose chief men among the Greeks are Aristotle and Theophrastus, and among us Pliny Secundus, say that this is the nature of the partridge: that it steals the eggs of another partridge, that is, eggs not its own, and sits on them and broods them, and that when the young have grown up, they fly away from it and leave their alien parent. Of this kind are the rich who plunder what belongs to others and, without reflecting on the judgment of God, make riches not with judgment, which they leave behind in the middle of their time, snatched away by sudden death, when it is said to them: fool, this night they will demand your soul from you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be? And nothing is more foolish than not to foresee the last things and to think brief things perpetual. Others, however, both on account of the history given above and on account of another, by which they say that the partridge is most quarrelsome and unclean, so much so that it defiles the one it has vanquished, interpret the devil under its name, because he has gathered to himself riches not his own, saying to the Lord: all these things I will give you, if you will fall down and worship me. His riches, which had been wickedly gathered by him, will leave him, and through the apostles they will return to the Lord, and he who seemed to himself most prudent will be, in the judgment of all, a fool. And what is said by the Seventy, the partridge cried out, is to be referred to the person of the heretics: that this partridge, the devil, has cried out through the chiefs of the heretics and has gathered what it did not bring forth, and has gathered to itself a multitude of the deceived, which afterwards will abandon it, and it will be proved by the judgment of all to be most foolish.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.12-13":["A throne of glory of the height from the beginning, the place of our sanctification, the expectation of Israel, O Lord, all who leave you will be confounded! Let those who depart be written in the earth, since they have forsaken the vein — or fountain — of living waters, the Lord. The partridge being forsaken for its folly, the expectation of Israel, that is, of the people of God and of the one who believes in the Lord, is he himself who made all things, whose throne is glorious and exalted from the beginning, and who is the place of sanctification of all believers; so that the Lord is not in a place, but wherever he is, that place is sanctified. On the contrary, those who forsake the Lord will be confounded with perpetual confusion, and departing or turning aside from him they will be written in the earth, blotted out from the book of the living. For just as he who can say with the Apostle, but our citizenship is in heaven, is written in the heavenly places, so he who leaves the Lord or turns aside from him will be written in the earth with those who mind earthly things. And the cause is plain why they are written in the earth: because they have left the fountain of life, the Lord, or the fountain of living waters, the Lord, who speaks in the Gospel: if anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink! He who believes in me, as the scripture says, rivers of living water will flow from his belly. Now this he said of the Spirit, which those who believed were to receive.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.14":["Heal me, O Lord, and I shall be healed; save me, and I shall be saved, since you are my praise! Many physicians in the Gospel had treated the woman with the issue of blood, who had spent all her substance on them, and yet she could be cured by none except by him who is the true physician and in whose wings is healing. Hence now too the prophet, having suffered reproaches from the people and having been often beset by plots, desires to be healed and to be saved by him who is his true praise and his true medicine.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.15":["Behold, they say to me: where is the word of the Lord? Let it come! But I was not troubled, following you as shepherd — or I have not labored following you — and I have not desired the day of man; you know. What went out from my lips was right in your sight — or is before your face —. Do not be a terror to me, my hope in the day of affliction, or do not become a stranger to me, sparing me in the worst day! Those who do not think that what has been spoken will come say to the prophet: where is the word of the Lord? let it come!, judging the delay to be a setting aside of the sentence.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.16":["But while they are saying these things, he says: 'I was not troubled, nor did I labor, following you as shepherd, or walking in your footsteps; nor was I content to stop there, but I have not desired the day of man, either a longer life or any of the prosperities of this world'. And he calls the same one as witness whom he calls as judge: you have known. There follows: what went out from my lips was right in your sight, meaning that he never lied, that he said nothing contrary to the will of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.17":["Do not, he says, be a terror to me, my hope in the day of affliction! This is clear according to the Hebrew; but according to what the Seventy translated, saying, do not become to me as a stranger, sparing me in the evil day, the sense is: 'do not spare me in the present world, which is evil, but render to me according to my sins, so that I may have everlasting rest! for I know that it is written: whom the Lord loves, he reproves; and he scourges every son whom he receives'. But the evil day is either the whole world or the day of judgment for those who are tormented on account of their sins.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.18":["Let those who persecute me be confounded, and let me not be confounded! Let them tremble, and let me not tremble! Bring upon them the day of affliction, and crush them with a double crushing! The prophet calls down imprecations against those who taunt him with the word of the Lord and say, where is the word of the Lord? let it come: that those who persecute him may be confounded, and may blush and return to salvation; that they, who lie, may tremble, and not he, who foretells true things; and that, when the day of vengeance has come, he may crush them with a double crushing, famine and sword.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.19-20":["Thus says the Lord to me: go and stand in the gate — or in the gates — of the sons of the people, through which [gate] — or through which [gates] — the kings of Judah go in and go out, in all the gates of Jerusalem, and you shall say to them: hear the word of the Lord, kings of Judah and all Judah — or Judea — and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem, who go in through these gates! 'Since', he says, 'they scorn to hear your words and do not come to you to inquire what the sentence of God may be, go you to the most frequented place, either of the gates of the temple or of those of the city, through which the kings and all the common people go in and go out, so that they may be compelled by necessity to hear, and you may preach the word of God out of season and in season, and no excuse may remain to them that they did not do it because they did not hear'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.21":["Thus says the Lord: guard your souls, and do not carry weights on the sabbath day, nor bring them in through the gates of Jerusalem! And do not cast out loads from your houses on the sabbath day, and you shall do no work, and sanctify the sabbath day, as I commanded your fathers! And they did not hear, nor did they incline their ear, and they hardened their neck — and what is not found in the Hebrew: above their fathers —, that they might not hear me and might not receive instruction. And it shall be, he says, if you hear me, says the Lord, so as not to bring in loads through the gates of this city on the sabbath day, and if you sanctify the sabbath day, so as not to do any work on it, there will enter through the gates of this city kings and princes sitting upon the throne of David and mounting in chariots and on horses, they and their princes, the men of Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and this city will be inhabited forever. And they will come from the cities of Judah and from round about Jerusalem and from the land of Benjamin and from the plains and from the hill country and from the south, bringing burnt offering and victims — or incense offerings — and sacrifice — or manna — and frankincense, and they will bring in an offering — or praise — into the house of the Lord. But if you will not hear me, to sanctify the sabbath day and not to carry a load and not to bring it in through the gates of Jerusalem on the sabbath day, I will kindle a fire in its gates, and it will devour the houses of Jerusalem and will not be extinguished. Lest I should tear in pieces the precept of the sabbath renewed through Jeremiah, I have chosen to set it out whole, so that we may know everything together. He guards his soul who does not carry the weights of sins on the day of 'rest' and of sabbath, nor bring them in through the gates of Jerusalem, which we ought to take as the virtues.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.22":["And do not, he says, cast out loads from your houses — for they are not to be carried, but utterly cast away — and do no work — either servile work or that of which it is written: food for the belly and the belly for foods; but God will destroy both it and them —, but that work is to be worked of which the Savior speaks: 'work the work that does not perish!' Sanctify, he says, the sabbath day, so that we may spend all the time of our life in sanctification, as our fathers Abraham, Isaac and Jacob did.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.23":["And when God had commanded these things, they did not incline their ear — that of the mind, surely, not of the flesh —, but they hardened their neck, rejecting the yoke of the law and, by metaphoran [metaphor], bearing the likeness of untamed animals.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.24-25":["Let us see what is the reward of those who do not carry loads on the sabbath day and who sanctify it. There will enter, he says, through the gates of this city kings, whose heart is in the hand of God and who reign over their bodies, and princes sitting upon the throne of David, so that they imitate the example of Christ, and mounting in chariots and on horses, of which it is written: the chariot of God is ten thousandfold, manifold, thousands of those who rejoice; the Lord is among them in Sinai, in the holy place, and elsewhere: your riding is salvation: every man who 'confesses' God and who dwells in Jerusalem, of which it has been said: was made in Salem, that is, in peace, his place, and his dwelling in Zion; and the Church of God will be inhabited forever.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.26":["And they will come from the cities of Judah and from round about Jerusalem, of which we have already spoken, and from the land of Benjamin, who is the 'son' 'of power' and 'of the right hand', and from the plains, which in Hebrew is called 'sefela' and signifies the plain understanding of the history, and from the hill country, namely the lofty doctrines, and from the south, of which it is written, God will come from the south, whence comes heat and full light, and where all cold is driven out. Bringing, he says, burnt offerings — consecrating themselves to God — and a victim or incense, so that they may say: a sacrifice to the Lord is a troubled spirit, and: we are the good odor of Christ in every place, and elsewhere: let my prayer be directed as incense in your sight; and sacrifice, for which the Seventy put the Hebrew word itself, 'manaa', which by a very bad custom, nay rather by the carelessness of scribes, is read in our books as 'manna'; and frankincense, of which it has been said, to what purpose do you bring me frankincense from Sheba?; and they will bring in an offering, which in Hebrew is called 'thoda' and can be rendered as 'thanksgiving', and which the Seventy translated as 'praise'. Into the house of David: no doubt, into the Church. These are the rewards of those who sanctify the sabbath and are weighed down by no weight.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.17.27":["'But if', he says, 'you will not hear my precepts, and do the things which I commanded not to be done, I will kindle a fire in its gates' — that is, of Jerusalem: that fire of which it is said, all committing adultery, like an oven, their hearts —, 'which will devour the houses or streets of Jerusalem' — which the Seventy translated as amphoda, Aquila and Symmachus as bareis, that is, 'towered houses', and which are called in Hebrew 'armanoth' — 'and this burning will never be extinguished', as the Apostle says: the fire will test what sort each one's work is, and again: if anyone's work is burned, he will suffer loss, but he himself will be saved, yet so as through fire. But if our Judaizers reject the figurative explanation, they will be compelled either to be Jews and, with the observance of the sabbath, to circumcise their foreskins, or else to find fault with the Savior, who on the sabbath commanded the paralytic to carry his bed, as the evangelist says: for this reason the Jews sought all the more to kill him, not only because he was breaking the sabbath, but also because he said that God was his own Father, making himself equal to God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.2":["The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying: Arise and go down into the potter's house, and there you shall hear my words! And I went down into the potter's house, and behold, he was doing a work upon the wheel — or stones —. And the vessel that he was making from clay with his hands was broken; and turning — or and again — he made it another vessel, as it had pleased in his eyes to make it. And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Can I not do with you as this potter, O house of Israel? says the Lord; behold, as clay in the hand of the potter, so are you in my hand, O house of Israel! Suddenly — or in sum — I will speak against a nation and against a kingdom, to root it out — or to take it away — and to destroy and to ruin it. If that nation repents of its evil, which I have spoken against it, I too will repent of the evil which I thought to do to it. And suddenly — or in sum — I will speak concerning a nation and a kingdom, to build and to plant it. If it does evil in my eyes, so as not to hear my voice, I will repent of the good which I spoke of, to do to it. Through all the senses, indeed, one arrives at judgment and understanding of mind, through hearing, smell, taste, touch; but what is seen with the eyes is retained more in the mind. Hence the prophet is ordered to go to the potter's house and there to hear the Lord's commands.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.3":["'And when,' he says, 'I had set out and had gone down into the potter's house, he was doing a work upon the wheel', which the Seventy, led astray by the ambiguity of the word, translated 'stones'; for 'abanim', according to the nature of the context and the difference of pronunciation, is used both for an 'instrument', that is, the potter's 'wheel', and for 'stones'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.4":["'And when,' he says, 'I saw a vessel being made from clay, it was suddenly broken', God's providence bringing this about, so that the craftsman's hand, without knowing it, should by its own mistake figure forth a parable. And again that potter, who had lost the vessel of clay as the wheel was running, made another for himself, as it seemed good to him.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.5-6":["And at once the Lord says to the prophet: 'If a potter,' he says, 'has this power, that from the same clay he makes again what had been broken, shall I not be able to do this in you, who, as far as lies in you, seem to have perished?'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.7-10":["And though they promise themselves prosperity, they will afterwards endure evils; while, on the other side, the saints fear and do not sin, and therefore by no means die by the sword, because they said: Evils will draw near to us, and the punishments which our sins have deserved will come upon us; of which God speaks more fully in Jeremiah: I will take it up and speak against a nation and a kingdom, to take it away and destroy it; if that nation turns from its evils, I too will repent of the things that I had thought to do to it. God, therefore, is not changed, who is always immutable; but we change him by our conversion. He rages, he is angry, he threatens, and says that he will inflict punishments: If we repent, he too will repent of his sentence. Again, according to the same Jeremiah, he promises prosperity: If we grow slack through negligence, he too will repent of his pledge, and will change what he promised. Of this we can have an example in the Ninevites and in Jerusalem, of whom the one were set free from the punishments that hung over them, the other lost what had been promised to their fathers.\n— Commentary on Amos","And to signify free will, he says that he announces both evils to this or that nation and kingdom and, again, good things; and yet that what he himself has foretold does not come about, but that the contrary happens, so that good things befall the evil, if they repent, and evils befall the good, if after the promises they have turned to sins. And we say this not because God does not know that this or that nation or kingdom will do this or that, but because he leaves man to his own will, so that he may receive either rewards or punishments by his own desert. Nor will what befalls be wholly man's at once, but belongs to the grace of him who has bestowed all things; for the freedom of the will must be preserved in such a way that the grace of the giver excels in all things, according to that prophetic word: Unless the Lord builds the house, those who build it have labored in vain; unless the Lord guards the city, he who guards it has watched in vain. For it is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.11":["Now therefore say to the man of Judah and to the inhabitant of Jerusalem, saying: These things — or thus — says the Lord: Behold, I am fashioning evil against you and devising a device against you; let each one turn back from his evil way, and make straight your ways and your pursuits! And they said: We have despaired — or we will be strong —; for we will walk after our own thoughts, and each one of us will do the depravity — or what has pleased his evil heart —. Therefore thus says the Lord: Ask among the nations: who has heard such horrible things as the virgin of Israel has done exceedingly? The Lord fulfills the parable which he had taught both by sight and by words, and says: 'Behold, I am fashioning evil against you, as a potter fashions clay' — evil, however, in the sense of Isaiah, who says: making peace and creating evil, not that it is evil in itself, but that it seems to be evil to those who suffer it — 'and devising a device against you' — that is, to pass sentence according to desert —; 'change your works and make straight your ways, so that the punishment too may be changed into prosperity!'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.12":["'But they, on the contrary,' he says, 'said: We will be strong' — in their evil works, that is, or, according to Aquila, we have despaired, and according to Symmachus, we have failed, both of which offend God, that one should either think that he can by no means be saved, or should have failed in spirit for appeasing God — 'and after our own thoughts,' he says, 'we will walk.' Where, then, is the power of free will and the judgment of one's own will apart from the grace of God, when it is a grave offense against God to follow one's own thoughts and to do the will of a depraved heart?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.13":["Therefore he adds, saying: 'Ask among the nations and all the peoples round about, who has done, who has heard, while serving idols, what the virgin of Israel has done exceedingly.' And he calls her a virgin because she had served the one God, as the prophet says: God is known in Judea, his name is great in Israel.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.14":["Will the snow of Lebanon fail from the rock of the field? Or can the cold waters that burst forth and flow down be torn away? LXX: Will breasts fail from the rock, or snow from Lebanon? Or will water violently carried off by the wind turn aside? Something of the same kind that line of Vergil also sounds: Sooner, therefore, shall the nimble stags graze in the air, and the seas leave the fishes bare upon the shore, than his face shall slip from our heart; and in another place: While rivers run into the seas, while shadows move over the slopes of the mountains, while the sky feeds the stars, ever shall your honor, your name and your praises abide. 'Just as,' he says, 'the snow cannot fail from the summits of Lebanon, nor is it overcome by any heat of the sun so as to melt entirely, and the streams flowing down from the mountains are by no means dried up at their springs, so my name, which is in itself stable and everlasting, cannot be changed; and yet, while all other things keep the order of nature, my people has forgotten me.' For there follows:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.15":["For my people has forgotten me, making libations in vain and stumbling in their ways, in the paths of the age — or everlasting —, so that they walked in them on a road not trodden. He who forgets God and forsakes him who says: I am the way, and pours libations to strange gods, stumbles in his own ways — not God's at all, but his own — and forsakes the ancient and everlasting paths, which are trodden by the footprints of all the saints who worship God. These, however, have walked on a road not trodden and, having abandoned the worship of God, have venerated idols; on account of which the punishment that follows is brought upon them:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.16":["So that their land should become a desolation and an everlasting hissing; everyone who passes by it shall be astonished and shall shake his head. 'And because they have forsaken,' he says, 'the worship of God and have followed the depraved paths of idolatry, therefore their land has been reduced to a wilderness and to a wonder and a hissing to all, so that those who saw the land and city, once most flourishing, collapsed into a desert and ashes should marvel and be astonished and show the confusion of their soul by the gesture of their body'; for this is what it is to shake the head and to show the astonishment of the mind by hissing. And this we understand to have been fulfilled more fully and more correctly after the coming of the Lord, when none of the Jews is permitted by law to enter the land and the once holy city; but when they come to mourn, they marvel at and weep over the prophecies of the prophets fulfilled in deed.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.17":["Like a burning wind I will scatter them before the enemy — or the enemies —; the back and not the face will I show them in the day of their perdition. To this day the sentence of God remains upon the Jews; they have been scattered throughout the whole world before the enemy, the devil, or the enemies, the demons, and, though day and night in the synagogues of Satan they call upon the name of God, God shows them his back and not his face, so that they may understand that he is always withdrawing and never coming to them. Now the day of the perdition of the Jews is the whole time after the Savior's passion until the consummation of the age, so that, after the fullness of the nations has come in, then all Israel may be saved.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.18":["And they said: Come and let us devise devices against Jeremiah! For the law shall not perish from the priest, nor counsel from the wise, nor the word from the prophet; come and let us strike him with the tongue, and let us not heed all his words! This was both then the thought of the Jews against Jeremiah, or against the Lord the Savior, and is today the thought of heretics against his servants: to contrive calumnies and to forestall holy men with accusation, and not to consider what truth those men speak, but what falsehood they themselves may contrive; for they boast that the law of God and counsel and utterance remain in their own priests and wise men and false prophets, though Scripture says: Into a perverse soul wisdom shall not enter.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.19-21":["Attend to me, O Lord, and hear the voice of my adversaries! Is evil repaid for good, that they have dug a pit for my soul? Remember that I stood in your sight to speak good for them and to turn away your indignation from them! Therefore give their children up to famine and lead them into the hands of the sword; let their wives be childless and widows, and let their husbands be put to death, let their young men be pierced by the sword in battle, let an outcry be heard from their houses! In the type of the Savior, indeed, Jeremiah suffers all these things from the people of the Jews, who were afterwards laid waste when the Babylonian came; but they are fulfilled more fully and more perfectly in Christ. And when the city was overthrown they were slaughtered by the Roman sword, not because of idolatry, which did not exist at that time, but because of the killing of the Son of God, when the whole people together cried out: Away, away with such a man; we have no king but Caesar! And their imprecation has been fulfilled by everlasting damnation: His blood be upon us and upon our children! For they had dug a pit for Christ and had said: Let us take him away from the land of the living! — he who had such great mercy toward them, and stood in the sight of the Father to speak good for them and to turn away his indignation from them, that on the cross also he said: Father, forgive them! For they do not know what they are doing. We pass quickly over what is plain, so as to linger on the more obscure passages, by no means following the ravings of certain people who interpret this even of a captivity of the heavenly Jerusalem, but pursuing the clear history and the most manifest prophecy with all confidence in the words and the senses.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.22":["For you will bring a robber upon them suddenly, because they have dug a pit to catch me and have hidden snares for my feet. But you, O Lord, know all their counsel against me unto death; do not be propitious to their iniquity, and let their sin not be blotted out from before your face; let them be falling — or stumbling — in your sight; in the time of your fury consume them or deal with them! If we understand it of Jeremiah, let us refer the sudden robber to Nebuchadnezzar; if of the Savior, which is truer and better, to the Roman army. And lest the sentence of God should seem unjust, he sets out what they did against Christ, the Son of God, and what they suffered.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.18.23":["But what he adds: do not be propitious to their iniquity, and let their sin not be blotted out from before your face, is by no means contrary to the earlier sentence, in which he entreats the Father for the people; but after the time given for repentance has passed and they persevere in their crime, they are punished not so much for themselves as for others, lest a sin left unpunished should harm the rest by its example. And what he adds: let them be stumbling — or falling — in your sight, is like that saying of Isaiah and of the apostle Peter: and you shall not stumble as on a stone of offense and a rock of scandal; which the prophet also mentions in the psalms: The stone which the builders rejected, this has become the head of the corner; this was done by the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.19.1-3":["Thus says the Lord: Go and take a potter's earthen flask, and some of the elders of the people and of the elders of the priests, and go out to the valley of the son of Hinnom, which is by the entrance of the earthen gate — or Charsith —, and you shall preach — or you shall cry or you shall read — there the words which I shall speak to you, and you shall say: Hear the word of the Lord, O kings of Judah and inhabitants of Jerusalem! For 'potter's flask', which in Hebrew is called 'bocboc', the Seventy translated 'little jar', and for 'earthen gate' Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself, 'Harsith', for which the Seventy, according to their custom, added the Greek 'chi' for the aspiration of the letter 'heth', so as to say 'Charsith' for 'Harsith', just as there is 'Chebron' for 'Hebron', and 'Hiericho' for 'Hieriho'. Now divine Scripture wishes the people to be taught not only by the ears but also by the eyes; for, as we have already said, what reaches the mind by sight is retained in the mind more than what reaches it by hearing. Take, he says, a flask — or an earthen little jar — and some of the elders of the people and of the priests, and go out to the valley of the son of Hinnom, of which we spoke above, where there was the shrine of Baal and the grove and wood watered by the springs of Siloam. 'And this valley,' he says, 'is by the gate' which in the Hebrew tongue is called 'Harsith', that is, 'earthen'. 'And you shall preach — or you shall read — there the words which I shall speak with you, so that what I am about to say may be heard there.' And the reason why, as we have already said, 'you shall preach' and 'you shall cry' and 'you shall read' are all set down, is that the Hebrew word 'carath' signifies three things. And he wishes both the kings of Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, that is, both the royal stock and the whole people, to hear what he is about to say, so that those who refuse to hear may be without excuse.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.19.3-5":["Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Behold, I will bring affliction — or evils — upon this place, so that the ears of everyone who hears of it — or of them — shall tingle, because they have forsaken me and have made this place strange and have poured libations in it to strange gods, whom neither they nor their fathers nor the kings of Judah knew; and they have filled this place with the blood of the innocent and have built the high places of the Baals to burn their sons with fire as a burnt offering to the Baals, which I did not command — or think of — nor speak, nor did they come up into my heart. And some foolishly refer this place to the heavenly Jerusalem. So that I need not always give warning of this, let it suffice to have said this once: that an explanation of this kind, nay rather heresy, is to be avoided, which overturns what is plain and tries to bring certain conjuring tricks into the churches of Christ. But no one doubts that they set up the idol of Baal in the temple of God, or else in the shrine which was in the valley of the son of Hinnom, where there were both the grove of Baal and his altar, upon which they sacrificed and burned their sons. These things the Lord neither thought of nor spoke, nor did they come up into his heart; not that God was ignorant of things to come, but that he says he does not know what is unworthy of his knowledge, according to that word of the Gospel: Depart from me, workers of iniquity, I do not know you! For the Lord knows those who are his, and he who does not know shall not be known. Or certainly these things too, like others, are to be taken of God anthrōpopathōs [in human terms]. Now every heretic forsakes God and makes the place strange to God's habitation, the place which he has polluted by his deceit, and pours libations to strange gods, whom neither he himself nor his fathers knew, namely the apostles and apostolic men; and the kings of Judah, that is, the patriarchs of the heretics, fill the place that was once God's with the blood of the deceived and the innocent — for unless a man is foolish and simple, he is not quickly overturned — and they build the high places of the Baals when they claim to be disputing about sublime things, and they burn to the idol their sons, whom they have begotten in heresy. All of which the Lord says he does not know, and that it never came up into his thought.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.19.6":["Therefore, behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and this place shall no longer be called Topheth and the valley of the son of Hinnom, but the valley of slaughter. LXX: Therefore, behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and this place shall no longer be called ruin and polyandrion of the son of Hinnom, but polyandrion of slaughter. What the valley of the son of Hinnom is, which in Hebrew is called 'gehennom', whence they think 'gehennam' [Gehenna] too was named, we have said above. But I wonder what the Seventy meant by putting diaptōsin, that is, 'ruin', for 'Topheth', and polyandrion, which signifies 'a multitude of men', for 'valley', unless perhaps because there the people fell and a multitude of men was slain, either spiritually in the error of idolatry or, according to the letter, by the Babylonian army, as is read more plainly in what follows.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.19.7-9":["And I will scatter the counsel of Judah and Jerusalem in this place, and I will overthrow them by the sword before their enemies and by the hand of those who seek their lives, and I will give their corpses as food to the birds of the heaven and to the beasts of the earth, and I will make this city an astonishment and a hissing; everyone who passes by it shall be astonished and shall hiss over all its wounds. And I will feed them with the flesh of their sons and with the flesh of their daughters, and each one shall eat the flesh of his friend in the siege and in the distress in which their enemies, and those who seek their lives, shall shut them in. Although we know that these things also befell the people in the Babylonian captivity, yet they refer more fully to the times of the Savior, when they were besieged by Vespasian and Titus and their city in the times of Hadrian collapsed into everlasting ashes, so that those who had offered their sons to idols should themselves afterwards, driven by the necessity of famine, turn them into use as food, and the flesh of all should be given to the birds of the heaven and to the beasts of the earth, so that those who had abused God's gifts for impiety and had sacrificed their own offspring to idols should make their bellies the tombs of their children.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.19.10-11":["And you shall break the flask before the eyes of the men who will go — or will go out — with you, and you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord of hosts: So will I break this people and this city, as a potter's vessel is broken, which cannot be restored any more. This is plainly said not of the Babylonian but of the Roman captivity; for after the Babylonians both the city was restored and the people was brought back into Judea and restored to its former abundance. But after the captivity which happened under Vespasian and Titus, and afterwards under Hadrian, the ruins of Jerusalem will remain until the consummation of the age, although the Jews think that a golden and jeweled Jerusalem is to be restored to them, and again victims and sacrifices and the marriages of the saints and the kingdom of the Lord the Savior on earth. Although we do not follow these things, yet we cannot condemn them, because many churchmen and martyrs have said them, so that each one may abound in his own sense and all things may be reserved to the judgment of the Lord. And just as an earthen vessel of clay, once it has been broken, cannot be re-formed into its old appearance, so too the people of the Jews and Jerusalem, overthrown, will not have their former state. Indeed, the very name of the city today is void, and it is called Aelia after Aelius Hadrian, and together with its former habitation it has also lost its former name, to strike down the pride of its inhabitants. And the names of the holy Cross and of the Resurrection signify not a city but a place, and not the greatness of the riches of old, through which the people of the Jews perished, but the glory of holiness, which our poor little Bethlehem possesses, having not gold and jewels, but the bread which was born in it.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.19.11-13":["And they shall be buried — or they shall bury — in Topheth, because there is no other place for burying. So will I do to this place, says the Lord, and to its inhabitants, that I will make this city like Topheth — or like ruin —. And the houses of Jerusalem and the houses of the kings of Judah shall be like the place Topheth or of ruin. But what is added in the Seventy: all the houses of the kings of Judah like the place Topheth, is not found in the Hebrew. And there follows:\n\nUnclean are all the houses on whose housetops — or roofs — they have sacrificed to all the host of heaven and have poured out libations to strange gods. What he had said above: this place shall no longer be called Topheth and the valley of the son of Hinnom, but the valley of slaughter, he now sets out more plainly: that so great a slaughter will take place in that very place that the people will be buried there in heaps and the place once of religion will become a tomb of the dead; that the city itself too, which overhangs this place, will become like Topheth — for which the Seventy translated 'ruin' —; and that the houses of Jerusalem also and the palaces of the kings will be turned into similar ruins. And the reason is given: because they were unclean and polluted by the crime of idolatry, in that on their housetops and roofs they sacrificed to the sun and the moon and the stars of heaven and burned incense, and were not content with this error, but sacrificed to demons and poured out libations to strange gods.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.19.14-15":["Then Jeremiah came from Topheth, where the Lord had sent him to prophesy, and he stood in the court of the house of the Lord and said to all the people: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Behold, I will bring upon this city and upon all its towns all the evils which I have spoken against it, because they have hardened their neck so as not to hear my words. After Jeremiah, in Topheth, in the presence of the elders of the people whom he had brought with him, broke the flask, or jar, and prophesied with the words of the Lord the breaking of the people and of the city of Jerusalem, he returns and stands in the court of the house of the Lord and speaks to the whole multitude, which had refused to go to the place Topheth, that the Lord is about to bring upon the city of Jerusalem and upon all its towns the evils which he has spoken against it. And lest we think the sentence cruel, he gives the reasons why he is about to bring evils: 'because,' he says, 'they have hardened their neck so as not to hear my words, not willing to repent even after many impieties.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.1-2":["And Pashhur son of Immer, the priest, who had been appointed prince in the house of the Lord, heard Jeremiah prophesying these words. And Pashhur struck Jeremiah the prophet and put him in the stocks that were in the upper gate of Benjamin in the house of the Lord. For 'Phassur' the Seventy translated 'Phaschor', which means 'blackness of mouth'; and for the 'stocks' which we have given, the Seventy and Theodotion translated 'cataracten' [a trapdoor], Symmachus basanistērion or streblōtērion, both of which signify 'tortures'; we, however, said 'neruum' [stocks] according to common usage, a kind of torture that we also read of in the Acts of the Apostles, when the apostles Paul and Silas were given into the custody of the prison. Now this man was the high priest of the temple, and, the dignity of the priesthood having been given to him, he abuses it perversely, not to teach and to correct by word, but to terrify by torments. Hence both the Savior and the apostle Paul are beaten at the command of the high priest. Nor is it any wonder if even today the servants of God are beaten by Phaschor, are sent into prison, are kept in dreadful custody; for this power is given by God, so that the faith of the prophets may be shown. Yet he who beats is not the greater, but he who is beaten is the stronger. And the prophet patiently accepts the judgment of God and does not cry out against the blows, but considers the one who commands them. 'Emmer' means 'word', from which 'blackness' is often begotten, not by the fault of the parent, but by the wickedness of the one who degenerates. And the 'torture' by which the prophet is tormented is seen in the 'right' part, which is what 'Beniamin' means, and in the 'lofty' gate, which indicates not truth but the unjust power of the high priest.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.3":["And when it had dawned on the morrow, Pashhur brought Jeremiah out of the stocks, and Jeremiah said to him: Not Pashhur has the Lord called your name, but terror on every side. Here too all have likewise translated both the name of the high priest and the kind of torture as above. And the name of the high priest is changed so that from the name the punishment of things to come may be shown. 'By no means,' he says, 'will you have blackness of mouth and the rule of unjust power, but you will be led captive to Babylon — for this is what 'terror on every side' or 'round about' signifies —, 'so that, trembling and uncertain of your own safety, you may look around this way and that and dread the adversaries coming against you.' For 'terror', which in the Hebrew is written 'magur', the Seventy and Theodotion translated metoikon, that is 'migrant'; Aquila's second edition 'stranger', the first 'looking around'; Symmachus 'carried off' or 'gathered' and 'compelled'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.4-6":["For thus says the Lord: Behold, I will give you into terror, and all your friends, and they shall fall by the sword of their enemies, and your eyes shall see it; and all Judah I will give into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall carry them over into Babylon and shall strike them with the sword. And I will give all the substance — or strength — of this city, and all its labor, and every price — or glory — and all the treasures of the kings of Judah I will give into the hand of their enemies, and they shall plunder them and take them and lead them into Babylon. But you, Pashhur, and all the inhabitants of your house shall go into captivity, and you shall come into Babylon, and there you shall die and there you shall be buried, and all your friends, to whom you prophesied a lie. According to the earlier interpretation, by which the name 'Phassur' had been changed so that he should be called 'Magur', all likewise translated so as to signify either 'terror' or 'sojourning' or 'removal' and 'transfer' and 'migration'. And he threatens that he, with his friends, is now at any moment to be taken and handed over into the hands of the enemy; and that the whole people of the tribe of Judah is to be held in the hands of the Babylonian king, some to be killed by the sword and others to be led into captivity; and that all the riches, both of the city and of the royal treasuries, are to be seized by the enemies; and that Pashhur himself, with all his kindred and household, is to be led into captivity and will die in Babylon, because he deceived the people by his lie, promising them by no means things true and sad, but prosperous things through a lie. At the same time the patience and the prudence of the prophet must be noted: sent into prison, he is silent and conquers the injury by silence, and yet he does not conceal what he knows is to come, so that at least in this way the false-prophet high priest may cease to sin and may entreat the mercy of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.7":["Others, however, think that Isaiah offered himself for this reason, because he supposed that prosperous things were to be announced to the people: but because he heard: Go, say to this people: With your ears you shall hear and shall not understand, and seeing you shall see and shall not know (Isa 6:9), for that reason, in what follows, when the voice of the Lord says to him: Cry, he does not cry at once, but asks, what shall I cry? And Jeremiah, to whom it had been said: Take the cup, and you shall give drink to all the nations to whom I shall send you (Jer 25:15), gladly accepting the cup of punishments to pledge it to the hostile nations, that they might drink and vomit and fall, after he heard, go, and first you shall give drink to Jerusalem, answered: You have deceived me, Lord, and I have been deceived (Jer 20:7). This is an observation of the Hebrews. For our part, however, we say that it was not of rashness but of obedience that he offered himself to the Lord to be sent.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","As for what is set down above, Why have you made us stray (Isa 63:17)?, in what sense it is to be taken Jeremiah teaches most fully: You have led me astray, O Lord, and I was led astray; you took hold of me, and you prevailed (Jer 20:7). For while you promise me mercy, and like a merciful father you conceal your severity, and like a skilful physician you hide the sharpest knife so as not to frighten the sick man before you heal him, you have made me careless.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","You have seduced me, O Lord, and I was seduced — or you have deceived me, O Lord, and I was deceived —; you were stronger than I and you prevailed — or you held fast and you were able —; I have become a derision all the day, all mock me, for long since I speak crying out iniquity, and I keep proclaiming devastation or for with my bitter word I shall laugh, I shall call upon transgression and misery. The prophet says that he was deceived by the Lord because, hearing at the beginning: I have given you as a prophet to the nations, and again: behold, I have set you this day over nations and over kingdoms, to uproot and to destroy and to ruin and to scatter and to build and to plant, he supposed that he would say nothing against the people of the Jews, but against the various nations round about, and therefore he willingly took up prophecy; and that the contrary came about, so that, preaching the captivity of Jerusalem, he endured persecutions and straits. And as for what he added: I have become a derision all the day, all mock me: it is because they think that he lied about everything, and that all the things he foretold were to come were lies; for the prophet had thought that what the Lord threatened would come at once, and the people supposed that what had not come at once would never come at all.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.8-9":["For the word of the Lord has become to me a reproach and a derision all the day. And I said: I will not remember him — or I will not name the Lord — nor will I speak any more in his name. And there came to be in my heart as it were a burning fire, shut up in my bones, and I fainted — or I was dissolved — not enduring to bear it; for I heard the insults — or the reproach — of many, and terror round about — or of those gathered from every side —: Persecute, and let us persecute him! 'When I cried out and said that the Babylonian army was already coming and that the sword of the enemy would plunder everything, the word of the Lord was turned for me into a reproach and a derision, since they think the delay of the prophecy is a lie; for which reason I resolved in my mind that I would no longer speak words to the people of God nor name his name. When, overcome by shame, I had resolved this modestly indeed, but foolishly, there came to be', he says, 'in my heart as it were a burning fire, shut up in my bones, and I fainted or I was dissolved on every side, so that I cannot bear it.' For the divine word, conceived in the mind and not brought forth by the mouth, burns in the breast; hence Paul too says: if I preach the Gospel, there is no glory for me; for necessity lies upon me; for woe is me if I do not preach the Gospel. For if I do this willingly, I have a reward; but if unwillingly, a stewardship has been entrusted to me. And at Athens, seeing the city given over to idolatry, he was stirred in spirit and burned with his whole mind; and a little later in the same book we read: and when Silas and Timothy had come from Macedonia, Paul was pressing on with the word, testifying to the Jews that Jesus is the Christ.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.8":["'And I cry out the Babylonian devastation and the iniquity of the enemies, by which my people is to be oppressed.' But if we follow the Seventy in what they said: for with my bitter word I shall laugh, I shall call upon transgression and misery, this is the sense: 'I know that the present sadness is to be exchanged for future joy, according to what is written: blessed are those who weep, for they shall laugh; and therefore I willingly endure misery and iniquity and affliction, so that I even desire them and call upon them, and make up for the brevity of the injury with the eternity of happiness'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.10-11":["All the men who were peaceable toward me and guarding my side: if somehow he may be deceived, and we may prevail against him and take our revenge on him! But the Lord is with me as a mighty warrior. When adversaries have risen up, and those who were once friends and peaceable have turned to war and have wished to lay snares for us, let us not be greatly concerned, but let us choose to say this, which the prophet too speaks: But the Lord is with me as a mighty warrior.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.10":["But many of the teachers in the Church even today endure similar things, hearing the reproach of many gathered round about against them and who say: Persecute, and let us persecute them!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.11":["Therefore those who persecute me shall fall and shall be weak — or therefore they persecuted and could not understand —, they shall be greatly confounded, because they did not understand the reproach or the everlasting ignominy, which will never be blotted out. Because they persecuted, therefore they could not understand the prophetic word, and vehement confusion followed upon their ignorance; nor did they understand the everlasting ignominy which will hold them fast and will be blotted out by no forgetting. Let them say, then, what they will, the men once peaceable and those who guarded my side, and let them desire to deceive, provided that so great a vindication and such great rewards attend the righteous man and teacher of the Church after persecution.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.12":["And you, Lord of hosts, tester of the righteous — or who test righteous things —, who see the reins and the heart, let me see, I pray, your vengeance on them! for to you I have revealed my cause. The Lord alone is the one who knows how to test righteousness, just as he alone is the one who beholds the inward parts of the heart; hence Jesus too, knowing the thoughts of men, is God not by advancement, as some think, but by nature. Something of the kind sounds in the Psalms as well: no one living shall be justified in your sight. If one who lives in virtues is not justified, how much more one who is dead in sins! And though the righteous man knows that he has God as his champion, yet through the impatience of human frailty he desires to see even now what he knows is to come. And he revealed his cause to him who says in another place: vengeance is mine, I will repay, says the Lord. Happy, then, the conscience whose cause is revealed to the Lord, as the Apostle says: everything that is made manifest is light.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.13":["Sing to the Lord, praise the Lord, for he has delivered the soul of the poor man from the hand of the most wicked! He who is poor in spirit and has not the riches which have received their consolation in this world, and of which Paul speaks: only that we should be mindful of the poor, when he has obtained vengeance from the Lord, praises the Lord in spirit and glories that he has been rescued from the hand of the most wicked. Yet all this comes about not by our merit, but by the grace of him who delivered the poor man; and he has not the riches of a pride that falls to ruin, but the humility of a poor man set free.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.14-15":["Cursed be the day on which I was born; the day on which my mother bore me, let it not be blessed! Cursed be the man — or the person —, who brought the news to my father, saying: A son, a male child, is born to you, and as it were made him glad with joy! Let that person be as the cities are which the Lord overthrew and did not repent! Let him hear a cry in the morning and wailing at the time of noon, who — or because he — did not kill me from the womb, so that my mother might become my grave, and her womb an everlasting conception! Why did I come forth from the womb, to see labor and sorrow, and that my days should be consumed in confusion? Those who think that souls were in the heavenly places and were cast down from a better state into a worse use this and testimonies of this kind, namely that it would have been better to dwell in the heavenly places than to dwell in the earthly ones and take on the body of humiliation, seeking certain new, nay rather already old, arguments for their heresy. We, however, reading that saying of blessed Job: cursed be the day on which I was born, and that night in which they said: behold, a male, and: cursed be the person who brought the news to my father, saying: a boy is born to you, fit them to this testimony, namely that it is better not to be than to live in torments, according to what is written: death is rest to a man for whom God has shut his way, and again: why is light given to the wretched, and life to those who are in bitterness of soul? And in the Gospel we read it said simply: it would have been better for him if he had not been born, not that one who has not been born exists, but that it is better not to be than to exist wretchedly; for it is one thing not to be at all, another, while one exists, to be tormented without any intermission, just as we prefer a quiet death to a miserable life. Hence Amos too calls the day a day of darkness, a day of affliction; and Jacob, because he lived in toil and distress, calls the days of his life few and evil; and the apostle Paul says: that he might rescue us from the present evil age, and again: redeeming the time, because the days are evil. The Hebrews reckon the fifth month, in which Jerusalem was taken and the temple overthrown, as that of Jeremiah's birth, by certain inextricable and incredible arguments. But if they were able to prove this, how they could interpret the testimony of Job I do not know; unless perhaps they claim that that day too, by a certain prefiguration and prophecy of things to come, belonged to the overthrow of the temple.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.20.16-18":["And as for his imprecating the likeness of the overthrown cities, I think it is said of Sodom and Gomorrah; and all time in mourning, so that in the morning there should be a cry and at noon wailing. And as for what he adds: who did not kill me in the womb, they think that God is meant. 'That there might be for me,' he says, 'an everlasting conception'; all of which is said hyperbolically. Finally he sets out the reasons why he prefers death to life, and not being at all to existing wretchedly, adding: why did I come forth from the womb, to see labor and sorrow, and that my days should be consumed in confusion?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.21.1-2":["The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord, when King Zedekiah sent to him Pashhur son of Malchiah, and Zephaniah son of Maaseiah, the priest, saying: Inquire of the Lord for us, for Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, is fighting against us; if perhaps the Lord will do for us according to all his wonders, and he will withdraw from us. Above, the high priest Phassur or Phaschor, who struck Jeremiah, had Immer as his father, but this Phassur is the son of Malchiah; this is said so that no one may think him the same man. Now King Zedekiah sends to the prophet without wishing either the people or the princes to know, so that by a secret message through envoys he may learn what sentence the Lord holds concerning the city of Jerusalem and the people of the Jews. And what he adds: for Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, is fighting against us, shows that Zedekiah asks this of the prophet when Jerusalem was already besieged. And it must be noted that in the prophets, and above all in Ezekiel and Jeremiah, the order of kings and of times is by no means kept, but, in reverse order, what according to the history happened later is related first, and what was done first is related later; for it is one thing to write history, another to write prophecy. Thus in the present place Zedekiah, who was captured with the city of Jerusalem, is written of as sending to Jeremiah, and that at the time when Jerusalem was being besieged; and afterward is narrated the history of Jehoiakim his brother, who was king before him, and of Jehoiachin, that is, Jeconiah, who was the son of Jehoiakim, concerning whom we shall speak in what follows.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.21.4-6":["And Jeremiah said to them: Thus shall you say to Zedekiah: Thus says the Lord God of Israel: Behold, I will turn back the vessels of war — or the weapons of war —, which are in your hands and with which you fight against the king of Babylon and the Chaldeans, who besiege you round about the walls, and I will gather those things — or those men — into the midst of this city, and I will vanquish you with an outstretched hand and with a strong arm — or a lofty one — and in fury and in indignation and in great wrath, and I will strike the inhabitants of this city; men and beasts shall die of a great pestilence. 'In vain', he says, 'do you wish to resist the besieging Chaldeans and prepare weapons of war, of which you will have the use only in the midst of the city, so that you may seem to be armed. For the rest, while they besiege you, I will vanquish you with an outstretched hand and with a strong arm and will strike you with my fury and indignation, so that both you and everything that breathes may die of famine and pestilence in the midst of the city.' And this indeed we read was done; for without any ring of assault or din of combatants the city was taken by siege alone, so that they had none to conquer, but only those to capture; better off, he says, were those wounded by the sword than those slain by famine.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.21.7":["And after this, says the Lord, I will give Zedekiah, king of Judah, and his servants and his people, and those who have been left in this city from the plague and the sword and the famine, into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, and into the hand of their enemies, and into the hand of those who seek their life; and he shall strike them with the edge of the sword, and he shall not be moved, nor spare, nor have mercy. This, which we translated: into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, and into the hand of their enemies, is not found in the Seventy; and for what we said: he shall not be moved, nor spare, nor have mercy, the Seventy translated: I will not spare, nor have mercy. And it is better according to the Hebrew, so that the cruel and inflexible sentence may seem to belong to the Babylonian king rather than to the Lord. First, then, prophecy was made concerning the whole city; now it is foretold particularly to Zedekiah and to his people, those who would be left after the plague, the sword and the famine, that he is to be captured by Nebuchadnezzar, the Babylonian king, and to be slain with his friends by the sword, and that he should hope for no mercy from him whose covenant and bond he had abandoned by perjury.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.21.8-9":["And to this people you shall say: Thus says the Lord: Behold, I set before you the way of life and the way of death. He who dwells in this city shall die by the sword and by famine and by plague; but he who goes out and deserts to the Chaldeans, who besiege you, shall live, and his soul shall be to him as a spoil; for I have set my face against this city for evil and not for good, says the Lord; into the hand of the king of Babylon it shall be given, and he shall burn it with fire. To those who had been sent by the king and had come to beseech the prophet to inquire of the Lord for them, he had given the answer above, and what they should report to King Zedekiah; now he urges them to answer the people, and gives counsel that they desert to the Chaldeans against the royal will; and no one is unaware that this was dangerous for the prophet. Hence he even calls down death upon himself and says: cursed be the day on which I was born, and: why did I come forth from the womb, to see labor and sorrow?; not that it is a light thing to counsel those not yet captured to hand themselves over to captivity of their own accord — as if men about to suffer shipwreck were ordered, before the shipwreck and the breaking up of the ship, to seize oars and planks and commit themselves to the waves — but that it is more tolerable to live as captives in whatever way than to be consumed by the sword, famine and plague. There are those who expound this passage according to tropology thus: that it is better to give oneself to secular studies, and above all to philosophy, than to remain in that church in which there is a famine of the word of God and the whole people dies by the sword of the heretics, by the dearth of doctrine and by the heretical plague.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.21.11":["And to the house of the king of Judah: Hear the word of the Lord, house of David! Thus says the Lord: Judge judgment in the morning, and rescue the one oppressed by violence from the hand of the one falsely accusing, lest perhaps my indignation go forth — or so that my indignation may not go forth — like fire, and be kindled, and there be none to quench it; and what follows: because of the wickedness of your pursuits, is not found in the Seventy. Because he had said above: and to this people you shall say: thus says the Lord, he now accordingly adds: and to the house of the king of Judah, so that there is understood: 'you shall say: thus says the Lord'; for by apo koinou [in common, i.e. shared by both clauses] both what precedes and what follows are joined, so that the sense is: 'and to this people you shall say: thus says the Lord, and to the house of the king of Judah you shall say: thus says the Lord'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.21.12":["Properly, however, the word is addressed to the royal house, chiefly on account of whose guilt the city is besieged, so that it may amend its error by repentance and obtain the mercy of the Lord. 'Judge', he says, 'judgment in the morning' — not in the darkness of iniquity, but in the light of righteousness — 'and rescue the one oppressed by violence from the hand of the false accuser, so that you do not respect persons in judgment, but, when the rich oppress the poor, the command of God may prevail more with you than the power of the persecutor! And if', he says, 'you do this, the fire of my fury will by no means be kindled among you, nor will it find material to consume.' And in this place an incredible mercy of God is shown: those of whom he had said above: for I have set my face against this city for evil and not for good; into the hand of the king of Babylon it shall be handed over, and fire shall burn it, he now, while the Lord's sentence is already bearing down upon them, calls to salvation; not that he does not know that the city of Jerusalem is to be captured, but so that free will may be preserved for man, and they may be seen to perish not through ignorance of the future but by their own will; just as the Savior too knew that the people would deny him and that he was to be crucified, inasmuch as he had often foretold this to the apostles, and nevertheless he admonished them, wishing to correct them toward repentance, so that whatever they afterward suffered befell them by their own fault, not by the harshness of the one who threatened.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.21.13-14":["Behold, I am against you, inhabitant of the solid and level valley, says the Lord, you who say: Who shall strike — or terrify — us, and who shall enter our houses? And I will visit upon you; and what follows: according to the fruit of your pursuits, says the Lord, is not found in the Seventy. And I will kindle, he says, a fire in its forest, and it shall devour everything round about it. For 'inhabitant of the solid and level valley' the Seventy translated: behold, I am against you who dwell in the level valley of Sor, for which Symmachus translated 'rock', Theodotion 'besieged', Aquila's first edition 'solid', the second 'Tyre'; for 'Sor' or 'Sur' in the Hebrew language means 'Tyre' and 'flint' and 'narrowed'. Now he speaks against Jerusalem, which was walled in by siege; or which, in the likeness of Tyre, is so surrounded by the Babylonian army, as by a great sea, that it cannot escape; or else which, like the hardest rock, thinks itself impregnable and strong because of the solidity and size of its walls, and says: 'who will be able to terrify us and who shall enter our houses?', whereas God, on the contrary, says: 'I will visit you; you will not be able to escape my eye. And I will visit you unto destruction and render to you the fruit of your wickedness, and I will kindle a fire in your forest: not the Babylonian, as you think, not the king of the Chaldeans, but my wrath will accomplish all these things.' And he calls Jerusalem and all the region around it a 'forest', because, having no fruit-bearing trees of good works, it has been made ready for burning. And fittingly he calls the 'valley' 'level', because it lies open to the enemies, and not a 'lofty mountain', which can be climbed only with difficulty; in accordance with which it is also said in Isaiah: the vision of the valley of Zion. Whatever is prophesied concerning the royal house and the mother city, let us refer to the ecclesiastical order and to the princes of the churches, those only, that is, who have given themselves over to pride and riches and wantonness. Nor will it be delivered from destruction at once simply because it is the royal house; just as among those who were of the stock of David very few were found who pleased the Lord, such as David himself, Hezekiah and Josiah, and a great part of the princes and of the royal stock provoked the wrath of the Lord against the whole people.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.1-2":["Thus says the Lord: Go down into the house of the king of Judah, and there you shall speak this word and say: Hear the word of the Lord, O king of Judah, who sit upon the royal seat of David, you and your servants and your people, who enter through these gates! Thus says the Lord: Do judgment and righteousness, and deliver him who is oppressed by force from the hand of the slanderer, and do not grieve the stranger and the orphan and the widow, nor oppress them unjustly — or impiously — and do not shed innocent blood in this place! For if you indeed do this word, there shall enter through the gates of this house kings of the race of David sitting upon his throne, and mounting chariots and horses, they and their servants and their people. But if you do not hear these words, by my own self I have sworn, says the Lord, that this house shall become a desolation. This vision, nay rather this word of the Lord, comes to the prophet either before Zedekiah sent messengers to him, or certainly after he had instructed the messengers what they should report to the king; Jeremiah is commanded not to speak to the king through messengers at all, but to go himself to the palace and speak to him there. And at the same time let us note the divine wisdom, in that through the messengers he orders sad tidings to be announced, mixed with prosperous ones too, should the king be willing to do repentance. Here, however, because he himself is commanded to go, he does not announce sad tidings and a captivity now on the very point of coming, but he advises what the king ought to do in order to escape the impending sentence of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.3-5":["Whatever was said to the royal house, let the bishops and their associates, the presbyters and deacons, and the whole ecclesiastical order understand: that if they do what has been commanded, and among other things do not shed innocent blood by causing even the least to stumble and by wounding the consciences of individuals, they will retain the dignity handed down to them by the Lord; but if they are unwilling to do it and despise it, they themselves will reduce the Church to desolation. Now those of the race of David enter through the gates of Jerusalem and sit upon the throne of him whose name means 'strong of hand', and they mount chariots and horses, when they rein in both their own disturbances and those of the people, and walk in the Church with composed step and with a choir of many virtues singing in harmony with them on every side. And that we may believe this to be true, he swears by himself, who, according to the Apostle, has no other greater by whom he could swear.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.3":["Now it is the proper work of kings to do judgment and righteousness, and to deliver from the hand of slanderers those oppressed by force, and to give help to the stranger and the orphan and the widow, who are more easily oppressed by the powerful. And to instill in them a greater care for the precepts of God, he added: 'Do not grieve, so that you not only rescue them, but do not even allow them, through your connivance, to be grieved by others, and do not shed innocent blood in this place!' For to punish murderers and the sacrilegious and poisoners is not the shedding of blood, but the ministry of the laws.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.4-5":["'If', he says, 'you do these things, O kings of Judah, you will hold your former power and will enter the gates of Jerusalem with pomp; but if you are unwilling to do them, O royal house, it will be not so much the Lord's cruelty as your own will that the whole city is reduced to desolation.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.6-7":["He threatens, therefore, the royal house and the city of Jerusalem and the temple, which he calls the head of Lebanon, that it is to be reduced to desolation together with all its cities, not by the power of the Babylonian king but by the command of the Lord, who says: I will sanctify against you one who slays. And Nebuchadnezzar and all his army are called 'holy' because he carries out the sentence of God. 'And they shall cut down', he says, 'your choice cedars' — all the powerful men and princes of the city — 'and cast into the fire, so that the flame devouring all things may consume them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.6":["For thus says the Lord concerning the house — or to the house — of the king of Judah: You are Gilead to me, the head — or the beginning — of Lebanon; if I do not make you a desolation, cities uninhabitable. And I will sanctify against you a man who slays, and his weapons, and they shall cut down your choice cedars and hurl — or cast — into the fire. And many nations shall pass through this city, and each shall say to his neighbor: Why has the Lord done thus to this great city? And they shall answer: Because they forsook the covenant of the Lord their God and worshiped strange gods and served them. Scripture records that Gilead, which the half-tribe of Manasseh possessed, lies beyond the Jordan; on this mountain Laban overtook Jacob as he fled, and the mountain, according to Genesis, received the name sōros, that is, 'mound of witness', because Jacob and Laban swore there, after stones had been gathered into a heap. Now it is the head or beginning of Lebanon, a lofty mountain which is planted all over with cedars, according to that word of David as he sings: and the Lord will shake the cedars of Lebanon, and elsewhere: I saw the wicked man exalted and lifted up like the cedars of Lebanon; and in Zechariah we read: Open your gates, O Lebanon, and let fire devour your cedars! Therefore in the present passage, since he was speaking to the royal house, he speaks by metaphoran [metaphor] to the temple, or to the house of the stock of Judah, because it is itself on a height, or because remedies for all sins were sought from the temple and the sanctuary; whence this same prophet also records: Is there no resin in Gilead, or is there no physician there? Why has the healing of the daughter of my people not gone up?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.7":["Let the royal house of our city, too, hear this, and its princes and cedars, which lift their crown up to the clouds and say in their pride: 'Who will not see us?', that they will quickly be consumed by the flame of the Lord if they are unwilling to submit to his precepts. But the sanctification of the slayer and of his weapons is one thing, that of the priests and of those who minister to the Lord another.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.8-9":["And when everything has been destroyed, many nations will pass through the city and the temple, which before they were not permitted to enter, and each will say to his neighbor, why the Lord has wrought so sudden and so great an overthrow upon a city so renowned and great. And those who are asked', he says, 'will answer and set forth the causes of the ruins, saying: because they forsook the covenant of the Lord their God and venerated idols in place of God.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.10-12":["Do not weep for the dead, nor mourn over him! Lament with weeping him who goes out, for he shall not return again, nor see the land of his birth! For thus says the Lord to Shallum son of Josiah, king of Judah, who reigned in place of Josiah his father, who went out from this place: He shall not return here any more, but in the place to which I have carried him away, there he shall die, and he shall not see this land any more. Josiah, a righteous king, had three sons, Jehoahaz and Jehoiakim and Zedekiah. The first of them Pharaoh Neco, king of Egypt, led captive into Egypt, and there he died; and he set up in his place as king his brother Eliakim, and changed his name to Jehoiakim. When he died, his son Jeconiah reigned, who was led into captivity by Nebuchadnezzar, the Babylonian king, together with his mother and the princes; in his place his uncle Zedekiah reigned, who, when Jerusalem was taken, was led into Babylon. And so it is asked who this is who ought not to be wept for, and who is to be led into captivity and will never return, since three were taken and carried away. The Hebrews think that this fits all of them, that is, Jehoahaz and Jeconiah and Zedekiah, and that all the sons of Josiah are called 'Sellem' or 'Sellum', which means 'consummation' or 'completion', because the kingdom of Judah came to its end in them. To me, however, this seems to be said properly of Zedekiah, about whom the prophecy of both the present and the previous chapter is, in whom the kingdom of Judah truly came to its end, under whom the city also was taken, and who, it is written, was led into Babylon and died there. He is 'Sellum', that is, 'consummation' and 'completion', son of Josiah, king of Judah, who reigned in place of Josiah his father. Jeconiah, on the other hand, was not the son but the grandson of Josiah, the son of Jehoiakim, so that from the beginning of the vision, when there sent to Jeremiah King Zedekiah Pashhur son of Malchiah, and Zephaniah son of Maaseiah, the priest, down to this chapter, we are to understand that everything is said to the king and about King Zedekiah.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.13-14":["Woe to him who builds his house in unrighteousness and his upper rooms not in judgment, who oppresses his friend for nothing and does not pay him his wage; who says: I will build myself a broad house and spacious upper rooms, who opens windows for himself and makes ceilings of cedar and paints them with red ochre! Will you reign, since you compare yourself to the cedar? Did not your father eat and drink, and do judgment and righteousness then, when it was well with him? He judged the cause of the poor and needy to his own good; was it not therefore, because he knew me?, says the Lord. But your eyes and heart are for avarice, and for shedding innocent blood, and for slander, and for the course of evil work. LXX: O you who build your house not with righteousness and your upper rooms not in judgment! With him his neighbor works for nothing, and he will not pay him his wage. You have built yourself a little house, upper rooms open to the wind, set off with windows and raftered with cedar and daubed with red ochre. Will you reign, because you contend against Achaz, your father? They shall not eat and they shall not drink; it was better for you to do judgment and good righteousness. They did not know, they did not judge judgment for the lowly, nor judgment for the poor. Is not this your not knowing me?, says the Lord. Behold, your eyes are not upright nor your heart good, but they are for your avarice and for shedding innocent blood, and for iniquity and murder, to do them. I have set down both editions in full, so that both the Hebrew truth and the difficulty of the common edition may be more easily recognized. Now the word is against Jehoiakim son of Josiah, king of Judah, of whom we spoke above, whom Pharaoh Neco, king of Egypt, set up in place of Jehoahaz his brother, whom he led bound into Egypt. And we read, and the history both of Kings and of Chronicles relates, that Jehoiakim son of Josiah reigned eleven years in Jerusalem, and reigned cruelly, and proved impious, and afterwards died; and yet his burial is not related, although holy Scripture has this custom, that it reports of all the kings both that they died and that they were buried. This one, however, it properly relates to have died and not been buried; of which we shall speak later. He laments, then, the aforesaid king, because he trusts in unrighteousness and thinks his royal dignity perpetual, and makes himself upper rooms, and oppresses his friends, and does not pay those who work for him their wage, and supposes the building of his palace to be everlasting.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.13-17":["We can, however, take this passage according to anagōgēn [the higher, spiritual sense] against the heretics, who build for themselves a house not great and most broad with the abundance of the Church, but a little one. And they build not with righteousness and judgment, desiring to plunder what belongs to others; whence it is said: You have built yourself a little house, upper rooms open to the wind, which are carried about by every wind of doctrine, and set off with windows; for they have no lasting building and no solid firmness. And raftered, he says, with cedar: they seem indeed to have the most beautiful rafters, but in the rains and the storm of persecutions they quickly rot and collapse. And daubed with red ochre: for they too promise the passion and the blood of the Lord, but they do not reign forever, because they contend with and provoke to anger 'araz', that is, 'cedar', their father. For every heretic is born in the Church, but is cast out of the Church, and contends and fights against his parent. And as for what he adds: They shall not eat and they shall not drink, 'the body and blood of the Savior' is understood; and the rest that is like these. And he says that all their error comes down from this, that they have not known God and do not have upright eyes, but their heart is bent on avarice, to plunder what belongs to others and to shed the blood of those they have deceived; for this is to commit murder. Obscure matters must be discussed more fully.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.15-17":["'Will you', says the divine word, 'be able to reign forever, since you desire to compare yourself to the lofty cedar, that is, to your father Josiah, a righteous king? Your father', he says, 'both ate and drank and enjoyed royal wealth; and yet he did not offend God in this, that he had riches, but pleased him in this, that he did judgment and righteousness, and therefore it was well with him, and will be, both in the present age and in the one to come. He judged', he says, 'the cause of the poor and needy, and to the refreshment of those whom he heard, and properly to his own good. And all these things turned out prosperously for him because he knew me, says the Lord. But your eyes, O Jehoiakim, turn aside to avarice, and, so that you may shed innocent blood, to slander and to the course of evil work.' But what sense the words have according to the Seventy I cannot understand; for while the rest agree with one another to some extent, as for what is added: Will you reign, since you contend with Achaz your father?, in place of which in the Hebrew is written 'araz', and this word means 'cedar', it is plain that it has no sense; and so too what follows: They shall not eat and they shall not drink, and the rest, which are so scattered and confused among themselves that without the truth of the Hebrew reading they have no intelligible meaning.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.18-19":["Therefore thus says the Lord to Jehoiakim son of Josiah, king of Judah: They shall not lament for him: Woe, brother, and woe, sister! They shall not cry out together for him: Woe, lord, and woe, renowned one! But with the burial of an ass he shall be buried, rotted and cast out beyond the gates of Jerusalem. This, which we have set down from the Hebrew: They shall not lament for him: Woe, brother, and woe, sister, is not found in the Seventy. And it is said properly against Jehoiakim, king of Judah, and the riddle which before seemed hidden is opened, and the ambiguity among the three brothers is resolved, so that the word is not about Jehoahaz nor about Zedekiah, but properly about Jehoiakim, who, the Hebrew history relates, was killed by raiding bands of the Chaldeans, of Syria, of the Ammonites and of the Moabites; whence also in Malachim [Kings] it is written that he died, and his burial is passed over in silence. In the book of Days we read that he was bound with chains and led into Babylon, and nothing further is reported of him. And finely he says that he is to be buried with 'the burial of an ass', so as to signify in other words that he would be unburied, that is, torn apart by beasts and birds. For this is the burial of an ass.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.20":["Go up to Lebanon and cry out, and in Bashan give your voice, and cry to those passing by, for all your lovers are crushed! I spoke to you; in your abundance you said: I will not hear! This is your way from your youth, that you have not heard my voice. The wind shall feed all your shepherds — or lovers — and your lovers — or friends — shall go into captivity; and then you shall be confounded and shall blush for all your wickedness. You who sit in Lebanon and nest in the cedars, how you groaned when pains came upon you like the pains of a woman in labor! By metaphoran of Lebanon and Bashan, regions and mountains beyond the Jordan, the word is directed to Jerusalem, because she trusted in Egypt in vain; or to Jehoiakim himself, who at that time was reigning in Jerusalem and had been set up as king by the Egyptians, because he hopes in vain for the help of the Egyptians, and because they too are to be overcome by the Babylonian king and led into captivity.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","And where we said: cry to those passing by, and in the Hebrew is written 'meabarim', the Seventy and Theodotion translated 'across the sea', Symmachus 'from opposite', to signify that the voice of the prophet must reach from Jerusalem as far as Mount Lebanon and Bashan.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.21-23":["And in saying: 'I spoke to you' — that is, God himself through the prophets — or: 'They spoke to you' — so that 'my prophets' is understood — 'and you in your abundance said: I will not hear', he convicts her of pride, and of having abused the greatness of her riches to despise God, and he relates that not only at this time but from the beginning, when she was led out of Egypt, she did not hear the voice of God; whence all her shepherds and princes too have been scattered hither and thither and have bowed their necks to the Babylonian captivity. And in adding: You who sit in Lebanon and nest in the cedars, he taunts her arrogance, which had grown out of the abundance of all things, and says that like a woman in childbirth sudden pain and an unexpected captivity will come upon her.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.24-26":["In commentaries and expositions, whose custom it is to set down the differing opinions of interpreters, I am accustomed to insert language of this kind: 'Some say this, others assert that, certain ones think thus.' For this both wretched Grunnius himself and, many years later, the disciple of Jovinian and of him have slandered and still slander me, saying that I set down my own opinions under others' names, which I do for the sake of kindness, so as not to seem to tear anyone apart by name. Since, then, kindness has been turned into slander, I now say both to him who is dead and to this one who lives and tries to restore his heresy, that their master Origen refers this passage to Christ: that he was torn like a ring from the hand of God the Father and sent into the land of captivity, into the valley of tears, and handed over to the cross; and that his mother too — no doubt the synagogue — was taken and cast out. And in recalling these things he is not afraid to understand what follows also: Land, land, land, hear the word of the Lord! Thus says the Lord: write this man abominated or barren, and the rest, of the Lord of majesty. And he writes this, lest his disciples dare deny it, in the fifth book of the Stromateis.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.24-27":["As I live, says the Lord: though Jeconiah son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, were a ring on my right hand, I will tear him — or you — from there, and I will give you into the hand of those who seek your life, and into the hand of those whose face you dread, and into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, — which is not found in the Seventy — and into the hand of the Chaldeans. And I will send you — or I will cast you — and your mother who bore you into a strange land, in which you were not born, and there you shall die; but to the land to which they lift up their soul, to return, there they shall not return. He had said above: 'To the house of the king of Judah you shall speak these things', and then: Go down into the house of the king of Judah, and again: Thus says the Lord concerning the house of the king of Judah; and in reversed order, after he has spoken about Zedekiah, who was the last of the kings in Jerusalem, he returns to Jehoiakim his brother, who reigned before him. This prophecy completed, he now speaks to the son of Jehoiakim, the grandson of Josiah, the king of Jerusalem, Jeconiah, who is also called by another name Jehoiachin, who was taken by Nebuchadnezzar and led into Chaldea with his mother and the princes and the craftsmen and many nobles, and died there. This, then, is what is said: 'If, as a ring does not leave the hand of the one who wears it and slips from the finger only with difficulty, Jeconiah were on my hand, yet I will tear him away and hand him over to the Babylonian king, and there he will die with his mother and all his companions, and he will no more see the land of Judea, which he longs for.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.24":["Wretched Grunnius, who opened his mouth to slander holy men and taught his tongue a lie, translated into Latin one book of Sextus the Pythagorean, a thoroughly pagan man, and divided it into two volumes, and dared to publish it under the name of the holy martyr Xystus, bishop of the city of Rome; in these volumes there is no mention of Christ, none of the Holy Spirit, none of God the Father, none of the patriarchs and prophets and apostles; and with his usual rashness and madness he named this book 'The Ring', and it is read through many provinces, and above all by those who preach apatheian [freedom from passion] and sinlessness. As, then, the Lord threatens that he will cast Jeconiah from his hand and finger like a ring, so I beseech the reader to throw away this wicked book, and, if he wishes, to read it as he reads the other books of the philosophers, not as a church volume.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.28":["Is this man Coniah an earthen vessel, and broken? Is he a vessel without any pleasure — or usefulness —? Why are he and his seed cast away and cast forth into a land which they did not know? For what we said: Is he an earthen vessel, and broken, Symmachus translated: Is he refuse or worthless sweepings, and cast away?; in place of which there is nothing in the Seventy, but they translated only this: Jeconiah has been dishonored like a vessel in which there is no usefulness. And when this is said of Jeconiah, son of Jehoiakim, does anyone dare to refer it to the type of Christ, and, from the fact that the Apostle says that the Lord Savior is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of all creation, that is, wisdom, word, truth, life and righteousness, to say that he is called the ring which is cast off or torn away from the hand of the Lord and handed over to King Nebuchadnezzar? They are cast away, he says, he and his seed, and cast forth into a land which they did not know, which no one doubts happened to Jeconiah. 'Iechonias' means 'the preparation of the Lord'; in the present passage the first syllable, that is, the name of the Lord, is taken away from him, and he is called 'Chonias', so that 'prepared for perdition and destruction' is understood.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.22.30":["Land, land, land, hear the speech of the Lord! Thus says the Lord: Write this man barren, a man who shall not prosper in his days; for there shall not be of his seed a man who sits upon the royal seat of David and has power any longer in Judah. If I should wish to note, place by place, how much the Seventy have either passed over or changed, it would become long, especially since the diligent reader can consider from each edition what has been changed, what added, what taken away. For 'barren' in the Hebrew is written 'ariri', which Aquila's first edition translated 'barren', the second anauxēton, that is, 'not growing', Symmachus 'empty', the Seventy and Theodotion 'abominable' and 'disowned'. And the question arises how the prophecy can stand that no one was begotten of his stock who sat upon the throne of David or was prince any longer in Judah, when our Lord and Savior was born of his seed, about whose birth Gabriel speaks to Mary: Behold, you will conceive in the womb and bear a son, and you will call his name Jesus. He will be great and will be called the Son of the Most High, and the Lord God will give him the seat of David his father, and he will reign over the house of Jacob forever, and of his kingdom there will be no end. We can therefore say this: that what is lacking in the Seventy, namely in his days he shall not prosper or shall not grow, has created the problem for those who do not know. For the Seventy translated: Write this man a disowned man, for there shall not grow from his seed a man who sits upon the throne of David, a prince any longer in Judah, which in the Hebrew is put twice; and those who wrote from the beginning, thinking this had been added in the Greek books, took it away. Let us answer, then, that in the days of Jeconiah no man succeeded him who sat upon his throne, but long afterward there was born of his seed one who obtained his royal seat. It can also be resolved thus: there will indeed not sit upon the throne of David a man and a human being, but God will sit, and his kingdom will not be earthly and brief, as David's was, but perpetual and heavenly, as Scripture says: He will reign over the house of Jacob forever, and of his kingdom there will be no end. Of Jehoiakim, then, was born Jeconiah, of Jeconiah Shealtiel, of Shealtiel Zerubbabel, and so in order one comes to Christ. But in the days of Jehoiachin no king, his son, succeeded him, as he himself had succeeded his father; but he was in captivity, and neither Shealtiel nor Zerubbabel nor anyone down to Christ obtained royal power. And this is so because in the Hebrew is written: in his days, and in the very time in which he lived, not shall there be a man who sits upon the royal seat of David. For all were captives, and of the stock of David no one thereafter held rule in the land of Judea. Whence Josephus too reports that princes of the priestly race and of the tribe of Levi ruled, whom Herod, son of Antipater the proselyte, succeeded, and that afterwards, under Vespasian, the kingdom of this race, nay rather the semblance of rule, was utterly destroyed.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.1-4":["Woe to the shepherds who destroy and tear the flock of my pasture — or O shepherds, who destroy and scatter the sheep of my pasture —, says the Lord. Therefore thus says the Lord, the God of Israel, to the shepherds who feed my people: You have scattered my flock, you have driven them out and have not visited them; behold, I will visit upon you the wickedness of your pursuits, says the Lord. And I will gather the remnant of my flock from all the lands to which I shall have driven them out there, and I will bring them back to their fields — or I will restore them to their pastures — and they shall grow and be multiplied. And I will raise up shepherds over them and they shall feed them; they shall fear no more and shall not be dismayed, and none shall be sought from the number, says the Lord. The prophetic discourse is addressed to the shepherds, or concerns the shepherds; and since we read it written of Jeconiah, the next-to-last king of Judea, who was of the stock of David: O land, land, land, hear the words of the Lord! Write this man down as rejected — or barren — and there shall not be of his seed a man who shall sit upon the throne of David, and all hope of the Jewish kingdom had been cut off, the discourse passes over to the princes of the Church, and, the synagogue with its shepherds being abandoned and condemned, it is addressed to the apostles, of whom it is said: and I will raise up shepherds over them and they shall feed them; they shall fear no more and shall not be dismayed, and none shall be sought from the number, says the Lord. For the apostles will feed the flock of the Church confidently and without any fear, and the remnant of the people of Israel will be saved from all the lands and will be brought back to their fields or to their pastures, and they will grow and be multiplied. But upon the evil shepherds, the scribes and Pharisees, the Lord will visit because of the wickedness of their pursuits. We can also understand this according to tropology of the princes of the Church who nevertheless do not rule the sheep of the Lord worthily: that, these being cast off and condemned, the people may be saved, handed over to others who have proved worthy, and the remnant may be saved. Those shepherds destroy the sheep who teach heresy; those tear and scatter them who make schisms; those drive them out who separate them from the Church against righteousness; those do not visit them who withhold their hand from the penitent. On all these the Lord will have mercy, restoring to them their former pastures and taking away the evil shepherds.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.5-6":["Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and I will raise up for David a righteous branch — or a righteous Rising — and a king shall reign and shall be wise — or shall understand — and shall do judgment and righteousness in the land. In those days Judah shall be saved and Israel shall dwell confidently, and this is the name by which they shall call him — or he shall call him — : the Lord our righteous one or our righteousness, which in Hebrew is said 'sadecenu'. This, which has been wrongly added in the Seventy: in the prophets, must be cut away entirely; for another chapter follows, which has been passed over by them, and when it is finished the title is: to the prophets or against the prophets, of which we shall speak in its own place. So, the shepherds of the synagogue having been cast off, namely the scribes and Pharisees, and the remnant out of Israel having been saved, and the apostles of the Gospel having been set in the place of the former princes, there is brought in the shepherd of shepherds and prince of princes and king of kings and lord of lords, namely Christ our Savior, who is properly the 'righteous branch' or 'righteous Rising', of whom we read: in his days righteousness shall rise and in another place: behold a man, the Rising is his name, and beneath him it shall rise, and he shall build a temple to the Lord, so that, as in Isaiah he is called 'Emmanuhel', that is, 'God with us', so in Jeremiah he receives the name 'our righteousness'; whence the Apostle too says: who was made for us wisdom from God, and righteousness and sanctification and redemption. Opposed to him, the Antichrist and the devil who dwells in him is called in Zechariah the 'foolish shepherd'. And he shall do, he says, judgment and righteousness in the land; for the Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son. In those days both the two and the ten tribes, Judah and Israel, shall be saved alike, and out of two rods, according to Ezekiel, one rod shall be made; and its name, if according to the Seventy the Lord shall have called him, shall be Iosedec, that is, the righteous Lord; if according to the Hebrew, who said: they shall call his name, it shall be said: the Lord our righteousness; for this is what adonai sadecenu means, for which Symmachus translated: O Lord, justify us!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.7-8":["Therefore, behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, and they shall no longer say: The Lord lives, who brought the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt, but: The Lord lives, who brought out and brought back the seed of the house of Israel from the land of the north and from all the lands to which I had driven them out there, and they shall dwell in their own land. This whole chapter is not found in the Seventy; and its sense is this: that the people of God is no longer to be freed from Egypt through Moses, but through Jesus Christ from the whole circle of the earth, into which it had been scattered. This is fulfilled in part even now in the world, and will be fulfilled wholly when from the east and the west, the north and the south they shall come and recline with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, so that, after the fullness of the nations has come in, then all Israel may be saved.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.9":["To the prophets or in the prophets or against the prophets. This title, as we said above, has been placed in the edition of the Seventy as the end of the preceding chapter, where we read it written: and this is the name which the Lord shall call him, Iosedec, in the prophets. Not knowing this, very many invent various ravings of explanation; and it would have been much better simply to confess ignorance than to make others heirs of their own unskilfulness. The discourse, moreover, is against the prophets, nay rather the false prophets of Jerusalem and Samaria, whom he now calls by the common and popular name 'prophets', of whom it is written in what follows: and in the prophets of Samaria I saw iniquity and immediately: and in the prophets of Jerusalem I saw horrible things. But when he puts the conjunction 'and', he shows that the things above were said of the prophets of the Lord, who themselves too proved like the false prophets.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","My heart is broken in the midst of me — or in me —, all my bones have trembled — or have been shaken —; I have become like a drunken man and like a man soaked — or overcome — with wine before the face of the Lord and before the face of his holy words or and before the face of the comeliness of his glory. Having considered the countenance of almighty God, that is, of the Father, and having considered the countenance of the Son, who according to the Apostle is called the 'radiance of his glory' and the 'form of the substance of God', the prophet shudders in both mind and body and understands that he is nothing, according to what is said in another place too: I have become like a beast of burden before you; or else he offers to God the victim of his conscience and humility, according to that which is written in the psalms: a sacrifice to God is a troubled spirit; a contrite and humbled heart God does not despise. But the bones which trembled or were shaken, let us understand to be those of which the same David sings: all my bones shall say: Lord, who is like you? He became as one drunk and like a man soaked — or overcome — with wine, having no understanding and no wisdom; for the Lord knows the thoughts of men, that they are vain. But if this is so, where are those who preach perfect righteousness in man? And if they answer that they say this of the saints, not of themselves, certainly I think no one is holier than Jeremiah, who, a virgin, a prophet, and sanctified in the womb, by his very name prefigures the Lord Savior. For Jeremiah means 'the exalted one of the Lord'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.10":["Because the land is filled with adulterers, because before the face of cursing — or of the oath — the land has mourned, the fields — or pastures — of the desert have dried up; their course has become evil and their strength unlike. This, which we have set down from the Hebrew: because the land is filled with adulterers, is not found in the Seventy, who said 'oath' for 'cursing'. And he gives the causes: that because of adulteries and curses, or superfluous swearing, nay rather perjuries, barrenness of the crops followed. Whatever you understand of the land of Judea according to the letter, refer to the congregation of believers, since because of adulteries and lies or perjuries there is in the churches a barrenness of the virtues and gifts of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.11-12":["For prophet and priest are polluted, and in my house I have found their evil, says the Lord. Therefore their way shall be like a slippery place in darkness; they shall be driven on and shall fall in it, for I will bring — or I will inflict — evils upon them, the year of their visitation, says the Lord. When evils are found in the Church of God, and especially in its princes, let us know that this has been fulfilled: prophet and priest are polluted; in my house I have found their evils, says the Lord. Now the house of Christ is the Church, of which Paul too writes to Timothy: that you may know how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God, which is the Church of the living God, the pillar and foundation of the truth. In the prophet understand the teacher, in the priest the dignity of the ministry; and if they have consented with a depraved mind, their way will be into a slippery place and into darkness, and they will not have the Lord saying: I have come as light into the world, that everyone who believes in me may not remain in darkness. Whence the holy man, fleeing all darkness, says: the light of your countenance, O Lord, has been signed upon us; you have given gladness in my heart. But when they are in darkness and on the slippery way, that is, in heretical error, at every motion they will be driven on and will fall. And the Lord inflicts evils upon them: not that they are evils in such a way that the Lord inflicts evils, but they are evils to those who undergo the punishments; otherwise the same things are both evils and goods: evils with respect to those whom they torment, goods with respect to those whom they amend. And this must be noted, that the year of the Lord's visitation is called the correction and torment of sinners, according to that which is written: I will visit their iniquities with the rod and their sins with stripes; but my mercy I will not scatter from him.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.13":["And in the prophets of Samaria I saw folly — or iniquity —; they prophesied in Baal and deceived my people Israel. I think that by the prophets of Samaria, according to the mystical senses, the heretics are properly meant, and all who boast of a knowledge falsely so named. And as the prophets of Samaria, whatever they spoke, prophesied in Baal, namely an idol consecrated to demons, so the heretics, whatever they speak inside the Church or outside the Church, speak in demons, in order to trip up the people 'Israhel', which formerly 'saw God'; whence too he says significantly: and in the prophets of Samaria I saw folly; for they do not have him of whom it is said: 'Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.14":["And in the prophets of Jerusalem I saw a likeness — or horrible things —, adultery and the path of falsehood; and they strengthened the hands of the most wicked, so that no one turned back from his wickedness — or from his most wicked way —; they have all become to me as Sodom, and its inhabitants as Gomorrah. 'Not only', he says, 'have these things been found in the conventicles of the heretics, but in the prophets of Jerusalem too, that is, in the teachers of the Church, I have seen like things, or horrible things: men adulterating the word of God and walking along the way of falsehood, so that they acquiesced in the deceits of the heretics and strengthened the hands of the most wicked and joined their evils to their own crimes, and led to destruction those whom they ought to have corrected. Let those who have done this not think themselves unpunished! For both they themselves and those whom they favor will be as Sodom, and all who dwell with them and do not withdraw from such men, as Gomorrah'. Let the most wicked doctrine exult, then, as much as it will, and let the prophets of Jerusalem glory that they have prevailed through falsehood and strengthened the hands of the most wicked: their end will be as Sodom and Gomorrah.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.15":["Therefore thus says the Lord of hosts to the prophets: Behold, I will feed them with wormwood — or with pains, and according to Symmachus with bitterness — and I will give them gall to drink — or bitter water —, for from the prophets of Jerusalem pollution has gone forth into all the land. Let us use this testimony against those who send out into the world letters full of lies and fraud and perjury, so as both to pollute the ears of the hearers and to injure the reputation of the simple: that what is written is fulfilled in them: from the prophets of Jerusalem pollution has gone forth into all the land; for it is not enough for them to devour their own iniquity and to injure their neighbors, but those whom they have once hated they try to defame throughout the whole world, and to sow blasphemies everywhere.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.16-17":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Do not listen to the words of the prophets who prophesy to you and deceive you! They speak the vision of their own heart and not from the mouth of the Lord. They say to those who blaspheme me — or cast away my word — : The Lord has spoken: peace shall be to you; and to everyone who walks in the perversity of his heart they have said: Evils shall not come upon you. Lest the people should think itself free of guilt if it acquiesced in perverse teachers, 'Do not', he says, 'listen to the words of the prophets who prophesy to you a lie and deceive you', — for the punishment will be equal for master and disciple — 'they do not speak from the mouth of the Lord, but the things they have feigned in their own heart. They say to those who blaspheme me' — namely to heretics and perverse men — 'or who cast away my speech, what do they say?: The Lord has spoken: peace shall be to you; do not fear harsh punishments nor be terrified by empty threats; peace shall be to you, and tranquillity, and whatever we say and announce to you, the Lord has spoken; nor shall evil come upon you, which you fear because of a bad conscience, but good, which the Lord has spoken to you.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.18":["For who has been present in the counsel of the Lord and seen and heard his speech? who has considered his word and heard? Where we translated 'in the counsel of the Lord' and in the Hebrew is written 'bassod', Aquila translated 'secret', Symmachus 'speech', the Seventy and Theodotion 'substance' or 'subsistence'. And the sense is: 'Do not believe, O untaught crowd, the prophets who announce false things to you, who say this: The Lord has spoken: peace shall be to you; evil shall not come upon you! For whence can they know the secrets of God, or at whose announcing have they learned the counsel of the Lord? How has the word of the divine dispensation reached them?' Some of our own think that in this place they have found where the substance of God is written of.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.19-20":["Behold, the whirlwind — or tempest and commotion — of the Lord's indignation goes forth, and a tempest bursting forth shall come upon the head of the ungodly, and the fury of the Lord shall not turn back until he does and until he fulfills the thought of his heart; in the last days you shall understand his counsel. He who had said above: 'It has been spoken to us by the Lord: peace shall be to you', and had rebuked them because they could not know the things to come nor know the sentence of God, now shows by contraries that they know nothing at all: for instead of peace and security a Babylonian tempest is coming, and coming not upon just anyone, but upon the heads of the ungodly, whether of the whole people or of those who announced false things to the people. Nor is the wrath and fury of God to be appeased as in times past, but what he foretold and very often threatened is to be fulfilled in deed, and his thought and sentence are to be proved by the punishments of the ungodly. 'When', he says, 'the last time of the captivity has come, and the exulting victor has taken hold of you and bound your hands with the grating of chains, then you shall understand his counsel, which you now vainly boast that you know'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.21-22":["I did not send the prophets, and they ran; I did not speak to them, and they prophesied. If they had stood in my counsel and made my words known to my people, I would surely have turned them from their evil way and from their most wicked thoughts. This sense the Apostle too unfolds to the Romans: and as they did not see fit to have God in knowledge, God handed them over to a reprobate sense, so that they do the things that are not fitting, filled with all iniquity, malice, fornication, wickedness, avarice and the rest. For when false teachers have once handed themselves over to lies and perjuries and to the deaths of those they have deceived, they run, not step by step nor with considered pace, but headlong, to the destruction both of themselves and of those whom they have deceived. Nor does the Lord speak to them, but they themselves speak as if from the mouth of the Lord, of whom it is also said: 'if they had stood in my counsel — that is: if they had been willing to acquiesce in my will — and had made my words known to my people, not flattering them and ruining them with adulation, so as to say: You have no sins, you possess perfect righteousness, holiness and chastity and righteousness are found in you alone; then I would not have handed them over to uncleanness and ignominy, to do the things that are not fitting and to follow their most wicked thoughts'. Let us contemplate the heretics: how, once despairing of salvation, they hand themselves over to gluttony and delights, eat meats, frequent the baths, are fragrant with musk, and, anointed with various unguents, seek beauty of body. For once and for all they do not hope for the things to come, nor do they believe in the resurrection. What they do not disclose in speech, they show in deeds; for if they believed, they would not do these things. And in this place, where it is written: if they had stood in my counsel, Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion and the Seventy translated similarly, as above.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.23-24":["Am I a God from near at hand, says the Lord, and not a God from afar? If a man is hidden in secret places, shall I not see him?, says the Lord; do I not fill heaven and earth?, says the Lord. LXX: I am a God drawing near, says the Lord, and not a God from afar. If a man shall hide in secret places, shall I not see him?, says the Lord; do I not fill heaven and earth?, says the Lord. Aquila and Symmachus translated similarly: Am I a God from near — or nearby — and not a God from afar? But the Seventy and Theodotion translated in a contrary sense, so as to say: I am a God drawing near, says the Lord, and not a God from afar: namely, the former assert that God knows not only things nearby but also those which are far off, and sees not only present things but future ones; while these think that God is present everywhere and that there is no place where God is not present. For God draws near to all, and especially to the saints, as a garment clings to the skin; but sinners, withdrawing far from him, will perish. This sense we read in the psalms too: Whither shall I go from your spirit, and whither shall I flee from your face? If I ascend into heaven, you are there; if I descend into hell, you are present; if I take my wings at dawn and dwell in the uttermost parts of the sea, even there your hand shall lead me and your right hand shall hold me. Amos too agrees with these words, saying: if they go down even to hell, from there my hand shall bring them out, and if they go up even to heaven, from there I will drag them down; and if they are hidden on the summit of Carmel, searching I will take them away from there, and if they have hidden themselves from my eyes in the depth of the sea, there I will command the serpent and it shall bite them. And again in the psalm above: For darkness, he says, shall not be darkened to you, and night shall be lit up as day; as its darkness, so also its light. And as for its being said frequently in the prophets: 'thus says the Lord', it is always added for this reason: that they should not be despised as words of the prophets, but that people should be continually reminded that the words they speak are God's.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.25-27":["I have heard what the prophets said who prophesy a lie in my name and say: I have dreamed, I have dreamed — or I have seen a dream —. How long is this in the heart of the prophets who prophesy a lie and prophesy the seductions — or the wishes — of their own heart? Who wish — or think — to make my people forget my name because of their dreams, which each one tells to his neighbor, as their fathers forgot my name because of Baal or in Baal. Since the preceding title is against the prophets or to the prophets, whom we clearly understand to be false prophets — and there are many kinds of prophesying, one of which is by dreams, such as was in Daniel —, therefore the prophetic discourse is directed also at those who believe in dreams and think that all they see is divine revelation, which is properly opened to the saints and servants of God. And if we read that Pharaoh and Nebuchadnezzar, ungodly kings, saw dreams which were true, it was not of the merit of those who saw them, but so that on their occasion the holy men Joseph and Daniel might become renowned, and the hard and untamable hearts of the tyrants might feel the majesty of the Lord by their own conscience. There are even today dreamers in the Church, and especially in our flock, who vaunt their errors as the prophecy of the Lord and frequently thrust upon us: I have dreamed, I have dreamed; whom the Lord rebukes, saying: 'How long is this in the heart of the prophets who prophesy a lie and prophesy the seductions of their own heart? And they do this in order that, as the ancient people which went out of Egypt forgot the name of God, so their descendants too may forget me'. But prophesying of this kind is not in the name of the Lord, but in the name of Baal, which is properly the idol of the Sidonians, or the Babylonian one, and is called by a kindred name Bel.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.28":["Let the prophet who has a dream tell the dream, and let him who has my word speak my word truly! what has chaff to do with wheat?, says the Lord; are not my words like fire, says the Lord, and like a hammer — or an axe — breaking the rock? It belongs to those to expound a dream who merit to have the word of God and to say: 'thus says the Lord', to whom the Lord has spoken, in whom there is truth and not fraudulent falsehood. What has the chaff of the heretics to do with the wheat of the Church? Of this John the Baptist speaks more fully: that Christ will purge his threshing floor and with the winnowing fan leave the chaff to be scattered by the blasts of the winds and burned with fire, but will store the wheat in the barns, that it may become heavenly bread and each of the believers may say: taste and see that the Lord is sweet! And beautifully is perverse doctrine compared to chaff, which has no marrow and cannot nourish the peoples of believers, but is threshed out of empty stalks.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.29":["And because the heretics are always wont to promise prosperity and to open the kingdoms of heaven to sinners, so as to say: 'The kingdoms of heaven are prepared for you; you can imitate the majesty of God, so as to be without sin; for you have received the power of free will and the knowledge of the law, through which you may attain what you will', and they deceive the wretched with blandishments, and especially silly women laden with sins, who are carried about by every wind of doctrine, always learning and never coming to the knowledge of the truth, and they deceive all their hearers with adulation, therefore the Lord, comparing his own words with the chaff of the heretics, says: 'are not my words like fire, says the Lord, and like a hammer breaking the rock? For my word', he says, 'announces future punishments, to deter men from sinning', and he threatens the chaff of sinners with burning, so that the hearts of the heretics, hard and untamable like flint, may be broken by the hammer of his word, as he takes away the heart of stone to put in its place a heart of flesh, namely a soft one, and one that can receive and feel the precepts of God. Something of the kind the Lord says through Ezekiel too: that the false prophets with their blandishments daub a wall without mortar, which afterward is overthrown by a violent rain and by the truth of judgment, and the false prophetesses sew pillows under every elbow of the hand, to make sinners rest and never soften the wrath of God with tears. For 'hammer' the Seventy translated 'axe', namely the one of which John the Baptist speaks: already the axe is laid at the roots of the trees. This axe cuts down the unfruitful trees and this hammer breaks the hardest stones. Whence too Nahum the prophet says: his fury — no doubt God's — shall consume principalities, and the rocks shall be broken by him. This against the heretics. But of churchmen it is written that hammer and axe were not heard in the house of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.30-32":["Therefore, behold, I am against the prophets, says the Lord, who steal my words each one from his neighbor; behold, I am against the prophets, says the Lord, who take up their tongues and say: He says — or they slumber a slumber — ; behold, I am against the prophets who dream a lie, says the Lord, who tell them and seduce my people in their lies and in their wonders — or astonishments and terrors — of their own, when I had not sent them nor commanded them, who have been of no profit to this people, says the Lord. Falsehood always imitates truth, and unless it has some likeness of what is right, it cannot deceive the innocent. As, then, among the former people the prophets lied and said: 'thus says the Lord' and: 'I saw the Lord' and: 'the word of the Lord which came to this man or that', so the heretics take up testimonies of the Scriptures from the Old and New Instrument and steal the words of the Savior each one from his neighbor, from prophets and apostles and evangelists, and take up their tongues, to utter with the mouth the poisons of the heart, and slumber a slumber — of whom it was truly said: they have slumbered their sleep and found nothing — or, according to the Hebrew: and they say: He says, so that 'the Lord' is understood, or certainly 'the divine word'. The Lord therefore threatens that he himself will come against masters of this kind, who seduce his people in their lies and in their astonishments and wonders. For they promise great and incredible and immense things in order to seduce the wretched; who have been of no profit to the people of God, and fulfill that apostolic word: teaching what they ought not, for the sake of base gain; who are wont to announce to the wicked prosperity, and the contrary.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.33-36":["If therefore this people or a prophet or a priest asks you, saying: What is the burden of the Lord — or what is the taking up of the Lord — ?, you shall say to them: You are the burden — or the taking up — ; for I will cast you away — or I will dash you down, — says the Lord. And the prophet and the priest and the people who says: The burden — or the taking up — of the Lord, I will visit — or I will avenge — upon that man and upon his house. These things you shall say each one to his friend and to his brother: What has the Lord answered and what has the Lord spoken? and the burden — or the taking up — of the Lord shall be remembered no more, for the burden — or the taking up — shall be to each one his own word. The Hebrew word 'massa' Aquila translates arma, that is, 'burden' and 'weight'; Symmachus, the Seventy and Theodotion 'taking up'. Wherever, then, what the Lord threatens is heavy and full of weight and toil and unbearable, in the title too it is called arma, that is, 'weight'; but wherever the Lord promises prosperity, or after a threat promises better things, there it is called 'vision' or certainly 'word of the Lord'; and it was clear from the very title of the prophecy, 'of weight' or 'of vision' and 'of the word of God', what kind of prophecy would follow. Since, therefore, the prophets were wont to announce sad things to the sinful people and to threaten punishments, in order to draw them back to repentance, while the Lord, merciful and compassionate, long deferred the sentence, the people, deceived and seduced by the fraud of the false prophets, thought that the things the Lord threatened would not come, and they had turned a serious matter into sport and jest, and when the prophets prophesied they would say as if mocking: 'Here again he sees a weight and burden of the Lord'. And so it came about that it was no longer called 'vision', but in jest and derision 'burden' and 'weight'. The Lord therefore commands that, whether the people or a prophet or a priest should ask Jeremiah what the weight or what the taking up of the Lord is, he should answer them and say: 'You are the burden and you the taking up; for I will take you up and cast you away and dash you down and make you perish. But if anyone hereafter, whether of the prophets or of the priests or of the people, dares to name the burden and weight of the Lord, I will visit', he says, 'upon that man and upon his house, and I will blot him out forever'. Let each one therefore say to his neighbor and friend by no means: What is the burden of the Lord?, but: 'What has the Lord answered and what has the Lord spoken? Forget the old word; let burden and weight or taking up no longer sound in your mouth, for to each man his own word and his own work will be reckoned as a burden and weight, according to that which is written: by your mouth you shall be justified and by your mouth you shall be condemned'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.23.36-40":["And you pervert the words of the living God, the Lord of hosts, our God. Thus shalt thou say — or shall you say — to the prophet: What has the Lord answered you and what has the Lord spoken? But if you say: The burden of the Lord — up to here it is not found in the Seventy; and there follows: because of this, thus says the Lord: Because you have said this word: The burden — or the taking up — of the Lord, and I sent to you saying: Do not say the burden — or the taking up — of the Lord, therefore, behold, I will lift — or I will take up — you, carrying you, and I will forsake you and the city which I gave to you and to your fathers, from before my face, and I will give you over to everlasting reproach and eternal ignominy, which shall never be blotted out by oblivion. The Lord commanded that the word of 'burden' and 'weight' and 'taking up' should by no means be spoken among the people, but that of 'answer' and 'word of God'. And because the people had disdained to fulfill this, he interprets the very word upon them and says: 'Since you have said what I did not wish, and this when I often sent prophets and ordered you not to say it, therefore I will fulfill in deed upon you your word of taking up, burden and weight; for I will take you up and lift you and carry you, and dash you down, and make you fall from on high to the ground — and not only you, but your city too, which I gave to your fathers — and I will give', he says, 'over to reproach and everlasting ignominy, which shall never be blotted out by oblivion'. We know that this was done at the time of the Babylonian captivity too; but it is fulfilled more fully and more perfectly after the passion and resurrection of the Savior, when the Lord said: your house shall be left to you desolate, and the sentence will remain until its end. Let us also say it otherwise, at least according to the Seventy: lēmma means not only 'taking up' but also 'gift and present'. Since, therefore, the people always promised itself prosperity, he says that they ought by no means to say this any longer; for they are unworthy of the gifts and presents of God, and are rather to be cast away and wholly abandoned by the help of God. And moreover, things are often shown both by words and by the interpretation of names, as the changed names of Abraham, of Sarah, and of Peter and the sons of Zebedee signify a change of things, and in this same prophet 'Phaschor' is called 'fear' or 'removal' and 'settler' or 'stranger'. It must be noted that the words: of the living God, the Lord of hosts, our God the Latin and Greek codices do not have, and the Hebrews read them in their volumes against themselves, which properly signifies the mystery of the Trinity.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.24.1-2":["The fifth book of the commentaries on Jeremiah, brother Eusebius, will take its beginning from two baskets, of which the one will show the sweetness of the right faith, the other the bitterness of the faithlessness of the heretics. Although Hananiah son of Azzur resists Jeremiah, and Shemaiah the Nehelamite desires that the prophet be sent into prison, and Zephaniah the priest swears himself to the words of the false prophets, yet truth can be shut up and bound, but it cannot be conquered; it is content with the fewness of its own and is not terrified by the multitude of its enemies. Therefore raise your hands to heaven with Moses and hang up the ancient serpent in the desert, and at once Amalek will be destroyed and the poisoned bites will not prevail, and the people of the Lord, secure, will cross the streams of the Jordan with Jesus [Joshua], and after the vast wilderness will eat the bread that was born in our little village of Bethlehem.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","The Lord showed me, and behold, two baskets — or, according to Symmachus, hampers — full of figs, placed before the temple of the Lord, after Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, had carried away Jeconiah, son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, and his princes and the smith and the setter from Jerusalem, and had brought them to Babylon. One basket had very good figs, as figs of the first season usually are, and one basket had very bad figs, which could not be eaten because they were bad. And the Lord said to me: What do you see, Jeremiah? And I said: Figs, good ones, very good, and bad ones, very bad, which cannot be eaten because they are bad. And the word of the Lord came to me, saying: Thus says the Lord God of Israel: As these figs are good, so will I know the exile of Judah, which I have sent out from this place into the land of the Chaldeans, for good. And I will set my eyes upon them for good, and I will bring them back into this land, and I will build them and not destroy them, and I will plant them and not pluck them up. And I will give them a heart, that they may know me, that I am the Lord, and they shall be to me for a people, and I will be to them for a God, because they will return to me with their whole heart. For as the very bad figs, which cannot be eaten because they are bad, thus says the Lord, so will I give — or hand over — Zedekiah, king of Judah, and his princes and the rest of Jerusalem, who have remained in this city and who dwell in the land of Egypt. And I will give them to vexation and affliction — or dispersion — to all the kingdoms of the earth, for a reproach and for a parable and for a proverb and for a curse in all the places to which I have cast them out. And I will send among them the sword and famine and pestilence, until they are consumed from the land which I gave to them and to their fathers. Some interpret the two hampers or baskets of good and bad figs as the law and the Gospel, the synagogue and the Church, the people of the Jews and that of the Christians, Gehenna and the kingdom of heaven, of which the one pertains to the punishment of sinners, the other to the dwelling-place of the saints. But we, knowing according to the Apostle Paul that the law is good and holy and the commandment good and holy, and that there is one God of both Testaments, should rather refer them to those who at the coming of the Lord the Savior believed and did not believe: so that those who, stirred up by the scribes and priests, cried out, saying: Crucify, crucify such a one, are the basket of bad figs; while those of the people who believed after his ascension are referred to the good figs and to the best basket and hamper.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.24.1-3":["It must also be noted that this vision came to the prophet in the times of Zedekiah, after Jeconiah had been led into 'exile'; for he did not say 'captivity', because Jeconiah had handed himself over of his own accord. And by the smiths and setters we ought to understand either interpreters and teachers of the law, or craftsmen and enclosers of gold and gems, an art that is most precious among the barbarian nations. For 'setters' the Seventy translated 'bound men', to signify the evil of captivity, and of their own they added 'rich men', which is not found in the Hebrew. And he compares the basket that had good figs, and very good ones, to the figs of the first season — which in Greek are called proima —, namely to Abraham and Isaac and Jacob and Moses and Aaron and Job and the other holy men, of whom one of the twelve prophets speaks: Like a grape in the desert I found Israel, and like figs on the fig tree I found their fathers; whence we too are called sons of Abraham, and on the contrary it is said to the Jews: If Abraham were your father, you would do his works. But these baskets, which had good and bad figs, were not outside and beyond the Church, but before the temple of the Lord, because all things lie open to his knowledge. Nor do these figs that are outside have as great a bitterness as those that after the confession of faith have been changed by transgression; nor do the good figs that are not in sight of the temple of God — such as were the philosophers of the world, who, by natural goodness and by the understanding of the Creator, seemed not so much to follow the virtues as to praise them — have as great a sweetness as the figs have that are in the temple of God, among which were the prophets and apostles, of whom one fig said: I gave you milk, not solid food, and: My little children, with whom I am in labor again until Christ is formed in you. Hence it is said that in sight of the temple of God the good figs were very good and the bad figs were very bad.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.24.5-10":["And lest we be thought to be setting down our own sense, Scripture itself lays itself open. As these figs, it says, are good, so will I know the exile of Judah, which I have sent out from this place into the land of the Chaldeans, for good, signifying Jeconiah and the princes who were captured with him. And on the contrary, of the basket that had bad figs: And as the very bad figs, he says, which cannot be eaten because they are bad, so will I give Zedekiah, king of Judah, and his princes and those who fled over into Egypt and who remained in this city, to vexation and affliction to all the kingdoms of the earth, when they were captured in Egypt too, and Nebuchadnezzar set his throne in Tahpanhes, and the Lord sent upon them the sword, famine and pestilence, until they were consumed from the land which he gave to their fathers. But what he said of the good figs: I will give them a heart, that they may know me, that I am the Lord, is like the apostolic saying: It is God who works in you both to will and to accomplish, in that not only our works but our will too rests on the help of God. In this place the ever-allegorical interpreter raves, and, wishing to do violence to the truth of the history, relates that some were captured from the heavenly Jerusalem and carried over into the land of the Chaldeans and will return again to their former place, such as Jeremiah and the other holy prophets, while others, who were sinners, will die in this land and in the valley of tears. I have set out the whole perikopēn [passage] of this vision together, so as not to divide the sense in the exposition.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.24.5-8":["But let us follow the simple and true history: that he called Jeconiah the basket of good figs, who by the counsel of Jeremiah and the command of God had handed himself over to the Babylonian king, and to whom the Lord also promises prosperity; and the basket of bad figs Zedekiah, who, contradicting the sentence of God, was captured, and with his eyes blinded was led to Babylon and died there. And to such an extent did God set his eyes upon them for good, those who acquiesced in his command, and bring them back into their land, and build them and not destroy them, and plant them and not pluck them up, and give them a heart, that they might know him, that he himself was the Lord, and that they might become his people and he himself might be their God, that even in the captivity he set his eyes upon them and permitted them to work the land, to build houses and to plant orchards in the region of Babylonia; Daniel, through miracles of signs, was suddenly made a prince from a captive; the three youths were gloriously delivered from the fire of the furnace; and when the seventy years were completed, under Zerubbabel and Jeshua the great priest, and Ezra and Nehemiah, the greatest part returned to Jerusalem, whose number is contained in the volume of that same Ezra.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.1":["The word that came to Jeremiah concerning all the people of Judea — or upon all the people of Judah — in the fourth year of Jehoiakim son of Josiah, king of Judah. These things are earlier than the preceding vision; for that one took place under Zedekiah, after Jeconiah had been carried off to Babylon, but these under Jehoiakim, the son of Josiah and father of Jeconiah. For it was not their concern, as I have already said before, to preserve the chronological order of prophecy, which the laws of history require, but to write in whatever way they knew to be useful to those who would hear and read; hence also in the Psalter some wrongly look for the order of the psalms according to the sequence of history, which is not observed in lyric song.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","This is the first year of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, which is not found in the Seventy; and there follows: which Jeremiah the prophet spoke to all the people of Judah and to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem, saying. In the fourth year of King Jehoiakim son of Josiah, father of Jeconiah, Nebuchadnezzar took up the rule in Babylon, and so it came about that the year which was the first of Nebuchadnezzar in Babylon was the fourth of King Jehoiakim in Jerusalem. Then, in the eighth year of his reign, he led into captivity Jeconiah with his mother and the princes, who had reigned only three months after the slaying of his father Jehoiakim. Now Jehoiakim was slain in the eleventh year of his reign in Jerusalem.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.3":["From the thirteenth year of Josiah son of Amon, king of Judah, up to this day, this is the twenty-third year, the word of the Lord has come to me, and I have spoken to you, rising by night and speaking; and you have not listened. In the thirteenth year of the reign of Josiah, who reigned in Jerusalem thirty-one years, Jeremiah began to prophesy, and he prophesied under him for nineteen years. His son Jehoahaz succeeded him in the kingdom; and when he was at once led into captivity by Neco, the Egyptian king, his brother Jehoiakim obtained the kingdom, in the fourth year of whose reign this word of the Lord comes to Jeremiah. And so it was the twenty-third year of Jeremiah the prophet since he began to speak to the people, and he never ceased to preach, but every day, rising at dawn and by night, he spoke to the people; and you have not, he says, listened. But if, as the new heretics sprung from the old would have it, the law was given once for all as a help, and precepts were established which we either do or do not do by the volition of our own free will, how is it that the prophet always presses himself upon them and daily repeats the commandments of God, which, once received, would surely have sufficed for those to whom they had been given, unless it is to show that we always need God's help, and that what has been given once can never suffice unless it is renewed daily by the Lord's admonition?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.4-5":["Nor did you incline your ears to hear, when I said: Return, each one, from his evil way and from your very wicked thoughts, and you will dwell in the land which the Lord gave to you and to your fathers from age and even unto age. So great was the hardness of the people that it would not even take on the bearing of one who listens and incline its ear, above all when the Lord was warning it that each one should turn back from his evil way and from his very wicked thoughts. And O infinite clemency: not to inflict punishment for the crime, but to call to repentance both for evil works, which the evil way signifies, and for very wicked thoughts, which even by themselves, without the deed, are reckoned as sin! And he promises a reward if they do what is commanded: that they should dwell in the land which was given to their fathers and taken away because of the vices of the sons. And his saying: from age and even unto age, or: from eternity even unto eternity, shows that God's gifts are perpetual, if those to whom they were given prove worthy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.4":["And the Lord sent to you all his servants, the prophets, rising at dawn and sending; and you have not listened. Not through one prophet but through all of them God always admonished his people, and as though he himself were stationed on watch and on guard, he rose at dawn to warn his people. And you have not, he says, listened, so that the more frequent the admonition, the greater were the sins of those who despised it.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.6-7":["And do not go after strange gods, to serve them and worship them, nor provoke me to anger in the works of your hands, and I will not afflict you; and you have not listened to me. 'And these things', he says, 'I was urging together with the earlier ones: that you should not serve strange gods and worship them, and that you should not, by doing these things, provoke me to anger because of the works of your hands, so that I should do what I did not wish and afflict you; and you have not listened to me'. And what follows: says the Lord, so that you provoked me to anger in the works of your hands, to your own harm, is not found in the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.8-9":["Therefore thus says the Lord of hosts: Because you have not listened to my words, behold, I will send and take all the families of the north, says the Lord, and Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, my servant. 'Because you have provoked me to anger and done evil to your own destruction, and have trampled with a proud foot him who called you to repentance, I will send', he says, 'to all the families, or nations, of the north, and I will bring their prince Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, my servant'. Now the Lord sends either angels to stir up the nations, or else he moves thoughts of such a kind that they do the will of the Lord. And as for his calling Nebuchadnezzar 'his servant', he is not called 'servant' in the way the prophets and all the saints are, who truly serve the Lord, but in that in the overthrow of Jerusalem he serves the will of the Lord, in accordance with what the Apostle too says: whom I have delivered to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme. And that the Chaldeans lie in the northern quarter with respect to the position of Jerusalem is doubtful to no one.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.9":["And I will bring them upon this land and upon its inhabitants and upon all the nations that are round about it, and I will slay them and make them an astonishment and a hissing and everlasting desolations. These are the rewards of those who despise the words of God and refuse to hear them; whatever evil, therefore, is brought upon us, our sins have brought about. And just as all the nations were then brought against Jerusalem, so today they are brought against the negligent Church, that he may slay them and make them an astonishment and a hissing and everlasting desolations, so that they may be an example and a marvel to all. Now hissing is a sign of marvel and astonishment, and everlasting desolation is found in those who do not have God as their protector.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.10-11":["And I will destroy from them the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bride and the voice of the bridegroom, the voice of the mill and the light of the lamp. And all its land shall become a desolation and an astonishment. This is proved even today in the assembly of the malignant, in that their teachers do not teach the word of God but hiss like a serpent. And there perishes in them the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, so that they never hear that saying of the Apostle: rejoice, again I say, rejoice!; also the voice of the bride, that is, of the Church's faith, and the voice of the bridegroom, that is, of the Lord the Savior, for he who has the bride is the bridegroom; the voice of the mill, so that grain is not ground in it and given to the peoples to eat; and the light of the lamp, namely the teaching and knowledge of the prophets. Indeed, of John the Baptist too it is said: he was a shining lamp, and another prophet says: a lamp to my feet, he says, is your word, O Lord, and a light to my paths. 'All', he says, 'the land of the heretics shall become a desolation and an astonishment, when its last end has been shown to be foolish'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.11-13":["And all these nations shall serve the king of Babylon seventy years. And when the seventy years are fulfilled, I will visit upon the king of Babylon and upon that nation, says the Lord, their iniquities, and upon the land of the Chaldeans, and I will make it everlasting desolations. And I will bring upon that land all my words which I have spoken against it, all that is written in this book, whatever Jeremiah prophesied against all the nations. Just as Jerusalem after seventy years receives back its former inhabitants and, its punishments completed, because it obeyed the sentence of God, enjoys its former happiness, so the king of Babylon, because he was lifted up into pride and judged that his reigning over the nations was the work of his own strength and not of the Lord's will, will be destroyed when the Medes and Persians come. Indeed, to this day only the remains of the city of Babylon survive, and the Lord has made it an everlasting desolation and has fulfilled all the words that are contained in the book of this very prophet. For in what follows the discourse of Jeremiah describes what evils Babylon is to suffer.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.14":["Because they served them, though they were many nations and great kings; and I will repay them according to their works and according to the deeds of their hands. This is not found in the Seventy. And it signifies that Jeremiah prophesied not only against Babylon but also against the other nations which were in the Babylonian army and fought against the people of the Lord. Indeed, in what follows words are spoken against Egypt and the Philistines and Moab and Ammon and Idumea and Damascus and Kedar and the kingdoms of Hazor and Elam, and at the last against Babylon and the land of Chaldea.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.15-16":["'In the ways', he says, 'of your sister Samaria you walked; therefore you must drink the cup of her whose sins you followed — and that a cup is often taken for punishments and torments, there are many examples, among which is this: A cup in the hand of the Lord, full of pure wine, mixed. And he has tilted it from this; yet its dregs are not emptied: all the sinners of the earth shall drink; and in Isaiah it is called 'the cup of ruin' and 'the draught of fury'; and God speaks to Jeremiah, saying: Take the cup of pure wine from my hand, and you shall give drink to all the nations to which I shall send you; and they shall drink and vomit, and go mad; for just as certain cathartics are given so that the noxious humor that is in bodies may be purged out, so the Lord too gives a most undiluted cup of torments, to take away from sinners whatever there is of gall and bitterness and restore them to their former health — ; and the cup itself will be deep and wide: deep in the magnitude of the punishments, wide in the length of the captivity; but when', he says, 'you have been made drunk and seized by sorrow, and have drunk the cup to the dregs and drained it to such a degree that you devour even the fragments of the cup itself, then you will be weary of your former fornication and will tear the breasts which had been broken in Egypt and which were subdued and crushed by your lovers — or I will take away all your feasts and new moons, so that in place of solemnity and gladness you may have eternal lamentation and grief —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"JER.25.15-17":["For thus says the Lord God of Israel to me: Take this cup of the wine of fury — or of pure wine — from my hand, and you shall give drink from it to all the nations to whom I shall send you; and they shall drink and be drunk — or vomit — and go mad before the sword which I shall send among them. And I took the cup from the hand of the Lord and gave drink to all the nations to whom the Lord sent me. The giving to drink of the cup, and of the cup 'of pure wine' or 'unmixed', which in Greek is called akratou, is a sign of the Lord's fury, so that all the nations which made war on the people of God drink of the cup of the Lord's fury, of which Isaiah too writes against Jerusalem: the bowl of fury, the cup of ruin you have drunk up and emptied; therefore arise! And this cup is drunk by all the nations for this reason: that they may vomit and go mad. This, indeed, a perverse interpreter takes in a good sense, as though, like a cathartic potion, it forces out whatever bile and phlegm and harmful humor there is in the breast, and restores former health. He also takes the Savior in place of Jeremiah, because he himself gave drink to all the nations, to whom he was also sent, so that, casting off idolatry, they might dedicate themselves to the worship of God. That this is contrary to holy Scripture what follows will show; for not as a remedy, as he would have it, but as a punishment are they given to drink from the undiluted cup.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.18":["To Jerusalem and to the cities of Judah and to its kings and to its princes, to make them a desolation and an astonishment and a hissing and a curse, as it is this day. 'I thought', he says, 'that I was giving drink to the nations alone, and therefore I had gladly offered myself for this ministry; but among the other nations, nay rather before the other nations, I gave drink to Jerusalem and to the cities of Judah and to its kings and princes, to make them an astonishment and a desolation and a hissing and a curse, as is also proved by the present example'. Hence he also says above: You have deceived me, O Lord, and I was deceived; you were stronger, and you prevailed!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.19-20":["To Pharaoh, king of Egypt, and to his servants and to his princes and to all his people and to all in general. After Jerusalem, Pharaoh, king of Egypt, drinks, and his allies; the princes drank, and the whole people, and everyone who is indeed not an Egyptian but dwells in its regions, whom the Seventy translated symmiktous, that is, 'people of mixed stock', a populace not of the Egyptian region but foreign and immigrant.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.20":["To all the kings of the land of Ausitis. It is called 'Vs' in Hebrew, and the history relates that Job too was from it: there was a certain man in the region of Ausitis, named Job. And yet it should be known that this short verse is not found in the Seventy, and that Theodotion translated 'island' for 'Vs'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","And to all the kings of the land of the Philistines and of Ashkelon and of Gaza and of Ekron and to the remnants of Ashdod. In general he set down the Philistines, that is, the land of the Palestinians, and in particular their cities Ashkelon, Gaza, Ekron, Ashdod; Gath alone he passed over in silence, which is contained in the 'remnants'. For this is what is written: and to the remnants of Ashdod; for the region of the city of Gath is neighboring and bordering on Ashdod, which in Hebrew is called Esdod. And Isaiah too writes that the Palestinians were captured and laid waste by the Babylonians.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.21-22":["To Idumea and Moab and the sons of Ammon and to all the kings of Tyre and to all the kings of Sidon. Idumea is the land in which are the mountains of Seir, and which in Hebrew is called Edom; Moab and Ammon are themselves the sons of Lot, neighbors of the Dead Sea. Tyre and Sidon are the chief cities on the coast of Phoenicia, which themselves too were overcome when the Babylonian came; Carthage is their colony, whence also the Poeni are so called, by a corruption of the word, as though Phoeni, and their language is to a great extent akin to the Hebrew language.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.22":["And to the kings of the islands which are beyond the sea. He passes over to Cyprus and Rhodes and the islands which are called Kyklades [the Cyclades]; for these too were occupied by the Babylonians.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.23":["And Dedan and Teman and Buz and all who are shorn round the hair. These nations are in the wilderness, neighboring on and intermingled with the regions of the Ishmaelites, whom they now call Saracens, and of whom it is said: who are shorn round the hair.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.24-25":["And to all the kings of the west, who dwell in the desert, and to all the kings of Zimri. This too is not found in the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.24":["And to all the kings of Arabia. This is not found in the Seventy. After Dedan, then, and Teman and Buz and the Ishmaelites, the order of the regions follows.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.25":["And to all the kings of Elam and to all the kings of the Medes. From the fact that Elam and the kings of the Medes follow Zimri, we judge that Zimri too is a region of Persia, unless perhaps, from the fact that Arabia came before, these too are to be understood as kings of the desert. Now Elam is a region of Persia beyond Babylon; hence also the Elamites, and the Medes too and the Persians, by whom Babylon was captured and destroyed; and they themselves also drank the undiluted cup when Alexander, king of the Macedonians, gave it them to drink.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.26":["And to all the kings of the north, near and far. Because he had enumerated the regions of Persia and Babylon, of Elam and of the Medes, he now sets down in general all the kings of the north, who are near and far. For 'north', which in Hebrew is called 'safun', the Seventy translated aphēliōtēn, which we can call 'the east wind'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Each one against his brother, and to all the kingdoms of the earth which are upon its face. So that it should not become lengthy to enumerate one by one all the regions of the eastern province, he set down in general all the kingdoms of the earth which dwell on the earth. And as for his saying: against his brother, there is understood 'I gave the undiluted drink', so that all should rage, vomit, go mad and fight one another in mutual battles. And what he added:\n\nKing Sheshach shall drink after them, and is not found in the Seventy, has this sense: 'All the nations round about', he says, 'will be subject to the Babylonian empire, and he will subject all things to his power, so that all the nations which the preceding discourse has recounted serve him and drink of his cup'; hence also in the vision against Babylon it is written: Babylon is a golden cup that makes all the earth drunk. But last of all the king of Babylon will drink this potion, given him to drink by the rider of the chariot, of the camel and of the ass, Cyrus, king of the Medes and Persians. But how Babylon, which in Hebrew is called 'Babel', is to be understood as 'Sesach' will not greatly trouble anyone who has even a little knowledge of the Hebrew language. Just as among us the Greek alphabet is read in order up to the last letter, that is, 'alpha, beta' and so on up to 'omega', and again, for the sake of children's memory, we are accustomed to reverse the order of reading and mix the last letters with the first, so that we say 'alpha omega, beta psi', so too among the Hebrews the first letter is 'aleph', the second 'beth', the third 'gimel', up to the twenty-second and last letter 'thau', of which the next-to-last is 'sin'. And so we read 'aleph thau, beth sin', and when we come to the middle, the letter 'lamed' meets 'chaph'; and just as, if it is read straight, we read 'Babel', so with the order reversed we read 'Sesach'. But the vowel letters between 'beth and beth and lamed' and 'sin and sin and chaph' are not set down in this name, according to the idiōma [idiom] of the Hebrew language; and I think it was prudently concealed by the holy prophet, so that he should not openly stir up against himself the madness of those who were besieging Jerusalem and were on the very point of taking it. We read that the Apostle too did this with regard to the Roman Empire, when he wrote of Antichrist: do you not remember that, when I was still with you, I used to tell you these things? And now you know what restrains, that he may be revealed in his own time, Antichrist being understood. For the mystery of iniquity is already at work; only he who holds now, until he be taken out of the midst. And then the wicked one will be revealed, whom the Lord Jesus will slay with the spirit of his mouth and destroy with the illumination of his coming. By 'he who holds' he indicates the Roman Empire; for unless it has been destroyed and taken out of the midst, according to the prophecy of Daniel, Antichrist will not come before. If he had chosen to say this openly, he would foolishly have stirred up the rage of persecution against the Christians and the Church then coming to birth. We have spoken of this passage at greater length than the brevity of commentaries allows, which the Greeks and Latins will perhaps disdain, because it is not found in their codices. But what will that profit them, when in what follows this same prophet says: how Sheshach has been taken, and the renowned one of the whole earth seized; how Babylon has become an astonishment among the nations? Allegorical interpreters refer this whole passage to all the nations which the devil has made drunk with the most undiluted cup of sins; for he himself too, they say, will at the last drink punishments and torments, he of whom the Apostle writes: whom the Lord Jesus will slay with the spirit of his mouth, whence also in another place he says: but the last enemy will be destroyed, death. It requires great powers to be able to carry the names of different nations over into their etymologiais [etymologies] and to fit individual vices to individual names.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.27":["And when he says that he gave it to drink to the other nations and to Jerusalem and the cities of Judea, he adds: Thus says the Lord Almighty, the God of Israel: Drink and be drunk, and vomit, and fall before the face of the sword which I shall send among you (Jer 25:27). And it is to be noted that this cup of the Lord's fury is his sword, which is sent into the midst of sinners. Here a question arises: how, after the drinking, the drunkenness and vomiting and the fall, it is said in Jeremiah that Jerusalem cannot arise, and now he speaks to her through Isaiah: Lift yourself up, lift yourself up, arise, Jerusalem. It is resolved thus: as long as someone drinks the cup, and is drunk, and raves, and vomits, and falls, he cannot arise; for he has not yet drained the cup of the Lord, nor come to the dregs, so as to have drunk it to the bottom. But now he speaks to Jerusalem in the past tense: who have drunk from the hand of the Lord the cup of his fury, and not, who are drinking.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","And you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Drink and be drunk and vomit and fall and do not rise, before the sword which I will send upon you! 'After', he says, 'you have given drink to all the nations and fulfilled the command of the Lord, you will again command these things with the words of the Lord and say: Drink and be drunk and vomit and fall and do not rise!' If 'to drink, to be drunk, to vomit and to fall' is a sign of salvation, so that, like a cathartic potion, everything harmful is expelled, how does there follow: and do not rise? And what the drink is that makes them fall forever he sets down more plainly, that is: before the sword which I will send among you.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.28-29":["And when they refuse to take the cup from the hand, to drink, you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord of hosts: Drinking you shall drink; for behold, in the city in which my name has been invoked, I am beginning to afflict; and will you, as though innocent, be exempt — or and will you be clean with cleanness — ? you shall not be exempt, or you shall not be clean. He covertly shows that those who have refused to do the command of God willingly will undergo it by necessity, and will hear: drinking you shall drink. 'Whether you will it', he says, 'or not, the sentence of God must be fulfilled. For if the city of Jerusalem, in which the name of God was known, as the prophet says: God is known in Judea; his name is great in Israel, drank the undiluted cup, the cup of fury, how much more will you not be clean, who worship idols in place of the name of God!'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.29":["For I am calling a sword upon all the inhabitants of the earth, says the Lord of hosts. This is the most undiluted drink, this the cup of the Lord's fury, which is called not upon Jerusalem alone but upon the whole earth and all the nations round about; of which he had said above: 'and I will send to Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, my servant, and I will bring them upon this land and upon its inhabitants and upon all the nations that are round about it.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.30-31":["And you shall prophesy to them all these words and say to them: The Lord will roar from on high — or will give answer — and from his holy dwelling he will utter his voice; roaring he will roar over its beauty — or he will answer over his place —, a chant as of those who tread will be sung together against all the inhabitants of the earth. The sound — or destruction — has come to the ends — or upon a part — of the earth, for the Lord has judgment with the nations; he himself is judged with all flesh; the wicked he has given over to the sword, says the Lord. First let us say how the truth of the translation stands. 'To all the nations', he says, 'you shall prophesy and say that the Lord roars from on high, that is, gives a sign of his fury, according to what is written: the lion will roar, and who will not fear? the Lord has spoken, and who will not prophesy?' and from his holy dwelling he will utter his voice, so as to terrify all who hear. And he will roar over its beauty, that is, over the temple. And when he has roared, a chant will be sung like that of those who tread in the winepresses, and at the shedding of one another's blood a mournful song will be sung together, which Symmachus calls katalegma, Aquila iasmon; and the sound of this chant and song will reach to the ends of the earth, for the judgment of the Lord is with the nations. But if the judgment of the Lord is with the nations, the merits of the nations too are diverse. This serves to expound that passage, as some would have it: he who does not believe is already judged. He is judged indeed in that he did not believe; but those very ones who do not believe will be afflicted with punishments that differ among themselves. He himself is judged with all flesh, so that no one goes away unjudged. But the wicked — that is, those who do not believe in the Lord — he gives over to the everlasting sword. Allegorical interpreters expound this passage according to the Seventy in such a way as to refer the Lord's open threat to a good sense. 'The Lord', they say, 'will give answer from on high, namely to those whom he is going to save, and from his holy place he will utter his voice, and he will answer a word in his place. But they, like vintagers, full of the fruits of that sentence, will answer. Upon all the inhabitants of the earth destruction will come, not upon the whole earth, but upon a part of the earth, namely, of those who do not believe; for the Lord will have judgment among the nations, and he himself will be judged with all flesh, but the wicked will be given over to the sword'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.32-33":["Thus says the Lord of hosts: Behold, affliction — or evil — shall go forth from nation to nation, and a great whirlwind — or tempest — shall go forth from the heights — or the ends — of the earth. And the slain of the Lord — or those wounded by the Lord — shall be on that day from the height of the earth to its height; they shall not be lamented, and they shall not be gathered up nor buried; they shall lie as dung upon the face of the earth. Let them blush who try to do violence to holy Scripture by expounding in a good sense things that are full of threatening. Now of this passage I think the Lord too spoke: nation will rise against nation and kingdom against kingdom, and the rest that is contained in that same saying of the Gospel. And let us understand that these things were done according to the history, when all the nations round about were subjugated by the Babylonian king and felt his cruel rule, or according to prophecy, as things that are to come long afterward at the consummation of the world. And they are called slain by the Lord, not because the Lord himself strikes them, but in that in the destruction of the most wicked the will and command of the Lord is fulfilled.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.34-35":["Howl — or shout for joy —, you shepherds, and cry out, and sprinkle yourselves with ashes, you nobles of the flock — or lament, you rams of the sheep —, for your days are fulfilled, that you should be slain, and your scatterings, and you shall fall like precious vessels — or like choice rams —. And flight shall perish from the shepherds, and deliverance from the nobles — or rams — of the flock. It should be noted that only in this place have the Seventy put 'jubilation' in a bad sense, for which the other translators rendered 'howling', as it is written in the Hebrew. Between the shepherds and the rams, or nobles, of the flock there is this difference: the shepherds pertain to rational beings, while the rams and nobles refer to the rich, who themselves too are called part of the flock. And as for what he adds: your days are fulfilled, that you should be slain, their days will be fulfilled then, when their sins have been fulfilled, and they will be scattered and will fall like precious vessels, so that once broken they cannot be restored, and the more precious they were before, the greater the loss in their breaking; or like choice rams, so that they may be a fat victim for those eager to devour them. Flight, he says, shall perish from the shepherds, when they have not shown repentance. Indeed, it is said to the Pharisees: brood of vipers, who showed you how to flee from the wrath to come? And in the Psalms we read: flight has perished from me. And deliverance, he says, from the nobles of the flock, or rams; apo koinou [in common, i.e. shared by both clauses] 'shall perish' is understood.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.36-37":["The voice of the cry of the shepherds and the howling of the nobles of the flock — or the jubilation of the rams —, for the Lord has laid waste their pastures. And the fields — or the beautiful things — of peace have fallen silent before the wrath of the fury of the Lord. Here too it should be noted that in the Seventy 'jubilation' is put for 'howling'. Now between the shepherds and the rams there is this difference: the shepherds are taken to be those in the Church who preside over the flock with wisdom and learning and teaching, but the rams those who seem indeed to be leaders among the people, but have in themselves nothing of teaching and wisdom, and through excessive simplicity are almost neighbors of folly. But when we have peace and do not understand either the good things or the beautiful things of peace, but give ourselves over to luxury and idleness and pleasures, then the good things of peace will grow quiet, or fall silent, and will be taken away from us because of the wrath of the Lord's fury, and that which is written will be fulfilled: when they say: peace and security, then sudden destruction will come upon them, at whose coming all things will fall silent.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.25.38":["He has forsaken, like a lion, the shade — or the lair — that is his — and, as is more correct, the tabernacle; for this is what the Hebrew 'soccho' means —, for their land has become a desolation — or trackless — before the wrath of the dove — or before the great sword — and before the wrath of the fury of the Lord. The Lord, of whom he had said above: from on high he will roar, and from his holy dwelling he will utter his voice; roaring he will roar over its beauty, will himself forsake his tabernacle, of which it is written: his place has been made in peace — or in Salem — and his dwelling in Zion; he himself will desert his seat and will fulfill what he spoke through this same prophet: I have left my house, I have forsaken my inheritance. And he has left, like a lion, his lair, so that all the beasts have power to lay waste his land. For while the lion is its guardian and protector, no one dares to approach it. Their land, he says, has become — without doubt either that of the people of the Jews, or else that of all the nations — a desolation and trackless before the wrath of the dove. He who has read above that Nebuchadnezzar is called the Lord's 'servant' will not be surprised that he is understood as the Lord's 'dove' — for which the Seventy translated 'great sword' —, although we can also take the 'dove' in the person of Jerusalem, because she is angry and sad that she has lost the guardianship of her lion and that her land has come into desolation.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.1":["In the beginning of King Jehoiakim, son of Josiah, king of Judah, this word came from the Lord, saying: Thus says the Lord: Stand in the court of the house of the Lord, and you shall speak to all the cities of Judah, from which they come to worship in the house of the Lord, all the words that I have commanded you to speak to them; do not withhold a word! If perhaps they will hear and turn, each one from his evil way, and I may repent of the evil — or I may rest from the evil —, which I am thinking to do to them because of the wickedness of their pursuits. This prophecy is earlier than the former one, although it was made under the same king. For that one was made in the fourth year of Jehoiakim, son of Josiah king of Judah, but this one in the beginning of the same king, as Scripture says: in the beginning of King Jehoiakim, son of Josiah, king of Judah, this word came from the Lord, saying. Therefore, as we have often said already, the order of history is not to be woven together in the prophets, since in the present case, under the same king, earlier things are spoken afterwards and later things before.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.2":["But he who is about to speak the word of the Lord ought to stand with Moses and to hear with the psalmist: you who stand in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God. And he is commanded to speak to all the cities of Judah, although the Seventy did not translate 'cities', lest perhaps it should seem unfitting to speak, in the court of the house of the Lord, to cities which were not present; but when he speaks to the peoples and to the citizens, he speaks to the cities themselves. And he fittingly stands in the court and vestibule of the temple of the Lord, so that, on the occasion of prayer to the Lord and of worshiping him, they may be compelled to hear the words of the prophet. 'Do not', he says, 'withhold a word; although it be sad, although the rage of those who hear be stirred up against you, nevertheless say what has been commanded you, fearing not the persecutions of those who are to be stirred up against you, but the command of the Lord who orders it!'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.3":["And you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord God; if perhaps even they will hear, and if perhaps they will be quiet. Something similar is written in Jeremiah too: If perhaps they will hear and repent, and in the Gospel: Perhaps they will respect my son. Now God speaks these words with the disposition of one who is in doubt, so that they may show the free choice of man, lest the foreknowledge of future things make immutable, whether for evil or for good, what God knew would come to be: for it is not because he knows what is to come that we must necessarily do what he foreknew; rather, what we are going to do by our own will, he, as God, knows will come to be.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel","Perhaps, he says, they will hear and turn. The ambiguous word 'perhaps' cannot befit the majesty of the Lord, but he speaks according to our feeling, so that free will may be preserved for man, lest by his foreknowledge man be compelled, as if by necessity, either to do something or not to do it; for it is not because God knows that something is going to be that it is therefore going to be, but because it is going to be, God knows it, as one who foreknows things to come. And yet it must be known, according to this same Jeremiah, that if the Lord has foretold evils and the people has repented, he himself too will repent over the things which he threatened to do; and if he has promised prosperity and the people has acted negligently, God changes his sentence and brings evils instead of good things. Something of this kind is also that saying in the Gospel: I will send my son; perhaps they will reverence him; which is surely said in the person of almighty God. Finally, in the present passage too he says: 'if perhaps they will hear and turn, each one from his evil way, so that, when they have turned, I too may repent of my sentence and not do what I am thinking to do to them. But I am thinking to do it because of the wickedness of their pursuits; and if these have been changed, my sentence too will be changed.' Let us read the history of Jonah and Nineveh.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.4-5":["And you shall say to them: Thus says the Lord: If you will not hear me, to walk in my law which I gave you, to hear the words of my servants, the prophets, whom I sent to you, rising up by night and sending, and you did not hear, I will make this house like Shiloh, and this city I will make a curse to all the nations of the earth. It is therefore placed in our power either to do something or not to do it, only in such a way that whatever good work we will, seek and accomplish, we refer to the grace of God, who, according to the Apostle, has given us both to will and to accomplish. But if it suffices to walk once in the law which was given to us through Moses, as the foolish heresy supposes, how is it that he added: to hear the words of my servants, the prophets? who surely were sent after the law, and not once but frequently, nor idly and as from one at ease, but always and as from one who is anxious.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.6":["'I sent', he says, 'to you my servants daily and rising up by night; and if you will not hear them, I will make this house' — that is, the temple of God — 'like Shiloh', where the tabernacle was. And when the temple has been destroyed, consequently the city too will be a curse to all the nations of the earth. And just as, when the temple had been built on the threshing floor of Ornan and on Mount 'Moria', that is, 'of vision', on which Abraham is related to have offered his son Isaac, the worship of Shiloh ceased and no sacrifices were afterwards celebrated there, so, when the Church had been built and spiritual victims had been offered in it, the ceremonies of the law ceased, and the city of the Jews was made a curse to all the nations of the earth, from which the Lord has freed us, as the Apostle says: Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, having been made a curse for us.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.7-9":["And the priests and the prophets — or false prophets — and all the people heard Jeremiah speaking these words in the house of the Lord. And when Jeremiah had finished speaking all that the Lord had commanded him to speak to all the people, the priests and the prophets — or false prophets — and all the people seized him, saying: Let him die the death, because he has prophesied in the name of the Lord, saying: This house shall be like Shiloh, and this city shall be made desolate, so that there is no inhabitant. The priests and the prophets, whom the Seventy more plainly translated as 'false prophets', are angry with Jeremiah because he preaches true things, and because, with the temple overthrown and the city deserted, their religion would perish, and the profits coming from religion; therefore they seize him and, with the people consenting with them, destine him for death, because he had said in the name of the Lord: This house shall be like Shiloh, and the city shall be made desolate, so that there is no inhabitant. If, then, at any time, because of the commandments of the Lord and the truth of the faith, either priests or false prophets or a deceived people are angry with us, let us not greatly care, but let us carry out the sentence of God, pondering in our mind by no means present evils, but future goods.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.9":["And all the people was gathered against Jeremiah in the house of the Lord. And the princes of Judah heard these words, and they went up from the king's house to the house of the Lord and sat in the entrance of the new gate of the Lord. Jeremiah was prophesying the words of the Lord in the temple and had said: I will make this house like Shiloh, and this city a curse to all the nations of the earth, and at once, a riot having been stirred up by the priests and prophets and people, the whole crowd is gathered against the prophet into the temple, where the prophet was and was being held by the hands of the priests and prophets and crowd.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.10":["When the princes of the city, who were staying in the royal house, had heard this, they crossed over, or went up from the king's house to the house of the Lord. And it is to be noted that to go to the house of the Lord is always an ascent. And they sat in the entrance of the new gate of the Lord. For it was the duty of the princes to sit in the gate of the house of the Lord and there to learn the truth of the business and of the riot. But the gate is called 'new' because those who sat in it and presided over the judgment resisted the slanders of the priests and false prophets.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.11":["And the priests and the prophets spoke to the princes and to all the people, saying: There is a judgment of death for this man, because he has prophesied against this city, as you have heard with your ears. While the princes of the city, who had run from the king's palace to the temple so that the riot of the people might be calmed, were sitting in the gate of the temple and in the new gate, and an assembly of the people had been gathered, the priests and false prophets accuse him; and the prophet would have perished, as far as it lay with the priests and prophets, if the accusers themselves had had the power of judgment. From this we understand that those who seemed devoted to religion were crueler toward the prophet, through envy of his holiness, than those who presided over public needs.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.12-15":["And Jeremiah said to all the princes and to all the people, saying: The Lord sent me to prophesy to this house — or upon this house — and to this city — or upon this city — all the words which you have heard. Now therefore make good your ways and your pursuits, and hear the voice of the Lord your God, and the Lord will repent of the evil which he has spoken against you — or and the Lord will rest from the evils which he has spoken against you —. As for me, behold, I am in your hands; do to me as is good and right in your eyes — or as is expedient for you — ! Nevertheless, know and understand that, if you kill me, you will bring innocent blood against your very selves and against this city and its inhabitants; for in truth the Lord sent me to you, to speak in your ears all these words. When, with the people present, the princes of the city were sitting in the gate, and the priests and prophets were accusing Jeremiah the prophet and pressing a capital charge, Jeremiah speaks to the princes and to all the people, whom the faction of the priests and false prophets had stirred up, prudently and at the same time humbly and steadfastly. Prudently, in that he said he had been sent by the Lord to speak against the temple and the city, and gave the counsel that, if they were willing to hear his counsel and to repent, the Lord too would change his sentence. Humbly, in what he says: behold, I am in your hands; do to me as is good and right in your eyes! And steadfastly: in truth the Lord sent me to you, to speak in your ears all these words. And in other words he says: 'If you are angry because I have spoken against the temple and the city of the Lord, and the safety of the city and of the temple is your concern, why do you add sins to sins and make both the city and its inhabitants guilty of my blood?' If, then, at any time we too have need of humility because of the straits of necessity, let us so take it up that we do not desert truth and steadfastness; for it is one thing proudly to insult the one who judges, which is a sign of folly, another so to avoid the impending danger that you subtract nothing from the truth.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.16":["And the princes and all the people said to the priests and the prophets: There is no judgment of death for this man, because he has spoken to us in the name of the Lord our God. The people, which had earlier been tripped up by the priests and false prophets, joins the princes of the city and speaks for Jeremiah, saying that he is by no means guilty of death, but has prophesied in the name of the Lord and from his mouth; for the untaught crowd, once it has received a reason, quickly changes its opinion. But the resentment of the accusers, especially of the priests and false prophets, cannot be changed; and therefore, while they accuse and persevere in the accusation, the people is changed, because he had given them hope of the Lord's pardon, if they made their ways good and heard the voice of the Lord their God, so that the Lord too would bend his sentence.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.17-19":["And there rose up men of the elders of the land and said to the whole assembly of the people, speaking: Micah of Moresheth was a prophet in the days of Hezekiah, king of Judah, and he spoke to all the people of Judah, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts: Zion shall be plowed like a field, and Jerusalem shall become a heap of stones, and the mountain of the house the heights of the forests. Did Hezekiah, king of Judah, and all Judah condemn him to death? Did they not fear the Lord and entreat the face of the Lord, and the Lord repented of the evil which he had spoken against them? So we are doing a great evil against our own souls. The princes of the city and the people understand the truth of the judgment; but the elders, whose proper task it was to know old things, unfold the history and prophecy of Micah of Moresheth, who prophesied under King Hezekiah, compare it with the prophecy of Jeremiah, for which a capital charge is being pressed against him, and show that Micah had said graver things and yet had suffered nothing from the righteous king Hezekiah, but that they, converted to repentance, had turned the sentence of the Lord in a good direction. For Micah said: Zion shall be plowed like a field, and Jerusalem shall become a heap of stones, and the mountain of the house the heights of the forests; and Jeremiah: I will make, he says, this house like Shiloh, and this city I will make a curse to all the nations of the earth. And they give counsel, thinking that what Micah had foretold would by no means come to pass, because for a long time it had not been done on account of the repentance of the people, and that this too, which Jeremiah spoke, would by no means come to be, if, according to his counsel, they made good their ways and their pursuits and heard the voice of the Lord their God, so that the Lord would not bring the evil with which he had threatened them. And at the same time they break the rage of the accusers and mingle themselves with them, saying: So we are doing a great evil against our own souls, not that they ought to do it, but because, if they did it, they would by no means harm the accused, but their own souls, which they could have freed by a change of their sentence.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.20-24":["There was also a man prophesying in the name of the Lord, Uriah, son of Shemaiah of Kiriath-jearim, and he prophesied against this city and against this land according to all the words of Jeremiah. And King Jehoiakim and all his mighty men and his princes heard these words, and the king sought to kill him; and Uriah heard and was afraid and fled and entered Egypt. And what follows: and King Jehoiakim sent men into Egypt, Elnathan, son of Achbor, and men with him into Egypt, is not found in the Seventy. Then is joined: and they brought Uriah out of Egypt and led him to King Jehoiakim; and he struck him with the sword and cast his corpse into the graves of the ignoble common people. Nevertheless the hand of Ahikam, son of Shaphan, was with Jeremiah, so that he should not be delivered into the hand of the people and they kill him. It is asked why, when Uriah, son of Shemaiah, from the town of Kiriath-jearim, had prophesied the same things as Jeremiah, and, struck with fear, fled to Egypt and, dragged back from there, was killed, Jeremiah was able to escape, who certainly had not fled, but, boldly persevering in his former statement, was freed both by the judgment of the crowd and of the princes and by the counsel of the elders against the accusers, the priests and false prophets. To this one must briefly answer that the judgment of God can by no means be known, when, in the same cause and for the same statement, one is punished and another is freed; unless perhaps we can answer this, that Uriah was slaughtered to the condemnation of both the accusers and the people, while Jeremiah was preserved by the judgment of God, so that he might preach to the remnant of the unhappy people and draw them back to repentance. This indeed we also read in the Acts of the Apostles: that the apostle James at once suffered the sentence of Herod and was crowned with martyrdom, but Peter and the other apostles were preserved for the teaching of the Lord. And the prophetic steadfastness is to be noticed, in that not even when dragged back from Egypt did Uriah change his statement, but, seeing death threatened against him, nonetheless spoke what the Lord had commanded. And that he feared and fled and entered Egypt is a sign not of unbelief but of prudence, lest we offer ourselves to dangers in vain. Otherwise we read that the Lord and Savior too slipped out of the hands of his persecutors, and that he commanded the apostles: when they persecute you in this city, flee to another!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.22":["It is also asked how Jehoiakim, king of Judah, a king of small dominion, weakened, and about to perish at any moment, had the power of sending into Egypt and of bringing Uriah out from there. This is easily solved if we consider that he had been appointed prince by Neco, king of Egypt, and that this prophecy was made in the beginning of his reign.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.26.24":["But although Jeremiah was freed by the help of the Lord, yet it is reckoned as a reward to him also through whom the Lord freed his prophet, namely to Ahikam, son of Shaphan, as we shall read later, when Jeremiah is freed from the danger of death out of the cistern of mud by the counsel and protection of Ebed-melech the eunuch.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.1":["In the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim, son of Josiah, king of Judah, this word came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying. This is not found in the edition of the Seventy, and many think it is the beginning of the following chapter, which is by no means so; rather, it is to be joined to the preceding one, so that whatever is reported as said and done we should believe to have been done in the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim; whence too he had the power of sending into Egypt, as to a friendly king. But the Seventy seem to me to have passed over this title in silence for this reason, lest they should seem to say it a second time; for they had already set down at the beginning: in the beginning of King Jehoiakim, son of Josiah, king of Judah, this word came from the Lord, saying.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.2-4":["Thus says the Lord to me: Make for yourself bonds and chains — or kloious [collars], which in Hebrew are called 'mutoth', and in common speech they call them 'boias' [neck-shackles] — and you shall put them on your neck and you shall send to the king of Edom and to the king of Moab and to the king of the sons of Ammon and to the king of Tyre and to the king of Sidon by the hand of the messengers who have come to Jerusalem to Zedekiah, king of Judah. And you shall command them to speak to their masters: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Thus shall you say to your masters. The previous vision came to the prophet in the beginning of the reign of Jehoiakim, son of Josiah, king of Judah, but this one under Zedekiah, who reigned last in Jerusalem and under whom the city was taken and overthrown. And Jeremiah is commanded to put chains, or wooden forks, which, as we have said, are called 'mutoth' in Hebrew, on his neck, and to send them to the kings of Edom, Moab, the sons of Ammon, Tyre and Sidon by the envoys who had come to Zedekiah, and to command them to announce to their masters that they must serve King Nebuchadnezzar and hear the things which the prophet's following discourse goes on to set out. And lest perhaps the envoys and the kings of these nations should answer: 'Why do you not command this to your own people?', he speaks similar things to King Zedekiah too, and to the priests and prophets. This passage the ever-allegorical interpreter, who flees from the truth of history, interprets of the heavenly Jerusalem: that its inhabitants must voluntarily take on bodies and descend into 'Babylon', that is, into the 'confusion' of this world, which is set in the evil one, and serve the Babylonian king, without doubt the devil; and that, if they will not do this, they will by no means bear heavy bodies, but will perish by sword and famine and pestilence, and will by no means become men, but demons. Let this stand as what he said, lest his defenders bring a false charge against us. But as for us, let us follow the simple and true history, lest we be wrapped in certain clouds and illusions.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.5":["I made the earth and man and the beasts that are upon the face of the earth, by my great strength and by my outstretched arm, and I gave it to him who pleased in my eyes. Although this scripture speaks anthrōpopathōs [in human terms], in the way that we men can speak and understand, yet the 'strength' of God and his 'arm' is he of whom the Apostle also speaks: 'Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God', and Isaiah: Lord, who has believed our hearing, and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed? John the evangelist also writes: all things were made through him, and without him nothing was made. David too speaks in his song: by the word of the Lord the heavens were established, and all their power by the spirit of his mouth. And as for his saying: I gave it to him who pleased in my eyes, this signifies that all things have been granted to the human race through the grace of God. I, he says, made the earth and man and the beasts. The order is reversed; for in Genesis the living creatures are made first and man last, but here he names man first and afterwards the things that are subject to man.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.6":["Now therefore I have given all these lands into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, my servant; and moreover I have also given him the beasts of the field, that they may serve him. And all the nations shall serve him and his son and his son's son. Of what great unhappiness is Israel, when in comparison with it Nebuchadnezzar is called 'servant of God'! It is written in the Gospel: the world was made through him, and the world did not know him; he came to his own, and his own did not receive him. Rightly therefore does the Creator hand over his creation to whom he will. Finally the devil too, as whose type Nebuchadnezzar went before, confesses: all these things have been handed over to me. And as for what he added: moreover I have also given him the beasts of the field, that they may serve him, either we ought simply to understand every kind of living creature — for together with man the things subject to him are also handed over — or certainly let us take the 'beasts' as the savage nations, because even those serve which before had not known how to serve.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.7":["And his 'son' and 'son's son' — according to the Hebrew — he calls Belshazzar and Evil-merodach, of whom Daniel writes.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Until the time of his land and of himself comes. Fittingly, lest the empire of Nebuchadnezzar be thought perpetual, he says that he himself too is to be taken by the Medes and Persians; for this is what it signifies: until the time of his land and of himself comes. But this too is not found in the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","And many nations and great kings shall serve him. He did not say 'all' — for this is properly due to the empire of Christ —, although according to Symmachus one does not read: many nations and great kings shall serve him, but: many nations and great kings shall subject him to servitude, so that he himself too, whom all the nations had served before, serves the Medes and Persians. This, which we have set down from the Hebrew: I have given him, that they may serve him, and all the nations shall serve him and his son and his son's son, until the time of his land and of himself comes and many nations and great kings subject him to servitude, is not read, as we have already said, in the edition of the Seventy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.8":["But the nation and kingdom which will not serve Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, and whoever will not bend his neck under the yoke of the king of Babylon, with sword and famine and pestilence I will visit upon that nation, says the Lord, until I consume them by his hand. Not only does the Lord subject sinful nations to Nebuchadnezzar, but the Apostle too speaks of sinners: whom I have handed over to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme; and in another place: I have handed over such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved. He also warns that the powers must be obeyed not only because of wrath, but also because of conscience, lest we be condemned by them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.9-11":["You therefore, do not listen to your prophets and diviners and dreamers and augurs and sorcerers, who say to you: You shall not serve the king of Babylon; for they prophesy a lie to you, to remove you far from your land and to drive you out, and you perish. But the nation that puts its neck under the yoke of the king of Babylon and serves him, I will leave it in its own land, says the Lord, and it shall till it and dwell in it. In this place too the allegorical interpreter raves, and exhorts those set in the heavenly Jerusalem not to hear their prophets and diviners, dreamers and augurs and sorcerers, but rather to serve Nebuchadnezzar and take on the body of humility, infancy, the wailing and the cradles of little ones; for if they do this, when their servitude and the condition of human mortality have been fulfilled, they will return to their land and dwell in it and work the works which they worked before. And he says that he suspects that those who have despised the commands of God to be weighed down with human bodies will become demons and unclean spirits and will by no means regain their former seat. But let us expound simply: that there are among the nations prophets, who pretend that they foretell things to come by a divine spirit; and diviners, of whom there is also the common proverb: 'the wise, they say, divine'; and dreamers, who imitate Joseph and Daniel; and augurs, who by the flight of birds and the voices of omen-birds announce what is to be done or not to be done; and sorcerers, whom we can call either poisoners or those who serve the phantasms of demons, who in Hebrew are called 'chassane'. 'All these', he says, 'deceive you and trip you up, so that you may not serve the king of Babylon'. For it is much better to take up servitude voluntarily and to have as a friend the one you serve, and to till your native land, than to serve as captives by force and necessity.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.12-15":["And to Zedekiah, king of Judah, I spoke according to all these words, saying: Put your necks under the yoke of the king of Babylon and serve him and his people, and you shall live. Why will you die, you and your people, by sword, famine and pestilence, as the Lord has spoken to the nation that will not serve the king of Babylon? Do not listen to the words of the prophets who say to you: Do not serve the king of Babylon; for they themselves speak a lie to you, for I did not send them, says the Lord, and they prophesy in my name falsely, so that I may drive you out and you perish, both you and the prophets who prophesy to you. After all the nations he passes to Zedekiah, king of Judah, and speaks with the same words with which he threatened the nations; for he who has sinned sins either like or greater than the other nations does not merit the privilege of the Israelite nation. Finally, because the rebellious people disdained to hear, it was consumed by sword, famine and pestilence. And it is to be observed in holy Scripture that instead of 'false prophets' it calls 'prophets' those who prophesy falsely in the name of the Lord. 'And they do this, so that I', he says, 'may drive you out and you perish, both you and the prophets who prophesy to you'; therefore the destruction both of those who are deceived and of those who deceive is alike. This, which we have set down from the Hebrew: under the yoke of the king of Babylon and serve him and his people, and you shall live. Why will you die, you and your people, by sword, famine and pestilence, as the Lord has spoken to the nation that will not serve the king of Babylon? do not listen to the words of the prophets who say to you, is not found in the Seventy; and therefore I admonish the prudent reader to understand how much is lacking, point by point, in the Greek and Latin codices.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.16-17":["And to the priests and to this people I spoke, saying: Thus says the Lord: Do not listen to the words of your prophets who prophesy to you, saying: Behold, the vessels of the Lord will return from Babylon now quickly; for they prophesy a lie to you. — This, which we have set down, 'now quickly', is not found in the Seventy, and likewise that which follows: — Do not listen to them, but serve the king of Babylon, that you may live! Why is this city given over to desolation? And after the nations and the king he speaks to the priests and to the people, because he had already announced destruction to the prophets above, saying: so that I may drive you out and you perish, both you and the prophets who prophesy to you. And he speaks the same things which he had spoken to the king and to the nations: that they should not hear the words of their prophets and say that the vessels which had been carried away with Jeconiah and the princes and his mother were to be brought back to the temple of the Lord right now; and he warns them to serve the king of Babylon and live, and that the city, voluntarily subjected, should by no means be handed over to burning. And in this is the clemency of the Lord: to hand them over to a lighter punishment, lest they endure a heavier one.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.27.18-22":["And if they are prophets and the word of the Lord is in them, let them meet — and what follows, which we are about to subjoin up to the end of this chapter, is not found in the Seventy — the Lord of hosts, that the vessels which had been left in the house of the Lord and in the house of the king of Judah and in Jerusalem may not come into Babylon. For thus says the Lord of hosts to the pillars and to the sea and to the bases — for which in the Hebrew is written 'mechonoth' — and to the rest of the vessels which remained in this city, which Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, did not take when he carried Jeconiah, son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, from Jerusalem into Babylon, and all the nobles of Judah and Jerusalem. For thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, to the vessels which have been left in the house of the Lord and in the house of the king of Judah and in Jerusalem: They shall be carried into Babylon and shall be there until the day of their visitation, says the Lord, and I will cause them to be brought and restored to this place. These words, as we have said, are not found in the Seventy, but have been translated from the Hebrew truth; in place of which they set down something else, which was not written, saying: to me, because thus said the Lord: and the rest of the vessels which the king of Babylon did not take, when he carried Jeconiah from Jerusalem, shall enter Babylon, says the Lord, setting down the sense rather than the words, perhaps thinking it irrational that God should speak to the pillars and to the sea and to the bases and to the rest of the vessels which had remained in Jerusalem — as if we did not read both that the Lord rebuked the morning worm and that he spoke to the sea: be silent and be still! And what he says: let them meet me or the Lord of hosts, shows that a true prophet can withstand the Lord with prayers, in accordance with which Moses too stood in the smiting against the Lord, to turn away the wrath of his fury; Samuel also did the same. And the Lord to Moses: let me alone, he says, I will strike this people! But when he says: let me alone, he shows that he can be held back by the prayers of the saints. 'Let the prophets meet', he says, 'and let them show whatever they foretell fulfilled in deed, and then the prophecy will be proved by its truth'. Now the pillars and the sea and the bases and the rest of the vessels we read of in Malachim [Kings] and at the end of this prophet's volume; and there are enumerated the vessels that were carried into Babylon when Zedekiah was taken and the city burned and the temple overthrown.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.28.1-3":["And it came to pass in that year, in the beginning of the reign of Zedekiah, king of Judah, in the fourth year, in the fifth month, Hananiah son of Azzur, the prophet from Gibeon, said to me in the house of the Lord before the priests and all the people, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: I have broken the yoke of the king of Babylon; yet two years of days, and I will cause all the vessels of the Lord to be brought back to this place. Those whom the Hebrew calls 'prophets', that is 'nebeim', the Seventy translated 'false prophets', in order to make the understanding plainer. Indeed, in the present passage too he is called 'prophet', that is 'nebia', and not 'false prophet'. Now the word of the Lord comes under King Zedekiah, in the fourth year of his reign and in the fifth month — while the prophet Ezekiel was not yet prophesying in Babylon to those who had gone into exile with Jeconiah — and he has the confidence to speak in the temple of the Lord against the prophet, because he promises prosperity to the people, and lies are gladly heard, especially those that promise joyful things. Jeremiah had said that the rest of the vessels too, whether of the temple or of the royal house and of the whole people, which Nebuchadnezzar had left behind, were to be carried over to Babylon; but this man, on the contrary, promises that even those which had been carried over are to be brought back.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.28.3-4":["Which Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, took from this place and carried over into Babylon. Jeconiah, son of Jehoiakim, king of Judah, and all the exile of Judah, who went into Babylon, I will bring back to this place, says the Lord; for I will break the yoke of the king of Babylon. For which the Seventy translated: Jeconiah and the exile of Judah, because I will break the yoke of the king of Babylon, briefly setting down the sense of the Hebrew truth rather than its words. Now Hananiah the 'prophet' — as he then seemed to the people — promises that not only the vessels but also King Jeconiah is to be brought back to Jerusalem, and that the yoke of the Babylonian king is to be broken, that is, his empire destroyed, and that this would come about before two years were yet completed, so that the nearness of the time of the promise might increase the greatness of the joy.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.28.6":["And Jeremiah the prophet said to Hananiah the prophet in the eyes of the priests and in the eyes of all the people who stood in the house of the Lord, and Jeremiah the prophet said: Amen, so may the Lord do; may the Lord raise up your words which you have prophesied, that the vessels may be brought back from the house of the Lord, and all the exile of Babylon, to this place! He wishes 'to be done' what the false prophet lyingly tells — for this is what 'amen' means, a word the Lord often uses in the Gospel: amen, amen I say to you —, and for the sake of the prosperity of affairs he desires that the other rather than himself be speaking the truth. Hence another prophet too testifies, saying: would that I were not a man having the spirit, and spoke a lie rather! Jonah, on the contrary, is grieved that he has lied, and is rebuked by the Lord, being shown that a lie on the prophet's part is more useful than the ruin of so great a multitude. And lest he should seem to approve the false prophet's prophecy, he adds the truth under the example of others, without insulting the liar:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.28.7-9":["Nevertheless hear this word which I speak in your ears and in the ears of all the people: the prophets who were before me and you from the beginning, and prophesied over many lands and over great kingdoms of battle and of affliction and of famine; the prophet who prophesied peace, when his word comes, the prophet will be known whom the Lord has sent in truth. Jeremiah could have said to Hananiah: 'You speak falsely, you deceive the people, you are not a prophet but a false prophet.' But had he said that, the false prophet too could have turned the same words back upon Jeremiah; therefore he does him no insult and speaks as though to a 'prophet'. 'Not only,' he says, 'am I a prophet, and you, but before me and you there were many others, among whom were Isaiah, Hosea, Joel and Amos and the rest. They prophesied,' he says, 'against many lands and kingdoms not small but great, announcing to them war and adversity and want of all things; and on the contrary there were others who promised peace and prosperity. The pronouncement of both was proved not by the flattery of a lie but by the outcome of events.' Under the example of others, then, he speaks of himself and of Hananiah: that when the end of events has come, then the truth of the prophets is shown. The Lord also said this same thing through Moses, that a prophet is shown by the fulfilment of his prophecy. And this must be noted, that not threateningly, not savagely, but with the confidence of truth he rebukes the liar and defers the matter to the future, so that those who hear may await the outcome of events.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.28.10-11":["And Hananiah the prophet took the chain — or the fork, which in Hebrew is called 'muthoth', — from the neck of Jeremiah the prophet and broke it. And Hananiah spoke in the sight of all the people, saying: Thus says the Lord: so will I break the yoke of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, in two years of days, from the neck of all the nations. And Jeremiah went on his way. The Seventy did not translate 'two years'; nor did they call Hananiah 'prophet', lest, that is, they should seem to call a prophet one who was not a prophet — as if many things in the holy Scriptures were not said according to the opinion of the time to which the events reported belong, and not according to what the truth of the matter held; thus Joseph too is called in the Gospel the 'father' of the Lord, and Mary herself, who knew that she had conceived of the Holy Spirit and had answered the angel: how will this be, since I have not known a man?, says to her son: son, why have you done so to us? behold, your father and I, sorrowing, were seeking you. And at the same time the prudence of Jeremiah, and his humility and patience, are to be considered. The false prophet does him injury by deed and, snatching the fork from his neck, breaks it — which he certainly could not have done with an iron one —; this man is silent and conceals his pain; for it had not yet been revealed to him by the Lord what he should say, so that holy Scripture might tacitly show that the prophets by no means speak by their own will alone, but also by the will of the Lord, above all concerning things to come, the knowledge of which belongs to God alone. He went, he says, and withdrew on his way, as though defeated, and fulfilling that prophetic word: I became as a man who does not hear and has no reproofs in his mouth.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.28.12-14":["And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, after Hananiah the prophet had broken the chain — or fork — from the neck of Jeremiah the prophet, saying: Go, and you shall say to Hananiah: Thus says the Lord: the chain — or fork — of wood you have broken, and you will make — or I will make — in their place forks — or chains — of iron. For thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: I have put a yoke of iron upon the neck of all these nations, that they may serve Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon. And what follows: and they will serve him; moreover I have also given him the beasts of the earth, is not found in the Seventy. And in the present passage, according to the Seventy, Hananiah is not written 'prophet', nor in what follows, lest, that is, as I said before, they should seem to call a 'false prophet' a 'prophet'; but what is that to the Hebrew truth? After Jeremiah the prophet had gone on his way and swallowed the injury in silence, the word of the Lord came to him, so that the prophet should not speak in his own words to the false prophet glorying in his lie, but should say: thus says the Lord, although Hananiah too, in breaking the wooden fork, had spoken with the same authority in the sight of the Lord: thus says the Lord; for falsehood always imitates truth. And what he added: you have broken forks of wood and you will make — or you have made — in their place chains of iron, shows this: that by refusing a lesser penalty he became the cause of a greater punishment among the people. In this passage too the allegorical interpreter raves, calling the wooden forks and chains ethereal and aerial bodies, namely those of demons and of the hostile powers, but the iron forks or chains our grosser bodies, which are woven together of sinews and bones, flesh and veins, so that those from the heavenly Jerusalem who refused to undergo lesser torments according to the quality of their sin are condemned into the chains of our bodies, and endure the wailing of infancy, the bonds and filth of swaddling clothes, and serve the devil, the king of Babylon, that is, of this world — Scripture saying: the world is set in the evil one — together with the beasts of the earth, which are bound fast in the bodies of brute animals. That unlearned commentator and follower of the Grunnian slander has compelled me to set forth openly the vices of others, which before I used to speak of with reserve, leaving them to the reader's prudence.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.28.15-17":["And Jeremiah the prophet said to Hananiah the prophet: Hear, Hananiah: the Lord has not sent you, and you have made this people trust in a lie. Therefore thus says the Lord: behold, I will send away — or cast out — you from the face of the earth; this year you will die. And what follows: because you have spoken against the Lord. And Hananiah the prophet died in that year, in the seventh month, is not found in the Seventy, in place of which they put only: and he died in the seventh month. Here too in the Seventy Hananiah is not called 'prophet', though — according to the Hebrew — holy Scripture calls him 'prophet', even if, where Jeremiah rebukes him, saying: hear, Hananiah, the Lord has not sent you, he left out 'prophet'; for how could he call him 'prophet' whom he denied had been sent by the Lord? But the truth and order of the history are kept, as we said above, not according to what was, but according to what was thought at that time. 'You have deceived,' he says, 'the people with a lie, so that they would not acquiesce in God's sentence; know, therefore, that you will die this year!' If, when we die, we are freed from the prisons of our bodies, according to that testimony which the heretics interpret wrongly: bring my soul out of prison, how is death now inflicted on the false prophet as a punishment? And this too must be noted, that Jeremiah, having suffered injury from the false prophet, is silent while the word of the Lord had not yet come to him, but afterwards, sent by the Lord, boldly rebukes the liar and announces his approaching death. And as to his dying in the 'seventh' month: those who are always accustomed to interpret this number as showing 'rest' will perhaps falsely claim that he died in the 'seventh' month for this reason, that he might be freed from the evils of the body, according to the saying they bring forward as written: 'death is rest to a man'. But we know that the bodies of believers are temples of God, if indeed the Holy Spirit dwells in them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.1-4":["And these are the words of the book which Jeremiah the prophet sent from Jerusalem to the remnant of the elders of the exile, and to the priests, and to the prophets, and to all the people whom Nebuchadnezzar had carried over from Jerusalem into Babylon, after Jeconiah the king went out, and the lady — or the queen — and the eunuchs and the princes of Judah and Jerusalem — or from Jerusalem or the nobles — and the craftsmen from Jerusalem, by the hand of Elasah son of Shaphan and of Gemariah son of Hilkiah, whom Zedekiah king of Judah sent to Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, into Babylon, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, to all the exile which I carried over from Jerusalem into Babylon: Build houses and dwell, and plant gardens — or orchards — and eat their fruit! Take wives and beget sons and daughters; give wives to your sons and give your daughters to husbands, and let them bear sons and daughters; and multiply, and do not be few in number! And seek the peace of the city — or of the land —, to which I have carried you over, and pray for it to the Lord, for in its peace there will be peace for you! This letter, nay rather little book, of Jeremiah the prophet is sent into Babylon through Zedekiah's envoys, Elasah and Gemariah, to those who had been carried over by Nebuchadnezzar with Jeconiah and his mother, so that, taking the occasion of the royal embassy, the prophet might also accomplish his own work and admonish the exiled people of the things that had been commanded him by the Lord. And he said finely: Jeconiah the king went out, and the lady, the eunuchs and the princes of Judah and the rest, and: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, to all the exile which I carried over from Jerusalem into Babylon, so that they may be seen to have been carried over not by the power of the king of Babylon but by the will of the Lord. And the word of God is directed first to the elders, then to the priests, third to the prophets, fourth to all the people, so that the prophet's letter too might reach those who were being admonished according to the order of age.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.5-7":["But he admonishes them not with his own words but with the Lord's, that they should build houses and dwell in them, and plant gardens or orchards and eat their fruit, take wives and beget sons and daughters, and multiply in the place of exile and not be few in number, and seek the peace of the city or land to which the Lord had carried them over, and pray for it to the Lord. And giving the reasons he says: 'for in the peace of that land there will be peace for you'. To Jeremiah, because captivity was to overtake Jerusalem after a short time, it is commanded that he not take a wife, that he not beget sons. Hence to us also it is said through the Apostle: the time has been shortened; it remains that those who have wives be as though they had none. But if, because of the narrowness of the time, the use of wives is taken away from those who have them, how much more is it commanded to those who do not have them that they not take them! Now the prophet's prophecy commands all this so that they may not give in to the false prophets, who were promising them a return to Jerusalem after a short time, but that they may know that they will stay a long time in Babylon, so that they ought to take wives, plant orchards and sow gardens, build houses and beget sons. And in what he added: seek the peace of the city or of the land, and again: for in its peace there will be peace for you, that saying of the Apostle is confirmed in which he enjoins: I beseech therefore, first of all, that supplications, prayers, intercessions, thanksgivings be made for all men, for kings and all who are in high station, that we may lead a quiet and tranquil life in all piety and chastity. Further, according to the mystical senses: after we have been cast out of Jerusalem, that is, the Church, because of our sins, and handed over to Nebuchadnezzar — of whom the same Apostle says: 'I have handed over such a one to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, that the spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord' and again: whom I have handed over to Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme —, we ought not to be secure, nor, sluggish with idleness, to despair utterly of salvation, but first to build houses — not upon sand, but upon rock — and such houses as the midwives too built in Exodus, because they feared the Lord; Then to plant gardens or orchards, such a paradise as the Lord too planted in Eden, and set in it the tree of life, of which it is written: it is a tree of life to those who lay hold of her; and he who holds her fast, blessed; third, to take wives, of whom one is wisdom, of whom Solomon writes: love her, and she will keep you; encircle her, and she will exalt you, and in another place: her I sought to take as a bride for myself, and I became a lover of her beauty. Nor does one spouse, wisdom, suffice us, unless we have the rest also: fortitude, temperance and righteousness, so that from them we may beget more sons. Let us also give our daughters to husbands, so that the truth of faith, which is understood in the sons, may be coupled with good works, which are referred to the daughters, and good works may be joined to the soundness of faith. And begetting such sons and daughters, let us be multiplied in number, so that, putting away the things of a little child and growing up to the perfect man, we may deserve to hear: I write to you, fathers, because you have known him who is from the beginning, and may say with the Apostle to our children: for in Christ Jesus through the Gospel I have begotten you. Let us also seek peace for the Church, our city and our land, so that we may deserve to return to it, from which by the Lord's judgment we have been carried over to dwell in the error of 'confusion'; for if it receives us, we shall have peace. And at the same time the clemency of the Lord is to be considered: he commands us to pray for our enemies and to do good to those who persecute us, so that we may not be content with our own salvation only, but may seek the salvation of our enemies also.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.8-9":["For thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Let not your prophets who are in your midst, and your diviners, lead you astray, and do not attend to your dreams which you dream, for these prophesy falsehoods to you in my name, and I did not send them, says the Lord. That there were prophets, nay rather false prophets, and diviners and dreamers in Babylon among those whom Nebuchadnezzar had led away with Jeconiah and his mother, the prophet Ezekiel testifies, writing against those whom Jeremiah too commands are not to be believed. But at the time when this letter was being sent, Ezekiel had not yet begun to prophesy in Babylon; for this word is sent at the beginning of King Zedekiah, while Ezekiel began to prophesy in the fifth year of the exile of Jeconiah, which was the same year of Zedekiah's reign. Further, according to tropology, we ought to take as false prophets those who take the words of the Scriptures otherwise than the Holy Spirit sounds them, and as diviners those who pronounce the conjectures of their own mind and the uncertainties of things to come as though true, without the authority of the divine words; and as dreamers, those who do not hear that which is written: give no sleep to your eyes nor slumber to your eyelids, of whom Jude the Apostle too speaks: likewise these also, dreaming, defile the flesh, but despise dominion, whose mind is by no means awake, but, weighed down by the slumber of arrogance and error, is surrounded by the horror of night; to whom the Apostle Paul also speaks: rise, you who sleep, and arise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.10-12":["For thus says the Lord: When seventy years begin to be fulfilled in Babylon, I will visit you, and I will raise up over you my good word, to bring you back to this place. For I know the thoughts which I think over you, says the Lord, thoughts of peace and not of affliction, to give you an end and patience — or hope —. And you will call upon me and you will go — or, according to Symmachus, you will find — and you will pray to me, and I will hear you. You will seek me and you will find, when you seek me with your whole heart, and I will be found by you or I will appear to you. 'Do not,' he says, 'believe your false prophets, diviners and dreamers, who promise you a near return to Jerusalem! For unless the seventy years have been completed — Cyrus, king of the Persians, releasing the captives —, you will by no means return to your fatherland; and then I will fulfill my promises, to bring you back to this place; for I know the thoughts which I think over you, says the Lord'. He says that he knows what he thinks, but that they, with their prophets, diviners and dreamers, do not know; the knowledge of things to come therefore belongs to God alone. 'To give you,' he says, 'an end and patience: an end of the captivity and patience in the present labors, or hope of things to come. Then you will call upon me and you will go to Jerusalem and you will pray, and I will hear you.' Certainly the Lord could have fulfilled what he had promised without the invocation and prayer of the captives, but he exhorts them to prayers, so that they may deserve to receive what has been promised.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.10-14":["According to anagōgēn [the higher, spiritual sense], we dwell in the 'confusion' of this world for as long as it takes until we deserve to receive the 'rest' of the number seven, and, repentance having been received, God fulfills what he promised, and we are brought back to our place, the Church; for this is why the Lord seemed to strike, that he might heal, and he will give us an end of our labor and of our patience. And we shall call upon him and shall return to the Church and shall pray, and we shall be heard. We shall seek and shall find him, when we have sought him with our whole heart, and then he will appear to us. A certain one interprets the 'seventy years' according to that which is written: the days of our years, in them seventy years; and when these have been completed, then we may return to the Lord with our whole heart and be heard, and there may be an end of our labor and patience; for now we possess all things in shadow and image.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.13":["You will seek and you will find me, when you seek with your whole heart, in accordance with that saying of the Gospel: ask, and you will receive; seek, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened to you.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.14-20":["And in this place a delirious interpreter dreams of the ruin of the heavenly Jerusalem and supposes that the prophecy is directed to those who dwell in the Babylonian region of this world, that they did well to descend of their own accord into these bodies and in the land of the Chaldeans to build houses, plant orchards, take wives, beget sons, and by good works to be restored after seventy years to their former place and to the heavenly Jerusalem; but that those who were unwilling by their own will to descend to earthly things would suffer these things which the Lord threatens against Zedekiah and his people, who were unwilling to imitate their brothers or to come into Babylon: that he sends upon them the sword and famine and plague, that is, the want of all things, and makes them like very bad figs, which utterly cannot be eaten, and pursues them with an everlasting sword and gives them over to vexation to all the kingdoms of the earth, so that, namely, they become not men at all but demons, aerial powers, and are, before all the angels who preside over the several provinces, for a curse and for astonishment and for hissing and for a reproach to all the nations. And that they would suffer this for the reason that they were unwilling to hear, in the heavenly Jerusalem, the words of the prophets, who exhorted them to descend to earthly things and take up the body of humiliation, and, having done penance, after the true sabbath-rest to possess their former place. So much he may say; and when his disciples and the dung of the Grunnian household hear it, they think they are hearing divine mysteries, and us, who despise these things, they hold as brute animals and call pēlousiōtas [men of mud], because, set in the mud of this body, we cannot perceive heavenly things.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.14":["※ And I will be found by you, says the Lord, and I will bring back your captivity and gather you from all the nations and from all the places to which I drove you out, says the Lord, and I will make you return from the place to which I made you go into exile, because you have said: The Lord has raised up prophets for us in Babylon. For thus says the Lord to the king who sits upon the throne of David, and to all the people, the inhabitant of this city, and to your brothers who did not go out with you into exile; thus says the Lord of hosts: Behold, I will send upon them the sword, famine and plague, and I will make them like bad figs, which cannot be eaten because they are very bad. And I will pursue them with the sword, with famine and with pestilence, and I will give them over to vexation to all the kingdoms of the earth, for a curse and for astonishment and for hissing and for a reproach to all the nations to which I am casting them out, because they did not hear my words, says the Lord, which I sent to them through my servants, the prophets, rising up by night and sending; and you did not hear, says the Lord. You, therefore, hear the word of the Lord, all the exile, which I sent out from Jerusalem into Babylon! ※ Up to this point, what I have marked beforehand with asterisks is not found in the Seventy; for the rest, in which either single verses or a few words have been passed over by them, I was unwilling, overcome by weariness, to note them, lest I should cause the reader disgust. Now the Lord promises those who were in exile that after seventy years of captivity he will make them return from all the nations and from all the places to which he had driven them out, and that, the captivity being released, they would recover their former state and fatherland.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.15-17":["'And since I,' he says, 'am going to do these things of my own accord and to restore you at a fixed time, you are deceived in vain and think that you have prophets in Babylon who promise you false things; so know, then, that you ought by no means to hope for a return now, but to build houses, plant orchards, take wives and beget sons and multiply in number and await the promised time. Hear what the Lord says to Zedekiah, who now reigns in Jerusalem, and to all the inhabitants of his city, that is, to your brothers, who were unwilling to obey my sentence and to migrate to Babylon with you: that they can by no means escape captivity, but will die by sword and famine and plague, and that he will make them like bad figs', which Theodotion and Aquila's first edition translated 'sudrinas', the second 'very bad', Symmachus 'last'; in Hebrew they are called 'suarim' [vile], but by the fault of copyists, in place of the middle syllable or letter 'alpha', the Greek 'delta' has crept in, so that 'sudrim' is read in place of 'suarim'. And just as the basket or hamper which had good figs is said to have had 'first-ripe' ones, so the other basket, which had bad figs, is written to have had 'last' figs.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.18-20":["'And I will pursue,' he says, 'them, who now dwell in the city of Jerusalem, with sword, famine and pestilence, so that, first by the siege, and then at once, those who have been able to remain and escape may be scattered into all lands and be an example of curse, astonishment and hissing and reproach to all among whom I shall cast them out, because they did not hear my words, says the Lord, which I spoke to them through my servants, rising up by night and sending, and I never ceased admonishing them to imitate you, who now in exile enjoy a secure leisure until the Lord's promise is fulfilled. But you, who obeyed my sentence and handed yourselves over to the Babylonian king, hear what I am about to say'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.21-23":["Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel, to Ahab son of Kolaiah and to Zedekiah son of Maaseiah, who prophesy to you in my name falsely: Behold, I will deliver them into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and he will strike them before your eyes. And from them a curse will be taken up by all the exile of Judah that is in Babylon, saying: May the Lord make you like Zedekiah and like Ahab, whom the king of Babylon fried in the fire, because they did folly — or iniquity — in Israel and committed adultery with the wives of their friends — or fellow citizens — and spoke a word in my name falsely, which I did not command them; I am judge and witness, says the Lord. The Hebrews say that these are the elders who did folly in Israel and committed adultery with the wives of their fellow citizens, to one of whom Daniel says: you who have grown old in evil days and to the other: seed of Canaan and not of Judah, beauty has deceived you and lust has perverted your heart! Thus you did to the daughters of Israel, and they in fear spoke with you; but a daughter of Judah did not endure your iniquity. And what the prophet now says: and they spoke a word in my name falsely, which I did not command them, they think signifies this: that they so deceived wretched silly women, who are carried about by every wind of doctrine, as to tell them that, since they were of the tribe of Judah, Christ was to be begotten of their seed; and these women, enticed by desire, offered their bodies as if they would be the mothers of Christ. But what is said in the present passage: whom the king of Babylon fried in the fire, seems to contradict the history of Daniel. For that asserts that they were stoned by the people at Daniel's sentence; but here it is written that the king of Babylon fried them in the fire. Hence by many, indeed by almost all the Hebrews, that very story is not received, being taken as a fable, nor is it read in their synagogues. 'For how,' he says, 'could it be that captives had the power of stoning their princes and prophets?' And they affirm that this is rather the truth, which Jeremiah writes: that the elders were indeed convicted by Daniel, but that sentence was pronounced against them by the king of Babylon, who as victor and lord had authority over the captives. How many from our own flock too, like Ahab and Zedekiah, prophesy falsehood in the name of the Lord and do folly in Israel and commit adultery with the wives of their fellow citizens, who were begotten in the same city of the Church! These the true Nebuchadnezzar fries in the fire of sin, as the prophet Hosea says: all who commit adultery are like an oven kindled by the one who bakes. Happy he who takes up the yoke from his youth and sits alone, because he is filled with bitterness, and can say with David: I have not sat in the council of the malignant, and with those who do iniquity I will not go in! And what the prophet has now added: I am judge and witness, says the Lord, has this sense: 'these things which I say about the two false prophets, who speak a word in my name falsely which I did not command them, I have by no means come to know by opinion, but I myself know them to be true, I from whom no one can hide nor escape the truth of my judgment'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.24-27":["And to Shemaiah the Nehelamite you shall say; and what follows: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Because you have sent books in your own name to all the people that is in Jerusalem, is not found in the Seventy; and again they added of their own: I did not send you in my name, and consequently, in order: and to Zephaniah son of Maaseiah, the priest, and again from the Hebrew: and to all the priests; and then the story is woven on: The Lord has made you priest in place of Jehoiada the priest, that you may be leader — or instructor and overseer — in the house of the Lord over every man who is possessed and prophesies, that you may put him in the stocks and in prison — or in custody and in the cataract, for which Symmachus put mochlon [a bar], and Aquila put the Hebrew word itself, 'sinac' —. And now why have you not rebuked Jeremiah the Anathothite, who prophesies to you? For on this account he sent to us in Babylon, saying: It is long; build houses and dwell, and plant gardens and eat their fruit! So Zephaniah the priest read this book in the ears of Jeremiah the prophet. Shemaiah, from the place 'Nehellami', which means 'torrent', had been led to Babylon with King Jeconiah and was prophesying falsely to the people that they would quickly return to Jerusalem. That he was a false prophet the following words of Jeremiah show: thus says the Lord to Shemaiah the Nehelamite: because Shemaiah has prophesied to you and I did not send him. Since, therefore, Jeremiah had sent a letter to those who were in Babylon, saying: build houses and dwell; plant gardens and eat their fruit; take wives and beget sons! And after other things had added: let not your prophets who are in your midst, and your diviners, lead you on! And then: for these prophesy falsely to you in my name, and I did not send them, says the Lord, Shemaiah, understanding that it had been written against him under the common name of the false prophets, sends a letter to Jerusalem, to Zephaniah son of Maaseiah, the priest, and to the rest of the priests, against Jeremiah, asking why he was not rebuked by Zephaniah the priest, whose office it is to discern between prophets, which speak by the Holy Spirit and which by the contrary one, and why he was not ordered to be shut up in prison, that he might pay the penalty of his lie and cease to trip up the people any further. Jehoiada was the priest who handed the rule to Joash after the killing of Athaliah and killed the priest of Baal. This, then, is what he writes: 'why do you not imitate Jehoiada the priest and kill Jeremiah the false prophet? For the Lord has appointed you in place of Jehoiada, that you may have care of the temple and especially discern who speak by the Holy Spirit and who by a demonic one' — and the Apostle too records that the discernings of spirits belong to the Lord's grace —; 'why,' he says, 'have you not rebuked Jeremiah the Anathothite?' What he himself deserved as a false prophet he turns back upon the prophet, and he forestalls the truth with a lie. Hence too the sons of darkness are thought more prudent than the sons of light in this generation, and while we act patiently and wait for the salvation of the wretched, the heretics forestall us and call us by their own name, the blind leading the blind into the pit.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.28":["He sent, he says, to us in Babylon, saying: It is far off. This is all that grieves him: that against his lie Jeremiah wrote the truth, that the return was far off and that they would return to Jerusalem after seventy years; and that therefore they ought to build houses, plant orchards and eat their fruit, take wives and beget sons, as the preceding discourse related.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.29":["When Zephaniah the priest had received this letter, which was written specifically in his name, he read it to Jeremiah, in a way insulting him and rebuking him by the very reading, as to why he dared to write such things to Babylon.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.31":["And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, saying: Send to all the exile, saying: Thus says the Lord to Shemaiah the Nehelamite: Because Shemaiah has prophesied to you and I did not send him, and he has made you trust in a lie, therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I will visit upon Shemaiah the Nehelamite and upon his seed; there shall not be a man sitting in the midst of this people, and he shall not see the good which I will do to my people, says the Lord, because he has spoken transgression against the Lord. The false prophet Shemaiah, truly a 'Nehellamites', who had taken up foreign and troubled waters from the 'torrent', is angry that Jeremiah has written true things against his lie, and he sends letters to Zephaniah the priest, asking why the prophet dared to write the truth, and he desires that he be shut up in prison so that he may not speak. Zephaniah too secretly accuses the prophet, while he reads the letter of the liar and boasts that he holds his accusation. How much more guilty of crime are those who defend false prophets and cherish liars and make the evil inventions of others their own sins!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.29.32":["Let the false prophet therefore hear, let the priest understand what he himself also deserves to hear through him: thus says the Lord: Behold, I will visit upon Shemaiah. This the Lord speaks, not the prophet: that he will visit upon Shemaiah the Nehelamite, not for remedy but for the punishment of the liar, according to that which is written: I will visit their iniquities with the rod, and their sins with scourges. Nor does he visit upon the false prophet alone, but also upon his seed, all the disciples whom he deceived by his error. There shall not be, he says, a man sitting in the midst of this people. Let the worst stock be blotted out of the assembly of the saints, and let him not sit in the midst of those at rest, who could not stand with those who stand, nor heard that saying: if he had stood in my substance. And whereas the Lord promises the perfection of all virtues when the sevenfold time is ended, he will not see the good which he claimed for himself in the present time. Now all this will come about because he has spoken transgression against the Lord, in saying that, the captivity of sins being already loosed, they would return to Jerusalem; such men the Apostle threatens: already you are filled, already you have become rich, without us you have reigned; and would that you did reign, that we also might reign with you!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.2-3":["This word, which came to Jeremiah from the Lord, saying: Thus says the Lord God of Israel, saying: Write for yourself in a book all the words which I have spoken to you! For behold, days will come, says the Lord, and I will turn back the turning of my people Israel and Judah, says the Lord, and I will turn them back — or I will make them settle — into the land which I gave to their fathers, and they shall possess it. While the false prophets in Babylon were promising that the people who had been taken captive with Jeconiah would soon return, and while Hananiah son of Azzur was preaching the same things in Jerusalem, Jeremiah the prophet both asserted that this would indeed come to pass, not within two years, as they lied, but when the seventy years were finished, and now he is ordered to write in a book and to hand down to remembrance the things which the Lord foretells are to come. From this it is plain that the time of the prophecy is by no means near, but that these things are to be fulfilled after a long time, when Israel and Judah are to return to their land, and that what Ezekiel prophesied is to be fulfilled: that the two rods are to be joined together with each other, and that David the king will rule, of whom he writes: and my servant David shall be king over them, and there shall be one shepherd of them all. Whatever, therefore, we said on that prophecy must be understood in the present passage as well, especially since Ezekiel in Babylon and Jeremiah in Jerusalem prophesied the same things, and at the same time.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.3":["The length of the book of Jeremiah the prophet overcomes our purpose, so that, however briefly, we nonetheless say much. Hence the present sixth book of the commentaries on Jeremiah will also contain the mystical promises which the Jews, and our Judaizers, think are to be fulfilled at the consummation of the world; for they cannot prove that they were already fulfilled under Zerubbabel. We, however, following the authority of the apostles and evangelists, and above all of the Apostle Paul, show that whatever is promised carnally to the people of Israel has been fulfilled spiritually in us and is being fulfilled today, and that there is no other contest between Jews and Christians but this: that, while they and we both believe that Christ the Son of God has been promised, the things that are to be under Christ are said by us to have been fulfilled, by them to be yet fulfilled. We, therefore, who believe that Christ has already come, must teach that the things which are said to be going to take place under Christ have been fulfilled, and that we are the sons of Abraham, of whom it is written: God is able from these stones to raise up sons to Abraham, to whom the promise was made: and in your seed all the nations shall be blessed. Teaching that this blessing was fulfilled in Christ, the vessel of election says: he did not say, he says, in seeds, but in seed, which is Christ. Pray, therefore, brother Eusebius, to the Lord Jesus Christ, that with the same labor and grace of the Spirit with which we have interpreted the promises of the other prophets, and especially of Isaiah, we may be able to explain those of this prophet also, who, as much as he seems simple and easy in his words, is in the same measure most profound in the majesty of his meanings.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.5-6":["And these are the words which the Lord spoke to Israel and to Judah, for thus says the Lord: We have heard a voice of terror — or of fear —; dread, and there is no peace. Ask and see whether a male gives birth! Why then have I seen the hand of every man upon his loins, as of a woman giving birth, and all faces have been turned into jaundice? First sad things are announced, so that after the greatness of the evils glad things may follow; for health is more welcome when sickness has been driven off, and the greatness of pain is turned into a greatness of joys. What he says, then, is this: so great will be the fear and so great the dread that, peace having been put to flight, all things will be filled with wars and blood, and that a womanish terror will take hold even of the men, whose proper task is to fight against adversaries, and they will put their hands by no means to arms but to holding their kidneys, as if a woman giving birth were clutching her flanks and loins. Hence also the faces of all will be turned into jaundice, testifying by the pallor of the countenance to the terror of the heart. Some interpret this passage according to tropology in such a way that they set down that testimony: From your fear, O Lord, we have conceived and travailed and brought forth; we have made the spirit of your salvation upon the earth, and they compare to this example that saying of the Apostle in which he says: My little children, with whom I am again in travail until Christ be formed in you; which plainly pertains not to terror but to joy, whereas the present Scripture signifies the time of the devastation and ruin of Israel.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.7":["Woe, for great is that day, nor is there any like it, and it is the time of the tribulation of Jacob! and from it he shall be saved. He foretells the time of misery in order to bring in the time of joy. 'Although,' he says, 'such great evils have gone before that the pain of all the men is compared to the pain of a woman giving birth, yet the time of the tribulation of Jacob, that is, of the people of God, will be changed into prosperity, and from it too he shall be saved' — 'time' is understood —, 'of which the discourse had spoken'. But by 'Jacob' understand the twelve tribes, which were saved by no means under Zerubbabel, as some falsely suppose, but by the calling of the Gospel.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.8-9":["And it shall be in that day, says the Lord of hosts, I will break his yoke from off your neck and I will burst his bonds, and strangers shall no longer rule over him, but they shall serve — or work for — the Lord their God and David their king, whom I will raise up for them. This is the David whom the Gospel also mentions: that he would grant us that, without fear, delivered from the hand of our enemies, we might serve him in holiness and righteousness before him all our days. For just as a first Adam and a second Adam are written of according to the truth of the body, so also the Lord and Savior is David, because according to the flesh he is from David, holy Mary contributing to him wholly whatever was of the stock of David, while she had the origin and conception from the Holy Spirit. And as for his saying: I will break his yoke from off your neck and I will burst his bonds, there is no doubt that under the type of Nebuchadnezzar it must be understood of the devil.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.10":["You therefore, do not fear, my servant Jacob, says the Lord, nor be afraid, Israel, for behold, I will save you from a far-off land, and your seed from the land of their captivity, and Jacob shall return and be at rest and abound in all things, and there shall be none whom he fears, since I am with you, says the Lord, to save you. For I will make a full end among all the nations among which I have scattered you; but you I will not bring to a full end, but I will chastise — or instruct — you in judgment, so that you may not seem to yourself guiltless or and cleansing, I will not cleanse you. This perikopē [passage] is not found in the Seventy, and in very many manuscripts of the common edition it has been added under asterisks from Theodotion. The divine word makes a promise and, more familiarly, calls Jacob and Israel 'his servant', just as Abraham, Isaac and Jacob are called 'servants of God' — Moses too and the other prophets, and the Apostle Paul at the beginning of his epistles glories in this title —, so that both the two and the ten tribes, that is, the twelve, may know that they are to be saved from a far-off land, that the captivity is to be loosed and peace restored, and that they are to be filled with all abundance, according to what is said in the psalm: Let there be peace in your strength and abundance in your towers!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.11":["And this will come about because they will enjoy the presence of the Lord, when the hostile nations which had captured them will perish and these will be set free from among the nations. And he teaches that they were handed over by no means to punishment but to instruction, so that they might be judged as his own, not destroyed as strangers; for he who does not believe has already been judged, that is, judged beforehand unto destruction. And what he added: so that you may not seem to yourself guiltless, or according to Symmachus: and cleansing, I will not cleanse you, or according to Aquila: when I have instructed you through judgment, I will by no means make you innocent, signifies that the whole world stands in need of the mercy of God, and that no one, however holy he may be, goes to the judge secure; against the new heresy sprung from the old, which supposes that in this age and in this mortal flesh, before this corruptible puts on incorruption and this mortal puts on immortality, there is perfection in anyone, and that the righteous man can fulfill all the virtues at once.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.12-15":["For thus says the Lord: Incurable is your fracture, most grievous your wound. There is none who judges your judgment for binding up; there is no benefit of healings for you. All your lovers have forgotten you, they will not seek you; for I have struck you with the blow of an enemy, with cruel chastisement — or strong —. Because of the multitude of your iniquity have become hard — or have been multiplied — your sins. Why do you cry out over your crushing? Incurable is your pain; because of the multitude of your iniquity and because of your hard sins I have done these things to you. He speaks as to a beautiful woman, to whom he had said above: I will chastise you in judgment, so that you may not seem to yourself guiltless or innoxia [guiltless, in the feminine]; and by metaphoran [metaphor] he speaks to Jerusalem, that she has been most grievously wounded by the judgment of God and can be healed by no other curing her except him who had struck her — there is none, he says, who judges your judgment — nor draw the skin of a scar over the deepest wound. 'Wherever you turn, there is no benefit for you, because you have offended him who is the true and only physician. All your lovers have forgotten you, whether the priests or the princes, or at any rate the protection of the angels by which, before you offended the Lord, you were walled about. They will not seek you, acting contrary to the Apostle, who sought the believers and not the things which were the believers'; for I have struck you with the blow of an enemy, with cruel chastisement.' A friend strikes in one way, an enemy in another; a father in one way, a foe in another. The one beats in order to correct, the other strikes in order to kill; hence the prophet too says tearfully: O Lord, do not rebuke me in your fury, nor chasten me in your wrath! But this has happened because because of the multitude of your iniquity your sins have become hard. And what follows: Why do you cry out over your crushing? Incurable is your pain because of the multitude of your iniquity, is not found in the Seventy, evidently because it is said a second time: because of the multitude of your iniquity and your hard sins, and those who copied from the beginning thought it had been added. And the sense is: 'That I struck you with the blow of an enemy, that I beat you with cruel chastisement, the multitude of your iniquity and your hard sins brought about, which could not be healed except by the most biting powder and the burning cautery and the sharpest steel, with which I might cut away the putrid and incurable flesh. And yet because of the multitude of your iniquity and your hard sins I have done this to you, not by my own will, but because the method of medicine compelled me'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.16-17":["Therefore all who devour you shall be devoured, and all your enemies shall be led into captivity, and those who lay you waste shall be laid waste, and all your plunderers I will give up as plunder. For I will draw a scar over you and from your wounds I will heal you, says the Lord, because they have called you cast out — or scattered —, Zion: this is she who had no one seeking her. And we know that these things were done under Zerubbabel too. When the Babylonians and Chaldeans laid waste the Assyrians, that is, Nineveh, and the Medes and Persians in turn captured the Babylonians and Chaldeans and Babylon was destroyed, then Zion began to have the Lord seeking her, and a scar was drawn over her wound and she was healed of her blows; which is fulfilled more fully and more perfectly in Christ.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.18":["Thus says the Lord: Behold, I will turn back the turning of the tents of Jacob, and on the dwellings — or captivity — of them I will have mercy, and the city shall be built on its height, and the temple shall be founded according to its order. And from them shall go forth praise and the voice of those playing, and I will multiply them and they shall not be diminished; and what follows: and I will glorify them and I will not make them few, is not found in the Seventy. And his sons, he says, shall be as from the beginning, and his assembly shall abide before me, and I will visit against all who afflict him. And his leader shall be from himself, and a prince shall be brought forth from his midst, and I will draw him close and he shall approach me; for who is this who draws his heart close so as to come near to me?, says the Lord; and again, what follows in the Hebrew is not found in the Greek: and you shall be, he says, my people, and I will be your God. A type of these things went before in Zerubbabel and Ezra, when the people returned and the city began to be built on its height and the worship of the temple to be observed, and the other things which are contained in the book of Ezra himself; but it was fulfilled more fully and more perfectly in the Lord and Savior and the apostles, when there was built the city on its height — of which it is written: a city that is set on a mountain cannot be hidden — and the temple according to its order and ceremonies was founded, so that whatever was done carnally among the former people might be fulfilled spiritually in the Church.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.19":["Then there went forth praise or thanksgiving — for this is what 'thoda' signifies —, so that all the apostles said: grace to you and peace, and the voice of those playing, not with that play with which the people ate and drank and rose up to play, but with that with which David played before the ark of the Lord. And they were multiplied and not diminished, so that the whole world believed in God the Savior, and they were glorified, so that what is written was fulfilled: Glorious things have been spoken of you, city of God!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.20":["And his sons, that is, the apostles, were as Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, the princes of the race of Israel, were from the beginning. Then the Lord visited against all who afflicted the people of God, namely the adverse powers.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.21":["And there was his leader from himself — no doubt the Lord and Savior, according to the flesh from the race of Israel — and a prince from his midst was brought forth. The Father drew him close to himself, and he approached him, so that the Son might say: I in the Father and the Father in me, because no one can so draw his heart close to the Lord, nor be joined to him as the Son is to the Father.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.22":["And what he says according to the Hebrew only, but not according to the Seventy: and you shall be my people, and I will be your God, we see fulfilled in deed, in part in Israel and wholly in the multitude of the nations.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.30.23-24":["Behold, the whirlwind of the Lord, fury going forth, a storm rushing down, shall come to rest upon the head of the wicked. The Lord will not turn back the anger of his indignation until he does and fulfills the purpose of his heart; in the last of days you will understand these things. The whirlwind of the Lord's fury and the rushing storm and tempest will rest upon the head of the wicked, whether of the demons or of those who blasphemed the Son of God; and he will not turn back the anger of his indignation until he does and fulfills the purpose of his heart, and until Jerusalem is surrounded by an army and utterly destroyed. Now just as a craftsman cannot be understood except when the work is completed, nor the diligence of medicine except after health has been attained, so, when the overthrow of Jerusalem and the casting off of the former people have come about, then believers will understand that the rejection of the Jews is the occasion of our salvation.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.1":["At that time, says the Lord, I will be God to all the families of Israel — or to the race of Israel — and they shall be to me for a people. Unless the thought of the Lord has been fulfilled and his fury has come to rest upon the head of the ungodly, the Lord of the universe cannot be God to the families of Israel; but this he says to the remnant, which has been saved. But if what is said is set against us: I will be God to the race of Israel or to all the families of Israel, let us take up an example: 'if you were children of Abraham, you would do the works of your father'; and the Apostle writes: behold Israel according to the flesh. From this he teaches that there is another Israel according to the spirit. That one, then, is Israel who discerns God with the mind, or is the most upright one of the Lord, and an Israel of this kind will be the people of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.2":["With this sense the words of Jeremiah also agree, spoken in the person of the Lord: As something warm in the desert, I found Israel with those slain by the sword (Jer 38:2 LXX; Vulg. 31:2). For when, he says, the whole world lay lifeless, wounded by the swords of idolatry, I found Israel in Abraham to have the warmth of faith, like a physician who after a battle, among the corpses of the dead, where he perceives that someone still has something of life in his beating veins, applies care to his wounds to restore him to health. For what is written ὡς θερμὸν, that is, as something warm, the Latin translator, deceived by the ambiguity of the word, rendered lupine, and in this most of the Greeks also err. And yet it should be known that in the Hebrew, for warmth, THODA (תודה) is written, which means grace: namely, that Israel was saved by the grace of God, and not by the merit of its own works.\n— Commentary on Isaiah","Thus says the Lord: the people that had remained from the sword found grace in the desert; Israel shall go to its rest. LXX: thus said the Lord: I found warm in the desert with those who had perished by the sword; go, and do not kill Israel! Ridiculously, in this place the Latin codices, through the ambiguity of the Greek word, translated 'lupines' for 'warm'; for the Greek THERMON signifies both, and it is not itself found in the Hebrew. For there is written 'hen', which Aquila, Symmachus and Theodotion translated as charin, that is, 'grace'; the Seventy alone put 'warm', thinking that the last letter was 'm'. For if we read 'hen' with the letter 'n', it means 'grace'; if with 'm', it is interpreted 'heat'. Now the sense according to the Hebrew is this: the people of the Jews, which had remained from the Roman sword, or at any rate had been able to escape the wrath of the Lord's fury, found grace in the desert of the nations, so that amid the crowd of the nations it might be saved in the Church; whence Israel shall go and shall find its rest, which it had always hoped for, which the prophecies of the prophets had promised it. Further, according to the Seventy this is the understanding: the Lord found the apostles and their companions warm and living in the desert of the nations, among those who had been killed by their own unbelief and did not have the warmth of life. Hence it is commanded to the angels and to those who are in the ministry of God that they should not kill all, that Israel should not be utterly destroyed, and it is said to them: 'go, and do not kill Israel; let there be some who live, let there be those who are warm with the ardor of faith, let there be those who escape the cold of unbelief and of death, whom the Lord may find in the desert!'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.3":["From afar the Lord appeared to me — or to him — : and with an everlasting love I have loved you; therefore I have drawn you, having pity. And again I will build you and you shall be built, virgin Israel. Again you shall be adorned with your timbrels — or you shall take up your timbrels — and you shall go forth in the dance of those who make merry. Again you shall plant vineyards on the mountains of Samaria; plant plantings and gather the vintage! for there shall be a day on which the watchmen on Mount Ephraim shall cry: arise, and let us go up into Zion to the Lord our God! Because Israel had offended the Lord and had said: we have no king but Caesar, and: come, let us kill him, and the inheritance will be ours, and had withdrawn far from God, therefore the Lord after a long time appeared to him — not in the time of Zerubbabel and Ezra, after they were taken captive again —, but with an everlasting love, which will be blotted out by no end, he loved him, and drew him, having pity; for he was saved not at all by merit, but by clemency.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.4-5":["And again, he says, I will build you and you shall be built, virgin Israel. Let us understand this properly of the Church; for they rave who sigh for a golden and jeweled Jerusalem, consecrating their own avarice in the mystery of the Lord's city. 'Again you shall be adorned with your timbrels, so that you may sing to the Lord in the churches, all the flesh of evil works in you having been consumed, and you shall go forth in the dance of those who make merry with the crowds of the nations, you shall plant vineyards on the mountains of Samaria', not at all in the valleys and low places, but on the mountains of Samaria, which after the captivity of the people of Israel were possessed by foreigners, to whom it is said: plant plantings and gather the vintage!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.6":["Then was the day of the Lord, on which the watchmen — namely the apostles and apostolic men — cried on mount 'Samaria' and on mount 'Ephraim', of which the one sounds 'guard', the other 'fruitfulness'; but what do the watchmen of Samaria say, nay rather, what do they cry on Mount Ephraim? 'Arise, you who lie down, leave lowly things, spurn the offerings of victims' — a sacrifice to the Lord is a troubled spirit —, 'let us go up into Zion, that is, into the Church, where there is 'contemplation' and the beholding of God; and when we are in Zion, nay rather have gone up to it, we shall go up likewise to the Lord our God'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.7":["For thus says the Lord: exult with gladness, Jacob, and neigh against the head of the nations; sound aloud, sing and say: save, O Lord, your people, the remnant of Israel! Significantly, not the whole of Israel is saved, but the remnant of Israel, the Lord commanding and saying: 'exult with gladness, you who are of Jacob, and neigh toward the head of the nations, referring all things to it, because the tail was once turned into the head; sound aloud and sing and say!' What is it that they are bidden to say? Save, O Lord, your people. Which people? Surely the remnant of Israel, which has been saved according to election, of whom Paul too speaks, taking up the testimony of Isaiah: unless the Lord of Sabaoth had left us seed, we should have become as Sodom and we should have been made like Gomorrah.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.8":["Behold, I will bring them from the land of the north and will gather them from the ends of the earth; and what follows: at the solemnity of the Passover; and it shall beget many sons, is not found in the Hebrew, but is read only in the Seventy, in place of which among the Hebrews is written: among whom shall be the blind and the lame and the woman with child and the woman giving birth together, a great church of those returning hither. The remnant of the people of Israel is gathered through the apostles and apostolic men, of whom we read above: the watchmen shall cry on the mount, and to whom it is commanded: sound aloud and sing and say, so that the remnant of Israel may be saved. And the Lord himself promises that he will bring them back from the land of the north, which is a most harsh wind, but by name is called right, from unbelief, from the cold of the Lord's love, and will gather them from the ends of the earth, not at another time, but at the solemnity of the Passover, that is, on the festal days of the Lord's passion, when the Lord was crucified and that was fulfilled which he himself promised in the Gospel: when I am lifted up, I will draw all things to myself. Then it begot a great people, so that that saying of Isaiah might be fulfilled: for a nation is born at once; for in one day three thousand and five thousand men believed. And what is written in the Hebrew: among whom shall be the blind and the lame and the woman with child and the woman giving birth together, a great church of those returning hither, although it has also been fulfilled according to the letter, in that the blind saw and the lame walked, yet it can better be understood according to anagōgēn [the higher, spiritual sense]: that those who before were blind with faithlessness afterwards believed in the Savior, and those who were lame, to whom Elijah once spoke: how long do you limp on both feet?, afterwards walked, and the people who sat in darkness and the shadow of death saw a great light, and the lame ran, and the woman with child bore sons, a great church of those returning to the faith. The Jews think that this was fulfilled when under Ezra, after the day of the Passover, they went out from Babylon to return to Jerusalem, in which there was a type and not the truth; for they will not be able to prove that at that time all the things which we have read and are going to read were fulfilled.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.9":["With weeping they shall come — or they shall go out — and in mercy I will lead them back, and I will bring them through torrents of waters in a straight way, and they shall not stumble — or they shall not go astray — in it, for I have become a father to Israel, and Ephraim is my firstborn. If we read according to the Hebrew: with weeping they shall come — for this is what 'iabu' indicates —, we shall say that sometimes weeping is also a sign of excessive joy, according to that saying: I weep for joy. But if according to the Seventy, who said: with weeping they shall go out and in mercy — or in consolation — I will lead them back, we shall set down that sense which is also spoken in the Psalms: going they went and wept, carrying their seeds; but coming they shall come with exultation, carrying their sheaves. For they wept when they were led away captive, and they received the greatest consolation when they were led back by the mercy of the Lord. And the Lord brought them, through the apostles and apostolic men, full of waters and of a most abundant river, in a straight way, namely that of faith, not in the faithlessness of the Jews. And they shall not, he says, stumble in it, because they have ceased to be blind, to whom it was once said: if you were blind, you would not have sin; but now you say, We see; your sin remains. We can also understand the 'straight way' as Christ, in whom whoever walks will not stumble. I have become, he says, a father to Israel, who has been led back, and Ephraim is my firstborn; for where once sin abounded, grace abounded the more. And Scripture testifies that Ephraim is in the type of the people gathered from the nations. For he was the younger son of Joseph and stole away the birthright of Manasseh, who by nature was the firstborn; but in the mystery of the cross, the hands being crossed, he who stood at Jacob's left received the blessing of his right hand, and he who had stood at the right, blessed with the left, was reduced to the second rank. And as Jacob from Esau, so Ephraim snatched the birthright from Manasseh, and the whole people of the ten tribes was called Ephraim, because Jeroboam son of Nebat, from this tribe, was the first to obtain the kingdom in Samaria.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.10-11":["Hear the word of the Lord, O nations, and declare it to the islands that are far off, and say: he who scattered Israel will gather him and will keep him as a shepherd his flock, for the Lord has redeemed Jacob and has delivered him from the hand of one more powerful — or stronger —. And they shall come and shall praise on Mount Zion and shall flow together to the good things of the Lord, over grain and wine and oil and the young of flocks and of herds. Their soul shall be like a watered garden — or like a fruit-bearing tree — and they shall hunger no more. Then the virgin shall rejoice in the dance — or virgins — and young men and old together, and I will turn their mourning into joy and will console them and make them glad from their sorrow, and I will inebriate the soul of the priests with fatness — or of the sons of Levi — and my people shall be filled with my good things, says the Lord. The calling of the nations is clearly shown, Scripture saying: hear the word of the Lord, O nations, and declare it to the islands that are far off, and say! What do they declare to the islands that are situated far off? That the Lord, who scattered Israel, himself will gather him — it was not at all, then, of the power of the adversaries that they were scattered, but of the Lord's will — and will keep him as a shepherd his flock — for the good shepherd lays down his life for his sheep —, for the Lord has redeemed Jacob at the price of his own blood and has delivered him from the hand of one more powerful or stronger. By this it is shown that the adversary powers are stronger than the nature of human frailty, and, as to the strength that is in each, that they are stronger by nature, but we stronger by faith, if only we deserve to be delivered by him who can bind the strong man and plunder his house.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.12":["And they shall come, he says, — no doubt delivered from the hand of the powerful — and shall praise their deliverer on Mount Zion, that is, in the Church, and shall flow together to the good things of the Lord, the abundance of all things, which is perceived not in the fruits and foods of this flesh, but in the variety of the virtues. Over grain, he says, and wine and oil — from which the bread of the Lord is made, and the type of his blood is fulfilled, and the blessing of sanctification is shown, Scripture saying: God, your God, has anointed you with the oil of gladness above your fellows — and the young of flocks, who are the simple in the Church, and of herds, which are horned and toss the adversaries. But that we may know that these blessings do not pertain at all to the body but to the soul, there follows: and their soul shall be like a watered garden or like a fruit-bearing tree, which is planted beside the streams of waters, and the paradise of the Lord in delights. And no more, he says, shall they hunger; not at all with that hunger of which it is written: blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, but with that which is changed by satiety and shuts out the want of all things.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.13-14":["Then the virgin shall rejoice in the dance — of whom the Apostle writes: for I have betrothed you to one husband, to present a chaste virgin to Christ — and the young men — to whom John speaks: I write to you, young men, because you have overcome the evil one — and the old, to whom the same testifies in mystical speech: I write to you, fathers, because you have known him who is from the beginning. And I will turn, he says, their mourning into joy — so that the resurrection may gladden those whom the cross had terrified — and I will console them and make them glad from their sorrow — according to what the Lord says: blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be consoled — and I will inebriate the soul of the priests, who have the knowledge of God, from whose mouth they ask the law of the Lord, who believe in him to whom the prophet sings: you are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek. But what follows according to the Seventy: of the sons of Levi, is not found in the Hebrew, and it is clear that here it is not at all said of those priests who are sons of Levi, but of those in whose type Melchizedek went before. And the 'inebriation' of the priests is proved in the apostles too, when they were fervent in faith and were said to be full of new wine; whence also the place in which the Lord was seized is called 'Gessemani', which in our language sounds 'valley of fatness'. And when the priests have been made fat with the teaching of the Lord and, inebriated at the banquet of Joseph, have said: your cup that inebriates, how excellent it is!, then this too will come about which the Lord now promises: and my people shall be filled with my good things. All these things are now granted in part; but then they will be given in full, when we see face to face and the body of our lowliness has been changed by the glory of the resurrection.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.15":["Then was fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet Jeremiah, saying: A voice was heard in Ramah, weeping and great wailing, Rachel weeping for her sons, and she would not be consoled, because they are not. Of Rachel was born Benjamin, in whose tribe Bethlehem does not lie. It is asked, therefore, how Rachel weeps for the sons of Judah, that is, of Bethlehem, as if they were her own. We shall answer briefly: because she was buried near Bethlehem in Ephrathah (from the inner lodging of her little body she received the name of mother); or else because Judah and Benjamin were two joined tribes, and Herod had commanded that the boys be killed not only in Bethlehem but in all its borders round about; by occasion of Bethlehem we understand that many of Benjamin also were slain. And she weeps for her sons and does not receive consolation according to a twofold understanding: either because she thought them dead for ever, or because she would not be consoled for those whom she knew would live. And as for the saying: in Ramah, let us not think it is the name of the place which is near Gibeah; but Ramah is interpreted height, so that the sense is: A voice was heard on high, that is, spread far and wide.\n— Commentary on Matthew","Thus says the Lord: a voice of lamentation was heard on high, of weeping and mourning, of Rachel bewailing her sons, and she would not be consoled over her sons, because they are not. LXX: thus said the Lord: a voice was heard in Ramah, lamentation and weeping and mourning, of Rachel bewailing her sons, and she would not be quieted, because they are not. Matthew took the testimony neither according to the Hebrew nor according to the Seventy; for we read in him, after the description of the slaughter of the infants: then was fulfilled what was spoken through Jeremiah the prophet, saying: a voice was heard in Ramah, wailing and much howling, of Rachel weeping for her sons, and she would not be consoled, because they are not. From this it is clear that the evangelists and apostles did not at all follow anyone's translation from the Hebrew, but, as Hebrews from Hebrews, expressed in their own words what they read in Hebrew. Rachel, the mother of Joseph, when she was coming to Bethlehem, was suddenly seized with the pain of childbirth and bore a son, whom the midwife, because she was dying in giving birth, called Benoni, that is, son of my sorrow; but his father Jacob changed the name and called him Benjamin, that is, son of the right hand. It is asked, then, how Matthew the evangelist transferred the testimony of the prophet to the killing of the little ones, when it is clearly written of the ten tribes, whose chief was of Ephrathah, and it is not at all in the tribe of Ephraim, but in the tribe of Judah. For this same place is also Bethlehem, diōnymos [double-named], whence also the names of both agree; 'Bethleem' is called 'house of bread', 'Ephratha' karpophoria, which we can call 'fruitfulness'. Since, then, Rachel was buried in Ephrathah, that is, in Bethlehem — as both holy Scripture and the inscription of her tomb testify to this day —, she is said to weep for the children who were killed near her and in her own regions. Some of the Jews interpret this place thus: that, when Jerusalem was taken under Vespasian, countless thousands of captives were sent to Rome by this road, by Gaza and Alexandria; others, that in the last captivity under Hadrian, when the city of Jerusalem too was overthrown, a numberless people of various ages and of both sexes was sold at the market of the Terebinth; and that for this reason it is abhorrent to the Jews to visit that most frequented market. Let them say what they will; we shall say that the evangelist Matthew rightly took the testimony for the place in which Rachel was buried and wept for the sons of the neighboring villages round about as if they were her own.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.16-17":["Thus says the Lord: let your voice cease from wailing and your eyes from tears, for there is a reward for your work, says the Lord, and they shall return from the land of the enemy — or of the enemies — and there shall be hope for your last end, says the Lord, and the sons shall return to their borders. This has not yet happened according to the letter — for we do not read that the ten tribes, which are in exile in the cities of the Medes and of the Persians, have returned to the land of Judea —, but according to the spirit it was fulfilled in the passion of the Lord and is being fulfilled up to now, when Israel is saved from the whole world and it is said to Rachel: let your voice cease from wailing and your eyes from tears!, and the sense is: 'cease to wail — for the Lord has looked upon your former works — and your sons shall return from the land of the enemy, and do not be held by present sorrow! for there is hope in your last end, says the Lord, and your sons shall return to their borders, which their fathers had, Abraham, Isaac and Jacob'. But we understand it better of the little ones: that they have the reward of their blood shed for Christ, and in place of the land of Herod the enemy they hold the kingdoms of heaven, and they will return to their former seat when, in place of the body of lowliness, they receive a glorious body. This is the last hope, when the righteous shall shine like the sun, and the infants, once little ones and sucklings, rise again without the increase of years and without the injuries and toil of the body. Into the perfect man, into the measure of the fullness of Christ.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.18":["Hearing I heard Ephraim going into exile — or lamenting — : you have chastised me and I was instructed like an untamed bullock — or like a calf, and I did not learn — ; convert me, and I shall be converted, for you are the Lord my God. For after you converted me — or I was taken captive —, I repented, and after I knew — or you showed me —, I struck my thigh — or I groaned — ; I was confounded and blushed — or from the day of confusion and I showed you —, because I bore the reproach of my youth. God says what he heard Ephraim saying and lamenting. Now no one doubts that it signifies the ten tribes, over which Jeroboam son of Nebat first ruled, who also made the golden calves in Dan and Bethel, so that the people, seduced by this error, might cease to worship and adore the God of Israel. You have chastised me, he says, and I was chastised. Every correction, which for the present seems to be of sadness, profits for salvation and afterwards brings peaceable fruits. And what he says: like an untamed bullock or a calf, and I did not learn, signifies this: that he was instructed with much toil and with blows, so that he might be turned to repentance, and he did not profit. 'Convert', he says, 'me, and I shall be converted' — therefore even this very thing, that we repent, we cannot accomplish unless we rely on the help of God — ; 'for after you have converted me and I have been converted to you, then I shall know that you are the Lord my God, and not at all my errors and sins. And after you converted me, I repented'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.19":["See how great is the help of God and how frail the human condition, that this very thing, that we repent, we are not at all able to accomplish unless the Lord has first converted us. 'And after', he says, 'you showed me either repentance itself or the knowledge of you, or I knew you, I struck my thigh.' This is the sign of one grieving and lamenting and bewailing his former error: that he strikes his thigh with his hand and confesses that he was foolish before. I was confounded, he says, and blushed or from the day of confusion. For what time is not one of our confusion, if we recall our ancient sins and call to memory all the things we have done ill? And what the Seventy said: and I showed you, signifies that, after he groaned and knew his own offenses, he then came to such progress that he even showed God to others who did not know him, according to what David too says in repentance: I will teach the wicked your ways, and the ungodly shall be converted to you. Because I bore, he says, the reproach of my youth. He says that he sinned through the ignorance of his age, so that he may more easily obtain pardon, according to what David too sings: the offenses of my youth and my ignorances do not remember! Whence also in what follows the Lord calls him 'a little one' and 'abounding in delights'; and this he says on account of the greatness of its riches and the fertility of the land, in which the tribe of Ephraim luxuriates to this day.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.20":["Is Ephraim an honorable son — or beloved — to me, is he a delicate child? for, since I spoke of him — or my words in him —, I will still remember him; therefore my bowels are troubled over him; pitying I will have pity on him, says the Lord. Since Ephraim repents and says at the beginning: you have instructed me and I was instructed like an untamed bullock, and at the end: because I bore the reproach of my youth, the Lord answers, and sustains him, turned to him with his whole mind, with such an oracle: 'Ephraim is a beloved son to me, whom I loved so much from the beginning that I preferred him to his brother Manasseh.' An honorable son, who against the order of nature received the honor of the firstborn by the condescension of the Lord; a delicate child, of whom he writes: the sons of Ephraim, bending and shooting the bow, turned back in the day of battle, against whom and to whom the whole book of the prophet Hosea is addressed, whom Jacob blesses. And let us take 'delights' in this place according to what is said in the psalm: delight in the Lord, and he will give you the petitions of your heart, for which in the Greek and in the Hebrew it has: abound in delights; whence also the paradise in Eden is called the paradise 'of delights'. 'For', he says, 'there were my words in him, with remembrance I will still be mindful of him.' Lest the blessing be thought gratuitous, and to have been conferred out of the indulgence of the giver rather than by the merit of him on whom it was bestowed, therefore he says: 'I will remember him, for my words were in him, not in his mouth, not on his lips, but in the innermost affection of the heart; for which cause also my bowels are troubled over him'. To him he speaks through Hosea too: what shall I do to you, Ephraim? what shall I do to you, Israel? shall I make you as Admah and as Zeboiim? my heart is turned within me, my bowels are troubled. I will not execute the fury of my wrath and I will not destroy Ephraim. Pitying I will have pity on him, says the Lord. 'My words indeed were in him, and he received all my commandments with an eager mind and kept them in his heart; but nevertheless pitying I will have pity on him', to show that all the righteousness of men needs the mercy of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.21-22":["Set up for yourself watchtowers, put for yourself bitternesses, direct your heart into the straight way in which you walked; return, virgin Israel, return to these your cities! How long will you be dissolved in delights, wandering daughter? for the Lord has created a new thing upon the earth: a woman shall encompass a man. LXX: set up for yourself lookouts, make punishment, give your heart to your shoulders; the way in which you walked, return, virgin Israel, return to your cities mourning! How long will you turn away, despised daughter? for the Lord has created you as salvation for a new planting; in your salvation men shall go about. Where we said: how long will you be dissolved in delights?, Symmachus put: how long will you sink into the deep? Now I have set out each edition in full, in order to show that a most obscure passage, one containing the mysteries of the Church, was either unknown to or omitted by the Seventy — or whoever else translated this prophet. The Hebrew word 'sionim' is rendered either as 'lookouts' or as 'watchtowers', as Aquila and Symmachus translated. Hence I wonder what the common edition meant by putting 'Sion' in place of 'sionim', that is, 'lookouts', and so confusing the reader's understanding that he would think that after Ephraim the word of God had suddenly been addressed to Zion and to the tribe of Judah, although the discourse is continuously to Ephraim, to whom he also spoke above: hearing I heard Ephraim, and: Ephraim is an honorable son to me or a delicate child, and to whom he now also speaks: 'set up for yourself watchtowers or lookouts, to announce to you beforehand that so great a happiness of all things is coming.' And what follows, 'bitternesses', which in Hebrew are called 'themrurim', for which Symmachus translated 'changes', indicates this: that she ought to weep, either for her ancient sins or from the greatness of her joy, and to be converted to God with her whole mind, and to set or direct her heart into the way by which she set out; for by that way she will return. And what the Seventy said in place of this: give your heart to your shoulders, signifies that she ought to join her thoughts to works, or to contemplate the shoulders of those carrying her, bringing her back from captivity; which Isaiah sets out more fully in the camels and chariots and litters, asserting that they are to be brought back. 'Return', he says, 'virgin Israel, return to your cities, which you deserted when they were captive! How long will you be dissolved in negligence and wander in deep error? Look at what I am about to say, and attend diligently to whence so great a blessedness is to be hoped for by you; hear what you had never known before! The Lord has created a new thing upon the earth: without the seed of a man, without any intercourse and conception, a woman shall encompass a man in the bosom of her womb, who, according to the increments of age indeed, through wailings and infancy will be seen to advance in wisdom and age, but a perfect man will be contained in a woman's belly for the usual months.' Hence both Symmachus and Aquila translated in accordance with our edition. But what the common edition meant in this place I could say, and could find some sense, were it not sacrilege to argue about the words of God with human sense. Theodotion, however, himself also agreeing with the common edition, translated: the Lord has created a new salvation, in salvation man shall go about, putting the singular for the plural; and at the same time this too is to be noted, that the nativity of the Savior and the conception of God is called 'creation'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.23-24":["Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: they shall still say this word in the land of Judah and in its cities, when I turn back their captivity: the Lord bless you, beauty of righteousness, holy mountain! And Judah shall dwell in it, and all its cities together, husbandmen and those who drive flocks. LXX: thus said the Lord of powers, the God of Israel: they shall still say this speech in the land of Judah and in its cities, when I bring back its exile: blessed be the Lord upon his righteous holy mountain, and those who dwell in the land of Judea and in every city of it with the husbandman; and he shall be lifted up in the flock. According to the Hebrew it is plain that at the return of Israel, its captivity having been turned back into its own land, the cities of Judah dwell, and it is said to them one by one: the Lord bless you, who is truly the beauty of righteousness and the holy mountain, in which whoever dwells will fear no snares; and Judah will dwell without iniquity in its cities, and there will be husbandmen and a multitude of cattle. This seems to have been fulfilled in part under Zerubbabel and Ezra, but let the fullness of the prophecy be referred to the times of Christ: either to the first coming, when these things were done spiritually, or to the second, when all things are fulfilled — according to us spiritually, according to the Jews and our Judaizers carnally. Further, according to the Seventy this is the sense: 'this speech shall still be spoken in the land of Judah and in its cities, when I turn back its captivity'. What shall be spoken? Blessed be the Lord upon his righteous holy mountain. The mountain which deserves to receive the name 'of righteousness and of holiness' is no other than the Savior; for the rest, it is foolish, with Jewish error, to believe an irrational and insensible mountain 'righteous and holy'. He it is of whom it is also written in what follows: and in every city of it — understood: of the Savior — with the husbandman; no doubt it signifies God, of whom it is written in the Gospel too: I am the vine, you the branches, and my Father is the husbandman; whence also the Apostle says: you are God's husbandry, God's building. And what follows: and he shall be lifted up in the flock, shows this: that in the several flocks the righteous and holy one and husbandman, the Lord himself, is lifted up, and in his servants and believers ascends to the heights.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.25":["For I have inebriated the weary soul — or for I have inebriated every thirsting soul — and every hungering soul I have satisfied — or I have filled —, therefore I awoke and saw, and my sleep was sweet to me. The change of persons makes the understanding of the prophets obscure. The Lord had said: they shall still say this word in the land of Judah and in its cities, when I turn back their captivity. And what shall they say? No doubt this, which follows: the Lord bless you, beauty of righteousness, holy mountain, and the rest. And again, while they say these things, the Lord answers: for I have inebriated the weary soul — or thirsting — and every hungering soul I have satisfied. And while he says these things, the people that had come from captivity answers: therefore I awoke and saw, and my sleep was sweet to me. For the Lord inebriates the weary or thirsting soul, and says in the Gospel: whoever thirsts, let him come to me and drink, and: whoever believes in me, as the Scripture says, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water; and he satisfies every hungering and thirsting soul, of which thirst and hunger the same testifies in the Gospel: blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be satisfied. And it is to be noted that 'inebriation' in this place is put in a good sense, of which in the Canticle too it is said: eat, friends, drink and be inebriated, dearest ones! With this inebriation Joseph too was inebriated with his brothers at midday.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.26":["And those who had been weary and had hungered, inebriated and satisfied, give thanks, answering: 'I awoke and saw the Lord', namely raising them up and saying: arise, you who sleep, and rise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you. 'And my sleep', he says, 'was sweet to me, so that I might imitate the words of my Lord, who says: I slept and was put to rest; and I rose up, because the Lord sustained me'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.27":["Behold, days are coming, says the Lord, and I will sow the house of Israel and the house of Judah with the seed of men and the seed of beasts. And as I watched over them to pluck up and to demolish and to scatter and to destroy and to afflict, so I will watch over them to build and to plant, says the Lord. In those days they shall no longer say: the fathers have eaten a sour grape and the teeth of the sons are set on edge; but each one shall die in his own iniquity; every man who eats the sour grape, his teeth shall be set on edge. 'House' and 'house', that is, the house of Israel and the house of Judah, is not in the Seventy, but only: Israel and Judah, so that it reads: and I will sow Israel and Judah. And what he added: with the seed of men and the seed of beasts, we ought to refer to the rational and the simple.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.28":["And as at the beginning of Jeremiah it was said to him: 'behold, I have set you this day over nations and over kingdoms, to pluck up and pull down and destroy and scatter and build and plant, — and I watched over them, to do what I threatened, — so', he says, 'now I will watch over them to build and to plant; God's indeed husbandry, God's building you are.' All promises of this kind are thought, according to the Jews and our Judaizers, to be fulfilled in a kingdom of a thousand years. But we, since the Apostle says: I planted, Apollos watered, but God gave the increase, and the prophet Isaiah records that the Savior is the builder of hedges and of houses, maintain that they were fulfilled spiritually at the first coming of Christ — and fulfilled in part, not in whole, because now we see in a mirror and in an enigma, and we do not know as we ought to know; but when that which is perfect has come, then that which is in part will be done away — or at any rate we believe that they are to be fulfilled at the second coming, when the Lord will appear in his majesty and the fullness of the nations has come in, so that all Israel may be saved, and not at all in part, through individuals, but that God may be all in all.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.29-30":["And what is brought in: in those days they shall no longer say: the fathers have eaten a sour grape and the teeth of the sons are set on edge, and the rest, we discussed more fully in the explanations of Ezekiel, when we interpreted that place: son of man, what is this parable to you among the sons of Israel, who say: the fathers have eaten a sour grape and the teeth of the sons are set on edge? As I live, says the Lord, if this parable shall be any longer in Israel! for all souls are mine; as the soul of the father, so also the soul of the son; the soul that sins, it shall die. By this we learn that it is not the Lord who makes death, but sin; for the soul that sins, it shall die. And this is said in the present place: that Israel will not at all stumble forever because of the sins of the fathers, but will be saved by its own merits and by faith in Christ after many ages. And it is to be observed that vices and sins are called 'a sour grape', so that the teeth of those who eat are set on edge and cannot perceive the sweetness of him of whom it is said: taste and see that the Lord is sweet! Whoever does not understand the Scriptures as the truth of the matter holds eats a sour grape; whence also all heretics, believing perverse things, cannot eat the bread that comes down from heaven, but their teeth are set on edge, not by the harshness of the foods, but by the fault of the teeth.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.31-32":["Behold, days are coming, says the Lord, and I will strike — or I will dispose — for the house of Israel and the house of Judah a new covenant — or testament — not according to the covenant — or testament — which I made with your fathers on the day when I took their hand to lead them out of the land of Egypt, a covenant — or testament — which they made void; and I ruled over them — or I neglected them —, says the Lord. And this will be the covenant — or testament — which I will strike with the house of Israel after those days, says the Lord: I will put my law in their inward parts — or in the mind — and in their heart I will write it, and I will be to them for God and they shall be to me for a people. And they shall no longer teach, each one his neighbor and each one his brother, saying: know the Lord, for all shall know me, from the least of them to the greatest, says the Lord, for I will be propitious to their iniquity and of their sin I will be mindful no more. The apostle Paul — or whoever else wrote the epistle — used this testimony to the Hebrews, and all churchmen after him say that all things were fulfilled at the first coming of the Savior, and that the New Testament, that is, the Gospel, succeeded to the Old Testament, and that from it the law of the letter has been exchanged for the law of the spirit, so that all the sacrifices too, and circumcision, and the sabbath, might be fulfilled spiritually. But that we put 'pactum' [covenant] for 'testamento' [testament] belongs to the Hebrew truth, although a testament too is rightly called a covenant, because in it is contained the will and the attestation of those who enter into a covenant. When Israel was led out of the land of Egypt, so great was God's familiarity with that people that he says he took it by the hand and gave a covenant, which they made void; and therefore the Lord neglected them.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.31-34":["From this it is clear that, according to the understanding of this perikopēs [passage], the foregoing is to be understood of the first coming of the Savior, when both peoples, Israel and Judah, were joined to each other. But if it raises a scruple for anyone why he said: I will dispose for the house of Israel and the house of Judah a new covenant — or testament — not according to the covenant which I made with your fathers, let him understand that the first Church of Christ was from the Jews, and that the Lord Savior came to them and said: I have not come except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel, and that the apostles confirmed this very thing: it was necessary that the word of God be spoken first to you; but since you reject it and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the nations. For it was not right to give the children's bread to dogs; but because the sons were unwilling to receive the father coming into his own, he gave to all the power that those who received him should become sons of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.33-34":["But now in the Gospel, after the cross, the resurrection and the ascension, he promises that he gives a covenant not on tablets of stone, but on fleshly tablets of the heart, and that, when the testament of the Lord has been written in the mind of believers, he himself is to them for God and they are to him for a people, so that they do not at all seek Jewish teachers and the traditions and commandments of men, but are taught by the Holy Spirit, if only they deserve to hear: you are the temple of God and the Spirit of God dwells in you. The Spirit, however, breathes where he wills, and has diverse graces, and the knowledge of the one God is the possession of all the virtues. 'And this', he says, 'will come about because I will be propitious to their iniquity and their sin I will remember no more.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.35-36":["Thus says the Lord, who gives the sun for the light of the day, the order of the moon and of the stars for the light of the night, who troubles the sea and its waves sound; the Lord of hosts is his name. If these laws — or these statutes — cease before me, says the Lord, then the seed of Israel too shall fail, so as not to be before me all the days. And at the beginning of Genesis we read that the sun was set in the heaven for the light of the day, and the moon too and the stars for the light of the night, and in the psalm: day to day utters the word and night to night shows knowledge, in that day and night succeed one another in turn. 'As', he says, 'the order of things, and especially of the heavenly stars, cannot be changed, and the waves of the sounding sea roll to the shores, and the terrible roar of the gulfs and of the swelling waves is heard, and it cannot advance further than has been prescribed by the command of God, according to that saying: you have set a bound which they shall not pass over, nor shall they turn back to cover the earth, so', he says, 'the seed and race of Israel will by the will of the Lord be perpetual and will never fail.' But 'laws' here are not to be understood as the Mosaic ones, but as the constitution and order of nature. Let us ask the Jews: 'if the heavens shall perish and all shall grow old like a garment, and it is said to God: but you yourself are, and your years do not fail, how can the seed of Israel be perpetual?' For either, when the heavens perish, the seed of Israel too will perish, or, if it is perpetual, then neither will the heavens perish. But if Scripture cannot lie and the heavens are going to perish, then the seed of Israel too will perish, especially since Jacob too says to his sons: come, and I will declare to you what is to come in the last days; and when it is said: in the last days, then the world will cease to be and another dispensation of things will come about. This against them. For the rest, for our own people the Gospel too shows that this world is not perpetual, saying: heaven and earth shall pass away, and: behold, I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the age. Let us also say it otherwise: that as long as this world lasts, the seed of Israel and the Jewish nation will remain, not in those who are now unbelieving, but in those who believed with the apostles and through the apostles, so that a remnant might be saved.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.37":["Thus says the Lord: if the heavens above can be measured and the foundations of the earth beneath searched out, I too will cast off the whole seed of Israel for all that they have done, says the Lord. LXX: thus says the Lord: if the heaven is raised higher and if the floor of the earth is lowered beneath, I too will not reject the race of Israel, says the Lord, for all that they have done. In this place the Hebrew differs much from the common edition. Let us speak first according to the Hebrew: 'if the heavens above can be measured and their height known, or the foundations of the earth searched out and their extremities comprehended by reason, I too', he says, 'will cast off the whole seed of Israel for all that they have done, says the Lord. But as that is impossible, that we should know the summits of the heavens and the foundations of the earth, so this too will be impossible, that I should cast off the whole seed of Israel. But if I cast off the whole seed of Israel, then the summit of the heavens and the extremities of the lands will be measured'. This syllogism is woven in the Gospel too, when an impossible thing is compared with an impossible one: a camel will more easily enter through the eye of a needle than a rich man into the kingdoms of heaven. For as that cannot happen, so neither will this be able to happen; but if this happens, then that too will happen which was thought impossible. Those err, then, who expound this place otherwise, citing also that testimony, that the Son could have asked the Father and brought twelve legions of angels to his aid. The Seventy translated the contrary of this sense, saying: if the heaven is exalted higher and the floor of the earth lowered beneath, I too, he says, will not reject the seed of Israel, says the Lord, for all that they have done. If this is so, the nation of Israel will be rejected; for as the heaven cannot be higher than what it is, nor the earth lower than what it is, so too the race of Israel will by no means be able to be approved. If we see the Jews glorying in this testimony according to the Hebrew, let us agree with them that the whole seed of Israel has not been cast off; for not all have been cast off, but only those who were unbelieving.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.38-39":["Calling, therefore, upon the Lord Savior, who has the key of David, who opens, and no one shuts, and who shuts, and no one opens, who also opened the sealed book of Isaiah and of all the prophets, and whom the twenty-four elders holding harps adored, because he alone could unlock the divine mysteries, let us approach the building of the city to which the prophetic word is directed: glorious things are spoken of you, city of God, and: the rush of the river gladdens the city of God. The Church, then, is built from the tower 'of obedience' or 'of grace' and 'of the gifts of God' — for this is what Ananehel means — to the gate of the corner; which, though it seems to have a lofty beginning, yet as long as we abide in this flesh we cannot possess the straight line of truth, but we stand in the corner and in broken lines; and the line of measure goes out further, over against it, that is, against the gate of the corner, upon the hill Gareb, which in our language is rendered either 'sojourning' or 'scab', so that they may teach us that we are strangers and pilgrims, and itching in our ears, and readily giving consent to the novelty of the worst doctrines. And it shall go round, he says, Goah, for which the Seventy translated: round about with chosen stones, which are rolled upon the face of the earth and are bound together by the corner stone, as the apostle Peter says: and you yourselves, as living stones, be built up, a spiritual house, a holy priesthood, offering spiritual sacrifices acceptable to God through Jesus Christ.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.38-40":["Behold, days are coming, says the Lord, and the city shall be built for the Lord from the tower of Hananel to the gate of the corner. And the rule — or, according to Symmachus, the line — of its measure shall go out further, over against it, upon the hill Gareb, and it shall go round Goah — or, according to the LXX, of chosen stones — and all the valley of ruins — for which Theodotion put the Hebrew word itself, Phagarim — and the ashes and all Asaremoth — which we better read Asademoth, for which Aquila translated suburbs — to the brook Kidron and to the corner of the eastern gate of the horses, the holy place of the Lord; it shall not be plucked up and shall not be pulled down for ever. Those who accept a thousand-year reign of Christ in the land of Judea, namely the Jews and our Judaizers, try to point out the tower of Hananel and the gate of the corner and the hill Gareb and Goah and the valley Phagarim and all Asademoth and the brook Kidron and the place of the corner of the eastern gate of the horses, and there, they say, the sanctuary of the Lord, that is, the temple, is to be founded and is to remain in perpetuity. And because they cannot show it fulfilled after the captivity, in the times of Zerubbabel and Ezra, they pass to the times of Christ, who they say will come at the consummation of the world, so that a golden and jeweled Jerusalem, according to the Revelation of John, may descend and be built round about in this space of land, that is, from that place to that place. And they catch at this little breeze for their supposition: that from the tower of Anathoth, which today is called Jeremiah's, three miles distant from Jerusalem, as far as the brook Kidron — which is recorded in the Gospel and is in the valley of Jehoshaphat, where there was a garden in which Judas the betrayer handed over the Savior — the foundations of the city are to be laid. 'We are going to read', they say, 'in what follows that Anamehel, son of Shallum, was the cousin of Jeremiah, whose field he bought; and this is the tower of Ananehel', ignorant of the Hebrew truth; for here according to the Hebrew is written: from the tower of Ananehel, namely with 'nun' as the middle letter, but there: behold, Anamehel, son of Shallum, your cousin, will come to you, with 'm' as the middle letter.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.31.40":["And all, he says, the valley Phagarim — which means of ruins — and the ashes — 'it shall go round' is understood —, so that, although we seem to ourselves to be on hills, we may yet always fear ruins and consider ashes and, repenting, say with David: for I have eaten ashes like bread and mingled my drink with weeping. Whence also it is said to those who lie fallen: does not he who falls rise again?, says the Lord. And the whole, he says, Sademoth, which we render as the region of death, dividing one name into two words: 'sade', which is called 'region', and 'moth', which means 'of death', for which Aquila rendered 'suburbs' or 'fields' and 'countryside'. But the 'region' 'of death' is the region of sinners, and the 'suburbs' the region of pleasures, which goes on to the brook Kidron — where the Lord was betrayed —, which means 'darkness'. See how many places the Church has, and that that apostolic saying: 'that it be without spot and wrinkle', is reserved for the future and for the heavenly places. You hear of corners, you hear of scab, you hear of ruins and ashes and the region of death and darkness, and you boast of your virtue and sinlessness! Then follows: and to the corner of the gate. Here too is a corner, so that no true righteousness, no sure victory may be shown in this world. The corner of the gate itself too, though it is 'eastern', whence the light comes forth, is yet called 'of the horses', to teach us that we need running and contest, and that at last we may deserve to hear with the Lord: you mounted your horses, and your riding is salvation. In the eastern gate, in the gate of the four-horse chariots, is the sanctification of the Lord, so that we may think ourselves perfect then, when we have said to the Lord: the chariot of God is ten-thousandfold, thousands of those rejoicing; the Lord is among them. A building of this kind, which is set upon the foundation of Christ, of which the Apostle too speaks: as a wise architect I laid the foundation, is never pulled down, but will remain in perpetuity. Obscure and difficult things must be discussed more broadly, so that we may run through plain things more briefly.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.1-3":["The word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord in the tenth year of Zedekiah, king of Judah; that is the eighteenth year of Nebuchadnezzar. Then the army of the king of Babylon was besieging Jerusalem, and Jeremiah the prophet was shut up in the court of the prison, which was in the house of the king of Judah. For Zedekiah, king of Judah, had shut him up, saying: Why do you prophesy, saying: Thus says the Lord? Not only the words but also the deeds of the prophets are an example to us for virtue. Jeremiah could have announced prosperity and enjoyed the friendship of King Zedekiah, but he preferred to obey God rather than men, and him who can kill both soul and body in Gehenna rather than him who could have power only over the body. And this must be considered: it was the tenth year of Zedekiah's reign, Jerusalem was already besieged and consumed by sword, famine and pestilence, captivity was near, and yet Zedekiah persists in his opinion; and something of his clemency is shown in that he ordered him to be shut up not in the prison but in the vestibule of the prison, in free custody, that is, so that he could not escape, as though all Jerusalem, enclosed by its fortifications, were not a common prison for its inhabitants. That is the eighteenth year of the reign of Nebuchadnezzar, who began to rule in the fourth year of King Jehoiakim. And the whole cause of the king's anger is this: that he speaks in the name of the Lord the things that had been commanded him.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.3-4":["Behold, I will give this city into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall take it. And Zedekiah, king of Judah, shall not escape from the hand of the Chaldeans, but shall be delivered into the hand of the king of Babylon, and his mouth shall speak with his mouth, and his eyes shall see his eyes. And he shall lead Zedekiah into Babylon, and there he shall be until I visit him, says the Lord. But if you fight against the Chaldeans, you shall have nothing prosperous. This was the cause of the king's anger: that he preferred truth to a lie, and said that both the city of Jerusalem and King Zedekiah were to be taken; and, what is more grievous, that he would see the face of the Babylonian king and, humbled and captive, would speak with the madness of a most violent king. For it is a heavier terror to see the one you fear, and to undergo the rebuke of words before the torment of punishments.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.5":["And into Babylon, he says, he shall lead Zedekiah, and there he shall be, for which the Seventy translated: and he shall enter Babylon; of which the one signifies being dragged unwilling, the other going of one's own will. And there, he says, he shall be: he puts an ambiguous word, so as not to seem to prophesy tortures and miseries. And what follows: until I visit him, says the Lord. And if you fight against the Chaldeans, you shall have nothing prosperous, is not in the Seventy. And he prudently tempered the sentence, which can be referred to a good and to a bad sense; for 'visitation', as I have often said, signifies both 'consolation' and 'punishment'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.6-7":["And Jeremiah said: The word of the Lord came to me, saying: Behold, Hanamel, the son of Shallum — or Salom —, your cousin — which in Hebrew is called 'dodach' —, is coming to you, saying: Buy for yourself my field, which is in Anathoth; for the purchase belongs to you by kinship. That the word of God had come secretly to Jeremiah no one could know unless he himself, to whom it had come, disclosed it. And it is foretold to him that Hanamel, his cousin, will come to him and offer him the possession of the field that had been his; and that the place itself is in Anathoth, among the suburban lands which were given by the law to the priests throughout the several tribes and cities; nor was it lawful for a possession to pass from tribe to tribe, nor from one family to another family — whence also the daughters of Zelophehad receive their lot among their brothers — and especially the suburban lands of the priests could be sold to no one else until the year of release, except to him whom nearness of blood required. So his cousin comes to him and offers the purchase, which is owed to him by kinship. Hilkiah and Shallum were full brothers; Jeremiah was the son of Hilkiah, Hanamel the son of Shallum. 'Chelchias' means 'portion of the Lord', 'Hieremias' 'loftiness of the Lord'; and rightly the 'height of the Lord' is born of the 'portion of the Lord'. 'Sellum', again, is rendered into our language as 'peace' or 'peaceable', 'Anamehel' as 'gift' or 'grace of God'; nor shall we wonder that 'peace' and 'grace' are joined to each other, since this is also the opening of the apostolic letters: grace to you and peace. First, then, we merit the peace of God, and after peace grace is born for us, which lies not in the choice of the one who possesses, but of the one who gives. And 'the grace of God' offers the purchase to him who is set in 'lofty places', so that, although he seems exalted, he nevertheless needs the grace of God. That which is often sung by the bride in the Song: my cousin, that is ho adelphidos mou, is called 'dodi' in the Hebrew; therefore he should be called not 'fratruelis' [cousin through a brother] but 'patradelfus', that is 'patruelis'. That Jeremiah was the son of Hilkiah, of the priests who were in Anathoth, in the land of Benjamin, the opening of this book also testifies.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.8-9":["And I understood that it was the word of the Lord, and I bought the field from Hanamel, the son of my uncle, which is in Anathoth, and I weighed out the silver to him, seven and ten silver staters. And I wrote it in a book and sealed it and called witnesses and weighed the silver in the balance. And I took the book of possession, sealed, the stipulations and the ratifications and the seals on the outside. It was indeed hard, almost inconsistent and worthy of laughter, that he who was prophesying that Jerusalem was on the very point of being taken and that all were to be led captive or to perish by sword, famine and pestilence, should buy a field in Anathoth which he was not going to possess. 'But I understood', he says, 'that it was the word of the Lord, and that my purchase was to be fitted to the argument and prophecy of the Lord; and therefore I acquiesced in his command to buy, knowing that the word of God had not come to me in vain about a matter of this kind, and I weighed out ten and seven shekels of silver', which we have rendered as 'staters'. Now a shekel has twenty obols, as is written at the end of the book of Ezekiel. And the prophet bought it for 'ten and seven' shekels, the number in which the servant of the Lord, David, sang on the day on which the Lord delivered him from the hand of all his enemies and from the hand of Saul, and he said: I will love you, O Lord, my strength! The Lord is my firmament and my refuge and my deliverer. My God is my helper, and I will hope in him; my protector and the horn of my salvation. That 'ten' is a mystic number is shown by the decalogue, which was written on tablets of stone by the finger of God, and by the day of fasting and propitiation in the seventh month; that 'seven' too, in which the true sabbath-keeping and rest are found, is holy we prove by many testimonies of the Scriptures, of which I would have set down at least a few, were it not idle to teach what is known.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.8":["And Hanamel, the son of my uncle, came to me according to the word of the Lord, to the vestibule — or court — of the prison, and said to me: Possess — or buy — my field, which is in Anathoth, in the land of Benjamin, because the inheritance belongs to you and you are the kinsman, so that you may possess or buy. What the word of the Lord had announced to the prophet as future was at once fulfilled in deed. 'There came', he says, 'to me Hanamel' — 'the grace of God' —, 'the son of my uncle' — that is, 'the son of peace' —; 'and he came into the vestibule of the prison and said to me the things which the Lord had foretold would be said by him'. And this priestly field, whose purchase and possession was being offered to Jeremiah, is in 'Anathoth', in the land of 'Beniamin', of which the first means 'obedience', the second 'son of the right hand'; and consequently he seeks the purchase of that place in which the 'obedience' and the 'power' of the Lord dwelt. For 'kinsman' the Seventy translated presbyteron, that is 'elder', which does not suit this passage.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.10-11":["In this number, then, the possession is bought by the prophet and priest, and it is written in a book and sealed, and witnesses are called and the silver is carefully weighed, so that all the rights of sale and purchase may be preserved and the possession may be certain, strengthened by stipulations and responses. Let them at least hear this who try to claim for themselves, by calling witnesses, wills that are false and sometimes not even wills at all!\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.12":["And I gave the book of possession to Baruch, the son of Neriah, the son of Maaseiah, in the eyes of Hanamel, my cousin, and in the eyes of the witnesses who were written in the book of purchase, in the eyes of all the Jews who were sitting in the court of the prison. Although the possession was at once to be abandoned — nay rather, it was being bought for posterity, and bought by one who had no sons, for he had not taken a wife —, yet, obedient to the command of the Lord, he performs everything duly and gives the sealed book of possession to Baruch, the son of Neriah, the son of Maaseiah. 'Baruch' in our language is called 'blessed'; he was the son of 'Neri', which means 'my lamp', as the prophet says: your word is a lamp to my feet and a burning light to my paths; and Neriah, Baruch's father, was in turn the son of 'Maasiae', that is, of 'making' and of 'the work of the Lord'. Let us notice, then, with what great privileges of virtue the disciple Baruch ministered to Jeremiah, as David says: he who walked in the undefiled way, he ministered to me. Hence also Elisha, the minister of Elijah, so pleased God that after the translation of his master he merited to receive even a double spirit. I say this for the admonition of those who use the ministry of wicked men and do not dare to cast off those whom they know to be bound to them by the glue of a bad conscience. And the book is handed to Baruch, a man so great and of such quality, in the sight of Hanamel, who had sold, and of the witnesses, who had signed and whose names were contained in the volume of the purchase, and in the presence, he says, of all the Jews who were sitting in the court of the prison, who, that is, had either come to console the prophet or, out of zeal for the fear of God, desired to hear the words of the Lord.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.13-14":["And I commanded Baruch before them, saying: Thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Take these books, this sealed book of purchase and this book which is open, and put them in an earthen vessel, that they may last many days. For thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: Houses and fields and vineyards shall yet be possessed in this land. With all those whom the preceding discourse has named present and looking on, Baruch, the minister and disciple, is commanded — not by the words of his teacher, but by the order of God who commands — to take the books, one sealed, the other open — a custom of purchases still kept to this day, so that what the seals hold shut up within, the open volume displays to those who wish to read it —, both of them in an earthen vessel, that they may last many days. Firm, therefore, and to endure long afterwards was the possession that was guarded with such great care, lest the books of the purchase, if placed outside, should lie open to plunder, or, if buried in the ground, should be dissolved by the moisture of the earth.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.15":["And all this is done so that those who saw it might understand that Jerusalem would again be inhabited and the fields possessed; and although they ought to have understood this by themselves without Jeremiah's word, yet they are admonished by the words of the Lord, and it is said to them: thus says the Lord of hosts, the God of Israel: houses and fields and vineyards shall yet be possessed in this land. This is what Jeremiah had said a little before: I understood that it was the word of the Lord; and for that reason he bought a field which he was not going to possess.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.16-17":["And I prayed to the Lord, after I had delivered the book of possession to Baruch, the son of Neriah, saying: Alas, alas, alas, Lord God — or you who are, Lord God —, behold, you made heaven and earth in your great strength and in your arm outstretched — or lofty —; not difficult for you — or impossible — shall be any word — or according to the Seventy nothing is hidden with you —, you who show mercy to thousands and repay the iniquity of the fathers into the bosom of their sons after them, most mighty, great, powerful — which in Hebrew is called 'gibbor' —, the Lord of hosts — or of powers — is your name! Great in counsel and incomprehensible in thought, whose eyes are open upon all the ways of the sons of Adam — or of men —, to render to each according to his ways and according to the fruit of his devices. After the purchase of the field had been duly performed, and after the sentence of the Lord in which he promised that houses, fields and vineyards would be possessed thereafter, the prophet prays to the Lord and expresses the grief of his heart with groans, saying: alas, alas, alas, Lord God, for which the Seventy translated ho ōn, that is, you who are, Lord God, according to what is said to Moses: go, say to the people of Israel: He who is has sent me; not that others do not exist, but it is one thing to subsist by the gift of the Creator, another by the eternity of one's nature. He praises God and from the creatures proclaims the Creator; and first he extols with his voice his power and mercy and righteousness toward the whole race of men, then he passes to Israel and describes in solemn speech how much he had bestowed on it. And after such great benefits he says that they, unmindful of his good gifts, provoked his clemency to bitterness, so that the city is besieged and, before the enemy's irruption into it takes place, has been consumed by sword and famine and pestilence. All this he set forth beforehand in order to bring in, as a consequence, that which seemed to be said in reproach of the divine sentence: and you say to me, Lord God: Buy the field for silver and call witnesses, when the city has been given into the hands of the Chaldeans? This, for the present, is what the whole perikopē [passage] of this place contains; now let us return to the particulars. You made heaven and earth in your great strength. And John says of the Son: all things were made through him, and without him nothing was made that was made; for this is the 'strength' of the Lord, as the Apostle confirms: 'Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God'. And in your outstretched arm or lofty, both of which are the mark of one who strikes; and this is the 'arm' of which Isaiah also speaks: and to whom has the arm of the Lord been revealed? No word shall be difficult for you — for the things that are impossible with men are possible with God — or, for whom nothing is hidden, according to the word of the psalmist, who says: for darkness shall not be dark to you, and the night shall be lit up as the day.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.18":["You who show mercy to thousands and repay the iniquity of the fathers in the bosom of their sons after them. Great is the clemency of the Creator, to extend his mercy to a thousand generations and to show righteousness at once in the next generation, which itself, however, is mixed with mercy; for he does not punish the offender at once, but waits for repentance, so that, if the children have imitated the vices of their parents, the long-deferred punishment is repaid. Most mighty, great, powerful, the Lord of hosts — or of powers — is your name! These names indicate the power of the Creator; but properly the name of God is 'Father', which is revealed in the Gospel through the Lord, who says: Father, I have manifested your name to men.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.19":["Great in counsel: and does anyone dare to thrust himself into the secret of the Lord and to pass judgment on his judgments! And incomprehensible in thought: him whom thought does not comprehend, how can speech comprehend? Whose eyes are open upon all the ways of the sons of Adam: in vain, then, does man think he can hide from the knowledge of God. And what he adds: to render to each according to his ways and according to the fruit of his devices, indicates this, that at times, because of his exceeding wisdom, his judgments seem unjust; a passage which the Apostle explains more fully to the Romans: do you not know that the goodness of God exhorts you to repentance? But according to your hardness and impenitent heart you treasure up for yourself wrath on the day of wrath and of the revelation of the righteous judgment of God. The later, therefore, the punishment of sinners, the more righteous it is, after the likeness of Pharaoh, who, warned by ten plagues, not punished, and persisting in his hardness, was at last overwhelmed by the waves of the Red Sea.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.20":["You who set signs and portents in the land of Egypt even to this day, and in Israel and among men — or the earthborn — and made for yourself a name as this day. And you brought your people Israel out of the land of Egypt with signs and with portents and with a strong hand and with an outstretched arm and with great terror. And you gave them this land, which you swore to their fathers that you would give them, a land flowing with milk and honey. And they entered and possessed it; and they did not obey your voice and did not walk in your law. All that you commanded them to do they did not do; and all these evils befell them. From the general he passes to the particular and runs through in brief speech what he had bestowed specially on Israel. You who set, he says, signs and portents in the land of Egypt, by which Egypt was afflicted, even to this day, and in Israel and among men or the earthborn. This which is said, 'even to this day', must be joined to what follows, so that we read: 'both in Israel and among all mortals your signs are accomplished daily'; or otherwise: 'you performed signs and portents not only in Egypt, but even to this day the same strength of your mercy has saved your people, and you succor the whole human race with the power of the Creator'. And this is to be noted, that he separates 'Israel' from 'men' and 'the earthborn', according to that saying: Israel is my firstborn son. And made for yourself a name as this day: 'your praises', he says, 'are celebrated in the speech of the whole world.'\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.21":["And you brought your people Israel out of the land of Egypt: he rightly said 'your people'; for at the time when it was brought out, it was serving the commands of the Lord. 'And you brought them out with signs and portents, by which Egypt was struck, and with a strong hand and with an outstretched arm and with great terror', when the Red Sea gave a way to the people of Israel as they crossed and overwhelmed the army of Egypt.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.22":["And you gave them this land, which you swore to their fathers that you would give them, namely, to Abraham and Isaac and Jacob. So they received the land of promise not by their own merit but by the virtues of their fathers, a land flowing with milk and honey; for they could not yet take solid food, but were nourished with the honey and milk of infancy, or at any rate with honey and milk, the fruitfulness and abundance of all things.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.23":["And they entered and possessed it. And at once there was nothing between possession and disobedience; for plenty begets security, security negligence, negligence contempt. And they did not obey, he says, your voice, and did not walk in your law. In vain, then, did they promise in the wilderness, saying: all that the Lord commands we will do; for the reward lies not in the pledge but in the deed; so as to beat back the impudence of those who think that a man can fulfill all that he has promised he will do. All that you commanded them to do they did not do; and certainly they had promised that they would do it. And all these evils befell them; 'evils' to those who suffered them, but 'goods' according to the sentence of the Lord, which renders to each according to his ways.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.24-25":["Behold, siegeworks have been built against the city, that it may be taken, and the city has been given into the hands of the Chaldeans who fight against it, before the face of the sword and of famine and of pestilence; and whatever you have spoken has come to pass, as you yourself see; and you say to me, Lord God: Buy the field for silver and call witnesses, when the city has been given into the hand of the Chaldeans? It was the tenth year of King Zedekiah; for so it is written: the word that came to Jeremiah from the Lord in the tenth year of Zedekiah, king of Judah; then the army of the king of Babylon was besieging Jerusalem, and Jeremiah the prophet was shut up in the court of the prison. And rightly it is now said: 'behold, siegeworks have been built against the city, that it may be taken, and the city has been delivered into the hand of the Chaldeans, and they have no one to conquer, only people to capture; for they have already been consumed by sword, famine and pestilence, and whatever you said we see fulfilled; how then do you say to me, Lord: buy the field for silver and call witnesses, when the city has been given into the hand of the Chaldeans?' Therefore he does not reproach but asks, and wishes to learn not so much for himself as for the others who were sitting in the court of the prison and perhaps were silently reproaching him, how the same prophet, whom they believed to announce the truth, both says that the city is to be taken and buys a field as though he were going to possess it.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.27":["And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, saying: Behold, I am the Lord, the God of all flesh; shall for me difficult — or impossible — be — or be hidden from me — any word? Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I will deliver this city into the hand of the Chaldeans and into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall take it. And the Chaldeans shall come — and shall enter — fighting — or the Chaldeans shall come — against this city, and they shall set it on fire and burn it, and the houses on whose roofs they sacrificed to Baal and poured out libations to strange gods to provoke me. To sad things he joins glad ones, and after the overthrow of Jerusalem he promises a return to the captive people. And first he sets out the causes of the offense and of the righteous fury of God, so that the greater the guilt of the sinners, the more ample the clemency of the Creator toward the sinners. I, he says, am the Lord, the God of all flesh; not of all the nations, nor of the people of Israel, nor indeed, as he is often accustomed to say of the saints, 'the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, the God of Jacob,' but the God, he says, of all flesh, so that he may be believed to have made both the rational and the brute animals himself. For there are those who acknowledge the providence of the Creator as far as rational creatures, but assert that the brutes either perish or live by chance happenings; and the prophetic word declares that there is nothing that escapes the providence and knowledge of God, because some things were created for their own sake, others for the use of men. Shall it be difficult for me — or impossible, or at any rate shall it be hidden from me — any word? We have also said above: the things that are impossible with men are possible with God; and here and in many other places we must take 'word' for 'things'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.28":["But what does it mean that the word came: therefore thus says the Lord? For what had gone before, that he should put a causal conjunction, saying: therefore thus says the Lord? 'Because', he says, 'it is my care to rule all things, to order everything and to render to each according to his ways, therefore I will deliver this city into the hand of the Chaldeans and into the hand of the king of Babylon, and they shall take it'; for first it is surrounded by an army and is taken in Nebuchadnezzar's absence, and Zedekiah is led to Riblah and there is handed over to the king.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.29":["And the Chaldeans, he says, shall come, fighting against this city. Aquila did better, who for what is written, 'shall come', translated eiseleusontai, that is, 'shall enter' the city; for they were not absent, so as to 'come', since they had surrounded Jerusalem, as Scripture testifies: then the army of the king of Babylon was besieging Jerusalem, and then: behold, siegeworks have been built against the city, that it may be taken, and the city has been given into the hands of the Chaldeans. How then shall they 'come' who were present? 'But those who were besieging the city shall enter', he says, 'and shall take it and set it on fire and burn it to the ground' — for the Hebrew word 'bau', by its ambiguity, means both 'shall come' and 'shall enter' — 'and the houses on whose roofs they sacrificed to Baal, the idol of the Sidonians, and poured out libations to strange gods to provoke me, so that they seemed to be heaping insults on the Creator, not through an error of religion but out of a kind of contentiousness'. And just as the world is written to be destined to perish, according to what is written: heaven and earth shall pass away, because it is set in the evil one, so too the very houses and places in which crimes were committed lie subject to the wrath of God. There are those who contentiously refer to the places themselves what is written: 'the place in which the Lord and Savior was crucified is spiritually called Gomorrah and Egypt'. But others think that the whole world is signified under the name of Egypt and Gomorrah; for as Gomorrah was destroyed by divine fire, so the world too is to be burned up by the judgment of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.30":["For the sons of Israel and the sons of Judah were continually — or alone — doing evil in my eyes from their youth. The Hebrew word 'ach' Aquila translated plēn, which signifies the conjunction 'nevertheless'; the first edition of Symmachus and the Seventy and Theodotion translated 'alone'; but the second edition of Symmachus rendered diolou, which we too have followed for the present, so as to say 'continually'. Let us then speak first according to the Hebrew: the sons of Israel and the sons of Judah continually doing evil. 'Both the ten', he says, 'and the two tribes did evil without ceasing, and their perseverance in the worst works was continual'. But if it was continual and always in the whole people, where is everlasting righteousness? But according to the Seventy, who said: alone doing evil, the question arises: had the other nations not done evil at the time when Israel and Judah were sinning? It is resolved thus: he who has the knowledge of God and departs from him sins alone in the eyes of God; but those who remained unbelieving offend as though he did not see and did not care. Hence also David, a holy man, because he had fallen into sin with Bathsheba, the wife of Uriah, afterwards in repentance says: against you alone have I sinned and done evil before you, that is, 'in your sight'. Then it is added: alone doing evil in the eyes and in my sight from their youth. And what follows: the sons of Israel, who until now embitter me by the work of their hands, says the Lord, is not in the Seventy and has been added from the Hebrew. But because they offended continually from their youth up to the present day, the sentence of God is therefore just, and Scripture rightly goes on:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.31":["In my fury and in my indignation this city has become to me from the day on which they built it until this day, on which it is taken away from my sight. If from the time when the foundations of the city were laid until this day, when it was taken and set on fire and removed from the sight of the Lord, it was always in vice and provoked the indignation of the Lord against itself, where is — as we have often said already — the respite from sins?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.32":["Because of the wickedness, he says, of the sons of Israel and of the sons of Judah, which they did, provoking me to anger, they and their kings, their princes and their priests and their prophets, the men of Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem. Because he had said above: in my fury and in my indignation this city has become to me from the day on which they built it until this day, on which it is taken away from my sight, and had shown that in general no one had been without sin, he now enumerates them part by part, both their kings and princes and priests and prophets, and, embracing everything in one word: the men, he says, of Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem. And he rightly did not say: 'my kings and my princes and my priests and my prophets', but, because they had sinned: their kings and their princes and their priests and their prophets.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.33":["And they turned to me their backs and not their faces. According to what is written elsewhere: and they turned against me a withdrawing shoulder. For he who prays, with neck bowed, is cast prone upon the ground; but he who turns his back shows by the very gesture of his body that he disregards the one who threatens. 'And this', he says, 'they were doing'.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","When I taught them at dawn and instructed them, and they would not listen, so as to receive discipline. 'When the darkness of error and all worship of idols had been put to flight, I desired daily to enlighten their hearts, confuted by my sentence, and to teach them what is right'. And to preserve free will, he joins on and says: and they would not listen, so as to receive discipline. There follows:\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.34":["And they set their idols in the house in which my name is invoked, to pollute it. Not only at that time did Judah set up in the temple of God the statue of an idol of which we read at the beginning of Ezekiel, but to this day in the house of God, which is interpreted as the Church, or in the heart and soul of believers, an idol is set up when a new dogma is constructed and, according to Deuteronomy, is worshiped in secret; do you not know, he says, that you are the temple of God and the Spirit of God dwells in you?\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.35":["And they built the high places — or altars — of Baal, which are in the valley of the son of Hinnom, to initiate their sons and their daughters to Moloch. For 'initiate' in the Hebrew is written 'ebir', which Aquila and Symmachus translated 'lead across', the Seventy and Theodotion 'offer'. Of the 'valley of the sons of Hinnom', which in Hebrew is called 'Gehenna', we have spoken more fully above: that it lies below the springs of Siloam and, by its pleasantness, because it is a well-watered place, provoked the people to luxury, which the worship of idols follows. And it is to be noted that 'altars' and 'high places' are called 'bamoth' in the Hebrew language; this for the sake of those who are in doubt what this word means in the book of Samuel and of Kings. Moloch is the idol of the Ammonites, whose name is rendered as 'king'; and divine Scripture signifies that in that very place the people served not only the idol Baal but also Moab and all the demons.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah","Which I did not command them, nor did it come up into my heart, that they should do this abomination and lead Judah into sin. Properly, the tribes of Judah and Benjamin worshiped the images of the demons in the shrine of Baal and of Moloch; but it is evident that the ten tribes, which were called Samaria, Joseph and Ephraim, worshiped the golden calves in Bethel and Dan. And so great was the evil that was done by the people that God testifies that he never thought, nor did it come up into his heart, what they were going to do. But all these things are said anthrōpopathōs [in human terms].\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.36":["And now, because of these things, thus says the Lord, the God of Israel, to this city, of which you say that it is delivered into the hand of the king of Babylon by the sword and by famine and by pestilence. Just as to those who hoped for help and trusted in the strength of the walls it is prophesied that Jerusalem is to be overthrown and the people on the very point of being captured, and before the captivity to perish by sword and famine and pestilence, so to those who despair and, after the overthrow of the city, expect no salvation, he promises his help, so that confidence and pride may merit a just sentence, and despair and humility the help of God.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.37-40":["Behold, I will gather them from all the lands to which I drove them out in my fury and in my wrath and in great indignation, and I will bring them back to this place and make them dwell confidently, and they shall be to me a people and I will be to them a God. And I will give them one heart and one way, that they may fear me all their days, and that it may be well with them and with their sons after them. And I will strike with them an everlasting covenant — or I will appoint an eternal testament — and I will not cease to do them good, and I will put my fear in their heart, that they may not depart from me. And I will rejoice over them when I have done them good — or and I will visit them, to do them good —, and I will plant them in this land in truth — or in faith — with my whole heart and with my whole soul. Many think that this was fulfilled in the time of Zerubbabel, son of Shealtiel, and of Jeshua, son of Jehozadak, the great priest, when Haggai and Zechariah prophesied under Ezra the priest, when the temple too was built and the walls were raised round about under Nehemiah, so that those whom he had previously cast out of Jerusalem in fury and wrath and in great indignation and scattered over the whole world, he afterwards brought back and made to dwell confidently, and they were the people of God and the Lord was their God, and the rest that Scripture goes on to relate. But how this can be fitted to that time: I will make them dwell confidently, and I will strike with them an everlasting covenant or I will appoint for them an eternal testament, cannot be proved at all, since we have read, and the sacred history relates, that they were often taken captive not only by the neighboring nations but also by the Persians and Macedonians and Egyptians and Romans, and are in servitude to this day. Everything, therefore, that we see fulfilled in our time, the time of faith, must be referred to the coming of the Savior; and the election — according to the Apostle — of the remnant has been saved, and they dwell confidently in Christ, and one heart has been given them, according to what is written: and of the multitude of believers there was one heart and soul.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.39-40":["And one, he says, way — him who says: I am the way and the life and the truth —, that they may fear me all their days; for the beginning of wisdom is the fear of the Lord. All, he says, their days: if this does not apply to the Jews, it must be taken of our people, with whom it has been and is and will be well, not only with themselves but also with their sons after them; for he has struck an everlasting covenant with us and will not cease henceforth to do us good. And what follows: and I will put my fear in their heart, that they may not depart from me, so grants free will that the very fear which is bestowed nevertheless remains the grace of the giver.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.41":["'And when I have done them good', he says, 'I will rejoice and be glad', namely, that his creature has been saved; whence also there is joy among the angels in heaven over one sinner who repents. And I will plant them, he says, in this land in truth or — as the Seventy translated — in faith, so as to signify properly the Christian people, whose religion is faith. With my whole heart and with my whole soul: if these are the words of the Lord the Savior, the heart and soul are rightly believed to be his, who says in the Gospel: 'I have power to lay down my soul, and I have power to take it up again'; but if we take it as spoken in the person of God the Father, it must be understood according to that saying: your new moons and sabbaths and feast days my soul hates.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.42-43":["For thus says the Lord: As I have brought upon this people all this great evil, so I will bring upon them all the good that I speak to them. And fields shall be possessed in this land, of which you say that it is desolate because neither man nor beast has remained, and that it has been given into the hand of the Chaldeans. Fields shall be bought with money and shall be written in a book, a seal shall be impressed, and a witness shall be called in the land of Benjamin and round about Jerusalem, in the cities of Judah and in the cities of the hill country and in the cities of the plain — or sephela — and in the cities of the nageb — that is: which are toward the south —, because I will turn their captivity, says the Lord. Although according to the letter these things went before in type after the return from the Chaldeans, when at the command of King Cyrus the people returned to Judea, yet spiritually they are fulfilled more truly and more fully in Christ and in the apostles. Then both men and beasts were brought back into the Church, according to what is written: men and beasts you will save, O Lord, all who are rational and all who are simple.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.32.44":["Then fields were bought with money, that we might make for ourselves friends of the mammon of iniquity, who might receive us into the eternal tabernacles; and they were written in a book — doubtless the book of the living —, and the sign of the standard of the Lord's cross and victory was impressed, and witnesses were called, the martyrs and the whole choir of the saints. In the land of Benjamin, where the 'strength' of the Lord is; and round about Jerusalem, in which is the 'vision of peace' and eternal security; in the cities of Judah, in which is the true 'confession' of Christ; and in the cities of the hill country, one of which is that of which he says: a city set on a mountain cannot be hidden; and in the cities of the plain — which in Hebrew are called 'sephela' —, so that from the deep and low places we may advance across the level of the plains to the heights; and in the cities which are toward the south — for which the Seventy rendered 'nageb' —, where there is 'midday' and the full light of truth. And when all these things have been done, what is written will be fulfilled: I will turn their captivity, says the Lord, of whom it is written: ascending on high he led captivity captive; he received or — as the Apostle says — he gave gifts to men.\n— Commentary on Jeremiah"],"JER.46.25":["Moreover the prophet Jeremiah too understands Amon, or No, as Alexandria in the vision against Egypt, to which he says: Egypt is a beautiful heifer; a goader will come to her from the north. He also adds this more plainly: The daughter of Egypt is confounded, she has been delivered into the hand of the people of the north, says the Lord of powers, the God of Israel. Behold, I will visit upon Amon Menno, that is, upon the people from Alexandria. For Amon, as we have said, means peoples, men the preposition 'from', and No Alexandria. And I will visit, he says, upon Pharaoh, and upon Egypt and upon her gods; and upon her kings, and upon Pharaoh, and upon those who hope in him; and I will give them into the hands of those who seek their lives, and into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and into the hand of his servants.\n— Commentary on Nahum"],"JER.49.9-10":["Jeremiah likewise, though with the order changed: If grape-gatherers had come upon you, would they not have left a cluster? If thieves in the night, they would surely have seized what was enough for them. But I have uncovered Esau, I have revealed his hidden things, and he shall not be able to hide. What he says is this: If thieves and robbers, who are accustomed by night to dig through houses and to seize what is in the houses, had come in to you, and, hidden by the darkness, had gone round the corners of your house, they would surely have carried off what they thought could be enough for them, and would have left something in your dwelling, either through being sated or through not knowing of it. If grape-gatherers had come into your vineyard, and wished either to lay it waste as enemies or, hired by you, to harvest it, however diligent their vintage had been, they would still have left among the branches clusters hidden by the vines and leaves. But the enemies who came to you at the Lord's command — and he means the Babylonians and the army of Nebuchadnezzar — have tracked down everything, all your secrets, and have ranged through the caverns and the holes of the grottoes in which you dwell. And indeed, that we may say something also about the nature of the place, the whole southern region of the Idumeans, from Eleutheropolis as far as Petra and Aila — for this is the possession of Esau — has little dwellings in caves; and because of the excessive heat of the sun, since it is a southern province, it uses underground huts. I have uncovered, he says, Esau, that is, what was pressed down under the earth I have brought into the open, and all the things that you were shutting up have been laid open, and, since I searched out your substance together with the enemies, no secret places could hide it.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"JER.49.15-16":["Jeremiah, of whom we made mention above, agrees in almost the same words, saying: Behold, I have given you small among the nations: contemptible among men; your arrogance has deceived you, and the pride of your heart, you who dwell in the caverns of the rock and strive to lay hold of the height of the hill; when you have exalted your nest like the eagle, from there I will drag you down, says the Lord. In prophetic interpretation we ought to follow our custom, so as first to lay the foundations of history, then, if we can, to raise up lofty towers and the tops of roofs. O Edom, he says, though you are the least among all the nations round about, and small in number in comparison with the other peoples, you are lifted up by pride beyond your strength. And though you dwell in grottoes, nay rather in the caverns of the rocks, lowly and poor, and not possessing the lofty roofs of buildings, you are borne up on high like an eagle, and you swell so much in thought that you believe you dwell among the stars; even if, beyond nature, you could penetrate the heights of heaven, from there I would drag you down and bring you down to the earth, says the Lord God. But that which is added in Jeremiah, And you strive to lay hold of the height of the hill, has opened the riddle, signifying by the hill Mount Zion; and by this he means to be understood either the city of Jerusalem itself or the temple which was founded in it.\n— Commentary on Obadiah"],"JER.49.24-27":["Some think that this prophecy is the same as the one we read in Jeremiah: Damascus is dissolved, it has turned to flight, trembling has seized it, anguish and pains have held it like a woman in labor (Jer 49:24, 27). And again: I will kindle a fire in the wall of Damascus, and it will devour the ramparts of Ben-hadad. But it must be known that Jeremiah describes the Babylonian captivity of the city of Damascus, that is, of the few whom the king of the Assyrians had left in it; whereas Isaiah announces the captivity by the Assyrians that is close at hand. Others suppose that the Roman captivity is foretold, since the people of the Jews too was taken captive, and Damascus, over which Aretas ruled, endured a like servitude: so that all that is written about it is to be transferred to the time of Christ and to the mysteries of the Apostles.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JER.49.28-33":["This, I think, is what the title in Jeremiah expresses: To Kedar and to the kingdoms of Hazor, which Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon struck (Jer 49:28); and at once there follows: Thus says the Lord: Arise and go up to Kedar, and lay waste the sons of the East: they will take their tents and their flocks; they will carry off for themselves their skins and all their vessels and their camels (ibid., 29); and again: For Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon has taken counsel against you, he has devised plans against you. Arise and go up against a nation at ease, dwelling confidently, says the Lord: no doors, no bars for them: they dwell alone. And their camels will be for plunder, and the multitude of their cattle for a prey. And I will scatter to every wind those who have the hair of their head cut round: and from all their borders I will bring destruction upon them, says the Lord: and Hazor will be a dwelling of dragons, deserted forever; no man will remain there, nor will a son of man inhabit it (ibid., 30ff.). I have set down the whole testimony of the prophecy from Jeremiah, so that you may understand without doubt what Kedar is. And consider how he has described exactly the nation of the Ishmaelites, that is, of the Saracens: because they dwell in tents; they hold whatever abodes the night drives them to; they have herds, and flocks, and droves of camels; they have neither doors nor bars, for they do not live in cities but dwell in the wilderness. These too, then, were destroyed by the Babylonians, in that they overthrew to the ground the city of Hazor, which was the metropolis of that nation in the desert; and yet, though their droves of camels and sheep were taken, and their skins and tents divided by lot, the destruction of that whole nation is not signified, because on dromedary camels they are wont to flee a hundred miles and more in a single day through the vast wilderness.\n— Commentary on Isaiah"],"JER.51.7":["Further, as to what he says: The day of the Lord is near upon all the nations, let us read the same Jeremiah and we shall see the cup of the Lord passed round to all the nations. Hence it is said in the same prophet: Babylon is a golden cup in the hand of the Lord, making all the nations drunk. For the Assyrians and Babylonians held all the nations as far as the Propontis and the Scythian sea, and the Ionian or the Aegean. Let us read Herodotus and the Greek and barbarian histories, and we shall see how what is said was fulfilled under the Babylonians and Assyrians: The day of the Lord is near upon all the nations.\n— Commentary on Obadiah","Let someone say: Why did they make golden calves? Our treasure is stored up in earthen vessels. For the men of the church are rustic and simple, but all the heretics are Aristotelians and Platonists. Indeed, that you may know that all worldly eloquence is called gold — that is, since their tongue, as if for its splendor, is called gold: \"Babylon is a golden cup in the hand of the Lord.\" See what he says: \"Babylon,\" of confusion. Therefore this world is a golden cup. From this golden cup all the nations are given drink.\n— Homily 11, on Psalm 77","In place of what we have as \"foreigners,\" in the Hebrew is written Philistim, which is interpreted \"the cup of the draught,\" because they are made drunk from the cup of Babylon, as Jeremiah says: \"The golden cup of Babylon, making all the earth drunk.\" See what he says, the golden cup of Babylon. Babylon is interpreted \"confusion\"; but the golden cup is the doctrines of the philosophers and the eloquence of the orators. For who has not been led astray by the philosophers? Who has not been seduced by the orators of this world? A golden cup they have — outwardly the splendor of eloquence; and inwardly poisons are stored, which could not be hidden unless they were concealed under the appearance of gold. You see indeed the honey of eloquence, and you do not suspect the deadly poisons.\n— Homily 62, on Psalm 82 (Alternate Series)"],"JER.51.9":["finally the angels — who were the guardians of the Jews at that time when the foolish multitude cried out and said: His blood be upon us and upon our children, and the veil of the temple was torn and all the mysteries of the Hebrews were laid open — answered the Lord who commanded them, and said: We have treated Babylon and she is not healed; let us leave her, namely the city of confusion and of vices — whence Josephus too relates in his History that, after the Lord was crucified and the veil of the temple was torn, or the lintel of the temple broke and fell, a voice of the heavenly powers was heard in the inner shrines of the temple: Let us pass over from these seats —.\n— Commentary on Ezekiel"],"LAM.2.18":["Understand my cry. A cry in the Scriptures is not of the voice, but of the heart. Thus to Moses it is said, 'Why criest thou to me?' though Moses had not cried out before. Understand my cry. And the apostle: 'Crying in our hearts, Abba, Father.' Surely he who cries out does not cry in the heart, but cries with the tongue. And how does the apostle say, Crying in our hearts? When therefore our groaning and our conscience make supplication, God understands that cry. Whence Jeremiah also says: 'Let not the pupil of my eye be silent.' See what he says, Let not the pupil of my eye be silent. And sometimes the pupil of the eye cries out to God. Surely if it cries, the tongue cries, not the pupil of the eye. But just as we cry in our hearts when we entreat the Lord with groaning, so when we pour out tears to God, at that time the pupil of our eye cries out to the Lord.\n— Homily 2, on Psalm 5","Hence it is that it is said in Jeremiah: \"The pupil of my eye was not silent.\" See, then, that even the pupil of our eye is not silent, but as often as we shed tears, so often do we cry out to God with our hands.\n— Homily 10, on Psalm 76"],"LAM.4.20":["Adadremmon, for which the LXX translated ῥοῶνος (of the pomegranate grove), is a city near Jezreel which was once called by this name, and today is called Maximianopolis, in the plain of Megiddo, in which Josiah, the just king, was wounded by the Pharaoh surnamed Necho; over him Jeremiah wrote Lamentations, which are read in the Church, and that he wrote them the book of Chronicles testifies. Just as, therefore, at that time, after the sinful kings, all the hope of the people was in Josiah, and when he was slain a great mourning was stirred up in the city, as we read in the Hebrew: The spirit of our mouth, Christ the Lord, was taken in our sins, to whom we said: In your shadow we shall live among the nations. Although others, according to the spiritual understanding, refer this to the Lord Jesus; so, when the Savior has been crucified, the mourning shall be renewed in Jerusalem, as once it was in the city of Adadremmon, in the plain of Megiddo.\n— Commentary on Zechariah"]}});
